{"record_id":"D51-B31-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Google Cloud KMS November 2019","subtopic":"Backward compatibility","concept":"Incompatible rolling deployment","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What deployment failure initiated the Google Cloud KMS incident?","answer":"A new KMS version introduced a feature that was incompatible with older versions of related components. The rollout began in one zone and created a mixed-version state.","context":"Google was rolling out a new version of a low-level key management service.","objective":"Deploy the new KMS version without disrupting dependent services.","attempted_solution":"Perform a staged rollout beginning with a single zone.","failure":"The new binary was not backward-compatible with older related components.","evidence":"Google's official incident report states that the new KMS binary added a feature incompatible with older versions of related components.","cause":"Backward-incompatible software was introduced during a rolling deployment.","root_cause":"Compatibility assumptions between concurrently deployed component versions were incorrect.","impact":"KMS errors eventually propagated into IAM, Storage, Compute Engine, BigQuery, and other services.","containment":"Google completed a roll-forward so affected tasks used a compatible version.","remediation":"Google committed to explicit backward-compatibility testing for implicated components.","lessons":["Mixed-version deployments require explicit compatibility guarantees.","A staged rollout does not protect against incompatibility if old and new versions cannot safely coexist."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Datastore Incident #19006 / November 2019 KMS incident","date":"2019-11-15","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B31-R02","D51-B31-R03"],"relationships":["causes","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B31-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Google Cloud KMS November 2019","subtopic":"Roll-forward recovery","concept":"Recovery constrained by persisted incompatible state","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why did Google need to roll forward rather than simply return to the previous KMS version?","answer":"Google reported that persisted keys created or processed by the new KMS version could not be processed by other components, making the newest version effectively required for compatibility during recovery.","context":"The KMS rollout created a mixed-version state before user-visible impact began.","objective":"Restore KMS consistency across tasks and dependencies.","attempted_solution":"Recover by changing the version state across affected tasks.","failure":"The backward-incompatible persisted state prevented a simple return to the previous version.","evidence":"Google explicitly states that a roll-forward was necessary because persisted keys could not be processed by other components.","cause":"Persistent state crossed a software-version compatibility boundary.","root_cause":"The deployment allowed incompatible persisted data to outlive the compatibility of older software.","impact":"Recovery had to proceed by converging tasks onto the newer version.","containment":"Google rolled forward the affected region.","remediation":"Explicit backward-compatibility testing was planned.","lessons":["Persistent state compatibility must be considered separately from binary compatibility.","Rollback is not always safe when a new version changes persisted state."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Datastore Incident #19006 / November 2019 KMS incident","date":"2019-11-15","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B31-R01","D51-B32-R02"],"relationships":["depends_on","blocks"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B31-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Google Cloud KMS November 2019","subtopic":"Shared dependency","concept":"Low-level dependency amplification","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How did a KMS failure propagate into unrelated-looking Google Cloud services?","answer":"KMS was a low-level dependency used for cryptographic operations by services such as IAM and Storage. Failures in that dependency therefore caused higher-level services to fail permission checks and other operations.","context":"Many services depended on KMS for cryptographic functions.","objective":"Maintain dependent services while KMS was degraded.","attempted_solution":"Continue serving dependent services while recovering KMS.","failure":"Dependent services could not complete operations requiring KMS.","evidence":"Google documents impact across IAM, Compute Engine, Storage, BigQuery, App Engine, Cloud Run, Bigtable, Cloud DNS, and other services.","cause":"Critical-service dependency on KMS.","root_cause":"A shared low-level dependency represented a broad correlated failure domain.","impact":"Multiple products experienced simultaneous errors.","containment":"KMS recovery allowed dependent services to progressively recover.","remediation":"Google planned stronger isolation of shared dependencies across regions.","lessons":["Dependency graphs determine real failure domains.","A highly shared low-level component requires stronger isolation and rollout controls than an isolated application service."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Datastore Incident #19006 / November 2019 KMS incident","date":"2019-11-15","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B31-R01","D51-B31-R04"],"relationships":["causes","depends_on"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B31-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Google Cloud KMS November 2019","subtopic":"Regional isolation","concept":"Rollout crossed intended failure domains","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What failure-domain assumption did the Google KMS incident expose?","answer":"Google's reliability model treated regions as failure-domain boundaries, but the rollout of the KMS component affected multiple regions involved in the incident. Google subsequently planned processes to prevent simultaneous multi-region impact.","context":"The KMS component was rolled out across infrastructure serving multiple regions.","objective":"Prevent one component rollout from simultaneously damaging multiple regional failure domains.","attempted_solution":"Use staged rollout procedures while relying on regional isolation.","failure":"Multiple regions experienced user-visible impact from the KMS failure.","evidence":"Google explicitly states that it would modify rollout processes so multiple regions would not be impacted simultaneously.","cause":"Deployment scope crossed the intended regional isolation boundary.","root_cause":"Rollout governance did not sufficiently enforce failure-domain isolation for the shared KMS component.","impact":"Multiple regions and global services were affected.","containment":"Google converged the affected KMS tasks to a healthy version.","remediation":"Regional rollout isolation and improved canary validation were planned.","lessons":["Failure domains must be enforced by deployment orchestration.","A physical regional boundary is ineffective if rollout control crosses it."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Datastore Incident #19006 / November 2019 KMS incident","date":"2019-11-15","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B31-R01","D51-B32-R01"],"relationships":["causes","violates"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B31-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Google Cloud KMS November 2019","subtopic":"Canary validation","concept":"Insufficient compatibility validation before wider exposure","knowledge_type":"LESSON","question":"What validation improvement did Google identify after the KMS incident?","answer":"Google planned changes to canary and rollout validation processes to improve detection speed and explicit backward-compatibility testing for components involved in the outage.","context":"The initial KMS rollout generated alerts before external user impact, but incompatibility later propagated into production impact.","objective":"Detect unsafe software-version interactions before broad rollout.","attempted_solution":"Use existing staged deployment and canary processes.","failure":"The process did not prevent incompatible versions from reaching a critical shared dependency.","evidence":"Google explicitly lists canary and rollout validation and backward-compatibility testing among the prevention actions.","cause":"Compatibility validation was insufficient for the new version interaction.","root_cause":"Deployment validation did not prove safe coexistence between versions and persisted state.","impact":"A low-level component entered a production failure state affecting many services.","containment":"Google completed a controlled roll-forward.","remediation":"Enhanced canary, rollout, and backward-compatibility testing.","lessons":["Canaries must test compatibility properties, not merely basic health.","Critical rollout validation should include mixed-version and persisted-state scenarios."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Datastore Incident #19006 / November 2019 KMS incident","date":"2019-11-15","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B31-R01","D51-B32-R03"],"relationships":["learned_from","remediated_by"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B32-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Google Cloud KMS November 2019","subtopic":"Detection","concept":"Internal alerts preceded customer impact","knowledge_type":"EXPERIENCE","question":"What did the KMS incident reveal about the relationship between internal detection and customer impact?","answer":"Google received KMS error-rate alerts shortly after the rollout began, while external customer impact occurred later after recovery actions changed the ratio of incompatible and previous-version tasks.","context":"Internal monitoring detected KMS degradation before users were affected.","objective":"Use early internal signals to prevent external impact.","attempted_solution":"Investigate the internal KMS alerts before customer impact became visible.","failure":"The incident nevertheless progressed into customer-facing degradation.","evidence":"Google records an alert six minutes after rollout and later user-visible degradation at 02:39 during the roll-forward.","cause":"Internal service degradation was not fully contained before external propagation.","root_cause":"Detection existed, but the response path did not prevent the incompatible state from becoming customer-impacting.","impact":"Multiple dependent services eventually degraded.","containment":"Engineers completed the roll-forward and restored KMS health.","remediation":"Google planned faster canary and rollout validation.","lessons":["Early detection only reduces impact when response can safely stop propagation.","Internal health thresholds should be connected to deployment gates."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Datastore Incident #19006 / November 2019 KMS incident","date":"2019-11-15","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B31-R05"],"relationships":["detected_by","failed_to_contain"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B32-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Google Cloud KMS November 2019","subtopic":"Recovery side effects","concept":"Roll-forward temporarily increased internal errors","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why did the Google KMS roll-forward temporarily increase internal errors?","answer":"As the roll-forward changed the ratio of tasks running the incompatible new version versus the previous version, KMS experienced a temporary increase in internal errors before the rollout completed.","context":"Recovery required bringing all affected tasks to the newer compatible version.","objective":"Converge the KMS fleet onto a consistent version.","attempted_solution":"Roll forward affected tasks to the new KMS version.","failure":"The transition itself temporarily increased internal KMS errors.","evidence":"Google explicitly states that the roll-forward produced a temporary increase in internal errors before KMS returned to a healthy state.","cause":"Mixed-version transition during recovery.","root_cause":"The recovery operation necessarily traversed a degraded intermediate state.","impact":"Internal errors increased during recovery before user-facing services recovered.","containment":"The roll-forward was completed and KMS returned to healthy operation.","remediation":"Future compatibility testing was planned to reduce unsafe rollout conditions.","lessons":["Recovery procedures can have transient failure amplification.","Recovery plans should explicitly model intermediate states rather than only the start and end states."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Datastore Incident #19006 / November 2019 KMS incident","date":"2019-11-15","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B31-R02","D51-B32-R03"],"relationships":["amplifies","recovery_path"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B32-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Google Cloud KMS November 2019","subtopic":"Long-tail recovery","concept":"Multi-location dependencies prolonged recovery","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why did some Google Cloud services recover later than the KMS component itself?","answer":"Services that integrated with KMS in multiple locations experienced longer-tail errors because each dependent location needed to return to a healthy state before complete service recovery.","context":"KMS was shared across multiple regional and global services.","objective":"Restore all dependent services after KMS recovery.","attempted_solution":"Restore KMS and allow dependent services to recover automatically.","failure":"Some services continued to experience errors after KMS itself was healthy.","evidence":"Google specifically notes that GCS had a longer tail of errors because it integrated with KMS in multiple locations.","cause":"Distributed dependency recovery occurred asynchronously.","root_cause":"Service-level recovery depended on multiple KMS locations rather than a single global health transition.","impact":"Customer-visible errors continued after the primary KMS incident was resolved.","containment":"Affected KMS locations were restored progressively.","remediation":"Dependency isolation and regional rollout improvements were planned.","lessons":["Dependency recovery must be measured at the consumer level.","A dependency being healthy in one location does not imply end-to-end recovery."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Datastore Incident #19006 / November 2019 KMS incident","date":"2019-11-15","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B31-R03","D51-B32-R04"],"relationships":["depends_on","extends"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B32-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Google Cloud KMS November 2019","subtopic":"Service-level impact","concept":"Permission dependency transformed infrastructure failure into application errors","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why did Cloud Run and other serverless products return application-level errors during the KMS incident?","answer":"Many operations required IAM permission checks, and IAM depended on KMS. As the dependency failed, permission checks failed and surfaced as HTTP 5XX responses and elevated service errors.","context":"Higher-level services depended indirectly on KMS through IAM.","objective":"Continue application requests during KMS degradation.","attempted_solution":"Allow higher-level services to operate while the underlying dependency recovered.","failure":"Authorization checks could not complete reliably.","evidence":"Google reports elevated Cloud Run errors caused primarily by permission checks and HTTP 5XX responses.","cause":"Indirect dependency failure through IAM.","root_cause":"Critical authorization operations depended on a shared low-level cryptographic service.","impact":"Application-facing APIs returned errors even though the application services themselves were not the original source of failure.","containment":"KMS and IAM recovery restored dependent operations.","remediation":"Shared-dependency isolation was identified as a follow-up area.","lessons":["Dependency graphs should include authorization and cryptographic paths.","Application error diagnosis must trace through infrastructure and control-plane dependencies."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Datastore Incident #19006 / November 2019 KMS incident","date":"2019-11-15","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B31-R03"],"relationships":["causes","depends_on"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B32-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Google Cloud KMS November 2019","subtopic":"Regional dependency isolation","concept":"Shared dependencies undermined regional failure isolation","knowledge_type":"LESSON","question":"What architectural change did Google identify for shared dependencies after the KMS incident?","answer":"Google stated that projects were already underway to split and isolate shared dependencies for the affected regions.","context":"The KMS failure affected multiple regional services through a common dependency.","objective":"Prevent shared low-level dependencies from coupling otherwise independent regions.","attempted_solution":"Rely on regional service architecture while sharing common infrastructure.","failure":"A common KMS dependency produced correlated regional impact.","evidence":"Google explicitly cites splitting and isolating shared dependencies as a prevention measure.","cause":"Shared dependency crossed regional boundaries.","root_cause":"The dependency topology did not provide the desired independence between regional failure domains.","impact":"Multiple regions were affected by one component failure.","containment":"Recovery of the shared KMS component restored services.","remediation":"Dependency splitting and regional isolation.","lessons":["Regional redundancy is incomplete when regions share critical control dependencies.","Dependency isolation should be verified at the service-graph level."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Datastore Incident #19006 / November 2019 KMS incident","date":"2019-11-15","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B31-R04","D51-B32-R03"],"relationships":["remediates","isolates"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B33-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub May 2019","subtopic":"Unexpected request surge","concept":"Unexpected internal traffic bypassed caching assumptions","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What triggered the Google Cloud Pub/Sub degradation in May 2019?","answer":"An internal user generated an unexpected surge of publish requests. The requests did not cache as expected and produced hotspotting in the underlying metadata storage system.","context":"Pub/Sub depended on metadata storage for publish and subscribe operations.","objective":"Process publish traffic while preserving metadata storage stability.","attempted_solution":"Allow the incoming publish workload to be processed normally.","failure":"Unexpected request volume concentrated load on metadata storage hotspots.","evidence":"Google's official postmortem identifies the unexpected publish surge and failed caching behavior as the trigger.","cause":"Unexpected request surge combined with ineffective caching.","root_cause":"The system's load-distribution assumptions did not hold for the observed request pattern.","impact":"Publish, subscribe, and administrative operations experienced elevated errors and latency.","containment":"Google rate-limited the source of the request surge.","remediation":"Additional caching and storage distribution improvements were planned.","lessons":["Caching assumptions must be validated against adverse request patterns.","Internal workloads can be production-critical failure sources."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub Incident #19001","date":"2019-05-28","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B33-R02","D51-B34-R01"],"relationships":["causes","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B33-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub May 2019","subtopic":"Hotspotting","concept":"Metadata storage hotspot caused service-wide degradation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How did hotspotting in metadata storage affect Pub/Sub?","answer":"The unexpected publish surge concentrated load on metadata storage, activating overload protection that rejected some requests and delayed others.","context":"Metadata storage managed information required by Pub/Sub publish and subscribe operations.","objective":"Maintain low-latency metadata operations under increasing request volume.","attempted_solution":"Continue processing requests through the existing metadata storage architecture.","failure":"Hotspotting overloaded the relevant storage paths.","evidence":"Google states that hotspotting triggered overload protection and caused request rejection and delay.","cause":"Concentrated metadata load.","root_cause":"Load distribution did not adequately absorb the request pattern.","impact":"Pub/Sub experienced elevated error rates and increased latency globally.","containment":"Rate limiting reduced the source traffic.","remediation":"Google planned additional caching, more storage servers, and schema/load-distribution changes.","lessons":["Average capacity can conceal hotspot capacity limits.","Distributed systems must control concentration as well as total load."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub Incident #19001","date":"2019-05-28","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B33-R01","D51-B33-R03"],"relationships":["causes","amplifies"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B33-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub May 2019","subtopic":"Scaling response","concept":"Adding resources did not remove a hotspot","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why did adding additional resources to the impacted storage servers fail to resolve the Pub/Sub incident?","answer":"Google attempted to provide additional resources to the impacted storage servers, but the underlying hotspot remained and error rates stayed elevated.","context":"The engineering response initially treated the problem partly as a resource-capacity issue.","objective":"Increase metadata-storage capacity enough to absorb the elevated load.","attempted_solution":"Add resources to the impacted storage servers.","failure":"Additional resources did not address the concentrated hotspot.","evidence":"Google explicitly states that the 22:48 resource increase did not address the hotspots.","cause":"The bottleneck was concentrated rather than simply a shortage of total resources.","root_cause":"The mitigation targeted capacity quantity rather than the request-distribution mechanism creating the hotspot.","impact":"Service degradation continued until traffic was controlled.","containment":"Non-essential traffic was disabled and later the offending request surge was rate-limited.","remediation":"Google planned improved hotspot detection and load distribution.","lessons":["Scaling out is not a universal response to overload.","Mitigation must target the mechanism producing the bottleneck."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub Incident #19001","date":"2019-05-28","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B33-R02","D51-B34-R02"],"relationships":["failed_to_mitigate","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B33-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub May 2019","subtopic":"Load shedding","concept":"Non-essential traffic reduction improved but did not complete recovery","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What happened when Google disabled non-essential internal traffic during the Pub/Sub incident?","answer":"Reducing non-essential internal traffic improved system behavior but did not fully recover the service because the primary request surge continued to overload the underlying storage system.","context":"Engineers attempted to reduce secondary load before identifying the exact source of the surge.","objective":"Lower storage-system pressure while investigation continued.","attempted_solution":"Disable non-essential internal traffic.","failure":"System behavior improved, but full recovery did not occur.","evidence":"Google explicitly states that the action improved system behavior but did not lead to full recovery.","cause":"Load reduction was incomplete because the primary surge remained.","root_cause":"Mitigation reduced secondary load without eliminating the dominant source.","impact":"Error rates remained elevated after the first load-shedding action.","containment":"The later rate limit on the request source effectively mitigated the incident.","remediation":"Playbooks were to be improved around rate limiting and load shedding.","lessons":["Load shedding is useful even when it is not sufficient.","Mitigation should prioritize the dominant load source once identified."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub Incident #19001","date":"2019-05-28","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B33-R03","D51-B34-R03"],"relationships":["mitigates","partially_resolves"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B33-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub May 2019","subtopic":"Dependency propagation","concept":"Pub/Sub degradation affected dependent products","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Which downstream services were affected by the Pub/Sub degradation?","answer":"Cloud SQL, Cloud Filestore, and App Engine Task Queues experienced indirect effects from elevated Pub/Sub errors and latency.","context":"Pub/Sub was used by other Google Cloud products as part of their workflows.","objective":"Maintain dependent product functionality during Pub/Sub degradation.","attempted_solution":"Allow dependent services to continue operating while Pub/Sub recovered.","failure":"Pub/Sub latency and errors propagated to dependent services.","evidence":"Google explicitly documents indirect impact on Cloud SQL, Cloud Filestore, and App Engine Task Queues.","cause":"Dependency on degraded Pub/Sub operations.","root_cause":"The downstream systems were coupled to Pub/Sub performance and availability.","impact":"Additional products experienced errors, latency, or failed administrative operations.","containment":"Pub/Sub recovery reduced downstream impact.","remediation":"The incident informed load-distribution and caching changes in the underlying storage path.","lessons":["Incident impact assessment must traverse dependency graphs.","A service may be operational while still causing significant downstream degradation."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub Incident #19001","date":"2019-05-28","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B33-R02","D51-B34-R04"],"relationships":["causes","propagates_to"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B34-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub May 2019","subtopic":"Detection and diagnosis","concept":"Detection preceded identification of the storage bottleneck","knowledge_type":"EXPERIENCE","question":"How long did diagnosis take after Google engineers were alerted to the Pub/Sub incident?","answer":"Google engineers were automatically alerted at 21:16, while the underlying storage-system cause was identified at 22:18.","context":"The service had already begun experiencing elevated errors before the underlying bottleneck was identified.","objective":"Move from symptom detection to causal diagnosis.","attempted_solution":"Investigate the service-level error signals and trace the underlying dependency.","failure":"Initial investigation required additional time before identifying the storage system as the responsible layer.","evidence":"Google's timeline records automatic alerting at 21:16 and identification of the storage system at 22:18.","cause":"The customer-facing symptom originated from a lower-level dependency.","root_cause":"Diagnosis required dependency-level investigation beyond the initial Pub/Sub service boundary.","impact":"Mitigation of the dominant load source was delayed until later in the incident.","containment":"Additional load reduction was used while diagnosis continued.","remediation":"Google planned better hotspot detection and improved incident playbooks.","lessons":["Detection and root-cause identification are separate stages.","Observability should support rapid traversal from service symptoms to underlying bottlenecks."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub Incident #19001","date":"2019-05-28","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B33-R02","D51-B34-R02"],"relationships":["detected_by","diagnoses"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B34-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub May 2019","subtopic":"Hotspot diagnosis","concept":"Resource scaling targeted the wrong failure mechanism","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What diagnostic mistake is represented by Google's initial resource-scaling attempt?","answer":"Google attempted to provide additional resources to affected storage servers, but the real problem was request hotspotting rather than insufficient aggregate resources.","context":"Error rates remained elevated despite the additional resources.","objective":"Reduce metadata-storage overload.","attempted_solution":"Increase resources available to impacted storage servers.","failure":"The mitigation did not address the hotspot and service errors remained elevated.","evidence":"Google explicitly reports that the additional resources did not address the hotspots.","cause":"The mitigation was aimed at capacity rather than request concentration.","root_cause":"The initial model of the failure mechanism was incomplete.","impact":"Recovery was delayed until the request source was identified and rate-limited.","containment":"Non-essential traffic was disabled while investigation continued.","remediation":"Google planned improved hotspot detection and load-distribution mechanisms.","lessons":["Mitigation should follow evidence about the bottleneck mechanism.","Scaling without understanding the constraint can consume time without reducing failure pressure."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub Incident #19001","date":"2019-05-28","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B33-R03","D51-B34-R01"],"relationships":["failed_to_mitigate","diagnosed_by"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B34-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub May 2019","subtopic":"Rate limiting","concept":"Rate limiting resolved the dominant overload source","knowledge_type":"LESSON","question":"Which mitigation ultimately reduced the Pub/Sub overload?","answer":"Google identified the source of the request surge and applied a rate limit at 00:19. The rate limit effectively mitigated the incident, after which automated storage mechanisms healed the service.","context":"Earlier resource scaling and general load shedding had not fully restored service.","objective":"Stop the request surge causing metadata-storage hotspotting.","attempted_solution":"Rate-limit the identified source of the unexpected publish requests.","failure":"The earlier mitigation attempts had not eliminated the dominant source of load.","evidence":"Google states that the rate limit effectively mitigated the issue and that the service subsequently healed.","cause":"Excess request rate was the direct pressure source.","root_cause":"The system lacked sufficient protection against the unexpected request pattern before the incident.","impact":"The incident persisted until request rate was constrained.","containment":"The rate limit stopped the dominant surge.","remediation":"Rate limiting and load-shedding improvements were added to future playbooks.","lessons":["Rate limiting can be a correctness-preserving containment mechanism under overload.","Effective mitigation should control the source of harmful work when possible."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub Incident #19001","date":"2019-05-28","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B33-R04","D51-B34-R05"],"relationships":["mitigates","restores"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B34-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub May 2019","subtopic":"Playbook design","concept":"Incident playbooks lacked sufficient hotspot-specific guidance","knowledge_type":"LESSON","question":"What playbook improvement did Google identify after the Pub/Sub incident?","answer":"Google planned to improve incident playbooks with specific guidance for rate limiting, load shedding, and hotspot detection.","context":"The incident required several different mitigation approaches before the dominant request source was identified.","objective":"Reduce future time-to-mitigation for similar overload incidents.","attempted_solution":"Use existing incident response procedures and general mitigation techniques.","failure":"Existing procedures did not immediately provide the most effective hotspot-specific response.","evidence":"Google explicitly names rate limiting, load shedding, and hotspot detection as playbook improvements.","cause":"Operational guidance did not sufficiently encode the observed failure mechanism.","root_cause":"Known overload patterns were not represented with sufficiently specific response procedures.","impact":"Engineers required multiple mitigation attempts before resolving the dominant cause.","containment":"Manual traffic reduction and rate limiting were applied.","remediation":"Update playbooks with the incident's operational learnings.","lessons":["Postmortem learning should become executable incident guidance.","Playbooks should map observable symptoms to mechanism-specific mitigations."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub Incident #19001","date":"2019-05-28","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B34-R01","D51-B34-R03"],"relationships":["learned_from","remediated_by"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B34-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub May 2019","subtopic":"Caching resilience","concept":"Caching failure exposed metadata storage directly","knowledge_type":"LESSON","question":"What role did caching failure play in the Pub/Sub incident?","answer":"The unexpected publish requests did not cache as expected, so more work reached the underlying metadata storage system and contributed to hotspotting.","context":"The metadata storage architecture relied partly on caching to reduce backend load.","objective":"Absorb request volume without overwhelming metadata storage.","attempted_solution":"Rely on the existing caching layer to shield metadata storage.","failure":"The observed request pattern bypassed the expected caching benefit.","evidence":"Google explicitly states that the surge did not cache as expected and later planned an additional caching layer.","cause":"Unexpected request behavior reduced cache effectiveness.","root_cause":"The architecture lacked sufficient protection when the expected cache behavior did not occur.","impact":"Metadata storage became overloaded and Pub/Sub degraded.","containment":"Request rate limiting reduced backend pressure.","remediation":"Google planned an additional caching layer.","lessons":["Reliability cannot depend on a single expected cache behavior.","Caching layers should be evaluated for bypass and adverse-pattern behavior."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Google Cloud","document":"Google Cloud Pub/Sub Incident #19001","date":"2019-05-28","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B33-R01","D51-B34-R03"],"relationships":["causes","remediated_by"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B35-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Cloudflare API November 2020","subtopic":"Partial network failure","concept":"Degraded switch escaped binary failure assumptions","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why was the Cloudflare November 2020 switch failure difficult to handle automatically?","answer":"The switch entered a partially functioning state: some control-plane protocols continued operating while other protocols and portions of the data plane failed. Connected systems therefore did not observe a simple complete-link failure.","context":"Cloudflare's control-plane infrastructure depended on redundant network switches.","objective":"Maintain network availability when one switch becomes unhealthy.","attempted_solution":"Rely on normal redundancy and link aggregation behavior.","failure":"The partially failed switch remained logically present while forwarding only some traffic correctly.","evidence":"Cloudflare describes a switch whose LACP and BGP remained operational while vPC and portions of packet forwarding failed.","cause":"Partial hardware/network failure.","root_cause":"The failure mode violated the assumptions of connected systems that distinguish available from unavailable peers.","impact":"The abnormal state propagated into etcd and later database failover behavior.","containment":"The switch eventually recovered; subsequent service-level containment followed.","remediation":"Cloudflare fixed associated software defects and adjusted resilience behavior.","lessons":["Failure detectors must account for partial and inconsistent failure states.","Redundancy based on binary healthy/unhealthy assumptions can fail under degraded components."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Cloudflare","document":"A Byzantine failure in the real world","date":"2020-11-27","evidence_type":"Official postmortem"},"related_records":["D51-B35-R02","D51-B36-R01"],"relationships":["causes","violates"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B35-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Cloudflare API November 2020","subtopic":"Consensus failure","concept":"Conflicting network views disrupted etcd leader election","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How did the partial switch failure affect etcd?","answer":"Different etcd members had conflicting network views: one node could not reliably reach the leader while another could. This caused repeated leader-election attempts and prevented a leader reachable by the isolated node from being established.","context":"Cloudflare used etcd for strongly consistent distributed state.","objective":"Maintain consensus and leader availability during network degradation.","attempted_solution":"Allow the existing RAFT-based leader-election mechanism to handle the node connectivity problem.","failure":"The partial network failure produced inconsistent connectivity rather than a simple node outage.","evidence":"Cloudflare documents repeated leader elections and ties caused by conflicting member views.","cause":"Partial network connectivity created asymmetric information between cluster members.","root_cause":"The consensus system assumed members were either available or unavailable rather than partially inconsistent.","impact":"The etcd cluster became read-only because leader elections blocked writes.","containment":"Recovery of the faulty switch restored normal connectivity.","remediation":"Cloudflare fixed the cluster-management defect and improved dependency resilience.","lessons":["Distributed systems must be evaluated under asymmetric connectivity.","Consensus correctness does not imply graceful behavior under every network-failure topology."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Cloudflare","document":"A Byzantine failure in the real world","date":"2020-11-27","evidence_type":"Official postmortem"},"related_records":["D51-B35-R01","D51-B35-R03"],"relationships":["causes","propagates_to"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B35-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Cloudflare API November 2020","subtopic":"Automated failover","concept":"Automatic promotion created a replica-availability gap","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What happened after Cloudflare automatically promoted a database replica?","answer":"The promoted database became primary immediately, but Cloudflare's cluster-management system required all replicas to be rebuilt after promotion. This created a period in which replicas were unavailable.","context":"Cloudflare's database architecture used primary, synchronous, and asynchronous replicas.","objective":"Restore database availability automatically after control-plane coordination degraded.","attempted_solution":"Promote the synchronous replica to primary automatically.","failure":"The primary recovered before its replicas were available.","evidence":"Cloudflare states that promotion succeeded without data loss but required rebuilding all database replicas.","cause":"Failover procedure coupled primary promotion with replica reconstruction.","root_cause":"The recovery design assumed replica rebuild could occur without materially affecting workloads requiring a replica.","impact":"One authentication database became overloaded because it depended heavily on an online replica.","containment":"Load was reduced and traffic was redirected to secondary data-center replicas.","remediation":"Cloudflare fixed the cluster-management limitation.","lessons":["Failover success must include post-failover capacity, not only primary availability.","Replica rebuild time is part of failover behavior."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Cloudflare","document":"A Byzantine failure in the real world","date":"2020-11-27","evidence_type":"Official postmortem"},"related_records":["D51-B35-R04","D51-B36-R02"],"relationships":["causes","amplifies"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B35-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Cloudflare API November 2020","subtopic":"Authentication database overload","concept":"Failover removed read-capacity redundancy","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why did Cloudflare's authentication database become overloaded after failover?","answer":"The authentication database normally depended on a replica to handle a large read load. When failover occurred and no replicas were available, the primary had to handle the full workload and became overwhelmed.","context":"The database served authentication for API and dashboard operations.","objective":"Maintain authentication performance after database failover.","attempted_solution":"Promote the synchronous replica and continue service normally.","failure":"The primary lacked the replica capacity normally required for performant operation.","evidence":"Cloudflare explicitly states that the database required an online replica and that the primary was overloaded when none were available.","cause":"Replica loss increased load on the primary.","root_cause":"Failover did not preserve the capacity profile required by the authentication workload.","impact":"API and dashboard availability degraded.","containment":"Cloudflare shed load and redirected some read traffic to secondary data-center replicas.","remediation":"Replica-rebuild and traffic-steering improvements followed.","lessons":["Redundancy must preserve performance capacity, not just data availability.","Failover testing should include workload-specific read/write load."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Cloudflare","document":"A Byzantine failure in the real world","date":"2020-11-27","evidence_type":"Official postmortem"},"related_records":["D51-B35-R03","D51-B36-R03"],"relationships":["amplifies","depends_on"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B35-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Cloudflare API November 2020","subtopic":"Cross-region traffic steering","concept":"Manual read routing limited available redundancy","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why did Cloudflare need manual traffic diversion to secondary replicas?","answer":"Secondary data-center replicas were online and available, but routing read queries to them was not automated. Engineers therefore manually diverted eligible API traffic to the secondary data center.","context":"Cloudflare had geographic database redundancy but lacked automated cross-data-center read steering for this failure mode.","objective":"Use healthy remote replicas to reduce load on the overloaded primary.","attempted_solution":"Manually redirect API reads to available secondary replicas.","failure":"The lack of automated steering increased response complexity and required human intervention.","evidence":"Cloudflare explicitly states that the steering process was not automated and was performed manually.","cause":"Operational traffic failover capability was incomplete.","root_cause":"Geographic redundancy existed at the data layer without equivalent automated routing control.","impact":"API recovery depended partly on manual traffic diversion.","containment":"Engineers manually diverted compatible API traffic.","remediation":"Cloudflare continued improving resilience and traffic steering.","lessons":["Redundancy is most valuable when traffic can automatically exploit it.","Data replication without routing automation leaves recovery capacity partially inaccessible."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Cloudflare","document":"A Byzantine failure in the real world","date":"2020-11-27","evidence_type":"Official postmortem"},"related_records":["D51-B35-R04","D51-B36-R04"],"relationships":["depends_on","mitigated_by"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B36-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Cloudflare API November 2020","subtopic":"Dashboard failover","concept":"Session state prevented seamless cross-region failover","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why could Cloudflare's dashboard not benefit from the same cross-region traffic steering as some API requests?","answer":"Dashboard user sessions stored session information in Redis and could not be moved across data centers without disruption. Therefore actions that improved API availability could worsen dashboard user experience.","context":"Cloudflare's API and dashboard had different session and traffic-routing characteristics.","objective":"Maintain both API and dashboard availability during database degradation.","attempted_solution":"Improve API availability by steering traffic to healthy remote replicas.","failure":"The dashboard session model could not move seamlessly across data centers.","evidence":"Cloudflare explicitly states that user sessions could not currently be moved across the ocean without disruption.","cause":"Session state was geographically constrained.","root_cause":"The dashboard architecture was not designed for simultaneous active-active operation across data centers.","impact":"Dashboard experience became worse during some API availability improvements.","containment":"Cloudflare moved authentication calls back toward the primary data center.","remediation":"Redesign of user-session behavior was already underway and received additional incident relevance.","lessons":["Active-active failover requires session-state compatibility.","Different customer interfaces may have different failover capabilities despite sharing backend services."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Cloudflare","document":"A Byzantine failure in the real world","date":"2020-11-27","evidence_type":"Official postmortem"},"related_records":["D51-B35-R05","D51-B36-R02"],"relationships":["blocks","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B36-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Cloudflare API November 2020","subtopic":"Auto-remediation","concept":"Fast automatic remediation became harmful under an abnormal failure mode","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why did Cloudflare reconsider the speed of automatic database promotion?","answer":"Cloudflare had automated database promotion because prior promotion times were considered too slow. During this incident, rapid promotion created a situation where the promoted primary lacked replicas and became overloaded, so Cloudflare adjusted the automation threshold afterward.","context":"Automatic failover was designed to reduce recovery time.","objective":"Restore a healthy primary database quickly.","attempted_solution":"Automatically promote a synchronous replica as soon as the coordination state indicated a problem.","failure":"Rapid promotion occurred before sufficient replica capacity was available.","evidence":"Cloudflare states that automatic promotion was previously considered desirable but that the incident showed the cure could be worse than the disease for at least one database.","cause":"Automation optimized for failover speed without enough workload-specific safety conditions.","root_cause":"The remediation trigger did not account sufficiently for post-promotion replica availability.","impact":"The authentication database became overloaded.","containment":"Load was shed and remote replicas were used.","remediation":"Cloudflare adjusted the automatic-promotion configuration.","lessons":["Automation speed is not equivalent to recovery quality.","Auto-remediation should consider post-action system capacity and safety conditions."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Cloudflare","document":"A Byzantine failure in the real world","date":"2020-11-27","evidence_type":"Official postmortem"},"related_records":["D51-B35-R03","D51-B35-R04"],"relationships":["causes","remediated_by"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B36-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Cloudflare API November 2020","subtopic":"Load shedding","concept":"Service degradation was used to preserve authentication capacity","knowledge_type":"EXPERIENCE","question":"How did Cloudflare contain overload on the primary authentication database?","answer":"Cloudflare reduced the rate of SSL certificate pushes, email delivery, and other features so the authentication database could devote more capacity to API and dashboard operations.","context":"The primary authentication database became overloaded after failover removed its normal replica capacity.","objective":"Protect critical authentication traffic by reducing lower-priority work.","attempted_solution":"Shed non-critical or lower-priority load from the database.","failure":"Normal workloads competed with critical authentication operations during the degraded state.","evidence":"Cloudflare explicitly documents dialing back several feature rates to give the database more capacity.","cause":"Shared database capacity was insufficient after failover.","root_cause":"Critical and non-critical workloads competed for the same degraded resource.","impact":"Without load shedding, authentication availability could have degraded further.","containment":"Feature rates were reduced.","remediation":"Cloudflare continued tuning involved systems for dependency failures.","lessons":["Load shedding should prioritize essential customer paths.","Degraded-mode operation should define which workloads can be sacrificed to preserve core functionality."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Cloudflare","document":"A Byzantine failure in the real world","date":"2020-11-27","evidence_type":"Official postmortem"},"related_records":["D51-B35-R04","D51-B36-R04"],"relationships":["mitigates","protects"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B36-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Cloudflare API November 2020","subtopic":"Redundancy modeling","concept":"Layered redundancy did not cover the combined failure mode","knowledge_type":"LESSON","question":"Why did Cloudflare describe the incident as difficult to model despite extensive redundancy?","answer":"Each layer had redundancy, but the failure propagated through degraded states and interactions between layers rather than through complete component failure. The combination created a failure sequence that individual redundancy mechanisms did not fully prevent.","context":"Cloudflare had redundant switches, databases, racks, power, and geographic replicas.","objective":"Understand why multiple redundancy layers did not prevent the incident.","attempted_solution":"Rely on independent redundancy at each infrastructure layer.","failure":"The combined failure mode crossed assumptions between network, consensus, database, and application layers.","evidence":"Cloudflare states that every system appeared redundant and no system fully failed, yet the cascade remained difficult to model and anticipate.","cause":"Interactions between degraded components.","root_cause":"Reliability analysis focused heavily on individual component failure rather than correlated degraded states.","impact":"A multi-layer cascade caused six hours and 33 minutes of API/dashboard impact.","containment":"Load shedding, manual traffic steering, and replica recovery restored service.","remediation":"Cloudflare fixed the cluster-management defect and continued tuning involved systems.","lessons":["Reliability testing must include composed failure modes.","Redundancy at individual layers does not guarantee resilience to cross-layer degraded-state interactions."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Cloudflare","document":"A Byzantine failure in the real world","date":"2020-11-27","evidence_type":"Official postmortem"},"related_records":["D51-B35-R01","D51-B35-R02","D51-B36-R02"],"relationships":["generalizes","derived_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B36-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Cloudflare API November 2020","subtopic":"Recovery completion","concept":"Replica rebuild completion restored normal performance","knowledge_type":"EXPERIENCE","question":"What event finally restored Cloudflare database performance to normal?","answer":"When the first database replica finished rebuilding, it automatically returned to service and performance resumed to normal levels. Cloudflare then restored previously reduced service rates and allowed asynchronous work to catch up.","context":"The authentication database had been overloaded because replica capacity was unavailable.","objective":"Restore sufficient replica capacity for normal workload distribution.","attempted_solution":"Wait for database replica reconstruction while reducing load and using remote redundancy.","failure":"Recovery remained degraded until a local replica became available.","evidence":"Cloudflare records that the first rebuilt replica immediately re-entered service and performance returned to normal.","cause":"Database workload required replica capacity for normal operation.","root_cause":"Replica rebuild time became a critical recovery dependency.","impact":"API and dashboard performance remained degraded until replica availability returned.","containment":"Load was reduced and remote read replicas were used.","remediation":"Cloudflare fixed the replica-rebuild limitation in its cluster management system.","lessons":["Recovery dependencies should be explicit in service-level objectives.","A nominally healthy primary may remain operationally degraded until supporting replicas recover."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Cloudflare","document":"A Byzantine failure in the real world","date":"2020-11-27","evidence_type":"Official postmortem"},"related_records":["D51-B35-R03","D51-B36-R02"],"relationships":["recovery_path","restores"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B37-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Spotify January 2023 outage","subtopic":"Maintenance dependency","concept":"Configuration dependency became active during repository restoration","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What actually triggered Spotify's January 2023 DNS outage?","answer":"Scheduled GitHub Enterprise maintenance did not directly cause the outage. When the repository was brought back from maintenance, a newly introduced DNS-component failure mode caused invalid configuration to be applied across the resolver fleet.","context":"Spotify's DNS resolvers obtained configuration from multiple sources including its Git repository.","objective":"Restore the Git repository without destabilizing internal DNS.","attempted_solution":"Complete scheduled Git repository maintenance and restore normal repository availability.","failure":"Restoration caused invalid DNS configuration to propagate across the resolver fleet.","evidence":"Spotify explicitly states that GHE going down did not cause the outage; the failure occurred as GHE came back up and invalid configuration was applied.","cause":"Repository restoration activated an invalid configuration path.","root_cause":"A DNS component change created a new failure mode involving repository-state transitions.","impact":"DNS resolver failures caused widespread internal and production-service failures.","containment":"Spotify mitigated internal tooling and then production traffic.","remediation":"Spotify fixed the bug and planned to decommission the failing DNS component.","lessons":["Maintenance recovery paths can trigger latent defects.","Failure analysis must distinguish the initiating dependency event from the actual fault activation mechanism."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Spotify Engineering","document":"Incident Report: Spotify Outage on January 14, 2023","date":"2023-02-15","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B37-R02","D51-B37-R03"],"relationships":["causes","activated_by"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B37-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Spotify January 2023 outage","subtopic":"Invalid configuration","concept":"Resolver crashloop caused by invalid fleet-wide configuration","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What happened to Spotify DNS resolvers when invalid configuration was applied?","answer":"Resolvers receiving the invalid configuration entered crash loops and could no longer serve internal DNS responses.","context":"The invalid configuration propagated across the DNS resolver fleet over approximately an hour.","objective":"Keep internal DNS service available while configuration changed.","attempted_solution":"Apply configuration generated through the normal dependency path.","failure":"The new configuration caused resolver processes to crash repeatedly.","evidence":"Spotify states that a resolver receiving invalid configuration entered a crashloop and became unable to serve internal DNS queries.","cause":"Invalid configuration was distributed to production resolvers.","root_cause":"The configuration pipeline lacked sufficient protection against the newly introduced invalid state.","impact":"Internal DNS availability progressively deteriorated.","containment":"Mitigation was applied first to internal tooling and later production traffic.","remediation":"The underlying bug was fixed and the failing component was scheduled for decommissioning.","lessons":["Configuration distribution is a production execution path and requires strong validation.","A single invalid configuration can become fleet-wide failure when propagation is highly automated."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Spotify Engineering","document":"Incident Report: Spotify Outage on January 14, 2023","date":"2023-02-15","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B37-R01","D51-B37-R04"],"relationships":["causes","propagates_to"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B37-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Spotify January 2023 outage","subtopic":"Fleet propagation","concept":"Failure expanded gradually across resolver fleet","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How did the Spotify DNS failure grow from a small issue into severe outage?","answer":"About 30 percent of the DNS resolver fleet was down by 00:36, followed by complete resolver-fleet failure by 01:15. The progressive loss of DNS capacity caused internal services and production traffic to fail.","context":"The invalid configuration was applied across the resolver fleet over time.","objective":"Maintain sufficient DNS capacity while defective configuration propagated.","attempted_solution":"Continue normal configuration application and service operation.","failure":"Resolver failures accumulated until the entire fleet was unavailable.","evidence":"Spotify's timeline records approximately 30 percent resolver loss at 00:36 and 100 percent at 01:15.","cause":"Invalid configuration propagated across multiple resolver instances.","root_cause":"The failure mode lacked an effective fleet-level propagation barrier.","impact":"Most Spotify functionality, including playback, became unavailable.","containment":"Mitigation was applied to internal tooling and production traffic after root-cause identification.","remediation":"Infrastructure changes were made to improve resilience against DNS outages.","lessons":["Fleet-wide configuration requires progressive exposure limits.","A gradual failure can be more dangerous than an immediate failure when it silently consumes redundancy."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Spotify Engineering","document":"Incident Report: Spotify Outage on January 14, 2023","date":"2023-02-15","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B37-R02","D51-B38-R01"],"relationships":["amplifies","propagates_to"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B37-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Spotify January 2023 outage","subtopic":"Internal tooling dependency","concept":"Incident response tools failed with production DNS","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why did Spotify's incident triage take longer than expected?","answer":"Internal tooling, including employee VPN access, was itself affected by the DNS failure, so responders could not use normal tools and had to develop alternative troubleshooting methods.","context":"The incident affected the same internal infrastructure used by response teams.","objective":"Investigate and mitigate the DNS failure.","attempted_solution":"Use normal incident-response tooling and internal connectivity.","failure":"The response team's own tooling was impaired by the same dependency failure.","evidence":"Spotify explicitly states that internal tooling was inaccessible and that responders had to use novel troubleshooting methods.","cause":"Response infrastructure shared the failed DNS dependency.","root_cause":"Incident-response tooling was not sufficiently independent from the production failure domain.","impact":"Triage took longer than expected.","containment":"Mitigation was eventually established for internal tooling before production traffic.","remediation":"Spotify made infrastructure changes to improve resilience against DNS outages.","lessons":["Incident-response tooling should have independence from critical production dependencies.","Break-glass access paths must be tested against the same failures they are intended to diagnose."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Spotify Engineering","document":"Incident Report: Spotify Outage on January 14, 2023","date":"2023-02-15","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B37-R03","D51-B38-R02"],"relationships":["causes","blocks"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B37-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Spotify January 2023 outage","subtopic":"Dependency complexity","concept":"Known dependency still produced an unanticipated failure mode","knowledge_type":"LESSON","question":"Why did existing safeguards around the Git repository not prevent the Spotify outage?","answer":"Spotify knew that its DNS component depended on the Git repository and had multiple safeguards for Git outages, but a newly introduced DNS-component failure mode caused invalid configuration to be applied specifically when the repository returned from maintenance.","context":"The dependency itself was known and had existing protections.","objective":"Survive Git repository maintenance without DNS disruption.","attempted_solution":"Rely on existing safeguards designed for Git outages.","failure":"The new failure mode was outside the conditions covered by those safeguards.","evidence":"Spotify explicitly states that the dependency was well-known and protected, but the new failure mode still caused the outage.","cause":"A new interaction bypassed existing failure safeguards.","root_cause":"Safeguards covered repository unavailability but not the invalid configuration state introduced during restoration.","impact":"DNS and downstream production services failed.","containment":"Mitigation and eventual service recovery were performed.","remediation":"Bug fix, resilience changes, and planned component decommissioning.","lessons":["Known dependency coverage does not guarantee coverage of all dependency-state transitions.","Resilience tests must include restoration and recovery states, not only steady-state outages."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Spotify Engineering","document":"Incident Report: Spotify Outage on January 14, 2023","date":"2023-02-15","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B37-R01","D51-B38-R03"],"relationships":["learned_from","related_to"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B38-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Spotify January 2023 outage","subtopic":"Detection","concept":"Automatic monitoring detected degradation before total resolver loss","knowledge_type":"EXPERIENCE","question":"When did Spotify's automatic monitoring alert engineers during the DNS outage?","answer":"Spotify's platform engineers were alerted at 00:40 UTC, after approximately 30 percent of the DNS resolver fleet had failed and initial user impact was visible.","context":"DNS failures had begun at 00:15 UTC.","objective":"Detect fleet degradation before total DNS failure.","attempted_solution":"Use automatic monitoring to identify abnormal service behavior.","failure":"Monitoring detected the incident before total resolver loss, but the fleet continued degrading until 01:15 UTC.","evidence":"Spotify records automatic alerting at 00:40 and complete resolver-fleet failure at 01:15.","cause":"Monitoring detected symptoms while the underlying failure continued to propagate.","root_cause":"Detection was not sufficient by itself to prevent continued fleet-wide configuration failure.","impact":"The incident progressed from partial to complete DNS resolver loss.","containment":"Root cause was identified at 02:00 and mitigation followed.","remediation":"DNS resilience improvements and component decommissioning were planned.","lessons":["Detection latency and containment latency are different metrics.","Alerting must be connected to actions capable of stopping fleet propagation."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Spotify Engineering","document":"Incident Report: Spotify Outage on January 14, 2023","date":"2023-02-15","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B37-R03","D51-B38-R02"],"relationships":["detected_by","failed_to_contain"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B38-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Spotify January 2023 outage","subtopic":"Triage impairment","concept":"Responder tooling failure increased diagnosis time","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What operational factor lengthened Spotify's diagnosis time?","answer":"The same DNS failure affected internal response tooling, including employee VPN access, forcing responders to use alternative troubleshooting methods and delaying normal triage.","context":"The outage affected both production traffic and internal infrastructure.","objective":"Diagnose the DNS failure quickly.","attempted_solution":"Use normal internal diagnostic and communication tools.","failure":"Those tools were unavailable or impaired because they depended on DNS.","evidence":"Spotify explicitly attributes longer-than-expected triage to inaccessible internal tooling.","cause":"Incident-response infrastructure shared the production dependency.","root_cause":"Responder independence from the affected failure domain was insufficient.","impact":"Root cause identification occurred at 02:00 after substantial DNS degradation.","containment":"Internal tooling was mitigated by 02:15.","remediation":"Infrastructure resilience improvements were made.","lessons":["Break-glass response systems should be independent of core service dependencies.","Operational resilience includes the ability of engineers to investigate the failure itself."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Spotify Engineering","document":"Incident Report: Spotify Outage on January 14, 2023","date":"2023-02-15","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B37-R04","D51-B38-R01"],"relationships":["amplifies","blocks"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B38-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Spotify January 2023 outage","subtopic":"Component lifecycle","concept":"Complexity-retirement response after recurring infrastructure risk","knowledge_type":"LESSON","question":"What component-level remediation did Spotify plan after the DNS outage?","answer":"Spotify planned to decommission the DNS component that failed as part of a broader project to remove complexity from its DNS infrastructure and increased the priority of that project.","context":"The incident exposed a failure mode in an existing DNS component.","objective":"Remove a component associated with a newly demonstrated failure mode.","attempted_solution":"Fix the component and improve surrounding infrastructure resilience.","failure":"The component represented sufficient architectural risk that Spotify chose to accelerate its planned removal.","evidence":"Spotify explicitly states that it fixed the bug and planned to decommission the failed DNS component.","cause":"A component-level defect exposed broader architectural complexity.","root_cause":"The component was part of a complexity area already targeted for simplification.","impact":"The component contributed to a major outage.","containment":"The immediate bug was fixed and service restored.","remediation":"Decommission the component and improve DNS resilience.","lessons":["Not every incident should end with patching the immediate defect.","When a component repeatedly increases complexity or failure surface, architectural removal can be a valid remediation path."],"validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source":"Spotify Engineering","document":"Incident Report: Spotify Outage on January 14, 2023","date":"2023-02-15","evidence_type":"Official incident report"},"related_records":["D51-B37-R05","D51-B38-R04"],