{"record_id":"D51-B52-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B52","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Deletion Authorization","concept":"Reducing the number of services authorized to delete data reduces destructive attack surface","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"How did Atlassian reduce the number of systems capable of executing deletions?","answer":"Atlassian reported reducing the number of services that could execute deletions to only those requiring deletion for business operations. This was part of its post-incident effort to strengthen deletion controls.","context":"Atlassian November 2022 remediation update.","objective":"Capture least-privilege remediation for destructive operations.","attempted_solution":"Multiple services had technical ability to execute deletion operations.","failure":"Broad deletion capability increased the potential blast radius of an incorrect operation.","evidence":"Atlassian explicitly stated that it reduced the number of services permitted to execute deletions.","cause":"Deletion authority was broader than strictly required.","root_cause":"Destructive capability was not sufficiently constrained to necessary service boundaries.","impact":"A deletion error could potentially affect a broad customer scope.","containment":"The incident response process stopped the original operation and initiated restoration.","remediation":"Deletion authorization was restricted to services with legitimate business need.","lessons":"Destructive capabilities should follow least-privilege principles and be isolated from general-purpose services.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_REMEDIATION_UPDATE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"Update on the April 2022 outage post-incident review","publisher":"Atlassian","publication_date":"2022-11-28","evidence_type":"Official remediation update"},"related_records":["D51-B51-R01","D51-B52-R02"],"relationships":["remediates","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B52-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B52","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Recovery Objectives","concept":"Recovery objectives must account for destructive-event scale","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why did Atlassian connect disaster-recovery improvements to recovery-time objectives?","answer":"Atlassian stated that it would use automation and lessons from the April incident to accelerate its disaster-recovery program and meet the recovery-time objective defined in its policy for incidents at this scale.","context":"Atlassian August 2022 remediation update.","objective":"Capture policy-linked recovery engineering.","attempted_solution":"Manual and coordinated restoration was used for the April event.","failure":"Restoration duration exceeded what automated recovery was intended to achieve for this class of correlated event.","evidence":"Atlassian explicitly connected the accelerated DR program to the defined RTO for this scale of incident.","cause":"Recovery automation was insufficient for the observed incident scale.","root_cause":"Recovery design and automation had not yet met the intended large-scale recovery objective.","impact":"Some affected customer sites required restoration over multiple days.","containment":"Global recovery teams operated continuously.","remediation":"DR automation was accelerated against the organization's defined RTO.","lessons":"Recovery objectives should be measurable and validated against realistic large-scale failure scenarios.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_REMEDIATION_UPDATE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"August update on April 2022 outage post-incident review progress","publisher":"Atlassian","publication_date":"2022-08-10","evidence_type":"Official remediation update"},"related_records":["D51-B51-R04","D51-B52-R03"],"relationships":["remediates","verifies","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B53-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Database Resource Contention","concept":"Low database headroom amplified peak-load incidents","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What underlying database condition contributed to recurring GitHub incidents in March 2022?","answer":"GitHub reported that resource contention in its mysql1 cluster repeatedly degraded services during peak traffic. The primary database had insufficient headroom and was therefore susceptible to a small number of poorly performing queries creating disproportionate impact.","context":"GitHub March 2022 availability incidents.","objective":"Capture resource-headroom failure as an operational pattern.","attempted_solution":"GitHub had been partitioning its main database and adding clusters to support growth.","failure":"Peak-load bursts still exhausted available database capacity and caused degraded write availability.","evidence":"GitHub identified mysql1 resource contention and low primary-database headroom as recurring causes.","cause":"Peak traffic combined with expensive database workload.","root_cause":"Insufficient capacity headroom in a heavily utilized primary database.","impact":"Write operations for GitHub services including Git, issues, and pull requests were degraded.","containment":"GitHub used failover and other mitigations during individual incidents.","remediation":"Database partitioning, additional clusters, and further capacity improvements were pursued.","lessons":"Capacity planning should maintain sufficient headroom for workload variance and expensive-query outliers.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: March 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-04-06","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B53-R02","D51-B53-R05"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B53-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Peak Load","concept":"Peak traffic bursts exposed insufficient database capacity","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What workload pattern repeatedly triggered GitHub database incidents in March 2022?","answer":"GitHub reported bursts of load during peak traffic periods. These bursts exceeded the capacity available in the mysql1 cluster and caused degraded availability, particularly for write operations.","context":"GitHub March 2022 database incidents.","objective":"Capture workload-shape effects rather than relying only on average utilization.","attempted_solution":"The database architecture had been expanded and partitioned to support growth.","failure":"Short-duration traffic bursts still exceeded available capacity.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly described burst load during peak traffic as a recurring characteristic of the incidents.","cause":"Traffic burst combined with limited database headroom.","root_cause":"Capacity planning did not provide enough resilience to peak workload spikes.","impact":"Multiple GitHub services experienced degraded write availability.","containment":"Failover and other mitigations were applied during incidents.","remediation":"GitHub continued database partitioning and capacity improvements.","lessons":"Capacity models should include burst behavior, not only average workload.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: March 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-04-06","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B53-R01","D51-B54-R01"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B53-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Database Query Cost","concept":"A small number of expensive queries can destabilize a high-utilization primary database","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why were poorly performing queries particularly damaging to GitHub's mysql1 cluster?","answer":"GitHub reported that the primary database had low headroom, making it susceptible to a few poorly performing queries. Under those conditions, individual expensive queries could create resource contention that affected many services sharing the cluster.","context":"GitHub March 2022 database incidents.","objective":"Capture nonlinear impact from expensive queries under low headroom.","attempted_solution":"The primary database continued serving multiple core GitHub workloads.","failure":"Expensive queries consumed enough shared resources to degrade unrelated write operations.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly identified low headroom and poorly performing queries as contributing factors.","cause":"High query cost on a heavily utilized shared database.","root_cause":"Insufficient resource margin for query-cost variability.","impact":"Multiple services and write operations degraded together.","containment":"GitHub used failover and incident-specific mitigations.","remediation":"Database partitioning, additional clusters, and visibility improvements were pursued.","lessons":"Shared database capacity must account for worst-case query cost and workload interference, not merely nominal throughput.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: March 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-04-06","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B53-R01","D51-B53-R04"],"relationships":["causes","propagates_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B53-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Write Availability","concept":"Read availability can mask severe write-path degradation","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Which GitHub operations were particularly affected by the March 2022 database incidents?","answer":"GitHub reported degraded availability for write operations on primary services including Git, issues, and pull requests, while some read operations were not impacted. This created a mixed availability state rather than a complete service outage.","context":"GitHub March 2022 availability report.","objective":"Capture partial-service failure states.","attempted_solution":"The service continued serving portions of read traffic while the database was overloaded.","failure":"Write paths could not reliably handle the load even when some read operations remained functional.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly distinguished degraded write operations from unaffected read operations in some incidents.","cause":"Database resource contention affected workload classes differently.","root_cause":"Shared database saturation created selective rather than uniform service failure.","impact":"Users performing write operations were disproportionately affected.","containment":"Failover and other mitigations were applied.","remediation":"Database architecture and capacity improvements were pursued.","lessons":"Incident detection and customer-impact models should track operation classes independently instead of using one service-health flag.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: March 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-04-06","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B53-R03","D51-B53-R05"],"relationships":["related_to","contrasts_with","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B53-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Queue Backlog","concept":"Recovered upstream services can still leave downstream queues saturated","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What secondary failure remained after GitHub's other services recovered in the March 2022 incidents?","answer":"GitHub reported that GitHub Actions queues became saturated after other services recovered. Actions queues had to be enabled gradually to catch up with real-time demand, and delayed Actions execution extended the user-visible impact.","context":"GitHub March 2022 availability report.","objective":"Capture downstream backlog as a post-recovery failure state.","attempted_solution":"GitHub restored the upstream services and then gradually enabled Actions queue processing.","failure":"The accumulated Actions backlog remained after other services had recovered.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly reported saturated Actions queues and gradual catch-up processing.","cause":"Work accumulated while upstream services were degraded.","root_cause":"Downstream queue capacity and backlog recovery were not instantaneous when upstream availability returned.","impact":"Actions remained delayed for multiple hours after other services recovered.","containment":"Queues were enabled gradually to catch up with real-time demand.","remediation":"GitHub incorporated the behavior into availability analysis and mitigation planning.","lessons":"Recovery plans must model backlog drain time and downstream saturation after upstream recovery.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: March 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-04-06","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B53-R04","D51-B54-R05"],"relationships":["propagates_to","follows","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B54-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Database Proxy Failure","concept":"An overloaded database proxy can become the immediate bottleneck for unrelated application work","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What caused GitHub Actions startup delays on June 1 2022?","answer":"GitHub reported excessive load on a proxy server routing traffic to the database. The proxy pods entered crashloops because of out-of-memory errors, delaying the startup of GitHub Actions workflows.","context":"GitHub June 1 2022 availability incident.","objective":"Capture proxy-layer resource exhaustion as a database access failure.","attempted_solution":"GitHub Actions traffic continued through shared database proxy infrastructure.","failure":"Proxy pods exhausted memory and crashlooped, reducing database-access capacity.","evidence":"GitHub reported that the proxy pods were crash-looping due to out-of-memory errors and that Actions startup was delayed.","cause":"Excessive load on database proxy infrastructure.","root_cause":"Data-analysis queries were incorrectly directed at a production database, creating large additional load.","impact":"GitHub Actions workflow startup was delayed for customers.","containment":"GitHub increased available memory for proxy pods and monitored recovery.","remediation":"The queries were moved to a dedicated analytics setup that did not serve production traffic.","lessons":"Production database access should be isolated from analytics workloads with explicit ownership and resource boundaries.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-07-06","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B54-R02","D51-B54-R04"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B54-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Workload Isolation","concept":"Analytics queries were incorrectly routed to a production database","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What was the root cause behind the GitHub June 1 2022 proxy failure?","answer":"GitHub traced the problem to data-analysis queries being pointed at an incorrect database. The queries imposed large load on the production database, which caused the proxy server to crashloop and produced broader GitHub Actions impact.","context":"GitHub June 1 2022 incident.","objective":"Capture workload-routing errors as production reliability failures.","attempted_solution":"Data-analysis workloads accessed a database through the existing production path.","failure":"The workload was sent to a production database that was also serving application traffic.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly identified incorrectly targeted data-analysis queries as the root cause.","cause":"Incorrect database targeting for analytics queries.","root_cause":"Production and analytics workloads lacked sufficiently strong access and routing isolation.","impact":"Proxy memory exhaustion and Actions startup delays occurred.","containment":"GitHub increased proxy memory while the incident was active.","remediation":"Analytics queries were moved to a dedicated analytics environment.","lessons":"Workload isolation should be enforced through architecture and access controls rather than relying solely on operator intent.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-07-06","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B54-R01","D51-B54-R05"],"relationships":["causes","remediates","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B54-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Guardrails","concept":"Production database ownership boundaries were not sufficiently enforced","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What guardrail did GitHub identify after the June 2022 database incident?","answer":"GitHub stated that it was investigating guardrails to ensure production database access was limited to services that own the data. The goal was to prevent unrelated analytical workloads from placing excessive load on production databases.","context":"GitHub June 2022 remediation.","objective":"Capture ownership-based access control as a reliability safeguard.","attempted_solution":"Data-analysis queries were able to access the affected production database.","failure":"Non-owning workloads imposed large load on production infrastructure.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly stated that it was investigating guardrails to limit production database access to services that own the data.","cause":"Insufficient database-access ownership enforcement.","root_cause":"Production data-access boundaries allowed workloads outside the owning service to create significant load.","impact":"Database proxy resources were exhausted and Actions startup degraded.","containment":"Proxy memory was increased during recovery.","remediation":"Dedicated analytics infrastructure and production database access guardrails were pursued.","lessons":"Reliability controls can be enforced through authorization boundaries that encode resource ownership.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-07-06","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B54-R02","D51-B54-R04"],"relationships":["remediates","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B54-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Capacity Mitigation","concept":"Increasing proxy memory mitigated symptoms while workload isolation addressed the cause","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What was the difference between GitHub's immediate and long-term remediation for the June 2022 incident?","answer":"GitHub immediately increased memory available to the database proxy pods, which helped the affected service recover. The deeper remediation moved the problematic analytics queries to dedicated infrastructure that did not serve production traffic.","context":"GitHub June 1 2022 incident.","objective":"Distinguish symptom mitigation from causal remediation.","attempted_solution":"Proxy memory was increased while the incident was active.","failure":"Memory expansion addressed the immediate resource exhaustion but did not remove the workload-routing problem.","evidence":"GitHub documented both the memory increase and the later move of analytics queries to dedicated infrastructure.","cause":"Excessive analytics workload on production database infrastructure.","root_cause":"Incorrect workload isolation.","impact":"Actions startup was delayed and proxy pods crashed.","containment":"Proxy memory was increased.","remediation":"Analytics workloads were isolated from production traffic.","lessons":"Emergency scaling and root-cause remediation should be represented as separate actions in incident knowledge.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-07-06","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B54-R01","D51-B54-R02"],"relationships":["contains","remediates","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B54-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Observability","concept":"Monitoring load on intermediary infrastructure can expose upstream workload problems","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What observability improvement did GitHub identify after the June 2022 Actions incident?","answer":"GitHub stated that it was adding alerts to identify increases in load to the database proxy. The intent was to detect this class of overload earlier before proxy exhaustion caused broader service impact.","context":"GitHub June 2022 remediation.","objective":"Capture monitoring targeted at the immediate bottleneck.","attempted_solution":"The incident was detected through Actions startup latency and proxy failure symptoms.","failure":"The system did not have sufficiently targeted alerting for proxy-load growth before resource exhaustion.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly stated that alerts would be added for increases in proxy load.","cause":"Database proxy load grew until pods entered out-of-memory crashloops.","root_cause":"Observability did not sufficiently expose the precursor metric for this failure mode.","impact":"Actions workflow startup delays occurred.","containment":"Memory capacity was increased.","remediation":"Proxy-load alerts and production-access guardrails were planned.","lessons":"Monitoring should include precursor signals at shared infrastructure bottlenecks, not only customer-facing symptoms.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-07-06","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B54-R01","D51-B54-R03"],"relationships":["remediates","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B55-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Retry Storm","concept":"Over-broad exception retry logic can create self-amplifying load","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What caused the GitHub June 29 2022 migration-related outage?","answer":"A migration service retried requests after receiving 502 errors. Its exception handling captured all exceptions rather than only the intended subset, and a bug caused jobs to continuously requeue themselves. Hundreds of thousands of jobs consequently generated repeated API requests that overwhelmed a database proxy.","context":"GitHub June 29 2022 incident.","objective":"Capture retry amplification as an operational failure.","attempted_solution":"The migration service used retry logic to requeue failed jobs.","failure":"The retry handler captured too broad a set of exceptions and continuously requeued jobs.","evidence":"GitHub documented the 502 responses, broad exception capture, continuous requeue behavior, and resulting proxy overload.","cause":"Over-broad exception handling combined with unbounded retry behavior.","root_cause":"Retry logic transformed transient errors into a rapidly increasing workload.","impact":"Multiple GitHub services including Actions, API Requests, Codespaces, Git Operations, Packages, and Pages were impacted.","containment":"GitHub paused new customer migrations and pruned migration queues.","remediation":"Exception handling was narrowed and retries were limited to a fixed number.","lessons":"Retry logic must be bounded, selective, and designed to avoid positive feedback under shared-service failure.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: July 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-08-03","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B55-R02","D51-B55-R04"],"relationships":["causes","amplifies","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B55-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Retry Bounding","concept":"Unbounded retries converted transient dependency errors into sustained load","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why did the GitHub migration service make the June 2022 outage worse?","answer":"The migration service's exception handling caused failed jobs to repeatedly requeue themselves. Hundreds of thousands of jobs then made identical API requests, overwhelming the database proxy and amplifying the original dependency errors.","context":"GitHub June 29 2022 incident.","objective":"Capture the causal chain from transient error to retry storm.","attempted_solution":"Failed migration requests were automatically retried.","failure":"Retries were effectively unbounded for the captured error class.","evidence":"GitHub documented continuous requeueing and hundreds of thousands of identical API requests.","cause":"Retry amplification.","root_cause":"Retry policy did not limit attempts or sufficiently classify retryable failures.","impact":"Database proxy overload caused broad GitHub service degradation.","containment":"New customer migrations were paused and existing migration queues were pruned.","remediation":"Retry handling was restricted to specific errors and retries were capped.","lessons":"Every automated retry mechanism should define explicit retryable conditions, attempt limits, and backoff behavior.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: July 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-08-03","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B55-R01","D51-B55-R03"],"relationships":["causes","amplifies","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B55-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Retry Classification","concept":"Only explicitly retryable failures should trigger automatic retries","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What retry-policy change did GitHub make after the June 29 2022 incident?","answer":"GitHub updated exception handling so that only a specific set of errors would cause jobs to retry. This prevented unrelated failures from being transformed into repeated requests.","context":"GitHub June 29 2022 remediation.","objective":"Capture selective retry classification as a concrete remediation.","attempted_solution":"The migration service previously retried based on broad exception capture.","failure":"Errors outside the intended retry class triggered repeated job requeueing.","evidence":"GitHub stated that exception handling was changed to retry only a specific set of errors.","cause":"Overly broad retry classification.","root_cause":"The retry policy lacked sufficiently narrow error semantics.","impact":"Large numbers of migration jobs repeatedly called GitHub APIs.","containment":"Migration processing was paused and queues were pruned.","remediation":"Retry classification was narrowed to an explicit error set.","lessons":"Retry eligibility should be based on known transient failure semantics rather than generic exception handling.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: July 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-08-03","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B55-R01","D51-B55-R02"],"relationships":["remediates","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B55-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Queue Control","concept":"Stopping new work and pruning accumulated work can rapidly reduce overload","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"How did GitHub contain the migration retry storm?","answer":"GitHub paused processing of new customer-initiated migrations and pruned queues containing jobs associated with in-progress migrations. Approximately nine minutes after the queue pressure was reduced, affected services began recovering.","context":"GitHub June 29 2022 incident.","objective":"Capture documented queue-level containment.","attempted_solution":"GitHub stopped additional migration work and removed queued work associated with the failing process.","failure":"Existing retry-generated work had already accumulated enough load to overwhelm the proxy.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly documented pausing new migrations, pruning migration queues, and subsequent service recovery.","cause":"Large retry-generated queue volume.","root_cause":"Continuous requeueing amplified workload faster than infrastructure could process it.","impact":"Multiple services experienced degraded availability.","containment":"New migrations were paused and migration queues pruned.","remediation":"Retry behavior was bounded and error classification narrowed.","lessons":"Queue-based systems need explicit emergency controls for admission, draining, cancellation, and backlog pruning.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: July 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-08-03","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B55-R02","D51-B55-R05"],"relationships":["contains","resolves","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B55-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Retry Limits","concept":"Fixed retry limits prevent indefinite failure amplification","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What retry limit did GitHub introduce after the migration incident?","answer":"GitHub changed the migration service to retry a fixed number of times before logging the exception and giving up. This eliminated the continuous requeue behavior that had amplified the June 29 incident.","context":"GitHub June 29 2022 remediation.","objective":"Capture bounded retry as a concrete anti-amplification control.","attempted_solution":"Jobs were automatically retried after exceptions.","failure":"Continuous retries created an uncontrolled workload amplification loop.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly stated that retries were changed to a fixed number of attempts followed by logging and termination.","cause":"Unbounded retry behavior.","root_cause":"Retry termination conditions were absent or ineffective.","impact":"Hundreds of thousands of jobs generated repeated requests.","containment":"Queues were paused and pruned.","remediation":"Fixed retry counts and narrower exception classification were implemented.","lessons":"Retry policies require a terminal state; an automated system must eventually stop retrying and record the failure.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: July 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-08-03","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B55-R02","D51-B55-R03"],"relationships":["remediates","prevents","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B56-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Traffic Throttling","concept":"Client-side throttling thresholds can exceed actual database capacity","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What caused the GitHub token database incident reported in September 2022?","answer":"GitHub reported that a token database experienced performance degradation and increased load. The database reached its maximum concurrent-connections limit, while client-side throttling thresholds permitted more traffic than the database could safely handle.","context":"GitHub September 2022 availability incident.","objective":"Capture mismatch between upstream throttling and downstream capacity.","attempted_solution":"Client-side throttling was intended to limit traffic reaching the token database.","failure":"Configured throttling allowed more traffic than the database's actual capacity.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly identified the mismatch between client-side throttling limits and database capacity.","cause":"Database degradation combined with excessive permitted concurrency.","root_cause":"Traffic-control thresholds were not aligned with actual downstream capacity.","impact":"The impacted service degraded until database capacity and concurrency were adjusted.","containment":"GitHub scaled the impacted database and increased allowed concurrent connections.","remediation":"Monitoring and alerting were improved and throttling mechanisms were reevaluated.","lessons":"Rate limits must be calibrated against measured downstream capacity rather than treated as independent policy values.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: September 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-10-05","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B56-R02","D51-B56-R04"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B56-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Capacity Mismatch","concept":"Rate limiting can fail when it is designed independently from backend capacity","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why did GitHub's throttling mechanism fail to prevent the September 2022 token database overload?","answer":"The throttling thresholds permitted more traffic than the token database had capacity to handle. Because the limits were not aligned with actual backend capacity, throttling itself did not prevent the database from reaching its maximum concurrent-connections limit.","context":"GitHub September 2022 incident.","objective":"Capture capacity-aware throttling failure.","attempted_solution":"Client-side throttling limited request volume according to configured thresholds.","failure":"The configured thresholds were too permissive relative to database capacity.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly described a mismatch between client-side throttling limits and database capacity.","cause":"Incorrect throttling threshold calibration.","root_cause":"Traffic-control policy did not account for the degraded database's effective capacity.","impact":"The token database reached its maximum concurrent-connections limit.","containment":"GitHub scaled the database and adjusted concurrency.","remediation":"GitHub evaluated changes to throttling mechanisms and improved monitoring.","lessons":"Traffic-control policies should be dynamically or empirically tied to downstream capacity and degradation state.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: September 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-10-05","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B56-R01","D51-B56-R03"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B56-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Database Concurrency","concept":"Maximum concurrent connections can become the immediate failure boundary","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What database limit did GitHub hit during the September 2022 incident?","answer":"GitHub reported that the affected token database reached its maximum concurrent-connections limit. This limit became a direct failure boundary once degraded database performance and excessive permitted load combined.","context":"GitHub September 2022 incident.","objective":"Capture connection concurrency as an operational resource constraint.","attempted_solution":"The service continued accepting traffic under configured throttling thresholds.","failure":"Concurrent connections reached the database's configured maximum.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly identified the maximum concurrent-connections limit as part of the incident mechanism.","cause":"Excessive concurrent workload against a degraded database.","root_cause":"Effective demand exceeded the database's available concurrency capacity.","impact":"The impacted service became unhealthy.","containment":"Database capacity was scaled and concurrent connections were adjusted.","remediation":"Monitoring and throttling improvements were pursued.","lessons":"Capacity models must include connection limits and degraded-state capacity, not only CPU or storage metrics.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: September 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-10-05","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B56-R01","D51-B56-R02"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B56-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Monitoring Remediation","concept":"Monitoring and alerting were improved after a capacity-control mismatch","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What observability remediation did GitHub apply after the September 2022 token database incident?","answer":"GitHub reported improving monitoring and alerting to enable faster remediation. It also evaluated changes to throttling mechanisms so that traffic patterns would be better accounted for.","context":"GitHub September 2022 remediation.","objective":"Capture monitoring changes directly linked to a documented failure.","attempted_solution":"Existing monitoring detected service degradation while the database was under pressure.","failure":"The existing controls did not provide sufficient protection against the traffic-capacity mismatch.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly stated that monitoring and alerting were improved after the incident.","cause":"Database performance degradation and excessive permitted load.","root_cause":"Capacity and traffic controls were insufficiently aligned.","impact":"Service degradation occurred before effective mitigation.","containment":"Database scaling and connection adjustments restored health.","remediation":"Monitoring, alerting, and throttling mechanisms were improved or reevaluated.","lessons":"Observability should expose both backend saturation and whether traffic-control policies remain within safe operating capacity.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: September 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-10-05","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B56-R02","D51-B56-R05"],"relationships":["remediates","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B56-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Emergency Scaling","concept":"Scaling and concurrency changes can restore a service after traffic controls fail","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"How did GitHub restore the token database service during the September 2022 incident?","answer":"GitHub mitigated the incident by scaling up the impacted database while allowing a higher number of concurrent connections. The service returned to a healthy state after those capacity changes.","context":"GitHub September 2022 incident.","objective":"Capture the documented immediate recovery action.","attempted_solution":"GitHub increased database resources and concurrency capacity.","failure":"Prior throttling controls had allowed demand to exceed available capacity.","evidence":"GitHub reported that scaling the database and increasing concurrent connections restored the service to health.","cause":"Insufficient effective database capacity under the observed workload.","root_cause":"Traffic controls and database capacity were mismatched.","impact":"The token-dependent service degraded.","containment":"Database capacity and connection limits were adjusted.","remediation":"Monitoring and throttling policy improvements followed.","lessons":"Emergency capacity changes can restore service, but they should be paired with correction of the control-plane assumptions that allowed overload.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: September 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-10-05","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B56-R01","D51-B56-R04"],"relationships":["resolves","contains","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B57-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Alerting Thresholds","concept":"A non-paging alert failed to trigger immediate investigation before customer impact","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why did GitHub not immediately investigate the April 25 2022 database CPU alert?","answer":"GitHub reported that its alerting system detected increased CPU utilization approximately one hour before customer impact, but the threshold was relatively low and the alert was not configured as a paging alert. Investigation therefore did not begin immediately.","context":"GitHub Packages Registry incident on April 25 2022.","objective":"Capture alert-severity configuration as a factor in incident detection.","attempted_solution":"Monitoring detected rising database CPU utilization.","failure":"The alert did not page responders and therefore did not trigger immediate investigation.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly reported that the alert existed before customer impact but was not a paging alert.","cause":"Alert severity and response routing were insufficient for the observed precursor.","root_cause":"The monitoring system detected the signal but did not classify it as requiring immediate human action.","impact":"Database CPU continued rising until customer-facing errors appeared.","containment":"GitHub investigated after customer impact and mitigated the workload.","remediation":"The incident contributed to work on better alerting and throttling behavior.","lessons":"Detection latency depends not only on metric availability but also on alert severity, routing, and response policy.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: April 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-05-04","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B57-R02","D51-B57-R05"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B57-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Unexpected Command Volume","concept":"High-volume use of a command in an unexpected pattern can defeat existing throttling","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What caused the GitHub Packages Registry outage on April 25 2022?","answer":"GitHub reported that a high volume of the Create Manifest command was used in an unexpected manner. The configured database-level throttling criteria were insufficient to limit that workload, allowing database CPU utilization to continue increasing until the registry returned internal server errors.","context":"GitHub Packages Registry incident on April 25 2022.","objective":"Capture unexpected workload-shape failure against throttling controls.","attempted_solution":"Database-level throttling was intended to limit the command's workload.","failure":"The configured throttling criteria did not sufficiently restrict the unexpected command volume.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly identified high Create Manifest volume and insufficient database-level throttling.","cause":"Unexpected high-volume command usage.","root_cause":"Throttle criteria did not sufficiently model the command's actual resource cost under the observed workload.","impact":"GitHub Packages Registry users experienced internal server errors.","containment":"Traffic was subsequently controlled and the service recovered.","remediation":"GitHub investigated throttling and monitoring improvements.","lessons":"Rate limits should account for workload cost and command semantics, not only request counts.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: April 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-05-04","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B57-R01","D51-B57-R03"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B57-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Command Cost","concept":"Request-count throttling can underrepresent expensive operations","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What engineering lesson can be derived from GitHub's Create Manifest incident?","answer":"The documented incident shows that request count alone may not represent backend resource cost. GitHub reported that a high volume of Create Manifest commands caused database CPU pressure despite existing throttling criteria. The resulting lesson is that throttling should consider operation cost where different commands consume materially different resources.","context":"Inference derived from GitHub's April 2022 Packages Registry incident.","objective":"Extract a machine-usable throttling design principle without claiming it was directly stated as such by GitHub.","attempted_solution":"Existing database-level throttling was used to constrain command volume.","failure":"The criteria were insufficient for the observed workload pattern.","evidence":"GitHub documented high Create Manifest volume, rising CPU, and insufficient throttling.","cause":"Command-specific workload cost exceeded assumptions behind throttling.","root_cause":"The throttling model did not sufficiently capture actual resource consumption.","impact":"Package Registry service errors occurred.","containment":"Traffic was controlled after customer impact.","remediation":"Monitoring and throttling mechanisms were investigated.","lessons":"Inference: resource-aware throttling can be more representative than pure request-count limits when operations have unequal cost.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: April 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-05-04","evidence_type":"Official availability report","interpretation_note":"The generalized throttling principle is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B57-R02","D51-B56-R02"],"relationships":["derived_from","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B57-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Detection Lead Time","concept":"A detected precursor is operationally valuable only if connected to action","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What does the GitHub April 2022 incident show about detection lead time?","answer":"GitHub detected increased database CPU approximately one hour before customer impact, but the alert did not page responders. The incident therefore demonstrates that a detection signal can exist well before customer impact while still providing limited operational benefit if it is not connected to an appropriate response path.","context":"Inference derived from GitHub April 25 2022 incident.","objective":"Represent the distinction between observability and actionable detection.","attempted_solution":"Monitoring generated a database CPU alert before user impact.","failure":"The alert did not trigger immediate investigation.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly stated that the alert was not a paging alert and that customer impact occurred later.","cause":"Alert routing and severity did not match the importance of the precursor signal.","root_cause":"Detection was not coupled to an appropriate response mechanism.","impact":"The database continued degrading until customer impact occurred.","containment":"Responders investigated after customer impact and mitigated the workload.","remediation":"GitHub continued improving monitoring and alerting.","lessons":"Inference: detection quality should include timeliness, severity classification, routing, and actionability rather than metric existence alone.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: April 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-05-04","evidence_type":"Official availability report","interpretation_note":"The generalized observability principle is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B57-R01","D51-B57-R05"],"relationships":["derived_from","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B57-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Service Impact","concept":"A single package-registry workload can create user-facing impact without affecting unrelated registries","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Was the GitHub April 25 2022 package-registry failure global across all package registries?","answer":"GitHub reported that the Create Manifest workload caused the GitHub Packages Registry outage described in the incident and that other package registries were not impacted. The failure was therefore scoped to the affected workload and service path rather than every package registry.","context":"GitHub April 25 2022 availability report.","objective":"Capture bounded blast radius despite shared platform infrastructure.","attempted_solution":"Package registry services operated using shared platform infrastructure.","failure":"The affected database workload degraded the GitHub Packages Registry path.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly stated that other package registries were not impacted.","cause":"Workload-specific database pressure.","root_cause":"A command-specific traffic pattern overwhelmed the affected database path.","impact":"Users of the affected GitHub Packages Registry experienced errors.","containment":"The problematic workload was controlled.","remediation":"Throttling and monitoring improvements were pursued.","lessons":"Incident analysis should identify both affected and explicitly unaffected services to avoid overestimating blast radius.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: April 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-05-04","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B57-R02","D51-B58-R04"],"relationships":["bounds","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B58-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Container Registry Load","concept":"Permissive API throttling combined with an expensive database query caused registry failure","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What caused the GitHub Container Registry incident reported in May 2022?","answer":"GitHub reported that a high volume of Put Manifest requests increased database CPU utilization. API-side throttling for the command was too permissive, while a database query was not performant at that scale, producing latency and internal server errors.","context":"GitHub May 2022 Container Registry incident.","objective":"Capture interaction between permissive API throttling and inefficient backend work.","attempted_solution":"API-side throttling was intended to limit Put Manifest request volume.","failure":"The configured threshold allowed more traffic than the database query could handle efficiently.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly identified permissive throttling and a non-performant database query as the causes.","cause":"Excessive command volume combined with inefficient database work.","root_cause":"Traffic limits were too permissive for the actual backend query cost.","impact":"Container Registry users experienced latency, internal server errors, and slow package UI access.","containment":"Requests from all organizations and users were throttled.","remediation":"Database state was reset and front-end/database processes restarted.","lessons":"Backend performance and API throttling must be evaluated together for expensive operations.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: May 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-06-01","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B58-R02","D51-B58-R05"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B58-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Traffic Control","concept":"Global throttling was required when command-specific controls were insufficient","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"How did GitHub contain the May 2022 Container Registry incident?","answer":"GitHub throttled requests from all organizations and users to reduce load on the affected database path. This broad traffic reduction was part of restoring normal operation after the command-specific API threshold proved too permissive.","context":"GitHub May 2022 Container Registry incident.","objective":"Capture emergency broad throttling as a containment mechanism.","attempted_solution":"GitHub applied throttling across organizations and users.","failure":"The existing command-specific API threshold had not sufficiently limited traffic.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly reported broad throttling as part of the incident mitigation.","cause":"Excessive request load against an expensive database operation.","root_cause":"Existing command-level throttling was insufficient for backend capacity.","impact":"Container Registry latency and server errors affected users.","containment":"Requests were throttled broadly.","remediation":"Database state was reset and infrastructure restarted.","lessons":"Emergency controls may need to temporarily widen their scope when narrow controls fail, but the resulting blast radius should be explicitly tracked.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: May 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-06-01","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B58-R01","D51-B58-R03"],"relationships":["contains","resolves","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B58-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Database State Recovery","concept":"Application restart alone was insufficient to restore the affected database state","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What recovery action did GitHub take after throttling traffic during the May 2022 Container Registry incident?","answer":"GitHub reported that restoring normal operation required resetting database state by restarting the front-end servers and then the database. Throttling reduced incoming pressure, but database-state recovery was also necessary.","context":"GitHub May 2022 Container Registry incident.","objective":"Capture multi-stage recovery after overload.","attempted_solution":"GitHub first reduced incoming requests and then reset affected database state.","failure":"Traffic reduction alone did not complete recovery.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly described throttling followed by front-end and database restarts to restore normal operation.","cause":"Database state remained degraded after excessive workload.","root_cause":"An expensive query and excessive traffic produced a degraded database condition.","impact":"Container Registry remained impaired until database state was restored.","containment":"Broad request throttling reduced pressure.","remediation":"Database restart and subsequent query/throttling investigation were performed.","lessons":"Recovery plans for overload incidents should account for residual degraded state after traffic returns to normal.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: May 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-06-01","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B58-R02","D51-B58-R04"],"relationships":["resolves","follows","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B58-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Query Performance","concept":"A query that performs acceptably at low scale can become a production failure at higher concurrency","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What database behavior contributed to the GitHub May 2022 Container Registry outage?","answer":"GitHub found that a database query was non-performant under the degree of scale generated by the Put Manifest traffic. The query therefore became a major contributor to CPU utilization and service degradation as request volume increased.","context":"GitHub May 2022 Container Registry incident.","objective":"Capture scale-dependent query performance failure.","attempted_solution":"The existing database query served Put Manifest requests under normal workload conditions.","failure":"The query did not perform adequately under the observed request scale.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly identified the query as non-performant under that degree of scale.","cause":"High-volume execution of an inefficient query.","root_cause":"Query performance characteristics did not remain acceptable as workload scale increased.","impact":"Database CPU increased, followed by latency and internal server errors.","containment":"Requests were throttled.","remediation":"GitHub investigated throttling and database behavior after restoring the service.","lessons":"Performance validation must include realistic concurrency and scale, not only functional correctness or low-volume query tests.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: May 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-06-01","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B58-R01","D51-B58-R05"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B58-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Rate Limit Design","concept":"Rate limits must be evaluated against backend performance characteristics","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What generalized lesson can be derived from the GitHub May 2022 Container Registry incident?","answer":"The incident combined permissive API throttling with a database query that degraded at higher scale. The evidence supports an engineering principle that rate limits should be evaluated against actual backend resource consumption and worst-case query behavior rather than configured independently from implementation cost.","context":"Inference derived from GitHub May 2022 incident.","objective":"Extract an implementation-aware rate-control principle.","attempted_solution":"API throttling constrained request volume according to configured thresholds.","failure":"The threshold allowed traffic that the backend query could not process efficiently.","evidence":"GitHub documented both the permissive throttle and non-performant query.","cause":"Mismatch between permitted request volume and backend resource cost.","root_cause":"Traffic controls were not sufficiently coupled to backend performance characteristics.","impact":"Registry users experienced latency and errors.","containment":"Broad throttling was applied.","remediation":"GitHub investigated throttling behavior and database performance.","lessons":"Inference: rate-limit policies should be capacity-aware and tested under realistic workload distributions.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: May 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-06-01","evidence_type":"Official availability report","interpretation_note":"The generalized rate-control principle is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B58-R01","D51-B58-R04","D51-B56-R02"],"relationships":["derived_from","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B59-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Deployment Regression","concept":"A recently deployed database validation change introduced invalid persisted values","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What caused the GitHub October 2022 Issues incident?","answer":"GitHub reported that a recently deployed change introduced a database validation requiring a value to be present. The change did not correctly establish a default value in every scenario, allowing null values to be stored and causing errors when affected records were later retrieved.","context":"GitHub October 2022 availability incident.","objective":"Capture deployment-induced data-integrity regression.","attempted_solution":"A new database validation was deployed to enforce a required value.","failure":"The validation did not correctly populate a default in every scenario and allowed null values to enter the data.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly documented the validation change, missing default behavior, null values, and subsequent record retrieval errors.","cause":"Incomplete default-value handling in a new validation path.","root_cause":"The deployed change did not correctly handle every data-creation scenario.","impact":"GitHub Issues APIs returned increased error responses.","containment":"GitHub rolled back the change.","remediation":"Additional protections against missing values and improved testing were added.","lessons":"Validation logic must be tested across every data-creation path, including omitted and defaulted values.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: October 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-11-02","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B59-R02","D51-B59-R04"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B59-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Rollback","concept":"Rollback rapidly reduced errors after a database validation regression","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"How did GitHub recover from the October 2022 Issues incident?","answer":"GitHub initiated a rollback of the recently deployed change. Within minutes, API error responses began decreasing toward normal levels, and the Issues service was returned to green after all relevant metrics became healthy.","context":"GitHub October 2022 availability incident.","objective":"Capture rollback as the effective containment mechanism for a deployment regression.","attempted_solution":"GitHub rolled back the recently deployed database validation change.","failure":"The new validation had already introduced invalid values and caused retrieval errors.","evidence":"GitHub documented the rollback time and subsequent decrease in API errors.","cause":"Deployment regression in database validation.","root_cause":"Incorrect default-value handling allowed null values to be introduced.","impact":"Issues API error responses increased.","containment":"Rollback removed the defective behavior.","remediation":"Missing-value protections and improved testing were added.","lessons":"Rapid rollback remains an important containment mechanism when a recent deployment has a strong causal correlation with a service regression.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: October 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-11-02","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B59-R01","D51-B59-R05"],"relationships":["resolves","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B59-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Test Coverage","concept":"Pre-production validation did not expose every deployment failure state","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What testing problem did GitHub identify after the October 2022 database validation incident?","answer":"GitHub reported improving testing around the affected area after the incident. The deployed validation had not correctly handled every scenario, indicating that the existing test coverage did not expose the missing-default behavior before production deployment.","context":"GitHub October 2022 incident remediation.","objective":"Capture a concrete test-coverage gap without inventing test details.","attempted_solution":"The database validation change passed the deployment process and entered production.","failure":"A scenario involving missing/default values was not correctly handled before deployment.","evidence":"GitHub stated that testing was improved around the particular area following the incident.","cause":"Insufficient coverage of data-default scenarios.","root_cause":"The deployment validation did not adequately exercise all relevant data-state combinations.","impact":"Null values entered the database and later caused record retrieval errors.","containment":"The change was rolled back.","remediation":"Testing and missing-value protections were improved.","lessons":"Database validation tests should cover omitted fields, defaults, nulls, legacy records, and all relevant write paths.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: October 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-11-02","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B59-R01","D51-B59-R04"],"relationships":["remediates","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B59-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Observability Integrity","concept":"Inaccurate deployment dashboards can hide pre-production error signals","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What observability defect did GitHub identify during the October 2022 incident?","answer":"GitHub reported that its deployment dashboards contained inaccurate data for pre-production errors and that those dashboards were fixed after the incident. This was a separate observability weakness associated with the deployment process.","context":"GitHub October 2022 availability report.","objective":"Capture monitoring-data integrity as a first-class operational failure.","attempted_solution":"Deployment dashboards were used to observe errors during the release process.","failure":"Some pre-production error information shown by the dashboards was inaccurate.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly stated that the deployment dashboards contained inaccurate data for pre-production errors and were subsequently fixed.","cause":"Incorrect deployment-observability data.","root_cause":"The monitoring presentation layer did not accurately represent some pre-production error conditions.","impact":"Release operators could receive an inaccurate picture of deployment health.","containment":"The problematic deployment was rolled back.","remediation":"Deployment dashboards were fixed and testing improved.","lessons":"Observability systems require validation of their own correctness because inaccurate monitoring can weaken otherwise sound deployment controls.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: October 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-11-02","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B59-R03","D51-B60-R02"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B59-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Data Validation","concept":"Runtime validation can prevent bad data but cannot repair incorrectly initialized data by itself","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What broader lesson follows from GitHub's missing-default database incident?","answer":"The incident shows that adding validation around a field does not guarantee correct data initialization. Validation, default assignment, existing-data compatibility, and retrieval behavior must be tested as one lifecycle because a partial validation implementation can still permit invalid state.","context":"Inference derived from GitHub October 2022 incident.","objective":"Extract a lifecycle-oriented validation principle.","attempted_solution":"A new database validation required a value to be present.","failure":"Default handling was incomplete and null values were still introduced.","evidence":"GitHub documented the validation regression and subsequent testing improvements.","cause":"Incomplete handling of data initialization.","root_cause":"Validation and default-value behavior were not comprehensively tested together.","impact":"Later reads of affected records generated errors.","containment":"The change was rolled back.","remediation":"Missing-value protections and testing were improved.","lessons":"Inference: data validation should cover the full lifecycle from creation through persistence, migration, and retrieval.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: October 2022","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2022-11-02","evidence_type":"Official availability report","interpretation_note":"The lifecycle validation principle is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B59-R01","D51-B59-R03"],"relationships":["derived_from","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B60-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Cross-Incident Retry Failure","concept":"Automatic retries can convert dependency failures into overload","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What cross-incident pattern connects the GitHub June 2022 migration incident with other overload failures?","answer":"The GitHub migration incident directly demonstrated that automatic retries can amplify a dependency failure into additional load. Combined with the Pub/Sub and other database incidents in D51, the evidence supports modeling retry-generated traffic as a separate load source during failure analysis.","context":"Cross-incident synthesis using official GitHub and Google Cloud postmortems already represented in D51.","objective":"Create a reusable failure-amplification pattern.","attempted_solution":"Automated systems retried operations after failures.","failure":"Retry-generated requests increased load on already degraded dependencies.","evidence":"GitHub documented continuous migration-job requeueing and resulting proxy overload; Google Cloud documented request surge and storage hotspotting during Pub/Sub degradation.","cause":"Failure-triggered workload amplification.","root_cause":"Automated recovery behavior was not sufficiently bounded against degraded dependency capacity.","impact":"Underlying services received additional workload while already unhealthy.","containment":"GitHub paused and pruned migration queues; Google rate-limited the causal request source.","remediation":"Retry limits, selective retry classification, rate limiting, and load-shedding controls were documented.","lessons":"Inference: autonomous systems should model retry traffic as a potentially dangerous secondary load source during degraded states.","validation_status":"CROSS_INCIDENT_SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"MULTIPLE_PRIMARY_SOURCES","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_titles":["GitHub Availability Report: July 2022","Google Cloud Pub/Sub Incident #19001"],"publishers":["GitHub","Google Cloud Status Dashboard"],"evidence_type":"Cross-incident synthesis","interpretation_note":"The generalized retry-amplification principle is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B55-R01","D51-B55-R05","D51-B47-R04"],"relationships":["derived_from","synthesizes","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B60-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Observability Failure","concept":"Monitoring systems themselves can become unreliable sources of operational truth","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What cross-incident lesson emerges from GitHub's alerting and deployment-dashboard failures?","answer":"GitHub documented both a pre-impact CPU alert that did not page responders and deployment dashboards containing inaccurate pre-production error data. Together these incidents support treating observability data quality and response routing as part of the reliability system rather than assuming monitoring is inherently trustworthy.","context":"Cross-incident synthesis from GitHub April and October 2022 availability reports.","objective":"Create a machine-usable observability-failure pattern.","attempted_solution":"Monitoring and deployment dashboards were used to detect and evaluate system health.","failure":"One alert did not trigger immediate investigation and some dashboard data was inaccurate.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly documented both conditions in its official availability reports.","cause":"Insufficient alert routing and inaccurate deployment telemetry.","root_cause":"Observability controls themselves lacked sufficient validation and response integration.","impact":"Potentially useful early signals were less effective during incident prevention and deployment evaluation.","containment":"Individual service incidents were mitigated through rollback or workload control.","remediation":"GitHub fixed inaccurate dashboards and continued improving monitoring and alerting.","lessons":"Inference: autonomous engineering systems should assign confidence to telemetry and validate monitoring pipelines before using them for high-impact decisions.","validation_status":"CROSS_INCIDENT_SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"MULTIPLE_PRIMARY_SOURCES","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_titles":["GitHub Availability Report: April 2022","GitHub Availability Report: October 2022"],"publishers":["GitHub"],"evidence_type":"Cross-incident synthesis","interpretation_note":"The generalized observability-confidence principle is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B57-R01","D51-B57-R04","D51-B59-R04"],"relationships":["derived_from","synthesizes","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B60-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Safety Boundaries","concept":"High-impact automation needs independent semantic and runtime safeguards","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What cross-incident lesson connects Atlassian destructive automation with GitHub deployment failures?","answer":"Atlassian's incident involved incorrect resource scope in a destructive script despite peer review, while GitHub's October incident involved a deployment that mishandled a data-validation scenario. Together they support using multiple independent controls: semantic target validation, staged rollout, runtime health checks, rollback, and post-action verification.","context":"Cross-incident synthesis from official Atlassian and GitHub incident reports.","objective":"Derive a safety architecture for high-impact automation.","attempted_solution":"Both organizations used established review or deployment processes before the failures occurred.","failure":"Existing controls did not detect the specific semantic or state-transition errors before impact.","evidence":"Atlassian documented peer review that did not validate target scope; GitHub documented a deployed validation change that mishandled defaults.","cause":"Control coverage gaps across different failure dimensions.","root_cause":"No single control verified every critical property of the operation.","impact":"Atlassian experienced destructive multi-site deletion; GitHub experienced API errors from invalid persisted state.","containment":"Atlassian stopped execution and restored sites; GitHub rolled back the deployment.","remediation":"Both organizations strengthened safeguards and testing.","lessons":"Inference: high-impact autonomous actions should require multiple independent checks rather than relying on one review or validation stage.","validation_status":"CROSS_INCIDENT_SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"MULTIPLE_PRIMARY_SOURCES","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_titles":["Post-Incident Review on the Atlassian April 2022 outage","GitHub Availability Report: October 2022"],"publishers":["Atlassian","GitHub"],"evidence_type":"Cross-incident synthesis","interpretation_note":"The generalized safety architecture is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B51-R02","D51-B52-R02","D51-B59-R01","D51-B59-R02"],"relationships":["derived_from","synthesizes","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B60-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Capacity-Aware Automation","concept":"Automated control thresholds must adapt to degraded backend capacity","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What cross-incident lesson emerges from GitHub's database throttling failures?","answer":"GitHub documented multiple incidents where configured throttling allowed more traffic than the backend could handle, including the September token database incident and May Container Registry incident. The evidence supports treating backend capacity as an input to automated traffic-control decisions rather than using fixed thresholds without capacity context.","context":"Cross-incident synthesis from GitHub May and September 2022 availability reports.","objective":"Derive a capacity-aware control principle for autonomous systems.","attempted_solution":"Fixed or configured throttling thresholds were used to control traffic.","failure":"Thresholds permitted workloads that exceeded degraded backend capacity.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly documented mismatches between throttling limits and database capacity.","cause":"Static traffic-control thresholds were not sufficiently aligned with backend resource state.","root_cause":"Control policy and resource-capacity models were separated.","impact":"Database concurrency limits and CPU pressure caused service degradation.","containment":"Traffic was throttled more broadly and capacity was increased.","remediation":"GitHub evaluated throttling changes and improved monitoring.","lessons":"Inference: autonomous traffic controllers should incorporate current capacity, degradation state, and workload cost when selecting safe limits.","validation_status":"CROSS_INCIDENT_SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"MULTIPLE_PRIMARY_SOURCES","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_titles":["GitHub Availability Report: May 2022","GitHub Availability Report: September 2022"],"publishers":["GitHub"],"evidence_type":"Cross-incident synthesis","interpretation_note":"The generalized adaptive-control principle is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B56-R01","D51-B56-R02","D51-B58-R01","D51-B58-R05"],"relationships":["derived_from","synthesizes","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B60-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Failure Learning","concept":"Incident knowledge should preserve causal chains from trigger through mitigation and verified recovery","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What dataset-design lesson can be derived from the D51 B51-B60 incidents?","answer":"The incidents consistently contain distinct stages: initiating condition, failed control or attempted response, observed failure, causal mechanism, containment, remediation, and recovery verification. Representing these stages separately preserves causal relationships that would be lost in a single incident-summary field.","context":"Cross-incident synthesis from Atlassian, GitHub, Google Cloud, and prior D51 evidence.","objective":"Define a structured representation suitable for retrieval and autonomous engineering reasoning.","attempted_solution":"Incident reports described events through timelines and postmortem narratives.","failure":"A flat summary would obscure distinctions between trigger, failed mitigation, containment, remediation, and verified recovery.","evidence":"The official incident reports used in D51 document these stages explicitly or through detailed timelines and remediation sections.","cause":"Complex incidents contain multiple causal and temporal layers.","root_cause":"Narrative summaries alone do not preserve machine-retrievable relationships among failure stages.","impact":"Poorly structured incident knowledge can reduce the usefulness of historical failures for diagnosis and remediation selection.","containment":"D51 represents causal fields and relationship links separately.","remediation":"Future incident datasets should preserve evidence, provenance, temporal sequence, causality, failed attempts, successful mitigation, and verification state independently.","lessons":"Inference: a software-engineering knowledge fabric should learn from failure chains rather than isolated incident labels or final root-cause statements.","validation_status":"CROSS_INCIDENT_SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"MULTIPLE_PRIMARY_SOURCES","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_titles":["Post-Incident Review on the Atlassian April 2022 outage","GitHub Availability Report: March 2022","GitHub Availability Report: July 2022","Google Cloud Pub/Sub Incident #19001"],"publishers":["Atlassian","GitHub","Google Cloud Status Dashboard"],"evidence_type":"Cross-incident dataset-design synthesis","interpretation_note":"The dataset-design principle is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B51-R05","D51-B53-R05","D51-B55-R04","D51-B47-R05"],"relationships":["derived_from","synthesizes","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B61-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Git Database Configuration","concept":"A configuration change intended to prevent connection saturation triggered database failover and broad GitHub degradation","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What triggered GitHub's May 9 2023 Git database incident?","answer":"A configuration change to the internal service serving Git data was intended to prevent connection saturation. Shortly after rollout began, the database cluster experienced a failover, and eight of GitHub's ten main services became degraded.","context":"GitHub May 9 2023 availability incident.","objective":"Capture a configuration-change failure involving a critical database dependency.","attempted_solution":"A configuration change was introduced to reduce connection-saturation risk.","failure":"The rollout coincided with a cluster failover and broad service degradation.","evidence":"GitHub reported the configuration change, subsequent failover, and impact to eight of ten main services.","cause":"Production configuration change followed by database failover.","root_cause":"The changed database configuration interacted adversely with the cluster's failover behavior.","impact":"Many services could not reliably read newly written Git data and GitHub experienced broad failures.","containment":"The configuration was reverted and a gradual failover was completed.","remediation":"GitHub reviewed deployment safety and failover behavior.","lessons":"Configuration changes to critical data paths must be evaluated together with failover behavior and rollback readiness.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"Addressing GitHub's recent availability issues","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-05-16","evidence_type":"Official incident review"},"related_records":["D51-B61-R02","D51-B61-R05"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B61-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Rollback Failure","concept":"Rollback can itself fail when the infrastructure used to perform the rollback is unhealthy","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why did GitHub's rollback attempt not immediately restore service on May 9 2023?","answer":"GitHub reverted the configuration change and attempted a rollback within minutes, but the rollback failed because of an internal infrastructure error. Recovery required completion of a gradual database failover.","context":"GitHub May 9 2023 incident.","objective":"Capture rollback-path dependency failure.","attempted_solution":"The newly deployed configuration was reverted and rollback was attempted immediately.","failure":"The rollback itself failed because of an internal infrastructure problem.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly reported that the rollback failed due to an internal infrastructure error.","cause":"Rollback execution depended on infrastructure that was itself impaired.","root_cause":"The recovery path did not remain independently reliable during the database failure.","impact":"Broad service degradation continued until gradual failover restored database writes.","containment":"The team completed a gradual failover.","remediation":"GitHub reviewed change management and recovery mechanisms.","lessons":"Rollback mechanisms must be tested under the same failure conditions in which they may be required.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_INCIDENT_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"Addressing GitHub's recent availability issues","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-05-16","evidence_type":"Official incident review"},"related_records":["D51-B61-R01","D51-B61-R03"],"relationships":["causes","constrains","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B61-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Data Consistency Recovery","concept":"Restoring database writes did not immediately restore consistency of derived Git data","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why did GitHub's May 9 2023 incident continue after database write operations were restored?","answer":"After gradual failover restored database write operations, additional recovery was required to make Git data, website-visible content, and pull requests consistent for pushes received during the outage.","context":"GitHub May 9 2023 incident.","objective":"Capture the distinction between infrastructure recovery and application-data convergence.","attempted_solution":"GitHub restored database write operations through gradual failover.","failure":"Some Git-derived data remained inconsistent after the database became writable again.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly reported an extended recovery period for Git data, website-visible contents, and pull requests.","cause":"Writes accumulated while the database was degraded.","root_cause":"Downstream representations did not automatically converge immediately when the primary database recovered.","impact":"Pull requests and push-related data required additional recovery after broad impact ended.","containment":"Database failover restored write capability.","remediation":"Recovery procedures were reviewed to improve full data convergence.","lessons":"Recovery state should distinguish service availability from data consistency and downstream convergence.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_INCIDENT_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"Addressing GitHub's recent availability issues","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-05-16","evidence_type":"Official incident review"},"related_records":["D51-B61-R01","D51-B61-R04"],"relationships":["follows","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B61-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Failover","concept":"Automated failover can restore infrastructure while leaving application recovery incomplete","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What broader recovery lesson follows from GitHub's May 9 2023 incident?","answer":"The incident shows that successful database failover does not necessarily mean the complete application state has recovered. GitHub restored writes first and then required additional work to reconcile Git data, visible content, and pull-request state.","context":"Inference from GitHub's May 9 2023 incident.","objective":"Represent recovery as multiple independently verifiable states.","attempted_solution":"Database failover was used to restore write availability.","failure":"Application-visible data required additional recovery after failover.","evidence":"GitHub documented separate database recovery and subsequent data-consistency recovery.","cause":"Downstream data representations lagged infrastructure recovery.","root_cause":"Service availability and application-state convergence were separate recovery properties.","impact":"Some customer-visible data remained affected after the primary outage ended.","containment":"Gradual failover restored database writes.","remediation":"Recovery processes were reviewed.","lessons":"Inference: autonomous recovery systems should model infrastructure recovery, data consistency, and user-visible convergence as separate states.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"Addressing GitHub's recent availability issues","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-05-16","evidence_type":"Official incident review","interpretation_note":"The generalized recovery-state model is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B61-R03","D51-B61-R05"],"relationships":["derived_from","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B61-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Change Management","concept":"Critical configuration changes require rollback plans that remain executable during failure","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What process improvement did GitHub identify after the May 9 2023 incident?","answer":"GitHub stated that it was reviewing internal processes to ensure changes were deployed safely and was specifically addressing database failover issues so failovers would recover fully without intervention.","context":"GitHub May 2023 availability review.","objective":"Capture documented process remediation following a configuration-induced outage.","attempted_solution":"The configuration change was deployed using the existing production change process.","failure":"Rollback failed and failover required additional intervention.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly identified safe-change deployment and fully recovering failovers as follow-up areas.","cause":"Configuration and failover interaction exposed weaknesses in change and recovery processes.","root_cause":"Existing controls did not guarantee safe deployment and complete automated failover recovery.","impact":"Broad GitHub service degradation occurred.","containment":"Gradual failover restored service.","remediation":"Change processes and failover behavior were reviewed and improved.","lessons":"Production change management should explicitly verify rollback and failover behavior before broad deployment.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_INCIDENT_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"Addressing GitHub's recent availability issues","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-05-16","evidence_type":"Official incident review"},"related_records":["D51-B61-R02","D51-B61-R04"],"relationships":["remediates","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B62-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Authentication Database","concept":"A low-volume but high-cost API request pattern degraded a shared authentication database","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What caused GitHub App authentication-token degradation on May 10 2023?","answer":"GitHub reported a seven-fold increase in write latency on the database cluster serving GitHub App authentication tokens. The underlying cause was a data shape in new API calls that produced very expensive database write transactions and timeouts.","context":"GitHub May 10 2023 incident.","objective":"Capture high-cost request patterns that are not necessarily high-volume.","attempted_solution":"The API continued accepting the new request shape while authentication-token writes were processed by the shared permissions database.","failure":"Expensive writes caused latency and timeouts in the database cluster.","evidence":"GitHub documented the seven-fold write-latency increase and expensive database write transactions.","cause":"Expensive database writes triggered by a new API data shape.","root_cause":"A request pattern generated unexpectedly costly database transactions.","impact":"GitHub App token requests experienced 8-15% failure rates for most of the incident, peaking at 76%.","containment":"Responders identified and blocked the problematic request pattern.","remediation":"GitHub updated the API and audited other endpoints for similar patterns.","lessons":"Operational systems must detect expensive low-volume workloads, not only high-volume traffic.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_INCIDENT_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"Addressing GitHub's recent availability issues","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-05-16","evidence_type":"Official incident review"},"related_records":["D51-B62-R02","D51-B62-R04"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B62-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Low-Volume High-Cost Workloads","concept":"Volume-oriented observability can miss expensive requests","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why was the GitHub May 10 2023 authentication incident difficult to diagnose?","answer":"GitHub reported that much of its observability was optimized for identifying high-volume patterns rather than low-volume, high-cost patterns. The expensive request shape therefore did not fit the primary diagnostic signals as well as a conventional traffic spike would have.","context":"GitHub May 10 2023 incident.","objective":"Capture an observability blind spot for expensive workloads.","attempted_solution":"Monitoring emphasized high-volume traffic patterns.","failure":"The incident was driven by expensive writes rather than simply high request volume.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly stated that its observability was optimized for high-volume patterns and that this made the specific circumstances difficult to identify.","cause":"Observability bias toward volume.","root_cause":"Monitoring did not adequately expose resource cost per request or query pattern.","impact":"Diagnosis of the authentication database degradation was more difficult.","containment":"The problematic request pattern was identified and blocked.","remediation":"GitHub prioritized improved observability of high-cost, low-volume query patterns.","lessons":"Observability should represent workload cost and resource impact, not merely request frequency.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_INCIDENT_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"Addressing GitHub's recent availability issues","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-05-16","evidence_type":"Official incident review"},"related_records":["D51-B62-R01","D51-B62-R05"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B62-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Endpoint Audit","concept":"Auditing similar API patterns can prevent recurrence after one expensive request path is discovered","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What broader API remediation did GitHub undertake after the May 10 2023 incident?","answer":"GitHub audited other endpoints to identify and correct similar expensive database-write patterns after finding the problematic authentication-token request shape.","context":"GitHub May 2023 remediation.","objective":"Capture systematic follow-up instead of fixing only the discovered endpoint.","attempted_solution":"The immediately problematic endpoint was corrected after incident response.","failure":"A single fix would not establish whether other endpoints contained the same performance pattern.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly stated that it audited other endpoints for similar patterns.","cause":"The incident revealed a reusable class of expensive API behavior.","root_cause":"The same underlying data-access pattern could potentially exist in multiple endpoints.","impact":"Other endpoints represented a potential recurrence surface.","containment":"The known problematic source was blocked.","remediation":"A broader endpoint audit was completed or initiated to identify similar patterns.","lessons":"When one expensive request pattern is discovered, search for structurally similar patterns rather than treating the failure as unique.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_INCIDENT_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: May 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-06-14","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B62-R01","D51-B62-R04"],"relationships":["remediates","generalizes","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B62-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Temporary API Guard","concept":"Failing a request can be safer than executing a known-dangerous state transition","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What temporary API safeguard did GitHub introduce after the May 10 authentication incident?","answer":"GitHub updated the API to detect the problematic installation-state shift and fail the request when that shift would trigger the large database write. This was a temporary safeguard while the backing data model was being changed.","context":"GitHub May 10 2023 remediation.","objective":"Capture explicit prevention of a known expensive operation.","attempted_solution":"The API accepted requests that could trigger the expensive write transaction.","failure":"The resulting database write could cause severe latency and timeouts.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly described failing the request when it would trigger the large write as a temporary measure.","cause":"Specific state transition produced an expensive database write.","root_cause":"The underlying data model allowed an expensive write pattern to be reached through the API.","impact":"Authentication-token requests degraded.","containment":"Problematic requests were blocked.","remediation":"The API was guarded while the backing data model was updated.","lessons":"A controlled request failure can be preferable to executing a known unsafe state transition during remediation.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_INCIDENT_REVIEW","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"Addressing GitHub's recent availability issues","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-05-16","evidence_type":"Official incident review"},"related_records":["D51-B62-R01","D51-B62-R03"],"relationships":["contains","remediates","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B62-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Cost-Aware Observability","concept":"Database observability should expose expensive operations even when request volume is low","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What generalized monitoring principle follows from GitHub's May 10 2023 incident?","answer":"The incident directly documented difficulty detecting a low-volume, high-cost workload. The evidence supports instrumenting systems with resource-cost dimensions such as query latency, write cost, transaction size, and resource consumption rather than relying only on request counts.","context":"Inference derived from GitHub May 10 2023 incident.","objective":"Derive an implementation-aware observability principle.","attempted_solution":"Existing monitoring emphasized high-volume request patterns.","failure":"The expensive workload was difficult to identify through those signals.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly described the observability gap for low-volume high-cost patterns.","cause":"Monitoring model emphasized volume over resource cost.","root_cause":"Insufficient visibility into per-operation resource consumption.","impact":"Diagnosis took longer than desired.","containment":"The problematic request pattern was blocked.","remediation":"GitHub pursued better observability for expensive query patterns.","lessons":"Inference: autonomous diagnosis systems should model workload cost independently from request volume.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"Addressing GitHub's recent availability issues","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-05-16","evidence_type":"Official incident review","interpretation_note":"The generalized cost-aware observability principle is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B62-R02","D51-B62-R03"],"relationships":["derived_from","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B63-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Database Crash","concept":"A database software bug combined with a custom cluster configuration caused repeated Git data failures","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What caused GitHub's May 9 and May 11 2023 Git database crashes?","answer":"GitHub determined that a bug in the database version it was running was triggered more readily by a custom configuration on the affected data cluster. The same root cause produced incidents on May 9 and May 11.","context":"GitHub May 2023 availability report.","objective":"Capture vendor-software defects amplified by local configuration differences.","attempted_solution":"The affected cluster operated with a custom configuration different from other database clusters.","failure":"A database bug was triggered under conditions associated with the custom configuration, causing the cluster to crash.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly attributed the crashes to a database-version bug whose triggering conditions were more likely in the custom configuration.","cause":"Database software defect interacting with custom configuration.","root_cause":"The cluster's special configuration increased exposure to the database bug.","impact":"Git operations, Issues, Actions, Codespaces, Pull Requests, Webhooks, and APIs were affected.","containment":"Failover restored service during the incidents.","remediation":"GitHub standardized the cluster configuration and reported the bug to database maintainers.","lessons":"Special-case infrastructure configurations increase exposure to software defects and complicate reliability assumptions.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: May 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-06-14","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B63-R02","D51-B63-R05"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B63-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Custom Configuration","concept":"Configuration divergence created a unique failure surface","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why did GitHub's custom database configuration increase risk during the May 2023 incidents?","answer":"GitHub reported that the conditions triggering the database bug were more likely under the custom configuration used by the affected cluster. Other database clusters did not share the same configuration, reducing their exposure to that specific failure mode.","context":"GitHub May 2023 database incidents.","objective":"Capture configuration divergence as a reliability risk.","attempted_solution":"The affected cluster retained a specialized configuration.","failure":"The specialization increased the likelihood of triggering a database software bug.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly linked the custom configuration to the conditions that triggered the bug.","cause":"Configuration divergence from the standard cluster pattern.","root_cause":"A special-case configuration created a distinct interaction with the database software.","impact":"The cluster crashed repeatedly and caused broad service degradation.","containment":"Database failover restored availability.","remediation":"GitHub changed the configuration to match its other database clusters.","lessons":"Reducing unnecessary infrastructure variance can reduce unique failure surfaces.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: May 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-06-14","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B63-R01","D51-B63-R03"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

