{"record_id":"D51-B63-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Human Approval","concept":"Manual intervention can be a deliberate safety gate rather than an automation failure","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What does GitHub's May 16 2023 failover incident show about human intervention?","answer":"The automation stopped because it could not establish that promotion was safe. A human then evaluated the cluster and triggered failover. This supports distinguishing unavailable automation from intentionally safety-gated automation: refusing an unsafe action can be the correct behavior when evidence is insufficient.","context":"Inference from GitHub May 16 2023 incident.","objective":"Represent approval gating in autonomous engineering systems.","attempted_solution":"Automated failover was used to select a new database primary.","failure":"Automation could not prove promotion was safe and therefore did not execute it.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly documented the data-loss concern and subsequent manual failover.","cause":"Insufficient evidence for safe automatic promotion.","root_cause":"The recovery policy required stronger evidence than the automation could establish.","impact":"Service degradation continued until manual evaluation and failover.","containment":"A responder manually triggered the failover after evaluation.","remediation":"GitHub sought faster response and architectural reduction of single-cluster dependency.","lessons":"Inference: autonomous systems should distinguish inability to prove safety from inability to execute a technically possible action.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: May 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-06-14","evidence_type":"Official availability report","interpretation_note":"The generalized approval-gating principle is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B63-R03","D51-B63-R05"],"relationships":["derived_from","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B63-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Single Point Dependency","concept":"Reducing dependency on a critical database cluster reduces correlated service impact","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What architectural remediation did GitHub identify after the May 2023 database crashes?","answer":"GitHub stated that it was addressing the database crash and working to reduce dependency on the affected cluster as a single point of failure for much of the site.","context":"GitHub May 2023 availability report.","objective":"Capture dependency-reduction remediation.","attempted_solution":"Many services depended on the affected database cluster.","failure":"A single cluster failure caused degradation across many service areas.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly identified reducing dependency on the cluster as long-term mitigation.","cause":"Broad service dependency on one database cluster.","root_cause":"The cluster represented a concentrated dependency for many site functions.","impact":"Multiple GitHub services degraded simultaneously.","containment":"Failover restored service after the cluster failure.","remediation":"Dependency on the cluster was targeted for reduction.","lessons":"Reliability architecture should identify concentrated dependencies and reduce correlated blast radius where practical.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: May 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-06-14","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B63-R01","D51-B63-R03"],"relationships":["remediates","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B64-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Regional Maintenance","concept":"Automated maintenance can create rolling global impact when faulty nodes are introduced sequentially","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What caused GitHub Copilot's April 25 2023 rolling outage?","answer":"GitHub reported that an automated maintenance process applied a faulty configuration sequentially across all three Copilot regions. Faulty nodes were placed into service before operators halted the rollout, producing rolling partial outages across the regions.","context":"GitHub Copilot April 25 2023 incident.","objective":"Capture sequential maintenance as a global rolling-failure mechanism.","attempted_solution":"Automated maintenance rolled the configuration across regions sequentially.","failure":"Faulty nodes entered service in each region before the rollout was stopped.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly described the faulty configuration, sequential regional rollout, and rolling partial outage.","cause":"Faulty configuration introduced by automated maintenance.","root_cause":"The maintenance process exposed multiple regions before the defect was detected and halted.","impact":"Approximately 6% of Copilot code-completion requests failed at peak impact.","containment":"Operators halted the rollout and automatic traffic rollover mitigated failed nodes and regions.","remediation":"GitHub reduced maintenance batch size and iteration speed and adjusted alerting thresholds.","lessons":"Sequential global rollout is safer than simultaneous rollout but still requires sufficiently small batches and fast detection.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: May 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-06-14","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B64-R02","D51-B64-R04"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B64-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Batch Size","concept":"Large maintenance batches increase the number of faulty nodes placed into service before detection","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What rollout change did GitHub plan after the April 2023 Copilot incident?","answer":"GitHub planned to reduce the batch size and iteration speed of its automated maintenance process. The goal was to reduce the number of faulty nodes that could enter service before the defect was detected and the rollout stopped.","context":"GitHub Copilot April 2023 remediation.","objective":"Capture batch-size reduction as a documented blast-radius control.","attempted_solution":"Automated maintenance used a larger sequential rollout process.","failure":"Faulty nodes entered service across regions before operators halted the rollout.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly stated that batch size and iteration speed would be reduced.","cause":"Maintenance rollout exposed too much capacity per iteration.","root_cause":"Rollout granularity was insufficient for the detection and containment speed available.","impact":"All three Copilot regions experienced partial degradation during the incident.","containment":"Operators halted the rollout and traffic rollover reduced impact.","remediation":"Smaller batches and slower iterations were planned.","lessons":"Deployment batch size should be selected based on detection latency and acceptable blast radius.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: May 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-06-14","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B64-R01","D51-B64-R03"],"relationships":["remediates","constrains","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B64-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Traffic Rollover","concept":"Automatic traffic rollover reduced impact from faulty regional nodes","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"How did GitHub contain the April 2023 Copilot maintenance failure?","answer":"GitHub reported that automatic traffic rollover from failed nodes and regions helped mitigate the incident while operators halted the faulty maintenance rollout.","context":"GitHub Copilot April 2023 incident.","objective":"Capture automatic traffic displacement as a containment mechanism.","attempted_solution":"Traffic rollover moved requests away from unhealthy nodes and regions.","failure":"The maintenance process had already placed faulty nodes into service.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly credited automated traffic rollover with mitigating the issue.","cause":"Faulty nodes entered service during automated maintenance.","root_cause":"Maintenance rollout exposed unhealthy capacity before detection.","impact":"Copilot requests failed intermittently across all three regions.","containment":"Automatic traffic rollover redirected traffic from failed nodes and regions.","remediation":"Rollout batch size and speed were reduced.","lessons":"Traffic-shifting mechanisms can reduce impact when deployment automation introduces partial regional failure.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: May 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-06-14","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B64-R01","D51-B64-R04"],"relationships":["contains","mitigates","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B64-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Detection Latency","concept":"Reducing rollout speed without improving detection leaves the same failure mechanism partially exposed","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"Why did GitHub also adjust alerting after the April 2023 Copilot incident?","answer":"GitHub planned both smaller, slower maintenance batches and lower alerting thresholds. The combination indicates that reducing exposure per rollout iteration and shortening detection time were complementary controls against the same failure mode.","context":"Inference based on GitHub's documented remediation actions.","objective":"Model the relationship between rollout exposure and detection latency.","attempted_solution":"Automated maintenance and existing alerting were used together.","failure":"Faulty nodes entered service before the rollout was stopped.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly documented both rollout changes and alerting-threshold changes.","cause":"Exposure per iteration exceeded the practical detection-and-stop window.","root_cause":"Rollout speed, batch size, and detection latency were not sufficiently balanced.","impact":"Partial outages occurred in all three Copilot regions.","containment":"Traffic rollover and operator halt reduced impact.","remediation":"Smaller batches, slower iterations, and faster alerting were pursued.","lessons":"Inference: safe rollout design should jointly model batch size, iteration speed, detection latency, and automatic containment capability.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: May 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-06-14","evidence_type":"Official availability report","interpretation_note":"The combined rollout-control principle is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B64-R01","D51-B64-R02","D51-B64-R03"],"relationships":["derived_from","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B64-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Global Rollout","concept":"Sequential regional deployment can still create global impact when every region is exposed before defect detection","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What does the April 2023 Copilot incident show about sequential regional rollout?","answer":"The maintenance process operated sequentially rather than changing every region simultaneously, but all three Copilot regions still experienced degraded service because faulty nodes were introduced before detection. Sequential rollout therefore reduces simultaneous exposure but does not by itself guarantee a small blast radius.","context":"Inference from GitHub's April 2023 Copilot incident.","objective":"Capture the limitation of sequential deployment as a standalone safety mechanism.","attempted_solution":"The automated maintenance process applied changes sequentially across regions.","failure":"The defect was detected only after faulty nodes had entered multiple regions.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly stated that the process ran sequentially across all three regions and caused rolling partial outages in all three.","cause":"Defective configuration combined with insufficient rollout granularity.","root_cause":"Sequential ordering did not provide enough isolation relative to detection speed.","impact":"All three Copilot regions experienced degraded service.","containment":"Rollout was halted and traffic rollover mitigated failed capacity.","remediation":"Batch size and iteration speed were reduced.","lessons":"Inference: rollout safety depends on exposure per step, not merely whether regions are updated sequentially.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: May 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-06-14","evidence_type":"Official availability report","interpretation_note":"The generalized rollout principle is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B64-R01","D51-B64-R02"],"relationships":["derived_from","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B65-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Database Failover","concept":"Automated database failover can leave downstream services unavailable when the failover does not fully recover dependencies","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What happened during GitHub's May 11 2023 Git database incident?","answer":"A database cluster serving Git data crashed and triggered automated failover. GitHub reported that the incident affected eight of ten main services, and additional recovery was required for pull-request data and search capabilities.","context":"GitHub May 11 2023 availability incident.","objective":"Capture incomplete recovery after automated database failover.","attempted_solution":"Automated failover was used after the Git database cluster crashed.","failure":"Although the failover restored core database availability, some downstream GitHub functionality required additional recovery.","evidence":"GitHub documented the crash, automated failover, broad service impact, and extended pull-request recovery.","cause":"Database cluster crash.","root_cause":"The cluster's failure exposed limitations in complete downstream recovery.","impact":"Git Operations, Issues, Actions, Codespaces, Pull Requests, Webhooks, and APIs were degraded.","containment":"Automated failover restored the database service.","remediation":"GitHub worked on database crash prevention and complete failover recovery.","lessons":"Failover success should be measured by end-to-end service recovery, not merely primary database promotion.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: May 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-06-14","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B65-R02","D51-B65-R05"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B65-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Search Recovery","concept":"Derived search state can remain inconsistent after primary database recovery","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why did GitHub Pull Requests require additional recovery after the May 11 2023 incident?","answer":"GitHub reported that pull-request functionality required additional time to fully recover search capabilities after the database failover. The primary database recovery therefore did not immediately restore all derived search behavior.","context":"GitHub May 11 2023 incident.","objective":"Capture downstream index or derived-state recovery after database failover.","attempted_solution":"Database failover restored core Git data availability.","failure":"Pull-request search capabilities remained in recovery after the primary service recovered.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly documented additional recovery time for pull-request search capabilities.","cause":"Derived search state lagged primary database recovery.","root_cause":"Application-level derived state had a separate recovery dependency.","impact":"Pull-request search remained degraded after the main outage ended.","containment":"Database failover restored the primary data service.","remediation":"GitHub improved failover and recovery handling.","lessons":"Recovery verification should include indexes, caches, search systems, and other derived state.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: May 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-06-14","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B65-R01","D51-B65-R03"],"relationships":["follows","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B65-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Failover Completeness","concept":"A failover mechanism can succeed technically while failing to restore the complete service contract","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What generalized lesson follows from GitHub's May 2023 failover incidents?","answer":"GitHub's incidents show that promoting a replacement database is only one part of recovery. Customer-visible functionality may still depend on replication, indexing, search, caches, queues, or application state. Therefore failover verification should test the full service contract.","context":"Inference from GitHub May 9 and May 11 2023 incidents.","objective":"Define end-to-end failover verification.","attempted_solution":"Database failover restored primary database operation.","failure":"Some downstream customer-visible capabilities required additional recovery.","evidence":"GitHub documented extended recovery for Git data and pull-request search after failover.","cause":"Downstream systems did not converge simultaneously with primary database recovery.","root_cause":"Failover represented infrastructure recovery rather than complete service recovery.","impact":"Users continued experiencing partial functionality after database recovery.","containment":"Failover restored primary database operation.","remediation":"GitHub pursued improvements to complete failover recovery.","lessons":"Inference: automated failover should have explicit post-failover verification gates for every critical dependent service.","validation_status":"CROSS_INCIDENT_SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"MULTIPLE_PRIMARY_SOURCES","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_titles":["Addressing GitHub's recent availability issues","GitHub Availability Report: May 2023"],"publishers":["GitHub"],"evidence_type":"Cross-incident synthesis","interpretation_note":"The end-to-end failover verification principle is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B61-R03","D51-B65-R02"],"relationships":["derived_from","synthesizes","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B65-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Database Standardization","concept":"Standardizing database configurations can remove special-case interactions with known software defects","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What configuration remediation did GitHub apply after the May 2023 database crashes?","answer":"GitHub updated the affected cluster's configuration to match its other database clusters. This removed the special configuration that made the database bug more likely to trigger on the affected cluster.","context":"GitHub May 2023 remediation.","objective":"Capture standardization as a reliability remediation.","attempted_solution":"The affected cluster operated under a custom database configuration.","failure":"The custom configuration increased exposure to a database software bug.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly stated that it updated the configuration to match the rest of its database clusters.","cause":"Custom configuration increased bug-triggering conditions.","root_cause":"Infrastructure variance created a unique failure surface.","impact":"Repeated Git database crashes affected many services.","containment":"Failover restored availability.","remediation":"The configuration was standardized and the database bug was reported to maintainers.","lessons":"Reducing unnecessary configuration divergence can improve predictability and reduce special-case failure modes.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: May 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-06-14","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B63-R02","D51-B65-R05"],"relationships":["remediates","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B65-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Vendor Defect","concept":"Operational postmortems can identify software defects that require upstream fixes","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What did GitHub do after identifying the database software bug?","answer":"GitHub reported the database bug privately to the database maintainers. The bug was accepted as a private bug and fixed, with the fix scheduled for a later database release.","context":"GitHub May 2023 database incident remediation.","objective":"Capture escalation of an operationally discovered vendor defect.","attempted_solution":"GitHub initially mitigated the incident through configuration changes and failover.","failure":"The underlying database software defect remained a potential source of recurrence.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly reported the bug to maintainers and stated that it had been fixed upstream.","cause":"Database software defect.","root_cause":"An implementation bug existed in the database version in use.","impact":"Repeated Git database crashes occurred.","containment":"Configuration changes reduced exposure and failover restored service.","remediation":"The defect was reported upstream and fixed.","lessons":"Operational incident knowledge should distinguish local mitigations from fixes that require upstream software changes.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: May 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-06-14","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B63-R01","D51-B65-R04"],"relationships":["remediates","escalates_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B66-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Database Topology","concept":"Corrupted database topology prevented applications from connecting to databases","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What caused GitHub's February 18 2023 sign-in incident?","answer":"GitHub found that database topology in 30% of deployments had become corrupted. Applications could not connect to the database because of the corrupted topology, producing system-wide errors and sign-in failures.","context":"GitHub February 18 2023 availability incident.","objective":"Capture configuration-state corruption as a service-connectivity failure.","attempted_solution":"GitHub attempted to remediate unhealthy sharding middleware deployments through redeployment.","failure":"The remediation propagated the problem to all Kubernetes pods before the corrupted topology was identified.","evidence":"GitHub documented the topology corruption in 30% of deployments and the propagation caused by redeployment.","cause":"Unhealthy sharding middleware deployments and corrupted database topology.","root_cause":"Database topology state became invalid across a significant subset of deployments.","impact":"Multiple services returned 500 responses and users experienced GitHub sign-in problems.","containment":"The correct topology was copied to all deployments.","remediation":"Topology monitoring and reload observability were improved.","lessons":"Distributed configuration state must be validated before broad propagation operations.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: February 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-03-01","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B66-R02","D51-B66-R03"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B66-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Remediation Propagation","concept":"A broad redeployment intended to repair unhealthy components propagated the failure","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"How did GitHub's remediation attempt make the February 2023 database incident worse?","answer":"GitHub initially performed a redeployment of the database infrastructure to remediate unhealthy sharding middleware deployments. Instead of isolating the problem, the redeployment propagated the issue to all Kubernetes pods and produced system-wide errors.","context":"GitHub February 18 2023 incident.","objective":"Capture remediation-induced blast-radius expansion.","attempted_solution":"Database infrastructure was redeployed to repair unhealthy middleware deployments.","failure":"The redeployment propagated the issue across all Kubernetes pods.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly reported that the remediation propagated the issue to all Kubernetes pods.","cause":"Broad remediation against an already-corrupted database topology state.","root_cause":"The remediation action lacked sufficient isolation from the corrupted configuration state.","impact":"Multiple services returned 500 responses and sign-in was affected.","containment":"Correct database topology was distributed to all deployments.","remediation":"Topology error monitoring and reload observability were added.","lessons":"Emergency remediation should be staged and scoped so that a bad state is not propagated further.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: February 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-03-01","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B66-R01","D51-B66-R04"],"relationships":["amplifies","causes","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B66-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Topology Repair","concept":"Correct-state propagation can restore distributed database connectivity","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"How did GitHub recover from the February 18 2023 topology incident?","answer":"GitHub identified that 30% of deployments had corrupted database topology and applied a correct copy of the topology to all deployments. This resolved the errors across services by approximately 05:00 UTC.","context":"GitHub February 18 2023 incident.","objective":"Capture restoration by replacing invalid distributed configuration state.","attempted_solution":"A known-good database topology was copied to all affected deployments.","failure":"The existing topology prevented applications from connecting to the database.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly documented copying the correct topology to all deployments and subsequent service recovery.","cause":"Corrupted database topology.","root_cause":"Distributed topology state was inconsistent or invalid in affected deployments.","impact":"System-wide application errors occurred.","containment":"Known-good topology was propagated.","remediation":"Monitoring was added to detect topology errors before production impact.","lessons":"Configuration repair should establish a known-good state and verify propagation across every deployment.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: February 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-03-01","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B66-R01","D51-B66-R05"],"relationships":["resolves","restores","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B66-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Configuration Observability","concept":"Topology reloads require observability for both successful and erroneous states","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What observability improvement did GitHub make after the February 2023 topology incident?","answer":"GitHub improved observability around topology reloads, including both successful and erroneous reloads, and added monitoring to detect database topology errors earlier.","context":"GitHub February 2023 remediation.","objective":"Capture configuration-state observability as a direct remediation.","attempted_solution":"Database topology was reloaded without sufficient visibility into whether the resulting state was correct.","failure":"Corrupted topology persisted across deployments and was not detected early enough.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly stated that it added a topology-error monitor and improved observability of topology reloads.","cause":"Insufficient visibility into distributed topology state.","root_cause":"Configuration reload correctness was not sufficiently observable.","impact":"Applications could not connect to databases across affected deployments.","containment":"Correct topology was distributed.","remediation":"Topology error monitoring and reload observability were improved.","lessons":"Configuration systems need success and failure telemetry for the state transition itself, not merely service-level symptoms.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: February 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-03-01","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B66-R01","D51-B66-R03"],"relationships":["remediates","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B66-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"State Verification","concept":"Configuration repair must verify the target state rather than assume propagation succeeded","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What engineering principle follows from GitHub's February 2023 topology incident?","answer":"GitHub's incident involved corrupted topology, a remediation that propagated the problem, and a later replacement with known-good topology. The evidence supports verifying configuration state after propagation rather than treating completion of the propagation action as proof of correctness.","context":"Inference derived from GitHub February 2023 incident.","objective":"Derive a configuration-state verification principle.","attempted_solution":"Topology was propagated through redeployment and later through a known-good configuration copy.","failure":"The first remediation propagated an incorrect state.","evidence":"GitHub documented the propagation failure and later successful replacement with correct topology.","cause":"Invalid topology state combined with broad remediation.","root_cause":"The remediation path lacked sufficient state verification before broad propagation.","impact":"System-wide errors resulted.","containment":"Known-good topology was propagated.","remediation":"Topology error monitoring and reload observability were added.","lessons":"Inference: autonomous configuration systems should verify semantic target state after each propagation stage before expanding scope.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: February 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-03-01","evidence_type":"Official availability report","interpretation_note":"The generalized state-verification principle is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B66-R02","D51-B66-R03","D51-B66-R04"],"relationships":["derived_from","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B67-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Maintenance Query Drain","concept":"A slow database query left running during maintenance blocked database resources","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What caused the February 2023 GitHub database maintenance incident?","answer":"GitHub reported that a slow-running query was not properly drained during routine maintenance. The query blocked all database resources and caused broad service degradation.","context":"GitHub February 28 2023 maintenance incident detailed in the March 2023 availability report.","objective":"Capture unsafe maintenance with active long-running queries.","attempted_solution":"Routine database maintenance proceeded while database workloads were active.","failure":"A slow-running query remained active and blocked database resources.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly stated that the query was not properly drained and blocked all database resources.","cause":"Long-running query remained active during maintenance.","root_cause":"Maintenance safeguards did not prevent disruptive operations while blocking work remained.","impact":"GitHub Pages, Codespaces, and Actions were affected.","containment":"The slow query was killed and the database was restarted.","remediation":"Maintenance was paused pending investigation and additional safety checks were added.","lessons":"Database maintenance should verify that long-running and blocking operations have been safely drained before proceeding.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: March 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-04-05","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B67-R02","D51-B67-R04"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B67-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Maintenance Safety","concept":"Maintenance should be paused when safe draining conditions cannot be established","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What immediate process change did GitHub make after the February 2023 maintenance incident?","answer":"GitHub paused maintenance until it could investigate and address the failure to drain long-running queries safely. It also updated maintenance procedures to perform additional safety checks before production changes.","context":"GitHub March 2023 availability report.","objective":"Capture fail-closed maintenance behavior.","attempted_solution":"Routine maintenance proceeded under the existing process.","failure":"A long-running query remained active and blocked database resources.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly stated that maintenance was paused and that additional safety checks were added.","cause":"Insufficient pre-maintenance drain verification.","root_cause":"The maintenance process did not sufficiently establish that blocking workloads had been removed.","impact":"Multiple services experienced degraded availability.","containment":"The query was killed and the database restarted.","remediation":"Maintenance was paused and safety checks were strengthened.","lessons":"Operational automation should refuse to proceed when critical preconditions cannot be proven.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: March 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-04-05","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B67-R01","D51-B67-R03"],"relationships":["constrains","remediates","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B67-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Blocking Queries","concept":"A single blocking database operation can consume resources needed by unrelated workloads","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why did one slow query have broad impact during GitHub's February 2023 maintenance incident?","answer":"GitHub reported that the slow-running query blocked all database resources. Because the database served multiple GitHub services, the blocked resources caused broad application-level errors rather than an isolated query failure.","context":"GitHub February 2023 maintenance incident.","objective":"Capture shared-resource amplification from a blocking operation.","attempted_solution":"Database maintenance continued while the query remained active.","failure":"The query blocked database resources required by other operations.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly described the query as blocking all database resources.","cause":"Blocking long-running query.","root_cause":"The database workload allowed one un-drained operation to monopolize critical resources.","impact":"Multiple GitHub services returned errors.","containment":"The query was killed and the database restarted.","remediation":"Additional long-query and blocking-process checks were added to maintenance.","lessons":"Shared database systems need safeguards against single-operation resource monopolization.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: March 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-04-05","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B67-R01","D51-B67-R05"],"relationships":["causes","propagates_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B67-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Maintenance Precondition","concept":"Maintenance safety checks must inspect both long-running queries and blocking processes","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What specific safety checks did GitHub add to database maintenance?","answer":"GitHub stated that its maintenance process was updated to perform additional safety checks for long-running queries and blocking processes before proceeding with production changes.","context":"GitHub March 2023 remediation.","objective":"Capture concrete precondition checks for database maintenance.","attempted_solution":"Maintenance used the existing operational process.","failure":"The process did not prevent maintenance from proceeding while a slow query was still active.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly identified long-running queries and blocking processes as new safety-check targets.","cause":"Insufficient precondition validation.","root_cause":"Maintenance did not establish that blocking database activity had been cleared.","impact":"Database resources became unavailable to application workloads.","containment":"Query termination and database restart restored service.","remediation":"Pre-maintenance safety checks were expanded.","lessons":"Maintenance automation should encode the exact failure preconditions discovered during previous incidents.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: March 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-04-05","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B67-R01","D51-B67-R02"],"relationships":["remediates","prevents","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B67-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Maintenance Verification","concept":"Production maintenance should validate workload state before performing disruptive changes","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What generalized maintenance lesson follows from GitHub's February 2023 incident?","answer":"The incident directly demonstrated that an active long-running query could turn routine maintenance into a broad outage. The evidence supports making workload-state inspection an explicit prerequisite for disruptive database operations.","context":"Inference from GitHub February 2023 maintenance incident.","objective":"Derive a machine-usable maintenance safety rule.","attempted_solution":"Routine maintenance was performed without sufficient verification that long-running work had drained.","failure":"An active slow query blocked database resources during maintenance.","evidence":"GitHub documented the query, resource blocking, and subsequent maintenance-safety changes.","cause":"Unsafe maintenance precondition.","root_cause":"Workload state was not sufficiently checked before disruption.","impact":"Multiple services degraded.","containment":"Query termination and database restart restored service.","remediation":"Long-query and blocking-process safety checks were added.","lessons":"Inference: maintenance systems should evaluate live workload state before executing disruptive actions and fail closed when safe preconditions are not satisfied.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: March 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-04-05","evidence_type":"Official availability report","interpretation_note":"The generalized maintenance-safety principle is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B67-R01","D51-B67-R02","D51-B67-R04"],"relationships":["derived_from","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B68-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"External Cloud Failure","concept":"A regional cloud-provider outage can disrupt a dependent service even when other regions remain healthy","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What caused the GitHub Codespaces February 2023 Southeast Asia incident?","answer":"GitHub reported that a datacenter outage at its cloud provider caused failures creating, starting, and connecting to Codespaces in the Southeast Asia region. Codespaces in other regions were not impacted.","context":"GitHub February 7 2023 Codespaces incident.","objective":"Capture regional dependency failure and bounded blast radius.","attempted_solution":"Codespaces relied on the affected cloud-provider datacenter for regional capacity.","failure":"The provider datacenter outage prevented Codespaces operations in the affected region.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly attributed the incident to a cloud-provider datacenter outage and stated that other regions were unaffected.","cause":"External regional datacenter outage.","root_cause":"Regional service capacity depended on a cloud-provider location that became unavailable.","impact":"Southeast Asia Codespaces operations were degraded for more than 20 hours.","containment":"New Codespace creations were redirected to a secondary location.","remediation":"GitHub evaluated increased regional redundancy and more regular failover exercises.","lessons":"External regional dependencies require tested failover paths and capacity in alternate locations.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: February 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-03-01","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B68-R02","D51-B68-R05"],"relationships":[