{"record_id":"D51-B68-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Regional Failover","concept":"Redirecting new work to a healthy region can preserve service while the failed region recovers","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"How did GitHub mitigate the February 2023 Codespaces regional outage?","answer":"GitHub redirected Codespace creations to a secondary region, allowing new Codespaces to be used while the Southeast Asia datacenter was unavailable. Existing Codespaces recovered when the affected datacenter recovered.","context":"GitHub February 7 2023 Codespaces incident.","objective":"Capture regional traffic and workload redirection as containment.","attempted_solution":"New Codespace creation requests were redirected to a secondary region.","failure":"The original Southeast Asia region was unavailable because of the provider outage.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly reported that secondary-region redirection allowed new Codespaces to be used.","cause":"Regional datacenter outage.","root_cause":"The affected region lacked sufficient independent capacity during provider failure.","impact":"New and existing Codespace operations in Southeast Asia were affected.","containment":"New creations were redirected to a secondary region.","remediation":"Regional redundancy and failover exercises were evaluated.","lessons":"Failover should distinguish new workload placement from recovery of already-running workload.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: February 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-03-01","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B68-R01","D51-B68-R03"],"relationships":["contains","mitigates","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B68-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Failover Exercise","concept":"Failover capability should be exercised regularly rather than assumed from architectural redundancy","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What reliability improvement did GitHub identify after the February 2023 Codespaces regional outage?","answer":"GitHub stated that it was evaluating expanded regional redundancy and had begun architectural changes to better handle temporary regional and datacenter outages, including more regular exercises of its failover capabilities.","context":"GitHub February 2023 Codespaces remediation.","objective":"Capture failover exercise as a concrete resilience practice.","attempted_solution":"Regional failover existed and was used during the incident.","failure":"The external datacenter outage exposed the need for broader redundancy and more practiced failover behavior.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly identified increased redundancy and regular failover exercises as follow-up actions.","cause":"Regional cloud-provider failure.","root_cause":"Resilience against regional provider outages required further architectural and operational validation.","impact":"Codespaces in Southeast Asia were unavailable or degraded.","containment":"New creations were redirected to a secondary region.","remediation":"Regional redundancy and failover exercises were expanded.","lessons":"Resilience mechanisms should be exercised under realistic failure conditions rather than treated as theoretical capabilities.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: February 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-03-01","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B68-R01","D51-B68-R02"],"relationships":["remediates","verifies","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B68-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Existing Workload Recovery","concept":"Failing over new workload placement does not automatically migrate existing workload state","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What happened to existing Codespaces during the February 2023 regional outage?","answer":"GitHub reported that existing Codespaces in the affected region recovered automatically when the datacenter recovered. The secondary-region mitigation primarily enabled new Codespaces to be created while the original region was unavailable.","context":"GitHub February 2023 Codespaces incident.","objective":"Capture different recovery semantics for new and existing workloads.","attempted_solution":"New workload creation was redirected to a healthy region while existing workloads remained associated with the affected region.","failure":"Existing workloads could not simply be treated as new workloads during the outage.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly distinguished secondary-region creation from automatic recovery of existing Codespaces after datacenter recovery.","cause":"Regional provider outage.","root_cause":"Workload placement and workload-state recovery were separate operational paths.","impact":"Existing and new Codespaces had different recovery behavior.","containment":"New creations were redirected.","remediation":"Broader regional redundancy and failover exercises were evaluated.","lessons":"Resilience plans must distinguish stateless placement from stateful or already-running workload recovery.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: February 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-03-01","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B68-R02","D51-B68-R03"],"relationships":["contrasts_with","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B68-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Regional Redundancy","concept":"Regional redundancy must be measured against the actual failure domain of the external provider","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What broader resilience lesson follows from the GitHub February 2023 Codespaces incident?","answer":"The incident occurred in a provider datacenter and was mitigated partly through a secondary region. This supports designing redundancy according to external-provider failure domains rather than assuming that separate application regions automatically provide independent infrastructure.","context":"Inference derived from GitHub February 2023 Codespaces incident.","objective":"Represent provider-aware redundancy.","attempted_solution":"Codespaces used regional deployment and a secondary-region failover path.","failure":"The affected provider datacenter outage still caused regional service degradation.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly attributed the failure to its cloud provider's datacenter outage and evaluated expanded regional redundancy.","cause":"External datacenter failure.","root_cause":"The service's regional resilience depended on the provider failure domain.","impact":"Southeast Asia Codespaces were affected while other regions remained healthy.","containment":"New workload creation was redirected.","remediation":"Regional redundancy and failover exercises were expanded.","lessons":"Inference: resilience architecture should model the actual independence of underlying infrastructure and provider failure domains.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: February 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-03-01","evidence_type":"Official availability report","interpretation_note":"The provider-aware redundancy principle is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B68-R01","D51-B68-R03","D51-B68-R04"],"relationships":["derived_from","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B69-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Database Host Failure","concept":"A shared primary-database host failure can affect many independent application services","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What caused GitHub's September 5 2023 database outage?","answer":"GitHub reported that the primary host for a shared datastore experienced an underlying filesystem write error. The operating-system disk became unreachable, affecting the majority of public-facing GitHub services.","context":"GitHub September 5 2023 availability incident.","objective":"Capture host-level storage failure as a shared-service failure.","attempted_solution":"Multiple public-facing services depended on the shared primary datastore.","failure":"The primary host's operating-system disk became unreachable and database availability was lost.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly documented the filesystem write error and unreachable disk.","cause":"Underlying host filesystem and storage failure.","root_cause":"Primary database host lost access to its operating-system disk.","impact":"SAML login, Actions, Issues, pull requests, Pages, API, Webhooks, Codespaces, and Packages were affected.","containment":"Database recovery restored service.","remediation":"GitHub reviewed storage and database resilience.","lessons":"Shared primary datastore hosts remain a concentration point even when application services appear logically separated.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: September 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-10-11","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B69-R02","D51-B69-R05"],"relationships":["causes","propagates_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B69-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Migration Constraints","concept":"Data migration changes can produce unexpected behavior when constraints are insufficiently validated before rollout","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What remediation did GitHub identify after the September 2023 GitHub Projects data-loss incident?","answer":"GitHub improved validation of data migrations in testing and during rollout and evaluated constraints on migrations to prevent the unexpected behavior that caused the incident.","context":"GitHub September 2023 availability report.","objective":"Capture migration-validation remediation after unexpected constraint behavior.","attempted_solution":"A data migration and constraint configuration were deployed to the GitHub Projects database.","failure":"A misconfigured index constraint caused GitHub Projects to become unavailable and required restoration to an earlier state.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly documented improved migration validation and constraint evaluation after the incident.","cause":"Misconfigured index constraint during a database change.","root_cause":"Migration validation did not sufficiently detect the constraint behavior before production impact.","impact":"GitHub Projects became fully unavailable for several hours and data created during mitigation required recovery.","containment":"Projects data was restored to the state at the beginning of the incident.","remediation":"Migration validation and constraint safeguards were improved.","lessons":"Database migrations should validate both schema semantics and production-like behavior before broad rollout.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: September 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-10-11","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B69-R03","D51-B69-R04"],"relationships":["causes","remediates","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B69-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Data Restoration","concept":"Restoring a database to a previous state can create a recovery gap for data written during the incident","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"How did GitHub recover GitHub Projects after the September 2023 incident?","answer":"GitHub restored Projects data to its state from the beginning of the incident. New project data created by users while the incident was being mitigated required additional recovery and was not fully available until later.","context":"GitHub September 2023 availability report.","objective":"Capture temporal data-loss and restoration semantics.","attempted_solution":"GitHub restored the database to the pre-incident state.","failure":"Data created during the mitigation window required separate recovery.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly stated that the initial restoration returned data to the beginning-of-incident state and later recovered newly created project data.","cause":"Recovery used an earlier known-good data state.","root_cause":"The restoration point did not automatically contain all writes performed during mitigation.","impact":"Project data created during the incident had delayed availability.","containment":"Known-good data state was restored.","remediation":"GitHub improved data restoration and migration validation.","lessons":"Recovery-point objectives and post-failure writes must be explicitly modeled during restoration.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: September 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-10-11","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B69-R02","D51-B69-R05"],"relationships":["restores","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B69-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Replication Recovery","concept":"Remediation actions can create a secondary replication interruption","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What secondary failure occurred during GitHub's September 2023 remediation?","answer":"GitHub reported that a database replication interruption caused by remediation steps created limited availability for Git Operations, APIs, and GitHub Issues for approximately 1.25 hours.","context":"GitHub September 2023 availability report.","objective":"Capture remediation-induced secondary failure.","attempted_solution":"Database remediation steps were used to recover from the primary failure.","failure":"Those remediation steps interrupted replication and caused another period of limited availability.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly attributed the secondary availability period to replication interruption caused by remediation steps.","cause":"Replication interruption during remediation.","root_cause":"Recovery actions interacted with replication state and created a secondary failure.","impact":"Git Operations, APIs, and Issues experienced additional limited availability.","containment":"Replication was restored.","remediation":"GitHub improved restoration and replication recovery processes.","lessons":"Recovery actions themselves require dependency and side-effect analysis.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_AVAILABILITY_REPORT","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: September 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-10-11","evidence_type":"Official availability report"},"related_records":["D51-B69-R03","D51-B69-R05"],"relationships":["amplifies","follows","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B69-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Recovery Verification","concept":"Recovery must include both data correctness and replication health","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What broader recovery lesson follows from GitHub's September 2023 incidents?","answer":"GitHub's incident involved restoration to a known-good data state, later recovery of writes created during mitigation, and a separate replication interruption caused by remediation. The evidence supports treating data correctness, write preservation, and replication health as separate recovery verification dimensions.","context":"Inference from GitHub September 2023 availability report.","objective":"Derive multi-dimensional recovery verification.","attempted_solution":"Database restoration and remediation were used to recover service.","failure":"Restoration did not automatically recover all incident-window writes, and remediation introduced a replication interruption.","evidence":"GitHub documented both recovery-state limitations and secondary replication impact.","cause":"Complex recovery state across database contents and replication.","root_cause":"Infrastructure recovery properties were not identical to complete application-data recovery.","impact":"Some data and services required additional recovery after primary restoration.","containment":"Known-good data state and replication recovery were applied.","remediation":"GitHub improved restoration and replication handling.","lessons":"Inference: recovery verification should separately validate primary data, incident-window writes, replication, and customer-visible derived state.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: September 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-10-11","evidence_type":"Official availability report","interpretation_note":"The generalized recovery-verification model is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B69-R03","D51-B69-R04"],"relationships":["derived_from","related_to","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B70-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Cross-Incident Recovery","concept":"A successful infrastructure action does not necessarily equal successful user-visible recovery","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What cross-incident pattern connects GitHub's 2023 database and regional failures?","answer":"Across GitHub's 2023 incidents, database failover, regional redirection, and database restoration each restored important infrastructure capabilities but left separate customer-visible states requiring validation or additional recovery. This supports treating infrastructure recovery and service-contract recovery as distinct states.","context":"Cross-incident synthesis from GitHub February, May, and September 2023 incidents.","objective":"Create a reusable recovery-state model.","attempted_solution":"Failover, redirection, and restoration mechanisms were used to recover infrastructure.","failure":"Some customer-visible functions remained degraded or required additional recovery after the infrastructure action completed.","evidence":"GitHub documented extended search/data recovery after failover, separate recovery for existing and new Codespaces, and incident-window data recovery after database restoration.","cause":"Multiple dependent state layers recover at different rates.","root_cause":"Infrastructure availability was not equivalent to complete application-state convergence.","impact":"Partial functionality persisted after initial infrastructure recovery.","containment":"Failover, redirection, and restoration were used.","remediation":"GitHub pursued improved failover, recovery, redundancy, and restoration mechanisms.","lessons":"Inference: autonomous recovery should maintain explicit states for infrastructure recovery, data consistency, dependency convergence, and user-visible recovery.","validation_status":"CROSS_INCIDENT_SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"MULTIPLE_PRIMARY_SOURCES","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_titles":["GitHub Availability Report: February 2023","Addressing GitHub's recent availability issues","GitHub Availability Report: September 2023"],"publishers":["GitHub"],"evidence_type":"Cross-incident synthesis","interpretation_note":"The generalized recovery-state model is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B61-R03","D51-B65-R03","D51-B68-R04","D51-B69-R03"],"relationships":["synthesizes","derived_from","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B70-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Cross-Incident Change Safety","concept":"Disruptive operations should require evidence-backed preconditions and bounded blast radius","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What cross-incident pattern connects GitHub's configuration, maintenance, migration, and rollout failures?","answer":"GitHub documented failures involving production configuration changes, database maintenance, topology remediation, data migrations, and automated maintenance rollouts. Across these incidents, broad or disruptive actions exceeded the safety evidence available at the time. This supports requiring explicit preconditions, small rollout scopes, verification gates, and rollback paths.","context":"Cross-incident synthesis from GitHub 2023 incidents.","objective":"Derive a generalized change-safety model.","attempted_solution":"Automated or operator-driven changes were executed through established operational processes.","failure":"Several actions introduced or propagated unhealthy state before sufficient validation occurred.","evidence":"GitHub documented failed rollbacks, propagated topology errors, unsafe maintenance, faulty maintenance configurations, and migration-related unexpected behavior.","cause":"Insufficient precondition verification or rollout isolation.","root_cause":"Change scope and execution speed exceeded the available evidence and containment capability.","impact":"Multiple services experienced degraded or unavailable states.","containment":"Rollback, failover, workload redirection, and known-good-state restoration were used.","remediation":"GitHub strengthened change management, safety checks, migration validation, rollout granularity, and monitoring.","lessons":"Inference: autonomous engineering actions should be gated by explicit safety evidence and should expand scope only after successful verification.","validation_status":"CROSS_INCIDENT_SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"MULTIPLE_PRIMARY_SOURCES","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_titles":["GitHub Availability Report: February 2023","GitHub Availability Report: March 2023","GitHub Availability Report: May 2023","GitHub Availability Report: September 2023"],"publishers":["GitHub"],"evidence_type":"Cross-incident synthesis","interpretation_note":"The generalized change-safety model is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B61-R02","D51-B64-R02","D51-B66-R02","D51-B67-R02","D51-B69-R02"],"relationships":["synthesizes","derived_from","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B70-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Cross-Incident Observability","concept":"Observability must detect state correctness, resource cost, and operational preconditions","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What cross-incident observability lesson emerges from GitHub's 2023 failures?","answer":"GitHub documented observability gaps involving low-volume high-cost database workloads, database topology errors, long-running queries, deployment alerting, and rollout detection. Together these incidents support treating observability as a multi-dimensional system covering resource cost, configuration correctness, workload preconditions, and detection latency.","context":"Cross-incident synthesis from GitHub 2023 incidents.","objective":"Create a reusable observability model for autonomous software engineering.","attempted_solution":"Existing monitoring and alerting were used to detect service degradation and operational risk.","failure":"Some important precursor states were difficult to detect or were not sufficiently actionable before impact.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly documented each of these observability limitations and subsequent improvements.","cause":"Monitoring focused on incomplete dimensions of system state.","root_cause":"Operational telemetry did not consistently represent the actual failure mechanisms.","impact":"Diagnosis and prevention were slower or less reliable than intended.","containment":"Incidents were mitigated through targeted operational actions.","remediation":"GitHub improved alerts, topology monitoring, deployment observability, and high-cost query visibility.","lessons":"Inference: an autonomous engineering brain should observe not only symptoms but also resource cost, configuration state, safety preconditions, and rollout health.","validation_status":"CROSS_INCIDENT_SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"MULTIPLE_PRIMARY_SOURCES","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_titles":["Addressing GitHub's recent availability issues","GitHub Availability Report: February 2023","GitHub Availability Report: March 2023","GitHub Availability Report: May 2023"],"publishers":["GitHub"],"evidence_type":"Cross-incident synthesis","interpretation_note":"The generalized observability model is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B62-R02","D51-B64-R04","D51-B66-R04","D51-B67-R04"],"relationships":["synthesizes","derived_from","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B70-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Cross-Incident Automation Safety","concept":"Safe automation must be capable of refusing execution when safety evidence is insufficient","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What cross-incident lesson connects GitHub's failed rollback, topology remediation, maintenance, and failover actions?","answer":"GitHub documented situations where an automated or planned action could not safely complete: rollback failed, remediation propagated bad state, maintenance proceeded with an active blocking query, and failover refused promotion because data-loss safety could not be established. Together these incidents support fail-closed automation for high-impact operations when safety evidence is incomplete.","context":"Cross-incident synthesis from GitHub 2023 incidents.","objective":"Represent refusal-to-act as a valid autonomous-engineering outcome.","attempted_solution":"Automation attempted rollback, remediation, maintenance, or failover actions.","failure":"Some actions either failed, amplified the incident, or correctly stopped because safety could not be proven.","evidence":"GitHub documented each of these operational states in official incident reports.","cause":"High-impact actions were operating under incomplete or unsafe state information.","root_cause":"Different automation paths lacked uniformly enforced safety preconditions.","impact":"Some operations caused additional degradation while one failover correctly required human evaluation.","containment":"Rollback, manual approval, known-good state restoration, and workload termination were used.","remediation":"Safety checks, monitoring, and recovery processes were improved.","lessons":"Inference: an autonomous system should treat NO_ACTION or REQUIRE_APPROVAL as legitimate outcomes when execution safety cannot be demonstrated.","validation_status":"CROSS_INCIDENT_SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"MULTIPLE_PRIMARY_SOURCES","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_titles":["Addressing GitHub's recent availability issues","GitHub Availability Report: February 2023","GitHub Availability Report: March 2023"],"publishers":["GitHub"],"evidence_type":"Cross-incident synthesis","interpretation_note":"The generalized fail-closed automation principle is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B61-R02","D51-B63-R03","D51-B66-R02","D51-B67-R02"],"relationships":["synthesizes","derived_from","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B70-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Cross-Incident Learning","concept":"Failure knowledge is more reusable when the failed control and successful containment are stored separately","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What dataset-design lesson follows from D51 B61-B70?","answer":"The incidents contain distinct attempted solutions, failure mechanisms, containment actions, remediation changes, and verification states. Separating these dimensions allows an engineering system to learn both what failed and which containment action worked without conflating the original cause with the response.","context":"Cross-incident synthesis from official GitHub incident reports used in D51 B61-B70.","objective":"Improve retrieval and reasoning over operational failure knowledge.","attempted_solution":"Incident reports were analyzed as structured causal records rather than flat summaries.","failure":"A single root-cause field cannot represent failed remediation, successful containment, secondary failures, and final recovery independently.","evidence":"The source incidents contain multiple stages in which attempted actions produced different outcomes.","cause":"Operational incidents are multi-stage state transitions.","root_cause":"Flat incident summaries lose distinctions between attempted, failed, contained, remediated, and verified states.","impact":"Unstructured failure knowledge can lead future systems to repeat failed actions or misinterpret successful containment as root-cause remediation.","containment":"D51 separates attempted_solution, failure, containment, remediation, validation_status, and execution_status.","remediation":"Future records should preserve causal, temporal, evidentiary, and response relationships independently.","lessons":"Inference: an autonomous software-engineering brain should learn from both failed actions and successful containment while preserving their provenance and execution state.","validation_status":"CROSS_INCIDENT_SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"MULTIPLE_PRIMARY_SOURCES","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_titles":["Addressing GitHub's recent availability issues","GitHub Availability Report: February 2023","GitHub Availability Report: March 2023","GitHub Availability Report: May 2023","GitHub Availability Report: September 2023"],"publishers":["GitHub"],"evidence_type":"Cross-incident dataset-design synthesis","interpretation_note":"The dataset-design principle is explicitly marked as inference."},"related_records":["D51-B61-R03","D51-B62-R04","D51-B63-R03","D51-B66-R02","D51-B67-R02","D51-B69-R04"],"relationships":["synthesizes","derived_from","learned_from"]}

{"record_id":"D51-B71-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Queue saturation","concept":"Customer-specific Git push workload exhausted shared worker capacity","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"How did a repository-specific workload cause delays for unrelated GitHub repositories?","answer":"A customer made a large number of pushes to a repository with a specific data shape. Jobs processing those pushes became throttled while communicating with the Git backend, increasing execution time and exhausting a shared worker pool. The depleted pool starved push processing for other repositories.","context":"GitHub June 7 2023 availability incident.","objective":"Process Git pushes across repositories without cross-tenant starvation.","attempted_solution":"Continue processing the incoming push workload through the existing asynchronous job-processing architecture.","failure":"Slow jobs consumed worker capacity until unrelated repositories experienced delayed push processing.","evidence":"GitHub reported that the slow jobs exhausted a worker pool and starved processing for other repositories.","cause":"Git-backend communication throttling increased job execution time.","root_cause":"A customer-specific data shape produced a workload that interacted poorly with Git-backend throttling and shared worker capacity.","impact":"GitHub Actions workflow runs and webhooks were delayed by as much as 55 minutes, and pull requests did not accurately reflect new commits.","containment":"Identify and temporarily disable the source producing the excessive workload, allowing the backlog to drain.","remediation":"GitHub updated Git-backend throttling to fail faster and reduced the Git client timeout inside the job.","lessons":"Shared worker pools require isolation, bounded work, and fast failure for pathological workloads.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-07-12","evidence_type":"Official availability report","source_ref":"turn0search0"},"related_records":["D51-B71-R02","D51-B72-R01"],"relationships":["causes","propagates_to","remediates","learned_from"]} 
{"record_id":"D51-B71-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Timeout design","concept":"Long-running downstream communication amplified worker exhaustion","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why was reducing the Git client timeout part of the remediation?","answer":"The incident involved jobs becoming slow while communicating with the Git backend. GitHub reduced the Git client timeout so jobs would not remain occupied unnecessarily when backend communication was unhealthy or excessively slow.","context":"June 7 2023 internal Git push processing incident.","objective":"Prevent slow downstream operations from monopolizing asynchronous workers.","attempted_solution":"Allow the existing client interaction to continue using its prior timeout behavior.","failure":"Slow operations remained active long enough to increase job execution time and contribute to worker-pool exhaustion.","evidence":"GitHub stated that it reduced the Git client timeout within the job as part of preventing recurrence.","cause":"Slow Git-backend communication.","root_cause":"The processing path did not fail quickly enough when backend communication became slow for a pathological workload.","impact":"Worker capacity was consumed and unrelated push processing was delayed.","containment":"Disable the identified load source and allow queued work to drain.","remediation":"Reduce client timeout and make Git-backend throttling fail faster.","lessons":"Timeouts are capacity-protection mechanisms, not merely latency settings; downstream stalls can become shared-resource failures.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-07-12","evidence_type":"Official availability report","source_ref":"turn0search0"},"related_records":["D51-B71-R01","D51-B72-R02"],"relationships":["related_to","remediates","learned_from"]} 
{"record_id":"D51-B71-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Detection latency","concept":"Monitoring detected the queue problem after customer impact had already begun","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What detection weakness was exposed by the June 7 incident?","answer":"GitHub's monitoring alerted first responders 19 minutes after the internal push-processing queue began experiencing increasing delays.","context":"June 7 2023 GitHub availability incident.","objective":"Detect queue degradation before substantial customer-visible backlog accumulates.","attempted_solution":"Rely on existing monitoring and alerting for the internal job queue.","failure":"The alert arrived after the queue had already been degrading for 19 minutes.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly reported the 19-minute interval before monitoring alerted first responders.","cause":"Increasing queue delays were not surfaced immediately enough by existing monitoring.","root_cause":"Existing detection coverage did not provide sufficiently early warning for this specific queue degradation pattern.","impact":"Customer-facing delays accumulated before responders were alerted.","containment":"Investigate after alerting and identify the source of excessive workload.","remediation":"GitHub identified additional repair items aimed at reducing detection, diagnosis, and recovery time.","lessons":"Queue health should be monitored using leading indicators such as latency, backlog growth, worker saturation, and age of oldest work item.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-07-12","evidence_type":"Official availability report","source_ref":"turn0search0"},"related_records":["D51-B71-R01","D51-B72-R03"],"relationships":["related_to","learned_from"]} 
{"record_id":"D51-B71-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Cross-tenant isolation","concept":"A single workload source propagated into unrelated workloads","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What architectural lesson can be derived from the cross-repository impact?","answer":"The incident demonstrates that shared worker capacity can create cross-workload coupling: a pathological workload in one repository can consume processing capacity needed by unrelated repositories. This is an inference from GitHub's reported worker-pool exhaustion and starvation behavior.","context":"June 7 2023 GitHub incident.","objective":"Prevent one workload class from consuming disproportionate shared capacity.","attempted_solution":"Use a shared worker pool for asynchronous Git push processing.","failure":"Worker exhaustion caused unrelated repositories to experience delays.","evidence":"GitHub reported that slow jobs exhausted a worker pool and starved processing for other repositories.","cause":"Shared capacity was consumed by unusually slow jobs.","root_cause":"Insufficient workload isolation and bounded resource consumption for this failure mode, stated here as an inference.","impact":"Impact crossed repository boundaries.","containment":"Disable the source generating excessive load.","remediation":"Fail faster and reduce client timeout; broader isolation is an architectural lesson rather than a claimed GitHub implementation.","lessons":"Capacity isolation, fairness controls, quotas, and bounded concurrency reduce cross-tenant blast radius.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-07-12","evidence_type":"Official postmortem plus explicit inference","source_ref":"turn0search0"},"related_records":["D51-B71-R01","D51-B72-R04"],"relationships":["derived_from","related_to","learned_from"]} 
{"record_id":"D51-B71-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Backlog recovery","concept":"Disabling the source stopped new pressure but recovery still depended on backlog drainage","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Why did service recover gradually after the problematic source was disabled?","answer":"After GitHub identified and temporarily disabled the source of excessive workload, the system gradually recovered as the existing backlog of jobs was completed.","context":"June 7 2023 GitHub incident.","objective":"Restore normal queue processing after eliminating the workload causing saturation.","attempted_solution":"Disable the source of abnormal load.","failure":"Disabling the source did not instantly eliminate already queued work.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly stated that recovery was gradual as the backlog was completed.","cause":"Existing queued work remained after new load was blocked.","root_cause":"The queue accumulated work faster than workers could process it during the incident.","impact":"Customer-visible delays persisted during backlog drainage.","containment":"Block the identified workload source.","remediation":"Improve throttling and timeout behavior to prevent equivalent backlog growth.","lessons":"Incident recovery must account for both stopping new load and draining accumulated backlog.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-07-12","evidence_type":"Official availability report","source_ref":"turn0search0"},"related_records":["D51-B71-R01","D51-B72-R05"],"relationships":["related_to","propagates_to","learned_from"]} 

{"record_id":"D51-B72-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Fail-fast recovery","concept":"Throttling behavior allowed a pathological workload to remain expensive","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What failed in the Git backend interaction during the June 7 incident?","answer":"Jobs processing a high-volume repository workload became throttled when communicating with the Git backend, increasing job execution time and consuming worker capacity.","context":"June 7 2023 GitHub availability incident.","objective":"Bound the resource cost of Git-backend operations.","attempted_solution":"Process Git operations through normal backend communication and throttling mechanisms.","failure":"Throttled operations remained expensive enough to exhaust workers.","evidence":"GitHub identified Git-backend throttling as the point where jobs became slow and later exhausted the worker pool.","cause":"Backend throttling increased job execution time.","root_cause":"The existing throttling behavior did not fail fast enough for this workload.","impact":"Push processing delays spread to unrelated repositories.","containment":"Disable the source of the increased load.","remediation":"Change throttling to fail faster.","lessons":"Rate limiting and throttling must be evaluated for their resource behavior under pathological workloads, not only for request rejection rates.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-07-12","evidence_type":"Official availability report","source_ref":"turn0search0"},"related_records":["D51-B71-R01","D51-B71-R02"],"relationships":["causes","remediates","learned_from"]} 
{"record_id":"D51-B72-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Client-side protection","concept":"Client timeout was used as a worker-protection boundary","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What remediation reduced the amount of time a job could remain blocked on Git communication?","answer":"GitHub reduced the Git client timeout within the job so slow Git-backend communication would not keep the worker occupied for as long.","context":"June 7 2023 GitHub availability incident.","objective":"Limit worker occupancy caused by slow downstream communication.","attempted_solution":"Use the existing Git client timeout.","failure":"The prior timeout behavior contributed to long-running jobs during backend throttling.","evidence":"GitHub reported reducing the Git client timeout as a preventive action.","cause":"Slow backend communication prolonged job execution.","root_cause":"The processing path lacked a sufficiently aggressive timeout for the observed failure mode.","impact":"Worker capacity was consumed and queue latency increased.","containment":"Disable the load source and process the backlog.","remediation":"Reduce Git client timeout.","lessons":"Every bounded worker should have explicit downstream time limits aligned with its capacity budget and recovery objectives.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-07-12","evidence_type":"Official availability report","source_ref":"turn0search0"},"related_records":["D51-B71-R02","D51-B72-R01"],"relationships":["remediates","related_to","learned_from"]} 
{"record_id":"D51-B72-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Traffic-source mitigation","concept":"Blocking the identified workload source was an effective containment action","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What immediate containment action stopped additional pressure on the overloaded queue?","answer":"GitHub temporarily disabled the source responsible for the increased workload. The system then gradually recovered as queued work completed.","context":"June 7 2023 GitHub incident.","objective":"Stop continued production of work faster than the worker pool could process it.","attempted_solution":"Continue normal processing while investigating the queue delay.","failure":"Continued load would prolong queue growth and worker exhaustion.","evidence":"GitHub stated that temporarily disabling the source allowed the system to gradually recover.","cause":"Sustained abnormal workload generation.","root_cause":"The triggering source generated a workload that exceeded safe processing behavior for the system.","impact":"Continued workload would have prolonged the degraded state.","containment":"Temporarily disable the source producing the excessive load.","remediation":"Improve throttling, timeout, detection, diagnosis, and recovery mechanisms.","lessons":"For overloaded asynchronous systems, source suppression can be a safer first containment step than attempting to process all incoming work normally.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-07-12","evidence_type":"Official availability report","source_ref":"turn0search0"},"related_records":["D51-B71-R05","D51-B72-R05"],"relationships":["contains","remediates","learned_from"]} 
{"record_id":"D51-B72-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Asynchronous dependency chains","concept":"A delayed internal queue propagated into multiple customer-visible asynchronous services","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"Which downstream effects resulted from the push-processing queue delay?","answer":"GitHub reported GitHub Actions workflow-run delays and webhook delays of up to 55 minutes, while pull requests did not accurately reflect new commits.","context":"June 7 2023 GitHub availability incident.","objective":"Maintain timely propagation of Git pushes into dependent asynchronous systems.","attempted_solution":"Continue processing pushes through the existing asynchronous queue.","failure":"Queue starvation delayed dependent asynchronous workloads.","evidence":"GitHub directly reported delays to Actions, webhooks, and pull-request commit visibility.","cause":"Push-processing queue delay.","root_cause":"Worker-pool exhaustion caused by slow jobs.","impact":"Multiple dependent GitHub services experienced stale or delayed state.","containment":"Disable excessive workload source.","remediation":"Fail faster at the Git backend and reduce client timeout.","lessons":"Queue dependencies should be mapped explicitly because a failure in one asynchronous stage can create stale state in multiple downstream systems.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-07-12","evidence_type":"Official availability report","source_ref":"turn0search0"},"related_records":["D51-B71-R01","D51-B71-R05"],"relationships":["propagates_to","related_to","learned_from"]} 
{"record_id":"D51-B72-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Recovery objectives","concept":"Detection, diagnosis, and recovery were separately identified improvement targets","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"What operational capabilities did GitHub identify for improvement after the June 7 incident?","answer":"GitHub identified additional repair items intended to reduce the time required to detect, diagnose, and recover from similar incidents.","context":"June 7 2023 GitHub availability incident.","objective":"Reduce the full incident-response cycle rather than focusing only on prevention.","attempted_solution":"Use existing monitoring and incident-response mechanisms.","failure":"The incident demonstrated that detection and recovery could take substantial time after degradation began.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly described additional work to reduce detection, diagnosis, and recovery time.","cause":"Existing operational paths were not sufficiently optimized for this failure mode.","root_cause":"Incident-response latency remained a contributor to customer impact.","impact":"Longer time-to-detection and recovery extended the degraded period.","containment":"Disable the triggering workload.","remediation":"Improve detection, diagnosis, and recovery mechanisms.","lessons":"Reliability engineering should optimize the complete incident lifecycle, not only prevention.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-07-12","evidence_type":"Official availability report","source_ref":"turn0search0"},"related_records":["D51-B71-R03","D51-B72-R03"],"relationships":["learned_from","remediates","related_to"]} 

{"record_id":"D51-B73-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Failover testing","concept":"A live failover test unintentionally caused a production outage","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"How did a planned high-availability failover test cause customer impact?","answer":"GitHub performed a live failover test of a newly completed redundant Internet edge facility. A network-path configuration issue on the secondary side prevented it from functioning correctly as primary, causing a production outage.","context":"GitHub June 29 2023 availability incident.","objective":"Validate that a redundant Internet edge facility could operate as the primary facility during failure.","attempted_solution":"Perform a live failover test against production traffic.","failure":"The secondary facility contained a network-path configuration problem that prevented correct primary operation.","evidence":"GitHub stated that the failover test exposed a configuration issue and inadvertently caused a production outage.","cause":"Incorrect network-path configuration in the secondary facility.","root_cause":"The redundant facility had not been validated sufficiently against the exact primary operating path before live failover.","impact":"GitHub was down in parts of North America, particularly the US East coast and South America, for approximately 32 minutes.","containment":"Revert the problematic change and restore the primary facility.","remediation":"Fix the network configuration and improve failover testing and configuration validation.","lessons":"Redundancy is not sufficient if the standby path is not proven to operate correctly under production failover conditions.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-07-12","evidence_type":"Official availability report","source_ref":"turn0search0"},"related_records":["D51-B73-R02","D51-B73-R05"],"relationships":["causes","related_to","learned_from"]} 
