{"record_id":"D51-B77-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Consumer coordination","concept":"A consumer stuck in repeated coordination failure stopped job dispatch","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"How did the Kafka consumer failure prevent background jobs from running?","answer":"The Kafka consumer became stuck in a loop and repeatedly failed to stabilize before its session timed out and coordination restarted. The queueing service continued accepting and recording work but could not pass jobs to workers.","context":"August 29 2023 GitHub background-job incident.","objective":"Dispatch accepted jobs reliably to workers.","attempted_solution":"Continue accepting and queueing work while the dispatcher maintained consumer-group coordination.","failure":"The dispatcher could accept work but could not forward jobs to workers.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly stated that the service accepted and recorded incoming work while being unable to pass jobs to workers.","cause":"Consumer coordination repeatedly failed.","root_cause":"CPU throttling and short session timeouts interacted to create an unstable consumer loop.","impact":"Asynchronous workloads were delayed by up to an hour.","containment":"Shift load to standby service and redeploy primary.","remediation":"Improve monitoring and prevent recurrence.","lessons":"Queue acceptance and worker dispatch are separate availability domains; monitoring must distinguish them.","validation_status":"VALIDATED_AGAINST_OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: August 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-09-13","evidence_type":"Official availability report","source_ref":"turn0search3"},"related_records":["D51-B77-R01","D51-B77-R03"],"relationships":["propagates_to","related_to","learned_from"]