{"record_id":"D51-B79-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Recovery verification","concept":"Configuration restoration and service restoration are distinct states","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What recovery-state distinction appears in the June 29 incident?","answer":"The incident demonstrates that reverting a configuration and restoring the customer-visible service are separate recovery states. GitHub reverted the change within two minutes, but traffic rebalancing and border-router reconvergence still required additional time.","context":"June 29 2023 GitHub failover incident.","objective":"Model recovery accurately from operator action through customer-visible restoration.","attempted_solution":"Treat rollback completion as equivalent to full service restoration.","failure":"Customer connectivity required additional network convergence after rollback.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly described both rapid reversion and subsequent traffic and routing convergence.","cause":"Distributed routing state needed to reconverge.","root_cause":"Failover state existed across multiple network components.","impact":"Recovery lagged behind the rollback operation.","containment":"Restore primary and allow convergence.","remediation":"Improve failover testing and recovery time.","lessons":"Recovery automation should verify externally observable service health rather than stopping at successful configuration changes.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-07-12","evidence_type":"Official postmortem plus explicit inference","source_ref":"turn0search0"},"related_records":["D51-B73-R03","D51-B73-R04"],"relationships":["derived_from","learned_from"]} 
{"record_id":"D51-B79-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Queue recovery","concept":"Stopping workload generation and draining backlog are separate recovery operations","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What common recovery principle appears in the June 7 and August 15 queue incidents?","answer":"Both incidents show that containment and recovery are separate stages: the problematic workload source must first be stopped or limited, after which already queued work must be processed before normal latency returns.","context":"Comparison of GitHub June 7 and August 15 2023 incidents.","objective":"Model queue incident recovery as source control followed by backlog drainage.","attempted_solution":"Assume stopping new work immediately restores service.","failure":"Existing queued work remained after new pressure was removed.","evidence":"GitHub described gradual recovery as backlogs were processed in both incidents.","cause":"Accumulated asynchronous backlog.","root_cause":"Input workload exceeded processing capacity.","impact":"Customer-visible delays persisted after containment.","containment":"Disable or block problematic sources.","remediation":"Improve capacity, timeout, throttling, and source controls.","lessons":"Recovery automation should track backlog age and drain rate after containment rather than declaring recovery immediately.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2023; GitHub Availability Report: August 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-07-12 and 2023-09-13","evidence_type":"Official postmortems plus comparative inference","source_ref":"turn0search0; turn0search3"},"related_records":["D51-B71-R05","D51-B76-R04"],"relationships":["derived_from","contrasts_with","learned_from"]} 
{"record_id":"D51-B79-R05","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Blast-radius reduction","concept":"Source-level controls reduced the scope of overloaded asynchronous systems","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What common mitigation pattern appears across GitHub's June and August queue incidents?","answer":"Both incidents used source-level containment rather than requiring complete shutdown of the affected asynchronous service: GitHub disabled the problematic push source in June and blocked events from increased-load webhook sources in August.","context":"Comparison of official GitHub June and August 2023 incidents.","objective":"Reduce overload while preserving unaffected workloads.","attempted_solution":"Continue processing all incoming work without selective admission control.","failure":"Shared capacity became overloaded.","evidence":"Both official reports document targeted source blocking as containment.","cause":"Excessive workload from identifiable sources.","root_cause":"Shared asynchronous capacity was insufficient for the triggering workloads.","impact":"Broad service degradation.","containment":"Block or disable contributing sources.","remediation":"Improve capacity and workload controls.","lessons":"Fine-grained admission control can reduce blast radius and preserve unaffected workloads during overload.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2023; GitHub Availability Report: August 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-07-12 and 2023-09-13","evidence_type":"Official postmortems plus comparative inference","source_ref":"turn0search0; turn0search3"},"related_records":["D51-B72-R03","D51-B75-R02"],"relationships":["derived_from","learned_from","remediates"]} 

{"record_id":"D51-B80-R01","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Operational failure taxonomy","concept":"Queue failures can originate from workload shape, sustained volume, or dispatcher coordination","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What distinct queue failure classes are represented by the June and August GitHub incidents?","answer":"The documented incidents represent at least three distinct mechanisms: workload-shape-induced slow jobs and worker exhaustion; sustained webhook volume causing queue backlog; and dispatcher coordination failure caused by resource throttling interacting with Kafka timeouts.","context":"GitHub June and August 2023 availability incidents.","objective":"Build mechanism-specific classification for asynchronous-system failures.","attempted_solution":"Classify all asynchronous incidents simply as queue overload.","failure":"The same symptom of delayed work can originate from materially different mechanisms.","evidence":"GitHub's official reports describe these three separate mechanisms.","cause":"Different workload, capacity, and coordination conditions.","root_cause":"Mechanism-specific system failures.","impact":"Delayed asynchronous work across multiple services.","containment":"Source blocking, load shedding, or standby activation depending on mechanism.","remediation":"Mechanism-specific controls.","lessons":"Incident knowledge bases should classify causal mechanisms, not merely customer-visible symptoms.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2023; GitHub Availability Report: August 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-07-12 and 2023-09-13","evidence_type":"Official postmortems plus comparative inference","source_ref":"turn0search0; turn0search3"},"related_records":