{"record_id":"D51-B80-R02","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Recovery-state modeling","concept":"Containment, backlog drainage, failover, and full recovery are different operational states","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What recovery-state model can be derived from these incidents?","answer":"The incidents support separating containment from recovery: stop or limit the trigger, restore processing capability, drain accumulated work or converge network state, and verify customer-visible service recovery.","context":"Synthesis of GitHub June, July, and August 2023 incidents.","objective":"Avoid declaring recovery solely from an internal mitigation action.","attempted_solution":"Treat the first successful remediation step as completion of the incident.","failure":"Customer-visible recovery could lag behind rollback, source blocking, standby activation, or capacity restoration.","evidence":"GitHub documented gradual queue recovery, network convergence after rollback, and delayed restoration after capacity loss.","cause":"Distributed systems retain backlog or convergence state after the trigger is removed.","root_cause":"Recovery is multi-stage rather than atomic.","impact":"Premature recovery declaration can obscure remaining customer impact.","containment":"Apply mechanism-specific immediate mitigation.","remediation":"Track service health through backlog drainage, convergence, and capacity restoration.","lessons":"Autonomous incident systems should model recovery states explicitly and require observable evidence before transitioning to recovered.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2023; GitHub Availability Report: July 2023; GitHub Availability Report: August 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date"