{"record_id":"D51-B80-R03","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Redundancy versus readiness","concept":"Redundant infrastructure has different value depending on whether activation paths are validated","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What distinction should an autonomous engineering system make between redundancy and usable redundancy?","answer":"The June failover incident shows that redundant infrastructure can contain an unvalidated activation path, while the August dispatcher incident shows a standby path being used for mitigation. Therefore redundancy should be modeled separately from verified failover readiness.","context":"GitHub June 29 and August 29 2023 incidents.","objective":"Represent actual recovery capability rather than counting redundant components.","attempted_solution":"Treat the existence of primary and secondary components as proof of resilience.","failure":"A standby network path failed during activation in June, while a standby dispatcher supported mitigation in August.","evidence":"GitHub explicitly documented both scenarios.","cause":"Different operational readiness of redundant components.","root_cause":"Redundancy does not itself prove functional failover capability.","impact":"Potentially large difference in incident recovery capability.","containment":"Use tested standby paths and controlled failover.","remediation":"Exercise failover and monitor standby readiness.","lessons":"Reliability inventories should distinguish configured redundancy, tested redundancy, and recently verified failover readiness.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2023; GitHub Availability Report: August 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-07-12 and 2023-09-13","evidence_type":"Official postmortems plus comparative inference","source_ref":"turn0search0; turn0search3"},"related_records":["D51-B73-R01","D51-B78-R05"],"relationships":["derived_from","contrasts_with","learned_from"]} 
{"record_id":"D51-B80-R04","dataset_id":"D51","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Failed Attempts","subtopic":"Capacity protection","concept":"Fast failure and selective admission are complementary overload controls","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"What combined overload-control strategy is supported by the June and August incidents?","answer":"The incidents support combining fast failure for expensive downstream operations with selective admission control for excessive workload sources. GitHub used faster Git-backend failure and shorter client timeout in June, and source blocking plus increased spike capacity in August.","context":"GitHub June and August 2023 availability incidents.","objective":"Prevent pathological workloads from consuming shared capacity indefinitely.","attempted_solution":"Rely on aggregate capacity without aggressive request-level and source-level controls.","failure":"Shared resources became saturated and asynchronous queues accumulated.","evidence":"GitHub documented both remediation patterns in its official reports.","cause":"Excessive or expensive workload.","root_cause":"Insufficient protection against pathological resource consumption.","impact":"Worker exhaustion or queue backlog.","containment":"Disable/block contributing sources.","remediation":"Fail faster, shorten timeouts, improve capacity, and manage sources.","lessons":"Robust overload protection operates at multiple levels: operation timeout, request admission, source control, and capacity scaling.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED_INFERENCE","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"OFFICIAL_POSTMORTEM","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub Availability Report: June 2023; GitHub Availability Report: August 2023","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2023-07-12 and 2023-09-13","evidence_type":"Official postmortems plus comparative inference","source_ref":"turn0search0; turn0search3"},"related_records":["D51-B72-R01","D51-B75-R05"],"relationships":["derived_from","learned_from","remediates"]} 
