{"record_id":"D52-B35-R01","dataset_id":"D52","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Successful Repairs","subtopic":"Cache rollback","concept":"Git HTTPS proxy cache-rewrite repair","knowledge_type":"VERIFIED_FACT","question":"How was the February 9 2026 GitHub HTTPS degradation diagnosed?","answer":"GitHub determined that a user-settings caching configuration change caused a large volume of simultaneous cache rewrites. Asynchronous rewrites overwhelmed shared background-work infrastructure and caused connection exhaustion in the Git HTTPS proxy.","context":"February 9 2026 GitHub web, API, Git, Actions, Copilot, and related service degradation.","objective":"Identify the cache-write amplification and proxy connection-exhaustion chain.","attempted_solution":"Deploy a caching configuration change that generated cache rewrites.","failure":"Background work and Git HTTPS proxy connections were exhausted.","evidence":"GitHub's February report explicitly describes the cache rewrite and connection-exhaustion mechanism.","cause":"Large volume of asynchronous cache rewrites.","root_cause":"Caching configuration caused write amplification against shared infrastructure.","impact":"Git HTTPS operations and multiple dependent services experienced failures.","containment":"Disable asynchronous cache rewrites and restart affected Git proxy services.","remediation":"Optimize caching, add self-throttling, strengthen rollback safeguards, and make proxy recovery automatic.","lessons":"Cache write amplification can become a cross-service failure when background infrastructure is shared.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_title":"GitHub availability report: February 2026","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2026-03-11","evidence_type":"Official availability report","source_ref":"GITHUB_AVAILABILITY_FEBRUARY_2026"},"related_records":["