{"record_id":"D53-B25-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Failed Repairs","subtopic":"Capacity monitoring","concept":"Database saturation detection","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can waiting for database saturation before applying protection be an incomplete repair strategy?","answer":"By the time saturation is visible, retries, queues, and dependent workloads may already have amplified the failure; earlier capacity monitoring and automatic protection can reduce secondary impact.","context":"GitHub August 26 2026 Actions database saturation incident.","objective":"Prevent database saturation from cascading into workflow failures.","proposed_repair":"Wait for saturation and then apply manual throttling.","reason":"Manual throttling was available as emergency containment.","attempted_repair":"Responders manually adjusted throttles after saturation developed.","failure":"Initial throttling was insufficient and required gradual tuning while workflow failures and delays continued.","evidence":"GitHub described manual throttle tuning and identified capacity monitoring and automatic circuit-breaking improvements as follow-up work.","rejected_result":"Reactive protection occurred after significant resource stress.","regression_risk":"Late protection allows queues and retries to amplify load.","lesson":"Capacity controls should activate before hard resource exhaustion when reliable leading indicators exist.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_title":"GitHub availability report: August 2026","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2026-09-09","evidence_type":"Official availability report","source_ref":"GITHUB_AVAILABILITY_AUGUST_2026"}],"related_records":["D53-B19-R02","D53-B20-R04"],"relationships":{"derived_from":["D53-B19-R02","D53-B20-R04"]}}
