{"record_id":"D53-B30-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Failed Repairs","subtopic":"Recovery completeness","concept":"Customer-workflow closure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is clearing infrastructure alarms insufficient to close a failed-repair record?","answer":"A repair is incomplete if customer workflows, queued work, dependent clients, or derived state remain impaired after infrastructure alarms normalize.","context":"Generalized from GitHub Actions, MCP, DNS, certificate, and database incidents.","objective":"Define a customer-visible completion criterion for repair records.","proposed_repair":"Close the repair when infrastructure health returns to normal.","reason":"Infrastructure health is a direct operational signal.","attempted_repair":"Infrastructure components recovered before all workflow effects had necessarily cleared.","failure":"Backlogs, delayed jobs, runner states, search results, or dependent operations could require additional recovery.","evidence":"Official GitHub reports document workflow and backlog recovery after infrastructure restoration.","rejected_result":"Infrastructure recovery was not equivalent to customer-workflow recovery.","regression_risk":"Premature closure can hide residual impact.","lesson":"Repair completion should require critical customer-workflow postconditions, not only infrastructure health.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_title":"GitHub availability reports: July-August 2026","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2026-09-09","evidence_type":"Official incident reports","source_ref":"GITHUB_AVAILABILITY_JULY_AUGUST_2026"}],"related_records":["D53-B21-R03","D53-B22-R04","D53-B26-R04"],"relationships":{"derived_from":["D53-B21-R03","D53-B22-R04","D53-B26-R04"]}}
