{"record_id":"D53-B31-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Failed Repairs","subtopic":"Deployment Capacity","concept":"Webhook throttling as containment rather than complete repair","knowledge_type":"EXPERIENCE","question":"Why was throttling webhook-triggered work not a complete repair?","answer":"Throttling helped stabilize the saturated system, but it intentionally reduced incoming work and did not remove the underlying lack of deployment headroom. GitHub subsequently identified additional headroom, autoscaling, safer deployment, and backlog-draining improvements as required work.","context":"August 6, 2026 GitHub Actions incident.","objective":"Distinguish emergency containment from durable repair.","attempted_solution":"Throttle incoming webhook-triggered work.","failure":"The measure reduced pressure but did not correct the underlying capacity and deployment-safety weakness.","evidence":"The report describes throttling as part of stabilization and separately lists headroom, autoscaling, and safer deployment improvements.","cause":"Incoming workload exceeded available recovery capacity.","root_cause":"Insufficient resilience to temporary capacity reductions during deployment.","impact":"Work intake was intentionally slowed while the service recovered.","containment":"Throttle inbound work.","remediation":"Increase capacity headroom and autoscaling and improve load shedding and backlog handling.","lessons":"A repair that suppresses demand may be effective containment without being a durable resolution.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_title":"GitHub availability report: August 2026","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2026-09-09","evidence_type":"Official availability report","source_ref":"GITHUB_AVAILABILITY_AUGUST_2026"}],"related_records":["D53-B31-R01","D53-B31-R02"],"relations