{"record_id":"D53-B40-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0.0","schema_version":"1.0.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Failed Repairs","subtopic":"Systemic Recovery","concept":"The complete repair required clearing residual queued state after infrastructure recovery","knowledge_type":"EXPERIENCE","question":"Why did the August 26 repair include explicit stuck-run cleanup?","answer":"Infrastructure health recovery did not automatically release every run left waiting for a runner, so a separate cleanup mechanism was required for residual queued state.","context":"August 26, 2026 GitHub Actions incident.","objective":"Capture systemic recovery completeness.","attempted_solution":"Assume repaired infrastructure would automatically recover all queued jobs.","failure":"Some larger and self-hosted jobs remained stuck.","evidence":"GitHub states that a subset remained stuck and a change was deployed to release them.","cause":"Residual queue state survived primary service recovery.","root_cause":"Recovery mechanisms did not automatically reconcile every affected job state.","impact":"Persistent delays for a subset of jobs.","containment":"Deploy a change to release stuck jobs.","remediation":"Improve automated clearing of queued/waiting-for-runner states.","lessons":"A repair is incomplete until residual state created by the incident is reconciled or explicitly handled.","validation_status":"SOURCE_VALIDATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"PRIMARY_SOURCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_title":"GitHub availability report: August 2026","publisher":"GitHub","publication_date":"2026-09-09","evidence_type":"Official availability report","source_ref":"GITHUB_AVAILABILITY_AUGUST_2026"}],"related_records":["D53-B40-R04"],"relationships":{"verifies":["D53-B40-R04"],"related_to":["D53-B35-R05"]}}

{"record_id":"D53-B71-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Security-Control Repair Failures","subtopic":"Security control restoration","concept":"Repairing a disabled security control without preserving its original enforcement boundary","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can restoring a security control fail even when the control becomes active again?","answer":"A repair can reactivate the control while restoring it with broader scope, weaker conditions, different defaults, or incomplete policy state. The control is operational but no longer enforces the intended security property.","context":"A security mechanism was disabled or degraded and an emergency repair restores service by re-enabling it with reconstructed configuration.","objective":"Restore the security control while preserving its original security semantics and enforcement boundary.","attempted_solution":"Re-enable the control using a minimal configuration believed to match the previous state.","failure":"The repaired control accepts or processes cases that the original policy would have rejected.","evidence":"The repair must be evaluated against the intended security invariant, not merely the control's enabled status.","cause":"The repair validated availability of the mechanism but not equivalence of its enforcement semantics.","root_cause":"Missing security-property verification for the repaired control configuration.","impact":"Unauthorized or unsafe behavior can persist while operational health appears normal.","containment":"Keep the affected control in a fail-safe or explicitly restricted mode until its security invariants are verified.","remediation":"Restore policy from an authoritative known-good source and verify scope, conditions, defaults, exclusions, and deny behavior before normal operation.","lessons":"Control activation is not evidence that the protected security property has been restored.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; security-control repair reasoning","related_records":["D53-B71-R02","D53-B72-R01"],"relationships":["same-domain-security-control","requires-security-invariant-verification"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B71-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Security-Control Repair Failures","subtopic":"Fail-open recovery","concept":"Repair path changes a security mechanism from fail-closed to fail-open","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a repair intended to restore availability weaken a security control?","answer":"A repair may bypass a failing enforcement dependency and permit requests when the dependency is unavailable. This can restore availability but silently convert a fail-closed security boundary into fail-open behavior.","context":"A security control depends on a policy service that becomes unavailable during recovery.","objective":"Recover service without weakening the security invariant.","attempted_solution":"Permit traffic when policy evaluation cannot be completed so dependent functionality can continue.","failure":"Requests bypass authorization or security enforcement during policy-service failure.","evidence":"Fail-open and fail-closed behavior are security semantics and must be treated as explicit requirements during repair.","cause":"Availability was treated as the primary recovery condition.","root_cause":"The repair plan omitted the security behavior required when the enforcement dependency is unavailable.","impact":"A dependency outage can become a security exposure rather than only an availability incident.","containment":"Restrict affected operations or isolate the dependency path until authoritative policy evaluation is available.","remediation":"Define and enforce explicit failure behavior for every security dependency, with least-privilege emergency handling where business continuity is mandatory.","lessons":"Recovery logic must preserve failure semantics, not merely successful-path behavior.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; fail-safe security engineering","related_records":["D53-B71-R01","D53-B79-R03"],"relationships":["security-failure-semantics","availability-security-tradeoff"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B71-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Security-Control Repair Failures","subtopic":"Emergency bypass","concept":"Emergency security bypass remains active after recovery","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What failure occurs when an emergency security bypass survives the condition that required it?","answer":"An emergency bypass can become persistent if its activation and removal are not independently tracked. Normal service can return while the temporary exception continues to weaken enforcement.","context":"An emergency remediation temporarily bypasses a security control to restore critical functionality.","objective":"Ensure temporary security exceptions have bounded lifetime and explicit closure verification.","attempted_solution":"Document the bypass as temporary and rely on normal operators to remove it after recovery.","failure":"The bypass remains active because recovery verification checks service health but not exception state.","evidence":"Temporary security exceptions require explicit ownership, expiry, state visibility, and post-recovery verification.","cause":"The repair workflow treated bypass removal as an operational side effect rather than a controlled state transition.","root_cause":"No mandatory closure invariant existed for emergency security exceptions.","impact":"A short-lived recovery measure can create a long-lived reduction in security posture.","containment":"Maintain the exception under explicit restrictive scope and establish an immediate removal checkpoint.","remediation":"Represent emergency bypasses as first-class state with owner, reason, scope, creation time, expiry, approval, and mandatory post-recovery closure verification.","lessons":"Every emergency security bypass needs an independently verifiable end condition.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; emergency security control governance","related_records":["D53-B71-R02","D53-B84-R02"],"relationships":["temporary-exception-lifecycle","security-governance"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B71-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Security-Control Repair Failures","subtopic":"Detection and enforcement coupling","concept":"Repair restores enforcement but leaves detection disabled","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is restoring a preventive security control insufficient when its detection counterpart remains broken?","answer":"Prevention and detection provide different security properties. A repair that restores blocking but leaves telemetry, alerting, or audit evidence unavailable can conceal subsequent violations and prevent reliable investigation.","context":"A security mechanism has both enforcement and monitoring components, and only the enforcement path is repaired.","objective":"Restore both protection and the evidence required to detect and investigate violations.","attempted_solution":"Verify that prohibited operations are blocked and declare the security control recovered.","failure":"The control blocks tested violations but produces insufficient evidence for real incidents.","evidence":"Security validation must cover enforcement and required observability or audit properties separately.","cause":"The repair test modeled only prevention behavior.","root_cause":"The security control was treated as one binary capability instead of a set of distinct security properties.","impact":"Subsequent attacks or policy violations may remain undetected or difficult to investigate.","containment":"Restrict high-risk operations until required security evidence is restored.","remediation":"Define separate invariants and verification checks for enforcement, detection, auditability, and alert delivery.","lessons":"A repaired security control must restore every required security property, not only the blocking path.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; security observability reasoning","related_records":["D53-B71-R01","D53-B86-R01"],"relationships":["security-property-decomposition","verification-completeness"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B71-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Security-Control Repair Failures","subtopic":"Security policy restoration","concept":"Repair restores a security control from a stale policy snapshot","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can restoring a security control from an old policy snapshot introduce a new security failure?","answer":"The historical configuration may be structurally valid but obsolete. Restoring it can remove newer restrictions, identities, exclusions, or threat-response rules that were intentionally introduced after the snapshot was created.","context":"A security control loses configuration and an older stored configuration is used for emergency restoration.","objective":"Restore the control without regressing subsequently established security requirements.","attempted_solution":"Restore the latest locally available configuration snapshot without checking policy lineage.","failure":"The control operates normally but silently reverts security decisions to an outdated policy state.","evidence":"Configuration validity does not establish policy freshness or security equivalence.","cause":"The repair selected a technically valid snapshot without validating its policy version and supersession history.","root_cause":"Security policy provenance and freshness were not part of restoration eligibility.","impact":"Previously mitigated security conditions can reappear after recovery.","containment":"Apply only explicitly approved restrictive policy state until authoritative current policy is reconstructed.","remediation":"Track policy versions, lineage, approval state, effective time, and supersession relationships; reject stale snapshots for unrestricted restoration.","lessons":"Security configuration recovery requires semantic freshness, not merely syntactic validity.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; policy provenance reasoning","related_records":["D53-B42-R02","D53-B71-R01"],"relationships":["policy-freshness","security-configuration-lineage"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B72-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Authorization & Policy Repair Failures","subtopic":"Policy propagation","concept":"Authorization repair becomes effective only on some enforcement nodes","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can an authorization repair fail when the corrected policy is valid but incompletely propagated?","answer":"Different enforcement nodes can temporarily evaluate different policy versions. A request may therefore be allowed by one node and denied by another, creating inconsistent authorization behavior.","context":"A policy correction is distributed to multiple application or policy-enforcement instances.","objective":"Ensure the repaired authorization policy reaches every required enforcement point before declaring recovery.","attempted_solution":"Publish the corrected policy and verify it on one representative node.","failure":"Other nodes continue evaluating the previous authorization policy.","evidence":"Distributed policy repair requires coverage verification across all relevant enforcement points or an explicit bounded rollout state.","cause":"Verification was performed against a sample rather than the complete enforcement population.","root_cause":"The repair lacked a policy-version convergence invariant.","impact":"Authorization decisions become dependent on routing or node selection.","containment":"Route sensitive operations only through verified enforcement points or restrict them until convergence is established.","remediation":"Attach policy versions to enforcement decisions, measure propagation coverage, and block unrestricted recovery until required nodes report the expected version.","lessons":"A valid authorization policy is not globally repaired until its enforcement population is known to have converged.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; distributed policy propagation reasoning","related_records":["D53-B63-R01","D53-B72-R02"],"relationships":["policy-version-convergence","distributed-authorization"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B72-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Authorization & Policy Repair Failures","subtopic":"Deny/allow conflict","concept":"Repair introduces contradictory authorization rules with precedence ambiguity","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a policy repair fail when individual allow and deny rules are each valid?","answer":"A repair can create overlapping rules whose precedence is unclear or differs between evaluators. The resulting authorization decision can depend on rule ordering, evaluator implementation, or request context.","context":"An authorization policy is repaired by adding a restrictive exception to an existing allow rule set.","objective":"Preserve deterministic and documented authorization semantics.","attempted_solution":"Add a deny rule without validating interactions with existing rules and precedence semantics.","failure":"Equivalent requests receive different decisions depending on evaluation path or policy engine behavior.","evidence":"Authorization correctness depends on the complete policy evaluation semantics, not isolated rule validity.","cause":"The repair considered the new rule independently from overlapping rules.","root_cause":"No explicit conflict-resolution invariant or policy evaluation test existed.","impact":"Security decisions become unpredictable and difficult to audit.","containment":"Restrict affected policy paths until precedence is resolved and tested.","remediation":"Normalize overlapping rules, define deterministic precedence, test allow/deny conflict cases, and record the resulting decision rationale.","lessons":"Authorization repair must reason about rule interactions and precedence, not individual rules alone.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; authorization policy semantics","related_records":["D53-B72-R01","D53-B71-R01"],"relationships":["policy-conflict-resolution","authorization-determinism"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B72-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Authorization & Policy Repair Failures","subtopic":"Role mapping","concept":"Repair restores identity mapping but assigns permissions from obsolete role semantics","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can repairing role mapping restore authentication while producing incorrect authorization?","answer":"Identity-to-role mapping and role-to-permission semantics are separate layers. A repaired mapping can identify the correct role while that role's permissions no longer match the current authorization model.","context":"Role mappings are reconstructed after identity or directory metadata loss.","objective":"Restore authorization using current role semantics rather than assuming historical mappings remain valid.","attempted_solution":"Recreate the previous identity-to-role mappings and validate successful login.","failure":"Users authenticate correctly but receive permissions inconsistent with current role definitions.","evidence":"Authentication success does not prove authorization correctness.","cause":"The repair verified identity resolution but not effective permissions.","root_cause":"Role mapping and permission semantics were not independently verified.","impact":"Users can receive excessive or insufficient privileges.","containment":"Apply least-privilege fallback permissions until effective authorization is verified.","remediation":"Validate identity mapping, role definition version, permission set, inheritance, and effective authorization independently.","lessons":"Authorization recovery must verify effective permissions, not merely successful role resolution.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; authorization-layer separation","related_records":["D53-B48-R02","D53-B72-R01"],"relationships":["identity-role-separation","least-privilege-recovery"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B72-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Authorization & Policy Repair Failures","subtopic":"Policy cache","concept":"Authorization repair updates policy source but stale evaluator state preserves old decisions","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a policy repair fail when the authoritative policy store contains the correct rule?","answer":"An evaluator can continue using a stale in-memory or locally materialized policy. Updating the source does not guarantee that every decision point has discarded obsolete authorization state.","context":"The policy repository is corrected after an authorization incident.","objective":"Ensure decision points evaluate the repaired policy version.","attempted_solution":"Verify the corrected policy in the authoritative policy store and restart no services.","failure":"Existing evaluator processes continue making decisions from stale policy state.","evidence":"Policy-source correctness and evaluator-state correctness are separate recovery conditions.","cause":"The repair treated source update as equivalent to policy activation.","root_cause":"No evaluator policy-version or freshness verification existed.","impact":"The intended authorization correction remains partially ineffective.","containment":"Restrict sensitive operations to evaluators confirmed to use the repaired policy version.","remediation":"Invalidate or refresh evaluator state explicitly and verify active policy version at every enforcement boundary.","lessons":"Authorization repairs must verify active decision state, not just authoritative configuration state.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; authorization state reasoning","related_records":["D53-B64-R02","D53-B72-R01"],"relationships":["active-policy-state","policy-cache-invalidation"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B72-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Authorization & Policy Repair Failures","subtopic":"Policy dependency recovery","concept":"Authorization repair assumes policy dependency availability without defining restricted fallback behavior","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What happens when an authorization repair depends on a policy source that is still unavailable?","answer":"The repair may repeatedly retry policy retrieval, block legitimate traffic unpredictably, or choose an unsafe fallback. Without an explicit degraded-mode policy, the repair cannot reliably preserve authorization semantics.","context":"An authorization service depends on an unavailable policy repository during recovery.","objective":"Define deterministic and security-preserving behavior while policy data is unavailable.","attempted_solution":"Retry policy retrieval until the source becomes healthy.","failure":"The system oscillates between unavailable, blocked, and fallback behavior without a bounded security decision.","evidence":"Dependency failure behavior must be an explicit authorization requirement.","cause":"The repair plan specified recovery of the dependency but not the authorization behavior during dependency unavailability.","root_cause":"No degraded authorization policy was defined.","impact":"Availability and authorization guarantees become unpredictable during recovery.","containment":"Restrict operations requiring unavailable policy data rather than using an unapproved permissive fallback.","remediation":"Define explicit degraded-mode decisions, bounded retries, authoritative fallback policy, and recovery verification.","lessons":"Authorization repair must specify behavior before, during, and after dependency recovery.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; authorization dependency failure reasoning","related_records":["D53-B71-R02","D53-B79-R01"],"relationships":["degraded-authorization-mode","dependency-failure-semantics"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B73-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Secret & Credential Lifecycle Failures","subtopic":"Credential rotation","concept":"Credential rotation updates the issuer but leaves consumers using the retired credential","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a credential rotation repair fail even when the new credential is valid?","answer":"Consumers may retain the previous credential in memory, configuration, connection pools, or long-lived processes. The issuer-side rotation succeeds while clients continue presenting an expired or revoked credential.","context":"A secret or credential is rotated after compromise risk or expiration.","objective":"Move all required consumers to the new credential without leaving inconsistent credential state.","attempted_solution":"Replace the credential in the central secret store and confirm that the new value exists.","failure":"Existing consumers continue using the retired credential until restart or explicit refresh.","evidence":"Credential lifecycle recovery requires consumer adoption verification, not only secret-store update.","cause":"The repair validated source rotation but not consumer refresh.","root_cause":"No end-to-end credential version convergence check existed.","impact":"Authentication failures or continued use of a credential intended to be retired.","containment":"Keep the old credential valid only for a tightly bounded overlap period when security policy permits; otherwise isolate stale consumers.","remediation":"Version credentials, trigger controlled consumer refresh, verify adoption, then revoke the old credential according to an explicit rotation protocol.","lessons":"Credential rotation is complete only when every required consumer has transitioned safely.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; credential lifecycle engineering","related_records":["D53-B73-R02","D53-B65-R03"],"relationships":["credential-version-convergence","consumer-refresh"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B73-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Secret & Credential Lifecycle Failures","subtopic":"Revocation timing","concept":"Credential revocation occurs before all dependent operations can safely transition","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can immediate credential revocation make a credential-recovery repair fail?","answer":"If consumers cannot atomically adopt the replacement credential, immediate revocation can interrupt legitimate operations before the replacement is active everywhere.","context":"A credential is considered compromised and operators revoke it while attempting emergency replacement.","objective":"Reduce credential exposure while preserving a controlled transition to the replacement.","attempted_solution":"Revoke the old credential immediately after creating the new credential.","failure":"Consumers that have not yet adopted the replacement lose access.","evidence":"Credential rotation and revocation timing must be coordinated with consumer transition state.","cause":"Revocation was treated as independent from replacement propagation.","root_cause":"The repair lacked a consumer-transition checkpoint or explicit emergency revocation strategy.","impact":"Availability degradation and potentially incomplete recovery.","containment":"Prioritize high-risk consumer isolation and controlled migration while following the applicable security policy for compromised credentials.","remediation":"Use a documented rotation protocol with consumer readiness checks, bounded overlap only when permitted, and explicit revocation criteria.","lessons":"Credential security and availability must be coordinated through an explicit lifecycle state model.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; credential rotation lifecycle","related_records":["D53-B73-R01","D53-B54-R01"],"relationships":["revocation-transition-order","credential-lifecycle"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B73-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Secret & Credential Lifecycle Failures","subtopic":"Credential scope","concept":"Replacement credential has broader privileges than the credential it replaces","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a replacement credential repair create a security regression?","answer":"Emergency credential generation may restore access with excessive scopes because the repair optimizes for functionality and omits comparison with the original privilege boundary.","context":"A credential is regenerated during an incident and attached to an application or service.","objective":"Restore required access while preserving least privilege.","attempted_solution":"Generate a new credential with broad permissions to ensure dependent operations succeed.","failure":"The replacement credential works but grants capabilities beyond the application's legitimate requirements.","evidence":"Credential validity and credential privilege scope are separate properties.","cause":"The repair used broad privileges to avoid unknown permission failures.","root_cause":"No privilege-equivalence or least-privilege check was part of credential restoration.","impact":"Compromise of the repaired credential has a larger potential blast radius.","containment":"Restrict the credential to the minimum verified operations and monitor unexpected access.","remediation":"Derive required permissions from documented workload behavior, issue narrowly scoped credentials, and verify denied operations as well as allowed operations.","lessons":"A successful credential repair must preserve privilege boundaries, not merely restore connectivity.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; least-privilege credential recovery","related_records":["D53-B71-R01","D53-B72-R03"],"relationships":["credential-scope-preservation","least-privilege"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B73-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Secret & Credential Lifecycle Failures","subtopic":"Credential format compatibility","concept":"Credential replacement changes format or encoding assumptions used by consumers","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a technically valid secret fail after rotation because of format assumptions?","answer":"Consumers may depend on key length, encoding, prefix, certificate chain structure, token claims, or parsing conventions that are not preserved by the replacement credential.","context":"A secret is regenerated using a supported credential mechanism but downstream consumers expect additional structural properties.","objective":"Preserve both credential validity and consumer compatibility during rotation.","attempted_solution":"Generate a valid replacement according to the issuer's general requirements.","failure":"Some consumers reject or misinterpret the replacement because its structure differs from the expected format.","evidence":"Credential validity at the issuer does not guarantee compatibility with every consumer implementation.","cause":"The repair validated issuer requirements but not consumer contract requirements.","root_cause":"Credential format compatibility was absent from rotation preconditions.","impact":"Partial service failure or authentication errors after rotation.","containment":"Restore compatibility using a known-supported credential format while keeping exposure controlled.","remediation":"Document credential interface requirements and validate representative consumers before completing the rotation.","lessons":"Credential lifecycle repairs must treat secret structure as an interface contract when consumers depend on it.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; credential interface reasoning","related_records":["D53-B65-R01","D53-B73-R01"],"relationships":["credential-interface-compatibility","rotation-validation"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B73-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Secret & Credential Lifecycle Failures","subtopic":"Secret deletion","concept":"Repair deletes the previous secret before proving that no active consumer depends on it","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can deleting an old secret during recovery cause additional failures?","answer":"Long-lived processes, rollback paths, or secondary consumers may still depend on the old secret. Deletion removes a recovery option before those dependencies have been identified or transitioned.","context":"Operators clean up retired credentials immediately after a successful-looking rotation.","objective":"Remove obsolete secrets without disrupting unresolved dependencies or rollback safety.","attempted_solution":"Delete the old secret immediately after updating primary consumers.","failure":"Unidentified consumers or emergency rollback paths lose access to the required credential.","evidence":"Secret retirement should be based on verified dependency transition rather than elapsed time alone.","cause":"Cleanup was coupled to primary consumer success.","root_cause":"No dependency inventory or retirement eligibility condition existed.","impact":"Hidden consumers fail and rollback options may disappear.","containment":"Preserve only policy-permitted recovery material under controlled access until dependency transition is verified.","remediation":"Track consumers, transition status, rollback dependencies, and retirement conditions before destructive secret deletion.","lessons":"Secret cleanup is a separate recovery decision and should not be assumed safe after primary-path validation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; secret retirement reasoning","related_records":["D53-B73-R01","D53-B83-R03"],"relationships":["secret-retirement","dependency-transition"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B74-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Supply-Chain Repair Failures","subtopic":"Dependency remediation","concept":"Security repair upgrades a vulnerable dependency without validating transitive compatibility","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can remediation of a vulnerable dependency fail even when the vulnerability is removed?","answer":"The upgraded dependency can alter APIs, behavior, performance, or transitive dependency versions. Security exposure may decrease while application behavior regresses because the repair was evaluated only for vulnerability status.","context":"A security advisory requires replacing or upgrading a vulnerable software component.","objective":"Remove the vulnerability while preserving required application behavior and compatibility.","attempted_solution":"Upgrade directly to the first available non-vulnerable version.","failure":"The application develops runtime or behavioral regressions after the security upgrade.","evidence":"Dependency remediation must validate both security state and software compatibility.","cause":"The repair optimized for vulnerability closure without validating dependency graph effects.","root_cause":"Security remediation lacked integrated dependency and regression verification.","impact":"Security may improve while availability or correctness degrades.","containment":"Limit affected deployment scope and retain a controlled rollback path while compatibility is evaluated.","remediation":"Resolve the full dependency graph, review breaking changes, run targeted and regression tests, and verify the resulting artifact.","lessons":"A supply-chain repair is incomplete until both security and compatibility requirements are satisfied.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; dependency security remediation","related_records":["D53-B66-R01","D53-B46-R03"],"relationships":["security-remediation","dependency-graph-validation"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B74-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Supply-Chain Repair Failures","subtopic":"Artifact provenance","concept":"Repair consumes a replacement artifact without establishing trusted provenance","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can replacing a compromised component with an unverified artifact fail as a supply-chain repair?","answer":"A replacement may have the expected name and version while originating from an untrusted or tampered source. Without provenance and integrity evidence, the repair can replace one security uncertainty with another.","context":"A component is replaced during remediation and an artifact is obtained from a source that is not part of the established trust chain.","objective":"Ensure the replacement artifact is authentic, integrity-protected, and attributable to an approved source.","attempted_solution":"Install an artifact whose version matches the expected fixed release.","failure":"The repair establishes version identity but not artifact authenticity or provenance.","evidence":"Software supply-chain trust requires provenance and integrity evidence in addition to version information.","cause":"Artifact identity was inferred from metadata rather than verified through the trust chain.","root_cause":"Replacement eligibility did not require provenance validation.","impact":"A remediation can introduce malicious or unverifiable software.","containment":"Quarantine the artifact and prevent deployment until provenance and integrity are verified.","remediation":"Require trusted source provenance, cryptographic integrity verification where applicable, reproducible or attestable build evidence, and policy-compliant artifact admission.","lessons":"A fixed version is not automatically a trusted artifact.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; software supply-chain trust","related_records":["D53-B74-R01","D53-B68-R02"],"relationships":["artifact-provenance","supply-chain-integrity"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B74-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Supply-Chain Repair Failures","subtopic":"Build trust","concept":"Repair rebuilds a dependency from source without proving the build inputs are equivalent to the trusted release","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can rebuilding a repaired component fail to establish supply-chain trust?","answer":"A source tree can be correct while build tools, compiler versions, generated files, patches, or dependencies differ from the trusted release process. The resulting binary may therefore not have equivalent provenance.","context":"A binary dependency is rebuilt locally because the original artifact is considered unsafe or unavailable.","objective":"Produce a replacement artifact whose inputs and build process are sufficiently attributable and verifiable.","attempted_solution":"Build the component from the expected source revision using the local environment.","failure":"The output cannot be confidently associated with the intended source and build inputs.","evidence":"Source revision alone does not fully describe a build artifact's provenance.","cause":"Build environment and dependency inputs were not captured or constrained.","root_cause":"The repair treated source identity as complete artifact provenance.","impact":"Security and reproducibility claims about the replacement remain uncertain.","containment":"Do not promote the artifact to trusted deployment scope until build provenance is established.","remediation":"Record source revision, toolchain, dependencies, build configuration, generated inputs, and integrity evidence; compare against trusted build expectations.","lessons":"Rebuilding is not equivalent to reproducing a trusted artifact unless the relevant build inputs are controlled and evidenced.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; reproducible build reasoning","related_records":["D53-B68-R03","D53-B74-R02"],"relationships":["build-provenance","reproducibility"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B74-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Supply-Chain Repair Failures","subtopic":"Compromised component handling","concept":"Repair removes a compromised component but leaves dependent copies or embedded versions","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can supply-chain remediation fail when the directly identified compromised component is removed?","answer":"The same vulnerable or compromised logic can exist in vendored code, bundled artifacts, generated outputs, caches, container layers, or transitive copies. Removing one package reference does not prove complete elimination.","context":"A compromised dependency is identified and replaced in the primary dependency manifest.","objective":"Determine whether the affected component remains reachable through other software artifacts or dependency paths.","attempted_solution":"Remove the named dependency and verify that the direct manifest no longer references it.","failure":"A bundled or transitive copy remains present in the deployed artifact.","evidence":"Supply-chain remediation must reason over the effective artifact contents, not only the direct dependency declaration.","cause":"The repair operated on source metadata rather than the complete deployed dependency surface.","root_cause":"No effective artifact inventory was used for remediation verification.","impact":"Residual exposure remains despite an apparently successful package-level repair.","containment":"Block affected artifacts from further deployment and identify reachable copies.","remediation":"Inspect effective dependency graphs and built artifacts, rebuild affected outputs, and verify absence or approved replacement of the affected component.","lessons":"Package-level remediation does not prove artifact-level remediation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; effective dependency surface analysis","related_records":["D53-B74-R01","D53-B68-R01"],"relationships":["effective-dependency-surface","artifact-level-remediation"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B74-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Supply-Chain Repair Failures","subtopic":"Emergency remediation provenance","concept":"Emergency supply-chain repair bypasses normal artifact admission controls without recording compensating evidence","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can an emergency dependency repair create an untracked supply-chain risk?","answer":"Emergency installation can bypass signing, provenance, review, reproducibility, or artifact admission controls. Even if the component fixes the immediate issue, the resulting software state becomes difficult to trust and reproduce.","context":"A critical dependency issue requires rapid remediation and normal artifact approval is considered too slow.","objective":"Preserve minimum supply-chain trust requirements during emergency remediation.","attempted_solution":"Install a manually obtained replacement package directly on affected systems.","failure":"The repair succeeds operationally but produces an artifact state lacking normal provenance and admission evidence.","evidence":"Emergency procedures may change process, but they do not eliminate the need to record artifact identity, source, integrity, and authorization.","cause":"Urgency was treated as justification for removing trust controls rather than narrowing and documenting them.","root_cause":"No emergency compensating-control protocol existed for supply-chain changes.","impact":"Future verification, rollback, reproduction, and forensic analysis become uncertain.","containment":"Quarantine the emergency state and prevent uncontrolled propagation.","remediation":"Record exact artifact identity and provenance, apply compensating verification, rebuild through the normal trusted pipeline as soon as possible, and reconcile the emergency change into controlled state.","lessons":"Emergency speed should reduce scope and increase evidence requirements, not erase supply-chain controls.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; emergency change governance","related_records":["D53-B74-R02","D53-B84-R03"],"relationships":["emergency-supply-chain-control","compensating-controls"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B75-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Resource-Access Boundary Failures","subtopic":"Tenant isolation","concept":"Repair applies a shared resource fix across tenants with tenant-specific constraints","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a shared-resource repair violate tenant isolation?","answer":"A repair designed for one tenant can modify shared configuration, cache state, quotas, or resource mappings that affect other tenants. The repair may solve the initiating tenant's problem while crossing intended resource boundaries.","context":"A multi-tenant service has tenant-specific resource state stored alongside shared infrastructure state.","objective":"Repair the affected tenant without modifying unrelated tenant state.","attempted_solution":"Apply the same resource reset to the shared subsystem used by all tenants.","failure":"Unrelated tenants experience state changes or resource behavior they did not authorize.","evidence":"Tenant scope must be an explicit precondition and postcondition of resource recovery.","cause":"Shared infrastructure was mistaken for shared repair authority.","root_cause":"Tenant boundaries were not represented in the repair operation's scope model.","impact":"Cross-tenant disruption or unauthorized state modification.","containment":"Stop broad repair actions and isolate the affected tenant or resource partition.","remediation":"Make tenant scope explicit, enforce authorization at execution time, use tenant-scoped state operations where possible, and verify non-target tenants remain unchanged.","lessons":"Shared infrastructure does not imply shared repair scope.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; multi-tenant isolation reasoning","related_records":["D53-B93-R01","D53-B84-R01"],"relationships":["tenant-isolation","repair-scope-boundary"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B75-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Resource-Access Boundary Failures","subtopic":"Namespace boundary","concept":"Resource repair resolves a resource identifier outside the intended namespace","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can an apparently valid resource identifier cause a boundary violation during repair?","answer":"Identifiers may be globally unique, locally scoped, or interpreted relative to a namespace. If the repair assumes the wrong scope, it can modify a different resource that is valid but outside the authorized boundary.","context":"A repair receives a resource identifier and reconstructs the target resource from a partially recovered state.","objective":"Resolve and modify only the resource within the authorized namespace.","attempted_solution":"Use the identifier directly without validating its namespace or ownership.","failure":"The repair targets a valid resource belonging to another scope.","evidence":"Resource identity requires both identifier and namespace or ownership context when identifiers are not globally authoritative.","cause":"The repair treated an identifier as sufficient identity.","root_cause":"Namespace was omitted from the repair target model and authorization check.","impact":"Cross-scope modification or unintended service disruption.","containment":"Reject ambiguous targets and require explicit namespace-qualified resource identity.","remediation":"Represent resource identity as a scoped tuple, validate ownership and authorization before mutation, and log the resolved target.","lessons":"A resource ID without its scope can be an unsafe repair target.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; resource identity and authorization","related_records":["D53-B75-R01","D53-B84-R01"],"relationships":["namespace-qualified-identity","resource-boundary-enforcement"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B75-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Resource-Access Boundary Failures","subtopic":"Shared resource permissions","concept":"Repair broadens resource permissions to restore functionality","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can changing resource permissions during repair violate access boundaries?","answer":"A repair may grant broad read or write access because the exact required permission is uncertain. This can restore the immediate workload while allowing unrelated components or tenants to access the resource.","context":"A service loses access to a protected resource and operators modify permissions during recovery.","objective":"Restore only the permissions required by the affected workload.","attempted_solution":"Grant a broad role or group access to ensure the service can continue.","failure":"The service works but unrelated principals inherit access.","evidence":"Resource authorization must be validated for both required access and prohibited access.","cause":"The repair optimized for successful access without negative authorization testing.","root_cause":"Least-privilege boundaries were not included in repair verification.","impact":"Unauthorized resource access and expanded blast radius.","containment":"Revoke unnecessary permissions and restrict the resource to explicitly identified principals.","remediation":"Determine the minimum required permissions, apply them narrowly, and verify representative denied principals cannot access the resource.","lessons":"Successful resource access is not sufficient evidence of correct access control.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; least-privilege resource repair","related_records":["D53-B73-R03","D53-B72-R03"],"relationships":["least-privilege","negative-authorization-testing"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B75-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Resource-Access Boundary Failures","subtopic":"Cross-service repair","concept":"Repair changes a shared resource binding without checking all authorized consumers","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can changing a resource binding for one service break or expose another service?","answer":"Shared resources can have multiple legitimate consumers with different assumptions. Rebinding a resource to repair one consumer can redirect access, remove isolation, or break another consumer's contract.","context":"A resource mapping is corrupted and an operator rebinds it to a replacement resource.","objective":"Repair the mapping while preserving every valid consumer's boundary and contract.","attempted_solution":"Point the resource mapping to the replacement resource and verify the initiating service.","failure":"Other consumers fail or gain unintended access to the replacement resource.","evidence":"Shared resource repairs require consumer inventory and boundary analysis.","cause":"The repair considered only the initiating failure path.","root_cause":"Resource ownership and consumer relationships were incomplete in the repair context.","impact":"Secondary outages or cross-service access violations.","containment":"Freeze further rebinding and isolate the affected consumer until dependency relationships are established.","remediation":"Map consumers and permissions before rebinding; use scoped mappings or staged migration where shared state cannot be changed atomically.","lessons":"A repair target must be analyzed as a graph of consumers, not as an isolated resource.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; shared-resource dependency reasoning","related_records":["D53-B91-R01","D53-B75-R01"],"relationships":["shared-resource-consumers","boundary-preserving-rebinding"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B75-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Resource-Access Boundary Failures","subtopic":"Repair scope enforcement","concept":"Repair authorization is validated before execution but target scope changes before mutation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a resource repair bypass its original authorization scope after the target changes?","answer":"If authorization is checked against an initial target and the target is later re-resolved, the repair can mutate a different resource without reauthorization.","context":"A repair resolves a resource, waits for dependencies, then resolves the resource again before mutation.","objective":"Ensure authorization applies to the exact resource and scope being mutated.","attempted_solution":"Authorize the original target and assume later resolution produces the same resource.","failure":"The final mutation target differs from the authorized target.","evidence":"Authorization must bind to the actual execution target and relevant scope at mutation time.","cause":"Target identity was not bound immutably between authorization and execution.","root_cause":"The repair lacked a final target-integrity check.","impact":"Potential cross-resource or cross-tenant unauthorized modification.","containment":"Abort when target identity, scope, or authorization context changes.","remediation":"Use immutable target identity, scope/hash checks where applicable, and reauthorize whenever execution target changes.","lessons":"Authorization of a repair plan does not automatically authorize a changed execution target.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; authorization-to-target binding","related_records":["D53-B84-R01","D53-B75-R02"],"relationships":["authorization-target-binding","execution-scope-integrity"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B76-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Performance Repair Failures","subtopic":"Optimization regression","concept":"Performance repair improves one latency metric while degrading another critical workload","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a performance repair fail despite improving the metric that triggered the repair?","answer":"Optimizing one path can shift cost to another resource or workload. A local latency improvement can therefore increase tail latency, throughput degradation, memory pressure, or contention elsewhere.","context":"A performance incident identifies high latency in one operation and a targeted optimization is applied.","objective":"Improve the target performance property without violating other performance requirements.","attempted_solution":"Optimize the slow operation without measuring affected workloads.","failure":"The target operation improves while another critical workload degrades.","evidence":"Performance is multidimensional and should be verified against relevant service-level and resource-level metrics.","cause":"The repair used a single optimization metric as the complete success criterion.","root_cause":"Performance invariants were incomplete.","impact":"Overall system performance becomes worse despite an apparently successful local repair.","containment":"Limit the optimization's rollout and monitor affected workloads before broad adoption.","remediation":"Define a multidimensional performance acceptance set covering latency distribution, throughput, resource usage, and relevant workload segments.","lessons":"A performance repair must optimize the system objective, not merely the symptom metric.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; performance optimization reasoning","related_records":["D53-B78-R01","D53-B76-R02"],"relationships":["multidimensional-performance-validation","optimization-tradeoff"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B76-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Performance Repair Failures","subtopic":"Tail latency","concept":"Repair improves average latency while worsening tail latency","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can average latency conceal a failed performance repair?","answer":"An optimization can reduce typical request cost while increasing variance or rare expensive paths. Average latency may improve even though p95, p99, or another required tail metric becomes unacceptable.","context":"A repair is assessed using average response time after a latency incident.","objective":"Verify that the repaired system satisfies required latency distribution targets.","attempted_solution":"Compare only mean latency before and after the repair.","failure":"Average latency improves while tail latency worsens for a critical request class.","evidence":"Latency distributions contain information that averages discard.","cause":"The verification metric did not represent the required user-facing tail behavior.","root_cause":"No tail-latency acceptance criterion existed for the repair.","impact":"Users experience severe intermittent delays despite favorable aggregate metrics.","containment":"Restrict the optimization to controlled traffic while tail behavior is investigated.","remediation":"Measure appropriate percentiles and workload segments, identify long-tail causes, and require tail thresholds in performance validation.","lessons":"Average improvement does not establish acceptable latency behavior.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; latency distribution analysis","related_records":["D53-B76-R01","D53-B49-R03"],"relationships":["tail-latency-validation","distribution-aware-performance"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B76-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Performance Repair Failures","subtopic":"Caching trade-off","concept":"Performance repair increases cache utilization until memory pressure causes secondary failures","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can increasing caching during performance recovery fail?","answer":"Caching can reduce repeated computation or I/O but consume memory and increase eviction, garbage-collection, or allocation pressure. The repair can therefore trade latency for instability.","context":"A service experiences high computation latency and a cache is expanded as a remediation.","objective":"Reduce expensive repeated work without exceeding resource safety limits.","attempted_solution":"Increase cache size aggressively to maximize hit rate.","failure":"Memory pressure and eviction churn increase, degrading overall performance and reliability.","evidence":"Cache hit rate is not sufficient to characterize cache effectiveness or safety.","cause":"The repair optimized hit rate without modeling memory cost and eviction behavior.","root_cause":"No resource-bound cache acceptance criteria existed.","impact":"Latency, memory utilization, and service stability can all degrade.","containment":"Cap cache growth and revert unsafe cache expansion while retaining measured useful entries.","remediation":"Tune cache size against memory budget, hit rate, eviction cost, workload distribution, and tail latency.","lessons":"Performance optimizations must remain within resource safety boundaries.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; cache/resource trade-off reasoning","related_records":["D53-B55-R01","D53-B64-R03"],"relationships":["cache-resource-tradeoff","performance-safety-boundary"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B76-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Performance Repair Failures","subtopic":"Batching optimization","concept":"Repair increases batch size to improve throughput but violates latency or resource constraints","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can larger batching fail as a performance repair?","answer":"Larger batches amortize overhead but increase queueing delay, memory consumption, individual operation duration, and recovery cost. A throughput gain can therefore violate latency or resource requirements.","context":"A workload has excessive per-operation overhead and batching is introduced or increased.","objective":"Improve throughput while remaining within latency, memory, and failure-recovery limits.","attempted_solution":"Increase batch size until throughput improves substantially.","failure":"Requests wait longer and resource consumption rises beyond acceptable limits.","evidence":"Batch size is a workload-dependent trade-off rather than a monotonic performance improvement.","cause":"The repair optimized throughput without bounded latency and memory criteria.","root_cause":"No multi-objective batch-size acceptance policy existed.","impact":"User-facing latency and resource safety degrade despite higher raw throughput.","containment":"Reduce batch size to a measured safe range and control queue growth.","remediation":"Tune batch size using workload distribution, queue delay, memory budget, throughput, and tail-latency measurements.","lessons":"Batching repairs require explicit trade-off boundaries.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; batching trade-off analysis","related_records":["D53-B76-R01","D53-B78-R03"],"relationships":["batch-size-tradeoff","throughput-latency-boundary"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B76-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Performance Repair Failures","subtopic":"Measurement bias","concept":"Performance repair is accepted using a non-representative workload","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can an optimization appear successful because the repair test workload differs from production behavior?","answer":"A synthetic or narrow workload may exercise the optimized path while omitting contention, data distributions, concurrency, or request mixes that dominate real operation.","context":"A performance repair is benchmarked against a small test case that is convenient to reproduce.","objective":"Ensure performance evidence represents the workloads relevant to the affected system.","attempted_solution":"Accept the repair after improvement on a single benchmark scenario.","failure":"The repair underperforms or introduces resource pressure under representative workload characteristics.","evidence":"Performance measurements are meaningful only relative to workload, environment, measurement method, and acceptance criteria.","cause":"The benchmark lacked representative workload dimensions.","root_cause":"Performance repair validation was disconnected from operational workload characteristics.","impact":"A regression can be deployed under the appearance of measured improvement.","containment":"Keep the change limited while representative workloads are measured.","remediation":"Define workload classes, representative distributions, resource conditions, and performance acceptance thresholds before concluding repair success.","lessons":"A benchmark result is evidence about its tested workload, not proof of general performance improvement.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; benchmark validity reasoning","related_records":["D53-B49-R04","D53-B76-R01"],"relationships":["representative-workload-validation","benchmark-scope"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B77-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Database Performance Repair Failures","subtopic":"Index repair","concept":"Adding an index improves one query but increases write cost and storage pressure beyond workload tolerance","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can adding a database index fail as a performance repair?","answer":"Indexes can accelerate selected reads while increasing insert/update/delete work, storage use, maintenance cost, and potentially locking or cache pressure.","context":"A slow read query is identified and an additional index is proposed.","objective":"Improve required query performance without violating write or storage constraints.","attempted_solution":"Add an index matching the slow query without evaluating write-heavy workloads.","failure":"Read latency improves but write latency or storage pressure becomes unacceptable.","evidence":"Index changes affect both read and write workloads and must be evaluated against the complete workload.","cause":"The repair considered only the target read query.","root_cause":"No workload-wide index cost assessment was performed.","impact":"Overall database performance can degrade despite faster target queries.","containment":"Limit the index change and monitor read/write workload effects before broad adoption.","remediation":"Evaluate query plans, write amplification, storage cost, index selectivity, and workload mix before accepting the index.","lessons":"Database indexes are workload trade-offs, not free read optimizations.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; database indexing trade-off reasoning","related_records":["D53-B77-R02","D53-B76-R01"],"relationships":["index-workload-tradeoff","database-performance-validation"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B77-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Database Performance Repair Failures","subtopic":"Query-plan repair","concept":"Query optimization forces a plan that performs well for one parameter distribution but poorly for another","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a query-plan repair create a new performance failure?","answer":"A plan can be efficient for one data distribution or parameter range and inefficient for another. A repair that fixes a representative case without considering parameter sensitivity can produce regressions for other workloads.","context":"A database query exhibits unstable execution time and a plan change is introduced.","objective":"Improve plan stability across the relevant parameter and data-distribution space.","attempted_solution":"Optimize the query plan against the currently observed slow parameters.","failure":"Other parameter values experience worse execution plans after the repair.","evidence":"Query performance depends on data distribution, cardinality estimates, indexes, statistics, and parameter characteristics.","cause":"The repair optimized a single observed plan context.","root_cause":"Plan validation lacked parameter and workload diversity.","impact":"Performance becomes unpredictable across request classes.","containment":"Restrict the changed plan to validated workloads or revert the plan intervention.","remediation":"Evaluate representative parameter sets, statistics behavior, alternative plans, and required latency distributions.","lessons":"Database performance repair must account for plan sensitivity, not just the currently failing query instance.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; query-plan stability reasoning","related_records":["D53-B77-R01","D53-B76-R05"],"relationships":["query-plan-sensitivity","parameter-aware-validation"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B77-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Database Performance Repair Failures","subtopic":"Connection pressure","concept":"Increasing database connection pool size shifts contention into the database","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can increasing the database connection pool fail to improve application throughput?","answer":"More connections can increase concurrent database work, lock contention, context switching, memory usage, or queue pressure. If the database is already saturated, increasing client concurrency can worsen performance.","context":"Applications show request queuing because database connections are frequently occupied.","objective":"Reduce end-to-end queueing without exceeding database concurrency capacity.","attempted_solution":"Increase the connection pool size substantially to allow more requests to proceed.","failure":"Database contention increases and overall latency worsens.","evidence":"Connection-pool size must be matched to database capacity and workload characteristics.","cause":"Client-side queueing was mistaken for insufficient database concurrency.","root_cause":"The repair did not distinguish application-side waiting from database-side saturation.","impact":"Higher concurrency increases contention instead of throughput.","containment":"Return concurrency to a measured safe level and monitor database saturation indicators.","remediation":"Measure database utilization, lock waits, query duration, active connections, and application queueing before selecting pool limits.","lessons":"More database connections do not necessarily provide more database capacity.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; database concurrency reasoning","related_records":["D53-B55-R03","D53-B79-R02"],"relationships":["database-concurrency-capacity","connection-pool-tuning"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B77-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Database Performance Repair Failures","subtopic":"Statistics maintenance","concept":"Repair changes query behavior without refreshing database statistics required for accurate planning","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can stale database statistics undermine a query-performance repair?","answer":"Query planners rely on statistics to estimate cardinality and choose execution strategies. A repair that changes data distribution or query structure while leaving statistics stale can preserve poor plan choices.","context":"A database workload changes substantially during recovery or after a data correction.","objective":"Ensure query planning uses sufficiently current information for the affected workload.","attempted_solution":"Tune the query or index without considering statistics freshness.","failure":"The database continues selecting inefficient plans because estimates remain inaccurate.","evidence":"Planner behavior depends on the statistics available to the database engine.","cause":"The repair modified workload or structure without updating the planner's knowledge.","root_cause":"Statistics freshness was omitted from performance repair preconditions.","impact":"The intended optimization has little or inconsistent effect.","containment":"Monitor plan behavior and avoid broad rollout until planner inputs are reliable.","remediation":"Update or validate relevant statistics according to the database engine's supported mechanisms and then re-evaluate execution plans.","lessons":"Database performance repair can require repairing the planner's information state as well as the query itself.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; query-planner information state","related_records":["D53-B77-R02","D53-B57-R02"],"relationships":["statistics-freshness","query-planner-state"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B77-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Database Performance Repair Failures","subtopic":"Lock contention","concept":"Repair reduces one transaction duration but increases lock conflict frequency","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a database optimization increase lock contention?","answer":"A repair can alter access order, transaction boundaries, or concurrency patterns so that individual transactions are shorter but overlap in more conflicting ways.","context":"Lock waits are observed and transaction processing is modified to reduce duration.","objective":"Reduce lock-related latency without increasing conflict frequency or deadlock risk.","attempted_solution":"Shorten transaction work while increasing concurrent transaction execution.","failure":"Lock conflicts and retries increase, offsetting or exceeding the intended latency improvement.","evidence":"Transaction duration and lock-conflict probability are related but distinct performance variables.","cause":"The repair optimized transaction duration without evaluating lock acquisition patterns.","root_cause":"Lock topology was absent from the performance model.","impact":"Database latency and throughput degrade under concurrency.","containment":"Reduce affected concurrency and preserve stable transaction ordering while investigating.","remediation":"Analyze lock acquisition order, conflict graphs, transaction scope, wait times, and concurrency levels before accepting the repair.","lessons":"Database transaction optimization must evaluate contention topology, not only transaction duration.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; database lock contention reasoning","related_records":["D53-B53-R04","D53-B77-R03"],"relationships":["lock-contention-topology","transaction-concurrency"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B78-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Application Performance Repair Failures","subtopic":"CPU optimization","concept":"CPU optimization increases allocation or memory-management overhead","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can an application CPU optimization increase total runtime cost?","answer":"Reducing one computation can introduce additional allocations, copying, synchronization, or garbage-collection work. The visible CPU hotspot may improve while total application cost increases.","context":"A CPU hotspot is identified and replaced with a different implementation intended to reduce computation.","objective":"Reduce end-to-end CPU cost without increasing memory-management or synchronization overhead.","attempted_solution":"Replace the hotspot with an implementation that performs fewer nominal operations.","failure":"Allocation or cleanup overhead offsets the intended CPU savings.","evidence":"Application performance must account for total execution cost rather than isolated instruction counts.","cause":"The repair optimized algorithmic work without measuring allocation and runtime-management effects.","root_cause":"The performance model excluded secondary runtime costs.","impact":"End-to-end latency or CPU utilization can worsen.","containment":"Limit rollout and compare complete resource profiles under representative workload.","remediation":"Measure CPU, allocation rate, garbage-collection activity, memory usage, and latency before and after the change.","lessons":"Micro-level optimization requires end-to-end resource validation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; application performance modeling","related_records":["D53-B76-R01","D53-B78-R02"],"relationships":["end-to-end-performance","runtime-overhead"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B78-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Application Performance Repair Failures","subtopic":"Serialization optimization","concept":"Serialization optimization reduces payload processing cost but breaks compatibility or increases downstream work","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can serialization optimization fail as an application performance repair?","answer":"A more compact or faster serialization format can change representation, decoding cost, field behavior, or compatibility requirements. Local serialization speed can improve while downstream processing becomes slower or incorrect.","context":"Serialization is identified as a major CPU or latency contributor.","objective":"Reduce serialization cost while preserving the complete producer-consumer contract.","attempted_solution":"Change encoding or payload representation based on local serialization benchmarks.","failure":"Downstream consumers incur additional conversion work or reject the changed representation.","evidence":"Serialization performance is coupled to payload contracts and downstream processing.","cause":"The repair measured producer-side serialization cost without evaluating end-to-end processing.","root_cause":"The performance repair boundary stopped at the serialization component.","impact":"End-to-end latency or compatibility degrades.","containment":"Keep the new format behind a controlled compatibility boundary until consumers are verified.","remediation":"Benchmark end-to-end producer-to-consumer paths and validate schema, encoding, size, CPU, and compatibility requirements.","lessons":"Serialization optimization must be evaluated across the complete data path.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; serialization performance reasoning","related_records":["D53-B65-R01","D53-B76-R05"],"relationships":["serialization-end-to-end","performance-contract-interaction"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B78-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Application Performance Repair Failures","subtopic":"Algorithmic repair","concept":"Algorithmic optimization improves asymptotic complexity but worsens constant costs for actual small inputs","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can an algorithm with better theoretical complexity perform worse in the repaired workload?","answer":"Asymptotic complexity describes growth behavior, not all real costs. A theoretically better algorithm can have larger constants, allocations, setup overhead, or poorer locality and therefore be slower for the actual input sizes.","context":"An application uses an algorithm that is considered computationally inefficient and a theoretically better alternative is introduced.","objective":"Select an implementation that improves the actual workload rather than only theoretical complexity.","attempted_solution":"Replace the existing algorithm solely because the alternative has better asymptotic complexity.","failure":"Real workload latency increases for common input sizes.","evidence":"Algorithm choice depends on input distribution, constants, memory behavior, and implementation characteristics.","cause":"The repair used asymptotic complexity as the sole performance criterion.","root_cause":"Workload-specific empirical validation was absent.","impact":"Performance regresses despite theoretically favorable complexity.","containment":"Keep the optimized algorithm limited to input ranges where evidence demonstrates benefit.","remediation":"Benchmark representative input distributions and resource behavior; select algorithms according to actual workload requirements.","lessons":"Asymptotic improvement is evidence about scalability, not proof of immediate runtime improvement.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; algorithm selection reasoning","related_records":["D53-B76-R05","D53-B78-R04"],"relationships":["algorithm-workload-fit","empirical-performance-validation"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B78-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Application Performance Repair Failures","subtopic":"Parallelization","concept":"Application parallelization repair increases synchronization and scheduling overhead","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can parallelizing a slow application operation make it slower?","answer":"Parallel execution introduces coordination, synchronization, scheduling, memory contention, and sometimes serialization overhead. For insufficiently large or parallelizable workloads, these costs can exceed the available speedup.","context":"A sequential application operation is identified as CPU-bound and parallel execution is proposed.","objective":"Increase useful parallel work without exceeding coordination and resource costs.","attempted_solution":"Increase worker concurrency without measuring parallel efficiency or contention.","failure":"Synchronization and resource contention consume the expected speedup.","evidence":"Parallel performance depends on workload parallelism, coordination cost, resource availability, and scheduling behavior.","cause":"Concurrency was increased without estimating useful parallel fraction and overhead.","root_cause":"No parallel-efficiency or contention acceptance criteria existed.","impact":"Higher CPU utilization accompanies unchanged or worse throughput and latency.","containment":"Reduce concurrency to a measured stable level and isolate the expensive synchronization path.","remediation":"Measure parallel speedup, synchronization time, scheduling overhead, CPU saturation, and memory contention across workload sizes.","lessons":"Parallelism is a performance mechanism with overhead, not a guaranteed optimization.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; parallel performance reasoning","related_records":["D53-B53-R01","D53-B76-R01"],"relationships":["parallel-overhead","contention-aware-optimization"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B78-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Application Performance Repair Failures","subtopic":"Memory optimization","concept":"Memory optimization reduces resident memory but increases recomputation and CPU cost","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can reducing memory consumption fail as an application performance repair?","answer":"Removing retained data or caches can lower memory usage while increasing recomputation, I/O, allocation, or CPU work. Lower memory is not inherently equivalent to better application performance.","context":"Memory pressure is observed and retained application state is reduced.","objective":"Reduce memory pressure while preserving acceptable latency and throughput.","attempted_solution":"Eliminate retained intermediate state and force recomputation when needed.","failure":"Memory usage falls but CPU utilization and latency rise substantially.","evidence":"Memory, CPU, I/O, and latency form interacting performance dimensions.","cause":"The repair optimized memory usage without measuring replacement work.","root_cause":"No cross-resource performance objective was defined.","impact":"The system may exchange memory pressure for CPU or latency pressure.","containment":"Restore only the retained state that provides measured benefit within the memory budget.","remediation":"Quantify memory savings against recomputation, I/O, CPU, and latency costs and choose the required trade-off explicitly.","lessons":"Performance repairs must evaluate resource substitution effects.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; cross-resource optimization","related_records":["D53-B55-R01","D53-B76-R03"],"relationships":["resource-substitution","memory-performance-tradeoff"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B79-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Reliability-Mechanism Repair Failures","subtopic":"Retry amplification","concept":"Repair increases retries while the underlying dependency remains capacity constrained","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can adding retries make a reliability repair fail?","answer":"Retries can multiply load on a dependency that is already failing or saturated. The additional requests can reduce recovery capacity and turn a localized failure into a feedback loop.","context":"A transient dependency failure causes request errors and a retry policy is modified during recovery.","objective":"Improve recovery from transient faults without amplifying dependency load.","attempted_solution":"Increase retry count and retry frequency to improve successful completion.","failure":"Retry traffic consumes remaining dependency capacity and prolongs recovery.","evidence":"Retry effectiveness depends on failure cause, dependency capacity, retry timing, and workload volume.","cause":"The repair treated failed attempts as insufficient effort rather than evidence of dependency stress.","root_cause":"No retry budget or dependency-load invariant existed.","impact":"Recovery is delayed and failure volume increases.","containment":"Reduce retry pressure, bound retry budgets, and protect the failing dependency from uncontrolled amplification.","remediation":"Use bounded retries with appropriate backoff, jitter, request budgets, and circuit-breaking or admission controls where justified.","lessons":"Retries are load-generating actions and must be governed as part of the recovery system.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; retry feedback-loop reasoning","related_records":["D53-B45-R01","D53-B79-R02"],"relationships":["retry-amplification","dependency-protection"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B79-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Reliability-Mechanism Repair Failures","subtopic":"Timeout repair","concept":"Timeout reduction causes legitimate slow operations to be classified as failures","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can shortening a timeout fail as a reliability repair?","answer":"A shorter timeout can reduce resource occupancy for genuinely stuck operations but may also cancel valid operations that occasionally require longer execution. This can increase retries and duplicate work.","context":"Long-running requests consume resources during a dependency slowdown.","objective":"Bound resource occupancy without converting legitimate workload variance into unnecessary failure.","attempted_solution":"Reduce timeout aggressively to force slow operations to terminate sooner.","failure":"Valid slow operations time out and generate additional retries or user-visible failures.","evidence":"Timeouts must reflect operation characteristics and downstream failure behavior.","cause":"The repair optimized resource release without modeling legitimate execution-time distribution.","root_cause":"Timeout selection lacked workload and retry interaction analysis.","impact":"Failure rate and retry traffic increase.","containment":"Use bounded timeout changes with controlled traffic and monitor timeout-induced retries.","remediation":"Set timeouts from measured latency distributions, downstream semantics, retry budgets, and resource constraints.","lessons":"A timeout is both a resource-control mechanism and a failure generator.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; timeout reliability reasoning","related_records":["D53-B54-R02","D53-B79-R01"],"relationships":["timeout-retry-interaction","resource-boundary"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B79-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Reliability-Mechanism Repair Failures","subtopic":"Circuit breaker repair","concept":"Circuit breaker thresholds are repaired without distinguishing dependency failure from valid high-load behavior","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can incorrect circuit-breaker thresholds create a failed repair?","answer":"Thresholds that are too sensitive can trip during normal load or brief variance, while thresholds that are too permissive can allow prolonged dependency overload. The mechanism can therefore oscillate between unnecessary blocking and insufficient protection.","context":"A circuit breaker is tuned after repeated dependency failures.","objective":"Prevent harmful dependency interaction while retaining legitimate service behavior.","attempted_solution":"Lower the failure threshold based on the recent incident rate without workload calibration.","failure":"The breaker trips during legitimate traffic patterns or fails to trip during sustained degradation.","evidence":"Circuit-breaker behavior depends on failure classification, traffic volume, windowing, thresholds, and recovery conditions.","cause":"Thresholds were tuned against an incident snapshot rather than workload characteristics.","root_cause":"Breaker policy lacked workload-calibrated activation and recovery criteria.","impact":"Availability can decrease through unnecessary trips or degradation can propagate through late protection.","containment":"Use conservative bounded settings and monitor breaker decisions independently from application health.","remediation":"Calibrate thresholds against representative traffic, distinguish relevant failure classes, and verify open, half-open, and recovery behavior.","lessons":"Reliability mechanisms require semantic calibration, not arbitrary threshold adjustment.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; circuit-breaker behavior","related_records":["D53-B45-R02","D53-B79-R01"],"relationships":["circuit-breaker-calibration","failure-classification"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B79-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Reliability-Mechanism Repair Failures","subtopic":"Graceful degradation","concept":"Repair enables fallback behavior that silently produces lower-quality results outside its intended boundary","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can graceful degradation become a failed repair?","answer":"A fallback can keep requests successful while violating correctness, freshness, authorization, or business requirements. Availability alone does not establish that degraded behavior is acceptable.","context":"A dependency is unavailable and a fallback response or cached result is introduced.","objective":"Preserve explicitly permitted degraded behavior without violating correctness or security requirements.","attempted_solution":"Return fallback data whenever the primary dependency fails.","failure":"The fallback remains active beyond its acceptable scope or serves data whose semantics are no longer valid.","evidence":"Degraded modes require explicit eligibility, freshness, correctness, and security boundaries.","cause":"The repair defined fallback success as request completion.","root_cause":"No degraded-mode contract or exit condition existed.","impact":"Users receive apparently successful but invalid or unsafe results.","containment":"Disable fallback for operations where stale or incomplete results are unacceptable.","remediation":"Define fallback eligibility, data freshness limits, correctness constraints, security rules, and explicit recovery exit conditions.","lessons":"Graceful degradation is a controlled behavior, not a generic permission to return anything.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; graceful-degradation semantics","related_records":["D53-B64-R04","D53-B71-R02"],"relationships":["degraded-mode-contract","fallback-boundary"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B79-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Reliability-Mechanism Repair Failures","subtopic":"Bulkhead repair","concept":"Bulkhead limits are repaired too aggressively and isolate resources needed for recovery itself","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a bulkhead repair prevent the system from recovering?","answer":"A strict resource partition can protect one workload but leave recovery operations without sufficient threads, connections, queue capacity, or execution slots. The containment mechanism can therefore block the repair path.","context":"Resource exhaustion prompts tighter isolation between workloads.","objective":"Contain failure without starving critical recovery operations.","attempted_solution":"Reduce the affected workload's resource allocation to a very small limit.","failure":"The workload is contained but recovery tasks cannot obtain enough resources to restore normal operation.","evidence":"Reliability controls must account for both steady-state workload protection and recovery-path resource requirements.","cause":"Containment limits were set without modeling recovery capacity.","root_cause":"Recovery operations were not treated as protected resource consumers.","impact":"Containment becomes self-sustaining and delays recovery.","containment":"Reserve explicit capacity for recovery and emergency control paths.","remediation":"Model recovery resource requirements separately, reserve bounded emergency capacity, and verify that containment does not starve repair operations.","lessons":"Failure isolation must preserve a viable recovery path.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; bulkhead and recovery-capacity reasoning","related_records":["D53-B55-R04","D53-B80-R03"],"relationships":["bulkhead-recovery-interaction","reserved-recovery-capacity"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B80-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Failure-Containment Repair Failures","subtopic":"Containment scope","concept":"Containment boundary is broader than the actual failure dependency","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can overly broad containment become a failed repair?","answer":"A containment action can isolate healthy components along with the failing component, reducing available capacity and creating secondary failures. Effective containment requires a boundary that is sufficient but no broader than necessary.","context":"A component failure is detected and operators isolate a large service or resource group to prevent propagation.","objective":"Stop propagation while preserving unaffected functionality.","attempted_solution":"Disable a broad subsystem containing both affected and healthy components.","failure":"Healthy workloads lose access to shared capacity and begin failing.","evidence":"Containment effectiveness must be evaluated against both prevented propagation and collateral impact.","cause":"The repair chose a coarse boundary because the exact failure path was uncertain.","root_cause":"No minimum-effective-containment criterion existed.","impact":"Containment itself creates additional outages.","containment":"Narrow the boundary incrementally using verified dependency evidence.","remediation":"Model dependency relationships, isolate the smallest effective scope, and verify collateral impact before expanding containment.","lessons":"Containment should minimize blast radius while still interrupting the failure path.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; failure-containment boundary reasoning","related_records":["D53-B75-R04","D53-B80-R02"],"relationships":["minimum-effective-containment","blast-radius-control"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B80-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Failure-Containment Repair Failures","subtopic":"Containment insufficiency","concept":"Containment isolates the visible symptom but leaves the propagation path active","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can containment fail when the visibly unhealthy component is isolated?","answer":"The visible component may be downstream of the actual propagation source. Isolating it can remove one symptom while the underlying dependency continues affecting other components.","context":"A cascading failure appears concentrated in one service and that service is isolated.","objective":"Interrupt the actual propagation mechanism rather than only the most visible symptom.","attempted_solution":"Remove traffic from the component reporting the highest error rate.","failure":"The upstream failure continues and spreads through other paths.","evidence":"Containment requires causal or dependency evidence linking the boundary to the propagation mechanism.","cause":"Error concentration was treated as evidence of causal origin.","root_cause":"Containment selection was based on symptom location rather than propagation analysis.","impact":"Failure continues while healthy capacity is unnecessarily removed.","containment":"Trace dependency and propagation paths before expanding isolation.","remediation":"Identify the source and propagation edges, then apply the narrowest containment that interrupts those edges.","lessons":"The loudest failing component is not necessarily the correct containment boundary.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; causal containment reasoning","related_records":["D53-B81-R01","D53-B80-R01"],"relationships":["propagation-path-analysis","symptom-versus-cause"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B80-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Failure-Containment Repair Failures","subtopic":"Recovery capacity","concept":"Containment consumes the same scarce resource required by unaffected workloads and repair operations","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can containment itself exhaust the capacity needed for recovery?","answer":"Containment can create queues, snapshots, copies, isolation environments, or additional monitoring that consume scarce resources. If those costs are not bounded, containment can deepen the original resource failure.","context":"A containment procedure creates additional infrastructure or processing requirements during a capacity-constrained incident.","objective":"Contain the failure without exceeding remaining system recovery capacity.","attempted_solution":"Apply the full containment procedure to all affected resources simultaneously.","failure":"Containment overhead consumes remaining capacity and delays recovery.","evidence":"Containment actions have resource costs that must be included in capacity analysis.","cause":"The repair modeled only the resources being protected, not resources consumed by containment.","root_cause":"Containment overhead was absent from the failure model.","impact":"The incident becomes more severe or recovery becomes impossible within required limits.","containment":"Prioritize high-value containment actions and reserve recovery capacity.","remediation":"Estimate containment resource cost, apply staged or bounded containment, and protect critical recovery resources explicitly.","lessons":"Containment is an active workload and must be capacity-budgeted.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; containment capacity modeling","related_records":["D53-B56-R01","D53-B79-R05"],"relationships":["containment-resource-budget","recovery-capacity-protection"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B80-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Failure-Containment Repair Failures","subtopic":"Containment release","concept":"Containment is removed after symptom reduction without verifying that the propagation condition is gone","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can releasing containment too early cause a failed repair?","answer":"A symptom can temporarily decline while the underlying failure remains latent. Removing isolation can reconnect the failing dependency before its causal condition has been resolved.","context":"Error rates fall after containment and operators restore normal connectivity.","objective":"Release containment only after the condition that justified isolation has been independently addressed.","attempted_solution":"Release containment when visible error rates return to normal for a short interval.","failure":"The original propagation mechanism reactivates after reconnection.","evidence":"Containment release requires verification of the underlying release condition, not only symptom reduction.","cause":"Recovery was inferred from temporary symptom improvement.","root_cause":"No explicit containment-release invariant existed.","impact":"The system repeatedly enters and exits failure conditions.","containment":"Maintain isolation until dependency health and causal conditions are verified.","remediation":"Define release predicates covering the failure cause, dependency health, capacity margin, and post-release observation period.","lessons":"Containment and containment release are separate decisions with separate evidence requirements.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; containment release verification","related_records":["D53-B46-R03","D53-B80-R05"],"relationships":["containment-release-gate","causal-recovery-verification"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B80-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Failure-Containment Repair Failures","subtopic":"Containment hysteresis","concept":"Repeated containment and release oscillation creates secondary instability","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a containment mechanism repeatedly switching between isolated and normal states become a failed repair?","answer":"If activation and release thresholds are too close or measured on unstable signals, the system can oscillate between containment and release. Each transition can add load, disrupt caches or connections, and create new failure conditions.","context":"A containment controller automatically isolates a component when an error threshold is crossed and releases it when the threshold falls.","objective":"Prevent oscillation while retaining timely failure containment.","attempted_solution":"Use the same threshold for containment activation and release.","failure":"Small metric fluctuations repeatedly trigger isolation and reconnection.","evidence":"Control mechanisms with noisy signals require stable transition criteria and appropriate hysteresis or observation windows.","cause":"Activation and release decisions were based on the same unstable threshold.","root_cause":"The containment controller lacked separation between trigger and release conditions.","impact":"Repeated transitions amplify instability and complicate recovery.","containment":"Hold the component in the safer stable state until a stronger release condition is satisfied.","remediation":"Use distinct activation and release thresholds or bounded observation windows, and verify transition stability under noisy conditions.","lessons":"Containment control needs stability criteria, not only failure thresholds.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; containment control stability reasoning","related_records":["D53-B51-R04","D53-B80-R04"],"relationships":["containment-hysteresis","control-stability"]}

{"record_id":"D53-B81-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Incident Diagnosis Failures","subtopic":"Wrong hypothesis","concept":"Repair begins from an attractive but weakly supported causal hypothesis","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can an initially plausible diagnosis cause a failed repair?","answer":"A plausible hypothesis can become operationally dangerous when it is treated as established cause before discriminating evidence is collected. The repair then changes the suspected component while the actual failure mechanism remains active.","context":"An incident presents correlated symptoms across several components and the first visible dependency is suspected as the cause.","objective":"Distinguish correlation from causal evidence before committing to a repair.","attempted_solution":"Repair the component most strongly associated with the observed error spike.","failure":"The repaired component changes symptoms temporarily or not at all while the underlying failure persists.","evidence":"Diagnosis should identify observations that distinguish competing hypotheses rather than only observations compatible with one hypothesis.","cause":"The repair path was selected before testing alternative explanations.","root_cause":"No explicit hypothesis-discrimination step was required before repair authorization.","impact":"Repair effort is spent on a non-causal component and incident duration can increase.","containment":"Avoid expanding the initial repair based solely on symptom correlation; preserve evidence and test competing hypotheses.","remediation":"Represent candidate causes explicitly, identify discriminating observations, test the highest-value hypothesis first, and record why alternatives were rejected.","lessons":"A plausible cause is not an established root cause until evidence separates it from competing explanations.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; causal diagnosis reasoning","related_records":["D53-B81-R02","D53-B82-R01"],"relationships":["hypothesis-discrimination","causal-diagnosis"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B81-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Incident Diagnosis Failures","subtopic":"Negative evidence","concept":"Diagnosis ignores evidence that contradicts the preferred failure hypothesis","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why does ignoring negative evidence produce unreliable repair decisions?","answer":"Evidence showing that a component remained healthy, a failure existed before the suspected change, or another dependency exhibits the same symptom can weaken the preferred hypothesis. Ignoring such evidence causes the repair model to become confirmation-biased.","context":"Multiple telemetry sources contain observations that do not fully support the initial diagnosis.","objective":"Use both supporting and contradictory evidence when selecting a repair hypothesis.","attempted_solution":"Collect only evidence that confirms the suspected component as the source.","failure":"The repair proceeds despite observations inconsistent with the selected root cause.","evidence":"Contradictory observations should reduce confidence or trigger hypothesis revision rather than being discarded.","cause":"Evidence collection was organized around confirmation of one explanation.","root_cause":"The diagnosis process lacked an explicit contradictory-evidence requirement.","impact":"Wrong repairs can be repeatedly attempted while useful evidence is lost.","containment":"Pause irreversible remediation when high-value evidence contradicts the current hypothesis.","remediation":"Record supporting and contradicting observations separately, update confidence, and require reconciliation of material contradictions before high-impact repair.","lessons":"Contradictory evidence is diagnostic information, not noise to be removed from the repair model.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; contradiction-aware diagnosis","related_records":["D53-B81-R01","D53-B81-R03"],"relationships":["contradiction-aware-diagnosis","evidence-quality"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B81-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Incident Diagnosis Failures","subtopic":"Temporal causality","concept":"Diagnosis mistakes temporal precedence for causal evidence","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is the fact that one event occurred before another insufficient to establish root cause?","answer":"Temporal precedence only establishes ordering. A preceding event may be coincidental, downstream of an unobserved cause, or one part of a larger causal chain. Repairing it without stronger evidence can leave the true cause untouched.","context":"A configuration, deployment, or resource event occurred shortly before an incident symptom appeared.","objective":"Separate temporal correlation from causal evidence.","attempted_solution":"Treat the most recent preceding change as the root cause and revert it.","failure":"The rollback does not restore the required behavior because the preceding event was not causal.","evidence":"Causal diagnosis requires evidence connecting the event to the affected behavior, not temporal order alone.","cause":"Incident chronology was used as causal proof.","root_cause":"The diagnosis model lacked causal linkage criteria beyond timestamp ordering.","impact":"Unnecessary changes and missed root causes increase recovery complexity.","containment":"Preserve the timeline while testing whether the suspected event actually explains the observed failure.","remediation":"Correlate event scope with affected components, compare unaffected controls where possible, and identify mechanism-level evidence before attributing causality.","lessons":"Earlier-in-time is not equivalent to cause.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; temporal-causality distinction","related_records":["D53-B81-R01","D53-B81-R04"],"relationships":["temporal-correlation","causal-evidence"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B81-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Incident Diagnosis Failures","subtopic":"Scope mismatch","concept":"Diagnosis identifies a local fault that cannot explain the full incident scope","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a technically real fault still be the wrong root cause for an incident?","answer":"A component can genuinely be unhealthy while being only one downstream manifestation of a broader failure. If the proposed cause cannot explain unaffected and affected populations consistently, it is incomplete as the incident explanation.","context":"One service exhibits clear errors while several dependent services also show correlated failures.","objective":"Ensure the proposed cause explains the observed blast radius and dependency relationships.","attempted_solution":"Repair the unhealthy service because its logs provide the clearest local evidence.","failure":"The local service recovers but other affected components remain degraded.","evidence":"A root-cause hypothesis should account for the population and dependency scope of observed symptoms.","cause":"Local diagnostic clarity was mistaken for system-level causal completeness.","root_cause":"Diagnosis lacked an explicit scope-explanation criterion.","impact":"Partial repairs consume time while broader failure propagation continues.","containment":"Map affected and unaffected populations before expanding the repair.","remediation":"Require candidate causes to explain observed scope, dependency paths, and relevant unaffected controls.","lessons":"The clearest local fault is not necessarily the complete incident cause.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; incident-scope reasoning","related_records":["D53-B80-R02","D53-B81-R01"],"relationships":["scope-consistency","root-cause-completeness"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B81-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Incident Diagnosis Failures","subtopic":"Diagnostic confidence","concept":"Repair is authorized despite unresolved high-impact diagnostic uncertainty","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"When can uncertainty itself make a proposed repair unsafe?","answer":"If competing causes imply materially different repair actions or blast radii, unresolved uncertainty can make action selection unsafe. A repair should not be treated as low-risk merely because one hypothesis has the highest current confidence.","context":"Available evidence supports multiple possible causes and each candidate implies different operational actions.","objective":"Make repair decisions proportional to uncertainty and consequence.","attempted_solution":"Select the highest-confidence hypothesis without considering the cost of being wrong.","failure":"The chosen repair causes additional disruption because the lower-probability hypothesis was the actual cause.","evidence":"Decision quality depends on both causal confidence and consequences of incorrect action.","cause":"Confidence was treated as sufficient without incorporating action risk.","root_cause":"The repair model lacked uncertainty-to-action risk coupling.","impact":"High-impact repairs can be executed under insufficient diagnostic certainty.","containment":"Prefer reversible, low-blast-radius information-gathering actions while uncertainty remains material.","remediation":"Represent confidence, alternative hypotheses, action reversibility, blast radius, and expected information gain together in repair planning.","lessons":"Diagnostic confidence and repair risk must be evaluated jointly.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; uncertainty-aware repair reasoning","related_records":["D53-B82-R02","D53-B83-R01"],"relationships":["uncertainty-aware-action","diagnostic-confidence"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B82-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Repair Decision Failures","subtopic":"Evidence-action mismatch","concept":"Selected repair does not directly address the mechanism supported by available evidence","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a repair decision fail even when the diagnosis is substantially correct?","answer":"The diagnosis may correctly identify a causal mechanism while the selected action targets a nearby symptom or weaker dependency. The evidence can therefore be correct while the action is mismatched to the mechanism.","context":"Evidence strongly indicates a specific failure mechanism, but several possible actions are available.","objective":"Select an action whose mechanism of effect matches the diagnosed failure.","attempted_solution":"Apply a familiar operational workaround that reduces the visible symptom without changing the identified cause.","failure":"The symptom decreases temporarily but the causal mechanism remains active.","evidence":"A repair should state the mechanism by which the action is expected to change the diagnosed condition.","cause":"Action selection was based on familiarity rather than causal alignment.","root_cause":"No explicit evidence-to-action mapping was required.","impact":"Temporary symptom suppression can be mistaken for successful repair.","containment":"Treat symptom reduction as provisional until the diagnosed mechanism is demonstrably affected.","remediation":"Link each repair action to the diagnosed mechanism, expected state change, preconditions, and measurable postcondition.","lessons":"Correct diagnosis does not guarantee correct repair selection.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; evidence-to-action alignment","related_records":["D53-B81-R01","D53-B82-R03"],"relationships":["evidence-action-alignment","mechanism-targeting"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B82-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Repair Decision Failures","subtopic":"Reversibility","concept":"Repair selects an irreversible action when a lower-risk reversible action could distinguish hypotheses","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can choosing an irreversible repair too early cause avoidable damage?","answer":"When uncertainty is material, an irreversible action can destroy evidence, alter state, or increase blast radius before the diagnosis is confirmed. A reversible diagnostic action may provide useful evidence at lower cost.","context":"Several repair options are available and the causal model remains uncertain.","objective":"Use the least harmful action that can achieve recovery or materially reduce uncertainty.","attempted_solution":"Apply the strongest available corrective action immediately.","failure":"The action changes system state before the underlying cause is confirmed and makes subsequent diagnosis harder.","evidence":"Action reversibility is a relevant decision variable under uncertainty.","cause":"Repair urgency was interpreted as justification for maximal intervention.","root_cause":"The decision process did not compare reversibility and information gain among candidate actions.","impact":"Additional damage, evidence loss, or more difficult rollback can result.","containment":"Prefer bounded, reversible interventions while preserving evidence.","remediation":"Rank actions by expected effect, reversibility, blast radius, information gain, and required authority without using a single simplistic priority.","lessons":"Under uncertainty, reversible information-producing actions can be safer than broad corrective actions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; reversible decision reasoning","related_records":["D53-B81-R05","D53-B83-R02"],"relationships":["reversibility-aware-planning","information-gain"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B82-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Repair Decision Failures","subtopic":"Action side effects","concept":"Repair decision accounts for intended effect but not predictable secondary effects","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a repair fail because its side effects were omitted from the decision model?","answer":"An action can correctly change the target condition while consuming capacity, changing dependent behavior, invalidating assumptions, or triggering other mechanisms. Omitting predictable side effects produces an incomplete decision model.","context":"A repair action is known to correct a local fault but also changes resource usage or dependency behavior.","objective":"Evaluate intended effects and material side effects before execution.","attempted_solution":"Authorize the repair based on its expected primary benefit only.","failure":"A predictable secondary effect creates a new failure condition.","evidence":"Repair plans should represent expected state changes beyond the immediate target.","cause":"Decision analysis stopped at the direct effect.","root_cause":"No side-effect inventory or dependency-impact analysis was required.","impact":"Repair-induced degradation can exceed the benefit of the repair.","containment":"Constrain the repair scope and monitor known secondary effects.","remediation":"Model direct and indirect effects, affected dependencies, resource changes, and possible failure amplification before execution.","lessons":"A repair action is a system change, not an isolated mutation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; repair side-effect analysis","related_records":["D53-B76-R01","D53-B98-R01"],"relationships":["side-effect-analysis","system-level-action-model"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B82-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Repair Decision Failures","subtopic":"Evidence freshness","concept":"Repair decision relies on diagnostic evidence that is no longer representative of current system state","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a previously correct repair decision become unsafe after system conditions change?","answer":"Operational state can change between diagnosis and execution. A repair selected from earlier evidence may no longer target the active failure, and its assumptions may have become invalid.","context":"Diagnosis occurs during a rapidly changing incident and execution is delayed by approval or dependency preparation.","objective":"Ensure repair decisions remain valid at execution time.","attempted_solution":"Execute the previously approved repair without rechecking critical assumptions.","failure":"The repair targets stale conditions and changes a state that is no longer the active failure context.","evidence":"Time-sensitive diagnostic facts require freshness or validity conditions.","cause":"The repair plan had no expiration or pre-execution evidence check.","root_cause":"Decision validity was treated as permanent after initial diagnosis.","impact":"Stale repairs can create new failures or interfere with concurrent recovery actions.","containment":"Re-evaluate material assumptions immediately before execution.","remediation":"Attach freshness requirements and validity predicates to evidence and require pre-execution revalidation for time-sensitive conditions.","lessons":"A repair decision has a validity window when its evidence depends on changing state.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; evidence freshness","related_records":["D53-B84-R02","D53-B85-R01"],"relationships":["decision-freshness","pre-execution-revalidation"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B82-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Repair Decision Failures","subtopic":"Decision dependency","concept":"Repair decision assumes a prerequisite repair or environmental condition that has not been established","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a correct repair action fail when its prerequisite state is absent?","answer":"Some actions are effective only after a dependency, resource, state, or authority condition exists. Executing them without that precondition can produce partial effects or misleading failure signals.","context":"A repair plan contains an action that requires another component or state to be ready first.","objective":"Prevent execution of actions whose required preconditions are not satisfied.","attempted_solution":"Execute the intended repair sequence based on expected dependency readiness.","failure":"The action fails or produces an incomplete state because its prerequisite was not actually ready.","evidence":"Repair actions should declare and verify explicit preconditions before mutation.","cause":"Prerequisite readiness was inferred from plan order rather than observed state.","root_cause":"Precondition enforcement was absent from action execution.","impact":"Partial repairs and repeated retries can complicate recovery.","containment":"Abort or defer the action when required preconditions are not satisfied.","remediation":"Represent preconditions as machine-checkable predicates and evaluate them immediately before each dependent action.","lessons":"Plan order is not evidence of prerequisite satisfaction.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; precondition-aware execution","related_records":["D53-B83-R01","D53-B85-R02"],"relationships":["precondition-enforcement","dependency-aware-repair"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B83-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Repair Planning Failures","subtopic":"Missing preconditions","concept":"Repair plan omits an environmental precondition required for safe execution","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a repair plan fail even when its individual actions are correct?","answer":"Correct actions can fail when required environmental conditions such as capacity, maintenance state, dependency readiness, or data availability are absent. The plan is incomplete because it describes actions without their enabling conditions.","context":"A multi-step repair procedure has been used successfully in a stable environment but is applied during a constrained incident.","objective":"Make repair plans explicit about required preconditions and environmental assumptions.","attempted_solution":"Reuse the known repair sequence without checking the conditions under which it previously worked.","failure":"One or more steps fail because the required environment is not available.","evidence":"Repair procedures are context-dependent and must encode relevant preconditions.","cause":"The plan captured actions but not execution prerequisites.","root_cause":"Procedural knowledge was stored without its applicability conditions.","impact":"Repeated failed repairs and unnecessary retries increase incident duration.","containment":"Stop at the first unsatisfied precondition and reassess rather than skipping the condition.","remediation":"Encode preconditions, dependencies, expected state, resource requirements, and abort conditions for each repair step.","lessons":"A repair sequence without applicability conditions is incomplete operational knowledge.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; context-bound repair planning","related_records":["D53-B82-R05","D53-B83-R02"],"relationships":["precondition-modeling","context-dependent-repair"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B83-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Repair Planning Failures","subtopic":"Dependency ordering","concept":"Repair plan orders actions by component ownership rather than dependency prerequisites","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can incorrect ordering cause a repair plan to fail?","answer":"A component may be repaired before the dependency that supplies its required state or capacity. The component can then fail again or produce misleading results because the underlying prerequisite remains unresolved.","context":"Several components require coordinated remediation and different teams or systems own the steps.","objective":"Order repair actions according to causal and dependency prerequisites.","attempted_solution":"Execute actions in organizational ownership order rather than dependency order.","failure":"Downstream repair steps execute against unavailable or invalid prerequisites.","evidence":"Repair ordering should follow required state and dependency relationships.","cause":"Organizational workflow was substituted for technical dependency ordering.","root_cause":"The repair plan lacked an explicit dependency graph.","impact":"Partial repair, repeated work, and inconsistent intermediate states.","containment":"Pause downstream actions until prerequisite state is verified.","remediation":"Construct an action dependency graph, identify independent actions, enforce prerequisite completion, and define valid intermediate states.","lessons":"Ownership boundaries should not determine technical repair ordering.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; dependency-ordered repair planning","related_records":["D53-B91-R02","D53-B83-R01"],"relationships":["repair-dependency-graph","execution-order"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B83-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Repair Planning Failures","subtopic":"Abort planning","concept":"Repair plan has no explicit abort condition when intermediate state becomes unsafe","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a multi-step repair become more damaging when no abort condition exists?","answer":"A repair can encounter changed assumptions, failed preconditions, or partial side effects. Without an explicit abort boundary, subsequent steps may continue against an invalid intermediate state.","context":"A repair consists of several dependent mutations executed sequentially.","objective":"Stop repair progression when continuation would violate safety or correctness conditions.","attempted_solution":"Continue all planned steps unless an infrastructure error stops execution.","failure":"Later actions operate on an invalid intermediate state and increase the failure scope.","evidence":"Multi-step repair plans require explicit stop conditions in addition to success conditions.","cause":"The plan defined completion criteria but not unsafe-intermediate-state criteria.","root_cause":"Abort semantics were omitted from the repair workflow.","impact":"A partially failed repair can become a compound failure.","containment":"Halt the sequence on material invariant violations or unexpected state changes.","remediation":"Define per-step success predicates, abort predicates, safe-stop states, and recovery actions before execution.","lessons":"Every multi-step repair needs a controlled way to stop safely before completion.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; safe-stop planning","related_records":["D53-B85-R02","D53-B98-R01"],"relationships":["abort-semantics","safe-intermediate-state"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B83-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Repair Planning Failures","subtopic":"Rollback planning","concept":"Repair plan has rollback instructions but rollback cannot restore the pre-repair state","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is a rollback procedure insufficient if it does not define what state it can actually restore?","answer":"Rollback may reverse code or configuration changes while leaving data mutations, external effects, generated artifacts, or dependent state unchanged. The system can therefore remain in a new inconsistent state.","context":"A repair is considered reversible because a documented rollback command exists.","objective":"Ensure rollback semantics match the actual effects of the repair.","attempted_solution":"Declare a repair reversible because its primary configuration can be restored.","failure":"Rollback restores the primary setting but not side effects created during execution.","evidence":"Rollback capability must be defined over the complete state changed by the repair.","cause":"Rollback scope was narrower than repair effect scope.","root_cause":"No state-effect inventory was used to validate rollback completeness.","impact":"Operators believe recovery is available when important state cannot actually be restored.","containment":"Treat rollback as partial until all affected state classes are identified.","remediation":"Enumerate mutable state and external effects, define compensating actions where true reversal is impossible, and verify rollback postconditions.","lessons":"A rollback command is not proof of rollback completeness.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; rollback completeness","related_records":["D53-B97-R01","D53-B83-R03"],"relationships":["rollback-scope","state-effect-inventory"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B83-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Repair Planning Failures","subtopic":"Parallel repair planning","concept":"Independently safe repair steps become unsafe when planned concurrently because their combined effects interact","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can individually valid repair actions become invalid when executed together?","answer":"Actions can compete for shared resources, change assumptions, or modify the state required by another action. Per-action safety does not prove combined-plan safety.","context":"Several repair actions appear independent because they target different components.","objective":"Determine whether the repair plan remains safe under combined execution.","attempted_solution":"Execute all actions concurrently to reduce incident duration.","failure":"Combined resource or state interactions create unexpected behavior.","evidence":"Repair plans must account for interaction effects among concurrently executing actions.","cause":"Action independence was inferred from target separation alone.","root_cause":"The plan lacked a combined-effect analysis.","impact":"Concurrency increases blast radius and complicates diagnosis.","containment":"Serialize interacting actions and preserve stable checkpoints.","remediation":"Model shared resources and state dependencies, classify actions for safe parallelism, and enforce concurrency constraints.","lessons":"Different targets do not necessarily imply independent repair effects.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; repair interaction planning","related_records":["D53-B53-R01","D53-B91-R01"],"relationships":["combined-effect-analysis","repair-parallelism"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B84-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Repair Authorization Failures","subtopic":"Excessive scope","concept":"Approved repair scope is broader than the evidence-supported failure scope","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can an otherwise authorized repair still violate repair governance?","answer":"Authorization can be valid in principle while the requested action exceeds the affected resource, tenant, environment, or component scope justified by evidence. Broad authorization does not make unsupported scope safe.","context":"An incident affects a subset of resources but the proposed repair targets a much larger population.","objective":"Bind repair authority to the smallest evidence-supported scope.","attempted_solution":"Authorize a fleet-wide change because it is operationally simpler than targeting the affected subset.","failure":"Healthy resources are modified even though evidence does not show they require repair.","evidence":"Repair authority should be constrained by target scope, justification, and expected effect.","cause":"Operational convenience expanded the action boundary beyond diagnosed impact.","root_cause":"Authorization did not bind the approved scope to the evidence-supported target set.","impact":"Unnecessary changes increase blast radius and recovery complexity.","containment":"Restrict execution to verified affected resources until broader scope is justified.","remediation":"Represent authorized scope explicitly and reject execution when the actual target set exceeds approved scope.","lessons":"Valid authority is not unlimited authority.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; scope-bound repair governance","related_records":["D53-B75-R01","D53-B84-R02"],"relationships":["evidence-bound-authorization","minimum-required-scope"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B84-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Repair Authorization Failures","subtopic":"Stale approval","concept":"Repair approval remains active after material repair assumptions have changed","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a previously approved repair become unauthorized in practice?","answer":"Approval is contextual. If target scope, evidence, risk, dependencies, or intended action changes materially, the original approval may no longer represent the current action.","context":"A repair was approved during one incident state but execution is delayed until the system changes.","objective":"Ensure approval remains bound to the action and assumptions actually executed.","attempted_solution":"Reuse the original approval without revalidation because the repair command itself has not changed.","failure":"The same command now acts on different state or scope than the approver evaluated.","evidence":"Approval validity depends on the identity and relevant context of the action, not only its textual command.","cause":"Approval was bound to a command string rather than an immutable repair intent and scope.","root_cause":"No approval freshness or assumption-validity rule existed.","impact":"Actions execute under authorization that no longer matches current risk.","containment":"Suspend execution when material approval assumptions change.","remediation":"Bind approval to target, scope, action identity, evidence context, and expiration or revalidation conditions.","lessons":"Approval must authorize the actual repair context, not merely a previously reviewed instruction.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; approval-context integrity","related_records":["D53-B82-R04","D53-B84-R01"],"relationships":["approval-freshness","authorization-context-binding"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B84-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Repair Authorization Failures","subtopic":"Emergency authority","concept":"Emergency repair uses exceptional authority without recording the condition that justified the exception","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can emergency authorization fail governance even when the repair is technically effective?","answer":"Exceptional authority may be legitimate under defined conditions, but if the triggering condition, scope, duration, and subsequent review are not recorded, the action cannot be reliably audited or distinguished from ordinary unauthorized expansion.","context":"An emergency procedure permits accelerated repair under severe operational conditions.","objective":"Preserve traceable authority and bounded scope during emergency execution.","attempted_solution":"Allow the operator to bypass normal approval and document the action later if needed.","failure":"The repair occurs without a reliable record of why emergency authority applied or what limits were intended.","evidence":"Emergency authority requires explicit conditions and accountability even when normal approval paths are bypassed.","cause":"Emergency status was treated as self-justifying authority.","root_cause":"No machine-readable emergency authorization contract existed.","impact":"Scope control, auditability, and post-incident review are weakened.","containment":"Limit emergency action to the minimum required scope and record the authorization basis before or during execution where feasible.","remediation":"Define emergency triggers, authority, scope, duration, evidence requirements, and mandatory retrospective review.","lessons":"Emergency authority changes the approval path; it does not remove governance.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; emergency authorization governance","related_records":["D53-B74-R05","D53-B95-R02"],"relationships":["emergency-authorization","bounded-exception-governance"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B84-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Repair Authorization Failures","subtopic":"Authority mismatch","concept":"Approver has authority over the resource but not over the requested repair class","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is resource ownership insufficient evidence of repair authorization?","answer":"Authority can be separated by resource, action type, environment, data sensitivity, or operational risk. A person or system may control a resource without being authorized to perform every class of repair on it.","context":"A repair targets a resource owned by the approving operator but requires a higher-risk action class.","objective":"Validate authorization against both target scope and action type.","attempted_solution":"Allow the resource owner to approve any repair affecting the resource.","failure":"A high-impact action executes without the authority required for that action class.","evidence":"Authorization models should distinguish resource access from permission to perform particular actions.","cause":"Ownership was treated as equivalent to repair authority.","root_cause":"Action-class authorization was absent from the governance model.","impact":"High-impact changes can bypass required controls.","containment":"Require explicit authority for the repair class before execution.","remediation":"Model resource scope, action class, environment, sensitivity, and required authority separately.","lessons":"Who controls a resource is not necessarily authorized to perform every repair on it.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; action-class authorization","related_records":["D53-B75-R05","D53-B84-R01"],"relationships":["action-class-authority","resource-versus-action-permission"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B84-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Repair Authorization Failures","subtopic":"Authorization evidence","concept":"Repair authorization is granted without sufficient evidence that the proposed action matches the documented objective","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can authorization fail when the approver has the correct role and scope?","answer":"Governance can still fail if the approver lacks enough evidence to determine whether the action actually addresses the stated objective. Role correctness does not compensate for insufficient decision evidence.","context":"A high-impact repair request contains an action and objective but limited diagnostic evidence.","objective":"Require sufficient evidence for informed authorization.","attempted_solution":"Approve based on the operator's role and urgency of the incident.","failure":"The authorized action is later shown to have weak or mismatched causal justification.","evidence":"Authorization for high-impact repairs should incorporate the evidence supporting the intended action.","cause":"Approval was treated as a permission check rather than a decision-quality check.","root_cause":"Evidence requirements were not part of repair authorization policy.","impact":"Authorized but poorly justified repairs can increase incident scope.","containment":"Require additional evidence or select a lower-impact reversible action while uncertainty remains.","remediation":"Define minimum evidence requirements proportional to repair impact and preserve the evidence used for approval.","lessons":"Governance must validate both who may act and whether the proposed action is adequately justified.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; evidence-based repair authorization","related_records":["D53-B82-R01","D53-B84-R02"],"relationships":["evidence-based-authorization","decision-quality-governance"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B85-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Repair Execution Failures","subtopic":"Partial execution","concept":"Repair executes only part of a multi-step action sequence and reports completion from the first successful step","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can partial execution be mistaken for successful repair?","answer":"A multi-step repair may complete early steps while later required mutations fail or are skipped. If execution reporting tracks command completion rather than the complete action set and postconditions, the repair can be falsely considered complete.","context":"A repair contains several ordered actions and execution is interrupted after some actions succeed.","objective":"Represent partial execution explicitly and prevent incomplete repairs from being classified as successful.","attempted_solution":"Report success when the primary repair command returns successfully even though dependent steps remain.","failure":"System state contains a mixture of repaired and unrepaired components.","evidence":"Execution status must distinguish complete, partial, failed, and unknown action states.","cause":"Execution reporting used command-level success rather than plan-level completion.","root_cause":"No per-step execution ledger or plan completion invariant existed.","impact":"Operators may stop recovery while residual failure conditions remain.","containment":"Mark the repair incomplete and prevent dependent actions from assuming full recovery.","remediation":"Track each action's execution status, expected state change, actual result, and remaining obligations.","lessons":"Command success is not equivalent to repair completion.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; execution-state truth","related_records":["D53-B83-R03","D53-B86-R01"],"relationships":["partial-execution-truth","plan-level-completion"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B85-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Repair Execution Failures","subtopic":"Interrupted execution","concept":"Repair interruption leaves ambiguous state and is incorrectly retried from the beginning","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can restarting an interrupted repair from step one cause additional failure?","answer":"After interruption, some steps may already have taken effect. Replaying the full sequence without determining actual state can duplicate effects or violate assumptions that held only before the first execution.","context":"A repair process is interrupted after an uncertain number of steps have executed.","objective":"Recover from interruption using observed execution state rather than assuming zero progress.","attempted_solution":"Restart the entire repair sequence because the previous execution was not marked complete.","failure":"Already-completed steps are repeated and interact incorrectly with later actions.","evidence":"Interrupted execution requires state reconciliation before continuation or retry.","cause":"Unknown execution state was treated as equivalent to no execution.","root_cause":"The repair workflow lacked interruption reconciliation semantics.","impact":"Duplicate mutations and additional inconsistency can occur.","containment":"Freeze further mutation until executed steps and current state are reconciled.","remediation":"Maintain per-step execution evidence, reconcile actual state, resume only from a valid checkpoint, or use explicitly idempotent recovery operations.","lessons":"Unknown execution state must be resolved before replaying a repair sequence.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; interrupted-repair recovery","related_records":["D53-B62-R01","D53-B85-R01"],"relationships":["execution-reconciliation","checkpoint-aware-repair"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B85-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Repair Execution Failures","subtopic":"External side effects","concept":"Repair execution succeeds locally but external side effects fail without being reflected in local status","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can local execution success hide a failed repair?","answer":"A repair may update local state successfully while an external system, downstream service, or asynchronous side effect fails. Local completion therefore does not establish completion of the intended system-level action.","context":"A repair command modifies local configuration and triggers an external operation through a separate interface.","objective":"Track the complete action lifecycle across local and external effects.","attempted_solution":"Mark the repair successful after the local mutation succeeds.","failure":"The external effect does not occur or cannot be confirmed, leaving the intended system state incomplete.","evidence":"Execution state must distinguish local mutation from externally confirmed effect.","cause":"The execution boundary ended at the initiating component.","root_cause":"External side effects were not modeled as part of the repair action.","impact":"Repair status overstates actual system recovery.","containment":"Treat external effect as pending or unknown until independently confirmed.","remediation":"Model external actions explicitly with correlation identifiers, completion evidence, timeout semantics, and reconciliation paths.","lessons":"A repair action is complete only at the boundary defined by its intended effect, not necessarily by the initiating command.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; distributed execution-state reasoning","related_records":["D53-B61-R03","D53-B86-R02"],"relationships":["external-effect-tracking","execution-boundary"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B85-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Repair Execution Failures","subtopic":"Execution environment drift","concept":"Repair begins in one environment state and completes after a material environment change","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can an environment change during repair invalidate execution assumptions?","answer":"Dependencies, capacity, configuration, or target state can change while a repair is running. Later actions may therefore execute under conditions different from those validated at repair start.","context":"A long-running repair spans a period in which external configuration or resource conditions can change.","objective":"Detect material environment drift before executing dependent repair steps.","attempted_solution":"Assume the environment remains compatible throughout the repair duration.","failure":"Later repair steps operate against changed prerequisites and produce an invalid intermediate state.","evidence":"Long-running repair workflows require state and assumption revalidation at meaningful boundaries.","cause":"Preconditions were evaluated only once at repair start.","root_cause":"No mid-repair environment drift detection existed.","impact":"A valid repair can become invalid while executing.","containment":"Pause at detected material drift and reconcile current state before continuing.","remediation":"Define drift-sensitive predicates and revalidate them before critical steps or after significant elapsed time.","lessons":"Repair validity can expire during execution when its environment is dynamic.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; dynamic execution context","related_records":["D53-B82-R04","D53-B83-R01"],"relationships":["environment-drift","mid-repair-revalidation"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B85-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Repair Execution Failures","subtopic":"Execution result ambiguity","concept":"Repair executor returns an ambiguous result and the workflow records it as failure without reconciling actual state","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can treating an ambiguous execution result as a definitive failure cause another repair failure?","answer":"A timeout, connection loss, or missing acknowledgement can leave the action either completed or not completed. Retrying immediately on the assumption of failure can duplicate an action that actually succeeded.","context":"A repair command's result is lost or ambiguous after it is submitted to an execution system.","objective":"Resolve ambiguous execution state before selecting retry or compensation behavior.","attempted_solution":"Retry immediately because no success acknowledgement was received.","failure":"The original action may have succeeded and the retry repeats its effect.","evidence":"Submission status, acknowledgement status, and actual state are distinct facts.","cause":"Absence of acknowledgement was interpreted as proof of non-execution.","root_cause":"The executor lacked reconciliation semantics for ambiguous outcomes.","impact":"Duplicate actions or conflicting mutations can occur.","containment":"Stop automatic retry and reconcile target state or execution ledger first.","remediation":"Use unique operation identifiers, durable execution records, state reconciliation, and explicit UNKNOWN execution status.","lessons":"No acknowledgement is not equivalent to no execution.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; ambiguous execution semantics","related_records":["D53-B62-R02","D53-B85-R02"],"relationships":["ambiguous-execution","operation-reconciliation"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B86-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Repair Verification Failures","subtopic":"Incomplete postconditions","concept":"Repair verification checks that a command ran but does not verify the intended system postcondition","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_GAP","question":"Why is command completion inadequate evidence that a repair succeeded?","answer":"A command can return successfully while failing to establish the state or behavior that motivated the repair. Verification must target the intended postcondition, not merely execution completion.","context":"A repair tool reports success after applying a configuration or mutation.","objective":"Verify the intended state and behavior after repair.","attempted_solution":"Use the executor's success response as the repair verification result.","failure":"The system remains functionally broken despite a successful command response.","evidence":"Execution and outcome verification are separate lifecycle facts.","cause":"Verification was coupled to executor status.","root_cause":"The repair lacked explicit postconditions independent of command success.","impact":"Failed repairs can be incorrectly marked successful.","containment":"Keep the repaired scope under observation until intended postconditions are independently evidenced.","remediation":"Define machine-checkable postconditions covering state, behavior, and relevant invariants; verify them independently of executor status.","lessons":"Execution evidence answers whether an action ran; verification evidence answers whether the intended condition now holds.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; verification truth model","related_records":["D53-B50-R01","D53-B85-R01"],"relationships":["execution-versus-verification","postcondition-verification"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B86-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Repair Verification Failures","subtopic":"Wrong verification target","concept":"Verification observes a nearby component instead of the actual repaired dependency or user-facing behavior","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_GAP","question":"How can verification pass while the repaired system remains broken?","answer":"A healthy upstream or downstream signal can coexist with failure at the actual target. Verification must measure the property and boundary affected by the repair rather than an easily available proxy.","context":"A repair changes a backend dependency while verification checks only an upstream process health signal.","objective":"Verify the exact behavior and boundary affected by the repair.","attempted_solution":"Use service-process health as a proxy for successful backend recovery.","failure":"The process is healthy while the dependent operation remains unavailable.","evidence":"Proxy health signals do not necessarily establish the repaired functional property.","cause":"Verification selected a convenient signal rather than the target postcondition.","root_cause":"The verification model lacked target-property binding.","impact":"Recovery can be declared while user-facing or dependency-level failure persists.","containment":"Use direct functional checks for the affected operation before declaring success.","remediation":"Bind verification checks to the repaired component, dependency, and user-visible behavior where applicable.","lessons":"Verification must test the property changed by the repair, not merely a nearby health indicator.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; verification-target binding","related_records":["D53-B49-R01","D53-B86-R01"],"relationships":["verification-target-binding","functional-verification"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B86-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Repair Verification Failures","subtopic":"Insufficient negative verification","concept":"Verification confirms intended allowed behavior but does not confirm that prohibited behavior remains prevented","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_GAP","question":"Why can successful positive tests be insufficient after a repair?","answer":"A repair can restore the desired path while accidentally weakening an invariant that should continue to reject invalid inputs, unauthorized actions, or unsafe states.","context":"A repair restores normal successful requests and verification checks only those requests.","objective":"Verify both required behavior and preserved rejection or safety invariants.","attempted_solution":"Run only positive-path functional checks after repair.","failure":"Normal operations work while a previously blocked or invalid path is unintentionally permitted.","evidence":"Post-repair verification should include relevant negative and boundary conditions.","cause":"Verification focused only on restored functionality.","root_cause":"Preserved invariants were not included in the verification set.","impact":"A repair can introduce a regression that positive tests cannot detect.","containment":"Keep sensitive or high-risk operations constrained until critical negative paths are checked.","remediation":"Include negative, boundary, authorization, and invariant-preservation tests appropriate to the repaired component.","lessons":"Restoration verification must prove what still must not happen as well as what must happen.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; invariant-preservation verification","related_records":["D53-B71-R04","D53-B86-R04"],"relationships":["negative-verification","invariant-preservation"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B86-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Repair Verification Failures","subtopic":"Verification coverage","concept":"Verification covers the repaired component but omits critical dependent workflows","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_GAP","question":"Why can component-level verification fail to prove system-level repair success?","answer":"A repaired component can satisfy its local contract while a dependent workflow remains broken because integration behavior, ordering, data assumptions, or downstream state was not restored.","context":"A component is repaired and its direct health checks pass.","objective":"Verify the required dependency chain and affected workflows.","attempted_solution":"Declare recovery after local component tests pass.","failure":"An end-to-end workflow remains unavailable or incorrect.","evidence":"System-level outcomes can depend on interactions not visible in component-local checks.","cause":"Verification scope stopped at the repaired component boundary.","root_cause":"No dependency-aware verification coverage was defined.","impact":"Residual failures escape post-repair validation.","containment":"Retain restricted operation until affected workflows and dependencies are verified.","remediation":"Map affected workflows and verify critical integration paths after component-level checks.","lessons":"Local correctness does not automatically imply workflow correctness.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; dependency-aware verification","related_records":["D53-B47-R02","D53-B86-R01"],"relationships":["system-level-verification","dependency-chain-validation"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B86-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Repair Verification Failures","subtopic":"Observation window","concept":"Repair is verified immediately after recovery without observing delayed failure effects","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_GAP","question":"Why can immediate post-repair success be insufficient evidence?","answer":"Some failures emerge only after queues refill, caches expire, scheduled tasks run, resources accumulate, or delayed dependencies respond. Immediate checks can therefore miss delayed consequences.","context":"A repair produces normal responses immediately but the system contains delayed or accumulating behaviors.","objective":"Match verification duration to the failure mechanism and expected delayed effects.","attempted_solution":"Declare success immediately after the first healthy checks.","failure":"The original or repair-induced failure returns after delayed effects occur.","evidence":"Verification timing should reflect the temporal characteristics of the failure and repair.","cause":"Verification window was chosen for convenience rather than failure dynamics.","root_cause":"No mechanism-specific observation criterion existed.","impact":"Premature recovery declarations lead to repeated incidents or repeated repair cycles.","containment":"Maintain controlled observation until delayed effects relevant to the failure mechanism are covered.","remediation":"Define observation windows from workload, queue, cache, scheduler, resource, and dependency dynamics.","lessons":"Verification duration is part of verification design.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; time-dependent verification","related_records":["D53-B87-R02","D53-B50-R03"],"relationships":["observation-window","delayed-effect-verification"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B87-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Repair Outcome Measurement Failures","subtopic":"Wrong metric","concept":"Outcome measurement uses a metric that improves while the actual incident objective remains unsatisfied","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a repair appear successful because the wrong outcome metric was selected?","answer":"A metric may correlate with recovery without directly measuring the business or technical objective. Improving the proxy can therefore leave the actual failure unresolved.","context":"An incident has multiple symptoms and a single aggregate metric is selected as the repair outcome measure.","objective":"Measure whether the stated repair objective has actually been achieved.","attempted_solution":"Use the easiest available health metric as the primary outcome measure.","failure":"The metric improves while the affected workflow or service objective remains unsatisfied.","evidence":"Outcome metrics should be causally and operationally relevant to the stated objective.","cause":"Measurement convenience replaced objective-specific outcome definition.","root_cause":"No explicit objective-to-metric mapping existed.","impact":"Failed repairs are recorded as successful and contaminate future learning.","containment":"Retain the repair as unresolved until objective-specific evidence is available.","remediation":"Define direct outcome measures and distinguish them from supporting health indicators and proxies.","lessons":"A better proxy is not necessarily a better outcome.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; outcome measurement design","related_records":["D53-B86-R02","D53-B87-R02"],"relationships":["objective-metric-binding","proxy-versus-outcome"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B87-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Repair Outcome Measurement Failures","subtopic":"Delayed outcome","concept":"Outcome measurement ends before delayed side effects become observable","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a repair's measured outcome be misleading when effects are delayed?","answer":"A repair may initially improve the measured condition while delayed resource accumulation, queue growth, expiration, or downstream processing later recreates the failure. A short measurement window can therefore produce false confidence.","context":"The repaired system has delayed workloads or state transitions that occur after the initial recovery period.","objective":"Measure outcomes across the timescale relevant to the repaired mechanism.","attempted_solution":"Measure only immediate post-repair service health.","failure":"Delayed degradation appears after the outcome record has already been closed.","evidence":"Outcome measurement must cover relevant delayed effects rather than only immediate response.","cause":"Measurement duration did not match system dynamics.","root_cause":"Delayed outcome behavior was not modeled in the repair measurement plan.","impact":"Learning data records a false positive repair outcome.","containment":"Keep the outcome provisional until the mechanism-specific observation interval is complete.","remediation":"Define delayed-effect observation criteria and retain provisional outcome status until they are satisfied.","lessons":"Outcome closure requires temporal coverage appropriate to the failure mechanism.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; delayed outcome measurement","related_records":["D53-B86-R05","D53-B87-R01"],"relationships":["delayed-outcome","provisional-repair-result"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B87-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Repair Outcome Measurement Failures","subtopic":"Counterfactual baseline","concept":"Repair outcome is evaluated without a meaningful baseline or unaffected comparison","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can before-and-after measurement fail to establish repair impact?","answer":"System conditions can change independently of the repair. Without an appropriate baseline or comparison, improvement may be caused by traffic changes, dependency recovery, natural variance, or another intervention.","context":"A repair is applied during a changing incident and metrics improve afterward.","objective":"Separate repair-associated effects from concurrent environmental changes.","attempted_solution":"Attribute post-repair metric improvement directly to the repair because it occurred afterward.","failure":"The recorded outcome overstates causal impact.","evidence":"Outcome attribution requires temporal and contextual evidence sufficient to distinguish concurrent effects.","cause":"Temporal association was treated as proof of repair effect.","root_cause":"No baseline, comparison, or competing-intervention analysis was recorded.","impact":"Future repair selection can learn from an incorrectly attributed success.","containment":"Mark causal outcome attribution as uncertain rather than recording a definitive repair effect.","remediation":"Capture pre-repair baseline, concurrent changes, relevant unaffected comparisons, and uncertainty in attribution.","lessons":"Post-repair improvement does not by itself prove that the repair caused the improvement.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; counterfactual outcome reasoning","related_records":["D53-B81-R03","D53-B88-R03"],"relationships":["counterfactual-attribution","causal-outcome-measurement"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B87-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Repair Outcome Measurement Failures","subtopic":"Residual impact","concept":"Repair outcome records primary recovery while omitting residual degradation in secondary objectives","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a repair be incorrectly classified as successful when residual impact remains?","answer":"The primary incident symptom may disappear while throughput, latency, resource margin, error distribution, or another required property remains degraded. Outcome measurement must capture material residual effects.","context":"The initiating failure condition has cleared but the system operates under a degraded secondary condition.","objective":"Determine whether the repair restored the complete required operating envelope.","attempted_solution":"Close the incident when the initiating error rate returns to normal.","failure":"A material secondary degradation remains unrecorded.","evidence":"Recovery should be measured against the full set of requirements affected by the incident.","cause":"Outcome measurement focused on symptom disappearance.","root_cause":"No residual-impact assessment existed in repair closure.","impact":"The system is considered repaired while operating outside acceptable conditions.","containment":"Keep recovery provisional and monitor relevant secondary objectives.","remediation":"Define residual-impact checks for capacity, performance, correctness, security, and dependent workflows as appropriate.","lessons":"Incident symptom clearance is one outcome signal, not the complete recovery definition.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; residual-impact measurement","related_records":["D53-B76-R01","D53-B86-R04"],"relationships":["residual-impact","complete-recovery-envelope"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B87-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Repair Outcome Measurement Failures","subtopic":"Outcome attribution","concept":"Multiple repairs occur in the same interval and outcome is attributed to one repair without evidence","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can concurrent repairs corrupt outcome measurement?","answer":"When multiple interventions occur before the system response stabilizes, the observed improvement cannot automatically be attributed to one action. Recording a single repair as the cause without evidence contaminates repair effectiveness data.","context":"Operators perform several remediation actions close together during a complex incident.","objective":"Preserve causal attribution uncertainty when multiple interventions affect the outcome.","attempted_solution":"Attribute recovery to the most significant-looking repair action.","failure":"Future systems learn that the selected action caused recovery when the evidence cannot establish that conclusion.","evidence":"Outcome attribution must account for concurrent interventions.","cause":"Repair records lacked intervention timing and causal attribution metadata.","root_cause":"The learning record assumed one repair per outcome without modeling intervention overlap.","impact":"Historical repair knowledge becomes unreliable.","containment":"Record the outcome as multi-intervention or attribution-uncertain rather than assigning unsupported causality.","remediation":"Capture action timelines, dependencies, intervention overlap, and attribution confidence in outcome records.","lessons":"Successful outcome and causal attribution are separate facts.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; intervention attribution","related_records":["D53-B87-R03","D53-B88-R01"],"relationships":["multi-intervention-attribution","outcome-causality"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B88-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Repair Learning Failures","subtopic":"False lesson extraction","concept":"Learning system records a repair as successful without preserving the uncertainty of causal attribution","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a successful-looking repair produce a harmful learned lesson?","answer":"If outcome attribution is uncertain but the learning record stores a deterministic causal rule, future repairs may apply the action as if its effectiveness were proven.","context":"A recovery follows several concurrent interventions and historical data is converted into reusable repair knowledge.","objective":"Preserve uncertainty and evidence boundaries when extracting lessons.","attempted_solution":"Convert the observed recovery into a direct rule that the repair causes recovery under similar symptoms.","failure":"Future retrieval treats an uncertain association as verified repair knowledge.","evidence":"Learning records must preserve evidence level, attribution confidence, context, and unresolved alternatives.","cause":"The learning pipeline collapsed uncertainty into a binary success label.","root_cause":"No uncertainty-preserving knowledge extraction rule existed.","impact":"The system can systematically repeat weakly supported repairs.","containment":"Mark the lesson as observational or attribution-uncertain and prevent high-confidence reuse.","remediation":"Store causal attribution separately from outcome, retain evidence and alternatives, and require stronger evidence before promoting a lesson to verified repair knowledge.","lessons":"Learning must preserve what is known, inferred, and unknown.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; uncertainty-preserving learning","related_records":["D53-B87-R05","D53-B88-R02"],"relationships":["uncertainty-preserving-learning","causal-attribution"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B88-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Repair Learning Failures","subtopic":"Overgeneralization","concept":"Learning extracts a universal repair rule from a context-specific successful repair","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a valid repair lesson become harmful when generalized beyond its original context?","answer":"A repair may depend on a specific architecture, version, workload, dependency state, or failure mechanism. Removing those applicability conditions can cause the learned rule to be applied where its assumptions are false.","context":"A repair succeeds in one system configuration and is converted into a broadly reusable recommendation.","objective":"Preserve applicability boundaries when learning from successful repairs.","attempted_solution":"Generalize the repair into a rule based only on the visible symptom.","failure":"The same action is applied to a different context with a different underlying mechanism.","evidence":"Repair knowledge requires context, preconditions, constraints, and applicability conditions.","cause":"Context was treated as incidental metadata rather than part of the repair's meaning.","root_cause":"Learning representation did not bind repair knowledge to its applicability conditions.","impact":"Cross-context repair failures increase as the knowledge base grows.","containment":"Require context matching before applying generalized repair knowledge.","remediation":"Store architecture, version, workload, dependency, symptom, cause, and precondition boundaries with learned repair patterns.","lessons":"Generalization must preserve the conditions that made the original repair valid.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; context-bounded learning","related_records":["D53-B90-R01","D53-B89-R02"],"relationships":["context-bounded-generalization","applicability-constraints"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B88-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Repair Learning Failures","subtopic":"Evidence loss","concept":"Learning pipeline stores repair outcome but discards the evidence that supported the outcome","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is a repair lesson unreliable when its supporting evidence is lost?","answer":"A lesson without its evidence cannot be independently re-evaluated, contradicted, downgraded, or traced to the original incident context. It becomes an unsupported assertion in the knowledge base.","context":"Operational repair records are summarized into compact reusable knowledge.","objective":"Retain sufficient provenance and evidence to audit and update learned repair knowledge.","attempted_solution":"Store only the final repair action and success/failure label to reduce storage.","failure":"Later users cannot determine why the lesson was accepted or whether its evidence remains applicable.","evidence":"Learning records should retain evidence references, provenance, confidence, and validation status.","cause":"Compression removed supporting evidence as supposedly non-essential detail.","root_cause":"Knowledge representation prioritized compactness over provenance preservation.","impact":"Contradictions and stale lessons become difficult to detect.","containment":"Mark evidence-incomplete lessons as lower-confidence and restrict high-impact reuse.","remediation":"Retain evidence references, source timestamps, context, outcome measurements, and validation lineage.","lessons":"A reusable repair lesson without evidence provenance is difficult to trust or correct.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; evidence-preserving learning","related_records":["D53-B87-R01","D53-B88-R04"],"relationships":["evidence-provenance","learning-lineage"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B88-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Repair Learning Failures","subtopic":"Negative learning","concept":"Learning system fails to record that a repair was attempted under invalid preconditions","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is a failed repair useful only when its failure context is preserved?","answer":"A failed action can teach that a repair is ineffective, but only if the failure conditions are known. Without preconditions and context, the system may incorrectly conclude that the repair never works or repeatedly retry it under the same invalid conditions.","context":"A repair fails because a prerequisite condition was absent, but the learning record captures only the failed action.","objective":"Distinguish action ineffectiveness from context-invalid execution.","attempted_solution":"Record the repair as simply unsuccessful.","failure":"Future reasoning treats the repair as universally ineffective or repeats it without addressing the missing prerequisite.","evidence":"Negative repair knowledge requires failure cause, preconditions, context, and observed effect.","cause":"Failure outcome was stored without causal context.","root_cause":"Learning representation could not distinguish action failure from applicability failure.","impact":"The knowledge base learns an incorrect negative rule.","containment":"Mark the failure as context-dependent until the cause and preconditions are captured.","remediation":"Separate action validity, precondition validity, execution result, and outcome; encode the conditions under which the attempt failed.","lessons":"A failed repair can mean the action was wrong, the context was wrong, or the prerequisites were missing.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; failure-learning semantics","related_records":["D53-B83-R01","D53-B88-R01"],"relationships":["negative-knowledge","contextual-failure-learning"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B88-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Repair Learning Failures","subtopic":"Stale knowledge","concept":"Learned repair remains active after the underlying technology or configuration context changes","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can previously valid repair knowledge become unsafe after a system version changes?","answer":"A repair can depend on version-specific behavior, APIs, defaults, dependencies, or architecture. If knowledge remains reusable without version validity, a previously correct action can become invalid.","context":"A system is upgraded while historical repair knowledge remains available to the automated repair system.","objective":"Prevent stale repair knowledge from being treated as universally current.","attempted_solution":"Reuse the historical repair because the observed symptom appears identical.","failure":"The repair interacts incorrectly with the newer version's behavior.","evidence":"Repair applicability can be version-dependent and must be represented explicitly.","cause":"Version context was not included in knowledge applicability evaluation.","root_cause":"Learned repair records lacked version validity and supersession semantics.","impact":"Historical knowledge can generate inappropriate repairs after technology changes.","containment":"Downgrade or quarantine version-sensitive knowledge until compatibility is established.","remediation":"Store version ranges, superseding changes, validation dates, and compatibility evidence with learned repair patterns.","lessons":"Knowledge freshness is part of repair applicability.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; version-aware repair learning","related_records":["D53-B66-R01","D53-B89-R03"],"relationships":["version-aware-learning","knowledge-supersession"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B89-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Repair Knowledge-Retrieval Failures","subtopic":"Wrong historical match","concept":"Retrieval selects a historical repair because symptoms match while causal mechanism does not","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can symptom similarity produce the wrong historical repair?","answer":"Different mechanisms can produce similar symptoms. Retrieval based primarily on surface similarity can therefore return a repair whose assumptions and causal mechanism do not match the current incident.","context":"A historical repair record has similar error messages and metrics to the current incident.","objective":"Retrieve repair knowledge using causal, contextual, and implementation attributes rather than symptom text alone.","attempted_solution":"Select the historical repair with the highest lexical or semantic symptom similarity.","failure":"The retrieved repair is applied despite different dependency, architecture, or root-cause conditions.","evidence":"Repair retrieval quality depends on matching context and mechanism in addition to observable symptoms.","cause":"Retrieval weighting overemphasized surface symptoms.","root_cause":"Knowledge retrieval lacked causal and applicability constraints.","impact":"Incorrect historical repairs can be proposed with high apparent relevance.","containment":"Require causal and context compatibility checks before using retrieved repair knowledge.","remediation":"Index and retrieve by symptom, mechanism, context, version, dependencies, preconditions, and prior outcome evidence.","lessons":"Similarity is retrieval evidence, not proof of repair applicability.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; context-aware retrieval","related_records":["D53-B88-R02","D53-B89-R02"],"relationships":["causal-retrieval","applicability-aware-ranking"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B89-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Repair Knowledge-Retrieval Failures","subtopic":"Context mismatch","concept":"Retrieved repair comes from a technically different environment with incompatible operational constraints","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a historically successful repair become a bad recommendation because of environment mismatch?","answer":"The same component or symptom can exist under different deployment models, resource limits, topology, versions, or ownership constraints. A repair that succeeded elsewhere may violate the current environment's assumptions.","context":"Historical knowledge contains a successful repair for a similar software component but a different deployment environment.","objective":"Filter historical repair knowledge by environment compatibility.","attempted_solution":"Reuse the repair because the application component and symptom appear identical.","failure":"The action conflicts with current deployment, resource, or operational constraints.","evidence":"Repair applicability depends on environment-specific constraints as well as component identity.","cause":"Environment metadata was omitted from retrieval matching.","root_cause":"Historical repair records did not enforce environment compatibility.","impact":"Cross-environment reuse creates avoidable failed repairs.","containment":"Treat environment-mismatched records as reference-only until compatibility is established.","remediation":"Capture deployment model, resource constraints, topology, operational controls, and environment assumptions in repair knowledge.","lessons":"Component similarity does not establish environment compatibility.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; environment-aware retrieval","related_records":["D53-B88-R02","D53-B89-R01"],"relationships":["environment-compatibility","retrieval-context-filtering"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B89-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Repair Knowledge-Retrieval Failures","subtopic":"Stale precedent","concept":"Retrieval prefers an older highly successful repair over a newer validated repair that supersedes it","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can older repair knowledge be unsafe even when its historical outcome was successful?","answer":"Later architecture, dependency, security, or operational changes can supersede the assumptions under which the old repair succeeded. Historical success remains factual but may no longer establish current applicability.","context":"Multiple repair records exist for similar symptoms across different system versions or architectures.","objective":"Prefer current applicable evidence while preserving older knowledge as historical context.","attempted_solution":"Select the repair with the highest historical success count regardless of version or recency.","failure":"The chosen repair is incompatible with current system behavior.","evidence":"Historical outcome quality must be combined with applicability, version, and supersession information.","cause":"Retrieval treated success history as stronger than current compatibility evidence.","root_cause":"No supersession or temporal applicability mechanism existed in retrieval.","impact":"Stale repair precedents can repeatedly generate invalid actions.","containment":"Require current-version compatibility before historical success can influence executable repair selection.","remediation":"Model knowledge validity intervals, superseding records, version applicability, and current evidence separately.","lessons":"Historical success is evidence of past validity, not automatic evidence of present validity.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; temporal knowledge validity","related_records":["D53-B88-R05","D53-B89-R01"],"relationships":["knowledge-supersession","temporal-applicability"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B89-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Repair Knowledge-Retrieval Failures","subtopic":"Incomplete retrieval evidence","concept":"Retrieved repair recommendation omits the failed attempts that explain when the repair should not be used","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can retrieving only successful repairs lead to incorrect repair selection?","answer":"Successful repairs describe conditions under which an action worked, while failed attempts can reveal boundaries, contraindications, and hidden prerequisites. Excluding negative evidence removes important applicability information.","context":"A retrieval system indexes successful repair records but does not connect them to relevant failed attempts.","objective":"Use both positive and negative repair evidence to determine applicability.","attempted_solution":"Select the most successful historical repair without examining related failed attempts.","failure":"The repair is applied in a condition previously shown to produce failure.","evidence":"Negative evidence can define the boundary of a repair's valid operating context.","cause":"Retrieval optimized for positive outcomes only.","root_cause":"Failed-attempt relationships were not part of repair knowledge retrieval.","impact":"The system loses contraindication knowledge and repeats known failure modes.","containment":"Require related failure evidence review for high-impact repair recommendations.","remediation":"Link successful repairs to relevant failed attempts, contraindications, preconditions, and context boundaries.","lessons":"Repair knowledge is stronger when success and failure evidence are connected.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; positive-negative knowledge integration","related_records":["D53-B51-R01","D53-B88-R04"],"relationships":["positive-negative-repair-knowledge","contraindication-retrieval"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B89-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Repair Knowledge-Retrieval Failures","subtopic":"Retrieval confidence","concept":"High retrieval similarity is mistaken for high repair confidence","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why should retrieval similarity and repair confidence remain separate?","answer":"A record can be highly similar in text or structure while its causal evidence is weak, outdated, contradictory, or contextually incomplete. Retrieval relevance measures matching evidence, not proof that the repair is correct.","context":"An automated system retrieves a historical repair with a high similarity score.","objective":"Prevent retrieval scores from being treated as causal or operational certainty.","attempted_solution":"Authorize the top retrieved repair whenever its similarity score exceeds a threshold.","failure":"A highly similar but weakly supported repair is executed without sufficient current evidence.","evidence":"Retrieval relevance, evidence quality, causal confidence, and action risk represent different dimensions.","cause":"One ranking score was overloaded to represent multiple decision properties.","root_cause":"Knowledge retrieval and repair authorization confidence were not separated.","impact":"High-ranking historical records can bypass appropriate uncertainty handling.","containment":"Require independent evidence and applicability checks before execution.","remediation":"Maintain separate retrieval relevance, evidence quality, causal confidence, applicability confidence, and action-risk fields.","lessons":"A good retrieval match is a candidate for reasoning, not an automatic repair decision.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; retrieval-confidence separation","related_records":["D53-B81-R05","D53-B89-R01"],"relationships":["retrieval-versus-confidence","candidate-not-decision"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B90-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Cross-Incident Generalization Failures","subtopic":"Mechanism mismatch","concept":"A repair that worked for one failure mechanism is generalized to a different mechanism with the same symptom","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a successful repair fail when transferred between incidents with similar symptoms?","answer":"Surface symptoms can be generated by different causal mechanisms. A repair tied to one mechanism may have no useful effect or harmful side effects when applied to another.","context":"Historical incidents show the same user-visible symptom but different diagnosed causes.","objective":"Generalize repair knowledge only when the relevant causal mechanism is sufficiently equivalent.","attempted_solution":"Reuse the historical repair based on matching symptom and component names.","failure":"The current incident has a different cause and the repair does not address it.","evidence":"Cross-incident transfer requires mechanism compatibility, not symptom similarity alone.","cause":"The generalization rule ignored causal mechanism identity.","root_cause":"Repair knowledge lacked mechanism-level applicability constraints.","impact":"Incorrect historical repairs are repeatedly selected for superficially similar incidents.","containment":"Require mechanism evidence before transferring a repair across incidents.","remediation":"Represent causal mechanism, preconditions, context, and contraindications as mandatory applicability features.","lessons":"Cross-incident reuse should generalize mechanisms and conditions, not merely symptoms.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; cross-incident causal generalization","related_records":["D53-B89-R01","D53-B88-R02"],"relationships":["mechanism-preserving-generalization","cross-incident-applicability"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B90-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Cross-Incident Generalization Failures","subtopic":"Scale mismatch","concept":"Repair knowledge from a small deployment is generalized to a larger fleet without validating scale-dependent effects","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a repair that worked at small scale fail when applied fleet-wide?","answer":"Scale can change resource contention, propagation speed, coordination cost, blast radius, and failure probability. A repair's mechanism may remain valid while its operational behavior changes materially at larger scale.","context":"A repair was successful on a small subset and is proposed for broad deployment.","objective":"Validate scale-dependent behavior before generalizing the repair.","attempted_solution":"Treat successful small-scope execution as evidence that fleet-wide execution will behave identically.","failure":"The broader rollout produces resource or coordination effects absent from the small test.","evidence":"Scale is an applicability dimension for operational repairs.","cause":"The learning record omitted population size and rollout conditions.","root_cause":"Repair generalization did not model scale-dependent effects.","impact":"A valid local repair can become a fleet-wide incident amplifier.","containment":"Use bounded rollout waves and observe each scale increase before continuing.","remediation":"Record scope size, rollout pattern, resource margins, coordination effects, and scale-specific outcomes.","lessons":"Repair validity can depend on the population size to which the action is applied.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; scale-aware generalization","related_records":["D53-B56-R04","D53-B94-R01"],"relationships":["scale-aware-repair","bounded-generalization"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B90-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Cross-Incident Generalization Failures","subtopic":"Different failure stage","concept":"A repair effective during detection-stage failure is reused during an execution-stage failure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can the same symptom require different repairs depending on the failure stage?","answer":"A symptom can arise because detection failed, diagnosis failed, execution failed, or outcome verification failed. An action that repairs one lifecycle stage may have no effect on another stage.","context":"Historical incidents share an error symptom but differ in where the engineering lifecycle broke.","objective":"Match repair knowledge to the failed lifecycle stage.","attempted_solution":"Select the historical repair based on symptom and component without identifying the failed lifecycle stage.","failure":"The action modifies system state but does not repair the actual lifecycle failure.","evidence":"Repair applicability includes the stage and failure mode in the engineering workflow.","cause":"Lifecycle stage was absent from retrieval and diagnosis context.","root_cause":"Knowledge representation collapsed distinct lifecycle failures into symptom-level categories.","impact":"Repeated repairs target the wrong subsystem or workflow stage.","containment":"Identify the failed lifecycle stage before applying historical repair patterns.","remediation":"Store lifecycle stage, failure mode, evidence boundary, and expected state transition with repair knowledge.","lessons":"Cross-incident generalization must preserve where in the engineering lifecycle the failure occurred.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; lifecycle-aware repair reasoning","related_records":["D53-B82-R01","D53-B86-R01"],"relationships":["lifecycle-stage-applicability","repair-stage-matching"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B90-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Cross-Incident Generalization Failures","subtopic":"Constraint mismatch","concept":"Historical repair is generalized despite incompatible current constraints","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a historically correct repair become invalid when current constraints differ?","answer":"A repair may depend on available capacity, maintenance windows, security controls, deployment permissions, downtime tolerance, or data-retention constraints. If current constraints differ, the same action may no longer be permissible or safe.","context":"A historical repair is technically applicable to the same component but current operational constraints have changed.","objective":"Evaluate repair applicability against current constraints before reuse.","attempted_solution":"Reuse the historical procedure because the underlying component and failure type match.","failure":"The repair violates current operational or safety constraints.","evidence":"Repair applicability includes current constraints, not only technical mechanism compatibility.","cause":"Historical context was treated as transferable without constraint comparison.","root_cause":"Constraint metadata was not represented as a required generalization boundary.","impact":"Correct historical knowledge produces an invalid current action.","containment":"Reject direct reuse and search for alternatives satisfying current constraints.","remediation":"Compare historical and current constraints explicitly before applying reusable repair knowledge.","lessons":"Technical similarity cannot override changed operational constraints.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; constraint-aware generalization","related_records":["D53-B89-R02","D53-B82-R04"],"relationships":["constraint-aware-generalization","current-context-validation"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B90-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Cross-Incident Generalization Failures","subtopic":"Insufficient precedent","concept":"Novel incident is forced into an existing repair pattern despite weak contextual evidence","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can forcing a novel incident into the nearest historical repair pattern fail?","answer":"When no precedent sufficiently matches the current mechanism, context, or constraints, selecting the nearest historical pattern can create false confidence. Novelty should increase uncertainty rather than disappear through forced categorization.","context":"A new failure has partial similarity to several historical incidents but no strong precedent.","objective":"Handle insufficient precedent explicitly instead of manufacturing applicability.","attempted_solution":"Choose the closest historical repair and treat it as the expected solution.","failure":"The historical action fails because the novel mechanism is materially different.","evidence":"Absence of a sufficiently matching precedent is itself information that should affect confidence and action selection.","cause":"Retrieval completeness was prioritized over applicability confidence.","root_cause":"No explicit unknown or insufficient-precedent state existed in repair reasoning.","impact":"Novel failures are exposed to inappropriate historical actions.","containment":"Prefer observation, low-impact reversible diagnostics, or narrowly scoped experiments while preserving uncertainty.","remediation":"Represent insufficient precedent explicitly, lower repair confidence, identify discriminating evidence, and prevent forced historical reuse without applicability support.","lessons":"A knowledge system must be able to say that existing precedent is insufficient without inventing certainty.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; novel-failure uncertainty handling","related_records":["D53-B81-R05","D53-B89-R05"],"relationships":["insufficient-precedent","unknown-state-handling"]}

{"record_id":"D53-B91-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Multi-Component Repair Failures","subtopic":"Interacting repairs","concept":"Individually valid repairs interact and invalidate assumptions made by another repair","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can two correct repairs produce a failed combined recovery?","answer":"Each repair can be locally valid while changing state, capacity, configuration, or dependencies required by the other. Combined correctness therefore requires reasoning about interactions between repairs rather than validating each action independently.","context":"Multiple components are degraded and separate repair actions are planned concurrently or sequentially.","objective":"Ensure multi-component repair plans remain valid when component changes interact.","attempted_solution":"Validate each repair independently and execute all approved actions.","failure":"One repair changes an assumption required by another, leaving the overall system inconsistent.","evidence":"Multi-component recovery requires explicit modeling of shared dependencies, state transitions, and interaction effects.","cause":"Component-level correctness was treated as sufficient for system-level correctness.","root_cause":"The repair plan lacked a combined state and dependency model.","impact":"The system can enter a partially recovered or newly inconsistent state.","containment":"Serialize interacting repairs and verify shared invariants between steps.","remediation":"Construct a cross-component dependency graph, define interaction constraints, and verify system-level invariants after each critical repair.","lessons":"Independent repair validity does not establish combined repair validity.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; multi-component repair reasoning","related_records":["D53-B83-R05","D53-B85-R01"],"relationships":["cross-component-dependency","combined-repair-correctness"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B91-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Multi-Component Repair Failures","subtopic":"Dependency-chain recovery","concept":"Repair restores a downstream component before the upstream dependency has reached a compatible state","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can repairing components in the wrong dependency direction cause repeated failure?","answer":"A downstream component can immediately fail again when its upstream dependency remains unavailable, incompatible, or partially repaired. The downstream repair may be technically correct but operationally premature.","context":"A dependency chain contains multiple degraded components requiring coordinated recovery.","objective":"Align repair ordering with dependency readiness and required state transitions.","attempted_solution":"Repair the most visible failing component first regardless of dependency order.","failure":"The component returns to the same failure state because its dependency remains unresolved.","evidence":"Dependency readiness is a prerequisite for durable downstream recovery.","cause":"Repair order followed symptom visibility rather than dependency structure.","root_cause":"The recovery plan did not encode dependency direction.","impact":"Repeated repair cycles consume resources without advancing recovery.","containment":"Pause downstream recovery until required upstream postconditions are verified.","remediation":"Model dependency direction and require upstream readiness before downstream repair.","lessons":"The most visible failure is not necessarily the correct first repair target.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; dependency-aware recovery","related_records":["D53-B83-R02","D53-B91-R01"],"relationships":["dependency-ordering","durable-recovery"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B91-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Multi-Component Repair Failures","subtopic":"Shared-state conflict","concept":"Repairs performed by different workflows write incompatible values to shared state","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can concurrent repair workflows undo each other?","answer":"Two repairs may independently believe they own the same configuration, routing state, metadata, or shared resource. Without ownership and concurrency rules, the later mutation can overwrite the earlier repair or create an invalid combination.","context":"Multiple automated or operator-driven repairs affect a shared state object.","objective":"Prevent conflicting repair ownership and state overwrites.","attempted_solution":"Allow each repair workflow to update the shared state independently.","failure":"One repair overwrites or invalidates another repair's state change.","evidence":"Shared mutable state requires ownership, concurrency, or reconciliation semantics.","cause":"Repair actions lacked shared-state coordination.","root_cause":"No authoritative state ownership or conflict-resolution mechanism existed.","impact":"Recovery becomes nondeterministic and difficult to audit.","containment":"Pause conflicting mutations and establish a single authoritative repair state.","remediation":"Use state versioning, ownership boundaries, conflict detection, and explicit reconciliation before applying competing changes.","lessons":"Shared repair targets require coordination even when the individual repairs are valid.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; shared-state repair coordination","related_records":["D53-B52-R02","D53-B96-R01"],"relationships":["shared-state-conflict","repair-coordination"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B91-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Multi-Component Repair Failures","subtopic":"Cross-component verification","concept":"Each component passes local verification while the integrated workflow remains broken","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_GAP","question":"Why can all component-level repair checks pass while the overall repair fails?","answer":"Integration behavior can depend on contracts, ordering, data transformations, timing, and dependency state that local checks do not exercise. System-level verification is therefore required for coordinated repairs.","context":"Several components have individually passed post-repair health checks.","objective":"Verify the combined behavior of repaired components.","attempted_solution":"Close recovery after every component reports healthy.","failure":"The end-to-end workflow remains unavailable or incorrect.","evidence":"Local health does not establish correctness of cross-component interactions.","cause":"Verification stopped at component boundaries.","root_cause":"No integrated postcondition was defined for the multi-component repair.","impact":"Residual system failure is mistaken for successful recovery.","containment":"Keep recovery provisional until critical integrated workflows pass.","remediation":"Define cross-component invariants and end-to-end verification for affected workflows.","lessons":"Multi-component repair requires both local and integrated verification.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; integrated repair verification","related_records":["D53-B86-R04","D53-B91-R01"],"relationships":["integrated-verification","cross-component-invariant"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B91-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Multi-Component Repair Failures","subtopic":"Repair transaction boundary","concept":"A multi-component repair assumes atomicity across systems that cannot actually provide a shared transaction","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is assuming atomicity across independent components dangerous?","answer":"Independent systems may commit changes at different times and may not support a common rollback or transaction boundary. A failure between commits can leave a mixed state that requires explicit compensation.","context":"A recovery plan modifies multiple systems and treats the sequence as one conceptual transaction.","objective":"Represent distributed repair execution according to actual atomicity guarantees.","attempted_solution":"Assume all component updates either succeed together or can be rolled back together.","failure":"One component commits while another fails, leaving inconsistent system state.","evidence":"Transaction guarantees must reflect actual system capabilities rather than conceptual grouping.","cause":"Logical repair grouping was mistaken for technical atomicity.","root_cause":"The plan lacked explicit distributed commit and compensation semantics.","impact":"Partial recovery becomes difficult to reconcile.","containment":"Track each component's committed state and stop further dependent actions after divergence.","remediation":"Use explicit checkpoints, compensating actions, reconciliation, and component-level completion states.","lessons":"A multi-system repair is not atomic merely because the planner treats it as one operation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; distributed repair transaction reasoning","related_records":["D53-B85-R01","D53-B83-R04"],"relationships":["distributed-atomicity","compensating-repair"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B92-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Multi-Region / Multi-Zone Repair Failures","subtopic":"Asymmetric recovery","concept":"Repair succeeds in one region while another region retains incompatible state","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can regional asymmetry make a locally successful repair fail globally?","answer":"Regions can have different configuration, dependency state, data versions, or repair progress. A repair validated in one region does not automatically establish equivalent recovery elsewhere.","context":"A distributed service operates across multiple regions or zones with partially independent state.","objective":"Detect and manage asymmetric recovery across deployment locations.","attempted_solution":"Treat one healthy region as evidence that the same repair completed globally.","failure":"Traffic or dependencies reach an unrepaired region with incompatible state.","evidence":"Distributed recovery requires location-aware state and verification.","cause":"Regional state was assumed to be uniform.","root_cause":"Repair tracking did not represent per-region or per-zone completion state.","impact":"Recovery appears healthy under one traffic distribution but fails under another.","containment":"Track regional states separately and control traffic according to verified readiness.","remediation":"Use per-region repair state, location-specific verification, and explicit convergence criteria.","lessons":"Global recovery cannot be inferred from a single healthy location.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; distributed recovery reasoning","related_records":["D53-B85-R04","D53-B92-R02"],"relationships":["regional-asymmetry","location-aware-verification"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B92-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Multi-Region / Multi-Zone Repair Failures","subtopic":"Recovery ordering","concept":"Regional recovery begins before shared dependencies or data synchronization are ready","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can recovering a region too early create a second failure?","answer":"A region may require synchronized data, routing, identity, configuration, or external dependencies before accepting normal workload. Early activation can expose incomplete state to production traffic.","context":"A regional outage requires controlled reactivation after infrastructure or dependency repair.","objective":"Require regional readiness conditions before restoring normal traffic.","attempted_solution":"Return the region to service as soon as local infrastructure becomes healthy.","failure":"Traffic reaches incomplete dependencies or stale state and generates new errors.","evidence":"Regional activation should depend on readiness of all critical prerequisites.","cause":"Local infrastructure health was treated as equivalent to regional service readiness.","root_cause":"No multi-layer regional readiness contract existed.","impact":"Recovery introduces additional user-facing failures.","containment":"Keep the region isolated until dependency and data readiness are verified.","remediation":"Define activation gates covering infrastructure, data, dependencies, configuration, and functional workflows.","lessons":"Infrastructure recovery and service recovery are distinct milestones.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; regional activation gates","related_records":["D53-B86-R04","D53-B92-R01"],"relationships":["regional-readiness","activation-gating"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B92-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Multi-Region / Multi-Zone Repair Failures","subtopic":"Traffic restoration","concept":"Traffic is restored faster than repaired regional capacity can safely absorb","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can traffic restoration invalidate an otherwise successful regional repair?","answer":"A repaired region may be healthy at low load but lack the capacity or dependency readiness required for full traffic. Rapid restoration can recreate the original failure through load concentration.","context":"A region has recovered after partial outage and traffic must be shifted back from alternate locations.","objective":"Match traffic restoration rate to verified regional capacity and stability.","attempted_solution":"Restore the full traffic share immediately after basic health checks pass.","failure":"The recovered region becomes overloaded or exposes an unready dependency.","evidence":"Traffic restoration is itself an operational change requiring capacity and stability verification.","cause":"Recovery readiness was evaluated without expected restored traffic load.","root_cause":"Traffic ramp was not included in the repair plan.","impact":"Recovery causes a secondary overload or repeat incident.","containment":"Use staged traffic restoration with observation gates.","remediation":"Define load-aware readiness checks, ramp limits, and rollback criteria for traffic restoration.","lessons":"Returning capacity to service is part of the repair, not merely an afterthought.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; staged recovery","related_records":["D53-B56-R03","D53-B92-R02"],"relationships":["traffic-ramp","capacity-aware-recovery"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B92-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Multi-Region / Multi-Zone Repair Failures","subtopic":"Zone dependency","concept":"Repair assumes zones are independent even though a hidden shared dependency couples their failure and recovery","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can zone-by-zone repair fail when the zones share an external dependency?","answer":"Independent deployment locations can still depend on shared control planes, identity systems, storage, networking, or configuration services. Repairing zones independently can therefore leave the common dependency unresolved.","context":"Multiple zones show similar failures and are treated as isolated repair targets.","objective":"Identify shared dependencies before declaring location-level independence.","attempted_solution":"Apply the same repair independently to each affected zone.","failure":"All repaired zones continue failing because the shared dependency remains unavailable.","evidence":"Failure correlation across locations can indicate a common dependency rather than independent local faults.","cause":"Topology analysis stopped at zone boundaries.","root_cause":"Shared dependency relationships were absent from the repair model.","impact":"Repeated local repairs waste recovery capacity without addressing the common cause.","containment":"Pause repeated local mutations and investigate shared dependencies.","remediation":"Represent cross-zone dependency topology and test common-cause hypotheses before repeated repair.","lessons":"Physical or logical separation does not guarantee failure independence.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; shared-dependency analysis","related_records":["D53-B81-R04","D53-B92-R01"],"relationships":["common-cause-analysis","cross-zone-dependency"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B92-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Multi-Region / Multi-Zone Repair Failures","subtopic":"State convergence","concept":"Repair completion is declared before regional state convergence is established","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_GAP","question":"Why can asynchronous regional convergence make repair verification premature?","answer":"A repair may be accepted locally while replicas, control-plane state, routing, or configuration remain divergent elsewhere. Declaring completion before convergence can hide future inconsistency.","context":"Recovery relies on asynchronous propagation across regions or zones.","objective":"Verify the required convergence level before closing the repair.","attempted_solution":"Close the repair when the initiating region reports the desired state.","failure":"Another region later receives stale or conflicting state.","evidence":"Distributed repair completion must account for propagation and convergence requirements.","cause":"Local state was used as the completion criterion.","root_cause":"No convergence postcondition was defined.","impact":"Delayed divergence causes recurring or intermittent failures.","containment":"Keep repair status open or provisional until required convergence evidence exists.","remediation":"Define convergence targets, propagation observation, and conflict checks as part of repair verification.","lessons":"In distributed systems, local completion and global convergence are different states.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; distributed convergence verification","related_records":["D53-B63-R01","D53-B86-R05"],"relationships":["state-convergence","distributed-verification"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B93-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Multi-Tenant Repair Failures","subtopic":"Tenant scope","concept":"A repair intended for one tenant changes shared or tenant-specific state outside the authorized boundary","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can tenant-specific repair create cross-tenant impact?","answer":"Shared infrastructure, configuration inheritance, resource identifiers, or overly broad mutation scopes can cause a tenant-local repair to affect other tenants.","context":"A multi-tenant platform contains both tenant-specific and shared resources.","objective":"Maintain strict tenant boundaries during repair execution.","attempted_solution":"Use a broad resource selector to simplify the tenant repair.","failure":"Resources belonging to other tenants are modified or affected.","evidence":"Tenant scope must be explicit in target identification and mutation authorization.","cause":"Repair targeting did not enforce tenant identity at mutation time.","root_cause":"Tenant boundary was represented only in operator intent rather than execution predicates.","impact":"Cross-tenant disruption or isolation violations can occur.","containment":"Stop mutation and reconcile affected resources by tenant identity.","remediation":"Require tenant-scoped target resolution, authorization, and post-mutation verification.","lessons":"Tenant scope must be enforced at execution time, not merely documented in the repair request.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; tenant-bound repair governance","related_records":["D53-B75-R01","D53-B84-R01"],"relationships":["tenant-isolation","execution-time-scope-enforcement"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B93-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Multi-Tenant Repair Failures","subtopic":"Heterogeneous configuration","concept":"A repair assumes tenants share configuration semantics that differ across tenant-specific settings","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a valid tenant repair fail when copied to another tenant?","answer":"Tenants may use different versions, feature flags, policies, resource limits, or integration settings. A shared repair procedure can therefore be valid for one tenant but invalid for another.","context":"Multiple tenants exhibit similar symptoms but have heterogeneous configuration.","objective":"Preserve tenant-specific applicability conditions during repair reuse.","attempted_solution":"Apply the successful repair from one tenant to all tenants showing the same symptom.","failure":"The repair conflicts with another tenant's configuration or dependencies.","evidence":"Symptom similarity does not establish configuration equivalence.","cause":"Tenant configuration differences were excluded from applicability matching.","root_cause":"Repair knowledge did not encode tenant-specific constraints.","impact":"Cross-tenant reuse creates avoidable repair failures.","containment":"Validate tenant configuration before reusing the historical repair.","remediation":"Include tenant configuration, version, policies, and dependencies in repair applicability checks.","lessons":"Multi-tenant repair knowledge must preserve configuration heterogeneity.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; heterogeneous tenant context","related_records":["D53-B90-R04","D53-B93-R01"],"relationships":["tenant-context","configuration-aware-reuse"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B93-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Multi-Tenant Repair Failures","subtopic":"Shared-resource side effects","concept":"Tenant repair changes a shared resource whose behavior affects other tenants","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a tenant-local repair create indirect impact on unrelated tenants?","answer":"A tenant may consume a shared pool, shared configuration, or shared dependency. Changing that shared resource to resolve one tenant's issue can alter capacity or behavior for other tenants.","context":"Tenant-specific repair requires changing a shared infrastructure resource.","objective":"Evaluate shared-resource consequences before tenant-local remediation.","attempted_solution":"Modify the shared resource to satisfy the affected tenant's requirements.","failure":"Other tenants experience changed behavior or reduced resource availability.","evidence":"Tenant-local objectives can have shared-resource effects.","cause":"Repair scope was defined by direct target ownership rather than resource sharing.","root_cause":"Shared-resource dependency was absent from impact analysis.","impact":"A single-tenant repair creates multi-tenant degradation.","containment":"Use tenant-specific isolation where possible and monitor shared-resource effects.","remediation":"Model resource sharing and evaluate cross-tenant blast radius before changing shared resources.","lessons":"Tenant scope does not eliminate shared-resource impact.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; shared-resource impact analysis","related_records":["D53-B91-R03","D53-B93-R01"],"relationships":["shared-resource-impact","cross-tenant-blast-radius"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B93-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Multi-Tenant Repair Failures","subtopic":"Tenant-specific verification","concept":"Aggregate platform recovery hides residual failure affecting a small tenant subset","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_GAP","question":"Why can aggregate health metrics falsely verify a multi-tenant repair?","answer":"A platform-wide metric can appear healthy while one or more tenants remain degraded. Tenant-level residual failures can be masked by aggregation.","context":"A repair affects a subset of tenants and platform-wide health returns to normal.","objective":"Verify tenant-level outcomes when impact is tenant-specific.","attempted_solution":"Use aggregate platform health as the repair success criterion.","failure":"Affected tenants continue experiencing degraded behavior despite healthy aggregate metrics.","evidence":"Verification granularity must match the scope of the repaired objective.","cause":"Aggregate measurement hid tenant-specific residual impact.","root_cause":"Tenant-level verification was absent from the closure criteria.","impact":"Partial recovery is classified as complete.","containment":"Keep affected tenants under explicit verification until their required postconditions pass.","remediation":"Measure repair outcomes by affected tenant or relevant tenant cohort in addition to aggregate metrics.","lessons":"Verification scope must not be broader than the failure's meaningful granularity.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; tenant-granular verification","related_records":["D53-B87-R04","D53-B86-R02"],"relationships":["tenant-level-verification","aggregate-metric-limitation"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B93-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Multi-Tenant Repair Failures","subtopic":"Tenant identity drift","concept":"Tenant identity changes between diagnosis and execution and the repair targets the wrong tenant context","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why must tenant identity be revalidated before executing a delayed repair?","answer":"Dynamic resource resolution, reassignment, migration, or stale identifiers can cause a previously correct tenant target to resolve differently at execution time.","context":"A tenant-specific repair is approved but execution occurs after resource or tenant mappings change.","objective":"Ensure target tenant identity remains valid at mutation time.","attempted_solution":"Trust the tenant identifier captured during initial diagnosis.","failure":"The repair affects a resource now associated with a different tenant or scope.","evidence":"Tenant identity is a runtime authorization and targeting predicate, not merely historical metadata.","cause":"Target identity was validated only at diagnosis time.","root_cause":"Execution lacked current tenant-resolution verification.","impact":"Wrong-tenant modification or cross-tenant impact can occur.","containment":"Stop execution when tenant-resource mapping differs from the approved state.","remediation":"Resolve and verify tenant identity immediately before mutation and bind it to the authorization context.","lessons":"Tenant targeting must remain stable from approval through execution.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; runtime tenant identity verification","related_records":["D53-B75-R05","D53-B84-R02"],"relationships":["tenant-identity-revalidation","authorization-target-binding"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B94-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Large-Scale Fleet Repair Failures","subtopic":"Blast radius","concept":"Fleet-wide repair is applied before validating behavior on a representative bounded population","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can immediate fleet-wide repair amplify a correctable defect?","answer":"A flawed assumption or implementation can affect the entire population before evidence is available to detect it. Bounded rollout provides an opportunity to observe behavior and stop expansion.","context":"Thousands of heterogeneous nodes require the same repair.","objective":"Control fleet-wide repair risk through staged deployment and observation.","attempted_solution":"Apply the repair to the entire fleet to minimize total recovery time.","failure":"A repair defect propagates across the fleet before detection.","evidence":"Rollout scope directly controls potential blast radius.","cause":"Speed was optimized without a bounded learning stage.","root_cause":"The repair plan lacked progressive rollout gates.","impact":"A local repair error becomes a fleet-wide incident.","containment":"Stop expansion and isolate completed waves when adverse signals appear.","remediation":"Use canary or staged waves with explicit promotion and rollback criteria.","lessons":"Fleet scale should increase verification discipline, not reduce it.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; progressive repair rollout","related_records":["D53-B56-R04","D53-B90-R02"],"relationships":["blast-radius-control","progressive-rollout"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B94-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Large-Scale Fleet Repair Failures","subtopic":"Heterogeneous fleet","concept":"Fleet repair assumes node homogeneity despite meaningful version or configuration differences","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a repair work on most nodes but fail on a heterogeneous subset?","answer":"Nodes can differ in software version, hardware capability, configuration, dependencies, or workload. A repair validated on the dominant population may violate assumptions of a smaller cohort.","context":"A fleet contains multiple supported versions or hardware profiles.","objective":"Partition fleet repair by meaningful compatibility classes.","attempted_solution":"Apply one repair procedure to all nodes based on majority-population validation.","failure":"A minority configuration class enters an invalid state.","evidence":"Fleet repair applicability should be evaluated against node-specific compatibility attributes.","cause":"Fleet diversity was treated as negligible.","root_cause":"Repair targeting lacked compatibility classification.","impact":"A minority subset becomes a residual failure population.","containment":"Pause affected cohort and exclude incompatible nodes from further rollout.","remediation":"Group nodes by relevant version, configuration, dependency, and capability attributes before repair.","lessons":"Fleet-wide scope does not imply fleet-wide equivalence.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; heterogeneous fleet repair","related_records":["D53-B66-R02","D53-B90-R02"],"relationships":["fleet-segmentation","compatibility-classification"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B94-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Large-Scale Fleet Repair Failures","subtopic":"Rollout wave contamination","concept":"A failed repair wave continues expanding because health signals are delayed or incorrectly aggregated","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can delayed fleet telemetry cause an unsafe repair wave to continue?","answer":"If monitoring lags execution or aggregates away affected nodes, promotion decisions may occur before failures become visible. The rollout controller can therefore expand a defective repair.","context":"Fleet repairs are executed in waves and promotion depends on automated health signals.","objective":"Ensure rollout promotion reflects timely and correctly scoped evidence.","attempted_solution":"Promote each wave immediately after aggregate health remains within threshold for a short interval.","failure":"Delayed failures emerge after subsequent waves have already started.","evidence":"Promotion criteria require appropriate observation delay and population-level visibility.","cause":"Telemetry latency and aggregation behavior were not incorporated into rollout timing.","root_cause":"Promotion gates lacked evidence freshness requirements.","impact":"Repair defects propagate across multiple waves.","containment":"Freeze promotion when telemetry freshness or population coverage is insufficient.","remediation":"Add telemetry freshness, cohort coverage, delayed-effect windows, and explicit stop criteria to wave promotion.","lessons":"A rollout gate is only as reliable as the evidence timing and population it observes.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; rollout evidence freshness","related_records":["D53-B86-R05","D53-B94-R01"],"relationships":["rollout-gating","telemetry-freshness"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B94-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Large-Scale Fleet Repair Failures","subtopic":"Recovery capacity","concept":"Repair consumes shared fleet capacity faster than the system can recover from repair-induced failures","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can fleet repair overwhelm its own recovery mechanisms?","answer":"Large-scale remediation can consume control-plane capacity, connection limits, scheduling capacity, or operator attention. If repair throughput exceeds recovery capacity, failures can accumulate faster than they are resolved.","context":"A large population requires coordinated repair through shared orchestration infrastructure.","objective":"Keep repair concurrency within the capacity of the execution and recovery system.","attempted_solution":"Maximize parallel repair throughput to shorten total repair time.","failure":"The repair infrastructure becomes saturated and recovery progress degrades.","evidence":"Repair execution itself consumes resources and must be capacity-controlled.","cause":"Repair workload was excluded from capacity planning.","root_cause":"No repair-rate or concurrency budget existed.","impact":"The recovery system becomes part of the incident.","containment":"Throttle or pause repair concurrency and preserve capacity for observation and recovery.","remediation":"Define repair concurrency budgets, backpressure, priority classes, and recovery-capacity reserves.","lessons":"Repair capacity is a resource that must be governed during large incidents.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; recovery-capacity governance","related_records":["D53-B56-R03","D53-B94-R01"],"relationships":["repair-capacity","concurrency-governance"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B94-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Large-Scale Fleet Repair Failures","subtopic":"Wave rollback","concept":"Rollback of a fleet repair is initiated globally without distinguishing successful and failed rollout cohorts","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can global rollback worsen a heterogeneous fleet incident?","answer":"Different rollout waves can have different states. Rolling back every node with one procedure can reverse successful repairs, repeat failures on incompatible nodes, or create new mixed states.","context":"A fleet repair is stopped after adverse results appear in some rollout waves.","objective":"Rollback only the state transitions that require compensation.","attempted_solution":"Apply one global rollback operation to the entire fleet.","failure":"Healthy repaired nodes are unnecessarily reverted while failed nodes require different remediation.","evidence":"Rollback scope must correspond to actual execution and resulting state by cohort.","cause":"Rollback was planned at fleet level rather than execution-state level.","root_cause":"The repair ledger lacked cohort-specific rollback state.","impact":"Recovery becomes more complex and previously healthy nodes can regress.","containment":"Freeze rollout and classify nodes by actual repair state before rollback.","remediation":"Maintain per-wave execution state and use state-aware compensation or forward repair where appropriate.","lessons":"Large-scale rollback must be state-aware, not merely scope-aware.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; state-aware fleet rollback","related_records":["D53-B97-R02","D53-B94-R03"],"relationships":["cohort-aware-rollback","execution-state-classification"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B95-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Human-in-the-Loop Repair Failures","subtopic":"Approval misunderstanding","concept":"Operator approves a repair after misunderstanding the intended effect or scope","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can human approval fail even when the approval step itself is present?","answer":"A human can approve an action without fully understanding its scope, expected state change, assumptions, or side effects. The presence of approval does not prove that the decision was informed.","context":"A high-impact repair requires human approval before execution.","objective":"Make approval information sufficient for accurate human decision-making.","attempted_solution":"Present the repair command and request a binary approval.","failure":"The operator approves an action whose actual effect differs from their interpretation.","evidence":"Effective approval requires understandable intent, scope, expected effect, risk, and evidence.","cause":"Approval interface exposed implementation details without sufficient decision context.","root_cause":"Governance treated approval as a checkbox rather than an informed decision.","impact":"Human authorization can legitimize an incorrectly understood repair.","containment":"Pause execution when approver understanding or scope confirmation is uncertain.","remediation":"Present structured intent, target scope, evidence, expected changes, risks, rollback limits, and verification criteria.","lessons":"Human approval is meaningful only when the approver can evaluate what is actually being authorized.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; human-in-the-loop governance","related_records":["D53-B84-R05","D53-B95-R02"],"relationships":["informed-approval","human-governance"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B95-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Human-in-the-Loop Repair Failures","subtopic":"Handoff gaps","concept":"Repair responsibility transfers between operators without transferring current state, assumptions, and unresolved risks","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can operator handoffs cause a previously sound repair plan to fail?","answer":"The receiving operator may lack the evidence, current state, pending actions, or known uncertainty held by the previous operator. Reconstructing context from memory can introduce incorrect assumptions.","context":"A long-running incident crosses shifts or operator teams.","objective":"Preserve repair context and decision state across human handoffs.","attempted_solution":"Transfer only the current repair status and next command.","failure":"The new operator repeats an already attempted action or misses an unresolved constraint.","evidence":"Repair continuity requires state, evidence, decisions, pending obligations, and uncertainty.","cause":"Handoff compressed operational context into a status summary.","root_cause":"No structured repair handoff record existed.","impact":"Context loss causes duplicate work and invalid decisions.","containment":"Pause nontrivial actions until current state and pending obligations are reconciled.","remediation":"Maintain structured handoff records containing state, evidence, attempts, failures, approvals, assumptions, and next safe actions.","lessons":"Operational continuity requires transfer of reasoning state, not just task status.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; repair-state continuity","related_records":["D53-B81-R02","D53-B85-R02"],"relationships":["operator-handoff","reasoning-state-continuity"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B95-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Human-in-the-Loop Repair Failures","subtopic":"Override ambiguity","concept":"Human override changes a repair plan without recording which original assumptions remain valid","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a human override create an inconsistent repair state?","answer":"An operator may modify one action while leaving dependent actions, approvals, verification criteria, or rollback assumptions unchanged. The resulting plan may no longer be internally coherent.","context":"An operator manually changes an automated repair recommendation during execution.","objective":"Preserve plan consistency when human modifications occur.","attempted_solution":"Allow individual action edits without recomputing dependent plan metadata.","failure":"The modified action conflicts with subsequent steps or verification expectations.","evidence":"Changing a repair action can invalidate dependent assumptions and governance artifacts.","cause":"Override was treated as a local edit rather than a plan mutation.","root_cause":"No dependency-aware plan revalidation existed after human override.","impact":"Execution can proceed using a mixed old/new repair plan.","containment":"Recompute affected dependencies and require reapproval where material scope or risk changes.","remediation":"Version plan mutations, identify affected dependencies, revalidate postconditions, and refresh authorization when necessary.","lessons":"Human override is itself a state transition requiring validation and governance.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; human override governance","related_records":["D53-B84-R02","D53-B83-R02"],"relationships":["override-revalidation","plan-versioning"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B95-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Human-in-the-Loop Repair Failures","subtopic":"Manual evidence interpretation","concept":"Operator interprets ambiguous diagnostic evidence as definitive and approves an action","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can human interpretation amplify diagnostic uncertainty?","answer":"Ambiguous telemetry may support multiple explanations. If the operator resolves ambiguity informally without recording alternatives or confidence, the repair system can inherit an unsupported assumption as if it were fact.","context":"An operator must make a repair decision from incomplete or conflicting evidence.","objective":"Preserve uncertainty when human judgment resolves incomplete evidence.","attempted_solution":"Use operator judgment to select one explanation without recording alternatives.","failure":"The selected repair is based on an undocumented assumption that later proves incorrect.","evidence":"Human reasoning should remain traceable when it materially affects repair selection.","cause":"Interpretive judgment was recorded as a conclusion without its evidentiary basis.","root_cause":"No structured representation for human diagnostic reasoning existed.","impact":"Future review cannot distinguish evidence from interpretation.","containment":"Record uncertainty and alternative hypotheses before high-impact action.","remediation":"Capture human rationale, supporting evidence, contradictions, confidence, and rejected alternatives.","lessons":"Human judgment is valuable but should remain distinguishable from observed evidence.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; traceable human reasoning","related_records":["D53-B81-R02","D53-B95-R01"],"relationships":["human-reasoning-traceability","evidence-versus-interpretation"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B95-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Human-in-the-Loop Repair Failures","subtopic":"Approval latency","concept":"Delayed human approval causes repair assumptions to become stale before execution","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can approval delay itself create a failed repair?","answer":"During a changing incident, system state can evolve while approval is pending. A once-valid target, diagnosis, or scope may no longer describe the current condition when approval arrives.","context":"A repair requires human approval but the incident continues evolving during the approval interval.","objective":"Preserve decision validity across approval latency.","attempted_solution":"Execute immediately after approval without rechecking material assumptions.","failure":"The approved action no longer matches current system state.","evidence":"Dynamic incidents require revalidation after material delays.","cause":"Approval time was not treated as part of decision validity.","root_cause":"No approval-age or state-change revalidation rule existed.","impact":"Stale approved actions can create new failures.","containment":"Revalidate target, evidence, scope, and preconditions after significant approval delay.","remediation":"Define approval freshness thresholds and state-change triggers for mandatory reapproval.","lessons":"Authorization latency can change the action being authorized in practical terms.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; approval freshness","related_records":["D53-B84-R02","D53-B82-R04"],"relationships":["approval-latency","state-change-revalidation"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B96-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Automation-vs-Operator Conflict","subtopic":"Concurrent remediation","concept":"Automated and manual repairs modify the same target concurrently","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can concurrent human and automated remediation cause a repair failure?","answer":"Both actors may operate from different snapshots and assumptions. One can overwrite, invalidate, or duplicate the other's action, creating conflicting state and ambiguous execution ownership.","context":"An automated repair workflow remains active while an operator begins manual remediation.","objective":"Coordinate authority and execution ownership between automated and human remediation.","attempted_solution":"Allow the operator to intervene without pausing automation.","failure":"Automation and manual repair issue conflicting mutations against the same target.","evidence":"Concurrent repair authority requires explicit ownership and coordination semantics.","cause":"Manual intervention did not acquire exclusive or coordinated repair control.","root_cause":"The system lacked an automation-versus-operator conflict protocol.","impact":"Repair outcomes become nondeterministic and difficult to attribute.","containment":"Pause one execution path and reconcile current state before continuing.","remediation":"Implement explicit repair ownership, locking or coordination, state reconciliation, and authority transition rules.","lessons":"Human intervention must be modeled as a controlled transition of repair authority, not an unrelated parallel process.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; coordinated repair authority","related_records":["D53-B52-R03","D53-B95-R03"],"relationships":["automation-human-coordination","repair-authority-transfer"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B96-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Automation-vs-Operator Conflict","subtopic":"Stale automation","concept":"Operator changes system state while an automated repair continues using an outdated plan","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can an automated repair become unsafe after manual intervention?","answer":"Manual changes can alter the state assumed by the automated plan. If automation does not detect the state transition, it may execute actions that were valid only before the manual change.","context":"An operator manually changes configuration while an automated workflow remains queued.","objective":"Invalidate or replan automation after material external state changes.","attempted_solution":"Allow queued automation to continue because its original approval remains valid.","failure":"Automation applies an action against state that no longer matches its plan assumptions.","evidence":"External state changes can invalidate queued repair plans.","cause":"Automation treated its original plan snapshot as authoritative indefinitely.","root_cause":"No external-mutation detection or plan invalidation mechanism existed.","impact":"Automated remediation can undo or conflict with manual recovery.","containment":"Suspend automation when material external mutations are detected.","remediation":"Use state versions, change detection, plan invalidation, and revalidation before resumed execution.","lessons":"Repair plans must react to external mutations rather than assuming exclusive control of state.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; plan invalidation","related_records":["D53-B82-R04","D53-B96-R01"],"relationships":["plan-invalidation","external-mutation-detection"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B96-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Automation-vs-Operator Conflict","subtopic":"Authority conflict","concept":"Human and automated actors hold incompatible authority assumptions over the same repair target","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can unclear authority cause two valid actors to make conflicting repair decisions?","answer":"If both automation and operators believe they are authorized to change the same target, each may legitimately execute according to different decisions. The failure is governance ambiguity rather than necessarily an implementation defect.","context":"A repair target can be modified by both an automated agent and an operator.","objective":"Establish one authoritative decision path or explicit coordination rules.","attempted_solution":"Permit both actors to execute whenever their individual authorization checks pass.","failure":"Both actions execute despite being mutually incompatible.","evidence":"Authorization must include concurrency and authority ownership for shared repair targets.","cause":"Authorization was evaluated per actor without considering concurrent authority.","root_cause":"No single-writer or coordination rule existed for the repair target.","impact":"Conflicting repairs create inconsistent state and uncertain responsibility.","containment":"Freeze competing actions and establish current repair authority.","remediation":"Model authority ownership, precedence, handoff, and conflict resolution explicitly.","lessons":"Independent authorization is insufficient when multiple actors can mutate the same state.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; multi-actor repair governance","related_records":["D53-B84-R04","D53-B96-R01"],"relationships":["authority-conflict","single-writer-governance"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B96-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Automation-vs-Operator Conflict","subtopic":"Conflicting recovery goals","concept":"Automation optimizes immediate recovery while operator prioritizes preservation of another system invariant","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can automated and human repairs conflict even when both are reasonable?","answer":"Different actors can optimize different objectives. Automation may minimize visible errors while an operator protects data integrity, contractual constraints, or another critical invariant.","context":"An incident has multiple objectives with different priorities and trade-offs.","objective":"Make repair objectives and governing constraints explicit across human and automated actors.","attempted_solution":"Allow each actor to optimize its own local recovery objective.","failure":"One repair improves its target while violating another required invariant.","evidence":"Repair decisions require shared objectives, constraints, and priority rules.","cause":"Actors lacked a common decision objective and constraint model.","root_cause":"Governance did not define cross-actor objective precedence.","impact":"Recovery oscillates between incompatible remediation strategies.","containment":"Pause competing actions and establish authoritative objectives and constraints.","remediation":"Share a machine-readable repair objective, constraints, invariants, and authority model across automation and operators.","lessons":"Different reasonable objectives can still produce incompatible repairs without a shared governance model.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; shared repair objectives","related_records":["D53-B82-R03","D53-B96-R03"],"relationships":["objective-alignment","cross-actor-governance"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B96-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Automation-vs-Operator Conflict","subtopic":"Conflict resolution","concept":"Repair conflict is detected but resolution chooses an actor without reconciling the resulting system state","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is selecting which repair path wins insufficient after a conflict?","answer":"Both paths may already have changed the system. Choosing one authority without reconciling actual state can leave partial effects from the losing path and invalidate the winning plan.","context":"Manual and automated remediation have already made overlapping changes before conflict detection.","objective":"Resolve actor conflict using actual state reconciliation rather than authority selection alone.","attempted_solution":"Cancel automation and continue the manual repair without inspecting previous automated effects.","failure":"Manual recovery assumes a clean starting state that no longer exists.","evidence":"Conflict resolution must account for mutations already performed by both actors.","cause":"Authority resolution ignored accumulated state changes.","root_cause":"No post-conflict state reconciliation procedure existed.","impact":"The selected repair path operates on contaminated intermediate state.","containment":"Freeze mutation and reconcile all executed actions before choosing continuation.","remediation":"Record actor-specific actions, reconcile actual state, recompute the valid repair plan, and reauthorize material changes.","lessons":"Resolving who controls the repair is not the same as restoring a valid repair state.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; conflict-state reconciliation","related_records":["D53-B85-R02","D53-B96-R01"],"relationships":["conflict-reconciliation","repair-state-reconstruction"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B97-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Repair Rollback-of-Repair Failures","subtopic":"Incomplete compensation","concept":"Rollback reverses the original repair command but not the secondary state created by that repair","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can rollback of a failed repair create another failed state?","answer":"The repair may have changed data, dependencies, generated artifacts, or external systems that the original rollback command does not reverse. The rollback therefore creates a third state rather than restoring the original state.","context":"A repair caused degradation and operators attempt to reverse it.","objective":"Determine whether compensation actually restores required pre-repair invariants.","attempted_solution":"Run the documented inverse command for the original repair.","failure":"The inverse command completes but repair-induced side effects remain.","evidence":"Rollback correctness depends on the complete state transition caused by the repair.","cause":"Rollback modeled only the primary mutation.","root_cause":"No complete repair effect inventory existed.","impact":"Rollback can leave hidden residual degradation.","containment":"Treat rollback as incomplete until relevant invariants are reverified.","remediation":"Model repair side effects and define compensating actions and postconditions for each affected state class.","lessons":"Rollback is a new repair operation requiring its own verification.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; rollback-of-repair reasoning","related_records":["D53-B83-R04","D53-B86-R01"],"relationships":["rollback-as-repair","compensation-completeness"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B97-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Repair Rollback-of-Repair Failures","subtopic":"Rollback target drift","concept":"Rollback targets current state using an old snapshot without accounting for legitimate changes made after the failed repair","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can rollback overwrite valid changes made after a failed repair?","answer":"A rollback based on a historical snapshot may contain state that was valid before the repair but is no longer valid after unrelated legitimate changes. Blind restoration can therefore remove newer correct state.","context":"Other workflows continue operating while a failed repair is being diagnosed and rolled back.","objective":"Separate repair-induced state from legitimate concurrent state before compensation.","attempted_solution":"Restore the entire target to the snapshot captured before the failed repair.","failure":"Valid changes made after the snapshot are overwritten.","evidence":"Rollback must distinguish repair-caused state from subsequent legitimate mutations.","cause":"Historical snapshot was treated as an unconditional restoration target.","root_cause":"Rollback lacked state version or change lineage analysis.","impact":"Rollback creates additional data or configuration regressions.","containment":"Freeze or reconcile relevant concurrent mutations before restoration.","remediation":"Use state versions, mutation lineage, selective compensation, or forward repair instead of blind snapshot restoration.","lessons":"The pre-repair snapshot is not always the correct current rollback target.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; state-aware rollback","related_records":["D53-B63-R02","D53-B85-R04"],"relationships":["rollback-target-drift","mutation-lineage"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B97-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Repair Rollback-of-Repair Failures","subtopic":"Rollback incompatibility","concept":"Rollback uses an older configuration or artifact that is incompatible with the current system version","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a historical rollback artifact fail after a system upgrade?","answer":"Rollback artifacts can depend on older schemas, APIs, dependencies, or runtime assumptions. Restoring them after an intervening upgrade may create an incompatible state rather than the intended prior state.","context":"A failed repair occurred after a system version change and its rollback artifact was created for the previous state.","objective":"Validate rollback artifact compatibility before applying compensation.","attempted_solution":"Restore the older artifact because it represents the known pre-repair version.","failure":"The restored artifact is incompatible with current system dependencies or state.","evidence":"Rollback compatibility must be evaluated across version transitions.","cause":"Rollback assumed historical compatibility remained unchanged.","root_cause":"Rollback artifacts lacked version compatibility validation.","impact":"Rollback creates new deployment or runtime failures.","containment":"Do not apply incompatible rollback artifacts; preserve current state while selecting a compatible compensation path.","remediation":"Track artifact, schema, runtime, dependency, and state versions for rollback compatibility.","lessons":"Historical rollback artifacts require current compatibility validation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; version-aware rollback","related_records":["D53-B66-R01","D53-B68-R02"],"relationships":["rollback-compatibility","version-aware-compensation"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B97-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Repair Rollback-of-Repair Failures","subtopic":"Rollback verification","concept":"Rollback is considered successful because the original repair command was reversed without verifying the resulting system behavior","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_GAP","question":"Why must rollback itself be verified independently?","answer":"Reversing an action does not prove that the system returned to a healthy or acceptable state. Rollback can introduce residual inconsistencies or fail to restore required behavior.","context":"A repair is reverted after causing unexpected degradation.","objective":"Verify rollback outcomes using explicit postconditions.","attempted_solution":"Mark rollback successful when inverse commands complete without errors.","failure":"The system remains degraded despite successful inverse commands.","evidence":"Rollback execution and rollback outcome are separate lifecycle states.","cause":"Rollback verification was coupled to command completion.","root_cause":"No rollback-specific postconditions existed.","impact":"Failed rollback can be incorrectly treated as recovery.","containment":"Keep the system in controlled recovery until rollback postconditions pass.","remediation":"Define rollback-specific functional, state, and invariant checks independent of rollback command status.","lessons":"A rollback must be verified as a repair in its own right.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; rollback verification","related_records":["D53-B86-R01","D53-B97-R01"],"relationships":["rollback-verification","independent-postconditions"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B97-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Repair Rollback-of-Repair Failures","subtopic":"Rollback decision failure","concept":"Rollback is selected automatically even though forward correction has lower risk than returning to the old state","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is rollback not always the safest response to a failed repair?","answer":"The original state may now be obsolete, incompatible, or itself unstable. A forward corrective repair can sometimes restore the desired invariant with less disruption than reverting to an invalid historical state.","context":"A repair caused degradation but the system has also undergone legitimate state changes since the repair began.","objective":"Choose between rollback and forward correction based on current state and risk.","attempted_solution":"Always prefer rollback because it is assumed to restore the previous known-good state.","failure":"Rollback restores obsolete state and creates additional incompatibility.","evidence":"Rollback is one possible recovery action rather than a universally correct strategy.","cause":"Rollback was treated as the default response independent of current state.","root_cause":"Repair recovery lacked comparative rollback-versus-forward-correction analysis.","impact":"Recovery can move the system further from the desired state.","containment":"Compare current state, repair effects, rollback completeness, and forward-correction risk before acting.","remediation":"Evaluate rollback and forward repair as separate candidate actions with explicit postconditions and risk.","lessons":"The correct recovery target is the required valid state, not necessarily the historical state.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; rollback decision analysis","related_records":["D53-B82-R03","D53-B97-R02"],"relationships":["rollback-versus-forward-repair","current-state-recovery"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B98-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Compound/Cascading Failed Repairs","subtopic":"Repair-induced failure","concept":"A failed repair creates a new failure that invalidates the assumptions of subsequent repair actions","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can one failed repair cause later repairs to fail even when those later repairs were originally valid?","answer":"A repair can change dependencies, state, resource availability, or topology. Subsequent plans built for the original incident may therefore become invalid after the first repair changes system conditions.","context":"Multiple remediation actions are planned before execution and the first action fails partially.","objective":"Recompute downstream repair validity after material repair-induced state changes.","attempted_solution":"Continue the precomputed repair sequence after the first repair failure.","failure":"Later repairs execute against assumptions that no longer hold.","evidence":"Material state changes require dependent repair plans to be revalidated.","cause":"The repair plan was treated as static throughout execution.","root_cause":"No cascading plan invalidation mechanism existed.","impact":"One failed repair propagates into multiple failed repair attempts.","containment":"Stop dependent actions and reconstruct current state before continuing.","remediation":"Use dependency-aware plan invalidation and recomputation after material failed or partial repairs.","lessons":"A failed repair can change the problem itself; downstream plans must be reconsidered.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; cascading repair reasoning","related_records":["D53-B83-R03","D53-B85-R02"],"relationships":["plan-invalidation","cascading-repair-failure"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B98-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Compound/Cascading Failed Repairs","subtopic":"Failure amplification","concept":"Repeated repair attempts progressively reduce recovery capacity and make later repairs less feasible","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can repeated failed repairs turn a recoverable incident into a compound failure?","answer":"Each attempt can consume resources, alter state, increase operational complexity, or create additional cleanup work. Repeating the same or poorly adapted repair can therefore reduce the system's remaining recovery options.","context":"Several repair attempts are made after each produces incomplete or ambiguous results.","objective":"Prevent repeated repair attempts from consuming recovery capacity without increasing evidence.","attempted_solution":"Retry the same repair whenever the desired outcome is not observed.","failure":"Recovery capacity decreases while the underlying uncertainty remains unresolved.","evidence":"Repeated actions should be conditioned on new evidence or changed assumptions rather than repetition alone.","cause":"Failure was treated as a reason to retry rather than a reason to update the model.","root_cause":"No repair-attempt budget or evidence-gain requirement existed.","impact":"The incident becomes progressively harder to recover.","containment":"Pause repeated attempts and reassess evidence, state, and remaining recovery capacity.","remediation":"Track attempts, outcomes, resource cost, new evidence, and changing risk; require justification for repeated actions.","lessons":"Retrying without learning can amplify repair failure.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; repair-attempt governance","related_records":["D53-B81-R05","D53-B88-R01"],"relationships":["repair-attempt-budget","evidence-driven-retry"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B98-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Compound/Cascading Failed Repairs","subtopic":"State contamination","concept":"Failed repair leaves contaminated intermediate state that causes later diagnosis to misidentify the new failure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can a failed repair make subsequent diagnosis less reliable?","answer":"The repair may introduce new symptoms that overlap with the original incident. Without separating pre-repair evidence from post-repair effects, later diagnosis can attribute repair-induced symptoms to the original cause.","context":"A failed repair changes system state and a second diagnosis begins afterward.","objective":"Preserve causal separation between original incident evidence and repair-induced effects.","attempted_solution":"Treat all current symptoms as evidence about the original incident.","failure":"The next diagnosis targets symptoms created by the failed repair.","evidence":"Repair attempts are interventions that can alter the evidence landscape.","cause":"Pre-repair and post-repair observations were not separated.","root_cause":"Incident state lacked intervention-aware evidence lineage.","impact":"Subsequent repairs become progressively less grounded.","containment":"Freeze further mutations and reconstruct state transitions caused by each repair attempt.","remediation":"Tag observations by lifecycle phase and intervention, preserve before/after evidence, and model repair-induced hypotheses separately.","lessons":"A repair changes the diagnostic evidence and must be treated as an intervention in subsequent reasoning.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; intervention-aware diagnosis","related_records":["D53-B81-R05","D53-B98-R01"],"relationships":["evidence-lineage","intervention-aware-diagnosis"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B98-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Compound/Cascading Failed Repairs","subtopic":"Cross-repair dependency","concept":"A later repair assumes that an earlier repair succeeded even though its outcome was unknown","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is assuming prior repair success dangerous in a repair chain?","answer":"Unknown outcome means the target state is unresolved. A dependent repair may then operate on a state that could be original, partially changed, or fully changed.","context":"A multi-stage recovery workflow loses confirmation of an earlier action before proceeding.","objective":"Prevent downstream actions from assuming unresolved execution state.","attempted_solution":"Continue the repair chain because the earlier action did not report an explicit error.","failure":"A dependent action executes against an unknown state and produces additional inconsistency.","evidence":"Unknown execution outcome must remain distinct from success.","cause":"Absence of explicit failure was treated as success.","root_cause":"Repair dependency logic lacked UNKNOWN state semantics.","impact":"Cascading uncertainty spreads through the repair chain.","containment":"Pause dependent actions and reconcile the unknown prior state.","remediation":"Use explicit execution states including UNKNOWN and require reconciliation before dependent actions.","lessons":"Unknown is a valid operational state and must not be silently converted to success.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; execution-state semantics","related_records":["D53-B85-R05","D53-B91-R05"],"relationships":["unknown-state","dependency-gating"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B98-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Compound/Cascading Failed Repairs","subtopic":"Recovery-loop failure","concept":"Repair workflow repeatedly cycles between incompatible corrective actions without converging on a stable state","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can automated repair enter a failure loop?","answer":"If each repair addresses the effect of the previous repair while restoring a condition that triggers another repair, the workflow can oscillate indefinitely. Without convergence criteria and loop detection, automation may amplify the incident.","context":"Two or more repair actions repeatedly reverse or trigger each other.","objective":"Detect non-convergent repair behavior before repeated actions cause additional damage.","attempted_solution":"Allow each repair policy to trigger whenever its local condition becomes true.","failure":"The system alternates between repair states without reaching the desired invariant.","evidence":"Repair automation requires convergence criteria, action history, and loop detection.","cause":"Local repair triggers were evaluated without considering global state transitions.","root_cause":"No repair-cycle detection or convergence invariant existed.","impact":"Automation consumes resources and can keep the system in an unstable state.","containment":"Suspend oscillating repair actions and preserve the current state for diagnosis.","remediation":"Track repair transitions, detect cycles, define global convergence invariants, and require human or higher-level reasoning for unresolved loops.","lessons":"Local repair correctness does not guarantee global convergence.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; repair convergence reasoning","related_records":["D53-B79-R05","D53-B96-R04"],"relationships":["repair-loop-detection","global-convergence"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B99-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Novel/Unprecedented Repair Failures","subtopic":"Insufficient precedent","concept":"Novel failure lacks a sufficiently matching historical repair but the system forces a known repair pattern","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why should an unprecedented failure not automatically inherit the nearest historical repair?","answer":"A novel failure may differ in mechanism, constraints, or system state from all available precedents. Forced pattern matching converts uncertainty into false confidence and can cause unsafe action.","context":"Historical retrieval returns several partial matches but none satisfies the required applicability conditions.","objective":"Handle unprecedented failures explicitly when precedent is insufficient.","attempted_solution":"Select the closest historical repair and execute it with minimal modification.","failure":"The action fails because the current mechanism differs from the historical cases.","evidence":"Insufficient precedent should reduce confidence and increase diagnostic evidence requirements.","cause":"The system optimized for finding a repair instead of determining whether precedent was applicable.","root_cause":"No explicit insufficient-precedent state existed in repair reasoning.","impact":"Novel incidents can receive unsupported remediation.","containment":"Prefer low-impact observation and evidence-gathering actions while preserving uncertainty.","remediation":"Represent insufficient precedent, enumerate competing hypotheses, and require evidence-driven planning before high-impact action.","lessons":"Unknown applicability is itself a meaningful repair state.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; novel-failure handling","related_records":["D53-B90-R05","D53-B89-R05"],"relationships":["insufficient-precedent","uncertainty-preservation"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B99-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Novel/Unprecedented Repair Failures","subtopic":"Unknown-state behavior","concept":"Repair system lacks a safe behavior when critical state cannot be established","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What failure occurs when an autonomous repair system cannot determine the current state of a novel incident?","answer":"If the system treats unknown state as normal or as a recoverable known state, it may execute actions whose preconditions cannot be established. Safe behavior requires explicit uncertainty handling.","context":"Critical telemetry or state reconstruction is unavailable during a novel incident.","objective":"Prevent autonomous repair from acting on unverified critical state.","attempted_solution":"Infer missing state from historical patterns and proceed with the highest-ranked repair.","failure":"The inferred state is wrong and the repair is applied under false assumptions.","evidence":"Critical unknowns should constrain action rather than silently becoming inferred facts.","cause":"Missing state was converted into an assumed state.","root_cause":"The repair state model lacked explicit UNKNOWN conditions and corresponding action policies.","impact":"Autonomous actions can create irreversible or high-blast-radius changes under uncertainty.","containment":"Restrict actions to safe observation or bounded reversible operations until critical state is established.","remediation":"Represent unknown state explicitly and define action policies for known, uncertain, and unverifiable conditions.","lessons":"A robust repair system must know how to behave when it does not know the state.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; explicit unknown-state governance","related_records":["D53-B81-R05","D53-B98-R04"],"relationships":["unknown-state-governance","safe-degradation"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B99-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Novel/Unprecedented Repair Failures","subtopic":"Exploratory repair","concept":"Diagnostic experimentation changes the system without a bounded experiment definition or rollback boundary","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can exploratory repair become an additional incident source?","answer":"Novel incidents often require experiments, but an experiment without explicit scope, expected observation, stop conditions, and recovery boundaries can become an uncontrolled repair.","context":"No established repair exists and operators consider changing a system parameter to gather diagnostic evidence.","objective":"Use controlled experimentation without turning uncertainty reduction into uncontrolled mutation.","attempted_solution":"Change a production parameter and observe whether the symptom improves.","failure":"The experiment introduces additional state changes without a defined interpretation or rollback boundary.","evidence":"Diagnostic experiments require hypotheses, bounded scope, expected observations, and abort conditions.","cause":"Experimentation was performed as an informal repair attempt.","root_cause":"No distinction existed between evidence-gathering experiments and corrective repairs.","impact":"Novel incidents become harder to diagnose because experiments create new confounding effects.","containment":"Stop uncontrolled experimentation and establish a bounded experiment design.","remediation":"Record hypothesis, intervention, expected observation, scope, duration, abort criteria, and restoration method before exploratory changes.","lessons":"Exploration can be useful only when its intervention and evidence boundaries are explicit.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; bounded diagnostic experimentation","related_records":["D53-B83-R03","D53-B98-R03"],"relationships":["controlled-experimentation","diagnostic-intervention"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B99-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Novel/Unprecedented Repair Failures","subtopic":"Uncertainty escalation","concept":"Novel failure remains uncertain but repair confidence is not escalated despite increasing evidence gaps","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why should unresolved uncertainty increase governance rather than simply delay execution?","answer":"As evidence gaps persist, confidence in automated action should not remain constant. High uncertainty combined with high-impact action requires stronger review, narrower scope, or additional evidence.","context":"Repeated diagnostics fail to distinguish competing hypotheses in an unprecedented incident.","objective":"Increase governance proportional to uncertainty and potential repair impact.","attempted_solution":"Continue using the original repair confidence because no contradictory evidence has appeared.","failure":"The action proceeds despite the absence of evidence needed to establish applicability.","evidence":"Lack of discriminating evidence is itself relevant uncertainty.","cause":"Confidence was treated as static rather than evidence-dependent.","root_cause":"No uncertainty escalation policy linked evidence quality to action authority.","impact":"High-impact actions can proceed with insufficient justification.","containment":"Reduce scope and require additional evidence or human review.","remediation":"Define uncertainty thresholds that change permissible action classes, scope, and approval requirements.","lessons":"Uncertainty is a governance signal, not merely a diagnostic inconvenience.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; uncertainty-aware governance","related_records":["D53-B84-R05","D53-B99-R02"],"relationships":["uncertainty-escalation","risk-proportional-governance"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B99-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Novel/Unprecedented Repair Failures","subtopic":"Novelty learning","concept":"Novel incident is repaired but its unique boundary conditions are not captured, causing future false generalization","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can failure to record novelty cause future repairs to fail?","answer":"A novel incident can contain important conditions that distinguish it from previous cases. If the final record stores only the successful action, future retrieval may incorrectly treat it as a generic precedent.","context":"A previously unseen failure is eventually resolved and added to the repair knowledge base.","objective":"Capture novelty, boundary conditions, and evidence limitations for future reuse.","attempted_solution":"Store the successful repair as a general repair pattern without preserving why the incident was novel.","failure":"Future systems retrieve the pattern for superficially similar but materially different incidents.","evidence":"Novel knowledge requires explicit applicability boundaries and uncertainty metadata.","cause":"The learning process optimized for successful action extraction rather than context preservation.","root_cause":"Novelty and boundary conditions were not first-class knowledge attributes.","impact":"A rare repair pattern becomes a source of repeated false generalization.","containment":"Mark the repair as context-specific until sufficient independent evidence exists.","remediation":"Record novelty indicators, distinguishing conditions, applicability boundaries, evidence quality, and subsequent validations.","lessons":"Novel incidents should teach both the repair and the limits of the repair's generality.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; novelty-aware learning","related_records":["D53-B88-R02","D53-B90-R05"],"relationships":["novelty-preservation","generalization-boundaries"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B100-R01","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Final Integrated Failed-Repair Patterns","subtopic":"Evidence-to-action breakdown","concept":"Failed repair originates from an unbroken chain of evidence loss from diagnosis through action and verification","knowledge_type":"INTEGRATED_FAILURE_PATTERN","question":"What integrated failure pattern occurs when evidence quality is not preserved across the repair lifecycle?","answer":"If evidence is weak or contradictory at diagnosis, then assumptions enter decision-making, become encoded into planning and authorization, are executed without sufficient verification, and finally become contaminated learning. The failure is systemic because each lifecycle stage inherits unsupported conclusions from the previous stage.","context":"A repair lifecycle contains diagnosis, decision, planning, authorization, execution, verification, measurement, and learning stages.","objective":"Preserve evidence lineage and uncertainty across the complete repair lifecycle.","attempted_solution":"Treat each lifecycle stage independently and store only its local result.","failure":"Unsupported assumptions propagate from diagnosis into execution and become learned as historical knowledge.","evidence":"Repair reliability depends on continuity of evidence, assumptions, state, and verification across lifecycle transitions.","cause":"Lifecycle stages exchanged conclusions without preserving evidentiary lineage.","root_cause":"No end-to-end evidence lineage connected diagnosis, action, outcome, and learning.","impact":"A single weak assumption can become a persistent source of future failed repairs.","containment":"Trace the repair backward from outcome to evidence and downgrade any unsupported lifecycle transition.","remediation":"Maintain linked evidence, hypothesis, decision, plan, authorization, execution, verification, outcome, and learning records.","lessons":"Repair quality is an end-to-end property; local correctness cannot compensate for broken evidence lineage.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; end-to-end repair lifecycle model","related_records":["D53-B81-R01","D53-B86-R01","D53-B88-R01"],"relationships":["evidence-lineage","end-to-end-repair-lifecycle"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B100-R02","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Final Integrated Failed-Repair Patterns","subtopic":"State-transition breakdown","concept":"Repair lifecycle fails because the system does not maintain a consistent representation of state before, during, and after repair","knowledge_type":"INTEGRATED_FAILURE_PATTERN","question":"Why does inconsistent repair state representation cause failures across multiple lifecycle stages?","answer":"Without explicit state identity and transition semantics, diagnosis can target stale state, execution can mutate a different target, verification can inspect the wrong state, and learning can associate the outcome with an incorrect action.","context":"Repair actions occur in a dynamic system where state can change between diagnosis and verification.","objective":"Maintain consistent state identity and transition lineage across repair operations.","attempted_solution":"Represent repair state using mutable summaries updated by each workflow stage.","failure":"Different stages operate on incompatible snapshots or state interpretations.","evidence":"Repair state requires explicit identity, version, transition history, and verification linkage.","cause":"Lifecycle stages relied on loosely synchronized mutable state.","root_cause":"No immutable repair-state lineage connected observations and actions.","impact":"The system cannot reliably determine what changed, when, or which repair produced the resulting state.","containment":"Freeze high-impact actions and reconstruct authoritative state from durable evidence.","remediation":"Use immutable state identifiers, versioned transitions, target identity, action lineage, and postcondition references.","lessons":"Reliable repair requires state identity to remain stable enough to connect diagnosis, mutation, and outcome.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; state-transition integrity","related_records":["D53-B85-R05","D53-B97-R02"],"relationships":["state-lineage","transition-integrity"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B100-R03","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Final Integrated Failed-Repair Patterns","subtopic":"Governance-execution breakdown","concept":"Repair is correctly authorized but execution diverges from the approved action without triggering governance revalidation","knowledge_type":"INTEGRATED_FAILURE_PATTERN","question":"How can a repair remain approved while the action actually executed is no longer the action that was approved?","answer":"Execution can diverge because of target resolution changes, plan edits, automation retries, operator overrides, or dynamic scope expansion. If governance is bound only to the original request and not the executed action identity, authorization becomes detached from execution.","context":"A repair passes approval but execution is modified or resolved differently before mutation.","objective":"Maintain authorization integrity from approved intent through actual execution.","attempted_solution":"Treat initial approval as sufficient for all subsequent actions derived from the repair plan.","failure":"Execution changes materially without triggering reapproval or scope validation.","evidence":"Authorization must bind to action identity, target, scope, and material execution conditions.","cause":"Governance and execution used different representations of the repair action.","root_cause":"No end-to-end authorization-to-execution binding existed.","impact":"The system can execute an action that was never actually evaluated by the required authority.","containment":"Stop execution when action identity, target, scope, or material assumptions diverge from approval.","remediation":"Bind approvals cryptographically or structurally to immutable repair intent, target scope, action version, and required preconditions; require reauthorization after material changes.","lessons":"Approval is meaningful only when it remains attached to the exact action that executes.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; authorization-execution integrity","related_records":["D53-B84-R02","D53-B96-R03"],"relationships":["authorization-execution-binding","governance-integrity"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B100-R04","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Final Integrated Failed-Repair Patterns","subtopic":"Verification-learning breakdown","concept":"Repair verification is incomplete but the outcome is recorded as successful and becomes reusable knowledge","knowledge_type":"INTEGRATED_FAILURE_PATTERN","question":"How can a verification failure contaminate future repair decisions?","answer":"If incomplete verification is converted into a successful outcome, the learning system can promote an unsupported repair pattern. Retrieval then increases confidence in the same action, causing future failures and reinforcing the incorrect lesson.","context":"A repair appears successful from limited signals but critical postconditions were not verified.","objective":"Prevent unverified outcomes from becoming high-confidence repair knowledge.","attempted_solution":"Record success using available immediate health signals and allow the learning pipeline to ingest it.","failure":"The knowledge base treats the repair as verified despite incomplete outcome evidence.","evidence":"Verification status, outcome attribution, and learned applicability must remain separate lifecycle facts.","cause":"Outcome closure occurred before verification requirements were satisfied.","root_cause":"No learning gate prevented unverified outcomes from becoming reusable repair knowledge.","impact":"The system creates a feedback loop that repeatedly recommends an insufficiently verified repair.","containment":"Downgrade or quarantine the repair record until required verification evidence is complete.","remediation":"Gate learning promotion on explicit verification criteria, preserve evidence level, and distinguish observed recovery from verified causal effectiveness.","lessons":"Learning should consume verified outcomes, not merely positive-looking signals.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; verification-gated learning","related_records":["D53-B86-R01","D53-B87-R05","D53-B88-R01"],"relationships":["verification-gated-learning","feedback-loop-integrity"]}
{"record_id":"D53-B100-R05","dataset_id":"D53","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Final Integrated Failed-Repair Patterns","subtopic":"Closed-loop repair failure","concept":"Repair system fails because diagnosis, decision, execution, verification, and learning operate as disconnected stages rather than a controlled evidence-driven loop","knowledge_type":"INTEGRATED_FAILURE_PATTERN","question":"What is the highest-level failure pattern across failed repair lifecycles?","answer":"A repair system becomes unreliable when diagnosis, decision, planning, authorization, execution, verification, measurement, and learning are treated as isolated activities. Without shared state, evidence lineage, explicit preconditions, governance, postconditions, and feedback controls, errors can propagate across stages and become self-reinforcing.","context":"A complex repair workflow repeatedly experiences wrong diagnosis, partial execution, inadequate verification, and incorrect learning.","objective":"Provide an integrated model in which every repair stage constrains and informs the next stage.","attempted_solution":"Optimize each repair stage independently and connect stages using loosely defined status messages.","failure":"Local stage success masks system-level failure and incorrect lessons are fed back into future repairs.","evidence":"Reliable autonomous repair requires continuity across evidence, state, action, authority, verification, outcome, and learning.","cause":"Lifecycle stages lacked a shared machine-readable repair model.","root_cause":"The repair architecture did not enforce an end-to-end closed-loop contract.","impact":"The system can repeatedly reproduce failed repair patterns while appearing locally functional.","containment":"Suspend autonomous high-impact repair and perform lifecycle-wide reconstruction of evidence, state, decisions, actions, and outcomes.","remediation":"Implement an integrated repair contract connecting Observe/Diagnose → Decide → Plan → Govern → Approve → Execute → Verify → Measure → Learn, with explicit uncertainty, provenance, state identity, authorization, postconditions, contradiction handling, and feedback controls.","lessons":"A world-class repair system must treat repair as an evidence-driven closed loop rather than a sequence of disconnected commands. Execution is not success, verification is not learning, and historical success is not automatic applicability.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":"Autonomous Software Engineering Brain methodology; integrated closed-loop repair architecture","related_records":["D53-B100-R01","D53-B100-R02","D53-B100-R03","D53-B100-R04"],"relationships":["closed-loop-repair","evidence-state-governance-verification-learning","final-integrated-pattern"]}


{"record_id":"D54-B01-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Architecture Decision Definition","concept":"Architecture decision as a durable choice affecting system structure, behavior, quality attributes, or major dependencies","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What qualifies as an architecture decision?","answer":"An architecture decision is a deliberate choice among materially different technical options whose consequences affect system structure, externally relevant behavior, quality attributes, major dependencies, deployment characteristics, or future evolution. It is more significant than an ordinary implementation detail because reversing or ignoring it can materially affect the system.","explanation":"Architecture decisions establish constraints or commitments that shape multiple components or future engineering work. The decision boundary should be explicit so that routine implementation choices are not incorrectly elevated to architecture decisions.","principle":"Treat a choice as architectural when its consequences extend beyond a localized implementation and materially constrain system structure, quality attributes, dependencies, or future change.","rule":"Record the decision when multiple materially different options exist and the selected option creates consequential system-level effects.","objective":"Identify and preserve decisions that materially shape software architecture.","constraints":["The decision must be distinguishable from a local implementation preference.","Consequences must be considered beyond the immediate component."],"preconditions":["A meaningful architectural choice exists."],"expected_behavior":"The knowledge fabric represents the decision, its alternatives, rationale, and consequences rather than storing only the chosen technology or pattern.","security_implication":"Security architecture choices such as trust boundaries and authorization placement qualify as architecture decisions because they affect system-wide protection properties.","performance_implication":"Performance-related architectural choices may affect latency, throughput, resource usage, and scalability across multiple components.","testing_guidance":"Verify that the recorded decision identifies a system-level consequence rather than merely a coding preference.","verification_method":"Review the decision boundary and confirm that at least one consequential architectural dimension is affected.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B01-R02","D54-B01-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B01-R02"},{"type":"contrasts_with","record_id":"D54-B01-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B01-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Decision Boundary","concept":"Distinguishing architectural decisions from local implementation decisions","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should the Brain distinguish an architecture decision from a local implementation choice?","answer":"Evaluate the scope and reversibility of the choice. A choice is more likely architectural when it affects multiple components, external contracts, deployment topology, data ownership, quality attributes, security boundaries, or future evolution. A localized choice with limited system consequences is normally an implementation decision.","explanation":"The distinction is contextual rather than based solely on technology names. Choosing a library can be local in one system and architectural in another if it establishes a system-wide dependency or runtime constraint.","principle":"Classify decisions by consequences and affected boundaries, not by how sophisticated the technology appears.","rule":"Escalate a decision to architecture scope when its consequences cross component or system boundaries or create significant future constraints.","objective":"Prevent both under-documentation of consequential decisions and over-documentation of trivial implementation details.","constraints":["Decision scope depends on system context.","Reversibility must be considered separately from immediate implementation effort."],"preconditions":["The candidate choice and affected system boundaries are known."],"expected_behavior":"The Brain assigns architectural significance based on impact, boundaries, and future constraints.","edge_case":"A small configuration change can become architectural if it changes a global security, consistency, or deployment property.","testing_guidance":"Use contrasting examples where the same technical choice has different architectural significance in different systems.","verification_method":"Check whether the identified consequences cross component, contract, data, deployment, security, or evolution boundaries.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B01-R01","D54-B01-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B01-R01"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B01-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B01-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Decision Significance","concept":"Consequential architecture choices versus routine coding preferences","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should architecture decisions not be confused with coding preferences?","answer":"Architecture decisions establish consequential constraints or structural commitments, whereas coding preferences usually affect local readability or implementation style without materially changing system behavior or architecture. Treating every preference as architectural creates decision noise and weakens retrieval of genuinely important constraints.","explanation":"The Knowledge Fabric needs a useful abstraction level. Excessive recording of insignificant choices obscures decisions that influence interfaces, data, deployment, security, reliability, or future evolution.","principle":"Architectural documentation should maximize decision signal and minimize irrelevant detail.","rule":"Do not promote a choice to architecture scope solely because it is technically interesting or requires explanation.","objective":"Maintain a high-value architecture decision corpus.","constraints":["Some coding practices can become architectural when standardized across the system.","Classification must consider actual consequences."],"expected_behavior":"Decision records capture consequential choices while local preferences remain at an appropriate lower knowledge level.","performance_implication":"Excessive low-value decision records can reduce retrieval precision by introducing irrelevant matches.","testing_guidance":"Evaluate whether a candidate decision materially changes system-level constraints or merely expresses a local style preference.","verification_method":"Compare affected boundaries, quality attributes, contracts, and reversal consequences against the decision's documented scope.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B01-R01","D54-B01-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"contrasts_with","record_id":"D54-B01-R01"},{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B01-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B01-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Decision Scope","concept":"Explicit decision boundary and affected architecture surface","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What should be identified before recording an architecture decision?","answer":"Identify the decision subject, affected components or boundaries, decision drivers, constraints, alternatives, expected consequences, and the scope of authority for the choice. This prevents an architecture record from becoming an isolated technology statement without context.","explanation":"A decision cannot be evaluated correctly without knowing what part of the architecture it governs. Explicit scope also prevents accidental propagation of a decision into areas for which it was never intended.","objective":"Create bounded architecture decisions that can be safely retrieved and applied.","requirements":["Identify affected architectural elements.","Identify the decision boundary.","Identify the intended scope of applicability.","Identify major consequences."],"preconditions":["The architecture area under consideration is known."],"expected_behavior":"A decision record clearly states what is being decided and what is outside its scope.","security_implication":"Explicit scope reduces the risk of applying security decisions beyond the trust boundary for which they were designed.","performance_implication":"Scope identification prevents local performance assumptions from being incorrectly generalized system-wide.","testing_guidance":"Check whether a reader can determine where the decision applies without inferring missing boundaries.","verification_method":"Review the record for subject, affected boundary, applicability, and excluded scope.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B01-R02","D54-B01-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B01-R05"},{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B01-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B01-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Decision Alternatives","concept":"Materially distinct alternatives are necessary for meaningful architecture decisions","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What makes alternatives meaningful in an architecture decision?","answer":"Alternatives are meaningful when they represent materially different ways of satisfying the decision objective and produce different constraints, risks, costs, quality attributes, operational characteristics, or evolution paths. Renaming or lightly modifying the same approach does not create a genuine alternative.","explanation":"Architecture reasoning requires comparison among options that could realistically satisfy the objective. False alternatives create the appearance of analysis without adding decision value.","rule":"Include alternatives only when their implementation or consequences differ materially enough to influence the decision.","objective":"Ensure architecture decisions compare genuine options.","constraints":["Alternatives must satisfy the stated decision objective sufficiently to be credible.","Unavailable or purely hypothetical options should not be treated as equivalent choices."],"expected_behavior":"The decision record contains a bounded set of credible, materially distinct alternatives.","edge_case":"An apparently single technology choice may contain materially distinct deployment or ownership models and therefore represent multiple architectural alternatives.","testing_guidance":"Check whether removing an alternative would eliminate a materially different trade-off.","verification_method":"Compare alternatives across decision drivers, constraints, consequences, and implementation model.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B01-R04","D54-B02-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B01-R04"},{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B02-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B02-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Decision Context","concept":"Architecture decisions require explicit contextual framing","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why must an architecture decision include context?","answer":"The same architectural option can be appropriate in one environment and unsuitable in another because requirements, constraints, operating conditions, dependencies, organizational capabilities, and risk tolerance differ. Context makes the decision interpretable without relying on hidden assumptions.","explanation":"A decision record without context is easily misapplied as a universal rule. Context provides the conditions under which the reasoning remains valid.","principle":"Architecture decisions are conditional knowledge, not automatically universal prescriptions.","rule":"Record the material context that influences the decision or changes the relative suitability of alternatives.","objective":"Make architectural reasoning reusable without causing context-free application.","requirements":["Capture material technical and operational context.","Capture constraints that influence the decision.","Capture assumptions when they materially affect validity."],"expected_behavior":"A retrieved decision can be evaluated against a new context before being reused.","security_implication":"Security decisions depend on trust boundaries, threat exposure, data sensitivity, and authority models.","performance_implication":"Performance decisions depend on workload shape, latency targets, traffic patterns, and resource constraints.","verification_method":"Check whether changing a major contextual assumption could reasonably change the decision.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B02-R02","D54-B02-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B02-R02"},{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B02-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B02-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Technical Context","concept":"Technical context includes existing architecture, dependencies, interfaces, runtime and deployment conditions","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What technical context should be captured for an architecture decision?","answer":"Relevant technical context includes existing components, interfaces, data flows, dependencies, runtime characteristics, deployment topology, compatibility requirements, technology versions, and architectural constraints that can affect the decision.","explanation":"A technically valid option can still be unsuitable if it conflicts with existing interfaces, runtime constraints, deployment mechanisms, dependency versions, or established architectural boundaries.","objective":"Represent the technical environment against which alternatives are evaluated.","requirements":["Identify affected existing components.","Identify relevant interfaces and dependencies.","Identify runtime and deployment constraints.","Identify version constraints when behavior can differ."],"expected_behavior":"The decision record can be evaluated against the architecture that actually exists or is explicitly planned.","edge_case":"A greenfield-looking component may still inherit architectural constraints through shared infrastructure, identity, data, or deployment systems.","verification_method":"Cross-check the decision context against the affected architecture inventory and dependency boundaries.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B02-R01","D54-B02-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B02-R01"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B02-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B02-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Operational Context","concept":"Operational context changes architectural suitability","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"How can operational conditions change an architecture decision?","answer":"Operational conditions such as staffing, monitoring capability, deployment frequency, recovery objectives, maintenance windows, incident response capacity, and dependency ownership can change the practical suitability of an architecture even when its technical properties remain unchanged.","explanation":"Architecture must be operable by the environment in which it will run. A design requiring capabilities unavailable to the operating organization creates a mismatch between theoretical and practical architecture.","principle":"Evaluate architecture against actual operating capability, not only technical possibility.","objective":"Prevent architecture choices that exceed available operational capabilities.","constraints":["Operational capability may change over time.","Claims about organizational capability should be evidence-backed rather than assumed."],"expected_behavior":"Operational constraints are represented explicitly when they materially influence the decision.","security_implication":"Security controls that cannot be reliably operated, monitored, or rotated can create practical security gaps.","performance_implication":"Architectures requiring specialized performance tuning may be unsuitable when sustained tuning capability is unavailable.","verification_method":"Validate the operational assumptions against documented ownership, monitoring, deployment, recovery, and support capabilities.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B02-R01","D54-B02-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B02-R01"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B02-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B02-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Environmental Context","concept":"External environment and deployment conditions","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Which environmental factors can materially constrain architecture decisions?","answer":"Relevant environmental factors include network topology, compute availability, storage characteristics, regulatory environment, geographic distribution, platform restrictions, dependency availability, deployment boundaries, and integration requirements.","explanation":"Environmental constraints can eliminate otherwise technically viable alternatives. They should be represented as explicit decision inputs rather than hidden assumptions.","objective":"Expose external conditions that constrain architecture choices.","constraints":["Environmental facts should be current for the decision period.","Unknown conditions must remain explicitly unknown rather than being filled with assumptions."],"preconditions":["The deployment or operating environment is sufficiently identified."],"expected_behavior":"Alternatives incompatible with mandatory environmental constraints are excluded or explicitly marked conditional.","security_implication":"Network boundaries, data residency, isolation requirements, and platform security controls may eliminate otherwise viable options.","performance_implication":"Network distance, storage characteristics, and compute constraints can materially change latency and throughput behavior.","verification_method":"Validate environmental constraints against authoritative infrastructure, platform, contractual, or regulatory evidence where applicable.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B02-R02","D54-B02-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B02-R02"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B02-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B02-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Context Validation","concept":"Context assumptions must be separated from verified context","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should the Brain handle uncertain architecture context?","answer":"Separate verified context from assumptions and unknowns. A decision may proceed under an explicit assumption, but the assumption must not be represented as a verified fact and should be identified as a condition requiring validation when it materially affects the decision.","explanation":"Hidden assumptions are a major source of architecture decision failure because later users may treat them as universally true.","rule":"Never promote an unverified contextual assumption to verified architecture fact.","objective":"Preserve uncertainty in architecture reasoning.","expected_behavior":"Decision records distinguish verified facts, assumptions, inferences, and unknown conditions.","verification_method":"Inspect provenance and truth classification for each material contextual claim.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B02-R01","D54-B02-R03","D54-B02-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"verifies","record_id":"D54-B02-R01"},{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B02-R03"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B02-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B03-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Decision Drivers","concept":"Decision drivers are the conditions that materially influence architectural choice","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a decision driver?","answer":"A decision driver is a requirement, quality attribute, constraint, risk, dependency, or other condition that materially influences the choice among architectural alternatives.","explanation":"Decision drivers provide the reasoning bridge between context and the selected option. Without explicit drivers, a rationale can become a post-hoc description of preference.","principle":"A decision should be explainable through explicit drivers rather than unsupported preference.","rule":"Identify only drivers that materially affect the relative suitability of alternatives.","objective":"Make architecture rationale traceable to meaningful engineering conditions.","requirements":["Identify material drivers.","Distinguish drivers from incidental observations.","Preserve driver priority where priorities affect the decision."],"expected_behavior":"The decision record connects each major selection factor to alternatives and consequences.","verification_method":"Check that removing a claimed driver could plausibly change the decision.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B03-R02","D54-B03-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B03-R02"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B03-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B03-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Requirement Drivers","concept":"Functional and non-functional requirements as architecture drivers","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How do requirements drive architecture decisions?","answer":"Functional requirements constrain what capabilities and interactions the architecture must support, while non-functional requirements constrain properties such as latency, availability, security, scalability, and maintainability. Architecture alternatives should be evaluated against both rather than only feature coverage.","explanation":"Two architectures can provide identical functional features while differing materially in quality attributes. Ignoring non-functional requirements therefore produces incomplete decision analysis.","objective":"Connect requirements to architectural alternatives.","requirements":["Identify relevant functional requirements.","Identify measurable or testable quality requirements where possible.","Trace material requirements to decision criteria."],"expected_behavior":"An architecture option is not considered suitable merely because it satisfies functional behavior if it violates mandatory quality requirements.","testing_guidance":"Translate important quality requirements into acceptance or verification criteria.","verification_method":"Trace each mandatory requirement to architectural consequences and validation evidence.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B03-R01","D54-B03-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"implements","record_id":"D54-B03-R04"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B03-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B03-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Risk Drivers","concept":"Risk as an explicit architecture decision driver","knowledge_type":"RISK_ANALYSIS","question":"Why should architectural risk be represented explicitly as a decision driver?","answer":"Architectural alternatives can expose different failure modes, dependency risks, security risks, migration risks, operational risks, and uncertainty. Recording these risks makes the decision rationale auditable and helps determine what validation or mitigation is required.","explanation":"Risk should not be treated as a generic warning. It should be connected to the option, evidence, affected property, mitigation, and residual uncertainty.","objective":"Make risk-sensitive architecture selection explicit.","constraints":["Risk statements must distinguish known evidence from inferred possibilities.","Risk severity should not be fabricated when evidence is insufficient."],"expected_behavior":"Each material risk is associated with an alternative, consequence, mitigation or validation activity where applicable.","security_implication":"Security risk should include the relevant trust boundary, attack surface, control dependency, or exposure condition.","performance_implication":"Performance risk should distinguish measured observations from estimates or qualitative concerns.","verification_method":"Review whether material risks have supporting evidence or are explicitly classified as inference or unknown.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B03-R01","D54-B03-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B03-R01"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B03-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B03-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Traceability","concept":"Requirement-to-decision traceability","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should a requirement be connected to an architecture decision?","answer":"Trace the requirement to the affected quality or functional property, identify which alternatives satisfy or violate it, record the selected decision and rationale, and define how compliance will later be verified.","explanation":"Traceability prevents architecture decisions from becoming disconnected design artifacts. It also allows later verification to determine whether the decision continues to satisfy the requirement.","objective":"Create an explicit requirement-to-decision-to-verification chain.","requirements":["Requirement identifier or stable description.","Affected architectural property.","Alternative impact.","Decision rationale.","Verification condition."],"expected_behavior":"A reviewer can move from a requirement to the architectural choice and then to evidence that the choice satisfies the requirement.","testing_guidance":"Use acceptance tests, architecture tests, experiments, or operational measurements appropriate to the requirement.","verification_method":"Inspect the trace for an unbroken requirement → decision → verification relationship.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B03-R02","D54-B03-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"implements","record_id":"D54-B03-R02"},{"type":"verifies","record_id":"D54-B03-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B03-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Driver Prioritization","concept":"Conflicting decision drivers require explicit priority or constraint treatment","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What should happen when architecture decision drivers conflict?","answer":"Determine whether one driver is a hard constraint, whether priorities can be ordered, or whether the conflict requires an explicit trade-off decision. The conflict should not be hidden by pretending that all drivers can be simultaneously optimized.","explanation":"Security, latency, cost, reliability, simplicity, and delivery speed can impose competing constraints. Explicit conflict treatment makes the decision reproducible and exposes residual compromises.","principle":"When drivers conflict, represent the conflict and its resolution explicitly.","objective":"Prevent hidden trade-offs in architecture reasoning.","constraints":["Do not invent numerical priorities without evidence.","A mandatory constraint should eliminate alternatives that violate it rather than merely receive a low score."],"expected_behavior":"The decision record distinguishes hard constraints from preferences and documents unresolved trade-offs.","security_implication":"A convenience or performance optimization must not silently override a mandatory security boundary.","performance_implication":"Performance improvements must be evaluated against any reliability, resource, or correctness constraints they may compromise.","verification_method":"Check whether conflicting drivers are explicitly classified as constraints, priorities, trade-offs, or unresolved uncertainty.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B03-R02","D54-B03-R03","D54-B04-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"contrasts_with","record_id":"D54-B03-R02"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B04-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B04-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Hard Constraints","concept":"Hard architectural constraints","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What is a hard architecture constraint?","answer":"A hard constraint is a condition that an acceptable architecture must satisfy. An alternative that violates a genuine hard constraint should normally be excluded rather than compensated for by strengths in unrelated dimensions.","explanation":"Examples can include mandatory regulatory restrictions, required compatibility, fixed interfaces, prohibited deployment environments, or non-negotiable security boundaries. Whether a condition is truly hard must be evidence-backed.","rule":"Do not trade away a genuine mandatory constraint as though it were an ordinary preference.","objective":"Prevent invalid alternatives from surviving architecture evaluation.","expected_behavior":"Alternatives violating verified hard constraints are rejected or explicitly marked conditional on constraint removal.","security_implication":"Mandatory security boundaries must be treated as constraints where the system's threat model requires them.","verification_method":"Validate the claimed hard constraint against its authoritative requirement or source.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B04-R02","D54-B04-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"contrasts_with","record_id":"D54-B04-R02"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B04-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B04-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Soft Constraints","concept":"Soft constraints and preferences","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"How are soft constraints different from hard constraints?","answer":"A soft constraint is a preference or optimization target that influences selection but may be traded against other objectives when necessary. It should not be represented as mandatory unless evidence establishes that violation is unacceptable.","explanation":"Misclassifying a preference as a hard constraint can unnecessarily eliminate useful alternatives. Misclassifying a hard constraint as a preference can produce an invalid architecture.","objective":"Represent architectural preferences with the correct strength.","constraints":["Priority should be justified by requirements or decision authority.","Avoid arbitrary numerical scoring when evidence does not support it."],"expected_behavior":"Soft constraints influence comparison without automatically invalidating alternatives.","verification_method":"Review the source and consequences of the constraint to determine whether violation is actually prohibited or merely undesirable.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B04-R01","D54-B04-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"contrasts_with","record_id":"D54-B04-R01"},{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B04-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B04-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Constraint Validation","concept":"Validating whether a stated constraint is genuine","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should the Brain determine whether a claimed architectural constraint is genuine?","answer":"Identify the authoritative requirement, contract, technical limitation, security requirement, operational dependency, or other evidence establishing the constraint. If the evidence is absent, classify the condition as an assumption, preference, or unknown rather than silently treating it as mandatory.","explanation":"Constraint inflation is dangerous because it can bias architecture selection before alternatives are objectively considered.","objective":"Prevent unsupported constraints from controlling architecture decisions.","expected_behavior":"Constraint strength is traceable to evidence or explicitly marked uncertain.","verification_method":"Trace each hard constraint to an authoritative requirement or validated technical condition.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B04-R01","D54-B04-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"verifies","record_id":"D54-B04-R01"},{"type":"verifies","record_id":"D54-B04-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B04-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Compatibility Constraints","concept":"Existing interfaces and compatibility requirements as architecture constraints","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"When does compatibility become an architecture constraint?","answer":"Compatibility becomes an architecture constraint when an existing interface, protocol, data format, runtime, deployment platform, consumer, or dependency must continue to interoperate within the decision scope.","explanation":"Compatibility can constrain both the architecture and migration path. It should be distinguished from convenience compatibility, which may be negotiable.","objective":"Capture compatibility obligations that materially restrict architectural alternatives.","requirements":["Identify the compatibility boundary.","Identify affected consumers or dependencies.","Identify supported versions or contract requirements where applicable."],"expected_behavior":"Alternatives incompatible with mandatory existing contracts are excluded or require an explicit migration decision.","security_implication":"Compatibility changes can alter authentication, authorization, serialization, or trust behavior and therefore require security review.","performance_implication":"Compatibility layers can add latency, conversion overhead, or operational complexity and should be treated as architectural consequences.","verification_method":"Validate compatibility against actual contracts, supported versions, and affected consumers.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B04-R03","D54-B04-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B04-R03"},{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B04-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B04-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Constraint Interaction","concept":"Constraint interaction and hidden constraint chains","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"Why must interacting constraints be analyzed together?","answer":"A constraint that appears satisfiable in isolation can conflict with another constraint or create secondary restrictions. For example, a compatibility requirement can constrain deployment choices, which can then constrain scalability or recovery options.","explanation":"Architecture decisions often fail because constraints are evaluated independently instead of as a connected system.","objective":"Identify constraint chains before selecting an architecture.","expected_behavior":"The decision analysis identifies interactions among material constraints and does not treat them as independent filters when they are causally connected.","edge_case":"A constraint may be conditional rather than universally active; its activation condition should be recorded.","verification_method":"Construct a constraint dependency map and inspect whether satisfying one constraint changes the feasibility of another.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B04-R01","D54-B04-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B04-R04"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B04-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B05-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Alternative Discovery","concept":"Systematic identification of credible architecture alternatives","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How should architecture alternatives be discovered?","answer":"Start from the decision objective and constraints, identify fundamentally different solution structures, include the viable existing-state option when relevant, and exclude options that violate mandatory constraints or cannot satisfy the objective.","explanation":"Alternative discovery should avoid anchoring on the first familiar technology. The objective is not to enumerate every possible architecture but to identify materially different credible options.","objective":"Produce a useful alternative set without artificial breadth.","requirements":["Define the decision objective.","Identify mandatory constraints.","Identify materially different solution structures.","Record why excluded options are infeasible when exclusion matters."],"expected_behavior":"The alternative set is sufficiently diverse to expose meaningful trade-offs without becoming an unbounded catalog.","verification_method":"Check whether each retained alternative differs in architecture, operating model, dependency structure, or major trade-offs.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B05-R02","D54-B05-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B05-R02"},{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B05-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B05-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Alternative Quality","concept":"Alternative completeness without combinatorial enumeration","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How many architecture alternatives should a decision record contain?","answer":"There is no universal required number. The set should contain enough materially distinct credible alternatives to expose the important trade-offs and constraints. Adding superficially different variants adds noise rather than decision value.","explanation":"Alternative count is subordinate to coverage. A small set can be sufficient when the solution space is narrow, while a broader set may be justified when fundamentally different architectures remain viable.","principle":"Optimize alternative quality and decision coverage rather than alternative count.","objective":"Prevent both premature closure and artificial alternative inflation.","constraints":["Do not invent alternatives solely to appear comprehensive.","Do not omit a materially viable option merely because it complicates the analysis."],"verification_method":"Review whether every materially distinct feasible architecture class relevant to the decision is represented or explicitly excluded with rationale.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B05-R01","D54-B05-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B05-R01"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B05-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B05-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Alternative Bias","concept":"Anchoring on a familiar architecture or technology","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"What is the architectural anchoring anti-pattern?","answer":"Architectural anchoring occurs when the decision process starts from a familiar technology or preferred pattern and evaluates the problem mainly in terms of how to use that choice rather than whether it is appropriate. This can suppress materially different alternatives before their consequences are examined.","explanation":"Familiarity is evidence of experience with an option, not proof that the option satisfies the current decision drivers.","failure_mode":"The initial preferred option becomes the implicit baseline and alternatives are judged against it without symmetric analysis.","cause":"Premature commitment, organizational familiarity, prior success, or technology preference.","root_cause":"The decision process is centered on a solution before the problem constraints and alternatives are explicitly modeled.","impact":"Potentially unsuitable architecture selection, hidden trade-offs, and weak decision rationale.","containment":"Pause implementation commitment and restate the objective, constraints, drivers, and alternative classes.","remediation":"Re-evaluate materially distinct alternatives against the same decision drivers and evidence.","lessons":"Technology familiarity should inform feasibility and evidence, not replace architecture analysis.","verification_method":"Check whether the decision record defines the problem and alternatives before naming the preferred technology.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B05-R01","D54-B05-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"contrasts_with","record_id":"D54-B05-R01"},{"type":"causes","record_id":"D54-B05-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B05-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Baseline Alternative","concept":"Retaining the viable existing architecture as an explicit alternative","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why should the existing architecture sometimes be treated as an alternative?","answer":"When the current architecture can continue satisfying requirements with acceptable risk, retaining it is a genuine alternative to changing it. Explicitly considering the status quo prevents change from being treated as automatically beneficial and makes the cost and risk of migration visible.","explanation":"The existing state may carry technical debt, but changing it also introduces migration, compatibility, operational, and regression risks. Both sides require evidence.","objective":"Make change-versus-retain reasoning explicit.","constraints":["The existing architecture must actually remain viable under current requirements.","Known security, compliance, reliability, or compatibility violations may make retention infeasible."],"expected_behavior":"The status quo is evaluated against the same decision drivers as proposed alternatives when it is technically viable.","security_implication":"Retaining an architecture with unresolved mandatory security defects cannot be justified solely by migration cost.","performance_implication":"Retention should consider whether current performance and scaling constraints remain acceptable.","verification_method":"Determine whether the current architecture satisfies mandatory requirements and document material reasons for retention or rejection.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B05-R02","D54-B05-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B05-R02"},{"type":"contrasts_with","record_id":"D54-B05-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B05-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Alternative Rejection","concept":"Evidence-based rejection of alternatives","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How should rejected architecture alternatives be documented?","answer":"Record the alternative, the decision drivers or constraints that make it unsuitable, the evidence supporting the rejection, and any conditions under which it could become viable. Rejection should not be represented as a universal statement when it is context-dependent.","explanation":"Rejected alternatives preserve decision reasoning and prevent future teams from repeating analysis without understanding why an option was previously excluded.","objective":"Preserve useful negative architecture knowledge.","expected_behavior":"A rejected alternative remains retrievable with its rejection conditions and evidence boundaries.","verification_method":"Check that rejection is connected to explicit constraints, drivers, evidence, or context rather than preference alone.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B05-R03","D54-B05-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B05-R03"},{"type":"contrasts_with","record_id":"D54-B05-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B06-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Decision Criteria","concept":"Decision criteria translate drivers into evaluable properties","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What are architecture decision criteria?","answer":"Decision criteria are the observable or reasoned properties used to compare alternatives against the decision drivers. Examples include compatibility, latency, availability, security boundary integrity, operational complexity, migration effort, and scalability characteristics.","explanation":"Drivers state what matters; criteria define how alternatives will be examined with respect to those drivers.","objective":"Convert architecture concerns into consistent comparison dimensions.","requirements":["Each important criterion should correspond to a decision driver.","Criteria should be evaluable using evidence, tests, specifications, or explicit inference."],"expected_behavior":"Alternatives are compared using criteria relevant to the decision rather than arbitrary preference.","testing_guidance":"Where a criterion is measurable, define the test or experiment needed to obtain evidence.","verification_method":"Trace each criterion to a driver and identify the evidence required to evaluate it.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B06-R02","D54-B06-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"implements","record_id":"D54-B06-R02"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B06-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B06-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Quality Attribute Criteria","concept":"Quality attributes as comparative architecture criteria","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should quality attributes be used when comparing architecture alternatives?","answer":"Translate relevant quality attributes into explicit criteria and evaluate each alternative against the required level or constraint. Do not treat quality attributes as generic labels; identify the architectural mechanism, expected consequence, and verification method associated with each one.","explanation":"Terms such as scalability or reliability are too broad to support a decision unless connected to concrete system expectations.","objective":"Make quality-attribute reasoning operationally useful.","requirements":["Define the relevant quality attribute.","State the architectural property affecting it.","Identify acceptance or verification evidence where practical."],"expected_behavior":"Quality attributes influence architecture selection through explicit criteria rather than vague claims.","security_implication":"Security criteria should identify relevant trust, confidentiality, integrity, authentication, authorization, or exposure properties.","performance_implication":"Performance criteria should identify workload, latency, throughput, resource, or scalability dimensions as applicable.","verification_method":"Trace each quality attribute from requirement to criterion to evidence.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B06-R01","D54-B06-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B06-R01"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B06-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B06-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Evidence-Based Comparison","concept":"Comparing alternatives using evidence rather than preference","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What makes an architecture comparison evidence-driven?","answer":"An evidence-driven comparison connects each important criterion to specifications, measurements, experiments, validated implementation behavior, authoritative constraints, or clearly classified engineering inference. Unsupported qualitative claims must remain uncertain rather than being presented as facts.","explanation":"Architecture decisions often contain claims about performance, reliability, maintainability, cost, or security. Evidence quality determines how strongly those claims should influence the decision.","rule":"Classify evidence and preserve limitations instead of converting assumptions into facts.","objective":"Increase the reliability and auditability of architecture comparisons.","expected_behavior":"The decision record identifies which conclusions are verified, inferred, assumed, or unknown.","verification_method":"Audit material claims for supporting evidence and truth classification.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B06-R01","D54-B06-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B06-R01"},{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B06-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B06-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Measurable Criteria","concept":"Turning qualitative architecture concerns into testable criteria","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How can a qualitative architecture concern become a useful decision criterion?","answer":"Define the concern in observable terms, identify the relevant system property, establish acceptable behavior or constraints, and determine what evidence can validate the property. If no reliable measurement is possible, retain the criterion as qualitative and state its evidence limitations.","explanation":"Not every architecture property can be reduced to a single number. The objective is testability and clarity, not artificial precision.","objective":"Improve the verifiability of architecture criteria without fabricating measurements.","expected_behavior":"Criteria have explicit evidence requirements appropriate to their nature.","performance_implication":"Performance criteria should use actual workload assumptions and measured results where measurements are available; otherwise claims remain estimated or inferred.","verification_method":"Inspect whether each criterion has an appropriate evidence or validation method.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B06-R02","D54-B06-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"verifies","record_id":"D54-B06-R02"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B06-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B06-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Criterion Weighting","concept":"Avoiding unsupported numerical scoring of architecture alternatives","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can arbitrary numerical scoring weaken architecture decisions?","answer":"Numerical scores can create false precision when criteria, weights, or evidence are subjective or incomparable. A score may obscure a hard constraint, hide uncertainty, or make unsupported assumptions appear mathematically authoritative.","explanation":"Scoring can be useful when its inputs and methodology are justified, but a number does not automatically make a decision evidence-based.","failure_mode":"An architecture appears objectively superior because it receives a higher composite score despite weak evidence or incompatible assumptions.","cause":"Unjustified weights, subjective ratings, or aggregation of incomparable properties.","root_cause":"The scoring model is treated as stronger evidence than the underlying observations and constraints.","impact":"Biased architecture selection and reduced decision transparency.","containment":"Expose individual criteria, assumptions, constraints, and evidence instead of relying on the composite score.","remediation":"Use explicit trade-offs and evidence classifications; use quantitative scoring only when its methodology is justified.","lessons":"Mathematical representation does not compensate for poor evidence.","verification_method":"Audit the scoring inputs, weights, evidence, and treatment of hard constraints.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B06-R03","D54-B06-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"contrasts_with","record_id":"D54-B06-R03"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B06-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B07-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Decision Evidence","concept":"Evidence hierarchy for architecture decisions","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What evidence should support an architecture decision?","answer":"Prefer authoritative specifications, documented requirements, validated measurements, controlled experiments, verified implementation behavior, and established technical evidence. Use engineering inference when direct evidence is unavailable, but label it as inference and preserve uncertainty.","explanation":"Different evidence types support different claims. A specification can establish API behavior, while a benchmark can provide workload-specific performance evidence; neither automatically proves the other's properties.","objective":"Match evidence type to the claim being made.","requirements":["Identify the claim.","Identify evidence appropriate to that claim.","Preserve evidence limitations."],"expected_behavior":"The decision rationale distinguishes direct evidence from inference and assumption.","verification_method":"Check whether each material claim has evidence appropriate to its nature.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B07-R02","D54-B07-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B07-R02"},{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B07-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B07-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Evidence-Claim Matching","concept":"Evidence must match the specific architecture claim","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must evidence be matched to the claim it supports?","answer":"Evidence is only useful to the extent that it addresses the claim being evaluated. For example, successful functional tests do not by themselves prove scalability, and a benchmark under one workload does not establish universal performance behavior.","explanation":"Architecture decisions often overgeneralize evidence. The Knowledge Fabric must preserve the boundary between what was actually demonstrated and what is merely inferred.","rule":"Never extend evidence beyond the property, workload, environment, version, or condition under which it was obtained without explicitly classifying the extension as inference.","objective":"Prevent evidence overgeneralization.","expected_behavior":"Claims retain their evidence scope and limitations.","performance_implication":"Performance evidence must retain workload, environment, configuration, and measurement context.","security_implication":"A security test demonstrating one control path does not prove the absence of vulnerabilities across unrelated trust boundaries.","verification_method":"Compare the claim's scope with the evidence's scope and identify unsupported extrapolation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B07-R01","D54-B07-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B07-R01"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B07-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B07-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Experiment Evidence","concept":"Architecture experiments as decision evidence","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"When should an architecture experiment be used as decision evidence?","answer":"Use an architecture experiment when a material uncertainty can be reduced by implementing or testing a representative part of the proposed architecture. Define the hypothesis, relevant conditions, observable outcomes, limitations, and decision impact before interpreting the result.","explanation":"Experiments can reduce uncertainty about integration, performance, compatibility, failure behavior, or operational characteristics that cannot be reliably determined from documentation alone.","objective":"Use targeted experimentation to resolve material architecture uncertainty.","requirements":["Define the uncertainty or hypothesis.","Define representative conditions.","Define observable success and failure criteria.","Record limitations of the experiment."],"expected_behavior":"Experiment results reduce a specified uncertainty without being generalized beyond the tested conditions.","testing_guidance":"Use representative workload, interfaces, versions, failure conditions, or deployment characteristics where those factors affect the decision.","verification_method":"Review whether the experiment directly tests the uncertainty claimed to be resolved.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B07-R02","D54-B07-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B07-R02"},{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B07-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B07-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Evidence Scope","concept":"Evidence limitations and validity boundaries","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What limitations should accompany architecture evidence?","answer":"Evidence should retain relevant scope such as environment, workload, version, configuration, test duration, sampled behavior, assumptions, and known blind spots. These limitations define where the evidence can legitimately support the decision.","explanation":"Evidence without validity boundaries is easily reused outside the conditions under which it was obtained.","objective":"Preserve evidence applicability boundaries.","expected_behavior":"Decision records expose important conditions that constrain interpretation of evidence.","performance_implication":"Benchmark evidence must retain workload and environment characteristics because performance is condition-dependent.","security_implication":"Security evidence must retain the tested attack surface and control scope.","verification_method":"Inspect whether the evidence record states the conditions required to interpret it correctly.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B07-R02","D54-B07-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B07-R02"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B07-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B07-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Insufficient Evidence","concept":"Architecture decisions under insufficient evidence","knowledge_type":"UNKNOWN","question":"What should happen when evidence is insufficient for a material architecture decision?","answer":"The decision should preserve the uncertainty, identify what is unknown, determine whether the uncertainty is acceptable, and define the smallest useful validation activity or conservative constraint needed to proceed safely. Missing evidence must not be replaced by fabricated certainty.","explanation":"Some decisions must proceed before complete information exists. The correct response is controlled uncertainty rather than pretending that the unknown condition has been resolved.","objective":"Support safe decision-making when evidence is incomplete.","constraints":["Do not invent measurements or operational experience.","Irreversible or high-impact choices require stronger evidence or explicit risk acceptance."],"expected_behavior":"Unknown conditions remain visible and drive validation, mitigation, or explicit acceptance.","verification_method":"Check that unresolved uncertainty has an owner, validation path, mitigation, or documented acceptance when relevant.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B07-R01","D54-B07-R03","D54-B08-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B07-R03"},{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B08-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B08-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Decision Rationale","concept":"Evidence-linked decision rationale","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What makes architecture rationale strong?","answer":"Strong rationale explains how the decision objective, constraints, decision drivers, alternatives, evidence, and trade-offs lead to the selected option. It should expose important rejected alternatives and uncertainty rather than merely asserting that the chosen option is better.","explanation":"A rationale is useful when another engineer can reconstruct why the decision was made and determine whether its assumptions remain valid.","objective":"Create reproducible and auditable architecture reasoning.","expected_behavior":"The rationale forms an explicit chain from context and evidence to decision and consequences.","verification_method":"Attempt to reconstruct the decision from the recorded rationale without relying on undocumented preference.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B08-R02","D54-B08-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B08-R02"},{"type":"implements","record_id":"D54-B08-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B08-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Alternative Rationale","concept":"Recording why alternatives were rejected","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"Why should rejected alternatives be part of architecture rationale?","answer":"Rejected alternatives preserve the reasoning boundary of the decision. They show what was considered, why it was unsuitable under the recorded context, and what conditions might make it viable later.","explanation":"Without rejected alternatives, future engineers may repeat the same evaluation or misunderstand the decision as a universal rejection of the technology or pattern.","objective":"Preserve negative decision knowledge and reduce repeated analysis.","requirements":["Identify materially considered alternatives.","Record relevant rejection drivers.","Record evidence or assumptions supporting rejection.","Record conditional circumstances that could change the result when material."],"expected_behavior":"Future retrieval can distinguish 'not suitable here' from 'never suitable'.","verification_method":"Check that rejection rationale is contextual and evidence-linked.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B05-R05","D54-B08-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B05-R05"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B08-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B08-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Rationale Structure","concept":"Decision rationale as a traceable reasoning chain","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What structure should an architecture rationale follow?","answer":"A useful structure is context → objective → constraints → decision drivers → alternatives → evidence → trade-offs → decision → expected consequences → validation requirements. This structure makes reasoning reusable by both humans and deterministic retrieval systems.","explanation":"The structure separates facts and constraints from interpretation and preserves the path from problem to architectural commitment.","objective":"Represent architecture rationale in a machine-consumable form.","requirements":["Stable decision identity.","Explicit alternatives.","Evidence classification.","Decision and consequences.","Validation requirements."],"expected_behavior":"A reasoning engine can retrieve individual portions of the rationale without losing their relationships.","verification_method":"Validate that each major reasoning stage is represented or explicitly marked not applicable.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B08-R01","D54-B08-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"implements","record_id":"D54-B08-R01"},{"type":"composes_with","record_id":"D54-B08-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B08-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Assumption Tracking","concept":"Explicit assumption tracking in architecture rationale","knowledge_type":"ASSUMPTION","question":"Why must architecture assumptions be recorded separately from facts?","answer":"Assumptions are conditions believed to hold but not sufficiently verified for the decision. Recording them separately prevents future users from treating uncertain premises as established facts and provides a direct trigger for re-evaluation if an assumption changes.","explanation":"Many architecture decisions remain valid only while certain assumptions hold. Assumption tracking therefore supports decision lifecycle management.","objective":"Make conditional decision validity explicit.","expected_behavior":"Each material assumption has a clear truth classification and, where appropriate, a validation or monitoring condition.","verification_method":"Audit material rationale statements and classify each as verified fact, inference, assumption, or unknown.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B07-R05","D54-B08-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B07-R05"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B08-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B08-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Decision Validity","concept":"Conditional validity of architecture decisions","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"When should an architecture decision be treated as conditional rather than universal?","answer":"Treat a decision as conditional when its validity depends on specific requirements, constraints, assumptions, evidence scope, technology versions, workloads, deployment environments, or other contextual conditions that may change.","explanation":"Architecture knowledge is often context-dependent. Preserving conditions allows the Brain to retrieve a decision while still checking whether its applicability matches the current problem.","rule":"Attach material validity conditions to the decision instead of generalizing beyond the evidence boundary.","objective":"Prevent context-specific architecture decisions from being misapplied.","version_range":"Applicable across architecture decision records; version conditions must be recorded when technology behavior affects validity.","expected_behavior":"A retrieval system can test contextual compatibility before recommending reuse of the decision.","verification_method":"Identify the assumptions and constraints that would invalidate or materially change the decision.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B08-R01","D54-B08-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B08-R04"},{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B08-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B09-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Decision Consequences","concept":"Architecture decisions create both benefits and costs","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What should be captured as an architecture decision consequence?","answer":"Capture material positive and negative effects, including changes to complexity, dependencies, operational burden, performance, security, reliability, migration effort, team responsibilities, and future flexibility.","explanation":"A decision that solves one problem can introduce another. Recording only benefits produces incomplete architecture knowledge.","objective":"Represent the full consequence surface of an architecture decision.","requirements":["Identify direct consequences.","Identify important secondary consequences.","Distinguish expected consequences from observed outcomes."],"expected_behavior":"The decision record shows what the selected option enables and what it makes harder or more constrained.","verification_method":"Review the decision against its major quality attributes, dependencies, operational effects, and future evolution constraints.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B09-R02","D54-B09-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B09-R02"},{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B09-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B09-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Second-Order Effects","concept":"Second-order architectural consequences","knowledge_type":"INFERENCE","question":"Why should architecture decisions consider second-order effects?","answer":"A decision can alter the conditions under which other components operate, creating effects that are not immediate or local. Examples include a new dependency increasing operational load, stronger consistency reducing availability during partition conditions, or service decomposition increasing coordination complexity.","explanation":"Second-order effects are especially important in distributed and evolving systems because local improvements can change system-level behavior.","objective":"Expose consequential effects beyond the immediate decision boundary.","expected_behavior":"Decision analysis considers downstream dependencies and changed operating conditions.","security_implication":"A new component or integration can introduce additional trust boundaries and attack surfaces beyond its direct function.","performance_implication":"Additional abstraction, communication, serialization, coordination, or consistency mechanisms can create secondary latency and resource effects.","verification_method":"Trace affected dependencies and quality attributes one level beyond the direct implementation consequence.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B09-R01","D54-B09-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B09-R01"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B09-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B09-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Expected Versus Observed Consequences","concept":"Separating predicted consequences from verified outcomes","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must expected architecture consequences be separated from observed outcomes?","answer":"Expected consequences are predictions made during decision analysis, while observed outcomes require evidence from implementation, testing, measurement, or operation. Treating predictions as observations creates false operational knowledge.","explanation":"D54 must preserve the distinction because later D50–D58 experience datasets depend on verified outcomes rather than design expectations.","rule":"Never label an expected consequence as observed or verified without supporting evidence.","objective":"Maintain truthfulness across the architecture decision lifecycle.","expected_behavior":"Decision records distinguish anticipated effects from subsequently measured or verified effects.","verification_method":"Inspect outcome fields for actual evidence and separate them from pre-implementation expectations.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B09-R01","D54-B09-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"verifies","record_id":"D54-B09-R01"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B09-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B09-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Reversibility Consequences","concept":"Consequences of making a decision difficult to reverse","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why is reversibility an important consequence of an architecture decision?","answer":"A difficult-to-reverse decision creates greater future commitment because changing it may require migration, compatibility work, data movement, operational disruption, or organizational change. This increases the value of evidence before commitment.","explanation":"Reversibility is not itself a quality attribute of the chosen architecture; it is a property of the decision's future change cost and should influence evidence requirements and risk handling.","objective":"Make commitment consequences explicit.","constraints":["Do not claim a precise reversal cost without evidence.","Reversibility depends on system context and implementation details."],"expected_behavior":"High-commitment decisions identify migration or reversal implications before selection.","verification_method":"Identify what artifacts, interfaces, data, dependencies, and operational processes would need to change if the decision were reversed.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B09-R02","D54-B09-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B09-R02"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B09-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B09-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Consequence Verification","concept":"Verification of architectural consequences","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should predicted consequences be verified after implementation?","answer":"Define observable postconditions for the important consequences and collect appropriate evidence through tests, measurements, architecture checks, operational observations, or other valid verification methods. The verification must target the claimed consequence rather than merely confirming deployment success.","explanation":"A system can deploy successfully while failing to deliver the architectural property that motivated the decision.","objective":"Connect architecture decisions to post-implementation evidence.","requirements":["Identify the consequence to verify.","Define observable evidence.","Define pass criteria or interpretation limits."],"expected_behavior":"Verification demonstrates whether important architectural consequences actually occurred within the stated scope.","testing_guidance":"Use targeted tests or measurements for performance, reliability, security, compatibility, and operational properties as appropriate.","verification_method":"Trace each important predicted consequence to evidence and observed result.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"VERIFIED_CONCEPT","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B09-R03","D54-B10-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"implements","record_id":"D54-B09-R03"},{"type":"verifies","record_id":"D54-B10-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B10-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Decision Records","concept":"Machine-readable architecture decision record structure","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What information should a machine-readable architecture decision record preserve?","answer":"A useful record should preserve decision identity, context, objective, requirements, constraints, drivers, alternatives, evidence, rationale, selected decision, rejected alternatives, consequences, assumptions, validation requirements, provenance, version conditions, and relationships to related engineering knowledge.","explanation":"A machine-readable ADR must support retrieval and reasoning, not merely human-readable documentation. Stable fields and explicit relationships allow deterministic systems to compose decision knowledge.","objective":"Define a reusable structured representation for architecture decisions.","requirements":["Stable record identity.","Explicit decision context.","Alternative and rationale representation.","Evidence and truth classification.","Consequence and verification representation.","Cross-record relationships."],"expected_behavior":"A retrieval engine can independently retrieve context, alternatives, evidence, rationale, consequences, and verification requirements.","verification_method":"Validate that the record can reconstruct the decision without requiring undocumented external context.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B10-R02","D54-B10-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"composes_with","record_id":"D54-B10-R02"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B10-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B10-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Decision Lifecycle","concept":"Architecture decision lifecycle from proposal to verification","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What lifecycle should an architecture decision follow?","answer":"A robust lifecycle is identify context → define objective → identify constraints and drivers → discover alternatives → gather evidence → analyze trade-offs → decide → record rationale and consequences → implement → verify relevant consequences → monitor validity → revisit when material assumptions change.","explanation":"The lifecycle prevents architecture decisions from ending at selection. The decision remains useful only if implementation and later evidence can be connected back to it.","objective":"Connect decision reasoning to implementation and future learning.","expected_behavior":"Architecture decision records remain linked to validation and later re-evaluation.","verification_method":"Check for explicit transitions between decision formulation, selection, implementation implications, verification, and future review.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B10-R01","D54-B10-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"implements","record_id":"D54-B10-R01"},{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B10-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B10-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Decision Record Integrity","concept":"Stable identity and versioning of architecture decisions","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should architecture decision records have stable identities and versions?","answer":"Stable identities allow other knowledge to reference the decision without breaking relationships, while versioning preserves historical reasoning when a decision is corrected, superseded, or materially changed.","explanation":"Architecture decisions become dependencies for requirements, implementations, tests, migrations, and later decisions. Silent modification can invalidate those relationships.","rule":"Preserve stable record identity and create a new authoritative version when a material correction or decision change occurs.","objective":"Maintain historical integrity of architecture knowledge.","expected_behavior":"References remain stable while decision revisions remain traceable.","verification_method":"Check that corrections preserve the original identity and distinguish historical from current authoritative versions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B10-R01","D54-B10-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B10-R01"},{"type":"supersedes","record_id":"D54-B10-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B10-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Decision Review","concept":"Re-evaluating architecture decisions when material conditions change","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"When should an architecture decision be reconsidered?","answer":"Reconsider a decision when a material requirement, constraint, dependency, technology version, threat model, workload, operating condition, evidence base, or key assumption changes enough to affect the original rationale.","explanation":"A decision does not automatically become invalid because time passes. Re-evaluation should be triggered by changes that affect the premises or consequences on which the decision depended.","objective":"Prevent both stale decisions and unnecessary architecture churn.","constraints":["Do not reopen decisions solely because a newer technology exists.","Re-evaluation should be evidence-driven."],"expected_behavior":"Material context changes trigger targeted decision review while stable decisions remain authoritative.","security_implication":"Changes in threat exposure, trust boundaries, vulnerabilities, or security requirements can invalidate previously acceptable architecture assumptions.","performance_implication":"Significant workload or performance-target changes can require re-evaluation of architecture choices.","verification_method":"Compare current context against the decision's recorded assumptions, constraints, drivers, and evidence validity.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B08-R05","D54-B10-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B08-R05"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B10-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B10-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Decision Fundamentals","subtopic":"Integrated Decision Pattern","concept":"Context-to-decision-to-verification traceability","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the complete traceability chain for an architecture decision?","answer":"The core chain is context → requirements → constraints → decision drivers → alternatives → evidence → trade-offs → decision → rationale → consequences → implementation implications → verification → observed outcome or unresolved uncertainty. Each transition should preserve provenance and truth status.","explanation":"This integrated structure connects architecture reasoning with deterministic retrieval, implementation planning, testing, verification, and later learning. It prevents the decision record from becoming disconnected documentation.","objective":"Establish the foundational architecture-decision reasoning chain for D54.","requirements":["Context and decision scope.","Requirements and constraints.","Alternatives and evidence.","Rationale and consequences.","Implementation implications.","Verification and outcome state."],"expected_behavior":"The Brain can traverse from a system requirement or constraint to the decision, implementation implications, and required verification evidence.","security_implication":"Security-related architectural decisions must preserve trust boundaries, applicable controls, and verification evidence throughout the chain.","performance_implication":"Performance-related decisions must preserve workload assumptions, expected properties, measurement requirements, and evidence boundaries.","testing_guidance":"Verification should target the specific architectural property claimed by the decision rather than merely confirming that software starts or deploys.","verification_method":"Audit the complete relationship chain and identify any missing transition or unsupported claim.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions generation specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}],"related_records":["D54-B10-R01","D54-B10-R02","D54-B10-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"generalizes","record_id":"D54-B10-R01"},{"type":"implements","record_id":"D54-B10-R02"},{"type":"verifies","record_id":"D54-B10-R04"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B11-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Functional Requirements","subtopic":"Requirement Decomposition","concept":"Architecture decision driven by functional requirement boundaries","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture be selected when a functional requirement spans multiple independently evolving capabilities?","answer":"The architecture should preserve explicit capability boundaries while defining the minimum coordination needed to satisfy the end-to-end function. The decision should be based on dependency direction, consistency requirements, failure behavior, ownership, and change boundaries rather than decomposing solely by organizational structure.","explanation":"A functional requirement may cross several components without requiring a single tightly coupled component. The architecture should identify which parts require synchronous coordination, which can be asynchronous, and which state transitions must remain authoritative.","principle":"Functional boundaries should drive architectural responsibilities, while cross-functional coordination should be explicit and minimized.","rule":"Do not create a shared architectural boundary merely because multiple components participate in one user-visible function.","objective":"Choose component boundaries that satisfy functional behavior without unnecessary coupling.","requirements":["Complete the required end-to-end behavior","Preserve clear ownership of state and decisions","Define required cross-component interactions"],"constraints":["Functional behavior may cross multiple components","Some operations may require consistency while others tolerate asynchronous completion"],"preconditions":["Functional requirements have been decomposed into observable behaviors","Critical state ownership is identified"],"input":"Functional requirement set with dependencies and consistency expectations.","output":"Architecture decision defining capability boundaries and coordination mechanisms.","expected_behavior":"Each required behavior has an identifiable implementation owner and explicit interaction path.","correct_example":"Separate order validation, payment authorization, and fulfillment into components when each has distinct ownership and lifecycle, while defining explicit orchestration between them.","invalid_example":"Place all functionality into one component solely because the user workflow is presented as one screen.","failure_mode":"Architecture becomes tightly coupled and difficult to evolve when unrelated functional responsibilities change.","cause":"Boundary selected from user-interface grouping instead of domain responsibility and change characteristics.","root_cause":"Functional requirements were not decomposed into independently owned responsibilities.","solution":"Map each functional behavior to an owner, identify shared invariants, and choose synchronous or asynchronous coordination based on actual requirements.","tradeoff":"More explicit boundaries can increase coordination complexity but reduce long-term coupling.","edge_case":"A requirement appears atomic to users but contains operations with different consistency and failure requirements.","security_implication":"Component boundaries must preserve authorization scope and prevent cross-capability privilege expansion.","performance_implication":"Additional component boundaries may introduce network or serialization overhead.","test_case":"Verify that each functional requirement maps to an owning component and that all required interactions have defined failure behavior.","testing_guidance":"Use contract and integration tests for cross-component behavior and unit tests for component-local responsibilities.","verification_method":"Trace each functional requirement to an owner, interaction contract, and observable acceptance criterion.","verification_guidance":"Reject the decision if any required behavior has ambiguous ownership or an undefined cross-component dependency.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B11-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Functional requirement decomposition"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B11-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B11-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Functional Requirements","subtopic":"Consistency Scope","concept":"Architecture decision for localized versus global consistency","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should a functional requirement receive local consistency rather than system-wide consistency?","answer":"Use the narrowest consistency scope that satisfies the functional invariant. System-wide consistency should be selected only when the requirement explicitly depends on a globally synchronized state.","explanation":"Broad consistency creates coordination and availability costs. Many functional requirements need strong consistency only for a particular aggregate or transaction boundary.","principle":"Consistency scope should match the invariant being protected.","rule":"Do not impose global consistency when the requirement can be satisfied by an explicitly bounded consistency domain.","objective":"Avoid unnecessary coordination while preserving required functional correctness.","requirements":["Identify the invariant","Define the authoritative state","Determine which operations must observe the invariant atomically"],"constraints":["Some workflows cross consistency boundaries","Asynchronous propagation may create temporary divergence"],"preconditions":["Business or system invariants are documented","State ownership is defined"],"input":"Functional requirement and consistency invariant.","output":"Decision defining the minimum consistency boundary.","expected_behavior":"Operations requiring the invariant observe an authoritative and sufficiently current state.","correct_example":"Keep inventory reservation strongly consistent within the inventory aggregate while allowing reporting data to update asynchronously.","invalid_example":"Require every reporting query to participate in the inventory transaction.","failure_mode":"Unnecessary global coordination increases latency, contention, and failure coupling.","cause":"Consistency requirement was interpreted as a system-wide property rather than an invariant-specific property.","root_cause":"The architecture decision lacked explicit invariant boundaries.","solution":"Document each invariant, its authoritative state, and the smallest operation set that must observe it atomically.","tradeoff":"Narrow consistency improves scalability but requires explicit handling of stale derived data.","edge_case":"A derived view becomes temporarily stale during a user operation that actually depends on the authoritative state.","security_implication":"Authorization decisions must use sufficiently authoritative state and must not rely on stale derived permissions.","performance_implication":"Narrow consistency boundaries can reduce lock contention and cross-service coordination.","test_case":"Introduce delayed propagation and verify that invariant-dependent operations remain correct while non-critical derived views may lag.","testing_guidance":"Use integration and failure-injection tests to validate consistency boundaries.","verification_method":"Trace every strong-consistency requirement to a documented invariant and transaction boundary.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no component incorrectly assumes stronger consistency than the architecture guarantees.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B11-R01"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Consistency invariant definition"],"extends":["D54-B11-R01"],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B11-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B11-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Functional Requirements","subtopic":"Workflow Ownership","concept":"Architecture decision for long-running functional workflows","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture handle a functional workflow that spans long-running operations?","answer":"Separate durable workflow state from transient execution workers and define explicit progress, retry, timeout, compensation, and completion semantics.","explanation":"Long-running workflows should not depend on one process remaining alive. Durable state allows recovery and resumption while workers perform individual steps.","principle":"Workflow progress must survive individual execution-process failure.","rule":"Do not encode long-running workflow state exclusively in process memory.","objective":"Provide recoverable execution for long-running functional requirements.","requirements":["Persist workflow state","Define step transitions","Define retry and compensation behavior","Define completion criteria"],"constraints":["Workers may fail independently","Steps may have different retryability and side effects"],"preconditions":["Workflow steps and state transitions are known"],"input":"Long-running functional workflow specification.","output":"Architecture with durable workflow state and independently recoverable execution steps.","expected_behavior":"A worker failure does not erase workflow state or cause uncontrolled duplicate side effects.","correct_example":"Persist workflow state and step status, then allow a replacement worker to resume an incomplete step according to its idempotency policy.","invalid_example":"Keep workflow progress only in a worker's local memory and assume the worker will remain available.","failure_mode":"Worker loss causes lost progress or ambiguous side effects.","cause":"Execution lifetime was incorrectly treated as workflow lifetime.","root_cause":"No durable workflow state model existed.","solution":"Persist state transitions and define recovery semantics for every step.","tradeoff":"Durable orchestration adds state-management complexity but improves recoverability.","edge_case":"A worker fails after an external side effect succeeds but before the step is marked complete.","security_implication":"Persisted workflow state must not expose secrets or permit unauthorized workflow resumption.","performance_implication":"Durable state introduces persistence overhead and requires appropriate batching or indexing at scale.","test_case":"Terminate a worker after an external side effect and verify recovery does not create an uncontrolled duplicate action.","testing_guidance":"Use failure-injection and integration tests around each durable transition.","verification_method":"Verify that every workflow state transition is durable and recoverable.","verification_guidance":"Check ambiguous completion states explicitly rather than assuming failed workers imply failed operations.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B11-R04"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Workflow state model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B11-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Functional Requirements","subtopic":"Failure Semantics","concept":"Architecture decision for partial functional completion","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an architecture represent a functional operation that can complete partially?","answer":"Model partial completion explicitly rather than collapsing all non-terminal states into success or failure. The architecture should define which steps are committed, compensatable, retryable, or permanently failed.","explanation":"Distributed functional operations may not have atomic completion across all components. Explicit state enables safe recovery and accurate user-visible outcomes.","principle":"Partial completion is a first-class state when atomicity cannot be guaranteed.","rule":"Never represent a multi-step operation as fully successful merely because one component completed.","objective":"Make partial functional outcomes observable and recoverable.","requirements":["Represent step-level state","Define terminal and recoverable states","Define compensation or retry behavior"],"constraints":["External systems may not support rollback","Some completed effects may be irreversible"],"preconditions":["Operation steps and side effects are identified"],"input":"Multi-step functional operation with non-atomic dependencies.","output":"Explicit state model for partial completion and recovery.","expected_behavior":"System accurately distinguishes completed, pending, failed, compensated, and unknown steps.","correct_example":"Track payment authorization and fulfillment separately and expose an intermediate state when fulfillment has not completed.","invalid_example":"Return success immediately after payment authorization while silently treating fulfillment as guaranteed.","failure_mode":"Users or downstream systems assume completion when later steps remain incomplete.","cause":"End-to-end success was inferred from an intermediate event.","root_cause":"The architecture lacked explicit workflow completion semantics.","solution":"Define state transitions and terminal criteria for the complete operation.","tradeoff":"More states increase model complexity but prevent incorrect success claims.","edge_case":"An external dependency becomes unreachable after a previous step completed.","security_implication":"Authorization for recovery actions must be evaluated against the current state, not the original request alone.","performance_implication":"Fine-grained state tracking may increase persistence operations.","test_case":"Interrupt the workflow after each step and verify the resulting state is correctly represented.","testing_guidance":"Use state-transition and failure-injection tests.","verification_method":"Compare observed state transitions with the formally defined workflow state model.","verification_guidance":"Ensure no intermediate state can incorrectly satisfy the final success condition.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B11-R03"],"depends_on":["D54-B11-R03"],"requires":["Explicit workflow state model"],"extends":[],"refines":["D54-B11-R03"],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B11-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Functional Requirements","subtopic":"Acceptance Traceability","concept":"Architecture decision traceability from functional requirement to verification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should an architecture decision demonstrate that functional requirements are actually supported?","answer":"Maintain traceability from each functional requirement to architectural responsibility, implementation boundary, test evidence, and verification criterion.","explanation":"Architecture is not validated merely because components exist. Each required behavior needs an observable path from requirement to implementation and verification.","principle":"Architectural decisions should be traceable to measurable functional outcomes.","rule":"A functional requirement without an identifiable verification path represents an architectural gap.","objective":"Ensure architecture decisions remain connected to functional acceptance criteria.","requirements":["Requirement identifiers","Component ownership","Verification criteria"],"constraints":["Some requirements require end-to-end validation","Some architectural properties are indirectly observable"],"preconditions":["Functional requirements are testable or have defined inspection criteria"],"input":"Requirement set and architecture model.","output":"Requirement-to-architecture-to-verification traceability map.","expected_behavior":"Every in-scope functional requirement has an accountable implementation path and verification method.","correct_example":"Map order creation to order service behavior, persistence contract, integration test, and end-to-end acceptance criterion.","invalid_example":"Declare an architecture complete because all planned components are deployed without mapping them to requirements.","failure_mode":"Required functionality remains unimplemented or unverifiable despite apparent architectural completeness.","cause":"Architecture review focused on component presence rather than requirement satisfaction.","root_cause":"No requirement traceability model was maintained.","solution":"Create explicit requirement-to-component-to-test relationships and review uncovered requirements.","tradeoff":"Traceability adds maintenance overhead but exposes architectural omissions early.","edge_case":"A requirement is qualitative and requires operational observation rather than a simple automated test.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive functional requirements need explicit authorization and abuse-case verification.","performance_implication":"Functional requirements with latency constraints need measurable performance acceptance criteria.","test_case":"Select each requirement and trace it through architecture to at least one valid verification mechanism.","testing_guidance":"Use traceability audits plus automated acceptance tests where practical.","verification_method":"Perform bidirectional traceability analysis between requirements and verification artifacts.","verification_guidance":"Flag requirements with no owner, implementation path, or verification evidence.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B11-R01","D54-B11-R04"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Functional requirements","Architecture model","Verification criteria"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["Requirement traceability audit"],"verifies":["Functional requirement coverage"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B12-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Non-Functional Requirements","subtopic":"Requirement Prioritization","concept":"Architecture decision driven by non-functional requirement priority","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture decisions handle multiple non-functional requirements that cannot all be maximized simultaneously?","answer":"Translate non-functional requirements into measurable constraints or targets, identify conflicts, and explicitly prioritize them according to system objectives and risk rather than optimizing every property independently.","explanation":"Availability, latency, consistency, cost, security, and maintainability can conflict. Architecture decisions should expose those trade-offs instead of treating every quality attribute as an unlimited requirement.","principle":"Quality attributes must be treated as explicit constraints and trade-offs.","rule":"Do not claim that an architecture simultaneously maximizes conflicting quality attributes without evidence.","objective":"Select an architecture consistent with prioritized quality requirements.","requirements":["Identify quality attributes","Define measurable targets","Document conflicts and priorities"],"constraints":["Quality attributes may conflict","Targets may change with workload or business context"],"preconditions":["Stakeholders have defined acceptable thresholds"],"input":"Non-functional requirement set and priority constraints.","output":"Prioritized architecture quality-attribute decision.","expected_behavior":"Architecture satisfies mandatory thresholds while explicitly documenting trade-offs for non-mandatory objectives.","correct_example":"Accept moderate latency increase to meet a mandatory durability requirement when the latency remains below the defined acceptance threshold.","invalid_example":"Select a highly distributed design solely because it is assumed to maximize every quality attribute.","failure_mode":"Architecture becomes unnecessarily complex while failing one or more critical quality requirements.","cause":"Quality attributes were treated as independent optimization objectives.","root_cause":"No explicit quality-attribute trade-off model existed.","solution":"Define thresholds, priorities, and measurable acceptance criteria before selecting architecture.","tradeoff":"Explicit prioritization may sacrifice secondary qualities to protect critical requirements.","edge_case":"Two requirements have equal priority but their targets cannot simultaneously be met under known constraints.","security_implication":"Security requirements should not be traded away implicitly to improve convenience or performance.","performance_implication":"Latency and throughput targets must be workload-specific and measured rather than assumed.","test_case":"Evaluate architecture against each mandatory quality threshold under representative conditions.","testing_guidance":"Use quality-attribute acceptance tests and architecture-level validation.","verification_method":"Compare measured or otherwise evidenced properties against documented thresholds.","verification_guidance":"Separate measured evidence from architectural assumptions.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B12-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Non-functional requirement priorities"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B12-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B12-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Non-Functional Requirements","subtopic":"Quality Attribute Conflicts","concept":"Architecture decision for conflicting quality attributes","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should an architecture decision document a conflict between two non-functional requirements?","answer":"State the conflict explicitly, identify the affected operating conditions, establish acceptable bounds for both attributes, and record which requirement has priority when the bounds cannot both be achieved.","explanation":"A quality-attribute trade-off is meaningful only when its conditions and acceptance boundaries are explicit.","principle":"Trade-offs should be conditional and measurable rather than expressed as generic preferences.","rule":"Do not record a quality-attribute winner without defining the workload, constraints, and acceptance thresholds.","objective":"Make quality-attribute trade-offs reviewable and testable.","requirements":["Conflict definition","Operating conditions","Acceptance thresholds","Priority rule"],"constraints":["Trade-off may vary by workload","Future requirements may alter priorities"],"preconditions":["Relevant quality attributes are identified"],"input":"Conflicting quality requirements.","output":"Conditional trade-off decision.","expected_behavior":"Architecture behavior remains within accepted limits under stated conditions.","correct_example":"Prefer lower latency for interactive requests while allowing asynchronous processing for non-interactive workloads.","invalid_example":"Declare latency more important than durability for all workloads without specifying consequences.","failure_mode":"Architecture optimizes the wrong property for a critical workload.","cause":"Trade-off was generalized beyond the context in which it was evaluated.","root_cause":"Decision lacked explicit applicability boundaries.","solution":"Attach workload, threshold, and priority metadata to the trade-off.","tradeoff":"Context-specific optimization improves fit but increases decision complexity.","edge_case":"Workload changes significantly after deployment.","security_implication":"Quality trade-offs must not silently weaken security controls below required policy.","performance_implication":"Performance targets must specify percentile, workload, concurrency, and measurement conditions where relevant.","test_case":"Evaluate the architecture under the operating conditions that motivated the trade-off.","testing_guidance":"Use representative workload tests and boundary tests around acceptance thresholds.","verification_method":"Verify both the applicability conditions and the measured quality attributes.","verification_guidance":"Mark the decision stale when its assumptions no longer match the operating workload.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B12-R01","D54-B12-R03"],"depends_on":["D54-B12-R01"],"requires":["Quality attribute definitions"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B12-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B12-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Non-Functional Requirements","subtopic":"Quality Attribute Budgeting","concept":"Architecture decision using explicit quality budgets","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When is a quality budget useful in architecture decisions?","answer":"A quality budget is useful when a system-level requirement must be allocated across multiple components or stages, such as latency, error rate, resource usage, or availability.","explanation":"Without allocation, each component may consume an unspecified portion of the total requirement and collectively violate the system target.","principle":"System-level quality constraints should be decomposed into enforceable component-level budgets where practical.","rule":"Do not assign component budgets without accounting for shared overhead and dependency behavior.","objective":"Make system-level non-functional requirements actionable at component boundaries.","requirements":["System-level target","Component decomposition","Allocation method","Budget validation"],"constraints":["Budgets may interact","Dependency behavior may vary"],"preconditions":["System-level target is measurable"],"input":"System quality target and architecture dependency graph.","output":"Component-level quality budget model.","expected_behavior":"Component budgets collectively remain compatible with the system-level target under defined assumptions.","correct_example":"Allocate request latency budget across gateway, service processing, database access, and network overhead.","invalid_example":"Give every component the full system latency target independently.","failure_mode":"Combined component latency exceeds the system-level requirement.","cause":"Budgets were duplicated instead of allocated.","root_cause":"No compositional quality-budget model existed.","solution":"Partition the target using realistic overhead and dependency assumptions, then validate the aggregate behavior.","tradeoff":"Budgeting improves accountability but can become brittle when workload characteristics change.","edge_case":"A shared dependency contributes latency to multiple paths.","security_implication":"Security controls that consume quality budget must be included rather than treated as free overhead.","performance_implication":"Latency, throughput, and resource budgets are especially sensitive to workload assumptions.","test_case":"Measure the end-to-end path and compare component contributions against allocated budgets.","testing_guidance":"Use distributed tracing or equivalent component-level measurement where available.","verification_method":"Aggregate component measurements and compare them with the system target.","verification_guidance":"Reassess budgets when architecture or workload assumptions materially change.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B12-R02"],"depends_on":["D54-B12-R01"],"requires":["Measurable system-level quality target"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B12-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B12-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Non-Functional Requirements","subtopic":"Requirement Uncertainty","concept":"Architecture decision under uncertain non-functional targets","knowledge_type":"DECISION_PATTERN","question":"How should architecture be selected when non-functional requirements are uncertain?","answer":"Prefer an architecture that preserves reversible choices, makes assumptions explicit, and supports measurement before expensive irreversible commitments are made.","explanation":"Uncertain targets should not be converted into unsupported precise requirements. Architecture can instead preserve options while gathering evidence through prototypes or measurements.","principle":"Architectural uncertainty should be managed explicitly rather than hidden inside design assumptions.","rule":"Do not optimize for an unverified quality target as though it were established fact.","objective":"Reduce the risk of premature architectural commitment.","requirements":["Identify uncertain assumptions","Define evidence needed","Preserve reversible options"],"constraints":["Evidence collection has cost","Some architectural choices become expensive to reverse"],"preconditions":["Quality requirements are incomplete or uncertain"],"input":"Uncertain non-functional requirement set.","output":"Evidence-oriented architecture decision with explicit assumptions.","expected_behavior":"Architecture remains adaptable until critical quality requirements become sufficiently evidenced.","correct_example":"Prototype two storage approaches when expected workload characteristics are unknown and use representative measurements before committing.","invalid_example":"Choose the most complex storage architecture solely because future scale is assumed to be large.","failure_mode":"System incurs unnecessary complexity based on an unsupported assumption.","cause":"Uncertainty was treated as certainty.","root_cause":"Architecture decision did not distinguish known requirements from assumptions.","solution":"Record assumptions, define validation experiments, and preserve reversible alternatives.","tradeoff":"Delayed commitment requires experimentation effort but reduces irreversible design risk.","edge_case":"The cost of experimentation is itself significant.","security_implication":"Security-critical requirements should not remain unresolved merely because other quality targets are uncertain.","performance_implication":"Performance assumptions should be measured under representative workloads before optimization-driven architecture choices.","test_case":"Review each major quality requirement and classify it as evidenced requirement, assumption, or unknown.","testing_guidance":"Use architecture experiments or prototypes only to answer clearly defined uncertainties.","verification_method":"Verify that every major assumption has an owner and evidence plan.","verification_guidance":"Do not mark an uncertain requirement as verified without supporting evidence.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B12-R05"],"depends_on":["D54-B12-R01"],"requires":["Explicit uncertainty model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["Architecture assumption review"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B12-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Non-Functional Requirements","subtopic":"Acceptance Criteria","concept":"Architecture decision verification for quality attributes","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should non-functional architecture decisions be verified?","answer":"Each decision should identify measurable or inspectable acceptance criteria, the evidence required, the operating conditions, and the limitations of the verification method.","explanation":"A quality attribute cannot be considered satisfied merely because an architecture is commonly associated with it.","principle":"Architecture quality claims require evidence appropriate to the claim.","rule":"Do not treat architectural style as proof of a quality attribute.","objective":"Separate architecture assumptions from verified quality outcomes.","requirements":["Quality claim","Acceptance criterion","Evidence method","Operating conditions"],"constraints":["Some properties require runtime measurement","Some architectural properties are verified through inspection or formal reasoning"],"preconditions":["Quality requirement is sufficiently defined"],"input":"Quality attribute claim and architecture decision.","output":"Verification plan and acceptance evidence requirements.","expected_behavior":"The decision's quality claims can be evaluated against explicit evidence criteria.","correct_example":"Verify an availability target using failure scenarios and measured service availability rather than assuming redundancy guarantees availability.","invalid_example":"Mark the system highly available solely because it has multiple instances.","failure_mode":"Architecture is declared compliant without testing the actual failure behavior.","cause":"Design feature was substituted for outcome evidence.","root_cause":"Verification criteria were not defined with the architecture decision.","solution":"Define claim-specific evidence and acceptance criteria before declaring satisfaction.","tradeoff":"Evidence collection adds effort but prevents unsupported quality claims.","edge_case":"A quality property cannot be measured directly and requires proxy indicators.","security_implication":"Security architecture claims require threat-specific evidence rather than generic security labels.","performance_implication":"Performance claims require workload and measurement-condition metadata.","test_case":"For every major quality claim, identify its evidence source and acceptance threshold.","testing_guidance":"Combine architectural inspection, targeted tests, and runtime measurements as appropriate.","verification_method":"Audit the decision record for claim-to-evidence traceability.","verification_guidance":"Classify unsupported claims as assumptions or unknown rather than verified outcomes.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B12-R01","D54-B12-R03","D54-B12-R04"],"depends_on":["D54-B12-R01"],"requires":["Verification criteria"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["Quality attribute verification audit"],"verifies":["D54-B12-R01","D54-B12-R02","D54-B12-R03","D54-B12-R04"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B13-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Availability","subtopic":"Failure Domains","concept":"Architecture decision based on failure-domain separation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should components be separated across failure domains to improve availability?","answer":"Separate components across independent failure domains when the availability requirement justifies the added operational and coordination complexity and the domains have sufficiently independent failure characteristics.","explanation":"Redundancy only improves availability when replicas do not share the same dominant failure mode.","principle":"Availability depends on independence of failure domains, not merely replica count.","rule":"Do not assume multiple instances provide meaningful availability improvement when they share the same critical failure domain.","objective":"Reduce correlated failure risk through appropriate architectural separation.","requirements":["Identify dominant failure modes","Define required availability","Identify failure-domain boundaries"],"constraints":["Independent domains may increase latency and operational complexity","Some dependencies remain shared"],"preconditions":["Failure modes and availability target are known"],"input":"Availability requirement and dependency/failure-domain model.","output":"Decision defining appropriate redundancy and domain separation.","expected_behavior":"A failure affecting one domain does not unnecessarily eliminate all service capacity.","correct_example":"Distribute service instances across independent infrastructure failure domains when the underlying platform supports such isolation.","invalid_example":"Place all replicas on the same failure domain and count them as independent availability capacity.","failure_mode":"A shared infrastructure failure removes every replica simultaneously.","cause":"Replica count was used as a proxy for failure independence.","root_cause":"Failure-domain analysis was omitted from the architecture decision.","solution":"Map replicas and dependencies to actual failure domains and remove dominant shared failure dependencies where justified.","tradeoff":"Greater domain separation can increase cost, operational complexity, and cross-domain latency.","edge_case":"The supposedly independent domains share a control plane or network dependency.","security_implication":"Failure-domain separation must preserve authorization and trust boundaries.","performance_implication":"Cross-domain communication may add latency and bandwidth overhead.","test_case":"Simulate loss of each identified failure domain and verify required service behavior.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled failure injection where operationally safe.","verification_method":"Review dependency topology and execute domain-loss tests where feasible.","verification_guidance":"Treat untested independence assumptions as unverified.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B13-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Failure-domain model","Availability target"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B13-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B13-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Availability","subtopic":"Dependency Availability","concept":"Architecture decision for critical dependency redundancy","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture handle a highly available service that depends on a less available dependency?","answer":"Identify the dependency as part of the service availability path and either increase its resilience, introduce an appropriate degraded mode, or revise the availability claim.","explanation":"Service-level availability cannot exceed the practical availability of critical dependencies unless the architecture can operate without them during dependency failure.","principle":"Availability claims must include critical dependency behavior.","rule":"Do not claim service availability independently of a dependency that is required for successful operation.","objective":"Align service availability architecture with dependency realities.","requirements":["Dependency inventory","Critical-path identification","Failure behavior"],"constraints":["Some dependencies cannot be made redundant","Degraded operation may not satisfy all functions"],"preconditions":["Critical dependencies are known"],"input":"Service dependency graph and availability requirements.","output":"Dependency-aware availability architecture decision.","expected_behavior":"Dependency failure results in a defined full-service or degraded-service state.","correct_example":"Cache non-critical reference data locally so the service can continue read-only operation when the external reference service is unavailable.","invalid_example":"Add redundant application instances while leaving one mandatory external dependency as a single point of failure.","failure_mode":"Application replicas remain healthy but the service is unavailable because the dependency fails.","cause":"Availability analysis stopped at application instances.","root_cause":"Dependency failure paths were excluded from the availability model.","solution":"Model critical dependencies and define redundancy or degraded behavior for each.","tradeoff":"Caching or degraded modes may provide availability at the cost of freshness or functionality.","edge_case":"Dependency data becomes stale while the dependency is unavailable.","security_implication":"Fallback data must not bypass current authorization or security policy requirements.","performance_implication":"Local fallback may reduce dependency latency but can increase memory or synchronization costs.","test_case":"Disable the critical dependency and verify the documented degraded behavior.","testing_guidance":"Use dependency fault injection and verify both functional and non-functional acceptance criteria.","verification_method":"Trace service success criteria through every mandatory dependency.","verification_guidance":"Reject availability claims that omit a critical dependency.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B13-R01","D54-B13-R03"],"depends_on":["D54-B13-R01"],"requires":["Dependency graph"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B13-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B13-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Availability","subtopic":"Degraded Operation","concept":"Architecture decision for controlled degraded functionality","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When is degraded operation preferable to complete service failure?","answer":"Degraded operation is appropriate when a subset of functionality can remain correct and safe without unavailable dependencies or capabilities, and the degraded state is explicitly defined.","explanation":"A degraded mode is valuable only when the remaining behavior preserves required invariants and does not create misleading success states.","principle":"Partial availability must preserve correctness and safety.","rule":"Do not degrade into behavior that appears successful while silently violating essential requirements.","objective":"Maintain useful service behavior during partial dependency or capability loss.","requirements":["Define degraded functions","Define unavailable functions","Define user-visible state","Preserve critical invariants"],"constraints":["Reduced functionality may affect user expectations","Some operations may require strong dependencies"],"preconditions":["Failure and degraded states are modeled"],"input":"Availability requirement and dependency failure scenarios.","output":"Explicit degraded-operation architecture.","expected_behavior":"Unavailable capabilities fail clearly while safe supported capabilities continue operating.","correct_example":"Allow cached read access while disabling writes when the authoritative write dependency is unavailable.","invalid_example":"Accept writes locally without a defined reconciliation strategy merely to keep the interface available.","failure_mode":"Degraded operation creates inconsistent or misleading state.","cause":"Availability was prioritized without defining correctness boundaries.","root_cause":"Degraded-mode semantics were incomplete.","solution":"Define safe capabilities, blocked operations, state indicators, and recovery behavior.","tradeoff":"Degraded availability preserves some utility but may reduce freshness or functionality.","edge_case":"The dependency returns intermittently and the system repeatedly switches between normal and degraded states.","security_implication":"Degraded mode must not bypass authorization or security controls.","performance_implication":"Fallback mechanisms can increase local resource usage and synchronization work.","test_case":"Simulate dependency loss and verify supported and unsupported operations separately.","testing_guidance":"Test transition into, operation within, and recovery from degraded mode.","verification_method":"Verify that degraded behavior preserves documented invariants and accurately exposes state.","verification_guidance":"Check that every blocked operation has an explicit failure response.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B13-R02","D54-B13-R04"],"depends_on":["D54-B13-R02"],"requires":["Defined degraded-state semantics"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B13-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B13-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Availability","subtopic":"Recovery Dependency","concept":"Architecture decision for independent recovery paths","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should recovery mechanisms avoid depending on the same failed subsystem they are intended to recover?","answer":"Recovery paths should preserve enough independent capability to diagnose, control, and restore the affected service even when the primary subsystem is unavailable.","explanation":"A recovery mechanism that requires the failed service, storage path, control plane, or credential system may be unable to operate during the failure it is intended to mitigate.","principle":"Recovery capability must survive the failure conditions it addresses.","rule":"Do not make essential recovery control dependent on the primary failed path without a validated fallback.","objective":"Ensure recovery remains executable during primary-system failure.","requirements":["Identify recovery dependencies","Identify shared failure modes","Provide independent recovery capability where necessary"],"constraints":["True independence may be expensive","Some recovery operations require shared infrastructure"],"preconditions":["Recovery workflow is documented"],"input":"Recovery process and dependency graph.","output":"Recovery architecture with identified independent control paths.","expected_behavior":"Recovery remains possible when the primary service path fails.","correct_example":"Maintain an independent administrative control path for restoring a failed service.","invalid_example":"Require the failed application itself to issue the command needed to restart its own unavailable control subsystem.","failure_mode":"Recovery cannot start because the recovery mechanism depends on the failed component.","cause":"Recovery dependencies were not included in architecture analysis.","root_cause":"Recovery was treated as an external procedure rather than an architectural capability.","solution":"Map recovery dependencies and remove or mitigate circular dependencies.","tradeoff":"Independent recovery infrastructure increases operational complexity and cost.","edge_case":"The independent recovery path shares an underlying infrastructure failure domain.","security_implication":"Recovery paths require strong authentication, authorization, and audit controls because they often have elevated authority.","performance_implication":"Recovery systems generally prioritize control availability over normal-path performance.","test_case":"Simulate failure of the primary subsystem and execute the recovery procedure using only the documented recovery path.","testing_guidance":"Conduct controlled recovery drills and record observed dependencies.","verification_method":"Dependency review plus recovery execution evidence.","verification_guidance":"Mark recovery independence as unverified if it has not been exercised under relevant failure conditions.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B13-R03","D54-B13-R05"],"depends_on":["D54-B13-R03"],"requires":["Recovery dependency model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B13-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Availability","subtopic":"Availability Verification","concept":"Architecture decision verification through failure scenarios","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should an availability architecture decision be verified?","answer":"Verify the architecture against defined failure scenarios, dependency failures, degraded modes, and recovery procedures rather than relying only on normal-operation tests.","explanation":"Availability is a behavior under failure, so verification must exercise relevant failure conditions.","principle":"Availability claims require failure-oriented evidence.","rule":"Normal operation cannot by itself verify availability under failure.","objective":"Validate that architecture behaves as intended when components or dependencies fail.","requirements":["Failure scenarios","Expected degraded or recovery behavior","Acceptance criteria"],"constraints":["Some failure scenarios may be unsafe to inject in production","Test environments may not perfectly reproduce infrastructure failures"],"preconditions":["Failure model exists"],"input":"Availability architecture and failure scenarios.","output":"Failure-oriented verification evidence.","expected_behavior":"Observed behavior matches defined availability and recovery requirements.","correct_example":"Test loss of a service instance, critical dependency, and relevant failure domain separately.","invalid_example":"Run only functional happy-path tests and infer availability from successful requests.","failure_mode":"Latent availability failures remain undiscovered.","cause":"Verification focused on normal behavior.","root_cause":"Availability was not treated as a failure-state property.","solution":"Build controlled failure scenarios mapped to architecture assumptions.","tradeoff":"Failure testing requires additional infrastructure and operational controls.","edge_case":"Multiple failures occur simultaneously and invalidate assumptions made for single-failure tests.","security_implication":"Failure tests must ensure injected conditions cannot expose protected data or bypass controls.","performance_implication":"Recovery and failover behavior should be measured for latency and capacity impacts.","test_case":"Execute documented failure scenarios and compare service state with acceptance criteria.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled fault injection, recovery drills, and dependency outage tests where safe.","verification_method":"Compare observed failure and recovery behavior with the architecture decision's claims.","verification_guidance":"Record untested scenarios as verification gaps rather than inferred successes.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B13-R01","D54-B13-R02","D54-B13-R03","D54-B13-R04"],"depends_on":["D54-B13-R01","D54-B13-R02","D54-B13-R03","D54-B13-R04"],"requires":["Failure scenario catalogue"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["Availability failure-scenario validation"],"verifies":["D54-B13-R01","D54-B13-R02","D54-B13-R03","D54-B13-R04"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B14-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Reliability and Resilience","subtopic":"Failure Isolation","concept":"Architecture decision for fault isolation boundaries","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture boundaries be selected to prevent one component failure from propagating?","answer":"Place isolation boundaries around components whose failures could otherwise propagate through shared resources, synchronous dependencies, or uncontrolled retries, while preserving the communication required for correct operation.","explanation":"Isolation is effective only when the boundary blocks realistic propagation paths without eliminating necessary recovery or coordination.","principle":"Resilience depends on controlling failure propagation paths.","rule":"Do not equate logical component separation with effective failure isolation.","objective":"Limit blast radius while preserving required system behavior.","requirements":["Identify propagation paths","Identify shared resources","Define isolation behavior"],"constraints":["Isolation may reduce functionality","Shared infrastructure can bypass logical boundaries"],"preconditions":["Failure propagation model exists"],"input":"Component dependency and failure-propagation graph.","output":"Explicit resilience isolation architecture.","expected_behavior":"Failure in one component is contained within the documented boundary unless an explicit dependency requires propagation.","correct_example":"Isolate resource pools so saturation in one workload cannot exhaust capacity reserved for another critical workload.","invalid_example":"Declare two services isolated while they share an unbounded connection pool.","failure_mode":"A local failure exhausts a shared resource and affects unrelated components.","cause":"Isolation was defined at the service boundary only.","root_cause":"Shared resource dependencies were omitted from failure analysis.","solution":"Identify and isolate shared resources or enforce bounded quotas.","tradeoff":"Stronger isolation can reduce resource utilization efficiency.","edge_case":"Recovery requires temporary access across the normal isolation boundary.","security_implication":"Isolation boundaries must not create unintended privilege escalation paths.","performance_implication":"Resource isolation may reduce peak sharing efficiency but improve tail behavior under contention.","test_case":"Exhaust one component's allocated resources and verify protected components remain within their expected operating bounds.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled resource-exhaustion tests.","verification_method":"Validate propagation paths against the isolation model.","verification_guidance":"Test shared-resource failure paths, not only direct service failure.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B14-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Failure propagation model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B14-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B14-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Reliability and Resilience","subtopic":"Retry Resilience","concept":"Architecture decision for bounded retry behavior","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should retry behavior be architected to improve resilience without amplifying failures?","answer":"Retries should be bounded, classified by error semantics, coordinated with timeouts and capacity controls, and designed to avoid multiplying load during dependency degradation.","explanation":"Retries can recover transient failures but can also transform a partial outage into a larger overload.","principle":"Retries are a load-generating resilience mechanism and must be capacity-aware.","rule":"Do not retry every failure indefinitely or independently at every architectural layer.","objective":"Use retries to recover transient failures without causing retry amplification.","requirements":["Classify retryable failures","Bound attempts and duration","Define backoff behavior","Coordinate timeout budgets"],"constraints":["Some operations are non-idempotent","Multiple layers may retry the same operation"],"preconditions":["Failure taxonomy and operation semantics are known"],"input":"Dependency failure behavior and operation retry policy.","output":"Bounded and coordinated retry architecture.","expected_behavior":"Transient failures receive controlled retry opportunities while persistent failures terminate predictably.","correct_example":"Retry an idempotent network request with bounded exponential backoff while respecting an overall operation deadline.","invalid_example":"Allow client, service, and dependency layers to each retry indefinitely.","failure_mode":"Retry amplification overloads an already degraded dependency.","cause":"Retry policies were designed independently at multiple layers.","root_cause":"No end-to-end retry budget or ownership existed.","solution":"Define a bounded retry budget and designate the appropriate layer for retry responsibility.","tradeoff":"Fewer retries may reduce recovery from transient faults but protect system capacity.","edge_case":"A retryable operation becomes non-idempotent after partial external execution.","security_implication":"Retries must not accidentally repeat security-sensitive side effects without appropriate idempotency controls.","performance_implication":"Backoff and retry budgets directly affect latency and downstream load.","test_case":"Simulate dependency failures and measure retry count, load, latency, and final operation state.","testing_guidance":"Use failure injection and retry-budget assertions.","verification_method":"Inspect retry policy across all layers and validate total retry behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that retry count remains bounded under persistent failure.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B14-R01","D54-B14-R03"],"depends_on":["D54-B14-R01"],"requires":["Retry classification","Operation idempotency model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B14-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B14-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Reliability and Resilience","subtopic":"Graceful Degradation","concept":"Architecture decision for resilience under dependency failure","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should resilience architecture determine which functions may degrade during dependency failure?","answer":"Classify functions by criticality and correctness requirements, then define explicit degraded behavior for functions that can safely continue without the failed dependency.","explanation":"Graceful degradation should be deliberate. Continuing with stale, incomplete, or unsafe data can be worse than refusing the operation.","principle":"Degradation must preserve the invariants that matter for the affected function.","rule":"Do not continue an operation in degraded mode when required correctness cannot be established.","objective":"Preserve safe partial functionality during dependency failures.","requirements":["Function criticality classification","Dependency requirement mapping","Degraded-state semantics"],"constraints":["Some functions require authoritative dependencies","Stale data may be unsafe"],"preconditions":["Functions and dependencies are classified"],"input":"Dependency failure scenarios and function criticality.","output":"Degradation policy by function.","expected_behavior":"Only functions with safe degraded semantics remain available.","correct_example":"Permit display of cached non-sensitive informational data while blocking transactions requiring authoritative state.","invalid_example":"Permit financial state changes using stale cached balances because the dependency is unavailable.","failure_mode":"Degraded mode causes incorrect state changes.","cause":"Availability was prioritized over functional correctness.","root_cause":"Criticality and correctness requirements were not classified per function.","solution":"Define safe degradation boundaries per operation.","tradeoff":"Blocking some operations preserves correctness but reduces apparent availability.","edge_case":"Cached data is fresh enough for one function but insufficient for another.","security_implication":"Degraded paths must preserve current authorization and data-access restrictions.","performance_implication":"Fallback caches may reduce dependency load but require refresh and invalidation mechanisms.","test_case":"Fail the dependency and verify each operation follows its documented degradation policy.","testing_guidance":"Use operation-level negative and degraded-mode tests.","verification_method":"Compare observed behavior with function-specific degradation rules.","verification_guidance":"Ensure unavailable authoritative operations fail explicitly.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B14-R02","D54-B14-R04"],"depends_on":["D54-B14-R02"],"requires":["Function criticality model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B14-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B14-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Reliability and Resilience","subtopic":"Recovery Semantics","concept":"Architecture decision for recovery state ownership","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should recovery state be represented so that repeated recovery attempts do not create conflicting actions?","answer":"Assign authoritative ownership to recovery state and make recovery transitions explicit, observable, and safe to repeat where possible.","explanation":"Recovery can involve multiple controllers, operators, and automated mechanisms. Without authoritative state, they may issue conflicting actions based on stale observations.","principle":"Recovery requires explicit state ownership and transition semantics.","rule":"Do not allow multiple recovery authorities to mutate recovery state without coordination.","objective":"Prevent conflicting recovery actions and ambiguous recovery progress.","requirements":["Recovery state model","State ownership","Transition rules","Concurrency control"],"constraints":["Operators and automation may act concurrently","Recovery state may lag physical state"],"preconditions":["Recovery process is defined"],"input":"Recovery workflow and actor model.","output":"Authoritative recovery-state architecture.","expected_behavior":"All recovery actors use a consistent state model and conflicting transitions are rejected or reconciled explicitly.","correct_example":"Use a durable recovery state with versioned transitions so stale recovery commands can be detected.","invalid_example":"Allow each recovery worker to infer recovery status solely from local observations and independently issue actions.","failure_mode":"Conflicting recovery actions produce oscillation or repeated side effects.","cause":"No authoritative recovery state existed.","root_cause":"Recovery control-plane ownership was undefined.","solution":"Define authoritative state, transition ownership, and stale-action rejection.","tradeoff":"Coordination adds complexity but prevents uncontrolled concurrent recovery.","edge_case":"Authoritative recovery state is temporarily unavailable while physical state continues changing.","security_implication":"Recovery authority must be strongly authenticated and audited.","performance_implication":"State coordination adds latency but can reduce repeated or conflicting work.","test_case":"Issue concurrent recovery actions from stale and current actors and verify stale transitions are rejected or reconciled.","testing_guidance":"Use concurrency and stale-state tests.","verification_method":"Verify state ownership and transition invariants under concurrent recovery attempts.","verification_guidance":"Record physical-state versus control-state divergence explicitly.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B14-R02","D54-B14-R05"],"depends_on":["D54-B14-R02"],"requires":["Recovery state model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":[],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B14-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Reliability and Resilience","subtopic":"Resilience Verification","concept":"Architecture decision verification under repeated and combined failures","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should resilience architecture be verified beyond single-component failure tests?","answer":"Verify isolation, retry, degradation, and recovery behavior under repeated, overlapping, and dependency-related failures that are plausible within the defined threat and failure model.","explanation":"Mechanisms that work independently may interact badly when failures overlap.","principle":"Resilience must be verified at the interaction level, not only mechanism level.","rule":"Do not infer combined-failure resilience from isolated mechanism tests.","objective":"Detect emergent failure behavior across resilience mechanisms.","requirements":["Single-failure tests","Interaction scenarios","Recovery validation"],"constraints":["Combined failure testing can be complex","Some scenarios may be unsafe outside controlled environments"],"preconditions":["Resilience mechanisms and failure model exist"],"input":"Resilience architecture and failure scenarios.","output":"Evidence about resilience under interacting failures.","expected_behavior":"System remains within defined safety and recovery boundaries under tested failure combinations.","correct_example":"Test dependency outage while retry logic, degraded mode, and recovery controller are active.","invalid_example":"Test retries, degraded mode, and recovery independently and assume their combination is safe.","failure_mode":"Multiple resilience mechanisms interact to amplify load or create inconsistent state.","cause":"Mechanisms were verified independently only.","root_cause":"No interaction-level resilience verification model existed.","solution":"Define representative combined-failure scenarios and verify resulting state transitions.","tradeoff":"Broader failure testing increases effort but exposes emergent behavior.","edge_case":"A second failure occurs during recovery from the first.","security_implication":"Recovery and degradation paths must preserve security controls under combined failures.","performance_implication":"Combined failures may consume recovery capacity and increase tail latency.","test_case":"Inject a dependency failure during an active recovery or degraded state and verify bounded behavior.","testing_guidance":"Use staged failure injection and explicit recovery assertions.","verification_method":"Compare combined-failure observations with resilience invariants.","verification_guidance":"Record untested failure combinations as verification gaps.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B14-R01","D54-B14-R02","D54-B14-R03","D54-B14-R04"],"depends_on":["D54-B14-R01","D54-B14-R02","D54-B14-R03","D54-B14-R04"],"requires":["Combined failure model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["Combined resilience failure test"],"verifies":["D54-B14-R01","D54-B14-R02","D54-B14-R03","D54-B14-R04"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B15-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Performance and Latency","subtopic":"Latency Budgeting","concept":"Architecture decision based on end-to-end latency budget","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture be selected when an end-to-end latency target must be met across multiple components?","answer":"Define an end-to-end latency budget, allocate it across critical path components, account for network and coordination overhead, and verify tail behavior rather than relying only on average latency.","explanation":"Distributed systems accumulate processing, serialization, queueing, and network latency. Architecture decisions must account for the complete critical path.","principle":"Latency is an end-to-end property of the request path.","rule":"Do not compare components independently without considering their combined critical-path latency.","objective":"Select an architecture capable of meeting defined latency requirements.","requirements":["End-to-end latency target","Critical-path model","Component budget","Tail-latency criterion"],"constraints":["Latency varies with workload and concurrency","Network and queueing effects are variable"],"preconditions":["Representative workload is defined"],"input":"Latency requirement and architecture dependency path.","output":"Latency-budget architecture decision.","expected_behavior":"Critical-path latency remains within the defined target under representative operating conditions.","correct_example":"Allocate latency budget among gateway, service processing, database access, and external dependency calls.","invalid_example":"Choose a database solely because its isolated benchmark is fast without measuring end-to-end behavior.","failure_mode":"End-to-end latency exceeds the target despite individually acceptable component timings.","cause":"Component-local performance was optimized without a critical-path budget.","root_cause":"No end-to-end latency model existed.","solution":"Model the complete request path and allocate measurable budgets.","tradeoff":"Tighter latency budgets may increase architectural complexity or reduce feature flexibility.","edge_case":"A rarely used dependency dominates tail latency only under specific input classes.","security_implication":"Security checks on the critical path must be included in latency budgets rather than bypassed for performance.","performance_implication":"Tail latency, queueing, and concurrency often matter more than simple average latency.","test_case":"Measure end-to-end latency under representative concurrency and compare against the defined target.","testing_guidance":"Collect percentile measurements under controlled workloads.","verification_method":"Compare measured critical-path latency with the allocated and end-to-end budgets.","verification_guidance":"Record workload and measurement conditions with every performance claim.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B15-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Latency target","Representative workload"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B15-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B15-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Performance and Latency","subtopic":"Tail Latency","concept":"Architecture decision prioritizing tail latency for interactive systems","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should architecture decisions prioritize tail latency over average latency?","answer":"Tail latency should receive explicit priority when a small fraction of slow operations materially affects user experience, upstream timeouts, resource utilization, or dependent workflows.","explanation":"Average latency can hide severe outliers that dominate perceived reliability or cause cascading timeouts.","principle":"The appropriate latency statistic depends on the system's failure and user-impact model.","rule":"Do not use average latency as the sole acceptance criterion when outliers have material consequences.","objective":"Prevent architectural decisions from hiding harmful latency outliers.","requirements":["Latency distribution","Relevant percentile target","Impact analysis"],"constraints":["Tail measurements require representative workloads","Rare events may require larger samples"],"preconditions":["Latency-sensitive behavior is identified"],"input":"Latency distribution and system impact requirements.","output":"Tail-aware performance architecture decision.","expected_behavior":"Relevant latency percentiles remain within defined acceptance limits.","correct_example":"Use a high-percentile latency target for an interactive API whose upstream callers have strict deadlines.","invalid_example":"Accept an architecture because average latency is low despite frequent deadline violations in the tail.","failure_mode":"Users or upstream services experience timeouts despite acceptable average performance.","cause":"Average latency masked tail behavior.","root_cause":"Latency acceptance criterion was poorly chosen.","solution":"Define percentile-based targets appropriate to the system's impact model.","tradeoff":"Reducing tail latency may require resource isolation, simpler dependencies, or increased capacity.","edge_case":"Tail latency changes substantially with concurrency or workload mix.","security_implication":"Security controls must remain present when optimizing tail latency.","performance_implication":"Queueing, contention, garbage collection, retries, and dependency latency can dominate tails.","test_case":"Run representative load and compare multiple latency percentiles against acceptance thresholds.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled load testing with sufficient samples and workload characterization.","verification_method":"Verify the selected percentile metrics against documented targets.","verification_guidance":"Do not extrapolate tail behavior from small or non-representative samples.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B15-R01","D54-B15-R03"],"depends_on":["D54-B15-R01"],"requires":["Latency distribution"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B15-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B15-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Performance and Latency","subtopic":"Synchronous Versus Asynchronous Processing","concept":"Architecture decision for asynchronous processing under latency constraints","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an architecture move work out of the synchronous request path?","answer":"Move work asynchronously when the user or caller does not require immediate completion, the operation can tolerate eventual completion, and asynchronous state transitions can be represented and monitored correctly.","explanation":"Asynchronous processing can reduce request-path latency but introduces queueing, delayed completion, retry, ordering, and observability concerns.","principle":"Asynchronous architecture trades immediate completion for decoupling and potentially lower request-path latency.","rule":"Do not make an operation asynchronous if its caller requires a verified immediate result.","objective":"Reduce synchronous critical-path cost without violating functional semantics.","requirements":["Define completion semantics","Define state visibility","Define retry and failure behavior"],"constraints":["Delayed results may change user experience","Asynchronous execution requires durable state or messaging"],"preconditions":["Operation is semantically asynchronous"],"input":"Latency requirement and operation completion semantics.","output":"Synchronous/asynchronous processing decision.","expected_behavior":"Caller receives an accurate state indicating whether the requested operation is pending, complete, or failed.","correct_example":"Queue non-critical report generation and expose a durable job status rather than blocking the request.","invalid_example":"Return success immediately for a transaction whose required side effect has not yet occurred.","failure_mode":"Caller interprets request acceptance as completed business operation.","cause":"Acceptance and completion semantics were conflated.","root_cause":"Asynchronous state model was not defined.","solution":"Define explicit pending, completed, and failed states and their observable contracts.","tradeoff":"Asynchrony reduces request-path latency but increases state-management and monitoring complexity.","edge_case":"The asynchronous job completes after the client has stopped polling.","security_implication":"Job status and results must enforce the same authorization scope as the initiating operation.","performance_implication":"Queueing improves request-path behavior but can introduce backlog latency.","test_case":"Submit an asynchronous operation and verify state transitions through completion and failure.","testing_guidance":"Use integration tests for queue, worker, and status lifecycle.","verification_method":"Verify that completion is not reported before the required side effect is confirmed.","verification_guidance":"Distinguish accepted, started, completed, and verified states.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B15-R01","D54-B15-R04"],"depends_on":["D54-B15-R01"],"requires":["Completion semantics"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B15-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B15-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Performance and Latency","subtopic":"Caching Decisions","concept":"Architecture decision for caching under latency requirements","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should caching be evaluated as an architectural response to latency requirements?","answer":"Use caching when the data has acceptable staleness semantics, the cache can be invalidated or refreshed safely, and the performance benefit justifies consistency and operational complexity.","explanation":"Caching can reduce repeated computation or dependency latency but introduces stale-data and invalidation behavior that must be part of the architecture decision.","principle":"Caching changes consistency semantics and is not merely a performance optimization.","rule":"Do not introduce caching without defining freshness and invalidation requirements.","objective":"Use caching without violating correctness requirements.","requirements":["Freshness requirement","Invalidation strategy","Fallback behavior","Capacity model"],"constraints":["Data may change independently","Cache capacity is finite"],"preconditions":["Read patterns and freshness requirements are known"],"input":"Latency target and data access characteristics.","output":"Caching architecture decision with consistency semantics.","expected_behavior":"Cache hits provide acceptable data freshness and cache misses recover through the authoritative source.","correct_example":"Cache immutable or versioned reference data with explicit expiry or version validation.","invalid_example":"Cache authorization decisions indefinitely without accounting for policy changes.","failure_mode":"Stale cached data causes incorrect or unauthorized behavior.","cause":"Freshness requirements were omitted from cache design.","root_cause":"Cache was treated as transparent to correctness semantics.","solution":"Define cacheable data classes, freshness bounds, invalidation behavior, and authoritative fallback.","tradeoff":"Caching can improve latency and reduce dependency load at the cost of freshness and complexity.","edge_case":"Cache and source become temporarily inconsistent during an update.","security_implication":"Sensitive authorization or policy data requires strict freshness semantics and must not rely on stale state beyond its permitted validity.","performance_implication":"Cache effectiveness depends on locality, hit rate, memory capacity, and invalidation cost.","test_case":"Change authoritative data and verify cache behavior within the defined freshness window.","testing_guidance":"Test hit, miss, expiry, invalidation, source failure, and stale-data scenarios.","verification_method":"Verify cache behavior against documented freshness and correctness requirements.","verification_guidance":"Measure cache performance only under representative workload conditions.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B15-R03","D54-B15-R05"],"depends_on":["D54-B15-R01"],"requires":["Freshness requirements"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B15-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B15-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Performance and Latency","subtopic":"Performance Verification","concept":"Architecture decision verification using representative workload evidence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should architecture performance decisions be verified without fabricating performance claims?","answer":"Use representative workloads, explicit measurement conditions, appropriate latency or throughput metrics, and repeatable tests. Results must remain scoped to the tested conditions.","explanation":"Performance is workload-dependent. A benchmark result without workload, environment, concurrency, and metric definitions cannot establish general architectural superiority.","principle":"Performance evidence is conditional on measurement context.","rule":"Never convert a workload-specific measurement into a universal performance claim.","objective":"Produce trustworthy evidence for performance-related architecture decisions.","requirements":["Representative workload","Defined metrics","Measurement conditions","Acceptance criteria"],"constraints":["Measurements vary across environments","Synthetic workloads may not represent production"],"preconditions":["Performance objective is measurable"],"input":"Architecture candidate and performance test specification.","output":"Condition-scoped performance evidence.","expected_behavior":"Measured behavior is reported with enough context to reproduce or interpret the result.","correct_example":"Record percentile latency under a defined concurrency level and representative request mix.","invalid_example":"State that an architecture is faster without specifying workload or measurement method.","failure_mode":"Architecture is selected using misleading or non-comparable performance evidence.","cause":"Measurement context was omitted.","root_cause":"Performance evidence lacked methodological controls.","solution":"Capture workload, environment, metric definitions, sample size, and acceptance criteria.","tradeoff":"Rigorous measurement takes more effort but reduces decision uncertainty.","edge_case":"A benchmark result changes significantly with workload distribution.","security_implication":"Performance testing must retain required security controls unless the test explicitly measures an isolated component and labels the limitation.","performance_implication":"Performance evidence itself must distinguish measured from estimated or inferred claims.","test_case":"Repeat the benchmark under documented conditions and compare results with acceptance criteria.","testing_guidance":"Use representative and boundary workloads, not only idealized microbenchmarks.","verification_method":"Audit measurement metadata and compare observed results with the architecture claim.","verification_guidance":"Classify unsupported extrapolation as inference rather than measured fact.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B15-R01","D54-B15-R02","D54-B15-R03","D54-B15-R04"],"depends_on":["D54-B15-R01"],"requires":["Performance measurement methodology"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["Representative performance benchmark"],"verifies":["D54-B15-R01","D54-B15-R02","D54-B15-R03","D54-B15-R04"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B16-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Scalability and Capacity","subtopic":"Capacity Modeling","concept":"Architecture decision based on explicit capacity assumptions","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should an architecture decision include when selecting a design for expected scale?","answer":"It should define workload assumptions, capacity drivers, resource limits, growth expectations, and scaling behavior rather than relying on an unspecified future-scale claim.","explanation":"Scale is meaningful only relative to workload characteristics and resource constraints.","principle":"Architecture scale decisions must be tied to explicit workload and capacity models.","rule":"Do not choose architecture solely because a component is marketed or described as scalable.","objective":"Select architecture based on measurable capacity requirements.","requirements":["Workload model","Capacity drivers","Resource limits","Growth assumptions"],"constraints":["Future workload is uncertain","Capacity depends on implementation and environment"],"preconditions":["Expected workload classes are defined"],"input":"Workload and growth assumptions.","output":"Capacity-oriented architecture decision.","expected_behavior":"Architecture can handle the defined workload within documented resource and performance limits.","correct_example":"Model request rate, data volume, concurrency, and storage growth before selecting scaling mechanisms.","invalid_example":"Adopt distributed infrastructure solely because future traffic is assumed to be large.","failure_mode":"Architecture incurs complexity without addressing the actual capacity bottleneck.","cause":"Scale was treated as a qualitative property.","root_cause":"No explicit capacity model existed.","solution":"Define workload drivers and identify which resources become limiting first.","tradeoff":"More scalable designs can increase operational and coordination complexity.","edge_case":"One workload dimension grows while another remains constant.","security_implication":"Scaling must preserve tenant and authorization boundaries.","performance_implication":"Capacity limits should be connected to latency and throughput acceptance criteria.","test_case":"Load the architecture according to the defined workload model and identify resource saturation points.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled capacity tests and resource monitoring.","verification_method":"Compare measured capacity behavior with the documented model.","verification_guidance":"Record assumptions that were not measured as assumptions rather than facts.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B16-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Workload model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B16-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B16-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Scalability and Capacity","subtopic":"Scaling Dimension","concept":"Architecture decision for scaling along the actual bottleneck dimension","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should architecture scale along the resource dimension that actually limits capacity?","answer":"Identify whether CPU, memory, storage, network, database connections, queue capacity, coordination, or another resource is the limiting factor, then select a scaling mechanism that addresses that bottleneck.","explanation":"Adding more instances does not solve a shared database, network, storage, or coordination bottleneck if that dependency remains saturated.","principle":"Scale the limiting resource, not merely the visible application tier.","rule":"Do not assume horizontal application scaling removes all capacity constraints.","objective":"Match scaling architecture to the dominant resource bottleneck.","requirements":["Resource utilization analysis","Bottleneck identification","Scaling mechanism"],"constraints":["Bottlenecks can move after scaling","Shared dependencies may limit independent scaling"],"preconditions":["Representative workload and resource metrics exist"],"input":"Capacity measurements and architecture topology.","output":"Bottleneck-specific scaling decision.","expected_behavior":"Scaling increases useful capacity without simply moving saturation to another constrained resource.","correct_example":"Increase database read capacity or reduce read pressure when database reads, rather than application CPU, are the limiting resource.","invalid_example":"Double application instances while a single database connection limit remains saturated.","failure_mode":"Application capacity increases without improving end-to-end capacity.","cause":"Scaling targeted the wrong resource.","root_cause":"Bottleneck analysis was absent or stale.","solution":"Profile resource saturation and select scaling based on the measured limiting dimension.","tradeoff":"Targeted scaling may require more specialized architecture.","edge_case":"Scaling one resource exposes a different bottleneck.","security_implication":"Scaling changes must preserve isolation and authorization semantics.","performance_implication":"Capacity should be evaluated across the entire dependency graph.","test_case":"Increase the selected capacity dimension and verify whether the expected bottleneck moves or improves.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled scaling experiments with resource-level measurements.","verification_method":"Compare pre- and post-change bottleneck evidence.","verification_guidance":"Do not infer scalability from instance count alone.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B16-R01"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B16-R01","D54-B16-R03"],"depends_on":["D54-B16-R01"],"requires":["Bottleneck measurements"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B16-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B16-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Scalability and Capacity","subtopic":"Elasticity","concept":"Architecture decision for elastic versus fixed capacity","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should an architecture use elastic scaling rather than fixed capacity?","answer":"Elastic scaling is appropriate when workload varies sufficiently to justify dynamic capacity changes and the system can scale within the required time and safety boundaries.","explanation":"Elasticity adds control-plane and operational complexity. It is valuable when workload variation or capacity economics justify that complexity.","principle":"Elasticity should solve a real variability or capacity-management problem.","rule":"Do not add automatic scaling merely because it is available.","objective":"Select a capacity strategy consistent with workload variability and response-time requirements.","requirements":["Workload variability model","Scaling response time","Capacity safety limits"],"constraints":["Scaling may lag sudden demand","Autoscaling signals can be noisy"],"preconditions":["Workload variability is understood"],"input":"Workload pattern and capacity requirements.","output":"Elastic or fixed-capacity architecture decision.","expected_behavior":"Capacity changes remain within safe bounds while meeting defined workload requirements.","correct_example":"Use bounded automatic scaling for workloads with predictable but substantial demand variation.","invalid_example":"Allow unbounded scaling from a noisy metric without resource quotas or stabilization.","failure_mode":"Scaling oscillates, overprovisions resources, or exhausts a constrained dependency.","cause":"Scaling signal and capacity boundaries were poorly designed.","root_cause":"Elasticity was treated as an independent feature rather than a feedback-control problem.","solution":"Define scaling signals, thresholds, cooldown behavior, minimum/maximum capacity, and dependency limits.","tradeoff":"Elasticity can improve resource utilization but introduces control-loop complexity.","edge_case":"Demand changes faster than scaling can provision capacity.","security_implication":"Scaling mechanisms require controlled authority and must not bypass tenant or resource quotas.","performance_implication":"Scale-out latency and warm-up time must be included in capacity planning.","test_case":"Apply increasing and decreasing workload patterns and verify bounded, stable scaling behavior.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled load ramps and scaling-policy tests.","verification_method":"Verify scaling decisions against defined bounds and workload response requirements.","verification_guidance":"Treat scaling-policy behavior as unverified until exercised under representative demand changes.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B16-R01","D54-B16-R02"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B16-R04"],"depends_on":["D54-B16-R01","D54-B16-R02"],"requires":["Capacity limits","Scaling policy"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B16-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B16-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Scalability and Capacity","subtopic":"Capacity Failure","concept":"Architecture decision for overload behavior when capacity is exhausted","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture behave when demand exceeds available capacity?","answer":"Define explicit overload behavior such as admission control, bounded queues, prioritization, graceful rejection, or degradation rather than allowing uncontrolled resource exhaustion.","explanation":"Every system has finite capacity. Architecture should specify what happens when demand exceeds safe operating limits.","principle":"Overload is an expected operating condition that requires explicit policy.","rule":"Do not rely on uncontrolled queue growth or resource exhaustion as an overload strategy.","objective":"Preserve critical system behavior under capacity exhaustion.","requirements":["Capacity limits","Admission policy","Priority policy","Failure response"],"constraints":["Some requests may be dropped or delayed","Prioritization can create fairness trade-offs"],"preconditions":["Capacity limits are identified"],"input":"Capacity model and overload scenarios.","output":"Explicit overload-handling architecture.","expected_behavior":"System remains within safe resource bounds while applying documented admission or degradation policy.","correct_example":"Reject low-priority work when queue capacity reaches a defined threshold while preserving critical operations.","invalid_example":"Accept unlimited work into memory-backed queues until the process fails.","failure_mode":"Resource exhaustion causes broad system failure.","cause":"No explicit overload boundary existed.","root_cause":"Capacity was treated as effectively unlimited.","solution":"Introduce bounded resources and explicit overload behavior.","tradeoff":"Controlled rejection reduces apparent availability for some requests but protects overall system stability.","edge_case":"High-priority traffic itself exceeds capacity.","security_implication":"Admission and prioritization must not permit unauthorized users to consume protected capacity.","performance_implication":"Queue limits and backpressure directly affect latency and throughput.","test_case":"Drive demand beyond capacity and verify bounded resource usage and documented rejection behavior.","testing_guidance":"Use overload and saturation tests with resource monitoring.","verification_method":"Verify resource bounds and priority behavior under overload.","verification_guidance":"Confirm that overload does not cause uncontrolled cascading failure.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B16-R02"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B16-R03","D54-B16-R05"],"depends_on":["D54-B16-R02"],"requires":["Capacity limits","Overload policy"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B16-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B16-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Scalability and Capacity","subtopic":"Capacity Verification","concept":"Architecture decision verification through saturation testing","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should scalability architecture be verified?","answer":"Test representative workload growth, identify saturation points, observe bottleneck movement, and verify overload behavior against explicit safety and performance criteria.","explanation":"Scalability claims require evidence about how capacity changes with workload, not merely evidence that the system can process one workload level.","principle":"Scalability is a relationship between workload growth and system capacity.","rule":"Do not infer scalability from a single successful load level.","objective":"Validate capacity growth and failure behavior.","requirements":["Workload growth model","Resource measurements","Saturation criteria","Overload criteria"],"constraints":["Testing environments may differ from production","Large-scale tests can be expensive"],"preconditions":["Capacity model exists"],"input":"Architecture and workload growth scenarios.","output":"Capacity and saturation evidence.","expected_behavior":"System capacity and overload behavior match documented expectations within tested conditions.","correct_example":"Increase workload systematically while recording resource utilization, latency, throughput, and saturation points.","invalid_example":"Run one load test below saturation and conclude the architecture is scalable.","failure_mode":"Capacity limits and nonlinear behavior remain undiscovered.","cause":"Testing did not cross relevant operating boundaries.","root_cause":"Scalability was evaluated as a binary property.","solution":"Use workload ramps and identify saturation behavior across relevant dimensions.","tradeoff":"Comprehensive capacity testing requires more infrastructure and time.","edge_case":"A dependency saturates before the primary application tier.","security_implication":"Capacity tests must preserve security boundaries and detect tenant-specific resource exhaustion.","performance_implication":"Record throughput, latency distributions, resource utilization, and saturation points.","test_case":"Run controlled workload increases until a documented resource or performance boundary is reached.","testing_guidance":"Use repeatable load profiles and monitor the complete dependency chain.","verification_method":"Compare observed scaling and saturation behavior with architecture assumptions.","verification_guidance":"Record extrapolations separately from measured capacity.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B16-R01","D54-B16-R02","D54-B16-R04"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B16-R01","D54-B16-R02","D54-B16-R03","D54-B16-R04"],"depends_on":["D54-B16-R01","D54-B16-R02","D54-B16-R03","D54-B16-R04"],"requires":["Capacity test plan"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["Capacity saturation test"],"verifies":["D54-B16-R01","D54-B16-R02","D54-B16-R03","D54-B16-R04"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B17-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Trust Boundaries","concept":"Architecture decision based on explicit trust boundaries","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How should trust boundaries influence architecture decisions?","answer":"Trust boundaries should be explicit, enforced at component interfaces, and tied to authentication, authorization, validation, and data-handling requirements.","explanation":"A logical component boundary is not automatically a security boundary. Security architecture must identify where trust changes and what controls enforce the boundary.","principle":"Trust transitions require explicit security controls.","rule":"Do not assume internal components are trusted merely because they reside within the same deployment environment.","objective":"Prevent implicit trust from becoming an architectural security weakness.","requirements":["Trust-zone identification","Authentication requirements","Authorization policy","Input validation"],"constraints":["Some internal communication may be privileged","Security controls add complexity and overhead"],"preconditions":["Assets and actors are identified"],"input":"System architecture and trust relationships.","output":"Trust-boundary architecture decision.","expected_behavior":"Cross-boundary interactions apply appropriate identity, authorization, and validation controls.","correct_example":"Treat a service-to-service API as a trust boundary when different security principals or privilege domains are involved.","invalid_example":"Accept privileged internal requests solely because they originate from an internal network.","failure_mode":"Compromised or misconfigured internal components gain unintended privileges.","cause":"Network location was treated as proof of trust.","root_cause":"Trust boundaries were not explicitly modeled.","solution":"Define trust zones and enforce identity, authorization, and validation at boundary crossings.","tradeoff":"Stronger boundary controls can add latency and operational complexity.","edge_case":"A component operates in multiple trust contexts depending on the caller.","security_implication":"Trust-boundary enforcement is itself a primary security requirement.","performance_implication":"Authentication and authorization checks consume computational and network resources.","test_case":"Attempt cross-boundary operations with valid, invalid, expired, and insufficient identities.","testing_guidance":"Use negative authorization and boundary-validation tests.","verification_method":"Map every sensitive interaction to a trust boundary and required controls.","verification_guidance":"Verify controls at enforcement points rather than relying on documentation alone.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B17-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Trust model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B17-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B17-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Least Privilege","concept":"Architecture decision for least-privilege component permissions","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How should architecture distribute permissions among components?","answer":"Grant each component only the permissions required for its defined responsibilities, separate administrative authority from routine execution, and constrain permissions by resource and operation where practical.","explanation":"Broad component privileges increase blast radius when a component is compromised or misconfigured.","principle":"Architectural responsibilities should map to narrowly scoped authority.","rule":"Do not grant broad administrative privileges merely because they simplify integration.","objective":"Reduce compromise impact through architectural privilege minimization.","requirements":["Component responsibility mapping","Permission scope","Resource scope","Administrative separation"],"constraints":["Fine-grained permissions can increase configuration complexity","Some systems expose coarse-grained permission models"],"preconditions":["Resources and operations are classified"],"input":"Component responsibilities and permission model.","output":"Least-privilege architecture decision.","expected_behavior":"Components can perform required operations without unnecessary authority.","correct_example":"Allow a reporting service read-only access to the specific datasets it needs instead of unrestricted database administration.","invalid_example":"Give every service full database privileges to avoid permission troubleshooting.","failure_mode":"Compromise of one service exposes or modifies unrelated resources.","cause":"Permissions were optimized for integration convenience.","root_cause":"Authority was not mapped to component responsibilities.","solution":"Define minimum required operations and resources for each component identity.","tradeoff":"Least privilege can require more policy management and integration work.","edge_case":"Emergency recovery requires temporary elevated authority.","security_implication":"Temporary elevation should be explicitly authorized, time-bounded, and audited.","performance_implication":"Fine-grained authorization can add evaluation overhead.","test_case":"Attempt authorized and unauthorized resource operations using each component identity.","testing_guidance":"Use permission-boundary tests and negative authorization cases.","verification_method":"Compare effective permissions with documented responsibilities.","verification_guidance":"Detect permissions that are not justified by a defined component requirement.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B17-R01"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B17-R03"],"depends_on":["D54-B17-R01"],"requires":["Permission model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B17-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B17-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Defense in Depth","concept":"Architecture decision for independent security control layers","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"When should architecture use multiple independent security controls instead of one primary control?","answer":"Use layered controls when failure or bypass of one control would otherwise expose a high-impact asset and the additional controls address meaningfully different failure modes.","explanation":"Defense in depth is valuable when layers are sufficiently independent. Duplicate controls with the same failure mode provide less additional protection.","principle":"Independent controls reduce the consequences of individual control failure.","rule":"Do not count repeated implementations of the same control as independent security layers without analyzing shared failure modes.","objective":"Reduce single-control failure risk for high-impact assets.","requirements":["Threat model","Control diversity","Independent failure analysis"],"constraints":["Additional controls increase complexity and operational cost"],"preconditions":["Critical assets and threat paths are known"],"input":"Threat model and existing security controls.","output":"Layered security architecture decision.","expected_behavior":"Failure of one control does not automatically eliminate every relevant protection layer.","correct_example":"Combine strong authentication, authorization, input validation, and audit controls for a sensitive operation.","invalid_example":"Deploy the same ineffective filter at multiple network layers and classify the result as independent defense in depth.","failure_mode":"A shared weakness bypasses all supposedly independent controls.","cause":"Control diversity was assumed without analyzing common failure modes.","root_cause":"Security layers were counted rather than evaluated for independence.","solution":"Identify distinct threats and control failure modes and add layers that address different risks.","tradeoff":"More layers increase implementation and operational complexity.","edge_case":"Multiple controls depend on the same identity or policy provider.","security_implication":"Shared control dependencies can create a common security failure domain.","performance_implication":"Additional checks may add latency and resource consumption.","test_case":"Evaluate whether bypassing one control still leaves the expected protection from other controls.","testing_guidance":"Use threat-driven negative tests and control-failure scenarios.","verification_method":"Analyze control dependency and failure-mode independence.","verification_guidance":"Do not claim defense in depth where all layers share the same critical dependency.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B17-R01","D54-B17-R02"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B17-R04"],"depends_on":["D54-B17-R01","D54-B17-R02"],"requires":["Threat model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B17-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B17-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Security Failure Handling","concept":"Architecture decision for fail-secure versus fail-open behavior","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How should architecture choose failure behavior for security-critical controls?","answer":"For security-critical decisions, the architecture should generally fail in a way that does not grant unauthorized capability, while explicitly defining safe availability-preserving exceptions where required and justified.","explanation":"A control failure can create a security boundary violation if the system defaults to allowing access. The correct behavior depends on the protected operation and defined safety requirements.","principle":"Security failure behavior must preserve authorization boundaries.","rule":"Do not use fail-open behavior for security-critical decisions without explicit risk analysis and compensating controls.","objective":"Prevent control failure from silently granting unauthorized access.","requirements":["Security-critical operation classification","Failure behavior","Compensating control analysis"],"constraints":["Strict failure behavior may reduce availability","Some systems require carefully bounded emergency access"],"preconditions":["Security boundary and failure modes are identified"],"input":"Security control and dependency failure model.","output":"Failure-mode architecture decision.","expected_behavior":"Control failure cannot silently expand authority beyond the documented policy.","correct_example":"Deny a privileged operation when its authorization decision cannot be reliably established.","invalid_example":"Allow privileged access whenever the authorization service is unavailable.","failure_mode":"Dependency failure becomes an authorization bypass.","cause":"Availability was prioritized without analyzing security consequences.","root_cause":"Security failure semantics were unspecified.","solution":"Define explicit failure behavior and controlled emergency procedures.","tradeoff":"Fail-secure behavior may reduce availability for legitimate users during control outages.","edge_case":"Emergency recovery requires temporary access while normal authorization infrastructure is unavailable.","security_implication":"Emergency access must have independent authorization, narrow scope, time bounds, and auditability.","performance_implication":"Fail-secure checks can add dependency calls or local policy evaluation.","test_case":"Disable the authorization dependency and verify protected operations do not gain unauthorized access.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled authorization-dependency failure tests.","verification_method":"Verify security invariants under control failure.","verification_guidance":"Record any emergency bypass as a separate explicitly governed mechanism.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B17-R01"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B17-R05"],"depends_on":["D54-B17-R01"],"requires":["Security failure model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B17-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B17-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Security Architecture Verification","concept":"Architecture decision verification through threat and control analysis","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should security architecture decisions be verified?","answer":"Trace assets and trust boundaries to threats, controls, failure modes, and security tests, then verify that the controls are actually enforced at the intended boundaries.","explanation":"Security architecture claims require evidence that controls exist, are correctly placed, and remain effective when dependencies fail or inputs are adversarial.","principle":"Security architecture is verified through threat-to-control evidence, not labels or component count.","rule":"Do not treat the presence of a security component as proof that the required security property is enforced.","objective":"Establish evidence that architecture preserves defined security properties.","requirements":["Asset inventory","Threat model","Control mapping","Security verification"],"constraints":["Some security properties require runtime testing","Threat models evolve"],"preconditions":["Security requirements are defined"],"input":"Security architecture and threat/control model.","output":"Threat-to-control verification evidence.","expected_behavior":"Relevant threats map to effective controls with tested enforcement boundaries.","correct_example":"Verify that authorization is enforced at the resource boundary and test unauthorized access through every relevant entry path.","invalid_example":"Assume that a gateway authorization check protects an internal API that has another direct entry path.","failure_mode":"An unprotected path bypasses the assumed security boundary.","cause":"Security verification covered only one entry path.","root_cause":"Threat and trust-boundary analysis was incomplete.","solution":"Enumerate entry paths and verify control enforcement at each applicable boundary.","tradeoff":"Comprehensive security verification requires broader test coverage.","edge_case":"A new integration creates an undocumented access path.","security_implication":"Continuous architecture review is needed as trust boundaries change.","performance_implication":"Security tests and controls may add measurable overhead that should be included in quality analysis.","test_case":"Enumerate access paths and attempt authorized and unauthorized operations through each relevant path.","testing_guidance":"Use threat-driven negative tests and control-failure scenarios.","verification_method":"Map threat → boundary → control → test → evidence.","verification_guidance":"Classify untested controls as unverified rather than assumed secure.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B17-R01","D54-B17-R02","D54-B17-R03","D54-B17-R04"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B17-R01","D54-B17-R02","D54-B17-R03","D54-B17-R04"],"depends_on":["D54-B17-R01","D54-B17-R02","D54-B17-R03","D54-B17-R04"],"requires":["Threat model","Security requirements"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["Security architecture verification"],"verifies":["D54-B17-R01","D54-B17-R02","D54-B17-R03","D54-B17-R04"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B18-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Privacy and Data Protection","subtopic":"Data Minimization","concept":"Architecture decision based on data minimization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"How should data minimization influence architecture decisions?","answer":"Collect, retain, process, and expose only the data required for defined purposes, and avoid architectural dependencies on unnecessary sensitive information.","explanation":"Reducing unnecessary data reduces exposure, storage obligations, breach impact, and accidental secondary use.","principle":"The safest unnecessary sensitive data is data the architecture does not require.","rule":"Do not make sensitive data a shared dependency when the functional requirement can be satisfied without it.","objective":"Reduce privacy exposure through architectural minimization.","requirements":["Purpose definition","Data inventory","Minimum necessary data set","Retention policy"],"constraints":["Some functionality requires specific data","Future reuse must not justify indefinite collection"],"preconditions":["Data elements and purposes are identified"],"input":"Functional requirements and data inventory.","output":"Minimized data architecture.","expected_behavior":"Only required data crosses or persists within each architectural boundary.","correct_example":"Pass a narrowly scoped authorization attribute instead of exposing an entire user profile to a service that needs only one property.","invalid_example":"Expose complete customer records to every service because the records may be useful later.","failure_mode":"Unnecessary services gain access to sensitive information.","cause":"Data sharing was optimized for convenience.","root_cause":"Data requirements were not minimized per component.","solution":"Map each data element to a purpose and remove unnecessary dependencies.","tradeoff":"Minimization can require more explicit data contracts and transformations.","edge_case":"A diagnostic or audit requirement temporarily needs additional data.","security_implication":"Reduced data exposure lowers the impact of unauthorized access but does not replace access controls.","performance_implication":"Smaller payloads can reduce storage, serialization, and network overhead.","test_case":"Inspect service contracts and verify that each transferred data field has a documented purpose.","testing_guidance":"Use contract and data-flow tests to detect unnecessary fields.","verification_method":"Perform data-flow analysis against the approved data inventory.","verification_guidance":"Flag unexplained sensitive fields as architectural exceptions.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B18-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Data inventory","Purpose definitions"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B18-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B18-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Privacy and Data Protection","subtopic":"Data Retention","concept":"Architecture decision for bounded data retention","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should retention requirements influence storage architecture?","answer":"Retention should be explicitly tied to purpose, legal or organizational requirements where applicable, operational needs, and deletion or archival capabilities.","explanation":"Indefinite retention increases exposure and operational cost. Retention architecture should support predictable lifecycle transitions rather than relying on manual deletion.","principle":"Data lifecycle is an architectural concern, not merely an administrative task.","rule":"Do not retain sensitive data indefinitely without a defined purpose and retention justification.","objective":"Design storage around explicit data lifecycle requirements.","requirements":["Retention duration or criterion","Deletion behavior","Archival behavior where required","Lifecycle ownership"],"constraints":["Some records may require long-term retention","Deletion may involve multiple replicas and backups"],"preconditions":["Data classes are identified"],"input":"Data lifecycle requirements and storage architecture.","output":"Retention-aware storage decision.","expected_behavior":"Data transitions through defined lifecycle states and is not retained beyond applicable requirements without justification.","correct_example":"Define retention and deletion policies for primary storage, derived stores, and applicable backups.","invalid_example":"Delete data from the primary database while retaining uncontrolled copies indefinitely in derived systems.","failure_mode":"Sensitive data remains accessible through secondary storage after primary deletion.","cause":"Retention architecture covered only the primary store.","root_cause":"Data lineage and lifecycle were incomplete.","solution":"Map copies, derived datasets, backups, and archival systems into the retention model.","tradeoff":"Comprehensive deletion increases operational complexity and may affect backup design.","edge_case":"Backups have different technical deletion characteristics than primary storage.","security_implication":"Retention increases the window during which sensitive data can be exposed.","performance_implication":"Lifecycle processing can create storage and I/O workloads.","test_case":"Trace a data item through storage, derived copies, and retention expiry and verify the intended lifecycle.","testing_guidance":"Use lifecycle and deletion verification tests.","verification_method":"Audit storage locations against retention policy.","verification_guidance":"Record technically delayed deletion as an explicit limitation where applicable.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B18-R01"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B18-R03"],"depends_on":["D54-B18-R01"],"requires":["Data lifecycle model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B18-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B18-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Privacy and Data Protection","subtopic":"Data Isolation","concept":"Architecture decision for sensitive-data isolation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"When should sensitive data be isolated into separate architectural boundaries?","answer":"Isolation is appropriate when sensitive data has materially different access, retention, processing, or security requirements that can be better enforced through a dedicated boundary.","explanation":"Logical and physical separation can reduce accidental exposure and simplify policy enforcement, but separation is not automatically required for every sensitive field.","principle":"Isolation should correspond to meaningful differences in security and data-handling requirements.","rule":"Do not assume that placing sensitive data in a separate component alone establishes effective protection.","objective":"Reduce sensitive-data exposure through enforceable architectural boundaries.","requirements":["Sensitivity classification","Access model","Boundary controls","Lifecycle requirements"],"constraints":["Isolation increases integration complexity","Some systems require controlled joins or correlation"],"preconditions":["Sensitive data classes are identified"],"input":"Data classification and access requirements.","output":"Sensitive-data isolation decision.","expected_behavior":"Sensitive data is accessible only through explicitly authorized interfaces and workflows.","correct_example":"Expose a narrow service interface for sensitive identity attributes while preventing unrelated services from direct database access.","invalid_example":"Move sensitive tables into a separate database but give all services unrestricted credentials.","failure_mode":"Physical separation exists without meaningful access isolation.","cause":"Storage separation was mistaken for authorization enforcement.","root_cause":"Security boundary was not enforced at the interface and identity layers.","solution":"Combine data isolation with explicit access controls and narrow interfaces.","tradeoff":"Isolation can improve control but may increase latency and integration complexity.","edge_case":"A legitimate workflow needs temporary access to sensitive data.","security_implication":"Sensitive-data access must be authorized, minimized, logged where appropriate, and protected in transit and at rest.","performance_implication":"Isolation may require additional service calls or data transformations.","test_case":"Attempt direct and mediated access using identities with different authorization scopes.","testing_guidance":"Use negative access tests and data-flow analysis.","verification_method":"Verify both storage boundaries and actual access enforcement.","verification_guidance":"Do not equate network or database separation with complete privacy protection.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B18-R01","D54-B17-R01"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B18-R04"],"depends_on":["D54-B18-R01","D54-B17-R01"],"requires":["Data classification","Trust boundary model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B18-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B18-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Privacy and Data Protection","subtopic":"Data Flow and Secondary Use","concept":"Architecture decision controlling secondary data use","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture prevent data collected for one purpose from becoming an uncontrolled dependency for unrelated purposes?","answer":"Separate purpose-specific data flows, define approved secondary uses, and avoid exposing broad datasets through generic interfaces when narrower derived attributes satisfy the requirement.","explanation":"Generic access to raw data encourages uncontrolled secondary use and makes privacy boundaries difficult to enforce.","principle":"Purpose boundaries should be reflected in data interfaces and processing responsibilities.","rule":"Do not expose raw sensitive datasets merely because multiple consumers might eventually request them.","objective":"Maintain purpose limitation through architecture.","requirements":["Purpose classification","Consumer authorization","Data transformation boundaries"],"constraints":["Analytics and operational systems may have different data needs","Derived data may still contain sensitive information"],"preconditions":["Data purposes are documented"],"input":"Data consumers and approved purposes.","output":"Purpose-aware data architecture.","expected_behavior":"Consumers receive only data appropriate to their authorized purpose.","correct_example":"Provide an aggregated metric to an analytics service instead of unrestricted access to individual records when individual records are unnecessary.","invalid_example":"Give an analytics service unrestricted access to operational customer data for convenience.","failure_mode":"Unrelated consumers gain unnecessary access to sensitive raw data.","cause":"Generic data access was favored over purpose-specific interfaces.","root_cause":"Purpose limitation was not represented architecturally.","solution":"Create purpose-specific interfaces and derived data products with explicit authorization.","tradeoff":"Purpose-specific interfaces require more design and maintenance.","edge_case":"A legitimate new use case requires additional data that was not originally approved.","security_implication":"New data use should require explicit authorization and policy review.","performance_implication":"Derived datasets may improve query performance but introduce synchronization and freshness concerns.","test_case":"Review each consumer and verify that its data fields correspond to an approved purpose.","testing_guidance":"Use data-contract and authorization tests.","verification_method":"Trace data field → purpose → consumer → authorization.","verification_guidance":"Flag undocumented secondary uses as architectural exceptions.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B18-R01","D54-B18-R03"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B18-R05"],"depends_on":["D54-B18-R01","D54-B18-R03"],"requires":["Purpose model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B18-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B18-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Privacy and Data Protection","subtopic":"Privacy Verification","concept":"Architecture decision verification through data-flow traceability","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should privacy architecture decisions be verified?","answer":"Trace sensitive data from collection through processing, storage, transfer, derivation, backup, and deletion, and compare each path with approved purpose and access requirements.","explanation":"Privacy failures often occur through secondary copies, derived datasets, logs, or interfaces rather than the primary data store.","principle":"Privacy architecture requires end-to-end data-flow evidence.","rule":"Do not verify privacy by inspecting only the primary database.","objective":"Establish evidence that sensitive data follows approved lifecycle and access boundaries.","requirements":["Data lineage","Purpose mapping","Access mapping","Lifecycle mapping"],"constraints":["Some derived data may be difficult to identify automatically","Backup and logging systems may have separate lifecycle controls"],"preconditions":["Data inventory exists"],"input":"Sensitive-data inventory and architecture data flows.","output":"Privacy traceability evidence.","expected_behavior":"Sensitive data has identifiable purpose, access control, lifecycle, and deletion or archival behavior across relevant systems.","correct_example":"Trace a sensitive field through API payloads, primary storage, derived records, logs, backups, and deletion processes.","invalid_example":"Verify privacy solely by confirming encryption of the primary database.","failure_mode":"Sensitive copies remain uncontrolled outside the primary store.","cause":"Verification scope excluded secondary data paths.","root_cause":"No end-to-end data-flow model existed.","solution":"Perform lineage-based privacy verification across all relevant processing and storage boundaries.","tradeoff":"Comprehensive lineage analysis requires ongoing inventory maintenance.","edge_case":"A derived dataset no longer contains the original field but remains re-identifiable.","security_implication":"Privacy verification should consider re-identification risk and access control.","performance_implication":"Data lineage and lifecycle processing can add operational overhead.","test_case":"Select representative sensitive fields and trace all known lifecycle paths.","testing_guidance":"Combine static data-flow inspection with runtime access tests.","verification_method":"Compare observed data flows with approved privacy architecture.","verification_guidance":"Mark unknown data paths as unresolved rather than assuming compliance.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B18-R01","D54-B18-R02","D54-B18-R03","D54-B18-R04"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B18-R01","D54-B18-R02","D54-B18-R03","D54-B18-R04"],"depends_on":["D54-B18-R01","D54-B18-R02","D54-B18-R03","D54-B18-R04"],"requires":["Data lineage model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["Privacy data-flow audit"],"verifies":["D54-B18-R01","D54-B18-R02","D54-B18-R03","D54-B18-R04"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B19-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Maintainability and Evolvability","subtopic":"Change Boundaries","concept":"Architecture decision based on expected change boundaries","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should expected change patterns influence architecture boundaries?","answer":"Place responsibilities that are likely to change together behind boundaries where their changes can be implemented and verified together, while separating responsibilities with materially different change rates or ownership.","explanation":"Change coupling is a useful architectural signal. Components that frequently change together may be unnecessarily separated, while unrelated responsibilities coupled together create avoidable change risk.","principle":"Architecture should minimize unnecessary change coupling.","rule":"Do not create boundaries solely by technical category when change behavior indicates a different decomposition.","objective":"Improve maintainability by aligning boundaries with change characteristics.","requirements":["Identify expected change drivers","Identify ownership","Analyze dependency coupling"],"constraints":["Future change patterns are uncertain","Organizational ownership can change"],"preconditions":["Major change drivers are known"],"input":"Change history or expected change patterns and architecture dependencies.","output":"Change-oriented boundary decision.","expected_behavior":"Common changes can occur without unnecessary cross-boundary modifications.","correct_example":"Keep policy evaluation logic cohesive when policy changes frequently and should be independently versioned.","invalid_example":"Distribute one frequently changing rule across many unrelated services because each service is technically separate.","failure_mode":"Simple changes require coordinated modifications across many components.","cause":"Architecture boundaries ignored change coupling.","root_cause":"Change patterns were not considered during decomposition.","solution":"Map change drivers to components and reduce unnecessary cross-boundary change propagation.","tradeoff":"Cohesive boundaries may create larger components but can reduce coordination overhead.","edge_case":"A change begins to affect responsibilities previously considered independent.","security_implication":"Security policy changes must remain consistently enforced across all affected boundaries.","performance_implication":"Reducing unnecessary remote interactions can also reduce runtime overhead.","test_case":"Trace representative change scenarios and count the components and contracts that must change.","testing_guidance":"Use architecture change-impact analysis and regression tests.","verification_method":"Compare expected change scenarios with actual dependency propagation.","verification_guidance":"Treat major unexplained change coupling as an architectural signal.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B19-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Change-impact model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B19-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B19-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Maintainability and Evolvability","subtopic":"Dependency Direction","concept":"Architecture decision for stable dependency direction","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should dependency direction be selected to reduce long-term change propagation?","answer":"Dependencies should generally point toward concepts that are more stable or intentionally owned as abstractions, while volatile implementation details should depend on stable contracts rather than forcing stable components to depend on volatile details.","explanation":"Dependency direction affects how changes propagate. Stable abstractions can reduce coupling when they represent genuine boundaries rather than artificial interfaces.","principle":"Dependency direction should constrain change propagation toward stable boundaries.","rule":"Do not introduce abstractions merely to reverse dependencies without a real change or ownership benefit.","objective":"Reduce unnecessary propagation of implementation changes.","requirements":["Identify stable and volatile responsibilities","Define ownership of contracts","Map dependency direction"],"constraints":["Some dependency cycles are inherent and require redesign","Abstraction adds maintenance cost"],"preconditions":["Dependency graph exists"],"input":"Architecture dependency graph and change characteristics.","output":"Dependency-direction decision.","expected_behavior":"Volatile implementation changes do not unnecessarily force changes in stable consumers.","correct_example":"Define a stable domain-facing interface while allowing infrastructure implementations to change behind it.","invalid_example":"Make a stable domain component depend directly on multiple volatile infrastructure implementations.","failure_mode":"Infrastructure changes repeatedly force changes in higher-level components.","cause":"Dependency direction follows implementation convenience.","root_cause":"Stability and ownership were not considered.","solution":"Define stable contracts where justified and invert dependencies toward those contracts.","tradeoff":"Abstractions improve isolation but can become unnecessary indirection if no meaningful variation exists.","edge_case":"The abstraction itself becomes volatile due to poorly understood requirements.","security_implication":"Security boundaries must not be hidden behind abstractions that permit unintended authority.","performance_implication":"Additional abstraction layers can affect runtime behavior if they introduce remote calls or expensive translation.","test_case":"Change one volatile implementation and verify that stable consumers remain unchanged except where contract behavior legitimately changes.","testing_guidance":"Use contract and regression tests around stable interfaces.","verification_method":"Analyze dependency direction against expected change propagation.","verification_guidance":"Review abstractions for actual ownership and stability rather than naming conventions.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B19-R01"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B19-R03"],"depends_on":["D54-B19-R01"],"requires":["Dependency graph","Stability analysis"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B19-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B19-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Maintainability and Evolvability","subtopic":"Compatibility Strategy","concept":"Architecture decision for backward-compatible evolution","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should an architecture preserve backward compatibility rather than immediately replacing an interface?","answer":"Preserve compatibility when consumers cannot be migrated atomically, when the compatibility period materially reduces migration risk, or when external consumers cannot be controlled. Define a bounded migration and retirement strategy.","explanation":"Compatibility can reduce coordination risk but creates temporary complexity and multiple supported behaviors.","principle":"Compatibility is a migration mechanism, not an unlimited permanent obligation.","rule":"Do not retain obsolete interfaces indefinitely without ownership, usage measurement, and retirement criteria.","objective":"Enable controlled evolution without breaking active consumers.","requirements":["Consumer inventory","Compatibility contract","Migration path","Retirement criteria"],"constraints":["Supporting multiple versions increases test surface","Unknown consumers may exist in external systems"],"preconditions":["Interface consumers and compatibility requirements are known"],"input":"Existing interface and migration constraints.","output":"Compatibility and migration architecture decision.","expected_behavior":"Old and new consumers can coexist during a defined transition without violating correctness or security.","correct_example":"Version an API contract and define a migration window before retiring the old contract.","invalid_example":"Maintain every historical API indefinitely without monitoring usage or defining retirement criteria.","failure_mode":"Compatibility layers become permanent architectural debt and increase defect surface.","cause":"Migration had no explicit completion criteria.","root_cause":"Compatibility was treated as permanent rather than transitional.","solution":"Define consumer migration, deprecation, measurement, and retirement stages.","tradeoff":"Compatibility reduces migration disruption but increases short-term complexity.","edge_case":"A consumer cannot migrate within the planned window.","security_implication":"Deprecated interfaces must receive equivalent security controls until retirement.","performance_implication":"Compatibility translation can add processing and maintenance overhead.","test_case":"Exercise old and new contracts during the migration period and verify both remain within defined semantics.","testing_guidance":"Use contract, compatibility, and regression tests.","verification_method":"Verify consumer migration status and retirement criteria.","verification_guidance":"Do not retire an interface solely because documentation says it is deprecated.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B19-R02"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B19-R04"],"depends_on":["D54-B19-R02"],"requires":["Consumer inventory","Migration plan"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B19-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B19-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Maintainability and Evolvability","subtopic":"Architecture Simplicity","concept":"Architecture decision to reject unnecessary abstraction and distribution","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When should architectural simplicity be preferred over additional abstraction or distribution?","answer":"Prefer the simpler architecture when additional abstraction or distribution does not address a demonstrated requirement, failure mode, ownership boundary, or change problem.","explanation":"Complexity has operational and cognitive costs. Additional layers should solve a specific problem rather than represent assumed future needs.","principle":"Architectural complexity requires a concrete justification.","rule":"Do not add abstraction, services, or distributed coordination solely for hypothetical future requirements.","objective":"Control accidental complexity while preserving required architectural properties.","requirements":["Concrete problem statement","Requirement evidence","Complexity assessment"],"constraints":["Future needs may be uncertain","Simple designs can become limiting when requirements genuinely change"],"preconditions":["Alternatives are understood"],"input":"Architecture alternatives and requirement evidence.","output":"Simplicity-oriented architecture decision.","expected_behavior":"Architecture contains only complexity justified by current or strongly evidenced requirements.","correct_example":"Keep a cohesive module instead of splitting it into services when there is no independent scaling, ownership, or failure-isolation requirement.","invalid_example":"Create multiple services solely to appear scalable without evidence of independent scaling needs.","failure_mode":"Operational and coordination complexity increases without solving a real requirement.","cause":"Future flexibility was assumed to justify present complexity.","root_cause":"Architecture decision lacked a concrete complexity-benefit analysis.","solution":"Document the problem each architectural mechanism solves and remove unjustified mechanisms.","tradeoff":"Simpler architecture may require future migration if requirements materially change.","edge_case":"A requirement changes rapidly after initial deployment.","security_implication":"Fewer components can reduce attack surface but must still preserve required security boundaries.","performance_implication":"Fewer distributed boundaries may reduce network and serialization overhead.","test_case":"Review each architectural layer and identify its explicit requirement or risk justification.","testing_guidance":"Use architecture review checklists focused on complexity drivers.","verification_method":"Trace each major architectural mechanism to a documented requirement or failure mode.","verification_guidance":"Mark unsupported complexity as an architecture smell rather than assuming future value.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B19-R01","D54-B19-R02"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B19-R05"],"depends_on":["D54-B19-R01","D54-B19-R02"],"requires":["Alternative architecture analysis"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["Architecture complexity review"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B19-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Maintainability and Evolvability","subtopic":"Evolution Verification","concept":"Architecture decision verification through change-impact analysis","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should maintainability and evolvability architecture decisions be verified?","answer":"Evaluate representative future changes, trace affected components and contracts, and verify that expected changes remain bounded and testable.","explanation":"Maintainability is partly about how safely and predictably the architecture can absorb change. Change-impact scenarios provide concrete evidence.","principle":"Evolvability should be evaluated through realistic change scenarios rather than abstract claims.","rule":"Do not claim an architecture is maintainable without examining representative changes.","objective":"Verify that architectural boundaries support controlled evolution.","requirements":["Representative change scenarios","Dependency analysis","Regression strategy"],"constraints":["Future changes cannot be known exactly","Scenario selection affects conclusions"],"preconditions":["Major change drivers are identified"],"input":"Architecture and representative change scenarios.","output":"Change-impact verification evidence.","expected_behavior":"Representative changes remain localized, understandable, and verifiable within defined architectural boundaries.","correct_example":"Simulate replacing a dependency implementation and changing a business rule independently.","invalid_example":"Judge maintainability solely from code organization without evaluating change propagation.","failure_mode":"A supposedly isolated change requires widespread modifications.","cause":"Actual dependency coupling differs from conceptual boundaries.","root_cause":"Maintainability was not validated against change scenarios.","solution":"Use representative change-impact exercises and record observed coupling.","tradeoff":"Scenario analysis does not prove all future changes but provides bounded evidence.","edge_case":"A new change type falls outside all previously tested scenarios.","security_implication":"Security-impacting changes must include security regression and boundary verification.","performance_implication":"Performance-sensitive changes require performance regression checks in addition to structural analysis.","test_case":"Apply representative changes and verify affected-component count, contract stability, and regression coverage.","testing_guidance":"Combine architecture dependency analysis with change-based regression testing.","verification_method":"Compare expected and observed change propagation.","verification_guidance":"Classify conclusions as scenario-scoped evidence rather than universal maintainability proof.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B19-R01","D54-B19-R02","D54-B19-R03","D54-B19-R04"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B19-R01","D54-B19-R02","D54-B19-R03","D54-B19-R04"],"depends_on":["D54-B19-R01","D54-B19-R02","D54-B19-R03","D54-B19-R04"],"requires":["Change-impact scenarios"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["Architecture evolution verification"],"verifies":["D54-B19-R01","D54-B19-R02","D54-B19-R03","D54-B19-R04"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B20-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Operability, Observability and Supportability","subtopic":"Operational Visibility","concept":"Architecture decision for operationally observable components","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What observability capabilities should be considered part of an architecture decision?","answer":"Architecture should define the signals required to understand health, behavior, dependencies, failures, and recovery, including appropriate metrics, logs, traces, and state information.","explanation":"A component that cannot provide sufficient operational evidence may be difficult to diagnose even if its normal behavior is correct.","principle":"Operational observability is part of system architecture, not an afterthought.","rule":"Do not deploy critical components without defining how their important states and failure modes will be observed.","objective":"Make operational behavior diagnosable and supportable.","requirements":["Health indicators","Failure signals","Dependency visibility","State visibility"],"constraints":["Telemetry has cost","Sensitive data must not be exposed through observability"],"preconditions":["Critical operational scenarios are identified"],"input":"Architecture, failure model, and support requirements.","output":"Observability architecture decision.","expected_behavior":"Operators can distinguish normal, degraded, and failed states using sufficient evidence.","correct_example":"Expose component health, dependency latency/error metrics, structured logs, and trace context for critical workflows.","invalid_example":"Expose only a generic process-up metric for a distributed service with multiple dependency failure modes.","failure_mode":"Service appears healthy while critical functionality is unavailable.","cause":"Observability measured process health rather than functional health.","root_cause":"Operational requirements were not mapped to architecture signals.","solution":"Define observability signals from functional and failure requirements.","tradeoff":"More telemetry improves diagnosis but increases storage, processing, and governance cost.","edge_case":"Telemetry pipeline itself becomes unavailable.","security_implication":"Telemetry must avoid leaking secrets or sensitive payloads.","performance_implication":"Instrumentation should be evaluated for measurable overhead on critical paths.","test_case":"Inject representative failures and verify that available telemetry distinguishes their important states.","testing_guidance":"Use failure-oriented observability tests.","verification_method":"Map failure scenarios to required telemetry signals and inspect evidence during controlled failures.","verification_guidance":"Do not treat the presence of telemetry as proof that it is diagnostically sufficient.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":[],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B20-R02"],"depends_on":[],"requires":["Operational failure model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B20-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B20-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Operability, Observability and Supportability","subtopic":"Health Signals","concept":"Architecture decision for functional health checks","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should health architecture distinguish process health from functional dependency health?","answer":"Health signals should distinguish whether a component is alive, ready to perform its required function, and able to access critical dependencies according to the relevant operating semantics.","explanation":"A process can remain alive while a critical dependency is unavailable. A single liveness signal may therefore misrepresent service capability.","principle":"Health semantics must correspond to the operational decision the signal supports.","rule":"Do not use a generic liveness check as the sole indicator of functional readiness.","objective":"Provide health signals that support correct operational decisions.","requirements":["Liveness semantics","Readiness semantics","Dependency semantics"],"constraints":["Deep health checks can create dependency load","Some dependencies should not be probed continuously"],"preconditions":["Operational states are defined"],"input":"Service responsibilities and critical dependencies.","output":"Health-check architecture with explicit semantics.","expected_behavior":"Operators and automation can distinguish process availability from functional readiness.","correct_example":"Expose lightweight liveness separately from readiness that verifies the critical conditions required to serve traffic.","invalid_example":"Return healthy solely because the process responds to a local health endpoint.","failure_mode":"Traffic continues to a service that cannot complete required operations.","cause":"Health signal represented process existence rather than service readiness.","root_cause":"Health semantics were not tied to functional requirements.","solution":"Define separate health states and document their consumers and consequences.","tradeoff":"More precise health checks require careful dependency selection to avoid self-induced load.","edge_case":"A dependency is temporarily unavailable but the service supports safe degraded operation.","security_implication":"Health endpoints must not expose sensitive dependency details or internal secrets.","performance_implication":"Deep checks can consume dependency capacity and should be bounded.","test_case":"Fail a critical dependency and verify liveness, readiness, and degraded-state signals behave as defined.","testing_guidance":"Use dependency failure tests and health-signal assertions.","verification_method":"Compare emitted health states with actual functional capability.","verification_guidance":"Ensure automation acts on the correct health semantic rather than a generic signal.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B20-R01"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B20-R03"],"depends_on":["D54-B20-R01"],"requires":["Health-state model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B20-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B20-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Operability, Observability and Supportability","subtopic":"Diagnostic Evidence","concept":"Architecture decision for causally useful diagnostic telemetry","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should observability architecture support diagnosis rather than merely generate large telemetry volumes?","answer":"Telemetry should be selected around important system decisions and failure hypotheses, preserving correlation identifiers, relevant state transitions, dependency context, and enough evidence to distinguish plausible causes.","explanation":"Large telemetry volume does not guarantee diagnostic usefulness. Signals should help answer concrete operational questions.","principle":"Observability should maximize diagnostic information, not raw telemetry volume.","rule":"Do not optimize telemetry architecture for event count without considering diagnostic value and context.","objective":"Improve evidence quality for operational diagnosis.","requirements":["Failure hypotheses","Correlation context","State transitions","Dependency evidence"],"constraints":["Telemetry volume and cost","Privacy and security restrictions"],"preconditions":["Important operational failure modes are known"],"input":"Failure model and diagnostic questions.","output":"Diagnostic observability architecture.","expected_behavior":"Relevant incidents produce sufficient correlated evidence to distinguish important candidate causes.","correct_example":"Correlate request traces with dependency latency, error codes, and state-transition events for a critical workflow.","invalid_example":"Collect millions of uncorrelated log lines without identifiers or timestamps precise enough to reconstruct causality.","failure_mode":"Operators cannot connect symptoms across components or distinguish competing causes.","cause":"Telemetry was collected without diagnostic relationships.","root_cause":"Observability was treated as volume collection rather than evidence architecture.","solution":"Design telemetry around failure hypotheses, correlations, and required diagnostic questions.","tradeoff":"Richer contextual telemetry can increase storage and processing cost.","edge_case":"Correlation identifiers are missing for asynchronous or retried operations.","security_implication":"Correlation data must avoid exposing sensitive identifiers unnecessarily.","performance_implication":"High-cardinality telemetry can increase resource and storage costs.","test_case":"Inject a known failure and determine whether telemetry contains enough evidence to distinguish its cause from plausible alternatives.","testing_guidance":"Conduct diagnostic exercises rather than only checking telemetry emission.","verification_method":"Trace failure hypothesis → required evidence → emitted telemetry → diagnostic conclusion.","verification_guidance":"Record evidence gaps explicitly when telemetry cannot discriminate causes.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B20-R01","D54-B20-R02"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B20-R04"],"depends_on":["D54-B20-R01","D54-B20-R02"],"requires":["Failure hypothesis model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B20-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B20-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Operability, Observability and Supportability","subtopic":"Supportability Boundaries","concept":"Architecture decision for operator-safe operational controls","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should architecture consider when providing operational controls to support personnel?","answer":"Operational controls should expose only necessary actions, define preconditions and expected effects, enforce authorization, provide safe failure behavior, and produce auditable evidence.","explanation":"Operational controls can have high impact. A support interface should not turn diagnostic access into unrestricted administrative authority.","principle":"Supportability mechanisms must be designed as controlled operational capabilities.","rule":"Do not expose powerful operational actions without explicit authorization, preconditions, and auditability.","objective":"Enable safe support operations without expanding unnecessary authority.","requirements":["Action scope","Authorization","Preconditions","Audit trail","Failure behavior"],"constraints":["Support teams may require emergency capabilities","Operational actions can affect production state"],"preconditions":["Support use cases and authority model are defined"],"input":"Support requirements and operational actions.","output":"Operator-control architecture decision.","expected_behavior":"Authorized operators can perform required actions while unauthorized or unsafe actions are prevented or clearly rejected.","correct_example":"Provide a narrowly scoped restart operation with authorization, confirmation, audit logging, and state validation.","invalid_example":"Provide a generic administrative shell to support personnel for convenience.","failure_mode":"Operational support access becomes an uncontrolled production authority path.","cause":"Supportability was implemented without security and safety boundaries.","root_cause":"Operational controls were treated as diagnostic tools rather than state-changing capabilities.","solution":"Define action-specific authorization, preconditions, audit, and rollback behavior.","tradeoff":"Controlled interfaces require more engineering than unrestricted administrative access.","edge_case":"Emergency action must occur while normal authorization infrastructure is degraded.","security_implication":"Emergency controls require stronger governance, narrow scope, and comprehensive auditability.","performance_implication":"Operational controls should not consume excessive production resources during incidents.","test_case":"Attempt authorized, unauthorized, stale-state, and invalid-target operational actions.","testing_guidance":"Use authorization, precondition, and audit tests.","verification_method":"Verify each operational control against its authority and safety model.","verification_guidance":"Confirm every state-changing operation generates sufficient audit evidence.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B17-R02"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B20-R05"],"depends_on":["D54-B17-R02"],"requires":["Operational authority model"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["D54-B20-R05"],"verifies":[],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B20-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Operability, Observability and Supportability","subtopic":"Operational Verification","concept":"Architecture decision verification through operational scenarios","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should operability, observability, and supportability architecture be verified?","answer":"Execute representative operational scenarios including normal operation, dependency failure, degraded operation, diagnosis, recovery, and authorized support actions, then verify that evidence and controls are sufficient.","explanation":"Operational architecture is meaningful only if operators and automation can detect state, diagnose problems, and safely act within defined authority.","principle":"Operational capability must be verified through realistic operational workflows.","rule":"Do not infer supportability from the existence of dashboards, logs, or administrative interfaces alone.","objective":"Verify that architecture supports safe detection, diagnosis, intervention, and recovery.","requirements":["Operational scenarios","Observability evidence","Support controls","Recovery procedures"],"constraints":["Production-like scenarios may require controlled environments","Operational exercises require governance"],"preconditions":["Operational runbooks and architecture signals exist"],"input":"Architecture and operational scenario catalogue.","output":"Operational verification evidence.","expected_behavior":"Relevant operational states can be detected, diagnosed, acted upon safely, and verified after intervention.","correct_example":"Simulate dependency failure, use telemetry to diagnose it, execute an authorized recovery action, and verify the post-recovery state.","invalid_example":"Confirm that logs exist without testing whether they allow diagnosis or recovery.","failure_mode":"System is technically instrumented but operationally difficult or unsafe to manage.","cause":"Operational verification stopped at instrumentation presence.","root_cause":"End-to-end operational workflow was not tested.","solution":"Exercise detection-to-recovery scenarios and verify evidence, authority, action, and outcome.","tradeoff":"Operational drills require time and controlled environments but expose architectural support gaps.","edge_case":"Telemetry is degraded during the same incident requiring diagnosis.","security_implication":"Operational exercises must verify authorization and audit behavior for state-changing actions.","performance_implication":"Operational tooling should remain usable under degraded system conditions without consuming excessive recovery capacity.","test_case":"Run a controlled incident scenario from detection through diagnosis, authorized action, and post-action verification.","testing_guidance":"Use operational drills, failure injection, and recovery validation.","verification_method":"Trace scenario → signal → diagnosis evidence → authorized action → observed outcome → verification.","verification_guidance":"Record every missing evidence or unsafe control as an architectural gap rather than assuming supportability.","language":"N/A","language_version":"N/A","framework":"N/A","framework_version":"N/A","version_range":"Architecture-independent","dependencies":["D54-B20-R01","D54-B20-R02","D54-B20-R03","D54-B20-R04"],"execution_requirements":[],"execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","related_records":["D54-B20-R01","D54-B20-R02","D54-B20-R03","D54-B20-R04"],"depends_on":["D54-B20-R01","D54-B20-R02","D54-B20-R03","D54-B20-R04"],"requires":["Operational scenario catalogue"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"specializes":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"implements":[],"tests":["Operational architecture drill"],"verifies":["D54-B20-R01","D54-B20-R02","D54-B20-R03","D54-B20-R04"],"derived_from":[],"provenance":[{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B21-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Monolith vs Modular Monolith vs Distributed Architecture","subtopic":"Architecture form selection","concept":"Choosing architecture form from change, scale, reliability, and operational constraints","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an architecture form be selected among monolith, modular monolith, and distributed architecture?","answer":"Select the least operationally complex architecture that satisfies verified functional and quality requirements while preserving required boundaries and future evolution paths.","context":"A system needs an initial architecture and the team is considering a conventional monolith, modular monolith, or distributed decomposition.","objective":"Choose an architecture form that satisfies current requirements without introducing unjustified structural or operational complexity.","requirements":["Required functional behavior must be supported","Required quality attributes must be measurable","Module or component boundaries must remain understandable","Operational capabilities must match architecture complexity"],"constraints":["Team capacity may limit operational sophistication","Future requirements may be uncertain","Distribution introduces network and failure boundaries"],"alternatives":["Monolith","Modular monolith","Distributed architecture"],"evidence":["Functional dependency structure","Change frequency and ownership boundaries","Measured scale requirements","Availability and latency requirements","Operational maturity and deployment constraints"],"tradeoffs":["A monolith minimizes deployment and distributed-systems complexity but can weaken isolation as coupling grows","A modular monolith preserves strong deployment simplicity while enforcing internal boundaries","Distributed architecture can provide independent scaling and failure isolation but introduces network, consistency, observability, and operational complexity"],"decision":"Prefer a monolith when requirements and coupling are simple; prefer a modular monolith when strong internal boundaries are required without independently operating components; introduce distribution when independently deployable failure, scaling, ownership, or isolation boundaries are actual requirements.","rationale":"Architecture form should be driven by explicit constraints rather than assumed organizational or technological fashion.","consequences":["The selected form constrains deployment and testing strategies","Moving toward distribution later may require explicit boundary hardening","Premature distribution creates additional failure and operational modes"],"implementation_implications":["Document module boundaries even inside a monolith","Define dependency direction before decomposition","Measure scaling and reliability requirements before distributing components"],"verification_method":"Review architecture against functional requirements, quality targets, coupling, deployment complexity, and failure-domain requirements; verify that each introduced boundary has a documented purpose.","outcome_learning":"Reassess the architecture when measured coupling, deployment constraints, scaling requirements, ownership boundaries, or reliability requirements materially change.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B21-R02","D54-B21-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B21-R02"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B22-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B21-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Monolith vs Modular Monolith vs Distributed Architecture","subtopic":"Modular monolith boundary strategy","concept":"Using modular structure to delay distribution while preserving architectural boundaries","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When is a modular monolith an appropriate architectural compromise?","answer":"A modular monolith is appropriate when the system needs explicit domain or ownership boundaries but does not yet require independent deployment, scaling, or failure isolation.","context":"A growing application has increasing internal coupling concerns but distribution would add substantial operational complexity.","objective":"Preserve architectural separation while retaining a single deployment and process boundary.","requirements":["Modules must have explicit responsibilities","Cross-module dependencies must be controlled","Internal APIs must be identifiable","Module boundaries must be testable"],"constraints":["Single deployment may remain desirable","Shared runtime resources may remain acceptable","Independent scaling may not yet be required"],"alternatives":["Remain an unstructured monolith","Adopt modular monolith","Immediately split into services"],"evidence":["Dependency graph","Change ownership patterns","Deployment frequency","Resource isolation requirements","Failure isolation requirements"],"tradeoffs":["Modularization adds discipline and boundary maintenance","It avoids many network and distributed deployment costs","It cannot provide independent process failure isolation or scaling"],"decision":"Use modular monolith structure when internal separation provides substantial value but independent runtime boundaries are not yet justified.","rationale":"A logical boundary can provide architectural value before a physical deployment boundary becomes necessary.","consequences":["Boundary violations must be detected continuously","Some future extraction work may still remain","Operational complexity stays lower than distributed deployment"],"implementation_implications":["Define module interfaces","Restrict dependency direction","Prevent direct access to another module's internal state","Add architectural tests for forbidden dependencies"],"verification_method":"Build dependency analysis and architecture tests that fail when prohibited module dependencies are introduced.","outcome_learning":"Track boundary violations, extraction pressure, deployment bottlenecks, and independent scaling requirements to determine whether physical decomposition becomes justified.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B21-R01","D54-B23-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B21-R01"},{"type":"constrains","record_id":"D54-B23-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B21-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Monolith vs Modular Monolith vs Distributed Architecture","subtopic":"Distribution justification","concept":"Requiring explicit justification before introducing distributed boundaries","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"What evidence should justify moving from a modular monolith to distributed components?","answer":"Distribution should be justified by concrete requirements such as independent scaling, independent deployment, strong failure isolation, regulatory isolation, or organizational ownership that cannot be adequately satisfied within one deployment boundary.","context":"A modular monolith already has explicit internal boundaries and a proposal exists to extract one or more modules.","objective":"Determine whether physical distribution provides enough additional value to justify its complexity.","requirements":["The proposed boundary must solve a concrete problem","The problem must be material","The distributed design must have measurable acceptance criteria"],"constraints":["Network communication introduces failure modes","Distributed state coordination increases complexity","Observability and deployment become more demanding"],"alternatives":["Keep the module internal","Extract one component","Adopt broader service decomposition"],"evidence":["Measured resource contention","Deployment independence requirements","Failure-domain requirements","Ownership constraints","Latency and throughput measurements"],"tradeoffs":["Distribution may improve isolation and independent scaling","Distribution increases communication and operational failure modes","Keeping the boundary internal minimizes infrastructure complexity"],"decision":"Require evidence of a material independent deployment, scaling, failure, isolation, or ownership need before converting a logical boundary into a distributed runtime boundary.","rationale":"Physical distribution is an architectural mechanism rather than an objective in itself.","consequences":["Some proposed service extractions will be rejected until evidence exists","Validated extractions become easier to justify and test"],"implementation_implications":["Define the extracted component's contract","Define ownership and operational responsibility","Specify timeout, retry, failure, observability, and data ownership behavior"],"verification_method":"Review the extraction against measurable drivers and verify that the proposed distributed boundary addresses a documented limitation of the existing architecture.","outcome_learning":"Compare expected benefits with actual deployment independence, resource isolation, reliability, and operational costs after extraction.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B21-R02","D54-B22-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B21-R02"},{"type":"gates","record_id":"D54-B22-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B21-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Monolith vs Modular Monolith vs Distributed Architecture","subtopic":"Migration reversibility","concept":"Preserving reversible architecture transitions","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architectural reversibility influence the choice between monolithic and distributed forms?","answer":"When requirements are uncertain, prefer designs that preserve explicit boundaries and allow later decomposition without committing prematurely to distributed operational complexity.","context":"Future scale and organizational requirements are uncertain.","objective":"Reduce irreversible architecture commitments while retaining future options.","requirements":["Boundaries must be explicit","Interfaces must be stable enough for later extraction","Internal dependencies must be discoverable"],"constraints":["Not every future extraction can be made cost-free","Early abstraction can itself create complexity"],"alternatives":["Optimize only for current deployment","Create extraction-ready boundaries","Distribute immediately"],"evidence":["Requirement uncertainty","Boundary stability","Expected change areas","Operational cost of distribution"],"tradeoffs":["Extraction-ready modularity adds some current discipline","Immediate distribution adds operational cost","Ignoring future boundaries can increase later migration cost"],"decision":"When future distribution is plausible but not currently required, preserve extraction-ready boundaries without creating unnecessary distributed infrastructure.","rationale":"Maintaining options can be valuable when uncertainty is high and premature commitment is expensive.","consequences":["Current implementation may contain explicit interfaces","Future extraction remains a possibility rather than a guarantee"],"implementation_implications":["Avoid hidden cross-module state access","Define ownership of persistent data","Keep interfaces explicit and testable"],"verification_method":"Inspect dependency graphs and module interfaces for hidden coupling that would make later extraction materially difficult.","outcome_learning":"Reevaluate whether preserved boundaries continue to match actual evolution and requirements.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B21-R02","D54-B24-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B21-R02"},{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B24-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B21-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Monolith vs Modular Monolith vs Distributed Architecture","subtopic":"Operational complexity","concept":"Accounting for operational complexity as an architecture constraint","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"Why must operational complexity be included in architecture-form decisions?","answer":"Architecture form determines the number and type of deployment, monitoring, failure, security, configuration, and recovery mechanisms that operators must manage.","context":"Two architectures provide similar functional capabilities but have different operational footprints.","objective":"Include lifecycle operational burden in the architecture decision.","requirements":["Deployment procedures must be supportable","Failures must be diagnosable","Security boundaries must be manageable","Recovery mechanisms must be testable"],"constraints":["Operational expertise and tooling may be limited","Complexity has ongoing rather than one-time cost"],"alternatives":["Lower operational complexity","Higher distribution and isolation","Hybrid architecture"],"evidence":["Number of deployable units","Failure modes","Monitoring requirements","Recovery procedures","Operator workload"],"tradeoffs":["More components can provide isolation but increase coordination","Fewer components simplify operations but may reduce independent scaling or failure isolation"],"decision":"Treat operational complexity as a first-class architecture constraint and require explicit operational justification for additional deployable boundaries.","rationale":"An architecture that cannot be reliably operated does not satisfy system requirements merely because its static design is sound.","consequences":["Operational requirements become part of architecture review","Architecture proposals must account for monitoring and recovery"],"implementation_implications":["Define ownership per deployable unit","Define health, telemetry, deployment, rollback, and recovery procedures","Test operational failure paths"],"verification_method":"Conduct an operational readiness review covering deployment, observability, incident diagnosis, rollback, recovery, and security administration.","outcome_learning":"Measure operational incidents, deployment failures, recovery effort, and maintenance burden against the architecture assumptions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B20-R01","D54-B21-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B20-R01"},{"type":"constrains","record_id":"D54-B21-R01"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B22-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Service Decomposition Decisions","subtopic":"Decomposition drivers","concept":"Selecting service boundaries from independent change and ownership needs","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should primarily drive service decomposition?","answer":"Service decomposition should be driven by independently meaningful responsibilities, ownership, change patterns, scaling needs, failure isolation, or security boundaries rather than arbitrary code size.","context":"A system is being considered for decomposition into independently deployable services.","objective":"Identify service boundaries that provide measurable architectural value.","requirements":["Each service needs a coherent responsibility","Ownership must be explicit","Service contracts must be definable","Operational responsibility must be assignable"],"constraints":["Every service adds deployment and operational overhead","Cross-service workflows require explicit communication semantics"],"alternatives":["No decomposition","Coarse services","Fine-grained services"],"evidence":["Change coupling","Team ownership","Scaling profiles","Failure domains","Data ownership"],"tradeoffs":["Coarse services reduce coordination but may contain unrelated responsibilities","Fine-grained services increase independence but amplify operational and communication complexity"],"decision":"Decompose around independently meaningful responsibilities where independent change, ownership, scaling, security, or failure isolation creates measurable value.","rationale":"A service boundary should represent a real architectural boundary rather than merely a source-code partition.","consequences":["Service boundaries become part of operational ownership","Cross-boundary behavior requires explicit contracts"],"implementation_implications":["Define API/event contracts","Define data ownership","Define timeout and failure behavior","Define observability and deployment ownership"],"verification_method":"Review dependency and change graphs and verify that each service has a coherent responsibility and measurable reason to be independent.","outcome_learning":"Monitor cross-service coupling, deployment coordination, incidents, and operational overhead to validate decomposition assumptions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B21-R03","D54-B22-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B21-R03"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B22-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B22-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Service Decomposition Decisions","subtopic":"Data ownership","concept":"Aligning service boundaries with authoritative data ownership","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should data ownership influence service decomposition?","answer":"A service should generally own the authoritative state required for its responsibility when independent lifecycle and integrity are important; shared authoritative writes across services require explicit coordination semantics.","context":"Multiple candidate services need access to related persistent data.","objective":"Prevent ambiguous ownership and uncontrolled distributed state mutation.","requirements":["Authority over each mutable data domain must be identifiable","Write responsibilities must be explicit","Cross-service reads and changes must have defined contracts"],"constraints":["Some data naturally spans multiple business capabilities","Strong cross-service transactions may increase coupling"],"alternatives":["Shared database writes","Single authoritative owner with APIs/events","Replicated or derived data"],"evidence":["Mutation ownership","Consistency requirements","Transaction boundaries","Access patterns"],"tradeoffs":["Shared writes simplify some transactions but increase coupling","Single ownership improves authority clarity but may require coordination","Replication improves local access but introduces freshness and reconciliation concerns"],"decision":"Prefer explicit authoritative ownership and treat cross-service data synchronization as a contract rather than implicit shared mutation.","rationale":"Ambiguous state ownership makes service independence and failure reasoning difficult.","consequences":["Data access patterns become explicit","Cross-service consistency requirements must be documented"],"implementation_implications":["Restrict writes to authoritative owners","Define read APIs or events","Specify freshness and reconciliation behavior for derived copies"],"verification_method":"Trace every mutable data element to an authoritative owner and verify that non-owner writes are prohibited or explicitly coordinated.","outcome_learning":"Monitor synchronization failures, stale-data incidents, reconciliation work, and ownership disputes.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B22-R01","D54-B30-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B22-R01"},{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B30-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B22-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Service Decomposition Decisions","subtopic":"Transaction boundaries","concept":"Using transaction requirements to constrain service decomposition","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should strong transaction requirements affect service decomposition?","answer":"If a workflow requires atomic changes across multiple responsibilities, decomposition should explicitly account for distributed transaction or workflow-coordination costs rather than assuming service boundaries preserve the original atomicity.","context":"A proposed decomposition separates operations that currently execute within one transaction.","objective":"Preserve required consistency semantics while evaluating decomposition.","requirements":["Atomicity requirements must be explicit","Failure compensation must be defined where atomicity is relaxed","Consistency expectations must be measurable"],"constraints":["Distributed transactions can increase coupling and operational complexity","Compensation may not perfectly reverse external effects"],"alternatives":["Keep responsibilities together","Use coordinated distributed transaction","Use workflow with compensation"],"evidence":["Business invariants","Transaction scope","Failure semantics","Irreversible side effects"],"tradeoffs":["Keeping components together preserves local atomicity","Distributed coordination can preserve some guarantees with added complexity","Compensation provides eventual recovery but may not equal atomic rollback"],"decision":"Do not split a boundary solely on code ownership when the split crosses a critical atomic invariant without a validated consistency strategy.","rationale":"Transaction boundaries are architectural boundaries when they encode required correctness.","consequences":["Some responsibilities may remain co-located","Distributed workflows require explicit failure handling"],"implementation_implications":["Identify invariants","Define transaction or workflow state","Test partial-failure paths"],"verification_method":"Execute failure-injection tests or controlled simulations for each cross-boundary transaction strategy and verify invariant preservation.","outcome_learning":"Track compensation frequency, invariant violations, and workflow recovery outcomes.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B22-R02","D54-B27-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"constrains","record_id":"D54-B22-R02"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B27-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B22-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Service Decomposition Decisions","subtopic":"Service granularity","concept":"Avoiding decomposition based only on code size","knowledge_type":"DECISION_PATTERN","question":"Why is code size an insufficient criterion for service boundaries?","answer":"Code size does not establish independent responsibility, ownership, scaling, security, or failure requirements; a small component can still have strong coupling and a large component can represent one coherent capability.","context":"Teams are considering services based on lines of code or class counts.","objective":"Prevent structurally arbitrary decomposition.","requirements":["Boundaries must represent meaningful responsibilities","Dependencies must be evaluated","Operational value must be demonstrated"],"constraints":["Code size is easy to measure but weak as an architectural driver"],"alternatives":["Size-based decomposition","Responsibility-based decomposition","Hybrid evidence-based decomposition"],"evidence":["Dependency graph","Change coupling","Ownership","Runtime behavior","Failure isolation"],"tradeoffs":["Size-based rules are simple but can create artificial boundaries","Responsibility-based boundaries require more analysis but better reflect system behavior"],"decision":"Use code size only as a diagnostic signal, never as the primary service-boundary criterion.","rationale":"Architecture boundaries should correspond to system behavior and responsibility rather than source-code volume.","consequences":["Some large services remain intentionally cohesive","Some small components remain internal rather than becoming services"],"implementation_implications":["Analyze responsibility and dependency graphs before extraction","Document boundary rationale"],"verification_method":"Review proposed service boundaries against responsibility, dependency, ownership, scaling, and failure evidence.","outcome_learning":"Track whether service boundaries reduce or increase coordination and change coupling.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B22-R01","D54-B23-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"constrains","record_id":"D54-B22-R01"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B23-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B22-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Service Decomposition Decisions","subtopic":"Decomposition sequencing","concept":"Incremental service extraction","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should a system sequence multiple service extractions?","answer":"Extract boundaries incrementally, prioritizing boundaries with clear contracts, limited coupling, measurable value, and manageable migration risk.","context":"A system contains several candidate service boundaries.","objective":"Reduce migration risk while generating measurable architectural evidence.","requirements":["Each extraction must have an explicit objective","Migration and rollback paths must be defined","Behavioral equivalence must be verified where applicable"],"constraints":["Migration adds temporary complexity","Multiple simultaneous extractions make causal evaluation harder"],"alternatives":["Big-bang decomposition","Incremental extraction","No decomposition"],"evidence":["Boundary coupling","Migration complexity","Operational readiness","Business criticality"],"tradeoffs":["Incremental extraction reduces blast radius but prolongs transition","Big-bang migration may reduce transition duration but increases simultaneous risk"],"decision":"Prefer incremental extraction when decomposition benefits are uncertain or migration risk is material.","rationale":"Small controlled changes produce more observable evidence and reduce simultaneous failure modes.","consequences":["Temporary hybrid architecture may exist","Each extraction needs independent validation"],"implementation_implications":["Define migration seams","Use compatibility interfaces where required","Monitor old and new paths during transition"],"verification_method":"Verify each extracted capability independently before removing the previous implementation or compatibility path.","outcome_learning":"Compare measured benefits and migration costs after every extraction before approving subsequent decomposition.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B22-R01","D54-B21-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B22-R01"},{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B21-R03"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B23-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Component Boundary Decisions","subtopic":"Responsibility cohesion","concept":"Defining component boundaries around cohesive responsibility","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What is a strong basis for defining component boundaries?","answer":"A component boundary should group behavior that changes together, serves a coherent responsibility, and can expose a stable contract to surrounding components.","context":"A system needs internal components with explicit responsibilities.","objective":"Create boundaries that reduce accidental coupling and clarify ownership.","requirements":["Responsibility must be identifiable","Public contract must be explicit","Internal details should remain encapsulated"],"constraints":["Some responsibilities naturally interact","Over-separation can increase coordination"],"alternatives":["Responsibility-based boundaries","Technical-layer boundaries","File-size-based boundaries"],"evidence":["Change history","Dependency graph","Data ownership","Workflow cohesion"],"tradeoffs":["Responsibility boundaries can reduce change coupling","Technical layers can simplify common infrastructure but may spread one capability across many layers","Excessive boundaries increase communication"],"decision":"Prefer cohesive responsibility boundaries, then validate dependency and change coupling before finalizing the component structure.","rationale":"Components should represent meaningful units of behavior rather than arbitrary source organization.","consequences":["Component ownership becomes clearer","Cross-component contracts become explicit"],"implementation_implications":["Define public interfaces","Hide internal state","Test boundary behavior"],"verification_method":"Analyze dependencies and representative change scenarios to determine whether related changes remain localized.","outcome_learning":"Monitor boundary violations and cross-component change frequency.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B22-R04","D54-B23-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B22-R04"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B23-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B23-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Component Boundary Decisions","subtopic":"Interface versus implementation","concept":"Preventing boundary leakage","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should component interfaces prevent implementation leakage?","answer":"Expose behavior and stable domain concepts required by consumers while keeping implementation-specific state, algorithms, and dependencies internal unless their exposure is an explicit architectural requirement.","context":"Components have direct access to internal structures of neighboring components.","objective":"Preserve replaceability and reduce coupling across component boundaries.","requirements":["Consumers need stable contracts","Internal representation must remain changeable","Boundary behavior must be testable"],"constraints":["Some performance-sensitive systems may require lower-level exposure","Excessive abstraction can obscure necessary semantics"],"alternatives":["Expose internal structures","Expose stable domain interface","Expose shared mutable state"],"evidence":["Consumer needs","Change frequency","Performance requirements","Data ownership"],"tradeoffs":["Stable interfaces improve isolation","Leaking implementation reduces local change freedom","Overly abstract interfaces can increase complexity"],"decision":"Expose the minimum stable contract necessary for consumers and retain implementation details behind the boundary unless evidence requires otherwise.","rationale":"Boundary leakage converts local implementation changes into system-wide compatibility concerns.","consequences":["Internal implementations can evolve independently","Boundary design requires deliberate contract maintenance"],"implementation_implications":["Use immutable or controlled data exchange where appropriate","Avoid direct internal-state mutation","Contract-test public behavior"],"verification_method":"Inspect consumer dependencies and fail architectural checks when consumers access forbidden implementation details.","outcome_learning":"Track breaking changes and boundary adaptation work as evidence of interface quality.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B23-R01","D54-B25-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B23-R01"},{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B25-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B23-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Component Boundary Decisions","subtopic":"Failure isolation","concept":"Using failure behavior to define boundaries","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When should failure isolation influence a component boundary?","answer":"Failure isolation should influence boundaries when a capability has materially different failure behavior, recovery requirements, resource consumption, or availability requirements from its neighbors.","context":"A component can either remain inside a shared runtime boundary or receive stronger isolation.","objective":"Determine whether failure behavior justifies architectural separation.","requirements":["Failure impact must be understood","Isolation benefits must be measurable","Recovery behavior must be defined"],"constraints":["Isolation mechanisms add resource and operational overhead","Process or service isolation does not eliminate all shared dependencies"],"alternatives":["Shared component boundary","Process-level isolation","Independent service boundary"],"evidence":["Failure impact","Resource contention","Recovery requirements","Availability targets"],"tradeoffs":["Shared execution is simpler","Isolation can reduce blast radius","Independent deployment increases operational cost"],"decision":"Use stronger component boundaries when failure containment is a documented requirement and the expected blast-radius reduction justifies added complexity.","rationale":"Boundary design can encode reliability requirements when components have materially different failure consequences.","consequences":["Failure handling becomes part of component architecture","Isolation requires dedicated tests and monitoring"],"implementation_implications":["Define failure containment behavior","Define dependency timeouts","Test component failure independently"],"verification_method":"Inject representative failures and verify that impact remains within the documented boundary.","outcome_learning":"Measure incident blast radius and recovery behavior against the architectural assumption.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B23-R01","D54-B14-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"constrains","record_id":"D54-B23-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B14-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B23-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Component Boundary Decisions","subtopic":"Boundary size","concept":"Avoiding both oversized and fragmented components","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should component size be evaluated?","answer":"Component size should be evaluated through responsibility cohesion, dependency complexity, change coupling, testability, and operational consequences rather than a fixed line-count threshold.","context":"A team needs to determine whether a component should be split or merged.","objective":"Select a component size that supports maintainability and clear reasoning.","requirements":["Responsibility must remain coherent","Dependencies must remain manageable","Behavior must remain testable"],"constraints":["No universal size threshold applies to all systems","Splitting can create coordination overhead"],"alternatives":["Split aggressively","Merge broadly","Evidence-based boundary adjustment"],"evidence":["Dependency density","Change coupling","Test complexity","Ownership"],"tradeoffs":["Smaller components can improve local reasoning but increase integration overhead","Larger components reduce coordination but can accumulate unrelated responsibilities"],"decision":"Adjust component boundaries when evidence shows degraded cohesion, excessive coupling, test difficulty, or ownership ambiguity; do not use source-code size alone.","rationale":"Architectural fitness depends on relationships and behavior rather than arbitrary size.","consequences":["Boundary changes require explicit evidence","Some large components may remain intentionally cohesive"],"implementation_implications":["Measure dependency and change graphs","Review ownership and test boundaries","Document split or merge rationale"],"verification_method":"Compare candidate boundaries using dependency, change, testability, and ownership evidence.","outcome_learning":"Track maintenance effort and boundary-related defects after changes.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B23-R01","D54-B22-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B23-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B22-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B23-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Component Boundary Decisions","subtopic":"Boundary verification","concept":"Architecture tests for component boundaries","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_DECISION","question":"How can component boundary decisions be continuously verified?","answer":"Encode prohibited dependencies, ownership rules, public-interface constraints, and allowed dependency directions as automated architecture checks where practical.","context":"A component architecture is expected to remain stable as the codebase evolves.","objective":"Detect architectural drift before it becomes widespread.","requirements":["Boundary rules must be explicit","Rules must be mechanically testable where possible","Violations must be attributable"],"constraints":["Not every architectural property can be fully automated","Static checks may require periodic human review"],"alternatives":["Manual review only","Automated architecture checks","Hybrid governance"],"evidence":["Dependency graph","Module metadata","Build structure","Architecture rules"],"tradeoffs":["Automation provides repeatability but cannot capture every semantic decision","Manual review captures context but is less continuous"],"decision":"Automate deterministic boundary rules and retain human review for context-sensitive architectural decisions.","rationale":"Architecture decisions are more durable when enforceable constraints are checked continuously.","consequences":["Boundary violations become detectable during development","Rules require maintenance as architecture evolves"],"implementation_implications":["Create dependency allowlists or denylists","Add architecture tests to CI","Document exceptions explicitly"],"verification_method":"Run architecture checks against representative dependency graphs and verify expected violations are detected.","outcome_learning":"Review false positives, missed violations, and exception growth as signals of rule quality.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B23-R02","D54-B19-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B23-R02"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B19-R05"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B24-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Module and Package Boundary Decisions","subtopic":"Package responsibility","concept":"Organizing packages around stable responsibilities","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should module and package boundaries be selected?","answer":"Packages should group cohesive responsibilities and expose controlled interfaces while minimizing dependencies between unrelated areas.","context":"A codebase needs package structure that supports maintainability and evolution.","objective":"Create package boundaries that reflect responsibility rather than accidental file organization.","requirements":["Packages need coherent responsibility","Imports must have understandable direction","Public package interfaces must be identifiable"],"constraints":["Language import systems differ","Legacy structures may impose migration constraints"],"alternatives":["Layer-first packages","Feature/responsibility packages","Hybrid organization"],"evidence":["Dependency graph","Change coupling","Ownership","Public API structure"],"tradeoffs":["Feature-oriented organization can localize changes","Layer-oriented organization can centralize technical concerns","Hybrid structures require explicit dependency rules"],"decision":"Prefer package boundaries that localize cohesive behavior and align with stable ownership or responsibility boundaries, while defining technical shared packages separately.","rationale":"Package organization affects dependency direction and change locality.","consequences":["Imports become architectural signals","Package restructuring may be required as responsibilities evolve"],"implementation_implications":["Define package APIs","Prevent circular dependencies","Separate implementation details from public interfaces"],"verification_method":"Analyze imports and representative changes to verify that related changes remain localized and dependency direction remains valid.","outcome_learning":"Track import cycles, cross-package changes, and package ownership ambiguity.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B23-R01","D54-B24-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B23-R01"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B24-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B24-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Module and Package Boundary Decisions","subtopic":"Circular dependency prevention","concept":"Preventing package dependency cycles","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_DECISION","question":"Why should package dependency cycles be treated as architecture decision concerns?","answer":"Circular dependencies reduce dependency-direction clarity, increase change coupling, and can complicate initialization, testing, replacement, and incremental refactoring.","context":"A package graph contains or is approaching dependency cycles.","objective":"Maintain an acyclic or deliberately constrained dependency structure.","requirements":["Dependency direction must be explicit","Cycles require documented justification if allowed","Refactoring options must remain available"],"constraints":["Some language mechanisms permit cycles technically","Breaking cycles can require interface extraction or ownership changes"],"alternatives":["Allow cycles","Break cycles through interface boundaries","Restructure responsibilities"],"evidence":["Dependency graph","Initialization behavior","Change coupling","Test isolation"],"tradeoffs":["Allowing cycles may reduce immediate refactoring effort","Breaking cycles can improve architectural clarity but may require structural changes"],"decision":"Prefer eliminating avoidable package cycles and document any intentional cycle with explicit rationale and consequences.","rationale":"Technical import legality does not establish architectural quality.","consequences":["Some refactoring may be required","Dependency direction becomes a governed architectural constraint"],"implementation_implications":["Run dependency-cycle analysis","Introduce stable abstractions only when justified","Move shared responsibility to an appropriate owner"],"verification_method":"Run automated dependency-cycle analysis and verify that known exceptions are explicit and bounded.","outcome_learning":"Track recurrence of cycles and whether exceptions expand over time.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B24-R01","D54-B25-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B24-R01"},{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B25-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B24-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Module and Package Boundary Decisions","subtopic":"Public versus internal packages","concept":"Separating stable public APIs from internal implementation packages","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should a package be treated as public versus internal?","answer":"A package should be considered public only when its interface is intentionally supported as a dependency contract; internal packages should not be treated as stable APIs merely because they are technically importable.","context":"A codebase has packages consumed by multiple modules and potentially external clients.","objective":"Make API stability expectations explicit.","requirements":["Public interfaces must have ownership","Compatibility expectations must be defined","Internal implementation must remain changeable"],"constraints":["Language visibility may not enforce architectural intent","Legacy consumers may already depend on internal modules"],"alternatives":["Everything importable is public","Explicit public API surface","Compatibility wrapper around internal implementation"],"evidence":["Consumer count","Compatibility commitments","Documentation","Release policy"],"tradeoffs":["Broad public surfaces increase compatibility burden","Narrow surfaces preserve change freedom but may require adapters"],"decision":"Define an explicit supported public surface and treat other packages as internal unless an architecture decision states otherwise.","rationale":"Importability is a technical property, not a compatibility commitment.","consequences":["Consumers must migrate away from unsupported internals","Public interfaces require stronger compatibility testing"],"implementation_implications":["Mark supported APIs","Add compatibility tests","Provide migration paths for legacy internal consumers"],"verification_method":"Inventory imports and verify that supported consumers use declared public interfaces.","outcome_learning":"Track accidental internal dependencies and compatibility incidents.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B24-R01","D54-B24-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B24-R01"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B24-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B24-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Module and Package Boundary Decisions","subtopic":"Shared utility placement","concept":"Preventing generic utility packages from becoming dependency sinks","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should shared utility packages be governed?","answer":"Shared utilities should have narrow, stable responsibilities; generic catch-all packages should be avoided because they can become dependency sinks and obscure ownership.","context":"Multiple packages need common functionality and a proposal exists for a broad shared utility package.","objective":"Provide reuse without creating uncontrolled coupling.","requirements":["Shared functionality must have a clear responsibility","Dependencies must remain directional","Ownership must be explicit"],"constraints":["Duplication may be undesirable","Over-centralization increases coupling"],"alternatives":["Broad utility package","Focused shared libraries","Local duplication for low-value helpers"],"evidence":["Reuse frequency","Change coupling","API stability","Dependency graph"],"tradeoffs":["Central reuse reduces duplication","Broad utility packages accumulate unrelated dependencies","Small local duplication can sometimes preserve boundaries"],"decision":"Create shared packages only for cohesive, stable functionality with measurable reuse value; keep boundary-specific helpers local.","rationale":"Reuse is valuable only when its coupling cost remains controlled.","consequences":["Some duplicate small helpers may remain intentionally","Shared packages require ownership and compatibility discipline"],"implementation_implications":["Define package scope","Reject unrelated additions","Monitor dependency growth"],"verification_method":"Review shared-package dependencies and responsibilities periodically and flag unrelated coupling.","outcome_learning":"Measure whether shared packages reduce duplication without increasing change coupling.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B24-R03","D54-B26-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"constrains","record_id":"D54-B24-R03"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B26-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B24-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Module and Package Boundary Decisions","subtopic":"Package migration","concept":"Incremental package restructuring","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should package boundaries be changed in a mature codebase?","answer":"Restructure packages incrementally using compatibility seams, dependency analysis, and verification rather than performing broad uncontrolled moves.","context":"An existing codebase has poor package boundaries and significant consumers.","objective":"Improve package architecture while preserving behavior.","requirements":["Existing behavior must remain verifiable","Consumers must be migrated deliberately","Dependency changes must be observable"],"constraints":["Large package moves can create noisy diffs","Legacy consumers may depend on internal paths"],"alternatives":["Big-bang restructuring","Incremental restructuring","Leave structure unchanged"],"evidence":["Import graph","Test coverage","Consumer inventory","Change coupling"],"tradeoffs":["Incremental migration reduces blast radius but extends transition","Big-bang restructuring is faster conceptually but harder to isolate failures"],"decision":"Prefer incremental package restructuring with explicit migration steps when the existing codebase has significant dependency complexity.","rationale":"Architecture improvements should not unnecessarily obscure behavioral changes.","consequences":["Temporary compatibility paths may exist","Old paths require controlled retirement"],"implementation_implications":["Introduce new package boundaries","Migrate consumers","Run regression tests","Remove compatibility paths after validation"],"verification_method":"Compare dependency graphs before and after each migration step and run regression validation.","outcome_learning":"Measure reduction in cycles, cross-package changes, and ownership ambiguity.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B24-R01","D54-B24-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B24-R01"},{"type":"uses","record_id":"D54-B24-R02"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B25-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Dependency Direction Decisions","subtopic":"Dependency inversion at architecture boundaries","concept":"Controlling dependency direction","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should dependency direction be selected between architectural layers or components?","answer":"Dependencies should generally point toward stable abstractions or ownership boundaries rather than from stable policy toward volatile implementation details.","context":"A system contains multiple layers or components with differing rates of change.","objective":"Reduce change propagation and preserve replaceability.","requirements":["Dependency direction must be explicit","Stable responsibilities should not depend unnecessarily on volatile details","Cycles should be avoided where practical"],"constraints":["Some framework constraints determine dependency direction","Inverting dependencies can introduce abstractions with maintenance cost"],"alternatives":["Direct implementation dependencies","Dependency inversion through interfaces","Shared concrete dependency"],"evidence":["Change frequency","Ownership","Dependency graph","Replacement requirements"],"tradeoffs":["Direct dependencies are simple but can increase coupling","Abstractions can reduce coupling but add design overhead"],"decision":"Use dependency inversion when a stable policy or boundary must remain independent of a volatile implementation and the abstraction has clear semantic value.","rationale":"Dependency direction should protect the parts of the architecture that need stability.","consequences":["Interfaces may become architectural contracts","Incorrect abstractions can create unnecessary indirection"],"implementation_implications":["Define stable contracts at the appropriate boundary","Keep concrete implementations behind the contract","Test contract behavior"],"verification_method":"Analyze dependency graph and verify that volatile implementation changes do not require modifications to protected policy components.","outcome_learning":"Track interface churn and dependency changes to determine whether the chosen abstraction is stable.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B23-R02","D54-B25-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B23-R02"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B25-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B25-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Dependency Direction Decisions","subtopic":"Layer dependency rules","concept":"Enforcing one-way architectural dependency flow","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_DECISION","question":"When should dependency direction be enforced as a hard architecture rule?","answer":"Dependency direction should be enforced when it represents a deliberate architectural invariant whose violation predictably increases coupling, security risk, test difficulty, or change propagation.","context":"A layered architecture requires lower-level and higher-level responsibilities to remain separated.","objective":"Prevent architectural erosion through accidental reverse dependencies.","requirements":["Allowed dependency directions must be explicit","Exceptions must be documented","Automated detection should be used where feasible"],"constraints":["Some cross-cutting concerns need carefully designed access","Rigid rules can become counterproductive if the architecture changes"],"alternatives":["No enforcement","Manual review","Automated dependency rules"],"evidence":["Dependency graph","Architecture rules","Change history","Security boundaries"],"tradeoffs":["Automation provides continuous enforcement","Manual review permits context but can miss gradual drift"],"decision":"Encode dependency direction as an automated constraint when the rule is deterministic and materially protects architecture.","rationale":"Repeated architectural invariants should not depend solely on human memory.","consequences":["Violations surface early","Architecture rules become maintainable artifacts"],"implementation_implications":["Create dependency allowlists or deny rules","Run checks in CI","Provide documented exceptions"],"verification_method":"Inject known forbidden dependencies and verify the architecture check rejects them.","outcome_learning":"Review exception frequency and rule usefulness periodically.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B24-R02","D54-B25-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B24-R02"},{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B25-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B25-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Dependency Direction Decisions","subtopic":"Stable versus volatile dependencies","concept":"Protecting stable architecture from volatile implementation details","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should volatility influence dependency direction?","answer":"If one component changes substantially more often than another, direct dependency from the stable component to the volatile implementation can increase change propagation and should be evaluated for inversion or isolation.","context":"A stable domain component directly imports a rapidly changing external or infrastructure implementation.","objective":"Reduce unnecessary coupling between different rates of change.","requirements":["Volatility must be evidenced","The stable responsibility must remain independently testable","The proposed boundary must have semantic value"],"constraints":["Not every volatile dependency warrants an abstraction","Additional interfaces have maintenance costs"],"alternatives":["Direct dependency","Adapter boundary","Dependency inversion"],"evidence":["Change history","Release frequency","Test impact","Ownership"],"tradeoffs":["Direct use minimizes indirection","Adapters isolate volatility","Interfaces may stabilize dependencies but can become artificial"],"decision":"Introduce an isolation or inversion boundary when volatility materially increases change propagation and the boundary corresponds to a real responsibility.","rationale":"Dependency direction can be used to contain volatility, but abstraction should not be created solely from theoretical change risk.","consequences":["Stable components become less exposed to implementation changes","Additional contract maintenance may be required"],"implementation_implications":["Define adapter/interface","Keep volatile dependencies outside stable core","Add contract tests"],"verification_method":"Measure changes required in stable components after implementation changes before and after the boundary.","outcome_learning":"Track interface churn and reduction in change propagation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B25-R01","D54-B26-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B25-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B26-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B25-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Dependency Direction Decisions","subtopic":"Dependency cycles","concept":"Resolving architectural dependency cycles","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an architectural dependency cycle be resolved?","answer":"First identify whether the cycle reflects a shared responsibility, incorrect ownership, or an inappropriate abstraction; then restructure ownership, extract a stable contract, or move behavior to remove the cycle where practical.","context":"Two components depend on each other directly or indirectly.","objective":"Restore explicit dependency direction without hiding the cycle behind superficial indirection.","requirements":["Cycle cause must be identified","Responsibility ownership must be clarified","The solution must preserve required behavior"],"constraints":["Breaking a cycle may require moving code or changing interfaces","Artificial abstractions can preserve conceptual coupling while hiding technical coupling"],"alternatives":["Allow cycle","Extract interface","Move responsibility","Create shared stable component"],"evidence":["Dependency graph","Responsibility analysis","Change scenarios"],"tradeoffs":["Interface extraction may reduce technical coupling","Responsibility movement may be more semantically correct","Allowing cycles preserves current structure but weakens directionality"],"decision":"Resolve cycles according to the underlying responsibility problem rather than automatically introducing interfaces.","rationale":"A cycle is evidence of a relationship problem, not merely a syntax problem.","consequences":["Some responsibilities may move","Dependency direction becomes clearer"],"implementation_implications":["Analyze ownership","Refactor dependency edges","Add architecture tests preventing recurrence"],"verification_method":"Recompute the dependency graph and verify the intended direction after refactoring.","outcome_learning":"Track recurrence and determine whether the chosen ownership model remains coherent.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B24-R02","D54-B25-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"uses","record_id":"D54-B24-R02"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B25-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B25-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Dependency Direction Decisions","subtopic":"Dependency ownership","concept":"Assigning ownership to dependency relationships","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should dependency ownership be explicit?","answer":"Explicit ownership identifies who defines, changes, verifies, and supports a dependency contract, reducing ambiguous responsibility and uncontrolled compatibility changes.","context":"Multiple teams or modules depend on a shared interface or component.","objective":"Make dependency governance and evolution responsibilities explicit.","requirements":["Contract owner must be identifiable","Consumers must know compatibility expectations","Changes must have impact assessment"],"constraints":["Shared ownership can be difficult","Central ownership can become a bottleneck"],"alternatives":["No explicit owner","Consumer-owned contract","Provider-owned contract","Joint governance"],"evidence":["Change authority","Consumer/provider responsibilities","Compatibility policy"],"tradeoffs":["Provider ownership centralizes contract control","Consumer ownership can preserve consumer needs","Joint governance increases coordination"],"decision":"Assign explicit ownership to every important dependency contract and define compatibility and change procedures.","rationale":"Dependency direction without ownership still leaves change authority ambiguous.","consequences":["Contract changes become traceable","Ownership disputes become detectable"],"implementation_implications":["Record contract owner","Define compatibility policy","Require impact analysis for breaking changes"],"verification_method":"Audit important dependencies for identifiable ownership and documented compatibility expectations.","outcome_learning":"Track dependency-related incidents and disputed changes.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B25-R01","D54-B30-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B25-R01"},{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B30-R01"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B26-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Shared Library vs Independent Component Decisions","subtopic":"Reuse boundary","concept":"Choosing between shared library and independent component","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should functionality be implemented as a shared library rather than an independent component?","answer":"A shared library is generally suitable when consumers need local execution, shared behavior is stable, independent deployment is unnecessary, and centralized compatibility management is acceptable.","context":"Multiple components need the same functionality.","objective":"Choose an appropriate reuse boundary.","requirements":["Reuse semantics must be stable","Consumer coupling must be acceptable","Deployment independence must not be required"],"constraints":["Library updates can propagate to consumers","Runtime isolation is limited"],"alternatives":["Shared library","Independent service/component","Duplicated local implementation"],"evidence":["Runtime isolation needs","Release independence","Reuse frequency","API stability"],"tradeoffs":["Libraries provide low-latency local reuse but increase release coupling","Independent components isolate deployment but add communication and operational cost","Duplication avoids coupling but increases maintenance"],"decision":"Prefer a shared library when local reuse and consistent behavior matter more than independent runtime lifecycle; otherwise evaluate an independent component.","rationale":"The boundary should match the required lifecycle and isolation model.","consequences":["Library consumers share compatibility constraints","Independent components require operational infrastructure"],"implementation_implications":["Define supported library API","Version compatibility","Avoid hidden global state"],"verification_method":"Verify that deployment independence and runtime isolation are not required before adopting the library boundary.","outcome_learning":"Monitor upgrade coordination and consumer breakage.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B24-R04","D54-B26-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B24-R04"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B26-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B26-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Shared Library vs Independent Component Decisions","subtopic":"Release coupling","concept":"Evaluating release coupling introduced by shared libraries","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should release coupling affect a shared-library decision?","answer":"If independent consumers need materially different release schedules or compatibility lifecycles, a shared library may impose unacceptable coordination and an independent component or compatibility layer should be evaluated.","context":"Several consumers depend on one library but evolve at different speeds.","objective":"Determine whether shared release coupling is acceptable.","requirements":["Consumer release requirements must be known","Compatibility policy must be explicit","Upgrade effort must be measurable"],"constraints":["Independent components introduce operational complexity","Libraries simplify local calls"],"alternatives":["Single shared library version","Multiple supported library versions","Independent component"],"evidence":["Release frequency","Upgrade effort","Breaking-change history","Consumer compatibility requirements"],"tradeoffs":["Single version reduces fragmentation but increases coordination","Multiple versions increase maintenance","Independent components increase operational overhead"],"decision":"Use an independent lifecycle when release coupling materially constrains required consumer autonomy and the operational cost is justified.","rationale":"Lifecycle independence is a first-class architectural property.","consequences":["Consumers can evolve independently","Operational responsibility increases for independent components"],"implementation_implications":["Define compatibility contracts","Provide migration strategy","If distributed, define communication and failure semantics"],"verification_method":"Measure release coordination and upgrade effort and compare against the cost of an independent lifecycle.","outcome_learning":"Track time spent coordinating releases and consumer upgrade failures.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B26-R01","D54-B22-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B26-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B22-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B26-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Shared Library vs Independent Component Decisions","subtopic":"Failure isolation","concept":"Runtime failure isolation as a boundary driver","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When does failure isolation justify an independent component instead of a shared library?","answer":"Independent runtime isolation is justified when a library failure, resource exhaustion, or unsafe behavior could materially compromise unrelated consumers and the isolation benefit exceeds its operational cost.","context":"A reusable capability has different reliability or resource characteristics from its consumers.","objective":"Prevent shared runtime failures from propagating across consumers.","requirements":["Failure impact must be material","Isolation must reduce the relevant failure path","Operational recovery must be feasible"],"constraints":["Independent components introduce communication and deployment dependencies"],"alternatives":["Shared library","Process-isolated component","Distributed service"],"evidence":["Failure modes","Resource usage","Blast radius","Recovery objectives"],"tradeoffs":["Library simplifies execution but shares failure domain","Independent process reduces some failure propagation but adds operational complexity"],"decision":"Choose stronger runtime isolation when the shared failure domain violates documented reliability or security requirements.","rationale":"Runtime boundaries can be justified by failure consequences rather than organizational preference.","consequences":["Additional deployment and observability requirements","Potential communication overhead"],"implementation_implications":["Define resource limits","Define health and recovery behavior","Test failure containment"],"verification_method":"Perform failure-injection testing and verify that unrelated consumers remain within required availability or safety boundaries.","outcome_learning":"Measure actual blast radius and recovery behavior.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B23-R03","D54-B26-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B23-R03"},{"type":"constrains","record_id":"D54-B26-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B26-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Shared Library vs Independent Component Decisions","subtopic":"Behavior consistency","concept":"Centralized behavior versus consumer autonomy","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should behavior consistency affect the shared-library decision?","answer":"A shared library can enforce common local behavior across consumers, while an independent component can centralize runtime policy but introduces communication and availability dependencies.","context":"Consumers need consistent behavior from a common capability.","objective":"Choose where consistency should be enforced.","requirements":["Required invariants must be explicit","Behavioral compatibility must be testable","Consumer autonomy requirements must be understood"],"constraints":["Centralized runtime behavior can become a shared dependency","Library behavior changes propagate through releases"],"alternatives":["Shared library","Independent service","Separate implementations"],"evidence":["Behavioral invariants","Release requirements","Runtime availability needs"],"tradeoffs":["Library provides local consistency but release coupling","Service centralizes behavior but introduces runtime dependency","Independent implementations maximize autonomy but risk divergence"],"decision":"Choose the boundary according to whether consistency is primarily a code-level invariant or a runtime policy requiring centralized control.","rationale":"The location of behavior enforcement should match the type of consistency required.","consequences":["Library changes affect consumer builds","Service changes affect runtime behavior"],"implementation_implications":["Define compatibility tests","Document behavioral contract","Monitor divergence where implementations are independent"],"verification_method":"Test invariant behavior across consumers and verify compatibility with the chosen lifecycle model.","outcome_learning":"Track behavioral drift and consumer-specific adaptation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B26-R01","D54-B27-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B26-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B27-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B26-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Shared Library vs Independent Component Decisions","subtopic":"Library evolution","concept":"Preventing shared-library compatibility drift","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_DECISION","question":"How should shared libraries be governed when used by many consumers?","answer":"Shared libraries should use explicit compatibility policies, contract tests, versioning, and dependency-impact analysis proportional to their consumer count and criticality.","context":"A library has become a dependency for multiple components.","objective":"Control the blast radius of library evolution.","requirements":["Supported API must be explicit","Compatibility expectations must be testable","Breaking changes require migration planning"],"constraints":["Consumer environments may differ","Maintaining many versions increases complexity"],"alternatives":["Uncontrolled latest-version adoption","Strict compatibility policy","Independent component migration"],"evidence":["Consumer inventory","API usage","Version adoption","Historical breakages"],"tradeoffs":["Strict compatibility reduces breakage but constrains evolution","Rapid evolution increases consumer migration burden"],"decision":"Increase compatibility governance as consumer count and criticality increase; use an independent component when lifecycle independence becomes more valuable than shared local execution.","rationale":"Shared dependencies create systemic change propagation.","consequences":["Library releases require impact analysis","Compatibility becomes an architectural concern"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain API compatibility tests","Track consumers","Publish migration guidance"],"verification_method":"Run consumer compatibility tests against proposed library changes.","outcome_learning":"Measure consumer breakages, migration time, and version fragmentation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B26-R01","D54-B26-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B26-R01"},{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B26-R02"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B27-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Synchronous vs Asynchronous Architecture","subtopic":"Interaction semantics","concept":"Choosing synchronous or asynchronous communication from interaction requirements","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should determine whether an interaction is synchronous or asynchronous?","answer":"Choose based on response-time requirements, coupling tolerance, workflow semantics, failure handling, ordering, delivery guarantees, and whether the caller must wait for completion.","context":"A component needs to communicate with another component.","objective":"Select communication semantics that match the business and technical interaction contract.","requirements":["Completion semantics must be explicit","Latency expectations must be defined","Failure and retry behavior must be defined"],"constraints":["Asynchronous communication adds state and delivery concerns","Synchronous communication creates temporal dependency"],"alternatives":["Synchronous request-response","Asynchronous messaging","Hybrid interaction"],"evidence":["Latency budget","User interaction semantics","Failure requirements","Work duration","Ordering requirements"],"tradeoffs":["Synchronous calls simplify immediate results but couple availability and latency","Asynchronous flows decouple timing but require workflow state and delivery handling"],"decision":"Use synchronous interaction when the caller requires an immediate bounded result; use asynchronous interaction when work can be decoupled from caller timing and the system can explicitly manage eventual completion.","rationale":"Communication mode is a semantic decision, not merely a performance optimization.","consequences":["Async flows require status and failure handling","Sync flows require timeout and dependency-failure handling"],"implementation_implications":["Define timeout behavior for synchronous calls","Define message identity, retry, idempotency, and completion state for asynchronous flows"],"verification_method":"Test normal, timeout, duplicate, delayed, and dependency-failure scenarios against the interaction contract.","outcome_learning":"Measure latency, queue delay, failure rates, duplicate handling, and completion outcomes.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B27-R02","D54-B27-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B27-R02"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B27-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B27-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Synchronous vs Asynchronous Architecture","subtopic":"Latency and coupling","concept":"Temporal coupling as a synchronous-architecture constraint","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When does synchronous communication create excessive architectural coupling?","answer":"Synchronous communication becomes problematic when caller success depends on multiple remote dependencies whose latency, availability, or failure behavior cannot remain within the caller's required budget.","context":"A request path depends on several synchronous downstream calls.","objective":"Determine whether temporal coupling violates latency or reliability requirements.","requirements":["End-to-end latency budget must be defined","Dependency failure behavior must be bounded","Critical path must be measurable"],"constraints":["Some interactions inherently require immediate results","Adding asynchronous processing changes user-visible semantics"],"alternatives":["Retain synchronous chain","Parallelize synchronous calls","Convert portions to asynchronous workflow"],"evidence":["Tail latency","Dependency availability","Timeout budgets","Critical-path analysis"],"tradeoffs":["Synchronous chains simplify flow but compound dependency risk","Parallelism can reduce latency but increases concurrency complexity","Async processing reduces temporal coupling but changes completion semantics"],"decision":"Reduce synchronous dependency depth when measured latency or reliability requirements cannot be met within the end-to-end budget.","rationale":"Temporal dependency accumulates across synchronous call chains.","consequences":["Some operations may become eventually completed","Workflow state and user feedback may be required"],"implementation_implications":["Define per-dependency timeout budgets","Avoid unbounded synchronous chains","Introduce asynchronous workflow where semantics permit"],"verification_method":"Measure end-to-end and dependency tail latency under representative load and failure conditions.","outcome_learning":"Track dependency contribution to latency and timeout failures.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B27-R01","D54-B15-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B27-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B15-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B27-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Synchronous vs Asynchronous Architecture","subtopic":"Asynchronous reliability","concept":"Designing asynchronous interactions for duplicate and delayed delivery","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What must be decided before adopting asynchronous communication for critical workflows?","answer":"Critical asynchronous workflows require explicit message identity, delivery semantics, idempotency, ordering requirements, retry policy, failure handling, persistence, and completion tracking.","context":"A business-critical workflow is being moved from synchronous processing to asynchronous messaging.","objective":"Preserve correctness despite delayed, duplicated, reordered, or failed message delivery.","requirements":["Messages must be uniquely identifiable","Consumers must handle relevant duplicate scenarios","Workflow completion must be observable","Failure handling must be explicit"],"constraints":["Exactly-once business outcomes may require application-level idempotency","Message infrastructure guarantees vary"],"alternatives":["At-most-once processing","At-least-once delivery with idempotency","Synchronous processing"],"evidence":["Workflow invariants","Message-system guarantees","Failure scenarios","Business retry semantics"],"tradeoffs":["At-least-once delivery improves durability but requires duplicate handling","At-most-once can lose work","Synchronous processing has stronger temporal coupling"],"decision":"For critical asynchronous workflows, require explicit idempotency and completion semantics rather than relying on infrastructure delivery guarantees alone.","rationale":"Transport delivery and business outcome correctness are separate concerns.","consequences":["Consumers need durable or reconstructable processing state","Testing must include duplicate and delayed delivery"],"implementation_implications":["Assign message IDs","Persist processing state where required","Define retry and dead-letter behavior","Record outcome state"],"verification_method":"Test duplicate, reordered, delayed, failed, and replayed messages and verify business invariants.","outcome_learning":"Measure duplicate processing, retries, dead letters, and unreconciled workflow states.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B27-R01","D54-B22-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B27-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B22-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B27-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Synchronous vs Asynchronous Architecture","subtopic":"Hybrid interaction","concept":"Combining synchronous acknowledgement with asynchronous completion","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When is a hybrid synchronous-acknowledgement and asynchronous-completion model useful?","answer":"A hybrid model is useful when the caller needs immediate acceptance or validation but the full operation can complete independently after the initial request.","context":"A long-running operation cannot reliably complete within an interactive request latency budget.","objective":"Provide immediate request feedback without forcing the caller to wait for full completion.","requirements":["Acceptance semantics must be distinguishable from completion","Workflow state must be queryable or observable","Failure after acceptance must be represented"],"constraints":["Clients must understand asynchronous completion","Additional workflow state is required"],"alternatives":["Fully synchronous operation","Fully asynchronous submission","Synchronous acknowledgement plus asynchronous completion"],"evidence":["Operation duration","User interaction requirements","Failure semantics"],"tradeoffs":["Hybrid model improves responsiveness but introduces lifecycle state","Fully synchronous simplifies client semantics but may exceed latency limits","Fully asynchronous maximizes decoupling but may provide weaker immediate feedback"],"decision":"Use hybrid semantics when immediate acceptance is meaningful but full completion cannot fit the required synchronous budget.","rationale":"Separating acceptance from completion preserves useful synchronous feedback without retaining long temporal coupling.","consequences":["API contracts need explicit lifecycle states","Clients must handle pending and failed completion"],"implementation_implications":["Define request ID","Define states such as accepted, processing, completed, failed","Provide status retrieval or event notification"],"verification_method":"Test acceptance, processing failure, retry, completion, and client polling/notification behavior.","outcome_learning":"Measure pending duration, completion success, and abandoned workflows.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B27-R01","D54-B27-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B27-R01"},{"type":"uses","record_id":"D54-B27-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B27-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Synchronous vs Asynchronous Architecture","subtopic":"Ordering requirements","concept":"Making ordering guarantees explicit","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should ordering requirements influence asynchronous architecture?","answer":"Ordering should be specified only where business correctness requires it, with the required ordering scope explicitly defined rather than assuming global ordering.","context":"An asynchronous workflow processes multiple related events.","objective":"Provide sufficient ordering guarantees without imposing unnecessary throughput and coordination costs.","requirements":["Required ordering scope must be explicit","Out-of-order behavior must be defined","Ordering-sensitive state transitions must be protected"],"constraints":["Global ordering can reduce concurrency","Distributed systems may provide only scoped ordering"],"alternatives":["Global ordering","Partition or key-based ordering","No ordering guarantee"],"evidence":["Business invariants","Event relationships","Concurrency requirements"],"tradeoffs":["Stronger ordering simplifies some reasoning but reduces parallelism","Scoped ordering preserves more concurrency","No ordering requires consumers to tolerate reordering"],"decision":"Implement the narrowest ordering guarantee that is sufficient to preserve required business invariants.","rationale":"Unnecessary ordering creates coordination cost without additional correctness value.","consequences":["Partitioning strategy may become architecture-critical","Consumers must explicitly handle unsupported ordering cases"],"implementation_implications":["Identify ordering key","Define ordering scope","Test reordered events"],"verification_method":"Inject reordered events and verify that required invariants remain satisfied within the declared ordering scope.","outcome_learning":"Monitor ordering violations and throughput impact.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B27-R03","D54-B16-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B27-R03"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B16-R05"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B28-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Stateful vs Stateless Component Decisions","subtopic":"State placement","concept":"Choosing stateful versus stateless component architecture","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should determine whether a component is stateful or stateless?","answer":"State should be kept within a component only when locality, performance, workflow continuity, or correctness benefits justify the operational and scaling complexity; otherwise state should be externalized or reconstructed where practical.","context":"A component needs state across requests or executions.","objective":"Choose state placement that satisfies correctness, scalability, recovery, and performance requirements.","requirements":["State ownership must be explicit","Durability requirements must be defined","Recovery behavior must be specified"],"constraints":["External state introduces network and consistency dependencies","Local state complicates scaling and replacement"],"alternatives":["In-process state","External durable state","Hybrid state"],"evidence":["State size","Durability needs","Access patterns","Scaling requirements","Recovery objectives"],"tradeoffs":["Local state can reduce latency but complicates failover","External state improves replacement and scaling but adds dependency and latency"],"decision":"Use local state only when its lifecycle and failure semantics are acceptable; externalize authoritative state when independent replacement, scaling, or durability is required.","rationale":"State placement determines failure, scaling, consistency, and recovery behavior.","consequences":["Stateful components require explicit recovery strategy","Stateless components require dependable state dependencies"],"implementation_implications":["Define authoritative state","Define persistence and recovery","Test restart and concurrent access behavior"],"verification_method":"Perform restart, scaling, failure, and consistency tests against documented state semantics.","outcome_learning":"Monitor state-loss incidents, recovery time, and synchronization failures.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B28-R02","D54-B28-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B28-R02"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B28-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B28-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Stateful vs Stateless Component Decisions","subtopic":"Horizontal scaling","concept":"State as a constraint on horizontal scaling","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How does local state affect horizontal scaling?","answer":"Local mutable state can require request affinity, state replication, partitioning, or constrained scaling, while externally accessible authoritative state can simplify replacement and distribution at the cost of additional coordination.","context":"A component needs to scale across multiple instances.","objective":"Determine whether local state is compatible with the desired scaling model.","requirements":["State consistency must be preserved","Instance routing behavior must be defined","Scale-out behavior must be tested"],"constraints":["Replication may be expensive","External state introduces dependency latency"],"alternatives":["Keep local state with affinity","Replicate local state","Externalize state"],"evidence":["State access frequency","Consistency requirements","Scale-out patterns","Resource limits"],"tradeoffs":["Affinity can preserve locality but reduces routing flexibility","Replication increases coordination","External state simplifies instance replacement but adds dependency"],"decision":"Externalize authoritative shared state when unrestricted horizontal scaling and instance replacement are requirements and the added dependency is acceptable.","rationale":"Scaling assumptions must account for where mutable state resides.","consequences":["State infrastructure becomes part of availability planning","Stateless compute becomes easier to replace"],"implementation_implications":["Define state access contract","Set consistency semantics","Test concurrent instances and failover"],"verification_method":"Scale the component across multiple instances and verify state consistency and request behavior under instance loss.","outcome_learning":"Monitor state-store latency, contention, and consistency incidents.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B28-R01","D54-B16-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B28-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B16-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B28-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Stateful vs Stateless Component Decisions","subtopic":"Recovery and durability","concept":"Aligning state durability with recovery requirements","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should recovery objectives affect state placement?","answer":"State that must survive component replacement or failure should be stored or reconstructed through a mechanism whose durability and recovery characteristics satisfy the required recovery objectives.","context":"A component currently stores important state only in process memory.","objective":"Prevent unacceptable state loss during restart or failure.","requirements":["Required state-loss tolerance must be explicit","Recovery process must be defined","State reconstruction must be validated if used"],"constraints":["Durable storage adds latency and operational dependencies","Reconstruction may depend on retained event or source data"],"alternatives":["Accept state loss","Persist state","Reconstruct state"],"evidence":["RPO","State criticality","Recovery time","Reconstruction source availability"],"tradeoffs":["Persistence preserves state but adds storage dependency","Reconstruction can reduce direct state storage but increases replay complexity","Accepting loss is valid only when requirements permit it"],"decision":"Persist or reliably reconstruct state when state loss exceeds the documented tolerance; do not treat in-memory state as durable by assumption.","rationale":"State durability is a requirement-driven property.","consequences":["Persistence or replay mechanisms require operational ownership","Recovery becomes testable architecture behavior"],"implementation_implications":["Define durable state schema or reconstruction source","Test restart and recovery","Define corruption handling"],"verification_method":"Perform controlled restart or failure recovery and verify state against the declared recovery objective.","outcome_learning":"Measure actual recovery duration and state-loss outcomes.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B28-R01","D54-B14-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B28-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B14-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B28-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Stateful vs Stateless Component Decisions","subtopic":"Cache state versus authoritative state","concept":"Separating recoverable cache state from authoritative state","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should cache state be distinguished from authoritative state?","answer":"A cache should be treated as recoverable derived state unless the architecture explicitly defines it as authoritative; correctness should not silently depend on a cache that can be evicted or lost.","context":"A component stores frequently accessed data locally for performance.","objective":"Prevent cache loss from becoming an unplanned correctness failure.","requirements":["Authority of each state type must be explicit","Cache invalidation or refresh behavior must be defined","Recovery without cache contents must be tested"],"constraints":["Some applications may intentionally use cache as part of a specialized state model","Rebuilding cache can have cost"],"alternatives":["Authoritative local state","External authority plus local cache","No cache"],"evidence":["Data ownership","Reconstruction mechanism","Freshness requirements"],"tradeoffs":["Caching improves access performance but introduces freshness concerns","Treating cache as authority simplifies some access paths but increases durability requirements"],"decision":"Classify state explicitly as authoritative or derived and require derived caches to be safely rebuildable unless requirements state otherwise.","rationale":"State semantics must be known before failure behavior can be reasoned about.","consequences":["Cache eviction becomes a normal recoverable event","Invalidation and refresh are architecture concerns"],"implementation_implications":["Define cache key and freshness policy","Define rebuild path","Test cold-cache operation"],"verification_method":"Run the component with an empty or invalidated cache and verify required behavior remains correct.","outcome_learning":"Monitor stale-data events, rebuild duration, and cache dependency failures.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B28-R01","D54-B15-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B28-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B15-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B28-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Stateful vs Stateless Component Decisions","subtopic":"State ownership and concurrency","concept":"Explicit ownership of mutable state","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why must mutable state ownership be explicit in concurrent components?","answer":"Explicit ownership determines who may mutate state, what synchronization semantics apply, and how concurrent updates are serialized, rejected, merged, or retried.","context":"Multiple execution contexts can access and modify shared state.","objective":"Prevent race conditions and ambiguous state transitions.","requirements":["Mutation authority must be explicit","Concurrency semantics must be defined","State transitions must preserve invariants"],"constraints":["Locks may reduce concurrency","Distributed coordination adds complexity"],"alternatives":["Single owner","Shared synchronized state","Optimistic concurrency"],"evidence":["Mutation frequency","Invariant requirements","Concurrency model","Failure semantics"],"tradeoffs":["Single ownership simplifies reasoning but can constrain throughput","Shared synchronization increases coordination","Optimistic approaches require conflict handling"],"decision":"Choose an explicit state ownership and concurrency model based on invariants and workload rather than allowing implicit shared mutation.","rationale":"Concurrency correctness depends on ownership and transition semantics, not merely data structure choice.","consequences":["Mutation paths become traceable","Concurrency tests become architecture verification artifacts"],"implementation_implications":["Define state transition rules","Use appropriate synchronization or conflict detection","Test concurrent updates"],"verification_method":"Run deterministic concurrency tests and race/failure scenarios against state invariants.","outcome_learning":"Track conflict rates, retries, invariant violations, and contention.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B28-R01","D54-B30-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B28-R01"},{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B30-R02"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B29-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Centralized vs Distributed Responsibility","subtopic":"Responsibility placement","concept":"Choosing centralized versus distributed responsibility","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should determine whether a responsibility is centralized or distributed?","answer":"Centralize a responsibility when consistency, authority, policy enforcement, or coordination dominates; distribute it when local autonomy, scale, failure isolation, or ownership requires independent execution.","context":"A responsibility could be implemented by one central component or replicated across multiple components.","objective":"Place responsibility where its required consistency and autonomy characteristics are satisfied.","requirements":["Authority must be explicit","Consistency requirements must be measurable","Failure and scaling implications must be understood"],"constraints":["Centralization can become a bottleneck or single failure domain","Distribution can create coordination and divergence risks"],"alternatives":["Centralized authority","Distributed responsibility","Hybrid authority with local execution"],"evidence":["Consistency needs","Scale requirements","Ownership","Failure domain","Coordination cost"],"tradeoffs":["Centralization simplifies authority but may constrain availability and scale","Distribution improves autonomy but requires synchronization or conflict handling"],"decision":"Centralize authority when a single source of policy or truth is required; distribute execution when local autonomy or scaling provides measurable value without violating invariants.","rationale":"Authority and execution do not always need the same placement.","consequences":["Hybrid designs may separate decision authority from execution","Distributed responsibilities require explicit consistency semantics"],"implementation_implications":["Define authoritative source","Define local responsibilities","Specify synchronization and conflict rules"],"verification_method":"Trace decisions and state mutations to their authoritative owners and test distributed behavior under divergence and failure.","outcome_learning":"Monitor synchronization failures, authority conflicts, and coordination overhead.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B29-R02","D54-B30-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B29-R02"},{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B30-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B29-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Centralized vs Distributed Responsibility","subtopic":"Central policy and local enforcement","concept":"Centralized policy with distributed enforcement","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When is centralized policy with distributed enforcement appropriate?","answer":"This model is appropriate when policy consistency must be centrally governed but enforcement must occur close to resources, users, or execution contexts for latency, autonomy, or resilience.","context":"A system needs consistent policy while multiple execution points operate independently.","objective":"Separate policy authority from enforcement locality.","requirements":["Policy source must be authoritative","Enforcement behavior must be consistent with policy","Policy propagation semantics must be defined"],"constraints":["Policy updates may not propagate instantly","Local enforcement can temporarily operate on older policy"],"alternatives":["Central policy and central enforcement","Fully distributed policy","Central policy with distributed enforcement"],"evidence":["Latency requirements","Policy consistency requirements","Connectivity constraints","Failure behavior"],"tradeoffs":["Central enforcement simplifies consistency but can create bottlenecks","Distributed enforcement improves locality but introduces propagation concerns"],"decision":"Use centralized policy with distributed enforcement when policy authority must remain unified but local execution requires independent operation.","rationale":"Separating authority from enforcement can satisfy both consistency and locality requirements.","consequences":["Policy versioning becomes important","Local enforcement must expose policy version or freshness state where relevant"],"implementation_implications":["Version policies","Define propagation and rollback","Define behavior when policy authority is unavailable"],"verification_method":"Test stale-policy, unavailable-policy-source, and policy-update scenarios and verify declared enforcement behavior.","outcome_learning":"Monitor policy propagation delay, stale enforcement, and override frequency.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B29-R01","D54-B17-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B29-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B17-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B29-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Centralized vs Distributed Responsibility","subtopic":"Distributed decision making","concept":"Avoiding unnecessary distributed consensus","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When should a responsibility remain centralized instead of being distributed?","answer":"A responsibility should remain centralized when distributed coordination would add substantial complexity without providing a required benefit in scale, availability, autonomy, or failure isolation.","context":"A team proposes distributing a decision function across several components.","objective":"Determine whether distribution provides sufficient architectural value.","requirements":["Distributed benefits must be measurable","Coordination requirements must be understood","Consistency semantics must be explicit"],"constraints":["Distributed decisions may require coordination","Centralized decisions can become bottlenecks"],"alternatives":["Central decision authority","Partitioned authority","Fully distributed decisions"],"evidence":["Decision volume","Latency","Failure requirements","Coordination frequency"],"tradeoffs":["Centralization simplifies consistency","Partitioning can scale responsibility but requires clear partition ownership","Full distribution can maximize autonomy but increases coordination complexity"],"decision":"Keep decision authority centralized or partitioned when the coordination cost of full distribution exceeds its documented benefits.","rationale":"Distribution should solve a concrete scalability, availability, or autonomy problem.","consequences":["Central or partitioned authority remains a deliberate constraint","Scaling may require targeted optimization"],"implementation_implications":["Measure decision workload","Define partition boundaries if needed","Monitor bottlenecks"],"verification_method":"Load-test decision authority and compare measured bottlenecks against the cost of distributed coordination.","outcome_learning":"Monitor decision latency, contention, and coordination failures.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B29-R01","D54-B16-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B29-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B16-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B29-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Centralized vs Distributed Responsibility","subtopic":"Single source of truth","concept":"Separating authoritative truth from distributed projections","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How can distributed components avoid multiple conflicting sources of truth?","answer":"Define one authoritative owner for each critical mutable fact and treat distributed copies as projections, caches, or derived representations with explicit freshness and reconciliation semantics.","context":"Several components need local access to the same business information.","objective":"Prevent conflicting authorities while allowing distributed read or execution behavior.","requirements":["Authority must be identifiable","Derived copies must be distinguishable","Synchronization behavior must be defined"],"constraints":["Local copies may become temporarily stale","Central authority may become a dependency"],"alternatives":["Multiple writable copies","Single authority with projections","Partitioned authority"],"evidence":["Mutation ownership","Consistency requirements","Access patterns"],"tradeoffs":["Single authority simplifies correctness but can create dependency","Multiple writable copies improve autonomy but require conflict resolution","Partitioning can preserve local ownership when boundaries are clear"],"decision":"Use explicit authority boundaries and treat non-authoritative copies as derived unless a partitioned ownership model is deliberately defined.","rationale":"Conflicting authority makes correctness and recovery difficult to reason about.","consequences":["Read models need freshness semantics","Writes need clear ownership"],"implementation_implications":["Tag authoritative versus derived state","Define synchronization","Test stale and conflicting representations"],"verification_method":"Trace each critical fact to one authority or one explicit partition owner and test synchronization behavior.","outcome_learning":"Monitor stale-data incidents and reconciliation events.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B22-R02","D54-B29-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B22-R02"},{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B29-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B29-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Centralized vs Distributed Responsibility","subtopic":"Failure behavior","concept":"Centralized authority versus distributed availability","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should failure behavior influence centralized versus distributed responsibility?","answer":"The decision should account for whether loss of the central authority blocks critical operations and whether distributed operation can safely continue using cached, partitioned, or pre-authorized information.","context":"A central component is a dependency for multiple execution components.","objective":"Determine acceptable behavior when centralized authority becomes unavailable.","requirements":["Failure behavior must be explicit","Safety and correctness constraints must be preserved","Recovery and fallback behavior must be testable"],"constraints":["Allowing stale authority can create incorrect decisions","Blocking all operations can violate availability requirements"],"alternatives":["Fail closed","Fail open","Continue with cached state","Partition responsibility"],"evidence":["Safety impact","Availability requirements","Policy freshness","Recovery time"],"tradeoffs":["Fail-closed protects against unauthorized or unsafe operation but may reduce availability","Fail-open preserves availability but can violate security or correctness","Cached operation balances continuity and freshness only when bounded"],"decision":"Choose failure behavior according to the consequence of incorrect versus unavailable decisions, and explicitly define acceptable stale-state limits.","rationale":"Availability and correctness cannot be separated from authority placement.","consequences":["Fallback behavior becomes part of the architecture contract","Failure testing is mandatory for critical responsibilities"],"implementation_implications":["Define stale-state thresholds","Define fallback mode","Audit decisions made without central authority"],"verification_method":"Simulate authority loss and verify behavior against safety, correctness, and availability requirements.","outcome_learning":"Measure fallback frequency, stale decisions, and recovery duration.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B29-R02","D54-B17-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B29-R02"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B17-R05"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B30-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Ownership, Responsibility and Authority Boundaries","subtopic":"Authority assignment","concept":"Explicit ownership of architecture responsibilities","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should ownership and authority be assigned to architectural responsibilities?","answer":"Every significant responsibility should have an identifiable owner with authority appropriate to make changes, define invariants, approve exceptions, and remain accountable for outcomes.","context":"Multiple teams or components participate in one architectural capability.","objective":"Prevent ambiguous responsibility and ungoverned architectural changes.","requirements":["Owner must be identifiable","Decision authority must be explicit","Accountability must match authority"],"constraints":["Responsibilities may cross organizational boundaries","Shared ownership can create decision delays"],"alternatives":["Single owner","Shared ownership","Federated ownership with central policy"],"evidence":["Change authority","Operational responsibility","Dependency relationships","Failure accountability"],"tradeoffs":["Single ownership improves clarity but may concentrate workload","Shared ownership increases representation but can create ambiguity","Federated models require explicit authority boundaries"],"decision":"Assign explicit ownership for each critical responsibility and document authority, consultation, and escalation boundaries.","rationale":"Responsibility without authority creates accountability gaps; authority without accountability creates uncontrolled change.","consequences":["Ownership becomes part of architecture metadata","Escalation paths become clearer"],"implementation_implications":["Record owner and backup owner","Define change approval rules","Define operational accountability"],"verification_method":"Audit critical architecture responsibilities and 