{"record_id":"D54-B30-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Ownership, Responsibility and Authority Boundaries","subtopic":"Decision rights","concept":"Separating responsibility from decision authority","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should responsibility and decision authority be separated or aligned?","answer":"A responsible party should have sufficient authority to perform required actions, while higher-risk decisions may require explicit approval from a separate authority according to governance requirements.","context":"A system contains operational responsibilities where not every actor should have unrestricted decision authority.","objective":"Align execution responsibility with controlled decision authority.","requirements":["Operational responsibility must be defined","Decision authority must be explicit","High-impact actions require appropriate authorization"],"constraints":["Excessive approval can slow operations","Excessive authority can increase risk"],"alternatives":["Full local authority","Central approval","Risk-based delegated authority"],"evidence":["Action impact","Risk classification","Operational response requirements","Governance constraints"],"tradeoffs":["Local authority improves speed","Central approval improves control but adds latency","Risk-based delegation balances speed and governance"],"decision":"Use explicit risk-based authority boundaries so routine responsibilities can operate autonomously while high-impact decisions require appropriate authorization.","rationale":"Execution ownership and authorization scope are related but not necessarily identical.","consequences":["Authority becomes contextual rather than universally broad","Approval paths require auditability"],"implementation_implications":["Classify actions by impact","Define authorization policy","Record approval and execution identity"],"verification_method":"Attempt representative authorized 