{"record_id":"D54-B30-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Ownership, Responsibility and Authority Boundaries","subtopic":"Boundary ambiguity","concept":"Detecting overlapping or missing ownership","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_DECISION","question":"How can architecture teams detect ownership gaps and overlaps?","answer":"Map critical responsibilities to owners, decision rights, operational duties, dependencies, and escalation paths; flag responsibilities with multiple conflicting authorities or no accountable owner.","context":"A complex architecture has overlapping team and component responsibilities.","objective":"Make ownership ambiguity observable and correctable.","requirements":["Critical responsibilities must be enumerated","Ownership mapping must be maintained","Conflicting authority must be detectable"],"constraints":["Ownership can change over time","Some responsibilities legitimately require collaboration"],"alternatives":["Informal ownership","Single-owner mapping","Responsibility matrix with explicit decision rights"],"evidence":["Architecture inventory","Service/component registry","Operational incidents","Decision records"],"tradeoffs":["Formal mapping adds maintenance effort","Informal ownership reduces documentation but increases ambiguity risk"],"decision":"Maintain an explicit responsibility-to-owner and authority mapping for critical architecture capabilities.","rationale":"Unmapped responsibility creates predictable gaps during change and failure.","consequences":["Ownership drift becomes detectable","Architecture governance gains a concrete verification artifact"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain responsibility registry","Record owner and authority","Review changes during architecture updates"],"verification_method":"Run an ownership completeness audit and flag missing, duplicate, or contradictory ownership assignments.","outcome_learning":"Track recurring ownership gaps and whether changes reduce escalation ambiguity.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B30-R01","D54-B30-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B30-R01"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B30-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B30-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Ownership, Responsibility and Authority Boundaries","subtopic":"Cross-boundary accountability","concept":"Defining accountability for cross-component workflows","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should accountability be assigned when a workflow crosses multiple components?","answer":"Assign ownership of the end-to-end outcome separately from ownership of individual component responsibilities, with explicit handoff, failure, and escalation semantics.","context":"A business workflow spans several components owned by different teams.","objective":"Ensure that end-to-end failures have an accountable owner even when implementation is distributed.","requirements":["End-to-end outcome owner must be identifiable","Component responsibilities must be explicit","Handoffs and failure escalation must be defined"],"constraints":["No single component may control the whole workflow","Failures may occur between components"],"alternatives":["Each component owns only its local behavior","One end-to-end owner","Shared workflow ownership with explicit coordinator"],"evidence":["Workflow dependency graph","Failure modes","Operational escalation patterns"],"tradeoffs":["Local-only ownership can leave cross-boundary failures unmanaged","End-to-end ownership improves accountability but requires coordination","A coordinator can simplify workflow control but adds responsibility"],"decision":"Assign explicit end-to-end accountability for critical cross-component outcomes while retaining component-level ownership for local behavior.","rationale":"Local correctness does not guarantee end-to-end correctness.","consequences":["Cross-boundary incidents have a defined escalation path","Workflow ownership becomes an architectural concern"],"implementation_implications":["Define workflow owner","Define handoff contracts","Track end-to-end correlation identifiers","Document escalation rules"],"verification_method":"Trace representative workflow failures and verify that responsibility and escalation paths identify an accountable outcome owner.","outcome_learning":"Measure unresolved cross-component incidents and handoff failures.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B30-R01","D54-B30-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B30-R01"},{"type":"uses","record_id":"D54-B30-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B30-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Ownership, Responsibility and Authority Boundaries","subtopic":"Boundary evolution","concept":"Updating ownership boundaries as architecture evolves","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should ownership boundaries be revisited?","answer":"Ownership boundaries should be revisited when responsibilities, dependency structures, operational burden, failure patterns, team capabilities, or architectural objectives materially change.","context":"The architecture has evolved and existing ownership assignments no longer clearly match responsibilities.","objective":"Keep ownership aligned with actual system structure and authority.","requirements":["Ownership changes must be traceable","New authority must be explicit","Transition responsibilities must be defined"],"constraints":["Ownership transitions can temporarily create dual responsibility","Frequent changes can create instability"],"alternatives":["Keep historical ownership","Transfer ownership","Split or consolidate responsibility"],"evidence":["Dependency changes","Incident patterns","Change ownership","Operational workload"],"tradeoffs":["Stable ownership reduces churn","Realignment can improve accountability and reduce coordination cost"],"decision":"Reassess ownership when architectural or operational evidence shows that responsibility and authority no longer align, while avoiding changes based only on organizational preference.","rationale":"Ownership is an architectural control that should follow responsibility and authority boundaries.","consequences":["Architecture records may require updates","Transition periods require explicit accountability"],"implementation_implications":["Update ownership registry","Transfer operational knowledge","Review access and approval permissions","Retire obsolete authority"],"verification_method":"Audit responsibilities, permissions, escalation paths, and dependency ownership after each material boundary change.","outcome_learning":"Measure coordination effort, incident resolution clarity, and ownership conflicts after realignment.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B30-R01","D54-B30-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B30-R01"},{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B30-R04"}]}

