{"record_id":"D54-B31-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Data Ownership","subtopic":"Read models","concept":"Separating authoritative data from derived representations","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should read-optimized representations be treated when another component owns authoritative data?","answer":"Treat read-optimized representations as derived state with explicit source authority, freshness expectations, rebuild mechanisms, and synchronization semantics.","context":"Consumers need query-specific representations that differ from the authoritative data model.","objective":"Improve read performance without creating accidental competing authority.","requirements":["Source authority must be identified","Freshness requirements must be explicit","Derived state must be rebuildable or recoverable"],"constraints":["Derived state can become stale","Rebuilding may require historical or source data"],"alternatives":["Direct authoritative reads","Derived read model","Independent writable copy"],"evidence":["Query patterns","Latency requirements","Freshness tolerance","Rebuild capability"],"tradeoffs":["Derived models improve query efficiency but introduce synchronization complexity","Direct reads preserve freshness but may impose workload on the authority","Independent writable copies create conflict-management requirements"],"decision":"Use derived read models when query requirements justify them and their non-authoritative status and rebuild semantics are explicit.","rationale":"Performance-oriented representations should not silently become competing sources of truth.","consequences":["Read freshness becomes a measurable property","Rebuild procedures become operational requirements"],"implementation_implications":["Record source version or event position where applicable","Define refresh/replay mechanism","Test cold rebuild"],"verification_method":"Delete or invalidate the derived representation and verify that it can be reconstructed to the required correctness and freshness level.","outcome_learning":"Monitor stale reads, synchronization lag, and rebuild failures.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B31-R01","D54-B37-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B31-R01"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B37-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B31-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Data Ownership","subtopic":"Cross-domain data","concept":"Avoiding duplicated mutable authority across domains","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"What should happen when multiple domains need to modify related information?","answer":"Separate authoritative ownership by domain when invariants permit it; otherwise evaluate whether the data represents one cohesive consistency boundary that should remain under one authority.","context":"Two domains both require mutation of closely related information.","objective":"Determine whether data should be split or retained under one authoritative boundary.","requirements":["Cross-domain invariants must be identified","Mutation dependencies must be explicit","Consistency requirements must be measurable"],"constraints":["Splitting can introduce distributed coordination","Keeping data together can increase coupling"],"alternatives":["Single authority","Partitioned authority","Duplicated writable data"],"evidence":["Invariant scope","Transaction requirements","Change ownership","Access patterns"],"tradeoffs":["Single authority simplifies consistency but may broaden ownership","Partitioning improves autonomy but requires coordination","Writable duplication requires conflict resolution"],"decision":"Keep data under one authority when critical invariants require atomic shared control; partition only when independent ownership can preserve those invariants.","rationale":"Data boundaries should follow correctness boundaries rather than convenience alone.","consequences":["Some domains may remain coupled intentionally","Partitioned data requires explicit cross-domain contracts"],"implementation_implications":["Document invariant boundaries","Define transaction or workflow coordination","Prohibit undocumented cross-owner mutation"],"verification_method":"Test critical invariants under concurrent cross-domain operations and verify preservation after failures.","outcome_learning":"Monitor reconciliation frequency and cross-domain consistency incidents.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B31-R01","D54-B22-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B31-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B22-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B31-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Data Ownership","subtopic":"Ownership transfer","concept":"Governed transfer of data authority","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should data ownership be transferred between architectural components?","answer":"Transfer authority through an explicit migration that defines the old and new owner, state reconciliation, write cutover, rollback conditions, and verification evidence.","context":"A mature system requires data ownership to move from one component to another.","objective":"Change authority without creating concurrent conflicting writers or silent data loss.","requirements":["New authority must be established before final cutover","Outstanding writes must be reconciled","Rollback conditions must be defined"],"constraints":["Live traffic may continue during migration","Historical clients may use the old interface"],"alternatives":["Immediate ownership switch","Dual-write transition","Staged cutover"],"evidence":["Write volume","Consumer inventory","Consistency requirements","Migration capabilities"],"tradeoffs":["Immediate cutover is simpler but riskier","Dual-write can reduce migration interruption but creates reconciliation complexity","Staged cutover provides observability but extends transition"],"decision":"Use an explicit staged ownership transfer when live traffic or migration risk makes immediate cutover unsafe.","rationale":"Ownership changes alter the system's source of truth and require stronger controls than ordinary refactoring.","consequences":["Temporary migration logic may exist","Ownership state must be observable"],"implementation_implications":["Define cutover marker","Reconcile old and new state","Disable old writes only after verification","Retain rollback evidence"],"verification_method":"Compare authoritative state before and after cutover and verify no conflicting writers remain.","outcome_learning":"Measure reconciliation discrepancies, cutover failures, and rollback frequency.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B31-R01","D54-B40-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B31-R01"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B40-R01"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B32-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Database Selection","subtopic":"Workload characterization","concept":"Selecting database technology from workload characteristics","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What evidence should drive database selection?","answer":"Database selection should begin with workload characteristics, consistency requirements, query patterns, data model, scale, durability, operational constraints, and required capabilities rather than technology popularity.","context":"A new system requires persistent storage and several database technologies are candidates.","objective":"Select storage technology against measurable system requirements.","requirements":["Read and write patterns must be characterized","Consistency and durability needs must be explicit","Capacity and performance requirements must be measurable","Operational constraints must be known"],"constraints":["Technology capabilities differ by version and configuration","Benchmarks from unrelated workloads may not transfer"],"alternatives":["Relational database","Document or key-value database","Other specialized storage"],"evidence":["Representative workload","Query patterns","Transaction requirements","Data relationships","Operational capabilities"],"tradeoffs":["General-purpose systems may provide broad capabilities","Specialized systems may optimize particular workloads while adding technology-specific constraints"],"decision":"Select database technology only after mapping documented workload and quality requirements to verified capabilities and operational constraints.","rationale":"Storage technology is an architecture decision whose suitability depends on workload semantics.","consequences":["Database evaluation becomes requirement-driven","Unverified benchmark claims cannot serve as decision evidence"],"implementation_implications":["Create representative workload tests","Validate required features on the target version","Document configuration assumptions"],"verification_method":"Run representative tests against the actual candidate version and configuration where measurable performance or behavior is part of the decision.","outcome_learning":"Compare production or controlled workload evidence against initial assumptions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B12-R01","D54-B32-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B12-R01"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B32-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B32-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Database Selection","subtopic":"Relational workload fit","concept":"Selecting relational storage for integrity and relational access patterns","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should relational database capabilities be treated as primary selection criteria?","answer":"Relational capabilities become strong selection criteria when the system requires structured relationships, transactional integrity across related records, mature constraint enforcement, or complex relational querying.","context":"A workload contains interconnected entities and invariants that span multiple records.","objective":"Determine whether relational database semantics match the dominant workload.","requirements":["Relationship semantics must be represented reliably","Integrity constraints must be enforceable","Required transactions must be supported"],"constraints":["Highly variable schema or specialized access patterns may require additional approaches","Scale characteristics must still be evaluated"],"alternatives":["Relational database","Non-relational database","Polyglot persistence"],"evidence":["Relationship density","Constraint requirements","Join/query patterns","Transaction scope"],"tradeoffs":["Relational systems provide strong integrity and query semantics but may impose schema and scaling considerations","Non-relational systems may simplify certain access patterns but require application-level handling of some invariants"],"decision":"Prioritize relational storage when relational integrity, transactions, and query semantics are central requirements, subject to measured workload constraints.","rationale":"Database selection should preserve required correctness semantics before optimizing secondary characteristics.","consequences":["Schema governance becomes important","Application design can leverage database constraints"],"implementation_implications":["Define keys and constraints","Model transaction boundaries","Test query plans and representative workloads"],"verification_method":"Verify required constraints, transaction behavior, and representative query performance on the target database version.","outcome_learning":"Monitor constraint violations, query behavior, and operational load.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B32-R01","D54-B34-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B32-R01"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B34-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B32-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Database Selection","subtopic":"Specialized storage","concept":"Using specialized storage only for demonstrated workload needs","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When should specialized database technology be introduced?","answer":"Specialized storage should be introduced when a measurable workload or data-model requirement cannot be adequately satisfied by the existing storage approach and the operational cost is justified.","context":"A workload contains specialized access or processing requirements.","objective":"Determine whether specialized persistence provides sufficient architectural value.","requirements":["Specialized requirement must be concrete","Alternative implementations must be evaluated","Operational ownership must be feasible"],"constraints":["Additional databases increase operational surface","Data synchronization may become necessary"],"alternatives":["Existing database","Specialized database","Application-level optimization"],"evidence":["Measured bottleneck","Required data model","Query characteristics","Operational cost"],"tradeoffs":["Specialized storage may improve a specific workload","Multiple storage technologies increase operational and consistency complexity"],"decision":"Introduce specialized storage only when a documented requirement remains materially unsatisfied after evaluating simpler alternatives.","rationale":"Polyglot persistence should solve a demonstrated problem rather than create technology fragmentation.","consequences":["Additional data lifecycle and operational responsibilities","Potential synchronization boundaries"],"implementation_implications":["Define source authority","Define synchronization if required","Add technology-specific operational tests"],"verification_method":"Demonstrate the limiting requirement using representative workload evidence and validate the proposed specialized capability.","outcome_learning":"Measure the actual benefit against added operational complexity.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B32-R01","D54-B31-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"constrains","record_id":"D54-B32-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B31-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B32-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Database Selection","subtopic":"Operational capability","concept":"Including operational maturity in database selection","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should operational capability be part of database selection?","answer":"A database must be evaluated not only for data semantics but also for backup, recovery, monitoring, upgrade, security, migration, capacity, and failure-management capabilities required by the system.","context":"Two storage technologies appear functionally capable but differ in operational requirements.","objective":"Ensure the selected storage can be safely operated throughout its lifecycle.","requirements":["Backup and restore must be supported","Security administration must be feasible","Monitoring and failure handling must be defined","Upgrade and migration paths must exist"],"constraints":["Team expertise and tooling vary","Operational requirements may dominate long-term cost"],"alternatives":["Technology with stronger operational fit","Technology with specialized functional fit","Managed or self-operated deployment depending on constraints"],"evidence":["Recovery procedures","Operational tooling","Security model","Upgrade process","Team capabilities"],"tradeoffs":["Functional specialization can increase operational burden","Operational simplicity can constrain specialized capabilities"],"decision":"Treat required operational capabilities as hard selection criteria where system continuity or governance depends on them.","rationale":"A database that cannot be reliably operated does not satisfy the complete architecture requirement.","consequences":["Operational readiness becomes part of technology evaluation","Migration and recovery are considered before adoption"],"implementation_implications":["Document backup/restore","Define monitoring","Test upgrades and recovery","Define ownership"],"verification_method":"Perform operational readiness tests for backup, restore, monitoring, upgrade, and failure recovery.","outcome_learning":"Track operational incidents and recovery performance against assumptions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B20-R05","D54-B32-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B20-R05"},{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B32-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B32-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Database Selection","subtopic":"Version and compatibility","concept":"Making database version assumptions explicit","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should database version affect an architecture decision?","answer":"Database architecture decisions must identify the target version and verify that required features, behavior, compatibility, and operational procedures apply to that version rather than relying on unspecified product-level assumptions.","context":"A proposed database feature is required by the architecture.","objective":"Prevent version mismatch from invalidating the decision.","requirements":["Target version must be recorded","Required features must be version-appropriate","Upgrade and compatibility implications must be known"],"constraints":["Feature behavior can change between versions","Deployment environments may constrain versions"],"alternatives":["Use current supported version","Use older constrained version","Avoid version-sensitive feature"],"evidence":["Official version documentation","Compatibility testing","Deployment constraints"],"tradeoffs":["Newer versions may provide needed capabilities but require upgrade validation","Older versions may provide operational stability but lack required features"],"decision":"Record the target database version as part of the architecture decision and verify version-specific assumptions before implementation.","rationale":"Version is part of the technical context, not incidental metadata.","consequences":["Database upgrades can trigger architecture reassessment","Unsupported version assumptions become detectable"],"implementation_implications":["Pin supported version range","Add compatibility tests","Document upgrade constraints"],"verification_method":"Validate required database behavior against the declared target version and configuration.","outcome_learning":"Review architecture records when major database versions or compatibility requirements change.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B32-R01","D54-B40-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B32-R01"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B40-R05"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B33-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Relational vs Non-Relational Storage","subtopic":"Data structure","concept":"Matching storage model to data relationships","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should data relationships influence relational versus non-relational storage selection?","answer":"Select a storage model according to the dominant relationship, access, consistency, and evolution characteristics of the data rather than treating relational or non-relational storage as universally preferable.","context":"A system contains structured entities with varying relationship and access requirements.","objective":"Match persistence semantics to actual data behavior.","requirements":["Relationship requirements must be explicit","Primary access paths must be known","Consistency requirements must be defined"],"constraints":["Data models can evolve","One system may legitimately contain multiple storage models"],"alternatives":["Relational model","Document-oriented model","Key-value or specialized model"],"evidence":["Relationship graph","Access patterns","Update patterns","Consistency requirements"],"tradeoffs":["Relational models support explicit relationships and constraints","Document models can localize aggregate-shaped data","Key-value models can optimize direct lookup patterns"],"decision":"Select the model that naturally expresses the dominant data relationships and access patterns while preserving required correctness guarantees.","rationale":"Storage structure should reduce impedance between required data semantics and persistence behavior.","consequences":["Data modeling becomes a first-class architecture activity","Cross-model integration may require explicit contracts"],"implementation_implications":["Define aggregate boundaries where applicable","Identify joins or lookup patterns","Define consistency behavior across storage models"],"verification_method":"Evaluate representative queries, mutations, constraints, and failure cases against the selected model.","outcome_learning":"Monitor query complexity, data duplication, and consistency issues.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B32-R01","D54-B33-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B32-R01"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B33-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B33-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Relational vs Non-Relational Storage","subtopic":"Aggregate-oriented data","concept":"Using aggregate boundaries to evaluate document storage","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When can document-oriented storage align with application boundaries?","answer":"Document-oriented storage can fit when application operations predominantly read and modify cohesive aggregates whose lifecycle can be managed without frequent cross-aggregate transactional joins.","context":"An application mostly processes self-contained business aggregates.","objective":"Determine whether document storage naturally represents the dominant access pattern.","requirements":["Aggregate boundaries must be stable enough","Cross-document consistency requirements must be understood","Document size and access patterns must be bounded"],"constraints":["Large or highly shared documents can create contention","Cross-document transactions may be more complex depending on technology"],"alternatives":["Relational normalized model","Document-oriented model","Hybrid projection model"],"evidence":["Aggregate access patterns","Mutation locality","Cross-aggregate operations","Document growth"],"tradeoffs":["Documents can localize aggregate reads and writes","Normalization can reduce duplication but increase joins","Document duplication can increase update complexity"],"decision":"Consider document storage when aggregate-local operations dominate and cross-aggregate consistency does not require broad transactional coupling.","rationale":"Storage should follow actual access and mutation boundaries.","consequences":["Aggregate design becomes tightly coupled to persistence","Cross-aggregate reporting may need separate read models"],"implementation_implications":["Define document ownership","Bound document growth","Design indexes for actual access paths"],"verification_method":"Test representative aggregate operations, growth behavior, consistency scenarios, and query performance.","outcome_learning":"Monitor document growth, update contention, duplication, and query complexity.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B33-R01","D54-B34-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B33-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B34-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B33-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Relational vs Non-Relational Storage","subtopic":"Schema flexibility","concept":"Distinguishing schema flexibility from absence of data contracts","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"Does a flexible storage schema eliminate the need for data contracts?","answer":"No. Flexible storage can reduce database-enforced structural constraints, but application-level validation, versioning, compatibility, and semantic contracts remain necessary.","context":"A team proposes non-relational storage because incoming records vary in structure.","objective":"Prevent schema flexibility from becoming uncontrolled data inconsistency.","requirements":["Required fields and semantic rules must be defined","Compatibility must be managed","Invalid data must be detectable"],"constraints":["Historical records may have multiple shapes","Strict schema enforcement may not be available at the storage layer"],"alternatives":["Strict database schema","Flexible storage with application validation","Flexible storage with versioned documents"],"evidence":["Input variability","Consumer requirements","Data quality rules","Evolution patterns"],"tradeoffs":["Strict schemas catch structural errors early","Flexible schemas support variation but shift governance into application or validation layers"],"decision":"Use schema flexibility only where variation is a real requirement and compensate with explicit versioned validation and data-quality contracts.","rationale":"Storage-level flexibility does not remove semantic correctness requirements.","consequences":["Validation becomes an explicit architecture responsibility","Historical compatibility must be considered"],"implementation_implications":["Version document schemas","Validate required invariants","Track malformed or unknown fields"],"verification_method":"Test valid, legacy, malformed, and future-version documents against declared compatibility rules.","outcome_learning":"Monitor schema drift, invalid records, and consumer compatibility failures.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B33-R01","D54-B40-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B33-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B40-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B33-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Relational vs Non-Relational Storage","subtopic":"Polyglot persistence","concept":"Managing multiple storage models","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"What conditions justify using both relational and non-relational storage in one system?","answer":"Multiple storage models are justified when materially different workloads require different persistence semantics and the additional synchronization, operational, and governance complexity is understood and accepted.","context":"A system has transactional business data and a separate workload with substantially different access characteristics.","objective":"Determine whether polyglot persistence creates enough value to justify additional complexity.","requirements":["Each store must have a clear purpose","Data authority must be defined","Synchronization and failure behavior must be explicit"],"constraints":["Multiple stores increase operational surface","Cross-store consistency is more difficult"],"alternatives":["Single general-purpose database","Multiple specialized stores","Single authority with derived specialized representation"],"evidence":["Workload measurements","Data access patterns","Consistency requirements","Operational capabilities"],"tradeoffs":["Multiple stores can fit workloads better","Single store simplifies operations","Derived specialized stores add synchronization complexity"],"decision":"Use multiple storage models only when workload-specific requirements materially justify their added lifecycle and consistency complexity.","rationale":"Technology diversity should correspond to distinct requirements rather than preference.","consequences":["Data architecture becomes more complex","Cross-store observability and recovery are required"],"implementation_implications":["Assign authoritative source","Define replication or projection path","Test partial-store failure"],"verification_method":"Validate each specialized workload and test failure or synchronization scenarios between stores.","outcome_learning":"Measure actual workload benefit against operational and consistency cost.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B32-R03","D54-B31-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B32-R03"},{"type":"uses","record_id":"D54-B31-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B33-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Relational vs Non-Relational Storage","subtopic":"Access-pattern coupling","concept":"Preventing storage model decisions from following transient query preferences","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should transient query requirements affect storage-model selection?","answer":"Query requirements should influence storage design when they represent durable workload characteristics; temporary or speculative queries should not automatically determine the authoritative data model.","context":"A proposed storage model is motivated by one anticipated query pattern.","objective":"Distinguish durable workload requirements from speculative optimization.","requirements":["Query workload must be representative","Optimization benefit must be measurable","Authoritative data semantics must remain valid"],"constraints":["Future queries are uncertain","Premature denormalization or duplication can increase maintenance cost"],"alternatives":["Redesign authoritative model","Add derived read model","Optimize query implementation"],"evidence":["Observed workload","Query frequency","Latency requirements","Data ownership"],"tradeoffs":["Changing authority can simplify a query but create broader coupling","Derived models can optimize reads without changing authority","Query optimization may provide sufficient improvement at lower architectural cost"],"decision":"Prefer query-specific projections or targeted optimization when a query pattern does not justify changing authoritative storage semantics.","rationale":"Authoritative data architecture should not be destabilized by speculative access requirements.","consequences":["Read models may be introduced","Storage architecture remains aligned with durable domain semantics"],"implementation_implications":["Measure query bottleneck","Define projection lifecycle","Avoid duplicating authority"],"verification_method":"Benchmark representative query workload and compare projection or optimization approaches before changing the authoritative model.","outcome_learning":"Track query performance and projection maintenance cost.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B33-R04","D54-B31-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B33-R04"},{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B31-R03"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B34-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Transaction and Consistency","subtopic":"Transaction boundary","concept":"Defining transaction scope around invariants","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should transaction boundaries be selected?","answer":"Transaction boundaries should encompass the smallest set of state changes that must become atomically visible to preserve a required invariant.","context":"A workflow changes several related pieces of persistent state.","objective":"Define transaction scope from correctness requirements rather than implementation convenience.","requirements":["Critical invariants must be identified","Atomicity requirements must be explicit","Failure behavior must be defined"],"constraints":["Larger transactions can reduce concurrency","Distributed transaction boundaries increase complexity"],"alternatives":["Broad transaction","Narrow invariant-based transaction","Application-level workflow coordination"],"evidence":["Business invariants","Write dependencies","Concurrency requirements","Failure scenarios"],"tradeoffs":["Broad transactions simplify atomicity but may increase locking or contention","Narrow transactions improve concurrency but may require workflow coordination"],"decision":"Use the narrowest transaction that fully protects the required invariant and move non-atomic work outside it when correctness permits.","rationale":"Transaction scope is a correctness boundary and a concurrency constraint.","consequences":["Some workflows require explicit intermediate states","Long-running operations should not unnecessarily hold database transactions"],"implementation_implications":["Define invariant checks","Minimize transaction duration","Use workflow state for non-atomic continuation"],"verification_method":"Inject failures at transaction boundaries and verify that protected invariants remain valid.","outcome_learning":"Monitor contention, rollback rates, and invariant violations.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B22-R03","D54-B34-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B22-R03"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B34-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B34-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Transaction and Consistency","subtopic":"Isolation","concept":"Selecting transaction isolation from anomaly requirements","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should transaction isolation be selected?","answer":"Choose isolation behavior based on the anomalies that must be prevented, the workload's concurrency characteristics, and the performance cost of stronger isolation.","context":"Concurrent transactions can observe or modify overlapping data.","objective":"Prevent correctness-critical anomalies without imposing unnecessary contention.","requirements":["Forbidden anomalies must be identified","Concurrency requirements must be measurable","Isolation behavior must be understood for the target database"],"constraints":["Isolation semantics differ by database technology and configuration","Stronger guarantees can reduce concurrency"],"alternatives":["Lower isolation","Stronger isolation","Explicit locking or conflict detection"],"evidence":["Invariant requirements","Concurrency workload","Database semantics","Observed anomalies"],"tradeoffs":["Stronger isolation simplifies some reasoning but can increase contention","Lower isolation improves concurrency but requires application tolerance or explicit controls"],"decision":"Select isolation semantics based on required anomaly prevention rather than selecting the strongest available level by default.","rationale":"Isolation is a correctness-performance tradeoff that must be workload-specific.","consequences":["Database-specific behavior must be documented","Concurrency tests become part of architecture validation"],"implementation_implications":["Document isolation configuration","Test concurrent scenarios","Define retry or conflict handling where applicable"],"verification_method":"Execute controlled concurrent transaction tests and verify expected anomaly behavior on the target database/version.","outcome_learning":"Monitor deadlocks, serialization failures, contention, and correctness incidents.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B34-R01","D54-B35-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B34-R01"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B35-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B34-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Transaction and Consistency","subtopic":"Idempotency","concept":"Using idempotency to control repeated transactional requests","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should idempotency be part of transaction architecture?","answer":"Idempotency should be designed into operations that can be retried or delivered more than once when repeating the operation could otherwise produce an incorrect or duplicated business outcome.","context":"A client or message system may retry requests after uncertain completion.","objective":"Prevent repeated delivery from producing multiple unintended business effects.","requirements":["Operation identity must be defined","Duplicate detection or equivalent semantics must be available","Outcome persistence must match retry behavior"],"constraints":["Not every operation is naturally idempotent","Idempotency records require lifecycle management"],"alternatives":["Natural idempotent operation","Idempotency key/state","Reject retries"],"evidence":["Retry behavior","External delivery semantics","Business side effects"],"tradeoffs":["Idempotency improves safe retry behavior but adds state","Rejecting retries simplifies state but may reduce reliability"],"decision":"Use explicit idempotency mechanisms when retries can repeat non-idempotent business effects and correctness requires one logical outcome.","rationale":"Execution count and business outcome count are not necessarily equivalent.","consequences":["Duplicate requests become manageable","Idempotency state requires retention and cleanup rules"],"implementation_implications":["Define idempotency key","Persist outcome where required","Test duplicate and concurrent requests"],"verification_method":"Submit identical requests concurrently and sequentially and verify the declared business outcome semantics.","outcome_learning":"Monitor duplicate attempts, conflicts, and idempotency-state growth.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B27-R03","D54-B34-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B27-R03"},{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B34-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B34-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Transaction and Consistency","subtopic":"Long-running workflows","concept":"Separating long-running workflows from database transaction lifetime","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"Why should long-running business workflows generally avoid holding one database transaction for their full duration?","answer":"Long-running transactions can increase locks, resource consumption, contention, recovery complexity, and dependency coupling; explicit workflow state is often required when completion spans multiple steps or external interactions.","context":"A business operation may take substantially longer than a normal database transaction.","objective":"Preserve correctness without holding transactional resources across long-running work.","requirements":["Intermediate states must be explicit","Recovery and compensation behavior must be defined","Final invariants must remain protected"],"constraints":["Some operations genuinely require atomic database scope","External side effects cannot necessarily participate in local transactions"],"alternatives":["One long transaction","Short transactions with workflow state","Distributed transaction"],"evidence":["Operation duration","Resource contention","External dependencies","Failure semantics"],"tradeoffs":["Long transaction simplifies local atomicity but increases resource coupling","Short transactions improve resource utilization but require workflow coordination","Distributed transactions increase coordination complexity"],"decision":"Use short transactional boundaries plus explicit workflow state when the operation crosses long delays or external dependencies and full atomicity is not required.","rationale":"Transaction lifetime should reflect database correctness requirements, not the total duration of a business process.","consequences":["Intermediate states become observable","Compensation or retry behavior may be required"],"implementation_implications":["Persist workflow state","Define state transitions","Protect each local invariant transactionally"],"verification_method":"Inject failure between workflow steps and verify recovery and invariant behavior.","outcome_learning":"Monitor stuck workflows, compensation frequency, and partial-completion states.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B27-R04","D54-B34-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B27-R04"},{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B34-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B34-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Transaction and Consistency","subtopic":"Constraint enforcement","concept":"Choosing database versus application enforcement of invariants","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Where should critical data invariants be enforced?","answer":"Enforce invariants at the strongest appropriate boundary, using database constraints when they represent storage-level integrity and application logic when the invariant depends on broader business context.","context":"A business invariant could potentially be enforced by application code, database constraints, or both.","objective":"Prevent invalid persistent state without creating unnecessary coupling.","requirements":["Critical invariants must be explicitly classified","Enforcement location must be authoritative","Concurrent writes must be considered"],"constraints":["Database constraints cannot express every business rule","Application-only checks may race under concurrent writers"],"alternatives":["Application enforcement","Database enforcement","Defense-in-depth enforcement"],"evidence":["Invariant semantics","Concurrency model","Database capability"],"tradeoffs":["Database enforcement protects against multiple writers but can couple schema to rules","Application enforcement is expressive but can be bypassed by other writers","Both can provide defense-in-depth when semantics are aligned"],"decision":"Use database constraints for invariants naturally expressible at the persistence boundary and application logic for rules requiring broader domain context; avoid duplicated rules that can diverge silently.","rationale":"The enforcement location should match the scope of the invariant.","consequences":["Database schema may encode important correctness rules","Application services remain responsible for domain-level validation"],"implementation_implications":["Document invariant owner","Test both database and application paths","Prevent bypass through unmanaged write paths"],"verification_method":"Attempt invalid state creation through all supported write paths and verify rejection or controlled handling.","outcome_learning":"Track integrity violations and discrepancies between application and persistence validation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B31-R01","D54-B34-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B31-R01"},{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B34-R01"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B35-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Strong vs Eventual Consistency","subtopic":"Consistency requirement classification","concept":"Classifying data by required consistency strength","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an architecture decide which data requires strong consistency?","answer":"Classify consistency requirements from business invariants, user-visible correctness, conflict tolerance, and allowed staleness rather than applying one consistency model to all data.","context":"Different data domains have different tolerance for stale or conflicting values.","objective":"Apply consistency strength proportionally to actual correctness requirements.","requirements":["Critical invariants must be identified","Allowed staleness must be measurable","Conflict tolerance must be explicit"],"constraints":["Stronger consistency can reduce availability or scalability depending on architecture","Different stores may expose different semantics"],"alternatives":["Strong consistency everywhere","Eventual consistency where tolerated","Mixed consistency by data domain"],"evidence":["Business invariants","User expectations","Workflow semantics","Staleness tolerance"],"tradeoffs":["Strong consistency simplifies some correctness reasoning","Eventual consistency can improve decoupling and scale but requires stale/conflict handling","Mixed models increase architecture complexity"],"decision":"Classify consistency per data or operation boundary and apply the minimum strength that preserves required correctness.","rationale":"Consistency is a requirement, not a universal architectural preference.","consequences":["Different data paths may have different semantics","Consumers must understand freshness and conflict behavior"],"implementation_implications":["Document consistency contract per API/data domain","Expose version or freshness metadata where required","Test stale reads"],"verification_method":"Inject delayed propagation and concurrent updates and verify behavior against the declared consistency contract.","outcome_learning":"Monitor stale-read incidents, conflicts, and user-visible consistency failures.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B34-R02","D54-B35-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B34-R02"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B35-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B35-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Strong vs Eventual Consistency","subtopic":"Staleness budget","concept":"Using explicit staleness budgets","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should acceptable data staleness be represented in an architecture decision?","answer":"Represent staleness as an explicit bounded requirement with measurable duration, affected data, consumer behavior, and failure handling rather than describing data as simply 'eventually consistent'.","context":"A derived or replicated view may temporarily lag authoritative state.","objective":"Convert vague eventual-consistency language into a verifiable contract.","requirements":["Maximum acceptable staleness must be defined","Affected consumers must be identified","Behavior beyond the staleness limit must be specified"],"constraints":["Propagation latency can vary","Some failures may prevent convergence"],"alternatives":["Unbounded eventual consistency","Bounded staleness","Strong consistency"],"evidence":["Consumer requirements","Propagation measurements","Business tolerance"],"tradeoffs":["Bounded staleness provides clearer guarantees but may require monitoring and fallback","Unbounded propagation is simpler but weakens predictability","Strong consistency increases coordination requirements"],"decision":"Use an explicit staleness budget whenever eventual consistency is acceptable but consumers still require predictable freshness.","rationale":"A measurable freshness contract makes asynchronous consistency testable.","consequences":["Freshness monitoring becomes required","Exceeding the budget becomes an operational condition"],"implementation_implications":["Record event/version timestamp","Measure propagation delay","Define stale-data fallback"],"verification_method":"Delay or interrupt propagation and verify detection and consumer behavior when the declared staleness budget is exceeded.","outcome_learning":"Track percentile propagation lag and budget violations.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B35-R01","D54-B31-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B35-R01"},{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B31-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B35-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Strong vs Eventual Consistency","subtopic":"User-facing consistency","concept":"Aligning consistency semantics with user-visible workflows","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should user-facing workflows influence consistency selection?","answer":"Consistency should reflect what users are required to observe after an operation, including whether immediate read-after-write visibility is necessary or delayed visibility is acceptable.","context":"A user performs a mutation and may immediately read the affected information through another path.","objective":"Prevent confusing or incorrect user-visible behavior caused by unspecified consistency.","requirements":["Read-after-write expectations must be explicit","User-visible stale states must be acceptable or prevented","Failure behavior must be defined"],"constraints":["Different read paths may use different replicas or projections","Strong read-after-write behavior can require coordination"],"alternatives":["Strong read-after-write","Session-aware reads","Eventual reads with explicit pending state"],"evidence":["Workflow semantics","User interaction requirements","Read routing","Propagation latency"],"tradeoffs":["Strong visibility simplifies user expectations but can increase coordination","Session-aware approaches preserve locality with additional routing semantics","Explicit pending states can preserve eventual architecture but change UX"],"decision":"Use the consistency model that directly satisfies the workflow's documented user-visible guarantees; expose pending or freshness state when delayed convergence is acceptable.","rationale":"Consistency is observable through workflow behavior, not only database internals.","consequences":["API and UI contracts may include freshness or processing states","Read paths require explicit consistency semantics"],"implementation_implications":["Define read-after-write policy","Propagate operation/version identifiers","Test immediate post-write reads"],"verification_method":"Perform mutation followed immediately by relevant reads through all supported access paths and verify declared semantics.","outcome_learning":"Monitor stale user-visible reads and support incidents related to propagation delay.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B35-R02","D54-B27-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B35-R02"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B27-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B35-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Strong vs Eventual Consistency","subtopic":"Conflict resolution","concept":"Choosing conflict resolution before accepting concurrent divergent writes","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What must be decided before using eventual consistency for concurrently writable data?","answer":"The architecture must define how conflicting updates are detected, represented, resolved, rejected, or reconciled before allowing concurrent writes to diverge.","context":"Multiple nodes or components can update related data without immediate coordination.","objective":"Prevent eventual convergence from becoming undefined conflict behavior.","requirements":["Conflict detection must be defined","Resolution authority must be explicit","Unresolvable conflicts must have an operational path"],"constraints":["Automatic merge may be unsafe for some business data","Human or centralized resolution may add latency"],"alternatives":["Last-write-wins","Version-based rejection","Domain-specific merge","Manual reconciliation"],"evidence":["Data semantics","Conflict frequency","Business impact","Versioning capabilities"],"tradeoffs":["Automatic policies reduce intervention but can discard valid changes","Explicit conflict rejection preserves correctness but requires retry/reconciliation","Domain-specific merge can preserve information but is more complex"],"decision":"Do not adopt concurrent eventual writes without a defined conflict model appropriate to the data semantics.","rationale":"Convergence alone does not guarantee correctness when concurrent values conflict.","consequences":["Conflict state becomes observable","Reconciliation logic may be required"],"implementation_implications":["Use versions or revision identifiers","Record conflicting values when required","Define resolution workflow"],"verification_method":"Generate concurrent conflicting updates and verify deterministic detection and resolution behavior.","outcome_learning":"Measure conflict frequency, resolution outcomes, and data-loss incidents.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B35-R01","D54-B28-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B35-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B28-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B35-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Strong vs Eventual Consistency","subtopic":"Consistency transition","concept":"Migrating consistency guarantees without silent semantic change","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an architecture handle a change from strong to eventual consistency?","answer":"Treat the change as a contract change requiring consumer analysis, explicit stale-state behavior, conflict handling, and validation of business invariants rather than as a storage-only optimization.","context":"A system proposes weakening consistency to improve scale or availability.","objective":"Determine whether required behavior remains correct under weaker consistency.","requirements":["Affected workflows must be inventoried","Staleness and conflict semantics must be explicit","Critical invariants must remain protected"],"constraints":["Existing consumers may implicitly rely on immediate visibility","Operational monitoring must detect convergence problems"],"alternatives":["Retain strong consistency","Introduce bounded eventual consistency","Use mixed consistency"],"evidence":["Consumer behavior","Workflow invariants","Measured coordination cost","Staleness tolerance"],"tradeoffs":["Weaker consistency may improve scalability but changes observable semantics","Strong consistency preserves existing guarantees but may constrain architecture"],"decision":"Approve weaker consistency only after identifying affected consumers and demonstrating that documented invariants and freshness requirements remain satisfied.","rationale":"Consistency strength is part of the externally observable architecture contract.","consequences":["API contracts may need versioning","Consumers may require asynchronous states or freshness indicators"],"implementation_implications":["Version consistency semantics","Add stale-data tests","Monitor convergence"],"verification_method":"Run representative workflows under delayed and conflicting propagation and verify documented invariants.","outcome_learning":"Compare production freshness and conflict metrics with the new contract.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B35-R01","D54-B40-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B35-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B40-R03"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B36-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Data Replication","subtopic":"Replication purpose","concept":"Choosing replication for availability, locality, or scale","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should justify data replication?","answer":"Replication should have an explicit purpose such as availability, geographic locality, read scalability, disaster recovery, or workload isolation, with its consistency and recovery consequences documented.","context":"A system proposes multiple copies of persistent data.","objective":"Ensure replication solves a defined architectural requirement.","requirements":["Replication purpose must be explicit","Source authority must be defined","Lag and failure semantics must be known"],"constraints":["Replication consumes storage and network resources","Copies may diverge temporarily"],"alternatives":["No replication","Synchronous replication","Asynchronous replication"],"evidence":["Availability requirements","Read workload","Geographic distribution","Recovery objectives"],"tradeoffs":["Replication improves availability or locality but adds synchronization complexity","Synchronous methods can strengthen consistency but increase coordination sensitivity","Asynchronous methods reduce coordination but permit lag"],"decision":"Select replication strategy according to the explicit availability, locality, scaling, or recovery requirement and its consistency tolerance.","rationale":"Replication is a mechanism with costs, not an objective independent of requirements.","consequences":["Replication lag becomes observable","Recovery and failover procedures must account for copy state"],"implementation_implications":["Define source authority","Track replication position or freshness","Test replica failure and recovery"],"verification_method":"Measure replication lag and simulate replica failure against the declared consistency and recovery requirements.","outcome_learning":"Monitor lag, divergence, recovery duration, and replica-related incidents.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B36-R02","D54-B13-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B36-R02"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B13-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B36-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Data Replication","subtopic":"Synchronous versus asynchronous replication","concept":"Selecting replication timing from consistency and failure requirements","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should synchronous versus asynchronous replication be selected?","answer":"Choose replication timing according to tolerated data loss, write latency, failure-domain requirements, and consistency guarantees rather than assuming one method is universally preferable.","context":"Critical data must be replicated across failure or geographic boundaries.","objective":"Balance durability and consistency requirements against write latency and availability constraints.","requirements":["Allowed data loss must be explicit","Replication acknowledgement semantics must be defined","Failure behavior must be tested"],"constraints":["Synchronous coordination can increase latency or availability sensitivity","Asynchronous replication can lose recent updates after source failure"],"alternatives":["Synchronous replication","Asynchronous replication","Hybrid strategy"],"evidence":["RPO","Latency budget","Failure domains","Network characteristics"],"tradeoffs":["Synchronous replication can reduce acknowledged-loss windows but requires coordination","Asynchronous replication reduces write-path coordination but introduces lag"],"decision":"Use synchronous semantics where the required recovery point cannot tolerate unreplicated acknowledged changes and latency/failure constraints permit it; otherwise use explicitly bounded asynchronous replication.","rationale":"Replication mode directly affects the relationship between acknowledged writes and recoverable copies.","consequences":["Write latency or failure behavior may change","Replication monitoring becomes critical"],"implementation_implications":["Define acknowledgement rule","Measure replication lag","Test primary and replica failure"],"verification_method":"Measure acknowledged-write durability under simulated replication and failure conditions.","outcome_learning":"Track actual lag and recovery-point behavior against the declared RPO.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B36-R01","D54-B13-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B36-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B13-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B36-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Data Replication","subtopic":"Replica reads","concept":"Managing read-after-write behavior with replicas","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should replica reads be handled when immediate visibility is required?","answer":"The architecture must explicitly route or coordinate reads that require read-after-write visibility rather than assuming every replica reflects the latest authoritative state.","context":"Writes occur against one authority while reads may be served by replicas.","objective":"Prevent user-visible stale reads when workflows require immediate visibility.","requirements":["Consistency-sensitive reads must be identifiable","Read routing must reflect the required guarantee","Fallback behavior must be defined"],"constraints":["Routing to the authority can increase load","Replica freshness varies"],"alternatives":["Always read authority","Session-aware routing","Accept eventual reads"],"evidence":["Workflow semantics","Replica lag","Read workload"],"tradeoffs":["Authority reads improve freshness but reduce read distribution","Session-aware routing preserves scalability with additional state","Eventual reads maximize distribution but may expose stale state"],"decision":"Use explicit consistency-aware routing for workflows requiring immediate post-write visibility and allow replica reads only where their freshness contract is acceptable.","rationale":"Replication changes the meaning of an otherwise simple read operation.","consequences":["Read APIs may need consistency modes","Replica lag becomes part of application behavior"],"implementation_implications":["Propagate write version or session marker","Route consistency-sensitive reads appropriately","Monitor lag"],"verification_method":"Perform write-then-read tests through each supported routing path under induced replica lag.","outcome_learning":"Track stale-read frequency and routing fallback usage.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B35-R03","D54-B36-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B35-R03"},{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B36-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B36-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Data Replication","subtopic":"Failover authority","concept":"Preventing unsafe replica promotion","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What must be established before promoting a replica to authoritative status?","answer":"Promotion requires explicit authority transfer, known replication state, conflict handling, client routing changes, and protection against simultaneous writers or split-brain conditions.","context":"The current authoritative database becomes unavailable and a replica may be promoted.","objective":"Restore service without creating conflicting authorities or corrupting state.","requirements":["Promotion authority must be explicit","Replication position must be assessed","Old authority must be prevented from accepting writes where necessary"],"constraints":["Failure detection may be uncertain","Network partitions can produce competing views"],"alternatives":["Manual promotion","Automated promotion","No promotion until authority is confirmed"],"evidence":["Failure detection","Replication position","Fencing capability","Recovery objectives"],"tradeoffs":["Automation reduces recovery time but requires safe fencing and decision logic","Manual promotion can provide stronger human control but increases recovery time"],"decision":"Permit promotion only when the architecture can establish a single authoritative writer and evaluate the candidate's recoverable state against the declared recovery requirements.","rationale":"Replica promotion changes data authority and therefore requires stronger controls than ordinary failover routing.","consequences":["Failover procedures become data-integrity controls","Promotion state must be auditable"],"implementation_implications":["Define fencing mechanism","Record promotion event","Validate replica state","Reconfigure clients"],"verification_method":"Perform controlled failover and partition scenarios and verify that only one authority can accept writes.","outcome_learning":"Measure recovery time, data-loss window, and promotion failures.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B36-R02","D54-B13-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B36-R02"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B13-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B36-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Data Replication","subtopic":"Replication validation","concept":"Verifying replicated data integrity","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_DECISION","question":"How should replicated data be verified beyond transport success?","answer":"Replication verification should compare logical or application-relevant state, not only transport or process health, and should detect missing, delayed, duplicated, or divergent records.","context":"A replication pipeline reports successful operation but correctness of the replica must be established.","objective":"Verify that replicated state satisfies the required integrity and freshness contract.","requirements":["Integrity checks must be defined","Lag must be measurable","Divergence detection must exist where required"],"constraints":["Full comparisons may be expensive","Large datasets may require sampled or incremental validation"],"alternatives":["Transport health only","Checksums or version comparisons","Logical reconciliation"],"evidence":["Data criticality","Dataset size","Replication semantics"],"tradeoffs":["Deep validation provides stronger assurance but costs resources","Lightweight checks reduce cost but may miss semantic divergence"],"decision":"Use validation depth proportional to data criticality, with logical reconciliation for high-value data where transport-level success is insufficient.","rationale":"Successful replication transport does not prove correct replicated state.","consequences":["Replication monitoring includes correctness signals","Reconciliation may become an operational process"],"implementation_implications":["Track record counts or versions","Use checksums or domain comparisons where appropriate","Define remediation"],"verification_method":"Intentionally introduce or simulate replication discrepancies and verify detection and recovery.","outcome_learning":"Track divergence incidents and false-positive reconciliation alerts.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B36-R01","D54-B36-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B36-R01"},{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B36-R04"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B37-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Caching and State Management","subtopic":"Cache eligibility","concept":"Determining whether data is safe to cache","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should determine whether data is cached?","answer":"Cache data when repeated access has measurable cost and the data can tolerate the selected freshness, invalidation, memory, security, and failure semantics.","context":"A frequently accessed data path is experiencing measurable latency or load pressure.","objective":"Determine whether caching solves the actual bottleneck without creating correctness problems.","requirements":["Cache benefit must be measurable","Freshness requirements must be defined","Invalidation or expiry semantics must be explicit"],"constraints":["Cached data may become stale","Cache storage can fail or evict entries","Sensitive data may require additional controls"],"alternatives":["No cache","Local cache","Shared cache","Derived read model"],"evidence":["Measured latency","Access frequency","Data volatility","Freshness tolerance"],"tradeoffs":["Caching can reduce repeated work but adds invalidation and memory management","No cache simplifies correctness but may leave a measured bottleneck"],"decision":"Introduce caching when measured repeated access cost justifies the added freshness, invalidation, resource, and failure complexity.","rationale":"Caching should be evidence-driven rather than speculative.","consequences":["Cold-cache behavior must remain correct","Cache failures become expected operating conditions"],"implementation_implications":["Define TTL or invalidation","Bound memory usage","Protect sensitive cached data","Test cache miss behavior"],"verification_method":"Compare representative workload with and without cache and test empty, stale, expired, and unavailable cache states.","outcome_learning":"Measure hit rate, latency benefit, stale-data incidents, and cache-related failures.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B15-R04","D54-B28-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B15-R04"},{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B28-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B37-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Caching and State Management","subtopic":"Invalidation","concept":"Selecting cache invalidation strategy","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should cache invalidation be selected?","answer":"Choose invalidation semantics according to data freshness requirements, update frequency, acceptable staleness, and the ability to observe authoritative changes.","context":"Cached values can become stale after authoritative updates.","objective":"Keep cached data within its declared freshness contract.","requirements":["Freshness requirement must be measurable","Invalidation trigger must be defined","Failure to invalidate must be detectable where critical"],"constraints":["Immediate invalidation can increase coordination","TTL alone may permit temporary stale data"],"alternatives":["Time-based expiry","Explicit invalidation","Version-based validation","Hybrid strategy"],"evidence":["Update frequency","Freshness budget","Change event availability","Read criticality"],"tradeoffs":["TTL is simple but bounded by time rather than actual change","Explicit invalidation improves freshness but increases coupling","Version validation can detect stale entries without requiring every invalidation to succeed"],"decision":"Use the simplest invalidation strategy that reliably satisfies the declared freshness requirement; combine mechanisms when one alone cannot provide the required bound.","rationale":"Cache correctness depends more on invalidation semantics than on cache technology.","consequences":["Invalidation paths become architecture dependencies","Monitoring may be required for freshness violations"],"implementation_implications":["Define cache key versioning","Handle missed invalidations","Test stale-cache scenarios"],"verification_method":"Change authoritative data and measure time until all relevant cache consumers satisfy the freshness contract.","outcome_learning":"Monitor stale duration, invalidation failures, and cache refresh frequency.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B37-R01","D54-B35-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B37-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B35-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B37-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Caching and State Management","subtopic":"Cache authority","concept":"Keeping cache state non-authoritative unless explicitly required","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should cache state normally remain non-authoritative?","answer":"A cache should normally be reconstructable from an authoritative source so eviction, corruption, or cache loss does not become permanent data loss or correctness failure.","context":"A system considers treating a cache as the primary location for frequently accessed state.","objective":"Determine whether the cache can safely be lost or rebuilt.","requirements":["Authoritative source must be identifiable","Rebuild path must exist if cache is derived","Cold-cache operation must be supported where required"],"constraints":["Some specialized systems intentionally use memory as primary state","Reconstruction may be expensive"],"alternatives":["Authoritative cache","Derived cache","Hybrid authoritative state"],"evidence":["Durability requirements","Reconstruction source","Failure tolerance"],"tradeoffs":["Authoritative cache can reduce storage path complexity but requires durability mechanisms","Derived cache simplifies recovery semantics"],"decision":"Treat cache as derived unless the architecture explicitly establishes durable authoritative semantics and verifies their recovery guarantees.","rationale":"Cache terminology should not obscure whether data can safely disappear.","consequences":["Cache eviction remains recoverable","Source authority remains clear"],"implementation_implications":["Document authority","Implement rebuild","Test complete cache loss"],"verification_method":"Delete or invalidate all cache state and verify recovery without violating data correctness.","outcome_learning":"Monitor cold-cache recovery and accidental cache-authority assumptions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B31-R03","D54-B37-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B31-R03"},{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B37-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B37-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Caching and State Management","subtopic":"Cache stampede","concept":"Controlling synchronized cache misses","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should cache stampede protection be part of architecture?","answer":"Stampede protection should be considered when many concurrent requests can miss or expire the same expensive entry and simultaneously regenerate it enough to threaten latency or backend capacity.","context":"A high-traffic cache contains entries with expensive regeneration cost.","objective":"Prevent synchronized misses from overwhelming the authoritative source.","requirements":["Regeneration cost must be measurable","Concurrent miss behavior must be defined","Fallback behavior must be explicit"],"constraints":["Coordination adds complexity","Serving stale data may violate freshness requirements"],"alternatives":["Uncontrolled regeneration","Single-flight or request coalescing","Stale-while-revalidate","Prewarming"],"evidence":["Request concurrency","Regeneration latency","Backend capacity","Freshness budget"],"tradeoffs":["Coalescing reduces duplicate work but adds coordination","Stale serving protects backend capacity but weakens freshness","Prewarming consumes resources proactively"],"decision":"Introduce stampede protection when measured concurrent regeneration can exceed backend capacity or violate latency requirements.","rationale":"Cache failure modes include aggregate request behavior, not only individual misses.","consequences":["Cache coordination becomes part of performance architecture","Failure of coordination must be bounded"],"implementation_implications":["Define regeneration ownership","Bound concurrent refreshes","Test simultaneous expiry"],"verification_method":"Simulate synchronized expiry under representative concurrency and measure backend load and response latency.","outcome_learning":"Monitor refresh concurrency, backend saturation, and stale-serving frequency.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B37-R01","D54-B15-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B37-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B15-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B37-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Caching and State Management","subtopic":"Sensitive state","concept":"Protecting sensitive data in caches","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should sensitive data affect caching decisions?","answer":"Sensitive data should be cached only when the security boundary, retention, access control, encryption, eviction, and exposure risks are explicitly acceptable and verifiable.","context":"A performance optimization would place sensitive or tenant-specific information into a shared or persistent cache.","objective":"Prevent cache optimization from creating unintended data exposure.","requirements":["Cache access must respect authorization","Tenant separation must be explicit","Retention and eviction must be controlled","Sensitive values must receive appropriate protection"],"constraints":["Caches may have different security controls than authoritative stores","Shared caches can create isolation risks"],"alternatives":["Do not cache sensitive data","Isolated cache","Encrypted protected cache"],"evidence":["Data classification","Tenant boundaries","Cache access model","Retention requirements"],"tradeoffs":["Avoiding cache minimizes exposure but may reduce performance","Protected caching adds key management and isolation complexity"],"decision":"Cache sensitive data only when its security controls are equivalent to the required data-protection boundary and cache-specific leakage paths are tested.","rationale":"Performance optimization must not weaken the system's data-protection architecture.","consequences":["Cache design becomes part of security review","Eviction and access logging may be required"],"implementation_implications":["Partition cache by tenant where required","Avoid authorization-bypassable keys","Protect sensitive values","Test cross-tenant access"],"verification_method":"Attempt unauthorized and cross-tenant cache retrievals and verify isolation and eviction behavior.","outcome_learning":"Monitor cache access anomalies and sensitive-data exposure events.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B18-R05","D54-B37-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B18-R05"},{"type":"constrains","record_id":"D54-B37-R01"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B38-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Event Sourcing, Logs and State Reconstruction","subtopic":"Event sourcing suitability","concept":"Selecting event sourcing when state history is architecturally valuable","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should event sourcing be considered?","answer":"Event sourcing should be considered when the sequence of domain state changes is itself valuable for auditability, reconstruction, temporal analysis, or domain behavior, and the system can support event schema evolution and replay.","context":"A system requires historical state transitions rather than only current state.","objective":"Determine whether an append-oriented event history provides sufficient value to justify its complexity.","requirements":["Event history must have meaningful business value","Events must represent stable domain facts","Replay and evolution must be supportable"],"constraints":["Event schemas evolve","Replay can be expensive","Events become durable compatibility contracts"],"alternatives":["Current-state storage","Audit log alongside state","Event-sourced authority"],"evidence":["Audit requirements","Temporal reconstruction needs","Domain event semantics","Replay requirements"],"tradeoffs":["Event sourcing provides rich history but increases schema and replay complexity","Current-state storage is simpler but may require separate audit mechanisms"],"decision":"Use event sourcing when durable state-transition history is a first-class requirement and replay, versioning, and operational costs are acceptable.","rationale":"Event sourcing is a persistence model with substantial lifecycle implications, not merely an audit feature.","consequences":["Events become long-lived contracts","Rebuild and replay tooling becomes necessary"],"implementation_implications":["Define event schemas and versions","Maintain deterministic projection logic","Test replay"],"verification_method":"Rebuild current state from retained events and compare it with the authoritative expected state.","outcome_learning":"Monitor replay duration, event compatibility, projection failures, and schema evolution burden.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B38-R02","D54-B31-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B38-R02"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B31-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B38-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Event Sourcing, Logs and State Reconstruction","subtopic":"Event versus audit log","concept":"Distinguishing event-sourced authority from auxiliary audit logging","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How is event sourcing different from adding an audit log to a state-based system?","answer":"Event sourcing uses the event history as the authoritative representation from which current state can be derived, while an audit log can record changes alongside an independently authoritative current-state store.","context":"A system needs historical records of changes.","objective":"Choose between event-sourced state and supplementary audit logging.","requirements":["Historical requirements must be defined","Authority of the event stream must be explicit","Reconstruction needs must be understood"],"constraints":["Event sourcing increases replay and schema responsibilities","Audit logs may not contain enough information for complete reconstruction"],"alternatives":["Audit log plus current state","Event-sourced authority","Both"],"evidence":["Reconstruction requirements","Audit semantics","State transition completeness"],"tradeoffs":["Audit logging can be simpler","Event sourcing provides reconstruction when events are complete and deterministic","Maintaining both increases storage and governance complexity"],"decision":"Use event sourcing only when event history must serve as authoritative reconstruction input; otherwise an audit log may satisfy historical traceability without replacing current-state authority.","rationale":"History and authority are distinct architectural properties.","consequences":["The chosen model determines recovery and schema obligations","Consumers must not assume audit logs are reconstructable state histories unless explicitly designed so"],"implementation_implications":["Document event authority","Define audit versus domain event semantics","Test reconstruction requirements"],"verification_method":"Attempt state reconstruction from the proposed historical record and verify whether it contains sufficient authoritative information.","outcome_learning":"Monitor audit completeness and reconstruction failures.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B38-R01","D54-B38-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B38-R01"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B38-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B38-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Event Sourcing, Logs and State Reconstruction","subtopic":"Replay determinism","concept":"Designing deterministic state reconstruction","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_DECISION","question":"What makes event-based state reconstruction reliable?","answer":"Reliable reconstruction requires sufficient event information, deterministic projection logic, explicit event ordering semantics, compatible schemas, and controlled handling of external dependencies.","context":"Current state is rebuilt by replaying historical events.","objective":"Ensure replay produces reproducible and explainable state.","requirements":["Events must contain sufficient reconstruction information","Projection logic must be deterministic or explicitly versioned","Ordering semantics must be defined","External nondeterminism must be controlled"],"constraints":["Historical events may outlive code versions","External services may change behavior"],"alternatives":["Deterministic replay","Versioned projection replay","Snapshot plus event replay"],"evidence":["Replay requirements","Event completeness","Projection dependencies","Historical schema versions"],"tradeoffs":["Deterministic projections simplify verification","Versioned projections increase maintenance","Snapshots reduce replay cost but add snapshot lifecycle complexity"],"decision":"Require deterministic or explicitly versioned projection behavior and isolate uncontrolled external dependencies from authoritative replay.","rationale":"A historical log is useful for reconstruction only if replay semantics remain sufficiently stable.","consequences":["Projection versions become durable artifacts","Replay tests become architecture verification"],"implementation_implications":["Version projection logic where necessary","Persist event schema version","Test replay from historical checkpoints"],"verification_method":"Replay identical event sequences multiple times and compare resulting state; replay historical versions where supported.","outcome_learning":"Track replay divergence, duration, and projection compatibility failures.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B38-R01","D54-B38-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B38-R01"},{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B38-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B38-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Event Sourcing, Logs and State Reconstruction","subtopic":"Snapshots","concept":"Using snapshots to bound reconstruction cost","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should snapshots supplement event replay?","answer":"Snapshots are useful when replaying the complete event history becomes materially expensive and a validated snapshot can safely serve as a reconstruction starting point followed by replay of later events.","context":"An event-sourced aggregate has a long history and reconstruction time is becoming significant.","objective":"Reduce reconstruction cost without losing authoritative event history.","requirements":["Snapshot correctness must be verifiable","Snapshot version must be identifiable","Events after the snapshot must remain replayable"],"constraints":["Snapshots add storage and lifecycle complexity","A corrupted snapshot must not permanently corrupt reconstructed state"],"alternatives":["Full replay","Periodic snapshots","Materialized current state"],"evidence":["Event count","Replay duration","Recovery objectives","Snapshot generation cost"],"tradeoffs":["Snapshots improve recovery performance but add another derived artifact","Full replay is simpler conceptually but may become operationally expensive"],"decision":"Introduce snapshots when measured replay cost violates recovery or operational requirements and snapshot correctness can be independently verified.","rationale":"Snapshotting is a performance optimization for reconstruction, not a replacement for authoritative history.","consequences":["Snapshot generation and validation become operational tasks","Recovery must support snapshot invalidation or fallback replay"],"implementation_implications":["Store snapshot event position","Validate snapshot against replay","Support fallback from earlier snapshot or full history"],"verification_method":"Corrupt or invalidate a snapshot and verify that reconstruction remains possible from a trusted historical point.","outcome_learning":"Measure recovery time, snapshot generation cost, and snapshot mismatch rates.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B38-R03","D54-B14-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B38-R03"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B14-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B38-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Event Sourcing, Logs and State Reconstruction","subtopic":"Event retention","concept":"Determining historical event retention from reconstruction requirements","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should event retention be determined in an event-sourced architecture?","answer":"Retain events for as long as required by reconstruction, audit, regulatory, analytical, or recovery requirements, subject to explicit deletion and privacy constraints.","context":"An event store contains potentially long-lived historical records.","objective":"Define retention without either losing required history or retaining data without justification.","requirements":["Reconstruction horizon must be defined","Legal and privacy constraints must be evaluated","Deletion behavior must be explicit"],"constraints":["Events may contain sensitive information","Deleting historical events can affect reconstruction"],"alternatives":["Indefinite retention","Bounded retention","Archived historical events"],"evidence":["Recovery requirements","Audit requirements","Data classification","Regulatory obligations"],"tradeoffs":["Long retention supports reconstruction but increases storage and data-governance burden","Short retention reduces exposure but can limit historical reconstruction"],"decision":"Set retention from documented business, recovery, audit, and data-governance requirements rather than assuming indefinite event retention.","rationale":"Event history is durable data and must follow explicit lifecycle rules.","consequences":["Retention becomes part of event architecture","Deletion can require projection or reconstruction analysis"],"implementation_implications":["Classify event data","Define retention tiers","Test deletion and archival behavior"],"verification_method":"Verify that retained history satisfies reconstruction requirements and that expired data follows documented deletion or archival behavior.","outcome_learning":"Review retention against actual reconstruction use and governance incidents.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B38-R01","D54-B18-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B38-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B18-R03"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B39-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Data Partitioning and Sharding","subtopic":"Partition-key selection","concept":"Selecting partition keys from access and distribution requirements","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should determine a data partition key?","answer":"A partition key should distribute workload adequately while preserving required access locality, query efficiency, ownership, and correctness semantics.","context":"A dataset may need to be divided across storage nodes or logical partitions.","objective":"Choose a partitioning strategy that balances distribution with access efficiency.","requirements":["Primary access patterns must be known","Expected key distribution must be understood","Cross-partition operations must be identified"],"constraints":["Poor keys can create hotspots","Changing partition keys later can require expensive migration"],"alternatives":["Tenant-based key","Entity-based key","Hash-based key","Composite key"],"evidence":["Access patterns","Key cardinality","Traffic distribution","Data growth"],"tradeoffs":["Locality-oriented keys improve related access but may create hotspots","Hashing distributes load but can weaken locality","Composite keys can balance both but increase design complexity"],"decision":"Select a partition key using measured or reasoned access distribution and locality requirements, with explicit analysis of hotspot and cross-partition behavior.","rationale":"Partitioning is an access and workload decision, not merely a storage decision.","consequences":["Partition key becomes a durable architectural constraint","Future repartitioning may be costly"],"implementation_implications":["Model representative key distributions","Test hotspot scenarios","Document cross-partition operations"],"verification_method":"Simulate representative workload distribution and verify partition balance and critical query behavior.","outcome_learning":"Monitor partition skew, hot partitions, and cross-partition query frequency.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B39-R02","D54-B16-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B39-R02"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B16-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B39-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Data Partitioning and Sharding","subtopic":"Hotspot avoidance","concept":"Preventing partition hotspots","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should hotspot risk affect partition-key selection?","answer":"Partition-key selection should evaluate both average distribution and worst-case concentration because a small number of high-volume keys can overload one partition despite balanced total record counts.","context":"Some tenants, users, entities, or time ranges may generate disproportionately high traffic.","objective":"Prevent localized overload from undermining horizontal distribution.","requirements":["Traffic distribution must be considered","High-cardinality and high-volume keys must be identified","Worst-case concentration must be bounded"],"constraints":["Splitting hot keys can complicate ordering and locality","Randomization can weaken targeted access"],"alternatives":["Natural key","Hash key","Composite or salted key","Hierarchical partitioning"],"evidence":["Traffic distribution","Per-key volume","Temporal patterns","Access locality"],"tradeoffs":["Natural keys preserve locality but may hotspot","Hashing improves distribution but weakens locality","Composite or salted approaches improve distribution with additional lookup complexity"],"decision":"Choose a partitioning strategy that protects against demonstrated or credible hotspot patterns while preserving required access semantics.","rationale":"Average distribution is insufficient when workload is highly skewed.","consequences":["Partition monitoring must include skew metrics","Hot-key mitigation may become part of capacity planning"],"implementation_implications":["Measure per-key traffic","Define split strategy","Test worst-case tenants/entities"],"verification_method":"Run skewed workload simulations and verify that no partition exceeds documented resource limits.","outcome_learning":"Monitor partition-level saturation and key concentration over time.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B39-R01","D54-B16-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B39-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B16-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B39-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Data Partitioning and Sharding","subtopic":"Cross-partition operations","concept":"Controlling cross-partition transaction and query costs","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should cross-partition operations influence sharding decisions?","answer":"Partitioning should account for the frequency and correctness requirements of operations that span partitions; excessive cross-partition coordination can negate the intended benefits of sharding.","context":"A workload contains queries or transactions that frequently access multiple partition keys.","objective":"Ensure partitioning does not create unacceptable coordination or query complexity.","requirements":["Cross-partition operations must be inventoried","Atomicity requirements must be explicit","Performance cost must be measurable"],"constraints":["Some global queries are unavoidable","Distributed transactions may be expensive or unsupported"],"alternatives":["Repartition by access pattern","Maintain secondary projections","Coordinate cross-partition operation"],"evidence":["Query traces","Transaction scope","Join frequency","Latency requirements"],"tradeoffs":["More locality can reduce cross-partition work but may create skew","Secondary projections improve reads but add synchronization","Distributed coordination preserves semantics with additional complexity"],"decision":"Adjust partitioning or introduce derived access structures when cross-partition operations materially violate performance or consistency requirements.","rationale":"A sharding strategy must be evaluated against the actual workload, not only distribution goals.","consequences":["Data model and query model become linked","Global operations may require specialized paths"],"implementation_implications":["Measure cross-partition traffic","Define distributed transaction behavior","Create projections only when justified"],"verification_method":"Execute representative cross-partition workload and measure latency, coordination, and correctness.","outcome_learning":"Track cross-partition frequency and cost as architecture health signals.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B39-R01","D54-B34-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B39-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B34-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B39-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Data Partitioning and Sharding","subtopic":"Repartitioning","concept":"Designing for partition-key evolution","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should future partition-key changes influence initial sharding design?","answer":"Where future growth or access-pattern uncertainty is material, architecture should preserve a feasible repartitioning path and avoid coupling application identity too tightly to physical partition placement.","context":"A dataset is expected to grow significantly or workload distribution is uncertain.","objective":"Reduce the cost and risk of future repartitioning.","requirements":["Partition location should not become an uncontrolled application dependency","Migration strategy must be conceivable","Partition metadata must be manageable"],"constraints":["Some databases tightly couple partitioning and access semantics","Repartitioning can require substantial data movement"],"alternatives":["Fixed partition strategy","Metadata-driven partitioning","Preallocated partition structure"],"evidence":["Growth uncertainty","Key distribution","Migration cost","Storage capabilities"],"tradeoffs":["Planning for repartitioning adds design complexity","Ignoring future change can create expensive migration constraints"],"decision":"When growth uncertainty is material, preserve explicit partition metadata and a tested migration strategy rather than assuming the initial key will remain permanently optimal.","rationale":"Partitioning decisions can become difficult-to-reverse structural constraints.","consequences":["Partition metadata becomes part of architecture","Migration tooling may be required before it is operationally urgent"],"implementation_implications":["Separate logical identity from physical placement","Define repartitioning process","Test movement of representative data"],"verification_method":"Perform a controlled repartitioning exercise on representative data and verify correctness and application compatibility.","outcome_learning":"Track skew and migration cost indicators as dataset size increases.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B39-R01","D54-B40-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B39-R01"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B40-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B39-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Data Partitioning and Sharding","subtopic":"Tenant partitioning","concept":"Using tenant boundaries for partitioning","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When can tenant identity be an appropriate partitioning dimension?","answer":"Tenant-based partitioning can be appropriate when tenant isolation, ownership, workload locality, or operational lifecycle align with the required partition boundaries and skew can be controlled.","context":"A multi-tenant system needs scalable storage and tenant-level operational boundaries.","objective":"Evaluate whether tenant identity provides a useful partitioning dimension.","requirements":["Tenant isolation requirements must be explicit","Tenant size distribution must be evaluated","Cross-tenant operations must be defined"],"constraints":["Large tenants can create hotspots","Global administration or reporting may span tenants"],"alternatives":["Tenant partitioning","Hash partitioning across tenants","Hybrid tenant-plus-hash partitioning"],"evidence":["Tenant size distribution","Access patterns","Isolation requirements","Global query workload"],"tradeoffs":["Tenant partitioning improves locality and isolation","Large tenants may require subpartitioning","Hashing improves balance but weakens tenant locality"],"decision":"Use tenant identity as a partitioning dimension when isolation and locality are valuable and the architecture has a strategy for unusually large tenants and global operations.","rationale":"Tenant boundaries can align data ownership and operational boundaries but do not automatically guarantee balanced load.","consequences":["Tenant lifecycle can map to storage lifecycle","Hot tenants require explicit mitigation"],"implementation_implications":["Define tenant-to-partition mapping","Test large-tenant behavior","Protect cross-tenant access"],"verification_method":"Simulate tenant distributions including highly skewed tenants and verify load balance and isolation.","outcome_learning":"Monitor per-tenant storage, traffic, and partition concentration.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B39-R01","D54-B18-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B39-R01"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B18-R04"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B40-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Data Migration and Schema Evolution","subtopic":"Migration strategy","concept":"Choosing migration strategy from risk and availability requirements","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should a data migration strategy be selected?","answer":"Select migration strategy according to dataset size, downtime tolerance, compatibility requirements, rollback capability, transformation complexity, and data correctness risk.","context":"A persistent data model must change while the system remains operational.","objective":"Change storage representation without unacceptable downtime or data loss.","requirements":["Migration correctness must be verifiable","Rollback or recovery conditions must be defined","Application compatibility during transition must be explicit"],"constraints":["Live traffic may continue","Large datasets may make rollback expensive","Old and new schemas may coexist temporarily"],"alternatives":["Offline migration","Expand-and-contract migration","Dual-write or staged migration"],"evidence":["Dataset size","Downtime requirement","Write volume","Transformation complexity","Rollback feasibility"],"tradeoffs":["Offline migration simplifies consistency but may require downtime","Expand-and-contract supports live operation but increases transition complexity","Dual-write can support gradual cutover but creates synchronization risks"],"decision":"Use the least complex migration strategy that satisfies availability and correctness requirements; for live systems with incompatible schema changes, evaluate staged compatibility patterns before direct cutover.","rationale":"Migration strategy must account for both data state and application behavior during transition.","consequences":["Temporary compatibility logic may exist","Migration observability becomes necessary"],"implementation_implications":["Define migration phases","Back up or preserve recovery state","Validate counts and invariants","Plan rollback"],"verification_method":"Run migration on representative data, compare pre/post invariants, and exercise rollback or recovery procedures.","outcome_learning":"Measure migration duration, discrepancy counts, rollback events, and operational impact.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B31-R05","D54-B40-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B31-R05"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B40-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B40-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Data Migration and Schema Evolution","subtopic":"Expand and contract","concept":"Using compatibility phases for live schema evolution","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When is an expand-and-contract migration appropriate?","answer":"Expand-and-contract is appropriate when old and new application versions must coexist while a schema evolves and the change can be represented through additive compatibility before old structures are removed.","context":"A live system cannot stop all application instances during schema migration.","objective":"Allow controlled schema evolution without requiring simultaneous application and database replacement.","requirements":["Old and new versions must coexist safely","New schema elements must be compatible with old behavior during expansion","Removal must occur only after consumers migrate"],"constraints":["Temporary duplicated fields or transformations may exist","Migration sequencing becomes critical"],"alternatives":["Immediate replacement","Expand-and-contract","Offline migration"],"evidence":["Deployment model","Compatibility constraints","Schema dependency graph"],"tradeoffs":["Expand-and-contract reduces coordinated cutover risk but increases temporary complexity","Immediate replacement is simpler but requires synchronized deployment","Offline migration may be safer for complex transformations but requires downtime"],"decision":"Use expand-and-contract when live compatibility is required and the schema change can be staged through additive and removal phases.","rationale":"Separating addition from removal reduces dependency on synchronized deployment.","consequences":["Temporary schema duplication may exist","Removal requires proof that old consumers are gone"],"implementation_implications":["Add new representation","Backfill data","Support dual reads if required","Migrate writers","Verify consumers","Remove old representation"],"verification_method":"Run mixed-version application tests through every migration phase and verify old and new consumers observe valid data.","outcome_learning":"Track migration lag, compatibility failures, and residual legacy consumers.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B40-R01","D54-B40-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B40-R01"},{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B40-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B40-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Data Migration and Schema Evolution","subtopic":"Backward and forward compatibility","concept":"Managing schema compatibility across application versions","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should schema compatibility be evaluated during rolling deployments?","answer":"Schema evolution must account for both older consumers reading newer representations and newer consumers encountering older representations where coexistence occurs; compatibility must be tested for the actual transition sequence.","context":"Multiple application versions may run simultaneously during deployment.","objective":"Prevent rolling deployment from creating incompatible data access behavior.","requirements":["Supported version combinations must be identified","Read and write compatibility must be tested","Removal timing must be explicit"],"constraints":["Not all schema changes are safely compatible","Long-lived clients may extend the compatibility window"],"alternatives":["Strict synchronized deployment","Backward-compatible evolution","Versioned schema contracts"],"evidence":["Deployment topology","Client lifetime","Schema usage","Serialization behavior"],"tradeoffs":["Compatibility increases deployment flexibility but constrains schema changes","Synchronized deployment reduces compatibility requirements but increases coordination"],"decision":"For rolling deployments, require explicit compatibility analysis for every schema change and prohibit removal of structures still required by supported consumers.","rationale":"Deployment order creates real architecture states that must be supported temporarily.","consequences":["Schema compatibility becomes part of release engineering","Legacy consumer detection becomes important"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain compatibility matrix","Test mixed-version scenarios","Instrument legacy access where feasible"],"verification_method":"Run old/new application combinations against each migration stage and verify successful reads and writes.","outcome_learning":"Monitor legacy-version access and compatibility failures during releases.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B40-R02","D54-B32-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B40-R02"},{"type":"relates_to","record_id":"D54-B32-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B40-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Data Migration and Schema Evolution","subtopic":"Data transformation correctness","concept":"Separating structural migration from semantic transformation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_DECISION","question":"How should complex data transformations be verified during migration?","answer":"Separate structural movement from semantic transformation where practical, define source-to-target invariants, and validate transformed records using deterministic reconciliation rather than relying only on migration completion status.","context":"A schema migration also changes how values are represented or derived.","objective":"Verify that transformed data preserves required meaning.","requirements":["Transformation rules must be explicit","Source-to-target invariants must be defined","Rejected or ambiguous records must be observable"],"constraints":["Some transformations are lossy by design","Large datasets may require sampled and aggregate validation"],"alternatives":["Single migration script","Staged transformation","Offline transformation plus validation"],"evidence":["Transformation specification","Source data quality","Target constraints","Business invariants"],"tradeoffs":["Staged transformation improves diagnosability but extends migration lifecycle","Single-step transformation is simpler but harder to isolate failures"],"decision":"For semantically significant transformations, use explicit transformation rules and independent reconciliation rather than treating successful script completion as proof of correctness.","rationale":"Migration execution status does not prove semantic correctness.","consequences":["Validation becomes a separate migration phase","Some records may require manual or automated remediation"],"implementation_implications":["Record transformation version","Capture rejected records","Compare source and target invariants","Produce reconciliation report"],"verification_method":"Run deterministic reconciliation on representative or complete datasets as feasible and verify required invariants.","outcome_learning":"Track discrepancy classes, remediation rates, and transformation regressions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B40-R01","D54-B40-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B40-R01"},{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B40-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B40-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Data Migration and Schema Evolution","subtopic":"Rollback and irreversibility","concept":"Handling migrations that cannot be safely rolled back","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an architecture handle an irreversible data migration?","answer":"If rollback cannot restore the original data semantics, the migration must use stronger preconditions, backups or preservation mechanisms, staged validation, and an explicit forward-recovery strategy before destructive steps.","context":"A migration permanently transforms or removes information and cannot be reversed through a simple schema rollback.","objective":"Prevent irreversible migration errors from becoming unrecoverable data loss.","requirements":["Recovery state must be preserved before destructive changes","Validation gates must precede irreversible steps","Forward recovery must be defined"],"constraints":["Storage and backup cost may increase","Some transformations cannot preserve every historical representation"],"alternatives":["Reversible migration","Preserved historical copy","Forward-only migration with recovery plan"],"evidence":["Lossiness of transformation","Backup/recovery capabilities","Validation confidence","Business criticality"],"tradeoffs":["Preserving historical data increases storage but improves recovery options","Irreversible migration can reduce long-term complexity after successful validation","Forward recovery may require specialized remediation"],"decision":"Do not perform irreversible migration steps until preservation, validation, and forward-recovery conditions are explicitly satisfied.","rationale":"Irreversibility changes migration risk from recoverable rollback failure to potential permanent data loss.","consequences":["Migration gates become stricter","Historical preservation may be temporarily or permanently required"],"implementation_implications":["Create verified backup or export","Run pre-migration reconciliation","Require explicit destructive-step approval","Document forward remediation"],"verification_method":"Validate restoration or reconstruction from preserved state before executing destructive migration steps and test the recovery procedure independently.","outcome_learning":"Record migration discrepancies, recovery effectiveness, and whether preserved artifacts were sufficient.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B40-R01","D54-B40-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B40-R01"},{"type":"requires","record_id":"D54-B40-R04"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B41-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Resource and contract boundaries","concept":"API boundaries should represent stable consumer-facing capabilities rather than mirror internal database tables or implementation classes.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should an API expose a resource boundary instead of directly exposing persistence entities?","answer":"Expose an API resource when the capability has a stable business meaning, controlled lifecycle, and consumer contract. Avoid coupling the public contract directly to persistence structures because storage models may change independently.","context":"A system needs an external API while its internal persistence model is expected to evolve.","objective":"Preserve API stability while allowing independent internal evolution.","requirements":["Stable consumer contract","Independent persistence evolution","Explicit validation and authorization boundaries"],"constraints":["Consumers depend on published API behavior","Internal schemas may change"],"alternatives":["Expose database entities directly","Expose stable domain resources","Expose operation-oriented endpoints"],"evidence":"Engineering architecture principle: external contracts should minimize unnecessary coupling to internal implementation details.","tradeoffs":{"direct_entity_exposure":{"benefits":["Low initial implementation effort"],"costs":["Strong storage coupling","Migration becomes externally visible"]},"stable_resource_contract":{"benefits":["Independent internal evolution","Clear ownership"],"costs":["Requires mapping and contract governance"]}},"decision":"Use stable resource or capability contracts rather than direct persistence-entity exposure for externally consumed APIs.","rationale":"The API and storage layers usually evolve at different rates and should therefore have independently governed boundaries.","consequences":["Additional mapping may be required","API compatibility must be explicitly managed"],"implementation_implications":["Define API schemas separately from persistence schemas","Version breaking contracts deliberately","Test contract behavior independently"],"verification_method":"Review API schemas for direct persistence leakage and verify that representative storage changes do not require incompatible API changes.","outcome_learning":"API stability improves when persistence representation is treated as an internal implementation concern.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B41-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Compatibility strategy","concept":"API compatibility policy should distinguish additive changes from breaking changes and define how consumers transition.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an API architecture handle contract evolution?","answer":"Adopt an explicit compatibility policy that identifies which changes are backward compatible, establishes a migration mechanism for breaking changes, and defines deprecation and removal rules.","context":"Multiple consumers use an API that must evolve without uncontrolled client failures.","objective":"Allow API evolution while controlling compatibility risk.","requirements":["Compatibility rules","Deprecation policy","Consumer migration path"],"constraints":["Unknown consumer upgrade timing","Existing integrations may remain on older contracts"],"alternatives":["Allow unrestricted breaking changes","Maintain indefinite compatibility","Use governed compatibility and deprecation"],"evidence":"Contract evolution requires explicit compatibility semantics because consumers and providers are independently deployed.","tradeoffs":{"unrestricted_breaking_changes":{"benefits":["Fast provider evolution"],"costs":["High consumer failure risk"]},"indefinite_compatibility":{"benefits":["Low immediate migration pressure"],"costs":["Long-term maintenance burden"]},"governed_evolution":{"benefits":["Predictable migration","Controlled technical debt"],"costs":["Requires lifecycle governance"]}},"decision":"Use governed compatibility rules with explicit deprecation and migration mechanisms.","rationale":"The architecture must balance provider evolution with the operational reality of independently upgraded consumers.","consequences":["Compatibility becomes an explicit architectural responsibility","Deprecated contracts require lifecycle tracking"],"implementation_implications":["Define compatibility tests","Document breaking-change criteria","Track deprecated versions","Provide migration guidance"],"verification_method":"Run compatibility tests against supported contract versions and verify that documented deprecation rules are enforceable.","outcome_learning":"Explicit compatibility policy reduces ambiguity during API evolution.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B41-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B41-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B41-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Idempotent operations","concept":"Retryable API operations should have explicitly defined idempotency semantics when duplicate execution could change state incorrectly.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should an API operation support idempotency?","answer":"Use an idempotency mechanism when clients or intermediaries may retry an operation and repeated execution could create duplicate or inconsistent business effects.","context":"A client submits a state-changing request across an unreliable network.","objective":"Prevent retry-induced duplicate business effects.","requirements":["Stable operation identity","Duplicate-request detection","Defined replay behavior"],"constraints":["Requests may be retried","Network acknowledgement may be lost after server-side execution"],"alternatives":["Rely on clients never retrying","Make operations naturally idempotent","Use explicit idempotency keys"],"evidence":"Network failures can leave clients uncertain whether a state-changing request completed.","tradeoffs":{"client_no_retry":{"benefits":["Simple server"],"costs":["Poor reliability"]},"natural_idempotency":{"benefits":["Simple retry model"],"costs":["Not feasible for every operation"]},"explicit_idempotency":{"benefits":["Controlled duplicate suppression"],"costs":["Requires storage and lifecycle rules"]}},"decision":"Define idempotency semantics for retryable state-changing operations and use explicit operation identity where natural idempotency is insufficient.","rationale":"Retry safety must be part of the API contract rather than an undocumented client assumption.","consequences":["Idempotency records may require retention rules","Business semantics must define duplicate handling"],"implementation_implications":["Generate or accept idempotency keys","Bind keys to relevant request scope","Return deterministic results for recognized duplicates"],"verification_method":"Submit the same logical operation repeatedly and verify that business state changes only according to the defined idempotency contract.","outcome_learning":"Retry safety depends on business semantics, not merely HTTP transport behavior.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B41-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"Error contract","concept":"API errors should expose stable machine-readable semantics while avoiding leakage of internal implementation details.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should an API error contract communicate?","answer":"Expose a stable error category, actionable machine-readable code, relevant request context, and safe human-readable information while avoiding secrets, internal stack traces, or infrastructure details.","context":"Consumers need to distinguish validation, authorization, conflict, dependency, and transient failures.","objective":"Make failures actionable without coupling consumers to internal implementation.","requirements":["Stable error semantics","Safe diagnostic information","Machine-readable classification"],"constraints":["Security-sensitive internal details","Different consumers require programmatic handling"],"alternatives":["Return raw exceptions","Use only HTTP status codes","Use structured error contracts"],"evidence":"Status codes alone may not provide sufficient domain-level information for reliable consumer behavior.","tradeoffs":{"raw_exceptions":{"benefits":["Low implementation effort"],"costs":["Information leakage","Unstable contract"]},"status_only":{"benefits":["Simple contract"],"costs":["Limited diagnostic semantics"]},"structured_errors":{"benefits":["Predictable client handling","Controlled disclosure"],"costs":["Requires schema governance"]}},"decision":"Use structured, stable API error contracts with controlled diagnostic detail.","rationale":"Clients need predictable failure semantics while internal implementation details must remain private.","consequences":["Error schemas become part of API compatibility","Error classification requires governance"],"implementation_implications":["Define stable error codes","Separate client-safe details from internal diagnostics","Test error responses as contract behavior"],"verification_method":"Exercise representative failure classes and verify stable machine-readable responses without internal secrets or stack traces.","outcome_learning":"Error behavior is part of an API contract and should be designed rather than exposed accidentally.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B41-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B41-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B41-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"API Design","subtopic":"API security boundary","concept":"Authentication, authorization, validation, rate controls, and audit requirements should be treated as API boundary concerns rather than assumed to be inherited from internal services.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Which security responsibilities belong at an API boundary?","answer":"The API boundary should explicitly enforce authentication and authorization context, input validation, abuse controls, and security-relevant audit requirements appropriate to the threat model.","context":"An API is exposed to consumers outside the immediate trust boundary of its implementation services.","objective":"Prevent untrusted input and unauthorized operations from crossing the service boundary.","requirements":["Authenticated identity where required","Authorization enforcement","Input validation","Abuse protection","Security auditability"],"constraints":["Different consumers may have different privileges","Internal services may operate under different trust assumptions"],"alternatives":["Trust downstream services entirely","Centralize all security in an external gateway","Layer boundary and downstream authorization"],"evidence":"Trust boundaries require explicit enforcement because successful authentication alone does not establish permission for a requested operation.","tradeoffs":{"trust_downstream":{"benefits":["Simpler edge"],"costs":["Larger implicit trust boundary"]},"gateway_only":{"benefits":["Centralized controls"],"costs":["Potential gaps in service-specific authorization"]},"layered_enforcement":{"benefits":["Defense in depth","Explicit ownership"],"costs":["Additional policy coordination"]}},"decision":"Treat the API as an explicit security boundary while preserving necessary downstream authorization checks.","rationale":"Authentication and authorization answer different questions and should not be collapsed into a single trust assumption.","consequences":["Security policies require coordination across layers","Authorization failures must be observable"],"implementation_implications":["Validate inputs at the boundary","Propagate authenticated identity securely","Authorize sensitive operations","Audit security-relevant decisions"],"verification_method":"Test unauthorized, malformed, over-privileged, and valid requests and verify expected enforcement at each applicable boundary.","outcome_learning":"Security architecture is stronger when trust transitions and authorization responsibilities are explicit.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B42-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Service Boundaries","subtopic":"Business capability boundaries","concept":"Service boundaries should align with cohesive business capabilities and ownership rather than arbitrary technical layers.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should service boundaries be established?","answer":"Establish boundaries around cohesive capabilities, data ownership, business invariants, and change ownership while minimizing unnecessary cross-service coordination.","context":"A system is being decomposed into independently deployable components.","objective":"Create boundaries that reduce coupling and preserve coherent responsibility.","requirements":["Clear ownership","Cohesive behavior","Controlled dependencies"],"constraints":["Some workflows span multiple capabilities","Existing systems may have tangled dependencies"],"alternatives":["Technical-layer services","Business-capability services","Fine-grained services for every component"],"evidence":"A service boundary determines ownership, deployment coupling, data interaction, and failure propagation.","tradeoffs":{"technical_layers":{"benefits":["Familiar decomposition"],"costs":["Cross-cutting coupling"]},"business_capabilities":{"benefits":["Clear ownership","Cohesion"],"costs":["Requires domain analysis"]},"extreme_granularity":{"benefits":["Small components"],"costs":["Coordination and operational complexity"]}},"decision":"Prefer cohesive capability-oriented boundaries where they reduce coupling and preserve ownership clarity.","rationale":"The boundary should isolate meaningful responsibilities rather than simply divide code by technical type.","consequences":["Some services may be larger than individual technical modules","Boundary quality must be revisited as ownership changes"],"implementation_implications":["Document service responsibility","Define owned data","Define dependency contracts","Measure cross-service interaction"],"verification_method":"Review representative workflows and verify that each service has coherent responsibility and identifiable ownership.","outcome_learning":"Service decomposition should optimize responsibility and coupling rather than component count.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B42-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Service Boundaries","subtopic":"Coupling control","concept":"A service boundary is valuable only when it reduces meaningful coupling; extracting highly chatty components can increase distributed-system coupling.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When can service decomposition make an architecture more coupled?","answer":"Decomposition can increase coupling when workflows require frequent synchronous calls, shared transactions, shared mutable state, or coordinated deployments across the resulting services.","context":"A monolithic module is being split into multiple services without changing its interaction model.","objective":"Evaluate whether decomposition actually reduces architectural coupling.","requirements":["Dependency analysis","Workflow analysis","Failure analysis"],"constraints":["Network communication introduces additional failure modes","Distributed transactions are harder to coordinate"],"alternatives":["Keep components together","Split into independently cohesive services","Use modular monolith boundaries first"],"evidence":"Distributed boundaries add communication and failure semantics that do not exist inside a single process.","tradeoffs":{"keep_together":{"benefits":["Lower communication complexity"],"costs":["Potential deployment coupling"]},"service_split":{"benefits":["Independent ownership/deployment when truly decoupled"],"costs":["Network and operational complexity"]},"modular_monolith":{"benefits":["Explicit boundaries without network cost"],"costs":["Less deployment independence"]}},"decision":"Use decomposition only when the resulting boundary removes more coupling than it introduces.","rationale":"Physical separation is not equivalent to architectural decoupling.","consequences":["Some components should remain co-located","Boundary decisions require workflow-level evidence"],"implementation_implications":["Measure call frequency and dependency direction","Identify shared transaction requirements","Consider modular boundaries before distributed deployment"],"verification_method":"Map representative workflows and calculate dependency, call, and transaction coordination patterns before and after decomposition.","outcome_learning":"Service count is not a valid proxy for architectural quality.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B42-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"qualifies","record_id":"D54-B42-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B42-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Service Boundaries","subtopic":"Data ownership alignment","concept":"A service boundary should normally align with authoritative ownership of the mutable data it governs.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should service boundaries consider data ownership?","answer":"Aligning service responsibility with authoritative mutable data reduces conflicting writes and makes business invariants easier to enforce within a coherent boundary.","context":"Multiple candidate services would otherwise update the same business entity.","objective":"Avoid distributed ownership of mutable state.","requirements":["Authoritative ownership","Controlled write paths","Invariant enforcement"],"constraints":["Some data must be shared read-only","Legacy systems may have overlapping ownership"],"alternatives":["Multiple independent writers","Single authoritative owner","Shared database with informal ownership"],"evidence":"Multiple authoritative writers create ambiguity about conflict resolution and invariant enforcement.","tradeoffs":{"multiple_writers":{"benefits":["Local autonomy"],"costs":["Conflict complexity"]},"single_owner":{"benefits":["Clear authority","Simpler invariants"],"costs":["Cross-boundary access may require contracts"]},"shared_db_informal":{"benefits":["Low initial effort"],"costs":["Hidden coupling"]}},"decision":"Prefer a single authoritative owner for mutable domain data unless multi-writer semantics are an explicit requirement.","rationale":"Ownership clarity provides a foundation for consistency, authorization, and lifecycle management.","consequences":["Consumers may need APIs/events rather than direct writes","Ownership changes require migration planning"],"implementation_implications":["Define write authority","Expose controlled mutation interfaces","Prevent undocumented alternate writers"],"verification_method":"Inventory write paths and verify that each mutable domain datum has an explicit authoritative owner.","outcome_learning":"Data ownership and service boundaries should be designed together.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B31-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B31-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B42-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Service Boundaries","subtopic":"Shared libraries","concept":"Shared libraries should contain stable technical capabilities rather than business state ownership that forces coordinated service releases.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When does a shared library undermine service independence?","answer":"A shared library undermines independence when frequent incompatible changes require multiple services to upgrade together or when it embeds mutable business state and policy that should have an explicit owner.","context":"Several services reuse common functionality through a versioned library.","objective":"Reuse implementation without creating hidden release coupling.","requirements":["Stable API","Controlled versioning","Clear ownership"],"constraints":["Services may release independently","Library changes propagate to consumers"],"alternatives":["Shared business library","Stable technical library","Duplicate small functionality"],"evidence":"Compile-time or package-level dependency can create release coupling even when runtime services are separately deployed.","tradeoffs":{"shared_business_library":{"benefits":["Reuse","Centralized behavior"],"costs":["Release coupling"]},"stable_technical_library":{"benefits":["Reuse with clearer boundaries"],"costs":["Requires disciplined scope"]},"duplication":{"benefits":["Independent evolution"],"costs":["Maintenance divergence"]}},"decision":"Prefer shared libraries for stable technical capabilities and avoid using them as substitutes for service-owned business state or rapidly changing policy.","rationale":"Reuse should not silently become shared ownership.","consequences":["Some business logic remains service-local","Small duplication may occasionally be preferable"],"implementation_implications":["Define library ownership","Version compatibility explicitly","Keep mutable state outside shared libraries"],"verification_method":"Review dependency graphs and release histories for coordinated upgrades caused by shared libraries.","outcome_learning":"Code reuse and architectural ownership are separate concerns.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B42-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Service Boundaries","subtopic":"Boundary evolution","concept":"Service boundaries should be revisable based on observed coupling, ownership changes, and workload characteristics rather than treated as permanent architectural facts.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should service boundaries evolve after deployment?","answer":"Use operational evidence, dependency analysis, ownership changes, and change patterns to reassess boundaries and permit consolidation, splitting, or reassignment when the original assumptions no longer hold.","context":"A deployed service landscape has accumulated new dependencies and responsibilities.","objective":"Keep architectural boundaries aligned with actual system behavior.","requirements":["Dependency visibility","Ownership visibility","Change-history evidence"],"constraints":["Migration itself carries risk","Existing consumers depend on current boundaries"],"alternatives":["Never change boundaries","Refactor opportunistically without evidence","Use evidence-driven boundary evolution"],"evidence":"Architecture assumptions can become invalid as requirements, workloads, and organizational ownership change.","tradeoffs":{"never_change":{"benefits":["Stability"],"costs":["Accumulating coupling"]},"opportunistic":{"benefits":["Fast response"],"costs":["Potentially inconsistent decisions"]},"evidence_driven":{"benefits":["Traceable decisions"],"costs":["Requires measurement"]}},"decision":"Reassess service boundaries using explicit evidence when material architectural assumptions change.","rationale":"Architecture is a set of decisions under assumptions, not an immutable topology.","consequences":["Boundary changes require migration plans","Architecture records must capture changed assumptions"],"implementation_implications":["Track dependencies and ownership","Record boundary decisions","Plan compatibility during restructuring"],"verification_method":"Compare documented boundary assumptions against dependency and operational data during architecture reviews.","outcome_learning":"Architecture decision records remain useful when they preserve assumptions and consequences for later reassessment.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B42-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B42-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B43-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Synchronous vs Asynchronous Communication","subtopic":"Interaction semantics","concept":"Synchronous communication is appropriate when the caller requires an immediate response and the dependency's availability can be part of the caller's availability requirement.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should synchronous service communication be preferred?","answer":"Prefer synchronous communication when the caller cannot proceed without the dependency's immediate result and the resulting availability and latency coupling are acceptable.","context":"A request requires information or a decision from another service before responding to its caller.","objective":"Choose communication semantics that match workflow requirements.","requirements":["Immediate result requirement","Defined latency budget","Acceptable dependency availability coupling"],"constraints":["Network failure is possible","Dependency latency affects caller latency"],"alternatives":["Synchronous request-response","Asynchronous message","Local replication/read model"],"evidence":"Synchronous calls create direct temporal and availability dependencies between participants.","tradeoffs":{"synchronous":{"benefits":["Simple request-response semantics","Immediate result"],"costs":["Latency and availability coupling"]},"asynchronous":{"benefits":["Decoupling","Buffering"],"costs":["Delayed result and more state management"]},"local_read_model":{"benefits":["Low dependency latency"],"costs":["Staleness and replication complexity"]}},"decision":"Use synchronous communication when immediate dependency results are required and the coupling fits the reliability and latency objectives.","rationale":"Communication style should follow business timing semantics rather than architectural fashion.","consequences":["Caller availability may depend on dependency availability","Timeout and retry behavior become critical"],"implementation_implications":["Set explicit timeout budgets","Define retry policy","Prevent unbounded call chains"],"verification_method":"Measure end-to-end latency and dependency failure behavior against stated service objectives.","outcome_learning":"Synchronous communication is a deliberate coupling decision, not merely a transport choice.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B43-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Synchronous vs Asynchronous Communication","subtopic":"Asynchronous workflows","concept":"Asynchronous communication is appropriate when work can be completed independently of the initiating request and delayed completion is acceptable.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should asynchronous communication be used for a workflow?","answer":"Use asynchronous communication when the initiating actor does not require immediate completion and the workflow benefits from decoupling, buffering, independent processing, or eventual completion.","context":"A request initiates processing that may take longer than the initiating interaction should remain open.","objective":"Separate initiation from completion when timing semantics permit it.","requirements":["Durable work representation","Completion tracking where needed","Retry and failure handling"],"constraints":["Completion is not immediate","Failures may occur after the initiator disconnects"],"alternatives":["Long synchronous request","Asynchronous command","Background processing with polling"],"evidence":"Asynchronous execution separates initiation availability from processing availability but requires explicit state and failure semantics.","tradeoffs":{"long_sync":{"benefits":["Simple caller model"],"costs":["Long-lived connection and availability coupling"]},"async_command":{"benefits":["Decoupling","Retryability"],"costs":["Workflow state required"]},"polling":{"benefits":["Simple client integration"],"costs":["Additional traffic and state complexity"]}},"decision":"Use asynchronous interaction when business semantics permit delayed completion and the architecture can represent work state explicitly.","rationale":"The asynchronous boundary should reflect a genuine temporal separation in the business process.","consequences":["Completion becomes observable state","Retries and duplicate delivery must be handled"],"implementation_implications":["Persist command identity","Track processing state","Define retry and dead-letter behavior where applicable"],"verification_method":"Inject processing delay and failures and verify that initiation remains independent while completion state remains recoverable.","outcome_learning":"Asynchronous architecture requires explicit lifecycle state rather than simply moving work to a background thread.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B43-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"contrasts_with","record_id":"D54-B43-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B43-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Synchronous vs Asynchronous Communication","subtopic":"Timeout and retry coupling","concept":"Retries across synchronous service boundaries must account for cumulative latency, duplicate execution, and downstream load amplification.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"What architectural risks arise when synchronous retries are used across service boundaries?","answer":"Retries can multiply load and extend end-to-end latency, and they can duplicate state-changing operations unless idempotency and bounded retry policies are defined.","context":"A service retries failed dependency requests to improve transient-failure tolerance.","objective":"Improve resilience without creating retry storms or duplicate effects.","requirements":["Bounded retry policy","Timeout budget","Idempotency where needed","Load protection"],"constraints":["Dependency may already be overloaded","Failure may be ambiguous"],"alternatives":["No retries","Unbounded retries","Bounded retries with backoff and idempotency"],"evidence":"Retrying a failing dependency increases request volume and can propagate overload through a call graph.","tradeoffs":{"no_retry":{"benefits":["No amplification"],"costs":["Lower transient-failure tolerance"]},"unbounded":{"benefits":["Persistent retry effort"],"costs":["Potential retry storm"]},"bounded":{"benefits":["Controlled resilience"],"costs":["Policy complexity"]}},"decision":"Use bounded, policy-driven retries with total latency budgets and idempotency safeguards where duplicate effects are possible.","rationale":"Resilience mechanisms must not convert dependency failure into systemic overload.","consequences":["Some transient failures will still surface","Retry configuration becomes part of architecture governance"],"implementation_implications":["Cap attempts","Use bounded backoff","Respect overall deadline","Classify retryable failures"],"verification_method":"Inject dependency failures and measure request amplification, latency, and duplicate effects under configured retry behavior.","outcome_learning":"Retry policy must be evaluated across the complete dependency path, not at one client in isolation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B41-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B41-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B43-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Synchronous vs Asynchronous Communication","subtopic":"Backpressure","concept":"Asynchronous systems require explicit backpressure or admission-control behavior when producers can generate work faster than consumers can safely process it.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why must asynchronous architectures define backpressure?","answer":"Without backpressure, durable queues, memory buffers, or downstream workers can accumulate unbounded work and transform producer bursts into resource exhaustion or unacceptable latency.","context":"A producer can submit work at a rate that exceeds consumer processing capacity.","objective":"Keep workload growth within sustainable processing capacity.","requirements":["Capacity limits","Queue monitoring","Admission or throttling behavior"],"constraints":["Producer and consumer rates may differ","Work may have different priorities"],"alternatives":["Unlimited buffering","Producer throttling","Bounded queues with rejection/deferment"],"evidence":"Buffering changes where overload appears but does not eliminate workload exceeding processing capacity.","tradeoffs":{"unlimited_buffer":{"benefits":["Absorbs bursts"],"costs":["Unbounded resource usage"]},"producer_throttle":{"benefits":["Protects consumers"],"costs":["Producer must handle throttling"]},"bounded_queue":{"benefits":["Explicit capacity boundary"],"costs":["Work may be rejected or delayed"]}},"decision":"Define explicit capacity and backpressure behavior for asynchronous workloads.","rationale":"A queue is a buffer, not an unlimited capacity guarantee.","consequences":["Producers need failure/defer handling","Queue capacity becomes an operational constraint"],"implementation_implications":["Set queue limits","Monitor age and depth","Define rejection or throttling semantics","Prioritize critical work where required"],"verification_method":"Generate workload above consumer capacity and verify bounded resource usage and documented producer behavior.","outcome_learning":"Sustainable asynchronous architecture requires explicit overload semantics.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B43-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B43-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B43-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Synchronous vs Asynchronous Communication","subtopic":"Hybrid interaction","concept":"A single workflow may legitimately combine synchronous validation or acceptance with asynchronous execution when the business process has distinct immediate and deferred stages.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When is a hybrid synchronous-asynchronous workflow appropriate?","answer":"Use a hybrid model when the caller needs immediate confirmation that a request is valid and accepted, while the substantive work can complete later.","context":"An operation requires immediate validation but potentially long-running processing.","objective":"Provide responsive acceptance semantics without holding a synchronous connection for long-running work.","requirements":["Immediate validation","Durable accepted-work state","Completion status"],"constraints":["Acceptance is not equivalent to completion","Failure can occur after acceptance"],"alternatives":["Fully synchronous","Fully asynchronous","Synchronous acceptance plus asynchronous execution"],"evidence":"Separating acceptance from completion can align interaction timing with actual workflow stages.","tradeoffs":{"fully_sync":{"benefits":["Simple semantics"],"costs":["Long latency and availability coupling"]},"fully_async":{"benefits":["Strong decoupling"],"costs":["Less immediate validation feedback"]},"hybrid":{"benefits":["Immediate acceptance","Deferred execution"],"costs":["Requires explicit state model"]}},"decision":"Use a hybrid model when acceptance and completion are distinct business states.","rationale":"The architecture should expose the real lifecycle rather than falsely equating request acknowledgement with completion.","consequences":["Clients must understand lifecycle states","State transitions require durable semantics"],"implementation_implications":["Return explicit accepted state","Persist operation identity","Expose completion or status retrieval","Define failure transitions"],"verification_method":"Test accepted, rejected, delayed, and failed executions and verify that acceptance never incorrectly represents successful completion.","outcome_learning":"Separating acceptance from outcome improves correctness for long-running workflows.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B43-R01","D54-B43-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"combines","record_id":"D54-B43-R01"},{"type":"combines","record_id":"D54-B43-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B44-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Event-Driven Architecture","subtopic":"Event semantics","concept":"An event should represent a fact that occurred, while a command represents an instruction to perform an action.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should commands and events be distinguished?","answer":"Commands express requested work and may be accepted or rejected, whereas events communicate facts about completed or recognized state changes. Keeping the semantics distinct prevents consumers from misinterpreting intent as historical fact.","context":"A distributed system communicates state changes and requested operations between components.","objective":"Preserve clear message semantics.","requirements":["Explicit message intent","Clear producer responsibility","Consumer interpretation rules"],"constraints":["Some transports can carry both message types","Poor naming can blur semantics"],"alternatives":["Use one generic message concept","Explicit command and event types","Events for all communication"],"evidence":"Commands and events have different ownership, acknowledgement, retry, and temporal semantics.","tradeoffs":{"generic":{"benefits":["Simple abstraction"],"costs":["Ambiguous semantics"]},"explicit":{"benefits":["Clear intent"],"costs":["More message taxonomy"]},"events_only":{"benefits":["Loose coupling"],"costs":["Poor fit for directed requests"]}},"decision":"Model commands and events as distinct semantic categories even if they share transport infrastructure.","rationale":"Message semantics should remain explicit independently of transport technology.","consequences":["Message contracts need semantic documentation","Consumers must not reinterpret events as commands"],"implementation_implications":["Use explicit naming","Define producer ownership","Document whether messages represent intent or fact"],"verification_method":"Review representative message contracts and classify each as command, event, query, or response without ambiguity.","outcome_learning":"Transport-level similarity does not imply semantic equivalence.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B44-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Event-Driven Architecture","subtopic":"Event ownership","concept":"The producer of an event should own the truth of the fact being represented, while consumers own their derived reactions.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should ownership be assigned in an event-driven architecture?","answer":"The authoritative domain producer should publish facts about state it owns. Consumers should derive local state or actions from those facts without becoming alternate authorities for the producer's data.","context":"Several services consume events describing changes to a business entity.","objective":"Prevent conflicting sources of truth.","requirements":["Explicit producer ownership","Consumer derivation boundaries","Event contract stability"],"constraints":["Consumers may maintain projections","Events can be retained for replay"],"alternatives":["Any service may publish authoritative facts","Single domain owner publishes facts","Shared event ownership"],"evidence":"Clear ownership reduces conflicting interpretations of authoritative state.","tradeoffs":{"shared_ownership":{"benefits":["Distributed autonomy"],"costs":["Ambiguous truth"]},"single_owner":{"benefits":["Clear authority"],"costs":["Requires domain boundary"]},"consumer_as_authority":{"benefits":["Local convenience"],"costs":["Potential divergence"]}},"decision":"Keep authoritative fact ownership with the domain that owns the underlying state and treat consumer state as derived unless explicitly designated otherwise.","rationale":"Event distribution should not create hidden ownership transfers.","consequences":["Consumers must handle stale or missing events appropriately","Producer contracts require lifecycle governance"],"implementation_implications":["Document event ownership","Define projection rebuild strategy","Avoid consumer-side mutation of producer-owned state"],"verification_method":"Trace event fields back to their authoritative owner and verify consumer writes do not silently become alternate sources of truth.","outcome_learning":"Event architecture works best when authority and derivation are explicit.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B31-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B31-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B44-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Event-Driven Architecture","subtopic":"Delivery semantics","concept":"At-least-once delivery requires consumers to tolerate duplicate delivery when the transport does not guarantee exactly-once business effects.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should consumers be designed when duplicate event delivery is possible?","answer":"Consumers should use idempotent processing, deduplication keys, or state-transition checks appropriate to the event semantics rather than assuming each delivery occurs exactly once.","context":"An event infrastructure may redeliver messages after processing failures or ambiguous acknowledgements.","objective":"Prevent duplicate delivery from producing incorrect business effects.","requirements":["Stable event identity","Duplicate-safe processing","Defined acknowledgement semantics"],"constraints":["Consumer may crash after applying an effect","Transport-level delivery does not prove business-level exactly-once effects"],"alternatives":["Assume single delivery","Deduplicate explicitly","Design naturally idempotent state transitions"],"evidence":"Delivery and business-effect semantics are separate properties.","tradeoffs":{"assume_once":{"benefits":["Simple implementation"],"costs":["Duplicate corruption risk"]},"deduplication":{"benefits":["Explicit duplicate control"],"costs":["Deduplication state"]},"natural_idempotency":{"benefits":["Robust semantics"],"costs":["May require domain-specific design"]}},"decision":"Design event consumers for duplicate-safe processing whenever duplicate delivery is possible.","rationale":"Correctness should not depend on an assumption weaker than the actual delivery guarantee.","consequences":["Event identity becomes part of the contract","Consumers require replay-safe behavior"],"implementation_implications":["Persist event identifiers when necessary","Use atomic state transitions where possible","Test duplicate delivery explicitly"],"verification_method":"Deliver the same event multiple times and verify that the resulting business state matches the single logical occurrence.","outcome_learning":"Exactly-once delivery and exactly-once business effects should not be treated as interchangeable claims.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B41-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B41-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B44-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Event-Driven Architecture","subtopic":"Event ordering","concept":"Ordering guarantees should be scoped to the business invariant that actually depends on order rather than globally assumed for every event.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When is event ordering architecturally necessary?","answer":"Ordering is necessary only when consumers cannot correctly process events independently and a business or state-transition invariant depends on sequence. The required ordering scope should then be explicitly defined.","context":"Events from a distributed workflow may arrive in different orders.","objective":"Avoid unnecessary global ordering constraints while preserving required sequence semantics.","requirements":["Identify order-sensitive invariants","Define ordering scope","Handle late events"],"constraints":["Global ordering can reduce scalability","Distributed producers may not share one sequence"],"alternatives":["Global ordering","Partition-scoped ordering","Order-independent consumers"],"evidence":"Ordering guarantees constrain parallelism and are valuable only where state semantics require them.","tradeoffs":{"global":{"benefits":["Simple consumer assumption"],"costs":["Scalability and availability constraints"]},"partitioned":{"benefits":["Localized ordering"],"costs":["More complex key design"]},"independent":{"benefits":["Maximum flexibility"],"costs":["Requires commutative or version-aware processing"]}},"decision":"Define the narrowest ordering guarantee that preserves required business invariants.","rationale":"Ordering is a correctness mechanism where required, but an unnecessary global guarantee creates architectural cost.","consequences":["Consumers must document ordering assumptions","Late or reordered events need defined behavior"],"implementation_implications":["Choose partition/order keys deliberately","Attach sequence or version information when required","Test out-of-order delivery"],"verification_method":"Inject reordered events and verify that documented invariants remain satisfied under the selected ordering guarantee.","outcome_learning":"Ordering should be driven by state semantics rather than transport convenience.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B44-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Event-Driven Architecture","subtopic":"Schema evolution","concept":"Event contracts require explicit evolution rules because retained events may outlive the software version that originally produced them.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why does event retention change schema-evolution requirements?","answer":"Retained events may be replayed by newer consumers or read by historical tooling, so event schemas must have compatibility or transformation rules that preserve the ability to interpret relevant historical records.","context":"Events are retained for replay, audit, analytics, or state reconstruction.","objective":"Preserve usable historical event data across software evolution.","requirements":["Schema lifecycle","Compatibility rules","Migration or transformation strategy"],"constraints":["Old events cannot necessarily be regenerated","Consumers may evolve independently"],"alternatives":["Break schemas freely","Maintain compatibility","Version and transform historical schemas"],"evidence":"Persistence extends the lifetime of a message contract beyond a single deployment.","tradeoffs":{"break_freely":{"benefits":["Fast evolution"],"costs":["Historical replay failure"]},"compatibility":{"benefits":["Simpler replay"],"costs":["Schema constraints"]},"version_transform":{"benefits":["Explicit evolution"],"costs":["Transformation maintenance"]}},"decision":"Treat retained event schemas as durable contracts and define explicit compatibility or transformation rules.","rationale":"Event retention turns transient communication structures into long-lived data contracts.","consequences":["Schema governance becomes part of event architecture","Replay testing becomes important"],"implementation_implications":["Version event schemas","Preserve historical interpretation rules","Test replay across supported versions"],"verification_method":"Replay representative historical events against the current consumer and verify correct interpretation or controlled transformation.","outcome_learning":"Event retention materially increases the lifecycle responsibility of message schemas.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B40-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B40-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B45-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Message Broker Architecture","subtopic":"Broker selection","concept":"A message broker should be selected according to delivery semantics, ordering, retention, throughput, failure handling, and operational requirements rather than generic popularity.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should drive message-broker selection?","answer":"Select a broker based on required delivery semantics, ordering model, retention, throughput, latency, durability, operational capabilities, and integration constraints.","context":"A distributed system needs durable asynchronous communication.","objective":"Select messaging infrastructure that satisfies actual workload and correctness requirements.","requirements":["Defined message semantics","Capacity requirements","Durability requirements","Operational ownership"],"constraints":["Messaging infrastructure adds operational responsibility","Different workloads may need different characteristics"],"alternatives":["Existing platform broker","Dedicated broker","Database-backed queue"],"evidence":"Broker capabilities materially affect delivery, ordering, retention, recovery, and operational behavior.","tradeoffs":{"existing_broker":{"benefits":["Lower platform diversity"],"costs":["May not fit workload"]},"dedicated":{"benefits":["Purpose-fit capabilities"],"costs":["Additional operations"]},"database_queue":{"benefits":["Existing operational model"],"costs":["Potential throughput and semantics limitations"]}},"decision":"Choose messaging infrastructure from explicit workload and correctness requirements rather than technology popularity.","rationale":"The broker is part of the communication semantics and should be evaluated as an architectural dependency.","consequences":["Broker capabilities constrain message architecture","Operational expertise becomes a selection criterion"],"implementation_implications":["Document required guarantees","Test failure and recovery behavior","Define ownership and lifecycle"],"verification_method":"Map workload requirements to broker capabilities and validate critical guarantees through representative tests or authoritative documentation.","outcome_learning":"Messaging technology should be selected as a semantic and operational decision, not only a performance decision.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B44-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B44-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B45-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Message Broker Architecture","subtopic":"Queue versus broadcast","concept":"Point-to-point queues and broadcast or publish-subscribe channels represent different consumption semantics and should be selected according to whether one or many independent consumers need the message.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should a queue be used instead of a publish-subscribe channel?","answer":"Use a queue when work should be distributed among competing consumers, and use publish-subscribe semantics when multiple independent consumer groups should each receive the event.","context":"A producer emits work or facts consumed by downstream components.","objective":"Match message distribution semantics to consumer requirements.","requirements":["Defined consumer topology","Independent consumer-group behavior"],"constraints":["Consumers may scale independently","Some events may have multiple legitimate reactions"],"alternatives":["Single queue","Publish-subscribe","Direct synchronous invocation"],"evidence":"Distribution semantics determine whether consumers compete for messages or independently observe the same logical event.","tradeoffs":{"queue":{"benefits":["Work distribution","Simple scaling"],"costs":["Consumers share delivery"]},"pubsub":{"benefits":["Independent reactions"],"costs":["More delivery state"]},"direct_call":{"benefits":["Immediate response"],"costs":["Tight coupling"]}},"decision":"Use competing-consumer queues for shared work and publish-subscribe semantics for independently owned reactions to the same fact.","rationale":"Message topology should encode intended ownership of processing rather than rely on consumer assumptions.","consequences":["Consumer-group identity becomes architectural metadata","Scaling behavior differs by topology"],"implementation_implications":["Document consumer groups","Define retry and dead-letter semantics per group","Avoid accidental duplicate processing across groups"],"verification_method":"Add multiple consumers and verify whether messages are distributed or independently delivered according to the declared topology.","outcome_learning":"Queue and publish-subscribe semantics are different architectural contracts even when implemented by the same infrastructure.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B45-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Message Broker Architecture","subtopic":"Dead-letter handling","concept":"Messages that cannot be processed successfully should have an explicit failure lifecycle rather than remaining indefinitely invisible or repeatedly consuming processing capacity.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should dead-letter or quarantine behavior be part of message architecture?","answer":"Persistent processing failures require isolation, observability, and controlled recovery so that one malformed or incompatible message does not indefinitely block useful work.","context":"A consumer repeatedly fails on a subset of messages.","objective":"Prevent poison messages from creating unbounded retry loops.","requirements":["Failure classification","Retry limits","Quarantine or dead-letter path","Recovery procedure"],"constraints":["Some failures are transient","Others require data or code correction"],"alternatives":["Retry forever","Drop failed messages","Bound retries and quarantine"],"evidence":"Repeated retries cannot resolve deterministic failures and can consume processing capacity indefinitely.","tradeoffs":{"retry_forever":{"benefits":["No manual intervention initially"],"costs":["Resource exhaustion","Hidden blockage"]},"drop":{"benefits":["Keeps pipeline moving"],"costs":["Data loss"]},"quarantine":{"benefits":["Preserves failed work","Limits repeated impact"],"costs":["Recovery workflow required"]}},"decision":"Use bounded retries followed by an observable quarantine or dead-letter mechanism when message loss is unacceptable.","rationale":"Failure must become explicit state that can be diagnosed and recovered rather than an invisible loop.","consequences":["Operations need replay/recovery tooling","Dead-letter queues require monitoring"],"implementation_implications":["Classify retryable failures","Record failure reason","Protect quarantine from unbounded growth","Provide controlled replay"],"verification_method":"Inject deterministic consumer failures and verify bounded retries, quarantine, observability, and successful controlled replay after correction.","outcome_learning":"Dead-letter handling is part of correctness and recoverability, not merely operations convenience.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B43-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B43-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B45-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Message Broker Architecture","subtopic":"Retention policy","concept":"Message retention should be based on replay, recovery, audit, and downstream consumption requirements rather than retaining messages indefinitely by default.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should message retention be determined?","answer":"Define retention from explicit recovery, replay, compliance, analytical, and consumer-lag requirements, then balance those needs against storage cost and operational lifecycle.","context":"A messaging platform retains messages after initial delivery.","objective":"Choose a retention period and storage policy that satisfies actual recovery and replay needs.","requirements":["Recovery objectives","Replay requirements","Storage lifecycle"],"constraints":["Longer retention increases storage and lifecycle burden","Historical data may require separate governance"],"alternatives":["Minimal retention","Fixed long retention","Requirement-driven retention tiers"],"evidence":"Retention is useful only when consumers or recovery processes have a defined need for historical messages.","tradeoffs":{"minimal":{"benefits":["Lower cost"],"costs":["Limited recovery"]},"long_fixed":{"benefits":["Broad replay window"],"costs":["Storage and governance burden"]},"requirement_driven":{"benefits":["Purpose-fit"],"costs":["More policy complexity"]}},"decision":"Set retention from explicit recovery and replay requirements and separate operational messaging retention from long-term archival needs when appropriate.","rationale":"Messaging systems should not become accidental indefinite data stores.","consequences":["Replay windows become explicit architectural constraints","Archival may require another storage layer"],"implementation_implications":["Document retention rationale","Monitor storage growth","Define archival and deletion policies"],"verification_method":"Verify that retention covers documented recovery/replay scenarios and that expiration behavior is observable and controlled.","outcome_learning":"Retention is a lifecycle decision connecting messaging, recovery, storage, and governance.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B44-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B44-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B45-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Message Broker Architecture","subtopic":"Broker failure","concept":"Messaging architecture must define producer and consumer behavior when the broker is unavailable or degraded.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should happen when the message broker becomes unavailable?","answer":"The architecture should explicitly define whether producers fail, buffer locally, degrade functionality, or use another durable path, and consumers should define restart, replay, and recovery behavior.","context":"A critical workflow depends on a message broker that can become unavailable.","objective":"Prevent broker failure from becoming undefined application behavior.","requirements":["Failure-mode definition","Durability expectations","Recovery procedure"],"constraints":["Local buffering can consume resources","Alternative paths may create duplicate or divergent messages"],"alternatives":["Fail closed","Local durable buffering","Synchronous fallback"],"evidence":"A broker is an infrastructure dependency and its failure changes application availability and durability semantics.","tradeoffs":{"fail_closed":{"benefits":["Clear semantics","No hidden buffering"],"costs":["Reduced availability"]},"local_buffer":{"benefits":["Temporary continuity"],"costs":["Local durability complexity"]},"fallback":{"benefits":["Potential availability"],"costs":["Multiple semantics and duplicate risk"]}},"decision":"Define broker failure behavior explicitly according to workflow criticality and durability requirements; do not introduce implicit fallbacks.","rationale":"Undefined fallback behavior can create duplicate, lost, or inconsistent work.","consequences":["Failure behavior becomes part of the workflow contract","Recovery testing is required"],"implementation_implications":["Document broker outage states","Bound local buffers if used","Reconcile recovered messages","Test restart and replay"],"verification_method":"Simulate broker unavailability and recovery and verify documented behavior, durability, duplicate handling, and recovery completeness.","outcome_learning":"Infrastructure failure semantics must be designed before they are encountered in production.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B45-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B45-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B46-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Workflow Orchestration","subtopic":"Workflow ownership","concept":"Long-running workflows benefit from explicit orchestration when coordination, state, retries, compensation, and visibility cannot be safely inferred from independent service calls.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should workflow orchestration be introduced?","answer":"Introduce explicit orchestration when a business workflow has multiple stateful steps, failure transitions, retries, timeouts, approvals, or compensating actions that need a coherent lifecycle.","context":"A business process spans multiple services and may remain active for an extended period.","objective":"Make workflow state and transitions explicit.","requirements":["State persistence","Step transitions","Failure handling","Visibility"],"constraints":["Workflow state becomes architectural data","Orchestration adds a coordination component"],"alternatives":["Implicit choreography","Explicit orchestration","Single service transaction"],"evidence":"Long-running multi-step workflows require state and failure semantics that are difficult to reason about when distributed implicitly.","tradeoffs":{"choreography":{"benefits":["Loose local coupling"],"costs":["Global workflow harder to understand"]},"orchestration":{"benefits":["Central workflow visibility"],"costs":["Coordinator responsibility"]},"single_transaction":{"benefits":["Strong local consistency"],"costs":["Limited scalability and duration"]}},"decision":"Use explicit orchestration when workflow state, transitions, or recovery need centralized lifecycle visibility.","rationale":"The complexity of the workflow should determine whether explicit coordination is justified.","consequences":["Workflow definitions become governed architecture","Coordinator availability and durability matter"],"implementation_implications":["Persist workflow state","Define deterministic transitions","Record step outcomes","Support restart/recovery"],"verification_method":"Interrupt workflows at each major state transition and verify that state can be recovered without violating transition rules.","outcome_learning":"Explicit workflow state reduces ambiguity in long-running distributed processes.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B46-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Workflow Orchestration","subtopic":"Choreography versus orchestration","concept":"Choreography can fit loosely coupled event reactions, while orchestration is preferable when one workflow has explicit sequencing and centralized business coordination.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should choreography and orchestration be distinguished architecturally?","answer":"Use choreography when independent participants react to facts without requiring a central coordinator. Use orchestration when the workflow requires explicit sequencing, decision points, deadlines, compensation, or centralized visibility.","context":"Multiple services participate in a multi-step business process.","objective":"Choose a coordination model that matches workflow semantics.","requirements":["Workflow ownership","Failure semantics","Visibility requirements"],"constraints":["Both approaches can create distributed complexity","Complex choreography may become difficult to trace"],"alternatives":["Choreography","Orchestration","Hybrid coordination"],"evidence":"The two approaches distribute coordination responsibility differently.","tradeoffs":{"choreography":{"benefits":["Participant autonomy"],"costs":["Global flow may be implicit"]},"orchestration":{"benefits":["Explicit flow"],"costs":["Coordinator dependency"]},"hybrid":{"benefits":["Context-specific fit"],"costs":["More architectural patterns"]}},"decision":"Select choreography or orchestration based on the required visibility, sequencing, and ownership semantics of each workflow.","rationale":"Neither pattern is universally appropriate; workflow semantics determine the coordination model.","consequences":["Different workflows may legitimately use different patterns","Architecture documentation must make coordination ownership explicit"],"implementation_implications":["Document workflow state machine","Identify coordinator ownership when present","Define event contracts for choreographed flows"],"verification_method":"Trace representative workflows from initiation to completion and verify that coordination responsibility is unambiguous.","outcome_learning":"Coordination should be explicit even when no dedicated orchestrator exists.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B46-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"contrasts_with","record_id":"D54-B46-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B46-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Workflow Orchestration","subtopic":"Compensation","concept":"Distributed workflows often require compensating actions rather than assuming that later failure can be handled by rolling back earlier independent commits.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should a workflow use compensation instead of rollback?","answer":"Use compensation when steps commit independently and cannot participate in one atomic transaction. Compensation should define what corrective business action is possible and what residual states require manual handling.","context":"A multi-service workflow performs irreversible or independently committed actions.","objective":"Define recovery semantics for partial workflow failure.","requirements":["Step outcome tracking","Compensation semantics","Manual exception path"],"constraints":["Some actions are irreversible","Compensation may not perfectly undo prior effects"],"alternatives":["Distributed transaction","Compensating workflow","Manual recovery only"],"evidence":"Independent commits cannot generally be undone through a single local rollback boundary.","tradeoffs":{"distributed_tx":{"benefits":["Atomic semantics where supported"],"costs":["Coordination complexity"]},"compensation":{"benefits":["Works across independent boundaries"],"costs":["May not restore exact prior state"]},"manual_only":{"benefits":["Simple automation"],"costs":["Slow and error-prone recovery"]}},"decision":"Use explicit compensation where business recovery semantics support corrective actions and document non-compensable states.","rationale":"Compensation is a business recovery mechanism, not a claim of transactional rollback.","consequences":["Workflow state must retain enough information for recovery","Some failures may require human intervention"],"implementation_implications":["Define compensation per step","Record completed actions","Make compensation idempotent where possible","Escalate non-compensable states"],"verification_method":"Inject failure after each compensable step and verify correct compensation or explicit escalation.","outcome_learning":"A compensation action should be evaluated by its business effect, not by whether it technically reverses every underlying operation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B34-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B34-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B46-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Workflow Orchestration","subtopic":"Workflow timeout","concept":"Long-running workflows require explicit timeout and expiration semantics so that stalled state does not remain indefinitely active.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should workflow timeouts be modeled explicitly?","answer":"Explicit timeouts prevent indefinite active states and provide deterministic transitions for stalled, abandoned, or externally blocked work.","context":"A workflow can remain waiting for an external dependency or human action.","objective":"Bound the lifetime of workflow states and define recovery behavior.","requirements":["Timeout policy","Expiration state","Recovery or escalation action"],"constraints":["Some business processes legitimately take a long time","Fixed timeouts may not fit all states"],"alternatives":["No timeout","Global fixed timeout","State-specific timeout"],"evidence":"A workflow without defined expiration can accumulate unresolved state and obscure operational health.","tradeoffs":{"none":{"benefits":["No premature expiration"],"costs":["Unbounded active state"]},"global_fixed":{"benefits":["Simple policy"],"costs":["Poor fit for varied workflows"]},"state_specific":{"benefits":["Business-aligned"],"costs":["More configuration"]}},"decision":"Define state-specific timeout or escalation semantics where indefinite waiting is not valid.","rationale":"Workflow lifetime is part of correctness and operability.","consequences":["Expired workflows need recovery paths","Timeout values become business-policy inputs"],"implementation_implications":["Persist deadlines","Emit timeout transitions","Distinguish timeout from failure","Support controlled recovery"],"verification_method":"Pause workflows beyond their defined deadlines and verify deterministic state transition and recovery behavior.","outcome_learning":"Timeout is a workflow state transition, not merely an infrastructure request timeout.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B46-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B46-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B46-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Workflow Orchestration","subtopic":"Human approval steps","concept":"Human approval should be represented as an explicit workflow state when authorization or governance requires a human decision before execution.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should human approval fit into an automated workflow?","answer":"Represent approval as a durable state with explicit authority, pending status, decision evidence, expiration, and transition rules rather than treating approval as an informal external interaction.","context":"An automated process must pause for an authorized human decision before a consequential action.","objective":"Make human governance auditable and recoverable.","requirements":["Explicit approver authority","Decision record","Workflow state","Expiration or escalation"],"constraints":["Approvers may be unavailable","Approval does not itself guarantee execution success"],"alternatives":["Out-of-band approval","Embedded workflow approval state","Automatic execution"],"evidence":"Governed actions require traceable authority and decision state when human approval is a prerequisite.","tradeoffs":{"out_of_band":{"benefits":["Simple workflow implementation"],"costs":["Weak traceability"]},"embedded":{"benefits":["Auditable lifecycle"],"costs":["Workflow complexity"]},"automatic":{"benefits":["High automation"],"costs":["May violate governance requirements"]}},"decision":"Model required human approval as an explicit workflow state with authoritative decision evidence.","rationale":"Governance requirements should be represented in the system state rather than inferred from informal communication.","consequences":["Approval state becomes part of workflow persistence","Authorization policy must define eligible approvers"],"implementation_implications":["Record approver identity and decision","Prevent unauthorized transitions","Define approval expiration","Separate approval from execution outcome"],"verification_method":"Test unauthorized approval, expired approval, approved execution, and rejected execution paths and verify complete state transitions.","outcome_learning":"Approval is an authorization event, not evidence that the subsequent operation succeeded.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B46-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B46-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B47-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Failure model","concept":"Distributed architecture decisions should begin with an explicit failure model because network communication introduces partial failure that local-process designs may not encounter.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should a distributed system document its failure model?","answer":"Because components can fail independently, messages can be delayed or duplicated, and network partitions can separate participants, the architecture must define which failures are tolerated and how state remains recoverable.","context":"An application is decomposed across independent processes or hosts.","objective":"Make distributed failure assumptions explicit.","requirements":["Failure taxonomy","Recovery semantics","Timeout behavior","State consistency rules"],"constraints":["Failures can be partial","Failure detection may be uncertain"],"alternatives":["Assume reliable network","Explicit failure model","Centralize components"],"evidence":"Independent components create failure modes not represented by ordinary in-process control flow.","tradeoffs":{"reliable_assumption":{"benefits":["Simple design"],"costs":["Incorrect failure behavior"]},"explicit_model":{"benefits":["Predictable recovery"],"costs":["Design complexity"]},"centralize":{"benefits":["Fewer distributed boundaries"],"costs":["Reduced independent scaling/deployment"]}},"decision":"Document and design against an explicit distributed failure model before relying on cross-process coordination.","rationale":"Recovery behavior cannot be validated without defining what failures are expected and what outcomes are acceptable.","consequences":["Timeouts and retries become architectural concerns","Failure injection becomes valuable verification"],"implementation_implications":["Classify dependency failures","Define state transitions under partial failure","Test delayed, duplicated, and unavailable dependencies"],"verification_method":"Perform failure-mode analysis and inject representative distributed failures against critical workflows.","outcome_learning":"Distributed reliability begins with explicit assumptions about partial failure.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B47-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Timeout semantics","concept":"Timeouts detect that a response was not observed within a bound; they do not prove that the remote operation did not execute.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why must timeout semantics distinguish lack of response from operation failure?","answer":"A timeout may occur after the remote operation has already committed its effect but before the response reaches the caller. Recovery therefore requires idempotency, status inspection, reconciliation, or another mechanism appropriate to the operation.","context":"A client times out while invoking a remote state-changing operation.","objective":"Prevent incorrect recovery actions after ambiguous remote execution.","requirements":["Operation identity","Outcome inspection where possible","Duplicate-safe retry or reconciliation"],"constraints":["Network failure can hide completed operations","The remote system may be unavailable during recovery"],"alternatives":["Retry blindly","Assume failure","Use operation identity and reconciliation"],"evidence":"Response delivery and remote execution are separate failure points.","tradeoffs":{"blind_retry":{"benefits":["Simple"],"costs":["Duplicate effects"]},"assume_failure":{"benefits":["No duplicate"],"costs":["May leave completed work misclassified"]},"reconcile":{"benefits":["Correct ambiguity handling"],"costs":["Requires state model"]}},"decision":"Treat remote timeouts as ambiguous outcomes unless the protocol provides stronger semantics.","rationale":"The absence of a response is not proof of absence of execution.","consequences":["Operation status may need to be queryable","Retries require idempotency or reconciliation"],"implementation_implications":["Assign operation identifiers","Expose status where appropriate","Define recovery transitions"],"verification_method":"Force response loss after remote execution and verify that recovery does not incorrectly duplicate or erase the completed operation.","outcome_learning":"Timeout is an observation about communication, not a definitive business outcome.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B41-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B41-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B47-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed locking","concept":"Distributed locks should be introduced only when the protected invariant truly requires coordination across independent participants and when lock failure semantics are explicitly defined.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When is a distributed lock justified?","answer":"A distributed lock may be justified when concurrent actors must coordinate around a shared invariant that cannot be enforced through ownership, idempotency, partitioning, or atomic state transitions. Lease expiry and lock-holder failure must be part of the design.","context":"Multiple independent workers may execute conflicting operations on shared resources.","objective":"Prevent unsafe concurrent actions without creating an unbounded coordination dependency.","requirements":["Explicit invariant","Lock ownership semantics","Failure and expiry behavior"],"constraints":["Lock holders can fail","Network partitions can affect lock visibility"],"alternatives":["Distributed lock","Single owner","Atomic state transition","Partitioned work"],"evidence":"Coordination primitives introduce their own failure and liveness semantics.","tradeoffs":{"lock":{"benefits":["Explicit mutual exclusion"],"costs":["Coordination failure complexity"]},"single_owner":{"benefits":["Simpler authority"],"costs":["Potential bottleneck"]},"atomic_state":{"benefits":["Often simpler"],"costs":["Requires suitable storage semantics"]}},"decision":"Prefer ownership or atomic state transitions where they satisfy the invariant; use distributed locking only when necessary and with explicit lease/failure semantics.","rationale":"Avoiding coordination is often simpler than making distributed coordination correct.","consequences":["Locking introduces availability and recovery considerations","Stale ownership must be addressed"],"implementation_implications":["Define lock scope","Define lease duration","Handle holder failure","Prevent lock misuse as a generic synchronization mechanism"],"verification_method":"Inject holder failure, delayed communication, and concurrent requests and verify invariant preservation and recovery.","outcome_learning":"Distributed locks are architectural coordination mechanisms, not generic substitutes for domain ownership.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B31-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"contrasts_with","record_id":"D54-B31-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B47-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Coordination minimization","concept":"Reducing cross-node coordination can improve resilience and scalability when business invariants can instead be enforced through ownership, partitioning, idempotency, or asynchronous convergence.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should unnecessary distributed coordination be avoided?","answer":"Every coordination dependency introduces latency, failure coupling, and additional state semantics. Where correctness permits, local ownership and asynchronous convergence can reduce those dependencies.","context":"A system currently coordinates multiple services for routine operations.","objective":"Reduce distributed coupling without weakening required invariants.","requirements":["Invariant analysis","Ownership model","Acceptable consistency semantics"],"constraints":["Some invariants genuinely require coordination","Removing coordination can increase staleness"],"alternatives":["Central coordination","Distributed coordination","Partitioned ownership and convergence"],"evidence":"Coordination requires participants to remain mutually reachable and agree on shared state transitions.","tradeoffs":{"central":{"benefits":["Explicit control"],"costs":["Central bottleneck"]},"distributed":{"benefits":["Shared control"],"costs":["Coordination complexity"]},"partitioned":{"benefits":["Lower coordination"],"costs":["Eventual convergence or constrained access"]}},"decision":"Minimize distributed coordination when equivalent correctness can be achieved through ownership, partitioning, idempotency, or controlled convergence.","rationale":"Less coordination generally means fewer coupled failure paths, provided invariants remain protected.","consequences":["Some reads may become eventually consistent","Ownership boundaries become more important"],"implementation_implications":["Identify coordination hotspots","Move invariants into authoritative boundaries","Use asynchronous propagation where acceptable"],"verification_method":"Compare invariant behavior and failure modes before and after removing coordination from representative workflows.","outcome_learning":"Coordination reduction is valuable only when correctness remains explicit and testable.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B47-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B47-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B47-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Partial failure recovery","concept":"Recovery logic should distinguish failed, unknown, pending, and successfully verified outcomes instead of collapsing all communication failures into one error state.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should distributed workflows model ambiguous outcomes explicitly?","answer":"A distributed operation may be completed remotely even when its response is lost, or remain pending while connectivity fails. Explicit outcome states prevent unsafe retries and incorrect business conclusions.","context":"A distributed workflow loses communication during a state-changing operation.","objective":"Preserve truthful knowledge of remote execution state.","requirements":["Explicit outcome states","Operation identity","Recovery/reconciliation path"],"constraints":["Remote state may temporarily be inaccessible","Some outcomes cannot be determined immediately"],"alternatives":["Binary success/failure","Pending/unknown states","Explicit outcome verification"],"evidence":"Distributed communication failures can hide the true state of a remote operation.","tradeoffs":{"binary":{"benefits":["Simple model"],"costs":["Incorrect conclusions"]},"pending_unknown":{"benefits":["Truthful uncertainty"],"costs":["More state handling"]},"verification":{"benefits":["Higher confidence"],"costs":["Requires query or evidence path"]}},"decision":"Represent ambiguous distributed outcomes explicitly and resolve them through verification or reconciliation where possible.","rationale":"Unknown is a legitimate system state when evidence is insufficient to establish success or failure.","consequences":["Workflow state models become richer","Recovery tooling must support reconciliation"],"implementation_implications":["Separate execution status from verification status","Persist evidence of observed outcomes","Avoid treating timeout as failure automatically"],"verification_method":"Simulate lost responses and verify that the system preserves an unknown or pending state until sufficient evidence resolves it.","outcome_learning":"Truthful uncertainty is safer than false success or false failure in distributed workflows.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B47-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B47-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B48-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"Deployment Architecture","subtopic":"Deployment independence","concept":"Independent deployment should be granted only where service boundaries, compatibility contracts, and operational ownership are mature enough to support independent change.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When does independent deployment provide meaningful architectural value?","answer":"Independent deployment is valuable when components have sufficiently stable contracts, independent ownership, and limited coordination requirements so that one component can change without requiring synchronized release of others.","context":"A system is being decomposed to allow services to deploy independently.","objective":"Ensure deployment independence reflects genuine architectural independence.","requirements":["Stable contracts","Independent ownership","Compatibility testing"],"constraints":["Shared schemas or synchronized workflows may prevent true independence"],"alternatives":["Synchronized deployment","Independent deployment","Modular monolith deployment"],"evidence":"Deployment separation does not remove runtime or contract coupling by itself.","tradeoffs":{"synchronized":{"benefits":["Simple compatibility"],"costs":["Release coupling"]},"independent":{"benefits":["Faster localized change"],"costs":["Contract governance"]},"modular_monolith":{"benefits":["Simple runtime"],"costs":["Shared deployment unit"]}},"decision":"Use independent deployment where contracts and ownership support it; do not treat separate deployment units as proof of decoupling.","rationale":"Deployment independence is an outcome of architectural boundaries, not merely a packaging choice.","consequences":["Compatibility testing becomes essential","Operational release coordination changes"],"implementation_implications":["Version contracts","Automate deployment validation","Track dependency compatibility"],"verification_method":"Deploy one component independently while keeping supported consumers unchanged and verify compatibility across representative workflows.","outcome_learning":"Independent deployment should be validated by independent change capability, not by service count.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B42-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B42-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B48-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"Deployment Architecture","subtopic":"Deployment strategy","concept":"Deployment strategy should match application risk, rollback capability, state compatibility, and traffic characteristics rather than using one rollout method universally.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should a deployment strategy be selected?","answer":"Select rollout strategy based on blast radius, rollback feasibility, data compatibility, traffic control, and validation requirements. Options may include rolling, blue-green, canary, or controlled batch deployment depending on system characteristics.","context":"A production application requires a controlled release strategy.","objective":"Reduce deployment risk while preserving operational continuity.","requirements":["Rollback or recovery plan","Health validation","Traffic control where required"],"constraints":["Stateful systems may complicate rollback","Some changes are irreversible"],"alternatives":["Rolling deployment","Blue-green deployment","Canary deployment","Controlled batch deployment"],"evidence":"Deployment strategies trade resource duplication, exposure scope, rollback complexity, and validation granularity.","tradeoffs":{"rolling":{"benefits":["Resource efficient"],"costs":["Mixed versions during rollout"]},"blue_green":{"benefits":["Fast traffic switch"],"costs":["Higher resource requirement"]},"canary":{"benefits":["Limited exposure"],"costs":["Traffic analysis complexity"]}},"decision":"Choose rollout strategy from application state compatibility, failure impact, and available traffic-control mechanisms.","rationale":"The deployment mechanism must fit the failure and rollback model of the software being changed.","consequences":["Deployment process becomes architecture-dependent","Observability must support release validation"],"implementation_implications":["Define health criteria","Validate compatibility during mixed-version periods","Automate rollback or traffic reversal where feasible"],"verification_method":"Execute controlled rollout tests including detected regression and verify that the documented recovery path works.","outcome_learning":"Deployment safety is a system property involving software, state, traffic, and observability.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B40-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B40-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B48-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"Deployment Architecture","subtopic":"Immutable versus mutable deployment","concept":"Immutable deployment artifacts reduce configuration drift when the runtime environment can be recreated from versioned inputs.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should deployment artifacts be treated as immutable?","answer":"Prefer immutable versioned artifacts when reproducibility, rollback, auditability, and environment consistency are important and the platform can recreate the required runtime from declared inputs.","context":"A service is deployed repeatedly across environments.","objective":"Reduce deployment drift and improve reproducibility.","requirements":["Versioned artifact","Reproducible build","Environment configuration separation"],"constraints":["Some environments require mutable operational configuration","External state cannot necessarily be packaged"],"alternatives":["Mutable in-place deployment","Immutable artifact replacement","Hybrid model"],"evidence":"Recreating the same artifact reduces variation caused by manual mutation after deployment.","tradeoffs":{"mutable":{"benefits":["Operational flexibility"],"costs":["Configuration drift"]},"immutable":{"benefits":["Reproducibility","Rollback clarity"],"costs":["Requires replacement deployment"]},"hybrid":{"benefits":["Flexibility with controlled immutability"],"costs":["Boundary complexity"]}},"decision":"Prefer immutable application artifacts while keeping environment-specific configuration and external state separately governed.","rationale":"Separating artifact identity from runtime configuration improves traceability without requiring all state to be embedded in the artifact.","consequences":["Operational fixes should become versioned changes where possible","Rollback requires compatible external state"],"implementation_implications":["Record artifact versions","Automate builds","Avoid manual production mutation","Manage configuration separately"],"verification_method":"Deploy the same artifact to multiple environments and verify reproducibility and identifiable artifact provenance.","outcome_learning":"Immutability is most effective when artifact, configuration, and external state have explicit boundaries.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B48-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"Deployment Architecture","subtopic":"Configuration management","concept":"Environment-specific configuration should be externalized from application artifacts while preserving versioned, validated, and auditable configuration state.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should deployment configuration be separated from application artifacts?","answer":"Keep environment-specific values outside the application artifact, but treat configuration as governed deployment input with validation, versioning, access control, and auditability.","context":"The same application artifact is deployed across multiple environments.","objective":"Avoid rebuilding application artifacts solely for environment-specific configuration.","requirements":["Configuration validation","Secret protection","Version/audit capability"],"constraints":["Secrets require restricted handling","Incorrect configuration can be as harmful as incorrect code"],"alternatives":["Embed configuration in artifacts","Externalize ungoverned files","Externalize governed configuration"],"evidence":"Configuration changes can alter application behavior and therefore require controlled lifecycle management.","tradeoffs":{"embedded":{"benefits":["Simple packaging"],"costs":["Environment coupling"]},"ungoverned_external":{"benefits":["Flexible"],"costs":["Drift and audit gaps"]},"governed_external":{"benefits":["Reusable artifacts","Controlled changes"],"costs":["Configuration platform/process"]}},"decision":"Externalize environment-specific configuration and govern it as versioned deployment input with appropriate secret controls.","rationale":"Configuration is part of system behavior and must be managed accordingly.","consequences":["Configuration changes require validation","Deployment provenance must include configuration identity"],"implementation_implications":["Validate schemas","Separate secrets from non-secret settings","Record configuration versions","Prevent unauthorized mutation"],"verification_method":"Deploy identical artifacts with controlled configuration variations and verify deterministic, expected behavior and configuration provenance.","outcome_learning":"Configuration should be treated as code-adjacent operational state rather than an untracked deployment detail.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B48-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B48-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B48-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"Deployment Architecture","subtopic":"Rollback and forward recovery","concept":"Rollback is not universally safe when a deployment changes persistent data or irreversible external effects; forward-compatible recovery may be required instead.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should deployment recovery use forward correction instead of rollback?","answer":"Use forward recovery when persistent schema changes, external side effects, or irreversible operations make reverting application code insufficient or unsafe. Rollback should be validated against the resulting data and contract state.","context":"A production release introduces a defect after persistent state changes have occurred.","objective":"Recover service behavior without corrupting evolved state.","requirements":["Recovery strategy","Data compatibility analysis","State-aware rollback decision"],"constraints":["Some data migrations are irreversible","External side effects cannot be undone automatically"],"alternatives":["Application rollback","Data rollback","Forward fix"],"evidence":"Code version and persistent state can evolve independently, making naive rollback unsafe.","tradeoffs":{"code_rollback":{"benefits":["Fast when compatible"],"costs":["May conflict with new data state"]},"data_rollback":{"benefits":["Restores prior state where feasible"],"costs":["Potential data loss and complexity"]},"forward_fix":{"benefits":["Preserves evolved state"],"costs":["Requires corrective release"]}},"decision":"Select rollback or forward recovery based on compatibility between deployed code, persistent state, and external effects.","rationale":"Recovery must preserve system correctness rather than merely restore an earlier binary version.","consequences":["Deployment planning must include data compatibility","Forward fixes may be the only safe recovery path"],"implementation_implications":["Design migrations for compatibility","Test recovery after partial rollout","Document irreversible transitions"],"verification_method":"Simulate failed deployment after state migration and verify that the selected recovery path preserves data integrity and supported contracts.","outcome_learning":"Rollback capability must be evaluated against state evolution, not assumed from artifact versioning alone.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B40-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B40-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B49-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Infrastructure and Runtime Architecture","subtopic":"Runtime placement","concept":"Runtime placement should be selected according to workload characteristics, availability requirements, isolation needs, operational ownership, and cost constraints.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should determine where an application component runs?","answer":"Choose runtime placement from workload behavior, resource requirements, isolation, latency, availability, compliance, operational capability, and lifecycle constraints rather than defaulting to a specific infrastructure model.","context":"A workload can potentially run on shared infrastructure, dedicated hosts, containers, or another managed runtime.","objective":"Select an operational environment that satisfies actual system constraints.","requirements":["Workload characterization","Security and isolation requirements","Operational capability"],"constraints":["Infrastructure choices impose lifecycle and operational responsibilities","Requirements may vary by workload"],"alternatives":["Shared runtime","Dedicated runtime","Managed platform"],"evidence":"Runtime architecture affects isolation, deployment, scaling, observability, and operational responsibility.","tradeoffs":{"shared":{"benefits":["Resource efficiency"],"costs":["Potential contention"]},"dedicated":{"benefits":["Isolation and predictability"],"costs":["Higher resource responsibility"]},"managed":{"benefits":["Reduced infrastructure management"],"costs":["Provider constraints"]}},"decision":"Select runtime placement from measurable workload and operational requirements rather than infrastructure preference.","rationale":"Runtime is an implementation of architectural constraints, not an independent objective.","consequences":["Infrastructure selection becomes workload-specific","Operational skills and ownership affect feasibility"],"implementation_implications":["Document resource profile","Define isolation boundary","Map operational responsibilities","Test runtime-specific behavior"],"verification_method":"Validate resource, latency, isolation, and operational requirements in the target runtime environment.","outcome_learning":"Runtime decisions should be traceable to workload and ownership constraints.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B49-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Infrastructure and Runtime Architecture","subtopic":"Resource isolation","concept":"Resource isolation should be proportional to the consequences of contention, security boundaries, and workload interference.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When is stronger runtime isolation justified?","answer":"Stronger isolation is justified when workloads have materially different security, reliability, performance, or trust requirements and contention could cause unacceptable impact.","context":"Multiple workloads could share infrastructure but have different risk and resource profiles.","objective":"Balance isolation strength against operational and resource cost.","requirements":["Threat model","Resource profile","Failure-impact analysis"],"constraints":["Strong isolation may increase cost and operational complexity","Weak isolation can allow noisy-neighbor effects"],"alternatives":["Shared process","Shared host with isolation","Dedicated runtime"],"evidence":"Isolation boundaries determine the degree to which workload failures or resource contention can propagate.","tradeoffs":{"shared_process":{"benefits":["Low overhead"],"costs":["Weak isolation"]},"shared_host":{"benefits":["Balanced efficiency"],"costs":["Residual contention"]},"dedicated":{"benefits":["Strong separation"],"costs":["Higher cost"]}},"decision":"Choose the minimum isolation boundary that satisfies security, reliability, and resource-containment requirements, increasing isolation where consequences justify it.","rationale":"Isolation should be risk-driven rather than universally maximized.","consequences":["Higher-risk workloads may require separate placement","Capacity planning must include isolation overhead"],"implementation_implications":["Define trust zones","Set resource limits","Monitor contention","Test failure containment"],"verification_method":"Induce resource pressure or workload failure and verify that defined isolation boundaries prevent unacceptable propagation.","outcome_learning":"Isolation is an architectural control whose strength should correspond to consequence and threat.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B49-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Infrastructure and Runtime Architecture","subtopic":"Resource limits","concept":"Explicit resource limits can protect shared infrastructure from unbounded workload consumption, but limits must be aligned with legitimate workload requirements.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should runtime resource limits be part of architecture?","answer":"Resource limits create explicit containment boundaries for CPU, memory, concurrency, storage, or other resources. They should be based on workload requirements and failure behavior rather than arbitrary values.","context":"A workload runs in an environment shared with other workloads.","objective":"Prevent uncontrolled resource consumption from destabilizing the environment.","requirements":["Workload baseline","Capacity model","Limit and overload behavior"],"constraints":["Too-low limits cause false failures","Too-high limits weaken containment"],"alternatives":["No limits","Fixed arbitrary limits","Measured and tested limits"],"evidence":"Shared resources can be exhausted by workloads whose demand exceeds expected capacity.","tradeoffs":{"none":{"benefits":["Maximum flexibility"],"costs":["Poor containment"]},"arbitrary":{"benefits":["Simple"],"costs":["Incorrect thresholds"]},"measured":{"benefits":["Evidence-based containment"],"costs":["Requires measurement"]}},"decision":"Use measured resource limits with explicit overload behavior where resource contention could threaten system stability.","rationale":"Resource constraints are part of predictable runtime behavior.","consequences":["Workloads may reject or degrade under pressure","Capacity planning becomes linked to runtime configuration"],"implementation_implications":["Measure normal and peak demand","Define graceful degradation","Monitor limit breaches","Test exhaustion behavior"],"verification_method":"Generate controlled resource pressure and verify bounded behavior, observability, and expected degradation or rejection.","outcome_learning":"A resource limit is useful only when its failure semantics are designed and observable.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B16-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B16-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B49-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Infrastructure and Runtime Architecture","subtopic":"Runtime observability","concept":"Runtime architecture should expose sufficient health, resource, dependency, and lifecycle signals to distinguish application failure from infrastructure failure.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What observability should runtime architecture provide?","answer":"Expose signals that allow operators to distinguish process health, resource pressure, dependency failures, startup readiness, and degraded behavior according to the system's operational objectives.","context":"A service may fail because of its own logic, resource exhaustion, or infrastructure dependencies.","objective":"Make runtime failure modes diagnosable.","requirements":["Health semantics","Resource visibility","Dependency visibility","Lifecycle signals"],"constraints":["Excess telemetry can create cost and noise","Health checks must represent meaningful states"],"alternatives":["Basic process health","Comprehensive runtime signals","Application-only telemetry"],"evidence":"Operational diagnosis requires distinguishing failure domains rather than observing only process existence.","tradeoffs":{"basic":{"benefits":["Low complexity"],"costs":["Poor diagnosis"]},"comprehensive":{"benefits":["Better failure localization"],"costs":["Telemetry overhead"]},"app_only":{"benefits":["Application focus"],"costs":["Infrastructure blind spots"]}},"decision":"Design runtime observability around meaningful failure domains and operational decisions rather than raw metric volume.","rationale":"Observability should reduce uncertainty about system state.","consequences":["Runtime interfaces include operational semantics","Health checks require careful definition"],"implementation_implications":["Separate liveness from readiness","Expose resource pressure","Track dependency health","Correlate deployment version with runtime behavior"],"verification_method":"Induce process, dependency, and resource failures and verify that available signals distinguish the resulting states.","outcome_learning":"Operational observability is an architectural capability, not merely a monitoring add-on.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B20-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B20-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B49-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Infrastructure and Runtime Architecture","subtopic":"Stateful runtime placement","concept":"Stateful workloads require infrastructure decisions that account for persistent state durability, recovery, identity, and placement constraints rather than treating them like interchangeable stateless replicas.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why does statefulness change runtime architecture?","answer":"Persistent or locally significant state creates recovery, placement, identity, and data durability requirements that can constrain scaling and replacement strategies.","context":"An application stores operational state associated with a particular runtime instance or attached persistent storage.","objective":"Ensure runtime lifecycle operations preserve required state semantics.","requirements":["State ownership","Durability model","Recovery strategy","Placement requirements"],"constraints":["Instances may be replaced","Storage may have different failure domains"],"alternatives":["Stateless redesign","Stateful runtime","Externalize state"],"evidence":"Instance-local or attached persistent state couples runtime lifecycle to data lifecycle.","tradeoffs":{"stateless_redesign":{"benefits":["Flexible scaling"],"costs":["May require architecture changes"]},"stateful":{"benefits":["Direct state locality"],"costs":["Placement and recovery complexity"]},"externalize":{"benefits":["Independent compute lifecycle"],"costs":["External dependency"]}},"decision":"Prefer externalized authoritative state when feasible; otherwise explicitly model stateful placement and recovery requirements.","rationale":"Separating compute lifecycle from durable state generally simplifies replacement and scaling, but workload semantics determine feasibility.","consequences":["Externalized state adds dependency","Stateful placement requires stronger recovery planning"],"implementation_implications":["Document state location","Test replacement and recovery","Separate durable identity from ephemeral runtime identity"],"verification_method":"Replace or restart runtime instances and verify that required state remains correct and recoverable according to the defined durability model.","outcome_learning":"Runtime replacement is only safe when state lifecycle is explicitly understood.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B36-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B36-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B50-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"External Dependencies and Vendor Architecture","subtopic":"Dependency selection","concept":"External dependencies should be selected from functional fit, reliability, security, lifecycle, integration, exit, and total ownership requirements rather than feature count alone.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an external technology or service dependency be evaluated?","answer":"Evaluate the dependency against required capabilities, reliability, security, compatibility, operational ownership, lifecycle stability, migration cost, and exit options.","context":"A system may adopt an external service or technology for a capability that could also be implemented internally.","objective":"Make external dependency decisions traceable to architectural requirements.","requirements":["Capability fit","Security assessment","Reliability assessment","Lifecycle analysis","Exit considerations"],"constraints":["External services create dependency and contractual risk","Internal implementation has its own cost"],"alternatives":["Build internally","Adopt external dependency","Use existing platform capability"],"evidence":"External dependencies transfer some implementation responsibility while introducing provider coupling.","tradeoffs":{"build":{"benefits":["Maximum control"],"costs":["Implementation and maintenance burden"]},"external":{"benefits":["Potentially faster capability acquisition"],"costs":["Provider dependency"]},"existing_platform":{"benefits":["Lower technology diversity"],"costs":["May constrain capability"]}},"decision":"Adopt an external dependency only when its capability and operational value justify the resulting coupling and lifecycle obligations.","rationale":"Dependency decisions should include long-term architectural consequences, not only initial implementation speed.","consequences":["Provider changes can affect architecture","Exit planning may become necessary"],"implementation_implications":["Document dependency contract","Assess security and reliability","Define ownership","Track version and lifecycle status"],"verification_method":"Review dependency against documented requirements and perform failure, compatibility, security, and lifecycle assessments before adoption.","outcome_learning":"External adoption is an architectural coupling decision, not merely a procurement decision.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B50-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"External Dependencies and Vendor Architecture","subtopic":"Vendor lock-in","concept":"Vendor lock-in risk should be evaluated by identifying which interfaces, data formats, operational processes, and proprietary capabilities would make future migration difficult.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should vendor lock-in risk be assessed?","answer":"Assess lock-in by mapping proprietary interfaces, data formats, operational dependencies, contractual constraints, migration tooling, and the effort required to reproduce or replace the dependency.","context":"A system is considering a proprietary external platform for a core capability.","objective":"Make dependency concentration and exit difficulty explicit.","requirements":["Dependency inventory","Data portability assessment","Migration analysis"],"constraints":["Avoiding all proprietary technology may be impractical","Abstraction can itself add cost"],"alternatives":["Accept lock-in","Build abstraction layer","Use portable interfaces and data"],"evidence":"Lock-in is determined by migration dependency and switching cost, not simply by whether technology is proprietary.","tradeoffs":{"accept":{"benefits":["Potentially simpler integration"],"costs":["Higher future switching cost"]},"abstraction":{"benefits":["Potential provider flexibility"],"costs":["Additional architecture"]},"portable_boundary":{"benefits":["Lower migration coupling"],"costs":["May limit provider-specific features"]}},"decision":"Accept vendor-specific coupling only where its value is understood and material exit dependencies are explicitly recorded.","rationale":"Avoiding every form of lock-in is unrealistic; unmanaged lock-in is the architectural risk.","consequences":["Some provider-specific capabilities may be deliberately retained","Exit assumptions must remain current"],"implementation_implications":["Keep portable domain contracts where valuable","Maintain export paths for critical data","Document proprietary dependencies"],"verification_method":"Perform a dependency and migration exercise against critical provider-coupled capabilities and data.","outcome_learning":"Lock-in should be measured by practical exit difficulty rather than treated as a binary property.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B50-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B50-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B50-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"External Dependencies and Vendor Architecture","subtopic":"Dependency failure isolation","concept":"Critical external dependencies should have explicit failure isolation and degradation behavior rather than allowing provider failure to propagate unpredictably through the system.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should critical external dependencies be isolated?","answer":"Define timeouts, bounded retries, circuit or admission controls where appropriate, fallback or degradation semantics, and explicit user-visible behavior based on the dependency's business criticality.","context":"A core workflow relies on an external service whose availability is outside the application's direct control.","objective":"Contain external dependency failure and preserve truthful system behavior.","requirements":["Failure classification","Timeout policy","Degradation behavior","Observability"],"constraints":["Fallback may not preserve equivalent semantics","Some dependencies are authoritative and cannot be bypassed"],"alternatives":["Hard dependency","Fallback","Graceful degradation","Queued deferred processing"],"evidence":"External failures can become application failures unless the dependency boundary has explicit failure semantics.","tradeoffs":{"hard":{"benefits":["Simple semantics"],"costs":["High availability coupling"]},"fallback":{"benefits":["Potential continuity"],"costs":["Different behavior and consistency"]},"degradation":{"benefits":["Controlled partial availability"],"costs":["Reduced functionality"]}},"decision":"Define dependency-specific failure behavior according to business criticality and semantic substitutability; do not assume every dependency can safely be replaced.","rationale":"Failure isolation must preserve correctness, not merely keep requests returning responses.","consequences":["Some failures will remain visible to users","Dependency-specific policies require maintenance"],"implementation_implications":["Set bounded timeouts","Classify retryable failures","Document fallback semantics","Monitor dependency health"],"verification_method":"Simulate provider timeout, failure, and degraded responses and verify containment and documented application behavior.","outcome_learning":"A fallback is valid only when its semantics are safe for the business operation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B43-R03","D54-B47-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B43-R03"},{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B47-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B50-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"External Dependencies and Vendor Architecture","subtopic":"Dependency version lifecycle","concept":"External dependency versions should be governed through compatibility testing, upgrade policy, and deprecation tracking rather than indefinite version pinning.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should external dependency versions be managed?","answer":"Pin or constrain versions according to reproducibility needs, monitor lifecycle status, test upgrades before adoption, and maintain an explicit response to unsupported or deprecated versions.","context":"An application relies on third-party libraries, APIs, platforms, or services with independent release cycles.","objective":"Balance reproducibility with security, compatibility, and lifecycle maintenance.","requirements":["Version identification","Compatibility testing","Lifecycle monitoring","Upgrade process"],"constraints":["Upgrades may introduce breaking behavior","Old versions may become unsupported"],"alternatives":["Never upgrade","Always use latest","Governed version lifecycle"],"evidence":"Dependency versions change independently from application release schedules and may affect compatibility or supportability.","tradeoffs":{"never":{"benefits":["Short-term stability"],"costs":["Accumulated upgrade debt"]},"latest":{"benefits":["Rapid access to changes"],"costs":["Higher immediate compatibility risk"]},"governed":{"benefits":["Controlled lifecycle"],"costs":["Requires maintenance process"]}},"decision":"Use governed dependency versioning with reproducible version identification and evidence-based upgrade testing.","rationale":"Dependency lifecycle is a continuing architectural concern rather than a one-time selection decision.","consequences":["Dependency inventory must remain current","Upgrade work becomes planned engineering activity"],"implementation_implications":["Record exact versions where reproducibility requires it","Test compatibility","Track deprecations","Review security and lifecycle changes"],"verification_method":"Perform a dependency upgrade in a controlled environment and verify compatibility, tests, security requirements, and deployment behavior.","outcome_learning":"Stable dependency management requires both reproducibility and deliberate evolution.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B41-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B41-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B50-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"External Dependencies and Vendor Architecture","subtopic":"Build versus buy lifecycle","concept":"Build-versus-buy decisions should compare total lifecycle responsibility rather than only initial implementation effort.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should build-versus-buy decisions be made for a non-core capability?","answer":"Compare internal engineering effort, ongoing maintenance, security responsibility, reliability requirements, integration cost, operational burden, provider dependency, migration risk, and strategic importance across the expected lifecycle.","context":"A team can implement a capability internally or adopt an external product or service.","objective":"Choose an implementation path based on complete lifecycle consequences.","requirements":["Lifecycle cost analysis","Capability fit","Operational responsibility","Risk analysis"],"constraints":["Estimates are uncertain","Strategic requirements may change"],"alternatives":["Build internally","Buy/adopt externally","Use an existing internal capability"],"evidence":"Initial implementation effort represents only one part of the lifecycle cost and risk of a capability.","tradeoffs":{"build":{"benefits":["Control","Customization"],"costs":["Long-term engineering ownership"]},"buy":{"benefits":["Potentially faster capability acquisition"],"costs":["External dependency and recurring responsibility"]},"reuse":{"benefits":["Lower duplication"],"costs":["May constrain capability fit"]}},"decision":"Select build, buy, or reuse by comparing lifecycle requirements and architectural consequences rather than initial delivery effort alone.","rationale":"The chosen solution must remain supportable and compatible with the system's long-term objectives.","consequences":["The decision may change as requirements or provider conditions change","Assumptions should be recorded for later reassessment"],"implementation_implications":["Document decision assumptions","Define acceptance criteria","Assess integration and exit paths","Review lifecycle periodically"],"verification_method":"Compare the selected option against documented functional, non-functional, operational, security, and lifecycle criteria and record unresolved assumptions.","outcome_learning":"Build-versus-buy is an architecture decision whose validity depends on lifecycle assumptions, not a permanent category preference.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B50-R01","D54-B50-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B50-R01"},{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B50-R02"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B51-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Service Boundaries","subtopic":"Domain and Responsibility Boundaries","concept":"Define service boundaries around cohesive business capabilities rather than technical layers.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should service boundaries be selected when decomposing a system?","answer":"Service boundaries should follow cohesive business responsibilities, data ownership, change patterns, and consistency requirements. A boundary is stronger when the service can own its decisions and data without excessive synchronous coordination with unrelated services.","context":"A system is being decomposed into independently changeable services.","objective":"Choose boundaries that reduce coupling while preserving necessary business consistency.","requirements":["Clear responsibility ownership","Controlled inter-service coupling","Independent change where justified"],"constraints":["Existing dependencies may cross proposed boundaries","Some workflows require coordination"],"alternatives":["Business-capability boundaries","Technical-layer boundaries","Organizational-team boundaries"],"evidence":"Architecture reasoning indicates that boundaries aligned with responsibility and ownership expose fewer accidental dependencies than boundaries based only on technical layers.","tradeoffs":"Business-capability boundaries may require reorganizing existing code; technical-layer boundaries can initially be simpler but often preserve cross-cutting coupling.","decision":"Prefer cohesive business-capability boundaries and validate them against data ownership and workflow dependencies.","rationale":"A boundary should isolate decisions and changes, not merely separate implementation technologies.","consequences":["Clearer ownership","Potential initial refactoring cost","Reduced accidental coupling when boundaries are valid"],"implementation_implications":["Map responsibilities before extracting services","Identify cross-boundary calls and shared mutable data","Define explicit contracts"],"verification_method":"Review dependency graph, ownership map, change-impact analysis, and cross-boundary transaction requirements.","outcome_learning":"Boundary quality should be reassessed when dependency patterns or business responsibilities materially change.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B51-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Service Boundaries","subtopic":"Coupling and Coordination","concept":"Avoid service decomposition that creates excessive synchronous coordination.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When does service decomposition create harmful coordination overhead?","answer":"Decomposition becomes questionable when normal business operations require frequent synchronous calls across many services, shared transactions, or tightly coupled deployment changes. The decision should compare the proposed independence benefits with coordination complexity.","context":"A candidate architecture splits one cohesive workflow across multiple services.","objective":"Determine whether the proposed boundaries provide meaningful independence.","requirements":["Measure dependency paths","Identify synchronous coordination","Assess transaction boundaries"],"constraints":["Some coordination is inherent to the business process"],"alternatives":["Keep capability together","Split into independently owned services","Use asynchronous coordination for suitable workflow steps"],"evidence":"Coupling is better evaluated through actual dependency and workflow behavior than through service count.","tradeoffs":"Fewer services can reduce coordination but increase internal scope; more services can improve isolation but increase operational and integration complexity.","decision":"Retain or split a boundary only after evaluating coordination frequency, ownership, consistency, and deployment independence.","rationale":"Service count is not itself an architecture objective.","consequences":["Avoids unnecessary distributed complexity","May result in larger services where cohesion is stronger"],"implementation_implications":["Inventory synchronous calls","Measure workflow fan-out","Identify shared state and distributed transaction requirements"],"verification_method":"Dependency analysis and representative workflow tracing.","outcome_learning":"Repeated cross-service coordination is evidence for reconsidering an existing boundary.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B51-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B51-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B51-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Service Boundaries","subtopic":"Shared Data","concept":"Shared mutable data weakens independent service ownership.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should shared mutable data affect service-boundary decisions?","answer":"A proposed service boundary should be reconsidered when multiple services require unrestricted mutation of the same data. Ownership should be assigned explicitly, with other services interacting through defined contracts or derived representations.","context":"Several services directly modify the same business entities.","objective":"Prevent ambiguous authority over mutable state.","requirements":["One authoritative owner per mutable business entity","Explicit access contracts","Controlled synchronization"],"constraints":["Legacy systems may already share tables","Migration may require temporary coexistence"],"alternatives":["Shared database writes","Single owner with APIs/events","Replicated read models"],"evidence":"Explicit ownership reduces ambiguity around validation, lifecycle, and consistency responsibilities.","tradeoffs":"Central ownership can add communication overhead; unrestricted shared writes reduce immediate integration work but increase coordination risk.","decision":"Prefer explicit ownership of mutable business state and restrict cross-boundary mutation.","rationale":"Independent services require clear authority over the state they govern.","consequences":["Clearer consistency authority","Migration effort may increase"],"implementation_implications":["Identify authoritative writers","Separate read access from mutation authority","Introduce contracts before removing legacy writes"],"verification_method":"Writer inventory, schema access review, and mutation-path analysis.","outcome_learning":"Unexpected concurrent writers are evidence of an incomplete ownership boundary.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B51-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B51-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B51-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Service Boundaries","subtopic":"Change Coupling","concept":"Deployment and change coupling should influence architectural boundaries.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When should different change rates justify separate boundaries?","answer":"Separate boundaries can be justified when capabilities have materially different change rates, release risks, ownership, or scaling requirements and the separation does not create excessive coordination.","context":"Two capabilities frequently evolve at different rates.","objective":"Evaluate whether independent change provides sufficient architectural value.","requirements":["Identify change frequency","Identify release coupling","Assess coordination cost"],"constraints":["Separation introduces contracts and operational overhead"],"alternatives":["Keep capabilities together","Separate by change boundary","Separate only deployment units while retaining logical cohesion"],"evidence":"Change coupling is a practical architectural concern because tightly coupled release cycles can negate intended independence.","tradeoffs":"Separation improves independent evolution but introduces contract-management cost.","decision":"Use change coupling as one factor in boundary decisions, not as the sole criterion.","rationale":"Different change rates matter when they materially affect release independence and risk.","consequences":["Potentially smaller change surfaces","Additional integration contracts"],"implementation_implications":["Track change dependencies","Define compatibility rules","Test cross-boundary contracts"],"verification_method":"Historical change analysis and dependency mapping.","outcome_learning":"Persistent coordinated releases may indicate insufficient boundary independence.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B51-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B51-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B51-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Service Boundaries","subtopic":"Boundary Validation","concept":"Architecture boundaries require validation against real workflows rather than diagrams alone.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_DECISION","question":"How should a proposed service boundary be validated?","answer":"Validate the boundary against representative workflows, data ownership, failure propagation, consistency requirements, deployment changes, and dependency paths. A visually clean decomposition is insufficient evidence by itself.","context":"A proposed service decomposition exists but has not been exercised against realistic workflows.","objective":"Determine whether the boundary is operationally coherent.","requirements":["Representative workflows","Dependency analysis","Failure analysis","Ownership analysis"],"constraints":["Architecture models may omit runtime dependencies"],"alternatives":["Diagram review only","Static dependency analysis","Workflow and failure-path validation"],"evidence":"Runtime workflow and failure dependencies can reveal coupling that static component diagrams do not expose.","tradeoffs":"Thorough validation requires more analysis but reduces boundary errors.","decision":"Require workflow and dependency validation before locking major service boundaries.","rationale":"Boundaries govern runtime interaction as well as code organization.","consequences":["Higher confidence before implementation","Additional architecture-analysis effort"],"implementation_implications":["Model normal and failure workflows","Trace state ownership","Document cross-boundary contracts"],"verification_method":"Architecture review plus workflow dependency and failure-path analysis.","outcome_learning":"Observed coupling should feed back into boundary refinement.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B51-R01","D54-B51-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"verifies","record_id":"D54-B51-R01"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B52-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"API and Contract Design","subtopic":"Contract Ownership","concept":"API contracts should have explicit ownership and compatibility responsibility.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Who should own an API contract in a distributed architecture?","answer":"The party responsible for the capability and its externally visible behavior should own the contract, including compatibility policy, lifecycle, documentation, and change validation.","context":"Multiple teams consume an API provided by one capability.","objective":"Establish clear contract authority.","requirements":["Named contract owner","Compatibility policy","Change process"],"constraints":["Consumers may have different release schedules"],"alternatives":["Provider-owned contract","Consumer-driven contract","Joint unmanaged contract"],"evidence":"Explicit ownership makes compatibility decisions and lifecycle accountability traceable.","tradeoffs":"Provider ownership centralizes responsibility but requires consumer-impact analysis.","decision":"Assign contract ownership to the capability owner while incorporating consumer compatibility evidence.","rationale":"The capability owner is best positioned to define intended behavior and lifecycle.","consequences":["Clearer change authority","Provider must maintain compatibility discipline"],"implementation_implications":["Version contract artifacts","Define deprecation rules","Automate compatibility checks where feasible"],"verification_method":"Contract ownership review and compatibility-test coverage.","outcome_learning":"Repeated consumer breakage indicates inadequate contract governance.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B52-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"API and Contract Design","subtopic":"Backward Compatibility","concept":"Compatibility requirements should be explicit before changing an API contract.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When should an API change preserve backward compatibility?","answer":"Backward compatibility should be preserved when independent consumers cannot migrate atomically or when the contract is explicitly governed as a compatibility boundary. Breaking changes may be acceptable when coordinated migration is feasible and the resulting simplification has sufficient value.","context":"An API contract needs structural or semantic modification.","objective":"Choose an appropriate compatibility strategy.","requirements":["Identify consumers","Determine migration coordination","Assess breakage impact"],"constraints":["Consumer deployment schedules differ"],"alternatives":["Backward-compatible extension","Versioned breaking change","Coordinated migration"],"evidence":"Compatibility strategy depends on deployment coupling and consumer impact rather than API aesthetics alone.","tradeoffs":"Compatibility can preserve flexibility for consumers but increase contract complexity.","decision":"Prefer additive or backward-compatible evolution when independent consumers exist; permit breaking changes only with controlled migration.","rationale":"Compatibility protects independently deployed consumers from unexpected failure.","consequences":["Potentially longer transition periods","Reduced migration risk"],"implementation_implications":["Add new fields/endpoints before removing old ones","Define deprecation windows","Test old and new contract behavior"],"verification_method":"Consumer inventory and automated contract compatibility testing.","outcome_learning":"Actual consumer usage should determine when deprecated behavior can be removed.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B52-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B52-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B52-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"API and Contract Design","subtopic":"Semantic Compatibility","concept":"API compatibility includes semantics, not only schema shape.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why must semantic behavior be included in API compatibility decisions?","answer":"A contract can remain structurally valid while changing validation, ordering, defaults, error meaning, side effects, or state transitions. Compatibility analysis must therefore cover observable behavior as well as message structure.","context":"An API schema change appears backward compatible but implementation behavior is also changing.","objective":"Prevent semantic regressions hidden by schema compatibility.","requirements":["Define observable behavior","Identify semantic invariants","Test representative behavior"],"constraints":["Some semantics may be undocumented"],"alternatives":["Schema-only compatibility","Schema plus behavioral compatibility"],"evidence":"Consumers depend on observable behavior even when that behavior is not represented by field definitions.","tradeoffs":"Behavioral compatibility testing requires more effort but catches failures that schema checks cannot detect.","decision":"Treat observable semantics as part of the API contract.","rationale":"A contract defines externally observable behavior, not merely serialized structure.","consequences":["Broader compatibility surface","More meaningful contract tests"],"implementation_implications":["Document semantic invariants","Test error and state-transition behavior","Include defaults and ordering where contractually relevant"],"verification_method":"Behavioral contract tests against representative consumers.","outcome_learning":"Consumer incidents should update explicit contract semantics.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B52-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"expands","record_id":"D54-B52-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B52-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"API and Contract Design","subtopic":"Error Contracts","concept":"Error behavior should be designed as part of the API contract.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should API error behavior influence contract decisions?","answer":"The contract should define stable error categories, relevant machine-readable information, and conditions under which retries are safe or unsafe. Error semantics should not be treated as incidental implementation detail.","context":"Consumers need reliable handling of API failures.","objective":"Make failure behavior predictable across contract versions.","requirements":["Stable error semantics","Retry guidance","Machine-readable error information"],"constraints":["Internal failure details may be unsuitable for exposure"],"alternatives":["Unstructured errors","Structured contract errors","Provider-specific error leakage"],"evidence":"Consumers often make control-flow decisions based on failure categories and retryability.","tradeoffs":"Detailed error contracts improve interoperability but create compatibility obligations.","decision":"Expose stable, intentionally designed error semantics appropriate to the contract boundary.","rationale":"Failure behavior is part of observable API behavior.","consequences":["More predictable clients","Additional contract maintenance"],"implementation_implications":["Define error categories","Separate safe client information from internal diagnostics","Test retry and non-retry cases"],"verification_method":"Negative contract tests and consumer behavior analysis.","outcome_learning":"Observed ambiguous errors should trigger contract clarification.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B52-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B52-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B52-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"API and Contract Design","subtopic":"Contract Testing","concept":"Contract verification should focus on agreed observable behavior.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_DECISION","question":"What should contract tests verify?","answer":"Contract tests should verify the agreed request and response structure, required semantics, error behavior, compatibility assumptions, and relevant boundary conditions. They should not merely confirm that a serialization format is syntactically valid.","context":"A service boundary is governed by an API contract.","objective":"Detect provider or consumer changes that violate the contract.","requirements":["Representative contract cases","Negative cases","Compatibility cases"],"constraints":["Contract tests cannot prove all internal implementation correctness"],"alternatives":["Schema validation only","Provider tests only","Contract tests covering observable behavior"],"evidence":"Boundary failures occur in observable interaction behavior as well as serialization.","tradeoffs":"Broader tests require maintenance but provide stronger boundary verification.","decision":"Use contract tests as boundary-focused verification rather than a replacement for internal tests.","rationale":"Contract testing should validate the agreement between independently evolving components.","consequences":["Earlier compatibility detection","Additional test maintenance"],"implementation_implications":["Version contract fixtures","Test required and optional behavior","Include failure semantics"],"verification_method":"Automated provider-consumer contract verification.","outcome_learning":"Failed contract tests should identify whether the contract, implementation, or consumer assumption requires correction.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B52-R01","D54-B52-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"verifies","record_id":"D54-B52-R01"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B53-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Asynchronous Communication","subtopic":"Synchronous vs Asynchronous Interaction","concept":"Use asynchronous communication when temporal decoupling materially benefits the workflow.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should asynchronous communication replace synchronous calls?","answer":"Asynchronous communication is appropriate when the producer does not require an immediate response, consumers can process independently, workload smoothing is valuable, or temporary consumer unavailability should not necessarily block the producer.","context":"A workflow currently performs synchronous cross-component communication.","objective":"Evaluate whether temporal decoupling improves the architecture.","requirements":["Define delivery semantics","Define failure handling","Define ownership of processing state"],"constraints":["Some decisions require immediate results"],"alternatives":["Synchronous request-response","Asynchronous event/message","Hybrid workflow"],"evidence":"Asynchronous communication changes temporal coupling and therefore must be selected based on workflow semantics rather than scalability claims alone.","tradeoffs":"It can improve temporal decoupling but introduces delivery, ordering, retry, and observability complexity.","decision":"Use asynchronous communication only where workflow semantics tolerate deferred processing.","rationale":"Asynchrony is an architectural tradeoff, not a universal performance optimization.","consequences":["Greater temporal independence","More complex failure and state tracking"],"implementation_implications":["Define message identity","Define retry and duplicate handling","Expose processing state where users need it"],"verification_method":"Failure-path and workflow-state analysis.","outcome_learning":"Unresolved user expectations about completion state indicate insufficient asynchronous workflow design.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B53-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Asynchronous Communication","subtopic":"Delivery Semantics","concept":"Message delivery semantics must be explicit before relying on asynchronous processing.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should delivery semantics affect messaging architecture?","answer":"The architecture should explicitly define whether processing requires at-most-once, at-least-once, or another supported delivery model, then design idempotency, retry, and failure handling accordingly.","context":"A message-based workflow is being introduced.","objective":"Align processing behavior with delivery guarantees.","requirements":["Defined delivery expectation","Duplicate handling","Failure recovery"],"constraints":["Infrastructure guarantees vary","Exactly-once end-to-end effects may require application-level controls"],"alternatives":["At-most-once processing","At-least-once with idempotent handling","Application-level effect deduplication"],"evidence":"Delivery and business-effect guarantees are distinct concerns and must be designed together.","tradeoffs":"Stronger effect guarantees generally require additional state, coordination, or deduplication logic.","decision":"Choose delivery semantics based on business consequences and explicitly design effect handling.","rationale":"Transport delivery alone does not define business outcome correctness.","consequences":["Predictable failure behavior","Potential additional idempotency state"],"implementation_implications":["Assign stable message identifiers","Persist processing state where required","Define retry boundaries"],"verification_method":"Duplicate, retry, crash-recovery, and redelivery tests.","outcome_learning":"Observed duplicate effects should feed back into idempotency design.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B53-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B53-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B53-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Asynchronous Communication","subtopic":"Ordering","concept":"Message ordering requirements should be limited to the scope actually required by the business process.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should ordering requirements be handled in event-driven workflows?","answer":"Define the smallest ordering scope required by the business invariant, such as per entity or workflow, rather than imposing global ordering when it is unnecessary.","context":"Events may arrive concurrently or out of order.","objective":"Preserve required business ordering without unnecessary coordination.","requirements":["Identify ordering-sensitive operations","Define ordering key or scope","Handle late events"],"constraints":["Global ordering can reduce concurrency","Distributed systems may not provide arbitrary ordering"],"alternatives":["Global ordering","Per-key ordering","Order-independent processing"],"evidence":"Ordering is a business requirement only when state transitions depend on sequence.","tradeoffs":"Stronger ordering reduces concurrency and can increase coordination complexity.","decision":"Use the narrowest ordering guarantee that preserves the required invariant.","rationale":"Unnecessary ordering is avoidable coupling.","consequences":["Higher concurrency potential","Application must handle out-of-order cases outside the guaranteed scope"],"implementation_implications":["Define ordering keys","Persist sequence information where necessary","Reject or reconcile invalid transitions"],"verification_method":"Out-of-order and concurrent event tests.","outcome_learning":"Ordering violations should identify whether the invariant or ordering scope was incorrectly specified.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B53-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B53-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B53-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Asynchronous Communication","subtopic":"Backpressure","concept":"Asynchronous systems require explicit overload and backpressure behavior.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should backpressure be part of an asynchronous architecture decision?","answer":"Without defined overload behavior, queued work can grow beyond processing capacity and degrade latency, availability, or resource safety. The architecture should define admission, throttling, queue limits, prioritization, and failure handling as appropriate.","context":"Producer throughput can exceed consumer processing capacity.","objective":"Prevent uncontrolled accumulation of asynchronous work.","requirements":["Capacity model","Queue limits","Overload behavior"],"constraints":["Some work may be non-droppable","Priorities may differ by business impact"],"alternatives":["Unlimited buffering","Bounded queues","Producer throttling","Load shedding"],"evidence":"Asynchronous buffering changes where overload appears but does not eliminate capacity constraints.","tradeoffs":"Strict limits protect resources but can reject work; unlimited queues defer failure and may consume excessive resources.","decision":"Use bounded capacity and explicit overload behavior appropriate to business criticality.","rationale":"Backpressure makes capacity limits explicit instead of allowing uncontrolled failure propagation.","consequences":["Predictable overload behavior","Potential rejected or delayed work"],"implementation_implications":["Define queue thresholds","Expose saturation metrics","Prioritize critical work"],"verification_method":"Load, saturation, and failure-injection testing.","outcome_learning":"Queue saturation patterns should inform capacity and prioritization changes.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B53-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B53-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B53-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Asynchronous Communication","subtopic":"Retry and Poison Messages","concept":"Retries require bounded policies and explicit handling of messages that repeatedly fail.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_DECISION","question":"How should asynchronous retry behavior be architected?","answer":"Retries should be bounded and based on failure classification. Repeatedly failing messages require a controlled terminal path such as quarantine, dead-letter handling, or explicit operator intervention rather than indefinite retry.","context":"Asynchronous processing can fail transiently or permanently.","objective":"Recover transient failures without creating retry storms or permanent blockage.","requirements":["Failure classification","Bounded retry policy","Terminal failure path"],"constraints":["Some failures are transient","Some messages are invalid or permanently unprocessable"],"alternatives":["Unbounded retry","Bounded retry with quarantine","Immediate rejection"],"evidence":"Transient and permanent failures have different recovery requirements.","tradeoffs":"Retries improve transient recovery but increase load and may amplify failures.","decision":"Use bounded, failure-aware retry with explicit terminal handling.","rationale":"Retry is a recovery mechanism, not a substitute for failure classification.","consequences":["More predictable recovery","Additional operational state"],"implementation_implications":["Record attempt counts","Use controlled retry delays","Preserve diagnostic context"],"verification_method":"Transient-failure, permanent-failure, and retry-storm tests.","outcome_learning":"Retry exhaustion patterns should refine failure classification and retry policy.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B53-R02","D54-B53-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B53-R02"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B54-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Workflow Orchestration","subtopic":"Orchestration vs Choreography","concept":"Choose orchestration or choreography based on workflow coordination and visibility requirements.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should a workflow use an explicit orchestrator?","answer":"An orchestrator is useful when a workflow has explicit sequencing, branching, compensation, timeout, authorization, or centralized progress semantics that benefit from a visible coordinator.","context":"A multi-step business workflow spans multiple components.","objective":"Choose an appropriate coordination model.","requirements":["Defined workflow semantics","Failure handling","Progress visibility"],"constraints":["Central coordination introduces a coordination component"],"alternatives":["Central orchestration","Event choreography","Hybrid coordination"],"evidence":"Explicit coordination can make complex workflow state and failure paths easier to reason about.","tradeoffs":"Orchestration improves visibility but can become an excessive central dependency if it owns unrelated business logic.","decision":"Use explicit orchestration when workflow coordination itself is a significant concern.","rationale":"Complex workflows benefit from an explicit representation of state and transitions.","consequences":["Central workflow visibility","Additional orchestration responsibility"],"implementation_implications":["Keep orchestration focused on coordination","Separate domain decisions from workflow control where appropriate","Persist recoverable workflow state"],"verification_method":"Workflow transition, failure, timeout, and compensation analysis.","outcome_learning":"Repeated hidden coordination indicates value in making workflow state explicit.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B54-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Workflow Orchestration","subtopic":"Workflow State","concept":"Long-running workflows require durable state when recovery must survive process or infrastructure failure.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should workflow state be persisted durably?","answer":"Persist workflow state when a workflow spans time, process restarts, retries, human approval, external dependencies, or recovery boundaries where in-memory state is insufficient.","context":"A workflow can remain active across multiple execution intervals.","objective":"Enable reliable workflow recovery and progress tracking.","requirements":["State identity","Recoverable transitions","Failure handling"],"constraints":["State persistence introduces storage and schema concerns"],"alternatives":["In-memory state","Durable workflow state","External state reconstruction"],"evidence":"Long-running workflows cannot safely depend solely on volatile process state when recovery is required.","tradeoffs":"Durability increases complexity but improves recoverability.","decision":"Persist the minimum workflow state required for deterministic recovery and observability.","rationale":"Recovery requires enough durable information to determine the next valid transition.","consequences":["Recoverable workflows","Additional state-management responsibility"],"implementation_implications":["Version workflow state","Record transition outcomes","Separate transient execution data from durable state"],"verification_method":"Restart, retry, partial-failure, and resume testing.","outcome_learning":"Recovery gaps should identify missing durable state or ambiguous transitions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B54-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B54-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B54-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Workflow Orchestration","subtopic":"Compensation","concept":"Distributed workflows often require compensating actions rather than distributed rollback.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should workflow compensation be selected when atomic rollback is unavailable?","answer":"Define compensating actions that restore acceptable business state when individual steps cannot be rolled back atomically. Compensation must account for partial completion and may itself require retries and verification.","context":"A workflow updates multiple independent systems without a shared transaction boundary.","objective":"Define recovery behavior for partially completed workflows.","requirements":["Identify irreversible steps","Define compensating actions","Verify compensation outcomes"],"constraints":["Some external effects cannot be reversed","Compensation may have independent failure modes"],"alternatives":["Distributed transaction","Compensating workflow","Manual recovery"],"evidence":"Independent systems generally require explicit recovery semantics when atomic rollback across all participants is unavailable.","tradeoffs":"Compensation can preserve business correctness but is more complex than local rollback.","decision":"Use compensation when the business process permits corrective actions and distributed atomicity is not appropriate.","rationale":"Business recovery can often be achieved without pretending independent systems share one transaction.","consequences":["Explicit partial-failure handling","Need for compensation verification"],"implementation_implications":["Record completed steps","Define compensation idempotency","Track unresolved compensation"],"verification_method":"Partial-failure and compensation-path testing.","outcome_learning":"Failed compensation should become a first-class workflow outcome rather than being hidden as a generic error.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B54-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B54-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B54-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Workflow Orchestration","subtopic":"Human Approval","concept":"Human approval should be represented as an explicit workflow state when it changes authorization or execution semantics.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should human approval be modeled in an automated workflow?","answer":"When approval changes whether an action may proceed, approval should be an explicit state transition with identity, scope, timing, and outcome rather than an informal side channel.","context":"A workflow contains actions requiring human authorization.","objective":"Make authorization and workflow state auditable and recoverable.","requirements":["Approval identity","Decision scope","Timestamp or validity boundary","Outcome recording"],"constraints":["Approvals can expire or become invalid after state changes"],"alternatives":["Implicit approval flag","Explicit approval state","External approval system with workflow reference"],"evidence":"Authorization affecting execution should be explicit and traceable.","tradeoffs":"Explicit approval state adds workflow complexity but improves auditability and control.","decision":"Represent consequential approval as an explicit workflow transition.","rationale":"Approval is part of execution governance when it controls action authorization.","consequences":["Traceable authorization","Additional state validation"],"implementation_implications":["Bind approval to specific action scope and version","Revalidate relevant state before execution","Record approval outcome"],"verification_method":"Approval replay, expiry, scope-change, and unauthorized-execution tests.","outcome_learning":"Invalidated approvals should become explicit workflow outcomes.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B54-R01","D54-B54-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"constrains","record_id":"D54-B54-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B54-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Workflow Orchestration","subtopic":"Workflow Verification","concept":"Workflow completion should be based on verified outcomes rather than task dispatch.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_DECISION","question":"What distinguishes workflow execution from workflow completion?","answer":"Dispatching an action proves that execution was attempted, not that the intended business outcome occurred. Completion should require outcome evidence appropriate to the workflow's invariant.","context":"A workflow executes actions against external systems.","objective":"Prevent false completion states.","requirements":["Execution evidence","Outcome evidence","Defined completion criteria"],"constraints":["Some outcomes may be externally observed with delay"],"alternatives":["Mark complete on dispatch","Mark complete on successful response","Mark complete after independent outcome verification"],"evidence":"Action execution and business outcome are distinct states.","tradeoffs":"Outcome verification adds latency and implementation effort but increases correctness of completion state.","decision":"Use outcome verification whenever workflow correctness depends on the resulting state rather than mere command acceptance.","rationale":"A command being accepted does not necessarily establish the intended final state.","consequences":["More accurate workflow state","Additional verification mechanisms"],"implementation_implications":["Define expected postconditions","Capture verification evidence","Represent pending verification separately"],"verification_method":"Postcondition checks and reconciliation against authoritative state.","outcome_learning":"Verification failures should feed workflow correction and learning mechanisms.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B54-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B54-R04"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B55-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Integration Architecture","subtopic":"Integration Style","concept":"Select integration style according to interaction semantics rather than organizational fashion.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an integration style be selected?","answer":"Evaluate request-response, messaging, events, batch exchange, and other integration forms against latency, coupling, reliability, ownership, consistency, and workflow requirements.","context":"Two independently governed systems need to exchange information or trigger actions.","objective":"Choose an integration mechanism aligned with business interaction semantics.","requirements":["Interaction requirements","Failure behavior","Data ownership","Timing expectations"],"constraints":["External systems may constrain available mechanisms"],"alternatives":["Synchronous API","Asynchronous messaging","Event publication","Batch integration"],"evidence":"Different integration styles impose different temporal and coupling characteristics.","tradeoffs":"Synchronous integration is direct but temporally coupled; asynchronous integration decouples timing but increases state-management complexity.","decision":"Select integration style from explicit interaction requirements and failure semantics.","rationale":"Integration technology should follow system interaction needs.","consequences":["Better alignment between contract and workflow","Potentially different operational complexity"],"implementation_implications":["Document interaction contract","Define retry and timeout behavior","Define ownership of exchanged data"],"verification_method":"Integration failure and workflow analysis.","outcome_learning":"Production interaction failures should feed back into integration-style decisions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B55-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Integration Architecture","subtopic":"External Dependency Isolation","concept":"External integration dependencies should be isolated behind explicit boundaries.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should external systems be isolated behind integration boundaries?","answer":"An integration boundary can isolate external schemas, failure behavior, authentication mechanisms, rate limits, and lifecycle changes from internal domain logic.","context":"An application depends on a third-party or externally governed system.","objective":"Prevent external coupling from spreading through internal architecture.","requirements":["Explicit adapter boundary","External contract mapping","Failure isolation"],"constraints":["External behavior can change independently"],"alternatives":["Direct external calls throughout the codebase","Central integration adapter","Dedicated integration service"],"evidence":"External systems have independent contracts and failure modes that should not implicitly become internal domain contracts.","tradeoffs":"Isolation adds abstraction and mapping code but limits coupling.","decision":"Encapsulate external dependencies behind a controlled integration boundary when their lifecycle or failure behavior differs materially from internal components.","rationale":"The boundary preserves internal architectural independence.","consequences":["Localized external changes","Additional adapter maintenance"],"implementation_implications":["Map external to internal models","Centralize external error handling","Keep external credentials and configuration isolated"],"verification_method":"Contract tests, failure injection, and external-schema change analysis.","outcome_learning":"Repeated external change impact outside the boundary indicates leakage of integration concerns.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B55-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B55-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B55-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Integration Architecture","subtopic":"Data Mapping","concept":"Integration mappings should preserve business meaning while isolating representation differences.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should data be mapped across integration boundaries?","answer":"Map external representations into explicit internal representations where semantic differences exist. Avoid assuming that identical field names or shapes imply identical business meaning.","context":"Two systems represent related information differently.","objective":"Prevent semantic coupling through direct data-model reuse.","requirements":["Explicit semantic mapping","Defined ownership","Transformation validation"],"constraints":["Mappings may evolve independently","Some fields may not have direct equivalents"],"alternatives":["Direct model reuse","Explicit mapping layer","Shared canonical model"],"evidence":"Representation compatibility does not guarantee semantic compatibility.","tradeoffs":"Explicit mapping adds transformation logic but protects internal meaning.","decision":"Use explicit mappings when external and internal semantics or lifecycle differ.","rationale":"Internal domain models should not be forced to mirror external representations without evidence that the semantics are equivalent.","consequences":["Clearer ownership","Additional transformation maintenance"],"implementation_implications":["Document field semantics","Test boundary transformations","Handle unmapped and newly introduced fields explicitly"],"verification_method":"Mapping tests using representative and edge-case payloads.","outcome_learning":"Mapping defects should update semantic contract documentation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B55-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B55-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B55-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Integration Architecture","subtopic":"Rate Limits","concept":"External rate limits are architectural constraints rather than merely client configuration details.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should external rate limits influence integration architecture?","answer":"Rate limits should be included in capacity planning, retry policy, concurrency control, caching, batching, and degradation behavior when an external dependency imposes them.","context":"An external dependency limits request frequency or concurrency.","objective":"Prevent integration overload and cascading failure.","requirements":["Known limit model","Concurrency control","Failure handling"],"constraints":["Limits may vary by account, endpoint, or time","Limits may not be perfectly predictable"],"alternatives":["Unbounded calls","Client-side throttling","Queue-based controlled access"],"evidence":"External capacity constraints directly affect integration reliability.","tradeoffs":"Throttling may increase latency but protects the dependency and the caller from uncontrolled failure.","decision":"Model external limits explicitly and enforce bounded request behavior.","rationale":"A dependency constraint becomes part of the system's effective capacity envelope.","consequences":["Predictable request behavior","Potential increased waiting time"],"implementation_implications":["Track limit responses","Throttle before hard failure where possible","Define degraded behavior"],"verification_method":"Rate-limit simulation and load testing.","outcome_learning":"Observed throttling patterns should update capacity assumptions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B55-R01","D54-B55-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"constrains","record_id":"D54-B55-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B55-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Integration Architecture","subtopic":"Dependency Failure Isolation","concept":"External dependency failure should not automatically propagate to unrelated internal capabilities.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_DECISION","question":"How should integration architecture limit external dependency failures?","answer":"Use explicit timeouts, bounded retries, isolation, fallback or degraded behavior where valid, and resource controls so an unavailable dependency does not consume unbounded internal capacity.","context":"An external dependency can become slow or unavailable.","objective":"Contain failure propagation.","requirements":["Timeout policy","Resource isolation","Degraded behavior where valid"],"constraints":["Some operations cannot safely degrade"],"alternatives":["Direct blocking dependency","Bounded dependency isolation","Asynchronous decoupling"],"evidence":"Failure propagation can consume internal resources when external calls are unbounded.","tradeoffs":"Isolation can reduce availability of dependent features while preserving unrelated capabilities.","decision":"Contain external dependency failure according to business criticality and resource boundaries.","rationale":"Dependency failure should be localized where the architecture permits it.","consequences":["Improved fault containment","Potential degraded feature behavior"],"implementation_implications":["Define per-dependency timeout budgets","Limit concurrent calls","Separate critical and non-critical paths"],"verification_method":"Timeout, dependency-outage, and resource-exhaustion tests.","outcome_learning":"Observed cascading failures should update isolation boundaries.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B55-R02","D54-B55-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B55-R02"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B56-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Deployment Architecture","subtopic":"Deployment Unit Selection","concept":"Deployment boundaries should reflect meaningful isolation requirements rather than arbitrary component boundaries.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should deployment units be selected?","answer":"Choose deployment units based on independent scaling, release cadence, fault isolation, security boundaries, resource requirements, and ownership. Logical component boundaries do not automatically require separate deployment units.","context":"A system contains multiple logical components that could be deployed together or separately.","objective":"Determine an appropriate deployment topology.","requirements":["Scaling analysis","Release analysis","Fault isolation","Resource analysis"],"constraints":["Separate deployments increase operational overhead"],"alternatives":["Single deployment unit","Multiple independently deployed units","Hybrid deployment"],"evidence":"Deployment independence provides value only when independent operational characteristics justify the additional complexity.","tradeoffs":"More deployment units increase isolation but also increase operational surface area.","decision":"Separate deployment units only where independent operational characteristics provide material value.","rationale":"Deployment architecture should optimize operational independence rather than component count.","consequences":["Controlled deployment complexity","Potentially fewer unnecessary runtime units"],"implementation_implications":["Map logical to deployment boundaries explicitly","Define shared configuration carefully","Measure independent scaling needs"],"verification_method":"Deployment dependency and resource-utilization analysis.","outcome_learning":"Persistent coordinated deployment changes may indicate that units should be reconsidered.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B56-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Deployment Architecture","subtopic":"Release Independence","concept":"Independent deployment is valuable only when release coupling is actually reduced.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When does separate deployment provide meaningful architectural value?","answer":"Separate deployment provides value when components can change, validate, roll back, and operate independently without frequent coordinated releases or shared runtime assumptions.","context":"A team proposes separating components solely to enable independent deployment.","objective":"Verify that deployment separation creates real independence.","requirements":["Change dependency analysis","Compatibility strategy","Independent validation"],"constraints":["Contracts may still create release coupling"],"alternatives":["Separate deployments","Coordinated deployment","Modular monolith"],"evidence":"Physical deployment separation does not eliminate logical or contract coupling.","tradeoffs":"Independent deployment can reduce release contention but adds operational complexity.","decision":"Require evidence of meaningful release independence before introducing separate deployment units.","rationale":"Deployment boundaries should solve an actual coordination problem.","consequences":["Avoids unnecessary deployment complexity","Preserves simpler topology where independence is weak"],"implementation_implications":["Track coordinated releases","Define contract compatibility","Measure deployment dependencies"],"verification_method":"Release-history and dependency analysis.","outcome_learning":"Frequent coordinated releases should trigger architectural reassessment.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B56-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B56-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B56-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Deployment Architecture","subtopic":"Configuration Management","concept":"Configuration ownership should be separated from deployment packaging when configuration lifecycle differs from application lifecycle.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should configuration be managed across deployment units?","answer":"Configuration should have explicit ownership, lifecycle, validation, and environment-specific handling. Configuration that changes independently should not be tightly embedded in immutable application artifacts without a deliberate reason.","context":"Application behavior depends on environment-specific or operational configuration.","objective":"Make configuration changes controlled and observable.","requirements":["Configuration ownership","Validation","Change tracking","Secret separation"],"constraints":["Some configuration must be supplied at deployment time"],"alternatives":["Embedded configuration","Externalized configuration","Hybrid configuration"],"evidence":"Configuration has distinct lifecycle and security characteristics from application code in many deployment models.","tradeoffs":"Externalization improves operational flexibility but requires stronger configuration governance.","decision":"Externalize configuration where independent lifecycle or environment variation justifies it, while preserving validation and access controls.","rationale":"Configuration should be managed according to its own change and security characteristics.","consequences":["More controlled configuration lifecycle","Additional configuration management surface"],"implementation_implications":["Validate configuration before activation","Separate secrets from ordinary configuration","Record effective configuration version"],"verification_method":"Configuration validation and deployment reproducibility tests.","outcome_learning":"Configuration drift should inform governance and deployment design.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B56-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B56-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B56-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Deployment Architecture","subtopic":"Resource Isolation","concept":"Resource isolation should be proportional to the consequences of contention.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should deployment units receive stronger resource isolation?","answer":"Stronger isolation is justified when workloads have materially different resource profiles, failure consequences, security requirements, or availability objectives and contention could affect important capabilities.","context":"Multiple workloads currently share runtime resources.","objective":"Prevent harmful resource contention.","requirements":["Resource profile analysis","Contention analysis","Business impact assessment"],"constraints":["Isolation consumes additional resources"],"alternatives":["Shared resources","Resource quotas","Separate deployment units"],"evidence":"Resource contention can couple otherwise independent workloads.","tradeoffs":"Isolation improves predictability but may reduce aggregate utilization.","decision":"Use the least costly isolation mechanism that reliably protects important workloads.","rationale":"Isolation should correspond to demonstrated contention risk.","consequences":["More predictable resource behavior","Potentially higher infrastructure cost"],"implementation_implications":["Measure CPU, memory, I/O, and concurrency behavior","Set explicit resource boundaries","Monitor saturation"],"verification_method":"Load and contention testing.","outcome_learning":"Observed contention should determine whether stronger isolation is needed.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B56-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B56-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B56-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Deployment Architecture","subtopic":"Rollback and Recovery","concept":"Deployment architecture should support a defined recovery path appropriate to change risk.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_DECISION","question":"What should deployment architecture consider for rollback?","answer":"Deployment decisions should account for application compatibility, data compatibility, migration state, configuration state, and the ability to restore a known-good operational state.","context":"A deployment mechanism is being selected for systems with persistent state.","objective":"Ensure failed releases have a controlled recovery path.","requirements":["Recovery target","Compatibility analysis","Data migration strategy"],"constraints":["Some data changes cannot be reversed safely"],"alternatives":["Immediate rollback","Forward fix","Rollback plus compatible data strategy"],"evidence":"Application rollback is insufficient when persistent data or external contracts have already changed.","tradeoffs":"Strong rollback capability can constrain deployment strategies; forward fixes may be necessary for irreversible changes.","decision":"Design deployment recovery around the complete system state, not application binaries alone.","rationale":"Recoverability includes data and contract compatibility.","consequences":["More deliberate release design","Reduced unsafe rollback assumptions"],"implementation_implications":["Use backward-compatible migrations where possible","Record deployment and schema versions","Define forward-recovery paths for irreversible changes"],"verification_method":"Failed-release and recovery drills.","outcome_learning":"Recovery exercises should update release architecture and migration practices.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B56-R03","D54-B56-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B56-R03"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B57-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Resilience Architecture","subtopic":"Failure Isolation","concept":"Failure domains should be designed so one component's failure does not unnecessarily consume shared capacity.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should failure domains influence architecture decisions?","answer":"Identify shared resources and dependencies that could transmit failure, then isolate critical capabilities where the consequences justify additional boundaries.","context":"Several capabilities depend on common infrastructure or runtime resources.","objective":"Limit correlated failure.","requirements":["Failure-domain mapping","Shared-resource analysis","Criticality assessment"],"constraints":["Complete isolation may be expensive or impossible"],"alternatives":["Shared failure domain","Partial isolation","Independent failure domains"],"evidence":"Shared dependencies can create correlated failures across otherwise separate components.","tradeoffs":"Isolation improves containment but can increase operational and resource costs.","decision":"Create stronger failure boundaries around critical or high-impact capabilities where shared failure is unacceptable.","rationale":"Failure isolation should follow consequence rather than component count.","consequences":["Reduced correlated-failure exposure","Additional infrastructure complexity"],"implementation_implications":["Map dependency failure propagation","Separate critical resources where justified","Test dependency outages"],"verification_method":"Failure-injection and dependency-outage testing.","outcome_learning":"Correlated incidents should trigger review of failure-domain assumptions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B57-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Resilience Architecture","subtopic":"Timeouts","concept":"Timeouts should be derived from workflow and dependency budgets rather than arbitrary constants.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should timeout values be selected?","answer":"Timeouts should reflect the maximum useful waiting period, upstream and downstream latency budgets, retry behavior, and business consequences of delayed responses.","context":"A component depends on another operation with variable latency.","objective":"Prevent indefinite resource consumption while preserving useful work.","requirements":["Latency budget","Dependency behavior","Retry interaction"],"constraints":["Some operations have naturally variable duration"],"alternatives":["No timeout","Fixed arbitrary timeout","Budget-derived timeout"],"evidence":"Timeouts are resource and workflow controls as well as latency settings.","tradeoffs":"Short timeouts improve resource protection but can reject valid slow operations; long timeouts preserve patience but consume resources longer.","decision":"Use explicit, context-derived timeout budgets.","rationale":"Timeout behavior should correspond to the maximum useful duration of the operation.","consequences":["More predictable resource usage","Potential need for asynchronous continuation for long operations"],"implementation_implications":["Propagate remaining time budget","Avoid retries that exceed the overall deadline","Record timeout causes"],"verification_method":"Latency distribution and timeout-boundary testing.","outcome_learning":"Timeout frequency and downstream latency should refine budgets.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B57-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B57-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B57-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Resilience Architecture","subtopic":"Circuit Breaking","concept":"Circuit breaking can isolate repeated dependency failure when continued calls provide little value.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When is circuit breaking appropriate?","answer":"Circuit breaking is useful when repeated calls to a failing dependency would consume local resources or amplify failure and when the caller can safely fail fast or use an alternative path.","context":"A dependency may remain unavailable for an extended period.","objective":"Prevent repeated failing calls from exhausting caller capacity.","requirements":["Failure classification","Fallback or fail-fast behavior","Recovery probing"],"constraints":["Some operations cannot proceed without the dependency"],"alternatives":["Unbounded retries","Circuit breaker","Queue for later processing"],"evidence":"Repeated dependency failures can consume caller resources without improving outcome.","tradeoffs":"Circuit breaking protects resources but can temporarily reject requests that might have succeeded.","decision":"Use circuit breaking where repeated dependency failure creates material resource or cascading-failure risk.","rationale":"Failure containment can be more valuable than repeated immediate attempts.","consequences":["Reduced dependency pressure","Need for controlled recovery behavior"],"implementation_implications":["Define open, recovery, and closed behavior","Avoid synchronized recovery spikes","Record circuit state"],"verification_method":"Dependency-outage and recovery testing.","outcome_learning":"Circuit behavior should be adjusted using observed failure duration and workload impact.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B57-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B57-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B57-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Resilience Architecture","subtopic":"Graceful Degradation","concept":"Degraded operation should preserve explicitly selected critical capabilities rather than silently changing business semantics.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should graceful degradation be designed?","answer":"Define which capabilities may degrade, what reduced behavior is acceptable, and how users or downstream systems learn that the system is operating in a degraded state.","context":"A dependency or subsystem may become partially unavailable.","objective":"Preserve acceptable functionality during partial failure.","requirements":["Defined degraded modes","User/system visibility","Business safety boundaries"],"constraints":["Some operations cannot safely degrade"],"alternatives":["Total failure","Partial degradation","Queued continuation"],"evidence":"Degradation is safe only when reduced behavior remains within defined business semantics.","tradeoffs":"Degradation can preserve availability but may provide incomplete functionality or delayed results.","decision":"Use explicit degraded modes only for capabilities with defined safe semantics.","rationale":"Silent semantic changes can be more harmful than explicit failure.","consequences":["More resilient selected workflows","Need for degraded-state communication"],"implementation_implications":["Define degraded-state transitions","Expose incomplete or delayed status","Prevent unsafe fallback behavior"],"verification_method":"Dependency-failure and degraded-mode tests.","outcome_learning":"User or downstream confusion indicates that degraded semantics need refinement.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B57-R01","D54-B57-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B57-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B57-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Resilience Architecture","subtopic":"Recovery Verification","concept":"Recovery mechanisms require verification that the system returned to an acceptable state.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_DECISION","question":"Why should recovery include explicit verification?","answer":"A recovery action such as restart, failover, or retry establishes that a recovery mechanism ran, not that the intended service state was restored. Recovery should therefore include relevant postconditions.","context":"A system has automatic or manual recovery mechanisms.","objective":"Distinguish recovery action from successful recovery outcome.","requirements":["Recovery postconditions","Health validation","State reconciliation where needed"],"constraints":["Some state may recover asynchronously"],"alternatives":["Assume recovery after action","Verify service health","Verify business state as well as infrastructure health"],"evidence":"Operational recovery can leave stale, inconsistent, or incomplete state even when processes are running.","tradeoffs":"Verification adds recovery time and complexity but improves correctness.","decision":"Verify recovery according to the actual service and business invariants.","rationale":"Process availability is not equivalent to restored system correctness.","consequences":["More trustworthy recovery state","Additional verification latency"],"implementation_implications":["Define recovery postconditions","Separate liveness from readiness and correctness checks","Reconcile state when necessary"],"verification_method":"Failure injection followed by automated recovery and postcondition verification.","outcome_learning":"Recovery mismatches should update resilience assumptions and procedures.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B57-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"verifies","record_id":"D54-B57-R04"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B58-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Observability Architecture","subtopic":"Observability Scope","concept":"Observability architecture should expose evidence needed to understand system behavior and outcomes.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should determine observability requirements?","answer":"Observability should be designed from important system states, decisions, dependencies, failure modes, user-visible outcomes, and operational questions rather than from metric volume alone.","context":"A system needs stronger diagnostic and operational visibility.","objective":"Define observability around meaningful questions and evidence.","requirements":["Operational questions","State visibility","Failure evidence","Outcome evidence"],"constraints":["Telemetry has storage and processing costs"],"alternatives":["Minimal logs","Broad telemetry collection","Question-driven observability"],"evidence":"Large telemetry volume does not guarantee that important causal information is available.","tradeoffs":"More telemetry can improve diagnosis but increases cost and noise.","decision":"Design observability around explicit diagnostic and operational questions.","rationale":"Useful observability is evidence-oriented rather than volume-oriented.","consequences":["Higher-value telemetry","Need for disciplined telemetry design"],"implementation_implications":["Define required events and states","Correlate related operations","Separate diagnostic detail from high-volume noise"],"verification_method":"Failure-scenario observability review.","outcome_learning":"Unanswered operational questions identify observability gaps.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B58-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Observability Architecture","subtopic":"Correlation","concept":"Cross-component correlation requires stable identifiers that preserve causal relationships without exposing sensitive data.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should distributed operations be correlated?","answer":"Use stable correlation or trace identifiers propagated across relevant boundaries, while keeping sensitive identifiers out of telemetry unless explicitly governed.","context":"A workflow crosses multiple services and asynchronous components.","objective":"Reconstruct distributed execution paths.","requirements":["Stable correlation identity","Propagation rules","Privacy controls"],"constraints":["Not all boundaries support automatic propagation","Sensitive identifiers require protection"],"alternatives":["Independent local identifiers","Shared correlation identifiers","Structured trace context"],"evidence":"Cross-component diagnosis requires a way to relate observations belonging to the same operation or workflow.","tradeoffs":"Propagation improves diagnosis but increases context-management and privacy requirements.","decision":"Propagate controlled correlation identifiers across relevant boundaries.","rationale":"Causal reconstruction depends on consistent identity across observations.","consequences":["Improved distributed diagnosis","Additional telemetry governance"],"implementation_implications":["Define identifier lifecycle","Propagate through synchronous and asynchronous boundaries","Prevent sensitive payload leakage"],"verification_method":"Cross-boundary trace reconstruction and privacy review.","outcome_learning":"Missing correlation points should identify propagation gaps.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B58-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B58-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B58-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Observability Architecture","subtopic":"Metrics vs Logs vs Traces","concept":"Telemetry types should be selected according to the diagnostic question they answer.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should metrics, logs, and traces be divided by purpose?","answer":"Metrics are suited to aggregated trends and thresholds, logs to discrete contextual events, and traces to distributed request or workflow paths. Architecture should combine them where a diagnostic question requires multiple evidence types.","context":"A system team is choosing telemetry coverage.","objective":"Avoid treating one telemetry type as sufficient for every diagnostic need.","requirements":["Defined diagnostic questions","Appropriate telemetry semantics","Correlation strategy"],"constraints":["Telemetry cost and retention vary"],"alternatives":["Metrics only","Logs only","Traces only","Purpose-driven combination"],"evidence":"Different telemetry forms expose different dimensions of system behavior.","tradeoffs":"Combined telemetry improves investigation but increases implementation and retention cost.","decision":"Select telemetry by diagnostic purpose and correlate complementary evidence where needed.","rationale":"Observability quality depends on evidence relevance, not a single telemetry mechanism.","consequences":["Better diagnostic coverage","More deliberate telemetry architecture"],"implementation_implications":["Define metric, event, and trace responsibilities","Avoid duplicating identical high-cardinality data","Set retention according to purpose"],"verification_method":"Scenario-based diagnostic testing.","outcome_learning":"Investigation gaps should reveal missing telemetry types or correlations.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B58-R01","D54-B58-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"expands","record_id":"D54-B58-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B58-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Observability Architecture","subtopic":"High-Cardinality Data","concept":"High-cardinality telemetry should be controlled according to diagnostic value and cost.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should high-cardinality telemetry fields be handled?","answer":"Include high-cardinality dimensions when they provide material diagnostic value, but control indexing, aggregation, retention, and exposure to avoid uncontrolled resource use.","context":"Telemetry needs detailed dimensions such as request or entity identifiers.","objective":"Balance diagnostic specificity against telemetry-system cost and risk.","requirements":["Value assessment","Cardinality controls","Retention strategy"],"constraints":["Some investigations require individual identifiers"],"alternatives":["Avoid all high-cardinality fields","Include without controls","Controlled inclusion"],"evidence":"Cardinality can materially affect telemetry storage and query behavior.","tradeoffs":"Removing detail harms diagnosis; unrestricted detail increases operational cost and query complexity.","decision":"Use high-cardinality data selectively with explicit cost and retention controls.","rationale":"Diagnostic value must justify telemetry cost and complexity.","consequences":["Better targeted diagnostic detail","Need for telemetry governance"],"implementation_implications":["Separate indexed and non-indexed fields where appropriate","Use bounded dimensions","Review retention"],"verification_method":"Telemetry load and query-behavior testing.","outcome_learning":"Actual query usage should determine which dimensions remain valuable.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B58-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B58-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B58-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Observability Architecture","subtopic":"Evidence Retention","concept":"Telemetry retention should be based on diagnostic, compliance, recovery, and learning requirements.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should telemetry retention periods be determined?","answer":"Retention should be derived from operational investigation windows, recovery needs, contractual or regulatory requirements where applicable, and the value of historical evidence.","context":"A system must define retention for operational telemetry.","objective":"Avoid both insufficient evidence and unnecessary retention.","requirements":["Retention objectives","Data classification","Access controls"],"constraints":["Storage cost","Privacy requirements","Different telemetry types have different value"],"alternatives":["Uniform retention","Purpose-specific retention","Minimal retention"],"evidence":"Retention requirements differ by purpose and data sensitivity.","tradeoffs":"Longer retention improves historical analysis but increases cost and data-governance exposure.","decision":"Use purpose-specific retention with explicit governance.","rationale":"Retention is an architecture and governance decision, not merely a storage setting.","consequences":["More intentional evidence preservation","More complex retention policies"],"implementation_implications":["Classify telemetry","Define deletion behavior","Protect retained evidence"],"verification_method":"Retention-policy validation and deletion testing.","outcome_learning":"Historical investigations should inform whether retention windows remain sufficient.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B58-R01","D54-B58-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"constrains","record_id":"D54-B58-R01"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B59-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Trust Boundaries","concept":"Security architecture should make trust boundaries explicit where data, identity, or authority changes.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should trust boundaries influence architecture decisions?","answer":"Identify points where identity, authorization context, data sensitivity, or administrative authority changes and apply explicit validation and protection requirements at those boundaries.","context":"A system crosses multiple security or administrative domains.","objective":"Make security assumptions explicit and enforceable.","requirements":["Boundary identification","Authentication or identity context","Authorization rules","Data protection"],"constraints":["Legacy systems may have implicit trust assumptions"],"alternatives":["Implicit network trust","Explicit trust-boundary controls","Zero-trust-style validation"],"evidence":"Security assumptions become risky when boundaries are implicit or assumed from network location alone.","tradeoffs":"Explicit controls add processing and implementation complexity but make trust assumptions inspectable.","decision":"Model and enforce security-relevant trust boundaries explicitly.","rationale":"Security architecture depends on assumptions about who or what is trusted and under what conditions.","consequences":["Clearer security controls","Additional boundary validation"],"implementation_implications":["Document trust zones","Validate identity and authorization at relevant boundaries","Protect sensitive data in transit and at rest as required"],"verification_method":"Threat modeling and boundary-control testing.","outcome_learning":"Security incidents or unexpected access paths should update trust-boundary models.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B59-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Least Privilege","concept":"Authorization should grant only the authority required for the intended operation and context.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should least privilege influence system architecture?","answer":"Permissions should be scoped to the minimum required resource, action, identity, and context while preserving legitimate workflows. Broad permanent authority should require explicit justification.","context":"A system needs service-to-service or user-to-resource authorization.","objective":"Reduce unnecessary authority and limit impact of compromise or misuse.","requirements":["Resource scope","Action scope","Identity context","Revocation mechanism"],"constraints":["Operational workflows may require temporary elevated actions"],"alternatives":["Broad role permissions","Fine-grained authorization","Time- or context-bound authority"],"evidence":"Excess authority increases the potential impact of unauthorized actions.","tradeoffs":"Fine-grained controls improve restriction but increase policy complexity and administration effort.","decision":"Use least-privilege authorization with explicit exceptions and controlled elevation where necessary.","rationale":"Authority should correspond to actual business and operational requirements.","consequences":["Reduced unnecessary privilege","More authorization-policy complexity"],"implementation_implications":["Separate read and write permissions","Scope service identities","Audit privileged operations"],"verification_method":"Authorization matrix review and negative access testing.","outcome_learning":"Denied legitimate actions and unnecessary grants should both feed authorization refinement.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B59-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B59-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B59-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Secrets Management","concept":"Secrets should have lifecycle and access controls separate from ordinary application configuration.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should secrets be separated from application configuration?","answer":"Secrets should be stored, accessed, rotated, and audited through controls appropriate to sensitive credentials rather than being treated as ordinary source-controlled configuration.","context":"Applications require credentials or other sensitive authentication material.","objective":"Reduce unauthorized exposure and improve credential lifecycle management.","requirements":["Access control","Rotation strategy","Auditability","Exposure minimization"],"constraints":["Applications need runtime access to secrets"],"alternatives":["Source-controlled secrets","Environment-provided secrets","Dedicated secret-management mechanism"],"evidence":"Secrets have security consequences that differ from ordinary configuration values.","tradeoffs":"Dedicated secret handling introduces infrastructure and operational requirements but reduces uncontrolled credential exposure.","decision":"Separate secret lifecycle and access management from ordinary application configuration.","rationale":"Sensitive credentials require stronger controls and lifecycle management.","consequences":["Improved credential governance","Additional secret-management dependency"],"implementation_implications":["Avoid embedding secrets in source","Limit runtime access","Define rotation and revocation procedures"],"verification_method":"Secret scanning, access review, and rotation testing.","outcome_learning":"Exposure findings should update secret boundaries and access policies.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B59-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B59-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B59-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Security Failure Modes","concept":"Security controls should fail in a manner consistent with the protected asset and business risk.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should fail-open versus fail-closed behavior be selected?","answer":"Choose failure behavior according to the consequence of unauthorized access versus denial of legitimate operation. Security-critical authorization commonly requires denying when required authorization evidence cannot be established, while availability-critical functions may require explicitly governed degraded behavior.","context":"A security control may become unavailable or unable to make a normal decision.","objective":"Define safe behavior during control failure.","requirements":["Asset criticality","Failure consequence analysis","Explicit degraded mode where applicable"],"constraints":["Availability and security objectives can conflict"],"alternatives":["Fail open","Fail closed","Controlled degraded mode"],"evidence":"Failure behavior changes the security and availability consequences of control outages.","tradeoffs":"Fail-closed behavior can protect access boundaries but may reduce availability; fail-open behavior may preserve availability but increase unauthorized-access risk.","decision":"Select failure behavior from explicit risk and asset-impact analysis rather than applying one universal rule.","rationale":"Security failure semantics are context dependent.","consequences":["Explicit security/availability tradeoff","Need for failure-mode testing"],"implementation_implications":["Document failure policy per control","Test control unavailability","Provide governed emergency procedures where required"],"verification_method":"Failure-injection and authorization-outage tests.","outcome_learning":"Actual outage consequences should inform future failure-policy decisions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B59-R01","D54-B59-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"constrains","record_id":"D54-B59-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B59-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Security Verification","concept":"Security architecture decisions require negative-path verification, not only successful authorization tests.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_DECISION","question":"What should security architecture verification include?","answer":"Verification should test unauthorized access, privilege boundaries, invalid identity context, expired authority, malformed security inputs, control failures, and relevant escalation paths in addition to legitimate flows.","context":"A security-sensitive architecture has been designed and implemented.","objective":"Verify that security boundaries resist invalid or unauthorized conditions.","requirements":["Positive tests","Negative tests","Boundary tests","Failure-mode tests"],"constraints":["Tests cannot prove absence of all vulnerabilities"],"alternatives":["Happy-path security tests","Threat-informed negative testing","Full security verification program"],"evidence":"Security failures frequently occur in invalid or unexpected paths rather than ordinary successful flows.","tradeoffs":"Broader security testing requires additional effort but provides stronger evidence of control behavior.","decision":"Require threat-informed negative-path verification for security-relevant architecture decisions.","rationale":"Security controls must be evaluated under adversarial and failure conditions.","consequences":["Improved evidence of control behavior","Additional testing scope"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain authorization matrices","Automate negative cases","Test expired and revoked authority"],"verification_method":"Security-focused negative and boundary testing.","outcome_learning":"Failed security tests should update architecture assumptions and control design.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B59-R01","D54-B59-R02","D54-B59-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"verifies","record_id":"D54-B59-R01"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B60-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Decision Reassessment","concept":"Architecture decisions should include explicit triggers for reassessment.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should an architecture decision be revisited?","answer":"Revisit a decision when its assumptions materially change, measured outcomes diverge from expectations, constraints change, new requirements appear, or evidence reveals previously unknown consequences.","context":"An architecture decision remains in use after implementation.","objective":"Prevent obsolete decisions from becoming permanent assumptions.","requirements":["Decision assumptions","Reassessment triggers","Outcome evidence"],"constraints":["Not every change warrants architectural review"],"alternatives":["Never revisit","Scheduled review only","Trigger-based reassessment"],"evidence":"Architecture decisions depend on context and assumptions that can change over time.","tradeoffs":"Frequent reassessment consumes engineering effort; insufficient reassessment preserves obsolete constraints.","decision":"Use explicit trigger-based reassessment supplemented by appropriate periodic review.","rationale":"Architecture should remain aligned with evidence and current constraints.","consequences":["More adaptive architecture governance","Need to track decision assumptions"],"implementation_implications":["Record assumptions and triggers","Link decisions to outcomes","Create review points for material changes"],"verification_method":"Decision-register review and outcome comparison.","outcome_learning":"Observed assumption failures should produce new decision evidence or superseding decisions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B60-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Decision Supersession","concept":"A new architecture decision should explicitly supersede rather than silently contradict an earlier decision.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should changed architecture decisions be represented?","answer":"Create an explicit successor decision that references the prior decision, identifies the changed assumptions or evidence, and defines the effective scope of the new decision.","context":"New evidence requires changing an existing architecture choice.","objective":"Preserve decision history while establishing the current authoritative decision.","requirements":["Previous decision reference","Change rationale","Effective scope","New consequences"],"constraints":["Legacy implementations may continue during migration"],"alternatives":["Edit old record","Delete old record","Create superseding decision"],"evidence":"Decision history is valuable for understanding why architecture changed.","tradeoffs":"Maintaining history adds documentation but prevents loss of reasoning and context.","decision":"Use explicit superseding decisions and preserve historical records as non-current decisions.","rationale":"Architecture evolution should remain traceable.","consequences":["Clear decision lineage","Need for current-state identification"],"implementation_implications":["Mark prior decision superseded","Reference migration requirements","Distinguish historical from current authority"],"verification_method":"Decision lineage and current-authority review.","outcome_learning":"Superseded decisions should remain useful as evidence about prior assumptions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B60-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B60-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B60-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Assumption Tracking","concept":"Architecture assumptions should be recorded separately from verified facts and decisions.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should architecture assumptions be explicitly tracked?","answer":"Unverified assumptions influence decisions and can become hidden dependencies. Recording them separately allows later evidence to confirm, invalidate, or refine the decision without confusing inference with established fact.","context":"An architecture decision depends on uncertain information about workload, behavior, or constraints.","objective":"Make uncertainty visible and testable.","requirements":["Explicit assumptions","Evidence status","Validation trigger"],"constraints":["Some assumptions cannot be validated immediately"],"alternatives":["Implicit assumptions","Documented assumptions","Assumptions with validation plans"],"evidence":"Decision quality depends partly on the accuracy of its underlying assumptions.","tradeoffs":"Explicit tracking adds documentation but improves future reassessment.","decision":"Record material assumptions with evidence status and validation criteria.","rationale":"Uncertainty should be represented rather than hidden inside rationale text.","consequences":["Improved decision transparency","Clearer reassessment triggers"],"implementation_implications":["Separate facts from assumptions","Assign validation methods","Link assumption results to decision updates"],"verification_method":"Assumption review against observed evidence.","outcome_learning":"Invalidated assumptions should trigger reassessment rather than silent adaptation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B60-R01","D54-B60-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B60-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B60-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Outcome Measurement","concept":"Architecture decisions should define measurable or observable consequences where practical.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_DECISION","question":"How should architecture decision outcomes be evaluated?","answer":"Define expected consequences and observable indicators before implementation when practical, then compare actual evidence against those expectations after implementation.","context":"An architecture decision has been implemented and needs outcome evaluation.","objective":"Determine whether the decision achieved its intended effects.","requirements":["Expected outcomes","Observable evidence","Comparison criteria"],"constraints":["Some architectural benefits are difficult to isolate causally"],"alternatives":["No outcome measurement","Qualitative review","Quantitative or evidence-based outcome review"],"evidence":"Outcome evidence enables learning and distinguishes intended effects from assumptions.","tradeoffs":"Measurement requires instrumentation and analysis; not every architecture benefit is directly measurable.","decision":"Define measurable or observable outcome evidence where practical and use qualitative evidence where direct measurement is unavailable.","rationale":"Architecture decisions should produce learning, not merely documentation.","consequences":["Improved decision feedback","Additional measurement effort"],"implementation_implications":["Capture relevant baseline information","Define outcome indicators","Record deviations from expected effects"],"verification_method":"Post-implementation architecture review using recorded evidence.","outcome_learning":"Outcome deviations should inform subsequent architecture decisions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B60-R01","D54-B60-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"verifies","record_id":"D54-B60-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B60-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Decision Quality and Learning","concept":"Architecture decision records should preserve evidence, uncertainty, consequences, and learning rather than only the selected option.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What makes an architecture decision record useful for future engineering reasoning?","answer":"A useful record preserves context, requirements, constraints, alternatives, evidence, assumptions, tradeoffs, decision rationale, consequences, implementation implications, verification, and observed outcomes. This allows future engineers to reason from evidence instead of repeating historical decisions without context.","context":"An organization wants architecture decisions to support future engineering work and autonomous reasoning.","objective":"Make architecture decisions reusable as engineering knowledge.","requirements":["Structured reasoning","Evidence provenance","Decision lineage","Outcome learning"],"constraints":["Historical evidence may be incomplete"],"alternatives":["Decision-only record","Narrative explanation","Structured evidence-backed decision record"],"evidence":"Decision rationale without context or outcome evidence is difficult to reuse safely.","tradeoffs":"Structured records require more discipline but provide stronger retrieval and reasoning value.","decision":"Represent architecture decisions as structured, evidence-aware records with explicit relationships and lifecycle state.","rationale":"Architecture knowledge becomes more useful when the reasoning chain is preserved.","consequences":["Higher retrieval and learning value","Greater record-maintenance discipline"],"implementation_implications":["Use stable decision IDs","Track supersession","Separate verified facts from inference","Link implementation and verification evidence"],"verification_method":"Schema validation, deduplication, relationship integrity, provenance review, and outcome-field completeness checks.","outcome_learning":"Future decisions should reference prior evidence where applicable rather than duplicating or blindly reusing historical choices.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B60-R01","D54-B60-R02","D54-B60-R03","D54-B60-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"integrates","record_id":"D54-B60-R01"},{"type":"integrates","record_id":"D54-B60-R02"},{"type":"integrates","record_id":"D54-B60-R03"},{"type":"integrates","record_id":"D54-B60-R04"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B61-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"API Architecture","subtopic":"API Boundary Selection","concept":"Choose API boundaries around stable business capabilities rather than internal implementation modules","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an architecture decision determine API boundaries?","answer":"An API boundary should expose a stable business capability or contract whose consumers can evolve independently from the implementation. Boundaries should not be created merely because internal classes, database tables, or modules exist. The decision should consider ownership, change frequency, security boundaries, consistency requirements, consumer needs, and operational responsibility.","context":"A system is being decomposed into externally consumable interfaces and internal components.","objective":"Create boundaries that minimize accidental coupling while preserving meaningful business capabilities.","requirements":["Consumers require a stable contract.","The owning component must have clear responsibility.","Contract changes must be governable."],"constraints":["Internal implementation may change independently.","Some capabilities may require transactional coordination."],"alternatives":["Expose internal modules directly.","Expose database-oriented APIs.","Expose capability-oriented APIs."],"evidence":"Capability-oriented boundaries reduce direct coupling to implementation details and make ownership explicit.","tradeoffs":"Capability-oriented interfaces require more deliberate contract design but provide stronger encapsulation than implementation-oriented interfaces.","decision":"Prefer capability-oriented API boundaries when consumers need an independently managed contract.","rationale":"Business capabilities generally provide more stable architectural boundaries than internal implementation structures.","consequences":["API design requires domain analysis.","Internal refactoring can occur without necessarily changing consumers.","Poorly chosen capability boundaries can still create coupling."],"implementation_implications":["Define explicit request and response contracts.","Keep persistence models separate from public API models where appropriate.","Assign ownership and compatibility policy to each API."],"verification_method":"Review each API boundary for business ownership, consumer independence, contract stability, and absence of unnecessary persistence leakage.","outcome_learning":"Boundary decisions should be revisited when ownership, consumer behavior, or change patterns materially change.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B61-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"API Architecture","subtopic":"Contract Stability","concept":"Separate public contract stability from internal implementation freedom","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"What should an architecture decision optimize when an API has multiple consumers?","answer":"The decision should protect the externally observable contract while allowing internal implementation changes. Compatibility requirements should be defined explicitly for fields, semantics, errors, ordering, authentication, and performance expectations rather than treating only the URL or method signature as the contract.","context":"An API is consumed by multiple independently deployed clients.","objective":"Prevent internal changes from unexpectedly breaking consumers.","requirements":["Consumer-visible behavior must be documented.","Compatibility expectations must be explicit."],"constraints":["Consumers may upgrade at different times.","Not every behavioral change is visible in a type definition."],"alternatives":["Freeze the entire implementation.","Version only the URL.","Define a behavioral contract."],"evidence":"An API contract includes observable semantics in addition to structural schema.","tradeoffs":"A broad behavioral contract improves predictability but increases documentation and verification obligations.","decision":"Define compatibility around observable behavior, not only interface shape.","rationale":"Consumers depend on semantics such as error handling, defaults, ordering, and consistency as well as fields and methods.","consequences":["Contract tests become more valuable.","Internal refactoring remains possible within the contract.","Behavioral compatibility requires explicit ownership."],"implementation_implications":["Document defaults and error semantics.","Define compatibility tests for critical consumer-visible behavior.","Treat undocumented behavior as unstable unless intentionally supported."],"verification_method":"Compare contract documentation with consumer-facing tests and inspect whether implementation changes alter observable behavior.","outcome_learning":"Consumer failures can reveal previously implicit contract dependencies that should be made explicit."],"validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B61-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B61-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B61-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"API Architecture","subtopic":"Synchronous versus Asynchronous APIs","concept":"Select synchronous or asynchronous interaction according to completion semantics and dependency behavior","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should an architecture use asynchronous interaction instead of a synchronous API call?","answer":"Asynchronous interaction is appropriate when the requester does not require immediate completion, work may be long-running, downstream dependencies are unreliable or variable in latency, or decoupling improves resilience. Synchronous interaction is appropriate when the caller requires an immediate result and the operation can meet its latency and reliability constraints within the request lifecycle.","context":"A capability may be implemented through either direct request-response or deferred processing.","objective":"Align interaction style with business completion semantics and operational constraints.","requirements":["Completion semantics must be explicit.","Clients must know whether work is accepted, completed, or failed."],"constraints":["Asynchronous processing introduces state tracking and eventual completion.","Synchronous processing couples caller availability to downstream availability."],"alternatives":["Synchronous request-response.","Asynchronous command plus status/result retrieval.","Event-driven processing."],"evidence":"Interaction style changes failure handling, latency, state management, and consumer expectations.","tradeoffs":"Asynchronous designs improve decoupling for suitable workloads but add workflow state, retry, correlation, and observability complexity.","decision":"Choose interaction style based on required completion semantics rather than implementation convenience.","rationale":"The correct model depends on whether the business operation requires immediate completion or merely reliable acceptance and later completion.","consequences":["Asynchronous APIs need correlation and lifecycle state.","Synchronous APIs need strict timeout and dependency controls."],"implementation_implications":["Define accepted versus completed states.","Provide correlation identifiers for deferred work.","Make retries and duplicate submissions safe."],"verification_method":"Trace representative success, timeout, retry, duplicate, and downstream-failure scenarios against the declared completion contract.","outcome_learning":"Observed latency and failure patterns should inform future interaction-boundary decisions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B61-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"API Architecture","subtopic":"API Versioning","concept":"Use compatibility strategy appropriate to contract evolution rather than automatically versioning every change","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should API versioning be decided?","answer":"Versioning should be driven by compatibility requirements and the ability of consumers to migrate. Backward-compatible changes may not require a new major contract, while incompatible semantic or structural changes may require an explicit migration mechanism. The architecture should define deprecation, coexistence, migration, and retirement rules before incompatible versions are introduced.","context":"A public API is expected to evolve over time.","objective":"Allow controlled evolution without unnecessary version proliferation.","requirements":["Compatibility rules must be explicit.","Consumers need a migration path for breaking changes."],"constraints":["Different consumers may migrate at different rates.","Multiple active versions increase operational and testing cost."],"alternatives":["No explicit versioning.","URL/header versioning.","Compatibility-first evolution with explicit major versions when required."],"evidence":"Versioning is a governance mechanism for incompatible contracts, not merely a naming convention.","tradeoffs":"Multiple versions reduce immediate breakage but increase maintenance and testing burden.","decision":"Use compatibility-first evolution and introduce explicit version boundaries when incompatibility cannot reasonably be avoided.","rationale":"This preserves consumer stability while limiting unnecessary long-term version proliferation.","consequences":["Deprecation policy becomes part of API governance.","Older versions require lifecycle ownership."],"implementation_implications":["Document supported versions.","Measure active consumers before retirement.","Provide migration guidance and compatibility tests."],"verification_method":"Review proposed changes against compatibility rules and verify deprecation and retirement controls.","outcome_learning":"Consumer migration behavior should influence future version-support windows.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B61-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B61-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B61-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"API Architecture","subtopic":"Error Contracts","concept":"Make API failure semantics explicit and machine-actionable","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should error behavior be part of an API architecture decision?","answer":"Consumers need to distinguish validation failures, authorization failures, transient dependency failures, conflicts, throttling, and permanent application failures. An explicit error contract allows clients to select safe behavior such as correction, retry, re-authentication, escalation, or abandonment.","context":"Multiple clients depend on an API whose operations can fail for different reasons.","objective":"Make failures predictable and safely actionable.","requirements":["Error categories must be distinguishable.","Retryable and non-retryable conditions must be defined."],"constraints":["Different consumers may have different recovery capabilities.","Internal exception types should not become accidental public contracts."],"alternatives":["Expose raw implementation errors.","Use a small generic error response.","Define stable machine-readable error categories."],"evidence":"Failure semantics directly affect client recovery behavior and operational safety.","tradeoffs":"Detailed error contracts require governance but reduce unsafe client assumptions.","decision":"Define stable machine-readable error semantics independently from internal exception representations.","rationale":"Clients need actionable contract semantics without coupling to internal implementation.","consequences":["Error categories require lifecycle management.","Observability should correlate external errors with internal causes."],"implementation_implications":["Define stable error codes/categories.","Separate safe client detail from sensitive internal diagnostics.","Document retryability and corrective action."],"verification_method":"Test representative failure classes and verify that clients can distinguish recovery strategies without inspecting internal implementation details.","outcome_learning":"Repeated ambiguous failures indicate missing contract categories or documentation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B61-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B61-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B62-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Integration Architecture","subtopic":"Integration Boundary","concept":"Choose integration boundaries around independently owned capabilities and explicit contracts","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an architecture choose integration boundaries between systems?","answer":"Integration boundaries should reflect independent ownership, lifecycle, security, reliability, and change characteristics. A boundary is useful when it allows systems to evolve independently while preserving the information and consistency guarantees required by the business workflow.","context":"Multiple systems must exchange data or invoke capabilities.","objective":"Avoid tight coupling while retaining required business semantics.","requirements":["Ownership must be identifiable.","Contract responsibilities must be explicit.","Required consistency guarantees must be known."],"constraints":["Existing systems may expose poorly aligned interfaces.","Cross-system workflows may require coordination."],"alternatives":["Direct database integration.","Shared service interface.","Event-based integration.","Anti-corruption or translation layer."],"evidence":"Integration boundaries determine coupling, failure propagation, and change coordination.","tradeoffs":"Additional boundaries and translation layers add complexity but can isolate incompatible models and lifecycles.","decision":"Prefer explicit service or event contracts over direct persistence coupling when independent evolution is required.","rationale":"Database-level coupling exposes implementation details and makes independent evolution difficult.","consequences":["Contracts require ownership and compatibility governance.","Translation may be required between different domain models."],"implementation_implications":["Define contract ownership.","Avoid making another system's database schema the integration contract.","Capture correlation and failure semantics."],"verification_method":"Map each integration to an owner, contract, failure model, and dependency lifecycle.","outcome_learning":"Integration incidents should be used to identify hidden coupling and strengthen explicit contracts.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B62-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Integration Architecture","subtopic":"Anti-Corruption Layer","concept":"Use translation boundaries when external models should not become internal domain models","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When is an anti-corruption or translation layer justified?","answer":"A translation layer is justified when an external system's terminology, lifecycle, data model, or failure semantics should not directly shape the internal domain model. It is especially valuable when the external contract is legacy, unstable, vendor-specific, or semantically incompatible with the internal model.","context":"An application integrates with an externally owned system whose model differs from the internal domain.","objective":"Prevent external model assumptions from spreading through internal architecture.","requirements":["Translation semantics must be explicit.","Ownership of the translation boundary must be defined."],"constraints":["Translation introduces mapping logic and maintenance cost."],"alternatives":["Directly adopt the external model.","Translate at the integration boundary.","Create a shared canonical model."],"evidence":"Model translation can isolate external coupling when semantic differences are material.","tradeoffs":"Translation increases code and test surface but can reduce architectural contamination.","decision":"Use a translation boundary when external and internal models have materially different ownership or semantics.","rationale":"The boundary localizes incompatibility instead of distributing it throughout the system.","consequences":["Mapping logic becomes a maintained architectural component.","Contract changes may be isolated to the boundary."],"implementation_implications":["Keep external DTOs separate from internal domain objects.","Test mapping for semantic loss and invalid states.","Track external contract versions."],"verification_method":"Change the external model in a controlled compatibility test and verify that internal domain semantics remain stable where the contract permits.","outcome_learning":"Repeated mapping exceptions can indicate that the boundary or internal model needs redesign.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B62-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B62-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B62-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Integration Architecture","subtopic":"Integration Failure Isolation","concept":"Prevent one integration dependency from unnecessarily propagating failure through unrelated capabilities","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an architecture decide whether an integration dependency needs isolation?","answer":"Isolation is justified when dependency failure can affect unrelated business capabilities, when recovery is slow or uncertain, or when the dependency has materially different availability characteristics. Isolation mechanisms may include asynchronous queues, bounded concurrency, timeouts, bulkheads, cached representations, or degraded modes, selected according to business semantics.","context":"A service depends on an external system whose availability and latency are not fully controlled.","objective":"Limit failure propagation while preserving correct business semantics.","requirements":["Timeout behavior must be defined.","Fallback behavior must not violate business invariants."],"constraints":["Isolation can introduce stale data or deferred completion.","Some operations cannot safely degrade."],"alternatives":["Direct unbounded dependency calls.","Bounded synchronous dependency.","Asynchronous isolation.","Explicit degraded mode."],"evidence":"Dependency failure propagation is an architectural coupling risk.","tradeoffs":"Isolation adds state and operational complexity but can reduce correlated failures.","decision":"Introduce isolation where dependency failure could materially affect unrelated or higher-priority capabilities.","rationale":"Failure containment should be proportional to dependency criticality and business impact.","consequences":["Failure behavior becomes explicit.","Some requests may complete later or with reduced functionality."],"implementation_implications":["Apply bounded timeouts and concurrency.","Define retry limits.","Separate safe fallback from business-critical operations."],"verification_method":"Inject dependency timeout, error, and overload conditions and verify that unrelated capabilities remain within their stated failure boundaries.","outcome_learning":"Dependency failure patterns should update isolation thresholds and fallback decisions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B62-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B62-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B62-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Integration Architecture","subtopic":"Canonical Data Models","concept":"Use canonical integration models only when shared semantic normalization provides sufficient value","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When should a canonical data model be introduced across integrations?","answer":"A canonical model is justified when many systems repeatedly exchange the same business concepts and independent pairwise mappings would create substantial duplication or inconsistency. It is not automatically appropriate because a canonical model can become a central coupling point and may flatten meaningful domain differences.","context":"Several systems exchange overlapping business information using different representations.","objective":"Evaluate whether semantic normalization outweighs centralized coupling.","requirements":["Shared semantics must be genuinely stable.","Ownership and evolution of the canonical model must be explicit."],"constraints":["Domain meanings may differ between systems.","A canonical model requires broad governance."],"alternatives":["Pairwise translation.","Canonical model.","Shared domain service."],"evidence":"Canonical models can reduce repeated transformations but can also impose a lowest-common-denominator abstraction.","tradeoffs":"Central consistency can reduce duplication while increasing governance and change coordination.","decision":"Adopt a canonical model only when repeated semantic alignment materially outweighs the coupling introduced by centralization.","rationale":"The model should solve a demonstrated integration problem rather than become an architectural default.","consequences":["Canonical changes may affect many integrations.","Model ownership becomes strategically important."],"implementation_implications":["Define semantic ownership.","Version the canonical contract carefully.","Allow domain-specific extensions where necessary."],"verification_method":"Compare mapping duplication, semantic inconsistency, change frequency, and number of affected integrations before and after adoption.","outcome_learning":"If the canonical model repeatedly requires incompatible exceptions, reconsider whether separate bounded models are more appropriate.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B62-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"contrasts_with","record_id":"D54-B62-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B62-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Integration Architecture","subtopic":"Integration Ownership","concept":"Assign explicit ownership for integration contracts and failure handling","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why must integration ownership be explicit?","answer":"Without clear ownership, contract changes, failures, security responsibilities, and lifecycle decisions can fall between teams. Ownership should identify who maintains the interface, defines compatibility, responds to failures, and approves material changes.","context":"An integration crosses organizational or system boundaries.","objective":"Prevent responsibility gaps at architectural boundaries.","requirements":["Contract owner must be identifiable.","Operational ownership must be identifiable.","Change authority must be defined."],"constraints":["Multiple teams may consume or contribute to an integration."],"alternatives":["Shared informal ownership.","Producer ownership.","Explicit producer and consumer responsibilities."],"evidence":"Integration boundaries create coordination responsibilities that must be assigned.","tradeoffs":"Explicit ownership adds governance overhead but reduces ambiguity during change and failure.","decision":"Assign explicit contract, operational, and change responsibilities to the integration boundary.","rationale":"Architectural boundaries without responsibility boundaries are difficult to operate safely.","consequences":["Ownership must be maintained as teams and systems change.","Escalation paths become part of the architecture record."],"implementation_implications":["Record owner and escalation metadata.","Define compatibility approval rules.","Link operational telemetry to ownership."],"verification_method":"Review each integration and confirm that contract, security, operational, and change responsibilities are assigned.","outcome_learning":"Incidents with unclear responsibility should trigger ownership review.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B62-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B62-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B63-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Messaging Architecture","subtopic":"Queue versus Direct Invocation","concept":"Choose messaging when temporal decoupling or buffering is a material requirement","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When is a message-based architecture justified instead of direct invocation?","answer":"Messaging is justified when producers and consumers should operate independently in time, when work must be buffered against variable consumer capacity, or when durable asynchronous processing is part of the business workflow. Direct invocation is simpler when immediate request-response semantics are required and dependency availability can be managed within the request boundary.","context":"A producer must trigger work in another component.","objective":"Match communication architecture to temporal and workload characteristics.","requirements":["Message ownership and delivery semantics must be defined.","Consumers must handle duplicates or redelivery when applicable."],"constraints":["Messaging introduces delivery, ordering, replay, and operational concerns."],"alternatives":["Direct synchronous call.","Durable queue.","Event stream."],"evidence":"Messaging changes temporal coupling and workload buffering characteristics.","tradeoffs":"Messaging improves decoupling but adds infrastructure and state-management complexity.","decision":"Use messaging when temporal decoupling, buffering, or durable asynchronous processing materially contributes to the required behavior.","rationale":"A message should solve a real coupling or workload problem rather than replace simple calls without need.","consequences":["Delivery semantics become architectural requirements.","Observability must cover message lifecycle."],"implementation_implications":["Define acknowledgement and retry semantics.","Define idempotency expectations.","Track message correlation and processing state."],"verification_method":"Exercise producer success, consumer delay, duplicate delivery, consumer failure, and message recovery scenarios.","outcome_learning":"Observed queue depth, processing latency, and retry patterns should inform capacity and topology decisions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B63-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Messaging Architecture","subtopic":"Delivery Semantics","concept":"Select message delivery guarantees according to business consequences of duplication and loss","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should message delivery semantics be selected?","answer":"Delivery semantics should be selected by determining whether loss, duplication, reordering, or delayed processing is acceptable for the business operation. At-least-once delivery commonly requires idempotent consumers, while stronger guarantees require additional coordination and may still depend on end-to-end business semantics.","context":"A system must define how messages behave under failures and retries.","objective":"Align messaging guarantees with actual business risk.","requirements":["Loss and duplication consequences must be identified.","Consumer behavior under redelivery must be defined."],"constraints":["Infrastructure guarantees do not automatically guarantee business-level exactly-once effects."],"alternatives":["At-most-once.","At-least-once.","Stronger coordinated delivery/effect semantics."],"evidence":"Delivery guarantees and business effect guarantees are related but not identical.","tradeoffs":"Stronger delivery coordination can increase complexity, latency, and operational coupling.","decision":"Choose the simplest delivery model that satisfies business loss and duplication requirements, with idempotency where duplicates are possible.","rationale":"Business consequences, not terminology alone, should determine required guarantees.","consequences":["Consumers may need deduplication or idempotency keys.","Failure semantics become explicit."],"implementation_implications":["Persist or derive stable operation identifiers where required.","Define retry and acknowledgement boundaries.","Do not equate successful delivery with successful business processing."],"verification_method":"Inject lost acknowledgements, duplicate deliveries, consumer crashes, and retries and verify business effects remain within the declared guarantee.","outcome_learning":"Duplicate or lost-effect incidents should feed back into delivery and idempotency decisions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B63-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B63-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B63-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Messaging Architecture","subtopic":"Ordering","concept":"Require message ordering only where business semantics depend on it","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should message ordering be an explicit architecture requirement?","answer":"Ordering should be required when processing sequence changes the valid business state or outcome. If messages are independent or commutative, imposing global ordering creates unnecessary throughput and availability constraints. Where ordering matters, the scope of ordering should be limited to the smallest meaningful key or aggregate.","context":"An event or message workflow contains potentially related messages.","objective":"Preserve required sequencing without imposing unnecessary global serialization.","requirements":["The business meaning of sequence must be identified.","Ordering scope must be explicit."],"constraints":["Global ordering can reduce parallelism.","Distributed systems may not provide a natural total order."],"alternatives":["No ordering requirement.","Per-key ordering.","Global ordering."],"evidence":"Ordering requirements should correspond to state-transition dependencies.","tradeoffs":"Stronger ordering simplifies some reasoning but can reduce scalability and increase coordination.","decision":"Use the narrowest ordering scope that preserves required business semantics.","rationale":"Per-aggregate or per-key ordering often preserves business correctness while allowing unrelated work to proceed independently.","consequences":["Partitioning and routing may become tied to the ordering key.","Consumers must handle delayed or invalid sequence conditions."],"implementation_implications":["Define ordering key semantics.","Detect unexpected sequence gaps where required.","Avoid assuming transport order equals business order."],"verification_method":"Submit concurrent and reordered messages and verify only business-required sequences are constrained.","outcome_learning":"Observed contention or ordering violations should inform whether the ordering scope is too broad or too narrow.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B63-R01","D54-B63-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"constrains","record_id":"D54-B63-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B63-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Messaging Architecture","subtopic":"Retry and Dead-Letter Handling","concept":"Treat retry policy and terminal failure handling as part of message architecture","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should retry and dead-letter behavior be decided at architecture level?","answer":"Retries can amplify overload, repeat non-idempotent effects, and delay recognition of permanent failures. Architecture should define which failures are retryable, retry limits and backoff behavior, when messages become terminal failures, and how those failures are investigated or replayed.","context":"A durable message workflow must survive transient failures without creating uncontrolled repeated work.","objective":"Create predictable recovery behavior for failed message processing.","requirements":["Retryable failure classes must be defined.","Retry limits must exist.","Terminal failures must remain observable."],"constraints":["Some failures are deterministic and should not be retried.","Repeated retries can increase downstream load."],"alternatives":["Unlimited automatic retry.","Bounded retry with dead-letter handling.","Immediate failure."],"evidence":"Failure classification materially changes system load and recovery behavior.","tradeoffs":"Retries improve transient-failure recovery but increase duplicate and overload risk.","decision":"Use bounded, failure-aware retries with explicit terminal handling for messages that cannot safely or usefully be retried.","rationale":"Recovery must not become an uncontrolled source of additional failure.","consequences":["Dead-letter or equivalent terminal state requires operational ownership.","Replay must be controlled."],"implementation_implications":["Use bounded exponential or otherwise justified backoff.","Classify permanent versus transient errors.","Record retry attempts and failure causes."],"verification_method":"Inject transient and permanent failures and verify retry counts, backoff, terminal routing, and replay behavior.","outcome_learning":"Retry exhaustion patterns should inform dependency reliability and error classification decisions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B63-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B63-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B63-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Messaging Architecture","subtopic":"Message Schema Evolution","concept":"Evolve message contracts without assuming all consumers upgrade simultaneously","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should message schemas evolve when consumers have independent deployment schedules?","answer":"Schema evolution should preserve compatibility across the supported producer and consumer versions. Changes should be classified as additive or incompatible, with migration sequencing, compatibility testing, and retirement rules for obsolete fields or versions.","context":"An event or command schema is consumed by independently deployed components.","objective":"Allow message evolution without breaking active consumers.","requirements":["Supported compatibility window must be defined.","Consumers must tolerate supported schema evolution."],"constraints":["Old consumers may remain active during deployment.","Historical messages may need replay."],"alternatives":["Breaking schema replacement.","Backward-compatible evolution.","Versioned parallel schemas."],"evidence":"Independent deployment creates temporal version skew between producers and consumers.","tradeoffs":"Compatibility support increases implementation and testing complexity but enables independent deployment.","decision":"Prefer backward-compatible schema evolution and explicit migration paths for incompatible changes.","rationale":"Temporal decoupling is valuable only when contract evolution respects version skew.","consequences":["Schema compatibility becomes part of deployment planning.","Historical data and replay requirements must be considered."],"implementation_implications":["Add fields without invalidating supported consumers where possible.","Define defaults and unknown-field behavior.","Test producer-consumer version combinations."],"verification_method":"Test supported producer and consumer version matrices, including replay of historical messages where applicable.","outcome_learning":"Observed compatibility failures should update the supported schema policy and contract tests.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B63-R01","D54-B63-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B63-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B64-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Deployment Architecture","subtopic":"Deployment Unit","concept":"Define deployment units around independent lifecycle and scaling requirements","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should deployment boundaries be selected?","answer":"A deployment unit should isolate components that need independent release, rollback, scaling, security, or resource characteristics. Splitting components merely because they are separate modules can increase operational complexity without providing meaningful independence.","context":"A system can be deployed as one unit or multiple independently deployable units.","objective":"Balance deployment independence against operational complexity.","requirements":["Independent lifecycle requirements must be identified.","Operational ownership must be defined."],"constraints":["Each additional unit adds deployment, networking, observability, and failure considerations."],"alternatives":["Monolithic deployment.","Multiple services.","Hybrid deployment units."],"evidence":"Deployment boundaries affect operational coupling as well as organizational independence.","tradeoffs":"More units can improve independent change and scaling but increase distributed-system complexity.","decision":"Create independent deployment units only when lifecycle, scaling, isolation, or ownership requirements justify the additional operational surface.","rationale":"Deployment independence has value only when the system actually benefits from it.","consequences":["Deployment topology becomes an architectural dependency.","More units require stronger operational automation."],"implementation_implications":["Define release ownership.","Define health and dependency behavior.","Automate deployment and rollback consistently."],"verification_method":"Evaluate each proposed deployment boundary against independent change, scale, isolation, and operational ownership requirements.","outcome_learning":"Deployment incidents and coordination delays should inform future boundary decisions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B64-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Deployment Architecture","subtopic":"Immutable versus Mutable Deployment","concept":"Prefer deployment models that make released artifacts identifiable and reproducible","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should guide the choice between mutable and immutable deployment practices?","answer":"An architecture should prefer artifact-based deployment when reproducibility, rollback, provenance, and environment consistency are important. Mutable in-place changes can be appropriate in constrained environments but increase the difficulty of determining exactly what is running.","context":"The organization needs controlled deployment and rollback behavior.","objective":"Ensure deployed state can be identified and reproduced.","requirements":["Artifact identity must be traceable.","Deployment state must be observable."],"constraints":["Some environments may impose mutable infrastructure constraints."],"alternatives":["Mutable in-place updates.","Immutable artifact replacement.","Hybrid approach."],"evidence":"Identifiable artifacts improve traceability of deployed software state.","tradeoffs":"Immutable replacement can require stronger automation and resource coordination but simplifies state reasoning.","decision":"Use immutable or artifact-identified deployment where reproducibility and rollback are material requirements.","rationale":"Knowing exactly which artifact is running reduces ambiguity during verification and incident analysis.","consequences":["Artifact metadata becomes operationally important.","Rollback can be tied to known versions."],"implementation_implications":["Record artifact digest/version.","Associate deployments with source and build provenance.","Verify deployed artifact identity."],"verification_method":"Compare declared deployment artifact identity with the artifact actually running in each environment.","outcome_learning":"Deployment drift should trigger investigation of mutable changes or incomplete deployment controls.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B64-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B64-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B64-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Deployment Architecture","subtopic":"Blue-Green versus Rolling Replacement","concept":"Select deployment rollout topology according to rollback, capacity, and compatibility requirements","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should a rollout topology be selected?","answer":"Blue-green replacement provides a distinct alternate environment and can simplify rapid traffic reversal, while rolling replacement updates instances incrementally and can reduce temporary capacity requirements. The decision should consider rollback speed, state compatibility, capacity, traffic control, and version interoperability.","context":"A service requires controlled production rollout.","objective":"Choose a rollout mechanism consistent with recovery and capacity requirements.","requirements":["Rollback semantics must be defined.","Mixed-version behavior must be understood for rolling approaches."],"constraints":["Blue-green may require additional capacity.","Rolling deployment can expose consumers to mixed versions."],"alternatives":["Blue-green.","Rolling.","Canary or other progressive rollout."],"evidence":"Rollout topology changes failure exposure and rollback mechanics.","tradeoffs":"Isolation and rapid reversal can require additional infrastructure capacity; incremental rollout can reduce capacity overhead while increasing mixed-version complexity.","decision":"Select rollout topology based on rollback, capacity, and version-compatibility requirements rather than convention.","rationale":"Deployment topology is a risk-control decision tied to system behavior.","consequences":["Version compatibility must be tested where overlap exists.","Capacity planning must account for rollout state."],"implementation_implications":["Define traffic switching or replacement order.","Test rollback from each rollout stage.","Validate data compatibility across versions."],"verification_method":"Exercise staged rollout and rollback while checking service health, data compatibility, and traffic behavior.","outcome_learning":"Rollout failures should update deployment sequencing and compatibility assumptions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B64-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B64-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B64-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Deployment Architecture","subtopic":"Configuration Management","concept":"Separate deployable artifact identity from environment-specific configuration","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should application configuration be separated from the deployment artifact?","answer":"Separating configuration from artifact identity allows the same tested artifact to be promoted across environments while environment-specific values are supplied through controlled configuration. Secrets, endpoints, feature settings, and operational parameters should not be embedded as mutable source-level assumptions.","context":"The same software artifact must operate in multiple environments.","objective":"Preserve artifact reproducibility while allowing environment-specific behavior.","requirements":["Configuration sources must be controlled.","Secrets must not be embedded in source or artifacts.","Configuration changes must be auditable where required."],"constraints":["Some configuration changes can alter behavior materially.","Configuration itself may require validation."],"alternatives":["Embed environment settings in builds.","Externalize configuration.","Compile selected immutable settings into artifacts."],"evidence":"Separating environment configuration reduces rebuild requirements and clarifies deployment identity.","tradeoffs":"External configuration adds configuration-management and validation requirements.","decision":"Externalize environment-specific configuration while treating behaviorally significant configuration as governed and version-aware.","rationale":"Artifact identity and environment configuration have different lifecycle concerns.","consequences":["Configuration becomes part of deployment verification.","Misconfiguration can produce failures even when the artifact is correct."],"implementation_implications":["Validate configuration schema.","Track effective configuration where safe.","Protect secrets through appropriate secret-management mechanisms."],"verification_method":"Deploy the same artifact under multiple configurations and verify configuration validation, secret handling, and expected behavior.","outcome_learning":"Configuration-related incidents should identify missing validation or unsafe defaults.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B64-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B64-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B64-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Deployment Architecture","subtopic":"Rollback Strategy","concept":"Design rollback around state compatibility rather than application artifact replacement alone","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why can application rollback fail even when an earlier artifact is available?","answer":"Rollback can be unsafe when the newer release has changed persistent data, external contracts, messages, or irreversible side effects. A deployment decision must therefore evaluate data and integration compatibility, not only whether an older executable artifact can be restored.","context":"A deployment requires a recoverable path after a failed release.","objective":"Ensure rollback is technically and semantically feasible.","requirements":["Rollback compatibility must be analyzed.","Persistent state changes must be considered.","External side effects must be identified."],"constraints":["Some state transitions cannot be reversed.","External consumers may already have observed the new behavior."],"alternatives":["Binary rollback only.","Backward-compatible migration.","Forward-fix strategy."],"evidence":"Runtime artifact replacement does not reverse persistent or external effects.","tradeoffs":"Backward compatibility increases migration complexity but can preserve rollback options.","decision":"Treat rollback as an end-to-end state compatibility decision rather than an artifact restoration operation.","rationale":"Recovery must account for the entire system state affected by a release.","consequences":["Migration design becomes part of release planning.","Some failures may require forward fixes rather than binary rollback."],"implementation_implications":["Use backward-compatible schema changes where rollback is required.","Record irreversible side effects.","Define recovery procedures before release."],"verification_method":"Test rollback after representative schema, message, and external-effect changes and verify that the prior version remains safe to operate.","outcome_learning":"Rollback failures should update migration and release compatibility rules.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B64-R03","D54-B64-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B64-R03"},{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B64-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B65-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Runtime Architecture","subtopic":"Process versus Thread versus Async Concurrency","concept":"Choose concurrency mechanisms according to workload characteristics and correctness requirements","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an architecture choose between processes, threads, and asynchronous execution?","answer":"Concurrency architecture should be selected according to workload characteristics, runtime semantics, isolation requirements, resource behavior, and correctness constraints. CPU-bound workloads may require parallel execution mechanisms, while I/O-heavy workloads may benefit from asynchronous or thread-based concurrency depending on runtime and library behavior.","context":"A component must handle concurrent work efficiently and safely.","objective":"Select a concurrency model that matches workload and runtime behavior.","requirements":["Workload must be characterized.","Shared-state risks must be identified.","Cancellation and failure behavior must be defined."],"constraints":["Language runtimes differ.","Libraries may not be concurrency-safe or asynchronous."],"alternatives":["Processes.","Threads.","Async tasks.","Sequential execution."],"evidence":"Concurrency mechanisms have different scheduling, isolation, sharing, and synchronization properties.","tradeoffs":"Higher concurrency can improve throughput or latency while increasing coordination and failure complexity.","decision":"Choose concurrency based on measured workload characteristics and required isolation rather than assuming one mechanism is universally superior.","rationale":"Concurrency is a workload and runtime decision, not a generic optimization.","consequences":["Testing must include concurrency-specific failures.","Resource limits become important."],"implementation_implications":["Measure CPU versus I/O behavior.","Define shared-state ownership.","Bound concurrency rather than assuming unlimited parallelism."],"verification_method":"Benchmark representative workloads and test race, cancellation, timeout, and resource-exhaustion scenarios.","outcome_learning":"Production contention and saturation data should inform concurrency limits and architecture changes.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B65-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Runtime Architecture","subtopic":"Resource Isolation","concept":"Isolate workloads when contention can violate critical service objectives","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should workloads receive separate resource pools?","answer":"Separate resource pools are justified when workloads have materially different criticality, resource profiles, failure behavior, or latency requirements and contention between them could violate important objectives. Isolation can occur at process, worker, service, scheduling, or infrastructure boundaries.","context":"Multiple workloads share runtime resources.","objective":"Prevent lower-priority or unbounded workloads from destabilizing critical work.","requirements":["Criticality and resource requirements must be identified.","Capacity limits must be defined."],"constraints":["Isolation consumes additional resources.","Over-isolation can fragment capacity."],"alternatives":["Shared pool.","Priority scheduling.","Separate resource pools."],"evidence":"Shared resource contention can create correlated latency and availability failures.","tradeoffs":"Isolation improves containment but may reduce aggregate utilization.","decision":"Introduce resource isolation where uncontrolled contention has unacceptable consequences.","rationale":"Critical workloads should not depend on unrestricted behavior of unrelated workloads.","consequences":["Capacity planning becomes more explicit.","Unused capacity may be less shareable."],"implementation_implications":["Define quotas and concurrency limits.","Separate critical and best-effort workloads where justified.","Monitor saturation per pool."],"verification_method":"Generate contention from non-critical workloads and verify that critical workload objectives remain within defined limits.","outcome_learning":"Observed contention should inform pool sizing and workload classification.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B65-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B65-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B65-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Runtime Architecture","subtopic":"Timeout Architecture","concept":"Define timeouts at dependency boundaries to prevent unbounded resource occupation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should timeout decisions be made for distributed operations?","answer":"Timeouts should reflect the maximum useful waiting period for the business operation and the latency budget of the dependency chain. They should be bounded at appropriate layers and coordinated with retries so that cumulative retry time does not exceed the caller's useful deadline.","context":"A request depends on one or more potentially slow external operations.","objective":"Prevent indefinitely occupied resources and uncontrolled latency propagation.","requirements":["Maximum useful operation duration must be known.","Retry budgets must fit within the overall deadline."],"constraints":["A timeout does not cancel work automatically in every system.","Downstream work may continue after the caller stops waiting."],"alternatives":["No timeout.","Single global timeout.","Layered deadline-aware timeouts."],"evidence":"Unbounded waiting can consume concurrency capacity and propagate latency failures.","tradeoffs":"Short timeouts may increase false failures; long timeouts can exhaust resources.","decision":"Use explicit, deadline-aware timeouts at dependency boundaries and coordinate them with cancellation and retry behavior.","rationale":"Timeouts should protect system capacity while preserving enough time for legitimate completion.","consequences":["Timeout values require measurement and review.","Cancellation semantics become important."],"implementation_implications":["Propagate remaining deadline where supported.","Avoid retrying beyond the caller's useful deadline.","Distinguish timeout from successful completion."],"verification_method":"Inject slow and non-responsive dependencies and verify timeout, cancellation, retry, and resource-release behavior.","outcome_learning":"Latency distributions and timeout-related failures should inform revised deadlines.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B65-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"constrains","record_id":"D54-B65-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B65-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Runtime Architecture","subtopic":"Backpressure","concept":"Use backpressure when producers can exceed safe consumer capacity","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should backpressure be part of an architecture decision?","answer":"Backpressure is required when producers can generate work faster than consumers can safely process it and unbounded buffering could exhaust memory, storage, concurrency, or downstream capacity. The architecture should define admission, queue limits, shedding, blocking, or rate-control behavior.","context":"A pipeline has variable producer and consumer rates.","objective":"Prevent uncontrolled accumulation and resource exhaustion.","requirements":["Safe processing capacity must be bounded.","Overflow behavior must be explicit."],"constraints":["Rejecting or delaying work may affect business behavior.","Buffering can shift rather than eliminate overload."],"alternatives":["Unbounded queue.","Bounded queue with backpressure.","Load shedding.","Rate limiting."],"evidence":"Producer-consumer rate mismatch can accumulate work without bound.","tradeoffs":"Backpressure protects system stability but can increase producer latency or reject work.","decision":"Use bounded capacity and explicit backpressure where sustained producer rate can exceed safe consumer capacity.","rationale":"Capacity limits must be reflected in admission behavior rather than hidden behind unlimited buffering.","consequences":["Clients may observe delays or rejection.","Capacity becomes a visible architectural property."],"implementation_implications":["Define queue limits.","Expose meaningful overload responses where applicable.","Prioritize critical work if business requirements demand it."],"verification_method":"Generate sustained overload and verify bounded resource use, admission behavior, and recovery after load decreases.","outcome_learning":"Queue depth and rejection patterns should inform capacity and scaling decisions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B65-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B65-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B65-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Runtime Architecture","subtopic":"Graceful Shutdown","concept":"Treat shutdown behavior as an architectural lifecycle requirement for stateful or in-flight work","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When does graceful shutdown require explicit architecture design?","answer":"Graceful shutdown requires explicit design when processes can hold in-flight requests, transactions, locks, queued messages, temporary state, or externally visible work. The architecture should define how new work is stopped, existing work is drained or cancelled, and incomplete operations are recovered safely.","context":"A service or worker must be restarted, redeployed, or terminated without corrupting work.","objective":"Prevent unsafe termination of in-flight operations.","requirements":["Admission stop behavior must be defined.","In-flight work handling must be defined.","Recovery of incomplete work must be possible where required."],"constraints":["Shutdown time is bounded.","Some operations cannot safely be interrupted at arbitrary points."],"alternatives":["Immediate termination.","Drain then terminate.","Cancellation with durable recovery."],"evidence":"Process termination can interrupt application state and external operations.","tradeoffs":"Graceful draining can increase shutdown time while reducing incomplete-work risk.","decision":"Define explicit shutdown lifecycle behavior for workloads where termination can affect correctness or durability.","rationale":"Lifecycle events are part of system behavior, not merely infrastructure details.","consequences":["Deployment systems must respect shutdown semantics.","Tests must cover termination during active work."],"implementation_implications":["Stop new admission before termination.","Drain or safely cancel in-flight work.","Persist recoverable progress where required."],"verification_method":"Terminate components during representative operations and verify no prohibited data corruption, lost durable work, or orphaned locks occurs.","outcome_learning":"Shutdown failures should inform lifecycle and recovery design.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B65-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B65-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B66-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Observability Architecture","subtopic":"Observability Scope","concept":"Design observability around questions required to operate and verify the system","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should determine the scope of observability architecture?","answer":"Observability should be designed around the questions operators, developers, security personnel, and governance functions must answer: what happened, where, when, for whom, under which version and configuration, and with what outcome. Telemetry should be selected for decision value rather than collected indiscriminately.","context":"A system requires diagnosis, verification, and operational visibility.","objective":"Provide sufficient evidence to understand behavior and failures.","requirements":["Critical workflows must be traceable.","Relevant versions and correlation identifiers must be available.","Telemetry must respect security and privacy requirements."],"constraints":["Telemetry consumes resources and can expose sensitive data.","Too much low-value telemetry can reduce signal quality."],"alternatives":["Logs only.","Metrics and logs.","Logs, metrics, traces, and domain events selected by operational questions."],"evidence":"Different telemetry types answer different operational questions.","tradeoffs":"Broader telemetry improves diagnostic coverage but increases cost, storage, and governance requirements.","decision":"Design observability from required operational and verification questions backward to telemetry signals.","rationale":"Telemetry should provide actionable evidence rather than merely increase data volume.","consequences":["Critical workflows require explicit observability requirements.","Telemetry becomes part of architecture design."],"implementation_implications":["Define correlation identifiers.","Capture outcome and error context safely.","Map important business operations to telemetry."],"verification_method":"Attempt to diagnose representative failures using only the designed telemetry and identify missing evidence.","outcome_learning":"Post-incident evidence gaps should become observability requirements.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B66-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Observability Architecture","subtopic":"Correlation and Traceability","concept":"Propagate stable correlation context across distributed workflow boundaries","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why is correlation context an architecture concern?","answer":"Without correlation context, distributed operations can produce fragmented evidence that cannot be reliably associated with a single business operation. The architecture should define stable identifiers and propagation rules across synchronous calls, asynchronous messages, jobs, and relevant audit records.","context":"A business operation crosses multiple components and communication mechanisms.","objective":"Reconstruct end-to-end execution from distributed evidence.","requirements":["Identifiers must be generated and propagated consistently.","Correlation data must not expose inappropriate sensitive information."],"constraints":["Not every technical event belongs to one business operation.","Identifiers can be lost at asynchronous boundaries without explicit propagation."],"alternatives":["Local identifiers only.","Request identifier propagation.","Explicit business-operation and technical-correlation identifiers."],"evidence":"Distributed diagnosis requires a way to associate related events without relying solely on timestamps.","tradeoffs":"Propagation adds contract and telemetry requirements but improves traceability.","decision":"Define explicit correlation propagation for workflows where cross-component reconstruction is required.","rationale":"Traceability must survive communication and execution boundaries.","consequences":["Message and API contracts may carry correlation metadata.","Telemetry schemas need consistent identifiers."],"implementation_implications":["Separate business operation identifiers from low-level request identifiers when their lifecycles differ.","Propagate correlation across asynchronous boundaries.","Validate identifier continuity."],"verification_method":"Trace a representative distributed workflow across synchronous and asynchronous components and verify complete correlation.","outcome_learning":"Missing correlation segments should be treated as observability defects.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B66-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B66-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B66-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Observability Architecture","subtopic":"Business Metrics versus Technical Metrics","concept":"Include business outcome signals when technical health alone cannot establish correctness","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should observability include business-level outcome signals?","answer":"A system can be technically available while producing incorrect, incomplete, delayed, or economically harmful business outcomes. Where correctness depends on domain behavior, observability should include appropriate business outcome measurements alongside technical metrics.","context":"Technical monitoring shows infrastructure and application health but not complete business correctness.","objective":"Detect divergence between technical operation and business outcome.","requirements":["Business outcome signals must have defined semantics.","Sensitive business data must be minimized."],"constraints":["Business metrics can be more sensitive and harder to normalize.","Some outcomes are delayed."],"alternatives":["Technical metrics only.","Technical plus domain outcome metrics."],"evidence":"Availability and resource health do not prove business correctness.","tradeoffs":"Business telemetry improves decision value but requires domain ownership and governance.","decision":"Include business outcome signals for workflows where technical health is insufficient to establish successful business behavior.","rationale":"Operational verification should measure the actual outcome when possible.","consequences":["Business teams may participate in observability design.","Outcome definitions require ownership."],"implementation_implications":["Define measurable success states.","Link outcome metrics to technical correlation context where appropriate.","Protect sensitive data."],"verification_method":"Create a failure where technical requests succeed but business outcome is incorrect and verify that observability detects the discrepancy.","outcome_learning":"Outcome gaps should inform future business-level telemetry.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B66-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B66-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B66-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Observability Architecture","subtopic":"Telemetry Retention","concept":"Set telemetry retention according to operational, legal, security, and diagnostic requirements","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should observability data retention be decided?","answer":"Retention should reflect how long telemetry remains useful for incident investigation, compliance or audit requirements, debugging, trend analysis, and security detection while considering sensitivity, storage cost, and deletion obligations. Retaining everything indefinitely is not inherently beneficial.","context":"The system generates logs, metrics, traces, and audit evidence.","objective":"Preserve useful evidence without unnecessary retention exposure or cost.","requirements":["Retention purpose must be defined.","Sensitive data handling must be governed.","Deletion or expiration behavior must be explicit."],"constraints":["Long retention increases storage and privacy exposure.","Some records may require longer retention than ordinary telemetry."],"alternatives":["Uniform retention.","Class-specific retention.","Indefinite retention."],"evidence":"Telemetry types have different operational value and sensitivity profiles.","tradeoffs":"Longer retention improves historical investigation but increases cost and exposure.","decision":"Use purpose- and sensitivity-aware retention classes rather than one universal retention period.","rationale":"Retention should be proportional to evidence value and obligations.","consequences":["Telemetry schemas require classification.","Deletion processes become part of observability architecture."],"implementation_implications":["Classify telemetry by purpose and sensitivity.","Define retention and deletion policies.","Prevent sensitive data from being retained unnecessarily."],"verification_method":"Inspect retention configuration and verify expiration behavior against declared classes.","outcome_learning":"Post-incident evidence needs and unnecessary retained data should inform retention revisions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B66-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B66-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B66-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Observability Architecture","subtopic":"Alerting versus Diagnostics","concept":"Separate high-signal alerting from detailed diagnostic telemetry","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"Why should alerting and diagnostic telemetry be designed as different concerns?","answer":"Alerts should identify conditions requiring action with sufficient signal quality, while diagnostic telemetry supports investigation after or around the event. Sending every diagnostic event as an alert creates noise and can hide important conditions.","context":"A system needs both automated operational response and detailed investigation evidence.","objective":"Maintain actionable alerting while preserving diagnostic depth.","requirements":["Alert conditions must have clear action semantics.","Diagnostic data must remain queryable without becoming alert noise."],"constraints":["Some conditions require contextual correlation before alerting.","High-volume telemetry can overwhelm alert channels."],"alternatives":["Alert on every error.","Separate alert rules from diagnostic telemetry.","Minimal telemetry and manual investigation."],"evidence":"Detection and investigation have different information-density requirements.","tradeoffs":"Separate design requires more deliberate signal classification but improves operational focus.","decision":"Design alerts as action-oriented signals derived from broader diagnostic evidence.","rationale":"Operational attention is scarce and should be directed toward conditions requiring action.","consequences":["Alert rules need ownership and lifecycle management.","Diagnostic systems must remain available independently of alerts."],"implementation_implications":["Define alert severity and actionability.","Include links or identifiers for investigation context.","Measure alert noise and missed conditions."],"verification_method":"Inject known failure conditions and verify alert generation, suppression, contextual evidence, and operator actionability.","outcome_learning":"False alerts and missed alerts should drive rule refinement rather than uncontrolled alert expansion.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B66-R01","D54-B66-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"contrasts_with","record_id":"D54-B66-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B67-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Trust Boundary","concept":"Make trust boundaries explicit at architecture interfaces","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should an architecture decision capture about trust boundaries?","answer":"A trust boundary identifies where assumptions about identity, authorization, data integrity, execution environment, or network control change. Security decisions should place explicit validation and authorization at boundaries rather than relying solely on controls in upstream components.","context":"A system crosses user, service, network, tenant, or privilege boundaries.","objective":"Prevent implicit trust from crossing architectural boundaries.","requirements":["Identity and authorization expectations must be explicit.","Boundary inputs must be validated according to risk."],"constraints":["Some boundaries are logical rather than network-visible.","Multiple layers may enforce overlapping controls."],"alternatives":["Implicit trust.","Perimeter-only controls.","Explicit boundary validation and layered controls."],"evidence":"Architectural boundaries change the assumptions under which data and operations are trusted.","tradeoffs":"Repeated validation can add cost but reduces reliance on a single control layer.","decision":"Treat material trust boundaries as explicit architecture elements with defined validation and authorization requirements.","rationale":"Security assumptions should be visible and testable rather than implicit.","consequences":["Boundary controls become part of interface design.","Security tests must cover cross-boundary behavior."],"implementation_implications":["Define identity propagation.","Validate authorization at appropriate enforcement points.","Separate trusted internal data from externally supplied data."],"verification_method":"Attempt unauthorized and malformed boundary crossings and verify that each material boundary enforces its declared controls.","outcome_learning":"Boundary failures should trigger review of trust assumptions and control placement.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B67-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Least Privilege","concept":"Scope permissions to the minimum authority required by a defined operation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should least privilege influence architecture decisions?","answer":"Permissions should be granted according to the actual operation, resource scope, lifecycle, and actor context rather than broad component ownership. Where possible, authority should be bounded by tenant, resource, action, time, and purpose.","context":"Services or users require access to protected capabilities and data.","objective":"Reduce the consequences of compromised or misused authority.","requirements":["Required permissions must be identifiable.","Authorization decisions must be enforceable and testable."],"constraints":["Fine-grained authorization increases policy complexity.","Operational workflows may require temporary elevated authority."],"alternatives":["Broad service-level permissions.","Role-only authorization.","Contextual least-privilege authorization."],"evidence":"Excess authority expands the potential impact of misuse or compromise.","tradeoffs":"Fine-grained permissions improve containment but increase policy and testing complexity.","decision":"Use the narrowest practical authority consistent with the required workflow and operational model.","rationale":"Authority should be proportional to actual responsibility.","consequences":["Permissions require lifecycle management.","Tests must cover denied as well as permitted operations."],"implementation_implications":["Separate read and write permissions where appropriate.","Scope permissions to resources or tenants.","Use temporary elevation when justified."],"verification_method":"Enumerate protected operations and verify that each actor has only required permissions, including negative authorization tests.","outcome_learning":"Denied-operation patterns and authorization incidents should inform permission refinement.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B67-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B67-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B67-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Secrets Management","concept":"Keep secret material outside ordinary application source and configuration paths","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should determine the architecture for application secrets?","answer":"Secret management should provide controlled access, appropriate rotation, limited exposure, auditability where required, and separation from ordinary source code and non-secret configuration. The architecture should also define what happens when secrets expire, rotate, or become unavailable.","context":"Applications require credentials, keys, tokens, or other sensitive secret material.","objective":"Reduce exposure and improve lifecycle control of secret material.","requirements":["Secret access must be authorized.","Secret lifecycle must be defined.","Secret material must not be unnecessarily logged or persisted."],"constraints":["Applications may require secrets during startup or runtime.","Rotation can require coordination with dependent systems."],"alternatives":["Embed secrets in source.","Store in ordinary configuration.","Use dedicated controlled secret storage."],"evidence":"Secret material has different lifecycle and exposure requirements from ordinary configuration.","tradeoffs":"Dedicated secret management adds infrastructure and operational requirements but improves lifecycle control.","decision":"Use controlled secret management appropriate to the deployment environment and avoid embedding long-lived secret material in application artifacts.","rationale":"Secret exposure can persist beyond the intended lifecycle of an application deployment.","consequences":["Applications need secret retrieval and failure handling.","Rotation becomes an architectural lifecycle event."],"implementation_implications":["Minimize secret scope.","Avoid logging secret values.","Design for rotation and revocation."],"verification_method":"Inspect source, artifacts, logs, configuration, and runtime access paths for unintended secret exposure and test rotation behavior.","outcome_learning":"Exposure findings should update secret handling controls and architecture boundaries.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B67-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B67-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B67-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Defense in Depth","concept":"Use independent control layers where failure of one control would create unacceptable exposure","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When is defense in depth architecturally justified?","answer":"Layered controls are justified when a single control failure could expose high-value assets or bypass a critical security property. Layers should have sufficiently independent failure modes and should not merely duplicate the same ineffective assumption.","context":"A system protects sensitive resources against multiple failure or attack paths.","objective":"Limit the impact of individual control failures.","requirements":["Critical security properties must be identified.","Control dependencies and common failure modes must be understood."],"constraints":["Additional controls increase complexity and operational burden.","Correlated controls may not provide meaningful independence."],"alternatives":["Single primary control.","Multiple independent controls.","Overlapping controls without defined purpose."],"evidence":"Independent control layers can reduce reliance on a single enforcement point.","tradeoffs":"Additional layers improve containment when independent but can create complexity if poorly designed.","decision":"Use layered controls when they materially reduce the consequences of plausible single-control failure.","rationale":"Defense in depth should be based on failure independence and asset impact rather than control count.","consequences":["Controls require coordinated testing.","Common dependencies must be monitored."],"implementation_implications":["Identify which layer enforces each security property.","Test individual control failure.","Avoid treating duplicated controls as independent without evidence."],"verification_method":"Disable or bypass individual controls in controlled tests and verify that remaining layers preserve required security properties.","outcome_learning":"Control failures and correlated weaknesses should update the defense architecture.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B67-R01","D54-B67-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B67-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B67-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Security Architecture","subtopic":"Security Failure Mode","concept":"Choose fail-open or fail-closed behavior according to the protected property and failure consequence","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should security-sensitive components decide their behavior when a dependency fails?","answer":"The decision should depend on what security property is being protected and the consequences of denying versus allowing the operation. Access-control enforcement for high-impact resources commonly requires denying access when authorization cannot be established, while some availability-sensitive non-security functions may require controlled degraded behavior.","context":"A security decision depends on a policy service, identity source, key service, or other dependency that can become unavailable.","objective":"Prevent dependency failure from creating an unacceptable security state.","requirements":["Protected property and failure consequence must be explicit.","Degraded behavior must be bounded."],"constraints":["Fail-closed can reduce availability.","Fail-open can create unauthorized access."],"alternatives":["Fail-open.","Fail-closed.","Context-dependent degraded mode."],"evidence":"Failure behavior changes the security and availability posture of the system.","tradeoffs":"Fail-closed protects against unauthorized access but can increase availability impact; fail-open preserves availability at the cost of potentially reduced security.","decision":"Select failure behavior based on the protected property, impact, and explicit risk requirements rather than using one universal rule.","rationale":"Security and availability consequences differ across operations.","consequences":["Failure behavior must be documented per protected operation.","Dependency health becomes part of security architecture."],"implementation_implications":["Define which operations may degrade.","Require explicit authorization for high-impact operations.","Test dependency-loss scenarios."],"verification_method":"Simulate policy or identity dependency failure and verify each protected operation follows its documented failure behavior.","outcome_learning":"Observed dependency outages should inform whether the selected failure posture remains acceptable.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B67-R01","D54-B67-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"constrains","record_id":"D54-B67-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B68-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"ADR Lifecycle","concept":"Treat architecture decisions as lifecycle-managed records rather than static documentation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should happen to an architecture decision after it is recorded?","answer":"An architecture decision should have a status, owner, evidence basis, affected scope, review triggers, and relationship to superseding or dependent decisions. Decisions should remain discoverable so later changes can determine whether the original rationale still applies.","context":"A system accumulates architecture decisions over multiple releases.","objective":"Preserve decision reasoning and prevent obsolete assumptions from becoming hidden constraints.","requirements":["Decision status must be explicit.","Supersession must be traceable.","Review triggers must be identifiable."],"constraints":["Some decisions remain valid for long periods.","Too frequent review can create unnecessary governance cost."],"alternatives":["Static ADR documents.","Versioned decision records.","Informal tribal knowledge."],"evidence":"Architecture decisions encode assumptions that can become invalid as systems and constraints change.","tradeoffs":"Lifecycle management adds governance overhead but preserves reasoning and change traceability.","decision":"Manage important architecture decisions as versioned lifecycle records with explicit status and relationships.","rationale":"The value of a decision record includes its historical reasoning and current applicability.","consequences":["Decision repositories require maintenance.","Superseded decisions remain useful as historical evidence."],"implementation_implications":["Use stable decision IDs.","Record supersedes, depends_on, and conflicts_with relationships.","Capture review triggers."],"verification_method":"Select a changed architecture area and verify that current decisions, superseded decisions, and relationships can be reconstructed.","outcome_learning":"Decision reversals should preserve both original rationale and evidence for the new decision.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B68-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Decision Reversal","concept":"Reverse an architecture decision only when new evidence or changed constraints invalidate its original assumptions","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an architecture decision be revisited or reversed?","answer":"A decision should be reconsidered when its assumptions, requirements, constraints, evidence, or observed outcomes materially change. Reversal should preserve the original record and create a linked successor decision explaining what changed and why.","context":"A previously accepted architecture choice no longer appears aligned with current conditions.","objective":"Enable controlled architectural change without losing historical reasoning.","requirements":["Changed assumptions must be identified.","New evidence must be recorded.","The successor decision must preserve traceability."],"constraints":["Migration cost may delay reversal.","Existing dependencies may make reversal gradual."],"alternatives":["Keep the original decision indefinitely.","Modify the original record silently.","Create a superseding decision."],"evidence":"Architecture evolves as requirements and evidence change.","tradeoffs":"Formal reversal increases documentation work but reduces historical ambiguity.","decision":"Use a superseding decision when material new evidence or constraints invalidate the previous choice.","rationale":"Historical decision integrity is preserved while allowing architecture to evolve.","consequences":["Migration strategy becomes part of the successor decision.","Old decisions remain useful as historical evidence."],"implementation_implications":["Record the changed assumptions.","Define migration stages.","Link old and new decision IDs."],"verification_method":"Review the successor decision and verify that the changed assumptions, evidence, migration implications, and relationship to the prior decision are explicit.","outcome_learning":"The reason for reversal should be retained as learning for future architecture decisions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B68-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B68-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B68-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Fitness Criteria","concept":"Convert important architecture requirements into observable acceptance criteria","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How can an architecture decision be verified after implementation?","answer":"Important architectural requirements should be translated into observable criteria such as latency bounds, isolation properties, compatibility rules, recovery behavior, security controls, capacity limits, or data integrity conditions. This makes the decision testable rather than purely descriptive.","context":"An architecture decision has been implemented and requires validation.","objective":"Connect architectural intent to measurable or testable evidence.","requirements":["Decision-critical properties must be observable.","Verification methods must be defined."],"constraints":["Some qualities require production-like testing or long-term observation.","Not every architectural property has a single scalar metric."],"alternatives":["Documentation review only.","Automated tests only.","Mixed static, dynamic, and operational verification."],"evidence":"Architecture decisions are more useful when their intended consequences can be checked.","tradeoffs":"Verification requires additional test and telemetry design.","decision":"Define explicit verification criteria for material architectural decisions.","rationale":"An untestable architectural requirement can remain unverified despite implementation claims.","consequences":["Architecture records become linked to tests and telemetry.","Verification gaps become visible."],"implementation_implications":["Map decision requirements to tests, metrics, or inspection procedures.","Record verification status separately from implementation status."],"verification_method":"Trace each material decision requirement to at least one appropriate verification mechanism.","outcome_learning":"Verification failures should trigger review of both implementation and original assumptions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B68-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B68-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B68-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Decision Conflicts","concept":"Resolve conflicting architecture decisions through explicit scope and precedence rather than silent inconsistency","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should conflicting architecture decisions be handled?","answer":"Conflicts should be identified explicitly and resolved by examining scope, authority, recency, dependencies, requirements, and evidence. A newer decision should not automatically invalidate an older one if both apply to different contexts.","context":"Two or more architecture decisions appear to prescribe incompatible behavior.","objective":"Prevent contradictory architectural rules from becoming implicit system behavior.","requirements":["Conflict scope must be identified.","Applicable decision authority must be explicit.","Resolution must be traceable."],"constraints":["Apparent conflicts may arise from different contexts or abstraction levels."],"alternatives":["Apply newest decision globally.","Ignore the conflict.","Record explicit scope and precedence."],"evidence":"Architecture decisions can coexist when their scopes differ, while genuine conflicts require resolution.","tradeoffs":"Explicit conflict analysis takes effort but prevents accidental policy selection.","decision":"Classify apparent conflicts by scope first, then resolve genuine conflicts through explicit successor or precedence decisions.","rationale":"Not every difference represents a contradiction; context must be preserved.","consequences":["Decision relationships become important retrieval metadata.","Architecture reviews need conflict detection."],"implementation_implications":["Record applies_to and scope constraints.","Use conflicts_with relationships where applicable.","Document resolution evidence."],"verification_method":"Run decision consistency review and confirm that overlapping decisions have compatible scope or explicit resolution.","outcome_learning":"Recurring conflict patterns may indicate missing architectural governance rules.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B68-R01","D54-B68-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B68-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B68-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Decision Evidence Quality","concept":"Distinguish observed evidence from assumptions and engineering inference in architecture decisions","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should architecture decisions distinguish evidence types?","answer":"A decision based on measured behavior, an official constraint, an experiment, an assumption, or general engineering inference has different confidence and verification implications. Explicit evidence classification prevents assumptions from being mistaken for observed facts.","context":"An architecture decision must justify a significant tradeoff.","objective":"Make decision confidence and uncertainty machine-readable.","requirements":["Evidence sources must be identifiable.","Observed results must be distinguishable from inference.","Uncertainty must be preserved."],"constraints":["Evidence may be incomplete or context-specific.","Some decisions must be made before sufficient empirical data exists."],"alternatives":["Single undifferentiated rationale.","Evidence-classified decision record.","Informal references."],"evidence":"Decision quality depends partly on the strength and applicability of supporting evidence.","tradeoffs":"Evidence classification adds metadata but improves later review and learning.","decision":"Record evidence type, provenance, verification state, and applicability for material architecture decisions.","rationale":"Future engineers need to know whether a claim was measured, documented, inferred, or assumed.","consequences":["Decision records become more useful for learning and reversal.","Unsupported assumptions become visible."],"implementation_implications":["Use explicit evidence_level and provenance fields.","Separate execution_status from validation_status.","Record contradictions where credible evidence conflicts."],"verification_method":"Audit decision records for evidence provenance and confirm that inferred claims are not represented as executed or verified results.","outcome_learning":"Later observations should update confidence without rewriting historical evidence.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B68-R01","D54-B68-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B68-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B69-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Architecture Evaluation","subtopic":"Architecture Experimentation","concept":"Use targeted experiments when uncertainty materially affects an architecture decision","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should an architecture decision be supported by an experiment?","answer":"An experiment is justified when a decision depends on uncertain behavior that can materially change the outcome and the uncertainty can be tested within a bounded environment. The experiment should define a hypothesis, conditions, measurements, stopping criteria, and limitations.","context":"Two architectural alternatives have uncertain performance, compatibility, reliability, or operational characteristics.","objective":"Reduce decision uncertainty with controlled evidence.","requirements":["Hypothesis must be explicit.","Measurements must correspond to decision criteria.","Experiment limitations must be documented."],"constraints":["Experiment results may not generalize to production.","Some architectural properties cannot be safely tested in isolation."],"alternatives":["Reason from established evidence.","Prototype or benchmark.","Production rollout without prior experiment."],"evidence":"Targeted experimentation can convert important assumptions into observed evidence when conditions are controlled.","tradeoffs":"Experiments consume time and may still leave uncertainty about production behavior.","decision":"Use bounded experiments when unresolved uncertainty could materially change the architecture choice and the uncertainty is experimentally testable.","rationale":"Evidence should be gathered where it can change the decision, not merely because experimentation is available.","consequences":["Experimental evidence must retain scope and limitations.","Results should not be generalized beyond tested conditions."],"implementation_implications":["Define representative workloads.","Record environment and version details.","Preserve raw measurements and interpretation separately."],"verification_method":"Review experiment reproducibility, measurement validity, and correspondence between results and decision criteria.","outcome_learning":"Unexpected results should update assumptions and may justify revisiting alternatives.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B68-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B68-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B69-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Architecture Evaluation","subtopic":"Proof of Concept","concept":"Use proof-of-concept implementations to resolve feasibility questions rather than prematurely committing production architecture","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"What distinguishes a useful architecture proof of concept from production implementation?","answer":"A proof of concept should isolate the uncertain feasibility question and minimize unrelated production concerns. Its purpose is to establish whether a capability, integration, performance property, or technical constraint is feasible under stated conditions; it should not automatically become production architecture without separate production-readiness evaluation.","context":"A proposed architecture depends on uncertain technical feasibility.","objective":"Reduce feasibility uncertainty before full implementation.","requirements":["The uncertainty being tested must be explicit.","PoC scope and limitations must be documented."],"constraints":["PoCs often omit security, operations, failure handling, and maintainability concerns."],"alternatives":["Full production implementation.","Bounded PoC.","Architecture reasoning without implementation."],"evidence":"A narrowly scoped prototype can answer feasibility questions at lower cost than full implementation.","tradeoffs":"PoCs reduce feasibility uncertainty but can create false confidence if production constraints are ignored.","decision":"Use PoCs for targeted feasibility questions and keep their evidence separate from production verification.","rationale":"Prototype success does not establish production readiness.","consequences":["A successful PoC still requires architecture and operational review.","PoC code may be discarded or rewritten."],"implementation_implications":["Mark PoC artifacts explicitly.","Record environment and scope.","Do not label PoC behavior as production verified."],"verification_method":"Check that PoC conclusions match the tested question and that unsupported production claims are excluded.","outcome_learning":"PoC limitations should be retained as part of decision evidence.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B69-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B69-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B69-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Architecture Evaluation","subtopic":"Cost Modeling","concept":"Include lifecycle and operational cost in architecture alternatives rather than evaluating implementation cost alone","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"What cost dimensions should an architecture decision consider?","answer":"Relevant costs can include implementation, infrastructure, operations, observability, security, staffing, training, migration, support, failure recovery, vendor dependence, and eventual retirement. The appropriate dimensions depend on the decision scope and time horizon.","context":"Several architecture alternatives satisfy the immediate functional requirements but differ operationally.","objective":"Compare total lifecycle implications without reducing architecture to initial implementation effort.","requirements":["Material cost categories must be identified.","Assumptions and time horizon must be explicit."],"constraints":["Future costs are uncertain.","Cost estimates can create false precision."],"alternatives":["Initial implementation cost only.","Lifecycle cost analysis.","Qualitative cost comparison."],"evidence":"Architecture creates long-lived operational and maintenance obligations beyond initial coding effort.","tradeoffs":"Broader cost analysis improves completeness but increases uncertainty and estimation effort.","decision":"Include materially relevant lifecycle cost dimensions and preserve uncertainty rather than presenting speculative estimates as facts.","rationale":"Architecture decisions create recurring obligations that can dominate initial implementation cost.","consequences":["Operational teams contribute to architecture evaluation.","Cost assumptions require later validation."],"implementation_implications":["Record cost drivers rather than unsupported precise forecasts.","Track actual operational costs after deployment.","Include migration and retirement costs."],"verification_method":"Compare decision assumptions with observed resource, support, and migration costs after implementation.","outcome_learning":"Observed lifecycle costs should improve future architecture estimation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B69-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B69-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B69-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Architecture Evaluation","subtopic":"Operational Readiness","concept":"Require operational readiness evidence before treating an architecture as production-ready","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should operational readiness cover before an architecture is considered production-ready?","answer":"Operational readiness should cover deployment, rollback or recovery, monitoring, alerting, capacity, security controls, backup or state recovery where relevant, ownership, incident procedures, dependency behavior, and verification of critical failure modes. Functional implementation alone is insufficient for production readiness.","context":"A technically functional architecture is approaching production deployment.","objective":"Verify that the architecture can be operated safely within its intended environment.","requirements":["Operational ownership must exist.","Critical failure and recovery procedures must be defined.","Required telemetry and controls must be available."],"constraints":["Readiness requirements vary by system criticality.","Some evidence requires realistic environment testing."],"alternatives":["Functional acceptance only.","Operational readiness review.","Progressive production validation."],"evidence":"Production operation introduces deployment, dependency, resource, and failure conditions not captured by ordinary functional tests.","tradeoffs":"Readiness review increases pre-production effort but exposes operational gaps earlier.","decision":"Require architecture-specific operational readiness evidence proportional to system criticality.","rationale":"Production readiness includes safe operation and recovery, not only feature correctness.","consequences":["Architecture approval depends on operational evidence.","Missing readiness evidence becomes an explicit risk."],"implementation_implications":["Define readiness checklist from architecture decisions.","Verify monitoring and recovery mechanisms.","Assign operational ownership before release."],"verification_method":"Execute a readiness review against declared architecture requirements and representative failure scenarios.","outcome_learning":"Production incidents should update readiness criteria and architecture review practices.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B66-R01","D54-B68-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B68-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B69-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Architecture Evaluation","subtopic":"Decision Sensitivity","concept":"Identify assumptions whose change would materially alter the selected architecture","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How can an architecture decision expose which assumptions matter most?","answer":"Decision sensitivity analysis identifies assumptions that, if changed, would cause a different alternative to become preferable under the same evaluation criteria. These assumptions should receive stronger evidence, monitoring, or review triggers than assumptions with little influence on the decision.","context":"An architecture decision depends on uncertain requirements, workload characteristics, costs, or operational conditions.","objective":"Focus validation and future monitoring on decision-critical uncertainty.","requirements":["Key decision variables must be identified.","Material threshold changes must be documented."],"constraints":["Sensitivity may be qualitative when precise models are unavailable.","Some variables interact rather than changing independently."],"alternatives":["Treat all assumptions equally.","Identify decision-sensitive assumptions.","Ignore uncertainty until failure."],"evidence":"Not all assumptions have equal influence on an architecture choice.","tradeoffs":"Sensitivity analysis requires additional reasoning but can focus evidence-gathering effort.","decision":"Identify assumptions whose material change could invalidate the decision and attach review or monitoring triggers to them.","rationale":"Architecture governance should concentrate attention on assumptions that can actually change the decision.","consequences":["Architecture records gain explicit review triggers.","Monitoring can detect changes before architecture becomes misaligned."],"implementation_implications":["Record decision thresholds where meaningful.","Track measurable assumptions.","Trigger review when thresholds are crossed."],"verification_method":"Review whether each material architecture decision identifies the assumptions that could invalidate it and whether corresponding evidence or monitoring exists.","outcome_learning":"Observed threshold crossings should trigger structured decision review.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B68-R01","D54-B69-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B68-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B70-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Architecture Decision Integration","subtopic":"Decision-to-Implementation Traceability","concept":"Connect architecture decisions to implementation constraints, tests, and operational evidence","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture decisions remain connected to implementation?","answer":"Material decisions should map to implementation constraints, relevant components or interfaces, verification tests, deployment controls, and operational measurements. This traceability allows engineers to determine whether the implementation still satisfies the decision's intent.","context":"Architecture decisions must remain useful after implementation begins.","objective":"Prevent architecture records from becoming disconnected documentation.","requirements":["Decision requirements must map to implementation evidence.","Verification status must remain distinct from implementation status."],"constraints":["Traceability can become stale when code evolves.","Not every implementation detail requires an architecture record."],"alternatives":["Documentation-only ADRs.","Decision-to-code and verification traceability.","Full code-level traceability for every decision."],"evidence":"Architecture intent has practical value only when it can be related to implementation and evidence.","tradeoffs":"Traceability requires maintenance but improves review, debugging, and change impact analysis.","decision":"Maintain lightweight traceability from material decisions to relevant implementation, tests, and operational evidence.","rationale":"The purpose is to preserve architectural intent without creating unnecessary documentation coupling.","consequences":["Architecture changes require traceability review.","Tests can serve as evidence for decision requirements."],"implementation_implications":["Reference component or contract identifiers.","Link decision requirements to tests and verification records.","Mark stale links explicitly."],"verification_method":"Select material decisions and trace each through implementation and verification evidence.","outcome_learning":"Broken traceability should trigger maintenance of the decision record or architecture review.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B68-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B68-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B70-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Architecture Decision Integration","subtopic":"Requirement-to-Decision Traceability","concept":"Trace architecture decisions back to the requirements and constraints that caused them","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should architecture decisions retain links to originating requirements?","answer":"Without requirement traceability, later engineers may preserve an architecture choice after the requirement has changed or may remove a constraint that remains essential. Linking decisions to requirements clarifies why the decision exists and whether it remains applicable.","context":"A system has accumulated architecture decisions across changing product requirements.","objective":"Keep architecture aligned with current requirements and constraints.","requirements":["Originating requirements or constraints must be identifiable.","Decision applicability must be reviewable."],"constraints":["Requirements can be ambiguous or change over time.","Some decisions are responses to technical constraints rather than explicit business requirements."],"alternatives":["Decision records without requirement links.","Explicit requirement and constraint relationships."],"evidence":"Architecture decisions are context-dependent responses to requirements and constraints.","tradeoffs":"Traceability adds metadata but supports impact analysis and decision review.","decision":"Link material architecture decisions to the requirements, constraints, or risks that motivated them.","rationale":"Decision meaning depends on the context that created the decision.","consequences":["Requirement changes can trigger architecture review.","Obsolete decisions become easier to identify."],"implementation_implications":["Use stable requirement and decision identifiers.","Record the specific constraint or risk addressed.","Review linked decisions when requirements materially change."],"verification_method":"Change a requirement in a controlled review and identify all linked decisions requiring reassessment.","outcome_learning":"Repeated requirement changes can reveal architecture decisions that should be made more adaptable.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B70-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D54-B70-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B70-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Architecture Decision Integration","subtopic":"Decision-to-Outcome Learning","concept":"Capture observed outcomes separately from the original architecture rationale","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture decisions learn from implementation outcomes?","answer":"The original rationale should remain historically intact while observed outcomes are recorded as separate evidence. Outcomes should state what was actually observed, under which conditions, and whether the observation supports, contradicts, or leaves the original assumptions unresolved.","context":"A deployed architecture has accumulated operational or experimental evidence.","objective":"Convert experience into future architecture knowledge without rewriting history.","requirements":["Observed outcomes must be provenance-backed.","Observation conditions must be recorded.","Contradictions must remain visible."],"constraints":["Operational evidence may be incomplete or environment-specific.","Correlation does not automatically establish causation."],"alternatives":["Rewrite the original decision.","Add unsupported success/failure claims.","Maintain linked outcome records."],"evidence":"Post-implementation observations can validate or challenge architecture assumptions.","tradeoffs":"Outcome capture requires evidence discipline but improves future decision quality.","decision":"Preserve original decisions and attach separately verified outcome evidence with explicit relationship and confidence.","rationale":"Historical rationale and later evidence answer different questions and should not be conflated.","consequences":["Architecture records become learning inputs.","Contradictory evidence remains queryable."],"implementation_implications":["Record observation source and environment.","Distinguish measured outcome from interpretation.","Link outcomes to affected decisions."],"verification_method":"Audit outcome records for provenance, conditions, validation status, and explicit relationship to the original decision.","outcome_learning":"Repeated outcome evidence should update future architecture alternatives and review triggers.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B70-R01","D54-B70-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B70-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B70-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Architecture Decision Integration","subtopic":"Decision Impact Analysis","concept":"Evaluate downstream consequences before changing a foundational architecture decision","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"What should be evaluated before replacing a foundational architecture decision?","answer":"Impact analysis should identify dependent decisions, interfaces, data models, deployment topology, security controls, operational procedures, tests, integrations, and migration requirements. The change should be evaluated as an architectural graph rather than an isolated record edit.","context":"A foundational decision is being reconsidered or replaced.","objective":"Identify architectural consequences before committing to change.","requirements":["Dependencies must be discoverable.","Affected implementation and operational areas must be identified.","Migration implications must be evaluated."],"constraints":["Some dependencies may be implicit or undocumented.","Impact may extend across organizational boundaries."],"alternatives":["Change the decision directly.","Perform dependency-aware impact analysis.","Full architecture redesign."],"evidence":"Architecture decisions create dependent constraints and assumptions across the system.","tradeoffs":"Impact analysis adds review effort but reduces unexpected migration and compatibility failures.","decision":"Perform dependency-aware impact analysis proportional to the decision's architectural reach before replacing a foundational decision.","rationale":"A decision graph captures consequences that a standalone record cannot.","consequences":["Change plans may become multi-stage migrations.","Some dependent decisions may need supersession."],"implementation_implications":["Traverse related decision relationships.","Identify affected tests and operational controls.","Create migration checkpoints."],"verification_method":"Trace direct and indirect relationships from the decision and confirm that material affected components and controls are included in the change plan.","outcome_learning":"Unexpected dependencies discovered during migration should become explicit relationships for future decisions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B68-R01","D54-B68-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B68-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B70-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Architecture Decision Integration","subtopic":"Architecture Knowledge Retrieval","concept":"Store decision context and relationships so future engineering tasks can retrieve applicable architectural constraints","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What makes architecture decision records useful to an autonomous software-engineering knowledge system?","answer":"Decision records should expose stable identifiers, scope, requirements, constraints, alternatives, evidence, rationale, consequences, implementation implications, verification state, provenance, and relationships. Retrieval should be able to identify decisions applicable to a component, technology, requirement, constraint, or change request without treating every historical decision as currently authoritative.","context":"Architecture decisions are consumed by an automated engineering knowledge system.","objective":"Make architecture knowledge retrievable, context-aware, and safe for downstream reasoning.","requirements":["Current versus superseded status must be distinguishable.","Applicability scope must be machine-readable.","Evidence and verification state must remain separate."],"constraints":["Historical decisions can remain relevant as evidence but not as current rules.","Semantic retrieval can surface related but inapplicable decisions."],"alternatives":["Store decisions as unstructured notes.","Structured decision records with relationships and status.","Flatten decisions into implementation rules."],"evidence":"Decision context and lifecycle metadata determine whether a historical record is applicable to a current engineering problem.","tradeoffs":"Structured metadata requires disciplined maintenance but enables safer retrieval and reasoning.","decision":"Represent architecture decisions as structured, provenance-backed, lifecycle-aware records with explicit relationships and applicability context.","rationale":"An autonomous engineering system must distinguish current constraints from historical evidence and contextually related alternatives.","consequences":["Retrieval can support architecture-aware reasoning.","Supersession and contradiction detection become possible.","Records must be kept synchronized with architecture evolution."],"implementation_implications":["Index decision IDs, topics, constraints, technologies, components, and relationships.","Expose status such as proposed, accepted, superseded, or rejected where applicable.","Never treat an unverified inference as executed operational fact."],"verification_method":"Run retrieval tests for current, superseded, conflicting, and context-specific decisions and verify that returned records preserve lifecycle and evidence metadata.","outcome_learning":"Retrieval failures should inform schema, relationship, and applicability improvements rather than adding duplicate records.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B68-R01","D54-B68-R05","D54-B70-R01","D54-B70-R03","D54-B70-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"integrates","record_id":"D54-B70-R01"},{"type":"integrates","record_id":"D54-B70-R03"},{"type":"integrates","record_id":"D54-B70-R04"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B71-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Architecture Decision Integration","subtopic":"Technology Selection","concept":"Select technologies based on architectural requirements and lifecycle constraints rather than popularity","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should technology selection be incorporated into an architecture decision?","answer":"Technology selection should evaluate the requirements and constraints that the technology must satisfy, including runtime behavior, security, performance, interoperability, operational maturity, maintainability, ecosystem support, migration cost, and lifecycle risk. Popularity alone is insufficient evidence.","context":"Multiple technologies can implement the required architectural capability.","objective":"Choose technology that fits the actual architectural context.","requirements":["Selection criteria must be explicit.","Material constraints must be evaluated.","Lifecycle implications must be considered."],"constraints":["Technology capabilities evolve.","Available skills and existing infrastructure constrain feasible choices."],"alternatives":["Established technology.","Newer technology with relevant capabilities.","Custom implementation."],"evidence":"Technology suitability depends on workload, constraints, integration requirements, and operational context.","tradeoffs":"A mature technology may reduce operational uncertainty while a newer technology may provide capabilities unavailable elsewhere.","decision":"Select technology against explicit architectural criteria and evidence, preserving assumptions and uncertainty.","rationale":"Technology is an implementation means for satisfying architectural requirements, not an objective independent of them.","consequences":["Technology decisions require lifecycle ownership.","Future replacement cost should be considered."],"implementation_implications":["Record supported versions and compatibility requirements.","Evaluate operational tooling and skills.","Define migration and exit considerations where vendor or ecosystem dependence is material."],"verification_method":"Score alternatives against predefined decision criteria without converting the result into an unsupported universal ranking.","outcome_learning":"Observed operational behavior should update future technology-selection criteria.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B71-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Architecture Decision Integration","subtopic":"Build versus Buy","concept":"Evaluate build versus buy using capability fit, lifecycle cost, control, integration, and exit considerations","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should a build-versus-buy architecture decision be evaluated?","answer":"The decision should compare the required capability with available products or services and assess functional fit, customization needs, security, integration, operational responsibility, lifecycle cost, data portability, vendor dependence, support, and exit strategy. Building should not be assumed to provide greater control without accounting for long-term ownership.","context":"A capability can be implemented internally or obtained from an external product or service.","objective":"Choose an approach that satisfies requirements with understood lifecycle consequences.","requirements":["Required capability must be defined.","External dependency risks must be evaluated.","Long-term ownership must be considered."],"constraints":["Purchased solutions may impose contractual or technical constraints.","Internal implementations require sustained maintenance."],"alternatives":["Build internally.","Buy or adopt an existing product.","Hybrid approach."],"evidence":"Both internal and external solutions create long-term ownership responsibilities.","tradeoffs":"Buying can reduce initial implementation effort while introducing dependency and customization constraints; building provides direct control while creating ongoing engineering responsibility.","decision":"Choose based on total lifecycle fit and risk rather than initial implementation effort alone.","rationale":"The architecture must account for the complete ownership model.","consequences":["The selected approach creates ongoing obligations.","Exit and migration considerations become relevant for external dependencies."],"implementation_implications":["Document integration and portability requirements.","Define ownership and support responsibilities.","Test critical vendor or internal capability assumptions."],"verification_method":"Review alternatives against functional, operational, security, lifecycle, and exit criteria.","outcome_learning":"Actual support, integration, and lifecycle costs should feed future build-versus-buy decisions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B71-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B71-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B71-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Architecture Decision Integration","subtopic":"Vendor Dependency","concept":"Treat material vendor dependence as an architectural constraint with explicit exit and continuity considerations","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When does vendor dependency become an architecture decision?","answer":"Vendor dependency becomes architectural when the vendor controls a critical capability, data boundary, deployment dependency, protocol, or lifecycle condition that materially affects system continuity or change freedom. The decision should document portability, contractual constraints, failure modes, and feasible exit paths.","context":"A critical system capability depends on an external vendor.","objective":"Make dependency consequences visible before they become irreversible constraints.","requirements":["Critical dependency scope must be known.","Exit or continuity assumptions must be documented."],"constraints":["Portability may be technically or economically limited.","Vendor contracts can constrain available options."],"alternatives":["Single vendor dependency.","Multiple providers.","Abstraction or portability layer."],"evidence":"Critical external dependencies can influence architecture beyond their immediate technical interface.","tradeoffs":"Portability mechanisms can increase cost and complexity while reducing dependency risk.","decision":"Document and govern material vendor dependencies according to their impact and reversibility.","rationale":"Dependency risk is proportional to the importance and difficulty of replacing the external capability.","consequences":["Vendor changes can trigger architecture review.","Portability requirements may constrain implementation choices."],"implementation_implications":["Identify vendor-controlled data and interfaces.","Define export or migration capabilities where justified.","Avoid unsupported assumptions about future vendor compatibility."],"verification_method":"Review critical vendor dependencies for ownership, portability, failure behavior, contractual constraints, and replacement feasibility.","outcome_learning":"Observed vendor incidents or migration difficulty should update dependency risk assessments.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B71-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B71-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B71-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Architecture Decision Integration","subtopic":"Technology Lifecycle","concept":"Include technology lifecycle and support horizon in architecture decisions","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should technology lifecycle status influence architecture decisions?","answer":"A technology choice creates future maintenance, upgrade, compatibility, and migration obligations. Architecture should consider whether the selected technology has a sustainable support path, whether critical dependencies can be upgraded safely, and what happens if the technology becomes unsupported.","context":"A new or existing technology is being considered for a long-lived system.","objective":"Avoid architectural choices whose lifecycle assumptions are incompatible with system longevity.","requirements":["Lifecycle assumptions must be explicit.","Upgrade and migration risks must be considered."],"constraints":["Future support status cannot always be known.","Replacing mature technology can also be expensive."],"alternatives":["Short-lived technology with planned replacement.","Long-lived supported technology.","Technology-agnostic abstraction."],"evidence":"Software dependencies evolve and can impose future compatibility and migration work.","tradeoffs":"Abstraction can improve replaceability but may hide useful technology-specific capabilities.","decision":"Include lifecycle sustainability and upgradeability in technology architecture decisions.","rationale":"Current technical suitability does not guarantee long-term architectural suitability.","consequences":["Dependency inventories become architecture inputs.","Upgrade planning becomes part of lifecycle governance."],"implementation_implications":["Record technology and version assumptions.","Monitor material lifecycle changes.","Test supported upgrade paths before dependency changes become urgent."],"verification_method":"Review dependency lifecycle assumptions and verify that critical upgrades or replacements have a feasible path.","outcome_learning":"Upgrade incidents should update lifecycle criteria and dependency management practices.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B71-R01","D54-B71-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B71-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B71-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Architecture Decision Integration","subtopic":"Abstraction Boundaries","concept":"Introduce abstraction only when it protects a meaningful change boundary or contract","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When is an abstraction layer justified in architecture?","answer":"An abstraction is justified when it isolates a meaningful variation point, protects a stable contract, enables controlled substitution, or prevents external implementation details from spreading. Abstraction that exists without a real change boundary can increase indirection and maintenance cost.","context":"A system may introduce an interface or adapter between consumers and an implementation.","objective":"Avoid both unnecessary coupling and unnecessary abstraction.","requirements":["The variation or boundary being protected must be identifiable.","The abstraction must preserve required semantics."],"constraints":["Abstractions add cognitive and testing overhead.","Over-generalized interfaces can hide important differences."],"alternatives":["Direct dependency.","Narrow abstraction.","Generic abstraction framework."],"evidence":"Abstraction has architectural value when it isolates meaningful change or ownership boundaries.","tradeoffs":"Appropriate abstraction reduces coupling; unnecessary abstraction increases complexity.","decision":"Use the narrowest abstraction that protects a demonstrated architectural boundary or variation point.","rationale":"Abstraction should solve a specific coupling or change-management problem.","consequences":["Interface design becomes part of architecture governance.","Over-generalization should be actively avoided."],"implementation_implications":["Keep interfaces focused on consumer needs.","Avoid exposing implementation-specific details.","Test semantic compatibility across implementations."],"verification_method":"Identify the change or ownership boundary protected by the abstraction and verify that consumers remain independent of implementation details.","outcome_learning":"Repeated abstraction leakage or unnecessary implementation branches should trigger interface review.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B71-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"contrasts_with","record_id":"D54-B71-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B72-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Architecture Risk","subtopic":"Risk Identification","concept":"Identify architecture risks from violated assumptions, concentrated dependencies, irreversible choices, and failure amplification","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should an architecture risk assessment examine?","answer":"Architecture risk assessment should examine assumptions, single points of failure, dependency concentration, security boundaries, data integrity, capacity limits, operational complexity, irreversible decisions, migration difficulty, and failure propagation. Risks should be tied to specific architecture elements and consequences.","context":"An architecture is being reviewed before or after implementation.","objective":"Make material architectural uncertainty and failure exposure explicit.","requirements":["Risk statements must identify cause, condition, and consequence.","Material assumptions must be traceable."],"constraints":["Risk probability may be uncertain.","Different risks may share common causes."],"alternatives":["Generic risk list.","Architecture-specific risk analysis.","Failure-mode-driven assessment."],"evidence":"Architecture risk arises from how components, dependencies, constraints, and failure modes interact.","tradeoffs":"Detailed risk analysis requires effort but improves prioritization of verification and mitigation.","decision":"Use architecture-specific risk analysis linked to concrete decisions, assumptions, and failure consequences.","rationale":"Generic risk statements provide less actionable information than context-bound architectural risks.","consequences":["Risks become inputs to architecture verification.","Risk ownership must be defined."],"implementation_implications":["Link risks to decision IDs and components.","Record mitigation and residual uncertainty.","Reassess when architecture changes."],"verification_method":"Review whether material risks have identifiable causes, consequences, owners, mitigations, and verification evidence.","outcome_learning":"Actual incidents should update risk models and expose previously missed assumptions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D54-B72-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Architecture Risk","subtopic":"Single Points of Failure","concept":"Treat a component as a single point of failure only in relation to the required system behavior and recovery capability","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should single-point-of-failure decisions be evaluated?","answer":"A component is architecturally significant as a single point of failure when its loss can violate required system behavior and no acceptable redundancy, degradation, or recovery path exists. The analysis should consider business criticality, failure duration, detection, recovery time, and tolerated service degradation.","context":"A critical capability depends on one component or dependency.","objective":"Determine whether redundancy or alternative recovery is required.","requirements":["Required availability or recovery objectives must be known.","Failure impact must be explicit."],"constraints":["Redundancy introduces cost and operational complexity.","Some components cannot be made fully redundant."],"alternatives":["Single instance with recovery.","Active or passive redundancy.","Graceful degradation.","Alternative service path."],"evidence":"Redundancy value depends on whether failure violates actual system requirements.","tradeoffs":"Redundancy can reduce failure exposure but adds synchronization, capacity, and operational complexity.","decision":"Assess single points of failure against business requirements and recovery capabilities rather than eliminating every singleton mechanically.","rationale":"Not every singleton is unacceptable; architectural significance depends on consequence and recovery.","consequences":["Recovery objectives become part of architecture evaluation.","Some singleton components may require stronger operational controls instead of redundancy."],"implementation_implications":["Document failure impact and recovery path.","Measure recovery behavior.","Test actual failover where redundancy is selected."],"verification_method":"Simulate component failure and compare observed recovery or degradation with declared system objectives.","outcome_learning":"Observed recovery gaps should update redundancy or resilience decisions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B72-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B72-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B72-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Architecture Risk","subtopic":"Blast Radius","concept":"Limit the blast radius of component and configuration failures where impact is material","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should blast radius influence architecture?","answer":"Architecture should constrain how far a failure, compromise, misconfiguration, or resource exhaustion can propagate. Useful boundaries include tenant isolation, workload segmentation, resource limits, dependency isolation, authorization scopes, and staged rollout boundaries.","context":"A failure in one component or environment could affect multiple unrelated workloads.","objective":"Contain failures to the smallest acceptable scope.","requirements":["Failure propagation paths must be understood.","Critical workloads must have defined isolation requirements."],"constraints":["Isolation can reduce resource sharing and increase operational complexity."],"alternatives":["Shared unrestricted resources.","Logical isolation.","Physical or deployment-level isolation."],"evidence":"Shared dependencies and unrestricted authority can amplify local failures into system-wide effects.","tradeoffs":"Stronger isolation improves containment but can increase cost and reduce utilization.","decision":"Apply isolation proportional to the potential impact and reversibility of failure.","rationale":"Blast-radius control is a risk-reduction mechanism, not an objective to maximize indiscriminately.","consequences":["Architecture requires explicit failure domains.","Testing must include cross-boundary failure scenarios."],"implementation_implications":["Define resource and authorization boundaries.","Separate high-impact workloads where justified.","Use staged deployment boundaries."],"verification_method":"Induce representative component, configuration, and resource failures and verify containment boundaries.","outcome_learning":"Unexpected propagation paths should become explicit architecture risks and remediation inputs.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B72-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B72-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B72-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Architecture Risk","subtopic":"Irreversibility","concept":"Give additional scrutiny to architecture choices that are expensive or difficult to reverse","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should irreversible architecture decisions receive additional evidence requirements?","answer":"Irreversible or high-cost decisions constrain future options and can make incorrect assumptions expensive to correct. Such decisions should receive stronger evidence, explicit assumptions, migration considerations, and review triggers than easily reversible choices.","context":"A decision affects data models, external contracts, vendor dependencies, or other difficult-to-replace foundations.","objective":"Reduce avoidable lock-in from uncertain foundational decisions.","requirements":["Reversibility must be assessed.","Critical assumptions must be explicit.","Exit or migration consequences must be understood."],"constraints":["Waiting for certainty can delay delivery.","Some decisions cannot be fully reversible."],"alternatives":["Commit immediately.","Delay until evidence improves.","Use an intentionally reversible intermediate design."],"evidence":"Decision reversibility changes the cost of being wrong.","tradeoffs":"Additional analysis can slow progress but may reduce long-term migration risk.","decision":"Apply greater evidence and review discipline to decisions whose reversal is materially costly.","rationale":"The consequence of incorrect assumptions should influence decision rigor.","consequences":["Foundational decisions may require experiments or prototypes.","Temporary designs may be preferred when uncertainty is high."],"implementation_implications":["Record exit paths where feasible.","Avoid irreversible commitments before critical uncertainties are tested.","Define review triggers."],"verification_method":"Review foundational decisions for reversibility, assumptions, evidence strength, and migration consequences.","outcome_learning":"Difficult reversals should become explicit evidence for future decision sensitivity analysis.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B69-R01","D54-B71-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B69-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B72-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Architecture Risk","subtopic":"Residual Risk","concept":"Record residual risk after mitigation rather than treating mitigation as proof that risk has disappeared","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should residual risk remain visible after an architecture mitigation is implemented?","answer":"A mitigation changes risk but does not necessarily eliminate it. The architecture record should retain remaining uncertainty, assumptions, dependencies, and failure conditions so future changes can determine whether the residual risk remains acceptable.","context":"An identified architecture risk has received one or more mitigations.","objective":"Prevent mitigated risks from becoming falsely represented as eliminated risks.","requirements":["Mitigation effect must be distinguishable from risk elimination.","Residual uncertainty must be recorded."],"constraints":["Risk magnitude may be qualitative.","Evidence may be incomplete after initial mitigation."],"alternatives":["Mark risk resolved after mitigation.","Maintain residual risk.","Continuously monitor risk."],"evidence":"Controls can reduce probability or impact without guaranteeing zero failure.","tradeoffs":"Maintaining residual risk metadata increases governance work but preserves realistic system assumptions.","decision":"Record residual risk explicitly and define review or monitoring triggers.","rationale":"Architecture reasoning must preserve uncertainty after mitigation.","consequences":["Risk records remain linked to architecture decisions.","Future changes can reassess existing residual risks."],"implementation_implications":["Record remaining failure modes.","Link mitigations to verification evidence.","Define triggers for reassessment."],"verification_method":"Review mitigated risks and verify that residual conditions and evidence remain explicit.","outcome_learning":"Actual failures or near misses should update residual-risk assessments.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B72-R01","D54-B72-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B72-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B73-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Architecture Change Management","subtopic":"Incremental Migration","concept":"Prefer staged migration when a large architectural change can be decomposed into independently verifiable steps","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should an architecture migration be staged?","answer":"Staged migration is appropriate when the target architecture can coexist with the current architecture for bounded periods and intermediate states can be verified. Each stage should have explicit compatibility, rollback, and completion criteria.","context":"A system must transition from an existing architecture to a target architecture without a single disruptive replacement.","objective":"Reduce migration risk through controlled intermediate states.","requirements":["Intermediate states must be safe.","Each stage must have verification criteria.","Compatibility must be defined during coexistence."],"constraints":["Dual operation can increase complexity.","Some migrations cannot safely coexist."],"alternatives":["Big-bang migration.","Staged migration.","Parallel replacement."],"evidence":"Smaller verifiable transitions can limit the scope of migration failures.","tradeoffs":"Staged migration reduces immediate change scope but can temporarily increase architectural complexity.","decision":"Use staged migration when safe intermediate states and verification checkpoints are feasible.","rationale":"Migration risk can be distributed across controlled transitions.","consequences":["Temporary compatibility mechanisms may be required.","Migration can take longer."],"implementation_implications":["Define stage entry and exit criteria.","Track old and new paths separately.","Plan retirement of temporary mechanisms."],"verification_method":"Verify each migration stage independently before advancing to the next stage.","outcome_learning":"Migration failures should identify unsafe intermediate states and improve future sequencing.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B64-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B64-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B73-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Architecture Change Management","subtopic":"Strangler Migration","concept":"Use incremental replacement around stable boundaries when legacy functionality can be isolated behind controlled interfaces","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When is an incremental replacement strategy around a legacy system appropriate?","answer":"Incremental replacement is appropriate when legacy capabilities can be isolated behind stable boundaries and individual slices can be moved without requiring simultaneous replacement of the entire system. It is less suitable when legacy behavior is inseparable, highly coupled, or dependent on global shared state.","context":"A legacy system must be modernized while maintaining ongoing operation.","objective":"Reduce migration scope by replacing bounded capabilities incrementally.","requirements":["Capability boundaries must be identifiable.","Traffic or responsibility can be redirected safely.","Legacy and replacement semantics can be compared."],"constraints":["Temporary duplication and routing complexity may arise.","Some legacy behavior may be poorly understood."],"alternatives":["Full rewrite.","Incremental replacement.","Wrap without replacement."],"evidence":"Bounded replacement reduces the amount of functionality that must change simultaneously when meaningful seams exist.","tradeoffs":"Incremental migration can reduce immediate risk while increasing temporary system complexity.","decision":"Use incremental replacement when stable seams and verifiable slices exist; avoid forcing the strategy where boundaries are artificial.","rationale":"The migration strategy must follow real architectural boundaries.","consequences":["Temporary coexistence requires lifecycle management.","Legacy retirement becomes part of the migration plan."],"implementation_implications":["Define capability slices.","Compare old and new behavior where possible.","Retire routing and compatibility layers after migration."],"verification_method":"Validate each migrated slice against functional, data, performance, security, and operational criteria before removing its legacy path.","outcome_learning":"Difficult slices may reveal hidden coupling that should influence subsequent migration boundaries.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B73-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B73-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B73-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Architecture Change Management","subtopic":"Feature Flags","concept":"Use feature flags as controlled rollout mechanisms rather than permanent architecture abstractions","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should feature flags be used in architecture change?","answer":"Feature flags are useful when behavior must be enabled progressively, tested under controlled conditions, or rapidly disabled without redeploying the artifact. They become harmful when temporary flags accumulate indefinitely or when core architecture is permanently fragmented by flag-dependent behavior.","context":"A release introduces behavior that should be controlled independently from artifact deployment.","objective":"Separate deployment from controlled activation where justified.","requirements":["Flag ownership and lifecycle must be defined.","Default behavior must be explicit.","Retirement criteria must exist."],"constraints":["Flags add behavioral combinations and testing complexity.","Some security or data changes cannot safely be hidden behind simple toggles."],"alternatives":["Immediate global activation.","Feature flag.","Separate deployment artifact."],"evidence":"Controlled activation can reduce rollout exposure when behavior is reversible and independently controllable.","tradeoffs":"Flags improve rollout control but increase state-space and maintenance complexity.","decision":"Use feature flags for bounded rollout or operational control and require explicit retirement of temporary flags.","rationale":"Flags should be lifecycle-managed mechanisms, not permanent substitutes for architecture.","consequences":["Testing must cover supported flag states.","Flag configuration becomes operational state."],"implementation_implications":["Record owner and expiration or review date.","Avoid sensitive security decisions relying solely on uncontrolled flags.","Remove obsolete branches."],"verification_method":"Test enabled, disabled, transition, and rollback states and verify that obsolete flags are removed after migration.","outcome_learning":"Flag-related incidents should update rollout and configuration governance.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B73-R01","D54-B64-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B64-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B73-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Architecture Change Management","subtopic":"Compatibility Window","concept":"Define explicit coexistence periods when old and new components must operate together","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should coexistence between old and new architecture be time-bounded and governed?","answer":"Coexistence creates additional interfaces, states, and operational paths. An explicit compatibility window defines how long these temporary conditions are supported and what evidence is required before retirement.","context":"A migration requires old and new implementations to operate simultaneously.","objective":"Prevent temporary migration complexity from becoming permanent architecture.","requirements":["Coexistence compatibility must be defined.","Retirement criteria must be explicit."],"constraints":["Migration schedules can change.","Some consumers may migrate slowly."],"alternatives":["Indefinite coexistence.","Fixed migration window.","Consumer-driven retirement with defined deadlines."],"evidence":"Temporary compatibility paths increase maintenance and testing surface.","tradeoffs":"Longer windows improve consumer flexibility but increase operational complexity.","decision":"Define supported coexistence conditions and retirement criteria before introducing temporary compatibility paths.","rationale":"Temporary architecture needs lifecycle governance to avoid permanent complexity.","consequences":["Migration progress must be observable.","Exceptions require explicit review."],"implementation_implications":["Track remaining legacy consumers.","Define final retirement conditions.","Test both sides during coexistence."],"verification_method":"Review active compatibility paths and verify that each has an owner, supported window, and retirement condition.","outcome_learning":"Migration delays should be analyzed rather than silently extending compatibility indefinitely.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B73-R01","D54-B73-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B73-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B73-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Architecture Change Management","subtopic":"Migration Completion","concept":"Define migration completion as removal of obsolete architecture and verification of the target state","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What constitutes completion of an architecture migration?","answer":"Migration is complete only when the target behavior is verified, required data and contracts are migrated, obsolete paths are no longer needed, temporary compatibility mechanisms are retired where intended, operational ownership is established, and residual risks are documented.","context":"A system has technically begun operating on a replacement architecture.","objective":"Prevent partial migration from being mistaken for completed architecture transition.","requirements":["Target state must be verified.","Obsolete components must have explicit retirement status.","Operational ownership must be established."],"constraints":["Legacy removal can be delayed by external dependencies.","Some compatibility components may become permanent by design."],"alternatives":["Declare completion when new path works.","Declare completion after legacy retirement.","Formal completion criteria."],"evidence":"A working replacement path does not prove that the old architecture has been safely retired.","tradeoffs":"Formal completion requires more evidence but prevents hidden legacy dependence.","decision":"Define migration completion through target-state verification plus explicit retirement or accepted continuation of legacy components.","rationale":"Architecture change includes both adoption and controlled removal of the superseded state.","consequences":["Legacy dependencies become visible.","Migration records remain useful after implementation."],"implementation_implications":["Track migration completion criteria.","Verify traffic and data ownership transfer.","Remove obsolete deployment and monitoring paths."],"verification_method":"Trace migration criteria and confirm target behavior, ownership, data state, and legacy retirement status.","outcome_learning":"Unexpected residual dependencies should become explicit architecture knowledge before declaring completion.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B73-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B73-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B74-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Architecture Verification","subtopic":"Architecture Conformance","concept":"Verify implemented architecture against explicit decisions and constraints rather than diagrams alone","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture conformance be verified?","answer":"Architecture conformance should compare implemented interfaces, dependencies, deployment boundaries, security controls, data flows, and operational behavior against accepted architecture decisions. Documentation alone cannot establish that implementation conforms to the intended architecture.","context":"A system has an approved architecture and an evolving implementation.","objective":"Detect divergence between architectural intent and implementation.","requirements":["Material architecture constraints must be testable or inspectable.","Current implementation state must be identifiable."],"constraints":["Some architectural properties are dynamic.","Architecture documentation can become stale."],"alternatives":["Documentation review.","Static dependency analysis.","Combined static and runtime conformance verification."],"evidence":"Implementation can diverge from architecture through incremental changes.","tradeoffs":"Conformance checks require maintenance but expose architectural drift.","decision":"Use explicit conformance verification for material architecture constraints.","rationale":"Architecture is an implemented system property, not solely a design document.","consequences":["Architecture drift becomes detectable.","Tests and tooling become linked to architecture records."],"implementation_implications":["Define forbidden and required dependencies where appropriate.","Inspect deployment and runtime relationships.","Track exceptions explicitly."],"verification_method":"Run static and dynamic checks appropriate to the architecture and compare results with current decision records.","outcome_learning":"Repeated drift should trigger architecture or implementation boundary review.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B70-R01","D54-B68-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B70-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B74-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Architecture Verification","subtopic":"Architecture Drift","concept":"Treat repeated divergence from an architecture decision as evidence for decision review rather than only implementation failure","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should repeated architecture drift trigger?","answer":"Repeated intentional or accidental deviation can indicate that the original decision is impractical, obsolete, insufficiently communicated, or poorly enforced. The response should distinguish implementation nonconformance from evidence that the architecture decision itself needs revision.","context":"Teams repeatedly bypass or modify an accepted architectural constraint.","objective":"Use drift as evidence without automatically accepting or rejecting the existing architecture.","requirements":["Deviation causes must be classified.","Decision applicability must be reviewed.","Intentional exceptions must be documented."],"constraints":["Some drift is caused by implementation shortcuts.","Some architecture decisions become obsolete."],"alternatives":["Enforce the old decision unconditionally.","Allow undocumented drift.","Review the decision and implementation causes."],"evidence":"Repeated deviations can reveal mismatch between intended architecture and practical constraints.","tradeoffs":"Reviewing decisions requires governance effort but can expose invalid assumptions.","decision":"Investigate repeated drift and determine whether the cause is nonconforming implementation, missing enforcement, or obsolete architecture.","rationale":"Drift is evidence about the interaction between architecture and implementation.","consequences":["Some decisions may be strengthened, replaced, or explicitly scoped.","Implementation teams receive clearer constraints."],"implementation_implications":["Record exception causes.","Measure repeated violations.","Link drift findings to affected decisions."],"verification_method":"Analyze recurring deviations and classify each by implementation defect, exception, enforcement gap, or decision invalidity.","outcome_learning":"Drift patterns should feed architecture evolution and governance rules.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B74-R01","D54-B68-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B74-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B74-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Architecture Verification","subtopic":"Architecture Tests","concept":"Translate material architecture constraints into automated or repeatable verification where feasible","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should architecture constraints be tested automatically where possible?","answer":"Automated architecture checks can detect forbidden dependencies, contract violations, boundary breaches, configuration errors, and other structural regressions earlier than manual review. Automation should target explicit architecture properties rather than encode arbitrary style preferences as architecture rules.","context":"An architecture contains constraints that can be represented as testable properties.","objective":"Make important architectural rules continuously verifiable.","requirements":["The architecture property must be explicit.","The test must distinguish meaningful violations from acceptable variation."],"constraints":["Some architecture properties require runtime or human judgment.","Overly rigid checks can block legitimate evolution."],"alternatives":["Manual review only.","Static automated checks.","Automated checks plus targeted human review."],"evidence":"Repeatable architecture constraints are suitable candidates for automated verification.","tradeoffs":"Automation reduces review burden but requires maintenance as architecture evolves.","decision":"Automate verification of stable, material architecture constraints where reliable checks are feasible.","rationale":"Continuous verification reduces the chance that incremental changes silently violate architecture.","consequences":["Architecture tests become part of the change workflow.","Rules require lifecycle management."],"implementation_implications":["Version architecture rules with the decisions they enforce.","Document intentional exceptions.","Avoid encoding unproven assumptions as hard constraints."],"verification_method":"Evaluate architecture tests against known compliant, noncompliant, and intentional-exception cases.","outcome_learning":"False positives and missed violations should refine the architecture test set.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B74-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B74-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B74-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Architecture Verification","subtopic":"Runtime Architecture Evidence","concept":"Use runtime evidence when architecture properties depend on actual deployment or operational behavior","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When is static architecture verification insufficient?","answer":"Static verification is insufficient when properties depend on runtime configuration, dynamic routing, dependency behavior, resource contention, failover, real traffic, or operational state. Such properties require runtime tests, controlled experiments, telemetry, or production evidence appropriate to the risk.","context":"An architecture appears structurally correct but contains dynamic operational properties.","objective":"Verify architecture properties that cannot be established from source structure alone.","requirements":["Dynamic properties must have suitable evidence sources.","Runtime evidence must retain environment and version context."],"constraints":["Production evidence may be difficult or risky to obtain.","Controlled tests may not reproduce all real conditions."],"alternatives":["Static analysis only.","Controlled runtime tests.","Production observation."],"evidence":"Some architecture guarantees depend on behavior that only exists during execution.","tradeoffs":"Runtime verification provides stronger behavioral evidence but requires additional environments and controls.","decision":"Use runtime evidence for architecture properties that cannot be established statically.","rationale":"Verification method should match the nature of the architectural property.","consequences":["Deployment configuration becomes part of evidence.","Evidence must be version and environment aware."],"implementation_implications":["Record artifact and configuration identity.","Capture representative telemetry.","Separate test evidence from production observation."],"verification_method":"Identify architecture claims that depend on runtime state and verify that each has an appropriate dynamic evidence source.","outcome_learning":"Differences between static expectations and runtime behavior should update architecture assumptions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B74-R01","D54-B68-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B68-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B74-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Architecture Verification","subtopic":"Exception Governance","concept":"Treat architecture exceptions as explicit, scoped, time-aware decisions rather than undocumented bypasses","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should exceptions to architecture constraints be governed?","answer":"An exception should identify the violated decision, reason, scope, owner, risk, compensating controls, duration or review trigger, and required follow-up. This prevents temporary exceptions from becoming invisible permanent architecture.","context":"A legitimate implementation requirement conflicts with an existing architecture constraint.","objective":"Allow controlled exceptions without losing architectural integrity.","requirements":["Exception scope must be explicit.","Risk and compensating controls must be recorded.","Review or expiration criteria must exist."],"constraints":["Some exceptions may be long-lived.","Immediate remediation may be impractical."],"alternatives":["Reject all exceptions.","Allow informal exceptions.","Governed architecture exception."],"evidence":"Unrecorded exceptions create hidden divergence and weaken architecture governance.","tradeoffs":"Exception governance adds process overhead but preserves traceability and risk visibility.","decision":"Permit exceptions only through explicit, scoped, reviewable architecture records.","rationale":"Controlled deviation is safer than undocumented deviation.","consequences":["Exceptions become searchable architecture knowledge.","Expired exceptions require cleanup or renewal."],"implementation_implications":["Link exception to decision ID.","Record compensating controls.","Monitor expiration or review triggers."],"verification_method":"Audit active exceptions for scope, ownership, evidence, compensating controls, and current validity.","outcome_learning":"Repeated exceptions should trigger review of the underlying architecture decision.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B74-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B74-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B75-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Architecture Documentation","subtopic":"Architecture Views","concept":"Use multiple architecture views when different stakeholders require different evidence about the same system","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should architecture decisions use multiple views instead of a single universal diagram?","answer":"Different stakeholders need different information: deployment, data flow, security boundaries, dependencies, runtime behavior, ownership, and business capabilities may not be understandable in one representation. Views should expose relevant concerns while remaining traceable to the same underlying architecture decisions.","context":"A complex system must communicate architecture to engineering, security, operations, and business stakeholders.","objective":"Provide context-specific architecture representations without creating contradictory models.","requirements":["Each view must have a defined purpose.","Views must reference consistent underlying architecture information."],"constraints":["Multiple views can become inconsistent.","Too many views increase maintenance cost."],"alternatives":["Single diagram.","Multiple purpose-specific views.","Unstructured documentation."],"evidence":"Different architectural concerns require different representations and levels of abstraction.","tradeoffs":"Multiple views improve comprehension but require consistency governance.","decision":"Use purpose-specific architecture views where stakeholder or concern differences materially affect understanding.","rationale":"A view is useful when it answers a defined architectural question.","consequences":["View ownership and synchronization become important.","Underlying decision records should remain authoritative for decision context."],"implementation_implications":["Label view purpose and scope.","Link views to decision and component identifiers.","Avoid duplicating authoritative facts unnecessarily."],"verification_method":"Review views for scope, purpose, consistency, and traceability to current architecture decisions.","outcome_learning":"Repeated inconsistencies should identify the authoritative source and improve view generation or governance.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B70-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B70-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B75-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Architecture Documentation","subtopic":"Decision Context","concept":"Preserve the decision context necessary to interpret an architecture choice correctly","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What context must remain attached to an architecture decision?","answer":"The decision should preserve the problem, requirements, constraints, alternatives, evidence, assumptions, scope, and consequences that made the choice appropriate. Removing this context can make a previously rational decision appear universally applicable when it was actually conditional.","context":"Architecture records will be consumed by engineers who were not present when decisions were made.","objective":"Prevent context loss from causing incorrect reuse of historical decisions.","requirements":["Decision applicability must be identifiable.","Material assumptions must be preserved."],"constraints":["Context can evolve.","Not every historical detail remains useful."],"alternatives":["Store only final decision.","Store decision plus rationale.","Store decision with structured context and provenance."],"evidence":"Architecture decisions are context-dependent and cannot always be interpreted correctly from the selected option alone.","tradeoffs":"Structured context increases record size but improves future reasoning.","decision":"Preserve the context necessary to understand applicability, alternatives, and consequences.","rationale":"Future reuse requires knowing why and under which conditions the decision was made.","consequences":["Historical decisions become more useful for reasoning.","Context maintenance becomes part of architecture governance."],"implementation_implications":["Store requirements and constraints explicitly.","Record assumptions and evidence provenance.","Maintain status and scope metadata."],"verification_method":"Remove the decision from its original discussion and test whether another engineer can correctly determine its applicability from the record.","outcome_learning":"Misinterpretation should identify missing contextual fields.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B68-R05","D54-B70-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B70-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B75-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Architecture Documentation","subtopic":"Architecture Currency","concept":"Keep current architecture state distinguishable from historical architecture decisions","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture documentation represent historical versus current state?","answer":"Current architecture descriptions should identify active components, boundaries, and decisions, while historical records should preserve superseded decisions and their reasoning. Mixing historical and current states without status creates ambiguity for implementation and automated retrieval.","context":"Architecture documentation contains decisions from multiple system generations.","objective":"Prevent historical architecture from being mistaken for current implementation guidance.","requirements":["Current state must be identifiable.","Historical decisions must retain lifecycle status."],"constraints":["Some historical information remains relevant as evidence.","Architecture may be transitioning between states."],"alternatives":["Overwrite old documentation.","Keep all versions without status.","Separate current state from lifecycle-managed historical decisions."],"evidence":"Architecture changes over time and historical reasoning remains useful without being current authority.","tradeoffs":"Explicit lifecycle metadata adds structure but reduces ambiguity.","decision":"Maintain clear distinction between current architecture state and historical decision records.","rationale":"An autonomous engineering system must know whether information describes current constraints or historical evidence.","consequences":["Retrieval can filter by status.","Architecture transitions can be represented explicitly."],"implementation_implications":["Use status and effective-scope metadata.","Record transition dates where relevant.","Link current decisions to superseded records."],"verification_method":"Query architecture knowledge for a current component and verify that superseded decisions are not presented as active constraints without context.","outcome_learning":"Retrieval ambiguity should trigger lifecycle metadata improvements.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B68-R01","D54-B68-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B68-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B75-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Architecture Documentation","subtopic":"Architecture as Code and Generated Views","concept":"Generate repeatable architecture views from authoritative structured sources when feasible","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should architecture views be generated from structured architecture data?","answer":"Generated views are useful when architecture information is already structured and the same facts must appear in multiple representations. Generation can reduce synchronization errors, but the authoritative source and generation limitations must remain explicit.","context":"Architecture information must be represented in diagrams, reports, dependency views, or machine-readable forms.","objective":"Reduce duplication and improve consistency across architecture representations.","requirements":["An authoritative source must exist.","Generated output must preserve important semantics.","Generation limitations must be known."],"constraints":["Not every architectural concept is easily represented automatically.","Generated views can become misleading if source data is incomplete."],"alternatives":["Manual diagrams.","Generated views.","Hybrid generated and curated documentation."],"evidence":"Repeated manual representation of the same facts creates synchronization risk.","tradeoffs":"Generation reduces repetitive maintenance but depends on accurate structured source data.","decision":"Generate repeatable architecture views from authoritative structured data when the representation can preserve the required semantics.","rationale":"Automation is useful where architecture information is already structured and stable.","consequences":["Source data quality becomes critical.","Generated views need validation."],"implementation_implications":["Record source and generation version.","Mark generated versus manually curated content.","Validate that important relationships are not omitted."],"verification_method":"Compare generated views with authoritative source data and test representative architecture changes for propagation.","outcome_learning":"Missing or misleading generated relationships should improve source schema or generation rules.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B75-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B75-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B75-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Architecture Documentation","subtopic":"Documentation Debt","concept":"Treat stale or contradictory architecture documentation as an engineering risk rather than a cosmetic documentation issue","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why is architecture documentation drift an engineering concern?","answer":"Stale architecture information can cause incorrect implementation, unsafe changes, invalid dependency assumptions, and incorrect automated reasoning. Material contradictions should therefore be detected, classified, and resolved or explicitly marked as uncertain.","context":"Architecture documentation and implementation have diverged over time.","objective":"Maintain trustworthy architecture knowledge for human and automated use.","requirements":["Contradictions must be detectable.","Current versus historical information must be distinguishable.","Unverified claims must not be treated as facts."],"constraints":["Some documentation becomes stale faster than it can be manually updated.","Dynamic runtime properties may require evidence outside documentation."],"alternatives":["Ignore documentation drift.","Periodic manual review.","Continuous or change-triggered consistency verification."],"evidence":"Architecture documentation can influence implementation and operational decisions.","tradeoffs":"Consistency verification requires maintenance but reduces reasoning based on stale information.","decision":"Treat material architecture-documentation drift as a knowledge-quality risk requiring explicit resolution or uncertainty marking.","rationale":"Architecture knowledge is part of engineering decision infrastructure.","consequences":["Documentation quality becomes measurable.","Contradictions become useful signals for architecture review."],"implementation_implications":["Track source and verification status.","Link documents to architecture records.","Detect stale relationships after structural changes."],"verification_method":"Compare selected architecture records with implementation and operational evidence and classify discrepancies.","outcome_learning":"Recurring documentation drift should inform automated synchronization or governance improvements.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B74-R01","D54-B75-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B74-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B76-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Architecture Decision Automation","subtopic":"Automated Decision Checks","concept":"Automate architecture checks only where decision rules are explicit and sufficiently stable","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When is automated architecture decision enforcement appropriate?","answer":"Automation is appropriate when the decision can be expressed as a reliable, testable rule and violations have sufficiently clear semantics. Rules should remain linked to the decision they enforce so that changing the architecture also updates the enforcement mechanism.","context":"An organization wants continuous automated enforcement of architecture decisions.","objective":"Prevent known architectural violations without converting uncertain guidance into rigid automation.","requirements":["Rule semantics must be explicit.","Rule ownership and lifecycle must be defined.","Exceptions must be governed."],"constraints":["Architecture rules can become obsolete.","Over-enforcement can block legitimate evolution."],"alternatives":["Manual review.","Automated rule enforcement.","Advisory automated checks."],"evidence":"Stable structural constraints can often be checked automatically.","tradeoffs":"Enforcement increases consistency but can create false positives if the rule is too broad or stale.","decision":"Automate enforcement for explicit, stable, material architecture constraints and use advisory checks where uncertainty remains.","rationale":"Automation should reflect verified architecture intent rather than encode assumptions prematurely.","consequences":["Rule maintenance becomes part of architecture governance.","Decision changes require corresponding rule review."],"implementation_implications":["Link each rule to a decision ID.","Test compliant and exception cases.","Version rules with architecture changes."],"verification_method":"Evaluate automated rules against known valid, invalid, and exceptional cases and confirm linkage to current decisions.","outcome_learning":"False positives and missed violations should trigger rule refinement or decision review.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B74-R03","D54-B74-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B74-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B76-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Architecture Decision Automation","subtopic":"Policy versus Guidance","concept":"Distinguish mandatory architecture constraints from contextual engineering guidance","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"Why should architecture knowledge distinguish policy from guidance?","answer":"Mandatory constraints define conditions that implementations must satisfy, while guidance describes recommended approaches whose applicability depends on context. Treating guidance as mandatory can overconstrain valid designs, while treating mandatory constraints as optional can violate requirements.","context":"An architecture knowledge system contains rules, principles, and recommendations.","objective":"Ensure downstream reasoning interprets architectural knowledge with the correct authority level.","requirements":["Authority level must be machine-readable.","Applicability conditions must be explicit."],"constraints":["Some guidance becomes mandatory in specific contexts.","Policies may have exceptions."],"alternatives":["Single undifferentiated rule type.","Policy and guidance classification.","Free-form recommendations."],"evidence":"Architecture statements differ in normative authority and applicability.","tradeoffs":"Classification adds metadata but improves automated interpretation.","decision":"Classify architecture statements by authority, scope, and applicability before automated enforcement or recommendation.","rationale":"Decision systems require clear distinction between constraints and contextual advice.","consequences":["Retrieval can prioritize applicable mandatory constraints.","Guidance can remain available without becoming an unconditional rule."],"implementation_implications":["Add authority and applicability fields.","Link mandatory constraints to verification mechanisms.","Record exceptions explicitly."],"verification_method":"Test retrieval and reasoning against contexts where the same guidance is applicable, optional, or overridden by a higher-authority constraint.","outcome_learning":"Misclassification should trigger schema or governance correction.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B75-R02","D54-B76-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B76-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B76-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Architecture Decision Automation","subtopic":"Decision Preconditions","concept":"Require architectural preconditions to be satisfied before applying context-dependent decisions","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should architecture decisions declare preconditions?","answer":"A decision can be valid only when specific workload, ownership, security, consistency, scale, or lifecycle conditions hold. Explicit preconditions prevent an autonomous engineering system from applying a context-bound decision to an incompatible situation.","context":"Architecture knowledge will be reused by automated reasoning across different systems or components.","objective":"Prevent out-of-context application of architecture decisions.","requirements":["Preconditions must be identifiable.","Applicability must be evaluated before decision reuse."],"constraints":["Some preconditions are qualitative or uncertain.","Incomplete context can prevent safe automatic application."],"alternatives":["Apply decision universally.","Apply based on topic similarity.","Evaluate explicit preconditions."],"evidence":"Architecture choices are often conditional on system context.","tradeoffs":"Precondition evaluation adds reasoning complexity but reduces inappropriate reuse.","decision":"Represent material applicability preconditions explicitly and require them to be satisfied or marked unknown before applying the decision.","rationale":"Context is part of the meaning of an architecture decision.","consequences":["Automated retrieval must preserve context metadata.","Unknown preconditions may require human review."],"implementation_implications":["Store prerequisites and applicability conditions.","Represent unknown state separately from false.","Prevent unconditional rule generation from conditional decisions."],"verification_method":"Test decisions against matching, non-matching, and incomplete-context scenarios.","outcome_learning":"Incorrect application should identify missing or ambiguous preconditions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B75-R02","D54-B70-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B70-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B76-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Architecture Decision Automation","subtopic":"Contradiction Handling","concept":"Do not resolve conflicting architecture evidence by silent overwrite","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an architecture knowledge system handle contradictory evidence?","answer":"Contradictory evidence should remain represented with provenance, scope, version, and confidence until the conflict is resolved. The system should distinguish true contradiction from different contexts, measurement conditions, or technology versions.","context":"Two credible architecture records make incompatible claims.","objective":"Preserve uncertainty and prevent false certainty in automated reasoning.","requirements":["Each claim must retain provenance.","Context and version must be comparable.","Conflict status must be explicit."],"constraints":["Conflicts may remain unresolved.","Evidence may be incomplete or non-comparable."],"alternatives":["Overwrite older evidence.","Choose one source automatically.","Represent contradiction explicitly."],"evidence":"Engineering evidence can differ across versions, workloads, environments, and assumptions.","tradeoffs":"Explicit contradiction handling complicates retrieval but prevents unsupported certainty.","decision":"Represent contradictions explicitly and resolve them only when scope, evidence, or authoritative context supports resolution.","rationale":"Unresolved uncertainty is safer than silently selecting an unsupported claim.","consequences":["Automated reasoning must account for contradiction status.","Decision confidence may remain conditional."],"implementation_implications":["Store provenance and applicability.","Distinguish contradiction from supersession.","Require evidence comparison before resolution."],"verification_method":"Introduce conflicting records and verify that retrieval preserves both claims and their context rather than silently collapsing them.","outcome_learning":"Resolved contradictions should preserve the evidence and reason for resolution.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B68-R05","D54-B75-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B75-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B76-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Architecture Decision Automation","subtopic":"Human Approval Boundary","concept":"Require human review when automated architecture reasoning crosses high-impact or insufficiently evidenced decision boundaries","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should an autonomous engineering system defer an architecture decision to human review?","answer":"Human review is appropriate when the decision has high irreversible impact, insufficient evidence, unresolved contradiction, significant security or compliance consequences, ambiguous requirements, or material uncertainty about applicability. Automation can prepare alternatives and evidence without silently converting uncertainty into authorization.","context":"An autonomous engineering system assists with architecture decisions.","objective":"Preserve human control over high-impact architectural choices.","requirements":["Decision authority must be explicit.","Uncertainty and evidence state must be visible.","Human approval must be auditable where required."],"constraints":["Human review adds latency.","Some low-risk decisions can be automated safely."],"alternatives":["Fully automated decision.","Human-only decision.","Risk-based automation with escalation."],"evidence":"Decision authority should correspond to consequence, reversibility, and evidence quality.","tradeoffs":"Escalation reduces automation speed but limits high-impact unsupported decisions.","decision":"Use risk- and evidence-based escalation for architecture decisions that exceed defined autonomous authority.","rationale":"Automation should support engineering judgment rather than silently exercise authority beyond its evidence and governance boundary.","consequences":["The knowledge system needs explicit escalation criteria.","Approval state becomes part of decision provenance."],"implementation_implications":["Define approval thresholds.","Record approver and decision evidence where governance requires it.","Prevent execution or architecture mutation before required approval."],"verification_method":"Test high-impact, ambiguous, contradictory, and low-risk scenarios and verify correct escalation behavior.","outcome_learning":"Escalation patterns should inform future authority boundaries and evidence requirements.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B76-R03","D54-B76-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B76-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B77-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Architecture Evolution","subtopic":"Evolutionary Architecture","concept":"Design architecture for controlled evolution rather than assuming requirements and workloads remain static","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should expected change influence architecture decisions?","answer":"Architecture should identify dimensions likely to change, isolate them where justified, and avoid premature generalization for changes that are merely hypothetical. Evolutionary design should balance expected change cost against present complexity.","context":"Requirements, workloads, technology, and organizational ownership are expected to evolve.","objective":"Preserve practical adaptability without overengineering for speculative futures.","requirements":["Material change dimensions must be identified.","Adaptability mechanisms must have a justified purpose."],"constraints":["Future change is uncertain.","Additional flexibility can increase present complexity."],"alternatives":["Optimize for current state.","Design for identified likely changes.","Generalize for many hypothetical changes."],"evidence":"Architecture must often survive changes in requirements, dependencies, scale, and ownership.","tradeoffs":"Adaptability can reduce future migration cost while increasing current complexity.","decision":"Design explicitly for material expected change while avoiding abstraction whose value depends only on speculative scenarios.","rationale":"Architecture should be adaptable where evidence supports the need.","consequences":["Change boundaries become architecture concerns.","Some future changes remain intentionally unsupported."],"implementation_implications":["Identify likely change axes.","Use stable contracts at justified boundaries.","Record assumptions that would trigger redesign."],"verification_method":"Review architecture against identified change scenarios and determine whether the intended changes can occur within declared boundaries.","outcome_learning":"Actual changes should validate or challenge the assumed change dimensions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B72-R04","D54-B69-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B69-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B77-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Architecture Evolution","subtopic":"Change Cost","concept":"Evaluate architecture changes by both implementation effort and resulting future change flexibility","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"Why should architecture decisions consider future change cost?","answer":"A design that is inexpensive to implement can create expensive future changes through tight coupling, irreversible state choices, or contract instability. Conversely, excessive flexibility can make current implementation unnecessarily complex. The decision should compare present and expected future costs under plausible change scenarios.","context":"Two architectures differ in current simplicity and future adaptability.","objective":"Balance immediate delivery against credible future change costs.","requirements":["Likely change scenarios must be explicit.","Future cost assumptions must be identified."],"constraints":["Future changes are uncertain.","Precise future cost estimates may not be possible."],"alternatives":["Optimize current implementation.","Optimize for known future changes.","Use flexible abstraction for all future changes."],"evidence":"Architecture creates path dependence and can change the cost of later modifications.","tradeoffs":"Future-proofing can reduce later migration effort while increasing present complexity and maintenance.","decision":"Consider future change cost when the change scenario is credible and materially affects the architecture choice.","rationale":"Only material and plausible future costs should justify present complexity.","consequences":["Architecture decisions become scenario-sensitive.","Review triggers can be tied to changes in assumptions."],"implementation_implications":["Record credible change scenarios.","Measure actual change effort after implementation.","Avoid unsupported long-term cost claims."],"verification_method":"Compare the architecture against stated future-change scenarios and verify whether the claimed flexibility is real and bounded.","outcome_learning":"Actual change effort should refine assumptions about architectural flexibility.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B77-R01","D54-B69-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B77-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B77-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Architecture Evolution","subtopic":"Modularity","concept":"Use modularity to isolate independently changing responsibilities rather than maximize component count","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should determine whether a responsibility deserves a separate architectural module?","answer":"A responsibility should be separated when its ownership, change rate, security boundary, resource profile, lifecycle, or conceptual cohesion differs materially from neighboring responsibilities. Component count alone is not evidence of modularity quality.","context":"A system can be organized into fewer larger modules or many smaller ones.","objective":"Create useful change and ownership boundaries without unnecessary fragmentation.","requirements":["Module boundaries must have identifiable responsibilities.","Dependencies should remain understandable."],"constraints":["More modules increase coordination and integration complexity.","Large modules can conceal unrelated responsibilities."],"alternatives":["Few broad modules.","Fine-grained modules.","Responsibility-driven modularity."],"evidence":"Useful modularity isolates concerns that change or operate independently.","tradeoffs":"Smaller modules can improve isolation but increase dependency and coordination surface.","decision":"Define modules around cohesive responsibilities and meaningful independence rather than an arbitrary component count.","rationale":"Modularity is valuable when it reduces change coupling or clarifies ownership.","consequences":["Module boundaries become architectural decisions.","Refactoring may be required as change patterns emerge."],"implementation_implications":["Measure dependency direction and coupling.","Keep module contracts explicit.","Avoid artificial fragmentation."],"verification_method":"Analyze change dependencies and verify that modules isolate intended responsibilities without excessive cross-module coordination.","outcome_learning":"Repeated cross-module changes should trigger boundary review.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B71-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B71-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B77-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Architecture Evolution","subtopic":"Dependency Direction","concept":"Prefer dependency direction that keeps stable policy independent from volatile implementation details where practical","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should dependency direction be considered in architecture decisions?","answer":"If stable business or policy logic depends directly on volatile infrastructure details, changes in those details can propagate widely. Architecture can reduce this coupling by directing dependencies toward stable contracts or domain abstractions when doing so preserves required semantics without excessive indirection.","context":"A system contains stable business logic and frequently changing infrastructure or integration details.","objective":"Limit propagation of volatile implementation changes.","requirements":["Stable and volatile responsibilities must be identified.","Dependency inversion must preserve semantics."],"constraints":["Abstraction can add complexity.","Some dependencies are naturally bidirectional at runtime even when code ownership is separated."],"alternatives":["Direct dependency on infrastructure.","Stable interface boundary.","Full dependency inversion framework."],"evidence":"Dependency direction affects the scope of change propagation.","tradeoffs":"Inversion can improve change isolation while increasing abstraction and wiring complexity.","decision":"Prefer dependency direction that protects stable policy from volatile implementation where the resulting boundary is justified.","rationale":"Change isolation is improved when stable responsibilities do not directly depend on frequently changing details.","consequences":["Interfaces and adapters may be introduced.","Architecture becomes more explicit about ownership."],"implementation_implications":["Keep policy interfaces narrow.","Place infrastructure adapters behind stable contracts.","Test policy independently where meaningful."],"verification_method":"Change a volatile implementation and verify that stable policy code does not require unnecessary modification.","outcome_learning":"Repeated adapter churn may indicate an incorrectly defined boundary.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B77-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B77-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B77-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Architecture Evolution","subtopic":"Architecture Fitness Functions","concept":"Continuously check measurable architectural properties that are likely to regress during evolution","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should an architecture use continuous fitness checks?","answer":"Continuous fitness checks are useful when an architectural property can be expressed as a repeatable test or measurement and regression would materially affect system quality. Examples include dependency restrictions, latency budgets, security properties, resource limits, compatibility, or deployment constraints.","context":"An architecture must remain aligned while many incremental changes are introduced.","objective":"Detect architectural regression continuously rather than only during major reviews.","requirements":["Property must be measurable or testable.","Threshold or rule must have architectural meaning."],"constraints":["Metrics can become stale or gamed.","Some architectural qualities require contextual judgment."],"alternatives":["Periodic review.","Continuous fitness checks.","Manual approval for every change."],"evidence":"Repeated automated checks can detect regression in stable architectural properties.","tradeoffs":"Continuous checks improve early detection but require maintenance and calibrated thresholds.","decision":"Use fitness checks for material, measurable architecture properties with explicit interpretation.","rationale":"Continuous verification is valuable where architectural quality can otherwise silently degrade.","consequences":["Architecture criteria become part of engineering workflows.","Threshold changes require governance."],"implementation_implications":["Link checks to decision records.","Document measurement assumptions.","Review thresholds when architecture or workload changes."],"verification_method":"Test fitness checks against known compliant, borderline, and violating states.","outcome_learning":"Repeated threshold violations should inform architecture review rather than merely trigger repeated warnings.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B74-R03","D54-B76-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B74-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B78-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Architecture Tradeoffs","subtopic":"Latency versus Consistency","concept":"Choose latency and consistency tradeoffs according to business correctness requirements","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should an architecture evaluate latency versus consistency tradeoffs?","answer":"The decision should identify which operations require fresh authoritative state and which can tolerate stale or asynchronously updated information. Lower latency achieved through caching, replication, or asynchronous processing may be acceptable only when the resulting consistency semantics preserve business correctness.","context":"A system must balance response time against freshness and coordination requirements.","objective":"Select consistency behavior that satisfies actual business consequences.","requirements":["Freshness requirements must be explicit.","Stale-read consequences must be understood."],"constraints":["Stronger consistency can increase latency or reduce availability.","Asynchronous updates introduce temporary divergence."],"alternatives":["Strong consistency.","Eventual consistency.","Hybrid consistency by operation."],"evidence":"Consistency requirements vary by business operation and data meaning.","tradeoffs":"Lower latency can require accepting stale or deferred state; stronger consistency can require coordination and latency.","decision":"Choose consistency per business operation and consequence rather than applying one global policy.","rationale":"Different operations can legitimately have different freshness requirements.","consequences":["APIs and data models must expose relevant consistency semantics.","Tests must cover stale and concurrent states."],"implementation_implications":["Document freshness guarantees.","Identify authoritative reads and writes.","Avoid hiding eventual consistency behind synchronous-looking contracts."],"verification_method":"Exercise concurrent updates and stale-read scenarios and compare outcomes with declared business requirements.","outcome_learning":"User-visible stale-state failures should trigger review of consistency boundaries.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B35-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B35-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B78-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Architecture Tradeoffs","subtopic":"Latency versus Cost","concept":"Evaluate latency optimization against infrastructure and operational cost under representative workloads","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When should architecture invest in lower latency?","answer":"Latency optimization is justified when measured or required response-time constraints materially affect business outcomes, user experience, resource utilization, or downstream capacity. Optimization should compare the benefit against added infrastructure, complexity, and operational cost.","context":"A system has latency requirements but multiple optimization strategies are available.","objective":"Avoid paying architectural complexity for latency improvements that do not materially matter.","requirements":["Latency objective must be explicit.","Workload and measurement conditions must be defined."],"constraints":["Latency varies by workload and environment.","Optimization can introduce caching, replication, or concurrency complexity."],"alternatives":["Optimize aggressively.","Meet a defined latency budget.","Accept higher latency."],"evidence":"Latency value depends on business requirements and measured workload behavior.","tradeoffs":"Lower latency can require additional capacity, caching, replication, or complexity.","decision":"Optimize toward an explicit latency requirement and verify benefit under representative conditions.","rationale":"Latency should be treated as an architectural requirement rather than an abstract objective.","consequences":["Performance budgets become architecture constraints.","Optimizations require regression verification."],"implementation_implications":["Measure end-to-end latency.","Separate average from tail behavior where relevant.","Track infrastructure impact of optimization."],"verification_method":"Benchmark representative workloads and compare latency and resource cost against the stated requirement.","outcome_learning":"Observed workload changes should trigger reassessment of optimization value.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B15-R01","D54-B69-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B15-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B78-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Architecture Tradeoffs","subtopic":"Availability versus Consistency","concept":"Make availability and consistency tradeoffs explicit when distributed coordination is involved","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should availability and consistency be evaluated in a distributed architecture?","answer":"The decision should identify which business invariants must remain protected during network partitions, dependency failures, or concurrent updates and which operations can degrade or accept temporary divergence. The architecture should define failure semantics rather than relying on a generic availability-versus-consistency label.","context":"A distributed system can experience communication failure while components remain individually operational.","objective":"Preserve required business correctness under distributed failure conditions.","requirements":["Critical invariants must be identified.","Partition behavior must be defined."],"constraints":["Strong coordination can reduce availability during communication failures.","Degraded operation can create reconciliation requirements."],"alternatives":["Prioritize immediate availability.","Prioritize invariant preservation.","Different policies by operation."],"evidence":"Distributed failure can prevent all components from observing the same state simultaneously.","tradeoffs":"Preserving strong consistency can reduce available operations during failures; accepting divergence can improve availability while requiring reconciliation.","decision":"Define consistency and availability behavior per business operation and failure consequence.","rationale":"The appropriate tradeoff depends on the invariant and failure semantics.","consequences":["Failure modes become part of API and workflow contracts.","Reconciliation may be required for selected operations."],"implementation_implications":["Define partition behavior.","Identify operations that must fail rather than diverge.","Design reconciliation where divergence is accepted."],"verification_method":"Simulate communication partition and verify each operation follows its declared behavior without violating protected invariants.","outcome_learning":"Partition incidents should update consistency boundaries and recovery assumptions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B35-R01","D54-B34-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B35-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B78-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Architecture Tradeoffs","subtopic":"Flexibility versus Simplicity","concept":"Add architectural flexibility only when credible change or variability justifies its complexity","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should architecture balance flexibility against simplicity?","answer":"Flexibility is justified when a credible requirement, change axis, deployment context, or integration variation is expected to materialize and the cost of accommodating it later is material. Otherwise, additional abstraction and configuration can increase present complexity without corresponding value.","context":"A design can either remain simple or introduce extension points for potential future requirements.","objective":"Avoid both rigid designs and speculative overengineering.","requirements":["The expected variation must be identifiable.","Flexibility mechanism must preserve understandable semantics."],"constraints":["Future requirements may not materialize.","Rigid designs can make later changes expensive."],"alternatives":["Simple fixed design.","Targeted extension point.","General-purpose framework."],"evidence":"Flexibility has value when it addresses real variation or costly anticipated change.","tradeoffs":"Additional flexibility increases implementation and testing surface.","decision":"Introduce only flexibility supported by credible requirements or material change risk.","rationale":"Architecture should pay complexity costs when there is a justified reason to do so.","consequences":["Extension points become architecture commitments.","Speculative flexibility is intentionally limited."],"implementation_implications":["Identify supported extension dimensions.","Avoid generic configuration without clear semantics.","Test supported variations."],"verification_method":"Review each extension point and identify the requirement or credible change scenario that justifies it.","outcome_learning":"Unused flexibility should be considered for removal when it creates ongoing complexity.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B77-R01","D54-B77-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B77-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B78-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Architecture Tradeoffs","subtopic":"Security versus Usability","concept":"Evaluate security controls against the actual protected property and user workflow rather than maximizing friction","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should architecture evaluate security and usability tradeoffs?","answer":"The decision should identify the protected asset or operation, threat consequence, user workflow, frequency, and available compensating controls. Security requirements should not be weakened merely for convenience, but controls should be designed to achieve the required protection without unnecessary operational friction.","context":"A security control materially changes user or system interaction.","objective":"Meet required security properties while preserving legitimate usability and operational workflows.","requirements":["Protected security property must be explicit.","Threat and consequence must be understood.","Alternative controls should be considered."],"constraints":["Some high-impact operations legitimately require stronger friction.","Usability failures can lead to unsafe workarounds."],"alternatives":["Maximum restriction.","Minimum restriction.","Risk-proportional control design."],"evidence":"Security controls affect both protection and user behavior.","tradeoffs":"Stronger controls can increase friction; poorly designed friction can reduce adoption or encourage bypasses.","decision":"Choose controls proportional to the protected property and threat consequence while minimizing unnecessary friction.","rationale":"Security architecture should protect defined properties rather than maximize restriction indiscriminately.","consequences":["Security and product stakeholders may share ownership.","Exceptions require explicit risk decisions."],"implementation_implications":["Use stronger controls for higher-impact operations.","Provide safe recovery and exception paths.","Measure control effectiveness and operational burden."],"verification_method":"Test authorized, unauthorized, recovery, and exception workflows and verify both security properties and intended usability constraints.","outcome_learning":"Observed bypass behavior or control failures should inform security architecture refinement.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B67-R02","D54-B67-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B67-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B79-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Architecture Decision Review","subtopic":"Review Triggers","concept":"Define explicit conditions that trigger architecture decision reassessment","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should trigger an architecture decision review?","answer":"Review triggers may include material requirement changes, workload changes, technology lifecycle changes, security findings, repeated architecture drift, performance regression, operational incidents, cost changes, dependency failures, or evidence that a decision's assumptions no longer hold.","context":"Architecture decisions must remain valid as system conditions evolve.","objective":"Detect when existing decisions require reassessment before they become obsolete constraints.","requirements":["Material assumptions must be identified.","Review triggers must be observable where possible."],"constraints":["Reviewing every minor change is impractical.","Some trigger thresholds are qualitative."],"alternatives":["Fixed periodic review.","Event-driven review.","Risk-based combination."],"evidence":"Decision applicability changes when the context or assumptions that supported the decision change.","tradeoffs":"Event-driven review reduces unnecessary work but requires meaningful triggers.","decision":"Use risk-proportional review triggers tied to decision assumptions and consequences.","rationale":"Architecture governance should react to material context changes rather than calendar time alone.","consequences":["Operational and engineering signals become architecture inputs.","Review ownership must be defined."],"implementation_implications":["Record review triggers with decisions.","Monitor measurable thresholds where applicable.","Create escalation for material evidence changes."],"verification_method":"Inspect material decisions and verify that each has appropriate reassessment triggers.","outcome_learning":"Missed trigger events should improve monitoring and decision metadata.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B68-R01","D54-B69-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B68-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B79-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Architecture Decision Review","subtopic":"Periodic Review","concept":"Use periodic review for long-lived decisions whose assumptions may change gradually without obvious event triggers","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When is periodic architecture review useful even without a known trigger?","answer":"Periodic review is useful for long-lived foundational decisions whose assumptions can drift gradually, especially around technology lifecycle, cost, capacity, security exposure, organizational ownership, and external dependencies. Periodic review should complement rather than replace event-driven review.","context":"A foundational architecture decision may remain unchanged while its surrounding environment evolves.","objective":"Detect slow-moving architectural obsolescence.","requirements":["Review scope must be proportional to decision impact.","Current evidence must be compared with original assumptions."],"constraints":["Periodic review consumes engineering capacity.","Not every decision warrants recurring review."],"alternatives":["No review until failure.","Fixed periodic review.","Risk-based periodic plus event-driven review."],"evidence":"Some architectural conditions deteriorate gradually rather than through a single observable event.","tradeoffs":"Periodic review adds governance effort but can detect slow drift before failure.","decision":"Apply periodic review to long-lived, high-impact decisions whose assumptions can change gradually.","rationale":"Review frequency should follow the rate and consequence of assumption change.","consequences":["Decision records require review metadata.","Low-impact decisions can remain event-driven."],"implementation_implications":["Define review interval based on risk and change rate.","Compare current evidence with original assumptions.","Record review outcome without rewriting historical rationale."],"verification_method":"Review selected foundational decisions and verify that current assumptions remain supported by available evidence.","outcome_learning":"Repeatedly unchanged low-risk decisions may justify less frequent review; changed assumptions should trigger deeper analysis.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B79-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B79-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B79-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Architecture Decision Review","subtopic":"Decision Review Outcome","concept":"Record review outcomes even when an architecture decision remains unchanged","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should unchanged architecture decisions still record review evidence?","answer":"A review that confirms a decision remains applicable is evidence that current conditions were considered. Recording the review prevents uncertainty about whether the decision is still active and distinguishes intentional continuation from simple documentation neglect.","context":"A mature architecture decision is periodically reassessed and remains valid.","objective":"Make decision lifecycle state and review activity explicit.","requirements":["Review date and scope must be recorded.","Evidence considered must be identifiable."],"constraints":["Review evidence can become repetitive if poorly scoped.","A review does not prove future validity."],"alternatives":["No record when unchanged.","Update original rationale.","Record a linked review outcome."],"evidence":"Lifecycle state is different from the original decision rationale.","tradeoffs":"Review records add metadata but preserve governance traceability.","decision":"Record meaningful review outcomes separately from the original decision rationale.","rationale":"The original decision should remain historically stable while review evidence demonstrates current applicability.","consequences":["Decision history becomes auditable.","Review records can become learning inputs."],"implementation_implications":["Link review outcome to decision ID.","Record evidence and changed assumptions.","Keep original decision rationale immutable except through explicit successor decisions."],"verification_method":"Inspect reviewed decisions and verify that current applicability and evidence are distinguishable from original rationale.","outcome_learning":"Repeated review outcomes can inform future review frequency and trigger design.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B79-R01","D54-B79-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B79-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B79-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Architecture Decision Review","subtopic":"Architecture Decision Metrics","concept":"Measure architecture governance by decision quality and outcome evidence rather than decision-record count","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should architecture decision governance be evaluated?","answer":"Useful measures can include decision lead time, evidence quality, unresolved contradiction rate, architecture drift, exception frequency, decision reversals, migration surprises, and whether decisions have traceable verification. Counting the number of architecture records alone does not measure architectural quality.","context":"An organization wants to evaluate its architecture decision process.","objective":"Measure whether decision governance improves engineering outcomes.","requirements":["Metrics must have defined semantics.","Measures must not reward documentation volume alone."],"constraints":["Many architecture outcomes are difficult to attribute to one decision.","Metrics can create undesirable incentives."],"alternatives":["Count ADRs.","Measure process activity.","Measure evidence quality and outcome signals."],"evidence":"Record quantity does not establish decision usefulness or correctness.","tradeoffs":"Outcome-oriented metrics are harder to collect but more meaningful than volume metrics.","decision":"Evaluate architecture governance using evidence quality, applicability, conformance, and outcome signals rather than record count.","rationale":"Architecture governance exists to improve decisions and system outcomes, not maximize documentation.","consequences":["Metrics require careful interpretation.","Teams should avoid optimizing for easy-to-count documentation activity."],"implementation_implications":["Track decision lifecycle and evidence state.","Measure architecture drift and repeated exceptions.","Review whether metrics create perverse incentives."],"verification_method":"Audit governance metrics and determine whether they correlate with decision quality and useful engineering outcomes.","outcome_learning":"Metric failures should cause metric redesign rather than behavior manipulation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B79-R03","D54-B69-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B79-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B79-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Architecture Decision Review","subtopic":"Decision Debt","concept":"Identify unresolved architecture decisions that materially block implementation or create hidden assumptions","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What is architecture decision debt?","answer":"Architecture decision debt consists of unresolved or undocumented choices whose uncertainty forces repeated local assumptions, inconsistent implementations, rework, or risky dependencies. It should be prioritized according to consequence and reversibility rather than the number of unresolved questions.","context":"Engineering teams repeatedly make local architecture choices because a shared decision has not been established.","objective":"Make consequential unresolved architecture choices visible and actionable.","requirements":["Decision debt must identify the unresolved choice and consequence.","Priority must consider impact and reversibility."],"constraints":["Not every unresolved design detail needs centralized decision-making.","Premature decisions can be worse than bounded uncertainty."],"alternatives":["Decide everything centrally.","Allow all local choices.","Escalate only materially consequential unresolved decisions."],"evidence":"Unresolved high-impact decisions can create inconsistent system behavior and hidden coupling.","tradeoffs":"Central decisions improve consistency but can slow local delivery if applied indiscriminately.","decision":"Track architecture decision debt only for unresolved choices whose consequences are material and shared.","rationale":"Decision governance should focus on choices that materially affect architecture or multiple components.","consequences":["Teams gain a mechanism for escalating consequential uncertainty.","Low-impact implementation details remain local."],"implementation_implications":["Record unresolved alternatives and assumptions.","Define decision owner and required evidence.","Close debt through explicit decision or documented acceptance of uncertainty."],"verification_method":"Review repeated local design choices and determine whether they indicate an unresolved shared architecture decision.","outcome_learning":"Recurring decision debt should inform clearer ownership and decision boundaries.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B76-R05","D54-B79-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B76-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B80-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Architecture Decision Synthesis","subtopic":"Decision Completeness","concept":"Evaluate an architecture decision as a complete reasoning chain from context through verified consequences","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What makes an architecture decision record complete enough for autonomous engineering use?","answer":"A decision should connect problem context, requirements, constraints, alternatives, evidence, trade-offs, selected approach, rationale, consequences, implementation implications, verification method, provenance, applicability, and outcome learning. Missing context can make an otherwise correct decision unsafe to reuse.","context":"Architecture decisions are being prepared for machine retrieval and engineering reasoning.","objective":"Create complete decision records that preserve reasoning and verification boundaries.","requirements":["Decision reasoning must be traceable.","Evidence and verification state must be distinct.","Applicability must be explicit."],"constraints":["Not every field is equally relevant to every decision.","Over-documentation can reduce signal quality."],"alternatives":["Minimal decision statement.","Narrative ADR.","Structured complete decision chain."],"evidence":"Autonomous reasoning requires context and evidence beyond the final selected option.","tradeoffs":"Structured completeness increases record richness while requiring disciplined maintenance.","decision":"Require decision-chain completeness proportional to decision impact and reuse potential.","rationale":"The knowledge system needs enough context to avoid applying a decision outside its intended conditions.","consequences":["Architecture records become directly usable as reasoning inputs.","Quality gates can detect missing decision components."],"implementation_implications":["Validate required fields.","Link decisions to requirements, implementation, tests, and outcomes.","Preserve provenance and contradiction status."],"verification_method":"Run completeness checks against material decisions and verify that all decision-critical reasoning stages are represented.","outcome_learning":"Missing fields discovered during reuse should improve the decision schema or authoring guidance.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B68-R05","D54-B70-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"integrates","record_id":"D54-B70-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B80-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Architecture Decision Synthesis","subtopic":"Decision Comparability","concept":"Compare architecture alternatives using common criteria while preserving context-specific differences","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should architecture alternatives be compared without creating misleading universal rankings?","answer":"Alternatives should be evaluated against criteria derived from the actual requirements and constraints, with evidence and uncertainty recorded for each criterion. The comparison should preserve context, assumptions, and trade-offs rather than collapsing them into an unsupported universal score.","context":"Multiple alternatives appear viable for an architecture decision.","objective":"Produce a transparent comparison that supports engineering reasoning.","requirements":["Criteria must derive from the decision context.","Evidence and uncertainty must remain visible.","Trade-offs must be explicit."],"constraints":["Criteria can conflict.","Quantitative scoring can create false precision when evidence is weak."],"alternatives":["Informal preference.","Weighted scoring.","Evidence-based qualitative or quantitative comparison with uncertainty."],"evidence":"Alternative suitability depends on context-specific requirements and evidence.","tradeoffs":"Structured comparison improves transparency but can imply precision beyond the evidence if poorly designed.","decision":"Use context-derived comparison criteria and preserve uncertainty instead of forcing a single universal ranking.","rationale":"Architecture decisions are conditional engineering choices, not globally ranked products.","consequences":["Different contexts can legitimately produce different selections.","Decision records retain alternative evidence for future reassessment."],"implementation_implications":["Record criterion definitions.","Separate measured evidence from inference.","Document why criteria matter to the decision."],"verification_method":"Review comparison tables or analyses for traceability from criteria to requirements and evidence.","outcome_learning":"Actual outcomes should validate or challenge the assumptions behind individual criteria.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B71-R01","D54-B69-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B69-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B80-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Architecture Decision Synthesis","subtopic":"Decision Reuse","concept":"Reuse architecture decisions through applicability matching rather than copying conclusions","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an autonomous engineering system reuse a prior architecture decision?","answer":"The system should retrieve the prior decision, compare its requirements, constraints, preconditions, evidence, versions, scope, and consequences with the current problem, and then determine whether it is applicable, partially applicable, contradictory, or only historical evidence. Reuse should preserve reasoning rather than copy the conclusion blindly.","context":"A current engineering problem resembles a previously decided architecture problem.","objective":"Accelerate reasoning while avoiding out-of-context decision reuse.","requirements":["Applicability conditions must be evaluated.","Historical and current status must be distinguished.","Contradictions must remain visible."],"constraints":["Similarity does not imply equivalence.","Current technology or requirements may differ."],"alternatives":["Copy previous decision.","Ignore historical decisions.","Reuse through contextual comparison."],"evidence":"Architecture decisions are context-bound and can become obsolete or inapplicable.","tradeoffs":"Contextual reuse requires more reasoning than direct copying but reduces inappropriate transfer.","decision":"Reuse prior architecture knowledge only after contextual applicability and evidence comparison.","rationale":"The value of historical decisions is transferable reasoning, not unconditional conclusions.","consequences":["Retrieval becomes a reasoning step rather than a final answer.","Similar problems may legitimately receive different decisions."],"implementation_implications":["Match requirements, constraints, versions, scope, and decision status.","Retrieve related alternatives and outcomes.","Escalate when applicability is uncertain."],"verification_method":"Test retrieval against same-topic but different-context decisions and verify that only applicable evidence is treated as current guidance.","outcome_learning":"Incorrect reuse should improve applicability metadata and retrieval logic.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B76-R03","D54-B76-R04","D54-B75-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B76-R03"},{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B76-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B80-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Architecture Decision Synthesis","subtopic":"Decision-to-Learning Loop","concept":"Feed verified architecture outcomes back into future decision evidence without fabricating general rules from isolated observations","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture knowledge learn from verified outcomes?","answer":"Verified outcomes should update evidence associated with the affected decision and may influence future alternatives, assumptions, thresholds, or review triggers. A single context-specific observation should not automatically become a universal architecture rule; applicability and evidence strength must be preserved.","context":"The architecture knowledge system receives verified implementation or operational outcomes.","objective":"Convert experience into reusable engineering knowledge without overgeneralization.","requirements":["Outcome provenance must be preserved.","Observation scope must remain explicit.","Generalization must require sufficient evidence."],"constraints":["Operational observations may be environment-specific.","Causal attribution can be uncertain."],"alternatives":["Ignore outcomes.","Convert every outcome into a rule.","Store verified outcomes with scoped applicability."],"evidence":"Architecture learning is valuable when observed outcomes are linked to decisions and interpreted within their conditions.","tradeoffs":"Conservative generalization reduces false rules but may slow knowledge accumulation.","decision":"Store verified outcomes as scoped evidence and generalize only when evidence supports broader applicability.","rationale":"Learning quality depends on preserving context and avoiding unsupported extrapolation.","consequences":["Architecture knowledge becomes cumulative and evidence-aware.","Contradictory outcomes can remain visible."],"implementation_implications":["Link outcome records to decisions.","Record environment, versions, workload, and verification state.","Update confidence rather than rewriting historical facts."],"verification_method":"Review learned knowledge and verify that each generalized claim has sufficient supporting evidence and preserved scope.","outcome_learning":"Repeated consistent outcomes can strengthen confidence; contradictory outcomes should trigger reassessment.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B70-R03","D54-B79-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B70-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B80-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Architecture Decision Synthesis","subtopic":"Architecture Decision Quality Gate","concept":"Lock an architecture decision only after context, alternatives, evidence, implementation implications, verification, and lifecycle state are internally consistent","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What final quality gate should be applied before an architecture decision enters an authoritative knowledge fabric?","answer":"The decision should pass checks for scope completeness, requirement and constraint traceability, alternative coverage, evidence provenance, contradiction status, implementation implications, verification method, lifecycle status, relationship integrity, and absence of unsupported execution or outcome claims. A decision that fails a material quality gate should remain provisional rather than being treated as authoritative.","context":"Structured architecture decisions are being admitted into an autonomous engineering knowledge fabric.","objective":"Prevent incomplete, contradictory, duplicated, or unverified architecture knowledge from becoming authoritative.","requirements":["Stable identity must exist.","Provenance must be present.","Validation and execution status must be truthful.","Relationships must resolve or be explicitly marked unresolved."],"constraints":["Some decisions legitimately contain uncertainty.","Not every decision can have execution evidence at creation time."],"alternatives":["Accept all authored decisions.","Manual review only.","Structured quality gate with evidence-aware lifecycle status."],"evidence":"Knowledge reliability depends on provenance, applicability, verification state, and structural integrity.","tradeoffs":"Quality gates increase ingestion effort but reduce downstream reasoning contamination.","decision":"Apply an evidence-aware quality gate before locking architecture decisions as authoritative knowledge.","rationale":"The Knowledge Fabric must distinguish structured engineering inference from verified operational fact.","consequences":["Provisional and authoritative records remain distinguishable.","Invalid or duplicate records are rejected or quarantined."],"implementation_implications":["Validate schema and stable IDs.","Run exact, near, semantic, and relationship deduplication.","Check contradiction and supersession metadata.","Reject fabricated execution or outcome claims."],"verification_method":"Run schema validation, deduplication, relationship integrity, contradiction checks, provenance checks, and truthfulness checks before dataset locking.","outcome_learning":"Quality-gate failures should feed back into dataset generation and schema improvements rather than being hidden.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B80-R01","D54-B80-R03","D54-B80-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"integrates","record_id":"D54-B80-R01"},{"type":"integrates","record_id":"D54-B80-R03"},{"type":"integrates","record_id":"D54-B80-R04"}]}

{"record_id":"D54-B81-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Decision framing","concept":"Decision scope and decision boundaries","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an architecture decision be scoped so that it solves the intended problem without silently deciding unrelated concerns?","answer":"Define the decision as a bounded change to architecture behavior, structure, or constraints. Explicitly state what is being decided, what remains unchanged, which requirements drive the decision, and which adjacent concerns are deferred.","context":"Architecture discussions often expand from one decision into unrelated technology, organizational, or implementation questions.","objective":"Produce a decision record with an explicit scope boundary.","requirements":["State the architectural concern being resolved","Identify affected components or boundaries","Separate decided concerns from deferred concerns","Capture assumptions that materially influence the decision"],"constraints":["Incomplete requirements may limit certainty","Some dependencies may be outside the decision owner's control"],"alternatives":["Broad architecture-wide decision","Narrow component-level decision","Layered decision with explicit deferred decisions"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision-boundary analysis","requirement traceability","dependency analysis"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Broader scope can reduce later rework but increases ambiguity","Narrow scope improves clarity but may defer important dependencies"],"decision":"Use the smallest decision boundary that fully addresses the identified architectural problem, while recording explicitly deferred concerns.","rationale":"A bounded decision is easier to evaluate, implement, verify, and later revise.","consequences":["Unrelated architecture concerns require separate decisions","Dependencies must be linked explicitly","Decision ownership becomes clearer"],"implementation_implications":["Create explicit affected-component boundaries","Record deferred decisions as follow-up items","Trace implementation changes back to the decision"],"verification_method":"Review whether every stated requirement is within scope and whether material adjacent concerns are explicitly deferred or linked.","outcome_learning":"After implementation, compare observed effects with the original decision boundary and create a follow-up decision if the boundary proved incomplete.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B81-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Assumption management","concept":"Explicit decision assumptions","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should material assumptions be recorded separately from requirements?","answer":"Requirements describe conditions that the system must satisfy, while assumptions describe conditions believed to be true when the decision is made. Recording assumptions separately makes invalidated premises discoverable and prevents them from being mistaken for verified facts.","context":"Architecture decisions frequently depend on expected traffic, organizational ownership, platform capabilities, dependency behavior, or operational conditions that may later change.","objective":"Make decision dependencies auditable when underlying assumptions change.","requirements":["Identify assumptions that materially affect the decision","Distinguish assumptions from verified requirements","Define how assumption validity can be checked"],"constraints":["Some future conditions cannot be verified at decision time","Assumptions may depend on external systems or organizations"],"alternatives":["Embed assumptions inside rationale","Maintain a separate assumption section","Convert verified assumptions into explicit requirements"],"evidence":{"basis":["assumption tracking","decision review","requirements traceability"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Explicit tracking adds documentation effort","Untracked assumptions create hidden decision dependencies"],"decision":"Record material assumptions explicitly and identify observable evidence that could invalidate them.","rationale":"An architecture decision remains trustworthy only while its material premises remain applicable.","consequences":["Decision reviews can target changed assumptions","Invalid assumptions can trigger reassessment","Documentation becomes more auditable"],"implementation_implications":["Store assumptions as structured fields","Associate important assumptions with validation signals","Trigger review when critical assumptions change"],"verification_method":"Check that each material assumption has either supporting evidence or an identified validation mechanism.","outcome_learning":"Review which assumptions held, failed, or changed after implementation and use those findings to improve future decision framing.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B81-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B81-R01"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B81-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Decision ownership","concept":"Decision authority and accountability","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should ownership of an architecture decision be established?","answer":"Assign decision authority to the role or team accountable for the affected architectural boundary, while identifying contributors, implementers, and approvers separately when those responsibilities differ.","context":"Architecture decisions can fail when technical authorship is confused with authority, implementation ownership, or operational accountability.","objective":"Make decision authority and downstream responsibilities explicit.","requirements":["Identify decision authority","Identify implementation ownership","Identify operational accountability where relevant","Record required approval boundaries"],"constraints":["Authority may span multiple teams","High-impact changes may require governance approval"],"alternatives":["Single technical owner","Consensus-only decision","Explicit authority with consulted and accountable parties"],"evidence":{"basis":["responsibility-boundary analysis","governance requirements","system ownership mapping"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Centralized authority can improve consistency but create bottlenecks","Distributed authority improves local speed but requires stronger boundary contracts"],"decision":"Separate decision authority from consultation, implementation, and operational accountability rather than treating them as one role.","rationale":"Different responsibilities require different forms of accountability and prevent ambiguity during implementation and review.","consequences":["Responsibility becomes traceable","Disagreements can be escalated through defined authority","Ownership changes must update the decision record"],"implementation_implications":["Store owner and accountable roles explicitly","Require governance approval where policy demands it","Update ownership metadata during organizational changes"],"verification_method":"Verify that the record identifies who can decide, who implements, and who is accountable for resulting operation.","outcome_learning":"Measure whether ownership ambiguity caused delays or defects and adjust responsibility boundaries accordingly.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B81-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Evidence quality","concept":"Evidence strength in architecture decisions","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should evidence strength influence confidence in an architecture decision?","answer":"Separate directly measured evidence, authoritative requirements, validated prototypes, established engineering principles, assumptions, and speculation. Confidence should reflect the strength and relevance of available evidence rather than the certainty of the language used in the decision.","context":"Architecture choices are sometimes justified with opinions or unverified assumptions presented as facts.","objective":"Prevent unsupported certainty from entering decision records.","requirements":["Classify evidence sources","Distinguish measured evidence from inference","Record important evidence gaps","Tie confidence to evidence quality"],"constraints":["Prototype results may not represent production conditions","External evidence may differ from the target workload"],"alternatives":["Narrative-only rationale","Evidence-classified rationale","Quantitative decision matrix where measurements exist"],"evidence":{"basis":["evidence classification","prototype validation","requirement traceability","measurement quality"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Detailed evidence classification increases record effort","Weak evidence can still support provisional decisions when uncertainty is explicit"],"decision":"Classify material evidence by strength and context, and lower confidence when important claims remain unverified.","rationale":"Decision quality depends not only on reasoning but also on the reliability and applicability of its premises.","consequences":["Uncertainty becomes visible","Future validation can target evidence gaps","Overconfident decisions become easier to identify"],"implementation_implications":["Store evidence type and verification state","Attach measurements where available","Avoid converting assumptions into facts"],"verification_method":"Audit each material rationale claim for evidence type, applicability, and verification status.","outcome_learning":"Compare predicted decision effects with measured results and update confidence patterns for future decisions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B81-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B81-R02"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B81-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Decision reversibility","concept":"Reversible versus irreversible architecture decisions","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should reversibility affect architecture decision strategy?","answer":"Decisions that are cheap to reverse can generally tolerate more experimentation, while decisions with high migration cost or irreversible external consequences require stronger evidence, explicit safeguards, and broader review before commitment.","context":"Architecture choices differ substantially in the cost and risk of changing them later.","objective":"Match decision rigor to reversal cost and consequence severity.","requirements":["Estimate reversal cost","Identify irreversible consequences","Define safeguards for high-cost decisions","Increase evidence requirements when reversal is difficult"],"constraints":["Reversal cost can change as system adoption grows","Some consequences cannot be fully reversed"],"alternatives":["Immediate commitment","Prototype before commitment","Phased commitment with reversible boundaries"],"evidence":{"basis":["migration-cost analysis","dependency analysis","blast-radius assessment"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Early experimentation can delay commitment","Premature commitment can create expensive migration paths"],"decision":"Use phased or reversible approaches where practical, and require stronger evidence for decisions that are costly or unsafe to reverse.","rationale":"Decision rigor should reflect the cost and consequence of being wrong.","consequences":["Low-cost decisions can move faster","High-cost decisions receive deeper validation","Architecture may temporarily contain transitional designs"],"implementation_implications":["Identify reversible boundaries","Prefer replaceable interfaces around uncertain choices","Document migration paths for high-cost commitments"],"verification_method":"Review whether decision rigor and validation effort are proportional to reversal cost and impact.","outcome_learning":"Measure actual reversal cost when decisions change and use the result to improve future architecture planning.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B81-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B81-R04"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}

{"record_id":"D54-B82-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Option evaluation","concept":"Comparable alternative definition","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should architecture alternatives be defined so that their comparison is meaningful?","answer":"Alternatives should represent materially different ways of satisfying the same decision objective. Each alternative should be evaluated against the same requirements, constraints, operational assumptions, and decision criteria.","context":"Comparisons become unreliable when one option is evaluated at production detail while another is described only conceptually.","objective":"Create comparable architecture alternatives.","requirements":["Use a common decision objective","Apply consistent evaluation criteria","Capture important dependencies and constraints","Avoid straw-man alternatives"],"constraints":["Some options may require different maturity levels","Evidence may be uneven across alternatives"],"alternatives":["Single preferred option","Common-criteria alternative analysis","Weighted decision matrix"],"evidence":{"basis":["comparative architecture analysis","requirement mapping","constraint analysis"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Equal criteria improve comparability but may hide option-specific risks","Weighted criteria make priorities explicit but introduce judgment"],"decision":"Define alternatives against a common decision objective and common mandatory criteria before applying context-specific tradeoffs.","rationale":"A decision cannot reliably compare options that are framed using different standards.","consequences":["Alternative analysis becomes auditable","Missing evidence becomes visible","Context-specific tradeoffs remain explicit"],"implementation_implications":["Create an alternative record for each materially distinct option","Map requirements and constraints to each option","Record unavailable evidence rather than inventing it"],"verification_method":"Check that each alternative addresses the same objective and has been evaluated against the same mandatory criteria.","outcome_learning":"Compare predicted and observed tradeoffs for alternatives selected in practice and improve future evaluation criteria.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B82-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Trade-off analysis","concept":"Decision criteria weighting","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When should decision criteria be weighted rather than treated as equally important?","answer":"Criteria should be weighted when requirements establish materially different priorities and a simple pass-or-fail comparison cannot distinguish acceptable alternatives. Mandatory constraints should remain gates rather than being compensated by favorable scores elsewhere.","context":"Architecture choices often involve competing performance, reliability, cost, security, and maintainability objectives.","objective":"Represent genuine priority differences without allowing critical constraints to be traded away.","requirements":["Identify mandatory constraints","Identify preference criteria","Document relative importance where necessary","Keep incompatible criteria explicit"],"constraints":["Weights can encode subjective judgment","Quantification may create false precision"],"alternatives":["Equal criteria","Weighted criteria","Hard constraints plus qualitative tradeoffs"],"evidence":{"basis":["requirements priority analysis","risk classification","stakeholder constraints"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Weighting clarifies priorities but can obscure uncertainty","Qualitative analysis preserves nuance but is harder to automate"],"decision":"Use hard gates for non-negotiable requirements and weighted or qualitative comparison only for criteria that are genuinely tradeable.","rationale":"Critical constraints should not become acceptable merely because another characteristic scores favorably.","consequences":["Decision criteria become more explicit","Critical requirements retain authority","Preference conflicts become visible"],"implementation_implications":["Represent constraints separately from preferences","Document weighting rationale","Avoid unsupported numeric precision"],"verification_method":"Verify that no alternative violates a mandatory requirement while compensating through unrelated advantages.","outcome_learning":"Compare the practical importance of criteria after implementation and revise future weighting assumptions accordingly.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B82-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B82-R01"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B82-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Uncertainty","concept":"Decision-making under incomplete evidence","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should an architecture decision handle important uncertainty that cannot be resolved immediately?","answer":"Identify the uncertainty, estimate its potential consequence, determine whether it can be reduced through experimentation, and choose a reversible or safeguarded approach when unresolved uncertainty materially affects the decision.","context":"Architecture decisions frequently precede complete workload data, dependency validation, or production evidence.","objective":"Prevent uncertainty from being hidden inside apparently definitive decisions.","requirements":["Identify material unknowns","Assess potential impact","Define evidence-gathering actions where feasible","Use safeguards when uncertainty remains"],"constraints":["Experiments may require time or representative environments","Some uncertainty is irreducible before deployment"],"alternatives":["Delay decision","Proceed with explicit uncertainty","Prototype or pilot before commitment"],"evidence":{"basis":["uncertainty analysis","risk assessment","prototype planning"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Waiting increases information but may delay delivery","Proceeding enables learning but exposes the system to uncertainty"],"decision":"Proceed conditionally when delay is costly, provided uncertainty, safeguards, and validation triggers are explicit.","rationale":"Uncertainty should be managed as a first-class decision variable rather than disguised as confidence.","consequences":["Decision records remain honest about unknowns","Validation work becomes targeted","Reassessment triggers become actionable"],"implementation_implications":["Track unresolved assumptions","Add feature flags, pilots, or isolation where appropriate","Define measurable decision review triggers"],"verification_method":"Confirm that each material uncertainty has either a mitigation, validation plan, or explicit acceptance rationale.","outcome_learning":"Record which uncertainties were resolved, remained irrelevant, or caused unexpected consequences.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B82-R01","D54-B81-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B82-R01"},{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B81-R05"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}
{"record_id":"D54-B82-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Prototype evidence","concept":"Architecture spikes and representative validation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When is a prototype useful as evidence for an architecture decision?","answer":"A prototype is useful when an unresolved architectural question can materially affect the decision and cannot be answered reliably through existing evidence. The prototype should test the specific uncertainty under representative constraints rather than merely demonstrate that a technology can run.","context":"Technology demonstrations can be misleading when they omit workload, failure, security, integration, or operational constraints.","objective":"Use prototypes to reduce decision-relevant uncertainty.","requirements":["State the question being tested","Define representative conditions","Define measurable acceptance criteria","Record prototype limitations"],"constraints":["Prototype environments may differ from production","Short experiments may not reveal long-term operational behavior"],"alternatives":["No prototype","Minimal technology demonstration","Targeted representative architecture spike"],"evidence":{"basis":["experimental design","risk-focused prototyping","acceptance criteria"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Representative prototypes require more effort","Weak prototypes can create false confidence"],"decision":"Use targeted prototypes only for decision-critical uncertainties and record their applicability limits.","rationale":"The value of a prototype comes from reducing a specific uncertainty, not from demonstrating technology novelty.","consequences":["Evidence becomes more decision-focused","Prototype results require interpretation","Unresolved limitations remain visible"],"implementation_implications":["Define test workloads and failure cases","Capture prototype configuration","Separate observed results from conclusions"],"verification_method":"Verify that prototype observations directly address the uncertainty and that limitations are recorded.","outcome_learning":"Compare prototype predictions with production behavior and update the evidence model when they diverge.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B82-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B82-R03"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}
{"record_id":"D54-B82-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Decision records","concept":"Decision rationale versus implementation detail","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What implementation detail belongs in an architecture decision record?","answer":"Include implementation details when they constrain the architectural choice, establish a required boundary, or materially affect verification and consequences. Avoid embedding incidental implementation details that can change without changing the architectural decision.","context":"Decision records can become obsolete when low-level implementation details are unnecessarily treated as architectural commitments.","objective":"Preserve durable architectural intent while minimizing accidental coupling to implementation details.","requirements":["Capture architecture-relevant implementation constraints","Separate tactical details where possible","Maintain traceability to implementation artifacts"],"constraints":["Some implementation choices become architectural when they affect guarantees or boundaries","The boundary between architecture and implementation is context-dependent"],"alternatives":["Include all implementation details","Exclude implementation detail entirely","Include only decision-relevant implementation constraints"],"evidence":{"basis":["architecture boundary analysis","implementation dependency analysis","change-impact analysis"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["More detail improves traceability but increases maintenance cost","Less detail improves durability but can weaken implementation guidance"],"decision":"Record implementation detail when changing it would alter the architectural decision, guarantees, or verification obligations.","rationale":"Architecture records should preserve decisions rather than accidental coding choices.","consequences":["ADRs remain more durable","Implementation teams retain appropriate freedom","Decision-impact analysis becomes clearer"],"implementation_implications":["Link detailed implementation artifacts externally","Record architecture-relevant constraints directly","Update the ADR when implementation changes alter the decision"],"verification_method":"Review whether each implementation detail would materially change the decision if removed or changed.","outcome_learning":"Track which documented details remained architecturally relevant and remove recurring incidental detail from future records.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B82-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B82-R04"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}

{"record_id":"D54-B83-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Consequences","concept":"Positive and negative consequences","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"Why should architecture decisions explicitly record both beneficial and adverse consequences?","answer":"Recording both prevents the rationale from becoming advocacy for the selected option. Consequences should cover operational, technical, security, performance, maintainability, cost, and organizational effects that are materially relevant to the decision.","context":"Decision records often explain why an option was selected without documenting what the organization must accept as a result.","objective":"Make accepted tradeoffs explicit and reviewable.","requirements":["Record material benefits","Record material costs and risks","Distinguish immediate from long-term consequences","Identify consequences requiring mitigation"],"constraints":["Some long-term effects remain uncertain","Not every consequence is measurable at decision time"],"alternatives":["Benefits-only rationale","Risk-only analysis","Balanced consequence analysis"],"evidence":{"basis":["tradeoff analysis","risk analysis","lifecycle impact assessment"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["More complete analysis increases documentation effort","Incomplete consequence analysis creates hidden costs"],"decision":"Record materially relevant positive and negative consequences and identify which negative consequences are accepted, mitigated, or deferred.","rationale":"A decision is meaningful only when its tradeoffs are explicit.","consequences":["Stakeholders can review accepted costs","Mitigation responsibilities become visible","Later outcome analysis has a baseline"],"implementation_implications":["Create consequence categories","Associate mitigations with responsible owners","Track consequences during operation"],"verification_method":"Check whether material consequences are represented across relevant technical and operational dimensions.","outcome_learning":"Compare observed consequences against predicted consequences and record systematic prediction errors.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B83-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Risk consequences","concept":"Decision consequence severity","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should consequence severity influence architecture decision review?","answer":"Consequences with high security, safety, availability, financial, privacy, or irreversible impact should receive stronger evidence, review, safeguards, and verification than low-impact consequences.","context":"Not all architecture decisions have the same failure cost or blast radius.","objective":"Scale decision governance to consequence severity.","requirements":["Classify material consequence severity","Identify high-impact failure modes","Define appropriate review and verification","Document acceptance authority"],"constraints":["Severity classification can be context-specific","Impact estimates may contain uncertainty"],"alternatives":["Uniform review process","Risk-tiered review","Fully quantitative risk scoring"],"evidence":{"basis":["impact analysis","failure-mode analysis","governance requirements"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Risk-tiering reduces unnecessary governance overhead","Overly coarse tiers may underrepresent unusual consequences"],"decision":"Use consequence severity to determine the depth of review and validation required, while preserving explicit qualitative reasoning where numeric scoring is unreliable.","rationale":"Decision governance should reflect potential harm rather than treating all architectural changes identically.","consequences":["High-impact decisions receive stronger controls","Low-impact decisions can move with less overhead","Review requirements become explainable"],"implementation_implications":["Define decision review tiers","Map sensitive architectural changes to approval requirements","Record verification obligations for high-impact choices"],"verification_method":"Check that review depth and approval requirements correspond to documented consequence severity.","outcome_learning":"Compare actual impact of changed architecture with predicted severity and recalibrate classification guidance.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B83-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B83-R01"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B83-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Operational consequences","concept":"Operational readiness as a decision consequence","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should operational burden be represented in architecture decisions?","answer":"Architecture choices should account for the monitoring, deployment, backup, recovery, incident response, capacity management, staffing, and maintenance responsibilities they introduce.","context":"A technically functional architecture can become difficult to operate if its operational requirements are omitted from the decision.","objective":"Include operational consequences in architecture selection.","requirements":["Identify new operational responsibilities","Identify required observability","Identify recovery and maintenance obligations","Consider staffing and operational maturity"],"constraints":["Operational capability may vary by organization","Some burden depends on future scale"],"alternatives":["Ignore operational impact","Evaluate technical and operational consequences together","Require operational readiness review"],"evidence":{"basis":["operability analysis","supportability assessment","lifecycle ownership analysis"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Operationally simpler architectures may constrain technical flexibility","Operational complexity may be justified by required capabilities"],"decision":"Treat material operational burden as a first-class architecture consequence rather than a post-deployment concern.","rationale":"An architecture must be supportable within the capabilities and responsibilities of its operating environment.","consequences":["Operational teams receive earlier visibility","Hidden support costs become decision inputs","Deployment readiness can be assessed earlier"],"implementation_implications":["Define monitoring and recovery requirements","Document ownership of operational tasks","Include support procedures in readiness verification"],"verification_method":"Review whether the selected architecture has identified owners and mechanisms for material operational responsibilities.","outcome_learning":"Measure actual support effort, incidents, recovery work, and maintenance burden after adoption.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B83-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"expands","record_id":"D54-B83-R01"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}
{"record_id":"D54-B83-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Migration consequences","concept":"Migration cost as architectural consequence","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When should migration cost influence the architecture decision itself?","answer":"Migration cost should influence a decision when the selected architecture affects future portability, replacement, schema evolution, dependency exit, data movement, or organizational transition in a materially significant way.","context":"A choice that appears inexpensive initially can create substantial future migration obligations.","objective":"Expose lifecycle lock-in and transition costs before commitment.","requirements":["Identify likely migration paths","Estimate major transition dependencies","Identify lock-in factors","Distinguish known from uncertain migration costs"],"constraints":["Future migration scope is often uncertain","Some lock-in is intentional and acceptable"],"alternatives":["Optimize only for current state","Explicitly include lifecycle migration cost","Prefer reversible boundaries"],"evidence":{"basis":["migration analysis","dependency mapping","lifecycle cost assessment"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Reducing lock-in can increase present complexity","Accepting lock-in can simplify current delivery while increasing future transition cost"],"decision":"Include material migration and lock-in consequences when they can affect long-term architectural flexibility or business continuity.","rationale":"Architecture decisions create future transition constraints even when migration is not immediately planned.","consequences":["Portability considerations become explicit","Intentional lock-in can be accepted consciously","Migration planning can begin earlier"],"implementation_implications":["Avoid unnecessary proprietary coupling when portability matters","Document migration-sensitive interfaces and data formats","Preserve export and recovery capabilities where required"],"verification_method":"Check whether major dependencies and future migration constraints are documented and consciously accepted or mitigated.","outcome_learning":"Compare actual migration effort with the decision's predicted transition cost when changes occur.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B83-R01","D54-B81-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B83-R01"},{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B81-R05"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}
{"record_id":"D54-B83-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Decision communication","concept":"Decision rationale accessibility","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What makes an architecture decision understandable to engineers who did not participate in the original decision?","answer":"The record should state the problem, requirements, constraints, considered alternatives, evidence, selected decision, rationale, and consequences without relying on undocumented meeting context.","context":"Architecture decisions lose value when their reasoning exists only in conversations or in the memory of original participants.","objective":"Make decision knowledge independently understandable and reusable.","requirements":["Record decision context","Record alternatives and rejected reasoning","Record evidence and uncertainty","Record implementation implications"],"constraints":["Historical context may be incomplete","Some sensitive information may require restricted access"],"alternatives":["Meeting notes","Concise ADR","Detailed decision record with linked evidence"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision traceability","knowledge preservation","architecture review"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["More complete records require maintenance","Overly verbose records can obscure the actual decision"],"decision":"Capture enough structured context to reconstruct why the decision was made without requiring access to undocumented conversations.","rationale":"Architecture knowledge should survive personnel and organizational changes.","consequences":["Onboarding becomes easier","Future reviews can reconstruct historical reasoning","Decision reuse becomes possible"],"implementation_implications":["Use structured decision fields","Link supporting evidence","Separate sensitive evidence from general rationale where necessary"],"verification_method":"Give the record to a reviewer without meeting context and check whether the decision and major tradeoffs remain understandable.","outcome_learning":"Track recurring questions from later reviewers and improve decision-record completeness.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B83-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B83-R01"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}

{"record_id":"D54-B84-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Implementation verification","concept":"Decision-to-implementation traceability","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an architecture decision be connected to its implementation?","answer":"Material decision requirements and constraints should map to implementation artifacts, configuration, interfaces, infrastructure, tests, or operational controls that realize them. The mapping should distinguish intended implementation from verified implementation.","context":"A documented decision does not prove that the implemented system follows the decision.","objective":"Create traceability between architecture intent and implementation evidence.","requirements":["Identify implementation artifacts affected by the decision","Map material constraints to implementation controls","Track verification independently from documentation"],"constraints":["Implementation may evolve after the original decision","Some architecture properties are distributed across multiple components"],"alternatives":["Documentation-only traceability","Artifact-linked traceability","Automated architecture conformance checks where feasible"],"evidence":{"basis":["requirements traceability","architecture conformance analysis","implementation verification"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Traceability improves verification but adds maintenance effort","Automation can reduce effort but may not capture semantic architecture properties"],"decision":"Link material architecture decisions to implementation artifacts and verification evidence without treating the link itself as proof of conformance.","rationale":"Architecture intent and implementation state are separate facts that require explicit verification.","consequences":["Implementation drift becomes more detectable","Verification can target decision-specific guarantees","Records remain useful after code changes"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain links to code/configuration/infrastructure","Define architecture-specific tests where practical","Track implementation and verification status separately"],"verification_method":"Verify each material decision requirement against actual implementation evidence and applicable tests.","outcome_learning":"Record implementation mismatches and determine whether they arose from drift, ambiguity, or an outdated decision.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}
{"record_id":"D54-B84-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Architecture conformance","concept":"Decision conformance verification","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture conformance be verified without assuming that documentation proves implementation?","answer":"Define observable properties that represent the decision and verify them against implementation, configuration, deployment topology, interfaces, tests, or runtime evidence as appropriate.","context":"Architecture conformance can fail silently when systems evolve independently of decision records.","objective":"Turn important architectural decisions into verifiable properties.","requirements":["Identify decision-derived properties","Identify suitable verification evidence","Separate static and runtime checks where relevant","Record unresolved conformance gaps"],"constraints":["Some architectural properties are difficult to verify automatically","Runtime behavior can depend on deployment context"],"alternatives":["Manual review","Static architecture checks","Runtime verification","Combined verification"],"evidence":{"basis":["architecture conformance verification","static analysis","runtime validation"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Automated checks improve repeatability but require maintenance","Manual review captures semantics but is less scalable"],"decision":"Use the least expensive verification method that can reliably establish each material architectural property, combining methods when necessary.","rationale":"Different architecture guarantees require different forms of evidence.","consequences":["Conformance becomes testable","Architecture drift can be detected","Verification scope becomes explicit"],"implementation_implications":["Define architecture invariants","Create targeted conformance tests or checks","Record runtime verification where static evidence is insufficient"],"verification_method":"Execute the defined conformance checks where execution is available; otherwise mark them as not executed rather than claiming verification.","outcome_learning":"Track recurring conformance failures and strengthen architecture checks where repeated drift occurs.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B84-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B84-R01"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}
{"record_id":"D54-B84-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Drift detection","concept":"Architecture decision drift","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What constitutes architecture decision drift?","answer":"Decision drift occurs when implementation, operational conditions, requirements, dependencies, or constraints materially diverge from the premises or consequences captured by the decision without an explicit reassessment or replacement decision.","context":"Systems evolve continuously while historical architecture records may remain unchanged.","objective":"Detect when a previously valid decision may no longer represent the current system.","requirements":["Identify decision premises","Monitor relevant implementation and environmental changes","Define reassessment triggers","Record superseding decisions"],"constraints":["Not every implementation change invalidates an architecture decision","Some changes are temporary or operationally isolated"],"alternatives":["Periodic manual review","Change-triggered review","Continuous architecture conformance monitoring"],"evidence":{"basis":["change-impact analysis","architecture conformance","decision lifecycle management"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Continuous detection can increase review volume","Periodic review may miss fast-changing risks"],"decision":"Treat material changes to decision premises or guarantees as reassessment triggers rather than assuming historical validity persists indefinitely.","rationale":"Architecture validity is conditional on the context and guarantees under which the decision was made.","consequences":["Outdated decisions become discoverable","Supersession becomes explicit","Architecture governance becomes evidence-driven"],"implementation_implications":["Define decision triggers","Link architecture changes to affected decisions","Maintain status such as active, superseded, or retired"],"verification_method":"Review whether material architecture or requirement changes have corresponding decision-impact assessments.","outcome_learning":"Analyze drift events to identify which triggers could have detected them earlier.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B84-R01","D54-B84-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B84-R01"},{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B84-R02"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}
{"record_id":"D54-B84-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Decision status","concept":"Active, superseded, and retired decisions","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should architecture decisions have explicit lifecycle status?","answer":"Lifecycle status prevents historical decisions from being interpreted as current guidance after they have been replaced, invalidated, or intentionally retired.","context":"Architecture repositories accumulate decisions over time and may contain contradictory historical choices.","objective":"Preserve historical knowledge without confusing it with current architecture direction.","requirements":["Define decision lifecycle states","Record superseding relationships","Preserve historical decisions","Identify current applicable decisions"],"constraints":["Multiple decisions may coexist for different system boundaries","A decision can remain historically relevant after being superseded"],"alternatives":["Delete obsolete decisions","Keep all decisions without status","Keep immutable history with explicit lifecycle status"],"evidence":{"basis":["architecture history management","decision supersession","knowledge traceability"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Preserving history increases repository size","Deleting history loses rationale for past architecture"],"decision":"Preserve decision history while explicitly marking active, superseded, retired, or otherwise applicable lifecycle states.","rationale":"Historical decisions provide context but must not silently override current architecture guidance.","consequences":["Historical reasoning remains available","Current guidance is easier to identify","Contradictory records can be interpreted temporally"],"implementation_implications":["Store lifecycle status","Link superseding decisions","Filter retrieval by applicability and time"],"verification_method":"Check that retrieval or review processes distinguish current decisions from historical records.","outcome_learning":"Measure whether stale decisions caused implementation confusion and improve lifecycle metadata or retrieval filtering.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B84-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B84-R03"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B84-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Contradiction handling","concept":"Conflicting architecture decisions","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should conflicting architecture decisions be handled?","answer":"Identify the scope, time, assumptions, and authority of each decision before declaring a contradiction. Decisions may legitimately differ when they apply to different boundaries or contexts; genuine conflicts should be resolved by an explicit superseding or reconciling decision.","context":"Architecture repositories may contain decisions that appear contradictory but were made for different contexts or system states.","objective":"Distinguish contextual variation from unresolved architectural contradiction.","requirements":["Compare scope","Compare applicability period","Compare assumptions and constraints","Identify decision authority","Create explicit reconciliation when conflict is genuine"],"constraints":["Historical context may be incomplete","Multiple valid architectures may coexist"],"alternatives":["Choose the newest record automatically","Delete one conflicting record","Analyze scope and create explicit reconciliation"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision-scope analysis","temporal applicability","architecture governance"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Detailed conflict analysis takes effort","Automatic latest-wins rules can hide legitimate contextual differences"],"decision":"Resolve apparent conflicts by comparing applicability and assumptions; create an explicit superseding or reconciling decision when both cannot remain valid.","rationale":"Contradiction detection requires context rather than simple textual comparison.","consequences":["False contradictions are reduced","Real conflicts become actionable","Historical reasoning remains preserved"],"implementation_implications":["Store scope and applicability metadata","Represent supersession relationships","Prevent ambiguous retrieval of mutually exclusive active decisions"],"verification_method":"Review conflicting records for scope, applicability, assumptions, and explicit resolution status.","outcome_learning":"Track recurring contradiction patterns to improve decision metadata and retrieval logic.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B84-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B84-R04"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}

{"record_id":"D54-B85-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Review triggers","concept":"Event-driven architecture decision review","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Which changes should trigger an architecture decision review?","answer":"Review should be triggered when material requirements, workload characteristics, security constraints, dependencies, failure modes, ownership, compliance obligations, or implementation guarantees change beyond the assumptions of the original decision.","context":"Periodic review alone may be too slow for architectures whose decision premises change rapidly.","objective":"Define evidence-based reassessment triggers.","requirements":["Identify decision-critical assumptions","Identify trigger categories","Define impact thresholds where practical","Record review outcomes"],"constraints":["Excessive triggers create review noise","Some changes have uncertain architectural impact"],"alternatives":["Fixed periodic review","Manual review only","Event-driven review with periodic backstop"],"evidence":{"basis":["change-impact analysis","risk triggers","architecture drift detection"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Event-driven review improves responsiveness but requires change detection","Periodic review is simpler but can delay reassessment"],"decision":"Use material change triggers for timely reassessment and retain periodic review as a backstop for missed changes.","rationale":"Architecture decisions are conditional and should be revisited when their governing conditions change.","consequences":["Important changes trigger earlier analysis","Review workload can be focused","Stale decisions become easier to detect"],"implementation_implications":["Define decision trigger metadata","Connect relevant change events to review workflows","Record trigger source and review disposition"],"verification_method":"Test whether known material changes generate the expected decision-review workflow.","outcome_learning":"Analyze false-positive and missed triggers to improve review criteria.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B84-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B84-R03"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}
{"record_id":"D54-B85-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Review cadence","concept":"Periodic architecture decision review","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When is periodic review useful even when no explicit architecture change has occurred?","answer":"Periodic review is useful when external conditions, dependencies, risks, organizational ownership, or system scale can change without producing a reliable architecture-change event.","context":"Some decision premises change gradually or outside the system's normal change-management process.","objective":"Detect stale decisions that event-driven review may miss.","requirements":["Define review cadence appropriate to decision criticality","Review assumptions and evidence","Check current applicability","Record review outcome"],"constraints":["Review effort can become administrative overhead","Stable low-impact decisions may require little review"],"alternatives":["No periodic review","Uniform periodic review","Risk-adjusted periodic review"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision aging analysis","risk-based governance","assumption review"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Frequent review improves freshness but increases cost","Infrequent review reduces overhead but increases staleness risk"],"decision":"Use risk-adjusted periodic review where decision premises can change without reliable event triggers.","rationale":"A periodic backstop protects against silent environmental drift.","consequences":["Old decisions receive explicit applicability checks","Review effort can be concentrated on material decisions"],"implementation_implications":["Assign review intervals based on decision impact and volatility","Record last review and next review state","Avoid treating review completion as proof that the architecture remains correct"],"verification_method":"Check that high-impact and high-volatility decisions receive review according to defined policy.","outcome_learning":"Adjust review cadence based on detected drift, missed changes, and unnecessary reviews.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B85-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B85-R01"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B85-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Decision aging","concept":"Aging architecture evidence","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"Why can the age of evidence matter even when an architecture decision has not changed?","answer":"Evidence may become less representative as workloads, dependencies, technology behavior, organizational conditions, or threat environments evolve. Evidence age should therefore be considered when it materially affects decision confidence.","context":"A decision can remain documented and internally consistent while the evidence supporting it becomes stale.","objective":"Detect decisions whose evidence no longer represents current conditions.","requirements":["Track evidence date or version where relevant","Identify volatile evidence","Define revalidation conditions","Avoid treating historical measurements as current facts"],"constraints":["Evidence aging differs by domain","Some stable principles remain useful for long periods"],"alternatives":["Ignore evidence age","Apply uniform expiry","Use context-dependent evidence freshness"],"evidence":{"basis":["evidence freshness","environmental change analysis","decision confidence management"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Revalidation costs resources","Ignoring stale evidence can create false confidence"],"decision":"Track evidence freshness when changing conditions can materially invalidate its applicability.","rationale":"Evidence is contextual and may lose relevance independently of the decision record.","consequences":["Stale evidence becomes visible","Revalidation can be targeted","Confidence can be adjusted without immediately replacing the decision"],"implementation_implications":["Store evidence timestamps or versions","Associate evidence with workload/environment context","Trigger revalidation when relevant conditions change"],"verification_method":"Review whether material evidence remains representative of current architecture conditions.","outcome_learning":"Measure which evidence categories become stale most frequently and adjust revalidation policies.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B85-R02","D54-B81-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B85-R02"},{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B81-R04"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B85-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Decision review outcome","concept":"Review without unnecessary redesign","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should happen when a decision review finds that the architecture remains valid?","answer":"The review should record the evidence supporting continued applicability rather than creating a new decision merely because a review occurred.","context":"Governance processes can create duplicate records when unchanged decisions are repeatedly re-documented.","objective":"Preserve decision stability while maintaining evidence of review.","requirements":["Record review date","Record evidence considered","Confirm applicability","Avoid duplicate decisions without material change"],"constraints":["Review may identify minor implementation changes without architectural impact"],"alternatives":["Create new ADR every review","Update review metadata on existing decision","Create new decision only when architecture materially changes"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision review","change-impact analysis","deduplication"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Updating existing metadata reduces duplication","New records can provide clearer history when the decision materially changes"],"decision":"Keep the existing decision active when review confirms applicability, recording the review evidence separately from a new architectural decision.","rationale":"Review is not itself an architectural change.","consequences":["Decision history remains clean","Duplicate records are reduced","Continued validity has an explicit evidence trail"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain review metadata","Link review evidence","Create a new decision only for material architectural change"],"verification_method":"Check that unchanged decisions are not duplicated solely because a review occurred.","outcome_learning":"Analyze review outcomes to identify decisions that require more frequent or less frequent reassessment.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B85-R01","D54-B84-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B85-R01"},{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B84-R04"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B85-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Decision change","concept":"Material versus non-material architecture changes","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should teams distinguish an implementation change from a change that requires a new architecture decision?","answer":"A new decision is warranted when the change alters material requirements, architectural boundaries, guarantees, dependencies, tradeoffs, or consequences represented by the existing decision. Pure implementation changes that preserve those properties do not necessarily require a new decision.","context":"Teams need to avoid both excessive ADR creation and silent architectural change.","objective":"Define a practical threshold for creating or superseding decisions.","requirements":["Identify decision-derived invariants","Assess change impact","Create a new decision when material tradeoffs change","Preserve implementation freedom where architecture is unaffected"],"constraints":["Materiality is context-dependent","Small implementation changes can accumulate into architectural drift"],"alternatives":["New ADR for every change","No ADR after initial decision","Impact-based decision threshold"],"evidence":{"basis":["change-impact analysis","architecture invariants","decision traceability"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Strict documentation captures more history but creates noise","Loose thresholds reduce overhead but can hide architectural drift"],"decision":"Use change impact on architectural guarantees and tradeoffs as the threshold for creating or superseding a decision.","rationale":"The purpose of an architecture decision record is to capture meaningful architectural choices rather than every implementation modification.","consequences":["Documentation remains focused","Material changes receive explicit reasoning","Minor implementation evolution remains flexible"],"implementation_implications":["Define architecture invariants","Perform impact assessment during significant changes","Link material changes to superseding decisions"],"verification_method":"Review representative changes and determine whether they alter recorded architectural properties or only implementation details.","outcome_learning":"Use post-change reviews to calibrate the materiality threshold and reduce both documentation gaps and noise.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B84-R03","D54-B85-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B84-R03"},{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B85-R04"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}

{"record_id":"D54-B86-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Learning","concept":"Predicted versus observed architecture outcomes","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should architecture decision outcomes be compared with the predictions made at decision time?","answer":"Record the decision's expected effects and later compare them with observed evidence using the same relevant dimensions. Differences should be classified as prediction error, changed conditions, implementation deviation, measurement error, or previously unknown behavior where evidence supports the classification.","context":"Without outcome comparison, architecture decisions cannot reliably contribute to organizational engineering learning.","objective":"Turn architecture decisions into a feedback mechanism for future decisions.","requirements":["Record expected outcomes","Define observable measures where practical","Capture actual outcomes","Explain material deviations"],"constraints":["Some outcomes emerge only over long periods","Causal attribution can be difficult"],"alternatives":["No outcome review","Subjective retrospective","Structured predicted-versus-observed comparison"],"evidence":{"basis":["outcome measurement","decision learning","causal interpretation"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Structured measurement requires planning","Unmeasured outcomes limit learning quality"],"decision":"Capture expected outcomes at decision time and compare them with later evidence without claiming causality beyond available evidence.","rationale":"Learning requires distinguishing what was expected from what actually occurred.","consequences":["Decision quality can be evaluated retrospectively","Prediction errors become reusable knowledge","Future architecture reasoning can improve"],"implementation_implications":["Define outcome metrics during decision creation","Store observed measurements separately","Classify deviations with evidence"],"verification_method":"Check that outcome assessments distinguish observed facts from interpretations and unsupported causal claims.","outcome_learning":"Use recurring prediction errors to improve alternative evaluation and evidence requirements.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}
{"record_id":"D54-B86-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Learning","concept":"Outcome attribution uncertainty","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"Why should architecture outcome learning distinguish correlation from causation?","answer":"Observed system changes can result from multiple simultaneous changes, workload shifts, operational practices, or external conditions. An architecture decision should not be credited or blamed for an outcome unless the available evidence supports the causal interpretation.","context":"Post-implementation measurements often occur after multiple independent changes.","objective":"Prevent false lessons from entering architecture knowledge.","requirements":["Record observed outcomes","Identify concurrent changes","State attribution confidence","Avoid unsupported causal claims"],"constraints":["Controlled experiments are uncommon in production architecture","Observational evidence can be confounded"],"alternatives":["Direct causal attribution","No outcome analysis","Evidence-qualified attribution"],"evidence":{"basis":["causal inference principles","change-impact analysis","observational evidence"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Cautious attribution reduces false conclusions","Strong causal proof may be expensive or unavailable"],"decision":"Use evidence-qualified causal language and explicitly record attribution uncertainty when multiple factors could explain an outcome.","rationale":"Architecture learning is only useful when conclusions do not exceed the evidence.","consequences":["Lessons become more trustworthy","Alternative explanations remain visible","Future validation can target uncertainty"],"implementation_implications":["Capture relevant concurrent changes","Record attribution confidence","Separate observation from causal interpretation"],"verification_method":"Audit outcome records for unsupported causal claims and missing alternative explanations.","outcome_learning":"Track which attribution assumptions were later confirmed or disproved.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B86-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B86-R01"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B86-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Learning","concept":"Reusable architecture decision patterns","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How can repeated architecture decisions become reusable knowledge without becoming rigid templates?","answer":"Extract recurring decision structures, evidence requirements, tradeoffs, and failure conditions while preserving context-specific applicability. Reusable knowledge should guide reasoning rather than dictate a universal answer.","context":"Organizations repeatedly make similar decisions across services, teams, or products.","objective":"Reuse decision knowledge without converting context-dependent choices into mandatory rules.","requirements":["Identify recurring decision dimensions","Preserve context conditions","Capture known failure modes","Separate reusable principles from specific decisions"],"constraints":["Similar decisions may have different constraints","Over-generalization can produce inappropriate architecture choices"],"alternatives":["Reuse complete prior decisions","Create generic decision guidance","Extract conditional reusable knowledge"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision history analysis","pattern extraction","context preservation"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Reuse reduces reasoning effort","Over-generalization increases decision risk"],"decision":"Extract conditional decision guidance from repeated decisions while retaining the original context and evidence boundaries.","rationale":"The useful part of historical architecture knowledge is often the reasoning structure rather than the selected technology.","consequences":["Future decisions can start from stronger evidence","Context remains necessary","Decision automation becomes safer"],"implementation_implications":["Link new decisions to prior relevant records","Store applicability conditions","Avoid copying decisions without re-evaluating requirements"],"verification_method":"Review reused guidance to ensure its original assumptions still apply to the new context.","outcome_learning":"Track where reused guidance required modification and use those cases to refine applicability conditions.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B86-R01","D54-B82-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B86-R01"},{"type":"builds_on","record_id":"D54-B82-R01"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B86-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Learning","concept":"Decision failure taxonomy","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should failed architecture decisions be classified for learning?","answer":"Classify failure according to the evidence-supported cause, such as incorrect requirements, invalid assumptions, inadequate evidence, poor alternative analysis, implementation deviation, operational mismatch, changed conditions, or unforeseen behavior.","context":"Calling a decision simply wrong does not reveal which part of the decision process failed.","objective":"Create actionable learning from unsuccessful architecture outcomes.","requirements":["Separate decision reasoning from implementation failure","Identify evidence-supported cause","Record uncertainty when cause is unresolved","Link corrective learning to future decisions"],"constraints":["Multiple causes may coexist","Post-hoc attribution may remain uncertain"],"alternatives":["Binary success/failure","Free-form retrospective","Structured failure taxonomy"],"evidence":{"basis":["root-cause analysis","decision outcome analysis","implementation traceability"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Structured taxonomy improves learning but requires discipline","Multiple-cause classification can be more complex"],"decision":"Use a structured, evidence-qualified failure taxonomy rather than treating every unsuccessful outcome as a failure of the architecture decision itself.","rationale":"Different failure causes require different corrective actions.","consequences":["Process weaknesses become distinguishable","Implementation problems are not misclassified as decision problems","Learning becomes reusable"],"implementation_implications":["Record decision, implementation, and environmental causes separately","Allow multiple supported cause categories","Store unresolved causality explicitly"],"verification_method":"Review failure records for evidence supporting each assigned cause and separation of decision from implementation factors.","outcome_learning":"Aggregate recurring failure causes to improve requirements, evidence gathering, review, and implementation verification.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B86-R01","D54-B86-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B86-R01"},{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B86-R02"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B86-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Learning","concept":"Successful outcome qualification","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should a positive architecture outcome still be qualified by evidence?","answer":"A favorable outcome does not prove that every part of the decision was correct or that the result will generalize. Success should be described using measured outcomes, scope, duration, assumptions, and relevant alternative explanations.","context":"Teams may turn successful architecture choices into universal recommendations without examining context.","objective":"Prevent success stories from becoming unsupported architectural rules.","requirements":["Record measurable outcomes","Preserve context","Identify limitations","Avoid universalizing context-specific success"],"constraints":["Success metrics may be incomplete","External factors may contribute to favorable results"],"alternatives":["Treat success as proof","Ignore outcome","Evidence-qualified success analysis"],"evidence":{"basis":["outcome measurement","contextual validity","causal uncertainty"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Qualification reduces overgeneralization","Detailed qualification requires additional analysis"],"decision":"Treat positive outcomes as context-specific evidence and preserve their assumptions and limitations.","rationale":"A successful result is evidence for a context, not automatic proof of universal superiority.","consequences":["Reusable knowledge becomes more precise","Future decisions can assess applicability","Overgeneralization risk is reduced"],"implementation_implications":["Store outcome context","Link measured results to decision assumptions","Require applicability checks before reuse"],"verification_method":"Check that successful outcomes include scope, evidence, and limitations before being reused as guidance.","outcome_learning":"Identify which successful decisions generalized and which depended strongly on their original context.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B86-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B86-R03"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}

{"record_id":"D54-B87-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Governance","concept":"Architecture decision review authority","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should governance review differ from technical architecture review?","answer":"Technical review evaluates architectural soundness and implementation implications, while governance review evaluates policy, risk, compliance, authority, and organizational constraints. They may involve the same people but represent different decision responsibilities.","context":"Architecture processes can conflate technical feasibility with organizational authorization.","objective":"Separate technical reasoning from governance approval.","requirements":["Identify technical review responsibilities","Identify governance approval responsibilities","Record required approvals","Preserve technical evidence separately from authorization"],"constraints":["Organizations may combine roles","High-impact decisions may require formal approval"],"alternatives":["Single review","Separate technical and governance review","Risk-tiered combined review"],"evidence":{"basis":["responsibility separation","governance analysis","risk management"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Separate reviews add coordination","Combined reviews can simplify low-risk decisions"],"decision":"Separate technical evaluation from governance authorization conceptually, while allowing organizational processes to combine them when responsibilities remain explicit.","rationale":"Technical validity does not automatically imply authorization to implement a change.","consequences":["Approval boundaries become clearer","Technical evidence is not confused with authorization","Auditability improves"],"implementation_implications":["Record reviewer and approver roles separately","Require policy-specific approvals where applicable","Preserve decision evidence independently of approval state"],"verification_method":"Check that technical conclusions and authorization status are represented as separate facts.","outcome_learning":"Review approval delays and escaped governance risks to improve the separation of responsibilities.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B87-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Governance","concept":"Decision evidence traceability","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What evidence should be retained to support later audit or reassessment of an architecture decision?","answer":"Retain the material requirements, constraints, evidence sources, assumptions, alternatives, decision rationale, approval state, implementation implications, and verification evidence necessary to reconstruct the decision without relying on undocumented memory.","context":"Architecture decisions may be reviewed long after their authors and original project context have changed.","objective":"Preserve enough provenance for trustworthy reassessment.","requirements":["Preserve material evidence references","Preserve decision version and status","Preserve verification state","Preserve supersession relationships"],"constraints":["Sensitive evidence may require controlled access","Not every intermediate discussion needs permanent retention"],"alternatives":["Store final decision only","Store all discussions","Store decision-critical evidence and references"],"evidence":{"basis":["provenance management","auditability","decision reconstruction"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Evidence retention improves auditability but creates storage and access-management requirements","Excessive retention can increase noise"],"decision":"Retain decision-critical provenance and evidence rather than every discussion artifact.","rationale":"Auditability requires reconstructable reasoning, not indiscriminate information retention.","consequences":["Later reviews become more reliable","Evidence access requirements become explicit","Historical reasoning survives personnel changes"],"implementation_implications":["Use immutable or versioned decision records where appropriate","Link evidence rather than duplicating large artifacts","Apply access controls to sensitive evidence"],"verification_method":"Attempt to reconstruct a past decision using only the retained record and linked evidence.","outcome_learning":"Identify missing provenance discovered during reviews and improve future evidence capture.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B86-R01","D54-B84-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B86-R01"},{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B84-R04"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B87-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Governance","concept":"Architecture decision immutability versus correction","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Should historical architecture decisions be edited after they are made?","answer":"Historical decision intent should generally remain reconstructable. Corrections to factual errors can be versioned or explicitly amended, while material changes in architecture should normally create a new or superseding decision rather than silently rewriting history.","context":"Silent edits can make historical reasoning impossible to reconstruct.","objective":"Preserve decision history while allowing factual correction.","requirements":["Preserve prior decision state","Record corrections explicitly","Create new decisions for material architectural changes","Maintain supersession links"],"constraints":["Some repositories permit controlled edits","Sensitive information may require redaction under policy"],"alternatives":["Mutable records","Immutable records only","Versioned records with explicit amendments"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision provenance","historical traceability","change management"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Immutable history improves traceability","Strict immutability can complicate factual corrections"],"decision":"Use versioned or append-oriented history that preserves prior states and distinguishes factual corrections from architectural changes.","rationale":"Historical reasoning is itself valuable architecture knowledge.","consequences":["Decision history remains auditable","Corrections become visible","Supersession is distinguishable from editing"],"implementation_implications":["Store record versions","Track amendment reason","Link new decisions to superseded decisions"],"verification_method":"Verify that a reviewer can reconstruct the decision state that existed at the time of implementation.","outcome_learning":"Review correction patterns to improve evidence quality and reduce recurring factual errors.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B87-R02","D54-B84-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B87-R02"},{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B84-R04"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B87-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Governance","concept":"Decision repository access and trust","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an architecture decision repository balance accessibility with information integrity?","answer":"Decision records should be discoverable by relevant engineering participants while changes, approvals, evidence references, and lifecycle transitions remain controlled and auditable according to organizational policy.","context":"Architecture knowledge loses value when inaccessible, but uncontrolled modification can undermine trust in historical decisions.","objective":"Balance knowledge access with record integrity.","requirements":["Define read access","Control modification authority","Track changes","Protect sensitive evidence"],"constraints":["Access requirements differ by organization","Some decisions contain confidential or security-sensitive information"],"alternatives":["Open repository","Restricted repository","Broad read access with controlled write access"],"evidence":{"basis":["knowledge accessibility","change control","information governance"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Broad access improves reuse","Restricted access can protect sensitive information","Controlled writes preserve integrity"],"decision":"Prefer broad appropriate read access with controlled, auditable modification and evidence access boundaries.","rationale":"Architecture knowledge must be usable without allowing untraceable changes to authoritative decisions.","consequences":["Reuse improves","Historical integrity is protected","Sensitive material can remain restricted"],"implementation_implications":["Implement role-based access where needed","Audit modifications","Separate decision visibility from sensitive evidence visibility"],"verification_method":"Verify that unauthorized modifications are prevented and authorized changes are traceable.","outcome_learning":"Review access incidents and knowledge-discovery gaps to refine repository governance.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B87-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B87-R02"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}
{"record_id":"D54-B87-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Governance","concept":"Decision policy versus decision content","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should architecture governance rules be separated from individual architecture decisions?","answer":"Governance defines how decisions are proposed, reviewed, approved, recorded, and retired, while individual decisions define specific architectural choices. Separating them allows governance policy to evolve without rewriting historical architecture reasoning.","context":"Embedding organizational process rules into every decision record creates duplication and makes historical interpretation difficult.","objective":"Separate reusable governance mechanics from context-specific architectural content.","requirements":["Define governance policy independently","Reference applicable policy from decisions","Preserve decision-specific reasoning","Track policy version where material"],"constraints":["Governance requirements can vary by organization or decision class","Historical decisions may have followed older policies"],"alternatives":["Embed governance rules in each decision","Central governance policy","Central policy with decision-level references"],"evidence":{"basis":["policy separation","decision lifecycle management","historical traceability"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Central policy reduces duplication","Decision-level context remains necessary for exceptions"],"decision":"Maintain reusable governance policy separately and record the applicable policy context within each decision.","rationale":"Process rules and architectural reasoning have different lifecycles and should not be conflated.","consequences":["Governance changes become easier to manage","Historical policy context remains traceable","Decision records remain focused"],"implementation_implications":["Version governance policies","Reference applicable policy from decisions","Record explicit exceptions where approved"],"verification_method":"Check that decisions can identify their governance context without duplicating the complete policy text.","outcome_learning":"Analyze governance exceptions and recurring process failures to improve future policy design.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B87-R01","D54-B87-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B87-R01"},{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B87-R02"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}

{"record_id":"D54-B88-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Automation","concept":"Automated architecture decision assistance","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What parts of architecture decision preparation can be safely automated?","answer":"Automation can assist with evidence retrieval, requirement extraction, alternative comparison, dependency discovery, contradiction detection, template validation, and traceability. The resulting recommendation should remain distinguishable from the authorized architectural decision.","context":"Knowledge systems can accelerate decision preparation but may lack complete context or authority.","objective":"Use automation to reduce decision preparation effort without confusing assistance with authorization.","requirements":["Preserve source provenance","Expose uncertainty","Separate generated analysis from approved decision","Allow human review where required"],"constraints":["Automated systems may miss organizational context","Retrieved evidence may be stale or contradictory"],"alternatives":["Manual decision preparation","Automated evidence assistance","Automated decision execution"],"evidence":{"basis":["knowledge retrieval","decision-support automation","human authorization boundaries"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Automation improves speed but can propagate retrieval errors","Manual review increases effort but can capture contextual constraints"],"decision":"Automate evidence and analysis support while keeping architectural authorization and final accountability explicit.","rationale":"Decision assistance and decision authority are distinct functions.","consequences":["Preparation can scale","Evidence gaps can be surfaced earlier","Human governance remains explicit"],"implementation_implications":["Store provenance for generated analysis","Expose conflicting evidence","Require approval for consequential decisions","Keep recommendation and decision states separate"],"verification_method":"Test whether automated outputs preserve provenance, uncertainty, and separation from authorized decisions.","outcome_learning":"Measure which automated recommendations required correction and use those cases to improve retrieval and reasoning.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B87-R01","D54-B87-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B87-R01"},{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B87-R02"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}
{"record_id":"D54-B88-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Automation","concept":"Automated alternative generation boundaries","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"What safeguards are required when software generates architecture alternatives?","answer":"Generated alternatives should be treated as candidate options until validated against requirements, constraints, evidence quality, security implications, operational feasibility, and implementation consequences.","context":"Automated reasoning can produce plausible alternatives that violate unstated constraints or depend on unsupported assumptions.","objective":"Prevent generated architecture alternatives from being mistaken for validated designs.","requirements":["Label generated alternatives","Check requirements and constraints","Record evidence provenance","Identify unsupported assumptions","Require appropriate review"],"constraints":["Generation systems may produce incomplete options","Novel alternatives may lack evidence"],"alternatives":["Manual alternatives only","Automated candidate generation","Automated selection without review"],"evidence":{"basis":["candidate-generation validation","constraint checking","provenance management"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Generation broadens exploration but increases validation workload","Manual exploration may miss less obvious alternatives"],"decision":"Use automated generation for candidate exploration, followed by explicit constraint, evidence, and consequence validation before adoption.","rationale":"Generation expands search space but does not establish architectural validity.","consequences":["Alternative discovery can improve","Validation workload becomes explicit","Unsupported proposals remain distinguishable"],"implementation_implications":["Attach generated provenance","Run requirement and constraint checks","Require human or policy-defined validation before decision adoption"],"verification_method":"Verify that generated candidates cannot enter the approved decision state without required validation evidence.","outcome_learning":"Track rejected generated alternatives and classify rejection causes to improve future candidate generation.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B88-R01","D54-B82-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B88-R01"},{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B82-R01"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}
{"record_id":"D54-B88-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Automation","concept":"Evidence provenance for decision-support systems","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why must automated architecture reasoning preserve evidence provenance?","answer":"Without provenance, reviewers cannot determine whether a recommendation came from an authoritative requirement, measured result, historical decision, inference, or unsupported generation. Provenance enables confidence assessment and correction when evidence changes.","context":"Automated decision-support systems can combine heterogeneous information sources with different reliability and applicability.","objective":"Make automated architecture reasoning auditable.","requirements":["Identify source of each material claim","Record evidence status","Distinguish fact from inference","Track evidence version where relevant"],"constraints":["Some sources may be inaccessible or confidential","Evidence can conflict"],"alternatives":["Unattributed synthesis","Source-linked synthesis","Full claim-level provenance"],"evidence":{"basis":["provenance tracking","evidence qualification","contradiction handling"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Detailed provenance increases metadata overhead","Weak provenance reduces auditability and trust"],"decision":"Preserve provenance for material claims and distinguish evidence, inference, and generated recommendations.","rationale":"Decision-support output is only as auditable as its evidence trail.","consequences":["Reviewers can challenge individual claims","Evidence updates can trigger reassessment","Unsupported assertions are easier to detect"],"implementation_implications":["Store source identifiers","Record evidence status and version","Link claims to decision alternatives"],"verification_method":"Audit generated recommendations and trace material claims back to their evidence sources.","outcome_learning":"Track provenance-related errors and improve source ranking and contradiction handling.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B88-R01","D54-B88-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B88-R01"},{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B88-R02"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}
{"record_id":"D54-B88-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Automation","concept":"Decision recommendation confidence","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should confidence be represented in automated architecture recommendations?","answer":"Confidence should reflect evidence quality, requirement coverage, alternative coverage, uncertainty, and contradiction state rather than merely the certainty of generated language.","context":"Automated systems can express recommendations confidently even when critical evidence is missing.","objective":"Prevent linguistic confidence from being mistaken for engineering certainty.","requirements":["Expose evidence quality","Expose unresolved uncertainty","Expose contradictions","Distinguish recommendation confidence from approval state"],"constraints":["Confidence is not always numerically measurable","High confidence in evidence does not guarantee the decision is correct"],"alternatives":["No confidence metadata","Single confidence score","Structured confidence factors"],"evidence":{"basis":["confidence calibration","evidence completeness","uncertainty analysis"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Structured factors are more informative but harder to summarize","Single scores are simple but can hide failure modes"],"decision":"Represent confidence using explicit supporting factors and keep it separate from authorization and verification state.","rationale":"Confidence is an evidence property, not a substitute for validation or approval.","consequences":["Weak evidence becomes visible","Reviewers can target uncertainty","Automated output becomes more auditable"],"implementation_implications":["Store evidence coverage and contradiction indicators","Separate confidence from validation status","Avoid treating generated confidence as proof"],"verification_method":"Check that confidence decreases or uncertainty is exposed when material evidence is missing or contradictory.","outcome_learning":"Calibrate confidence using later verified outcomes without claiming that confidence alone predicts correctness.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B88-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B88-R03"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}
{"record_id":"D54-B88-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Automation","concept":"Human review based on decision risk","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should automated architecture assistance determine when human review is required?","answer":"Human review should be required according to decision impact, uncertainty, evidence quality, security or privacy implications, irreversibility, and organizational authority rather than simply because automation was used.","context":"Not every automated suggestion requires the same level of review, while some low-volume decisions have high consequences.","objective":"Apply proportionate human oversight to automated architecture decision support.","requirements":["Classify decision impact","Identify uncertainty","Identify governance constraints","Define review requirements"],"constraints":["Review capacity is limited","Risk classifications vary by organization"],"alternatives":["Human review for everything","No human review","Risk-based review"],"evidence":{"basis":["risk-based governance","decision impact","automation oversight"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Universal review increases safety margin but creates bottlenecks","Risk-based review is more efficient but depends on accurate classification"],"decision":"Use risk- and uncertainty-based review thresholds, with mandatory human authorization for decisions that exceed defined governance boundaries.","rationale":"Oversight effort should correspond to the consequences of incorrect or unauthorized architecture decisions.","consequences":["Low-risk assistance can scale","High-impact decisions receive stronger review","Automation remains subordinate to governance"],"implementation_implications":["Define review tiers","Attach decision risk metadata","Block unauthorized transitions to approved state"],"verification_method":"Test representative decisions across risk tiers and verify that required review gates are enforced.","outcome_learning":"Review escalations and missed escalations to improve decision-risk classification.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B88-R01","D54-B88-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B88-R01"},{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B88-R04"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}

{"record_id":"D54-B89-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Decision quality","concept":"Architecture decision completeness","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What minimum information should be present before an architecture decision is considered decision-ready?","answer":"A decision should normally include the problem or objective, requirements, constraints, alternatives, evidence, tradeoffs, selected decision, rationale, consequences, implementation implications, verification approach, and known uncertainty appropriate to its scope.","context":"Incomplete decision records can hide missing reasoning and make later verification difficult.","objective":"Define a completeness gate without forcing irrelevant fields into every decision.","requirements":["Cover material decision dimensions","Allow explicit not-applicable states","Record evidence and uncertainty","Define verification"],"constraints":["Small decisions may not need extensive analysis","Some evidence cannot exist before implementation"],"alternatives":["Fixed mandatory template","Contextual completeness checklist","Minimal free-form record"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision completeness","traceability","verification planning"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Structured completeness improves consistency","Overly rigid templates create unnecessary documentation"],"decision":"Use a contextual completeness checklist that requires material decision dimensions while permitting justified not-applicable fields.","rationale":"Completeness should be measured against decision needs rather than document length.","consequences":["Missing reasoning becomes visible","Small decisions avoid unnecessary bureaucracy","Decision readiness becomes testable"],"implementation_implications":["Implement field-level completeness checks","Require justification for omitted material dimensions","Distinguish completeness from correctness"],"verification_method":"Run a completeness audit and confirm that all material dimensions are populated or explicitly marked not applicable with justification.","outcome_learning":"Track recurring missing fields discovered after implementation and update completeness guidance.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B84-R01","D54-B87-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B84-R01"},{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B87-R02"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B89-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Decision quality","concept":"Decision correctness versus decision completeness","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"Why should decision completeness and decision correctness be evaluated separately?","answer":"A complete record can contain an incorrect architectural choice, while a technically sound choice can be poorly documented. Completeness evaluates whether required reasoning and evidence are represented; correctness requires validation against requirements, implementation, and outcomes.","context":"Documentation quality can be mistaken for architecture quality.","objective":"Keep documentation, reasoning, and technical correctness as separate validation dimensions.","requirements":["Evaluate completeness separately","Evaluate requirement satisfaction","Evaluate implementation conformance","Evaluate observed outcomes where available"],"constraints":["Correctness may not be fully knowable before implementation","Some outcomes require long-term observation"],"alternatives":["Single quality score","Separate quality dimensions","Documentation-only review"],"evidence":{"basis":["requirements validation","architecture conformance","decision completeness"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Multiple dimensions improve accuracy but require more structured evaluation","Single scores simplify reporting but hide failure categories"],"decision":"Track completeness, evidence quality, conformance, and outcome validation as separate properties.","rationale":"No single documentation metric establishes that an architecture decision is correct.","consequences":["False confidence is reduced","Verification gaps become explicit","Learning can target the actual failure dimension"],"implementation_implications":["Use separate validation fields","Do not equate populated records with verified decisions","Link outcome evidence independently"],"verification_method":"Audit decisions to ensure completeness status is not used as a substitute for implementation or outcome verification.","outcome_learning":"Analyze which quality dimension most often failed in later reviews and improve corresponding controls.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B89-R01","D54-B84-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B89-R01"},{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B84-R02"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B89-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Decision quality","concept":"Requirement traceability in architecture decisions","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should architecture decisions demonstrate that important requirements were considered?","answer":"Material requirements should be explicitly mapped to decision criteria, alternatives, constraints, or verification obligations. A requirement should not be assumed to be addressed merely because it appears elsewhere in project documentation.","context":"Architecture choices can optimize one concern while unintentionally violating another requirement.","objective":"Create traceability from requirements to architectural reasoning.","requirements":["Identify material requirements","Map requirements to decision considerations","Record unresolved requirement conflicts","Map critical requirements to verification"],"constraints":["Requirements may evolve","Some requirements may be qualitative"],"alternatives":["Narrative mention","Requirement-to-decision mapping","Automated traceability where feasible"],"evidence":{"basis":["requirements traceability","architecture analysis","verification planning"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Explicit mapping adds effort","Implicit coverage is easier but harder to audit"],"decision":"Maintain explicit traceability for requirements that materially influence the architecture or its verification obligations.","rationale":"Architecture decisions should demonstrate how important requirements affected the selected structure and tradeoffs.","consequences":["Requirement omissions become detectable","Conflicting requirements become visible","Verification planning improves"],"implementation_implications":["Assign stable requirement identifiers where available","Link requirements to decision fields","Verify critical architectural requirements independently"],"verification_method":"Trace each material requirement through decision reasoning to implementation or verification evidence.","outcome_learning":"Record requirements discovered after implementation and analyze why they were absent from the original decision.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B89-R01","D54-B82-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B89-R01"},{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B82-R01"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}
{"record_id":"D54-B89-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Decision quality","concept":"Constraint traceability","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"Why should architectural constraints be traced separately from preferences?","answer":"Constraints define boundaries that an architecture must respect, while preferences influence selection among valid options. Separating them prevents an attractive characteristic from being treated as justification for violating a non-negotiable condition.","context":"Architecture comparisons can incorrectly trade mandatory security, regulatory, availability, or compatibility requirements against optional benefits.","objective":"Protect hard architectural boundaries during decision analysis.","requirements":["Identify mandatory constraints","Identify preferences separately","Verify alternatives against constraints before comparison","Document justified exceptions"],"constraints":["Some constraints are conditional rather than absolute","Constraint interpretation can require domain expertise"],"alternatives":["Single criteria list","Constraint gates plus preference comparison","Weighted scoring of all criteria"],"evidence":{"basis":["constraint analysis","requirement classification","risk governance"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Hard gates simplify safety boundaries","Some organizations require explicit exception processes for legitimate deviations"],"decision":"Evaluate mandatory constraints as gates and preferences as tradeoff criteria, with explicit authorization for exceptions.","rationale":"Non-negotiable constraints should not be diluted by aggregate scoring.","consequences":["Invalid alternatives are removed early","Exceptions become auditable","Preference tradeoffs remain meaningful"],"implementation_implications":["Represent constraints explicitly","Implement pre-comparison validation","Record exception authority and rationale"],"verification_method":"Check that no selected architecture violates a mandatory constraint without an explicit, authorized exception.","outcome_learning":"Review constraint exceptions and failures to refine requirement classification and governance.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B89-R03","D54-B82-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B89-R03"},{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B82-R02"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B89-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Decision quality","concept":"Decision review checklist automation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What aspects of architecture decision quality can be checked deterministically?","answer":"Structural checks can verify required fields, stable identifiers, lifecycle status, relationship integrity, provenance presence, requirement links, decision-state consistency, and required verification metadata. Semantic correctness still requires appropriate engineering analysis or evidence.","context":"Knowledge systems can automate quality gates but cannot infer all architecture correctness from record structure.","objective":"Automate objective quality checks without overclaiming semantic validation.","requirements":["Validate schema","Validate references","Validate required metadata","Separate deterministic checks from semantic review"],"constraints":["Semantic architecture correctness cannot be reduced entirely to structural validation","Some fields require domain interpretation"],"alternatives":["Manual review only","Structural automation","Automated structural plus human semantic review"],"evidence":{"basis":["schema validation","referential integrity","architecture review"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Automation improves consistency","Structural checks can create false confidence if treated as correctness proof"],"decision":"Automate deterministic integrity and completeness checks while preserving separate semantic and implementation verification.","rationale":"Machine validation is valuable when its scope is explicit.","consequences":["Malformed decisions are caught early","Review effort focuses on substantive reasoning","False verification claims are reduced"],"implementation_implications":["Implement schema and relationship validators","Track validation status separately from execution and verification","Flag missing evidence rather than infer it"],"verification_method":"Run deterministic validators and separately review whether semantic claims have supporting evidence.","outcome_learning":"Track defects found after automated validation to identify missing semantic checks.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B89-R01","D54-B89-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B89-R01"},{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B89-R02"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}

{"record_id":"D54-B90-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Final decision state","concept":"Decision closure criteria","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When is an architecture decision sufficiently closed for implementation?","answer":"A decision is implementation-ready when its scope, requirements, constraints, alternatives, evidence, rationale, consequences, ownership, implementation implications, required approvals, and verification approach are sufficiently established for its context, with material uncertainty explicitly recorded.","context":"Teams may implement architecture choices before unresolved decision-critical questions are visible.","objective":"Define a practical readiness gate for architectural commitment.","requirements":["Complete material decision dimensions","Resolve or explicitly accept critical uncertainty","Obtain required authorization","Define implementation verification"],"constraints":["Not all uncertainty can be eliminated","Readiness thresholds vary with decision impact"],"alternatives":["Immediate implementation","Decision readiness gate","Prototype or pilot before closure"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision completeness","risk-based governance","verification planning"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Readiness gates reduce premature commitment","Excessive gates can delay low-risk decisions"],"decision":"Use a context- and risk-appropriate readiness gate before implementation, with unresolved critical uncertainty explicitly escalated or accepted.","rationale":"Implementation should follow an informed commitment rather than an undocumented assumption of completeness.","consequences":["Decision gaps become visible before implementation","High-impact choices receive stronger scrutiny","Low-risk decisions can avoid unnecessary process"],"implementation_implications":["Define readiness states","Require approval where applicable","Link implementation tasks to decision records"],"verification_method":"Audit readiness criteria before allowing implementation of decisions subject to governance controls.","outcome_learning":"Review decisions that required substantial redesign after closure and identify which readiness signal was missing.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B89-R01","D54-B87-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B89-R01"},{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B87-R01"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}
{"record_id":"D54-B90-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Final decision state","concept":"Decision execution versus decision verification","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should architecture decision implementation be distinguished from verification?","answer":"Implementing a selected architecture establishes that implementation work occurred; it does not prove that the architecture satisfies its intended requirements or guarantees. Verification must independently establish relevant properties using appropriate evidence.","context":"A completed implementation can still violate requirements, architectural boundaries, security constraints, or performance expectations.","objective":"Prevent implementation completion from being mistaken for architecture verification.","requirements":["Track implementation state separately","Define verification criteria","Record verification evidence","Preserve failed verification results"],"constraints":["Some properties require runtime or production evidence","Verification scope may evolve with system maturity"],"alternatives":["Implementation implies completion","Post-implementation review","Explicit implementation and verification states"],"evidence":{"basis":["verification methodology","requirements satisfaction","architecture conformance"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Explicit verification requires additional effort","Conflation reduces short-term process overhead but creates false confidence"],"decision":"Represent implementation and verification as independent lifecycle states with explicit evidence for each material architecture property.","rationale":"Execution establishes action; verification establishes evidence about the resulting state.","consequences":["False completion claims decrease","Verification gaps become visible","Failed verification can trigger corrective decisions"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain separate status fields","Link tests and runtime evidence","Create remediation or superseding decisions when guarantees fail"],"verification_method":"Confirm that implemented decisions cannot automatically transition to verified without required evidence.","outcome_learning":"Classify post-implementation verification failures and feed them into decision and implementation improvements.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B84-R01","D54-B84-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B84-R01"},{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B84-R02"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}
{"record_id":"D54-B90-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Final decision state","concept":"Decision closure after outcome review","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should happen to an architecture decision after its implementation and outcome have been evaluated?","answer":"The decision should retain its historical reasoning and be updated with outcome evidence or linked outcome records. If the architecture remains applicable, its status can remain active; if it is replaced or no longer applicable, it should be explicitly superseded or retired.","context":"Architecture decisions have value after implementation because they explain historical intent and provide evidence for future decisions.","objective":"Close the decision lifecycle without destroying historical knowledge.","requirements":["Record implementation status","Record verification evidence","Record outcome evidence where available","Set lifecycle status appropriately","Preserve historical reasoning"],"constraints":["Long-term outcomes may remain unavailable","A decision can remain active after initial implementation"],"alternatives":["Archive immediately","Keep indefinitely without outcome data","Lifecycle closure with outcome and status updates"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision lifecycle","outcome learning","historical traceability"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Long-term tracking creates maintenance effort","Premature archival loses useful learning"],"decision":"Close the implementation phase separately from the overall decision history and update lifecycle status only when applicability changes.","rationale":"Implementation completion does not erase the decision's historical and learning value.","consequences":["Decision history remains reusable","Outcome evidence can accumulate","Supersession becomes explicit"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain implementation and lifecycle states","Attach outcome records","Preserve supersession chains"],"verification_method":"Check that completed decisions retain implementation, verification, outcome, and lifecycle metadata rather than being deleted or ambiguously archived.","outcome_learning":"Use accumulated outcomes to refine future decision criteria, assumptions, and architecture guidance.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B90-R02","D54-B84-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B90-R02"},{"type":"supports","record_id":"D54-B84-R04"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B90-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Final decision state","concept":"Decision supersession","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should a new architecture decision supersede an existing one?","answer":"Supersession is appropriate when a new decision materially changes the architectural choice, governing assumptions, constraints, or intended guarantees for the same scope. The new decision should link to the prior record and explain what changed and why.","context":"Replacing architecture decisions without explicit linkage can destroy the reasoning chain that explains architectural evolution.","objective":"Maintain an auditable evolution path between architecture decisions.","requirements":["Identify prior decision","Describe material change","Record new evidence or requirements","Preserve historical decision","Update applicability state"],"constraints":["Different scopes should not be falsely linked as supersessions","Minor implementation changes may not require supersession"],"alternatives":["Edit old decision","Create unrelated new decision","Create linked superseding decision"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision versioning","change-impact analysis","architecture history"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Explicit supersession increases traceability","Creating new records requires relationship maintenance"],"decision":"Use explicit supersession for material architectural changes within the same decision scope and preserve the prior record as historical context.","rationale":"Architecture evolution should be represented as a traceable chain rather than disconnected choices.","consequences":["Historical reasoning remains available","Current guidance is identifiable","Change drivers become easier to analyze"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain supersession relationships","Record changed assumptions and evidence","Ensure retrieval favors currently applicable decisions"],"verification_method":"Verify that superseded decisions are retained but not presented as current guidance for the affected scope.","outcome_learning":"Analyze supersession causes to identify recurring requirement, technology, or assumption changes.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B84-R04","D54-B84-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B84-R04"},{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D54-B84-R05"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED"}
{"record_id":"D54-B90-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Architecture Decision Lifecycle","subtopic":"Final decision state","concept":"Architecture decision knowledge loop","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What complete lifecycle should an architecture decision follow to support continuous engineering learning?","answer":"A robust lifecycle connects problem framing, requirements, constraints, alternatives, evidence, tradeoffs, decision, authorization, implementation, verification, outcome measurement, learning, reassessment, and eventual supersession or retirement. Each state should remain distinguishable and evidence-backed.","context":"Architecture decision records are most valuable when they form a learning loop rather than static documentation.","objective":"Connect architecture reasoning to implementation evidence and future decision improvement.","requirements":["Represent decision stages explicitly","Preserve provenance","Separate execution from verification","Capture outcomes and learning","Support reassessment and supersession"],"constraints":["Not every decision requires identical lifecycle depth","Some outcome evidence may never become available"],"alternatives":["Static ADR documentation","Implementation-linked ADRs","Evidence-driven closed-loop decision lifecycle"],"evidence":{"basis":["architecture decision lifecycle","traceability","verification","outcome learning"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":["Closed-loop lifecycle requires more structured data and maintenance","Static records require less effort but provide less reusable learning"],"decision":"Use an evidence-driven lifecycle in which architecture decisions remain connected to implementation, verification, outcomes, learning, and future reassessment while preserving distinct lifecycle states.","rationale":"The long-term value of architecture knowledge comes from connecting decisions to what was actually implemented and observed.","consequences":["Architecture knowledge becomes reusable engineering evidence","Decision quality can improve over time","Historical and current architecture states remain distinguishable"],"implementation_implications":["Use stable decision identifiers","Maintain explicit relationships between decisions, requirements, implementations, tests, outcomes, and superseding decisions","Keep validation and execution states separate","Preserve provenance and contradiction status"],"verification_method":"Audit the lifecycle graph for traceable paths from decision context through implementation, verification, outcome, and subsequent decision changes without claiming unverified states.","outcome_learning":"Continuously compare predicted and observed outcomes, classify decision failures and successes, and update reusable architecture guidance only when evidence supports the generalization.","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B90-R01","D54-B90-R02","D54-B90-R03","D54-B90-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"integrates","record_id":"D54-B90-R01"},{"type":"integrates","record_id":"D54-B90-R02"},{"type":"integrates","record_id":"D54-B90-R03"},{"type":"integrates","record_id":"D54-B90-R04"}],"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING"}

{"record_id":"D54-B91-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Architecture Fitness Functions","subtopic":"Decision Validation","concept":"Architecture fitness functions convert important architectural qualities into repeatable checks","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an architecture decision be validated against its intended architectural qualities?","answer":"Define measurable or mechanically checkable fitness functions for the qualities that materially motivated the decision, then evaluate them continuously or at defined verification points.","context":"A decision justified by scalability, latency, reliability, security, or maintainability can drift away from its intended outcome during implementation.","objective":"Make architectural intent observable and verifiable rather than relying only on documentation.","requirements":["Each fitness function must correspond to a stated architectural concern","Thresholds or acceptance conditions must be explicit where measurable","Checks must be repeatable"],"constraints":["Not every architectural quality is directly measurable","Metrics can become misleading when used without context"],"alternatives":["Document qualitative expectations only","Use architecture fitness functions","Use periodic architecture reviews without automated checks"],"evidence":{"basis":["measurable acceptance conditions","architecture quality attributes","continuous verification principles"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"fitness_functions":"Higher verification precision but require instrumentation and maintenance","qualitative_review":"Lower implementation cost but weaker repeatability"},"decision":"Use fitness functions for high-impact architectural qualities where objective or repeatable checks can be defined.","rationale":"Critical architectural intent should have observable verification mechanisms whenever practical.","consequences":["Additional validation logic may be required","Thresholds may need revision as workload and requirements change"],"implementation_implications":["Map each fitness function to an architectural requirement","Define measurement source and evaluation frequency","Record threshold changes as decision changes"],"verification_method":"Execute the defined checks against representative environments and compare results with documented acceptance conditions.","outcome_learning":"Review whether fitness functions actually detect architectural regression; revise ineffective checks.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B91-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B91-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B91-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Architecture Fitness Functions","subtopic":"Threshold Selection","concept":"Fitness-function thresholds must reflect requirements and operating context","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should acceptance thresholds for architecture fitness functions be selected?","answer":"Derive thresholds from explicit requirements, known workload characteristics, risk tolerance, or validated baselines rather than arbitrary target values.","context":"A fitness function without a meaningful threshold may generate measurements without providing a decision signal.","objective":"Ensure architecture checks distinguish acceptable behavior from meaningful deviation.","requirements":["Threshold rationale must be documented","Measurement conditions must be reproducible","Thresholds must correspond to a relevant requirement or risk"],"constraints":["Workloads evolve","Measurement noise may produce false alarms","Some qualities require multidimensional assessment"],"alternatives":["Arbitrary fixed thresholds","Requirement-derived thresholds","Baseline-relative thresholds"],"evidence":{"basis":["requirements traceability","measurement validity","risk-based acceptance"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"fixed":"Simple but may become irrelevant","requirement_derived":"Traceable but requires explicit requirements","baseline_relative":"Adaptable but can normalize gradual degradation"},"decision":"Prefer requirement-derived thresholds, supplemented by validated baselines when absolute thresholds are unavailable.","rationale":"Architectural verification should remain traceable to the reason the decision was made.","consequences":["Threshold ownership and review become necessary","Baseline changes must be controlled"],"implementation_implications":["Store threshold rationale with the fitness function","Record environment and workload assumptions","Review thresholds when requirements change"],"verification_method":"Trace each threshold to a requirement, risk, or validated baseline and test its behavior under representative conditions.","outcome_learning":"Determine whether threshold breaches correlate with meaningful architectural degradation.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B91-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B91-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B91-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Architecture Fitness Functions","subtopic":"Non-functional Verification","concept":"Multiple fitness functions may be required when one quality metric cannot represent architectural intent","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should an architecture decision use multiple fitness functions?","answer":"Use multiple functions when the decision affects independent qualities or when optimizing one metric could hide degradation in another important dimension.","context":"Optimizing latency alone may increase cost, reduce reliability, or weaken security.","objective":"Prevent single-metric optimization from misrepresenting architecture quality.","requirements":["Each critical quality must have appropriate verification coverage","Conflicting metrics must be interpreted together"],"constraints":["More checks increase maintenance cost","Metrics can conflict"],"alternatives":["Single primary metric","Multiple independent functions","Composite score"],"evidence":{"basis":["quality attribute trade-offs","multi-objective architecture evaluation"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"single_metric":"Simple but incomplete","multiple_metrics":"More complete but requires interpretation","composite_score":"Compact but can obscure individual failures"},"decision":"Use multiple independently interpretable fitness functions for materially different architectural qualities.","rationale":"Preserving individual signals makes architectural trade-offs visible.","consequences":["Reviewers must interpret multiple signals","Automated gates may require explicit policy"],"implementation_implications":["Keep metric semantics separate","Avoid hiding critical failures inside an aggregate score"],"verification_method":"Demonstrate that each critical quality can independently trigger an actionable architectural review.","outcome_learning":"Identify whether any important architectural quality remains unobserved.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B91-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B91-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B91-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Architecture Fitness Functions","subtopic":"False Signals","concept":"Architecture verification must distinguish meaningful regression from measurement noise","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should noisy architecture measurements affect decision validation?","answer":"Use suitable aggregation, sampling, tolerance, or repeated evaluation so that transient measurement noise does not automatically invalidate an architecture decision.","context":"Distributed systems and performance measurements can vary because of workload, infrastructure, and environmental conditions.","objective":"Prevent unstable measurements from causing inappropriate architecture changes.","requirements":["Measurement variability must be characterized","Failure conditions must be meaningful"],"constraints":["Excessive smoothing can hide real regressions","Different environments may have different variance"],"alternatives":["Single observation","Repeated measurements","Statistical or tolerance-based evaluation"],"evidence":{"basis":["measurement variability","repeatability","verification design"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"single":"Fast but sensitive to noise","repeated":"More reliable but more expensive","tolerance_based":"Robust but requires sound limits"},"decision":"Use repeatable measurement and explicit tolerance appropriate to the quality being evaluated.","rationale":"Architectural decisions should respond to meaningful evidence rather than isolated observations.","consequences":["Verification may take longer","Measurement methodology becomes part of architecture governance"],"implementation_implications":["Define sampling methodology","Preserve test environment metadata","Distinguish signal from transient noise"],"verification_method":"Repeat the same evaluation under controlled conditions and assess variance before interpreting failures.","outcome_learning":"Update measurement methodology if repeated false alarms or missed regressions occur.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B91-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B91-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B91-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Architecture Fitness Functions","subtopic":"Decision Gates","concept":"Architecture fitness functions can act as explicit decision gates when requirements are critical","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should a fitness function become a release or architecture decision gate?","answer":"Make a fitness function a gate when violating its condition represents an unacceptable requirement or risk rather than a merely informational deviation.","context":"Some architectural properties are mandatory, while others are optimization targets.","objective":"Separate hard architectural constraints from advisory measurements.","requirements":["Gate conditions must be explicitly justified","Failure handling must be defined","Exceptions must be governed"],"constraints":["Over-gating can slow delivery","Under-gating can allow unacceptable architecture drift"],"alternatives":["Informational monitoring","Hard gate","Risk-based conditional gate"],"evidence":{"basis":["requirement criticality","risk-based governance","acceptance criteria"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"informational":"Flexible but weaker enforcement","hard_gate":"Strong protection but lower flexibility","conditional":"Balanced but more complex"},"decision":"Use hard gates only for explicitly critical requirements and conditional governance for risk-sensitive thresholds.","rationale":"Not every architectural metric warrants blocking progress.","consequences":["Critical failures receive stronger enforcement","Exception handling must be auditable"],"implementation_implications":["Associate each gate with an owner and requirement","Record approved exceptions and expiry conditions"],"verification_method":"Test both passing and failing conditions and confirm that only intended failures trigger governance action.","outcome_learning":"Review whether gates prevent important regressions without creating unjustified delivery friction.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B91-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"builds_on","record_id":"D54-B91-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B92-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Architecture Risk Assessment","subtopic":"Risk Identification","concept":"Architecture decisions should explicitly identify risks introduced by the chosen alternative","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should an architecture decision risk assessment contain?","answer":"Identify credible failure modes, affected qualities, exposure conditions, impact, uncertainty, mitigation, detection mechanisms, and residual risk.","context":"A decision can satisfy its primary objective while introducing secondary risks.","objective":"Make material architectural risk visible before implementation and review.","requirements":["Risk must be linked to the decision","Impact and trigger conditions must be explicit","Mitigation and detection must be distinguishable"],"constraints":["Risk estimates may contain uncertainty","Not every risk is quantifiable"],"alternatives":["Informal risk discussion","Structured risk assessment","External risk framework"],"evidence":{"basis":["failure-mode analysis","risk-based architecture evaluation"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"informal":"Low overhead but inconsistent","structured":"Higher discipline but more documentation","external_framework":"Potentially comprehensive but may add process overhead"},"decision":"Use structured risk assessment for architecture decisions with material operational, security, reliability, or financial consequences.","rationale":"High-impact decisions require explicit treatment of failure and uncertainty.","consequences":["Architecture records become more detailed","Risk ownership becomes necessary"],"implementation_implications":["Record trigger, impact, mitigation, detection, and residual risk","Connect important risks to verification mechanisms"],"verification_method":"Review whether each material risk has a defined mitigation or explicit acceptance decision.","outcome_learning":"Compare predicted risks with observed issues and update future decision heuristics.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B92-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B92-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B92-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Architecture Risk Assessment","subtopic":"Residual Risk","concept":"Accepting residual architectural risk is a decision that should be explicit","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should residual risk be represented after mitigation?","answer":"Record the remaining risk, why additional mitigation is disproportionate or unavailable, who accepts it, and what evidence would trigger reassessment.","context":"Mitigation rarely eliminates every architectural risk.","objective":"Prevent residual risk from disappearing after a mitigation is documented.","requirements":["Residual risk must remain visible","Acceptance criteria must be explicit","Reassessment triggers must be defined"],"constraints":["Risk acceptance authority may differ by organization","Some residual risk cannot be quantified precisely"],"alternatives":["Treat mitigation as complete","Explicit residual-risk acceptance","Avoid the architecture entirely"],"evidence":{"basis":["risk acceptance","residual risk management"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"implicit":"Low documentation but weak accountability","explicit":"Higher accountability with additional governance","avoidance":"Lower selected risk but potentially higher cost or complexity"},"decision":"Record and govern residual risk separately from mitigation status.","rationale":"A mitigation action does not prove that the underlying risk no longer exists.","consequences":["Risk records may remain open after implementation","Review triggers become part of lifecycle governance"],"implementation_implications":["Store residual risk with owner and acceptance state","Link monitoring signals to reassessment triggers"],"verification_method":"Inspect decision records for explicit residual-risk state after mitigation completion.","outcome_learning":"Compare residual-risk predictions with actual incidents and observed deviations.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B92-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B92-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B92-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Architecture Risk Assessment","subtopic":"Uncertainty","concept":"Architecture risk should distinguish known facts from assumptions and unknowns","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should uncertainty be represented in architecture risk assessment?","answer":"Separate observed evidence, validated assumptions, unvalidated assumptions, and unknown conditions so risk treatment reflects evidence quality.","context":"Early architecture decisions often depend on incomplete workload, dependency, or operational information.","objective":"Avoid presenting assumptions as established facts.","requirements":["Evidence status must be explicit","Critical assumptions require validation plans","Unknowns must have defined handling"],"constraints":["Uncertainty may remain until production exposure","Some assumptions cannot be fully tested beforehand"],"alternatives":["Treat all inputs equally","Classify evidence and assumptions","Exclude uncertain inputs"],"evidence":{"basis":["evidence classification","assumption tracking","decision uncertainty"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"equal_treatment":"Simple but obscures uncertainty","classified":"More accurate but more metadata","exclusion":"Conservative but may prevent useful decisions"},"decision":"Classify evidence and assumptions explicitly, with validation plans for decision-critical uncertainty.","rationale":"Architecture decisions are stronger when uncertainty is visible rather than hidden.","consequences":["Decision records carry uncertainty metadata","Validation work may be required before final commitment"],"implementation_implications":["Mark assumptions with owners and validation conditions","Avoid converting assumptions into factual requirements"],"verification_method":"Review each high-impact assumption and confirm that its evidence state and validation path are recorded.","outcome_learning":"Track which assumptions proved valid or invalid and feed those outcomes into future decisions.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B92-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B92-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B92-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Architecture Risk Assessment","subtopic":"Risk Prioritization","concept":"Risk prioritization should consider consequence, exposure, detectability, and uncertainty","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should multiple architecture risks be prioritized?","answer":"Prioritize according to decision-relevant consequence and exposure while accounting for detectability and uncertainty; avoid relying on a single arbitrary numerical score when the underlying factors are not comparable.","context":"A low-frequency catastrophic failure and a frequent low-impact degradation require different treatment.","objective":"Allocate validation and mitigation effort according to meaningful risk differences.","requirements":["Prioritization factors must be explicit","Critical low-probability risks must not be automatically discarded","Scoring limitations must be understood"],"constraints":["Risk scoring can create false precision","Different risk dimensions may not be directly comparable"],"alternatives":["Simple severity ranking","Composite numerical score","Structured qualitative prioritization"],"evidence":{"basis":["risk consequence","exposure","detectability","uncertainty"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"simple":"Easy but may omit important dimensions","numeric":"Comparable but can create false precision","qualitative":"Context-rich but less mechanically sortable"},"decision":"Use explicit risk dimensions and contextual prioritization rather than treating a single score as the complete decision.","rationale":"Architecture risk is multidimensional and context dependent.","consequences":["Review requires interpretation","Risk models need documented assumptions"],"implementation_implications":["Preserve underlying risk dimensions","Record why a risk received its treatment"],"verification_method":"Audit high-impact risks to ensure prioritization reflects more than an unexplained aggregate score.","outcome_learning":"Compare prioritization decisions with observed failures and revise risk assumptions where necessary.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B92-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"builds_on","record_id":"D54-B92-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B92-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Architecture Risk Assessment","subtopic":"Risk Verification","concept":"Material architecture risks require verification evidence rather than documentation alone","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How can architecture risk mitigation be verified?","answer":"Associate mitigation claims with tests, measurements, design constraints, operational controls, or other evidence that can demonstrate the intended risk reduction.","context":"A documented mitigation may exist without actually reducing the relevant failure mode.","objective":"Distinguish planned mitigation from demonstrated risk reduction.","requirements":["Mitigation claims must have evidence paths","Evidence must target the stated risk"],"constraints":["Some risks can only be evaluated under realistic conditions","Evidence may be probabilistic"],"alternatives":["Documentation review","Design inspection","Test or operational evidence"],"evidence":{"basis":["risk-to-control traceability","verification evidence"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"documentation":"Low cost but weak evidence","inspection":"Useful for design properties but limited for runtime behavior","testing":"Stronger behavioral evidence but may require realistic environments"},"decision":"Require evidence appropriate to the risk mechanism before treating a mitigation as verified.","rationale":"Risk reduction is a claim that should be supported by evidence.","consequences":["Verification work becomes part of architecture implementation","Some risks remain explicitly unverified"],"implementation_implications":["Link risk to verification artifact","Separate implemented mitigation from verified effectiveness"],"verification_method":"Trace each critical mitigation to a corresponding verification result or explicitly mark it unverified.","outcome_learning":"Assess whether verification evidence predicted real-world behavior.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B92-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B92-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B93-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"ADR Evidence Quality","subtopic":"Evidence Classification","concept":"Architecture decision evidence should distinguish facts from assumptions and interpretations","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What evidence categories should an architecture decision record distinguish?","answer":"Separate observed facts, authoritative requirements, measurements, validated experiments, assumptions, engineering inference, and stakeholder preferences where those distinctions affect the decision.","context":"Combining evidence types without labeling them can make a weak assumption appear equivalent to measured evidence.","objective":"Preserve evidence provenance and reasoning quality.","requirements":["Evidence origin must be identifiable","Interpretation must not be presented as observation","Decision-critical assumptions must be visible"],"constraints":["Evidence categories can overlap","Some evidence is context dependent"],"alternatives":["Unstructured evidence list","Typed evidence categories","Formal evidence hierarchy"],"evidence":{"basis":["provenance tracking","evidence classification","decision traceability"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"unstructured":"Simple but ambiguous","typed":"More precise with moderate metadata cost","formal_hierarchy":"Strong discipline but potentially excessive for small decisions"},"decision":"Use typed evidence categories and provenance for decision-critical evidence.","rationale":"Decision quality depends on understanding what is known versus inferred.","consequences":["ADR structure becomes more explicit","Evidence maintenance may be required"],"implementation_implications":["Store evidence type, source, date/version, and applicability","Preserve distinction between measurement and interpretation"],"verification_method":"Review decision evidence and classify each item according to its actual epistemic status.","outcome_learning":"Identify recurring evidence weaknesses in completed decisions.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B93-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B93-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B93-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"ADR Evidence Quality","subtopic":"Source Traceability","concept":"Decision evidence should remain traceable to its originating artifact or observation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should architecture decision evidence retain source traceability?","answer":"Source traceability allows reviewers to verify relevance, version, interpretation, and continued validity of the evidence supporting a decision.","context":"Requirements, benchmarks, dependency capabilities, and operational measurements can change over time.","objective":"Prevent architecture records from becoming unverifiable historical assertions.","requirements":["Evidence should identify its source","Relevant version or time context should be preserved","Source interpretation should be distinguishable from source content"],"constraints":["Some evidence sources may become unavailable","Dynamic external information can change"],"alternatives":["Store conclusions only","Store source references","Store references plus captured evidence metadata"],"evidence":{"basis":["provenance","version awareness","auditability"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"conclusion_only":"Low maintenance but poor auditability","reference":"Better traceability but source may change","captured_metadata":"Stronger historical traceability with storage cost"},"decision":"Preserve source references and sufficient contextual metadata for decision-critical evidence.","rationale":"A decision should remain understandable even when its original context changes.","consequences":["ADR maintenance includes provenance","Historical context becomes recoverable"],"implementation_implications":["Record source identifier, version/date, applicability, and interpretation","Avoid mutable links as the only evidence representation"],"verification_method":"Attempt to reconstruct the evidence basis of a historical decision from its recorded provenance.","outcome_learning":"Identify evidence sources that repeatedly become unavailable or ambiguous.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B93-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B93-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B93-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"ADR Evidence Quality","subtopic":"Contradictory Evidence","concept":"Conflicting evidence should be represented rather than silently discarded","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should contradictory architecture evidence be handled?","answer":"Record the conflict, explain differences in context or measurement, identify which evidence is more applicable, and preserve unresolved uncertainty when it cannot be conclusively resolved.","context":"Two benchmarks or stakeholder requirements may appear inconsistent because they represent different workloads or priorities.","objective":"Prevent selective evidence use from creating false certainty.","requirements":["Conflicting evidence must be visible","Applicability conditions must be compared","Resolution rationale must be documented"],"constraints":["Contradictions may remain unresolved","Evidence may have different quality levels"],"alternatives":["Choose one source silently","Average contradictory evidence","Analyze context and preserve disagreement"],"evidence":{"basis":["contradiction handling","contextual applicability","evidence provenance"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"silent_selection":"Simple but weakly defensible","averaging":"Compact but may destroy meaningful differences","explicit_analysis":"More work but preserves uncertainty"},"decision":"Represent contradictory evidence explicitly and resolve only when evidence quality and applicability justify resolution.","rationale":"Conflicting evidence contains information about uncertainty and context.","consequences":["ADR records may contain unresolved evidence states","Review becomes more transparent"],"implementation_implications":["Store contradiction relationships","Record applicability conditions and resolution status"],"verification_method":"Inspect decisions with conflicting evidence and verify that the conflict and resolution basis are recorded.","outcome_learning":"Determine which evidence source or contextual distinction proved predictive after implementation.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B93-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B93-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B93-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"ADR Evidence Quality","subtopic":"Decision Confidence","concept":"Decision confidence should reflect evidence strength and uncertainty rather than personal certainty","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should confidence be represented in an architecture decision?","answer":"Confidence should summarize the strength and applicability of evidence while remaining separate from the decision itself and from subjective certainty.","context":"A well-reasoned decision can still have low confidence when critical workload or dependency information is missing.","objective":"Make uncertainty explicit without turning confidence into an unexplained score.","requirements":["Confidence basis must be explainable","Uncertainty sources must be identified","Confidence must not replace evidence"],"constraints":["Confidence is context dependent","Numerical confidence can imply false precision"],"alternatives":["No confidence field","Qualitative confidence","Numerical confidence score"],"evidence":{"basis":["evidence quality","uncertainty classification"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"none":"Simple but hides uncertainty","qualitative":"Transparent if defined","numeric":"Sortable but risks false precision"},"decision":"Use explainable qualitative confidence with explicit supporting evidence and uncertainty factors.","rationale":"Confidence is useful only when its basis is understandable.","consequences":["Decision records require confidence rationale","Confidence may change without changing the decision immediately"],"implementation_implications":["Separate confidence from decision status","Record reasons for confidence changes"],"verification_method":"Review confidence statements and determine whether the stated evidence supports them.","outcome_learning":"Compare confidence with eventual evidence quality and decision outcomes.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B93-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B93-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B93-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"ADR Evidence Quality","subtopic":"Evidence Expiration","concept":"Time-sensitive evidence can lose applicability without invalidating the historical decision context","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should time-sensitive evidence be handled in architecture decision records?","answer":"Record temporal applicability and review conditions so aging evidence can trigger reassessment without rewriting historical facts.","context":"Technology capabilities, traffic patterns, costs, security conditions, and organizational constraints change over time.","objective":"Prevent stale evidence from silently governing current decisions.","requirements":["Evidence timestamp or version must be known","Expiration or review conditions should be defined where relevant","Historical context must remain preserved"],"constraints":["Not all evidence has a meaningful expiration date","Review frequency may vary"],"alternatives":["Treat evidence as permanent","Periodic evidence review","Trigger-based evidence review"],"evidence":{"basis":["temporal validity","version awareness","decision lifecycle"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"permanent":"Low maintenance but high staleness risk","periodic":"Predictable but may review unnecessarily","trigger_based":"Efficient but requires trigger design"},"decision":"Use temporal metadata and trigger-based review for evidence whose applicability can materially change.","rationale":"Historical evidence should remain intact while current applicability is reassessed separately.","consequences":["ADR records can have historical and current evidence states","Review triggers become important"],"implementation_implications":["Store evidence version/date","Define material change triggers"],"verification_method":"Test whether known changes in requirements or technology trigger evidence reassessment.","outcome_learning":"Track which evidence types become stale most frequently.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B93-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B93-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B94-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Decision Validation","subtopic":"Architecture Experiments","concept":"A reversible architecture experiment can reduce uncertainty before an irreversible commitment","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should an architecture decision use an experiment before full implementation?","answer":"Use an experiment when a decision-critical uncertainty can be isolated and tested at materially lower cost or risk than committing to the full architecture.","context":"Unknown performance, integration behavior, operational complexity, or dependency constraints can make early architecture selection uncertain.","objective":"Obtain targeted evidence before making high-cost commitments.","requirements":["The experiment must target a specific uncertainty","Success and failure conditions must be defined","Experiment scope must prevent misleading conclusions"],"constraints":["Prototype behavior may differ from production","Experiments consume engineering time"],"alternatives":["Commit directly","Run targeted experiment","Build full production path"],"evidence":{"basis":["uncertainty reduction","experimental validation","reversibility"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"direct_commit":"Fast but higher uncertainty","experiment":"Slower initially but can reduce uncertainty","full_build":"Highest fidelity but highest cost"},"decision":"Prefer targeted experiments when unresolved uncertainty materially affects the architecture decision and can be isolated.","rationale":"Small evidence-generating actions can be preferable to large speculative commitments.","consequences":["Decision cycle may include explicit experimentation","Prototype limitations must be documented"],"implementation_implications":["Define hypothesis, variables, environment, measurements, and interpretation limits","Do not treat prototype success as automatic production validation"],"verification_method":"Check whether experiment results directly answer the decision-critical hypothesis.","outcome_learning":"Record whether the experiment changed the decision or merely increased confidence.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B94-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B94-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B94-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Decision Validation","subtopic":"Hypothesis Definition","concept":"Architecture experiments require explicit hypotheses and falsifiable acceptance conditions","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"What makes an architecture experiment decision-useful?","answer":"The experiment should state what is expected, under which conditions, what observation would challenge the assumption, and how the result will affect the decision.","context":"Experiments that merely demonstrate that a prototype can run may not answer the actual architecture question.","objective":"Connect experimentation directly to decision reasoning.","requirements":["Hypothesis must be explicit","Test conditions must be bounded","Result interpretation must be predefined"],"constraints":["Some architecture properties are difficult to falsify experimentally","Results can be environment-specific"],"alternatives":["Open-ended prototype","Hypothesis-driven experiment","Full benchmark"],"evidence":{"basis":["hypothesis testing","decision traceability"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"open_ended":"Flexible but weak decision linkage","hypothesis_driven":"Focused and traceable","full_benchmark":"Detailed but potentially excessive"},"decision":"Use hypothesis-driven experiments for decision-critical unknowns.","rationale":"An experiment has architectural value only when its result changes or validates reasoning.","consequences":["Experiment design becomes part of ADR evidence","Failed hypotheses become valuable decision evidence"],"implementation_implications":["Record hypothesis and expected observation","Record what decision state each possible result would support"],"verification_method":"Review experiment records and determine whether each result maps to a defined decision consequence.","outcome_learning":"Capture failed hypotheses as reusable architectural knowledge.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B94-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B94-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B94-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Decision Validation","subtopic":"Prototype Limits","concept":"Prototype evidence must not be generalized beyond its tested conditions","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should prototype limitations affect architecture decision evidence?","answer":"Document which production properties the prototype does not establish, including scale, failure behavior, security controls, operational complexity, and integration conditions when they were not tested.","context":"A prototype can validate one technical property while leaving several architectural risks unresolved.","objective":"Prevent overgeneralization from experimental evidence.","requirements":["Test scope must be explicit","Untested properties must remain unresolved","Evidence applicability must be bounded"],"constraints":["Prototype environments are often simplified","Production interactions may be emergent"],"alternatives":["Treat prototype as proof","Treat prototype as partial evidence","Reject all prototype evidence"],"evidence":{"basis":["experimental scope","evidence applicability"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"proof":"Simple but risks overclaiming","partial_evidence":"Accurate but requires careful interpretation","reject":"Conservative but discards useful information"},"decision":"Treat prototype results as bounded evidence tied to the properties and conditions actually tested.","rationale":"Evidence strength is determined by what was observed, not by the existence of a prototype.","consequences":["ADRs may retain unresolved questions after experiments","Additional validation may be required"],"implementation_implications":["Store tested dimensions and excluded dimensions","Do not mark untested production properties as verified"],"verification_method":"Compare experiment claims against actual experiment scope and environment.","outcome_learning":"Track which prototype assumptions later required production correction.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B94-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B94-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B94-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Decision Validation","subtopic":"Pilot Validation","concept":"A controlled pilot can validate architecture interactions that isolated experiments cannot","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When is a pilot preferable to an isolated architecture experiment?","answer":"Use a pilot when the decision depends on interactions among components, users, operational processes, or real workload characteristics that cannot be represented adequately in an isolated experiment.","context":"Architecture failures can emerge from integration and operational interactions rather than individual component behavior.","objective":"Validate system-level assumptions before broad adoption.","requirements":["Pilot scope must be controlled","Success criteria must be defined","Rollback or containment must exist"],"constraints":["Pilot evidence may still be limited in scale","Real environments introduce additional variables"],"alternatives":["Component experiment","Controlled pilot","Full rollout"],"evidence":{"basis":["system interaction validation","controlled rollout"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"component":"Cheap but limited interaction evidence","pilot":"Higher fidelity with bounded exposure","full_rollout":"Highest exposure and evidence but highest risk"},"decision":"Use controlled pilots when interaction effects are decision-critical and isolated testing cannot represent them.","rationale":"System-level assumptions require system-level evidence when component tests are insufficient.","consequences":["Pilot governance and observability become architecture concerns","Results may lead to decision revision"],"implementation_implications":["Define pilot boundary, telemetry, rollback, and evaluation criteria","Separate pilot evidence from broad production claims"],"verification_method":"Evaluate pilot observations against predefined architectural acceptance criteria.","outcome_learning":"Capture interaction effects that were absent from isolated experiments.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B94-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B94-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B94-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Decision Validation","subtopic":"Decision Reversal Criteria","concept":"Validation should define conditions under which evidence invalidates the chosen architecture","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should architecture validation define reversal criteria before evidence is collected?","answer":"Predefined reversal criteria reduce post-hoc reinterpretation and make clear which observations would require reconsideration of the decision.","context":"Teams can become attached to an architecture and reinterpret negative evidence after implementation.","objective":"Preserve evidence-driven decision revision.","requirements":["Material invalidation conditions must be explicit","Criteria must correspond to decision assumptions","Exceptions require documented reasoning"],"constraints":["Some architecture decisions can tolerate partial failure","Evidence may be ambiguous"],"alternatives":["No reversal criteria","Predefined reversal conditions","Automatic reversal"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision integrity","predefined acceptance criteria"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"none":"Flexible but vulnerable to bias","predefined":"More objective but requires careful design","automatic":"Strong enforcement but unsuitable for ambiguous evidence"},"decision":"Define review or reversal triggers for material decision assumptions without assuming every trigger requires automatic replacement.","rationale":"Architecture governance should allow evidence to change the decision.","consequences":["Decision records can move from accepted to reassessment","Review criteria become auditable"],"implementation_implications":["Record invalidation conditions and review owner","Separate trigger detection from final architectural replacement"],"verification_method":"Check whether material assumption failures produce the documented reassessment path.","outcome_learning":"Measure whether predefined triggers actually captured meaningful decision failures.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B94-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"builds_on","record_id":"D54-B94-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B95-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Architecture Review Triggers","subtopic":"Requirement Change","concept":"Material requirement changes can invalidate architecture decisions","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should a changed requirement trigger architecture decision review?","answer":"Trigger review when the changed requirement affects an assumption, constraint, quality attribute, integration boundary, or trade-off that materially influenced the original decision.","context":"A decision can remain technically sound while no longer satisfying changed business or system requirements.","objective":"Keep architecture aligned with current requirements without unnecessary rework.","requirements":["Material requirements must be traceable to decisions","Change impact must be assessed","Historical decision context must remain preserved"],"constraints":["Not every requirement change is architecturally significant","Frequent review can create process overhead"],"alternatives":["Review every change","Review only material changes","Review on a fixed schedule"],"evidence":{"basis":["requirement traceability","change impact analysis"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"every_change":"High coverage but excessive overhead","material_only":"Efficient but requires impact analysis","scheduled":"Predictable but can delay response"},"decision":"Use impact-based review triggers tied to decision assumptions and constraints.","rationale":"Architecture should react to changes that alter its decision basis, not every textual modification.","consequences":["Requirement-to-decision traceability becomes valuable","Change assessment is required"],"implementation_implications":["Link requirements to affected decisions","Define materiality criteria"],"verification_method":"Trace representative requirement changes and confirm that material architectural impacts trigger review.","outcome_learning":"Refine materiality rules based on missed or unnecessary reviews.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B95-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B95-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B95-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Architecture Review Triggers","subtopic":"Technology Change","concept":"Material technology or dependency changes can alter architecture trade-offs","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should a dependency or platform change trigger architecture review?","answer":"Review the decision when a technology change alters supported capabilities, security posture, compatibility, performance assumptions, lifecycle status, cost, or operational constraints that were material to the original choice.","context":"A previously acceptable dependency can change characteristics without changing the application's functional requirements.","objective":"Detect architecture drift caused by external technology evolution.","requirements":["Technology assumptions must be identifiable","Material lifecycle or capability changes must be monitored","Historical evidence must not be silently rewritten"],"constraints":["External ecosystems change continuously","Not every dependency update affects architecture"],"alternatives":["Review every version update","Review only material changes","Ignore until failure"],"evidence":{"basis":["dependency lifecycle","technology assumptions","version-aware architecture"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"every_update":"High effort","material_only":"Lower effort but needs impact rules","ignore":"Low effort but reactive"},"decision":"Trigger architecture review for technology changes that alter material decision assumptions or constraints.","rationale":"Architecture decisions depend on technology characteristics that can evolve independently of application requirements.","consequences":["Dependency monitoring can become architecture governance input","Version changes may require evidence reassessment"],"implementation_implications":["Track decision-critical dependencies and assumptions","Define impact categories"],"verification_method":"Simulate a material dependency capability or lifecycle change and verify that affected decisions enter review.","outcome_learning":"Record which external changes historically affected architectural decisions.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B95-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B95-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B95-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Architecture Review Triggers","subtopic":"Operational Evidence","concept":"Repeated production evidence can trigger architecture reassessment even when requirements remain unchanged","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Can operational evidence trigger architecture review without a requirement change?","answer":"Yes. Repeated incidents, persistent performance degradation, unexpected capacity behavior, or recurring operational work can reveal that an architectural assumption is invalid or incomplete.","context":"Production behavior can expose constraints that were not visible during design and testing.","objective":"Use verified operational evidence as an input to architecture evolution.","requirements":["Operational evidence must be credible and attributable","Patterns should be distinguished from isolated noise","Review impact must be assessed"],"constraints":["Operational incidents may have non-architectural causes","Correlation does not automatically establish architectural causation"],"alternatives":["Ignore operational evidence","Review every incident","Trigger review from material recurring patterns"],"evidence":{"basis":["operational feedback","incident patterns","architecture learning"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"ignore":"Low process cost but loses learning","every_incident":"High sensitivity but excessive churn","recurring_pattern":"Balanced but requires pattern analysis"},"decision":"Use recurring or materially significant operational evidence as an architecture review trigger after causal relevance is assessed.","rationale":"Architecture should learn from verified system behavior while avoiding reaction to unrelated incidents.","consequences":["Operational data can influence architecture lifecycle","Evidence quality becomes important"],"implementation_implications":["Link incidents to affected architectural assumptions","Record causal confidence and alternative explanations"],"verification_method":"Review whether recurring architecture-related operational signals lead to appropriate reassessment.","outcome_learning":"Determine which operational patterns reliably indicate architectural weaknesses.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B95-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B95-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B95-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Architecture Review Triggers","subtopic":"Cost Drift","concept":"Material cost changes can invalidate an architecture trade-off","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"When should cost changes trigger architecture decision review?","answer":"Review a decision when cost changes materially alter the trade-off that justified the architecture, considering infrastructure, operations, licensing, migration, staffing, and failure-related costs where relevant.","context":"An architecture selected under one cost structure may have different economics after scale or pricing changes.","objective":"Keep architecture decisions aligned with current cost constraints without optimizing cost in isolation.","requirements":["Cost assumptions must be documented","Materiality threshold must be explicit","Non-cost requirements must remain visible"],"constraints":["Cost data can be uncertain","Cost optimization can degrade other qualities"],"alternatives":["Ignore cost changes","Review on fixed budget breach","Review when decision trade-off materially changes"],"evidence":{"basis":["cost assumptions","architecture trade-offs","constraint changes"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"ignore":"Stable process but stale economics","budget_breach":"Reactive","tradeoff_change":"More directly tied to decision reasoning"},"decision":"Trigger review when cost changes materially alter the original architecture trade-off or constraint set.","rationale":"Cost is one decision dimension and should be evaluated together with required qualities.","consequences":["Cost assumptions require lifecycle tracking","Architecture review may be triggered by external pricing changes"],"implementation_implications":["Record cost model assumptions","Separate observed costs from forecasts"],"verification_method":"Recalculate material cost assumptions and assess whether the original decision remains consistent with stated constraints.","outcome_learning":"Compare forecast and realized cost drivers after implementation.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B95-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B95-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B95-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Architecture Review Triggers","subtopic":"Scheduled Review","concept":"Periodic architecture review is useful for decisions with long-lived assumptions even when no explicit trigger fires","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When is periodic architecture review appropriate?","answer":"Use periodic review when architecture decisions have long-lived external assumptions, accumulated change, or insufficient event-based observability to guarantee that material drift will be detected promptly.","context":"Some architecture drift is gradual and may not produce a discrete trigger.","objective":"Provide a fallback mechanism for detecting accumulated decision drift.","requirements":["Review scope must be bounded","Periodic review must examine decision assumptions and evidence","Review must not rewrite historical facts"],"constraints":["Periodic reviews consume engineering capacity","Stable systems may gain little from frequent review"],"alternatives":["Event-triggered review only","Periodic review","Hybrid trigger plus periodic review"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision drift","lifecycle governance"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"event_only":"Efficient but can miss gradual drift","periodic":"Broad coverage but higher overhead","hybrid":"More robust but requires both mechanisms"},"decision":"Use periodic review as a complement to event triggers when assumptions can drift gradually or remain weakly observable.","rationale":"Architecture lifecycle controls should cover both discrete and gradual change.","consequences":["Review cadence becomes part of governance","Stale decisions are less likely to persist indefinitely"],"implementation_implications":["Define review interval by decision criticality and change rate","Prioritize assumptions rather than rereading unchanged prose"],"verification_method":"Check whether scheduled reviews identify stale assumptions not caught by event triggers.","outcome_learning":"Adjust review cadence according to observed decision drift.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B95-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B95-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B96-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Decision Drift","subtopic":"Definition","concept":"Architecture decision drift occurs when implementation context diverges materially from the decision basis","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What constitutes architecture decision drift?","answer":"Decision drift occurs when requirements, constraints, assumptions, technology characteristics, workload, or operational conditions change enough that the original rationale no longer represents the current system context.","context":"An ADR can remain unchanged while the system and its environment evolve substantially.","objective":"Distinguish stable decisions from decisions whose underlying basis has materially changed.","requirements":["Original decision basis must be reconstructable","Current context must be comparable","Materiality must be assessed"],"constraints":["Drift can be gradual","Not every environmental change matters"],"alternatives":["Treat ADR as permanent","Review every change","Assess material drift"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision traceability","context evolution"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"permanent":"Low maintenance but high staleness risk","every_change":"High overhead","material_drift":"Requires impact analysis but preserves focus"},"decision":"Define drift in terms of material changes to the decision's assumptions, constraints, or intended outcomes.","rationale":"Decision validity depends on context, not document age alone.","consequences":["ADR status can change without code changes","Context comparison becomes a lifecycle capability"],"implementation_implications":["Track assumptions and constraints explicitly","Compare current evidence with decision-time evidence"],"verification_method":"Perform before-and-after context comparison and identify material differences.","outcome_learning":"Catalog recurring drift sources to improve future architecture monitoring.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B96-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B96-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B96-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Decision Drift","subtopic":"Detection","concept":"Decision drift is easier to detect when assumptions and constraints are explicit and traceable","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What makes architecture decision drift detectable?","answer":"Explicit assumptions, requirements, constraints, evidence, and expected outcomes provide comparison points against current system conditions.","context":"Unstructured decision documents make it difficult to determine what has actually changed.","objective":"Make decision validity inspectable over time.","requirements":["Decision basis must be structured","Current-state evidence must be available","Comparison criteria must be defined"],"constraints":["Current-state data may be incomplete","Some assumptions are qualitative"],"alternatives":["Document narrative only","Structured decision record","Automated drift detection"],"evidence":{"basis":["structured traceability","change detection"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"narrative":"Flexible but difficult to compare","structured":"More metadata but easier to assess","automated":"Efficient where measurable but incomplete for qualitative assumptions"},"decision":"Structure decision records around explicit decision inputs and outcomes so drift can be assessed systematically.","rationale":"Drift cannot be reliably detected when the original decision basis is implicit.","consequences":["ADR schema becomes an architecture control mechanism","Some drift detection can be automated"],"implementation_implications":["Store machine-readable assumptions and constraints","Link current measurements to decision expectations"],"verification_method":"Attempt to detect a known material context change using only the structured decision record.","outcome_learning":"Identify decision fields that consistently enable or hinder drift detection.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B96-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B96-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B96-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Decision Drift","subtopic":"Partial Drift","concept":"A decision can remain valid for some conditions while becoming invalid for others","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should partial architecture decision drift be represented?","answer":"Represent which assumptions or operating conditions have drifted instead of immediately marking the entire decision invalid when only part of its applicability has changed.","context":"A storage architecture may remain suitable for one workload while a new workload violates its original capacity assumptions.","objective":"Avoid all-or-nothing decision status when applicability is conditional.","requirements":["Applicability conditions must be explicit","Affected dimensions must be identified","Unaffected validity must remain visible"],"constraints":["Partial validity increases metadata complexity","Interactions among changed assumptions may be difficult to isolate"],"alternatives":["Invalidate entire decision","Keep decision unchanged","Record scoped partial drift"],"evidence":{"basis":["conditional applicability","context-specific architecture"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"full_invalidation":"Simple but may cause unnecessary replacement","unchanged":"Simple but can hide risk","scoped_drift":"Precise but more complex"},"decision":"Use scoped reassessment when evidence shows that only specific decision conditions have changed.","rationale":"Architecture decisions are often conditional rather than universally applicable.","consequences":["Decision status may include affected scopes","Replacement can be targeted"],"implementation_implications":["Record affected workloads, tenants, components, or environments","Avoid global replacement when local reassessment is sufficient"],"verification_method":"Test whether the decision still satisfies unaffected conditions after a scoped context change.","outcome_learning":"Learn which decision dimensions tend to drift independently.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B96-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B96-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B96-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Decision Drift","subtopic":"Architecture Debt","concept":"Unreviewed decision drift can become architecture debt","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How can decision drift become architecture debt?","answer":"When outdated decisions continue to constrain implementation or create recurring work after their original assumptions cease to hold, the resulting mismatch becomes architecture debt.","context":"Teams may preserve obsolete boundaries, technologies, or operational patterns because the original decision is treated as permanent.","objective":"Recognize stale decision constraints as a form of architectural debt.","requirements":["Decision-to-implementation relationship must be visible","Recurring cost or friction must be attributable where possible","Debt must be distinguishable from intentional constraints"],"constraints":["Causality may be difficult to prove","Some old decisions remain intentionally valid"],"alternatives":["Ignore stale decisions","Rewrite implementation without review","Assess and explicitly manage decision debt"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision drift","architecture debt","traceability"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"ignore":"No immediate work but increasing mismatch risk","rewrite":"May remove constraints without understanding rationale","assess":"More disciplined but requires analysis"},"decision":"Treat materially obsolete decision constraints as candidates for explicit architecture debt management.","rationale":"Old decision logic can impose real engineering cost even when the original rationale is no longer applicable.","consequences":["Architecture debt can be linked to decision records","Debt remediation can target obsolete reasoning"],"implementation_implications":["Record obsolete assumptions and replacement candidates","Preserve original rationale for historical traceability"],"verification_method":"Identify implementation costs attributable to obsolete decisions and validate the causal relationship.","outcome_learning":"Track recurring categories of decision debt to improve future lifecycle management.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B96-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B96-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B96-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Decision Drift","subtopic":"Drift Remediation","concept":"Decision drift should be corrected at the decision or implementation level depending on the source of mismatch","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should a team decide whether to change an architecture decision or its implementation?","answer":"Determine whether the original decision basis remains valid. If the decision remains valid but implementation diverged incorrectly, correct implementation; if the decision basis is invalidated, reassess or replace the decision.","context":"Not every architecture mismatch means the architecture decision was wrong.","objective":"Avoid replacing sound decisions when implementation is the actual problem.","requirements":["Original rationale must be reviewed","Implementation conformance must be assessed","Current evidence must be considered"],"constraints":["Causality can be ambiguous","Both decision and implementation may have changed"],"alternatives":["Change decision","Correct implementation","Reassess both"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision-to-implementation traceability","drift diagnosis"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"decision_change":"Can address changed context but may cause broad churn","implementation_fix":"Preserves valid architecture but may require refactoring","both":"Most complete but highest effort"},"decision":"Diagnose whether drift originates in context, decision validity, implementation conformance, or a combination before choosing remediation.","rationale":"Correct remediation depends on the source of the mismatch.","consequences":["Architecture review becomes diagnostic rather than purely documentary","Implementation and decision records must remain linked"],"implementation_implications":["Record conformance status","Separate decision revision from implementation correction"],"verification_method":"Trace observed mismatch back to assumptions, decision, and implementation before remediation.","outcome_learning":"Capture recurring drift causes and improve architecture controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B96-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B96-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B97-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Decision Retirement","subtopic":"Retirement Criteria","concept":"Architecture decisions should have explicit retirement conditions when their applicability is temporary or conditional","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should an architecture decision be retired?","answer":"Retire a decision when its scope no longer exists, its assumptions are permanently invalidated, a replacement decision supersedes it, or its architectural constraints have been removed.","context":"Retired decisions remain useful historical evidence but should not continue governing current implementation.","objective":"Separate historical knowledge from active architectural authority.","requirements":["Retirement reason must be recorded","Replacement relationship should be explicit where applicable","Historical record must remain immutable"],"constraints":["Retirement can be confused with deletion","Some decisions remain historically relevant"],"alternatives":["Delete obsolete ADR","Leave active forever","Retire with historical preservation"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision lifecycle","historical traceability"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"delete":"Low clutter but loses rationale","permanent_active":"Preserves history but creates ambiguity","retire":"Preserves history while clarifying current authority"},"decision":"Retire obsolete decisions while preserving their historical evidence and linking replacements where applicable.","rationale":"Historical architecture knowledge remains valuable even when no longer authoritative.","consequences":["Decision repository contains explicit lifecycle states","Retrieval must distinguish active from retired decisions"],"implementation_implications":["Add retirement state and effective scope","Link superseding decision when one exists"],"verification_method":"Confirm retired decisions cannot be mistaken for current architectural authority.","outcome_learning":"Review retirement reasons to identify recurring architecture evolution patterns.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B97-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B97-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B97-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Decision Retirement","subtopic":"Supersession","concept":"A replacement decision should explicitly supersede rather than silently overwrite a previous decision","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should a new architecture decision link to the decision it replaces?","answer":"Explicit supersession preserves the reasoning history, identifies what changed, and prevents loss of the earlier decision's context.","context":"Overwriting an ADR can make it impossible to understand why the architecture changed.","objective":"Maintain a traceable decision evolution chain.","requirements":["Previous decision must remain recoverable","Replacement rationale must be recorded","Effective scope must be clear"],"constraints":["Decision history can become large","Some replacements are partial rather than complete"],"alternatives":["Overwrite","Create independent decision","Explicit supersession"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision lineage","historical traceability"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"overwrite":"Simple but destructive","independent":"Preserves history but can create ambiguity","supersession":"Traceable but requires relationship management"},"decision":"Use explicit supersession relationships for decisions that replace earlier architectural authority.","rationale":"Architecture evolution is easier to understand when decision lineage is preserved.","consequences":["Decision graph becomes navigable","Historical and current authority can be distinguished"],"implementation_implications":["Store supersedes/superseded_by relationships","Define effective date or scope"],"verification_method":"Traverse the decision lineage and verify that current authority can be determined without deleting history.","outcome_learning":"Analyze supersession chains to identify recurring architecture change drivers.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B97-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B97-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B97-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Decision Retirement","subtopic":"Reversal","concept":"Reversing a decision should preserve the evidence that caused reversal","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What should be preserved when an architecture decision is reversed?","answer":"Preserve the original decision, reversal trigger, new evidence, alternatives reconsidered, and rationale for the reversal.","context":"A reversed decision can provide valuable learning about assumptions and evidence quality.","objective":"Turn decision reversal into reusable engineering knowledge.","requirements":["Original decision must remain immutable","Reversal evidence must be linked","Reason for reversal must be explicit"],"constraints":["Reversal may result from changed context rather than original error","Evidence can be incomplete"],"alternatives":["Delete old decision","Mark failed","Preserve and document reversal"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision learning","historical traceability"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"delete":"Low clutter but loses learning","failed_label":"Simple but may misclassify context change","preserve":"Higher value with additional metadata"},"decision":"Preserve reversed decisions and explicitly distinguish changed context from invalid original reasoning where possible.","rationale":"A reversal is an evidence event, not merely a documentation update.","consequences":["Decision history becomes a source of learning","Reversal analysis can improve future decisions"],"implementation_implications":["Capture reversal cause category","Link new evidence and replacement decision"],"verification_method":"Review reversal records and verify that a future engineer can reconstruct why the decision changed.","outcome_learning":"Classify reversals into assumption failure, context change, implementation issue, or new evidence.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B97-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B97-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B97-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Decision Retirement","subtopic":"Partial Retirement","concept":"Architecture decision scope can be retired partially when only some applicability conditions disappear","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should an architecture decision be retired when only part of its scope remains?","answer":"Narrow the active scope while preserving the decision for the conditions where it remains valid, and document the removed applicability conditions.","context":"A decision may apply to one subsystem or workload after being replaced elsewhere.","objective":"Avoid unnecessary global replacement of conditionally valid architecture knowledge.","requirements":["Remaining scope must be explicit","Removed scope must be recorded","Conflicting authority must be eliminated"],"constraints":["Scope management can become complex","Partial retirement may require multiple relationships"],"alternatives":["Retire globally","Keep globally active","Narrow scope"],"evidence":{"basis":["conditional applicability","decision scope"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"global":"Simple but may remove valid knowledge","global_active":"Simple but can create conflicts","narrowed":"Precise but requires scope metadata"},"decision":"Use scoped retirement when evidence supports continued validity under defined conditions.","rationale":"Architecture decisions may be valid for only part of a system's context.","consequences":["Decision authority becomes scope-aware","Retrieval must apply context filters"],"implementation_implications":["Record affected components/environments/workloads","Ensure superseding decisions cover removed scope"],"verification_method":"Check that each active scope has exactly one applicable architectural authority where required.","outcome_learning":"Track patterns of partial retirement to improve decision granularity.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B97-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B97-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B97-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Decision Retirement","subtopic":"Historical Authority","concept":"Retired architecture decisions remain evidence but should not be interpreted as current instructions","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should retired decisions be used by engineering systems?","answer":"Retired decisions should remain retrievable for historical reasoning, but current architecture retrieval should distinguish them from active decisions and avoid treating them as current authority unless explicitly requested.","context":"Knowledge systems may retrieve old decisions during debugging or architecture analysis.","objective":"Preserve historical learning without creating stale implementation guidance.","requirements":["Lifecycle state must be machine-readable","Historical retrieval must remain possible","Current-authority retrieval must filter retired decisions"],"constraints":["Some analyses require historical context","Legacy behavior may still depend on retired decisions"],"alternatives":["Delete retired records","Treat all records equally","Separate historical and active authority"],"evidence":{"basis":["knowledge lifecycle","retrieval correctness","decision authority"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"delete":"Simpler retrieval but loses history","equal":"Easy but risks stale guidance","separate":"More robust but requires lifecycle-aware retrieval"},"decision":"Maintain retired decisions as historical evidence with explicit lifecycle semantics.","rationale":"Historical context is valuable but must not silently override current architecture authority.","consequences":["Knowledge retrieval requires lifecycle awareness","Historical debugging remains supported"],"implementation_implications":["Filter by decision status for current implementation queries","Expose historical records when reasoning about evolution"],"verification_method":"Run current-architecture retrieval and verify retired decisions are not returned as authoritative without historical context.","outcome_learning":"Evaluate whether lifecycle-aware retrieval reduces stale guidance.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B97-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B97-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B98-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Rejection Rationale","concept":"Rejected alternatives preserve information about considered architectural trade-offs","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should rejected architecture alternatives be recorded?","answer":"Rejected alternatives preserve the option space, trade-offs considered, and reasons they were unsuitable under the decision's constraints.","context":"Future requirements may change and make a previously rejected alternative viable.","objective":"Preserve architectural reasoning beyond the selected option.","requirements":["Material alternatives should be identifiable","Rejection reason must be contextual","Evidence supporting rejection should be retained"],"constraints":["Recording every trivial alternative creates noise","Alternative applicability can change"],"alternatives":["Record selected option only","Record all alternatives","Record material alternatives"],"evidence":{"basis":["option-space preservation","decision traceability"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"selected_only":"Low overhead but loses reasoning","all":"Comprehensive but noisy","material_only":"Preserves useful reasoning with bounded documentation"},"decision":"Record material rejected alternatives and their context-specific rejection reasons.","rationale":"Rejected alternatives reduce future rediscovery cost and reveal trade-offs.","consequences":["ADRs become more useful for future reassessment","Decision records contain additional context"],"implementation_implications":["Capture alternative, evidence, rejection condition, and potential future trigger","Avoid generic statements such as 'too complex' without context"],"verification_method":"Review whether material alternatives can be understood without reconstructing the original discussion.","outcome_learning":"Identify rejected alternatives that 