{"record_id":"D54-B98-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Conditional Rejection","concept":"Alternative rejection should state the condition under which the alternative was unsuitable","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should an alternative's rejection reason be expressed?","answer":"State the specific requirement, constraint, risk, cost, capability gap, or evidence that made the alternative unsuitable under the decision context.","context":"An alternative may be rejected because it violates a particular constraint rather than because it is inherently poor.","objective":"Avoid turning contextual trade-offs into universal technology judgments.","requirements":["Rejection must be context-bound","Evidence should support material rejection claims","Changing conditions should be identifiable"],"constraints":["Evidence can become stale","Some rejection reasons are qualitative"],"alternatives":["Generic rejection","Contextual rejection","Numerical scoring only"],"evidence":{"basis":["contextual trade-offs","decision evidence"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"generic":"Concise but weakly reusable","contextual":"More informative","numeric_only":"Comparable but may hide reasoning"},"decision":"Express alternative rejection in terms of the conditions that made it unsuitable for the decision.","rationale":"Architecture choices are conditional rather than universal rankings.","consequences":["Future reviews can identify when rejection conditions no longer apply","Decision records avoid unjustified technology judgments"],"implementation_implications":["Store rejected condition and evidence","Define possible re-evaluation triggers"],"verification_method":"Change the relevant constraint and determine whether the recorded rejection logic correctly signals possible reconsideration.","outcome_learning":"Track which rejection conditions most often change over time.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B98-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B98-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B98-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Rediscovery Prevention","concept":"Recorded rejected alternatives reduce repeated architecture analysis when similar decisions recur","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How can rejected alternatives improve future architecture work?","answer":"Index rejected alternatives by decision context, constraints, evidence, and applicability so future decision makers can reuse prior analysis instead of restarting from an empty option space.","context":"Organizations repeatedly encounter similar architecture questions across systems and time.","objective":"Convert decision history into reusable engineering knowledge.","requirements":["Alternative records must be retrievable","Context must be represented","Applicability must be distinguishable"],"constraints":["Historical alternatives may become stale","Different systems may have materially different constraints"],"alternatives":["No reuse","Manual document search","Structured decision knowledge retrieval"],"evidence":{"basis":["knowledge reuse","decision history"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"none":"Maximum rediscovery cost","manual":"Some reuse but inconsistent","structured":"Higher setup cost with better retrieval potential"},"decision":"Represent material rejected alternatives as reusable, context-bound knowledge.","rationale":"Decision history has value when future retrieval can distinguish comparable contexts.","consequences":["Architecture knowledge systems can retrieve prior option analysis","Stale alternatives require lifecycle metadata"],"implementation_implications":["Index by problem, constraints, evidence, and rejection conditions","Apply version and lifecycle filtering"],"verification_method":"Issue a repeated architecture question and verify whether prior alternatives can be retrieved with relevant context.","outcome_learning":"Measure which historical alternatives are repeatedly reused or rediscovered.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B98-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B98-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B98-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Alternative Reconsideration","concept":"A rejected alternative should identify conditions that could make it viable later","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"When should a rejected alternative include reconsideration triggers?","answer":"Include reconsideration triggers when the rejection depends on mutable constraints, evolving technology, workload, cost, or requirements.","context":"An alternative rejected because of scale, cost, capability, or maturity may become viable after environmental change.","objective":"Make architecture evolution more efficient.","requirements":["Mutable rejection conditions should be identified","Triggers must be meaningful","Historical rejection must remain intact"],"constraints":["Not every alternative needs future monitoring","Trigger maintenance has cost"],"alternatives":["No trigger","Generic periodic review","Specific reconsideration triggers"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision evolution","conditional applicability"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"none":"Simple but loses proactive learning","periodic":"Broad but potentially inefficient","specific":"Efficient when well defined"},"decision":"Record reconsideration triggers when the rejection rationale depends on conditions likely to change materially.","rationale":"The future value of an alternative depends on knowing when its original rejection no longer applies.","consequences":["Architecture monitoring can include option-space changes","Rejected alternatives remain actionable historical knowledge"],"implementation_implications":["Link triggers to measurable or observable changes","Avoid maintaining triggers for permanently irrelevant options"],"verification_method":"Change a stated rejection condition and verify that the alternative becomes eligible for reassessment.","outcome_learning":"Record whether trigger-based reconsideration produces useful architecture changes.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B98-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B98-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B98-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Alternative Evidence","concept":"Rejected alternatives should retain evidence sufficient to distinguish evidence-based rejection from preference","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How can architecture records distinguish evidence-based rejection from stakeholder preference?","answer":"Record the explicit requirement or constraint, evidence, and trade-off that caused rejection, while separately identifying preferences or organizational policies that influenced the choice.","context":"Architecture decisions can be shaped by both technical evidence and legitimate organizational constraints.","objective":"Avoid misrepresenting preference as technical fact.","requirements":["Technical evidence and preference must be distinguishable","Organizational constraints should be explicit","Rejection rationale must be traceable"],"constraints":["Preferences can interact with technical constraints","Not all stakeholder considerations are measurable"],"alternatives":["Treat all reasons as technical","Separate evidence and preference","Ignore preference"],"evidence":{"basis":["evidence classification","stakeholder constraints"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"combined":"Simple but ambiguous","separated":"More transparent","ignore":"Technically narrow but incomplete"},"decision":"Represent technical evidence, organizational constraints, and preferences as distinct decision inputs.","rationale":"A legitimate constraint does not become an objective technical fact merely because it influenced the decision.","consequences":["Decision reasoning becomes more transparent","Future context changes can be assessed more accurately"],"implementation_implications":["Use typed evidence and constraint fields","Record stakeholder preference separately from measured behavior"],"verification_method":"Review rejection rationale and classify each reason according to its actual source.","outcome_learning":"Identify whether preference-driven decisions require additional technical validation.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B98-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B98-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B99-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Decision Dependencies","subtopic":"Dependency Graph","concept":"Architecture decisions can depend on assumptions or decisions made elsewhere","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should dependencies between architecture decisions be explicit?","answer":"Explicit dependencies reveal which decisions may become invalid when another decision, requirement, constraint, or shared assumption changes.","context":"A storage decision may depend on consistency requirements established by another domain decision.","objective":"Support impact analysis when architecture context changes.","requirements":["Decision dependencies must be identifiable","Dependency direction should be clear","Shared assumptions should be traceable"],"constraints":["Decision graphs can become complex","Not all conceptual relationships imply dependency"],"alternatives":["Independent ADRs","Manual cross-references","Structured dependency relationships"],"evidence":{"basis":["dependency analysis","architecture traceability"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"independent":"Simple but weak impact analysis","manual":"Usable but inconsistent","structured":"Better analysis with relationship maintenance"},"decision":"Represent material decision dependencies explicitly.","rationale":"Architecture evolution requires understanding downstream consequences of upstream changes.","consequences":["Decision changes can trigger impact analysis","Knowledge retrieval becomes graph-aware"],"implementation_implications":["Use typed relationships such as depends_on, constrains, supersedes, or conflicts_with","Avoid creating relationships merely for topical similarity"],"verification_method":"Change an upstream decision and verify that materially dependent decisions are discoverable.","outcome_learning":"Identify recurring dependency patterns to improve future decision modeling.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B99-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B99-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B99-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Decision Dependencies","subtopic":"Decision Conflicts","concept":"Conflicting architecture decisions require explicit conflict detection and resolution","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should conflicting architecture decisions be handled?","answer":"Identify the conflicting assumptions or authorities, determine scope and precedence, and create an explicit resolution or replacement decision rather than leaving contradictory active decisions unresolved.","context":"Different teams may make locally reasonable decisions that conflict at system boundaries.","objective":"Prevent contradictory architecture authority.","requirements":["Conflict must be explicit","Scope and authority must be assessed","Resolution must be traceable"],"constraints":["Conflicts can be legitimate under different scopes","Centralized resolution may be inappropriate for local decisions"],"alternatives":["Ignore conflict","Apply informal precedence","Explicit conflict resolution"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision consistency","scope and authority"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"ignore":"Low effort but high ambiguity","informal":"Fast but weak traceability","explicit":"More governance but clearer authority"},"decision":"Represent material decision conflicts explicitly and resolve them through scope, precedence, or supersession rules.","rationale":"Contradictory active authority can produce inconsistent implementations.","consequences":["Decision repositories require conflict states","Cross-team architecture coordination becomes more transparent"],"implementation_implications":["Define decision authority and scope","Record resolution rationale and affected decisions"],"verification_method":"Inject a known conflicting decision pair and verify that the conflict is detected and resolved explicitly.","outcome_learning":"Track recurring conflict sources such as overlapping ownership or unclear boundaries.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B99-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B99-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B99-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Decision Dependencies","subtopic":"Transitive Impact","concept":"A decision change can affect indirectly dependent decisions through shared assumptions","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"Why should architecture impact analysis consider transitive decision dependencies?","answer":"A decision can affect another decision indirectly through a shared constraint, interface, quality attribute, or intermediate decision, so impact analysis should traverse material dependency paths where justified.","context":"Changing a communication strategy may affect retry policy, workflow design, observability, and capacity decisions indirectly.","objective":"Avoid incomplete impact analysis.","requirements":["Dependency relationships must have semantic types","Traversal should distinguish material from incidental relationships","Impact scope must remain bounded"],"constraints":["Unbounded graph traversal creates noise","Indirect effects may require human validation"],"alternatives":["Direct dependencies only","Full graph traversal","Typed transitive analysis"],"evidence":{"basis":["dependency propagation","architecture graph reasoning"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"direct_only":"Simple but may miss effects","full":"Broad but noisy","typed":"More precise but requires relationship semantics"},"decision":"Use typed dependency traversal for material transitive impact analysis.","rationale":"Architecture effects can propagate through decision dependencies rather than only direct references.","consequences":["Decision analysis can identify second-order impacts","Relationship quality becomes important"],"implementation_implications":["Assign semantics to dependency edges","Stop traversal at scope or relevance boundaries"],"verification_method":"Modify an upstream decision and trace expected direct and indirect affected decisions.","outcome_learning":"Compare predicted dependency propagation with actual architecture changes.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B99-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B99-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B99-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Decision Dependencies","subtopic":"Shared Assumptions","concept":"Shared assumptions should be represented as first-class relationships when multiple decisions depend on them","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should shared architecture assumptions be modeled?","answer":"Represent a material shared assumption separately and link dependent decisions to it when doing so improves change impact analysis and prevents inconsistent updates.","context":"Multiple decisions may depend on the same latency, availability, regulatory, capacity, or data-retention assumption.","objective":"Avoid updating one decision while leaving other dependent decisions inconsistent.","requirements":["Shared assumption must be identifiable","Dependent decisions must be linked","Assumption changes must be reviewable"],"constraints":["Too many shared abstractions create unnecessary graph complexity","Assumptions may be difficult to measure"],"alternatives":["Duplicate assumption text","Central shared assumption","Typed dependency relationship"],"evidence":{"basis":["shared constraint management","traceability"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"duplicate":"Simple but prone to divergence","central":"Consistent but adds abstraction","typed_relationship":"Flexible but requires graph maintenance"},"decision":"Model materially shared assumptions explicitly when their change could affect multiple decisions.","rationale":"Shared decision inputs are potential consistency failure points.","consequences":["Assumption changes can trigger multiple decision reviews","Decision graph becomes more expressive"],"implementation_implications":["Give assumptions stable identifiers","Link decisions to assumption versions or states"],"verification_method":"Change a shared assumption and verify that all dependent decisions become discoverable for review.","outcome_learning":"Identify assumptions that create disproportionate decision coupling.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B99-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B99-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B99-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Decision Dependencies","subtopic":"Boundary Ownership","concept":"Decision dependency should not be confused with ownership dependency","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"Should a team owning an implementation automatically own dependent architecture decisions?","answer":"No. Decision dependency describes reasoning or constraint relationships, while decision authority should follow explicit architectural scope and governance responsibilities.","context":"A platform team may own infrastructure while application teams own decisions about business-facing behavior.","objective":"Prevent organizational ownership from being inferred incorrectly from technical dependency.","requirements":["Decision authority must be explicit","Technical dependencies and organizational ownership must be separate concepts"],"constraints":["Organizations often align ownership with technical boundaries","Responsibility may be distributed"],"alternatives":["Infer authority from dependency","Assign central authority","Explicit ownership and dependency metadata"],"evidence":{"basis":["architecture governance","responsibility separation"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"inferred":"Simple but ambiguous","central":"Consistent but potentially over-centralized","explicit":"Clearer but requires governance metadata"},"decision":"Represent decision dependency and decision authority as separate attributes.","rationale":"Technical dependency does not logically establish governance authority.","consequences":["Architecture graphs become more accurate","Cross-team decisions require explicit coordination"],"implementation_implications":["Store owner/authority separately from dependency edges","Define escalation path for conflicts"],"verification_method":"Review cross-team decisions and verify that authority is not inferred solely from technical dependency.","outcome_learning":"Identify ownership ambiguities that repeatedly cause architecture conflicts.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B99-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B99-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B100-R01","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Architecture Decision Closure","subtopic":"Lifecycle Completion","concept":"An architecture decision should reach an explicit lifecycle state rather than remain indefinitely ambiguous","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What constitutes closure for an architecture decision?","answer":"Decision closure should identify the chosen state, implementation implications, verification status, active scope, unresolved risks, review triggers, and lifecycle authority.","context":"An architecture decision is not complete merely because a choice has been documented.","objective":"Create a complete decision lifecycle from choice through evidence and governance.","requirements":["Decision state must be explicit","Implementation and verification status must be distinct","Unresolved risks must remain visible","Review conditions must be defined"],"constraints":["Some decisions remain conditionally open","Verification may occur after implementation"],"alternatives":["Close at selection","Close after implementation","Close only after defined verification state"],"evidence":{"basis":["decision lifecycle","verification separation","governance"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"selection_only":"Fast but incomplete","implementation":"Better but may confuse implementation with verification","defined_verification":"More complete but requires lifecycle discipline"},"decision":"Use explicit lifecycle states that distinguish selected, implemented, verified, active, reassessment, superseded, and retired conditions as applicable.","rationale":"Decision authority and evidence evolve after the initial choice.","consequences":["Architecture repositories need lifecycle semantics","Retrieval must understand decision state"],"implementation_implications":["Define state transitions","Prevent verified status from being inferred solely from implementation completion"],"verification_method":"Audit decision lifecycle transitions and verify that each state has explicit evidence requirements.","outcome_learning":"Identify lifecycle states that repeatedly cause ambiguity or missing evidence.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B100-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B100-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B100-R02","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Architecture Decision Closure","subtopic":"Implementation Traceability","concept":"Architecture decisions should be traceable to implementation constraints and verification artifacts","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should an architecture decision remain connected to implementation?","answer":"Link the decision to affected components, interfaces, configuration constraints, tests, verification artifacts, and relevant operational signals without claiming that linkage itself proves implementation correctness.","context":"Architecture documentation can become disconnected from actual implementation after the initial design phase.","objective":"Maintain a usable bridge between architecture reasoning and engineering execution.","requirements":["Affected implementation scope must be identifiable","Verification artifacts must be separately represented","Implementation status must not imply verification status"],"constraints":["Large systems can have many implementation artifacts","Traceability links require maintenance"],"alternatives":["Document only","Manual references","Structured implementation traceability"],"evidence":{"basis":["architecture-to-implementation traceability","verification separation"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"document_only":"Low cost but weak operational value","manual":"Useful but inconsistent","structured":"Higher maintenance but stronger reasoning support"},"decision":"Maintain structured traceability between material architecture decisions and implementation/verification artifacts.","rationale":"Architecture knowledge becomes more useful when it can guide and inspect implementation without conflating states.","consequences":["Implementation changes can reveal affected decisions","Architecture verification becomes more connected to engineering workflows"],"implementation_implications":["Link decisions to components, interfaces, tests, and verification evidence","Track stale links as architecture maintenance signals"],"verification_method":"Select an architecture decision and verify that its implementation and verification scope can be reconstructed.","outcome_learning":"Measure which traceability links provide useful debugging or review value.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B100-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D54-B100-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B100-R03","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Architecture Decision Closure","subtopic":"Outcome Measurement","concept":"Architecture decisions should define how their intended outcomes will be evaluated after adoption","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"Why should an architecture decision define post-implementation outcome measurement?","answer":"Outcome measurement determines whether the architecture delivered the qualities and constraints that motivated the decision and provides evidence for future reassessment.","context":"A decision can be implemented correctly yet fail to produce the expected system-level outcome.","objective":"Close the reasoning loop between decision, implementation, and observed outcome.","requirements":["Expected outcomes must be explicit","Measurement method must be appropriate to the outcome","Observed result must remain separate from expectation"],"constraints":["Some outcomes are difficult to isolate causally","Measurement may require production evidence"],"alternatives":["No post-implementation measurement","One-time validation","Ongoing outcome measurement"],"evidence":{"basis":["outcome verification","architecture learning"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"none":"Low cost but no outcome learning","one_time":"Moderate evidence","ongoing":"Higher insight but additional operational cost"},"decision":"Define post-implementation outcome evaluation for material architecture decisions, with duration proportional to the expected behavior and risk.","rationale":"Architecture reasoning is incomplete without checking whether intended outcomes materialized.","consequences":["Architecture decisions can produce measurable learning","Some decisions require operational observation after deployment"],"implementation_implications":["Define expected outcome, metric or evidence source, observation window, and interpretation rules","Avoid claiming causality from correlation alone"],"verification_method":"Compare observed post-implementation evidence with documented expected outcomes and assumptions.","outcome_learning":"Record whether the decision met, partially met, or failed its intended outcomes and why.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B100-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D54-B100-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B100-R04","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Architecture Decision Closure","subtopic":"Learning Capture","concept":"Architecture decision outcomes should be converted into reusable evidence and learning without rewriting historical reasoning","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"How should lessons from completed architecture decisions be captured?","answer":"Preserve the original decision and append verified outcome evidence, assumption results, unexpected effects, and reusable lessons as separate lifecycle information.","context":"Changing the original ADR to match the final outcome destroys the distinction between prediction and observation.","objective":"Enable learning while preserving historical decision integrity.","requirements":["Original decision must remain recoverable","Observed outcomes must be separated from original expectations","Lessons must have provenance"],"constraints":["Outcome causality may remain uncertain","Lessons can become overgeneralized"],"alternatives":["Rewrite original rationale","Add outcome section","Create separate learning records linked to decision"],"evidence":{"basis":["historical integrity","experience learning","provenance"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"rewrite":"Simple but destroys prediction history","append":"Good traceability","separate_learning":"Strong reuse but more data modeling"},"decision":"Preserve original decision reasoning and record verified outcomes and lessons as distinct evidence linked to the decision.","rationale":"Learning requires knowing what was predicted, what occurred, and what was inferred afterward.","consequences":["Decision knowledge becomes longitudinal","Future reasoning can compare prediction with outcome"],"implementation_implications":["Use separate prediction and observed-outcome fields","Record evidence status and causal confidence"],"verification_method":"Audit completed decisions and verify that predicted and observed states remain distinguishable.","outcome_learning":"Use verified outcome differences to refine future architecture heuristics without converting local observations into universal rules.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":["D54-B100-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D54-B100-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D54-B100-R05","dataset_id":"D54","dataset_version":"D54-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Architecture Decision Closure","subtopic":"Final Quality Gate","concept":"A decision dataset should not be considered complete until decision reasoning, implementation implications, verification, lifecycle, and learning are represented","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_DECISION","question":"What final quality gate should be applied to an architecture decision record?","answer":"Verify that the record identifies context, requirements, constraints, alternatives, evidence, trade-offs, decision, rationale, consequences, implementation implications, verification method, lifecycle state, and outcome-learning path, with unsupported execution claims excluded.","context":"Architecture decision records are intended to support future reasoning rather than merely document historical choices.","objective":"Ensure decision records are complete, truthful, reusable, and implementation-aware.","requirements":["All material decision dimensions must be represented","Evidence provenance must be explicit","Execution and verification status must remain truthful","Duplicates and superficial variants must be removed"],"constraints":["Not every field is equally applicable to every decision","Evidence may remain inferential","Some decisions require later operational validation"],"alternatives":["Minimal ADR","Narrative ADR","Structured evidence-driven ADR"],"evidence":{"basis":["D54 architecture-decision scope","implementation-aware knowledge methodology","truthful validation model"],"status":"INFERENTIAL"},"tradeoffs":{"minimal":"Low authoring cost but limited reasoning reuse","narrative":"Readable but harder to machine process","structured":"Higher authoring discipline with stronger retrieval, reasoning, and validation utility"},"decision":"Use structured, evidence-driven, lifecycle-aware architecture decision records as the final D54 quality standard.","rationale":"The Knowledge Fabric requires architecture decisions that can support retrieval, reasoning, implementation planning, debugging, verification, and future learning without fabricating evidence.","consequences":["D54 records become directly reusable by downstream reasoning systems","Quality validation must include schema completeness, semantic distinctness, provenance, and lifecycle truthfulness"],"implementation_implications":["Validate every record against D54 required fields","Run exact, near-duplicate, semantic, and implementation-level deduplication","Verify relationship integrity and stable identifiers","Keep NOT_EXECUTED distinct from VERIFIED or EXECUTED states"],"verification_method":"Run schema validation, required-field coverage, duplicate detection, relationship integrity checks, provenance checks, contradiction review, and implementation-coverage audit before locking D54.","outcome_learning":"After downstream use, record retrieval failures, missing decision dimensions, incorrect assumptions, and validated outcomes as separate learning evidence rather than silently rewriting historical records.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D54 Architecture Decisions scope and master quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D54-B100-R01","D54-B100-R02","D54-B100-R03","D54-B100-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"consolidates","record_id":"D54-B100-R01"},{"type":"consolidates","record_id":"D54-B100-R02"},{"type":"consolidates","record_id":"D54-B100-R03"},{"type":"consolidates","record_id":"D54-B100-R04"}]}

{"record_id":"D55-B01-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Alternative Identification","concept":"Rejected alternatives must preserve the candidate that was genuinely considered","knowledge_type":"REJECTED_ALTERNATIVE","question":"What should qualify as a rejected alternative?","answer":"A rejected alternative is a technically plausible option that was sufficiently considered against the decision context but was not selected because documented requirements, constraints, evidence, trade-offs, or risks favored another option.","context":"Architecture and engineering decisions frequently consider multiple technically viable approaches before selecting one.","objective":"Preserve decision reasoning rather than recording only the selected solution.","requirements":["Candidate must be technically relevant to the decision","Consideration must be meaningful enough to support comparison","Rejection must have an identifiable reason"],"constraints":["Do not record arbitrary or never-considered ideas as rejected alternatives","Do not infer rejection reasons without evidence"],"alternatives":["Record only the selected option","Record materially considered rejected alternatives"],"evidence":["Decision context","Alternative comparison","Documented constraints","Evaluation evidence"],"tradeoffs":{"traceability":"Higher documentation effort","future_reuse":"Preserves reasoning that may become valuable when conditions change"},"decision":"Record materially considered rejected alternatives together with their rejection rationale.","rationale":"A decision without its meaningful alternatives loses information about the search space and makes future reassessment harder.","consequences":["Future engineers can understand what was considered","Repeated evaluation of previously rejected options can be avoided when conditions have not changed","Historical reasoning becomes queryable"],"implementation_implications":["Store candidate identity separately from rejection reason","Associate evidence and decision context with the candidate","Preserve timestamps or decision versions when available"],"verification_method":"Check that every rejected-alternative record identifies a real candidate, a decision context, and an evidence-backed rejection reason.","outcome_learning":"If future decisions repeatedly reconsider the same alternative, evaluate whether the original rejection rationale was insufficiently discoverable or the underlying conditions changed.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B01-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"defines_boundary_for","record_id":"D55-B01-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B01-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Alternative Identification","concept":"Rejected alternatives differ from hypothetical options","knowledge_type":"REJECTED_ALTERNATIVE","question":"Why should hypothetical alternatives be separated from rejected alternatives?","answer":"A hypothetical option was merely possible, whereas a rejected alternative was actually considered in the decision process. Treating every conceivable option as rejected creates false historical evidence and weakens decision traceability.","context":"Architecture records may list many theoretically possible designs even when only a subset received serious evaluation.","objective":"Prevent the knowledge base from confusing explored alternatives with unexamined possibilities.","requirements":["Alternative status must distinguish considered from hypothetical","Evidence must support claimed consideration"],"constraints":["Do not convert brainstorming ideas into historical decisions without evidence"],"alternatives":["Mark all possible options as rejected","Separate considered alternatives from hypothetical candidates"],"evidence":["Decision records","Design reviews","Evaluation artifacts","Experiment results"],"tradeoffs":{"completeness":"More conservative historical representation","recall":"Some informal alternatives may be omitted if evidence is unavailable"},"decision":"Only alternatives supported as materially considered should receive rejected-alternative status.","rationale":"Historical engineering knowledge must distinguish evidence from reconstruction or speculation.","consequences":["Lower risk of fabricated decision history","Clearer retrieval semantics","Potential loss of undocumented informal discussion"],"implementation_implications":["Use an alternative_status field","Allow statuses such as CONSIDERED_REJECTED, HYPOTHETICAL, UNKNOWN","Require provenance for historical claims"],"verification_method":"Audit rejected alternatives and verify that each has evidence of actual consideration.","outcome_learning":"If undocumented alternatives repeatedly prove important, improve decision-capture practices rather than retroactively inventing historical evidence.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B01-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D55-B01-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B01-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Candidate Scope","concept":"Alternative identity must be specific enough for later retrieval","knowledge_type":"REJECTED_ALTERNATIVE","question":"How specific should a rejected alternative be?","answer":"The alternative should identify the actual architectural, technological, process, or implementation choice under consideration rather than using vague labels such as 'another database' or 'different architecture'.","context":"Generic labels make historical decisions difficult to retrieve or compare.","objective":"Make rejected alternatives actionable knowledge rather than vague historical notes.","requirements":["Candidate identity must identify the relevant design choice","Scope must be distinguishable from competing alternatives"],"constraints":["Avoid unnecessary implementation detail when it was not part of the decision"],"alternatives":["Generic candidate label","Context-specific candidate description"],"evidence":["Architecture proposal","Technology evaluation","Design comparison"],"tradeoffs":{"specificity":"Improves retrieval and reasoning","maintenance":"More precise records require more structured capture"},"decision":"Represent the candidate at the same decision granularity as the selected alternative.","rationale":"Alternatives can only be compared meaningfully when they operate at comparable decision boundaries.","consequences":["Better retrieval","Reduced ambiguity","Clearer comparison across decisions"],"implementation_implications":["Capture candidate name/type/scope","Link candidate to the decision boundary","Avoid mixing system-level and component-level alternatives in one record"],"verification_method":"Check whether an independent engineer can identify what was actually being rejected from the record alone.","outcome_learning":"If users repeatedly misinterpret candidate scope, refine the candidate representation or decision boundary.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B01-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Decision Boundary","concept":"A rejected alternative requires an explicit decision boundary","knowledge_type":"REJECTED_ALTERNATIVE","question":"Why must the decision boundary be recorded with a rejected alternative?","answer":"Without a decision boundary, it is unclear which choice was rejected, what requirements applied, and whether the same alternative remains rejected under a different context.","context":"The same technology or architecture can be appropriate for one component and inappropriate for another.","objective":"Prevent context-free reuse of historical rejection decisions.","requirements":["Decision scope must be identifiable","Applicable requirements and constraints must be associated"],"constraints":["Historical rejection must not be generalized beyond its original boundary"],"alternatives":["Store rejection globally","Bind rejection to its original decision context"],"evidence":["System scope","Requirements","Constraints","Decision record"],"tradeoffs":{"reuse":"Context binding reduces misleading reuse","storage":"Requires richer relationships"},"decision":"Bind every rejected alternative to a defined decision boundary and context.","rationale":"Rejection is conditional on the problem being solved, not an intrinsic property of the alternative.","consequences":["Historical decisions become safer to reuse","Context changes can trigger reassessment"],"implementation_implications":["Store decision_id or decision_scope","Associate requirements and constraints","Permit multiple records for the same candidate under different contexts"],"verification_method":"Attempt to apply the rejection to a materially different context and confirm that the model can identify the contextual limitation.","outcome_learning":"If the same candidate receives different decisions under different contexts, retain both as context-specific evidence rather than treating them as contradictions.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B01-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B01-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B01-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B01","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Context Dependence","concept":"The same candidate may be rejected in one context and selected in another","knowledge_type":"REJECTED_ALTERNATIVE","question":"Should rejection of an alternative be treated as globally valid?","answer":"No. Rejection should normally be interpreted as conditional on the requirements, constraints, evidence, scale, risk tolerance, and operating context that existed at the time of the decision.","context":"Engineering alternatives can change suitability when workload, requirements, technology maturity, or constraints change.","objective":"Prevent historical rejection records from becoming permanent prohibitions.","requirements":["Context must be preserved","Conditions for validity must remain explicit"],"constraints":["Do not infer that a historical rejection applies universally"],"alternatives":["Permanent rejection flag","Contextual rejection with reassessment conditions"],"evidence":["Original decision context","Changed requirements","New evidence"],"tradeoffs":{"simplicity":"Permanent flags are simpler","correctness":"Contextual decisions preserve conditional validity"},"decision":"Treat rejection as context-bound unless explicit evidence establishes a broader constraint.","rationale":"Architecture suitability is generally a relationship between an option and a problem context.","consequences":["Previously rejected alternatives may legitimately be reconsidered","Decision knowledge remains temporally and contextually bounded"],"implementation_implications":["Represent applicability conditions","Capture reassessment triggers","Do not expose rejection as an unconditional recommendation"],"verification_method":"Check whether the record states the conditions under which the rejection was made.","outcome_learning":"When conditions change materially, compare the original rejection evidence against current requirements before reusing it.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B01-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B01-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B02-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Rejection Reasons","concept":"Requirements mismatch is a valid rejection reason","knowledge_type":"REJECTION_REASON","question":"When should an alternative be rejected because of requirements mismatch?","answer":"An alternative should be recorded as rejected for requirements mismatch when credible evidence shows that it cannot satisfy one or more mandatory requirements within the applicable constraints.","context":"Architecture alternatives may differ in their ability to satisfy mandatory functional or non-functional requirements.","objective":"Capture requirement-driven rejection explicitly.","requirements":["Requirement must be mandatory or decision-critical","Evidence must connect the alternative to the unmet requirement"],"constraints":["Do not treat preferences as mandatory requirements without evidence"],"alternatives":["Modify the requirement","Reject the alternative","Modify the alternative"],"evidence":["Requirements specification","Feasibility analysis","Evaluation results"],"tradeoffs":{"requirement_change":"May preserve the alternative but changes system obligations","rejection":"Preserves requirements but removes an option"},"decision":"Use requirements mismatch as the rejection reason when the unmet requirement is genuinely binding.","rationale":"A technically attractive alternative is not viable if it violates a mandatory requirement.","consequences":["Requirement traceability improves","Future requirement changes can reopen the alternative"],"implementation_implications":["Link rejected alternative to specific requirement IDs","Record whether the requirement was mandatory","Capture any assumptions behind the assessment"],"verification_method":"Verify that the rejected alternative can be mapped to the specific unmet requirement and supporting evidence.","outcome_learning":"If the requirement later changes, reassess the alternative instead of automatically retaining the rejection.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B02-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"contrasts_with","record_id":"D55-B02-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B02-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Rejection Reasons","concept":"Constraint violation differs from capability limitation","knowledge_type":"REJECTION_REASON","question":"How does a constraint-based rejection differ from a capability-based rejection?","answer":"A capability limitation means the alternative cannot provide required behavior or properties, while a constraint violation means it may technically provide them but conflicts with binding limits such as deployment restrictions, organizational policies, budget, regulatory requirements, or platform boundaries.","context":"Architecture evaluation often mixes intrinsic technical limitations with external constraints.","objective":"Preserve the causal distinction behind rejection.","requirements":["Rejection reason must identify whether the problem is capability or constraint","Constraint must be explicit"],"constraints":["Do not classify organizational preference as a hard constraint without evidence"],"alternatives":["Capability limitation","Constraint violation","Both"],"evidence":["Technical evaluation","Policy","Platform restrictions","Resource constraints"],"tradeoffs":{"classification":"More precise reasoning","capture_effort":"Requires additional metadata"},"decision":"Represent capability limitations and constraint violations as distinct rejection reasons.","rationale":"A constraint may disappear without changing the alternative's technical capabilities.","consequences":["Future reassessment becomes more precise","Historical reasoning becomes easier to analyze"],"implementation_implications":["Use structured rejection_reason categories","Permit multiple causal reasons","Link each reason to supporting evidence"],"verification_method":"Check whether removing the stated constraint would change the technical feasibility assessment.","outcome_learning":"If the alternative becomes viable after a constraint changes, classify the original rejection as conditionally obsolete rather than technically disproven.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B02-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"differentiates","record_id":"D55-B02-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B02-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Rejection Reasons","concept":"Risk dominance can justify rejection without proving technical impossibility","knowledge_type":"REJECTION_REASON","question":"Can an alternative be rejected even when it is technically feasible?","answer":"Yes. An alternative can be technically feasible yet rejected because its security, reliability, operational, financial, migration, or other material risks are unacceptable relative to the decision context.","context":"Architecture selection is not limited to feasibility; risk exposure can materially affect suitability.","objective":"Capture risk-based rejection without misrepresenting feasibility.","requirements":["Risk must be material to the decision","Risk assessment must be distinguishable from technical impossibility"],"constraints":["Do not claim an alternative is impossible when it is merely considered too risky"],"alternatives":["Reject on risk","Mitigate risk","Accept risk"],"evidence":["Risk analysis","Threat assessment","Reliability analysis","Operational constraints"],"tradeoffs":{"risk_reduction":"May require selecting a less attractive but lower-risk option","capability":"The rejected option may still provide valuable technical capabilities"},"decision":"Record risk as a separate rejection rationale when feasibility exists but acceptable risk cannot be established.","rationale":"Feasibility and acceptability are different decision dimensions.","consequences":["Decision records become more accurate","Risk changes can trigger reassessment"],"implementation_implications":["Store risk category and severity","Record assumptions and mitigation options","Avoid collapsing risk into capability failure"],"verification_method":"Verify that the evidence demonstrates material risk rather than impossibility.","outcome_learning":"If mitigations become available, reassess whether the original risk-based rejection remains valid.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B02-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"precedes","record_id":"D55-B02-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B02-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Rejection Reasons","concept":"Total cost and lifecycle burden can be rejection factors","knowledge_type":"REJECTION_REASON","question":"When is lifecycle cost a valid reason to reject an alternative?","answer":"Lifecycle cost is a valid rejection factor when acquisition, implementation, operation, staffing, maintenance, migration, or retirement costs materially conflict with the decision's economic constraints or value objectives.","context":"An option with acceptable technical properties may impose unsustainable lifecycle burden.","objective":"Preserve economic reasoning without reducing architecture decisions to initial purchase price.","requirements":["Cost scope must be defined","Lifecycle assumptions must be explicit"],"constraints":["Avoid unsupported numerical cost claims","Do not treat short-term cost as total lifecycle cost"],"alternatives":["Select lower lifecycle burden","Accept higher cost for materially greater value","Reduce scope"],"evidence":["Cost model","Staffing assumptions","Operational requirements","Migration estimates"],"tradeoffs":{"cost":"Lower cost may involve capability or flexibility trade-offs","lifecycle":"Higher initial investment can sometimes reduce recurring burden"},"decision":"Use lifecycle cost as a rejection reason when the assessed burden conflicts materially with the decision constraints or value case.","rationale":"Architecture creates long-lived operational and maintenance obligations beyond initial implementation.","consequences":["Economic assumptions become traceable","Future cost changes can justify reassessment"],"implementation_implications":["Separate one-time and recurring costs","Record major assumptions","Track changes affecting lifecycle economics"],"verification_method":"Review whether the rejection depends on explicit lifecycle assumptions rather than unsupported cost assertions.","outcome_learning":"Compare actual lifecycle burden with the assumptions used in the decision when operational evidence becomes available.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B02-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B02-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B02-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B02","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Rejection Reasons","concept":"Operational complexity can independently justify rejection","knowledge_type":"REJECTION_REASON","question":"Why should operational complexity be represented separately from implementation complexity?","answer":"An alternative may be straightforward to implement but difficult to operate reliably, observe, recover, secure, or staff. Operational burden therefore represents a distinct rejection dimension.","context":"Architecture evaluation must account for the long-term operation of the resulting system.","objective":"Prevent implementation effort from masking operational cost and risk.","requirements":["Operational burden must be evaluated separately when material","Relevant operational activities must be identifiable"],"constraints":["Do not label an option operationally complex without identifying the burden"],"alternatives":["Accept operational burden","Select a simpler operational model","Automate or reduce operational responsibilities"],"evidence":["Runbooks","Operational dependencies","Support model","Observability requirements"],"tradeoffs":{"simplicity":"Lower operational burden may reduce flexibility","capability":"Complex options may provide additional capabilities"},"decision":"Treat material operational complexity as an independent rejection dimension.","rationale":"A solution must remain viable after deployment, not only during implementation.","consequences":["Operational requirements influence architecture selection","Future platform improvements may reopen rejected alternatives"],"implementation_implications":["Capture operational dependencies","Record support and observability implications","Associate rejection with concrete operational constraints"],"verification_method":"Check whether the rejection identifies operational tasks, dependencies, or failure-handling burdens.","outcome_learning":"Measure actual operational effort after deployment and compare it with the assumptions that caused the alternative to be rejected.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B02-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B02-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B03-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Evidence","subtopic":"Evidence Requirements","concept":"A rejection rationale should distinguish evidence from assumption","knowledge_type":"EVIDENCE_CLASSIFICATION","question":"Why must assumptions be distinguished from evidence in rejected-alternative records?","answer":"An assumption describes what the decision makers believed or expected, while evidence provides support for the claim. Mixing them makes later reassessment unable to determine which parts of the rejection were empirically supported.","context":"Architecture decisions often occur before complete operational evidence exists.","objective":"Preserve epistemic quality of rejection reasoning.","requirements":["Claims must identify supporting evidence or assumptions","Unverified assumptions must not be represented as established facts"],"constraints":["Evidence may be incomplete at decision time","Absence of evidence must not be converted into evidence of failure"],"alternatives":["Store conclusions only","Store claims with evidence and assumption status"],"evidence":["Measured results","Documentation","Experiments","Validated constraints","Explicit assumptions"],"tradeoffs":{"traceability":"Improves reassessment","capture_effort":"Requires evidence classification"},"decision":"Separate evidence, assumption, inference, and uncertainty in rejection records.","rationale":"Decision quality depends partly on the reliability of the information supporting the rejection.","consequences":["Weak assumptions can be identified later","Reassessment becomes targeted"],"implementation_implications":["Add evidence_type and evidence_status","Preserve source references","Record uncertainty where material"],"verification_method":"Review rejection records and classify every major causal claim as evidence, assumption, inference, or unknown.","outcome_learning":"When operational evidence becomes available, update the status of assumptions without rewriting the original decision history.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B03-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B03-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B03-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Evidence","subtopic":"Evidence Strength","concept":"Evidence strength should affect confidence in rejection rationale","knowledge_type":"EVIDENCE_CLASSIFICATION","question":"How should evidence strength affect a rejected-alternative record?","answer":"The record should preserve the strength and limitations of the evidence supporting rejection. Strong measured evidence supports higher confidence, while assumptions, limited experiments, or indirect evidence should result in appropriately qualified confidence.","context":"Not all architecture evidence has equal reliability or coverage.","objective":"Prevent weak evidence from appearing equivalent to validated evidence.","requirements":["Evidence source and limitations must be preserved","Confidence must reflect evidence quality"],"constraints":["Confidence must not be fabricated from record completeness"],"alternatives":["Uniform confidence","Evidence-sensitive confidence"],"evidence":["Benchmark results","Production observations","Controlled experiments","Expert analysis"],"tradeoffs":{"precision":"Evidence-sensitive confidence improves reasoning","complexity":"Requires explicit confidence methodology"},"decision":"Tie confidence to evidence quality, relevance, coverage, and uncertainty.","rationale":"A rejection supported by weak evidence should remain easier to challenge than one supported by strong evidence.","consequences":["Search and reassessment can prioritize uncertain decisions","Historical reasoning becomes more transparent"],"implementation_implications":["Store evidence_level separately from confidence","Record evidence limitations","Allow confidence updates without changing historical rationale"],"verification_method":"Compare confidence with the quality and directness of the supporting evidence.","outcome_learning":"Downgrade or upgrade confidence when stronger evidence confirms or contradicts the original assumptions.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B03-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D55-B03-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B03-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Evidence","subtopic":"Comparative Evidence","concept":"Comparative evidence is more useful when rejection depends on relative trade-offs","knowledge_type":"EVIDENCE_CLASSIFICATION","question":"When should evidence compare a rejected alternative against the selected option?","answer":"Comparative evidence is appropriate when the rejection depends on relative performance, risk, cost, complexity, reliability, or another property rather than an absolute failure.","context":"Many architecture decisions select one viable option over another rather than rejecting an option as impossible.","objective":"Capture the actual basis of comparative rejection.","requirements":["Comparison dimensions must be relevant","Compared options must share the same evaluation context"],"constraints":["Avoid incomparable measurements","Do not infer superiority from unrelated metrics"],"alternatives":["Absolute feasibility evidence","Comparative evaluation"],"evidence":["Common benchmark","Common requirements","Comparable risk analysis","Common operational assumptions"],"tradeoffs":{"comparability":"Improves decision relevance","measurement_cost":"Controlled comparisons can require additional work"},"decision":"Use comparative evidence when rejection is based on relative suitability among viable alternatives.","rationale":"The reason for rejection may be that another option better satisfies the same objectives, not that the rejected option fails independently.","consequences":["Decision rationale becomes more faithful","Future context changes can alter the comparison"],"implementation_implications":["Record comparison dimensions","Preserve measurement conditions","Avoid copying isolated metrics without context"],"verification_method":"Verify that comparative evidence was generated under materially comparable assumptions and evaluation criteria.","outcome_learning":"Re-run comparisons when workload, requirements, or system constraints materially change.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B03-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B03-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B03-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Evidence","subtopic":"Evidence Limitations","concept":"Missing evidence is not equivalent to negative evidence","knowledge_type":"EVIDENCE_CLASSIFICATION","question":"How should lack of evidence affect rejection of an alternative?","answer":"Lack of evidence should normally be represented as uncertainty or insufficient validation rather than as proof that the alternative is unsuitable. The distinction matters because future evidence may change the decision.","context":"Architecture choices are often made under time, resource, or information constraints.","objective":"Prevent unsupported rejection claims.","requirements":["Evidence absence must be explicitly represented","Rejection confidence must reflect uncertainty"],"constraints":["Do not convert untested behavior into failure without evidence"],"alternatives":["Reject as unsuitable","Record insufficient evidence","Run additional validation"],"evidence":["Available evaluation results","Known information gaps","Experiment feasibility"],"tradeoffs":{"speed":"Deciding with incomplete evidence may accelerate delivery","uncertainty":"More validation may reduce uncertainty at additional cost"},"decision":"Represent insufficient evidence as uncertainty unless available evidence independently supports rejection.","rationale":"An untested alternative and a disproven alternative carry different knowledge states.","consequences":["Future experiments can target evidence gaps","False certainty is reduced"],"implementation_implications":["Capture evidence_status","Identify unresolved questions","Record whether rejection was provisional"],"verification_method":"Check whether rejection claims are supported by positive evidence rather than merely absence of testing.","outcome_learning":"Reconsider provisional rejections when the missing evidence becomes available.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B03-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B03-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B03-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B03","topic":"Evidence","subtopic":"Evidence Gaps","concept":"Evidence gaps should produce explicit reassessment conditions","knowledge_type":"EVIDENCE_CLASSIFICATION","question":"What should happen when a rejected alternative lacks decisive evidence?","answer":"The record should identify the missing evidence, explain why a decision was still made, and define what future evidence would be sufficient to reassess the alternative.","context":"Some decisions must be made before uncertainty can be fully eliminated.","objective":"Convert unresolved uncertainty into actionable future knowledge.","requirements":["Evidence gap must be explicit","Reassessment evidence must be identifiable"],"constraints":["Do not invent future outcomes","Do not treat a future validation plan as completed evidence"],"alternatives":["Ignore the evidence gap","Document and monitor the evidence gap"],"evidence":["Known unknowns","Experiment requirements","Decision constraints"],"tradeoffs":{"decision_speed":"Allows progress under uncertainty","future_work":"Creates validation or monitoring obligations"},"decision":"Record material evidence gaps together with explicit reassessment conditions.","rationale":"A rejected alternative may become viable when the missing evidence is obtained.","consequences":["Future engineers know what information matters","Decision debt becomes visible"],"implementation_implications":["Store evidence_gap and reassessment_trigger","Link future validation tasks where available","Preserve provisional status"],"verification_method":"Confirm that every material evidence gap has a concrete description and reassessment condition.","outcome_learning":"Close evidence gaps through measured validation and update the alternative's decision status accordingly.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B03-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B03-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B04-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Rejection Analysis","subtopic":"Trade-offs","concept":"A rejected alternative may have superior properties in dimensions that were not decisive","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should rejected alternatives represent strengths that the selected option does not have?","answer":"The record should preserve material strengths of the rejected option alongside the reasons it was not selected. This prevents the historical decision from being rewritten as though the rejected option had no value.","context":"Architecture decisions often involve explicit trade-offs rather than universally superior options.","objective":"Capture balanced reasoning and prevent hindsight bias.","requirements":["Material strengths must be recorded when they influenced comparison","Rejection reason must remain distinct from strengths"],"constraints":["Do not turn balanced comparison into a ranking","Do not omit strengths solely because the option was rejected"],"alternatives":["Record only weaknesses","Record strengths, weaknesses, and decision relevance"],"evidence":["Comparative evaluation","Requirements mapping","Trade-off analysis"],"tradeoffs":{"fidelity":"More faithful historical record","complexity":"Requires multidimensional representation"},"decision":"Preserve both material strengths and rejection factors for rejected alternatives.","rationale":"A rejected alternative can still be valuable under changed priorities or constraints.","consequences":["Future reassessment becomes more informative","Trade-off reasoning is retained"],"implementation_implications":["Separate strengths from rejection_reason","Map each strength to relevant decision dimensions","Capture which dimensions were decisive"],"verification_method":"Check whether the record can explain both why the alternative was attractive and why it was not selected.","outcome_learning":"When priorities change, use preserved strengths to identify candidates for reassessment.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B04-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B04-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B04-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Rejection Analysis","subtopic":"Trade-offs","concept":"Rejection rationale should identify decisive dimensions","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"What makes a trade-off materially relevant to rejection?","answer":"A trade-off is materially relevant when changing its importance could reasonably change the alternative selection under the same general decision context.","context":"Not every difference between alternatives affects the final decision.","objective":"Focus rejected-alternative records on decision-relevant trade-offs.","requirements":["Decision-relevant dimensions must be identified","Non-decisive differences should not dominate the record"],"constraints":["Do not infer decisiveness solely from the existence of a difference"],"alternatives":["Record every observed difference","Record material decision dimensions"],"evidence":["Decision criteria","Weighting or priority information","Decision rationale"],"tradeoffs":{"completeness":"Capturing every difference increases noise","focus":"Capturing decisive dimensions improves retrieval"},"decision":"Prioritize trade-offs that materially influenced the rejection.","rationale":"The purpose of the record is to preserve decision reasoning, not to create an exhaustive feature comparison.","consequences":["Lower information noise","Better future reassessment"],"implementation_implications":["Mark decisive and supporting factors","Preserve secondary factors only when they add future value"],"verification_method":"Remove a recorded trade-off and assess whether the rejection rationale materially changes.","outcome_learning":"If previously secondary dimensions become decisive in later decisions, promote them in future records without rewriting history.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B04-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D55-B04-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B04-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Rejection Analysis","subtopic":"Conditional Trade-offs","concept":"Trade-off reversibility determines reassessment value","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"Why should reversible and irreversible trade-offs be distinguished?","answer":"A rejection based on a reversible trade-off may become obsolete when technology, cost, staffing, or requirements change, whereas an irreversible constraint may remain materially binding. Distinguishing them improves reassessment prioritization.","context":"Architecture conditions evolve at different rates and with different degrees of reversibility.","objective":"Identify which rejection factors are most likely to change.","requirements":["Trade-off reversibility must be assessed when material","Reassessment triggers should reflect reversible factors"],"constraints":["Do not assume a factor is permanent without evidence"],"alternatives":["Treat all trade-offs as static","Classify trade-offs by reversibility"],"evidence":["Technology roadmap","Organizational constraints","Migration characteristics","Physical or contractual limits"],"tradeoffs":{"classification":"Improves future decision maintenance","uncertainty":"Reversibility itself may be uncertain"},"decision":"Record whether major rejection factors are reversible, persistent, or uncertain.","rationale":"Future reassessment should focus on factors capable of changing the decision.","consequences":["Better prioritization of decision reviews","Reduced repeated analysis of stable constraints"],"implementation_implications":["Add reversibility classification","Capture expected change triggers","Avoid fixed expiration dates without evidence"],"verification_method":"Review major rejection factors and classify their potential to change.","outcome_learning":"Update the classification when external conditions demonstrate that a factor has changed or remained stable.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B04-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B04-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B04-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Rejection Analysis","subtopic":"Decision Sensitivity","concept":"Sensitivity to changing assumptions is a key property of rejection rationale","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How can decision sensitivity improve rejected-alternative knowledge?","answer":"Decision sensitivity identifies assumptions or criteria whose change could reverse the rejection. Recording these dependencies helps future engineers determine whether reassessment is warranted.","context":"Some rejected alternatives depend strongly on a small number of uncertain assumptions.","objective":"Expose the conditions that control rejection status.","requirements":["Material assumptions must be identifiable","Potential reversal conditions must be explicit"],"constraints":["Do not claim a reversal without actual evidence","Sensitivity can be qualitative when quantitative analysis is unavailable"],"alternatives":["Static rationale","Rationale with sensitivity conditions"],"evidence":["Scenario analysis","Requirement changes","Threshold analysis","Expert assessment"],"tradeoffs":{"maintenance":"Sensitive decisions require more monitoring","reuse":"Future reassessment becomes more targeted"},"decision":"Record material assumptions whose change could invalidate the rejection.","rationale":"A rejection that depends on one uncertain factor should not be treated like one supported by stable constraints.","consequences":["Decision monitoring becomes actionable","Future experiments can target high-sensitivity assumptions"],"implementation_implications":["Store reversal_conditions","Link conditions to evidence","Track relevant architecture signals where feasible"],"verification_method":"Identify whether changing each recorded critical assumption could plausibly alter the rejection.","outcome_learning":"Monitor high-sensitivity assumptions and trigger reassessment when their observed state changes materially.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B04-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B04-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B04-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B04","topic":"Rejection Analysis","subtopic":"Trade-off Preservation","concept":"Rejected alternatives preserve option value","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"Why is preserving the strengths of rejected alternatives useful even after a decision is made?","answer":"Preserving rejected alternatives retains option value: a previously rejected approach may become useful if requirements, constraints, technology maturity, or operational conditions change.","context":"Architecture decisions are rarely permanent in changing systems.","objective":"Make historical decisions useful for future architecture evolution.","requirements":["Candidate identity and strengths must remain retrievable","Reassessment conditions must be recorded"],"constraints":["Option value does not imply that the alternative should be selected later"],"alternatives":["Discard rejected options","Preserve conditional future options"],"evidence":["Original trade-offs","Reassessment conditions","Technology or environment changes"],"tradeoffs":{"retention":"Requires storage and maintenance","future_value":"Can reduce repeated discovery effort"},"decision":"Preserve materially valuable rejected alternatives when future conditions could plausibly change their suitability.","rationale":"Discarding alternatives destroys potentially useful reasoning and increases repeated analysis.","consequences":["Architecture evolution can reuse prior analysis","Decision history remains useful beyond the original project"],"implementation_implications":["Store candidate strengths and reversal conditions","Enable retrieval by changed constraint or requirement","Retain original context"],"verification_method":"Check whether the record explains what future condition could make the alternative worth reconsidering.","outcome_learning":"When a recorded condition changes, compare current evidence against the original rejection before deciding whether to reopen the alternative.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B04-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D55-B04-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B05-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Reassessment","subtopic":"Reconsideration Conditions","concept":"Reassessment conditions should be explicit and observable where possible","knowledge_type":"REASSESSMENT_CONDITION","question":"What makes a useful condition for reconsidering a rejected alternative?","answer":"A useful reconsideration condition identifies a material change in requirements, constraints, evidence, technology, workload, risk, or operational state that could alter the original rejection rationale.","context":"Rejected alternatives should remain conditionally reusable rather than permanently discarded.","objective":"Define actionable triggers for future reassessment.","requirements":["Condition must relate directly to the original rejection","Condition should be observable or assessable"],"constraints":["Do not define vague triggers such as 'when technology improves' without relevant criteria"],"alternatives":["No reassessment condition","Specific contextual trigger"],"evidence":["Original rejection rationale","Known uncertainty","Environmental dependencies"],"tradeoffs":{"monitoring":"Specific triggers enable targeted monitoring","complexity":"More triggers increase maintenance effort"},"decision":"Define reassessment conditions in terms of changes that could invalidate the original rejection.","rationale":"A trigger is useful only when it has causal relevance to the original decision.","consequences":["Reassessment becomes evidence-driven","Decision debt becomes manageable"],"implementation_implications":["Link triggers to rejection factors","Represent threshold or qualitative conditions when justified","Avoid automatic reversal"],"verification_method":"Trace every trigger to at least one material rejection factor.","outcome_learning":"When a trigger occurs, perform reassessment rather than automatically selecting the alternative.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B05-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B05-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B05-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Reassessment","subtopic":"Requirement Change","concept":"Changed requirements can invalidate a historical rejection","knowledge_type":"REASSESSMENT_CONDITION","question":"When should requirement changes trigger reassessment of a rejected alternative?","answer":"Reassessment is warranted when a requirement that materially contributed to rejection changes in scope, priority, mandatory status, or measurable threshold such that the original incompatibility may no longer apply.","context":"Requirements evolve as business objectives and system constraints change.","objective":"Prevent obsolete requirement-based rejection from becoming permanent.","requirements":["Changed requirement must be linked to original rejection","Materiality of the change must be assessed"],"constraints":["Minor requirement wording changes should not automatically trigger architecture reassessment"],"alternatives":["Retain rejection","Reassess alternative against current requirements"],"evidence":["Updated requirements","Original requirement mapping","Impact analysis"],"tradeoffs":{"stability":"Avoids unnecessary architecture churn","adaptability":"Allows legitimate reconsideration"},"decision":"Trigger reassessment when a requirement change materially affects the original rejection condition.","rationale":"The causal basis for rejection may no longer exist after a meaningful requirement change.","consequences":["Architecture can adapt deliberately","Historical decision remains intact"],"implementation_implications":["Version requirements","Link rejection reasons to requirement IDs","Compare original and current thresholds"],"verification_method":"Confirm that the changed requirement directly affects the reason the alternative was rejected.","outcome_learning":"Record whether reassessment confirms or reverses the original rejection and preserve both states.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B05-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B05-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B05-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Reassessment","subtopic":"Technology Change","concept":"Technology maturity can change the validity of a rejection","knowledge_type":"REASSESSMENT_CONDITION","question":"When can technology maturity justify reconsidering a rejected alternative?","answer":"Technology maturity can justify reassessment when the original rejection depended materially on limitations such as insufficient capability, reliability, ecosystem support, security evidence, tooling, or operational maturity and credible new evidence addresses those limitations.","context":"Technology ecosystems evolve over time.","objective":"Distinguish legitimate technology-driven reassessment from novelty-driven churn.","requirements":["Original limitation must be identified","New evidence must address the limitation"],"constraints":["Newer technology is not automatically better","Marketing claims are insufficient evidence"],"alternatives":["Retain rejection","Conduct targeted reassessment"],"evidence":["Updated technical evidence","Operational experience","Compatibility evidence","Security or reliability validation"],"tradeoffs":{"innovation":"May unlock previously unsuitable approaches","stability":"Reassessment has cost and can introduce change risk"},"decision":"Reassess when new evidence directly addresses the limitation that caused the original rejection.","rationale":"Technology age alone is not the causal factor; changed capability or evidence is.","consequences":["Previously rejected options remain discoverable","Technology evaluation becomes evidence-driven"],"implementation_implications":["Record original technology limitation","Capture evidence freshness","Separate maturity claims from vendor assertions"],"verification_method":"Verify that new evidence addresses the original rejection criterion rather than merely demonstrating novelty.","outcome_learning":"Update the alternative status only after comparative reassessment against current requirements and constraints.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B05-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B05-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B05-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Reassessment","subtopic":"Workload Change","concept":"Workload changes can reverse performance or scalability-based rejection","knowledge_type":"REASSESSMENT_CONDITION","question":"When should workload changes trigger reassessment?","answer":"Workload changes should trigger reassessment when the original rejection depended materially on traffic volume, data size, concurrency, access distribution, latency requirements, or capacity assumptions that have changed.","context":"Performance and scalability suitability depend on workload characteristics.","objective":"Prevent stale workload assumptions from permanently eliminating alternatives.","requirements":["Original workload assumption must be identifiable","Current workload must be materially different"],"constraints":["Do not reassess solely because workload increased slightly without affecting the rejection criterion"],"alternatives":["Retain historical rejection","Re-evaluate against current workload"],"evidence":["Observed workload","Capacity model","Performance testing","Forecast changes"],"tradeoffs":{"reassessment_cost":"Requires new analysis or testing","adaptability":"Can prevent unnecessary architectural constraints"},"decision":"Reassess when workload changes materially affect the original performance or capacity rationale.","rationale":"Architecture suitability is workload-dependent.","consequences":["Performance assumptions remain explicit","Previously rejected options may become viable or less viable"],"implementation_implications":["Version workload assumptions","Store relevant thresholds","Associate rejection with measurable workload factors"],"verification_method":"Compare current workload characteristics with those used in the original rejection.","outcome_learning":"Use measured current workload rather than historical estimates when reassessing.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B05-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B05-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B05-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B05","topic":"Reassessment","subtopic":"Constraint Change","concept":"Constraint removal can reopen a previously rejected alternative","knowledge_type":"REASSESSMENT_CONDITION","question":"When should removal of a constraint trigger reconsideration?","answer":"A rejected alternative should be reconsidered when a binding constraint that materially caused its rejection is removed, relaxed, or replaced and no independent rejection reason remains sufficient.","context":"Organizational, platform, regulatory, contractual, or deployment constraints can change.","objective":"Make rejection status responsive to meaningful constraint changes.","requirements":["Original constraint must be causally linked to rejection","Current constraint state must be verified"],"constraints":["Do not assume that removing one constraint removes all rejection reasons"],"alternatives":["Retain rejection","Reassess remaining rejection factors"],"evidence":["Updated policy","Platform change","Contract change","Organizational change"],"tradeoffs":{"adaptability":"Can restore viable options","stability":"Requires controlled reassessment"},"decision":"Use material constraint removal as a reassessment trigger, not an automatic reversal.","rationale":"Constraint removal changes the decision space but does not determine the new decision.","consequences":["Historical decisions remain traceable","Architecture options can be reopened systematically"],"implementation_implications":["Link constraints to rejection reasons","Track constraint versions","Require current-state validation before reassessment"],"verification_method":"Verify that the removed constraint was actually material to the original rejection.","outcome_learning":"Record whether reassessment confirms the old rejection or changes the candidate status.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B05-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B05-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B06-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Temporal Validity","subtopic":"Decision Aging","concept":"Rejected alternatives require temporal context","knowledge_type":"TEMPORAL_DECISION_KNOWLEDGE","question":"Why should the decision time be preserved for a rejected alternative?","answer":"The decision time establishes which requirements, technology state, constraints, evidence, and assumptions applied when the alternative was rejected. Without temporal context, later users may incorrectly treat historical reasoning as current evidence.","context":"Engineering environments evolve continuously.","objective":"Prevent stale decision knowledge from being mistaken for current truth.","requirements":["Decision timestamp or version should be preserved when available","Relevant environmental state should be traceable"],"constraints":["Do not infer exact historical state when unavailable"],"alternatives":["Store timeless rejection","Store time-bounded decision context"],"evidence":["Decision date","Requirement version","Technology state","Evaluation artifacts"],"tradeoffs":{"traceability":"Improves historical accuracy","maintenance":"Requires version metadata"},"decision":"Preserve temporal context for rejected alternatives.","rationale":"Decision validity depends partly on the state of the environment at decision time.","consequences":["Stale decisions are easier to identify","Historical comparisons become possible"],"implementation_implications":["Store decision_time and applicable versions","Track superseding decisions","Avoid silently updating historical rationale"],"verification_method":"Check whether the record can identify the temporal context of the rejection.","outcome_learning":"Use temporal changes as one signal for reassessment rather than assuming automatic expiration.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B06-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B06-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B06-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Temporal Validity","subtopic":"Staleness","concept":"Aging evidence can reduce confidence without automatically invalidating a rejection","knowledge_type":"TEMPORAL_DECISION_KNOWLEDGE","question":"Does old evidence automatically invalidate a rejected alternative?","answer":"No. Older evidence may remain relevant if the underlying conditions are stable, but its freshness should be considered when technology, workloads, threats, dependencies, or requirements evolve.","context":"Evidence has different rates of obsolescence depending on what it measures.","objective":"Avoid simplistic time-based invalidation.","requirements":["Evidence freshness should be assessed relative to its subject","Changed conditions must be considered"],"constraints":["Do not assign arbitrary expiration periods without evidence"],"alternatives":["Automatic expiration","Condition-based freshness assessment"],"evidence":["Evidence date","Environment changes","Current validation"],"tradeoffs":{"simplicity":"Expiration is easy to implement","accuracy":"Condition-based assessment better reflects evidence validity"},"decision":"Assess evidence freshness based on whether the conditions underlying it have changed materially.","rationale":"Calendar age alone does not determine whether an engineering observation remains relevant.","consequences":["Less unnecessary reassessment","Better prioritization of stale decisions"],"implementation_implications":["Store evidence timestamp","Store relevant version/environment identifiers","Define domain-specific freshness signals where justified"],"verification_method":"Determine whether the conditions measured by the evidence remain materially comparable.","outcome_learning":"Refresh evidence when its underlying assumptions or environment change materially.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B06-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B06-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B06-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Temporal Validity","subtopic":"Superseding Decisions","concept":"A later decision should supersede rather than erase the earlier rejection","knowledge_type":"TEMPORAL_DECISION_KNOWLEDGE","question":"How should a later decision that changes the status of an alternative be represented?","answer":"The later decision should be recorded as a new decision state linked to the earlier rejection rather than overwriting the historical record. This preserves both the original reasoning and the reason the status changed.","context":"Architecture decisions evolve over time.","objective":"Maintain temporal decision lineage.","requirements":["Historical state must remain immutable","Later decision must link to prior state"],"constraints":["Do not rewrite original evidence to match current conclusions"],"alternatives":["Overwrite rejection","Append superseding decision"],"evidence":["New requirements","New evidence","Reassessment results"],"tradeoffs":{"history":"Preserves provenance","storage":"Requires versioned records"},"decision":"Use linked superseding decisions to represent status changes.","rationale":"A changed decision does not make the original decision nonexistent; it changes the current state under new conditions.","consequences":["Decision evolution becomes auditable","Contradictions can be understood temporally"],"implementation_implications":["Store supersedes relationships","Keep original record immutable","Represent current status separately from historical status"],"verification_method":"Verify that a later decision can be traced to the earlier rejection and its changed conditions.","outcome_learning":"Analyze why the original rationale changed to improve future decision capture.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B06-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B06-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B06-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Temporal Validity","subtopic":"Contradiction Handling","concept":"Apparently contradictory rejection decisions may be valid under different contexts","knowledge_type":"TEMPORAL_DECISION_KNOWLEDGE","question":"How should the dataset handle two decisions that reject and later accept the same alternative?","answer":"The records should be treated as context- and time-dependent decisions when the applicable requirements, evidence, constraints, or environment differ. The later decision should not be used to erase the earlier rejection.","context":"The same candidate can change status as conditions evolve.","objective":"Avoid false contradiction classification.","requirements":["Context and temporal scope must be compared","Differences in decision conditions must be represented"],"constraints":["Do not manufacture contextual differences merely to reconcile contradictory records"],"alternatives":["Mark contradiction automatically","Analyze contextual and temporal differences"],"evidence":["Decision versions","Requirement changes","Reassessment evidence"],"tradeoffs":{"clarity":"Contextual analysis improves interpretation","complexity":"Requires relationship-aware reasoning"},"decision":"Classify apparently conflicting decisions as context-dependent unless evidence establishes a genuine unresolved contradiction.","rationale":"Engineering decisions are conditional statements about a particular state of the system and environment.","consequences":["Knowledge retrieval can distinguish historical from current status","True contradictions remain visible when context does not explain them"],"implementation_implications":["Store temporal scope","Compare decision contexts","Use relationship types such as supersedes or context_differs"],"verification_method":"Compare the relevant requirements, constraints, evidence, and time before labeling records contradictory.","outcome_learning":"Use genuine unresolved contradictions as candidates for explicit architecture review.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B06-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D55-B06-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B06-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B06","topic":"Temporal Validity","subtopic":"Current Status","concept":"Historical rejection and current recommendation are separate knowledge states","knowledge_type":"TEMPORAL_DECISION_KNOWLEDGE","question":"Why should current status be separated from historical rejection?","answer":"A historical rejection describes what was decided under earlier conditions, while current status describes what should be considered under the present context. Combining them can cause historical evidence to be mistaken for a current recommendation.","context":"The Knowledge Fabric must support both historical reasoning and current decision support.","objective":"Prevent temporal state confusion.","requirements":["Historical and current states must be separately represented","Current state must have current supporting evidence"],"constraints":["Do not infer current suitability solely from historical records"],"alternatives":["Single mutable status","Historical lineage plus current assessment"],"evidence":["Original decision","Current requirements","Current evidence"],"tradeoffs":{"traceability":"Separate states preserve history","maintenance":"Current status requires reassessment discipline"},"decision":"Separate immutable historical rejection from current assessment status.","rationale":"Decision history and current decision support have different evidentiary requirements.","consequences":["Historical learning is preserved","Current decisions can be updated without corrupting history"],"implementation_implications":["Use current_status separately","Link current assessment to historical alternatives","Require fresh evidence for current recommendations"],"verification_method":"Confirm that current status cannot be derived solely from the historical rejection field.","outcome_learning":"Update current assessment through explicit reassessment while retaining the original rejection record.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B06-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B06-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B07-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Decision Traceability","subtopic":"Rationale Lineage","concept":"Every rejection reason should trace to a decision criterion or constraint when possible","knowledge_type":"TRACEABILITY","question":"What should a rejection rationale trace back to?","answer":"A rejection rationale should trace to the requirement, constraint, risk criterion, evidence finding, trade-off, or other explicit decision criterion that made the alternative unsuitable in context.","context":"Unstructured rejection statements are difficult to validate or reassess.","objective":"Create a traceable chain from alternative to decision basis.","requirements":["Causal rejection reason must be identifiable","Supporting criterion or constraint should be linked"],"constraints":["Do not fabricate traceability where source information does not exist"],"alternatives":["Free-text rationale only","Structured rationale lineage"],"evidence":["Decision criteria","Requirement IDs","Risk records","Evaluation artifacts"],"tradeoffs":{"traceability":"Improves verification and retrieval","capture_effort":"Requires structured relationships"},"decision":"Represent rejection rationale as traceable causal relationships wherever evidence permits.","rationale":"Traceability makes it possible to determine whether the original reason still applies.","consequences":["Reassessment becomes more efficient","Weak rationale becomes easier to identify"],"implementation_implications":["Link rejection_reason to source criterion","Preserve evidence references","Allow unknown provenance status"],"verification_method":"For each material rejection reason, verify that a supporting criterion or evidence source exists.","outcome_learning":"Use broken traceability as a signal to improve future decision capture rather than filling gaps with assumptions.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMEN