{"record_id":"D55-B07-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Decision Traceability","subtopic":"Evidence Lineage","concept":"Evidence lineage should preserve the path from source evidence to rejection conclusion","knowledge_type":"TRACEABILITY","question":"Why is evidence lineage important for rejected alternatives?","answer":"Evidence lineage allows future users to determine what source supported the rejection, under what conditions it was produced, and whether it remains applicable.","context":"Rejected alternatives may be reused long after the original decision.","objective":"Make historical evidence auditable and reassessable.","requirements":["Evidence source must be identifiable when available","Evaluation conditions should be preserved where material"],"constraints":["Do not claim direct evidence when only secondary interpretation exists"],"alternatives":["Store conclusion only","Store source and evaluation lineage"],"evidence":["Test results","Benchmarks","Design reviews","Operational observations"],"tradeoffs":{"auditability":"Improved","storage":"More metadata required"},"decision":"Preserve evidence lineage for material rejection conclusions.","rationale":"Without lineage, a rejection may become an unsupported assertion over time.","consequences":["Evidence can be refreshed","Historical claims can be challenged constructively"],"implementation_implications":["Store evidence references","Capture source version and evaluation context","Distinguish primary and secondary evidence"],"verification_method":"Follow the evidence reference from rejection record to its source and verify contextual compatibility.","outcome_learning":"Replace or augment stale evidence when reassessment requires current evidence.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B07-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B07-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B07-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Decision Traceability","subtopic":"Assumption Lineage","concept":"Critical assumptions require traceability even when direct evidence is unavailable","knowledge_type":"TRACEABILITY","question":"How should critical assumptions be represented when they caused rejection?","answer":"Critical assumptions should be explicitly labeled as assumptions, linked to the decision context, and associated with the consequence they had on the rejection. They must not be presented as measured facts.","context":"Early architecture decisions frequently depend on incomplete information.","objective":"Preserve uncertainty without losing decision reasoning.","requirements":["Assumption must be identifiable","Impact on rejection must be described"],"constraints":["Do not upgrade assumptions to facts during later summarization"],"alternatives":["Omit assumptions","Record assumptions with uncertainty"],"evidence":["Decision discussion","Expert judgment","Available partial evidence"],"tradeoffs":{"transparency":"Improves epistemic clarity","complexity":"Requires uncertainty representation"},"decision":"Preserve critical assumptions as first-class decision evidence states.","rationale":"An assumption may explain the rejection even when direct evidence did not exist.","consequences":["Future validation can target assumptions","Historical reasoning remains faithful"],"implementation_implications":["Store assumption_status","Link assumptions to rejection factors","Record expected validation evidence"],"verification_method":"Check that each assumption is clearly distinguished from measured or externally verified evidence.","outcome_learning":"Update assumption status when validation evidence becomes available without altering the historical record.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B07-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B07-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B07-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Decision Traceability","subtopic":"Requirement Traceability","concept":"Rejected alternatives should remain traceable to the requirements that eliminated them","knowledge_type":"TRACEABILITY","question":"How does requirement traceability improve rejected-alternative knowledge?","answer":"It identifies exactly which requirements prevented selection and allows the rejection to be reassessed when those requirements change.","context":"Architecture decisions are often driven by a subset of all system requirements.","objective":"Enable targeted future reassessment.","requirements":["Material requirements must be identifiable","Requirement versions should be preserved when relevant"],"constraints":["Do not associate unrelated requirements merely to increase traceability density"],"alternatives":["Broad requirement list","Material requirement linkage"],"evidence":["Requirements baseline","Decision matrix","Architecture review"],"tradeoffs":{"precision":"Material linkage reduces noise","maintenance":"Requirement changes require relationship maintenance"},"decision":"Link rejected alternatives to the specific requirements that materially contributed to rejection.","rationale":"Targeted traceability provides stronger causal information than generic requirement association.","consequences":["Requirement changes can trigger precise reassessment","Retrieval can identify alternatives affected by requirement changes"],"implementation_implications":["Use requirement IDs","Store requirement version","Mark mandatory versus preference where known"],"verification_method":"Remove each linked requirement and assess whether the rejection rationale materially changes.","outcome_learning":"When linked requirements evolve, automatically flag the rejection for review rather than automatically reversing it.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B07-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B07-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B07-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B07","topic":"Decision Traceability","subtopic":"Constraint Traceability","concept":"Constraint traceability identifies what must change before reconsideration","knowledge_type":"TRACEABILITY","question":"What value does constraint traceability provide for rejected alternatives?","answer":"Constraint traceability shows which external or technical boundaries caused rejection and therefore which changes could legitimately reopen the alternative.","context":"Constraints can be temporary, permanent, or conditional.","objective":"Connect rejection reasoning to actionable change conditions.","requirements":["Material constraints must be identifiable","Constraint origin or scope should be preserved when available"],"constraints":["Do not assume a constraint is removable without evidence"],"alternatives":["Store rejection without constraints","Link rejection to causal constraints"],"evidence":["Policy","Platform boundary","Deployment requirement","Resource limitation"],"tradeoffs":{"actionability":"Improves reassessment targeting","maintenance":"Requires constraint versioning"},"decision":"Trace material rejection factors to their governing constraints.","rationale":"A constraint-linked rejection provides a direct path from environmental change to reassessment.","consequences":["Changed constraints become detectable reassessment signals","Historical decision logic becomes clearer"],"implementation_implications":["Store constraint identifiers","Track constraint lifecycle","Represent constraint removal separately from alternative status"],"verification_method":"Verify that each material constraint linked to rejection has evidence of its applicability at decision time.","outcome_learning":"When a constraint changes, assess whether all remaining rejection factors still apply.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B07-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B07-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B08-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Decision Learning","subtopic":"Failure to Reuse","concept":"Repeated reconsideration of the same rejected alternative indicates a retrieval or traceability problem","knowledge_type":"LEARNING_SIGNAL","question":"What can repeated reconsideration of the same rejected alternative indicate?","answer":"Repeated reconsideration may indicate that the original rationale was difficult to discover, poorly linked to current decision context, inadequately evidenced, or legitimately obsolete. The cause should be determined rather than assuming repetition is wasteful.","context":"Engineering teams may repeatedly evaluate the same options across projects or over time.","objective":"Convert repeated decision activity into learning signals.","requirements":["Repeated consideration must be detectable","Original and later contexts must be comparable"],"constraints":["Repetition is not automatically a process failure","Different contexts may justify independent consideration"],"alternatives":["Ignore repeated evaluation","Analyze why repetition occurred"],"evidence":["Decision history","Search/retrieval logs","Reassessment records"],"tradeoffs":{"analysis":"Requires historical comparison","learning":"Can improve knowledge retrieval and decision quality"},"decision":"Treat repeated reconsideration as a learning signal requiring causal analysis.","rationale":"Repeated evaluation can reveal either knowledge-management failure or legitimate environmental change.","consequences":["Knowledge capture can improve","Genuine changing conditions remain distinguishable"],"implementation_implications":["Link repeated candidate evaluations","Compare contexts and rejection reasons","Record whether repetition was avoidable"],"verification_method":"Compare repeated decisions for contextual differences and evidence availability.","outcome_learning":"Improve retrieval or decision documentation when repetition resulted from inaccessible prior reasoning.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B08-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B08-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B08-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Decision Learning","subtopic":"Knowledge Reuse","concept":"A rejected alternative should be reusable as evidence, not copied as a universal rule","knowledge_type":"LEARNING_SIGNAL","question":"How should rejected-alternative knowledge be reused by future decisions?","answer":"Future decisions should reuse the candidate's historical rationale, evidence, assumptions, and conditions as inputs to reasoning while independently checking whether the current context matches.","context":"Historical engineering knowledge is useful but context-dependent.","objective":"Enable knowledge reuse without creating rigid architectural rules.","requirements":["Historical context must remain accessible","Current applicability must be evaluated"],"constraints":["Do not transform conditional rejection into a universal prohibition"],"alternatives":["Copy historical decision","Use historical record as contextual evidence"],"evidence":["Original decision","Current requirements","Current constraints"],"tradeoffs":{"reuse":"Reduces repeated analysis","independence":"Preserves current-context reasoning"},"decision":"Use rejected-alternative records as contextual evidence rather than automatic decisions.","rationale":"Historical reasoning informs but does not replace current engineering judgment.","consequences":["Faster informed evaluation","Lower risk of stale architecture rules"],"implementation_implications":["Expose historical rationale separately from current recommendation","Require context matching","Surface evidence age and confidence"],"verification_method":"Check whether retrieval returns both historical rejection and current applicability conditions.","outcome_learning":"Measure whether reused records reduce duplicated analysis without increasing incorrect decisions.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B08-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B08-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B08-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Decision Learning","subtopic":"Alternative Discovery","concept":"Rejected-alternative records can improve future alternative discovery","knowledge_type":"LEARNING_SIGNAL","question":"How can rejected alternatives improve future architecture exploration?","answer":"They reveal previously considered solution paths, their strengths, weaknesses, and contextual failure conditions, allowing future engineers to expand or narrow the candidate search space using historical evidence.","context":"Architecture work often repeats similar problem structures across systems.","objective":"Use historical rejected alternatives as structured search-space knowledge.","requirements":["Candidate identity must be retrievable","Reasoning and conditions must be preserved"],"constraints":["Historical alternatives should not constrain exploration when current conditions differ materially"],"alternatives":["Start every decision from an empty search space","Use historical alternatives as candidate knowledge"],"evidence":["Prior decisions","Evaluation evidence","Reassessment outcomes"],"tradeoffs":{"efficiency":"Reduces repeated discovery effort","bias":"Historical options can anchor future exploration if presented uncritically"},"decision":"Use rejected alternatives to inform candidate discovery while preserving independent current evaluation.","rationale":"Historical alternatives contain useful search-space information but can also create anchoring bias.","consequences":["Candidate generation can become more informed","Decision systems must distinguish retrieval from recommendation"],"implementation_implications":["Label historical candidates explicitly","Preserve rejected status and context","Do not rank alternatives solely by historical frequency"],"verification_method":"Evaluate whether historical candidates expand relevant search without suppressing contextually appropriate new alternatives.","outcome_learning":"Track cases where historical knowledge caused missed alternatives or improved discovery.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B08-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B08-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B08-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Decision Learning","subtopic":"Anchoring Risk","concept":"Historical rejection knowledge can create anchoring bias","knowledge_type":"LEARNING_SIGNAL","question":"What risk arises when rejected alternatives are heavily reused?","answer":"Future engineers may inherit historical rejection without rechecking changed assumptions, causing an outdated decision to constrain the current solution space.","context":"Knowledge reuse can improve efficiency while also influencing future exploration.","objective":"Preserve the benefit of historical knowledge without turning it into an unverified rule.","requirements":["Historical status must be clearly labeled","Current-context validation must remain explicit"],"constraints":["Do not suppress useful historical knowledge merely because anchoring is possible"],"alternatives":["Hide historical rejection","Expose it with context and uncertainty"],"evidence":["Historical decision","Changed context","Current validation"],"tradeoffs":{"reuse":"Higher reuse efficiency","bias_control":"Requires contextual presentation"},"decision":"Expose rejected alternatives with context, evidence strength, and reassessment conditions rather than as unconditional exclusions.","rationale":"Transparent context allows historical knowledge to inform without silently determining the current decision.","consequences":["Better balance between reuse and exploration","Current reasoning remains responsible for final selection"],"implementation_implications":["Display historical status separately","Surface stale or weak evidence","Require current applicability assessment"],"verification_method":"Test retrieval behavior with changed contexts and verify that historical rejection is not treated as an unconditional constraint.","outcome_learning":"Use observed anchoring failures to improve retrieval ranking and context presentation.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B08-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B08-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B08-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B08","topic":"Decision Learning","subtopic":"Knowledge Quality","concept":"Reassessment outcomes should update knowledge quality without rewriting history","knowledge_type":"LEARNING_SIGNAL","question":"How should later evidence affect a rejected-alternative record?","answer":"Later evidence should update the alternative's current evidence state, confidence, or reassessment status while preserving the original rejection and its original supporting information.","context":"Learning systems need to improve knowledge without corrupting historical provenance.","objective":"Support continuous learning with immutable historical lineage.","requirements":["Original decision must remain traceable","Later evidence must be separately attributable"],"constraints":["Do not rewrite historical evidence as though it existed at the original decision time"],"alternatives":["Overwrite old rationale","Append new evidence and reassessment"],"evidence":["New tests","Operational observations","Updated requirements"],"tradeoffs":{"history":"Preserves provenance","complexity":"Requires versioned knowledge"},"decision":"Append and relate new evidence and reassessment results rather than overwriting historical records.","rationale":"Learning requires both historical truth and current understanding.","consequences":["Knowledge evolution becomes auditable","Contradictions can be analyzed over time"],"implementation_implications":["Use evidence versions","Link reassessment records","Maintain immutable original fields"],"verification_method":"Verify that a reviewer can reconstruct both the original and current knowledge states.","outcome_learning":"Use changed evidence to improve future decision reasoning while preserving the original decision context.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B08-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B08-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B09-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Rejected Alternative Lifecycle","subtopic":"Status Management","concept":"Rejected status should distinguish permanent-looking constraints from provisional decisions","knowledge_type":"DECISION_STATE","question":"Should every rejected alternative have the same lifecycle status?","answer":"No. A rejection may be definitive within a stable constraint, provisional because evidence is incomplete, superseded by a later decision, or pending reassessment after a trigger.","context":"Rejected alternatives have different levels of temporal and evidentiary stability.","objective":"Represent lifecycle state explicitly.","requirements":["Status must reflect available evidence and decision history","Status transitions must be traceable"],"constraints":["Do not infer permanence from absence of a reassessment plan"],"alternatives":["Single REJECTED status","Structured rejection lifecycle"],"evidence":["Decision state","Evidence completeness","Reassessment conditions","Superseding decisions"],"tradeoffs":{"simplicity":"Single status is easier","precision":"Lifecycle states preserve more reasoning"},"decision":"Use structured status semantics for rejected alternatives where lifecycle state materially affects interpretation.","rationale":"A provisional rejection should not be consumed like a stable historical constraint.","consequences":["Retrieval can prioritize current and uncertain records appropriately","Decision maintenance becomes clearer"],"implementation_implications":["Possible states include REJECTED_CONTEXTUAL, REJECTED_PROVISIONAL, SUPERSEDED, REASSESSMENT_PENDING","Preserve transition evidence"],"verification_method":"Verify that status corresponds to documented decision and evidence state.","outcome_learning":"Review status transitions to identify stale or unmanaged alternatives.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B09-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B09-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B09-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Rejected Alternative Lifecycle","subtopic":"Provisional Rejection","concept":"Provisional rejection should preserve unresolved uncertainty","knowledge_type":"DECISION_STATE","question":"When is provisional rejection appropriate?","answer":"Provisional rejection is appropriate when the alternative is not selected under the current decision constraints but material uncertainty remains about evidence, future requirements, technology maturity, or another condition that could change the result.","context":"Some architecture decisions must proceed despite incomplete information.","objective":"Prevent provisional decisions from being mistaken for established engineering facts.","requirements":["Uncertainty must be explicit","Reassessment condition should be identifiable"],"constraints":["Provisional status should not be used merely because evidence is imperfect"],"alternatives":["Definitive rejection","Provisional rejection","Delay decision"],"evidence":["Known evidence gaps","Decision deadlines","Unresolved assumptions"],"tradeoffs":{"delivery":"Allows progress despite uncertainty","future_work":"Creates reassessment obligations"},"decision":"Use provisional rejection when uncertainty is material and decision timing requires a current choice.","rationale":"A provisional state accurately represents decisions made under unresolved uncertainty.","consequences":["Future work becomes explicit","Knowledge consumers can calibrate confidence"],"implementation_implications":["Record unresolved questions","Define reassessment triggers","Separate provisional state from technical failure"],"verification_method":"Check whether the record identifies the uncertainty that prevents definitive rejection.","outcome_learning":"Resolve the uncertainty through targeted evidence collection and update the status.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B09-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B09-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B09-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Rejected Alternative Lifecycle","subtopic":"Supersession","concept":"Superseded rejection remains historical evidence","knowledge_type":"DECISION_STATE","question":"What happens to a rejected alternative after a later decision changes its status?","answer":"The original rejection remains part of historical decision lineage, while the later decision becomes the current state for its applicable context. The historical record should not be deleted merely because it is superseded.","context":"Decision systems require historical provenance and current state separately.","objective":"Maintain auditable evolution of architecture decisions.","requirements":["Original record must remain retrievable","Superseding decision must be linked"],"constraints":["Current status must not be inferred by reading only the oldest record"],"alternatives":["Delete superseded rejection","Retain linked historical state"],"evidence":["Later decision","Reassessment evidence","Changed conditions"],"tradeoffs":{"history":"Preserves provenance","storage":"Requires versioned state management"},"decision":"Retain superseded rejection as historical lineage and expose the later decision as current where applicable.","rationale":"Supersession changes state but does not erase history.","consequences":["Auditability improves","Learning can examine why decisions changed"],"implementation_implications":["Use supersedes relationships","Maintain current-state projection separately","Preserve original evidence"],"verification_method":"Confirm that both historical and current states are independently recoverable.","outcome_learning":"Analyze supersession causes to improve future decision thresholds and evidence requirements.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B09-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B09-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B09-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Rejected Alternative Lifecycle","subtopic":"Reassessment Pending","concept":"A triggered reassessment should not automatically change alternative status","knowledge_type":"DECISION_STATE","question":"What should happen when a reconsideration trigger occurs?","answer":"A trigger should move the alternative into a reassessment-needed state or equivalent workflow, but the original rejection should remain active until current evidence supports a new decision.","context":"Environmental changes can invalidate assumptions without determining the correct replacement architecture.","objective":"Separate trigger detection from decision reversal.","requirements":["Trigger detection must be distinct from decision outcome","Current evidence must support status change"],"constraints":["Do not automatically select an alternative because one rejection condition changed"],"alternatives":["Automatic reversal","Reassessment workflow"],"evidence":["Changed condition","Current evaluation","Updated requirements"],"tradeoffs":{"automation":"Automatic reversal is fast but risky","control":"Explicit reassessment preserves decision governance"},"decision":"Treat reassessment triggers as signals for review, not automatic selection events.","rationale":"A changed condition only changes the decision space; it does not determine the optimal current choice.","consequences":["Reduced architecture churn","More reliable status transitions"],"implementation_implications":["Represent REASSESSMENT_PENDING","Require fresh evidence","Record reassessment outcome separately"],"verification_method":"Trigger a known condition change and verify that the system requests reassessment rather than silently changing the selected architecture.","outcome_learning":"Measure whether triggers identify meaningful obsolete decisions and refine trigger quality accordingly.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B09-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B09-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B09-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B09","topic":"Rejected Alternative Lifecycle","subtopic":"Closure","concept":"A rejected alternative can be closed when its decision context is permanently removed or superseded","knowledge_type":"DECISION_STATE","question":"When can a rejected alternative be considered closed?","answer":"Closure is appropriate when the decision context is permanently retired, the relevant alternative is no longer applicable, or a superseding decision explicitly establishes a new state and no meaningful future reuse remains within the defined scope.","context":"Not every historical alternative needs indefinite active monitoring.","objective":"Control decision-lifecycle maintenance without deleting provenance.","requirements":["Closure reason must be explicit","Historical record must remain preserved"],"constraints":["Closure must not mean deletion of historical evidence"],"alternatives":["Keep all alternatives permanently active","Close inactive alternatives while preserving history"],"evidence":["System retirement","Scope retirement","Superseding decision","Permanent constraint"],"tradeoffs":{"maintenance":"Reduces active review burden","history":"Requires preserved archival state"},"decision":"Allow explicit closure states while retaining immutable historical evidence.","rationale":"Active reassessment should focus on alternatives that can still affect future decisions.","consequences":["Lower maintenance noise","Historical decision lineage remains available"],"implementation_implications":["Define closure criteria","Separate archival state from deletion","Preserve provenance and relationships"],"verification_method":"Verify that closed records remain retrievable as historical evidence but are not incorrectly surfaced as active candidates.","outcome_learning":"Review closure patterns to identify alternatives that were prematurely archived.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B09-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B09-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B10-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Decision Record Quality","concept":"A high-quality rejected-alternative record must preserve candidate, reason, evidence, and reconsideration conditions","knowledge_type":"QUALITY_CRITERIA","question":"What minimum information makes a rejected-alternative record useful for engineering reasoning?","answer":"At minimum, the record should identify the candidate, decision context, material rejection reason, supporting evidence or explicit uncertainty, relevant trade-offs, and conditions that could justify reconsideration when such conditions are meaningful.","context":"D55 is intended to preserve reusable decision knowledge rather than simple historical labels.","objective":"Define minimum information density for useful records.","requirements":["Candidate identity","Decision context","Rejection reason","Evidence state","Trade-offs","Reassessment conditions when applicable"],"constraints":["Fields should not be fabricated merely to satisfy completeness","Unknown values must remain explicitly unknown"],"alternatives":["Minimal rejection label","Structured decision knowledge"],"evidence":["Decision record","Evaluation evidence","Requirements and constraints"],"tradeoffs":{"record_depth":"Richer records improve reuse","capture_effort":"Requires disciplined documentation"},"decision":"Require decision-relevant structure while allowing legitimately unavailable fields to remain unknown.","rationale":"False completeness is worse than explicit uncertainty.","consequences":["Higher retrieval value","Lower fabrication risk"],"implementation_implications":["Validate required fields","Allow UNKNOWN/NOT_AVAILABLE states","Preserve provenance per material claim"],"verification_method":"Run schema and semantic completeness checks without treating field population alone as evidence quality.","outcome_learning":"Use retrieval failures and reassessment outcomes to refine required fields.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B10-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B10-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B10-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Deduplication","concept":"Repeated rejection records should be merged only when their decision contexts and knowledge content are materially equivalent","knowledge_type":"DEDUPLICATION","question":"When should two rejected-alternative records be deduplicated?","answer":"Records should be deduplicated when they represent the same candidate, decision boundary, rejection rationale, evidence state, and materially equivalent context without adding new information. Records with meaningful contextual or temporal differences should remain separate and related.","context":"The same alternative may be considered repeatedly across systems or time.","objective":"Reduce duplicate knowledge without erasing meaningful context.","requirements":["Candidate identity must be compared","Decision context must be compared","Evidence and rationale must be compared"],"constraints":["Do not merge records merely because candidate names match"],"alternatives":["Exact-name deduplication","Context-aware semantic deduplication"],"evidence":["Decision identifiers","Context metadata","Rationale and evidence"],"tradeoffs":{"compression":"Reduces redundancy","history":"Over-aggressive merging can destroy decision lineage"},"decision":"Use context-aware deduplication rather than name-only deduplication.","rationale":"Identical alternatives can have legitimately different rejection reasons under different contexts.","consequences":["Lower duplicate rate","Preserved contextual knowledge"],"implementation_implications":["Compare semantic content","Preserve source relationships","Create supersedes/context-differs links when records are related but not duplicates"],"verification_method":"Review candidate pairs for material differences before merging.","outcome_learning":"Track deduplication errors to improve semantic similarity thresholds and context comparison.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B10-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B10-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B10-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Contradiction Detection","concept":"A true contradiction exists when materially equivalent contexts contain incompatible rejection conclusions without an explanatory difference","knowledge_type":"CONTRADICTION_ANALYSIS","question":"When should two rejected-alternative records be considered contradictory?","answer":"They should be considered potentially contradictory when they concern materially equivalent decision contexts and evidence states but reach incompatible conclusions about the same candidate without a documented change, uncertainty, or contextual distinction explaining the difference.","context":"Knowledge Fabric must distinguish contradictions from legitimate contextual variation.","objective":"Identify unresolved decision conflicts without forcing false consistency.","requirements":["Contexts must be compared","Decision conclusions must be compared","Possible explanatory changes must be checked"],"constraints":["Do not label historical differences as contradictions automatically"],"alternatives":["Automatic contradiction on differing conclusions","Context-aware contradiction analysis"],"evidence":["Decision context","Requirement versions","Evidence versions","Reassessment records"],"tradeoffs":{"sensitivity":"May surface more candidate contradictions","precision":"Context analysis reduces false positives"},"decision":"Use context-aware contradiction detection and preserve unresolved contradictions when no explanation is available.","rationale":"Contradiction is a relationship between claims under comparable conditions, not merely different conclusions over time.","consequences":["Unresolved decision conflicts become reviewable","Legitimate evolution remains distinguishable"],"implementation_implications":["Compare context hashes or structured context fields","Link explanatory supersession records","Assign contradiction status separately from decision status"],"verification_method":"Test pairs of records with changed and unchanged contexts to verify correct distinction between evolution and contradiction.","outcome_learning":"Use confirmed contradictions to identify missing decision criteria, evidence quality problems, or inconsistent architecture governance.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B10-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B10-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B10-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Retrieval","concept":"Rejected alternatives should be retrievable by candidate, rejection reason, changed condition, and decision context","knowledge_type":"RETRIEVAL_KNOWLEDGE","question":"Which retrieval dimensions make rejected-alternative knowledge useful?","answer":"Useful retrieval dimensions include candidate identity, problem domain, rejection reason, affected requirement or constraint, evidence type, decision context, reassessment condition, and temporal state.","context":"The dataset is intended for retrieval-augmented reasoning and autonomous software-engineering workflows.","objective":"Make rejection knowledge discoverable when future decisions resemble historical decisions.","requirements":["Stable identifiers","Structured rejection metadata","Contextual relationships"],"constraints":["Retrieval must not equate frequency with correctness","Historical status must remain distinguishable from current suitability"],"alternatives":["Keyword-only retrieval","Structured multi-dimensional retrieval"],"evidence":["Record schema","Decision relationships","Query requirements"],"tradeoffs":{"recall":"Multiple retrieval dimensions improve discovery","complexity":"Requires richer indexing and metadata"},"decision":"Index rejected alternatives using multiple semantic and relational dimensions rather than candidate name alone.","rationale":"Future users often encounter the same constraint or failure mode without knowing the original candidate name.","consequences":["Higher potential reuse value","Better support for reasoning from historical failures"],"implementation_implications":["Index rejection reasons and constraints","Support relationship-aware retrieval","Preserve confidence and evidence status in retrieval metadata"],"verification_method":"Test queries by candidate, constraint, rejection reason, and reassessment condition and verify relevant records are discoverable.","outcome_learning":"Use retrieval misses to identify missing metadata or relationship links.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B10-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B10-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B10-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B10","topic":"Rejected Alternatives","subtopic":"Quality Validation","concept":"D55 quality validation must test decision usefulness rather than record completeness alone","knowledge_type":"QUALITY_VALIDATION","question":"How should D55 rejected-alternative records be validated?","answer":"Validation should test factual and semantic consistency, provenance, distinctness, context binding, rejection rationale, evidence classification, reassessment conditions, relationship integrity, retrieval usefulness, and absence of unsupported claims. Field completeness alone is insufficient.","context":"A rejected-alternative dataset can appear structured while still containing shallow, duplicate, or unsupported knowledge.","objective":"Ensure D55 contributes reliable decision intelligence to the Knowledge Fabric.","requirements":["Semantic validation","Deduplication","Provenance validation","Relationship validation","Retrieval validation"],"constraints":["Do not mark records verified merely because they pass schema validation","Do not fabricate execution or operational outcomes"],"alternatives":["Schema-only validation","Multi-layer knowledge validation"],"evidence":["Record structure","Semantic review","Relationship graph","Retrieval tests"],"tradeoffs":{"quality":"Higher validation effort","reliability":"Substantially stronger downstream usefulness"},"decision":"Validate D55 at structural, semantic, evidence, relationship, deduplication, and retrieval levels.","rationale":"The dataset's value depends on correct decision reasoning, not merely valid JSON.","consequences":["Unsupported or duplicate records can be removed","Knowledge Fabric retrieval becomes more reliable"],"implementation_implications":["Separate validation_status from execution_status","Run exact and semantic deduplication","Check contradiction and relationship integrity","Test representative retrieval queries"],"verification_method":"Apply schema validation, semantic review, deduplication checks, provenance checks, relationship checks, contradiction analysis, and retrieval tests before dataset locking.","outcome_learning":"Use validation failures to improve future D55 records and the dataset-generation methodology without fabricating evidence.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Rejected Alternatives scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B10-R01","D55-B10-R02","D55-B10-R03","D55-B10-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"validates","record_id":"D55-B10-R01"},{"type":"validates","record_id":"D55-B10-R02"},{"type":"validates","record_id":"D55-B10-R03"},{"type":"validates","record_id":"D55-B10-R04"}]}

{"record_id":"D55-B11-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Purpose","concept":"Architecture governance must control architectural decisions without replacing engineering execution","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should architecture governance control?","answer":"Architecture governance should establish decision authority, required constraints, review criteria, evidence expectations, exception handling, and traceability while leaving implementation execution to the responsible engineering teams. Governance is effective when it controls architectural risk and decision integrity without becoming a substitute for engineering ownership.","context":"A software organization needs consistent architectural control across teams while preserving delivery autonomy.","objective":"Define an appropriate boundary between architectural governance and engineering execution.","requirements":["Architecture decisions must have accountable owners","Material architectural risks must be reviewable","Exceptions must be traceable","Engineering teams must retain implementation responsibility"],"constraints":["Governance cannot inspect every implementation detail","Overly centralized approval can delay delivery","Different systems may require different review depth"],"alternatives":["Centralized architecture approval for all changes","Team-owned architecture with no formal governance","Risk-based governance with defined review thresholds"],"evidence":["Architecture decisions affect long-lived system constraints","Review effort is better allocated according to architectural risk","Clear ownership reduces ambiguity between governance and implementation"],"tradeoffs":["Centralized control increases consistency but can reduce delivery autonomy","No governance increases local autonomy but can permit unmanaged architectural risk","Risk-based governance requires explicit classification criteria"],"decision":"Use risk-based architecture governance with explicit decision ownership, review thresholds, and exception mechanisms.","rationale":"This separates governance of architectural risk from implementation ownership and avoids making every engineering change a centralized approval event.","consequences":["Material decisions require stronger evidence","Low-risk changes can proceed with lighter controls","Governance criteria must remain explicit and reviewable"],"implementation_implications":["Define architecture decision classes","Define review thresholds","Record decision ownership","Provide an exception workflow","Maintain traceability from decision to implementation"],"verification_method":"Inspect representative architectural changes and verify that review depth corresponds to documented risk classification and that accountable ownership is recorded.","outcome_learning":"Measure review latency, exception frequency, architectural defects, and governance bypasses; adjust thresholds when evidence shows systematic over-control or under-control.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B11-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Scope","concept":"Governance scope should be defined by architectural impact rather than organizational visibility","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_BOUNDARY","question":"How should the scope of architecture governance be determined?","answer":"Governance scope should be determined by architectural impact, such as changes to system boundaries, security properties, data ownership, consistency guarantees, availability characteristics, external contracts, or irreversible infrastructure commitments. Team size, project visibility, or organizational seniority should not by themselves determine governance depth.","context":"An organization needs a consistent way to decide which engineering changes require architectural review.","objective":"Create a repeatable boundary for architecture governance.","requirements":["Scope criteria must be explicit","Material architectural properties must be covered","Review requirements must be reproducible","Governance must apply consistently across teams"],"constraints":["Impact may be difficult to estimate before implementation","Some changes affect several architectural dimensions simultaneously"],"alternatives":["Govern every change","Govern only large projects","Govern changes based on architectural impact"],"evidence":["Architectural risk is associated with system properties and dependencies","Small changes can have large cross-system consequences","Large projects can contain low-impact changes"],"tradeoffs":["Broad governance increases detection coverage but creates review overhead","Impact-based governance is more efficient but requires reliable classification"],"decision":"Use architectural-impact criteria to determine governance scope and review depth.","rationale":"Architectural consequences are a more direct governance signal than project size or organizational status.","consequences":["Teams need impact-classification guidance","Review records must explain why a change did or did not require deeper governance"],"implementation_implications":["Define impact dimensions","Define materiality thresholds","Require rationale for classifications near thresholds","Audit classification consistency"],"verification_method":"Sample changes from multiple teams and compare their assigned governance scope against documented architectural impact.","outcome_learning":"Track incorrectly classified changes and update impact criteria based on observed architectural consequences.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B11-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D55-B11-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B11-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Decision Authority","concept":"Decision authority should correspond to the scope and reversibility of architectural consequences","knowledge_type":"AUTHORITY_MODEL","question":"How should architectural decision authority be assigned?","answer":"Decision authority should be assigned according to the scope, risk, reversibility, and cross-boundary impact of a decision. Local teams can own decisions contained within their bounded context, while decisions affecting shared platforms, organizational constraints, security boundaries, or multiple independent systems may require broader authority.","context":"Multiple engineering teams share platforms and architectural constraints.","objective":"Prevent both unauthorized cross-boundary decisions and unnecessary centralized approval.","requirements":["Decision owners must be identifiable","Authority boundaries must be explicit","Cross-boundary decisions must have affected-party representation","Escalation paths must exist"],"constraints":["Authority models vary by organization","Cross-team dependencies may change over time"],"alternatives":["Central architecture board","Fully decentralized authority","Delegated authority with escalation for cross-boundary decisions"],"evidence":["Local teams generally possess implementation context","Cross-boundary decisions create external consequences","Authority ambiguity creates delayed or conflicting decisions"],"tradeoffs":["Central authority improves consistency but can reduce local responsiveness","Decentralized authority improves speed but requires strong boundary definitions","Delegation requires reliable escalation mechanisms"],"decision":"Use delegated architectural authority with explicit escalation for cross-boundary or high-impact decisions.","rationale":"This preserves local engineering context while creating controls for decisions whose consequences exceed a single team's boundary.","consequences":["Teams need documented authority boundaries","Escalations become part of normal architecture governance","Shared decisions require broader traceability"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain decision-owner metadata","Define escalation conditions","Record affected architectural boundaries","Prevent silent ownership changes"],"verification_method":"Review architectural decision records and verify that authority matches decision scope and that escalated decisions contain evidence of appropriate participation.","outcome_learning":"Analyze disputed ownership cases and revise authority boundaries when recurring ambiguity appears.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B11-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B11-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B11-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Evidence","concept":"Governance decisions require evidence proportional to decision risk","knowledge_type":"EVIDENCE_POLICY","question":"How much evidence should an architecture review require?","answer":"Evidence requirements should increase with architectural risk, irreversibility, uncertainty, and cross-system impact. Low-risk reversible changes may rely on concise rationale and local validation, whereas high-impact decisions should include alternatives, constraints, measurable assumptions, dependency analysis, security or reliability implications, and validation plans.","context":"Architecture governance must avoid both evidence-free decisions and excessive documentation.","objective":"Make evidence requirements proportional to architectural risk.","requirements":["Evidence expectations must be defined","High-risk decisions must expose assumptions","Evidence must be traceable to decision claims","Unverified assumptions must be distinguishable from facts"],"constraints":["Evidence collection has cost","Some architectural properties cannot be fully validated before implementation"],"alternatives":["Uniform evidence requirements","Minimal documentation for every decision","Risk-proportional evidence requirements"],"evidence":["Decision uncertainty varies between architectural changes","Irreversible decisions have greater cost of incorrect assumptions","Explicit assumptions improve later reassessment"],"tradeoffs":["More evidence improves reviewability but increases decision latency","Less evidence accelerates decisions but increases uncertainty"],"decision":"Use risk-proportional evidence requirements with explicit separation of facts, assumptions, analysis, and validation results.","rationale":"This preserves decision speed for low-risk work while increasing traceability where incorrect architectural assumptions can be costly.","consequences":["Decision records need evidence classification","Reviewers can challenge unsupported assumptions","Evidence depth becomes part of governance policy"],"implementation_implications":["Add evidence-type metadata to decision records","Record unresolved assumptions","Require validation plans for high-impact claims","Preserve source provenance"],"verification_method":"Audit sampled decisions for evidence completeness relative to their assigned risk class.","outcome_learning":"Measure which unsupported assumptions later become defects or decision reversals and use that evidence to improve governance requirements.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B11-R02","D55-B11-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D55-B11-R02"},{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B11-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B11-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B11","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Failure","concept":"Architecture governance fails when approval becomes disconnected from evidence and consequences","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is a significant failure mode in architecture governance?","answer":"A major governance failure occurs when approval becomes a procedural checkpoint rather than an evidence-based assessment of architectural consequences. A recorded approval alone does not establish that assumptions were tested, alternatives were considered, risks were understood, or implementation constraints were satisfied.","context":"An organization has formal architecture approval but still experiences repeated architectural failures.","objective":"Distinguish governance activity from effective governance.","requirements":["Approval must identify what was evaluated","Material assumptions must be visible","Decision consequences must be tracked","Verification must be possible"],"constraints":["Approval workflows can become bureaucratic","Reviewers may have incomplete implementation context"],"alternatives":["Approval-only governance","Evidence-based review","Post-implementation architecture audits"],"evidence":["A process completion signal does not itself establish technical correctness","Untracked assumptions can survive formal approval","Outcome feedback is necessary for governance learning"],"tradeoffs":["Evidence-based governance requires more effort","Approval-only governance is faster but provides weaker technical assurance"],"decision":"Treat approval as one governance event rather than evidence of architectural correctness; require decision evidence and subsequent validation for material decisions.","rationale":"Separating governance approval from technical verification prevents procedural completion from being mistaken for architectural success.","consequences":["Architecture governance needs post-decision feedback","Approval records cannot substitute for validation evidence"],"implementation_implications":["Link decisions to verification activities","Track unresolved assumptions","Record post-implementation findings","Distinguish approval status from validation status"],"verification_method":"Compare approved architecture decisions with their associated evidence and later validation results.","outcome_learning":"Track decisions where formal approval occurred but later evidence contradicted assumptions; use these cases to improve governance controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B11-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"contrasts_with","record_id":"D55-B11-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B12-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Review Levels","concept":"Review depth should be tiered by architectural risk","knowledge_type":"REVIEW_MODEL","question":"Why should architecture reviews use multiple levels?","answer":"A tiered review model allows governance effort to match architectural risk. Routine local changes can receive lightweight review, changes with meaningful cross-component impact can receive structured review, and high-impact or difficult-to-reverse decisions can receive deeper independent assessment.","context":"An organization must review architectural changes without creating a single heavy process for every change.","objective":"Design proportional architecture review levels.","requirements":["Review levels must have objective criteria","Escalation must be possible","Each level must have defined evidence expectations","Review outcomes must be traceable"],"constraints":["Risk classification can be uncertain","Different domains may have different risk profiles"],"alternatives":["Single review level","No formal review levels","Risk-tiered review levels"],"evidence":["Architectural changes differ in impact and reversibility","Uniform review effort creates either excessive overhead or insufficient scrutiny"],"tradeoffs":["Tiering improves efficiency but requires classification discipline","Uniform review simplifies process but ignores risk differences"],"decision":"Adopt risk-tiered architecture reviews with explicit entry and escalation criteria.","rationale":"Review depth becomes proportional to architectural consequence rather than organizational preference.","consequences":["Teams need clear classification rules","Review policies become more measurable","Escalation paths must be operational"],"implementation_implications":["Define review tiers","Define required artifacts per tier","Define automatic escalation conditions","Record final review level"],"verification_method":"Sample decisions across tiers and verify that required evidence and reviewers match the declared level.","outcome_learning":"Measure defects and review delays by tier to identify misclassification or inefficient review requirements.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B12-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Review Independence","concept":"High-impact architecture decisions may require reviewers independent from implementation ownership","knowledge_type":"INDEPENDENT_REVIEW","question":"When is independent architecture review useful?","answer":"Independent review is useful when a decision has substantial cross-system consequences, high uncertainty, difficult reversibility, regulatory or security implications, or strong incentives for the implementation owner to overlook competing risks. Independence should be proportional to the decision's consequence rather than applied universally.","context":"A high-impact architectural change is primarily evaluated by the team proposing it.","objective":"Identify when independent review adds meaningful assurance.","requirements":["Independence criteria must be explicit","Reviewers must have relevant architectural competence","Independence must not remove implementation context","Review findings must be traceable"],"constraints":["Independent reviewers may lack detailed domain context","Additional review increases elapsed time"],"alternatives":["Owner-only review","Peer review within the same team","Independent cross-team or architecture review"],"evidence":["Independent assessment can expose assumptions shared by the implementation team","Excessive independence can reduce contextual accuracy"],"tradeoffs":["Independence increases challenge capacity but can increase coordination cost","Local review preserves context but may reproduce team assumptions"],"decision":"Require independent review selectively for high-impact, high-uncertainty, or difficult-to-reverse decisions.","rationale":"Selective independence targets the cases where an additional perspective can materially reduce architectural blind spots.","consequences":["Some decisions require reviewers outside the implementation team","Review ownership must remain distinct from implementation ownership"],"implementation_implications":["Define independence triggers","Maintain qualified reviewer pools","Capture dissenting findings","Record how review findings affected the decision"],"verification_method":"Audit high-risk decisions and verify that applicable independence triggers resulted in an appropriately independent review.","outcome_learning":"Compare later architectural issues across independently reviewed and locally reviewed decisions without treating the comparison as causal proof.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B12-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"specializes","record_id":"D55-B12-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B12-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Review Inputs","concept":"Architecture review should evaluate constraints, alternatives, evidence, consequences, and validation strategy","knowledge_type":"REVIEW_CRITERIA","question":"What should a substantive architecture review examine?","answer":"A substantive review should examine the problem context, requirements, constraints, alternatives considered, evidence supporting assumptions, selected decision, expected consequences, implementation implications, material security and performance effects, and the method by which the decision will be validated.","context":"A review process exists but reviewers use inconsistent evaluation criteria.","objective":"Create a consistent minimum review surface.","requirements":["Review criteria must be explicit","Decision assumptions must be inspectable","Consequences must be considered","Validation must be planned"],"constraints":["Review depth should remain proportional to risk","Not every criterion has equal relevance to every decision"],"alternatives":["Reviewer discretion without a checklist","Fixed exhaustive checklist","Risk-adapted minimum review criteria"],"evidence":["Structured review surfaces reduce omission of decision-critical information","Overly exhaustive checklists create irrelevant review work"],"tradeoffs":["Structured criteria improve consistency but require maintenance","Pure discretion is flexible but less reproducible"],"decision":"Use a mandatory core review surface with risk-specific additional criteria.","rationale":"A stable minimum prevents omission while allowing domain-specific review depth.","consequences":["Review records become more comparable","Criteria must evolve as recurring failure modes are discovered"],"implementation_implications":["Define mandatory fields","Define risk-specific review extensions","Require explicit unknowns where evidence is missing","Record review findings"],"verification_method":"Evaluate review records for presence of required criteria and traceability of identified concerns to decision outcomes.","outcome_learning":"Use recurring review findings and post-implementation failures to update the review criteria library.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B12-R01","D55-B12-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"defines_review_surface_for","record_id":"D55-B12-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B12-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Review Exceptions","concept":"Review exceptions require explicit rationale and compensating controls","knowledge_type":"EXCEPTION_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should an architecture review exception be governed?","answer":"An architecture review exception should identify the requirement being bypassed, the reason, affected risks, scope, duration where applicable, accountable owner, compensating controls, and conditions for reassessment. An exception should not silently remove the underlying architectural requirement.","context":"A delivery constraint prevents a team from completing the standard architecture review process.","objective":"Allow controlled exceptions without converting them into undocumented permanent bypasses.","requirements":["Exception scope must be explicit","Owner must be identified","Risk must be documented","Compensating controls must be defined where appropriate","Expiration or reassessment must be defined when applicable"],"constraints":["Some emergencies require rapid decisions","Temporary exceptions can become permanent through neglect"],"alternatives":["Block all exceptions","Allow informal exceptions","Formal time-bounded or condition-bounded exceptions"],"evidence":["Exceptions change the risk posture","Undocumented bypasses reduce governance traceability"],"tradeoffs":["Formal exceptions add process overhead","Informal exceptions increase governance uncertainty"],"decision":"Use explicit, traceable exceptions with defined ownership, risk rationale, and reassessment conditions.","rationale":"This preserves delivery flexibility while preventing exceptions from becoming invisible architectural policy changes.","consequences":["Exception records become governance artifacts","Expired or unresolved exceptions require follow-up"],"implementation_implications":["Create exception metadata","Link exceptions to affected decisions","Generate reassessment triggers","Track compensating controls"],"verification_method":"Audit active exceptions and verify ownership, scope, rationale, and reassessment status.","outcome_learning":"Measure expired exceptions, repeated exceptions, and incidents associated with exception scope to identify systemic governance gaps.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B12-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B12-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B12-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B12","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Review Failure Mode","concept":"Architecture reviews can fail through checklist compliance without substantive challenge","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What happens when architecture review becomes checklist compliance?","answer":"A review can become ineffective when reviewers verify the presence of documents without challenging assumptions, alternatives, consequences, dependencies, or validation evidence. A complete form does not establish that the underlying architectural reasoning is sound.","context":"An organization reports high architecture-review completion but recurring architectural defects remain.","objective":"Prevent procedural review from replacing technical reasoning.","requirements":["Reviewers must evaluate content, not only presence","Material assumptions must be challengeable","Findings must be recorded","Validation must remain distinct from approval"],"constraints":["Deep review requires expertise","Not every reviewer can independently validate every technical claim"],"alternatives":["Checklist-only compliance","Unstructured expert review","Structured evidence-based review with substantive challenge"],"evidence":["Presence of documentation is not equivalent to correctness","Architectural assumptions require technical evaluation"],"tradeoffs":["Substantive review requires expertise and time","Checklist-only review is efficient but weak as technical assurance"],"decision":"Treat checklists as review aids rather than proof of architectural quality.","rationale":"The governance objective is informed risk assessment, not merely document completion.","consequences":["Reviewers need technical challenge criteria","Governance metrics should distinguish completion from review effectiveness"],"implementation_implications":["Add explicit challenge questions","Record unresolved concerns","Require evidence for material claims","Track validation separately"],"verification_method":"Inspect completed reviews for substantive findings, challenged assumptions, and links to evidence rather than only checklist completion.","outcome_learning":"Analyze repeated architectural failures after apparently complete reviews to identify weak review criteria or insufficient reviewer expertise.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B12-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"failure_mode_of","record_id":"D55-B12-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B13-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Standards","concept":"Architecture standards should define constraints and intent rather than prescribe unnecessary implementation details","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_STANDARD","question":"What should an architecture standard contain?","answer":"An architecture standard should define the architectural property, required outcome, applicable scope, constraints, rationale, exceptions, and verification expectations. It should avoid prescribing implementation details that are not necessary to achieve the intended property unless those details are themselves mandatory constraints.","context":"An organization is creating standards for common architectural concerns.","objective":"Create standards that remain useful without unnecessarily constraining implementation.","requirements":["Standard intent must be explicit","Applicability must be defined","Mandatory constraints must be distinguishable from guidance","Verification must be possible"],"constraints":["Overly specific standards become brittle","Under-specified standards may not provide meaningful control"],"alternatives":["Prescriptive implementation standards","Principle-only standards","Outcome-oriented standards with explicit mandatory constraints"],"evidence":["Architectural goals can often be achieved through multiple implementations","Mandatory implementation details may age faster than architectural requirements"],"tradeoffs":["Prescriptive standards improve uniformity but reduce implementation flexibility","Outcome-oriented standards preserve flexibility but require stronger verification"],"decision":"Prefer outcome-oriented standards with only justified mandatory implementation constraints.","rationale":"This preserves architectural intent while reducing unnecessary coupling to implementation choices.","consequences":["Standards require clear verification criteria","Teams retain controlled implementation flexibility"],"implementation_implications":["Separate mandatory rules from recommendations","Document applicability","Define measurable verification conditions","Review standards as technologies evolve"],"verification_method":"Inspect standards and determine whether each mandatory constraint is justified by an explicit architectural requirement or risk.","outcome_learning":"Track recurring interpretation disputes and implementation failures to refine standard wording.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B13-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Standard Exceptions","concept":"Standards need explicit exception semantics to remain governable under legitimate context differences","knowledge_type":"STANDARD_EXCEPTION","question":"Why should architecture standards define exception mechanisms?","answer":"Explicit exception mechanisms allow teams to handle legitimate contextual differences without silently violating standards. An exception should preserve traceability to the standard, explain why compliance is unsuitable, identify affected risks, and define compensating measures or reassessment conditions when needed.","context":"A common architecture standard cannot be applied unchanged to every system.","objective":"Prevent both rigid standard enforcement and uncontrolled deviation.","requirements":["Exceptions must reference the affected standard","Rationale must be recorded","Risk impact must be assessed","Ownership must be explicit"],"constraints":["Some systems have unique technical constraints","Emergency situations may require expedited handling"],"alternatives":["No exceptions","Informal exceptions","Formal exception process"],"evidence":["Context can invalidate otherwise useful assumptions","Untracked deviations weaken architectural consistency"],"tradeoffs":["Formal exceptions increase governance work","No formal exception path encourages hidden deviations"],"decision":"Provide a formal, traceable exception mechanism for architecture standards.","rationale":"Governed exceptions are preferable to invisible divergence when a standard is not appropriate for a specific context.","consequences":["Standards remain enforceable without being absolute","Exception volume becomes a signal for standard quality"],"implementation_implications":["Link exceptions to standard identifiers","Track active and closed exceptions","Define reassessment criteria","Review repeated exceptions"],"verification_method":"Review exception records for traceability, rationale, risk treatment, and lifecycle status.","outcome_learning":"Identify standards with frequent exceptions and determine whether the standard or its applicability boundary needs revision.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B13-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"governs_exceptions_to","record_id":"D55-B13-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B13-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Standard Verification","concept":"Architecture standards are incomplete when compliance cannot be objectively verified","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_POLICY","question":"How should architecture-standard compliance be verified?","answer":"Each mandatory architecture standard should have a defined verification method appropriate to its requirement. Verification may use automated checks, architecture tests, configuration inspection, runtime evidence, review evidence, or other appropriate mechanisms. A standard without a feasible verification method should be treated as an architectural control requiring refinement.","context":"An organization has many architecture standards but inconsistent evidence of compliance.","objective":"Make architectural standards testable and auditable.","requirements":["Mandatory requirements need verification methods","Verification evidence must be traceable","Automated verification should be used where reliable","Manual verification must identify accountable reviewers"],"constraints":["Not every architectural property is directly machine-checkable","Verification mechanisms can themselves become outdated"],"alternatives":["Documentation-only compliance","Manual review only","Automated and manual verification based on property type"],"evidence":["Unverifiable requirements are difficult to enforce consistently","Different architectural properties require different verification mechanisms"],"tradeoffs":["Automation improves repeatability but can miss semantic properties","Manual review handles nuanced properties but is less reproducible"],"decision":"Define a verification method for every mandatory architecture requirement and distinguish automated evidence from human assessment.","rationale":"Governance needs observable evidence rather than unsupported compliance assertions.","consequences":["Standards must include verification metadata","Verification tooling becomes part of governance infrastructure where justified"],"implementation_implications":["Map standards to verification mechanisms","Version verification rules with standards","Store evidence references","Detect stale verification rules"],"verification_method":"For each mandatory standard, demonstrate a repeatable path from requirement to compliance evidence.","outcome_learning":"Track false positives, false negatives, and unverifiable standards to improve verification mechanisms.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B13-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"verifies","record_id":"D55-B13-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B13-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Standard Conflict","concept":"Conflicting architecture standards require precedence rules and explicit conflict resolution","knowledge_type":"STANDARD_CONFLICT","question":"How should conflicting architecture standards be handled?","answer":"When architecture standards conflict, the organization should not require teams to satisfy mutually incompatible requirements. Standards should have explicit precedence or applicability rules, and unresolved conflicts should be escalated to the authority responsible for the affected architectural boundaries.","context":"Two approved standards impose incompatible architectural constraints on the same system.","objective":"Prevent contradictory governance requirements from reaching implementation teams unresolved.","requirements":["Standards must have identifiers and scope","Conflicts must be detectable","Precedence or conflict-resolution rules must exist","Final resolution must be traceable"],"constraints":["Standards can be created by different groups","Conflicts may emerge only when applied to a specific architecture"],"alternatives":["Require both standards regardless of conflict","Let teams choose silently","Use explicit precedence and conflict resolution"],"evidence":["Mutually incompatible requirements cannot both be satisfied without changing assumptions","Undocumented conflicts create inconsistent implementation decisions"],"tradeoffs":["Formal conflict resolution adds governance work","Unresolved conflicts transfer architectural risk to implementation teams"],"decision":"Use explicit applicability and precedence rules, with escalation for unresolved conflicts.","rationale":"Standards are useful only when their combined constraints are internally coherent for the applicable architecture.","consequences":["Governance must maintain standard relationships","Conflicts become reviewable architectural knowledge"],"implementation_implications":["Model standard dependencies and precedence","Detect overlapping scope","Record conflict resolutions","Version affected standards together when required"],"verification_method":"Run periodic checks for overlapping standards and inspect identified conflicts for documented resolution.","outcome_learning":"Track recurring conflicts to identify duplicate or poorly scoped standards.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B13-R01","D55-B13-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"constrains","record_id":"D55-B13-R01"},{"type":"depends_on","record_id":"D55-B13-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B13-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","batch":"B13","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Standard Failure Mode","concept":"Architecture standards become harmful when they survive after their assumptions have expired","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_FAILURE","question":"What is the risk of retaining obsolete architecture standards?","answer":"An obsolete architecture standard can force inappropriate implementation choices, create unnecessary exceptions, or preserve constraints whose original risk no longer exists. Standards therefore require ownership, review triggers, evidence of continued relevance, and controlled retirement.","context":"An organization has accumulated architecture standards over several years without systematic review.","objective":"Prevent outdated governance constraints from becoming architectural debt.","requirements":["Standards need owners","Review triggers must exist","Original rationale should be traceable","Retirement must be controlled"],"constraints":["Historical rationale may be incomplete","Removing a standard can expose previously controlled risks"],"alternatives":["Permanent standards","Periodic manual review","Trigger-based lifecycle management"],"evidence":["Technology and system constraints change","Standards encode assumptions that can become obsolete"],"tradeoffs":["Lifecycle management requires maintenance","Permanent standards reduce maintenance but increase risk of stale constraints"],"decision":"Manage architecture standards through explicit lifecycle states including active, review-required, superseded, and retired.","rationale":"A governed standard should remain aligned with the architectural risk it was created to control.","consequences":["Standards become lifecycle-managed knowledge","Retirement requires impact assessment"],"implementation_implications":["Store owner and review metadata","Track originating rationale","Define review triggers","Link superseding standards"],"verification_method":"Audit standards for ownership, review status, rationale, and evidence of continued applicability.","outcome_learning":"Analyze standards generating repeated exceptions or no longer affecting architecture to identify retirement candidates.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B13-R02","D55-B13-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B13-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B14-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Review Cadence","concept":"Review cadence should be triggered by architectural change and risk rather than calendar frequency alone","knowledge_type":"REVIEW_TRIGGER","question":"When should architecture governance review be triggered?","answer":"Architecture review should be triggered by material architectural change, new dependencies, changed risk exposure, significant requirement changes, technology lifecycle events, or evidence that an existing decision no longer holds. Calendar-based review can supplement these triggers but should not be the sole mechanism.","context":"An organization wants to determine when existing architecture requires renewed governance attention.","objective":"Define meaningful review triggers.","requirements":["Material change triggers must be defined","Risk changes must be considered","Lifecycle events must be detectable","Review outcomes must be recorded"],"constraints":["Not every change is architecturally material","Some triggers are difficult to detect automatically"],"alternatives":["Fixed annual review","Review only after incidents","Event-driven review with periodic supplementary review"],"evidence":["Architectural assumptions can become invalid before a scheduled review","Material changes create new evidence requiring reassessment"],"tradeoffs":["Event-driven review is more responsive but requires trigger detection","Calendar review is predictable but can miss timely reassessment"],"decision":"Use event-driven architecture review triggers supplemented by periodic portfolio-level review.","rationale":"This aligns governance attention with actual architectural change while retaining a mechanism for discovering unmanaged drift.","consequences":["Systems need identifiable review triggers","Governance records must capture why reassessment occurred"],"implementation_implications":["Define trigger taxonomy","Map triggers to architecture decisions","Generate review tasks from material events where feasible","Retain periodic portfolio review"],"verification_method":"Inspect whether known material architectural events resulted in appropriate review or documented non-applicability.","outcome_learning":"Measure missed triggers and unnecessary reviews to refine trigger definitions.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B14-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Trigger Classification","concept":"Architecture review triggers should distinguish decision-changing events from operational noise","knowledge_type":"TRIGGER_CLASSIFICATION","question":"How should architecture review triggers be classified?","answer":"Triggers should be classified according to whether they can invalidate architectural assumptions, alter requirements, change risk, introduce new dependencies, or materially change operational characteristics. Events that do not affect these properties should not automatically force full architecture review.","context":"A governance system receives many operational and development events and must decide which require architecture reassessment.","objective":"Prevent excessive review while preserving detection of meaningful architectural change.","requirements":["Trigger categories must be defined","Impact criteria must be explicit","Escalation must be possible","Non-material events must be distinguishable"],"constraints":["Event meaning is context-dependent","The same event can be material in one system and immaterial in another"],"alternatives":["Review every event","Review only incidents","Context-aware trigger classification"],"evidence":["Architectural impact depends on system context","Not every implementation event changes architecture"],"tradeoffs":["Broad triggers improve sensitivity but increase review noise","Narrow triggers reduce noise but can miss architectural drift"],"decision":"Classify triggers using context-aware impact criteria linked to affected architectural properties.","rationale":"Governance should react to changes that can invalidate decisions rather than to event volume alone.","consequences":["Trigger rules require architectural context","Some events need lightweight assessment before escalation"],"implementation_implications":["Associate triggers with decision assumptions","Define impact predicates","Record trigger classification","Allow manual escalation"],"verification_method":"Replay representative events and verify that materially relevant events are escalated while irrelevant events are not unnecessarily promoted.","outcome_learning":"Analyze false-positive and false-negative trigger classifications to improve governance detection.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B14-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D55-B14-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B14-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Drift","concept":"Architecture review should be triggered when implementation materially diverges from an approved architectural decision","knowledge_type":"DRIFT_GOVERNANCE","question":"When does implementation drift require architecture review?","answer":"Implementation drift should trigger architecture reassessment when the implementation changes an architectural invariant, boundary, dependency, consistency model, security property, availability characteristic, external contract, or other material decision assumption. Minor implementation variation that preserves the architectural intent does not necessarily require a new architecture decision.","context":"Implemented systems evolve incrementally and may diverge from their original architectural descriptions.","objective":"Distinguish harmless implementation evolution from material architecture drift.","requirements":["Architectural intent must be identifiable","Material properties must be defined","Drift must be detectable or reviewable","Reassessment must update the authoritative decision record"],"constraints":["Some drift is difficult to detect automatically","Architecture documentation may itself be stale"],"alternatives":["Treat all divergence as failure","Ignore implementation drift","Assess drift against architectural invariants"],"evidence":["Implementation can evolve without changing architecture","Material divergence can invalidate original reasoning"],"tradeoffs":["Strict drift detection creates noise","No drift governance allows architectural documentation to become misleading"],"decision":"Govern implementation drift according to changes in architectural intent and invariants rather than textual differences alone.","rationale":"The meaningful boundary is architectural behavior and constraints, not documentation similarity.","consequences":["Architecture records need explicit invariants or decision properties","Drift detection may combine automated and human methods"],"implementation_implications":["Record architectural invariants","Map implementation controls to decisions","Define drift indicators","Create reassessment workflow"],"verification_method":"Compare selected architectural decisions with implementation evidence and determine whether material invariants remain satisfied.","outcome_learning":"Record recurring drift patterns and update architecture controls where drift repeatedly bypasses intended governance.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B14-R01","D55-B14-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"specializes","record_id":"D55-B14-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B14-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Requirement Change","concept":"Material requirement changes can invalidate architecture decisions even when implementation remains unchanged","knowledge_type":"REASSESSMENT_TRIGGER","question":"Why should requirement changes trigger architecture reassessment?","answer":"A material requirement change can invalidate the assumptions or trade-offs underlying an architecture decision even if the existing implementation has not changed. Governance should therefore assess whether the decision still satisfies the new requirement before treating the architecture as unchanged.","context":"Business or product requirements change after architecture decisions have already been implemented.","objective":"Prevent obsolete architecture from being preserved merely because implementation has not changed.","requirements":["Requirements must be traceable to affected decisions","Materiality criteria must exist","Reassessment must evaluate changed constraints and trade-offs"],"constraints":["Minor requirement wording changes may have no architectural effect","Requirement interpretation can be uncertain"],"alternatives":["Review only implementation changes","Review every requirement change","Review requirement changes that can alter architectural assumptions"],"evidence":["Architecture decisions are conditional on requirements and constraints","Changed requirements can change the feasibility or desirability of alternatives"],"tradeoffs":["Broad reassessment creates overhead","Narrow reassessment risks missed architectural invalidation"],"decision":"Trigger architecture reassessment for requirement changes that can alter architectural constraints, invariants, or decision trade-offs.","rationale":"Architecture remains valid only relative to the requirements it was designed to satisfy.","consequences":["Requirement management and architecture governance need traceability","Some requirement changes require decision revision rather than code modification"],"implementation_implications":["Link requirements to architecture decisions","Define material requirement-change triggers","Record reassessment outcomes","Separate requirement satisfaction from implementation status"],"verification_method":"Select changed requirements and verify whether affected architecture decisions were identified and reassessed when materiality criteria were met.","outcome_learning":"Track requirement changes that later cause architecture rework to refine materiality criteria.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B14-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"complements","record_id":"D55-B14-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B14-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B14","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Review Trigger Failure","concept":"Architecture governance becomes reactive when reassessment depends only on incidents","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is incident-only architecture review insufficient?","answer":"Incident-triggered review detects architectural weaknesses only after an adverse event has occurred. It should be one trigger among several, supplemented by requirement changes, dependency changes, technology lifecycle events, observed drift, capacity changes, and other evidence that can invalidate architectural assumptions before an incident.","context":"An organization performs architecture reviews primarily after production incidents.","objective":"Expand governance from purely reactive review toward evidence-based reassessment.","requirements":["Incident review must remain available","Pre-incident triggers must be defined","Triggers must map to architectural assumptions","Review outcomes must be recorded"],"constraints":["Predictive triggers can generate false positives","Not every risk change is observable early"],"alternatives":["Incident-only review","Calendar-only review","Multi-trigger architecture reassessment"],"evidence":["Incidents provide important evidence but occur after failure","Architectural assumptions can change before incidents"],"tradeoffs":["More triggers increase proactive coverage but require monitoring","Incident-only review minimizes governance work but limits early detection"],"decision":"Use incidents as one architecture-review trigger within a broader event-driven reassessment model.","rationale":"Architecture governance should respond to evidence of changing assumptions, not only realized failures.","consequences":["Governance must monitor multiple trigger classes","Incident findings remain valuable but are not the sole source of learning"],"implementation_implications":["Integrate incident findings with architecture decisions","Define proactive trigger classes","Track unresolved architectural risks"],"verification_method":"Audit architecture reviews over a period and classify their triggers to determine whether governance depends disproportionately on incidents.","outcome_learning":"Measure pre-incident reassessments that identified meaningful risks and use the results to refine proactive triggers.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B14-R01","D55-B14-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"failure_mode_of","record_id":"D55-B14-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B15-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Decision Traceability","concept":"Architecture governance should preserve traceability from requirement through decision to implementation and verification","knowledge_type":"TRACEABILITY_MODEL","question":"What should architecture decision traceability connect?","answer":"A useful architecture traceability chain connects relevant requirements and constraints to the architecture decision, evidence and alternatives, implementation implications, verification activities, and observed outcomes. The purpose is to make the reasoning and resulting obligations recoverable rather than merely storing a decision title.","context":"An organization needs to understand why an architectural constraint exists and whether it remains satisfied.","objective":"Create end-to-end architectural decision traceability.","requirements":["Requirements must be identifiable","Decisions must have stable identifiers","Implementation implications must be traceable","Verification evidence must be linked","Outcome information must be retained"],"constraints":["Not every implementation detail needs direct mapping","Traceability can become expensive if modeled at excessive granularity"],"alternatives":["Decision-only records","Requirement-to-decision links","End-to-end traceability for material decisions"],"evidence":["Architectural decisions derive meaning from requirements and constraints","Verification requires knowing what property was intended"],"tradeoffs":["Detailed traceability improves reasoning recovery but increases maintenance","Minimal traceability reduces maintenance but weakens governance evidence"],"decision":"Maintain end-to-end traceability for material architecture decisions while avoiding unnecessary mapping of low-value implementation details.","rationale":"Material decisions need enough context to explain their origin, implementation impact, and verification state.","consequences":["Decision records become connected governance artifacts","Changes can expose affected decisions more reliably"],"implementation_implications":["Use stable decision IDs","Maintain requirement and implementation links","Link verification evidence","Track supersession and reassessment"],"verification_method":"Select material decisions and verify that their requirement, implementation, and verification relationships are recoverable.","outcome_learning":"Measure broken traceability links and use them to improve architecture information maintenance.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B15-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Traceability Granularity","concept":"Traceability granularity should follow architectural significance rather than mapping every artifact indiscriminately","knowledge_type":"TRACEABILITY_POLICY","question":"How granular should architecture traceability be?","answer":"Traceability should be detailed enough to recover the reasoning and verify material architectural obligations but should not require one-to-one mapping of every code artifact. Requirements, major decisions, architectural constraints, material implementation controls, verification evidence, and significant outcomes generally warrant explicit relationships.","context":"A governance system is becoming difficult to maintain because every artifact is required to have direct architecture links.","objective":"Balance traceability usefulness against maintenance cost.","requirements":["Material relationships must remain explicit","Traceability rules must be consistent","Low-value relationships should not obscure important ones"],"constraints":["Artifact inventories can become very large","Architectural significance is context-dependent"],"alternatives":["Full artifact-level mapping","Decision-level mapping only","Risk-based traceability granularity"],"evidence":["Traceability has maintenance cost","Not all implementation artifacts carry architectural significance"],"tradeoffs":["Fine granularity improves local navigation but increases maintenance burden","Coarse granularity is easier to maintain but may lose verification context"],"decision":"Use risk- and significance-based traceability granularity.","rationale":"Traceability should optimize recovery of architectural reasoning and assurance rather than maximize link count.","consequences":["Governance must define materiality criteria","Some implementation artifacts will intentionally remain indirectly mapped"],"implementation_implications":["Define traceability levels","Mark mandatory relationship types","Allow aggregation for low-significance artifacts","Audit material links"],"verification_method":"Review traceability coverage of high-impact decisions and determine whether required relationships are present without unnecessary link proliferation.","outcome_learning":"Identify missing relationships discovered during incidents or reassessments and adjust granularity rules.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B15-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"refines","record_id":"D55-B15-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B15-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Traceability Integrity","concept":"Broken or stale traceability links are governance defects","knowledge_type":"TRACEABILITY_FAILURE","question":"Why must architecture traceability itself be validated?","answer":"Traceability is only useful when relationships remain accurate. A link to a deleted requirement, superseded decision, obsolete implementation component, or outdated verification artifact can create false confidence. Governance should therefore validate the integrity and lifecycle status of important relationships.","context":"An architecture repository contains many links accumulated over multiple releases.","objective":"Prevent stale traceability from misleading architecture reviews.","requirements":["Referenced entities must have stable identity","Lifecycle state must be visible","Broken links must be detectable","Superseded relationships must remain historically interpretable"],"constraints":["Entities evolve and may be renamed","Historical records should not be rewritten merely to hide change"],"alternatives":["No traceability validation","Manual validation only","Automated integrity checks plus targeted review"],"evidence":["Traceability depends on relationship correctness","Lifecycle changes can invalidate naive references"],"tradeoffs":["Automated integrity checks improve detection but require metadata","Manual review can assess semantics but is expensive"],"decision":"Validate traceability integrity mechanically where possible and review semantic validity for material relationships.","rationale":"Relationship existence and relationship correctness are separate properties.","consequences":["Governance repositories require relationship integrity checks","Historical supersession must remain visible"],"implementation_implications":["Use stable IDs","Track entity lifecycle state","Detect dangling references","Represent supersession explicitly","Separate historical from current relationships"],"verification_method":"Run integrity checks for missing references and sample material relationships for semantic validity.","outcome_learning":"Classify broken links by cause and improve repository lifecycle controls accordingly.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B15-R01","D55-B15-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"verifies","record_id":"D55-B15-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B15-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Traceability and Change Impact","concept":"Decision traceability enables more precise change-impact analysis","knowledge_type":"CHANGE_IMPACT","question":"How can architecture traceability support change-impact analysis?","answer":"When requirements, decisions, implementation implications, and verification obligations are linked, a change can be analyzed by traversing affected relationships rather than relying solely on informal knowledge. This does not guarantee complete impact discovery, but it provides a structured starting point for identifying potentially affected architectural constraints and evidence.","context":"A material requirement or platform change may affect several architectural decisions.","objective":"Use architecture knowledge relationships to improve impact analysis.","requirements":["Relevant relationships must exist","Decision scope must be explicit","Affected entities must have stable identifiers","Impact analysis must preserve uncertainty"],"constraints":["Incomplete traceability can miss dependencies","Relationship traversal may over-approximate impact"],"alternatives":["Manual impact analysis","Repository search","Relationship-based impact analysis with human validation"],"evidence":["Explicit relationships expose known dependencies","Impact analysis requires interpretation beyond simple textual matching"],"tradeoffs":["Graph-based traversal improves coverage of known relationships but can produce false positives","Manual analysis provides context but depends heavily on expert memory"],"decision":"Use traceability relationships as an impact-analysis input, followed by contextual engineering validation.","rationale":"Structured relationships improve discoverability while avoiding the false assumption that graph traversal alone proves impact.","consequences":["Architecture repositories become useful for change planning","Incomplete relationships remain a known limitation"],"implementation_implications":["Expose decision dependency graphs","Classify relationship types","Propagate affected verification obligations","Record analyst-confirmed impacts separately from inferred impacts"],"verification_method":"Compare predicted impact candidates with engineer-confirmed impacts for representative architectural changes.","outcome_learning":"Measure missed and irrelevant impact candidates to improve relationship coverage and traversal rules.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B15-R01","D55-B15-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"uses","record_id":"D55-B15-R01"},{"type":"depends_on_integrity_of","record_id":"D55-B15-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B15-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B15","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Traceability Failure Mode","concept":"Traceability can become misleading when links are treated as proof rather than evidence of relationships","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is a dangerous misuse of architecture traceability?","answer":"A traceability link identifies a recorded relationship; it does not by itself prove correctness, completeness, causality, or current applicability. Treating links as proof can conceal missing relationships, stale assumptions, or incorrect mappings.","context":"An architecture repository is heavily linked and reviewers assume that linked artifacts fully describe architectural dependencies.","objective":"Prevent false confidence from highly connected architecture records.","requirements":["Relationship semantics must be explicit","Current validity must be distinguishable","Inference must be separated from verified fact","Important relationships require evidence where appropriate"],"constraints":["Repositories often contain inferred or manually maintained relationships","Complete dependency knowledge is rarely guaranteed"],"alternatives":["Treat all links as authoritative","Treat links as navigation only","Use typed relationships with evidence and validation state"],"evidence":["A relationship record and the truth of the relationship are separate properties","Architectural repositories can become stale"],"tradeoffs":["Rich relationship metadata increases model complexity","Simple links are easier to maintain but less informative"],"decision":"Represent relationship type, provenance, and validation state where relationship correctness materially affects governance.","rationale":"Typed and evidence-aware relationships reduce the risk of mistaking navigation metadata for verified architectural truth.","consequences":["Architecture knowledge models become more expressive","Relationship maintenance becomes more deliberate"],"implementation_implications":["Define relationship types","Store provenance for important relationships","Separate inferred and verified relationships","Retain historical relationship state"],"verification_method":"Inspect material relationships and determine whether their type and validation state support the claims made from them.","outcome_learning":"Track incorrect relationships discovered during reviews and update relationship creation and validation controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B15-R03","D55-B15-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"failure_mode_of","record_id":"D55-B15-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B16-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Metrics","concept":"Architecture governance metrics should measure decision quality and risk control rather than activity volume alone","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_METRICS","question":"What should architecture governance metrics measure?","answer":"Useful architecture governance metrics should measure properties such as review timeliness, unresolved material risks, decision reversals, exception patterns, traceability integrity, validation coverage, and recurring architectural failure modes. Counts of reviews or approvals alone measure activity rather than governance effectiveness.","context":"An organization wants metrics to evaluate whether architecture governance is functioning effectively.","objective":"Define metrics that provide evidence about governance quality.","requirements":["Metrics must have clear definitions","Activity metrics must be separated from outcome indicators","Metrics must support trend analysis","Measurement limitations must be explicit"],"constraints":["Architecture outcomes can have long feedback cycles","Some metrics are indirect indicators"],"alternatives":["Approval counts","Composite governance score","Multiple descriptive indicators with contextual interpretation"],"evidence":["Process volume does not establish technical effectiveness","Outcome indicators require interpretation and time"],"tradeoffs":["More meaningful metrics require richer data","Simple counts are easy to collect but weakly diagnostic"],"decision":"Use a portfolio of descriptive governance indicators rather than a single architecture-quality score.","rationale":"Architecture quality is multidimensional and a single score can conceal important failure modes or create misleading optimization pressure.","consequences":["Governance reporting becomes multidimensional","Metric interpretation requires context"],"implementation_implications":["Define metric semantics","Separate leading and lagging indicators","Record measurement periods","Track data quality and coverage"],"verification_method":"Review metric definitions and verify that reported values can be traced to source records and do not conflate activity with outcomes.","outcome_learning":"Analyze which indicators correlate with useful governance discoveries and retire metrics that encourage counterproductive behavior.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B16-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Leading Indicators","concept":"Leading indicators can expose governance pressure before architectural outcomes are observable","knowledge_type":"LEADING_INDICATOR","question":"What are useful leading indicators for architecture governance?","answer":"Leading indicators can include increasing exception requests, repeated unresolved review findings, growing decision latency, rising dependency complexity, missing verification evidence, or increasing numbers of decisions requiring reassessment. These indicators do not prove architectural failure but can identify areas requiring investigation.","context":"Architecture outcomes may take months to become visible through incidents or major rework.","objective":"Detect governance stress before lagging outcomes appear.","requirements":["Indicators must be defined precisely","Signals must be distinguishable from causal conclusions","Thresholds should trigger investigation rather than automatic judgment"],"constraints":["Leading indicators may have false positives","Organizational behavior can distort measurements"],"alternatives":["Use only incidents and defects","Use leading indicators","Use leading and lagging indicators together"],"evidence":["Some governance problems manifest as process or evidence degradation before technical outcomes","Signals are not equivalent to causes"],"tradeoffs":["Leading indicators improve early visibility but require careful interpretation","Lagging indicators are more concrete but arrive later"],"decision":"Use leading indicators as investigation triggers alongside outcome evidence, not as standalone proof of architectural quality.","rationale":"Early signals are useful when their uncertainty is preserved and validated through additional evidence.","consequences":["Governance dashboards need contextual interpretation","Threshold breaches should initiate analysis rather than automatic remediation"],"implementation_implications":["Define indicator semantics","Track signal provenance","Correlate indicators with later findings","Avoid opaque composite scores"],"verification_method":"Review historical signals against subsequent documented findings to assess whether they were useful for investigation.","outcome_learning":"Retain indicators with demonstrated diagnostic value and recalibrate thresholds using observed false-positive and false-negative behavior.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B16-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B16-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B16-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Outcome Metrics","concept":"Architecture decision reversals are informative only when interpreted against decision context and evidence quality","knowledge_type":"OUTCOME_METRIC","question":"What can decision reversals tell architecture governance?","answer":"A decision reversal can indicate changed requirements, new evidence, invalid assumptions, implementation constraints, or an initially unsuitable decision. Reversal count alone is not a reliable quality score because legitimate evolution and improved evidence can also cause reversals.","context":"An organization observes that some architecture decisions are frequently replaced.","objective":"Use decision reversal data without creating misleading incentives.","requirements":["Reversal reasons must be classified","Original assumptions should be available","Changed evidence must be recorded","Reversal should be distinguished from correction"],"constraints":["Some reversals are expected during system evolution","Historical decision context may be incomplete"],"alternatives":["Treat reversals as failures","Ignore reversals","Analyze reversals by cause and context"],"evidence":["Architecture decisions are conditional and can become invalid","New evidence can legitimately change decisions"],"tradeoffs":["Simple reversal counts are easy to report but ambiguous","Cause-based analysis is more informative but requires richer records"],"decision":"Analyze decision reversals by cause, original evidence, changed conditions, and consequences rather than treating reversal frequency as a standalone quality measure.","rationale":"This preserves learning value without penalizing legitimate architectural adaptation.","consequences":["Decision records need supersession rationale","Governance analytics require contextual categories"],"implementation_implications":["Classify reversal causes","Link replacement decisions","Preserve original records","Record changed assumptions or evidence"],"verification_method":"Sample reversed decisions and verify that the replacement rationale and triggering evidence are documented.","outcome_learning":"Aggregate reversal causes to identify recurring invalid assumptions, changing constraints, or governance gaps.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B16-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"provides_outcome_signal_for","record_id":"D55-B16-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B16-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Metric Gaming","concept":"Architecture metrics can create harmful behavior when optimized as targets instead of interpreted as evidence","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_FAILURE","question":"How can architecture metrics distort governance behavior?","answer":"Metrics can become harmful when teams optimize the measured quantity instead of the underlying architectural objective. For example, minimizing review duration can encourage insufficient analysis, while maximizing documented decisions can reward unnecessary governance activity. Metrics should therefore be treated as evidence and interpreted with context rather than used as simplistic targets.","context":"An organization introduces architecture governance metrics for management reporting.","objective":"Prevent measurement from distorting architectural decision behavior.","requirements":["Metric purpose must be explicit","Context must accompany reported values","Target-setting must be cautious","Outcome evidence must remain available"],"constraints":["Management often prefers simple numerical targets","Some governance effects are difficult to measure directly"],"alternatives":["Strict metric targets","No metrics","Descriptive metrics with contextual review"],"evidence":["Measured behavior can diverge from intended outcomes when incentives are attached","Architecture quality is multidimensional"],"tradeoffs":["Targets improve focus but can create gaming pressure","Descriptive metrics preserve nuance but require interpretation"],"decision":"Use architecture metrics primarily for diagnosis and trend analysis, with limited use of targets where measurement semantics are robust.","rationale":"Governance metrics should illuminate system behavior rather than become a substitute for architectural judgment.","consequences":["Metric dashboards require explanatory context","Management should avoid interpreting isolated values as architecture quality"],"implementation_implications":["Document metric limitations","Track measurement coverage","Require contextual commentary for material changes","Review incentives created by metrics"],"verification_method":"Inspect governance reporting for evidence that metrics are interpreted with context and not used as standalone architecture-quality judgments.","outcome_learning":"Monitor behavior changes after metric introduction and retire or redesign metrics that produce counterproductive incentives.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B16-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"failure_mode_of","record_id":"D55-B16-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B16-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B16","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Metric Data Quality","concept":"Architecture governance metrics require explicit measurement provenance and coverage boundaries","knowledge_type":"METRIC_PROVENANCE","question":"Why should architecture metrics include provenance and coverage information?","answer":"A metric without provenance can be difficult to interpret or reproduce. Governance metrics should identify their source records, measurement period, population, exclusions, calculation semantics, and data-quality limitations so reviewers can determine what the number actually represents.","context":"Architecture metrics are aggregated from multiple repositories and review records.","objective":"Make governance measurements reproducible and interpretable.","requirements":["Metric definitions must be stable","Source population must be identifiable","Calculation semantics must be documented","Missing or excluded data must be visible"],"constraints":["Data may come from heterogeneous systems","Historical data may have incomplete coverage"],"alternatives":["Report raw values without metadata","Document only the formula","Provide full metric provenance and coverage metadata"],"evidence":["A numerical result depends on its population and calculation method","Missing data can materially alter interpretation"],"tradeoffs":["Provenance metadata increases reporting complexity","Unqualified metrics are easier to display but harder to trust"],"decision":"Require provenance and coverage metadata for material architecture governance metrics.","rationale":"A metric is useful for engineering reasoning only when its scope and derivation can be understood.","consequences":["Metrics become auditable artifacts","Historical comparisons may require normalization when definitions change"],"implementation_implications":["Store metric definitions and versions","Record source populations","Track exclusions","Version calculation logic"],"verification_method":"Recalculate selected metrics from recorded source populations and compare results with reported values.","outcome_learning":"Record discrepancies and update metric pipelines when provenance gaps or definition drift are discovered.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B16-R01","D55-B16-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"supports","record_id":"D55-B16-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B17-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Security Architecture Governance","concept":"Security-significant architecture decisions require security properties to be explicit in governance","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should security affect architecture governance?","answer":"Architecture governance should explicitly identify security boundaries, trust assumptions, sensitive data flows, privilege boundaries, threat-relevant dependencies, and security invariants when they materially affect a decision. Security review should be integrated according to risk rather than treated as an unrelated approval step.","context":"An architectural decision changes trust boundaries, access paths, data handling, or security-sensitive dependencies.","objective":"Ensure security consequences are represented in architecture governance.","requirements":["Security-relevant assumptions must be identified","Affected trust boundaries must be documented","Material security risks require appropriate review","Security verification must be distinguishable from approval"],"constraints":["Security impact depends on system context","Not every architecture decision has material security implications"],"alternatives":["Separate security review for every decision","Ignore security unless an incident occurs","Risk-based security integration into architecture governance"],"evidence":["Architectural boundaries influence security properties","Security consequences can arise from design choices before implementation"],"tradeoffs":["Integrated review improves contextual reasoning but requires security expertise","Separate review can provide independence but may duplicate architecture analysis"],"decision":"Integrate security analysis into architecture governance whenever the decision can materially affect security properties, with independent security review for defined high-risk cases.","rationale":"Security is an architectural property that can be affected by boundaries, data flows, dependencies, and authority models.","consequences":["Security assumptions become part of architecture decision records","Some architectural decisions require specialized security evidence"],"implementation_implications":["Record security invariants","Link threat assessments where applicable","Define security review triggers","Track security verification evidence"],"verification_method":"Review security-significant decisions for documented boundaries, assumptions, risks, and corresponding verification evidence.","outcome_learning":"Analyze security findings against architectural decisions to identify recurring governance omissions.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B17-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Security Exceptions","concept":"Security-related architecture exceptions require explicit risk acceptance and compensating controls","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_EXCEPTION","question":"How should security-sensitive architecture exceptions be handled?","answer":"A security-sensitive architecture exception should identify the affected security property, threat or risk, scope, accountable risk owner, compensating controls, residual uncertainty, and reassessment conditions. Technical approval alone should not silently transfer unresolved security risk.","context":"A system cannot immediately satisfy an established security architecture requirement.","objective":"Govern security deviations without hiding residual risk.","requirements":["Security requirement must be identified","Residual risk must be documented","Risk ownership must be explicit","Compensating controls must be documented","Reassessment conditions must exist"],"constraints":["Emergency delivery may require temporary deviation","Residual risk may not be precisely quantifiable"],"alternatives":["Block all deviations","Allow engineering approval only","Formal security exception with risk ownership"],"evidence":["Security deviations can alter exposure and trust assumptions","Compensating controls can change but do not erase residual risk"],"tradeoffs":["Formal exceptions add process overhead","Unrecorded deviations create invisible security risk"],"decision":"Require explicit security exception governance with documented residual risk and accountable ownership.","rationale":"Security governance must preserve visibility of risk even when immediate compliance is impractical.","consequences":["Security exceptions become auditable risk artifacts","Temporary deviations require lifecycle management"],"implementation_implications":["Create security exception records","Link affected architecture decisions","Track compensating controls","Define reassessment or expiration triggers"],"verification_method":"Audit security exceptions for risk ownership, compensating controls, scope, and reassessment status.","outcome_learning":"Analyze recurring security exceptions to identify architectural standards or platform capabilities requiring improvement.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B17-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"specializes","record_id":"D55-B17-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B17-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Security Verification","concept":"Security architecture claims require verification evidence appropriate to the claimed property","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_VERIFICATION","question":"How should security architecture claims be verified?","answer":"Security architecture claims should be linked to verification methods appropriate to the property being claimed. Depending on the claim, evidence may include design review, configuration inspection, automated policy checks, security testing, threat-model validation, access-control testing, or runtime evidence. Passing one security test does not prove unrelated security properties.","context":"Architecture documentation states that a system satisfies several security properties.","objective":"Prevent broad security claims from being inferred from narrow verification evidence.","requirements":["Security claims must be explicit","Verification scope must match the claim","Evidence must identify limitations","Unverified properties must remain distinguishable"],"constraints":["Security properties are often interdependent","Testing cannot establish every possible security condition"],"alternatives":["Documentation-only security claims","Single security test","Property-specific verification evidence"],"evidence":["Verification establishes only what its method and scope support","Security properties require context-specific assurance"],"tradeoffs":["Broader verification increases assurance effort","Narrow verification is cheaper but supports narrower claims"],"decision":"Require security claims to be bounded by explicit verification scope and evidence.","rationale":"This prevents overclaiming security assurance from incomplete testing or design review.","consequences":["Security architecture records become more precise","Verification evidence must retain scope and limitations"],"implementation_implications":["Map claims to verification methods","Record test scope","Store verification status separately from design approval","Track unresolved security assumptions"],"verification_method":"Inspect security claims and determine whether corresponding evidence actually covers the stated property.","outcome_learning":"Record security findings that escape claimed controls and use them to refine verification coverage.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B17-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"verifies","record_id":"D55-B17-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B17-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Security Dependency Changes","concept":"Changes in security-relevant dependencies can invalidate existing architecture assumptions","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_REASSESSMENT","question":"When should a security-relevant dependency change trigger architecture reassessment?","answer":"A dependency change should trigger security architecture reassessment when it changes trust boundaries, privileges, data handling, attack surface, authentication or authorization assumptions, cryptographic properties, or other material security characteristics relied upon by the architecture decision.","context":"A system replaces or substantially changes an external service, library, identity provider, platform, or infrastructure dependency.","objective":"Detect security architecture assumptions invalidated by dependency changes.","requirements":["Security-relevant dependency properties must be identifiable","Affected decisions must be traceable","Material changes must trigger reassessment"],"constraints":["Dependency behavior may not be fully visible","Minor version changes may or may not affect security properties depending on context"],"alternatives":["Review every dependency change","Review only incidents","Review changes that can alter security assumptions"],"evidence":["Dependencies participate in system security boundaries and trust relationships","Security impact depends on changed properties rather than dependency name alone"],"tradeoffs":["Broad reassessment increases workload","Narrow reassessment requires accurate security-impact classification"],"decision":"Use security-property impact criteria to determine whether dependency changes require architecture reassessment.","rationale":"Security governance should respond to changes in security assumptions, not simply dependency replacement events.","consequences":["Dependency governance needs security-impact metadata","Architecture decisions need dependency traceability"],"implementation_implications":["Track security-relevant dependency attributes","Link dependencies to architecture decisions","Define reassessment triggers","Preserve verification evidence across dependency changes"],"verification_method":"Review material dependency changes and determine whether affected security properties and architecture decisions were reassessed.","outcome_learning":"Analyze security findings associated with dependency changes to improve trigger criteria.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B17-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B17-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B17-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B17","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Security Governance Failure","concept":"Treating security approval as equivalent to security verification creates false assurance","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why should security approval and security verification remain separate states?","answer":"Security approval records a governance judgment at a particular point in time, while security verification provides evidence about specific security properties. Approval can exist before implementation or testing is complete, and verification can reveal limitations that require the decision to be revisited.","context":"A project marks architecture as security-approved before all security verification activities have completed.","objective":"Prevent governance approval from being mistaken for completed security assurance.","requirements":["Approval state must be separate from verification state","Pending verification must remain visible","Verification evidence must be linked to claims"],"constraints":["Delivery workflows may prefer a single status field","Some organizations use approval as a release gate"],"alternatives":["Single security-approved status","Separate approval and verification states","Approval blocked until every verification activity completes"],"evidence":["Approval and verification answer different questions","Some verification necessarily occurs after implementation"],"tradeoffs":["Separate states preserve truth but increase workflow complexity","Single state is simpler but can overstate assurance"],"decision":"Represent security governance approval and security verification as separate lifecycle states.","rationale":"The distinction preserves accurate knowledge about what has been authorized, what has been verified, and what remains uncertain.","consequences":["Security dashboards need multiple status dimensions","Release controls must define which verification states are mandatory"],"implementation_implications":["Model approval separately from verification","Link verification evidence","Prevent VERIFIED from being inferred from APPROVED","Track incomplete assurance explicitly"],"verification_method":"Inspect security decision records and verify that approval does not automatically imply completed verification.","outcome_learning":"Analyze cases where approved designs later fail verification and use them to improve governance sequencing.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B17-R01","D55-B17-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"failure_mode_of","record_id":"D55-B17-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B18-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Privacy Architecture Governance","concept":"Privacy-sensitive architecture decisions require explicit data-use and data-lifecycle considerations","knowledge_type":"PRIVACY_GOVERNANCE","question":"What privacy concerns should architecture governance capture?","answer":"Privacy-sensitive architecture decisions should identify relevant data categories, collection purpose, processing purpose, retention behavior, access boundaries, sharing paths, deletion requirements, and material privacy risks. Governance should distinguish documented design intent from verified runtime behavior.","context":"An architecture decision introduces or changes collection, processing, storage, sharing, or retention of potentially sensitive information.","objective":"Ensure privacy implications are considered as architectural properties.","requirements":["Relevant data categories must be identified","Purpose and processing boundaries must be explicit","Retention and deletion implications must be considered","Access and sharing paths must be represented"],"constraints":["Privacy requirements depend on jurisdiction and system context","Not every data element carries the same sensitivity"],"alternatives":["Privacy review only after implementation","Generic privacy checklist","Risk-based privacy architecture analysis"],"evidence":["Data architecture determines collection, processing, access, and retention behavior","Privacy requirements can impose architectural constraints"],"tradeoffs":["Detailed privacy analysis requires data-flow knowledge","Generic controls may miss system-specific processing risks"],"decision":"Integrate privacy analysis into architecture governance when architecture materially affects data collection, processing, retention, access, or sharing.","rationale":"Privacy properties are often determined or constrained by architectural choices before implementation.","consequences":["Architecture decisions need data-lifecycle context","Privacy verification becomes linked to architectural claims"],"implementation_implications":["Model relevant data flows","Record retention and deletion requirements","Link privacy controls to decisions","Define privacy review triggers"],"verification_method":"Inspect privacy-sensitive architecture decisions for data-flow, purpose, retention, access, and verification evidence appropriate to the context.","outcome_learning":"Analyze privacy findings and data-lifecycle exceptions to improve architectural review criteria.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B18-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Data Lifecycle","concept":"Architecture governance should distinguish data retention requirements from storage convenience","knowledge_type":"DATA_LIFECYCLE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why should retention be an explicit architecture concern?","answer":"Data retention should be governed by requirements and justified operational or legal needs rather than by indefinite storage convenience. Architecture decisions should identify retention assumptions, deletion behavior, archival boundaries, and dependencies that prevent timely removal where those properties materially affect the system.","context":"A system retains application or user data across operational and archival stores.","objective":"Ensure data lifecycle behavior is an explicit architectural decision where relevant.","requirements":["Retention requirements must be identifiable","Deletion behavior must be considered","Derived copies must be considered","Retention exceptions must be traceable"],"constraints":["Some systems require historical records","Copies can exist across caches, backups, replicas, and derived stores"],"alternatives":["Indefinite retention","Fixed retention for all data","Requirement-driven retention by data category and purpose"],"evidence":["Data can propagate across multiple architectural components","Retention behavior affects storage, privacy, operations, and recovery design"],"tradeoffs":["Shorter retention can reduce exposure and storage burden but may affect historical capabilities","Longer retention can support operational needs but increases lifecycle obligations"],"decision":"Represent material retention and deletion requirements explicitly in architecture decisions and data-flow design.","rationale":"Retention is an architectural behavior when data propagates across storage and recovery mechanisms.","consequences":["Deletion requirements may affect multiple subsystems","Architecture reviews must account for secondary data copies where relevant"],"implementation_implications":["Map data lifecycle across stores","Define deletion semantics","Document backup/archive treatment","Verify derived-store handling"],"verification_method":"Trace representative data through its lifecycle and verify that retention and deletion behavior matches the documented architectural requirement.","outcome_learning":"Record deletion failures or unexpected retained copies and update architecture controls accordingly.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B18-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"specializes","record_id":"D55-B18-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B18-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Privacy Verification","concept":"Privacy architecture claims should specify the scope and limitations of verification evidence","knowledge_type":"PRIVACY_VERIFICATION","question":"How should privacy architecture claims be verified?","answer":"Privacy claims should be mapped to evidence that tests or inspects the specific property being claimed, such as data-flow restrictions, access controls, retention behavior, deletion behavior, or purpose-specific processing. A general privacy review should not be treated as proof of every privacy property.","context":"An architecture record states that a system satisfies privacy requirements.","objective":"Prevent broad privacy assurance claims from exceeding available evidence.","requirements":["Privacy claims must be explicit","Verification scope must match the claim","Evidence limitations must be visible","Runtime behavior must not be inferred solely from design documentation"],"constraints":["Some privacy properties require production or integration evidence","Verification coverage can vary by environment"],"alternatives":["Documentation-only assurance","Generic privacy review","Property-specific privacy verification"],"evidence":["Design intent and runtime behavior can differ","Specific privacy properties require corresponding evidence"],"tradeoffs":["Detailed verification increases assurance effort","Generic review is faster but supports weaker claims"],"decision":"Bound privacy claims by explicit verification scope and distinguish design approval from verified behavior.","rationale":"Accurate governance requires preserving the difference between intended architecture and demonstrated behavior.","consequences":["Privacy records need evidence links","Unverified properties remain explicitly unresolved"],"implementation_implications":["Map privacy requirements to verification activities","Record environment and scope","Track incomplete verification","Preserve evidence provenance"],"verification_method":"Compare documented privacy claims with the exact properties covered by their verification evidence.","outcome_learning":"Use privacy verification gaps and findings to improve architecture requirements and test coverage.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B18-R01","D55-B18-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"verifies","record_id":"D55-B18-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B18-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Privacy Boundary Changes","concept":"Changes in data-sharing boundaries can require architecture reassessment even without changes to storage technology","knowledge_type":"PRIVACY_REASSESSMENT","question":"When should a change in data sharing trigger architecture reassessment?","answer":"A data-sharing change should trigger privacy architecture reassessment when it changes who or what can access data, why data is processed, where it flows, which systems receive it, how long it is retained, or which privacy assumptions the existing decision relies upon. The storage technology itself need not change for the architectural privacy posture to change.","context":"An existing system begins sharing a data set with an additional internal or external component.","objective":"Detect privacy-impacting architectural changes that are not obvious infrastructure changes.","requirements":["Data-sharing boundaries must be represented","Affected privacy assumptions must be identifiable","Material sharing changes must trigger reassessment"],"constraints":["Data flows can be dynamic","Shared data may be transformed before transfer"],"alternatives":["Review only storage changes","Review every data transfer","Review transfers that alter privacy properties"],"evidence":["Data-sharing boundaries are part of data architecture","Privacy impact depends on purpose, recipients, and access conditions"],"tradeoffs":["Broad review increases coverage but creates workload","Narrow review requires reliable impact classification"],"decision":"Use privacy-property impact criteria to trigger reassessment for material changes in data sharing.","rationale":"Privacy architecture is affected by data flow and purpose, not merely by storage implementation.","consequences":["Data-flow changes become governance events","Architecture records need recipient and purpose context"],"implementation_implications":["Map data-sharing relationships","Record recipient classes","Define privacy-impact triggers","Link changes to affected decisions"],"verification_method":"Review material data-sharing changes and determine whether relevant privacy decisions were reassessed.","outcome_learning":"Analyze privacy findings related to sharing changes and refine trigger criteria.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B18-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B18-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B18-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B18","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Privacy Governance Failure","concept":"Privacy architecture governance fails when data lineage ends at the primary system of record","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is primary-store-only data lineage insufficient for privacy governance?","answer":"Privacy-relevant data can propagate into caches, search indexes, analytics systems, logs, backups, exports, derived datasets, and other secondary stores. Limiting architecture governance to the primary system of record can therefore miss material retention, access, sharing, or deletion behavior.","context":"A privacy architecture review documents the main database but not downstream data stores.","objective":"Prevent incomplete data architecture from producing false privacy assurance.","requirements":["Material downstream data stores must be considered","Data propagation boundaries must be identifiable","Lifecycle requirements must address applicable derived copies"],"constraints":["Not every transient copy has equal architectural significance","Some infrastructure-managed copies are difficult to enumerate"],"alternatives":["Primary-store-only governance","Map every transient copy","Risk-based lineage across material downstream stores"],"evidence":["Architectural data flows can create secondary representations","Lifecycle obligations may depend on copies outside the primary database"],"tradeoffs":["Broader lineage improves coverage but increases complexity","Narrow lineage simplifies governance but can omit important behavior"],"decision":"Use risk-based data lineage that includes material downstream representations relevant to privacy requirements.","rationale":"The architectural data lifecycle extends beyond the primary system of record when downstream stores affect access, retention, or deletion properties.","consequences":["Privacy governance requires broader architectural data-flow visibility","Some transient infrastructure behavior may remain explicitly bounded as out of scope"],"implementation_implications":["Classify downstream stores","Map material data propagation","Link lifecycle controls to stores","Record lineage uncertainty"],"verification_method":"Trace selected sensitive data flows through material downstream systems and compare observed paths with architecture records.","outcome_learning":"Record unexpected data copies discovered during verification and improve lineage coverage rules.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B18-R02","D55-B18-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"failure_mode_of","record_id":"D55-B18-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B19-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Maintainability Governance","concept":"Architecture governance should preserve maintainability constraints that materially affect long-term system change","knowledge_type":"MAINTAINABILITY_GOVERNANCE","question":"What maintainability concerns belong in architecture governance?","answer":"Governance should capture maintainability properties when architectural choices materially affect coupling, change isolation, deployment independence, testing complexity, operational ownership, technology lifecycle, or the ability to evolve interfaces and data models. Maintainability should be expressed as architectural constraints or expected properties rather than vague preferences.","context":"An organization evaluates architectures partly on their ability to evolve safely over time.","objective":"Make material maintainability consequences explicit in architecture decisions.","requirements":["Material maintainability concerns must be identified","Expected evolution boundaries must be explicit","Ownership implications must be visible","Verification methods should exist for significant maintainability claims"],"constraints":["Maintainability is partly context-dependent","Some long-term effects are difficult to measure before implementation"],"alternatives":["Ignore maintainability until technical debt appears","Use generic maintainability principles","Capture context-specific maintainability constraints"],"evidence":["Architecture influences coupling and change propagation","Technology and ownership choices affect long-term maintenance effort"],"tradeoffs":["Additional architectural constraints may limit short-term implementation flexibility","Ignoring maintainability can increase future change cost"],"decision":"Represent material maintainability requirements explicitly when they affect architectural choice or long-term system evolution.","rationale":"Maintainability is an architectural concern when structure, ownership, and dependency choices constrain future change.","consequences":["Architecture decisions include explicit evolution assumptions","Maintainability evidence can be reassessed as the system changes"],"implementation_implications":["Define change boundaries","Record ownership and dependency assumptions","Identify lifecycle-sensitive technologies","Define maintainability verification indicators"],"verification_method":"Evaluate whether documented maintainability assumptions correspond to observable architecture properties and actual change boundaries.","outcome_learning":"Analyze recurring change friction, dependency coupling, and maintenance findings to reassess architectural assumptions.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B19-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Technology Lifecycle","concept":"Technology lifecycle risk should be represented when architecture depends on components approaching support or compatibility boundaries","knowledge_type":"LIFECYCLE_GOVERNANCE","question":"When should technology lifecycle affect architecture governance?","answer":"Technology lifecycle should become an explicit architecture concern when a dependency's support horizon, compatibility constraints, ecosystem viability, or migration difficulty materially affects system risk or future change. Lifecycle status should be evidence-backed and time-sensitive rather than treated as permanent architecture truth.","context":"An architecture relies on a technology whose future maintenance or compatibility may become constrained.","objective":"Prevent lifecycle risk from remaining invisible until forced migration is required.","requirements":["Lifecycle assumptions must be identified","Relevant version information must be recorded","Migration implications must be considered","Review triggers must exist for material lifecycle changes"],"constraints":["Lifecycle information changes over time","Support status depends on vendor, project, deployment, and organizational context"],"alternatives":["Ignore lifecycle until failure","Migrate immediately whenever lifecycle changes","Risk-based lifecycle governance"],"evidence":["Technology lifecycle affects future maintenance and migration constraints","Architecture decisions can create migration coupling"],"tradeoffs":["Early migration can reduce future risk but has immediate cost","Delayed migration reduces short-term disruption but can increase future constraints"],"decision":"Treat material technology lifecycle changes as architecture reassessment triggers rather than automatic migration commands.","rationale":"Lifecycle information should update architectural risk assessment while leaving migration timing dependent on system context and evidence.","consequences":["Architecture repositories need version and lifecycle metadata","Lifecycle events can initiate reassessment"],"implementation_implications":["Track dependency versions","Record lifecycle assumptions","Define reassessment thresholds","Estimate migration constraints without fabricating certainty"],"verification_method":"Inspect lifecycle-sensitive architecture decisions and verify that current dependency information and reassessment triggers are maintained.","outcome_learning":"Compare planned lifecycle assumptions with actual migration effort and update governance heuristics based on observed evidence.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B19-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"specializes","record_id":"D55-B19-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B19-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Evolution Boundaries","concept":"Architecture governance should make intended change boundaries explicit when evolvability is a material requirement","knowledge_type":"EVOLVABILITY_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why should architecture decisions define evolution boundaries?","answer":"Explicit evolution boundaries identify which components, interfaces, data contracts, or capabilities are expected to change independently and which are intentionally coupled. This helps reviewers assess whether the architecture supports the required evolution model and whether implementation changes violate intended boundaries.","context":"A system is expected to evolve independently across teams or components.","objective":"Make architectural assumptions about future change explicit.","requirements":["Expected change boundaries must be identified","Coupling assumptions must be visible","Interface stability expectations must be documented","Boundary violations must be reviewable"],"constraints":["Future changes cannot be predicted completely","Some coupling is intentional"],"alternatives":["No explicit evolution model","Document all future changes","Document expected independent-change boundaries"],"evidence":["Architecture structure influences change propagation","Explicit boundaries improve reasoning about dependency impact"],"tradeoffs":["Explicit boundaries require maintenance","Undocumented boundaries allow flexibility but make impact reasoning harder"],"decision":"Document only material evolution boundaries that influence architecture selection, dependency design, or governance risk.","rationale":"The goal is not to predict every future change but to make significant coupling assumptions inspectable.","consequences":["Architecture records contain evolution assumptions","Boundary changes can trigger reassessment"],"implementation_implications":["Define independent-change expectations","Map interface ownership","Record coupling rationale","Add boundary-change review triggers"],"verification_method":"Evaluate representative changes and determine whether they respect documented evolution boundaries or appropriately trigger reassessment.","outcome_learning":"Record boundary violations and resulting change costs to refine architecture assumptions.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B19-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"specializes","record_id":"D55-B19-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B19-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Maintainability Verification","concept":"Maintainability claims require observable evidence rather than subjective architectural labels","knowledge_type":"MAINTAINABILITY_VERIFICATION","question":"How should maintainability claims be verified?","answer":"Maintainability claims should be tied to observable architectural or engineering evidence such as dependency structure, change isolation, testability, deployment boundaries, ownership clarity, interface stability, or measured change impact where appropriate. Labels such as modular or maintainable are insufficient without supporting evidence.","context":"An architecture review describes a proposed design as highly maintainable.","objective":"Prevent subjective maintainability claims from being accepted without evidence.","requirements":["Claims must be specific","Verification evidence must correspond to the claim","Measurement limitations must be explicit"],"constraints":["Maintainability is multidimensional","Some evidence emerges only after implementation"],"alternatives":["Expert assertion","Static structural evidence","Combination of structural and operational evidence"],"evidence":["Maintainability is an outcome of multiple architectural properties","Some properties can be inspected structurally while others require observed change behavior"],"tradeoffs":["Structural evidence is available earlier but incomplete","Operational evidence is more concrete but arrives later"],"decision":"Verify maintainability claims using property-specific evidence and distinguish predicted maintainability from observed maintainability.","rationale":"Separating architectural intent from observed outcomes prevents premature certainty.","consequences":["Architecture reviews can contain explicit evidence gaps","Post-implementation evidence becomes useful for learning"],"implementation_implications":["Define maintainability properties","Map claims to evidence","Track observed change outcomes","Preserve uncertainty before operational validation"],"verification_method":"Review maintainability claims and determine whether evidence directly supports the stated property.","outcome_learning":"Compare predicted maintainability properties with observed change behavior and update architectural reasoning accordingly.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B19-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"verifies","record_id":"D55-B19-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B19-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B19","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Maintainability Failure","concept":"Optimizing architecture solely for initial delivery can create ungoverned long-term coupling","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What architecture-governance failure occurs when only initial delivery is considered?","answer":"An architecture optimized exclusively for initial delivery can introduce coupling, migration barriers, ownership ambiguity, or technology constraints that make later evolution disproportionately costly. Governance should therefore consider the expected lifecycle and material future-change requirements without assuming that every future scenario must be optimized in advance.","context":"A design minimizes initial delivery effort but creates strong coupling between components expected to evolve independently.","objective":"Ensure architecture governance considers relevant lifecycle consequences.","requirements":["Material future-change requirements must be identified","Initial and lifecycle trade-offs must be documented","Coupling consequences must be explicit"],"constraints":["Future requirements are uncertain","Over-optimizing for hypothetical future needs can also create unnecessary complexity"],"alternatives":["Optimize only for initial delivery","Optimize for every possible future","Optimize for known material evolution requirements"],"evidence":["Architecture choices can create long-lived coupling","Uncertain future requirements should not automatically justify maximal flexibility"],"tradeoffs":["Future flexibility can increase present complexity","Present simplicity can increase future migration or coordination cost"],"decision":"Evaluate lifecycle trade-offs against known material evolution requirements rather than optimizing exclusively for initial delivery or hypothetical futures.","rationale":"Architecture should balance present constraints with evidence-supported future-change needs.","consequences":["Decision records must make lifecycle assumptions visible","Some designs may accept future migration cost deliberately"],"implementation_implications":["Record expected lifecycle horizon","Identify major coupling commitments","Document accepted future migration risks","Define reassessment triggers"],"verification_method":"Review architecture decisions for explicit lifecycle assumptions and determine whether significant coupling consequences were considered.","outcome_learning":"Use observed evolution costs to improve future architecture trade-off analysis without treating every past cost as proof of a bad decision.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B19-R01","D55-B19-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"failure_mode_of","record_id":"D55-B19-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B20-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Ownership","concept":"Every material architecture governance artifact requires explicit accountable ownership","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_OWNERSHIP","question":"Why should architecture governance artifacts have explicit owners?","answer":"Material architecture decisions, standards, exceptions, review criteria, and verification requirements need accountable owners so that their validity, lifecycle, and unresolved issues have a responsible authority. Ownership does not imply that one person performs every task; it establishes accountability for governance state.","context":"An organization has architecture artifacts but unclear responsibility for maintaining them.","objective":"Prevent architecture governance artifacts from becoming ownerless and stale.","requirements":["Owner identity must be explicit","Ownership scope must be defined","Ownership changes must be traceable","Unowned artifacts must be detectable"],"constraints":["Ownership may change with organizational structure","Shared accountability can become ambiguous"],"alternatives":["Team-level ownership only","Individual ownership only","Explicit accountable owner with supporting contributors"],"evidence":["Governance artifacts require maintenance as assumptions change","Unclear ownership increases risk of stale decisions and standards"],"tradeoffs":["Explicit ownership improves accountability but requires lifecycle administration","Collective ownership distributes work but can obscure final accountability"],"decision":"Assign an explicit accountable owner to every material architecture governance artifact while allowing multiple contributors.","rationale":"Accountability must remain unambiguous even when governance work is collaborative.","consequences":["Ownership becomes part of governance metadata","Ownership changes become reviewable lifecycle events"],"implementation_implications":["Store owner identifiers","Define ownership responsibilities","Create ownership-transfer procedures","Flag ownerless artifacts"],"verification_method":"Audit material architecture artifacts for current accountable ownership and valid ownership relationships.","outcome_learning":"Analyze stale or disputed artifacts to identify ownership-model weaknesses.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B20-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Handover","concept":"Ownership transfer must preserve architectural context and unresolved obligations","knowledge_type":"OWNERSHIP_TRANSFER","question":"What should happen when the owner of an architecture decision changes?","answer":"Ownership transfer should preserve the decision's context, rationale, evidence, consequences, unresolved assumptions, verification state, exceptions, and reassessment obligations. Changing the owner should not silently reset lifecycle state or erase historical accountability.","context":"An architecture decision moves from one team or owner to another because of organizational or system changes.","objective":"Preserve governance continuity during ownership changes.","requirements":["Historical owner must remain traceable","New owner must receive decision context","Open obligations must be transferred","Lifecycle state must remain intact"],"constraints":["Organizational changes may happen quickly","Historical context may be incomplete"],"alternatives":["Change owner field only","Recreate the decision under the new owner","Transfer ownership while preserving decision identity and history"],"evidence":["Architecture reasoning is historical knowledge","Open verification and reassessment obligations survive ownership changes"],"tradeoffs":["Full context transfer requires effort","Owner-only metadata changes are simpler but risk losing obligations"],"decision":"Transfer ownership while preserving stable decision identity, historical ownership, evidence, and unresolved obligations.","rationale":"Governance continuity requires preserving the decision's knowledge state rather than treating ownership as a new decision.","consequences":["Ownership changes become lifecycle events","Governance repositories need historical ownership records"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain stable decision IDs","Record ownership transition events","Carry open obligations forward","Require acceptance by the new accountable owner"],"verification_method":"Sample ownership transfers and verify that decision history and outstanding governance obligations remain intact.","outcome_learning":"Analyze missed obligations after ownership changes to improve handover controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B20-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"extends","record_id":"D55-B20-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B20-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Knowledge Retention","concept":"Retired architecture decisions should remain historically traceable without being treated as current guidance","knowledge_type":"HISTORICAL_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should retired architecture decisions be retained?","answer":"Retired decisions should remain accessible as historical records when they explain past architecture, migration rationale, compatibility constraints, or why current decisions replaced earlier approaches. Their lifecycle state must clearly distinguish historical knowledge from active guidance.","context":"An architecture repository contains superseded decisions that may still explain current system structure.","objective":"Preserve useful architectural history without creating ambiguity about current authority.","requirements":["Retirement state must be explicit","Replacement relationships should be recorded","Historical evidence should remain immutable where appropriate","Current guidance must be distinguishable"],"constraints":["Historical records may contain obsolete assumptions","Deleting history can remove useful reasoning context"],"alternatives":["Delete retired decisions","Keep them indistinguishable from active decisions","Retain with explicit lifecycle and supersession metadata"],"evidence":["Past decisions can explain current constraints and migration paths","Historical records can become misleading if lifecycle state is unclear"],"tradeoffs":["Retention preserves reasoning but requires strong lifecycle labeling","Deletion simplifies current views but loses historical context"],"decision":"Retain material retired decisions with explicit historical status and links to their superseding decisions.","rationale":"Architectural history can be valuable evidence while remaining unsuitable as current guidance.","consequences":["Repositories need active versus historical views","Search and retrieval must respect lifecycle state"],"implementation_implications":["Store lifecycle state","Record supersession links","Preserve historical evidence","Filter current recommendations by active status"],"verification_method":"Inspect retired decisions and verify that current architecture tooling does not present them as active requirements or recommendations.","outcome_learning":"Use historical decisions during reassessment to understand prior assumptions and migration consequences.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B20-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"depends_on_lifecycle_model_of","record_id":"D55-B20-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B20-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Access","concept":"Architecture governance information should be accessible according to decision relevance while protecting sensitive architectural details","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_ACCESS","question":"How should access to architecture governance records be controlled?","answer":"Architecture governance records should be accessible to people who need them for decision-making, implementation, review, or verification, while sensitive information such as security weaknesses, credentials, private data, or confidential infrastructure details should receive appropriate access controls. Governance usefulness and information protection must be considered together.","context":"Architecture records are shared across engineering, security, operations, and management groups.","objective":"Balance architectural transparency with protection of sensitive information.","requirements":["Access roles must be defined","Sensitive fields must be protected","Decision context must remain usable for authorized reviewers","Access decisions must be auditable where appropriate"],"constraints":["Over-restriction can prevent effective architecture review","Over-disclosure can create security or confidentiality risk"],"alternatives":["Open architecture repository","Highly restricted repository","Role-aware access with sensitivity classification"],"evidence":["Architecture records can contain both broadly useful design information and sensitive implementation details","Review effectiveness depends on sufficient context"],"tradeoffs":["Broad access improves collaboration but increases exposure","Strict access reduces exposure but can fragment architectural knowledge"],"decision":"Use role- and sensitivity-aware access controls while preserving sufficient context for authorized architecture work.","rationale":"Architecture governance requires information availability for legitimate engineering purposes without treating all architectural information as equally shareable.","consequences":["Governance records need sensitivity metadata","Access policy becomes part of architecture knowledge management"],"implementation_implications":["Classify sensitive content","Define role-based access","Separate general decision rationale from sensitive evidence where appropriate","Audit access to high-sensitivity records"],"verification_method":"Review access policies and sample sensitive architecture records to verify that authorized users can obtain necessary context while restricted information remains controlled.","outcome_learning":"Analyze access-related review failures and unauthorized exposure events to refine governance information boundaries.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B20-R01","D55-B20-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"constrains_access_to","record_id":"D55-B20-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B20-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"D55-v1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B20","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Closure","concept":"Architecture governance work is incomplete when approval exists but unresolved obligations lack disposition","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CLOSURE","question":"When is an architecture governance activity actually closed?","answer":"Governance closure should require more than approval. The decision or review should have a recorded outcome, identified owner, resolved or explicitly accepted findings, documented exceptions, defined verification obligations, and a known lifecycle state. Outstanding work may remain after approval, but it must be represented rather than implicitly considered complete.","context":"An architecture review is marked approved while several verification activities and exceptions remain open.","objective":"Distinguish governance approval from complete governance closure.","requirements":["Approval status must be separate from closure status","Open obligations must be explicit","Exceptions must have disposition","Ownership must remain assigned"],"constraints":["Some verification occurs after implementation","Governance workflows may require early approval to unblock delivery"],"alternatives":["Approval equals closure","Block all approval until every obligation completes","Separate approval, obligation, and closure states"],"evidence":["Architectural governance can span design and implementation lifecycle stages","Open obligations can remain material after approval"],"tradeoffs":["Separate lifecycle states improve truthfulness but increase workflow complexity","Single completion status is simpler but can conceal unresolved work"],"decision":"Model approval, verification, open obligations, and final governance closure as distinct states.","rationale":"A decision can be approved for progression while still carrying unresolved verification or implementation obligations.","consequences":["Governance systems need explicit lifecycle state transitions","Closure becomes evidence-based rather than approval-based"],"implementation_implications":["Define state transitions","Track open obligations","Prevent premature closure","Link final closure to verification evidence where required"],"verification_method":"Inspect closed architecture governance records and verify that required obligations and findings have documented dispositions before closure.","outcome_learning":"Analyze reopened decisions and post-closure findings to identify weaknesses in closure criteria.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B20-R01","D55-B20-R03","D55-B20-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"integrates","record_id":"D55-B20-R01"},{"type":"distinguishes_state_from","record_id":"D55-B20-R03"}]}

{"record_id":"D55-B21-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Ownership","concept":"Explicit Architecture Ownership","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why should architecture governance assign explicit ownership for architectural concerns?","answer":"Each governed architectural concern should have an identifiable owner accountable for maintaining its definition, applicable standards, review criteria, evidence requirements, and lifecycle status. Ownership prevents architectural responsibilities from becoming implicit or collectively assumed, which can cause unresolved risks, inconsistent interpretations, and stale governance rules.","context":"Architecture governance spans multiple domains, teams, and technical boundaries. Without explicit accountability, review findings and architectural obligations may remain unresolved even when the organization has a formal governance process.","objective":"Establish accountable ownership for architectural policies, controls, standards, risks, and conformance obligations.","requirements":["Every governed concern has an accountable owner or owning role.","Ownership boundaries are documented.","Ownership changes are traceable.","Escalation exists when ownership is disputed or absent."],"constraints":["Ownership should not create unnecessary centralization.","The owner must have sufficient authority and technical context to maintain the governed concern.","Organizational structures may vary."],"prerequisites":["Defined architecture governance scope.","Defined organizational or system boundaries."],"dependencies":["Architecture governance model","Architecture standards","Architecture review process"],"alternatives":["Central architecture ownership","Domain-owned architecture governance","Federated ownership with central policy coordination"],"evidence":"Explicit accountability is a general governance control that improves traceability of decisions, exceptions, risks, and compliance obligations.","tradeoffs":["Central ownership improves consistency but may slow domain-specific decisions.","Federated ownership improves local responsiveness but requires stronger coordination and common standards."],"decision":"Assign explicit ownership to each material architectural governance concern while allowing ownership to be centralized, domain-based, or federated according to organizational context.","rationale":"Governance requires an accountable party capable of maintaining and responding to each architectural obligation.","consequences":["Ownership must be maintained as systems and organizational structures change.","Governance tooling should expose ownership and unresolved ownership gaps."],"implementation_implications":["Represent owner identity or role as structured governance metadata.","Require ownership for material architecture standards, exceptions, risks, and review findings.","Define escalation for missing or disputed ownership."],"verification_method":"Inspect governance records for material concerns without accountable ownership and verify that ownership changes remain traceable.","outcome_learning":"Measure unresolved ownership gaps, overdue governance actions, and repeated escalation caused by unclear accountability.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B21-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Decision Rights","concept":"Architecture Decision Authority","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance define decision authority?","answer":"Governance should explicitly define which architectural decisions require centralized approval, which may be delegated to domain teams, and which can be made autonomously within established guardrails. Decision authority should correspond to impact, risk, scope, and reversibility rather than organizational title alone.","context":"Different architecture decisions have different blast radii and risk profiles. Treating all decisions identically can either create excessive bureaucracy or permit insufficiently controlled changes.","objective":"Create proportional decision authority based on architectural impact and risk.","requirements":["Decision authority is explicitly defined.","Delegation boundaries are documented.","High-impact decisions have appropriate review or approval.","Delegated decisions remain traceable."],"constraints":["Authority models must fit organizational governance.","Low-risk decisions should not be unnecessarily escalated.","Delegation cannot remove accountability."],"prerequisites":["Architecture governance principles","Risk classification model","Defined organizational boundaries"],"dependencies":["Architecture decision records","Architecture review process","Risk governance"],"alternatives":["Fully centralized architecture authority","Fully delegated domain authority","Risk-based delegated authority"],"evidence":"Risk-based delegation is a governance mechanism for balancing architectural control with delivery autonomy.","tradeoffs":["Centralization increases consistency but can increase decision latency.","Delegation increases autonomy but requires reliable guardrails and traceability."],"decision":"Use risk- and impact-based decision authority with explicit delegation boundaries.","rationale":"Architectural control should be proportional to potential consequences rather than uniformly centralized.","consequences":["Governance must classify decision impact consistently.","Delegated decisions require sufficient evidence and traceability."],"implementation_implications":["Define decision classes and corresponding authority levels.","Encode escalation thresholds in governance policies where practical.","Record the authority under which material decisions were made."],"verification_method":"Review a representative set of architecture decisions and verify that each decision was made within its assigned authority boundary.","outcome_learning":"Track unauthorized decisions, unnecessary escalations, approval latency, and governance exceptions caused by unclear authority." ,"code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B21-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Role Separation","concept":"Separation of Architecture Governance Duties","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why can separation of architecture governance duties be important?","answer":"Separating incompatible governance responsibilities can reduce conflicts of interest and improve independent review. For material systems, the person or team proposing an architecture may not be the sole authority determining whether its risks, controls, and conformance evidence are acceptable.","context":"Architecture governance may involve proposal, review, approval, implementation, and verification. Combining all responsibilities without safeguards can weaken independent challenge.","objective":"Preserve appropriate independence between architectural proposal, approval, and verification activities.","requirements":["Material conflicts of interest are identifiable.","Review authority is sufficiently independent for the risk level.","Approval and verification responsibilities are traceable."],"constraints":["Small teams may require combined roles.","Complete separation is not always practical.","Independence should be proportional to risk."],"prerequisites":["Defined governance roles","Risk classification"],"dependencies":["Architecture review","Architecture approval","Verification controls"],"alternatives":["Strict role separation","Risk-based separation","Single-team governance with documented conflict controls"],"evidence":"Independent review is a common control for reducing unchecked self-approval and improving challenge of material risks.","tradeoffs":["Greater separation can improve assurance but increase process cost and latency.","Small organizations may need compensating controls."],"decision":"Apply risk-based separation of governance duties, with compensating controls where full separation is impractical.","rationale":"Governance assurance should reflect the potential consequences of the architectural decision.","consequences":["Material decisions may require reviewers outside the implementation team.","Small teams need explicit compensating controls."],"implementation_implications":["Define incompatible roles for high-risk decisions.","Record reviewers and approvers separately where required.","Permit documented compensating controls for constrained environments."],"verification_method":"Check material governance records for appropriate reviewer independence or documented compensating controls.","outcome_learning":"Measure governance findings associated with insufficient independent review and repeated conflict-of-interest exceptions.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B21-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Ownership Lifecycle","concept":"Architecture Ownership Transfer","knowledge_type":"LIFECYCLE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should happen when responsibility for an architectural asset changes?","answer":"An ownership transfer should explicitly identify the outgoing and incoming owners, effective time, affected governance obligations, unresolved risks, open exceptions, and required acceptance evidence. Ownership should not be considered transferred merely because organizational responsibility changed informally.","context":"Systems, teams, vendors, and organizational structures change over time. Governance records can become stale if ownership transitions are not explicitly managed.","objective":"Maintain governance accountability across organizational and system ownership transitions.","requirements":["Outgoing and incoming ownership are recorded.","Transfer timing is defined.","Open governance obligations are transferred or resolved.","Acceptance is traceable."],"constraints":["Ownership transitions may occur during incidents, reorganizations, or acquisitions.","Some responsibilities may be shared rather than singular."],"prerequisites":["Ownership metadata","Governance obligation inventory"],"dependencies":["Architecture ownership","Risk register","Exception register"],"alternatives":["Immediate transfer","Staged transfer","Temporary transitional ownership"],"evidence":"Explicit handover records preserve accountability and prevent governance obligations from being orphaned.","tradeoffs":["Formal transfer increases administrative work but improves continuity.","Informal transfer is faster but weakens traceability."],"decision":"Require explicit governance ownership transfer for material architectural assets and obligations.","rationale":"Accountability must survive organizational and system lifecycle changes.","consequences":["Ownership changes become governance events.","Transition procedures must account for unresolved obligations."],"implementation_implications":["Add ownership-transfer state and effective timestamp to governance records.","Require acceptance by the incoming owner for material assets.","Flag assets with expired or missing ownership."],"verification_method":"Audit transferred assets and confirm that ownership, obligations, risks, and exceptions have an unbroken accountability trail.","outcome_learning":"Track orphaned governance obligations and defects caused by incomplete ownership transitions.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B21-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B21","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Escalation","concept":"Escalation of Unresolved Architecture Governance Issues","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should an architecture governance issue be escalated?","answer":"A governance issue should be escalated when it exceeds the authority, risk tolerance, time boundary, or technical scope of its current owner or review level. Escalation should preserve the original evidence, context, attempted resolution, and reason for escalation.","context":"Governance issues can become stalled when ownership exists but authority or expertise is insufficient to resolve them.","objective":"Prevent material governance issues from remaining unresolved because they exceed local decision authority.","requirements":["Escalation thresholds are defined.","Escalation preserves evidence and context.","Time-sensitive issues have explicit escalation paths.","Final disposition is recorded."],"constraints":["Escalation should not be triggered for every minor disagreement.","Urgency and impact can require accelerated paths."],"prerequisites":["Decision authority model","Risk classification"],"dependencies":["Architecture review","Risk governance","Exception governance"],"alternatives":["Hierarchical escalation","Risk-based escalation","Expert arbitration"],"evidence":"Defined escalation paths reduce the likelihood that unresolved governance risks remain indefinitely assigned to an authority unable to resolve them.","tradeoffs":["More escalation can increase management overhead.","Insufficient escalation can allow material risks to persist."],"decision":"Use risk-, authority-, and time-based escalation rules for unresolved architecture governance issues.","rationale":"Escalation should occur when the current governance level cannot responsibly resolve the issue.","consequences":["Governance workflows require explicit escalation states.","Escalated issues need higher-level ownership and disposition."],"implementation_implications":["Define escalation thresholds and target roles.","Track escalation history and elapsed time.","Prevent silent closure of unresolved material issues."],"verification_method":"Review overdue governance issues and verify that those exceeding defined thresholds were escalated and dispositioned.","outcome_learning":"Measure escalation frequency, resolution latency, repeated escalations, and issues that remained unresolved beyond their risk tolerance." ,"code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B22-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Taxonomy","concept":"Architecture Classification Scheme","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should architecture governance classify systems and architectural concerns?","answer":"A consistent classification scheme allows governance controls, review depth, evidence requirements, and decision authority to vary according to system characteristics such as criticality, exposure, data sensitivity, and change impact.","context":"Applying identical governance controls to every system can create unnecessary overhead for low-risk systems while providing insufficient scrutiny for high-impact systems.","objective":"Create a stable classification basis for proportional architecture governance.","requirements":["Classification dimensions are defined.","Classification criteria are observable.","Classification outcomes are traceable.","Reclassification is supported when system characteristics change."],"constraints":["Classification must avoid arbitrary labels.","Dimensions should be relevant to governance decisions.","Different systems may require multiple classification dimensions."],"prerequisites":["Governance objectives","Risk model"],"dependencies":["Risk governance","Review policy","Security and data classification"],"alternatives":["Single-tier classification","Multi-dimensional classification","Domain-specific classification"],"evidence":"Classification provides a mechanism for applying proportional governance requirements.","tradeoffs":["Simple classifications are easier to operate but may lose important distinctions.","Multi-dimensional classifications are more expressive but increase governance complexity."],"decision":"Use a documented classification scheme whose dimensions directly influence governance controls.","rationale":"Governance requirements should be proportional to materially relevant architectural characteristics.","consequences":["Systems may need periodic reclassification.","Governance tooling should preserve classification history."],"implementation_implications":["Define classification attributes and allowed values.","Map classifications to review, evidence, and approval requirements.","Record classification rationale."],"verification_method":"Sample classified systems and verify that classification criteria are applied consistently and produce the intended governance requirements.","outcome_learning":"Measure classification disputes, reclassification frequency, and governance failures caused by incorrect classification.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B22-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Criticality Classification","concept":"System Criticality as a Governance Input","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should system criticality influence architecture governance?","answer":"System criticality should influence review depth, evidence requirements, approval authority, resilience expectations, and reassessment frequency. Criticality should be based on documented consequences of failure rather than system naming or perceived importance alone.","context":"A system supporting essential business or safety-relevant functions may require stronger governance controls than a low-impact internal utility.","objective":"Use consequence-based criticality to determine proportional architecture governance.","requirements":["Criticality criteria are documented.","Consequences of failure are considered.","Criticality affects defined governance controls.","Changes in system role trigger reassessment."],"constraints":["Criticality is context-dependent.","Business importance can change over time.","Criticality should not be inferred solely from technology choice."],"prerequisites":["System context","Impact assessment"],"dependencies":["Risk classification","Resilience governance","Architecture review"],"alternatives":["Business-criticality classification","Technical-criticality classification","Combined consequence-based classification"],"evidence":"Consequence-based classification aligns governance intensity with potential impact.","tradeoffs":["Detailed criticality analysis improves proportionality but requires more assessment effort.","Coarse classifications simplify operations but can obscure important differences."],"decision":"Treat documented consequence of failure as a primary input to architecture criticality classification.","rationale":"Governance intensity should correspond to potential impact rather than technology labels.","consequences":["Criticality may change as system usage changes.","Architecture governance must support reassessment."],"implementation_implications":["Capture impact dimensions supporting criticality.","Map criticality classes to review and evidence requirements.","Require reassessment after material changes in system role."],"verification_method":"Compare assigned criticality with documented failure consequences and verify that governance controls match the classification.","outcome_learning":"Track classification corrections and governance incidents associated with underestimated criticality.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B22-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Scope Boundaries","concept":"System Boundary Definition for Governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why must architecture governance define explicit system boundaries?","answer":"Governance controls require a known scope. Explicit system boundaries identify which components, interfaces, data flows, dependencies, and externally managed services are subject to a particular architectural obligation or review.","context":"Ambiguous boundaries can cause governance gaps where important dependencies are assumed to belong to another system or team.","objective":"Prevent governance omissions caused by unclear architectural scope.","requirements":["System boundaries are documented.","External dependencies are identified.","Interfaces crossing governance boundaries are represented.","Boundary changes trigger appropriate reassessment."],"constraints":["Distributed systems may have fluid boundaries.","Third-party components may have limited governance visibility.","A single system can participate in multiple governance domains."],"prerequisites":["System inventory","Architecture context"],"dependencies":["Integration governance","Data governance","Security governance"],"alternatives":["Component-based boundaries","Service-based boundaries","Business-capability boundaries","Hybrid boundaries"],"evidence":"Explicit scope boundaries improve traceability of governance obligations and dependencies.","tradeoffs":["Strict boundaries simplify accountability but can hide cross-system concerns.","Hybrid boundaries better represent distributed architectures but require more governance metadata."],"decision":"Define governance scope using explicit system boundaries plus documented cross-boundary dependencies.","rationale":"Governance must identify both what is controlled and what can materially affect the controlled system.","consequences":["Boundary definitions require maintenance.","Cross-boundary dependencies become governance objects."],"implementation_implications":["Maintain structured boundary metadata.","Represent external dependencies and interfaces.","Trigger review when material boundaries change."],"verification_method":"Inspect architecture scope records and verify that material components, interfaces, and dependencies are either governed directly or explicitly covered by another control domain.","outcome_learning":"Measure governance findings caused by omitted components or undocumented cross-boundary dependencies.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B22-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Applicability","concept":"Control Applicability Determination","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"How should architecture governance determine whether a control applies to a system?","answer":"Control applicability should be determined using explicit criteria derived from system characteristics, architectural boundaries, risk, regulatory or organizational obligations, and the control's intended purpose. Applicability decisions should be recorded rather than inferred from missing evidence.","context":"A governance control may legitimately be inapplicable to one architecture while mandatory for another. Treating absence of evidence as non-applicability creates audit ambiguity.","objective":"Make governance-control applicability explicit and reproducible.","requirements":["Applicability criteria are documented.","Applicability decisions are recorded.","Inapplicability has a rationale.","Material changes can trigger reassessment."],"constraints":["Criteria may vary by governance domain.","Applicability should not be used to bypass controls without evidence.","Some controls require multiple conditions."],"prerequisites":["Governance control catalogue","System classification"],"dependencies":["Architecture standards","Risk model","Compliance obligations"],"alternatives":["Manual applicability assessment","Rule-based applicability","Hybrid automated and expert assessment"],"evidence":"Explicit applicability decisions improve auditability and reduce ambiguity between missing evidence and legitimate non-applicability.","tradeoffs":["Structured applicability increases governance metadata but reduces interpretation ambiguity.","Fully automated applicability can be efficient but may mishandle context-sensitive controls."],"decision":"Represent applicability as an explicit governed state with supporting rationale and evidence.","rationale":"A control cannot be reliably audited when its applicability is implicit.","consequences":["Governance records must distinguish applicable, not applicable, unknown, and pending states.","Applicability logic requires maintenance."],"implementation_implications":["Define controlled applicability states.","Store evidence supporting applicability or non-applicability.","Flag stale applicability decisions."],"verification_method":"Review control records and verify that applicable and non-applicable states are explicitly justified.","outcome_learning":"Measure disputed applicability decisions and controls repeatedly marked incorrectly or left unknown.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B22-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B22","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Scope Changes","concept":"Boundary Change Governance","knowledge_type":"LIFECYCLE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should happen when the boundary of a governed architecture changes?","answer":"A material boundary change should trigger reassessment of affected ownership, controls, dependencies, risks, interfaces, and governance applicability. The previous and new boundaries should remain traceable so that governance history is not overwritten.","context":"Adding or removing components, services, data domains, or external dependencies can invalidate previous governance conclusions.","objective":"Preserve governance correctness after architecture scope changes.","requirements":["Material boundary changes are detectable or declared.","Affected controls are reassessed.","Ownership and dependencies are updated.","Historical scope remains traceable."],"constraints":["Some boundary changes occur incrementally.","Automated detection may be incomplete.","Not every implementation change changes governance scope."],"prerequisites":["Defined architecture boundary","Change-management process"],"dependencies":["Architecture conformance","Change governance","Dependency management"],"alternatives":["Manual reassessment","Change-triggered automated reassessment","Periodic reassessment"],"evidence":"Governance conclusions are dependent on the architecture scope against which they were evaluated.","tradeoffs":["Change-triggered reassessment provides timely control but requires reliable change signals.","Periodic review is simpler but can leave stale governance states between reviews."],"decision":"Use material boundary changes as explicit governance reassessment triggers.","rationale":"A governance decision cannot remain assumed valid when its underlying scope materially changes.","consequences":["Governance state becomes lifecycle-sensitive.","Change events need linkage to affected governance records."],"implementation_implications":["Define material boundary-change criteria.","Link architecture changes to affected governance obligations.","Preserve historical scope versions."],"verification_method":"Review boundary-changing events and verify that affected governance controls were reassessed when required.","outcome_learning":"Track governance defects discovered after unreviewed boundary changes.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B23-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Standards","concept":"Standard Applicability Mapping","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"How should architecture standards be mapped to governed systems?","answer":"Each architectural standard should have explicit applicability criteria and should map to the systems, components, or architecture classes to which it applies. The mapping should distinguish mandatory requirements from recommendations and should remain versioned.","context":"A standards catalogue without applicability mapping forces teams and reviewers to infer which standards apply.","objective":"Make architecture-standard obligations explicit and machine-processable.","requirements":["Standards have applicability rules.","Mandatory and advisory requirements are distinguished.","Mappings are versioned.","Exceptions can be linked to specific obligations."],"constraints":["Standards may apply at different architectural layers.","One system may be subject to multiple standards.","Standards can overlap or conflict."],"prerequisites":["Architecture standards catalogue","System classification"],"dependencies":["Architecture guardrails","Exception governance","Conformance assessment"],"alternatives":["Manual standards selection","Classification-based mapping","Rule-based mapping"],"evidence":"Explicit mappings improve consistency and reduce ambiguity in architecture conformance assessment.","tradeoffs":["Detailed mappings improve precision but increase maintenance cost.","Broad mappings simplify management but can produce irrelevant obligations."],"decision":"Maintain explicit, versioned applicability mappings between architecture standards and governed scopes.","rationale":"Standards cannot be consistently enforced when applicability is implicit.","consequences":["Changes to standards or system classifications may require remapping.","Governance tooling should expose applicable obligations."],"implementation_implications":["Represent standards, versions, applicability rules, and mappings as separate entities.","Record effective dates.","Link exceptions and conformance findings to the applicable standard version."],"verification_method":"Select governed systems and verify that applicable standards can be deterministically identified from recorded metadata.","outcome_learning":"Measure incorrect applicability findings and standards that repeatedly require manual interpretation.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B23-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Mandatory Controls","concept":"Distinguishing Standards from Recommendations","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should architecture governance distinguish mandatory standards from recommendations?","answer":"Mandatory requirements define conditions that must be satisfied or formally excepted, while recommendations provide guidance without the same conformance obligation. Mixing the two makes compliance status ambiguous and can cause teams to treat advisory guidance as mandatory or mandatory controls as optional.","context":"Architecture guidance often contains both enforceable constraints and preferred practices.","objective":"Make governance obligations semantically unambiguous.","requirements":["Requirement strength is explicit.","Mandatory controls have defined exception mechanisms.","Recommendations do not produce false non-conformance.","Requirement changes are versioned."],"constraints":["Organizations may use different terminology.","Some recommendations become mandatory in specific contexts."],"prerequisites":["Standards catalogue","Governance policy"],"dependencies":["Exception management","Conformance assessment"],"alternatives":["Mandatory/advisory classification","Multiple obligation levels","Binary compliance model"],"evidence":"Explicit requirement strength is necessary for meaningful conformance status and exception handling.","tradeoffs":["Multiple levels provide nuance but increase interpretation complexity.","Binary classification is simpler but may not represent useful advisory guidance."],"decision":"Represent requirement strength explicitly and separately from implementation status.","rationale":"Governance needs to distinguish obligation from guidance.","consequences":["Conformance models must account for requirement strength.","Recommendations can be measured separately from mandatory compliance."],"implementation_implications":["Add requirement-strength metadata to standards.","Prevent advisory recommendations from automatically producing mandatory non-conformance.","Allow context-specific strengthening when documented."],"verification_method":"Inspect standards and verify that every requirement has explicit obligation strength.","outcome_learning":"Measure disputes caused by ambiguous requirement strength.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B23-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Standards Versioning","concept":"Versioned Architecture Standards","knowledge_type":"LIFECYCLE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why should architecture standards be versioned?","answer":"Versioning preserves the exact governance requirements against which an architecture was assessed. Without versioning, a later modification to a standard can make historical conformance results impossible to interpret accurately.","context":"Architecture standards evolve as technologies, risks, organizational requirements, and external obligations change.","objective":"Preserve temporal accuracy and reproducibility of architecture conformance assessments.","requirements":["Every governed standard has an identifiable version.","Effective periods are recorded.","Historical assessments reference the applicable version.","Superseded versions remain retrievable when required."],"constraints":["Some standards may be continuously maintained.","Version granularity should reflect meaningful requirement changes."],"prerequisites":["Standards catalogue","Conformance records"],"dependencies":["Architecture review","Evidence management"],"alternatives":["Versioned releases","Effective-dated standards","Immutable policy snapshots"],"evidence":"Historical governance claims require a stable reference to the requirements that existed when the assessment occurred.","tradeoffs":["Fine-grained versioning improves precision but increases lifecycle overhead.","Coarse versioning is simpler but can obscure individual requirement changes."],"decision":"Treat material architecture standards as versioned governance artifacts with effective periods.","rationale":"Conformance must remain interpretable after standards evolve.","consequences":["Assessments become linked to specific standard versions.","Standards retirement must preserve historical references."],"implementation_implications":["Use immutable version identifiers for released standards.","Store effective dates and supersession relationships.","Prevent historical assessments from silently resolving to current standards."],"verification_method":"Select historical conformance assessments and verify that the exact governing standard version remains identifiable.","outcome_learning":"Track historical assessments that become ambiguous because their standard version is unavailable.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B23-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Standards Conflicts","concept":"Conflicting Architecture Standards","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should architecture governance handle conflicting standards?","answer":"Conflicting standards should be identified explicitly, analyzed for scope and authority, and resolved through an established precedence or exception mechanism. A system should not be declared conformant merely because each conflicting standard is satisfied independently when their requirements cannot coexist.","context":"Multiple standards can impose incompatible requirements across security, performance, data, infrastructure, or organizational domains.","objective":"Provide a traceable method for resolving incompatible architecture obligations.","requirements":["Conflicts are explicitly identified.","Authority and scope are considered.","Resolution rationale is recorded.","Temporary unresolved conflicts have risk ownership."],"constraints":["Some conflicts may require organizational policy decisions.","External obligations may have higher authority than internal preferences."],"prerequisites":["Versioned standards","Requirement hierarchy"],"dependencies":["Architecture decisions","Exception governance","Risk governance"],"alternatives":["Predefined precedence hierarchy","Case-by-case arbitration","Standards harmonization"],"evidence":"Explicit conflict resolution prevents inconsistent conformance interpretations and hidden policy contradictions.","tradeoffs":["Predefined precedence improves speed but may not cover novel conflicts.","Case-by-case analysis is flexible but slower and harder to standardize."],"decision":"Use explicit precedence rules where possible and documented governance arbitration for unresolved conflicts.","rationale":"Conflicting obligations require an authoritative resolution rather than independent interpretation by implementation teams.","consequences":["Governance must track standard conflicts.","Resolution outcomes may require standard updates."],"implementation_implications":["Represent conflict relationships between standards.","Record the selected governing requirement and rationale.","Create follow-up actions when standards need harmonization."],"verification_method":"Review identified conflicts and verify that each has either a documented resolution or an explicitly owned unresolved state.","outcome_learning":"Track recurring standard conflicts as candidates for policy simplification or standards consolidation.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B23-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B23","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Standards Retirement","concept":"Architecture Standard Retirement","knowledge_type":"LIFECYCLE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What governance controls are required when an architecture standard is retired?","answer":"Retiring a standard should define its retirement date, successor if one exists, treatment of existing systems, treatment of open exceptions, and whether historical assessments remain valid. Retirement should not silently erase the requirements under which previous architectures were evaluated.","context":"Architecture standards can become obsolete because of technology changes, organizational policy changes, or replacement by a newer standard.","objective":"Retire obsolete standards without losing historical governance integrity.","requirements":["Retirement status and effective date are recorded.","Successor relationships are explicit when applicable.","Existing systems receive defined treatment.","Historical assessments remain traceable."],"constraints":["Some systems may require transitional periods.","A retired standard may remain relevant to historical audits."],"prerequisites":["Versioned standards","Standards ownership"],"dependencies":["Exception governance","Conformance history","Migration governance"],"alternatives":["Immediate retirement","Retirement with transition period","Replacement by successor standard"],"evidence":"Lifecycle retirement must preserve historical and transitional governance state.","tradeoffs":["Immediate retirement simplifies the catalogue but can create unmanaged transition risk.","Transition periods improve migration control but extend governance complexity."],"decision":"Retire standards through explicit lifecycle states with defined transition treatment.","rationale":"Retirement changes future applicability but should not invalidate historical governance evidence.","consequences":["Some systems may temporarily operate under legacy requirements.","Governance tooling must distinguish active, transitional, and retired standards."],"implementation_implications":["Store retirement and transition metadata.","Link retired standards to successors where applicable.","Review open exceptions affected by retirement."],"verification_method":"Audit retired standards and verify that historical references, active migrations, and successor mappings remain intact.","outcome_learning":"Measure systems that remain on retired standards beyond approved transition periods.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B24-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Review Intake","concept":"Architecture Review Intake Criteria","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should determine whether an architecture change enters formal review?","answer":"Formal review should be triggered by defined criteria such as material risk, criticality, security impact, data sensitivity, external exposure, architectural boundary changes, major dependency changes, or significant changes to resilience and capacity characteristics.","context":"Review processes become inefficient when every change receives the same level of governance, while purely informal review can miss high-impact architectural changes.","objective":"Define transparent criteria for initiating architecture review.","requirements":["Review triggers are explicit.","Triggers are applicable to defined system classes.","Urgent changes have an emergency path.","Review decisions are traceable."],"constraints":["Trigger criteria must avoid excessive false positives.","Not every code change is an architecture change.","Emergency situations may require retrospective review."],"prerequisites":["System classification","Architecture change definition"],"dependencies":["Architecture review process","Risk governance","Change governance"],"alternatives":["Threshold-based review","Risk-based review","Mandatory review for selected architecture classes"],"evidence":"Explicit review triggers improve consistency and reduce dependence on subjective interpretation.","tradeoffs":["Broad triggers increase assurance but review volume.","Narrow triggers reduce process cost but increase omission risk."],"decision":"Use documented risk- and impact-based review triggers supplemented by mandatory classes for defined high-impact architectures.","rationale":"Review intensity should correspond to potential architectural consequences.","consequences":["Change processes need integration with architecture governance.","Trigger criteria require periodic calibration."],"implementation_implications":["Encode review-trigger attributes where practical.","Link changes to affected architecture records.","Provide emergency-review and retrospective-review states."],"verification_method":"Sample material architecture changes and determine whether the defined triggers correctly caused review.","outcome_learning":"Track missed-review events and unnecessary reviews to refine trigger thresholds.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B24-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Review Depth","concept":"Risk-Proportional Architecture Review Depth","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should the depth of architecture review vary with risk?","answer":"Review depth should increase with architectural impact, uncertainty, criticality, exposure, irreversibility, and potential failure consequences. Low-risk changes may require lightweight evidence, while high-risk changes can require multidisciplinary review, experiments, formal analysis, or explicit approval.","context":"A uniform review process either burdens low-risk work or fails to provide adequate scrutiny for high-risk architecture.","objective":"Align review effort with architectural risk and uncertainty.","requirements":["Review levels are defined.","Criteria for selecting review depth are explicit.","High-risk reviews require stronger evidence.","Review outcomes are recorded."],"constraints":["Risk assessment may be uncertain.","Review effort must remain operationally practical.","Some domains require specialist review."],"prerequisites":["Risk model","Architecture classification"],"dependencies":["Architecture standards","Decision authority","Verification"],"alternatives":["Fixed review depth","Risk-tiered review","Continuous review with dynamic escalation"],"evidence":"Proportional review is a governance mechanism for allocating assurance effort according to potential consequences.","tradeoffs":["More intensive review improves assurance but increases time and resource use.","Lightweight review improves throughput but can miss complex risks."],"decision":"Adopt risk-tiered review depth with escalation when evidence or uncertainty exceeds the assigned tier.","rationale":"Governance effort should scale with risk and uncertainty rather than architecture size alone.","consequences":["Review classification becomes an explicit governance decision.","Unexpected findings may require escalation to a deeper review level."],"implementation_implications":["Define review tiers and evidence requirements.","Allow reviewers to escalate review depth.","Record the selected tier and rationale."],"verification_method":"Compare review depth against system risk classification and verify that high-risk changes received appropriate assurance.","outcome_learning":"Measure defects discovered after review and frequency of review-tier escalation.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B24-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Review Evidence","concept":"Minimum Evidence Package for Architecture Review","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should a minimum architecture review evidence package contain?","answer":"The evidence package should contain enough architecture context, requirements, constraints, affected components, dependencies, risks, relevant standards, alternatives or rationale where applicable, validation evidence, and unresolved issues for reviewers to evaluate the change without relying on undocumented assumptions.","context":"Architecture review quality depends on the evidence available to reviewers, not only on the existence of a review meeting.","objective":"Define a reproducible evidence baseline for architecture review.","requirements":["Context is documented.","Affected scope is identifiable.","Relevant requirements and constraints are available.","Risks and unresolved issues are explicit.","Evidence references are traceable."],"constraints":["Evidence depth should be proportional to risk.","Some proprietary or sensitive evidence may require controlled access."],"prerequisites":["Architecture documentation","Governance standards"],"dependencies":["Architecture decisions","Verification evidence","Risk register"],"alternatives":["Fixed evidence package","Risk-tiered evidence package","Reviewer-defined evidence"],"evidence":"Structured review evidence improves reproducibility and reduces governance decisions based on undocumented assumptions.","tradeoffs":["Comprehensive evidence improves assurance but increases preparation effort.","Minimal evidence reduces overhead but can weaken review quality."],"decision":"Use a minimum evidence baseline with risk-based extensions.","rationale":"Every review needs enough evidence to support an informed and reproducible assessment.","consequences":["Incomplete evidence becomes an explicit review state.","Review templates should distinguish required from optional evidence."],"implementation_implications":["Define evidence categories and completeness checks.","Link evidence to requirements and architecture scope.","Prevent approval from implying that absent evidence was validated."],"verification_method":"Review completed architecture assessments and verify that required evidence categories are present or explicitly marked unavailable.","outcome_learning":"Track recurring evidence gaps and their relationship to later findings.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B24-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Review Findings","concept":"Architecture Review Finding Classification","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should architecture review findings be classified?","answer":"Findings should be classified by characteristics such as severity, affected requirement, risk, confidence, ownership, remediation status, and whether they represent non-conformance, uncertainty, observation, or improvement opportunity. Classification enables consistent treatment and prevents materially different findings from being handled identically.","context":"Architecture reviews produce findings with different levels of consequence and certainty.","objective":"Create structured handling for architecture review findings.","requirements":["Finding type is explicit.","Severity and confidence are distinguishable.","Affected requirement or risk is traceable.","Ownership and disposition are recorded."],"constraints":["Severity must not be confused with confidence.","Finding categories should be stable enough for trend analysis."],"prerequisites":["Review process","Risk model"],"dependencies":["Conformance governance","Issue management","Exception governance"],"alternatives":["Simple pass/fail findings","Multi-dimensional finding classification","Risk-only classification"],"evidence":"Structured classification supports consistent remediation, escalation, and trend analysis.","tradeoffs":["More dimensions improve precision but increase data-entry and governance complexity.","Simple categories are easier to operate but lose important context."],"decision":"Use structured, multi-dimensional finding classification where each dimension has a defined semantic meaning.","rationale":"Architecture governance needs to distinguish impact, certainty, obligation, and remediation state.","consequences":["Finding schemas require controlled vocabularies.","Historical findings may need migration when classifications evolve."],"implementation_implications":["Separate severity from confidence and status.","Link findings to requirements, controls, systems, and evidence.","Require disposition for material findings."],"verification_method":"Inspect review findings and verify that severity, confidence, ownership, and disposition are not conflated.","outcome_learning":"Measure recurring finding classes and their remediation effectiveness.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B24-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B24","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Review Closure","concept":"Architecture Review Closure Criteria","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When can an architecture review be considered closed?","answer":"A review should close only when required evidence has been evaluated, material findings have an explicit disposition, required approvals have been obtained, unresolved risks have accepted ownership or an approved exception, and the resulting governance state is recorded.","context":"A meeting or reviewer sign-off alone does not establish that all governance obligations have been addressed.","objective":"Define closure as a verifiable governance state rather than an administrative event.","requirements":["Required evidence is assessed.","Material findings have dispositions.","Required authority has acted.","Residual risks are owned or excepted.","Closure state is traceable."],"constraints":["Some low-risk findings may remain open under defined conditions.","Emergency changes may require retrospective closure."],"prerequisites":["Review evidence package","Finding classification","Decision authority"],"dependencies":["Exception governance","Risk governance","Architecture conformance"],"alternatives":["Immediate closure after approval","Conditional closure","Closure with tracked remediation"],"evidence":"Explicit closure criteria prevent incomplete reviews from being represented as fully resolved governance outcomes.","tradeoffs":["Strict closure criteria improve assurance but can delay delivery.","Conditional closure provides flexibility but requires strong follow-up controls."],"decision":"Define closure as satisfaction of evidence, finding, authority, and residual-risk criteria, with explicit conditional states where permitted.","rationale":"Governance closure must represent a known state rather than simply the end of a review activity.","consequences":["Reviews may remain conditionally open.","Governance systems need closure prerequisites."],"implementation_implications":["Implement closure validation checks.","Distinguish closed, conditionally closed, rejected, and withdrawn states.","Link closure to the evidence and findings evaluated."],"verification_method":"Audit closed reviews and verify that all closure prerequisites were satisfied or explicitly governed exceptions exist.","outcome_learning":"Track findings discovered after review closure and identify closure criteria that failed to detect them.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B25-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Exceptions","concept":"Exception Eligibility Criteria","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What makes an architecture exception eligible for formal consideration?","answer":"An exception should be considered only when the requester identifies the affected requirement, concrete deviation, business or technical reason, impact, duration, compensating controls, accountable owner, and evidence needed for risk assessment. A general preference or undocumented inconvenience is not sufficient evidence of a governed exception.","context":"Exception mechanisms can become a bypass for architecture standards if eligibility is not controlled.","objective":"Prevent informal deviations from becoming permanent uncontrolled exceptions.","requirements":["Affected requirement is identified.","Deviation is explicit.","Reason and impact are documented.","Owner and duration are defined.","Compensating controls are considered where applicable."],"constraints":["Some emergencies may require abbreviated evidence followed by retrospective completion.","Exceptions cannot override obligations outside organizational authority."],"prerequisites":["Versioned standards","Exception policy","Risk classification"],"dependencies":["Architecture standards","Risk governance","Approval authority"],"alternatives":["Strict exception eligibility","Risk-based eligibility","Emergency exception path"],"evidence":"Structured eligibility criteria make exceptions auditable and distinguish legitimate deviations from governance bypasses.","tradeoffs":["More evidence requirements reduce misuse but increase exception-request effort.","Flexible emergency paths improve response speed but require retrospective governance."],"decision":"Require explicit eligibility evidence before granting a standard architecture exception, with a controlled emergency path.","rationale":"Exceptions alter normal governance obligations and therefore require explicit justification.","consequences":["Exception requests may be rejected before risk review if minimum information is missing.","Emergency exceptions require later reconciliation."],"implementation_implications":["Define mandatory exception fields.","Validate affected standard version and requirement.","Require accountable ownership and expiry where appropriate."],"verification_method":"Sample approved exceptions and verify that eligibility criteria were satisfied before approval or that an emergency pathway was properly invoked.","outcome_learning":"Measure rejected exceptions, incomplete requests, and recurring reasons for exception requests.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B25-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Exception Duration","concept":"Time-Bounded Architecture Exceptions","knowledge_type":"LIFECYCLE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why should architecture exceptions generally have explicit duration?","answer":"A time boundary prevents a temporary deviation from becoming an unreviewed permanent architecture state. The exception should specify an expiry or reassessment date, responsible owner, and conditions for renewal or closure.","context":"Temporary exceptions often persist when their expiration and follow-up responsibilities are not explicit.","objective":"Prevent exception accumulation and governance drift.","requirements":["Exception duration or reassessment date is explicit.","Owner is accountable for renewal or closure.","Expired exceptions are detectable.","Renewal requires reassessment."],"constraints":["Some deviations may be permanent by design but should then be represented as a policy or standard change rather than indefinite exception.","External dependencies may affect remediation timelines."],"prerequisites":["Exception register","Ownership model"],"dependencies":["Architecture standards","Risk governance","Review process"],"alternatives":["Fixed expiry","Periodic reassessment","Permanent exception with periodic review"],"evidence":"Time-bounded exceptions provide a mechanism for reassessing whether the original deviation remains justified.","tradeoffs":["Short expiry periods improve governance freshness but increase administrative work.","Long periods reduce overhead but increase stale-exception risk."],"decision":"Require an explicit expiry or reassessment boundary for material architecture exceptions.","rationale":"The validity of an exception is context-dependent and should not be assumed indefinitely.","consequences":["Expired exceptions become governance alerts or non-conformance states.","Renewals require new evidence."],"implementation_implications":["Track exception lifecycle dates.","Prevent silent automatic renewal unless explicitly governed.","Link expiry events to responsible owners."],"verification_method":"Query the exception register for expired or overdue exceptions and verify that each has been closed, renewed with evidence, or escalated.","outcome_learning":"Measure expired exceptions, repeated renewals, and exceptions that should have resulted in standard changes.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B25-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Compensating Controls","concept":"Compensating Controls for Architecture Exceptions","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should compensating controls be required for architecture exceptions?","answer":"When a deviation removes or weakens a material control, the exception assessment should determine whether compensating controls can reduce the resulting risk. Compensating controls should be specific, owned, verifiable, and evaluated for effectiveness rather than merely listed as intentions.","context":"An exception can create a control gap that requires another mechanism to maintain an acceptable risk posture.","objective":"Preserve risk controls when direct conformance is not feasible.","requirements":["Removed or weakened control is identified.","Residual risk is assessed.","Compensating controls are explicit when needed.","Effectiveness can be verified.","Ownership is assigned."],"constraints":["A compensating control may not provide equivalent protection.","Some requirements cannot be substituted.","Effectiveness may depend on operational evidence."],"prerequisites":["Exception assessment","Risk analysis"],"dependencies":["Architecture standards","Security controls","Verification"],"alternatives":["Direct remediation","Compensating control","Risk acceptance without compensation"],"evidence":"Compensating controls can reduce residual risk when direct compliance is impractical, but their effectiveness must be evaluated in context.","tradeoffs":["Compensation can preserve delivery flexibility but adds control complexity.","Direct remediation may be simpler long term but less feasible in the short term."],"decision":"Require documented compensating controls when exception analysis determines that the deviation creates a material controllable gap.","rationale":"A governed exception should address the risk introduced by non-conformance rather than only record the deviation.","consequences":["Exception verification must include compensating-control evidence.","Ineffective compensation can require escalation or remediation."],"implementation_implications":["Link compensating controls to the exact exception and affected requirement.","Define verification criteria.","Track control ownership separately from exception ownership when necessary."],"verification_method":"Evaluate whether compensating controls exist, are implemented, and have evidence supporting their intended effect.","outcome_learning":"Measure exceptions where compensating controls failed or required repeated strengthening.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B25-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Exception Approval","concept":"Risk-Proportional Exception Approval","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture exception approval authority be determined?","answer":"Exception approval authority should correspond to the risk, scope, duration, and consequences of the deviation. Material exceptions should not be approved solely by the team requesting the deviation when that team lacks the authority to accept the resulting risk.","context":"Exception approval is a governance act because it authorizes departure from an established architectural obligation.","objective":"Align exception approval authority with residual risk and organizational accountability.","requirements":["Approval thresholds are defined.","Residual risk is visible to the approver.","Approval authority matches risk.","Approval is traceable."],"constraints":["Organizations have different authority structures.","Emergency exceptions may use temporary authority with retrospective review."],"prerequisites":["Risk classification","Decision authority","Exception eligibility"],"dependencies":["Architecture standards","Risk governance","Review process"],"alternatives":["Central exception authority","Domain authority","Risk-tiered authority"],"evidence":"Approval authority should be capable of accepting the consequences associated with the exception.","tradeoffs":["Central approval improves consistency but can create bottlenecks.","Delegated approval improves speed but requires strong risk thresholds."],"decision":"Use risk-tiered exception approval with explicit authority thresholds and emergency controls.","rationale":"Exception approval is meaningful only when the approver has authority over the resulting risk.","consequences":["Some exceptions require higher-level review.","Approval workflows must expose residual risk and affected requirements."],"implementation_implications":["Map risk classes to approval roles.","Prevent approval by unauthorized roles.","Record approval context and evidence."],"verification_method":"Audit approved exceptions and verify that the approver had authority appropriate to the recorded residual risk.","outcome_learning":"Track unauthorized approvals and exceptions escalated because initial authority was insufficient.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B25-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B25","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Exception Closure","concept":"Architecture Exception Closure","knowledge_type":"LIFECYCLE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What conditions should close an architecture exception?","answer":"An exception should close when the affected requirement is restored, the exception is superseded by an approved policy or architecture change, the system is retired, or an explicit governance decision establishes a different permanent state. Closure should preserve the exception history and evidence.","context":"Closing an exception by deleting its record destroys useful governance history and can obscure why a deviation existed.","objective":"Close exceptions while preserving traceability and architectural learning.","requirements":["Closure reason is explicit.","Resolution evidence is recorded where applicable.","Historical exception data remains available.","Affected standards and conformance states are updated."],"constraints":["A closed exception does not imply that the original deviation never occurred.","Permanent changes may require a standards or architecture decision update."],"prerequisites":["Exception record","Closure criteria"],"dependencies":["Architecture standards","Conformance assessment","Architecture decisions"],"alternatives":["Remediation closure","Policy supersession","System retirement","Formal permanent deviation"],"evidence":"Exception closure should represent a state transition supported by evidence or an authoritative governance decision.","tradeoffs":["Detailed closure evidence improves learning but increases record complexity.","Minimal closure records are easier to maintain but weaken historical traceability."],"decision":"Use explicit exception closure states with preserved history and linked resolution evidence.","rationale":"Exception lifecycle information is valuable for auditability and future governance decisions.","consequences":["Closed exceptions remain queryable.","Permanent deviations may require changes outside the exception register."],"implementation_implications":["Define controlled closure reasons.","Link closure evidence to the original requirement and exception.","Prevent deletion of material exception history."],"verification_method":"Inspect closed exceptions and verify that closure reason, supporting evidence, and resulting conformance state are consistent.","outcome_learning":"Analyze closed exceptions to identify recurring standards that require redesign or clarification.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B26-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Technical Debt Governance","concept":"Architecture Technical Debt Identification","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What distinguishes architectural technical debt from ordinary implementation backlog items?","answer":"Architectural technical debt represents a deliberate or accumulated architectural compromise that increases future cost, risk, complexity, constraint, or inability to evolve. Governance should identify the architectural mechanism and consequence rather than merely labeling an unresolved task as technical debt.","context":"Technical debt terminology can become meaningless when every incomplete improvement is classified as architectural debt.","objective":"Create evidence-based identification of architectural technical debt.","requirements":["The architectural compromise is explicit.","Current consequence is identified.","Future impact or constraint is described.","Ownership is assigned.","Evidence supports the debt classification."],"constraints":["Debt may be intentional or accidental.","Future impact is often uncertain.","Not every defect is architectural debt."],"prerequisites":["Architecture context","Risk assessment"],"dependencies":["Architecture decisions","Architecture review","Change planning"],"alternatives":["Manual debt registry","Architecture-review-derived debt","Automated detection plus expert validation"],"evidence":"Architectural debt should be tied to a concrete architectural compromise and its consequences.","tradeoffs":["Explicit debt modelling improves prioritization but requires governance effort.","Informal tracking is cheaper but loses consistency and traceability."],"decision":"Classify architectural debt using explicit compromise, consequence, ownership, and evidence fields.","rationale":"A precise definition prevents architectural debt from becoming a generic label for unfinished work.","consequences":["Debt records become actionable governance objects.","Debt classification may be challenged when evidence is weak."],"implementation_implications":["Store architectural debt separately from generic task lists.","Link debt to affected architecture elements and decisions.","Record confidence when future impact is uncertain."],"verification_method":"Review debt records and verify that each identifies a concrete architectural compromise and consequence.","outcome_learning":"Measure debt items that repeatedly lack actionable consequences or are routinely reclassified as ordinary maintenance.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B26-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Debt Prioritization","concept":"Risk-Based Architectural Debt Prioritization","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architectural technical debt be prioritized?","answer":"Architectural debt should be prioritized using factors such as current risk, affected criticality, operational impact, security implications, cost of delay, coupling, reversibility, and expected future remediation cost. Age alone is insufficient to determine priority.","context":"Large debt inventories require prioritization to prevent important risks from being hidden among low-impact improvement items.","objective":"Prioritize architectural debt using consequence and remediation value rather than chronology alone.","requirements":["Priority factors are explicit.","Risk and impact are considered.","Uncertainty is represented where material.","Prioritization can be reassessed."],"constraints":["Future cost estimates may be uncertain.","Different debt types may not be directly comparable.","Business priorities can change."],"prerequisites":["Debt inventory","Risk classification"],"dependencies":["Architecture risk governance","Portfolio planning"],"alternatives":["Age-based priority","Cost-based priority","Risk-based priority","Multi-factor prioritization"],"evidence":"Risk and consequence provide a stronger governance basis than age alone for prioritizing architectural debt.","tradeoffs":["Multi-factor prioritization is richer but harder to explain and maintain.","Simple prioritization is easier but can misallocate remediation effort."],"decision":"Use documented multi-factor prioritization with explicit uncertainty and periodic reassessment.","rationale":"Debt remediation should target material architectural consequences rather than merely old records.","consequences":["Debt priorities may change without the underlying debt changing.","Governance requires historical priority reasoning for material items."],"implementation_implications":["Capture prioritization factors separately.","Avoid encoding subjective scores without definitions.","Reassess priority when architecture context changes."],"verification_method":"Sample prioritized debt and verify that priority is supported by documented impact and risk factors.","outcome_learning":"Compare predicted debt impact with observed consequences and remediation outcomes.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B26-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Debt Acceptance","concept":"Explicit Acceptance of Architectural Debt","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should significant architectural debt be explicitly accepted?","answer":"Explicit acceptance makes the compromise visible, identifies who owns its consequences, records the rationale for tolerating it, and creates a basis for later reassessment. Without acceptance, material debt can persist as an undocumented architectural assumption.","context":"Teams may intentionally defer architectural improvements because of delivery constraints or competing priorities.","objective":"Make intentional architectural compromise explicit and governable.","requirements":["Material debt has an accountable owner.","Reason for acceptance is recorded.","Known consequences are documented.","Reassessment conditions are defined when appropriate."],"constraints":["Acceptance does not make a risk disappear.","The accepting authority must have appropriate authority.","Not all minor debt requires formal approval."],"prerequisites":["Debt classification","Decision authority"],"dependencies":["Architecture decisions","Risk governance","Exception governance"],"alternatives":["Formal debt acceptance","Implicit team acceptance","Risk acceptance integrated into another governance record"],"evidence":"Explicit acceptance converts an implicit compromise into a traceable governance state.","tradeoffs":["Formal acceptance improves accountability but increases process overhead.","Informal acceptance is faster but weakens traceability."],"decision":"Require explicit acceptance for material architectural debt while allowing lightweight handling for low-impact items.","rationale":"Material architectural compromises should not remain invisible merely because they are intentional.","consequences":["Debt acceptance can become a trigger for future reassessment.","Governance must distinguish accepted risk from resolved risk."],"implementation_implications":["Link debt acceptance to the affected architecture and risks.","Record acceptance authority and effective period where relevant.","Prevent accepted debt from being represented as compliant architecture."],"verification_method":"Audit material debt and verify that acceptance, ownership, and residual consequences are explicitly recorded.","outcome_learning":"Measure accepted debt that later causes material incidents, rework, or architecture changes.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B26-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Debt Aging","concept":"Architectural Debt Aging and Staleness","knowledge_type":"LIFECYCLE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why should architectural debt be reviewed for staleness?","answer":"Architectural debt records can become inaccurate when systems, dependencies, risks, or business conditions change. Periodic or event-triggered reassessment should determine whether the debt remains valid, has increased in consequence, has been resolved, or should be reclassified.","context":"A debt record can remain open for years even after its underlying architecture has materially changed.","objective":"Prevent stale architectural debt records from distorting governance decisions.","requirements":["Debt has a last-assessed timestamp.","Material changes can trigger reassessment.","Stale records are detectable.","Resolution or reclassification is recorded."],"constraints":["Review frequency should be proportional to impact.","Not every debt item requires frequent manual reassessment."],"prerequisites":["Debt inventory","Architecture change events"],"dependencies":["Architecture lifecycle","Risk governance"],"alternatives":["Fixed periodic review","Event-triggered review","Risk-based hybrid review"],"evidence":"Debt validity depends on current architecture context and can become stale independently of record age.","tradeoffs":["Frequent review improves freshness but consumes resources.","Event-driven review reduces unnecessary work but depends on reliable change detection."],"decision":"Use risk-based periodic reassessment supplemented by material architecture-change triggers.","rationale":"Debt governance requires current context rather than permanent assumptions.","consequences":["Debt state can change without remediation work occurring.","Governance tooling should flag stale records."],"implementation_implications":["Track assessment timestamps and triggering events.","Define stale thresholds by debt class.","Require reassessment after material architecture changes."],"verification_method":"Query debt records exceeding reassessment thresholds and verify that they are reassessed or explicitly governed as deferred.","outcome_learning":"Measure stale records and the proportion whose classification changes during reassessment.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B26-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B26","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Debt Retirement","concept":"Architectural Debt Resolution Verification","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should governance verify that architectural debt has actually been resolved?","answer":"Debt should be marked resolved only when the architectural condition that created the debt has been changed and the relevant requirement, risk, or consequence has been verified against the new state. Closing a remediation task alone is insufficient evidence.","context":"Administrative completion of a ticket does not necessarily prove that the architectural compromise no longer exists.","objective":"Separate task completion from verified architectural debt resolution.","requirements":["Original debt condition is identified.","Remediation changes are linked.","Relevant architecture state is assessed.","Resolution evidence is recorded."],"constraints":["Some debt consequences may take time to observe.","Resolution may require multiple changes across system boundaries."],"prerequisites":["Debt record","Architecture verification criteria"],"dependencies":["Architecture review","Testing","Conformance assessment"],"alternatives":["Task-based closure","Evidence-based closure","Independent verification"],"evidence":"Resolution should be established by evidence that the original architectural condition has changed, not solely by workflow status.","tradeoffs":["Evidence-based closure requires more governance effort but reduces false resolution.","Task-only closure is efficient but weakly verifies architecture state."],"decision":"Require evidence-based resolution for material architectural debt.","rationale":"Governance must distinguish implementation activity from verified change in architectural condition.","consequences":["Some remediation tasks can complete while debt remains open.","Verification becomes part of debt lifecycle."],"implementation_implications":["Link remediation work to debt records.","Define debt-specific resolution evidence.","Keep execution status separate from validation status."],"verification_method":"Inspect resolved debt and verify evidence that the original architectural compromise no longer exists or has been formally superseded.","outcome_learning":"Track debt reopened after claimed resolution and identify weak verification patterns.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B27-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Risk Governance","concept":"Architecture Risk Register","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What role should an architecture risk register play in governance?","answer":"An architecture risk register should provide a structured record of material architecture-related uncertainties, threats, consequences, affected assets, owners, controls, treatment decisions, and current status. It should complement rather than duplicate operational issue tracking.","context":"Architecture risks can span systems and persist beyond individual implementation tasks.","objective":"Maintain traceable governance of material architectural risks.","requirements":["Risk statements are explicit.","Affected architecture scope is identified.","Owner and treatment are recorded.","Status and reassessment information are maintained."],"constraints":["Risk registers can become stale if disconnected from architecture changes.","Operational incidents should not automatically be treated as architecture risks without analysis."],"prerequisites":["Architecture context","Risk methodology"],"dependencies":["Architecture decisions","Conformance","Exception governance"],"alternatives":["Central risk register","Domain risk registers","Integrated architecture-risk model"],"evidence":"Structured risk records support governance visibility and traceability of architectural uncertainty.","tradeoffs":["Centralized records improve cross-system visibility but can become large.","Distributed records preserve domain context but require correlation."],"decision":"Maintain explicit architecture-risk records linked to affected architecture objects and governance actions.","rationale":"Material architectural uncertainty needs persistent governance visibility.","consequences":["Risks can remain open across implementation cycles.","Risk state should be reassessed after material architecture changes."],"implementation_implications":["Link risks to systems, components, decisions, controls, and exceptions.","Represent uncertainty separately from severity.","Track risk treatment and residual state."],"verification_method":"Sample architecture risks and verify that scope, owner, treatment, and current status are traceable.","outcome_learning":"Measure risks that become incidents, are retired without evidence, or repeatedly reappear after closure.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B27-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Risk Treatment","concept":"Architecture Risk Treatment Options","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"What treatment options should architecture governance consider for material risks?","answer":"Architecture risk treatment can include avoidance, reduction, transfer where appropriate, acceptance within authorized tolerance, or further investigation when uncertainty prevents an informed decision. The selected treatment should identify its assumptions and verification criteria.","context":"Architecture risks can arise from design choices, dependencies, operational constraints, or uncertain technology behavior.","objective":"Make risk treatment explicit and connected to architectural action.","requirements":["Treatment option is explicit.","Rationale is documented.","Residual risk is considered.","Verification or reassessment criteria are defined."],"constraints":["Risk transfer does not eliminate the underlying architectural dependency.","Acceptance requires appropriate authority.","Further investigation can delay decisions."],"prerequisites":["Risk identification","Decision authority"],"dependencies":["Architecture decisions","Verification","Exception governance"],"alternatives":["Avoidance","Reduction","Transfer","Acceptance","Investigation"],"evidence":"Explicit treatment choices make the relationship between risk assessment and architecture action traceable.","tradeoffs":["Avoidance may constrain capability.","Reduction can increase complexity.","Transfer can introduce dependency on another party.","Acceptance preserves delivery flexibility but retains exposure."],"decision":"Record a deliberate risk treatment for each material architecture risk, including residual risk and verification criteria.","rationale":"A risk register without treatment does not establish how governance responds to the risk.","consequences":["Treatment becomes part of architecture governance state.","Changing architecture context can invalidate treatment assumptions."],"implementation_implications":["Model treatment separately from risk description.","Link treatment to decisions, controls, and verification evidence.","Trigger reassessment when assumptions change."],"verification_method":"Review material risks and verify that treatment, authority, residual risk, and verification criteria are explicit.","outcome_learning":"Compare selected treatments with subsequent risk outcomes to improve governance guidance.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B27-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Residual Risk","concept":"Residual Architecture Risk Acceptance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should residual architecture risk be governed after controls are applied?","answer":"Residual risk should be reassessed after proposed controls or architectural changes and explicitly compared with the applicable risk tolerance. If residual risk exceeds the authorized tolerance, the architecture should require additional treatment, escalation, or an authorized exception rather than being treated as conformant by default.","context":"Risk treatment can reduce but rarely prove that all risk has disappeared.","objective":"Prevent governance from confusing control implementation with elimination of residual risk.","requirements":["Residual risk is separately assessed.","Risk tolerance is explicit.","Excess residual risk triggers action.","Acceptance authority is appropriate."],"constraints":["Risk estimates can contain uncertainty.","Some residual risks are inherent to system purpose.","Tolerance may differ by system classification."],"prerequisites":["Risk model","Risk tolerance","Control assessment"],"dependencies":["Architecture risk governance","Exception management","Architecture review"],"alternatives":["Residual-risk acceptance","Further mitigation","Architecture redesign"],"evidence":"Residual risk is a distinct governance state after treatment and should not be inferred from control presence alone.","tradeoffs":["Further mitigation can increase cost and complexity.","Acceptance preserves feasibility but retains exposure."],"decision":"Require explicit residual-risk assessment and authorized acceptance when residual exposure remains material.","rationale":"Controls reduce risk but do not automatically prove acceptable residual exposure.","consequences":["Governance records need pre- and post-treatment risk states.","Architecture conformance and risk acceptance remain distinct concepts."],"implementation_implications":["Record residual risk independently.","Link risk acceptance to authority and evidence.","Trigger escalation when residual risk exceeds tolerance."],"verification_method":"Review treated risks and verify that residual risk was assessed against the correct tolerance and dispositioned by authorized personnel.","outcome_learning":"Compare residual-risk assessments with observed outcomes to calibrate risk assumptions.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B27-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Risk Dependencies","concept":"Architecture Risk Dependency Mapping","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should architecture risks be linked to affected components and dependencies?","answer":"Risk dependency mapping identifies which architecture elements, interfaces, decisions, and external dependencies contribute to or are affected by a risk. This supports targeted treatment and makes risk reassessment possible when the architecture changes.","context":"Architecture risks often arise from interactions rather than isolated components.","objective":"Connect architectural risk to the concrete system structures that influence it.","requirements":["Affected architecture elements are identified.","Dependency relationships are represented.","Risk changes can trigger reassessment.","Treatment can target contributing factors."],"constraints":["Complex architectures may have indirect dependencies.","Dependency information can become stale.","Not all causal relationships are deterministic."],"prerequisites":["Architecture model","Risk register"],"dependencies":["Dependency governance","Architecture boundaries","Change governance"],"alternatives":["Flat risk register","Direct dependency mapping","Graph-based risk relationships"],"evidence":"Risk treatment is more actionable when the architecture structures associated with the risk are explicit.","tradeoffs":["Detailed mappings improve traceability but increase maintenance effort.","Flat records are simpler but weaker for impact analysis."],"decision":"Link material architecture risks to affected architecture objects and relevant dependency relationships.","rationale":"Risk governance needs structural context to support effective treatment and reassessment.","consequences":["Architecture changes can affect risk state.","Risk records become connected governance objects rather than isolated entries."],"implementation_implications":["Represent risk-to-architecture relationships explicitly.","Track relationship confidence when dependency evidence is uncertain.","Trigger review from material dependency changes."],"verification_method":"Select material risks and verify that their affected architecture scope and dependencies are represented accurately enough for treatment analysis.","outcome_learning":"Measure risks whose actual affected scope differed from governance records.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B27-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B27","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Risk Reassessment","concept":"Architecture Risk Reassessment Triggers","knowledge_type":"LIFECYCLE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Which events should trigger architecture risk reassessment?","answer":"Material architecture changes, new dependencies, significant changes in system criticality, security exposure, data sensitivity, operational behavior, external obligations, or evidence that invalidates risk assumptions should trigger reassessment when relevant.","context":"Architecture risk is not static because the system and its environment change.","objective":"Keep architecture risk assessments aligned with current conditions.","requirements":["Material reassessment triggers are defined.","Trigger evidence is traceable.","Reassessment can be prioritized by risk.","Historical risk assessments remain preserved."],"constraints":["Not every change materially affects every risk.","Trigger detection may be incomplete.","Risk assessments can contain uncertainty."],"prerequisites":["Risk register","Architecture change model"],"dependencies":["Architecture lifecycle","Change governance","Risk management"],"alternatives":["Periodic reassessment","Event-triggered reassessment","Hybrid reassessment"],"evidence":"Risk assumptions can become invalid when architecture or environmental conditions change.","tradeoffs":["Event-triggered reassessment improves timeliness but depends on reliable triggers.","Periodic reassessment is simpler but may delay recognition of changed risk."],"decision":"Use risk-based reassessment triggers supplemented by periodic review for material architecture risks.","rationale":"Risk governance requires both responsiveness to change and protection against missed change events.","consequences":["Risk state can become pending reassessment.","Governance must track the trigger that caused reassessment."],"implementation_implications":["Define trigger categories.","Link reassessments to triggering events.","Flag risks whose assumptions are invalidated."],"verification_method":"Review material architecture changes and verify that affected risks were reassessed or explicitly determined not to be affected.","outcome_learning":"Measure missed reassessment triggers and stale risk assessments.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B28-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Evidence","concept":"Evidence Provenance for Architecture Governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should architecture governance evidence have provenance?","answer":"Governance evidence should identify its source, generation or collection context, relevant version or time, and verification state so reviewers can distinguish authoritative evidence from assumptions, generated artifacts, and unverified claims.","context":"Architecture governance frequently combines documentation, tests, diagrams, configuration data, measurements, and human assessments.","objective":"Preserve evidence trustworthiness and traceability.","requirements":["Evidence source is identifiable.","Collection or generation context is recorded where relevant.","Version or timestamp is available when material.","Verification state is explicit."],"constraints":["Some evidence sources may be confidential.","Not every artifact supports the same provenance granularity."],"prerequisites":["Evidence model","Governance review process"],"dependencies":["Verification","Auditability","Architecture standards"],"alternatives":["Basic source reference","Structured provenance metadata","Cryptographically linked evidence"],"evidence":"Provenance allows reviewers to understand what an evidence item actually establishes and under which conditions.","tradeoffs":["Detailed provenance improves assurance but increases metadata requirements.","Minimal provenance is easier but weakens auditability."],"decision":"Require provenance metadata for material architecture governance evidence.","rationale":"Evidence without provenance can be difficult to interpret or reproduce.","consequences":["Governance evidence becomes a traceable object.","Evidence collection workflows need provenance capture."],"implementation_implications":["Record source type, source identity, time/version, and verification status.","Distinguish generated artifacts from independently verified evidence.","Preserve provenance when evidence is transformed."],"verification_method":"Sample material governance evidence and verify that provenance is sufficient to identify its origin and verification state.","outcome_learning":"Track governance findings caused by ambiguous or unverifiable evidence.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B28-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Evidence Freshness","concept":"Freshness of Architecture Governance Evidence","knowledge_type":"LIFECYCLE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why does evidence freshness matter in architecture governance?","answer":"Evidence can lose relevance as architecture, dependencies, configurations, standards, and operating conditions change. Governance should define freshness requirements according to evidence type and risk, and should distinguish old but historically valid evidence from evidence intended to represent the current state.","context":"A test or assessment can accurately describe an earlier state without proving that the current architecture remains compliant or safe.","objective":"Prevent stale evidence from being misinterpreted as current evidence.","requirements":["Evidence has temporal context where relevant.","Freshness expectations are defined for material evidence.","Stale evidence is detectable.","Historical evidence remains distinguishable from current evidence."],"constraints":["Different evidence types have different validity periods.","Some evidence remains valid until a specific change occurs rather than for a fixed duration."],"prerequisites":["Evidence provenance","Architecture lifecycle"],"dependencies":["Review process","Conformance assessment","Change governance"],"alternatives":["Fixed freshness intervals","Event-based invalidation","Risk-based freshness"],"evidence":"Evidence validity depends on what it establishes and whether relevant underlying conditions have changed.","tradeoffs":["Fixed intervals are simple but may over-refresh or under-refresh.","Event-based invalidation is precise but requires reliable change signals."],"decision":"Use evidence-type and risk-based freshness rules with event-triggered invalidation where practical.","rationale":"Current-state claims require evidence that remains relevant to the current architecture.","consequences":["Governance assessments can become stale without underlying architecture changes being defective.","Evidence records require validity state."],"implementation_implications":["Track evidence timestamp and validity conditions.","Define invalidation triggers for material architecture changes.","Do not silently reuse expired evidence."],"verification_method":"Review governance assessments and determine whether their evidence was valid for the state being assessed.","outcome_learning":"Measure stale-evidence findings and identify evidence types requiring better invalidation rules.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B28-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Evidence Integrity","concept":"Integrity of Architecture Governance Evidence","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What does evidence integrity mean in architecture governance?","answer":"Evidence integrity means that the governance artifact used for an assessment has not been altered in a way that changes its relevant meaning without the alteration being detectable or documented. Integrity mechanisms should be proportional to the importance and threat model of the evidence.","context":"Architecture governance may rely on reports, configuration snapshots, test results, diagrams, measurements, and externally supplied evidence.","objective":"Protect material governance conclusions from undocumented evidence alteration.","requirements":["Material evidence has appropriate integrity protection.","Transformations are traceable.","Integrity failures are detectable where required.","Original evidence remains identifiable when necessary."],"constraints":["Integrity controls vary by threat model.","Not all evidence requires cryptographic protection.","Evidence may be transformed for analysis."],"prerequisites":["Evidence provenance","Threat model"],"dependencies":["Auditability","Security governance","Verification"],"alternatives":["Access control","Checksums or hashes","Immutable evidence storage","Cryptographic signatures"],"evidence":"Integrity controls increase confidence that evidence reviewed corresponds to the evidence that was originally produced or collected.","tradeoffs":["Stronger integrity controls add storage and operational complexity.","Weak controls may be sufficient for low-risk evidence."],"decision":"Apply integrity controls proportionally to evidence sensitivity, governance importance, and threat model.","rationale":"Evidence trust requirements vary and should be explicit rather than assumed.","consequences":["Material evidence may require stronger storage or signing mechanisms.","Evidence transformations require provenance links."],"implementation_implications":["Classify evidence integrity requirements.","Preserve hashes or signatures where justified.","Record transformations that affect evidence representation."],"verification_method":"Test selected material evidence paths for integrity controls appropriate to their classification.","outcome_learning":"Track evidence integrity failures and identify weak points in governance evidence pipelines.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B28-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Evidence Chain","concept":"Traceability from Governance Claim to Evidence","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should architecture governance claims link directly to supporting evidence?","answer":"A governance claim should be traceable to the evidence that supports it, including the applicable requirement or control and the scope to which the evidence applies. This prevents unsupported conclusions and makes reassessment and audit more efficient.","context":"A review may contain many artifacts, but only some directly support a particular conformance or risk conclusion.","objective":"Create claim-to-evidence traceability for governance assessments.","requirements":["Claims are identifiable.","Supporting evidence is linked.","Scope applicability is clear.","Unsupported claims are distinguishable."],"constraints":["Evidence can support multiple claims.","Some claims require expert interpretation rather than direct measurement."],"prerequisites":["Evidence provenance","Governance claim model"],"dependencies":["Conformance assessment","Architecture review","Risk governance"],"alternatives":["Document-level references","Claim-level traceability","Evidence graph"],"evidence":"Direct claim-to-evidence relationships reduce ambiguity about what evidence actually supports a governance conclusion.","tradeoffs":["Fine-grained traceability improves auditability but increases record complexity.","Document-level references are simpler but may require manual interpretation."],"decision":"Provide direct traceability from material governance claims to supporting evidence and applicable requirements.","rationale":"Governance conclusions should be reconstructable from their supporting evidence.","consequences":["Evidence becomes reusable across related claims.","Missing evidence becomes an explicit state rather than an implicit assumption."],"implementation_implications":["Represent claims, requirements, evidence, and scope as linked entities.","Support one-to-many and many-to-many evidence relationships.","Record interpretation or assessment method when evidence is indirect."],"verification_method":"Select governance claims and verify that their supporting evidence and applicable scope can be reconstructed without undocumented assumptions.","outcome_learning":"Measure unsupported claims and evidence links that repeatedly fail reassessment.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B28-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B28","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Evidence Retention","concept":"Retention of Architecture Governance Evidence","knowledge_type":"LIFECYCLE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance evidence retention be determined?","answer":"Retention should reflect the lifecycle of the governed architecture, applicable organizational obligations, audit needs, risk, and the value of historical evidence for reconstructing past decisions and states. Retention should distinguish required evidence from redundant artifacts.","context":"Architecture governance evidence can become important long after a system or standard changes.","objective":"Preserve evidence needed for governance history without retaining unnecessary data indefinitely.","requirements":["Retention requirements are defined by evidence class.","Historical assessments remain reconstructable where necessary.","Retention and deletion actions are traceable.","Sensitive evidence follows applicable handling requirements."],"constraints":["Retention obligations vary by organization and jurisdiction.","Sensitive evidence may require controlled retention.","Not every generated artifact has long-term value."],"prerequisites":["Evidence classification","Governance retention policy"],"dependencies":["Auditability","Data governance","Security controls"],"alternatives":["Fixed retention period","Lifecycle-based retention","Risk-based retention"],"evidence":"Historical governance evidence can be necessary to reconstruct why a past architecture was approved or considered conformant.","tradeoffs":["Long retention improves historical traceability but increases storage and data-management obligations.","Short retention reduces burden but may destroy important governance context."],"decision":"Use evidence-class and lifecycle-based retention with explicit treatment of historical governance records.","rationale":"Retention should preserve necessary governance history without indiscriminate accumulation.","consequences":["Evidence categories require retention rules.","Deletion can become a governed lifecycle event."],"implementation_implications":["Classify governance evidence by retention requirement.","Preserve links from historical decisions and assessments to retained evidence.","Record authorized deletion or archival events."],"verification_method":"Audit representative governance evidence classes and verify that retention behavior matches documented policy.","outcome_learning":"Measure cases where missing historical evidence prevents governance reconstruction or where retained data provides no governance value.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B29-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Auditability","concept":"Governance Audit Trail","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should an architecture governance audit trail preserve?","answer":"A governance audit trail should preserve material state changes, approvals, reviews, exceptions, findings, evidence references, ownership changes, and relevant lifecycle transitions with enough temporal and actor context to reconstruct what happened.","context":"Governance requires more than the current state when accountability or historical reconstruction is necessary.","objective":"Enable reconstruction of material architecture governance events.","requirements":["Material governance events are recorded.","Event time is available.","Actor or accountable role is identifiable.","Previous and resulting states are reconstructable where required."],"constraints":["Audit detail should be proportional to governance importance.","Sensitive actor information requires appropriate access control."],"prerequisites":["Governance event model","Evidence provenance"],"dependencies":["Architecture review","Exception governance","Decision governance"],"alternatives":["Snapshot-only records","Event-based audit trail","Hybrid snapshots and events"],"evidence":"Historical event context is necessary to understand how a governance state was reached.","tradeoffs":["Detailed audit trails increase storage and implementation complexity.","Minimal records reduce cost but weaken reconstruction."],"decision":"Maintain an auditable history of material architecture governance state transitions.","rationale":"Current state alone cannot reliably explain historical accountability or decision context.","consequences":["Governance systems must preserve material historical transitions.","Audit data requires access and retention controls."],"implementation_implications":["Define material governance event types.","Capture actor, time, affected object, prior state where necessary, and resulting state.","Prevent unauthorized alteration of audit history."],"verification_method":"Select historical governance cases and verify that the material sequence of events can be reconstructed.","outcome_learning":"Identify governance events for which missing history repeatedly prevents root-cause or accountability analysis.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B29-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance State","concept":"Distinguishing Current State from Historical State","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should architecture governance preserve historical states separately from current state?","answer":"Historical governance states explain what was known, required, approved, or excepted at a particular time. Overwriting them with current values can create false historical conclusions and make it impossible to determine whether an earlier assessment was reasonable under the requirements then in force.","context":"Architecture standards, ownership, risks, and system structures evolve continuously.","objective":"Maintain temporal correctness of governance records.","requirements":["Current state is distinguishable from historical state.","Material changes preserve relevant history.","Historical assessments reference their governing context."],"constraints":["Not every field requires full temporal history.","Retention requirements determine how much history is preserved."],"prerequisites":["Versioned standards","Audit trail"],"dependencies":["Architecture lifecycle","Evidence retention"],"alternatives":["Mutable records","Versioned records","Event-sourced governance history"],"evidence":"Governance claims are time-dependent and cannot always be evaluated accurately using present-day values.","tradeoffs":["Full versioning improves reconstruction but increases storage and data complexity.","Selective history is simpler but may omit important context."],"decision":"Preserve historical governance context for material objects and assessments.","rationale":"Temporal context is necessary for accurate governance reconstruction.","consequences":["Governance records need version or effective-time semantics.","Historical queries become a supported governance capability."],"implementation_implications":["Use immutable versions or temporal state where appropriate.","Link assessments to the versions of standards, architecture, and evidence they evaluated.","Avoid destructive updates to material historical facts."],"verification_method":"Reconstruct selected historical assessments and verify that their original governance context remains distinguishable from current state.","outcome_learning":"Measure historical cases where overwritten state caused ambiguity or incorrect retrospective conclusions.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B29-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Access","concept":"Controlled Access to Governance Records","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should architecture governance records have controlled access?","answer":"Governance records can contain security architecture, risk information, sensitive dependencies, proprietary design details, and personnel accountability data. Access should therefore be governed according to the sensitivity and purpose of the information while preserving sufficient visibility for authorized review.","context":"Architecture governance artifacts are not automatically safe for unrestricted organizational access.","objective":"Protect governance information without preventing legitimate governance activities.","requirements":["Sensitive governance data is classified.","Access is role- or attribute-appropriate.","Material access is auditable where required.","Governance evidence remains available to authorized reviewers."],"constraints":["Over-restricting records can prevent effective independent review.","Different governance artifacts have different sensitivity."],"prerequisites":["Information classification","Access-control model"],"dependencies":["Security governance","Privacy governance","Auditability"],"alternatives":["Role-based access","Attribute-based access","Document-level access controls"],"evidence":"Governance information can itself introduce security or privacy risk if broadly exposed.","tradeoffs":["Stronger access controls improve confidentiality but can reduce review accessibility.","Broad access improves transparency but increases information exposure."],"decision":"Apply sensitivity-aware access control to architecture governance records while preserving required governance visibility.","rationale":"Governance data must be protected according to the risks created by its disclosure.","consequences":["Review workflows must support authorized cross-team access.","Access denials should not silently block mandatory governance processes."],"implementation_implications":["Classify governance records.","Define access roles or attributes.","Audit sensitive access where justified.","Provide controlled sharing mechanisms for external reviewers."],"verification_method":"Review access permissions for representative governance records and verify that access matches documented sensitivity and reviewer roles.","outcome_learning":"Track unauthorized access attempts and governance delays caused by inappropriate access restrictions.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B29-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Tamper Resistance","concept":"Protection Against Unauthorized Governance Record Modification","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should material governance records be protected from unauthorized modification?","answer":"Unauthorized modification can change the apparent history of approvals, exceptions, findings, evidence, or architecture states. Material governance records therefore require integrity and authorization controls appropriate to their sensitivity and accountability requirements.","context":"Governance records can influence compliance, risk acceptance, architecture approval, and historical accountability.","objective":"Preserve trust in material governance history.","requirements":["Modification authority is controlled.","Material changes are auditable.","Unauthorized modification is detectable where required.","Historical evidence is preserved according to retention policy."],"constraints":["Control strength should match threat model and governance importance.","Authorized corrections may be necessary."],"prerequisites":["Audit trail","Access control","Evidence integrity"],"dependencies":["Security governance","Governance evidence"],"alternatives":["Database access control","Append-only event history","Signed governance artifacts"],"evidence":"Governance conclusions depend on the integrity of the records supporting them.","tradeoffs":["Strong tamper resistance increases implementation complexity.","Weak controls reduce cost but increase risk of untraceable governance alteration."],"decision":"Protect material governance records using authorization and integrity controls proportional to their governance significance.","rationale":"Accountability requires confidence that historical governance records have not been silently changed.","consequences":["Correction mechanisms must preserve history.","Governance platforms require stronger controls for high-impact records."],"implementation_implications":["Separate ordinary editing from controlled amendments.","Record material changes in the audit trail.","Use immutable or append-only mechanisms where justified."],"verification_method":"Test representative governance records for unauthorized modification resistance and verify that authorized amendments preserve history.","outcome_learning":"Track governance integrity incidents and identify record classes requiring stronger controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B29-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B29","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Audit Queries","concept":"Reconstructable Governance History","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What does it mean for architecture governance history to be reconstructable?","answer":"A governance history is reconstructable when an authorized reviewer can determine the relevant architecture scope, applicable standards, decisions, approvals, exceptions, evidence, findings, ownership, and state transitions for a material historical event without relying on undocumented memory.","context":"Auditability is not satisfied merely by storing many records if relationships between those records cannot be reconstructed.","objective":"Define practical reconstructability as a governance quality requirement.","requirements":["Related governance objects are linkable.","Temporal context is available.","Material state transitions are preserved.","Evidence references remain resolvable."],"constraints":["Some historical evidence may legitimately expire under retention policy.","Reconstruction quality depends on the granularity of preserved records."],"prerequisites":["Audit trail","Evidence provenance","Historical state model"],"dependencies":["Architecture decisions","Standards versioning","Exception governance"],"alternatives":["Document-centric reconstruction","Event-centric reconstruction","Graph-based reconstruction"],"evidence":"Governance accountability depends on relationships among records rather than isolated artifacts.","tradeoffs":["Rich relationships improve reconstruction but require stronger data modelling.","Simpler records are easier to maintain but may create fragmented history."],"decision":"Treat reconstructability of material governance events as an explicit governance quality requirement.","rationale":"The purpose of audit history is to explain how a material state was reached, not merely to retain disconnected artifacts.","consequences":["Governance data models must preserve relationships.","Audit validation should test reconstruction rather than only record existence."],"implementation_implications":["Define required relationships among architecture, standards, evidence, decisions, risks, and approvals.","Create reconstruction queries or reports.","Test historical reconstruction periodically."],"verification_method":"Choose a historical architecture event and attempt to reconstruct its governance context using only retained records and evidence links.","outcome_learning":"Record reconstruction gaps and use them to improve governance data relationships and retention.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B30-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Automation","concept":"Policy-as-Code for Architecture Guardrails","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_AUTOMATION","question":"When is policy-as-code appropriate for architecture governance?","answer":"Policy-as-code is appropriate when a governance rule can be expressed with sufficiently precise inputs, deterministic evaluation semantics, and an actionable result. It is most useful for repeatable checks such as required metadata, prohibited configurations, dependency restrictions, or defined architecture guardrails.","context":"Automation can improve consistency but cannot reliably replace expert judgment for ambiguous or context-heavy architectural decisions.","objective":"Automate deterministic architecture governance checks without misrepresenting automated evaluation as complete architectural judgment.","requirements":["Policy semantics are explicit.","Required inputs are available.","Evaluation behavior is testable.","Automated results identify their scope and limitations."],"constraints":["Policies can become stale.","Incomplete inputs can produce false conclusions.","Complex architecture judgments may not be safely reducible to deterministic rules."],"prerequisites":["Governance rules","Structured architecture metadata"],"dependencies":["Architecture standards","Conformance assessment","Policy testing"],"alternatives":["Manual review","Policy-as-code","Hybrid automated and expert review"],"evidence":"Deterministic governance rules can be automated when their inputs and semantics are sufficiently explicit.","tradeoffs":["Automation improves repeatability and scale but can amplify incorrect policies.","Manual review handles ambiguity better but is slower and less uniform."],"decision":"Use policy-as-code for deterministic guardrails and retain human or expert review for context-dependent architectural judgments.","rationale":"Automation should match the determinism of the governance rule.","consequences":["Automated governance requires policy lifecycle management.","Automated pass status must not imply complete architectural verification."],"implementation_implications":["Version policies independently.","Test policies against positive, negative, and boundary cases.","Record policy version and input scope with each result.","Separate policy evaluation from final governance approval where required."],"verification_method":"Test governance policies against known compliant, non-compliant, boundary, and incomplete-input cases and verify deterministic outcomes.","outcome_learning":"Measure false positives, false negatives, policy drift, and cases requiring human override.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B30-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Automated Conformance","concept":"Automated Architecture Conformance Checks","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_AUTOMATION","question":"What conditions are required for automated architecture conformance checks?","answer":"Automated conformance requires an explicit requirement, machine-accessible evidence or architecture state, deterministic evaluation logic, defined scope, and a clear interpretation of the result. A check should report unknown or insufficient-evidence states rather than treating missing information as conformance.","context":"Automated governance systems often operate over incomplete architecture information.","objective":"Prevent automated conformance systems from producing false assurance.","requirements":["Requirement is explicit.","Input scope is known.","Missing inputs produce an explicit uncertain state.","Evaluation logic is versioned.","Results retain provenance."],"constraints":["Architecture information may be incomplete or stale.","Some requirements require expert interpretation.","Automated evidence may not establish actual runtime behavior."],"prerequisites":["Policy-as-code capability","Architecture metadata","Evidence model"],"dependencies":["Architecture standards","Verification","Evidence provenance"],"alternatives":["Binary automated checks","Three-state checks including unknown","Hybrid automated assessment"],"evidence":"Automated evaluation is only as reliable as its inputs and rule semantics.","tradeoffs":["Binary checks are simple but can create false assurance when evidence is missing.","Explicit unknown states preserve uncertainty but require downstream handling."],"decision":"Require automated conformance checks to distinguish pass, fail, and insufficient-evidence or unknown states where inputs are incomplete.","rationale":"Missing evidence must not silently become evidence of compliance.","consequences":["Governance dashboards need explicit uncertainty states.","Automation must expose input coverage."],"implementation_implications":["Define result semantics before implementation.","Record rule version, input evidence, and evaluation timestamp.","Prevent stale results from being presented as current without freshness validation."],"verification_method":"Run conformance checks against complete, non-conformant, and incomplete-input cases and verify correct state classification.","outcome_learning":"Measure false assurance caused by missing or stale inputs.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B30-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Automation Assurance","concept":"Testing Governance Policies","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_AUTOMATION","question":"How should architecture governance policies be tested before being relied upon?","answer":"Governance policies should be tested with representative compliant cases, violations, boundary conditions, missing inputs, conflicting inputs, version transitions, and known historical cases. Policy execution should be distinguished from verification that the policy itself correctly represents the intended governance requirement.","context":"A policy can execute correctly while implementing an incorrect or incomplete governance rule.","objective":"Separate policy execution correctness from governance-policy correctness.","requirements":["Policy behavior is tested.","Boundary and missing-input cases are covered.","Policy version is traceable.","Expected policy semantics are independently reviewed."],"constraints":["Test cases may not cover all architecture variants.","Policy correctness can require expert review.","Changing requirements can invalidate old policy tests."],"prerequisites":["Versioned policy","Governance requirement"],"dependencies":["Architecture standards","Policy-as-code","Verification"],"alternatives":["Unit testing only","Scenario-based policy testing","Formal rule verification where appropriate"],"evidence":"Executable correctness does not prove that a policy accurately expresses the intended governance obligation.","tradeoffs":["Broader testing increases assurance but requires maintenance.","Minimal testing reduces effort but increases policy regression risk."],"decision":"Test governance policies at both implementation level and requirement-semantic level.","rationale":"Governance automation needs assurance about both code behavior and policy meaning.","consequences":["Policy changes require regression testing.","Governance requirements need traceability to policy tests."],"implementation_implications":["Map policy requirements to test cases.","Include negative, boundary, incomplete-input, and regression cases.","Track execution status separately from policy verification status."],"verification_method":"Inspect policy test suites and verify coverage of positive, negative, boundary, missing-input, and regression scenarios.","outcome_learning":"Use policy failures and false results to improve both implementation and underlying governance rules.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B30-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Automation","concept":"Human Override of Automated Governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_AUTOMATION","question":"How should human overrides of automated architecture governance results be handled?","answer":"An override should be an explicit governance event containing the automated result, reason for override, supporting evidence, authorized actor or role, affected scope, and resulting disposition. Overrides should not silently alter the original automated result.","context":"Automated governance may encounter incomplete context or legitimate exceptions that require human judgment.","objective":"Preserve both automated evidence and human governance reasoning.","requirements":["Original automated result remains preserved.","Override reason is explicit.","Authority is appropriate.","Supporting evidence is recorded.","Override scope and duration are clear where relevant."],"constraints":["Overrides can become an uncontrolled bypass mechanism.","Some results should not be overridable without formal exception authority."],"prerequisites":["Automated conformance","Decision authority","Exception policy"],"dependencies":["Architecture exceptions","Audit trail","Policy-as-code"],"alternatives":["No override","Formal override workflow","Exception-based override"],"evidence":"Explicit override records preserve traceability when human judgment supersedes automated evaluation.","tradeoffs":["Formal overrides improve accountability but add workflow overhead.","Unstructured overrides are faster but weaken governance assurance."],"decision":"Require explicit, authorized, auditable overrides for automated architecture governance results.","rationale":"Human judgment can complement automation, but it must not erase the automated evidence or bypass authority controls.","consequences":["Overrides become measurable governance events.","Repeated overrides can reveal defective policies or inappropriate automation."],"implementation_implications":["Store original result immutably where appropriate.","Require structured override reasons and evidence.","Trigger review when override frequency exceeds defined thresholds."],"verification_method":"Audit overridden governance results and verify preservation of the original result, authorization, rationale, and supporting evidence.","outcome_learning":"Analyze recurring overrides to identify policy defects, missing context, or inappropriate automation boundaries.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B30-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","batch":"B30","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Automation","concept":"Automation Drift Detection","knowledge_type":"LIFECYCLE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance detect drift between governance policies and actual architectural requirements?","answer":"Governance automation should periodically compare active policies with their governing standards, versions, applicability rules, and known requirements. Drift indicators include policies referencing retired requirements, missing mappings, unexplained manual overrides, stale inputs, and behavior inconsistent with approved governance semantics.","context":"Automated policies can remain operational after the requirements they encode have changed.","objective":"Detect governance automation that no longer accurately represents current architectural obligations.","requirements":["Policies have owners and versions.","Policy-to-requirement relationships are traceable.","Requirement changes can identify affected policies.","Drift states are explicit."],"constraints":["Semantic drift may not be detectable automatically.","Policy equivalence can require expert analysis.","Not every standards change affects every policy."],"prerequisites":["Versioned standards","Policy catalogue","Requirement-to-policy traceability"],"dependencies":["Policy testing","Architecture standards","Governance review"],"alternatives":["Periodic manual review","Change-triggered policy review","Automated dependency analysis plus expert review"],"evidence":"Governance automation remains valid only while its encoded semantics remain aligned with governing requirements.","tradeoffs":["Automated drift detection improves scale but cannot guarantee semantic completeness.","Manual review catches contextual changes but is slower."],"decision":"Maintain explicit policy-to-requirement traceability and use change-triggered plus periodic review to detect governance-policy drift.","rationale":"Executable governance rules can become stale even when they continue to execute successfully.","consequences":["Policy lifecycle becomes dependent on standards lifecycle.","Drift can require policy updates, retirement, or temporary suspension."],"implementation_implications":["Track policy dependencies on standards and requirements.","Generate review triggers when governing requirements change.","Distinguish execution success from governance correctness."],"verification_method":"Select changed standards and verify that dependent policies are identified and reassessed before being treated as current governance controls.","outcome_learning":"Measure policy drift incidents, stale mappings, and repeated overrides caused by outdated automation.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}

{"record_id":"D55-B31-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Baselines","concept":"Current-State Architecture Baseline","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What is the purpose of maintaining a current-state architecture baseline?","answer":"A current-state architecture baseline establishes an explicit, evidence-backed representation of the architecture that actually exists at a defined point in time. It provides the reference against which proposed changes, risks, conformance, technical debt, and target-state transitions can be evaluated.","context":"Governance cannot reliably assess architectural change when the existing system state is undocumented, stale, or based only on intended design.","objective":"Maintain a trustworthy architectural reference for governance and change analysis.","requirements":["Identify major components and responsibilities","Represent relevant dependencies and boundaries","Record significant technology and integration choices","Associate the baseline with a defined time or version","Distinguish observed implementation from intended architecture"],"constraints":["The baseline may become stale","Large systems may require multiple abstraction levels","Operational implementation may differ from documentation"],"prerequisites":["Architecture inventory","System ownership information","Relevant deployment and integration evidence"],"dependencies":["Architecture documentation","Configuration or deployment evidence","System ownership"],"alternatives":["Design-only architecture documentation","Runtime discovery without formal baseline","No maintained baseline"],"evidence":"A baseline is useful only when it represents the system state being governed rather than an obsolete target design.","tradeoffs":["Maintaining a baseline requires governance effort","Detailed baselines increase maintenance cost","Higher-level baselines may omit implementation-critical details"],"decision":"Maintain a versioned current-state architecture baseline at an abstraction level appropriate to governance decisions.","rationale":"Governance decisions require a stable reference for determining what exists before evaluating what should change.","consequences":["Architecture changes can be compared against a known state","Stale baseline detection becomes necessary","Baseline ownership must be assigned"],"implementation_implications":["Version architecture representations","Record baseline timestamps or source versions","Link major components to evidence","Define baseline refresh triggers"],"verification_method":"Compare documented baseline elements against authoritative implementation or deployment evidence and record discrepancies.","outcome_learning":"Repeated baseline discrepancies should identify documentation gaps, ownership failures, or automated discovery opportunities.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B31-R02","D55-B31-R03"],"relationships":["CURRENT_STATE_REFERENCE","GOVERNANCE_BASELINE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B31-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Baselines","concept":"Baseline Versioning","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should architecture baselines be versioned?","answer":"Versioning preserves the historical sequence of architectural states and allows governance teams to determine what changed, when it changed, and which approved or implemented transition produced the change.","context":"Without version history, architecture governance loses traceability between architectural changes and their causes, approvals, consequences, and evidence.","objective":"Provide temporal traceability for architecture state changes.","requirements":["Unique baseline identifiers","Effective timestamps or version markers","Change descriptions","Relationship to relevant decisions or changes"],"constraints":["Not every implementation change warrants a full architecture baseline revision","Historical records must remain interpretable after terminology changes"],"prerequisites":["Defined baseline ownership","Change identification process"],"dependencies":["Architecture repository","Change management records"],"alternatives":["Mutable single baseline","Periodic undocumented snapshots"],"evidence":"Historical comparison requires immutable or reconstructable baseline versions rather than an overwritten representation.","tradeoffs":["Versioning increases repository volume","Fine-grained versioning improves traceability but increases maintenance overhead"],"decision":"Use explicit versions for material architecture baselines and preserve prior versions as historical evidence.","rationale":"Governance needs to distinguish current architecture from previously approved or observed states.","consequences":["Architecture evolution becomes auditable","Historical comparisons become possible","Repository retention becomes a governance concern"],"implementation_implications":["Assign stable baseline identifiers","Store change metadata","Prevent silent replacement of historical baselines","Define materiality rules for baseline updates"],"verification_method":"Verify that a historical baseline can be retrieved and compared with its successor without ambiguity.","outcome_learning":"Frequent inability to reconstruct architectural history indicates insufficient change capture or baseline versioning controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B31-R01","D55-B31-R04"],"relationships":["BASELINE_VERSIONING","CHANGE_TRACEABILITY"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B31-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Baselines","concept":"Baseline Evidence Binding","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"How should architecture baseline claims be supported?","answer":"Material baseline claims should be linked to evidence sources such as approved architecture artifacts, configuration records, deployment definitions, interface specifications, inventories, or verified implementation observations. The evidence source and observation time should be identifiable.","context":"An architecture baseline can become misleading when it records assertions without indicating how those assertions were established.","objective":"Increase trustworthiness and auditability of architecture baselines.","requirements":["Evidence source identification","Observation or effective time","Clear relationship between claim and evidence","Distinction between observed and inferred information"],"constraints":["Some architectural properties cannot be directly observed","Evidence may have different reliability levels"],"prerequisites":["Evidence classification scheme","Architecture repository"],"dependencies":["Source systems and documentation"],"alternatives":["Unreferenced architecture descriptions","Manual attestation only"],"evidence":"Evidence binding allows reviewers to challenge or refresh baseline claims rather than treating documentation as unquestionable truth.","tradeoffs":["Evidence maintenance adds effort","Highly dynamic systems may require automated evidence collection"],"decision":"Bind material baseline claims to identifiable evidence and preserve whether each claim is observed, derived, or inferred.","rationale":"Governance quality depends on knowing the evidentiary basis of architecture statements.","consequences":["Reviewers can assess evidence quality","Stale evidence can be detected","Architecture records become more auditable"],"implementation_implications":["Add evidence references to architecture records","Record evidence timestamps","Classify evidence strength","Define refresh requirements"],"verification_method":"Sample baseline claims and determine whether each has traceable and appropriately classified evidence.","outcome_learning":"Unsupported claims should become candidates for stronger evidence collection or removal from authoritative baseline status.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B31-R01","D55-B31-R02"],"relationships":["EVIDENCE_BINDING","BASELINE_ASSURANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B31-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Baselines","concept":"Baseline Change Detection","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should architecture governance detect baseline divergence?","answer":"Governance should detect divergence when material implementation, dependency, boundary, technology, data-flow, security, or deployment changes occur without a corresponding architecture baseline update or approved transition record.","context":"A baseline loses governance value when implementation changes silently accumulate outside architectural records.","objective":"Identify architectural drift between governed representation and implemented state.","requirements":["Defined material-change criteria","Change or evidence signals","Comparison capability","Ownership for resolving divergence"],"constraints":["Some changes are intentionally below baseline materiality thresholds","Automated detection may produce incomplete or noisy results"],"prerequisites":["Current baseline","Change evidence","Materiality policy"],"dependencies":["Deployment systems","Configuration repositories","Change management"],"alternatives":["Periodic manual review","Continuous automated comparison","Hybrid review"],"evidence":"Divergence detection is a governance mechanism for identifying where the authoritative architecture representation no longer matches implementation evidence.","tradeoffs":["Continuous detection improves freshness but requires integration","Manual review may miss changes between review cycles"],"decision":"Use risk-based divergence detection combining materiality rules with available implementation evidence.","rationale":"Not every implementation change needs governance escalation, but material architectural divergence should not remain invisible.","consequences":["Drift becomes an explicit governance condition","False positives require triage","Baseline owners need remediation responsibilities"],"implementation_implications":["Define architectural drift signals","Classify severity","Create remediation workflow","Link detected divergence to affected baseline elements"],"verification_method":"Introduce known material changes and verify that the governance process identifies or records the resulting divergence.","outcome_learning":"Repeated undetected divergence should trigger review of detection coverage and materiality rules.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B31-R01","D55-B31-R03"],"relationships":["ARCHITECTURE_DRIFT","CONFORMANCE_MONITORING"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B31-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B31","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Baselines","concept":"Baseline Authority","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What makes an architecture baseline authoritative?","answer":"An architecture baseline is authoritative when its scope, owner, effective version, evidence basis, approval or acceptance status, and relationship to implementation are explicitly defined. A document is not authoritative merely because it is stored in an architecture repository.","context":"Multiple conflicting architecture representations commonly exist in complex organizations.","objective":"Prevent ambiguity about which architecture representation governance should use.","requirements":["Defined scope","Named ownership","Version identity","Status or approval state","Evidence basis"],"constraints":["Different views may be authoritative for different purposes","Ownership can change over time"],"prerequisites":["Architecture repository","Governance ownership model"],"dependencies":["Architecture standards","Decision governance"],"alternatives":["Single universal architecture artifact","Multiple scoped authoritative views"],"evidence":"Authority must be explicit when multiple architecture descriptions coexist.","tradeoffs":["Multiple authoritative views require reconciliation","A single representation may become too broad or difficult to maintain"],"decision":"Define authority per architecture scope and purpose rather than assuming one artifact represents every governance concern.","rationale":"Explicit authority reduces conflicts between logical, deployment, data, security, and implementation views.","consequences":["Architecture artifacts require status metadata","Conflicting views can be escalated systematically"],"implementation_implications":["Record scope and authority metadata","Define precedence rules","Retire superseded authoritative views"],"verification_method":"For a selected architecture question, verify that reviewers can identify the authoritative representation and its evidence basis.","outcome_learning":"Repeated authority ambiguity indicates governance metadata or repository structure problems.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B31-R01","D55-B31-R03"],"relationships":["AUTHORITY_MODEL","ARCHITECTURE_BASELINE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B32-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Target Architecture","concept":"Target-State Architecture Definition","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What is the role of a target-state architecture in governance?","answer":"A target-state architecture describes the intended future architecture sufficiently to guide transformation, constrain design choices, identify dependencies, and evaluate whether proposed changes move the system toward agreed objectives.","context":"Governance requires a future reference in addition to understanding the current implementation state.","objective":"Provide a controlled architectural direction for transformation.","requirements":["Defined objectives","Scope and boundaries","Target capabilities or qualities","Major structural constraints","Traceability to strategic or system requirements"],"constraints":["The target state may evolve","Long-range details may be uncertain","Over-specification can constrain legitimate implementation alternatives"],"prerequisites":["Current-state baseline","Requirements","Architecture principles"],"dependencies":["Architecture decisions","Transformation roadmap"],"alternatives":["Detailed fixed target architecture","Capability-oriented target state","No explicit target state"],"evidence":"A target architecture is useful when it provides enough direction to evaluate transition decisions without pretending that uncertain future details are known.","tradeoffs":["More detail increases guidance but reduces flexibility","Less detail preserves flexibility but may weaken governance"],"decision":"Define target architecture at the level necessary to govern strategic direction while leaving justified implementation flexibility.","rationale":"Governance should control architectural outcomes and critical constraints without unnecessarily prescribing every implementation detail.","consequences":["Transition decisions gain a common reference","Target-state assumptions require periodic review"],"implementation_implications":["Separate mandatory target properties from illustrative designs","Record assumptions","Link target elements to requirements and decisions"],"verification_method":"Check whether proposed changes can be evaluated against explicit target-state objectives and constraints.","outcome_learning":"Repeated target-state ambiguity should lead to clarification of required outcomes or architectural boundaries.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B32-R02","D55-B32-R03"],"relationships":["TARGET_STATE","ARCHITECTURE_DIRECTION"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B32-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Target Architecture","concept":"Target-State Assumption Management","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should target-state architecture assumptions be explicitly recorded?","answer":"Target-state assumptions should be recorded because future architecture depends on conditions that may change, such as expected capabilities, technology availability, organizational constraints, regulatory requirements, or dependency timelines. Explicit assumptions make those dependencies reviewable.","context":"Unrecorded assumptions can become hidden constraints and make a target architecture appear more certain than its evidence supports.","objective":"Make uncertainty and dependency conditions visible in target-state governance.","requirements":["Assumption statement","Origin or rationale","Expected validity period when known","Impact if invalidated","Review condition"],"constraints":["Future conditions cannot always be predicted","Some assumptions are difficult to quantify"],"prerequisites":["Target architecture","Requirement analysis"],"dependencies":["External dependencies","Strategic planning"],"alternatives":["Implicit assumptions","Fixed commitments without assumption tracking"],"evidence":"Explicit assumptions allow governance to distinguish architectural commitments from conditional expectations.","tradeoffs":["Assumption tracking adds governance overhead","Insufficient tracking hides risk"],"decision":"Record material target-state assumptions and define reassessment triggers where their invalidation could change architecture direction.","rationale":"Architecture governance should expose uncertainty rather than convert assumptions into undocumented facts.","consequences":["Target architecture becomes easier to reassess","Additional review metadata is required"],"implementation_implications":["Assign stable assumption identifiers","Link assumptions to affected architecture elements","Track invalidation events"],"verification_method":"Review target architecture records and verify that material conditional claims have identifiable assumptions or evidence.","outcome_learning":"Frequently invalidated assumptions should become explicit risk or planning inputs.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B32-R01","D55-B32-R04"],"relationships":["ASSUMPTION_MANAGEMENT","TARGET_STATE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B32-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Target Architecture","concept":"Target-State Constraint Classification","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"How should target-state architectural constraints be classified?","answer":"Target-state constraints should be distinguished according to their governance strength, such as mandatory requirements, architectural guardrails, preferred practices, or illustrative guidance. This prevents recommendations from being mistaken for hard constraints.","context":"Ambiguous constraint strength can cause either unnecessary restriction or failure to enforce required architectural properties.","objective":"Make target-state constraints enforceable according to their intended authority.","requirements":["Explicit constraint classification","Rationale for mandatory constraints","Clear ownership","Review mechanism"],"constraints":["Organizations may use different terminology","Constraint strength may vary by architecture scope"],"prerequisites":["Governance policy","Architecture principles"],"dependencies":["Requirements","Compliance obligations"],"alternatives":["Uniform mandatory constraints","Unclassified guidance"],"evidence":"Constraint classification is necessary to distinguish required conformance from recommended design guidance.","tradeoffs":["Detailed classification increases governance clarity","Too many categories may increase complexity"],"decision":"Classify target-state constraints by enforcement strength and preserve the rationale for material mandatory constraints.","rationale":"Governance requires reviewers to know which architectural properties are obligations versus guidance.","consequences":["Conformance assessment becomes more precise","Exception processes can focus on actual mandatory constraints"],"implementation_implications":["Define constraint categories","Attach category metadata to target requirements","Use category-aware review rules"],"verification_method":"Select representative target constraints and verify that their required enforcement strength is unambiguous.","outcome_learning":"Frequent disputes over constraint strength indicate unclear governance policy or poor requirement translation.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B32-R01","D55-B32-R05"],"relationships":["CONSTRAINT_CLASSIFICATION","TARGET_STATE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B32-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Target Architecture","concept":"Target-State Feasibility Review","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should a target-state feasibility review establish?","answer":"A target-state feasibility review should determine whether the intended architecture is plausibly achievable within known technical, operational, organizational, security, data, dependency, and resource constraints. It should distinguish established feasibility evidence from assumptions requiring validation.","context":"A target architecture can be strategically desirable while still containing infeasible or insufficiently validated elements.","objective":"Prevent governance from treating unvalidated target architecture assumptions as implementation certainty.","requirements":["Constraint analysis","Dependency assessment","Risk identification","Evidence classification","Validation needs"],"constraints":["Long-term feasibility may remain uncertain","Some validation requires experiments or implementation"],"prerequisites":["Target architecture","Constraints","Dependencies"],"dependencies":["Architecture experiments","Capacity analysis","Security analysis"],"alternatives":["Desk review only","Prototype-based feasibility review","Formal architecture experiment"],"evidence":"Feasibility confidence should correspond to the evidence available rather than the level of architectural detail.","tradeoffs":["More validation increases confidence but consumes resources","Limited validation leaves uncertainty"],"decision":"Use risk-based feasibility reviews and explicitly identify unresolved feasibility questions.","rationale":"Governance should expose uncertainty before it becomes an expensive implementation commitment.","consequences":["High-risk target elements receive validation attention","Architecture plans may change based on evidence"],"implementation_implications":["Track feasibility questions","Define validation owners","Record experiment outcomes","Update target architecture when assumptions fail"],"verification_method":"Trace each material feasibility concern to evidence, an accepted uncertainty, or a planned validation activity.","outcome_learning":"Failed feasibility assumptions should update both architectural guidance and future estimation practices.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B32-R02","D55-B32-R03"],"relationships":["FEASIBILITY_GOVERNANCE","ARCHITECTURE_VALIDATION"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B32-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B32","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Target Architecture","concept":"Target-State Evolution","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should a target-state architecture be revised?","answer":"A target-state architecture should be revised when material requirements, assumptions, constraints, dependencies, technology conditions, risk posture, or strategic objectives change sufficiently to invalidate existing architectural direction.","context":"A target architecture that cannot evolve becomes a governance obstacle rather than a useful control.","objective":"Keep future-state architecture aligned with materially changed conditions.","requirements":["Change triggers","Impact assessment","Revision authority","Version history","Traceability to changed evidence"],"constraints":["Frequent revisions can create instability","Minor changes should not trigger unnecessary governance cycles"],"prerequisites":["Target-state baseline","Assumption and requirement tracking"],"dependencies":["Architecture decisions","Strategic planning","Risk management"],"alternatives":["Fixed target architecture","Periodic scheduled revision","Event-triggered revision"],"evidence":"Revision should be driven by material changes rather than arbitrary documentation activity.","tradeoffs":["Event-driven revision improves relevance","Excessive sensitivity creates governance churn"],"decision":"Use materiality-based triggers combined with scheduled health reviews to determine whether target architecture remains valid.","rationale":"Both emerging changes and slow assumption drift can invalidate architectural direction.","consequences":["Target-state history becomes traceable","Governance must distinguish material from minor change"],"implementation_implications":["Define revision triggers","Preserve prior target states","Record reasons for revision","Reassess dependent roadmap elements"],"verification_method":"Review target-state revisions and verify that each material revision has an identifiable trigger and impact assessment.","outcome_learning":"Revision frequency and trigger patterns should inform future target-state planning and governance thresholds.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B32-R01","D55-B32-R02"],"relationships":["TARGET_STATE_LIFECYCLE","ARCHITECTURE_REASSESSMENT"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B33-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Transition Architecture","concept":"Transition Architecture","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why is transition architecture needed between current and target states?","answer":"Transition architecture defines an intermediate architectural state or set of controlled states that enable incremental movement from the current architecture toward the target architecture when a direct transition is impractical, risky, or operationally disruptive.","context":"Complex transformations frequently require staged architecture rather than an immediate replacement of the current state.","objective":"Govern intermediate architecture states as deliberate steps rather than accidental temporary conditions.","requirements":["Defined starting state","Defined transition objective","Temporary or intermediate constraints","Exit criteria","Relationship to target state"],"constraints":["Intermediate states may increase complexity","Temporary designs can become permanent","Multiple transition states may overlap"],"prerequisites":["Current-state baseline","Target-state architecture"],"dependencies":["Transformation roadmap","Architecture decisions"],"alternatives":["Big-bang migration","Incremental transition architecture","Parallel replacement"],"evidence":"Explicit transition states make temporary architectural conditions governable and measurable.","tradeoffs":["Staged transitions reduce migration risk but may temporarily increase operational complexity"],"decision":"Use explicit transition architecture when incremental migration creates materially different architectural states that require governance.","rationale":"Governance should not treat transitional architecture as an undocumented exception to the target state.","consequences":["Intermediate states receive explicit ownership and exit conditions","Temporary complexity becomes visible"],"implementation_implications":["Define transition-state boundaries","Record intended duration","Specify exit criteria","Monitor divergence from target state"],"verification_method":"Verify that each governed transition state has an explicit purpose, owner, and exit condition.","outcome_learning":"Transition states that persist beyond intended periods should trigger reassessment of migration feasibility or architecture direction.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B33-R02","D55-B33-R03"],"relationships":["TRANSITION_ARCHITECTURE","TARGET_STATE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B33-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Transition Architecture","concept":"Transition Exit Criteria","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What makes an architecture transition complete from a governance perspective?","answer":"A transition is governance-complete when its defined target conditions, required controls, migration obligations, validation evidence, and decommissioning or handover activities have been satisfied and the resulting architecture has an authoritative state representation.","context":"Implementation activity alone does not prove that an architectural transition has reached its intended state.","objective":"Prevent partially completed transitions from being treated as closed.","requirements":["Explicit exit criteria","Verification evidence","Residual risk assessment","Updated architecture baseline","Ownership handover where applicable"],"constraints":["Some target properties require observation over time","Operational acceptance may be separate from technical completion"],"prerequisites":["Transition architecture","Acceptance criteria"],"dependencies":["Testing","Operational readiness","Architecture baseline"],"alternatives":["Deployment-based completion","Checklist-only completion","Evidence-based completion"],"evidence":"Transition closure requires evidence that intended architectural conditions have been achieved, not merely that implementation work occurred.","tradeoffs":["Stronger closure criteria increase assurance but delay formal closure"],"decision":"Require evidence-based exit criteria before marking a material architecture transition complete.","rationale":"Governance should distinguish implementation activity from verified architectural outcome.","consequences":["Incomplete transitions remain visible","Closure requires cross-functional evidence"],"implementation_implications":["Define measurable exit conditions","Attach evidence to closure","Update authoritative architecture state"],"verification_method":"Evaluate transition closure records against every mandatory exit criterion and supporting evidence.","outcome_learning":"Repeated failed exit criteria can reveal unrealistic transition planning or inadequate validation methods.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B33-R01","D55-B33-R04"],"relationships":["TRANSITION_CLOSURE","VERIFICATION"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B33-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Transition Architecture","concept":"Temporary Architecture Risk","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should temporary architecture receive explicit risk governance?","answer":"Temporary architecture can introduce additional interfaces, duplicated capabilities, compatibility layers, data synchronization paths, or operational complexity. These risks should be explicitly identified because temporary status does not make their consequences temporary.","context":"Migration periods often create architectural conditions that are more complex than either the current or target state.","objective":"Control risks introduced by transitional designs.","requirements":["Temporary-state risk assessment","Owner","Mitigation","Exit or removal condition"],"constraints":["Risk may be acceptable during bounded migration","Temporary components may become difficult to remove"],"prerequisites":["Transition architecture","Risk assessment"],"dependencies":["Migration plan","Operational controls"],"alternatives":["No separate temporary risk assessment","Standard architecture risk assessment","Time-bounded transition risk register"],"evidence":"Temporary architecture can persist or create dependencies if not governed explicitly.","tradeoffs":["Additional governance effort improves visibility","Over-governance can slow low-risk transitions"],"decision":"Assess material risks introduced specifically by transitional architecture and assign controls or exit conditions.","rationale":"Temporary status should not exempt architecture from risk analysis.","consequences":["Transition risk becomes traceable","Temporary components require lifecycle ownership"],"implementation_implications":["Tag transition-specific components","Track temporary dependencies","Monitor risk until removal"],"verification_method":"Review transition risk records and verify that material temporary components have owners, controls, and exit conditions.","outcome_learning":"Persistent temporary components should be treated as normal architecture candidates for reassessment.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B33-R01","D55-B33-R05"],"relationships":["TRANSITION_RISK","TEMPORARY_COMPONENT_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B33-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Transition Architecture","concept":"Transition Dependency Control","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"How should dependencies between transition states be governed?","answer":"Dependencies between transition states should be explicit, ordered where necessary, and associated with prerequisites or compatibility conditions. A transition should not be considered independently complete when its downstream architectural assumptions remain unsatisfied.","context":"Large transformations often contain dependent migrations where one architectural state enables another.","objective":"Prevent unsafe or logically inconsistent transition sequencing.","requirements":["Dependency identification","Prerequisite definition","Ordering where required","Dependency status"],"constraints":["Some dependencies can execute in parallel","Dependencies may be technical, organizational, data-related, or operational"],"prerequisites":["Transition roadmap","Architecture dependency model"],"dependencies":["Migration workstreams","System interfaces"],"alternatives":["Independent workstream planning","Dependency-aware transition governance"],"evidence":"Explicit transition dependencies reduce ambiguity about sequencing and readiness.","tradeoffs":["Dependency modeling requires maintenance","Overly strict sequencing can reduce legitimate parallelism"],"decision":"Govern material transition dependencies explicitly while allowing parallel execution where evidence supports independence.","rationale":"Governance should constrain true dependencies without imposing unnecessary serial execution.","consequences":["Readiness becomes more measurable","Blocked transitions become identifiable"],"implementation_implications":["Represent dependency types","Define satisfaction criteria","Track blocked and satisfied dependencies"],"verification_method":"Trace each material transition dependency to evidence showing whether its prerequisite is satisfied.","outcome_learning":"Unexpected dependency failures should update future transition planning and dependency discovery methods.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B33-R02","D55-B33-R03"],"relationships":["TRANSITION_DEPENDENCY","MIGRATION_READINESS"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B33-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B33","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Transition Architecture","concept":"Transition Architecture Retirement","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should obsolete transition architecture be retired?","answer":"Obsolete transition architecture should be retired after its purpose is satisfied or the transition direction changes, with dependent components, documentation, risks, and governance records updated so the temporary state does not remain falsely authoritative.","context":"Migration artifacts often survive their intended transition and become undocumented permanent architecture.","objective":"Prevent transitional designs from accumulating as unmanaged permanent complexity.","requirements":["Retirement trigger","Dependency review","Updated baseline","Residual-risk review","Historical record preservation"],"constraints":["Some transition artifacts may be retained for compatibility","Retirement can require coordinated changes"],"prerequisites":["Transition inventory","Target-state validation"],"dependencies":["Current-state baseline","Change management"],"alternatives":["Manual cleanup","Lifecycle-managed transition components"],"evidence":"Retirement is necessary when a temporary architectural state no longer represents an intended or supported condition.","tradeoffs":["Aggressive retirement reduces complexity but may remove useful compatibility","Delayed retirement increases architectural debt"],"decision":"Give transition components explicit retirement criteria and preserve historical records after retirement.","rationale":"Governance must manage the full lifecycle of temporary architecture.","consequences":["Temporary complexity becomes measurable","Retirement work becomes visible"],"implementation_implications":["Track lifecycle state","Identify dependents before retirement","Update architecture authority after removal"],"verification_method":"Verify that retired transition components no longer appear as active authoritative architecture elements and that dependent records are updated.","outcome_learning":"Repeated retirement delays should inform migration planning and ownership accountability.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B33-R01","D55-B33-R02"],"relationships":["TRANSITION_RETIREMENT","ARCHITECTURE_CLEANUP"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B34-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Roadmaps","concept":"Architecture Roadmap Governance","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should architecture governance control in an architecture roadmap?","answer":"Architecture governance should control the architectural outcomes, dependencies, decision points, constraints, risks, and validation gates represented in the roadmap rather than treating the roadmap as a generic project schedule.","context":"Architecture roadmaps connect architectural direction to transformation activity but should not duplicate detailed project management.","objective":"Maintain architectural coherence across transformation planning.","requirements":["Current and target states","Transition milestones","Dependencies","Architecture decision points","Validation gates","Risk visibility"],"constraints":["Project schedules may change independently","Architecture roadmaps should avoid unnecessary implementation detail"],"prerequisites":["Current-state baseline","Target-state architecture","Transition architecture"],"dependencies":["Transformation planning","Architecture decisions"],"alternatives":["Project-only roadmap","Architecture-only roadmap","Integrated roadmap with distinct governance layers"],"evidence":"Architecture roadmaps are most useful when they expose architectural dependencies and decision points rather than duplicating task-level schedules.","tradeoffs":["Integration improves coherence but requires coordination","Separation reduces coupling but may hide architectural dependencies"],"decision":"Maintain architecture-specific roadmap information linked to relevant delivery plans without duplicating all project-management detail.","rationale":"Governance needs architectural sequencing and dependencies while preserving appropriate separation of responsibilities.","consequences":["Architecture milestones become traceable","Roadmap ownership must be explicit"],"implementation_implications":["Define architecture milestone types","Link milestones to decisions and transitions","Record dependencies and validation gates"],"verification_method":"Review roadmap entries and verify that material architecture dependencies and decision gates are represented.","outcome_learning":"Repeated roadmap surprises should inform dependency discovery and milestone design.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B34-R02","D55-B34-R03"],"relationships":["ARCHITECTURE_ROADMAP","TRANSFORMATION_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B34-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Roadmaps","concept":"Architecture Milestone Definition","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What makes an architecture roadmap milestone governance-useful?","answer":"A governance-useful architecture milestone represents a meaningful architectural state change, decision, validation point, dependency satisfaction, or risk-control condition rather than merely a date or project task completion.","context":"Task completion does not necessarily demonstrate architectural progress.","objective":"Make roadmap milestones meaningful for architecture governance.","requirements":["Defined architectural condition","Completion evidence","Owner","Dependencies where applicable","Relationship to target state"],"constraints":["Some architecture progress is continuous","Not every technical task has architectural significance"],"prerequisites":["Target architecture","Transition plan"],"dependencies":["Delivery evidence","Architecture validation"],"alternatives":["Calendar-based milestones","Task-based milestones","State-based architecture milestones"],"evidence":"State-based milestones provide stronger architectural meaning than dates alone.","tradeoffs":["State-based milestones require explicit acceptance conditions","Date-only milestones are easier to maintain but less informative"],"decision":"Prefer state-based architecture milestones with evidence-based completion criteria.","rationale":"Governance should measure architectural state changes rather than activity volume.","consequences":["Milestone closure requires evidence","Roadmap quality improves when architectural outcomes are explicit"],"implementation_implications":["Define milestone acceptance criteria","Attach evidence","Link milestones to affected architecture elements"],"verification_method":"Verify that milestone completion can be demonstrated by an observable architectural condition or documented decision.","outcome_learning":"Milestones repeatedly closed without meaningful architectural change should be redesigned.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B34-R01","D55-B34-R04"],"relationships":["ARCHITECTURE_MILESTONE","EVIDENCE_BASED_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B34-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Roadmaps","concept":"Roadmap Dependency Governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should architecture roadmap dependencies be governed separately from schedule dependencies?","answer":"Architecture dependencies describe conditions in which one architectural change requires another capability, interface, data state, platform, decision, or constraint to exist. These dependencies can remain relevant even when project schedules change.","context":"Project scheduling and architectural dependency structures represent different concerns.","objective":"Preserve architectural dependency integrity across changing delivery schedules.","requirements":["Dependency type","Source and target","Satisfaction condition","Owner","Status"],"constraints":["Some dependencies are conditional","Schedule changes may invalidate planned sequencing"],"prerequisites":["Architecture dependency model","Roadmap"],"dependencies":["Transformation planning","Architecture decisions"],"alternatives":["Schedule-only dependency tracking","Architecture dependency register"],"evidence":"Architectural dependencies can outlive individual project schedules and therefore require their own governance representation.","tradeoffs":["Separate tracking adds metadata","Embedding everything in project plans risks losing architecture-level relationships"],"decision":"Maintain material architectural dependencies independently while linking them to delivery schedules when relevant.","rationale":"Architectural dependency integrity should survive project planning changes.","consequences":["Dependency impact remains visible during rescheduling","Architecture governance can challenge unsafe sequencing"],"implementation_implications":["Define architectural dependency categories","Track dependency satisfaction","Link to delivery work without making delivery systems authoritative for architecture"],"verification_method":"Change a project schedule and verify that material architectural dependency relationships remain intact and reviewable.","outcome_learning":"Frequent dependency surprises indicate weaknesses in architecture discovery or dependency classification.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B34-R01","D55-B34-R04"],"relationships":["ARCHITECTURE_DEPENDENCY","ROADMAP_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B34-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Roadmaps","concept":"Architecture Roadmap Risk Gates","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architectural risks influence roadmap progression?","answer":"Material architectural risks should influence roadmap progression through explicit review or validation gates when proceeding without mitigation or evidence could create irreversible cost, security exposure, operational fragility, or architectural inconsistency.","context":"A roadmap should not progress solely because scheduled work is complete when material architecture uncertainty remains unresolved.","objective":"Connect architecture risk with transformation progression decisions.","requirements":["Risk severity criteria","Gate conditions","Evidence requirements","Escalation ownership"],"constraints":["Not every risk requires blocking progression","Risk tolerance varies by system and context"],"prerequisites":["Architecture risk model","Roadmap milestones"],"dependencies":["Risk governance","Architecture validation"],"alternatives":["No gates","Uniform blocking gates","Risk-based gates"],"evidence":"Risk-based gates preserve delivery flexibility while requiring evidence for material uncertainties.","tradeoffs":["Gates can prevent costly errors but may slow delivery","Weak gates can allow unresolved high-impact risk to propagate"],"decision":"Use risk-based architecture gates for material unresolved architectural risks.","rationale":"Governance should focus control effort on risks whose consequences justify intervention.","consequences":["Some roadmap milestones require architecture evidence","Risk ownership becomes explicit"],"implementation_implications":["Define gate thresholds","Link risks to milestones","Specify required evidence or mitigation"],"verification_method":"Select material roadmap risks and verify that progression conditions reflect their severity and uncertainty.","outcome_learning":"Repeated gate overrides should trigger review of risk thresholds or governance authority.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B34-R02","D55-B34-R03"],"relationships":["RISK_GATE","ROADMAP_CONTROL"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B34-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B34","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Roadmaps","concept":"Roadmap Reassessment Trigger","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What changes should trigger architecture roadmap reassessment?","answer":"Roadmap reassessment should be triggered by material changes to requirements, target architecture, architectural decisions, critical dependencies, risk exposure, technology lifecycle, regulatory constraints, or transformation assumptions.","context":"A roadmap can become invalid even when individual project plans remain internally consistent.","objective":"Keep architectural sequencing aligned with current evidence and decisions.","requirements":["Defined reassessment triggers","Impact analysis","Roadmap versioning","Affected dependency review"],"constraints":["Minor changes should not cause unnecessary roadmap churn","Some changes are detected only after implementation evidence appears"],"prerequisites":["Architecture roadmap","Target-state and decision records"],"dependencies":["Architecture change governance","Risk management"],"alternatives":["Fixed periodic review","Event-driven review","Hybrid review"],"evidence":"Material architectural changes can invalidate sequencing and dependencies even without changing individual task plans.","tradeoffs":["Event-driven review improves responsiveness","Frequent reassessment can increase governance overhead"],"decision":"Use materiality-based event triggers supplemented by periodic roadmap health reviews.","rationale":"Both sudden changes and gradual drift can make a roadmap obsolete.","consequences":["Roadmap validity becomes an explicit governance concern","Historical roadmap versions remain useful evidence"],"implementation_implications":["Define trigger taxonomy","Record reassessment outcomes","Propagate changes to dependent milestones"],"verification_method":"Review roadmap revisions and verify that material triggers are identifiable and affected dependencies were reassessed.","outcome_learning":"Trigger patterns should refine roadmap risk and planning assumptions over time.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B34-R01","D55-B34-R04"],"relationships":["ROADMAP_REASSESSMENT","ARCHITECTURE_CHANGE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B35-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Dependencies","concept":"Architecture Dependency Classification","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should architecture dependencies be classified by type?","answer":"Dependency classification distinguishes different causes and failure modes, such as technical interfaces, data dependencies, platform dependencies, organizational dependencies, regulatory dependencies, or temporal prerequisites. Classification improves analysis and ownership.","context":"A generic dependency label is often insufficient for determining how a dependency can fail or be satisfied.","objective":"Make architecture dependency governance actionable.","requirements":["Dependency category","Source","Target","Condition","Owner","Status"],"constraints":["Some dependencies span multiple categories","Classification must not obscure the underlying relationship"],"prerequisites":["Architecture dependency model"],"dependencies":["Architecture inventory","Roadmap"],"alternatives":["Unclassified dependency graph","Single dependency category","Multi-dimensional dependency classification"],"evidence":"Different dependency types require different validation and governance mechanisms.","tradeoffs":["Classification adds metadata","Overly complex taxonomies reduce usability"],"decision":"Use a concise dependency taxonomy that supports materially different governance actions.","rationale":"Governance should identify not only that a dependency exists but what kind of dependency it is.","consequences":["Ownership and validation can become dependency-specific","Dependency analysis becomes more precise"],"implementation_implications":["Define controlled categories","Allow multiple classifications where justified","Record dependency satisfaction conditions"],"verification_method":"Sample dependencies and verify that their classifications correspond to distinct governance or validation needs.","outcome_learning":"Repeated classification ambiguity should trigger taxonomy refinement.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B35-R02","D55-B35-R03"],"relationships":["DEPENDENCY_CLASSIFICATION","ARCHITECTURE_GRAPH"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B35-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Dependencies","concept":"Critical Dependency Identification","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What makes an architecture dependency critical?","answer":"A dependency is critical when failure, delay, or incorrect assumptions about it can materially affect architectural integrity, security, availability, data correctness, compliance, transformation feasibility, or other explicitly governed outcomes.","context":"Not every dependency warrants the same governance intensity.","objective":"Focus dependency governance on relationships with material consequences.","requirements":["Impact assessment","Dependency condition","Failure consequence","Evidence of criticality"],"constraints":["Criticality can vary by scenario","Impact may be difficult to estimate before implementation"],"prerequisites":["Dependency inventory","Risk criteria"],"dependencies":["Architecture risk governance"],"alternatives":["Treat all dependencies equally","Risk-based dependency criticality"],"evidence":"Criticality should derive from potential consequence and uncertainty rather than dependency count alone.","tradeoffs":["Risk-based classification improves focus","Some lower-rated dependencies may still combine into systemic risk"],"decision":"Classify critical dependencies using consequence, uncertainty, and architectural centrality rather than frequency alone.","rationale":"Governance capacity should be concentrated where dependency failure has material architectural consequences.","consequences":["Critical dependencies receive stronger monitoring","Non-critical relationships can use lighter controls"],"implementation_implications":["Define criticality criteria","Record affected outcomes","Review critical dependencies during architecture changes"],"verification_method":"Check whether identified critical dependencies have explicit rationale and corresponding governance controls.","outcome_learning":"Unexpected dependency failures should feed back into criticality criteria and dependency discovery.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B35-R01","D55-B35-R04"],"relationships":["CRITICAL_DEPENDENCY","ARCHITECTURE_RISK"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B35-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Dependencies","concept":"Dependency Ownership","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should material architecture dependencies have explicit owners?","answer":"Explicit ownership ensures that someone is accountable for validating the dependency condition, monitoring changes, coordinating resolution, and escalating unresolved risk. Ownership does not necessarily mean the owner controls both sides of the dependency.","context":"Unowned dependencies commonly become invisible until they fail or block transformation.","objective":"Establish accountability for material architectural relationships.","requirements":["Owner identity","Ownership scope","Escalation path","Review responsibility"],"constraints":["Cross-organizational dependencies may require shared accountability","Ownership may change during organizational restructuring"],"prerequisites":["Dependency inventory","Governance ownership model"],"dependencies":["Architecture teams","System owners","Delivery teams"],"alternatives":["Shared informal responsibility","Explicit dependency ownership"],"evidence":"Accountability is required to maintain dependency status and respond to changes.","tradeoffs":["Ownership metadata requires maintenance","Shared ownership may be necessary for cross-boundary relationships"],"decision":"Assign explicit accountability for material dependencies and define escalation when ownership is disputed or absent.","rationale":"Governance cannot reliably manage material dependencies without accountable stewardship.","consequences":["Dependency status has an accountable maintainer","Ownership gaps become governance findings"],"implementation_implications":["Store owner references","Define owner responsibilities","Detect missing or stale ownership"],"verification_method":"Review material dependencies and verify that each has a current accountable owner or formally documented shared ownership.","outcome_learning":"Repeated ownership failures should inform organizational boundary and governance design.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B35-R01","D55-B35-R02"],"relationships":["DEPENDENCY_OWNERSHIP","ACCOUNTABILITY"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B35-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Dependencies","concept":"Dependency Change Impact","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should governance assess a change to a critical architecture dependency?","answer":"Governance should identify affected architecture elements, decisions, risks, contracts, roadmap milestones, operational assumptions, and downstream dependencies before accepting a material dependency change.","context":"Changing a dependency can have effects beyond the directly connected components.","objective":"Prevent local dependency changes from creating unrecognized system-level consequences.","requirements":["Impact propagation","Affected relationship discovery","Risk reassessment","Decision review where applicable"],"constraints":["Dependency graphs may be incomplete","Indirect impacts can require expert analysis"],"prerequisites":["Dependency graph","Architecture baseline"],"dependencies":["Change management","Architecture decisions"],"alternatives":["Local impact review","Graph-assisted impact analysis","Cross-domain architecture review"],"evidence":"Dependency changes can propagate through architectural relationships and should therefore be evaluated beyond the immediate interface.","tradeoffs":["Broad impact analysis increases assurance but can increase review effort"],"decision":"Use dependency-aware impact analysis for material architectural dependency changes.","rationale":"The architectural consequence of a change is not necessarily limited to the changed component.","consequences":["Change reviews become more systematic","Incomplete dependency models become visible risks"],"implementation_implications":["Trace transitive dependencies where justified","Record affected decisions and roadmap elements","Reassess critical risks"],"verification_method":"Select a material dependency change and verify that directly and materially indirectly affected architecture elements were considered.","outcome_learning":"Unexpected downstream effects should improve dependency graph completeness and impact analysis rules.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B35-R02","D55-B35-R05"],"relationships":["DEPENDENCY_IMPACT","CHANGE_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B35-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B35","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Dependencies","concept":"Dependency Obsolescence","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should obsolete architecture dependencies be handled?","answer":"Obsolete dependencies should be explicitly identified, validated as no longer active, removed or marked historical, and disconnected from active governance calculations so that stale relationships do not distort architecture risk or change analysis.","context":"Dependency repositories can accumulate relationships that no longer exist after migrations or interface retirement.","objective":"Maintain dependency model integrity over time.","requirements":["Obsolescence criteria","Validation evidence","Lifecycle status","Historical preservation"],"constraints":["Some relationships may be dormant rather than obsolete","Historical dependencies can remain useful for forensic analysis"],"prerequisites":["Dependency inventory","Current-state baseline"],"dependencies":["Architecture lifecycle","System retirement"],"alternatives":["Delete obsolete dependencies","Lifecycle status with historical retention"],"evidence":"Explicit dependency lifecycle prevents stale relationships from being interpreted as active architecture.","tradeoffs":["Retention improves history but requires status filtering","Deletion reduces clutter but loses historical context"],"decision":"Retain historical dependency information with explicit inactive or retired status where it has governance value.","rationale":"Governance requires both current accuracy and historical traceability.","consequences":["Active and historical dependencies can be distinguished","Repository queries require lifecycle-aware filtering"],"implementation_implications":["Define dependency lifecycle states","Record retirement evidence","Exclude inactive dependencies from active conformance calculations"],"verification_method":"Verify that retired dependencies are excluded from active architecture assessments while remaining retrievable when historical context is required.","outcome_learning":"Frequent stale dependencies should inform automated lifecycle detection and ownership practices.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B35-R01","D55-B35-R04"],"relationships":["DEPENDENCY_LIFECYCLE","ARCHITECTURE_MODEL_INTEGRITY"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B36-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Cross-Team Boundaries","concept":"Architecture Boundary Ownership","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why should architecture boundaries have explicit ownership?","answer":"Architecture boundaries define responsibility for interfaces, data, behavior, security controls, reliability expectations, and change coordination. Explicit ownership prevents important boundary responsibilities from falling between organizational teams.","context":"Many architecture failures occur at boundaries rather than inside individual components.","objective":"Make cross-team architectural responsibilities explicit.","requirements":["Boundary definition","Owning parties","Interface responsibilities","Escalation path","Change coordination mechanism"],"constraints":["Some boundaries have shared ownership","Organizational ownership can change independently of technical architecture"],"prerequisites":["System and component inventory","Organizational ownership model"],"dependencies":["API or interface governance","Security governance"],"alternatives":["Component-only ownership","Boundary-specific ownership"],"evidence":"Cross-team boundaries require explicit accountability because no single component owner necessarily controls the full interaction.","tradeoffs":["Explicit boundary governance adds coordination","Unclear ownership creates unmanaged integration risk"],"decision":"Assign ownership responsibilities to material architecture boundaries as well as components.","rationale":"Architectural correctness often depends on behavior spanning multiple ownership domains.","consequences":["Interface and boundary issues gain accountable owners","Cross-team changes require explicit coordination"],"implementation_implications":["Record boundary owners","Define shared responsibilities","Link boundaries to contracts and risks"],"verification_method":"Review material boundaries and verify that responsibility for interface, security, data, and operational concerns is identifiable.","outcome_learning":"Repeated boundary disputes should trigger refinement of ownership or organizational architecture.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B36-R02","D55-B36-R03"],"relationships":["BOUNDARY_OWNERSHIP","CROSS_TEAM_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B36-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Cross-Team Boundaries","concept":"Interface Responsibility","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What responsibilities should be governed at a cross-team interface?","answer":"A material interface should have explicit responsibility for contract definition, compatibility, security expectations, error behavior, observability requirements, change coordination, and lifecycle management appropriate to the interface type.","context":"A technical interface can remain syntactically valid while architectural responsibilities around it remain undefined.","objective":"Govern interfaces as managed architectural boundaries rather than only technical endpoints.","requirements":["Contract ownership","Compatibility policy","Error semantics","Security expectations","Change process"],"constraints":["Interface responsibilities vary by protocol and domain","Some responsibilities are shared"],"prerequisites":["Boundary ownership","Interface definition"],"dependencies":["API governance","Security architecture","Operational governance"],"alternatives":["Endpoint ownership only","Interface contract governance"],"evidence":"Interface behavior includes operational and governance properties beyond syntax.","tradeoffs":["Broader interface governance improves predictability but increases contract management effort"],"decision":"Govern material interfaces using explicit ownership and contract responsibilities appropriate to their risk and role.","rationale":"Reliable architecture requires predictable behavior across ownership boundaries.","consequences":["Interface changes become more traceable","Compatibility and operational concerns are surfaced earlier"],"implementation_implications":["Associate contracts with owners","Define lifecycle states","Record material interface guarantees"],"verification_method":"Review interface records and verify that material behavioral and ownership responsibilities are explicit.","outcome_learning":"Repeated interface incidents should refine contract requirements and governance checks.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B36-R01","D55-B36-R04"],"relationships":["INTERFACE_GOVERNANCE","BOUNDARY_CONTRACT"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B36-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Cross-Team Boundaries","concept":"Shared Responsibility Boundary","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture govern boundaries with shared responsibility?","answer":"Shared-responsibility boundaries should define responsibility domains explicitly, including which party owns decisions, implementation, monitoring, incident response, security controls, and change approval for each relevant concern.","context":"Declaring a boundary jointly owned without defining responsibility by concern can leave important tasks ambiguous.","objective":"Convert shared ownership into explicit responsibility allocation.","requirements":["Responsibility matrix","Decision authority","Escalation rules","Change responsibilities"],"constraints":["Responsibility can vary by environment or deployment model","Some controls may remain jointly operated"],"prerequisites":["Boundary definition","Participating team ownership"],"dependencies":["Operating model","Security and compliance controls"],"alternatives":["Single owner","Joint ownership with explicit responsibility matrix"],"evidence":"Shared ownership is effective only when responsibility for individual concerns is explicit.","tradeoffs":["Detailed responsibility mapping improves clarity but requires maintenance"],"decision":"Use concern-specific responsibility allocation for materially shared architecture boundaries.","rationale":"Shared ownership without explicit accountability can create governance gaps.","consequences":["Responsibility disputes become easier to resolve","Change and incident processes can identify accountable parties"],"implementation_implications":["Define responsibility categories","Map each concern to an accountable party","Review ownership after organizational changes"],"verification_method":"Select a shared boundary and verify that responsibility for each material concern can be identified without interpretation.","outcome_learning":"Recurring responsibility conflicts should inform boundary redesign or governance refinement.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B36-R01","D55-B36-R02"],"relationships":["SHARED_RESPONSIBILITY","BOUNDARY_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B36-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Cross-Team Boundaries","concept":"Boundary Change Coordination","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should a cross-team boundary change require architecture coordination?","answer":"A boundary change should receive architecture coordination when it materially changes interface contracts, data ownership, security trust relationships, reliability assumptions, dependency structure, or responsibilities between teams.","context":"Local component changes can become architectural changes when they alter ownership or system boundaries.","objective":"Identify boundary changes that require cross-team architectural governance.","requirements":["Materiality criteria","Affected-team identification","Impact assessment","Coordination path"],"constraints":["Not every implementation change affects the boundary","Boundary impact can be indirect"],"prerequisites":["Boundary inventory","Change classification"],"dependencies":["Change management","Interface governance"],"alternatives":["Team-local change review","Boundary-aware change classification"],"evidence":"Boundary changes can alter system-level properties even when internal component behavior appears unchanged.","tradeoffs":["Additional coordination may slow local changes","Failure to coordinate can create integration or ownership risk"],"decision":"Use boundary-impact criteria to determine when local changes require cross-team architecture coordination.","rationale":"Governance should intervene based on architectural impact rather than organizational origin of the change.","consequences":["Material boundary changes receive broader review","Teams retain autonomy for non-material internal changes"],"implementation_implications":["Define boundary-impact signals","Link changes to affected boundaries","Notify responsible teams"],"verification_method":"Evaluate representative changes and verify that material boundary impacts trigger the intended coordination path.","outcome_learning":"Missed boundary impacts should refine change classification rules.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B36-R02","D55-B36-R05"],"relationships":["BOUNDARY_CHANGE","CROSS_TEAM_COORDINATION"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B36-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B36","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Cross-Team Boundaries","concept":"Boundary Escalation","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should happen when teams cannot agree on an architecture boundary?","answer":"An unresolved material boundary dispute should be escalated using an explicit governance path that identifies the disputed concern, evidence, affected requirements, risks, alternatives, and decision authority. Escalation should resolve accountability rather than bypass evidence.","context":"Cross-team architecture disputes can block delivery or result in undocumented compromises.","objective":"Provide a controlled mechanism for resolving material boundary conflicts.","requirements":["Dispute statement","Evidence","Affected requirements","Risk analysis","Decision authority","Recorded outcome"],"constraints":["Escalation should not replace technical collaboration for routine issues","Decision authority must be appropriate to impact"],"prerequisites":["Boundary ownership model","Architecture governance process"],"dependencies":["Architecture decision governance","Risk governance"],"alternatives":["Informal negotiation","Formal escalation","Independent architecture review"],"evidence":"Structured escalation preserves traceability when local agreement cannot be reached.","tradeoffs":["Formal escalation adds latency","Unstructured resolution can create hidden architecture divergence"],"decision":"Use evidence-based escalation for material unresolved boundary disputes.","rationale":"Governance needs a repeatable path when cross-team negotiation reaches an architectural impasse.","consequences":["Boundary disputes become traceable","Decisions can be reviewed later"],"implementation_implications":["Define escalation thresholds","Capture evidence and alternatives","Link outcome to architecture records"],"verification_method":"Review escalated disputes and verify that evidence, authority, and outcome are recorded.","outcome_learning":"Recurring boundary disputes may indicate structural ownership problems or unclear architecture principles.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B36-R01","D55-B36-R04"],"relationships":["BOUNDARY_ESCALATION","ARCHITECTURE_DISPUTE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B37-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance","concept":"Platform Architecture Governance","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should architecture governance establish for shared platforms?","answer":"Platform architecture governance should define the platform's supported capabilities, boundaries, consumers, architectural constraints, lifecycle expectations, reliability and security responsibilities, extension mechanisms, and conditions under which exceptions are permitted.","context":"Shared platforms can amplify both architectural benefits and architectural constraints across many consumers.","objective":"Govern shared platforms so that reuse does not create uncontrolled coupling or hidden constraints.","requirements":["Capability boundaries","Consumer expectations","Support model","Security requirements","Lifecycle policy","Extension rules"],"constraints":["Platform requirements may conflict across consumers","Platform governance should not unnecessarily dictate application internals"],"prerequisites":["Platform architecture","Consumer landscape"],"dependencies":["Technology lifecycle","Security governance","Service ownership"],"alternatives":["Consumer-local governance","Central platform governance","Hybrid governance"],"evidence":"Shared platforms require stronger architectural boundary definition because multiple systems depend on their behavior.","tradeoffs":["Central standards improve consistency but can reduce local flexibility","Weak platform governance increases fragmentation"],"decision":"Govern shared platforms through explicit capability, boundary, lifecycle, and consumer-contract rules.","rationale":"Platform decisions can propagate architecture constraints across many dependent systems.","consequences":["Platform changes require broader impact analysis","Consumer expectations become explicit"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain platform architecture records","Define supported and unsupported usage","Track dependent systems"],"verification_method":"Review platform governance records and verify that material consumer and lifecycle expectations are explicit.","outcome_learning":"Recurring consumer conflicts should inform platform capability boundaries and governance rules.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B37-R02","D55-B37-R03"],"relationships":["PLATFORM_GOVERNANCE","SHARED_ARCHITECTURE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B37-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance","concept":"Platform Consumer Contract","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"Why should shared platforms define explicit consumer contracts?","answer":"Consumer contracts define supported usage, interfaces, service expectations, compatibility assumptions, security responsibilities, and lifecycle boundaries between the platform and its users. They prevent consumers from depending on undocumented behavior.","context":"Shared platform adoption can create hidden coupling when consumers rely on accidental implementation details.","objective":"Make platform-consumer relationships explicit and governable.","requirements":["Supported interfaces","Behavioral expectations","Compatibility policy","Consumer responsibilities","Deprecation process"],"constraints":["Contracts vary by platform capability","Some implementation behavior may remain intentionally private"],"prerequisites":["Platform architecture","Interface governance"],"dependencies":["Service ownership","Technology lifecycle"],"alternatives":["Documentation-only guidance","Formal consumer contracts","Platform-specific policies"],"evidence":"Explicit contracts reduce ambiguity about what platform consumers may depend upon.","tradeoffs":["Contract maintenance adds work","Overly broad contracts constrain platform evolution"],"decision":"Define contracts for material platform capabilities while keeping internal implementation details outside the supported dependency surface.","rationale":"Platforms require controlled dependency boundaries to evolve without breaking consumers unexpectedly.","consequences":["Unsupported consumer dependencies become identifiable","Deprecation becomes more manageable"],"implementation_implications":["Define contract scope","Version material interfaces","Record consumer responsibilities"],"verification_method":"Review consumer dependencies and determine whether each material dependency is within the supported platform contract.","outcome_learning":"Frequent unsupported dependencies should trigger platform boundary or documentation improvements.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B37-R01","D55-B37-R04"],"relationships":["PLATFORM_CONTRACT","CONSUMER_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B37-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance","concept":"Platform Extension Governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should extensions to a shared platform be governed?","answer":"Platform extensions should be governed according to their effect on platform boundaries, security, reliability, compatibility, operational support, and future evolution. Extensions that materially change shared behavior should receive architecture review rather than being treated as isolated consumer customization.","context":"Extension mechanisms can unintentionally turn consumer-specific requirements into platform-wide commitments.","objective":"Prevent uncontrolled platform expansion and hidden coupling.","requirements":["Extension classification","Impact assessment","Ownership","Lifecycle expectations","Compatibility analysis"],"constraints":["Some extensions are intentionally consumer-local","Platform extensibility should preserve platform integrity"],"prerequisites":["Platform boundary","Extension mechanism"],"dependencies":["Platform contracts","Architecture review"],"alternatives":["Unrestricted extensions","Central approval for every extension","Risk-based extension governance"],"evidence":"Extensions can alter shared architecture even when implementation occurs in a single consumer environment.","tradeoffs":["Risk-based review preserves agility","Uncontrolled extensions increase platform complexity"],"decision":"Apply architecture governance to extensions based on their architectural and operational impact.","rationale":"Governance should control shared consequences rather than every customization equally.","consequences":["Platform growth becomes more deliberate","High-impact extensions become traceable"],"implementation_implications":["Define extension classes","Identify shared-impact signals","Require lifecycle ownership"],"verification_method":"Review extensions and verify that shared-impact extensions received appropriate architectural assessment.","outcome_learning":"Recurring extension-related complexity should inform platform capability design.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B37-R01","D55-B37-R02"],"relationships":["PLATFORM_EXTENSION","ARCHITECTURE_CONTROL"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B37-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance","concept":"Platform Change Impact","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should material platform changes include consumer impact analysis?","answer":"A material platform change can propagate to many dependent systems even when the platform's internal architecture remains valid. Consumer impact analysis identifies affected contracts, compatibility assumptions, migration needs, risks, and required communication or validation.","context":"Shared platforms create architectural fan-out that can make local changes systemically significant.","objective":"Prevent platform changes from producing unmanaged consumer disruption.","requirements":["Consumer inventory","Affected contract analysis","Compatibility assessment","Migration requirements","Validation plan"],"constraints":["Consumer inventories may be incomplete","Impact depends on actual rather than declared dependencies"],"prerequisites":["Platform dependency inventory","Consumer contracts"],"dependencies":["Change management","Compatibility governance"],"alternatives":["Platform-local review","Consumer-aware impact analysis"],"evidence":"The architectural impact surface of a shared platform is determined partly by its consumers.","tradeoffs":["Broader analysis increases confidence but requires accurate dependency information"],"decision":"Require consumer-aware impact analysis for platform changes that can alter material supported behavior or constraints.","rationale":"Shared-platform governance must account for downstream architectural effects.","consequences":["Platform releases gain explicit architectural impact assessment","Consumer dependency quality becomes important"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain consumer relationships","Classify change impact","Link affected consumers to migration plans"],"verification_method":"For a representative material platform change, verify that affected consumers and compatibility implications were assessed.","outcome_learning":"Unexpected consumer impact should improve dependency discovery and contract quality.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B37-R02","D55-B37-R05"],"relationships":["PLATFORM_CHANGE","CONSUMER_IMPACT"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B37-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B37","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance","concept":"Platform Governance Boundary","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Where should platform governance stop?","answer":"Platform governance should control shared architectural properties and supported platform behavior while avoiding unnecessary control over consumer-internal implementation that does not affect platform contracts, shared risks, or governed architecture boundaries.","context":"Excessive platform governance can turn shared infrastructure into an organizational bottleneck.","objective":"Define an effective boundary between platform governance and consumer autonomy.","requirements":["Explicit governance scope","Materiality criteria","Supported contract definition","Exception path"],"constraints":["The boundary varies by platform risk and criticality","Security or regulatory requirements may expand governance scope"],"prerequisites":["Platform architecture","Governance principles"],"dependencies":["Consumer architecture","Security and compliance governance"],"alternatives":["Centralized control","Minimal platform governance","Risk-based boundary"],"evidence":"Governance should be proportional to shared architectural impact rather than organizational ownership alone.","tradeoffs":["Narrow governance preserves autonomy","Broad governance can improve consistency but increase coupling and approval overhead"],"decision":"Use a risk- and contract-based platform governance boundary.","rationale":"The platform should govern shared consequences while allowing consumer implementation freedom where safe.","consequences":["Consumer teams retain defined autonomy","Governance scope becomes reviewable"],"implementation_implications":["Document platform governance boundary","Define material-impact criteria","Review scope as platform responsibilities evolve"],"verification_method":"Evaluate representative consumer changes and determine whether governance involvement matches their actual platform impact.","outcome_learning":"Repeated over- or under-governance should trigger boundary reassessment.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B37-R01","D55-B37-R04"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_BOUNDARY","PLATFORM_AUTONOMY"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B38-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Technology Lifecycle","concept":"Technology Lifecycle Governance","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why should technology lifecycle status be part of architecture governance?","answer":"Technology lifecycle status affects architecture risk, supportability, security exposure, migration planning, compatibility, and long-term maintainability. Governance should therefore know whether material technologies are supported, approaching end of support, obsolete, or subject to replacement plans.","context":"Architecture decisions can become invalid when underlying technologies leave their supported lifecycle.","objective":"Integrate technology lifecycle information into architectural governance.","requirements":["Technology inventory","Lifecycle status","Source or evidence","Review triggers","Migration or exception path"],"constraints":["Lifecycle dates can change","Vendor or ecosystem information may be uncertain","Technology support varies by environment"],"prerequisites":["Technology inventory","Architecture baseline"],"dependencies":["Vendor information","Security governance","Roadmap"],"alternatives":["Manual periodic review","Automated lifecycle monitoring","Hybrid lifecycle governance"],"evidence":"Technology lifecycle is an architectural risk input rather than merely procurement information.","tradeoffs":["Lifecycle tracking requires maintenance","Automated monitoring may require external evidence sources"],"decision":"Track material technology lifecycle status and connect lifecycle changes to architecture risk and roadmap review.","rationale":"Unsupported technology can invalidate otherwise sound architectural assumptions.","consequences":["Lifecycle events can trigger architecture reassessment","Technology debt becomes more visible"],"implementation_implications":["Assign lifecycle metadata","Define status categories","Create reassessment triggers","Link technologies to affected architecture elements"],"verification_method":"Sample material technologies and verify that lifecycle status and evidence are current enough for governance use.","outcome_learning":"Repeated lifecycle surprises should improve inventory freshness and lifecycle evidence collection.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B38-R02","D55-B38-R03"],"relationships":["TECHNOLOGY_LIFECYCLE","ARCHITECTURE_RISK"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B38-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Technology Lifecycle","concept":"Technology Support Status","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance distinguish technology support states?","answer":"Technology support states should distinguish categories such as actively supported, maintenance-only, deprecated, unsupported, unknown, or organizationally restricted according to the evidence available. The classification should not imply more certainty than the source supports.","context":"A binary supported/unsupported classification can hide important lifecycle differences and uncertainty.","objective":"Represent technology lifecycle risk accurately enough for architectural decisions.","requirements":["Controlled lifecycle states","Evidence source","Effective date when available","Scope of support"],"constraints":["Support status may differ by version, platform, or vendor offering","Sources can conflict"],"prerequisites":["Technology inventory","Lifecycle evidence"],"dependencies":["Technology versions","Security information"],"alternatives":["Binary lifecycle status","Detailed lifecycle classification"],"evidence":"Lifecycle state is meaningful only when the affected version and scope are clear.","tradeoffs":["More states improve precision but increase governance complexity"],"decision":"Use version- and scope-aware lifecycle states with explicit uncertainty where evidence is incomplete.","rationale":"Architecture governance needs accurate lifecycle context rather than oversimplified labels.","consequences":["Version-specific risk becomes visible","Unknown lifecycle status can be treated as uncertainty rather than assumed support"],"implementation_implications":["Record technology version","Capture lifecycle source","Separate unknown from supported"],"verification_method":"Check lifecycle records for version, scope, evidence source, and classification consistency.","outcome_learning":"Frequent unknown states should motivate stronger inventory and evidence acquisition.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B38-R01","D55-B38-R04"],"relationships":["LIFECYCLE_STATUS","VERSION_AWARE_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B38-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Technology Lifecycle","concept":"Lifecycle Triggered Architecture Review","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should a technology lifecycle event trigger architecture review?","answer":"A lifecycle event should trigger architecture review when it materially changes supportability, security exposure, operational risk, compatibility, cost assumptions, or the feasibility of the existing architecture.","context":"Not every lifecycle change requires architecture intervention, but material lifecycle changes can invalidate architectural assumptions.","objective":"Connect technology lifecycle events to proportionate architectural reassessment.","requirements":["Materiality criteria","Affected architecture mapping","Risk assessment","Review or exception path"],"constraints":["Lifecycle changes can occur with short notice","Architecture impact may depend on actual usage"],"prerequisites":["Technology lifecycle inventory","Architecture dependency mapping"],"dependencies":["Security risk","Roadmap","Technology management"],"alternatives":["Automatic review for every lifecycle change","Risk-based review","Periodic lifecycle review"],"evidence":"Architecture impact depends on how the technology is used and what architectural properties depend on it.","tradeoffs":["Automatic review increases assurance but creates noise","Risk-based review requires impact classification"],"decision":"Use risk-based lifecycle triggers tied to affected architecture elements and usage criticality.","rationale":"Governance should respond to material architectural consequences rather than lifecycle labels alone.","consequences":["Lifecycle changes become architectural signals","Affected systems require traceable reassessment"],"implementation_implications":["Map technologies to architecture elements","Define impact thresholds","Create lifecycle-triggered review workflow"],"verification_method":"Test representative lifecycle events and verify that material architectural impacts trigger the intended governance path.","outcome_learning":"Missed lifecycle impacts should improve technology-to-architecture dependency mapping.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B38-R01","D55-B38-R02"],"relationships":["LIFECYCLE_TRIGGER","ARCHITECTURE_REASSESSMENT"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B38-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Technology Lifecycle","concept":"Technology Replacement Governance","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should governance establish before replacing a material technology?","answer":"Technology replacement governance should establish the affected architectural scope, requirements, compatibility constraints, migration approach, transition risks, validation criteria, rollback or contingency considerations where relevant, and retirement conditions for the replaced technology.","context":"Replacing a technology can alter architecture beyond the technology itself.","objective":"Govern technology replacement as an architectural transition rather than a procurement-only activity.","requirements":["Affected architecture mapping","Requirements","Compatibility analysis","Migration plan","Validation criteria","Retirement criteria"],"constraints":["Replacement may require coexistence","Migration sequencing can constrain options"],"prerequisites":["Technology lifecycle evidence","Architecture baseline","Target or transition architecture"],"dependencies":["Roadmap","Architecture decisions","Testing"],"alternatives":["Direct replacement","Incremental migration","Compatibility layer transition"],"evidence":"Technology replacement is architecturally significant when other components depend on its behavior or characteristics.","tradeoffs":["Incremental migration reduces disruption but extends transition complexity","Direct replacement may reduce duration but increase migration risk"],"decision":"Govern material technology replacements as architecture transitions with explicit compatibility and validation criteria.","rationale":"Replacement changes should be governed according to their system-level architectural impact.","consequences":["Replacement plans become traceable","Retirement is treated as part of architecture lifecycle"],"implementation_implications":["Map dependencies","Define compatibility tests","Record transition state","Verify retirement"],"verification_method":"Verify that a replacement plan identifies affected architecture elements and measurable acceptance conditions.","outcome_learning":"Replacement defects should update technology dependency and migration governance practices.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B38-R03","D55-B38-R05"],"relationships":["TECHNOLOGY_REPLACEMENT","ARCHITECTURE_TRANSITION"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B38-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B38","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Technology Lifecycle","concept":"Technology Lifecycle Exception","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should continued use of an unsupported technology be governed?","answer":"Continued use of an unsupported technology should be explicitly classified as an architecture risk or exception with documented justification, affected scope, compensating controls where appropriate, ownership, review date or trigger, and a path toward remediation when remediation is required.","context":"Unsupported technology can remain necessary temporarily, but ungoverned exceptions convert known lifecycle risk into invisible architecture debt.","objective":"Make unavoidable lifecycle exceptions visible and controlled.","requirements":["Justification","Affected scope","Risk assessment","Owner","Review trigger","Remediation plan where required"],"constraints":["Replacement may be infeasible in the short term","Compensating controls cannot restore unsupported vendor support"],"prerequisites":["Lifecycle evidence","Risk assessment","Exception governance"],"dependencies":["Security controls","Migration roadmap"],"alternatives":["Immediate replacement","Formal lifecycle exception","Unsupported use without governance"],"evidence":"An exception record provides governance visibility but does not make unsupported technology supported.","tradeoffs":["Formal exceptions preserve operational continuity but carry residual risk","Immediate replacement may introduce migration risk"],"decision":"Govern continued unsupported use through explicit, time- or condition-bounded exception management.","rationale":"Governance should distinguish accepted temporary risk from normal supported architecture.","consequences":["Unsupported technology becomes visible","Exceptions require periodic reassessment"],"implementation_implications":["Link exceptions to affected technology versions","Track review conditions","Connect remediation to roadmap"],"verification_method":"Verify that each active unsupported-technology exception has current ownership, rationale, risk treatment, and review status.","outcome_learning":"Repeated extension of exceptions should trigger architecture or investment reassessment.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B38-R01","D55-B38-R04"],"relationships":["LIFECYCLE_EXCEPTION","TECHNICAL_RISK_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B39-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Third-Party Architecture","concept":"Third-Party Architecture Dependency Governance","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why should third-party architecture dependencies be governed as architectural relationships?","answer":"Third-party dependencies can affect availability, security, data handling, compatibility, technology lifecycle, regulatory obligations, and system behavior. Governance should therefore represent material third-party dependencies explicitly rather than treating them only as procurement records.","context":"External components and services can become critical architectural dependencies.","objective":"Make material third-party architecture dependencies visible and governable.","requirements":["Dependency identity","Service or component scope","Criticality","Contractual or technical constraints","Lifecycle information"],"constraints":["External evidence may be incomplete","Vendor conditions can change independently"],"prerequisites":["Dependency inventory","Third-party assessment"],"dependencies":["Procurement","Security governance","Architecture baseline"],"alternatives":["Procurement-only tracking","Architecture-integrated third-party governance"],"evidence":"External dependencies can materially affect architecture even when internal teams do not control their implementation.","tradeoffs":["Integrated governance improves visibility but requires coordination with procurement and vendor management"],"decision":"Represent material third-party dependencies in architecture governance and connect them to relevant risk and lifecycle records.","rationale":"Architectural dependency exists regardless of whether the dependency is internally owned.","consequences":["Vendor changes can trigger architecture review","External dependency concentration becomes visible"],"implementation_implications":["Map vendors to architecture elements","Record material constraints","Track lifecycle and risk signals"],"verification_method":"Review critical third-party dependencies and verify that architectural impact and ownership are represented.","outcome_learning":"Unexpected vendor impacts should improve third-party dependency mapping and risk criteria.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B39-R02","D55-B39-R03"],"relationships":["THIRD_PARTY_DEPENDENCY","EXTERNAL_ARCHITECTURE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B39-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Third-Party Architecture","concept":"Third-Party Contract-to-Architecture Traceability","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should material third-party contractual constraints be linked to architecture records?","answer":"Material contractual constraints can affect data location, service usage, availability expectations, interoperability, portability, retention, support, or termination conditions. Linking them to architecture records makes those constraints visible during architectural change and review.","context":"Important architectural constraints can be hidden when contractual information is separated completely from architecture governance.","objective":"Preserve traceability between external obligations and architectural design constraints.","requirements":["Constraint identification","Affected architecture elements","Contract source","Validity or review status"],"constraints":["Contract interpretation may require legal expertise","Not every contractual clause is architectural"],"prerequisites":["Third-party dependency inventory","Contract classification"],"dependencies":["Legal or procurement governance","Architecture decisions"],"alternatives":["Contract repository only","Architecture-linked material constraint records"],"evidence":"Only material architecture-relevant contractual constraints need to enter architecture governance.","tradeoffs":["Linking constraints improves traceability but requires cross-functional coordination"],"decision":"Link material architecture-relevant third-party constraints to affected architecture elements while retaining contractual authority in the appropriate source system.","rationale":"Architecture governance should know which external obligations constrain architectural choices without becoming the legal system of record.","consequences":["Architectural reviews expose relevant external constraints","Contract changes can trigger architecture reassessment"],"implementation_implications":["Classify architecture-relevant clauses","Reference authoritative contract records","Define review triggers"],"verification_method":"Sample material third-party constraints and verify that affected architecture elements can be identified and the authoritative source located.","outcome_learning":"Missed contractual constraints should improve cross-functional architecture review and dependency classification.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B39-R01","D55-B39-R04"],"relationships":["CONTRACT_TRACEABILITY","ARCHITECTURE_CONSTRAINT"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B39-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Third-Party Architecture","concept":"Vendor Change Impact","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should a vendor change trigger architecture governance?","answer":"A vendor change should trigger architecture governance when it materially affects supported behavior, interfaces, data handling, availability, security, technology lifecycle, compatibility, cost assumptions, or exit and portability conditions.","context":"External changes can alter architecture without an internal code change.","objective":"Detect architectural impact from material third-party changes.","requirements":["Vendor change signal","Affected dependency mapping","Impact assessment","Reassessment path"],"constraints":["Vendor notifications may be incomplete or late","Actual impact depends on how the service is used"],"prerequisites":["Third-party dependency inventory","Vendor monitoring"],"dependencies":["Architecture baseline","Technology lifecycle","Risk governance"],"alternatives":["Manual vendor review","Continuous vendor monitoring","Risk-based monitoring"],"evidence":"Vendor changes can modify the assumptions on which architecture depends.","tradeoffs":["Monitoring improves responsiveness but may require external information sources","Manual review can miss time-sensitive changes"],"decision":"Use risk-based vendor-change triggers connected to material architecture dependencies.","rationale":"Governance must respond to external architectural changes when their consequences are material.","consequences":["Vendor changes become architecture signals","Dependency mapping quality becomes critical"],"implementation_implications":["Define monitored vendor events","Map vendors to architecture elements","Create reassessment workflow"],"verification_method":"Evaluate representative vendor changes and verify that material architectural effects trigger review.","outcome_learning":"Missed external changes should improve monitoring scope and dependency mapping.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B39-R01","D55-B39-R02"],"relationships":["VENDOR_CHANGE","EXTERNAL_CHANGE_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B39-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Third-Party Architecture","concept":"Third-Party Exit Architecture","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why should exit considerations be part of third-party architecture governance?","answer":"Exit considerations address how the architecture can migrate, replace, or discontinue a material external dependency, including data portability, interface replacement, operational continuity, contractual constraints, and dependency removal.","context":"An external dependency may be technically convenient but architecturally difficult to replace.","objective":"Make dependency reversibility and exit constraints visible for material third-party services.","requirements":["Exit scenario","Affected data and interfaces","Migration constraints","Continuity considerations","Residual dependencies"],"constraints":["Full portability may not be technically or contractually available","Exit planning may be proportionate to dependency criticality"],"prerequisites":["Third-party dependency inventory","Architecture dependency analysis"],"dependencies":["Contract governance","Data architecture","Transition architecture"],"alternatives":["No exit analysis","Formal exit architecture","Risk-based exit planning"],"evidence":"Exit feasibility is an architectural property when external dependency concentration can materially constrain future choices.","tradeoffs":["Exit planning consumes effort","Ignoring exit constraints can increase future migration cost"],"decision":"Perform proportionate exit architecture analysis for material third-party dependencies.","rationale":"Governance should expose architectural lock-in and migration constraints before they become urgent.","consequences":["Exit risks become visible","Migration assumptions can be tested or documented"],"implementation_implications":["Record exit constraints","Identify replacement interfaces","Maintain relevant portability requirements"],"verification_method":"Review material third-party dependencies and verify that exit feasibility or accepted uncertainty is documented.","outcome_learning":"Difficult exits should update future dependency selection criteria and architecture decisions.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B39-R01","D55-B39-R03"],"relationships":["EXIT_ARCHITECTURE","DEPENDENCY_REVERSIBILITY"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B39-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B39","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Third-Party Architecture","concept":"Third-Party Dependency Concentration","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why should concentration of critical third-party dependencies be visible to architecture governance?","answer":"Concentration occurs when multiple critical architecture capabilities depend on the same external provider, technology, or service boundary. Such concentration can create correlated failure, lifecycle, availability, commercial, or migration risk.","context":"Individual dependencies may appear acceptable while their combined concentration creates systemic exposure.","objective":"Identify architectural risk created by correlated external dependencies.","requirements":["Dependency mapping","Criticality classification","Concentration analysis","Risk ownership"],"constraints":["Concentration can be intentional","Alternative providers may not exist or may create other risks"],"prerequisites":["Third-party dependency inventory","Criticality assessment"],"dependencies":["Risk governance","Architecture roadmap"],"alternatives":["Independent dependency review","Concentration-aware architecture analysis"],"evidence":"Systemic dependency risk can arise from relationships among individually acceptable dependencies.","tradeoffs":["Diversification may increase complexity or cost","Concentration may provide operational simplicity"],"decision":"Assess concentration of material third-party dependencies where correlated impact could be significant.","rationale":"Architecture governance should evaluate dependency structure rather than isolated components only.","consequences":["Systemic external dependency risk becomes visible","Diversification or mitigation can be considered explicitly"],"implementation_implications":["Group dependencies by provider or shared failure domain","Assess correlated impact","Record accepted concentration rationale"],"verification_method":"Analyze critical third-party dependencies for shared providers or common failure domains and verify material concentrations are governed.","outcome_learning":"Observed concentration-related failures should refine dependency risk models and architecture selection criteria.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B39-R01","D55-B39-R04"],"relationships":["DEPENDENCY_CONCENTRATION","SYSTEMIC_ARCHITECTURE_RISK"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B40-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Emergency Architecture Change","concept":"Emergency Architecture Change Governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should material emergency architecture changes be governed?","answer":"Emergency architecture changes should use an expedited governance path that preserves necessary authorization, risk awareness, evidence capture, implementation traceability, and post-change review while allowing response speed appropriate to the emergency.","context":"Operational emergencies may require architectural changes before normal review cycles can complete.","objective":"Balance urgent response with architectural accountability.","requirements":["Emergency classification","Authorized decision path","Minimal risk assessment","Change traceability","Post-change review"],"constraints":["Information may be incomplete","Normal review timelines may be unsafe during an emergency"],"prerequisites":["Emergency change policy","Architecture ownership"],"dependencies":["Incident management","Change management","Security governance"],"alternatives":["Normal architecture review","Expedited architecture review","Uncontrolled emergency change"],"evidence":"Emergency conditions justify reduced review latency, not elimination of accountability and retrospective verification.","tradeoffs":["Faster action reduces delay but increases uncertainty","Heavy approval during emergencies can obstruct response"],"decision":"Use predefined expedited architecture governance for genuine emergencies with mandatory retrospective review.","rationale":"Architecture governance must remain effective under time pressure without imposing normal-cycle latency.","consequences":["Emergency architecture changes remain auditable","Post-event workload is required"],"implementation_implications":["Define emergency criteria","Specify authorized roles","Capture minimum decision evidence","Schedule retrospective review"],"verification_method":"Review emergency changes and verify that authorization, evidence, implementation traceability, and retrospective assessment exist.","outcome_learning":"Emergency patterns should inform resilience improvements and changes to normal architecture governance where recurring emergencies expose structural weaknesses.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B40-R02","D55-B40-R03"],"relationships":["EMERGENCY_CHANGE","EXPEDITED_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B40-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Emergency Architecture Change","concept":"Emergency Change Authority","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should emergency architecture change authority define?","answer":"Emergency change authority should define who may authorize urgent architectural changes, under which conditions, what minimum information is required, what limits apply, and who is responsible for subsequent review and normalization of the architecture record.","context":"Undefined emergency authority can create either dangerous delay or uncontrolled architectural decisions.","objective":"Make urgent architecture decision authority explicit before emergencies occur.","requirements":["Authorized roles","Trigger conditions","Scope limits","Minimum evidence","Post-event accountability"],"constraints":["Authority must remain bounded","Emergency roles may vary by system criticality"],"prerequisites":["Governance model","Emergency procedures"],"dependencies":["Incident management","Risk ownership"],"alternatives":["Single emergency authority","Tiered emergency authority","Normal authority with expedited process"],"evidence":"Predefined authority reduces ambiguity when normal governance participants are unavailable.","tradeoffs":["Broader emergency authority improves speed but increases risk of inappropriate decisions","Narrow authority improves control but may delay response"],"decision":"Define bounded, risk-appropriate emergency architecture authority before emergencies occur.","rationale":"Governance should not invent decision rights during an incident.","consequences":["Emergency decisions have clearer accountability","Post-event review can evaluate whether authority was used appropriately"],"implementation_implications":["Document authority matrix","Define escalation when authorized roles are unavailable","Record emergency decisions"],"verification_method":"Conduct a governance exercise or document review to verify that emergency decision authority is unambiguous.","outcome_learning":"Authority gaps identified during exercises should be resolved before actual emergencies.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B40-R01","D55-B40-R04"],"relationships":["EMERGENCY_AUTHORITY","GOVERNANCE_ACCOUNTABILITY"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B40-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Emergency Architecture Change","concept":"Emergency Architecture Reconciliation","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should emergency changes be reconciled with the architecture baseline afterward?","answer":"Emergency changes can create temporary or permanent divergence from the governed architecture. Reconciliation determines the resulting architectural state, updates authoritative records, identifies residual risks, and determines whether the emergency change should be retained, modified, or reversed.","context":"Without reconciliation, emergency changes can become undocumented permanent architecture.","objective":"Restore architectural truth after urgent changes.","requirements":["Post-change architecture assessment","Baseline update","Residual risk review","Decision or retirement outcome"],"constraints":["Some evidence may require stabilization time","Immediate reconciliation may not be possible during an ongoing incident"],"prerequisites":["Emergency change record","Current-state baseline"],"dependencies":["Incident closure","Architecture review"],"alternatives":["Immediate baseline mutation","Deferred reconciliation","Formal post-event architecture review"],"evidence":"Emergency implementation does not automatically establish the intended long-term architecture.","tradeoffs":["Rapid reconciliation improves accuracy","Premature documentation may record unstable temporary state"],"decision":"Reconcile emergency architectural changes after sufficient stabilization and within a defined governance timeframe.","rationale":"The architecture baseline should represent the resulting governed state, not merely the emergency action itself.","consequences":["Temporary emergency conditions can be distinguished from permanent changes","Architecture history remains traceable"],"implementation_implications":["Link emergency changes to baseline updates","Record resulting state","Create follow-up actions for unresolved risk"],"verification_method":"Review closed emergency changes and verify that resulting architecture status and residual risks are recorded.","outcome_learning":"Repeated emergency changes that become permanent should inform architecture resilience and normal governance improvements.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B40-R01","D55-B40-R05"],"relationships":["ARCHITECTURE_RECONCILIATION","BASELINE_UPDATE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B40-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Emergency Architecture Change","concept":"Emergency Change Retrospective","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should an architecture governance retrospective examine after an emergency change?","answer":"The retrospective should examine why the emergency was required, whether the architectural design or controls contributed, whether the expedited governance path was used correctly, what risks remain, and whether architectural or governance changes could reduce recurrence.","context":"An emergency change can provide evidence about weaknesses in architecture, resilience, operational controls, or governance.","objective":"Convert emergency architecture events into evidence for continuous improvement.","requirements":["Event context","Architecture contribution analysis","Governance-path assessment","Residual risk","Improvement actions"],"constraints":["Retrospectives should distinguish evidence from speculation","Not every emergency indicates an architecture defect"],"prerequisites":["Emergency change record","Relevant operational evidence"],"dependencies":["Incident review","Architecture governance"],"alternatives":["No retrospective","Incident-only retrospective","Architecture-specific retrospective"],"evidence":"Retrospective analysis is useful when grounded in observed facts and explicitly separated from hypotheses.","tradeoffs":["Retrospectives consume time","Skipping them loses learning opportunities"],"decision":"Conduct evidence-based architecture retrospectives for material emergency changes.","rationale":"Repeated emergencies can reveal architectural or governance weaknesses that normal review does not expose.","consequences":["Architecture learning becomes traceable","Governance rules can be improved from evidence"],"implementation_implications":["Record observed facts separately from hypotheses","Link findings to architecture elements","Track improvement actions"],"verification_method":"Verify that retrospective findings distinguish evidence, inference, and unresolved questions and that material actions have owners.","outcome_learning":"Validated recurring causes should feed architecture standards, resilience planning, and governance controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B40-R01","D55-B40-R03"],"relationships":["ARCHITECTURE_LEARNING","EMERGENCY_RETROSPECTIVE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B40-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B40","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Emergency Architecture Change","concept":"Emergency Governance Normalization","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"When should an emergency architecture change return to normal governance?","answer":"An emergency change should return to normal governance once the immediate emergency condition has ended and the architecture can be evaluated using ordinary decision, conformance, risk, lifecycle, and roadmap controls. Any temporary emergency authority should then terminate or explicitly transition to normal authority.","context":"Emergency governance is intended to reduce response latency, not become a permanent alternative governance model.","objective":"Prevent emergency mechanisms from creating uncontrolled permanent architecture.","requirements":["Emergency exit condition","Normal governance reassessment","Authority transition","Record reconciliation"],"constraints":["The emergency may recur","Some emergency changes may require long-term remediation"],"prerequisites":["Emergency change record","Post-event assessment"],"dependencies":["Architecture baseline","Normal governance process"],"alternatives":["Automatic return","Explicit normalization review","Permanent emergency exception"],"evidence":"Governance normalization restores ordinary accountability once emergency conditions no longer justify expedited treatment.","tradeoffs":["Explicit normalization improves control but adds post-event work","Automatic return is simpler but can miss unresolved architectural risk"],"decision":"Require explicit normalization or documented transition to an appropriate long-term governance state after material emergency changes.","rationale":"Temporary governance authority should not silently become permanent architectural authority.","consequences":["Emergency status has a defined lifecycle","Long-term exceptions become visible"],"implementation_implications":["Define emergency closure criteria","Transition records to normal governance","Review unresolved risks and exceptions"],"verification_method":"Review closed emergency changes and verify that each has either returned to normal governance or has a documented long-term governance state.","outcome_learning":"Repeated prolonged emergency states should trigger review of resilience, governance, and architecture assumptions.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B40-R01","D55-B40-R03","D55-B40-R04"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_NORMALIZATION","EMERGENCY_LIFECYCLE"]}

{"record_id":"D55-B41-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Change Control","concept":"Controlled Architecture Change","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture changes be controlled without making legitimate evolution unnecessarily slow?","answer":"Architecture changes should pass through a proportional control process based on architectural impact, risk, affected boundaries, reversibility, and operational consequences. Low-impact reversible changes can use lightweight review, while changes affecting shared contracts, security boundaries, persistence models, reliability characteristics, or major dependencies require stronger evidence and approval.","context":"An architecture evolves continuously as requirements, technologies, constraints, and operational conditions change.","objective":"Ensure architecture evolution remains intentional, traceable, and proportionate to risk.","requirements":["Every material architecture change must have an identifiable change description.","The affected architectural boundaries and dependencies must be known.","Review depth must reflect architectural risk.","The resulting decision must remain traceable."],"constraints":["Excessive governance can delay delivery.","Insufficient governance can create undocumented architectural drift.","Different changes have different levels of reversibility and impact."],"prerequisites":["Architecture baseline exists.","Architecture ownership is defined.","Change classification criteria are available."],"dependencies":["Architecture Baselines","Architecture Review Process","Architecture Risk Governance"],"alternatives":["Unrestricted architectural changes","Uniform mandatory review for every change","Risk-proportional architecture change control"],"evidence":"Risk-proportional control reduces unnecessary governance overhead while preserving stronger controls for changes with greater architectural consequences.","tradeoffs":{"speed_vs_control":"Higher control increases review effort but improves traceability and risk management.","consistency_vs_flexibility":"Standard change criteria improve consistency while allowing proportional treatment."},"decision":"Use risk-proportional architecture change control with explicit impact classification.","rationale":"Architectural changes differ substantially in risk, reversibility, and blast radius, so identical approval requirements are inefficient.","consequences":["Material changes become traceable.","High-impact changes receive stronger scrutiny.","Low-risk changes can progress with reduced administrative overhead."],"implementation_implications":["Define change-impact categories.","Record affected architecture elements.","Require evidence proportional to impact.","Link approved changes to updated architecture artifacts."],"verification_method":"Review sampled architecture changes and verify that classification, evidence, approval, implementation, and resulting architecture state are traceable.","outcome_learning":"Measure review lead time, escaped architecture defects, exception frequency, and post-change rework to refine control thresholds.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B41-R02","D55-B41-R03"],"relationships":["ARCHITECTURE_CHANGE_CONTROL","RISK_PROPORTIONAL_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B41-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Change Control","concept":"Architecture Change Classification","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What factors should determine the governance classification of an architecture change?","answer":"Change classification should consider affected architectural boundaries, security and privacy impact, data-model impact, public or shared contracts, availability and reliability implications, dependency blast radius, reversibility, regulatory significance, and migration complexity.","context":"A governance process needs a repeatable method for distinguishing low-risk changes from changes requiring deeper architectural review.","objective":"Create consistent risk-based classification of architecture changes.","requirements":["Classification criteria must be explicit.","Criteria must address technical and organizational impact.","Classification must be reproducible by different reviewers."],"constraints":["Impact may be uncertain before implementation.","Some changes affect multiple criteria simultaneously.","Classification should not depend solely on implementation size."],"prerequisites":["Architecture change record","Known architecture boundaries","Risk classification model"],"dependencies":["Architecture Risk Governance","Architecture Baselines"],"alternatives":["Classify by code size","Classify by team ownership","Classify by architectural impact"],"evidence":"Architectural risk is better represented by affected boundaries and consequences than by source-code size or ticket complexity.","tradeoffs":{"simplicity_vs_accuracy":"Fewer criteria simplify governance but can miss important architectural risks.","early_classification_vs_information":"Early classification enables planning but may require later reassessment when new information appears."},"decision":"Classify architecture changes using explicit impact and risk dimensions rather than implementation size alone.","rationale":"A small implementation can have major architectural consequences, while a large implementation can remain architecturally isolated.","consequences":["Review decisions become more consistent.","Changes can be escalated when new impact is discovered.","Governance becomes less dependent on subjective estimates of coding effort."],"implementation_implications":["Define classification dimensions.","Record evidence supporting classification.","Allow reclassification during implementation.","Link classification to required review depth."],"verification_method":"Compare independent classifications of representative changes and assess whether the same architectural risk factors produce consistent classifications.","outcome_learning":"Track reclassification rates and post-implementation discoveries to identify missing classification criteria.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B41-R01","D55-B41-R04"],"relationships":["CHANGE_CLASSIFICATION","ARCHITECTURE_RISK"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B41-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Change Control","concept":"Architecture Change Traceability","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What information should make an architecture change traceable from proposal through implementation?","answer":"A traceable architecture change should identify the originating requirement or problem, affected architecture elements, alternatives considered, decision authority, evidence, approval status, implementation reference, verification results, and resulting architecture state.","context":"Architecture governance loses value when decisions cannot be connected to the changes that implemented them.","objective":"Maintain an auditable chain from architectural intent to resulting system state.","requirements":["The change must have a stable identifier.","The decision and implementation must be linkable.","Verification evidence must be recorded.","The resulting architecture state must be identifiable."],"constraints":["Traceability metadata creates maintenance overhead.","Implementation references can change over time.","Some evidence may be generated outside the architecture repository."],"prerequisites":["Architecture change record","Decision repository","Implementation tracking mechanism"],"dependencies":["Architecture Decision Records","Architecture Baselines","Governance Evidence Repository"],"alternatives":["Document-only traceability","Ticket-only traceability","Linked decision-to-implementation traceability"],"evidence":"Linked traceability provides stronger governance evidence than isolated documents because it preserves relationships between intent, decision, implementation, and verification.","tradeoffs":{"traceability_vs_overhead":"More links improve auditability but increase metadata maintenance.","historical_accuracy_vs_current_state":"Historical decisions must remain preserved even when the current architecture changes."},"decision":"Maintain explicit links between architecture change requests, decisions, implementations, verification evidence, and resulting baselines.","rationale":"Architectural intent cannot be reliably evaluated without knowing how and where it was implemented.","consequences":["Architecture history becomes reconstructable.","Audits can identify incomplete changes.","Future decisions can use historical evidence."],"implementation_implications":["Use stable change identifiers.","Maintain bidirectional references where practical.","Preserve historical records rather than overwriting them."],"verification_method":"Select completed architecture changes and verify that each can be traced across proposal, decision, implementation, verification, and resulting state.","outcome_learning":"Measure broken links, missing evidence, and reconstruction effort during reviews.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B41-R01","D55-B41-R05"],"relationships":["TRACEABILITY","ARCHITECTURE_HISTORY"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B41-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Change Control","concept":"Change Review Escalation","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should an architecture change be escalated to a higher governance level?","answer":"Escalation is appropriate when a change crosses ownership boundaries, materially changes security or trust boundaries, affects shared contracts, introduces significant operational risk, creates irreversible migration consequences, conflicts with established architecture constraints, or exposes unresolved architectural uncertainty.","context":"Not every architecture review can be completed at the same governance level.","objective":"Ensure changes with broader consequences receive appropriate authority and expertise.","requirements":["Escalation triggers must be explicit.","Escalated decisions must preserve prior evidence.","The receiving authority must have defined responsibility."],"constraints":["Too many escalation triggers can create bottlenecks.","Escalation should not replace technical analysis.","Urgent changes may require temporary expedited paths."],"prerequisites":["Defined governance hierarchy","Change classification","Architecture ownership"],"dependencies":["Decision Authority","Architecture Risk Governance","Emergency Change Governance"],"alternatives":["No escalation","Automatic escalation of all changes","Rule-based escalation"],"evidence":"Explicit escalation criteria reduce ambiguity when changes exceed the authority or risk tolerance of the original review level.","tradeoffs":{"speed_vs_authority":"Escalation increases decision time but improves oversight for high-impact changes.","centralization_vs_locality":"Higher-level review provides broader perspective but can reduce local decision speed."},"decision":"Use explicit risk, scope, ownership, and architectural-boundary triggers for escalation.","rationale":"Governance authority should expand when architectural consequences exceed the original decision context.","consequences":["High-impact changes receive broader review.","Ownership conflicts become visible earlier.","Routine changes remain locally governed."],"implementation_implications":["Define escalation thresholds.","Record escalation reasons.","Preserve the complete decision history across governance levels."],"verification_method":"Review escalated and non-escalated changes for consistency against documented escalation rules.","outcome_learning":"Monitor inappropriate escalations, missed escalations, and escalation turnaround time.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B41-R02","D55-B41-R05"],"relationships":["ESCALATION","DECISION_AUTHORITY"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B41-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B41","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Change Control","concept":"Architecture Change Closure","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What conditions should close an architecture change from a governance perspective?","answer":"An architecture change should be considered closed only when the approved implementation is complete or formally abandoned, required verification has been performed, architecture artifacts have been updated, outstanding exceptions are recorded, and the resulting state is traceable to the approved decision.","context":"Approval of an architecture change does not itself establish that the intended architecture was achieved.","objective":"Prevent governance records from declaring architectural changes complete before the resulting state is verified.","requirements":["Implementation status must be known.","Verification status must be explicit.","Architecture documentation must reflect the resulting state.","Unresolved deviations must remain visible."],"constraints":["Some changes are intentionally incremental.","Verification may occur after deployment.","Emergency changes may initially lack complete documentation."],"prerequisites":["Approved architecture change","Implementation tracking","Verification evidence"],"dependencies":["Architecture Baselines","Change Traceability","Governance Evidence"],"alternatives":["Close at approval","Close at implementation","Close after implementation and verification"],"evidence":"Separating approval from closure prevents governance systems from confusing authorization with successful realization of architectural intent.","tradeoffs":{"closure_speed_vs_assurance":"Later closure provides stronger evidence but extends lifecycle management.","documentation_effort_vs_accuracy":"Updated architecture artifacts improve accuracy but require maintenance."},"decision":"Close architecture changes only after implementation state, verification, and resulting architecture documentation are reconciled.","rationale":"A governance decision describes intended change; closure should establish whether the intended change actually became the governed architecture state.","consequences":["Incomplete changes remain visible.","Architecture repositories better represent actual state.","Post-change verification becomes part of governance rather than an optional activity."],"implementation_implications":["Define closure criteria.","Separate approval, implementation, and verification statuses.","Require explicit treatment of deviations."],"verification_method":"Audit closed changes and verify that closure criteria are satisfied independently of approval status.","outcome_learning":"Track reopened changes and closure defects to improve lifecycle criteria.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B41-R03","D55-B41-R04"],"relationships":["CHANGE_CLOSURE","ARCHITECTURE_VERIFICATION"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B42-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Review Quality","concept":"Evidence-Based Architecture Review","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What makes an architecture review evidence-based rather than opinion-based?","answer":"An evidence-based architecture review explicitly connects requirements, constraints, alternatives, assumptions, measurable risks, relevant technical evidence, and verification results to the decision under review.","context":"Architecture reviews can become subjective when participants rely primarily on authority, familiarity, or preference.","objective":"Improve the reproducibility and technical defensibility of architecture reviews.","requirements":["Review claims should have identifiable evidence.","Requirements and constraints must be explicit.","Assumptions must be distinguishable from verified facts.","Material uncertainty must be recorded."],"constraints":["Some architectural questions cannot be fully resolved before implementation.","Evidence quality varies.","Review participants may have conflicting interpretations."],"prerequisites":["Review scope","Architecture proposal","Evidence sources"],"dependencies":["Architecture Decision Records","Decision Evidence Quality","Architecture Experiments"],"alternatives":["Opinion-led review","Checklist-only review","Evidence-based review"],"evidence":"Explicit evidence linkage allows reviewers to distinguish documented facts, assumptions, measurements, experiments, and engineering judgments.","tradeoffs":{"review_depth_vs_speed":"More evidence increases review effort but improves decision defensibility.","certainty_vs_uncertainty":"Explicit uncertainty can complicate decisions but prevents unsupported certainty."},"decision":"Require evidence classification and traceability for material architecture review claims.","rationale":"Architecture governance should evaluate reasoning and evidence rather than merely approving a proposed design.","consequences":["Unsupported claims become easier to identify.","Review discussions become more reproducible.","Evidence gaps become actionable work items."],"implementation_implications":["Classify evidence types.","Link claims to evidence.","Record unresolved assumptions.","Use experiments when uncertainty materially affects the decision."],"verification_method":"Inspect review records for claim-to-evidence links and explicit treatment of assumptions and uncertainty.","outcome_learning":"Track recurring unsupported claims and evidence gaps across reviews.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B42-R02","D55-B42-R03"],"relationships":["EVIDENCE_BASED_REVIEW","ARCHITECTURE_REVIEW"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B42-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Review Quality","concept":"Architecture Review Assumption Management","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should assumptions be handled during architecture governance?","answer":"Material assumptions should be explicitly recorded, linked to the decisions that depend on them, assigned an owner where appropriate, and periodically validated or retired as evidence becomes available.","context":"Architecture decisions often depend on incomplete information about workload, dependencies, technology behavior, organizational constraints, or future requirements.","objective":"Prevent hidden assumptions from becoming unrecognized architecture risks.","requirements":["Material assumptions must be identifiable.","Decision dependencies on assumptions must be traceable.","Validation criteria should exist for assumptions that can be tested."],"constraints":["Some assumptions cannot be fully eliminated.","Assumption validation may require experiments or operational evidence.","Over-documenting trivial assumptions reduces signal quality."],"prerequisites":["Architecture decision","Evidence model","Review process"],"dependencies":["Architecture Risk Governance","Decision Evidence Quality"],"alternatives":["Implicit assumptions","Document all assumptions equally","Govern and validate material assumptions"],"evidence":"Explicit assumptions improve reasoning transparency and make uncertainty actionable.","tradeoffs":{"documentation_vs_noise":"Selective documentation preserves signal while avoiding unnecessary metadata.","early_decision_vs_certainty":"Decisions may proceed under assumptions when their risk is understood and controlled."},"decision":"Govern assumptions according to materiality and decision dependency.","rationale":"Only assumptions capable of materially changing an architectural decision require governance attention.","consequences":["Critical assumptions become visible.","Validation work can be prioritized.","Architecture reviews can distinguish uncertainty from established facts."],"implementation_implications":["Add assumption records or fields.","Link material assumptions to affected decisions.","Define validation or retirement conditions."],"verification_method":"Sample architecture decisions and verify that material assumptions are explicit and that important assumptions have validation status.","outcome_learning":"Measure assumptions invalidated after implementation and identify recurring sources of inaccurate assumptions.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B42-R01","D55-B42-R04"],"relationships":["ASSUMPTION_MANAGEMENT","DECISION_UNCERTAINTY"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B42-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Review Quality","concept":"Architecture Review Independence","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"When is independent architecture review useful?","answer":"Independent review is useful when a decision has significant cross-team impact, high irreversibility, material security or reliability consequences, substantial uncertainty, or strong conflicts of interest among participants.","context":"The people proposing an architecture can possess detailed context but may also have incentives or assumptions that limit critical challenge.","objective":"Introduce appropriate independent scrutiny without making every architecture decision bureaucratic.","requirements":["Independence should be proportional to risk.","Reviewers must have sufficient technical context.","Independent review must not replace accountable decision ownership."],"constraints":["External reviewers may lack local context.","Additional review increases lead time.","Independence does not guarantee correctness."],"prerequisites":["Risk classification","Defined architecture ownership","Review criteria"],"dependencies":["Architecture Risk Governance","Decision Authority"],"alternatives":["No independent review","Independent review for every decision","Risk-triggered independent review"],"evidence":"Independent review can expose assumptions and boundary effects that participants closest to a proposal may overlook, particularly for high-impact decisions.","tradeoffs":{"independence_vs_context":"Independent reviewers provide challenge but may require additional context acquisition.","speed_vs_scrutiny":"Additional scrutiny increases review time for selected decisions."},"decision":"Use risk-triggered independent architecture review for material decisions rather than universally.","rationale":"The value of independent review is highest where consequence, uncertainty, or conflict warrants additional challenge.","consequences":["High-risk decisions receive stronger scrutiny.","Routine decisions avoid unnecessary review overhead.","Decision accountability remains explicit."],"implementation_implications":["Define independence triggers.","Document reviewer role and scope.","Separate review findings from final decision authority."],"verification_method":"Evaluate whether decisions meeting defined independence criteria received appropriate review and whether findings were addressed or explicitly rejected.","outcome_learning":"Track material issues discovered through independent review and missed issues in non-reviewed decisions.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B42-R01","D55-B42-R05"],"relationships":["INDEPENDENT_REVIEW","GOVERNANCE_ASSURANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B42-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Review Quality","concept":"Architecture Review Decision Criteria","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should architecture reviewers evaluate consistently?","answer":"Review criteria should cover requirement satisfaction, architectural constraints, alternatives, security and privacy boundaries, reliability and performance implications, scalability, operability, maintainability, dependencies, migration impact, risks, evidence quality, and verification strategy where applicable.","context":"Inconsistent review criteria cause important concerns to be evaluated unevenly across architecture proposals.","objective":"Provide a reusable evaluation structure while allowing context-specific criteria.","requirements":["Core criteria should be defined.","Context-specific criteria must remain possible.","Criteria should map to architectural risks and requirements."],"constraints":["Not every criterion applies to every architecture.","A long checklist can encourage superficial compliance.","Criteria should not substitute for technical reasoning."],"prerequisites":["Architecture review scope","Requirement set","Risk model"],"dependencies":["Architecture Standards","Architecture Risk Governance"],"alternatives":["Unstructured review","Fixed exhaustive checklist","Core criteria plus context-specific evaluation"],"evidence":"A consistent core evaluation model improves review completeness while context-specific analysis preserves architectural relevance.","tradeoffs":{"consistency_vs_flexibility":"Core criteria improve comparability while optional criteria accommodate different systems.","completeness_vs_attention":"Too many criteria can dilute attention from material risks."},"decision":"Use a core architecture review framework augmented by risk- and context-specific criteria.","rationale":"Governance requires consistency without reducing architecture review to mechanical checklist completion.","consequences":["Reviews become more comparable.","Material context-specific risks can still be examined.","Reviewers need judgment about criterion applicability."],"implementation_implications":["Define mandatory core dimensions.","Require applicability decisions for major dimensions.","Record material omissions with rationale."],"verification_method":"Audit reviews for coverage of applicable core and context-specific criteria.","outcome_learning":"Analyze recurring review findings to refine the core criteria set.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B42-R01","D55-B42-R05"],"relationships":["REVIEW_CRITERIA","ARCHITECTURE_ASSURANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B42-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B42","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Review Quality","concept":"Architecture Review Finding Disposition","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture review findings be handled after a review?","answer":"Each material finding should receive an explicit disposition such as accepted remediation, design change, accepted risk, additional evidence required, deferred action with owner and trigger, or rejected finding with rationale.","context":"Review findings have little governance value if they are recorded without a clear outcome.","objective":"Ensure review findings produce traceable decisions and accountable follow-up.","requirements":["Material findings require explicit disposition.","Unresolved findings require ownership or documented acceptance.","Disposition rationale must remain traceable."],"constraints":["Not every finding requires immediate remediation.","Some findings may reflect disagreement rather than defects.","Deferred findings require future reassessment conditions."],"prerequisites":["Completed architecture review","Finding classification","Decision authority"],"dependencies":["Governance Exceptions","Architecture Risk Governance","Decision Traceability"],"alternatives":["Record findings only","Mandatory remediation of every finding","Explicit finding disposition"],"evidence":"Explicit disposition distinguishes unresolved risk from completed action and prevents review records from falsely implying closure.","tradeoffs":{"closure_vs_risk_acceptance":"Accepting risk can close an action while preserving residual risk explicitly.","remediation_vs_delivery":"Immediate remediation may delay delivery; deferred action requires stronger governance visibility."},"decision":"Require explicit disposition for every material architecture review finding.","rationale":"A review is incomplete when material findings lack an accountable outcome.","consequences":["Unresolved risks remain visible.","Accepted risks become distinguishable from missed findings.","Review closure becomes auditable."],"implementation_implications":["Define finding statuses.","Assign owners for open actions.","Record risk acceptance separately from remediation.","Require reassessment triggers for deferred findings."],"verification_method":"Sample completed reviews and verify that every material finding has a valid disposition and supporting evidence.","outcome_learning":"Track recurring findings, deferred findings, and findings reopened after implementation.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B42-R03","D55-B42-R04"],"relationships":["REVIEW_FINDING","FINDING_DISPOSITION"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B43-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Compliance","concept":"Architecture Conformance Evidence","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"What constitutes useful evidence that an implemented system conforms to its governed architecture?","answer":"Useful conformance evidence links architectural requirements or constraints to observable implementation characteristics, configuration, interfaces, deployment topology, runtime behavior, or automated checks that can demonstrate whether the governed architecture is realized.","context":"Architecture documents describe intended structure, but governance requires evidence about actual implementation state.","objective":"Establish an evidence model for architecture conformance.","requirements":["Conformance claims must identify the architectural rule or requirement.","Evidence must correspond to observable implementation state.","Evidence should be reproducible where practical."],"constraints":["Some architecture properties are difficult to observe directly.","Runtime and deployment state can change independently of source code.","Documentation alone may not prove implementation conformance."],"prerequisites":["Architecture standards","Architecture baseline","Conformance criteria"],"dependencies":["Architecture Baselines","Governance Automation","Architecture Verification"],"alternatives":["Documentation-only conformance","Manual inspection only","Evidence-backed conformance"],"evidence":"Conformance is stronger when architectural claims are mapped to observable implementation or operational evidence.","tradeoffs":{"automation_vs_coverage":"Automated checks provide repeatability but cannot capture every architectural property.","runtime_vs_static_evidence":"Runtime evidence can validate deployed behavior while static evidence can cover structural properties."},"decision":"Use multiple evidence types mapped explicitly to architecture requirements and constraints.","rationale":"No single evidence source reliably establishes all forms of architecture conformance.","consequences":["Conformance claims become more defensible.","Evidence collection can be automated where appropriate.","Gaps between intended and actual architecture become visible."],"implementation_implications":["Define conformance assertions.","Map assertions to evidence sources.","Record evidence timestamps or versions when state can change."],"verification_method":"For sampled architecture constraints, verify that each has identifiable evidence capable of supporting the conformance claim.","outcome_learning":"Track recurring conformance evidence gaps to improve architecture standards and tooling.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B43-R02","D55-B43-R03"],"relationships":["CONFORMANCE","ARCHITECTURE_EVIDENCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B43-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Compliance","concept":"Architecture Conformance Drift","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance detect drift between approved architecture and implemented architecture?","answer":"Architecture drift should be detected by comparing governed architectural constraints and baselines with current implementation, configuration, deployment, dependency, and interface evidence. Detection should distinguish intentional approved changes from unauthorized or undocumented divergence.","context":"Systems can gradually diverge from their approved architecture through local optimizations, emergency changes, dependency changes, or incomplete documentation.","objective":"Detect and classify divergence between intended and actual architecture.","requirements":["The governed baseline must be identifiable.","Current-state evidence must be available.","Known approved changes must be distinguishable from unexplained differences."],"constraints":["Current state may change frequently.","Some architectural properties cannot be automatically measured.","False positives can create governance fatigue."],"prerequisites":["Architecture baseline","Current-state evidence","Approved change records"],"dependencies":["Architecture Baselines","Architecture Change Control","Governance Automation"],"alternatives":["Periodic manual comparison","Continuous automated drift detection","Hybrid automated and manual detection"],"evidence":"Comparing governed constraints with current-state evidence enables explicit identification of architectural divergence.","tradeoffs":{"continuous_detection_vs_noise":"Frequent detection can identify drift earlier but may produce more transient differences.","automation_vs_semantics":"Automated comparison scales better but may not understand architectural intent."},"decision":"Use a hybrid drift-detection model combining automated structural checks with human review of semantic differences.","rationale":"Architectural drift has both machine-detectable and context-dependent dimensions.","consequences":["Unauthorized divergence becomes more visible.","Governance teams can prioritize material drift.","Not every detected difference is automatically a violation."],"implementation_implications":["Define machine-checkable constraints.","Compare current state against versioned baselines.","Classify detected differences.","Link accepted drift to approved decisions."],"verification_method":"Introduce known architectural deviations and verify that the governance system detects and correctly classifies them.","outcome_learning":"Measure drift detection latency, false positives, and unresolved drift age.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B43-R01","D55-B43-R04"],"relationships":["ARCHITECTURE_DRIFT","CONFORMANCE_MONITORING"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B43-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Compliance","concept":"Architecture Conformance Exception","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should a legitimate architecture deviation be distinguished from architecture non-conformance?","answer":"A deviation should be treated as an accepted exception only when it has an identifiable reason, defined scope, responsible authority, documented risk, expiration or review condition where appropriate, and traceability to the affected architecture requirement.","context":"Not every departure from a standard or baseline is necessarily unauthorized or permanent.","objective":"Prevent governance from treating every deviation identically while preserving accountability.","requirements":["Deviation scope must be explicit.","Reason and risk must be documented.","Authorization must be identifiable.","Review or expiration conditions should be defined when the deviation is temporary."],"constraints":["Permanent exceptions can become hidden architecture debt.","Temporary exceptions can persist if not monitored.","Urgent deviations may initially have incomplete evidence."],"prerequisites":["Architecture standard","Exception process","Decision authority"],"dependencies":["Governance Exceptions","Architecture Drift Detection"],"alternatives":["Treat all deviations as violations","Allow undocumented deviations","Governed architecture exceptions"],"evidence":"Explicitly governed exceptions preserve architectural flexibility while maintaining visibility of residual risk.","tradeoffs":{"flexibility_vs_standardization":"Exceptions allow necessary adaptation but reduce uniformity.","temporary_relief_vs_debt":"Temporary exceptions can unblock delivery while creating future remediation obligations."},"decision":"Represent legitimate deviations as explicit, governed exceptions rather than silently modifying conformance criteria.","rationale":"Separating exceptions from baseline rules preserves both architectural standards and accountable flexibility.","consequences":["Exceptions remain visible.","Standards retain stable meaning.","Exception debt can be measured and reviewed."],"implementation_implications":["Create exception identifiers.","Link exceptions to affected standards and decisions.","Track owner, risk, review date, and closure conditions."],"verification_method":"Audit known deviations and verify that each is either governed by an active exception or correctly classified as non-conformance.","outcome_learning":"Measure exception age, recurrence, and conversion to permanent architecture changes.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B43-R02","D55-B43-R05"],"relationships":["ARCHITECTURE_EXCEPTION","CONFORMANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B43-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Compliance","concept":"Architecture Conformance Automation Boundary","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Which architecture conformance checks are suitable for automation?","answer":"Automation is well suited to deterministic properties such as dependency restrictions, interface presence, naming or structural rules, prohibited technology usage, configuration constraints, deployment topology conditions, and other machine-observable invariants. Semantic tradeoffs and context-dependent decisions generally require human or higher-level reasoning.","context":"Governance automation must be designed around properties that can be evaluated reliably.","objective":"Define an appropriate boundary between automated and human architecture governance.","requirements":["Automated rules must be deterministic enough for their intended use.","Rule failures must be actionable.","Automation must not imply broader architectural correctness than the checked property establishes."],"constraints":["Architectural intent may be semantic.","Rules can become stale as architecture evolves.","Over-automation can create false confidence."],"prerequisites":["Defined architecture rules","Machine-observable system metadata","Rule ownership"],"dependencies":["Policy-as-Code","Architecture Standards","Conformance Evidence"],"alternatives":["Fully manual governance","Automate deterministic checks","Automate all architecture decisions"],"evidence":"Deterministic architectural invariants are suitable for automated checking, while contextual decisions require interpretation beyond simple rule evaluation.","tradeoffs":{"automation_vs_semantics":"Automation improves repeatability but cannot fully evaluate contextual architectural reasoning.","coverage_vs_false_confidence":"More checks increase coverage but can create false confidence if unmeasured properties are ignored."},"decision":"Automate deterministic conformance assertions and preserve human governance for contextual architectural judgments.","rationale":"Automation should provide reliable evidence rather than pretending to replace architectural reasoning.","consequences":["Routine conformance checks become faster.","Human reviewers can focus on material semantic issues.","Rule coverage remains an explicit limitation."],"implementation_implications":["Tag rules by evidence scope.","Version architecture rules.","Report unchecked architectural properties explicitly."],"verification_method":"Validate automated rules against known conforming and non-conforming cases and inspect whether rule results are interpreted within their declared scope.","outcome_learning":"Track false-positive and false-negative findings and retire rules that no longer represent governed architecture.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B43-R01","D55-B43-R02"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_AUTOMATION","CONFORMANCE_RULE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B43-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B43","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Compliance","concept":"Architecture Non-Conformance Remediation","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should material architecture non-conformance be remediated?","answer":"Material non-conformance should be classified by risk and consequence, assigned an accountable owner, and resolved through correction, approved architecture change, governed exception, or explicit risk acceptance. The selected disposition should preserve traceability to the original conformance finding.","context":"Detecting architecture non-conformance is insufficient if findings remain unresolved or are silently ignored.","objective":"Turn conformance findings into accountable governance outcomes.","requirements":["Material findings require disposition.","Risk must be assessed.","The chosen resolution must be traceable.","Verification must establish whether remediation or exception conditions were satisfied."],"constraints":["Immediate remediation may be impractical.","Some findings may be caused by obsolete architecture rules.","Emergency fixes may temporarily bypass normal processes."],"prerequisites":["Conformance finding","Risk assessment","Architecture ownership"],"dependencies":["Architecture Change Control","Governance Exceptions","Conformance Evidence"],"alternatives":["Immediate remediation for all findings","Ignore low-visibility findings","Risk-proportional remediation"],"evidence":"Risk-proportional remediation distinguishes urgent architectural risk from low-impact deviations while preserving governance visibility.","tradeoffs":{"remediation_cost_vs_risk":"Expensive remediation may not be justified for low-risk findings.","speed_vs_completeness":"Temporary controls can reduce immediate risk while permanent remediation is planned."},"decision":"Use risk-proportional remediation with explicit exception or risk-acceptance paths.","rationale":"Different non-conformances have different consequences and remediation costs.","consequences":["Material risks receive prioritization.","Accepted residual risk remains visible.","Architecture rules can be corrected when the standard itself becomes obsolete."],"implementation_implications":["Define remediation statuses.","Track owners and deadlines or review triggers.","Require verification after remediation."],"verification_method":"Sample closed non-conformance findings and verify that the recorded disposition, implementation, and verification agree.","outcome_learning":"Measure remediation age, recurrence, and percentage of findings caused by obsolete rules.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B43-R02","D55-B43-R03"],"relationships":["NON_CONFORMANCE","REMEDIATION"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B44-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Knowledge Management","concept":"Architecture Knowledge Ownership","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should ownership of architecture knowledge be governed?","answer":"Architecture knowledge should have identifiable ownership for accuracy, lifecycle maintenance, review, and retirement. Ownership should apply to architectural artifacts and decisions without implying that one individual must perform every technical activity represented by the knowledge.","context":"Architecture repositories become unreliable when no accountable owner maintains their accuracy and lifecycle.","objective":"Establish accountability for the quality and currency of architecture knowledge.","requirements":["Material architecture artifacts require ownership.","Ownership responsibilities must be defined.","Ownership transfer must be possible when organizational responsibilities change."],"constraints":["Architecture knowledge often spans multiple teams.","Ownership can become ambiguous in shared platforms.","A named owner does not guarantee correctness."],"prerequisites":["Architecture repository","Ownership model","Architecture domains"],"dependencies":["Architecture Catalogs","Governance Roles","Architecture Baselines"],"alternatives":["No explicit owner","Single enterprise architecture owner","Domain-aligned ownership with governance coordination"],"evidence":"Explicit ownership improves accountability for maintaining architecture information and responding to governance findings.","tradeoffs":{"centralization_vs_domain_context":"Central ownership can improve consistency while domain ownership provides better local knowledge.","ownership_vs_collaboration":"Accountability must not prevent collaborative architecture maintenance."},"decision":"Assign accountable owners by architecture domain or artifact type with defined collaboration and escalation paths.","rationale":"Architecture knowledge is distributed, so ownership should reflect the boundaries of responsibility.","consequences":["Stale artifacts have accountable owners.","Ownership conflicts become escalatable.","Architecture maintenance responsibilities become explicit."],"implementation_implications":["Define owner fields.","Define ownership responsibilities.","Support delegation without losing accountability.","Review ownership during organizational changes."],"verification_method":"Sample architecture artifacts and verify that ownership is identifiable and consistent with current organizational responsibilities.","outcome_learning":"Track stale artifacts and unresolved ownership gaps.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B44-R02","D55-B44-R03"],"relationships":["ARCHITECTURE_OWNERSHIP","KNOWLEDGE_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B44-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Knowledge Management","concept":"Architecture Knowledge Currency","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should governance determine whether architecture documentation is sufficiently current?","answer":"Architecture knowledge should be considered current when it accurately represents the governed state within its defined scope and freshness expectations. Currency should be assessed using change events, review triggers, implementation evidence, and explicit artifact ownership rather than an arbitrary universal update interval.","context":"Architecture artifacts can become stale even when no scheduled review has occurred.","objective":"Govern the currency of architecture knowledge based on actual change and risk.","requirements":["Artifacts must have defined scope.","Material change events should trigger reassessment.","Currency status should be observable."],"constraints":["Different architecture artifacts change at different rates.","Frequent updates can create documentation noise.","Some artifacts intentionally represent historical rather than current state."],"prerequisites":["Artifact ownership","Architecture baseline","Change records"],"dependencies":["Architecture Change Control","Architecture Baselines","Architecture Repository"],"alternatives":["Fixed periodic updates","Update only when manually requested","Event- and risk-driven currency management"],"evidence":"Change-triggered reassessment better reflects actual architectural evolution than a single fixed update interval.","tradeoffs":{"freshness_vs_effort":"More frequent reassessment improves currency but increases maintenance cost.","historical_integrity_vs_currentness":"Historical records should remain immutable while current views are updated."},"decision":"Use event- and risk-driven currency controls supplemented by periodic review where appropriate.","rationale":"Architecture freshness should correspond to the rate and consequences of change.","consequences":["Material changes trigger documentation reassessment.","Historical decisions remain distinguishable from current architecture.","Staleness becomes measurable."],"implementation_implications":["Record artifact scope and status.","Link changes to affected artifacts.","Define reassessment triggers.","Preserve historical versions."],"verification_method":"Compare selected architecture artifacts with recent approved changes and verify that material changes caused appropriate reassessment.","outcome_learning":"Measure stale-artifact incidents and time from architectural change to documentation reconciliation.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B44-R01","D55-B44-R04"],"relationships":["KNOWLEDGE_CURRENCY","ARCHITECTURE_DOCUMENTATION"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B44-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Knowledge Management","concept":"Architecture Knowledge Classification","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should architecture knowledge be classified by lifecycle state?","answer":"Architecture knowledge should distinguish states such as proposed, approved, implemented, verified, deprecated, superseded, rejected, and historical where those states are meaningful. This prevents users from treating architectural intent or obsolete decisions as current verified architecture.","context":"Architecture repositories commonly contain information from different stages of the architecture lifecycle.","objective":"Prevent semantic confusion between proposed, current, historical, and rejected architecture knowledge.","requirements":["Lifecycle state must be explicit where ambiguity is possible.","State transitions should be traceable.","Historical information must remain distinguishable from current state."],"constraints":["Not every artifact requires every state.","State definitions must remain stable enough for retrieval and automation.","A document's existence does not establish its approval or implementation."],"prerequisites":["Architecture lifecycle model","Repository metadata model"],"dependencies":["Architecture Baselines","Decision Traceability"],"alternatives":["Document-only storage","Folder-based lifecycle inference","Explicit lifecycle metadata"],"evidence":"Explicit lifecycle state prevents consumers from interpreting architecture records based solely on location or document age.","tradeoffs":{"metadata_vs_simplicity":"Lifecycle metadata increases structure but improves machine interpretation.","historical_retention_vs_noise":"Retaining history supports traceability but requires state-aware retrieval."},"decision":"Represent meaningful architecture lifecycle states explicitly in governance metadata.","rationale":"Architecture knowledge must distinguish intent, authorization, realization, and historical status.","consequences":["Retrieval can filter by current or historical state.","Automation can avoid acting on obsolete decisions.","Governance audits become easier to reconstruct."],"implementation_implications":["Define controlled lifecycle states.","Record transition evidence.","Prevent state changes from silently overwriting history."],"verification_method":"Test retrieval against proposed, approved, rejected, superseded, and current records and verify correct lifecycle interpretation.","outcome_learning":"Track retrieval errors caused by lifecycle ambiguity.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B44-R02","D55-B44-R05"],"relationships":["KNOWLEDGE_LIFECYCLE","ARCHITECTURE_STATE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B44-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Knowledge Management","concept":"Architecture Repository Integrity","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What governance controls help preserve the integrity of an architecture repository?","answer":"Repository integrity requires stable identifiers, version history, controlled state transitions, ownership, provenance, access control, change traceability, and mechanisms for detecting inconsistent or orphaned references.","context":"Architecture repositories can accumulate duplicate, conflicting, stale, or orphaned information over time.","objective":"Maintain reliable architecture knowledge for human and machine consumers.","requirements":["Records need stable identity.","Changes must be attributable.","Relationships should remain resolvable.","Conflicting states must be detectable."],"constraints":["Repository structures evolve.","Legacy artifacts may lack metadata.","Strong controls can increase maintenance effort."],"prerequisites":["Architecture repository","Metadata schema","Access model"],"dependencies":["Architecture Knowledge Ownership","Architecture Catalogs","Governance Evidence"],"alternatives":["Unstructured document repository","Central controlled repository","Structured architecture knowledge repository"],"evidence":"Structured metadata and traceability controls improve the ability to determine provenance, current state, and relationships between architecture records.","tradeoffs":{"integrity_vs_flexibility":"Stronger structure improves consistency but may increase contribution friction.","history_vs_storage":"Retaining history increases storage and retrieval complexity."},"decision":"Govern architecture repositories as structured, versioned knowledge systems rather than document stores alone.","rationale":"Architecture governance depends on relationships, lifecycle state, provenance, and traceability that unstructured documents cannot reliably express.","consequences":["Repository consumers gain stronger semantic context.","Maintenance requirements increase.","Repository quality becomes measurable."],"implementation_implications":["Use stable IDs.","Version records.","Validate references.","Track provenance and lifecycle state.","Provide integrity checks."],"verification_method":"Run repository integrity checks for duplicate identifiers, broken relationships, invalid lifecycle transitions, missing owners, and inconsistent state.","outcome_learning":"Track integrity violations and their root causes to improve repository governance.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B44-R03","D55-B44-R05"],"relationships":["REPOSITORY_INTEGRITY","ARCHITECTURE_KNOWLEDGE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B44-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B44","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Knowledge Management","concept":"Architecture Knowledge Retirement","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should obsolete architecture knowledge be retired, superseded, or preserved as historical evidence?","answer":"Architecture knowledge should be retired from active guidance when it no longer governs current decisions, but historical records should generally remain preserved when they provide decision, compliance, incident, migration, or learning value. Retirement should change lifecycle status rather than silently deleting traceability.","context":"Deleting obsolete architecture records can remove the evidence needed to understand why the current architecture exists.","objective":"Control obsolete knowledge without destroying useful architectural history.","requirements":["Current guidance must be distinguishable from historical records.","Retirement decisions must be traceable.","Historical dependencies must remain resolvable where required."],"constraints":["Large repositories can accumulate substantial history.","Some records may contain sensitive information requiring separate access controls.","Not all obsolete artifacts have long-term value."],"prerequisites":["Lifecycle states","Repository ownership","Retention policy"],"dependencies":["Architecture Knowledge Classification","Architecture Decision Records"],"alternatives":["Permanent deletion","Keep everything active","Retire from active guidance while preserving relevant history"],"evidence":"Historical architecture knowledge can provide essential context for migration, incident analysis, and understanding prior decisions.","tradeoffs":{"retention_vs_noise":"Retention preserves context but requires lifecycle-aware retrieval.","deletion_vs_traceability":"Deletion reduces storage but can destroy important historical evidence."},"decision":"Retire obsolete architecture knowledge from active use while preserving material historical records according to retention requirements.","rationale":"Historical context should remain available without allowing obsolete guidance to be mistaken for current architecture.","consequences":["Current retrieval can exclude retired records.","Historical analysis remains possible.","Repository lifecycle management becomes explicit."],"implementation_implications":["Define retirement criteria.","Mark records as retired or superseded.","Preserve relationships needed for historical reconstruction.","Apply appropriate retention and access controls."],"verification_method":"Verify that retired records are excluded from current architecture guidance while remaining retrievable through historical queries where permitted.","outcome_learning":"Review frequently accessed retired records to identify missing current guidance or recurring architectural lessons.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B44-R03","D55-B44-R04"],"relationships":["KNOWLEDGE_RETIREMENT","ARCHITECTURE_HISTORY"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B45-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Risk Governance","concept":"Architecture Risk Acceptance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What information should support explicit acceptance of an architecture risk?","answer":"Risk acceptance should identify the risk, affected assets or boundaries, likelihood and consequence assessment where meaningful, existing controls, residual risk, decision authority, acceptance rationale, review trigger or expiry when appropriate, and evidence supporting the assessment.","context":"Some architecture risks cannot be eliminated economically or immediately and therefore require explicit governance decisions.","objective":"Prevent implicit or undocumented acceptance of material architecture risk.","requirements":["Risk must be explicitly identified.","Residual risk must be understood.","Acceptance authority must be appropriate.","Review conditions should be defined for material or temporary acceptance."],"constraints":["Risk estimates can contain uncertainty.","Acceptance thresholds vary by context.","Risk acceptance does not remove the underlying technical risk."],"prerequisites":["Architecture risk assessment","Risk ownership","Decision authority"],"dependencies":["Architecture Risk Governance","Governance Exceptions"],"alternatives":["Implicit acceptance","Mandatory elimination of all risks","Explicit risk acceptance"],"evidence":"Explicit risk acceptance separates an intentional governance decision from an unnoticed or unowned architectural weakness.","tradeoffs":{"risk_reduction_vs_cost":"Additional controls may reduce risk while increasing cost or complexity.","short_term_delivery_vs_long_term_risk":"Acceptance can enable delivery while preserving residual exposure that requires monitoring."},"decision":"Require explicit, evidence-backed acceptance for material residual architecture risks.","rationale":"Unaccepted risk can become invisible organizational debt.","consequences":["Residual risks become visible.","Accountability for acceptance is explicit.","Accepted risks can be reassessed when conditions change."],"implementation_implications":["Define risk acceptance records.","Identify acceptance authority.","Link accepted risks to affected architecture decisions.","Define reassessment triggers."],"verification_method":"Audit accepted architecture risks and verify identity, evidence, authority, residual-risk treatment, and review conditions.","outcome_learning":"Track accepted risks that later materialize or require repeated extensions.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B45-R02","D55-B45-R03"],"relationships":["RISK_ACCEPTANCE","RESIDUAL_RISK"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B45-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Risk Governance","concept":"Architecture Risk Escalation","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Which architecture risks require escalation beyond the originating architecture team?","answer":"Risks should be escalated when they exceed defined risk tolerance, cross organizational or system boundaries, affect critical security or reliability properties, create significant irreversible consequences, remain materially uncertain, or cannot be controlled within the authority of the originating team.","context":"Architecture risks can exceed the decision scope of the team that discovers them.","objective":"Ensure material architecture risks reach an authority capable of managing their consequences.","requirements":["Escalation criteria must be explicit.","Risk evidence must accompany escalation.","The receiving authority must be identifiable."],"constraints":["Over-escalation creates governance bottlenecks.","Under-escalation can leave material risks unmanaged.","Risk magnitude may change as implementation progresses."],"prerequisites":["Risk model","Risk ownership","Governance hierarchy"],"dependencies":["Decision Authority","Architecture Risk Assessment"],"alternatives":["Local-only risk handling","Escalate all risks","Threshold-based escalation"],"evidence":"Risk escalation based on impact, boundary, authority, and uncertainty aligns decision scope with potential consequences.","tradeoffs":{"local_speed_vs_enterprise_visibility":"Local handling is faster while escalation improves broader risk visibility.","consistency_vs_context":"Enterprise thresholds improve consistency but require contextual interpretation."},"decision":"Use explicit escalation thresholds based on consequence, boundary, authority, and uncertainty.","rationale":"Risk should be governed at a level capable of controlling or accepting its potential consequences.","consequences":["Enterprise-impacting risks become visible.","Local teams retain authority for bounded risks.","Escalation rationale becomes auditable."],"implementation_implications":["Define escalation thresholds.","Record escalation evidence.","Track receiving authority and disposition.","Allow reassessment as risk changes."],"verification_method":"Review representative risks and compare escalation decisions against documented thresholds.","outcome_learning":"Measure missed escalations, unnecessary escalations, and time to risk disposition.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B45-R01","D55-B45-R04"],"relationships":["RISK_ESCALATION","ARCHITECTURE_RISK"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B45-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Risk Governance","concept":"Architecture Risk Treatment Selection","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should architecture teams choose between reducing, transferring, avoiding, accepting, or monitoring a risk?","answer":"Risk treatment should be selected by considering the risk mechanism, available controls, residual risk, cost and complexity, reversibility, affected boundaries, operational constraints, and decision authority. Treatment should be explicit rather than assumed from implementation activity.","context":"Different architecture risks require different responses, and adding controls is not always the most appropriate treatment.","objective":"Make architecture risk treatment deliberate and traceable.","requirements":["Risk treatment options must be explicit.","Residual risk must be considered.","Treatment must align with decision authority and constraints."],"constraints":["Some risks have no complete elimination path.","Controls can introduce secondary risks.","Treatment effectiveness may be uncertain before implementation."],"prerequisites":["Risk identification","Risk analysis","Architecture constraints"],"dependencies":["Architecture Risk Assessment","Risk Acceptance"],"alternatives":["Avoid","Reduce","Transfer","Accept","Monitor"],"evidence":"Risk treatment should address the causal mechanism and consequences rather than applying controls generically.","tradeoffs":{"risk_vs_complexity":"Additional controls can reduce one risk while increasing architectural complexity.","immediate_vs_residual":"A treatment may reduce current exposure without eliminating the underlying cause."},"decision":"Select risk treatment based on causal mechanism, residual exposure, feasibility, and architectural consequences.","rationale":"Different risks require different responses and may have materially different treatment costs and side effects.","consequences":["Risk responses become explainable.","Secondary architectural effects can be considered.","Residual risk remains visible after treatment."],"implementation_implications":["Record selected treatment.","Define expected risk change.","Verify control effectiveness where measurable.","Preserve residual-risk assessment."],"verification_method":"Review treated risks and determine whether selected treatment addresses the documented risk mechanism and whether residual risk was reassessed.","outcome_learning":"Compare expected and observed risk reduction to improve treatment selection.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B45-R01","D55-B45-R05"],"relationships":["RISK_TREATMENT","RESIDUAL_RISK"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B45-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Risk Governance","concept":"Architecture Risk Dependency","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why should dependencies between architecture risks be represented explicitly?","answer":"Risk dependencies should be represented when one architectural risk changes the likelihood, consequence, detectability, or treatment effectiveness of another risk. Explicit dependencies prevent teams from evaluating material risks as independent when they share causal mechanisms or controls.","context":"Architecture risks frequently interact through shared infrastructure, dependencies, trust boundaries, operational controls, or migration decisions.","objective":"Improve risk reasoning by preserving meaningful relationships between risks.","requirements":["Material risk relationships should be identifiable.","Shared causes and controls should be representable.","Risk changes should propagate to dependent assessments where appropriate."],"constraints":["Over-modeling insignificant relationships creates noise.","Risk dependencies can be conditional.","The absence of a known dependency does not prove independence."],"prerequisites":["Risk register","Architecture dependency model","Risk ownership"],"dependencies":["Cross-System Dependency Governance","Architecture Risk Assessment"],"alternatives":["Independent risk records","Shared risk categories","Explicit risk dependency graph"],"evidence":"Related risks can invalidate isolated risk assessments when they share causes, controls, or consequences.","tradeoffs":{"model_completeness_vs_complexity":"Explicit relationships improve reasoning but increase governance model complexity.","automation_vs_judgment":"Dependency propagation can be automated for simple cases but may require expert interpretation."},"decision":"Represent material causal and control dependencies between architecture risks.","rationale":"Architecture risk is often systemic rather than isolated to a single component.","consequences":["Risk assessments can account for shared causes and controls.","Changes to one risk can trigger reassessment of related risks.","Governance analysis becomes more interconnected."],"implementation_implications":["Define relationship types.","Link risks to architecture components and controls.","Trigger reassessment for material dependency changes."],"verification_method":"Review known shared-risk scenarios and verify that material dependencies are represented and considered during reassessment.","outcome_learning":"Track incidents where unmodeled risk dependencies contributed to unexpected outcomes.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B45-R02","D55-B45-R03"],"relationships":["RISK_DEPENDENCY","ARCHITECTURE_RISK_GRAPH"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B45-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B45","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Risk Governance","concept":"Architecture Risk Reassessment Trigger","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What events should trigger reassessment of an architecture risk?","answer":"Reassessment should be triggered by material architecture changes, changed requirements, new dependencies, significant technology changes, control failures, relevant incidents, changed workload or operating conditions, new evidence, risk materialization, or expiry of a defined review condition.","context":"Architecture risk assessments become stale when the assumptions and system conditions underlying them change.","objective":"Keep architecture risk assessments aligned with meaningful changes in system context.","requirements":["Material reassessment triggers must be defined.","Risk status must be updateable without losing history.","Trigger evidence should be traceable."],"constraints":["Frequent changes can create reassessment overhead.","Not every change materially affects every risk.","Some signals may be delayed or incomplete."],"prerequisites":["Risk register","Architecture change process","Risk ownership"],"dependencies":["Architecture Change Control","Architecture Risk Acceptance","Architecture Knowledge Currency"],"alternatives":["Fixed periodic reassessment","Event-driven reassessment","Combined event-driven and periodic review"],"evidence":"Risk depends on system context, so material context changes can invalidate prior assessments even when the risk record itself has not changed.","tradeoffs":{"freshness_vs_effort":"More triggers improve freshness but increase reassessment workload.","history_vs_currentness":"Versioned assessments preserve historical reasoning while supporting current reassessment."},"decision":"Use material event triggers supplemented by periodic reassessment for significant architecture risks.","rationale":"Risk currency should respond to changes that can alter assumptions, exposure, or controls.","consequences":["Stale risk assessments are easier to detect.","Risk governance becomes connected to architecture change.","Historical assessments remain available for comparison."],"implementation_implications":["Define trigger categories.","Link triggers to affected risks.","Record reassessment outcomes and evidence.","Preserve prior risk states."],"verification_method":"Inject or identify representative risk-changing events and verify that affected risks are marked for reassessment.","outcome_learning":"Measure reassessment latency and identify recurring trigger types that produce meaningful risk changes.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B45-R01","D55-B45-R04"],"relationships":["RISK_REASSESSMENT","ARCHITECTURE_CHANGE_TRIGGER"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B46-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Metrics","concept":"Architecture Governance Leading Indicators","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Which leading indicators can help identify architecture governance problems before they become major incidents?","answer":"Potential leading indicators include increasing architecture exceptions, repeated review findings, growing decision latency, rising unresolved architecture risks, increasing drift, frequent emergency changes, stale architecture artifacts, and repeated bypassing of governance controls.","context":"Governance metrics should provide early signals rather than only report historical failures.","objective":"Use measurable signals to identify deteriorating architecture governance conditions.","requirements":["Metrics must have defined meanings.","Signals should be connected to actionable governance responses.","Metrics must not be treated as universal quality scores."],"constraints":["Metrics can be gamed.","Higher values are not inherently good or bad without context.","Different systems have different baseline rates."],"prerequisites":["Governance event data","Defined metric semantics","Architecture lifecycle records"],"dependencies":["Governance Metrics","Architecture Drift","Exception Management"],"alternatives":["Outcome-only metrics","Activity metrics","Leading and lagging indicators"],"evidence":"Repeated governance exceptions, unresolved risks, and stale artifacts can indicate systemic architecture-management problems before downstream failures occur.","tradeoffs":{"measurement_vs_overhead":"More metrics improve observability but increase collection and interpretation effort.","standardization_vs_context":"Common metrics improve comparison while context-specific interpretation remains necessary."},"decision":"Use a balanced set of leading and lagging governance indicators with explicit interpretation rules.","rationale":"No single metric can represent architecture governance quality across different systems and contexts.","consequences":["Early governance deterioration becomes more observable.","Metric interpretation requires contextual analysis.","Teams can investigate trends before failures occur."],"implementation_implications":["Define metric semantics.","Record measurement context.","Set investigation thresholds rather than simplistic quality scores.","Link metrics to governance actions."],"verification_method":"Review whether significant governance issues were preceded by measurable indicators and whether the indicators generated actionable investigation.","outcome_learning":"Retire metrics that repeatedly fail to provide useful signal and refine thresholds based on observed evidence.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B46-R02","D55-B46-R03"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_METRICS","LEADING_INDICATORS"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B46-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Metrics","concept":"Architecture Governance Metric Semantics","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why must architecture governance metrics have explicit semantic definitions?","answer":"Every governance metric should define what is measured, population or scope, calculation method, time window, data source, exclusions, interpretation limits, and intended use. Without these semantics, identical metric names can represent materially different conditions.","context":"Architecture governance metrics are often reused across teams and systems with different measurement assumptions.","objective":"Prevent misleading comparisons and unsupported conclusions from governance metrics.","requirements":["Metric definitions must be machine-readable where practical.","Calculation semantics must be stable or versioned.","Interpretation limitations must be documented."],"constraints":["Data sources can change.","Historical metrics may not be directly comparable after definition changes.","Some metrics require qualitative interpretation."],"prerequisites":["Metric catalog","Governance data sources","Measurement ownership"],"dependencies":["Governance Metrics","Evidence Quality"],"alternatives":["Name-only metrics","Informal metric definitions","Versioned metric specifications"],"evidence":"A metric value has meaning only relative to its population, calculation, timeframe, and measurement assumptions.","tradeoffs":{"standardization_vs_local_need":"Standard definitions improve consistency while local extensions may be required.","definition_stability_vs_evolution":"Stable definitions aid comparison but metrics must evolve when governance needs change."},"decision":"Version and govern metric definitions alongside metric values.","rationale":"Metric values without semantic context are unsafe inputs for architecture governance decisions.","consequences":["Historical values can be interpreted correctly.","Metric changes become traceable.","Cross-system comparisons require explicit compatibility checks."],"implementation_implications":["Assign metric identifiers.","Version calculation definitions.","Store scope and timeframe.","Record source and exclusions."],"verification_method":"Recalculate selected metrics from their declared inputs and verify that stored values match the metric definition.","outcome_learning":"Track interpretation errors and definition changes to improve the metric catalog.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B46-R01","D55-B46-R04"],"relationships":["METRIC_SEMANTICS","METRIC_VERSIONING"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B46-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Metrics","concept":"Architecture Governance Metric Anti-Gaming","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How can architecture governance metrics reduce the risk of incentivizing undesirable behavior?","answer":"Metrics should be used as signals for investigation rather than isolated performance targets, should be interpreted with context, and should be balanced with counter-metrics or qualitative review where optimization of one measure could undermine architecture outcomes.","context":"Teams may change behavior to improve measured values without improving the underlying architecture.","objective":"Reduce metric-driven behavior that creates false governance improvement.","requirements":["Metric use must define intended interpretation.","High-impact decisions should not rely on a single activity metric.","Metrics should be reviewed for unintended incentives."],"constraints":["Some metrics inevitably influence behavior.","Counter-metrics can increase complexity.","Not all unintended effects are predictable."],"prerequisites":["Metric catalog","Governance objectives","Review process"],"dependencies":["Governance Metrics","Continuous Improvement"],"alternatives":["Single target metric","Balanced metrics","Metrics as diagnostic signals"],"evidence":"Optimizing a proxy can diverge from the underlying objective when the proxy does not capture all relevant consequences.","tradeoffs":{"simplicity_vs_behavioral_safety":"Simple metrics are easier to operate but may create stronger unintended incentives.","measurement_vs_judgment":"Metrics provide evidence but cannot replace contextual governance judgment."},"decision":"Treat architecture governance metrics primarily as diagnostic evidence and combine them with outcome and qualitative review.","rationale":"Architecture governance objectives are multidimensional and cannot safely be represented by a single activity count.","consequences":["Metric values are less likely to become simplistic targets.","Reviewers must interpret evidence.","Governance reporting becomes more nuanced."],"implementation_implications":["Document metric intended use.","Identify possible gaming modes.","Add counter-indicators where material.","Review metric behavior periodically."],"verification_method":"Analyze whether changes in metric values correspond to the intended architectural outcome rather than merely increased reporting activity.","outcome_learning":"Record unintended metric incentives and modify metric design when evidence shows systematic distortion.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B46-R01","D55-B46-R05"],"relationships":["METRIC_GOVERNANCE","ANTI_GAMING"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B46-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Metrics","concept":"Architecture Governance Trend Analysis","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance trends be interpreted?","answer":"Trends should be evaluated against comparable scope, time windows, architecture lifecycle changes, organizational changes, and measurement-definition versions. A trend should not be interpreted as improvement or deterioration without understanding changes in the measured population and system context.","context":"Governance indicators can change because the system changed, the measurement changed, or the governance process changed.","objective":"Prevent misleading trend conclusions.","requirements":["Metric definition versions must be preserved.","Scope changes must be visible.","Major contextual changes should be annotated."],"constraints":["Historical data may be incomplete.","Organizational changes can alter metric populations.","Some trends are confounded by multiple simultaneous changes."],"prerequisites":["Versioned metrics","Measurement history","Architecture change history"],"dependencies":["Metric Semantics","Architecture Change Control"],"alternatives":["Raw time-series comparison","Context-aware trend analysis","No trend analysis"],"evidence":"Metric values are not directly comparable when definitions, populations, or operating contexts differ materially.","tradeoffs":{"comparability_vs_continuity":"Changing metrics may improve relevance while reducing direct historical comparability.","analysis_depth_vs_speed":"Contextual analysis improves accuracy but requires additional effort."},"decision":"Perform context-aware trend analysis using versioned definitions and annotated scope changes.","rationale":"Governance trends are meaningful only when measurement context remains sufficiently comparable.","consequences":["Trend conclusions become more defensible.","Definition changes remain visible.","Some historical comparisons may be explicitly classified as non-comparable."],"implementation_implications":["Version metrics.","Annotate major architecture and organizational changes.","Store measurement scope.","Flag incomparable periods."],"verification_method":"Test trend reports across a known metric-definition change and verify that the system does not falsely treat incompatible values as directly comparable.","outcome_learning":"Record recurring sources of trend ambiguity and improve measurement metadata.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B46-R02","D55-B46-R05"],"relationships":["TREND_ANALYSIS","METRIC_CONTEXT"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B46-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B46","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Metrics","concept":"Architecture Governance Outcome Measurement","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance measure whether governance actions actually improved architectural outcomes?","answer":"Outcome measurement should connect governance interventions to observable changes such as reduced recurring architecture defects, improved conformance, reduced unresolved risk, improved change reliability, fewer emergency deviations, or improved maintainability where those outcomes can be meaningfully measured. Causal claims should remain proportional to available evidence.","context":"Governance activity counts do not demonstrate that governance improved the architecture.","objective":"Evaluate governance through outcomes rather than activity volume alone.","requirements":["Governance interventions must be identifiable.","Outcome measures must have defined semantics.","Alternative explanations should be considered before causal conclusions."],"constraints":["Architecture outcomes may have long feedback cycles.","Multiple interventions can affect the same outcome.","Some outcomes are difficult to quantify."],"prerequisites":["Governance intervention records","Outcome metrics","Architecture history"],"dependencies":["Governance Metrics","Continuous Improvement"],"alternatives":["Activity-only measurement","Outcome measurement","Mixed activity and outcome measurement"],"evidence":"Outcome measures provide stronger evidence of governance effectiveness than counts of reviews, meetings, or approvals alone, but causal attribution may remain uncertain.","tradeoffs":{"measurement_depth_vs_effort":"Outcome analysis requires more evidence than activity counting.","causal_confidence_vs_practicality":"Strong causal claims require controlled evidence that may be unavailable in normal architecture governance."},"decision":"Use outcome-oriented measures while explicitly distinguishing correlation from verified causal effects.","rationale":"Governance exists to influence architectural outcomes, so measurement should extend beyond process activity.","consequences":["Governance improvements can be evaluated more meaningfully.","Some results remain uncertain rather than being overstated.","Governance teams must maintain outcome evidence."],"implementation_implications":["Define expected outcomes for major governance interventions.","Track relevant baseline and follow-up measures.","Record confounding changes.","Avoid unsupported causal attribution."],"verification_method":"For selected governance interventions, compare defined outcomes before and after intervention while documenting relevant contextual changes.","outcome_learning":"Use observed outcomes to refine governance controls, metrics, and intervention selection.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B46-R01","D55-B46-R04"],"relationships":["OUTCOME_MEASUREMENT","GOVERNANCE_EFFECTIVENESS"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B47-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Automation","concept":"Policy-as-Code Architecture Governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What makes an architecture governance policy suitable for implementation as policy-as-code?","answer":"A policy is suitable for policy-as-code when its subject, inputs, evaluation logic, allowed states, violations, and required evidence can be represented deterministically or with explicitly bounded uncertainty. The policy should also have a defined owner and lifecycle.","context":"Architecture governance increasingly uses automated checks to evaluate repeatable architectural constraints.","objective":"Identify policies that can be reliably automated without overstating automation coverage.","requirements":["Policy semantics must be explicit.","Required inputs must be available.","Evaluation outcomes must be interpretable.","Policy versions must be traceable."],"constraints":["Some architecture judgments are contextual.","Incomplete inputs can create false conclusions.","Policies can become obsolete as architecture evolves."],"prerequisites":["Architecture standards","Machine-observable metadata","Policy lifecycle"],"dependencies":["Governance Automation","Architecture Conformance"],"alternatives":["Manual policy evaluation","Deterministic policy-as-code","Fully autonomous architecture governance"],"evidence":"Policies with explicit deterministic conditions are appropriate for automated evaluation; contextual architectural decisions require additional reasoning and review.","tradeoffs":{"automation_vs_context":"Automation improves consistency but cannot fully encode contextual judgment.","speed_vs_policy_maintenance":"Automation is fast at runtime but requires ongoing policy maintenance."},"decision":"Implement deterministic architecture constraints as policy-as-code while explicitly declaring automation boundaries.","rationale":"Automation should enforce what can be represented reliably and leave contextual decisions to appropriate governance processes.","consequences":["Repeatable checks become faster.","Policy semantics become more explicit.","Policy maintenance becomes an ongoing governance responsibility."],"implementation_implications":["Version policies.","Validate inputs.","Record policy evaluation evidence.","Define failure and unknown states.","Assign policy ownership."],"verification_method":"Evaluate policies against conforming, violating, and incomplete-input cases and verify correct classification.","outcome_learning":"Track policy false positives, false negatives, and unknown evaluations.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B47-R02","D55-B47-R03"],"relationships":["POLICY_AS_CODE","GOVERNANCE_AUTOMATION"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B47-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Automation","concept":"Governance Policy Unknown State","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should automated architecture governance behave when required evidence is unavailable or ambiguous?","answer":"Automated governance should distinguish compliant, non-compliant, and unknown or insufficient-evidence states when inputs cannot establish the policy outcome. Missing evidence should not automatically be interpreted as compliance.","context":"Automated governance often evaluates incomplete metadata, transient infrastructure state, or partially observable architecture properties.","objective":"Prevent false compliance caused by incomplete evidence.","requirements":["Unknown must be representable.","Missing inputs must be distinguishable from negative findings.","Policy consumers must know whether a result is definitive."],"constraints":["Some workflows may require fail-closed behavior.","Unknown states can increase manual review workload.","Evidence availability may vary by environment."],"prerequisites":["Policy evaluation model","Evidence availability model"],"dependencies":["Policy-as-Code","Conformance Evidence"],"alternatives":["Treat missing as compliant","Treat missing as violation","Explicit unknown state"],"evidence":"Absence of evidence does not establish conformance when the policy requires evidence to evaluate a condition.","tradeoffs":{"fail_closed_vs_availability":"Fail-closed handling increases assurance for critical policies but can block workflows when evidence systems are unavailable.","automation_vs_manual_review":"Unknown states require additional review for cases that cannot be automatically resolved."},"decision":"Represent insufficient evidence as an explicit unknown state and define policy-specific handling for unknown results.","rationale":"Automated governance must preserve uncertainty rather than converting missing information into unsupported compliance.","consequences":["False compliance decreases.","Manual review may increase for incomplete evidence.","Critical policies can define stronger fail-closed behavior where justified."],"implementation_implications":["Define result states.","Record missing evidence.","Set policy-specific unknown handling.","Avoid silently coercing unknown to pass."],"verification_method":"Remove required policy inputs and verify that evaluation returns the declared unknown state rather than compliance.","outcome_learning":"Measure unknown-result frequency and recurring evidence availability failures.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B47-R01","D55-B47-R04"],"relationships":["UNKNOWN_STATE","GOVERNANCE_EVIDENCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B47-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Automation","concept":"Governance Policy Versioning","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why must architecture governance policies be versioned?","answer":"Policy versioning preserves the exact rule set used to evaluate an architecture state or change. It allows historical results to remain interpretable when policies evolve and prevents current rules from being incorrectly applied to historical governance decisions.","context":"Architecture policies change as technologies, requirements, risks, and organizational standards evolve.","objective":"Preserve temporal correctness of automated and manual governance decisions.","requirements":["Policies require stable identifiers and versions.","Evaluation records must reference the applied version.","Policy changes must have traceable rationale."],"constraints":["Frequent policy changes increase version-management overhead.","Some policy changes are clarifications rather than semantic changes.","Historical re-evaluation may be useful but must be distinguishable from original evaluation."],"prerequisites":["Policy repository","Policy lifecycle","Governance evidence store"],"dependencies":["Architecture Standards","Policy-as-Code"],"alternatives":["Overwrite current policy","Version policies","Store only evaluation results"],"evidence":"Historical governance results cannot be interpreted reliably without knowing the policy semantics under which they were produced.","tradeoffs":{"historical_fidelity_vs_storage":"Versioning preserves evidence but requires additional lifecycle management.","policy simplicity_vs_traceability":"Single mutable rules are simpler operationally but weaken historical reproducibility."},"decision":"Version governance policies and record the exact applied version with each material evaluation.","rationale":"Governance evidence is time-dependent when policy definitions change.","consequences":["Historical evaluations remain interpretable.","Policy changes become auditable.","Policy storage and lifecycle complexity increase."],"implementation_implications":["Assign immutable policy versions.","Record evaluation references.","Maintain policy change rationale.","Support explicit re-evaluation under newer policy versions."],"verification_method":"Change a policy definition and verify that prior evaluations still reference the original policy version.","outcome_learning":"Analyze policy changes and re-evaluation results to identify unstable or ambiguous governance rules.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B47-R01","D55-B47-R05"],"relationships":["POLICY_VERSIONING","GOVERNANCE_PROVENANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B47-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Automation","concept":"Governance Policy Enforcement Point","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Where should automated architecture policies be enforced?","answer":"Policy enforcement should occur at lifecycle points where the relevant architectural evidence is available and where violations can still be acted upon, such as design review, dependency integration, build validation, deployment validation, or periodic conformance assessment.","context":"A correct policy applied at the wrong lifecycle point may detect violations too late to prevent expensive remediation.","objective":"Place governance controls at effective intervention points.","requirements":["The enforcement point must have required evidence.","Policy failure must have an actionable response.","Late-stage controls should not be the only mechanism when earlier prevention is practical."],"constraints":["Earlier enforcement may have incomplete information.","Multiple enforcement points may be required.","Some violations are only observable at runtime."],"prerequisites":["Policy definition","Lifecycle model","Evidence availability"],"dependencies":["Policy-as-Code","Architecture Lifecycle Checkpoints"],"alternatives":["Single final-stage enforcement","Early-only enforcement","Layered enforcement"],"evidence":"Controls are generally more actionable when evaluated near the point where relevant decisions or changes occur, while some properties require later evidence.","tradeoffs":{"early_detection_vs_evidence_completeness":"Earlier checks can prevent rework but may operate with incomplete information.","control_duplication_vs_defense":"Multiple checkpoints increase coverage but may duplicate evaluation."},"decision":"Use layered enforcement at lifecycle points appropriate to the evidence and intervention opportunity.","rationale":"Architecture governance needs both early prevention and later verification where required.","consequences":["Violations can be detected earlier where possible.","Runtime or deployment properties remain covered by later checks.","Policy placement becomes an explicit architecture decision."],"implementation_implications":["Map policies to lifecycle stages.","Define evidence prerequisites.","Define response behavior for each enforcement point.","Avoid treating one checkpoint as universal."],"verification_method":"Trace representative policies through lifecycle stages and verify that each is enforced at a point where evidence and corrective action are available.","outcome_learning":"Measure detection timing and remediation cost by enforcement stage.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B47-R02","D55-B47-R05"],"relationships":["ENFORCEMENT_POINT","LIFECYCLE_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B47-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B47","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Automation","concept":"Governance Automation Verification","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should automated architecture governance controls themselves be verified?","answer":"Governance automation should be tested against known compliant, violating, boundary, incomplete-evidence, and policy-version scenarios. The verification should establish what the automation actually detects rather than assuming that a rule's existence proves enforcement correctness.","context":"A faulty governance rule can systematically produce false compliance or false violations.","objective":"Verify the correctness and limitations of automated architecture governance.","requirements":["Rules require positive and negative test cases.","Boundary conditions must be tested.","Unknown-input behavior must be tested.","Policy version behavior must be tested where applicable."],"constraints":["Test cases may not represent every real architecture state.","Some semantic properties cannot be fully automated.","Rule changes require regression coverage."],"prerequisites":["Policy definitions","Test cases","Governance automation runtime"],"dependencies":["Policy Versioning","Policy Unknown State"],"alternatives":["No testing","Manual spot checks","Structured policy verification"],"evidence":"Governance automation is itself software or executable logic and therefore requires verification appropriate to its failure modes.","tradeoffs":{"test_coverage_vs_effort":"Broader policy testing increases confidence but requires maintenance.","automation_vs_review":"Automated tests improve repeatability while architectural review remains useful for semantic scope."},"decision":"Treat governance automation as governed implementation requiring versioned tests and explicit verification evidence.","rationale":"Incorrect automated governance can scale an error across many decisions or deployments.","consequences":["Policy regressions become detectable.","Automation limitations become documented.","Governance confidence is based on tested behavior."],"implementation_implications":["Maintain policy test suites.","Include negative and unknown cases.","Run regression checks after policy changes.","Record execution and verification status accurately."],"verification_method":"Execute policy test suites where infrastructure permits and verify expected classification across positive, negative, boundary, and unknown cases.","outcome_learning":"Use failed policy tests and false findings to improve policy definitions and implementation.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B47-R01","D55-B47-R03","D55-B47-R04"],"relationships":["POLICY_VERIFICATION","GOVERNANCE_TESTING"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B48-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Lifecycle","concept":"Architecture Governance Lifecycle Stage Gates","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why should architecture governance use lifecycle checkpoints rather than a single architecture approval?","answer":"Lifecycle checkpoints allow architecture governance to reassess decisions as requirements, evidence, implementation details, risks, and operational conditions become clearer. A single approval cannot reliably represent all later architectural states.","context":"Architecture decisions evolve from concept through implementation and operation.","objective":"Connect governance controls to meaningful architecture lifecycle transitions.","requirements":["Checkpoint purposes must be explicit.","Entry and exit evidence must be defined.","Later checkpoints must be able to reopen earlier assumptions when material changes occur."],"constraints":["Too many checkpoints increase process overhead.","Some projects require iterative rather than linear progression.","Approval at one stage must not be mistaken for verification of later states."],"prerequisites":["Architecture lifecycle","Decision records","Review criteria"],"dependencies":["Architecture Lifecycle Checkpoints","Architecture Change Control"],"alternatives":["Single approval","Fixed stage-gate process","Risk-proportional lifecycle checkpoints"],"evidence":"Architecture information and evidence become available progressively, making reassessment necessary at selected lifecycle transitions.","tradeoffs":{"control_vs_speed":"More checkpoints increase oversight but may slow delivery.","early_certainty_vs_evolution":"Early decisions must remain revisable when later evidence changes the context."},"decision":"Use risk-proportional architecture checkpoints with explicit entry, exit, and reassessment conditions.","rationale":"Architecture governance should follow the evolution of architectural knowledge rather than treating the initial design as permanently authoritative.","consequences":["Material changes can trigger reassessment.","Approval and implementation states remain distinct.","Governance can adapt to new evidence."],"implementation_implications":["Define lifecycle checkpoints.","Specify evidence expectations.","Define reopen triggers.","Track checkpoint decisions separately."],"verification_method":"Review architecture changes across lifecycle stages and verify that required checkpoints occurred and that material changes triggered reassessment.","outcome_learning":"Measure late-discovered architectural issues and identify which checkpoints could have surfaced them earlier.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B48-R02","D55-B48-R03"],"relationships":["LIFECYCLE_CHECKPOINT","ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B48-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Lifecycle","concept":"Architecture Reassessment Trigger","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should cause a previously approved architecture to return to governance review?","answer":"Reassessment should occur when material requirements change, assumptions become invalid, architecture boundaries change, significant dependencies are introduced or removed, risk materially changes, implementation diverges from the approved design, or operational evidence contradicts important architectural expectations.","context":"Architecture approval is conditional on the context and evidence available at the time of decision.","objective":"Prevent obsolete approvals from governing materially changed architecture.","requirements":["Reassessment triggers must be explicit.","Previous approval history must remain preserved.","New evidence must be evaluated against the affected decision."],"constraints":["Over-triggering creates unnecessary review.","Some changes are intentionally local and reversible.","Operational evidence may be ambiguous."],"prerequisites":["Approved architecture decision","Change monitoring","Risk model"],"dependencies":["Architecture Change Control","Architecture Risk Reassessment"],"alternatives":["Approval remains permanent","Periodic reassessment only","Event-driven reassessment"],"evidence":"Material context changes can invalidate the assumptions or constraints underlying an earlier architecture decision.","tradeoffs":{"stability_vs_adaptability":"Stable approvals reduce governance overhead while reassessment protects against obsolete assumptions.","sensitivity_vs_noise":"Lower trigger thresholds detect more changes but increase review noise."},"decision":"Use explicit material-change triggers to reopen architecture decisions when their governing context changes.","rationale":"Architecture approval should remain valid only while its material assumptions and boundaries remain applicable.","consequences":["Obsolete decisions become visible.","Architecture governance responds to evidence rather than calendar events alone.","Historical decisions remain preserved."],"implementation_implications":["Define reopening triggers.","Link triggers to affected decisions.","Record reassessment outcomes.","Distinguish reaffirmation from new architectural decisions."],"verification_method":"Apply known material changes to approved architecture records and verify that affected decisions become eligible for reassessment.","outcome_learning":"Track reassessment triggers that repeatedly identify meaningful decision changes.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B48-R01","D55-B48-R04"],"relationships":["REASSESSMENT_TRIGGER","DECISION_REVIEW"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B48-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Lifecycle","concept":"Architecture Gate Evidence Sufficiency","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should governance determine whether an architecture lifecycle checkpoint has sufficient evidence?","answer":"Evidence sufficiency should be defined relative to the decision being made: the checkpoint should have enough trustworthy information to evaluate applicable requirements, constraints, risks, alternatives, implementation implications, and unresolved uncertainty for that stage.","context":"Requiring every possible piece of evidence at every stage can prevent progress, while accepting insufficient evidence can create avoidable architectural risk.","objective":"Make lifecycle checkpoint evidence proportional to decision scope and uncertainty.","requirements":["Evidence expectations must be stage-specific.","Missing material evidence must be visible.","Unknowns must be distinguished from verified facts."],"constraints":["Evidence availability changes over time.","Some uncertainty is unavoidable during early architecture stages.","Evidence sufficiency is context-dependent."],"prerequisites":["Checkpoint definition","Decision scope","Evidence model"],"dependencies":["Evidence-Based Architecture Review","Architecture Review Criteria"],"alternatives":["Require complete evidence before every checkpoint","Allow arbitrary evidence sufficiency","Stage-appropriate evidence criteria"],"evidence":"The evidence needed to make a concept-level decision differs from the evidence needed to authorize production implementation.","tradeoffs":{"evidence_depth_vs_speed":"More evidence can improve decisions but may delay necessary exploration.","certainty_vs_experimentation":"Some decisions can reasonably proceed with bounded uncertainty when experiments are planned."},"decision":"Define evidence sufficiency according to checkpoint purpose, risk, and uncertainty rather than a universal evidence threshold.","rationale":"Governance should enable informed decisions at the appropriate lifecycle stage.","consequences":["Early-stage exploration remains possible.","High-risk decisions require stronger evidence.","Evidence gaps become explicit governance items."],"implementation_implications":["Define checkpoint evidence criteria.","Classify evidence confidence.","Record unresolved uncertainty and planned validation."],"verification_method":"Review checkpoint records across lifecycle stages and determine whether evidence expectations match the decision scope and risk.","outcome_learning":"Analyze checkpoint failures caused by insufficient evidence to improve stage-specific criteria.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B48-R01","D55-B48-R05"],"relationships":["EVIDENCE_SUFFICIENCY","LIFECYCLE_GATE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B48-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Lifecycle","concept":"Architecture Gate Rejection and Rework","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance handle a proposal that does not satisfy a lifecycle checkpoint?","answer":"The proposal should be returned with explicit findings, required evidence or design changes, responsible owners, and conditions for resubmission. Rejection should not erase the proposal's history or convert unresolved findings into silent architectural decisions.","context":"Lifecycle governance must distinguish rejection, deferral, and approval with conditions.","objective":"Make unsuccessful architecture reviews actionable and traceable.","requirements":["Reasons for non-approval must be explicit.","Required remediation or evidence must be identifiable.","Resubmission must preserve prior review history."],"constraints":["Some proposals require multiple iterations.","Review findings may be disputed.","A rejection may reflect timing or insufficient evidence rather than fundamentally incorrect architecture."],"prerequisites":["Checkpoint review","Finding model","Decision authority"],"dependencies":["Architecture Review Finding Disposition","Decision Traceability"],"alternatives":["Reject without explanation","Approve conditionally without tracking","Structured return-for-rework"],"evidence":"Explicit findings and resubmission conditions convert review failure into actionable architecture learning rather than process noise.","tradeoffs":{"review_detail_vs_speed":"Detailed findings improve rework quality but require reviewer effort.","flexibility_vs_control":"Conditional progression can preserve momentum while maintaining unresolved-risk visibility."},"decision":"Use explicit return-for-rework or deferral states with traceable findings and resubmission criteria.","rationale":"Architecture review should improve the proposal rather than merely produce a binary administrative outcome.","consequences":["Rejected or deferred proposals remain analyzable.","Rework becomes accountable.","Recurring design issues become measurable."],"implementation_implications":["Define review outcome states.","Link findings to resubmission conditions.","Preserve version history.","Prevent silent conversion of rejection into approval."],"verification_method":"Review non-approved proposals and verify that findings, ownership, and resubmission conditions are recorded.","outcome_learning":"Analyze recurring rejection causes to improve standards, guidance, and early architecture support.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B48-R03","D55-B48-R05"],"relationships":["GATE_REJECTION","ARCHITECTURE_REWORK"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B48-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B48","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Lifecycle","concept":"Architecture Gate Exit Verification","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should lifecycle checkpoint exit verification be separated from approval?","answer":"Approval authorizes progression based on available evidence, while exit verification establishes whether the conditions required for progression were actually satisfied. Separating the concepts prevents authorization from being interpreted as proof that implementation or evidence requirements were completed.","context":"Architecture governance often involves conditional approvals, planned evidence generation, and later implementation verification.","objective":"Maintain semantic separation between authorization and verified completion.","requirements":["Approval status and verification status must be distinct.","Exit criteria must be explicit.","Evidence supporting completion must be traceable."],"constraints":["Some evidence arrives asynchronously.","Certain checkpoints may permit conditional progression.","Verification may depend on downstream implementation."],"prerequisites":["Lifecycle checkpoint","Exit criteria","Verification model"],"dependencies":["Architecture Change Closure","Conformance Evidence"],"alternatives":["Approval equals completion","Approval with implicit verification","Separate approval and exit verification"],"evidence":"Authorization and verification answer different questions and should not be represented as a single state.","tradeoffs":{"process_simplicity_vs_truthfulness":"Combining statuses is simpler but can obscure incomplete conditions.","speed_vs_assurance":"Conditional progression can accelerate delivery while requiring explicit follow-up verification."},"decision":"Maintain separate approval, implementation, and verification states for architecture lifecycle checkpoints.","rationale":"Governance records must accurately represent what was authorized versus what was actually established.","consequences":["Incomplete evidence remains visible.","Audit reconstruction improves.","Conditional approvals become explicit rather than ambiguous."],"implementation_implications":["Define separate status fields.","Link verification evidence to exit criteria.","Prevent automatic conversion from approval to verified completion."],"verification_method":"Inspect checkpoint records with conditional approval and verify that approval does not automatically mark exit criteria as verified.","outcome_learning":"Track checkpoints where verification later fails or materially changes the original decision.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B48-R02","D55-B48-R04"],"relationships":["GATE_VERIFICATION","AUTHORIZATION_SEPARATION"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B49-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Resilience","concept":"Architecture Governance During Organizational Change","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance remain reliable during organizational restructuring?","answer":"Governance should preserve stable architecture identities, decisions, ownership responsibilities, and historical records while allowing current accountability mappings to change. Organizational restructuring should trigger ownership reassessment without rewriting historical decision provenance.","context":"Teams, reporting structures, and responsibilities can change while architecture decisions and systems continue to exist.","objective":"Preserve architecture governance continuity through organizational change.","requirements":["Historical ownership must remain attributable.","Current ownership must be updated.","Unowned architecture artifacts must be detectable.","Decision authority must be re-established after organizational changes."],"constraints":["Organizational mappings can change rapidly.","Historical records may contain outdated team names.","New ownership may initially be ambiguous."],"prerequisites":["Architecture ownership model","Stable architecture identifiers","Organizational change event"],"dependencies":["Architecture Knowledge Ownership","Decision Authority","Architecture Repository Integrity"],"alternatives":["Rewrite historical ownership","Freeze governance during restructuring","Preserve history and remap current ownership"],"evidence":"Separating historical provenance from current accountability preserves decision traceability while adapting governance to organizational reality.","tradeoffs":{"continuity_vs_reassignment":"Preserving history supports auditability while current ownership must remain actionable.","speed_vs_completeness":"Immediate reassignment may be difficult when organizational boundaries are still changing."},"decision":"Preserve historical ownership metadata and separately establish current accountable ownership after organizational changes.","rationale":"Governance continuity requires both historical truth and current accountability.","consequences":["Historical decisions remain reconstructable.","Current ownership gaps become visible.","Organizational changes trigger governance maintenance work."],"implementation_implications":["Version ownership mappings.","Detect orphaned architecture records.","Define temporary ownership where necessary.","Review decision authority after restructuring."],"verification_method":"Simulate or review organizational changes and verify preservation of historical attribution and establishment of current ownership.","outcome_learning":"Measure time to resolve orphaned architecture ownership after organizational changes.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B49-R02","D55-B49-R03"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_CONTINUITY","OWNERSHIP_REASSIGNMENT"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B49-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Resilience","concept":"Architecture Governance During Incident Response","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance operate during urgent incidents when normal review timelines are impractical?","answer":"Emergency architecture governance should permit predefined expedited decision paths while preserving minimum necessary authorization, risk awareness, traceability, and mandatory post-incident review. Emergency status should not silently convert into permanent architecture authority.","context":"Operational incidents can require immediate architectural changes before normal review processes can complete.","objective":"Balance urgent risk reduction with governance traceability.","requirements":["Emergency authority must be predefined.","The change and rationale must be recorded.","Post-incident review must be required for material emergency changes.","Temporary deviations must have explicit lifecycle status."],"constraints":["Incident response prioritizes rapid risk reduction.","Evidence may be incomplete during an incident.","Emergency changes can become permanent unintentionally."],"prerequisites":["Emergency change procedure","Decision authority","Incident governance"],"dependencies":["Emergency Architecture Changes","Architecture Change Closure","Governance Exceptions"],"alternatives":["Apply normal review regardless of urgency","Allow unrestricted emergency changes","Predefined emergency governance path"],"evidence":"Emergency conditions can justify expedited governance while still requiring accountability and later reconstruction.","tradeoffs":{"speed_vs_review":"Immediate action may reduce operational harm while reducing pre-change architectural analysis.","temporary_vs_permanent":"Emergency exceptions can become debt if not reviewed after stabilization."},"decision":"Use a predefined emergency architecture governance path with minimum controls and mandatory post-incident reconciliation.","rationale":"Urgency changes the timing and evidence available for governance but does not eliminate the need for accountability.","consequences":["Incident responders can act within defined authority.","Emergency changes remain traceable.","Post-incident architecture reconciliation becomes mandatory."],"implementation_implications":["Define emergency authority.","Record emergency rationale.","Mark emergency changes distinctly.","Require post-incident review and baseline reconciliation."],"verification_method":"Review emergency architecture changes and verify that emergency authority, change records, post-incident review, and final disposition are present.","outcome_learning":"Analyze emergency changes to identify recurring architectural weaknesses and opportunities for preventive redesign.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B49-R01","D55-B49-R04"],"relationships":["EMERGENCY_GOVERNANCE","INCIDENT_ARCHITECTURE_CHANGE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B49-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Resilience","concept":"Architecture Governance During Acquisition or Integration","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should architecture governance examine when integrating an acquired or externally developed system?","answer":"Governance should establish system boundaries, ownership, dependencies, contracts, security and trust boundaries, data responsibilities, operational assumptions, technology constraints, lifecycle status, risks, and integration obligations before treating the integrated architecture as governed current state.","context":"External systems may enter an organization with incomplete architectural knowledge or different governance assumptions.","objective":"Establish reliable architectural understanding during system integration.","requirements":["External architecture evidence must be assessed.","Ownership and boundaries must be explicit.","Material integration risks must be recorded.","Unknown architectural properties must remain identifiable."],"constraints":["Documentation may be incomplete.","Internal and external architecture standards may differ.","Integration timelines can be constrained."],"prerequisites":["Integration scope","System documentation","Ownership model"],"dependencies":["Third-Party Architecture Governance","Cross-System Dependency Governance"],"alternatives":["Integrate without architecture assessment","Full redesign before integration","Risk-based architecture integration assessment"],"evidence":"Integration changes dependencies and boundaries, so architecture governance requires explicit understanding of the resulting system context.","tradeoffs":{"integration_speed_vs_assurance":"Rapid integration can accelerate business goals while leaving architectural unknowns that require controlled follow-up.","standardization_vs_compatibility":"Immediate standardization may be impractical when integrating external systems."},"decision":"Perform risk-based architecture assessment before and during integration, explicitly tracking unknowns and accepted deviations.","rationale":"External architecture assumptions cannot safely be treated as equivalent to internally governed architecture without evidence.","consequences":["Integration risks become visible.","Unknown architecture areas receive explicit treatment.","Post-integration governance has a clearer baseline."],"implementation_implications":["Create integration architecture baseline.","Map external dependencies.","Record deviations and unknowns.","Define post-integration reassessment."],"verification_method":"Review integrated systems and verify that major boundaries, dependencies, ownership, risks, and deviations are represented in the governed architecture.","outcome_learning":"Track integration findings and recurring external architecture gaps to improve future integration controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B49-R01","D55-B49-R05"],"relationships":["ARCHITECTURE_INTEGRATION","EXTERNAL_SYSTEM_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B49-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Resilience","concept":"Architecture Governance Failure Recovery","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance recover when governance records or processes become incomplete after a major disruption?","answer":"Recovery should first establish the authoritative current system state and identify missing governance evidence, then reconstruct material decisions and changes from available sources, explicitly mark uncertainty, restore ownership and controls, and create corrective actions for governance gaps.","context":"Major incidents, migrations, reorganizations, or tooling failures can leave architecture governance records incomplete.","objective":"Restore trustworthy architecture governance without fabricating missing history.","requirements":["Known facts must be separated from reconstructed or uncertain information.","Current architecture state must be established.","Missing historical evidence must remain identified.","Recovered governance controls must be verified."],"constraints":["Historical evidence may be incomplete or contradictory.","Current system state may differ from documented state.","Reconstruction can require multiple evidence sources."],"prerequisites":["Available architecture artifacts","System state evidence","Governance ownership"],"dependencies":["Repository Integrity","Architecture Baselines","Evidence Quality"],"alternatives":["Recreate missing records without uncertainty","Ignore historical gaps","Evidence-based governance recovery"],"evidence":"Governance recovery requires explicit uncertainty because missing evidence cannot be safely replaced with assumptions presented as fact.","tradeoffs":{"reconstruction_depth_vs_speed":"More reconstruction improves history but delays restoration.","current_assurance_vs_historical_completeness":"Current-state assurance may be achievable before complete historical reconstruction."},"decision":"Restore governance using evidence-backed reconstruction with explicit uncertainty and separate current-state verification from historical recovery.","rationale":"Trustworthy recovery requires preserving the distinction between known, inferred, and missing information.","consequences":["Governance gaps become visible.","Current architecture can be governed even when historical records remain incomplete.","Future audits can distinguish original evidence from reconstruction."],"implementation_implications":["Classify recovered evidence.","Record provenance.","Re-establish ownership and baselines.","Create remediation for missing governance controls."],"verification_method":"Review recovered governance records and verify that uncertain or reconstructed information is explicitly marked and that current-state claims have supporting evidence.","outcome_learning":"Analyze the failure that caused governance loss and implement controls that reduce recurrence.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B49-R02","D55-B49-R05"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_RECOVERY","EVIDENCE_RECONSTRUCTION"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B49-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B49","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Resilience","concept":"Architecture Governance Continuity Planning","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should architecture governance continuity planning preserve during major organizational or technical disruption?","answer":"Continuity planning should preserve access to critical architecture decisions, current baselines, ownership and decision authority, governance standards, emergency procedures, dependency information, risk records, and sufficient evidence to reconstruct material changes.","context":"Architecture governance itself can become a dependency during large-scale operational or organizational disruption.","objective":"Ensure architecture governance remains usable when normal processes or systems are degraded.","requirements":["Critical governance knowledge must have continuity arrangements.","Emergency authority must remain identifiable.","Governance records must remain recoverable.","Continuity procedures require periodic validation."],"constraints":["Continuity mechanisms can become stale.","Not all governance information has equal criticality.","Access during disruption may be restricted."],"prerequisites":["Architecture criticality model","Governance repository","Continuity planning"],"dependencies":["Governance Resilience","Emergency Architecture Changes"],"alternatives":["No dedicated continuity planning","Backups only","Risk-based governance continuity"],"evidence":"Governance continuity depends on both information availability and the ability to make authorized decisions during disruption.","tradeoffs":{"continuity_cost_vs_resilience":"Additional continuity controls increase cost but reduce dependence on a single governance path.","availability_vs_security":"Emergency access must remain controlled rather than becoming unrestricted."},"decision":"Define risk-based continuity controls for critical architecture governance information, authority, and decision processes.","rationale":"Architecture governance cannot function if critical decisions and ownership become unavailable during disruption.","consequences":["Emergency architecture decisions have clearer authority.","Critical governance knowledge is recoverable.","Continuity mechanisms become part of governance assurance."],"implementation_implications":["Classify critical governance artifacts.","Maintain recoverable copies or alternate access paths.","Define emergency decision authority.","Test recovery procedures."],"verification_method":"Conduct controlled continuity exercises and verify that critical architecture decisions, ownership, standards, and emergency authority can be recovered and used.","outcome_learning":"Record recovery gaps and update continuity controls after exercises or actual disruptions.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B49-R01","D55-B49-R04"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_CONTINUITY","ARCHITECTURE_RESILIENCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B50-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Maturity","concept":"Architecture Governance Capability Assessment","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How can an organization assess its architecture governance capabilities without reducing governance quality to a single score?","answer":"Capability assessment should examine distinct dimensions such as decision traceability, ownership, standards, conformance, risk management, evidence quality, automation, lifecycle integration, exception handling, repository integrity, and learning mechanisms. Each dimension should be assessed against explicit evidence and limitations rather than collapsed into an unsupported overall rating.","context":"Organizations need to identify architecture governance gaps without confusing a maturity label with evidence of effectiveness.","objective":"Provide an evidence-based view of governance capabilities and gaps.","requirements":["Assessment dimensions must be explicit.","Evidence supporting each assessment must be identifiable.","Unknown or insufficient evidence must remain visible.","Capability assessment must distinguish process existence from effectiveness."],"constraints":["Capability models can become checklist exercises.","A capability can exist without producing reliable outcomes.","Different systems require different governance depth."],"prerequisites":["Governance model","Evidence repository","Architecture lifecycle information"],"dependencies":["Governance Metrics","Evidence Quality","Continuous Improvement"],"alternatives":["Single maturity score","Checklist compliance","Dimension-based evidence assessment"],"evidence":"A multidimensional evidence model provides more actionable information than reducing governance capability to one aggregate value.","tradeoffs":{"comparability_vs_context":"Standard dimensions improve comparison while context-specific interpretation remains necessary.","assessment_depth_vs_effort":"Detailed assessment produces better evidence but requires more work."},"decision":"Assess architecture governance capability by explicit dimensions with evidence, uncertainty, and scope rather than a single overall score.","rationale":"Architecture governance is multidimensional and a single rating can hide material weaknesses.","consequences":["Specific gaps become actionable.","Assessment evidence is preserved.","Different domains can have different capability profiles."],"implementation_implications":["Define capability dimensions.","Collect evidence per dimension.","Record scope and limitations.","Separate capability existence from outcome effectiveness."],"verification_method":"Perform a capability assessment and verify that every claimed capability has identifiable evidence and explicit limitations.","outcome_learning":"Use recurring capability gaps to prioritize governance improvements.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B50-R02","D55-B50-R03"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_CAPABILITY","EVIDENCE_ASSESSMENT"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B50-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Maturity","concept":"Architecture Governance Gap Analysis","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance gaps be prioritized after capability assessment?","answer":"Governance gaps should be prioritized according to affected architectural risk, business or operational consequence, dependency relationships, evidence quality, remediation feasibility, recurrence, and urgency. Priority should describe remediation need rather than functioning as a generic quality score.","context":"Capability assessments can identify many gaps, but not all gaps require the same response or timing.","objective":"Convert governance assessment evidence into actionable improvement work.","requirements":["Gap identity and evidence must be preserved.","Priority criteria must be explicit.","Material uncertainty must remain visible.","Ownership and next action must be defined for actionable gaps."],"constraints":["Priority can change as architecture context changes.","Some governance gaps are systemic rather than local.","High-effort remediation may require staged treatment."],"prerequisites":["Capability assessment","Architecture risk information","Governance ownership"],"dependencies":["Architecture Risk Governance","Governance Metrics","Continuous Improvement"],"alternatives":["Treat all gaps equally","Prioritize by management preference","Evidence- and risk-informed gap prioritization"],"evidence":"Risk and consequence provide stronger prioritization signals than gap count alone.","tradeoffs":{"risk_vs_effort":"High-risk gaps may require action despite high remediation cost.","short_term_vs_systemic":"Local fixes may provide immediate improvement while systemic causes require longer-term work."},"decision":"Prioritize governance gaps using explicit risk, consequence, dependency, evidence, and feasibility criteria.","rationale":"Governance improvement requires allocation of limited attention and resources to material weaknesses.","consequences":["Improvement work becomes traceable.","High-impact systemic gaps become visible.","Priority can be reassessed as evidence changes."],"implementation_implications":["Record gap evidence.","Define prioritization criteria.","Assign owners and target conditions.","Track remediation status separately from assessment status."],"verification_method":"Review prioritized gaps and verify that priority is supported by documented evidence and criteria rather than unexplained preference.","outcome_learning":"Compare predicted gap significance with observed outcomes to refine prioritization criteria.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B50-R01","D55-B50-R04"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_GAP_ANALYSIS","IMPROVEMENT_PRIORITIZATION"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B50-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Maturity","concept":"Architecture Governance Improvement Experiment","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should an architecture governance improvement be evaluated before broad adoption?","answer":"A governance improvement should define the problem, intervention, expected mechanism, measurable outcomes, scope, duration, risks, comparison approach where feasible, and criteria for adoption, modification, or rejection. The experiment should distinguish observed evidence from interpretation.","context":"Governance processes themselves can introduce unintended costs or fail to address the intended architectural problem.","objective":"Use controlled learning to improve governance mechanisms.","requirements":["The improvement hypothesis must be explicit.","Outcome measures must be defined.","Scope and limitations must be documented.","Adoption decisions must be evidence-backed."],"constraints":["Organizational experiments may lack controlled environments.","Governance changes can affect multiple variables simultaneously.","Some outcomes require long observation periods."],"prerequisites":["Identified governance problem","Baseline evidence","Improvement owner"],"dependencies":["Governance Metrics","Continuous Improvement","Decision Evidence Quality"],"alternatives":["Immediate organization-wide adoption","Pilot or controlled governance experiment","No experimentation"],"evidence":"Pilot evaluation can reveal whether a governance change improves the intended outcome and what secondary effects it introduces.","tradeoffs":{"learning_vs_rollout_speed":"Experiments delay broad rollout but reduce uncertainty.","measurement_vs_organizational_cost":"Detailed evaluation increases evidence quality but requires coordination."},"decision":"Use bounded governance experiments for material process changes when uncertainty or potential side effects justify validation.","rationale":"Governance mechanisms are themselves system interventions and should be evaluated based on evidence where practical.","consequences":["Unsuccessful governance changes can be rejected before broad adoption.","Successful interventions gain supporting evidence.","Experimental scope and limitations remain explicit."],"implementation_implications":["Define experiment records.","Capture baseline and follow-up measures.","Record confounders and limitations.","Document adoption decision and rationale."],"verification_method":"Review completed governance experiments and verify that hypotheses, measures, observations, limitations, and adoption decisions are traceable.","outcome_learning":"Feed experiment results into standards, policies, metrics, and future governance decisions.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B50-R01","D55-B50-R05"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_EXPERIMENT","CONTINUOUS_LEARNING"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B50-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Maturity","concept":"Architecture Governance Learning Loop","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should a complete architecture governance learning loop contain?","answer":"A complete learning loop connects governance decisions and controls to implementation evidence, conformance findings, operational outcomes, incidents, exceptions, risk changes, review findings, and subsequent policy or architecture changes. The loop should preserve provenance and distinguish observation from interpretation.","context":"Governance improves when actual architecture outcomes feed back into future decisions and controls.","objective":"Establish continuous evidence-driven learning from architecture governance activity.","requirements":["Governance outcomes must be captured.","Feedback must connect to prior decisions or controls.","Changes resulting from learning must be traceable.","Uncertainty and contradictory evidence must remain visible."],"constraints":["Causal attribution may be difficult.","Operational feedback may arrive long after architectural decisions.","Not every event produces actionable learning."],"prerequisites":["Governance evidence repository","Architecture decision history","Outcome metrics"],"dependencies":["Governance Metrics","Architecture Risk Reassessment","Continuous Improvement"],"alternatives":["Periodic governance review","Event-driven learning","Continuous evidence-driven governance loop"],"evidence":"Connecting decisions to subsequent evidence enables architecture governance to learn from actual outcomes rather than relying only on design-time assumptions.","tradeoffs":{"learning_depth_vs_overhead":"Detailed feedback collection improves learning but requires governance instrumentation.","speed_vs_confidence":"Rapid adaptation can use preliminary evidence while stronger conclusions require additional validation."},"decision":"Maintain an explicit feedback loop from governed architecture decisions through implementation and outcomes back into future governance controls.","rationale":"Architecture governance should adapt based on observed evidence and not remain fixed after initial approval.","consequences":["Governance controls can evolve based on evidence.","Recurring architectural failures can inform standards and decisions.","Historical relationships become increasingly valuable for learning."],"implementation_implications":["Link outcomes to decisions and controls.","Record evidence provenance.","Capture lessons with confidence and limitations.","Trigger policy or architecture reassessment when material learning emerges."],"verification_method":"Trace selected architecture decisions through implementation, outcomes, lessons, and subsequent governance changes to verify a complete feedback chain.","outcome_learning":"Use verified outcomes and lessons to refine architecture standards, policies, review criteria, and risk controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B50-R03","D55-B50-R05"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_LEARNING_LOOP","ARCHITECTURE_FEEDBACK"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B50-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B50","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance Maturity","concept":"Architecture Governance Closure and Reassessment","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"When can an architecture governance improvement cycle be considered complete?","answer":"A governance improvement cycle can be closed when the identified problem has a documented disposition, the intended intervention has been implemented or intentionally abandoned, relevant outcomes have been evaluated to the extent practical, residual limitations are recorded, and conditions for future reassessment are defined.","context":"Governance improvement should not be considered complete merely because a new process or policy was introduced.","objective":"Separate governance implementation from verified learning and sustainable closure.","requirements":["Problem disposition must be explicit.","Implementation status must be distinct from outcome verification.","Residual limitations must remain visible.","Future reassessment triggers should be defined where appropriate."],"constraints":["Some governance outcomes require long-term observation.","Causal certainty may remain limited.","Improvement can be continuous rather than permanently final."],"prerequisites":["Governance improvement initiative","Outcome evidence","Governance ownership"],"dependencies":["Governance Learning Loop","Governance Metrics","Architecture Reassessment"],"alternatives":["Close after implementation","Close after management approval","Close after implementation and outcome assessment"],"evidence":"Implementation establishes that a governance change occurred, while outcome assessment determines what can actually be learned from it.","tradeoffs":{"closure_speed_vs_learning":"Early closure reduces administrative effort but can miss delayed effects.","certainty_vs_continuity":"Some improvements require ongoing monitoring rather than definitive closure."},"decision":"Close governance improvement cycles only after implementation status, available outcome evidence, limitations, and future reassessment conditions are explicitly recorded.","rationale":"Governance improvement should preserve the distinction between deploying a control and establishing that the control produced the intended effect.","consequences":["Learning evidence remains connected to governance changes.","Incomplete outcomes are not represented as proven success.","Future reassessment remains possible."],"implementation_implications":["Separate implementation and outcome statuses.","Record evidence and limitations.","Define follow-up triggers.","Preserve historical improvement records."],"verification_method":"Audit completed governance improvement cycles and verify that implementation, outcome evidence, limitations, and reassessment conditions are separately represented.","outcome_learning":"Feed verified lessons into future governance standards, policies, architecture reviews, and risk controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B50-R03","D55-B50-R04"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_CLOSURE","CONTINUOUS_REASSESSMENT"]}

{"record_id":"D55-B51-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Architecture Quality Gates","subtopic":"Design Validation Gates","concept":"Architecture quality gates should prevent designs from progressing when mandatory architectural constraints have not been demonstrated.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What is the purpose of an architecture quality gate?","answer":"An architecture quality gate is a defined governance checkpoint that evaluates whether required architectural properties, constraints, risks, and evidence have been satisfied before a system proceeds to a controlled lifecycle stage.","context":"Architecture governance requires repeatable controls rather than relying only on informal design reviews.","objective":"Prevent materially nonconforming or insufficiently evidenced architecture decisions from advancing.","requirements":["Define mandatory evaluation criteria","Specify required evidence","Identify approval authority","Record gate outcomes"],"constraints":["Criteria must be relevant to the system","Gates must not become unnecessary bureaucracy","Evidence must be auditable"],"prerequisites":["Defined architecture principles","Applicable standards","Known system scope"],"dependencies":["Architecture governance policy","Architecture review process"],"alternatives":["Informal peer review","Post-implementation review"],"evidence":["Architecture requirements","Risk assessments","Design documentation","Verification results"],"tradeoffs":["Stronger control can increase review effort","Automated gates improve repeatability but require maintainable rules"],"decision":"Use explicit architecture quality gates for lifecycle stages where architectural risk or organizational policy requires controlled progression.","rationale":"Explicit gates make architectural compliance observable and reviewable.","consequences":["More predictable governance","Potential delivery delay when evidence is incomplete","Clearer escalation paths"],"implementation_implications":["Represent gate criteria explicitly","Persist gate evidence","Record pass, fail, waiver, and pending states","Integrate applicable checks into delivery workflows"],"verification_method":"Review gate definitions against applicable architecture policies and test representative compliant and noncompliant cases.","outcome_learning":"Gate effectiveness should be measured using escaped violations, false-positive governance findings, review latency, and waiver frequency.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B51-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Architecture Quality Gates","subtopic":"Security Gate Criteria","concept":"Security-sensitive architecture changes require explicit security criteria rather than generic architectural approval.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should security requirements be incorporated into architecture quality gates?","answer":"Security-sensitive gates should evaluate applicable trust boundaries, data exposure, identity assumptions, authorization controls, threat scenarios, security requirements, and evidence that required controls are addressed.","context":"A design may conform structurally while still introducing material security exposure.","objective":"Ensure security architecture concerns are evaluated before risky designs progress.","requirements":["Identify security-sensitive changes","Define required security evidence","Establish escalation criteria","Record unresolved security risks"],"constraints":["Security criteria must be proportionate to risk","Governance must not imply that a review guarantees security"],"prerequisites":["Security requirements","Threat model or equivalent risk analysis"],"dependencies":["Security governance","Architecture review"],"alternatives":["Separate security review","Embedded security review"],"evidence":["Threat analysis","Trust-boundary documentation","Security control mapping"],"tradeoffs":["Earlier security review increases design effort but can reduce expensive late remediation"],"decision":"Integrate risk-based security criteria into architecture gates.","rationale":"Security constraints are architectural concerns and should be evaluated before irreversible implementation decisions.","consequences":["Earlier identification of architectural security risks","Additional evidence requirements"],"implementation_implications":["Create risk-based security gate rules","Link findings to architecture decisions","Require explicit treatment of accepted residual risks"],"verification_method":"Evaluate sample architecture changes against defined security gate criteria and verify that high-risk omissions trigger escalation.","outcome_learning":"Track security findings discovered before implementation versus after deployment to assess gate effectiveness.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B51-R01"],"relationships":["QUALITY_GATE_SECURITY_EXTENSION"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B51-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Architecture Quality Gates","subtopic":"Performance Gate Criteria","concept":"Performance-sensitive architectures should have measurable acceptance criteria tied to expected workload and service objectives.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should performance become an architecture quality gate?","answer":"Performance should become a formal architecture gate when latency, throughput, resource consumption, capacity, or scalability constraints materially affect system requirements, cost, or reliability.","context":"Architecture decisions can create performance characteristics that are difficult or expensive to change later.","objective":"Prevent architectural choices that violate material performance requirements.","requirements":["Define measurable performance objectives","Specify representative workload assumptions","Identify acceptable evidence"],"constraints":["Performance results depend on workload and environment","Synthetic measurements must not be presented as universal guarantees"],"prerequisites":["Performance requirements","Workload model"],"dependencies":["Capacity planning","Performance testing"],"alternatives":["Design-time analysis","Prototype benchmarking","Production measurement"],"evidence":["Benchmarks","Capacity models","Load-test results","Resource estimates"],"tradeoffs":["Early benchmarking costs time but can expose unsuitable designs before implementation"],"decision":"Apply performance gates when performance is a material architectural constraint.","rationale":"Explicit criteria prevent subjective claims from substituting for measurable requirements.","consequences":["Performance assumptions become traceable","Additional validation may be required"],"implementation_implications":["Store workload assumptions with gate evidence","Define thresholds appropriate to requirements","Revalidate when workload assumptions materially change"],"verification_method":"Compare architecture evidence against declared performance objectives under representative conditions.","outcome_learning":"Monitor requirement satisfaction, benchmark reproducibility, and performance regressions after deployment.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B51-R01"],"relationships":["QUALITY_GATE_PERFORMANCE_EXTENSION"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B51-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Architecture Quality Gates","subtopic":"Gate Evidence Completeness","concept":"A gate decision should distinguish absence of evidence from evidence that demonstrates nonconformance.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should missing evidence and failed evidence be represented separately?","answer":"Missing evidence means the required property has not been demonstrated, while failed evidence indicates that available evidence contradicts the required criterion. Treating them as identical can obscure remediation and risk decisions.","context":"Governance systems frequently lose useful diagnostic information when every unsuccessful gate is represented as a generic failure.","objective":"Preserve the reason for a gate outcome.","requirements":["Represent evidence state explicitly","Distinguish missing, failed, passed, waived, and not-applicable states"],"constraints":["Not-applicable must require justification where appropriate"],"prerequisites":["Defined gate criteria"],"dependencies":["Governance evidence repository"],"alternatives":["Binary pass/fail"],"evidence":["Evidence records","Gate evaluation results"],"tradeoffs":["Richer states increase model complexity but improve governance diagnosis"],"decision":"Use multi-state gate outcomes instead of a binary-only representation.","rationale":"Different failure causes require different governance responses.","consequences":["Better remediation traceability","More detailed reporting"],"implementation_implications":["Define controlled outcome states","Persist evidence references","Prevent silent conversion of missing evidence into pass"],"verification_method":"Test gate evaluation using passed, failed, missing, waived, and not-applicable evidence scenarios.","outcome_learning":"Analyze recurring missing-evidence patterns separately from actual architectural nonconformance.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B51-R01"],"relationships":["GATE_OUTCOME_STATE_MODEL"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B51-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B51","topic":"Architecture Quality Gates","subtopic":"Gate Override Governance","concept":"Quality gate overrides require explicit authority, rationale, scope, duration, and risk ownership.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture quality gate overrides be governed?","answer":"A gate override should be treated as a controlled exception containing the affected gate, reason, risk assessment, approving authority, scope, expiration or review condition, and required compensating actions where applicable.","context":"Operational urgency can make temporary exceptions necessary, but uncontrolled overrides can silently disable architecture governance.","objective":"Allow controlled exceptions without converting them into permanent bypasses.","requirements":["Record override rationale","Identify authority","Define scope and duration","Track compensating controls"],"constraints":["Overrides must not erase the original gate result","Emergency conditions may require accelerated review"],"prerequisites":["Gate definitions","Exception policy"],"dependencies":["Governance exception management"],"alternatives":["No override","Permanent gate modification"],"evidence":["Override record","Risk assessment","Approval evidence"],"tradeoffs":["Controlled overrides preserve flexibility while adding governance overhead"],"decision":"Use explicit, auditable override records rather than undocumented gate bypasses.","rationale":"Traceability preserves governance integrity while supporting exceptional circumstances.","consequences":["Improved auditability","Additional administrative work"],"implementation_implications":["Link overrides to gate instances","Require expiration or reassessment","Prevent indefinite undocumented bypasses"],"verification_method":"Audit override records for authority, rationale, scope, expiration, and subsequent closure or reassessment.","outcome_learning":"Review recurring overrides to identify obsolete gate criteria or systemic planning problems.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B51-R04"],"relationships":["GATE_OVERRIDE_TO_EXCEPTION"]}

{"record_id":"D55-B52-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"Governance in CI/CD","subtopic":"Automated Architecture Checks","concept":"Architecture governance can be embedded into delivery pipelines through deterministic checks for machine-verifiable constraints.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_AUTOMATION","question":"What architecture governance controls are suitable for CI/CD automation?","answer":"Suitable controls include dependency restrictions, prohibited technology checks, interface compatibility checks, required metadata validation, infrastructure policy checks, security configuration checks, and architectural boundary validation when the rules are objectively testable.","context":"Automating deterministic governance controls reduces reliance on manual detection.","objective":"Detect preventable architecture violations earlier in the delivery lifecycle.","requirements":["Rules must be machine-evaluable","Failures must be explainable","Results must be traceable"],"constraints":["Automation cannot replace context-sensitive architectural judgment","Rules must remain version-controlled"],"prerequisites":["Defined architecture policies","Automated delivery pipeline"],"dependencies":["Policy-as-code","Repository metadata","Build and test systems"],"alternatives":["Manual architecture review"],"evidence":["Pipeline results","Policy definitions","Violation records"],"tradeoffs":["Automation improves repeatability but requires rule maintenance"],"decision":"Automate deterministic architecture governance checks within delivery workflows.","rationale":"Machine-verifiable constraints are more consistently enforced when evaluated automatically.","consequences":["Earlier feedback","Potential pipeline friction from inaccurate rules"],"implementation_implications":["Version governance rules","Expose actionable failure messages","Track policy versions with results"],"verification_method":"Test rules against compliant and intentionally violating repository configurations.","outcome_learning":"Measure escaped violations, false positives, remediation time, and rule maintenance frequency.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B52-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"Governance in CI/CD","subtopic":"Policy Versioning","concept":"Automated governance results must be interpreted against the exact policy version that produced them.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_AUTOMATION","question":"Why should architecture policy versions be recorded with CI/CD results?","answer":"Policy versions establish which governance rules were applied at evaluation time and prevent historical results from being misinterpreted after rules change.","context":"Architecture policies evolve as requirements, technology, and organizational standards change.","objective":"Maintain reproducibility and historical interpretability of automated governance decisions.","requirements":["Assign stable policy versions","Record versions with evaluation results","Preserve historical policy definitions"],"constraints":["Version identifiers must be immutable"],"prerequisites":["Version-controlled governance rules"],"dependencies":["CI/CD pipeline","Governance repository"],"alternatives":["Use only current policy definitions"],"evidence":["Policy revision history","Pipeline evaluation records"],"tradeoffs":["Version retention requires storage and lifecycle management"],"decision":"Bind automated governance results to immutable policy versions.","rationale":"Historical governance evidence requires knowing exactly which rules were evaluated.","consequences":["Improved auditability","More explicit policy lifecycle management"],"implementation_implications":["Include policy version in result records","Retain superseded definitions according to evidence-retention requirements"],"verification_method":"Reproduce historical evaluations using their recorded policy versions.","outcome_learning":"Analyze policy changes against changes in violation rates and remediation patterns.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B52-R01"],"relationships":["POLICY_VERSION_TO_EVIDENCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B52-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"Governance in CI/CD","subtopic":"Blocking Versus Advisory Controls","concept":"Architecture policies should distinguish violations that must block progression from findings intended only to inform engineers.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_AUTOMATION","question":"Why should CI/CD architecture controls distinguish blocking and advisory findings?","answer":"Not every architectural concern has the same risk or remediation urgency. Blocking controls should be reserved for mandatory constraints, while advisory findings can provide guidance without preventing delivery.","context":"Treating every governance finding as blocking can cause teams to bypass governance entirely.","objective":"Align enforcement strength with architectural risk and policy authority.","requirements":["Classify control severity","Define blocking criteria","Provide remediation guidance"],"constraints":["Severity must be governed rather than arbitrary"],"prerequisites":["Architecture policy taxonomy"],"dependencies":["Pipeline enforcement mechanism"],"alternatives":["All-blocking model","All-advisory model"],"evidence":["Policy classification","Risk analysis"],"tradeoffs":["Strict blocking increases enforcement but can increase delivery friction"],"decision":"Use risk- and policy-based enforcement levels.","rationale":"Governance effectiveness depends on proportional enforcement.","consequences":["Clearer developer feedback","More governance classification work"],"implementation_implications":["Represent severity explicitly","Require authority for changing blocking status"],"verification_method":"Evaluate controls against policy classification and verify that only designated mandatory violations block progression.","outcome_learning":"Monitor bypass behavior, recurring violations, and false-positive blocking events.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B52-R01"],"relationships":["ENFORCEMENT_SEVERITY_MODEL"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B52-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"Governance in CI/CD","subtopic":"Governance Feedback","concept":"Automated governance findings should provide actionable remediation context rather than only a failure indicator.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_AUTOMATION","question":"What information should an automated architecture governance failure provide?","answer":"A useful finding should identify the violated rule, affected artifact, applicable policy version, severity, evidence, expected condition, detected condition, remediation guidance, and escalation path when applicable.","context":"Developers need enough information to understand and correct governance failures without reconstructing the entire evaluation.","objective":"Reduce governance remediation time and ambiguity.","requirements":["Provide deterministic finding identifiers","Reference policy and evidence","Describe expected and observed conditions"],"constraints":["Guidance must not claim certainty beyond the rule evidence"],"prerequisites":["Structured governance rules"],"dependencies":["CI/CD reporting"],"alternatives":["Generic failure messages","Manual investigation"],"evidence":["Policy evaluation output","Affected artifact"],"tradeoffs":["Detailed findings require richer rule metadata"],"decision":"Make automated governance findings structured and actionable.","rationale":"Actionable feedback improves adoption and reduces repeated violations.","consequences":["Better remediation workflow","More metadata required per rule"],"implementation_implications":["Define structured finding schema","Link findings to source artifacts and policy versions"],"verification_method":"Inject known violations and verify that generated findings contain the required diagnostic fields.","outcome_learning":"Measure mean remediation time and recurrence of identical findings.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B52-R02"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_FINDING_FEEDBACK"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B52-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B52","topic":"Governance in CI/CD","subtopic":"Governance Drift Detection","concept":"CI/CD governance should detect divergence between declared architecture policies and the controls actually enforced by delivery systems.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_AUTOMATION","question":"What is governance enforcement drift?","answer":"Governance enforcement drift occurs when the policies considered authoritative differ from the rules, versions, configurations, or pipeline stages actually used to evaluate systems.","context":"Governance can become ineffective even when policy documents remain correct if enforcement mechanisms diverge from them.","objective":"Detect discrepancies between governance intent and automated enforcement.","requirements":["Define authoritative policy source","Track enforcement versions","Compare declared and deployed controls"],"constraints":["Not all governance policies can be fully automated"],"prerequisites":["Policy inventory","Enforcement inventory"],"dependencies":["CI/CD configuration","Policy repository"],"alternatives":["Periodic manual audit"],"evidence":["Policy definitions","Pipeline configurations","Evaluation logs"],"tradeoffs":["Continuous drift detection requires additional metadata and monitoring"],"decision":"Treat enforcement configuration as governed architecture evidence.","rationale":"Policy correctness alone does not demonstrate effective enforcement.","consequences":["Greater governance visibility","Additional configuration auditing"],"implementation_implications":["Record active rule versions","Detect disabled or stale controls","Escalate unexplained differences"],"verification_method":"Compare authoritative policy versions with active pipeline enforcement configurations.","outcome_learning":"Track drift incidents and time-to-correction.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B52-R01","D55-B52-R02"],"relationships":["POLICY_TO_ENFORCEMENT_DRIFT"]}

{"record_id":"D55-B53-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Architecture Conformance in Delivery","subtopic":"ADR-to-Implementation Traceability","concept":"Architecture decisions should remain traceable to implementation evidence where conformance is governed.","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"How can implementation be checked against an architecture decision?","answer":"Implementation conformance can be assessed by linking the applicable architecture decision to enforceable constraints, affected components, verification evidence, and documented deviations.","context":"An approved decision does not by itself prove that implementation follows the decision.","objective":"Maintain traceability from architectural intent to implementation evidence.","requirements":["Identify affected implementation units","Define enforceable consequences","Record verification evidence"],"constraints":["Some architectural decisions are not directly machine-verifiable"],"prerequisites":["Architecture decision records","Implementation scope"],"dependencies":["Repository metadata","Verification systems"],"alternatives":["Manual conformance review"],"evidence":["Code configuration","Tests","Deployment configuration","Review results"],"tradeoffs":["Traceability requires metadata discipline"],"decision":"Link material architecture decisions to implementation and verification evidence.","rationale":"Governance must distinguish approved intent from demonstrated conformance.","consequences":["Improved traceability","Additional lifecycle metadata"],"implementation_implications":["Assign stable decision identifiers","Reference them from affected artifacts or verification records"],"verification_method":"Trace selected decisions forward to implementation evidence and identify unverified consequences.","outcome_learning":"Measure decisions lacking implementation evidence and recurring conformance deviations.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B53-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Architecture Conformance in Delivery","subtopic":"Conformance Evidence Freshness","concept":"Architecture conformance evidence can become stale when implementation or governing assumptions change.","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"Why should architecture conformance evidence have freshness criteria?","answer":"Evidence should be reassessed when relevant implementation, dependencies, requirements, configuration, or architectural assumptions materially change because previous evidence may no longer represent the current system.","context":"A previously valid conformance result is not necessarily permanent.","objective":"Prevent stale evidence from being treated as current proof.","requirements":["Define evidence validity conditions","Identify material change triggers","Record assessment timestamps or versions"],"constraints":["Not every change requires full architectural reassessment"],"prerequisites":["Conformance evidence model"],"dependencies":["Change management","Architecture repository"],"alternatives":["Periodic reassessment","Event-triggered reassessment"],"evidence":["Change records","Version identifiers","Conformance results"],"tradeoffs":["Frequent reassessment improves currency but increases governance cost"],"decision":"Use change-triggered freshness rules supplemented by periodic review where appropriate.","rationale":"Evidence validity should reflect the system state it actually covers.","consequences":["Reduced stale evidence","Need for change-impact classification"],"implementation_implications":["Associate evidence with versions or states","Define invalidation triggers"],"verification_method":"Introduce controlled changes and verify that affected evidence is marked for reassessment.","outcome_learning":"Track stale-evidence incidents and unnecessary reassessment frequency.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B53-R01"],"relationships":["CONFORMANCE_EVIDENCE_FRESHNESS"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B53-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Architecture Conformance in Delivery","subtopic":"Deviation Detection","concept":"Conformance governance should identify intentional and unintentional deviations separately.","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"Why must intentional architecture deviations be distinguished from accidental nonconformance?","answer":"Intentional deviations may have approved rationale and governance authority, while accidental deviations represent uncontrolled divergence. Combining them obscures risk and weakens exception accountability.","context":"Real systems can diverge from architecture decisions for legitimate reasons or through implementation drift.","objective":"Preserve the distinction between governed exceptions and uncontrolled deviations.","requirements":["Link intentional deviations to approved exceptions","Detect unexplained divergence","Track deviation lifecycle"],"constraints":["Approval must not be inferred merely from documentation"],"prerequisites":["Exception management"],"dependencies":["Conformance checking","Architecture decisions"],"alternatives":["Single deviation category"],"evidence":["Approved exception","Implementation evidence","Conformance finding"],"tradeoffs":["More precise classification increases governance complexity"],"decision":"Represent approved exceptions and unexplained deviations as separate governance states.","rationale":"Different states require different actions and accountability.","consequences":["Better risk visibility","More explicit exception records"],"implementation_implications":["Require exception identifiers for intentional deviations","Escalate unexplained findings"],"verification_method":"Test conformance workflows with approved, expired, and undocumented deviations.","outcome_learning":"Review recurring deviations for architectural decision quality or implementation process problems.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B53-R01"],"relationships":["CONFORMANCE_TO_EXCEPTION"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B53-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Architecture Conformance in Delivery","subtopic":"Conformance Scope","concept":"Architecture conformance checks must define their scope explicitly to avoid false claims of complete architectural validation.","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"Why must the scope of an architecture conformance assessment be explicit?","answer":"A conformance assessment may evaluate only selected components, constraints, environments, or properties. Explicit scope prevents a partial check from being interpreted as complete architectural validation.","context":"Architectures contain properties that may not be machine-verifiable or may fall outside a specific review.","objective":"Make conformance claims proportional to evaluated evidence.","requirements":["Define components covered","Define rules evaluated","Identify excluded areas"],"constraints":["Scope must be precise enough to interpret results"],"prerequisites":["Architecture model","Conformance rules"],"dependencies":["Evidence repository"],"alternatives":["Unscoped conformance declaration"],"evidence":["Evaluation scope","Rule results"],"tradeoffs":["Detailed scope documentation requires additional effort"],"decision":"Require explicit scope for every material conformance assessment.","rationale":"Evidence supports only the claims within its evaluated boundary.","consequences":["More accurate governance reporting","Reduced ambiguity"],"implementation_implications":["Store scope identifiers with results","Prevent generic full-conformance labels unless justified"],"verification_method":"Review assessment records and verify that claims do not exceed evaluated scope.","outcome_learning":"Track findings caused by misunderstood conformance scope.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B53-R01"],"relationships":["CONFORMANCE_SCOPE_CONTROL"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B53-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B53","topic":"Architecture Conformance in Delivery","subtopic":"Conformance Closure","concept":"A conformance finding should remain open until remediation or an explicitly governed disposition is demonstrated.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should an architecture conformance finding be considered closed?","answer":"A finding should be closed only when corrective evidence demonstrates resolution, an approved exception governs the remaining deviation, or another explicitly authorized disposition has been recorded.","context":"Closing findings based only on acknowledgement does not demonstrate architectural resolution.","objective":"Prevent unresolved architecture violations from disappearing administratively.","requirements":["Define closure criteria","Require evidence or approved disposition","Preserve historical finding state"],"constraints":["Closure criteria should be proportionate to finding severity"],"prerequisites":["Finding lifecycle"],"dependencies":["Exception governance","Verification evidence"],"alternatives":["Manual acknowledgement closure"],"evidence":["Remediation verification","Approved exception","Reassessment result"],"tradeoffs":["Evidence-based closure requires more effort but improves integrity"],"decision":"Use evidence-based closure for material architecture conformance findings.","rationale":"Governance closure should represent a verified state, not merely administrative completion.","consequences":["More reliable governance records","Potentially longer open-finding periods"],"implementation_implications":["Separate acknowledgement from closure","Require closure evidence","Track reopened findings"],"verification_method":"Attempt closure without evidence and verify that governance prevents invalid closure.","outcome_learning":"Analyze reopened findings and aging by severity.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B53-R03","D55-B53-R04"],"relationships":["CONFORMANCE_CLOSURE_CONTROL"]}

{"record_id":"D55-B54-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Architecture Governance Observability","subtopic":"Governance Telemetry","concept":"Architecture governance requires observable lifecycle events to determine whether controls are operating as intended.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_OBSERVABILITY","question":"What should architecture governance telemetry capture?","answer":"Governance telemetry should capture review requests, evaluations, findings, approvals, exceptions, escalations, evidence changes, policy versions, and closure events where such records are necessary for governance assurance.","context":"Governance without observable lifecycle activity is difficult to audit or improve.","objective":"Make governance operation measurable without conflating telemetry with business or system runtime observability.","requirements":["Capture material governance events","Preserve event ordering where required","Associate events with governed artifacts"],"constraints":["Do not collect unnecessary sensitive information","Retention must follow applicable policy"],"prerequisites":["Governance event model"],"dependencies":["Architecture repository","Review system"],"alternatives":["Periodic manual reports"],"evidence":["Governance event records"],"tradeoffs":["More telemetry improves visibility but increases storage and data-management requirements"],"decision":"Capture structured governance lifecycle events for material controls.","rationale":"Observable governance enables auditing, diagnosis, and continuous improvement.","consequences":["Improved governance metrics","Additional event storage"],"implementation_implications":["Define event taxonomy","Use stable identifiers","Record policy and artifact versions"],"verification_method":"Trace representative governance workflows through their expected lifecycle events.","outcome_learning":"Identify missing events, unexplained state transitions, and governance bottlenecks.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B54-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Architecture Governance Observability","subtopic":"Governance Latency","concept":"Governance latency measures the time required for architectural review and decision processes and can reveal process bottlenecks.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_METRIC","question":"Why should architecture governance latency be measured?","answer":"Measuring governance latency helps identify bottlenecks in review, evidence collection, approval, escalation, and exception handling without treating shorter duration as universally better.","context":"Governance must remain sufficiently rigorous while avoiding avoidable delays.","objective":"Understand where governance processes consume time.","requirements":["Define lifecycle start and end events","Measure relevant stages separately","Interpret latency with risk and complexity context"],"constraints":["Latency metrics must not encourage bypassing necessary controls"],"prerequisites":["Governance event telemetry"],"dependencies":["Review workflow"],"alternatives":["Aggregate cycle time only"],"evidence":["Timestamped governance events"],"tradeoffs":["Detailed metrics improve diagnosis but require more instrumentation"],"decision":"Measure governance latency by lifecycle stage and context.","rationale":"Stage-level metrics reveal actionable bottlenecks better than a single aggregate duration.","consequences":["Improved process diagnosis","Additional telemetry requirements"],"implementation_implications":["Capture timestamps consistently","Segment by review type and complexity"],"verification_method":"Validate that lifecycle timestamps produce consistent stage-duration calculations.","outcome_learning":"Use trends to identify unnecessary queues, repeated review loops, and evidence bottlenecks.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B54-R01"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_LATENCY_METRIC"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B54-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Architecture Governance Observability","subtopic":"Governance Failure Monitoring","concept":"Governance systems themselves require monitoring for unavailable, stale, or malfunctioning controls.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_OBSERVABILITY","question":"Why should architecture governance mechanisms be monitored as systems?","answer":"A governance control that fails silently, becomes unavailable, evaluates stale policies, or stops producing evidence can create false assurance even when the architecture itself is unchanged.","context":"Governance mechanisms are operational processes and technical systems with failure modes.","objective":"Detect failures in governance enforcement and evidence production.","requirements":["Monitor control availability","Detect stale evaluations","Detect missing expected events"],"constraints":["Monitoring must distinguish control inactivity from legitimate absence of governed changes"],"prerequisites":["Governance telemetry"],"dependencies":["Policy enforcement systems"],"alternatives":["Manual periodic audit"],"evidence":["Control execution logs","Expected-event models"],"tradeoffs":["Monitoring adds complexity but protects against silent governance failure"],"decision":"Monitor material governance controls for operational health and evidence continuity.","rationale":"A broken control cannot provide reliable governance assurance.","consequences":["Earlier detection of governance failures","Additional monitoring requirements"],"implementation_implications":["Define control health indicators","Alert on unexplained absence or stale execution"],"verification_method":"Simulate control failure and verify detection without falsely reporting architectural nonconformance.","outcome_learning":"Track governance control outages and duration of unobserved failures.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B54-R01"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_CONTROL_HEALTH"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B54-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Architecture Governance Observability","subtopic":"Governance Audit Trail Integrity","concept":"Governance audit records must preserve enough integrity and context to support reconstruction of material governance decisions.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What makes a governance audit trail useful?","answer":"A useful audit trail preserves actor or authority context where applicable, timestamps, affected artifacts, policy versions, evidence references, decisions, exceptions, and relevant state transitions in a tamper-evident or otherwise controlled repository.","context":"Governance reconstruction requires more than final approval status.","objective":"Enable reliable reconstruction of material governance actions.","requirements":["Preserve causal relationships","Protect records from unauthorized modification","Maintain contextual identifiers"],"constraints":["Audit data may contain sensitive information","Retention must be governed"],"prerequisites":["Governance event model"],"dependencies":["Access control","Evidence repository"],"alternatives":["Final-state records only"],"evidence":["Audit records","Integrity controls"],"tradeoffs":["Detailed immutable evidence increases storage and management requirements"],"decision":"Maintain structured audit trails for material governance actions.","rationale":"Final-state records alone may not explain how or why a governance state was reached.","consequences":["Improved auditability","Greater retention requirements"],"implementation_implications":["Use stable identifiers","Protect historical records","Record policy and evidence references"],"verification_method":"Reconstruct selected governance decisions from stored events and evidence.","outcome_learning":"Measure reconstruction failures and missing-context incidents.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B54-R01"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_AUDIT_TRAIL"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B54-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B54","topic":"Architecture Governance Observability","subtopic":"Governance Signal Quality","concept":"Governance metrics require quality controls because incomplete or biased evidence can produce misleading conclusions.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_METRIC","question":"Why must governance metrics include evidence-quality considerations?","answer":"A metric based on missing events, inconsistent classifications, duplicate findings, or incomplete scope can create a misleading picture of governance performance. Metric interpretation should therefore include data-quality status and scope.","context":"Governance analytics depend on the quality of underlying lifecycle records.","objective":"Prevent unreliable governance measurements from driving inappropriate process changes.","requirements":["Track metric input completeness","Detect duplicate or inconsistent records","State measurement scope"],"constraints":["Metrics should not be treated as absolute indicators without context"],"prerequisites":["Structured governance telemetry"],"dependencies":["Evidence repository"],"alternatives":["Use raw counts without quality assessment"],"evidence":["Metric input records","Data-quality checks"],"tradeoffs":["Quality checks add processing effort but improve interpretability"],"decision":"Attach evidence-quality and scope metadata to material governance metrics.","rationale":"Measurement quality is part of governance assurance.","consequences":["More defensible analytics","More complex metric pipelines"],"implementation_implications":["Validate input completeness","Track metric provenance","Expose uncertainty or limitations"],"verification_method":"Inject missing and duplicate governance events and verify that metric quality indicators detect the problem.","outcome_learning":"Track recurring data-quality defects in governance telemetry.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B54-R01","D55-B54-R04"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_METRIC_DATA_QUALITY"]}

{"record_id":"D55-B55-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Security Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Security Architecture Approval","concept":"Security-sensitive architectural decisions require explicit ownership of security risk and evidence of applicable security analysis.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should security architecture governance establish?","answer":"Security architecture governance should establish when security review is required, which evidence is expected, who owns security risk decisions, how unresolved risks are escalated, and how approved exceptions are tracked.","context":"Security architecture concerns can affect trust boundaries, data exposure, identity, authorization, resilience, and system composition.","objective":"Make security-related architectural accountability explicit.","requirements":["Define security review triggers","Assign risk ownership","Require appropriate evidence","Track exceptions"],"constraints":["Security governance does not guarantee absence of vulnerabilities"],"prerequisites":["Security requirements","Architecture governance framework"],"dependencies":["Security review process","Risk management"],"alternatives":["Ad hoc security review"],"evidence":["Threat analysis","Security architecture review","Risk acceptance"],"tradeoffs":["Formal review increases control but requires specialist capacity"],"decision":"Use risk-based security architecture governance integrated with general architecture governance.","rationale":"Security concerns are architectural and should be governed before material commitments become difficult to change.","consequences":["Clearer accountability","Potential review overhead"],"implementation_implications":["Define review triggers","Link findings to architecture decisions","Preserve risk acceptance evidence"],"verification_method":"Review representative architecture changes and verify correct security governance routing.","outcome_learning":"Measure security findings discovered at architecture stage versus later stages.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B55-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Security Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Trust Boundary Governance","concept":"Architectural trust boundaries should be explicitly identified when security decisions depend on interactions across differing trust assumptions.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should trust boundaries be governed architecturally?","answer":"Trust boundaries identify locations where assumptions about identity, authorization, data handling, or component trust change. Governing them helps ensure that security controls are evaluated where those assumptions change.","context":"Distributed systems frequently contain multiple security domains and communication boundaries.","objective":"Prevent implicit trust assumptions from becoming unmanaged architectural dependencies.","requirements":["Identify relevant trust boundaries","Document trust assumptions","Map applicable controls"],"constraints":["Trust models are context-dependent"],"prerequisites":["System context","Security requirements"],"dependencies":["Threat analysis"],"alternatives":["Component-level security review only"],"evidence":["Trust-boundary model","Security controls"],"tradeoffs":["Explicit modeling requires design effort but improves reasoning about security boundaries"],"decision":"Require explicit trust-boundary treatment for architectures where security domains differ.","rationale":"Security controls depend on the assumptions crossing boundaries.","consequences":["Improved security traceability","Additional architecture documentation"],"implementation_implications":["Represent trust boundaries in architecture artifacts","Associate controls and verification evidence"],"verification_method":"Review architecture diagrams and security evidence for identified boundaries and corresponding controls.","outcome_learning":"Track security findings associated with undocumented or misunderstood trust boundaries.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B55-R01"],"relationships":["SECURITY_BOUNDARY_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B55-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Security Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Least-Privilege Architecture","concept":"Architecture governance should challenge unnecessary privilege dependencies and require explicit justification for privileged interactions.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How can architecture governance support least privilege?","answer":"Governance can require identification of privileged components and interactions, justification for elevated authority, separation of duties where appropriate, and verification that privileges are constrained to necessary scope.","context":"Architectural privilege dependencies can expand the impact of compromised or misused components.","objective":"Reduce unnecessary architectural privilege concentration.","requirements":["Identify privileged operations","Document authority boundaries","Require justification"],"constraints":["Privilege requirements depend on system function"],"prerequisites":["Security model","System interaction model"],"dependencies":["Identity and authorization architecture"],"alternatives":["Generic security review"],"evidence":["Authorization model","Privilege analysis"],"tradeoffs":["Fine-grained privilege can increase design and operational complexity"],"decision":"Govern material privilege dependencies as explicit architectural concerns.","rationale":"Privilege is an architectural dependency when component authority affects system risk.","consequences":["Better visibility of authority concentration","Potential complexity in authorization design"],"implementation_implications":["Track privileged boundaries","Verify intended scope","Review privilege changes"],"verification_method":"Evaluate representative privileged interactions against documented architectural authority requirements.","outcome_learning":"Monitor recurring excessive-privilege findings and exception frequency.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B55-R02"],"relationships":["ARCHITECTURAL_LEAST_PRIVILEGE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B55-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Security Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Security Exception Governance","concept":"Security architecture exceptions require explicit residual-risk ownership and review conditions.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What distinguishes a governed security architecture exception from an undocumented security gap?","answer":"A governed exception identifies the affected control or requirement, residual risk, scope, authority, rationale, duration or reassessment condition, and any compensating measures. An undocumented gap lacks this governance evidence.","context":"Security requirements may occasionally conflict with delivery constraints or technical limitations.","objective":"Ensure deviations from security architecture requirements remain visible and accountable.","requirements":["Document residual risk","Identify risk owner","Define scope and review condition"],"constraints":["Exception approval does not prove that the residual risk is eliminated"],"prerequisites":["Security requirements","Exception policy"],"dependencies":["Risk management"],"alternatives":["Immediate remediation","Architecture redesign"],"evidence":["Exception record","Risk assessment","Compensating controls"],"tradeoffs":["Exceptions provide flexibility but create explicit residual risk"],"decision":"Govern security architecture deviations through explicit exception records.","rationale":"Controlled risk acceptance is materially different from untracked nonconformance.","consequences":["Improved visibility of residual risk","Ongoing review obligations"],"implementation_implications":["Link exceptions to controls and architecture decisions","Require expiration or reassessment"],"verification_method":"Audit security exceptions for authority, residual-risk evidence, scope, and current status.","outcome_learning":"Review repeated exceptions to identify structural architecture or policy issues.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B55-R01"],"relationships":["SECURITY_EXCEPTION_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B55-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B55","topic":"Security Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Security Architecture Reassessment","concept":"Security architecture governance should trigger reassessment when material changes alter threat exposure, trust assumptions, data handling, or security controls.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What changes should trigger security architecture reassessment?","answer":"Triggers can include material trust-boundary changes, new sensitive-data flows, major identity or authorization changes, significant external exposure, critical dependency changes, or changes to relevant security requirements.","context":"Security properties can change even when the overall system architecture appears similar.","objective":"Keep security governance aligned with material architectural changes.","requirements":["Define reassessment triggers","Evaluate change impact","Preserve previous security evidence"],"constraints":["Triggers should be risk-based rather than every-change rules"],"prerequisites":["Security architecture baseline"],"dependencies":["Change management","Architecture repository"],"alternatives":["Fixed periodic review"],"evidence":["Change records","Security impact analysis"],"tradeoffs":["Event-driven reassessment is responsive but requires reliable change classification"],"decision":"Use risk-based architectural change triggers for security reassessment.","rationale":"Material security assumptions can change independently of general architecture labels.","consequences":["Earlier detection of security-impacting changes","Need for change-impact analysis"],"implementation_implications":["Define trigger taxonomy","Link changes to affected security decisions"],"verification_method":"Simulate representative security-impacting changes and verify appropriate reassessment routing.","outcome_learning":"Track missed triggers and unnecessary reassessments.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B55-R01","D55-B55-R04"],"relationships":["SECURITY_REASSESSMENT_TRIGGER"]}

{"record_id":"D55-B56-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Data Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Data Ownership Controls","concept":"Architecture governance should make ownership of material data domains explicit because ownership affects access, lifecycle, quality, and change authority.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why is data ownership an architecture governance concern?","answer":"Data ownership influences who defines data semantics, approves access, governs lifecycle decisions, resolves quality disputes, and authorizes material schema or integration changes.","context":"Data responsibilities often cross application and organizational boundaries.","objective":"Prevent ambiguous authority over critical data domains.","requirements":["Identify accountable owners","Define decision scope","Map data domains to systems and interfaces"],"constraints":["Ownership may be distributed by domain or responsibility"],"prerequisites":["Data domain model"],"dependencies":["Data governance","Architecture repository"],"alternatives":["Application-centric ownership"],"evidence":["Ownership records","Data domain definitions"],"tradeoffs":["Explicit ownership improves accountability but may require organizational alignment"],"decision":"Represent material data ownership as an architectural governance relationship.","rationale":"Data architecture decisions require clear authority and accountability.","consequences":["Clearer change governance","Reduced ownership ambiguity"],"implementation_implications":["Link data domains to accountable roles","Record ownership changes","Use ownership in change routing"],"verification_method":"Review critical data domains and verify that accountable ownership is defined.","outcome_learning":"Track ownership-related escalations and unresolved data decisions.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B56-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Data Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Data Classification","concept":"Architecture governance should use data classification to determine which architectural controls apply to data flows and storage.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How does data classification affect architecture governance?","answer":"Data classification can determine applicable controls for storage, transmission, access, retention, replication, integration, and architectural exposure. Governance should link classifications to enforceable requirements rather than relying on labels alone.","context":"Different data categories can carry materially different architectural constraints.","objective":"Translate data classification into architecture requirements.","requirements":["Define classification taxonomy","Map classifications to controls","Apply classifications consistently"],"constraints":["Classification schemes must be contextually defined"],"prerequisites":["Data governance policy"],"dependencies":["Data flow architecture","Security architecture"],"alternatives":["Uniform controls for all data"],"evidence":["Data classification records","Control mappings"],"tradeoffs":["More precise controls increase governance complexity"],"decision":"Use risk- and policy-based data classification as an input to architecture controls.","rationale":"Architectural requirements should reflect the properties and sensitivity of governed data.","consequences":["More targeted controls","Additional classification maintenance"],"implementation_implications":["Propagate classification metadata where appropriate","Evaluate architecture changes against applicable data controls"],"verification_method":"Test representative data flows with different classifications and verify correct governance requirements.","outcome_learning":"Track misclassification findings and control exceptions.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B56-R01"],"relationships":["DATA_CLASSIFICATION_TO_ARCHITECTURE_CONTROL"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B56-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Data Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Data Lineage Governance","concept":"Material data lineage should be governed where architectural decisions depend on data origin, transformation, ownership, or downstream use.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should data lineage become an architecture governance requirement?","answer":"Data lineage should be governed when traceability of data origin, transformation, movement, or downstream dependency is material to security, compliance, correctness, operational risk, or architectural decision-making.","context":"Distributed systems can obscure how data moves and changes across components.","objective":"Preserve sufficient lineage for material architectural dependencies.","requirements":["Identify governed lineage scope","Record important transformations and boundaries","Maintain lineage evidence"],"constraints":["Full lineage may be impractical for every data element"],"prerequisites":["Data flow model"],"dependencies":["Integration architecture","Data governance"],"alternatives":["Point-in-time documentation"],"evidence":["Data flow maps","Transformation metadata","Interface definitions"],"tradeoffs":["Detailed lineage improves traceability but adds maintenance effort"],"decision":"Require lineage proportionate to architectural and data risk.","rationale":"Governance should focus lineage effort where loss of traceability creates material consequences.","consequences":["Better impact analysis","Additional metadata requirements"],"implementation_implications":["Define lineage criticality levels","Link lineage to systems and interfaces"],"verification_method":"Trace selected material data flows from origin through governed transformations and destinations.","outcome_learning":"Track lineage gaps discovered during architecture changes and incidents.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B56-R02"],"relationships":["DATA_LINEAGE_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B56-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Data Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Data Retention Architecture","concept":"Retention architecture should be governed where storage lifecycle affects requirements, cost, privacy, security, or operational recovery.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should data retention be considered during architecture governance?","answer":"Retention choices affect storage duration, deletion behavior, backup lifecycle, replication, recovery design, privacy obligations, operational cost, and historical availability. Material retention requirements should therefore influence architecture decisions.","context":"Retention is not merely an operational storage setting when it affects system behavior and obligations.","objective":"Ensure architecture decisions account for material data lifecycle requirements.","requirements":["Define retention requirements","Consider primary and secondary copies","Address deletion and recovery interactions"],"constraints":["Retention requirements vary by data class and context"],"prerequisites":["Data classification","Data lifecycle policy"],"dependencies":["Storage architecture","Backup architecture"],"alternatives":["Operational-only retention configuration"],"evidence":["Retention policy","Lifecycle design","Deletion verification"],"tradeoffs":["Longer retention can improve historical availability but increase storage and governance burden"],"decision":"Treat material retention requirements as architectural constraints.","rationale":"Retention influences multiple system components and lifecycle behaviors.","consequences":["More explicit lifecycle design","Additional validation requirements"],"implementation_implications":["Model retention across primary, replica, backup, and archival paths where relevant","Verify deletion behavior"],"verification_method":"Trace retention requirements through storage and recovery architecture.","outcome_learning":"Track retention-related exceptions and failed lifecycle assumptions.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B56-R02"],"relationships":["DATA_RETENTION_ARCHITECTURE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B56-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B56","topic":"Data Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Data Contract Governance","concept":"Data contracts should define governed expectations between producers and consumers when incompatible data changes can create architectural failures.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What role do data contracts play in architecture governance?","answer":"Data contracts make important expectations about schema, semantics, compatibility, ownership, and change behavior explicit between data producers and consumers.","context":"Distributed data dependencies can fail when assumptions are implicit or changed without coordination.","objective":"Govern changes to shared data interfaces through explicit architectural contracts.","requirements":["Define contract ownership","Specify compatibility expectations","Establish change procedures"],"constraints":["Contracts should cover material guarantees rather than every implementation detail"],"prerequisites":["Shared data interface"],"dependencies":["Schema governance","Integration governance"],"alternatives":["Informal schema coordination"],"evidence":["Contract definitions","Compatibility checks","Consumer impact analysis"],"tradeoffs":["Explicit contracts increase coordination effort but reduce ambiguity"],"decision":"Use governed data contracts for material shared data interfaces.","rationale":"Explicit contracts provide a stable governance boundary between independently changing components.","consequences":["Improved compatibility management","Additional contract maintenance"],"implementation_implications":["Version contracts","Automate compatible-change checks where possible","Link changes to affected consumers"],"verification_method":"Test representative compatible and incompatible contract changes.","outcome_learning":"Track contract violations and consumer-impact incidents.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","