{"record_id":"D55-B57-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Cloud and Hybrid Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Deployment Model Governance","concept":"Cloud, on-premises, and hybrid deployment choices should be governed by explicit architectural requirements rather than platform preference alone.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should govern cloud versus on-premises architectural decisions?","answer":"Deployment-model decisions should consider requirements, security constraints, data handling, latency, resilience, integration dependencies, operational capabilities, cost assumptions, portability needs, and lifecycle implications.","context":"Deployment models create architectural consequences beyond infrastructure hosting location.","objective":"Ensure deployment choices are justified against system requirements and constraints.","requirements":["Document decision drivers","Identify material constraints","Record tradeoffs"],"constraints":["Cost and capability estimates are context-dependent"],"prerequisites":["System requirements","Deployment constraints"],"dependencies":["Security architecture","Operations architecture"],"alternatives":["Cloud-first","On-premises-first","Hybrid"],"evidence":["Requirements analysis","Risk assessment","Capacity and cost assumptions"],"tradeoffs":["Different deployment models optimize different constraints"],"decision":"Govern deployment model selection through explicit architectural evidence.","rationale":"Hosting preference alone is insufficient to establish architectural suitability.","consequences":["More traceable deployment decisions","Need for explicit assumptions"],"implementation_implications":["Record deployment constraints and dependencies","Review decisions when assumptions materially change"],"verification_method":"Evaluate deployment decision records against stated requirements and constraints.","outcome_learning":"Reassess decisions when actual operating characteristics diverge materially from assumptions.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B57-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Cloud and Hybrid Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Cloud Dependency Governance","concept":"Material dependencies on platform-specific capabilities should be explicitly governed because they can constrain portability and future architecture changes.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should cloud-specific dependencies be governed?","answer":"Platform-specific services can provide valuable capabilities while creating coupling through APIs, data formats, identity models, operational tooling, or proprietary behavior. Material dependencies should therefore be identified and accepted deliberately.","context":"Cloud architectures often combine portable components with platform-specific services.","objective":"Make architectural coupling visible and intentional.","requirements":["Identify platform-specific dependencies","Document rationale","Assess lifecycle and exit implications"],"constraints":["Portability is not always a requirement"],"prerequisites":["Technology architecture"],"dependencies":["Technology lifecycle governance"],"alternatives":["Avoid all platform-specific services","Use platform-native capabilities"],"evidence":["Dependency inventory","Architecture decision","Portability analysis"],"tradeoffs":["Native capabilities can improve functionality or operations while increasing coupling"],"decision":"Govern material platform-specific dependencies without treating portability as universally mandatory.","rationale":"The relevant issue is informed architectural coupling, not avoidance of all vendor-specific technology.","consequences":["Clearer dependency awareness","Potential additional lifecycle analysis"],"implementation_implications":["Record critical platform dependencies","Identify migration-sensitive interfaces and data"],"verification_method":"Review architecture inventory for material platform-specific dependencies and associated decisions.","outcome_learning":"Track dependencies that later constrain required architecture changes.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B57-R01"],"relationships":["CLOUD_DEPENDENCY_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B57-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Cloud and Hybrid Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Hybrid Boundary Governance","concept":"Hybrid architectures require explicit governance of boundaries between environments because connectivity, identity, data movement, and failure behavior cross infrastructure domains.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should be governed at hybrid architecture boundaries?","answer":"Hybrid boundaries should address network connectivity, identity and authorization, data movement, latency assumptions, failure modes, operational ownership, monitoring, and dependency behavior across environments.","context":"Hybrid systems have architectural interactions that span distinct infrastructure and operational domains.","objective":"Prevent unmanaged assumptions at environment boundaries.","requirements":["Identify boundary dependencies","Assign ownership","Document failure and security assumptions"],"constraints":["Boundary controls depend on the specific deployment architecture"],"prerequisites":["Hybrid architecture model"],"dependencies":["Security architecture","Network architecture","Operations governance"],"alternatives":["Treat environments as independent systems"],"evidence":["Connectivity architecture","Identity model","Data-flow model"],"tradeoffs":["Explicit boundary governance increases design effort but improves cross-environment reliability"],"decision":"Treat hybrid boundaries as first-class governance objects.","rationale":"Cross-environment interactions can become critical failure and security points.","consequences":["Improved boundary traceability","Additional ownership coordination"],"implementation_implications":["Define boundary contracts","Associate monitoring and incident responsibilities"],"verification_method":"Review hybrid boundary scenarios for connectivity, identity, data, and failure assumptions.","outcome_learning":"Analyze incidents and changes that cross deployment boundaries.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B57-R01","D55-B57-R02"],"relationships":["HYBRID_BOUNDARY_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B57-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Cloud and Hybrid Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Cloud Resilience Assumptions","concept":"Cloud deployment does not automatically establish resilience; architecture governance should verify the actual failure assumptions and recovery mechanisms.","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"Why should cloud resilience assumptions be explicitly governed?","answer":"Cloud infrastructure can still experience component, dependency, configuration, identity, connectivity, regional, or service-level failures. Governance should evaluate the resilience mechanisms relevant to declared requirements rather than assuming the hosting model provides them automatically.","context":"Resilience is an architectural property dependent on system design and failure assumptions.","objective":"Prevent unsupported resilience claims based solely on deployment model.","requirements":["Define failure scenarios","Map recovery mechanisms","Verify against resilience requirements"],"constraints":["Failure scenarios are system-specific"],"prerequisites":["Resilience requirements","Failure model"],"dependencies":["Deployment architecture","Recovery architecture"],"alternatives":["Provider-dependent resilience assumption"],"evidence":["Failure analysis","Recovery tests","Architecture design"],"tradeoffs":["Higher resilience can require redundancy and operational complexity"],"decision":"Govern resilience from explicit failure and recovery requirements rather than hosting assumptions.","rationale":"Resilience depends on architecture and verified behavior, not deployment labels.","consequences":["More precise resilience claims","Additional validation work"],"implementation_implications":["Document failure domains","Test material recovery paths"],"verification_method":"Evaluate declared resilience objectives against representative failure scenarios and recovery evidence.","outcome_learning":"Update architecture assumptions from observed recovery behavior.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B57-R03"],"relationships":["CLOUD_RESILIENCE_GOVERNANCE"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B57-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B57","topic":"Cloud and Hybrid Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Cloud Architecture Review Triggers","concept":"Material cloud architecture changes should trigger governance reassessment when they alter dependencies, exposure, data handling, resilience, or operational ownership.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Which cloud changes should trigger architecture governance reassessment?","answer":"Triggers may include major platform changes, new managed services, significant network exposure changes, identity-model changes, material data-location changes, altered resilience topology, or changes in operational ownership.","context":"Cloud environments can change rapidly through infrastructure and managed-service adoption.","objective":"Keep architecture governance aligned with material cloud changes.","requirements":["Define material-change triggers","Assess architectural impact","Preserve prior decision context"],"constraints":["Routine low-impact configuration changes need not trigger full architecture review"],"prerequisites":["Cloud architecture baseline"],"dependencies":["Change management","Technology lifecycle governance"],"alternatives":["Periodic review only"],"evidence":["Change records","Architecture impact assessment"],"tradeoffs":["Frequent review improves currency but can create unnecessary governance load if triggers are too broad"],"decision":"Use risk-based change triggers for cloud architecture reassessment.","rationale":"Rapid platform evolution makes static architecture approval insufficient for material changes.","consequences":["Improved architectural currency","Need for effective change classification"],"implementation_implications":["Define cloud-specific trigger taxonomy","Link changes to affected architecture decisions"],"verification_method":"Test representative cloud changes and verify correct review routing.","outcome_learning":"Track missed and unnecessary reassessment triggers.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B57-R02","D55-B57-R03"],"relationships":["CLOUD_REVIEW_TRIGGER"]}

