{"record_id":"D55-B58-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Multi-Team Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Cross-Team Dependency Decisions","concept":"Architecture decisions affecting multiple teams should explicitly capture downstream dependencies and required coordination.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should cross-team architecture decisions identify affected dependencies?","answer":"A decision made by one team can impose interface, deployment, data, operational, or lifecycle constraints on other teams. Explicit dependency identification allows affected parties to evaluate and govern those consequences.","context":"Distributed ownership does not eliminate architectural coupling.","objective":"Prevent local architecture decisions from creating unmanaged cross-team consequences.","requirements":["Identify affected teams","Map dependency types","Record required coordination"],"constraints":["Dependency scope should be proportionate to materiality"],"prerequisites":["System dependency map"],"dependencies":["Architecture decision repository"],"alternatives":["Local decision without dependency analysis"],"evidence":["Dependency records","Impact analysis"],"tradeoffs":["Broader impact analysis increases decision effort but reduces surprise dependencies"],"decision":"Require impact analysis for material cross-team architecture decisions.","rationale":"Architectural responsibility follows consequences, not only organizational ownership.","consequences":["Improved coordination","Potentially longer decision preparation"],"implementation_implications":["Link decisions to dependent systems and teams","Notify affected owners when required"],"verification_method":"Trace a cross-team decision to affected dependencies and verify required coordination evidence.","outcome_learning":"Track dependency surprises and post-decision escalations.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"R