{"record_id":"D55-B58-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B58","topic":"Multi-Team Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Federated Governance","concept":"Federated architecture governance requires common minimum standards combined with domain-level decision authority.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"When is federated architecture governance useful?","answer":"Federated governance is useful when multiple domains require local decision autonomy while still needing common architectural principles, interfaces, security requirements, or enterprise constraints.","context":"Large systems can make fully centralized architectural decision-making impractical.","objective":"Balance local autonomy with system-wide architectural coherence.","requirements":["Define non-negotiable enterprise constraints","Define domain decision scope","Provide escalation paths"],"constraints":["Federation can create inconsistent interpretations without clear boundaries"],"prerequisites":["Enterprise architecture principles","Domain boundaries"],"dependencies":["Governance roles","Decision authority"],"alternatives":["Centralized governance"],"evidence":["Governance charter","Decision boundaries","Escalation records"],"tradeoffs":["Federation improves responsiveness but requires stronger boundary definitions"],"decision":"Use federated governance where domain autonomy is necessary and common constraints can be explicitly defined.","rationale":"Governance structure should reflect architectural and organizational boundaries.","consequences":["Greater domain autonomy","Need for cross-domain consistency mechanisms"],"implementation_implications":["Define minimum common controls","Document delegated authority","Review cross-domain conflicts"],"verification_method":"Evaluate decisions from multiple domains against common constraints and delegated authority boundaries.","outcome_learning":"Track cross-domain conflicts and repeated policy interpretation differences.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B58-R01","D55-B58-R03"],"relationships":["FEDERATED_ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE"]}

{"record_id":"D55-B59-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Architecture Governance Change Management","subtopic":"Governance Policy Change","concept":"Changes to architecture governance policies should themselves be governed because policy changes alter the constraints applied to future architecture decisions.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should architecture governance policies have a controlled change process?","answer":"Governance policies define constraints, approval authorities, evidence requirements, and enforcement behavior. Uncontrolled changes can create inconsistent decisions and make historical governance results difficult to interpret.","context":"Governance rules are themselves architectural control mechanisms.","objective":"Ensure policy changes are deliberate, traceable, and versioned.","requirements":["Record policy rationale","Review impact","Version policy changes","Define effective date"],"constraints":["Emergency policy changes may require accelerated handling"],"prerequisites":["Governance policy repository"],"dependencies":["Policy versioning","Architecture review"],"alternatives":["Uncontrolled policy editing"],"evidence":["Policy change record","Impact analysis","Approval"],"tradeoffs":["Controlled changes increase process overhead but preserve governance consistency"],"decision":"Apply lifecycle governance to architecture governance policies.","rationale":"Governance rules require the same traceability principles they impose on architecture decisions.","consequences":["Improved policy consistency","More explicit policy lifecycle"],"implementation_implications":["Use immutable versions","Record effective periods","Link evaluations to policy versions"],"verification_method":"Trace a policy change from proposal through approval and subsequent enforcement.","outcome_learning":"Assess whether policy changes reduce recurring violations or introduce unintended ambiguity.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B59-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Architecture Governance Change Management","subtopic":"Governance Impact Analysis","concept":"Material governance changes should include impact analysis on active architecture decisions, exceptions, controls, and delivery workflows.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should be assessed before changing an architecture governance rule?","answer":"Impact analysis should identify affected architecture decisions, standards, quality gates, exceptions, automated controls, teams, and historical evidence whose interpretation may change.","context":"A policy change can have effects beyond future architecture reviews.","objective":"Prevent governance changes from silently invalidating existing controls or interpretations.","requirements":["Identify affected governance objects","Assess compatibility","Define migration or transition handling"],"constraints":["Impact scope depends on policy semantics"],"prerequisites":["Governance dependency mapping"],"dependencies":["Policy repository","Decision repository"],"alternatives":["Apply new policy only to future work"],"evidence":["Impact assessment","Affected-object inventory"],"tradeoffs":["Thorough impact analysis requires effort but reduces transition ambiguity"],"decision":"Perform impact analysis for material governance policy changes.","rationale":"Policy changes can affect both current and historical governance states.","consequences":["More predictable transitions","Additional change-management work"],"implementation_implications":["Link policies to dependent controls and records","Define grandfathering or reassessment rules"],"verification_method":"Change a representative policy and verify identification of affected governance objects.","outcome_learning":"Track transition defects caused by incomplete policy impact analysis.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B59-R01"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_POLICY_IMPACT_ANALYSIS"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B59-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Architecture Governance Change Management","subtopic":"Policy Transition","concept":"Governance policy transitions should define how in-flight architecture work is treated when rules change.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should governance handle work that is already in progress when a policy changes?","answer":"The transition policy should define whether existing work remains under the previous rule set, must migrate to the new requirements, or requires case-specific reassessment based on risk and lifecycle stage.","context":"Immediate application of new governance rules can disrupt active architecture work, while indefinite grandfathering can preserve outdated controls.","objective":"Make policy transition behavior explicit.","requirements":["Define effective date","Classify in-flight work","Specify reassessment conditions"],"constraints":["Transition treatment must respect mandatory requirements"],"prerequisites":["Policy versioning","Lifecycle stage model"],"dependencies":["Governance change management"],"alternatives":["Immediate universal migration","Permanent grandfathering"],"evidence":["Transition policy","Affected work inventory"],"tradeoffs":["Migration increases effort while grandfathering can preserve obsolete constraints"],"decision":"Define explicit transition rules based on lifecycle stage and material risk.","rationale":"Predictable transition rules reduce ambiguity during governance evolution.","consequences":["Clearer treatment of in-flight work","Potential migration effort"],"implementation_implications":["Record applicable policy version per work item","Trigger reassessment where transition rules require it"],"verification_method":"Test policy changes against work at multiple lifecycle stages.","outcome_learning":"Review transition exceptions and migration defects.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B59-R01","D55-B59-R02"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_POLICY_TRANSITION"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B59-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Architecture Governance Change Management","subtopic":"Governance Communication","concept":"Material governance changes require communication that identifies what changed, when it takes effect, and which architecture workflows are affected.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What information should accompany a material architecture governance change?","answer":"Communication should identify the changed policy or control, rationale, effective date, affected populations, required actions, transition rules, and authoritative version of the new guidance.","context":"Governance controls are ineffective if affected teams continue operating from obsolete assumptions.","objective":"Reduce ambiguity during governance transitions.","requirements":["Publish authoritative change information","Identify affected teams","Provide transition instructions"],"constraints":["Communication does not substitute for formal policy approval"],"prerequisites":["Approved governance change"],"dependencies":["Governance repository","Stakeholder management"],"alternatives":["Informal notification"],"evidence":["Change announcement","Policy version"],"tradeoffs":["Broader communication requires effort but reduces interpretation divergence"],"decision":"Use structured communication for material governance changes.","rationale":"Governance adoption depends on affected participants knowing the authoritative requirements.","consequences":["Improved transition clarity","Additional communication workload"],"implementation_implications":["Link communications to immutable policy versions","Retain communication evidence where material"],"verification_method":"Verify that affected stakeholders can identify the current policy and transition requirements.","outcome_learning":"Track repeated questions and misapplication after policy changes.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B59-R01","D55-B59-R03"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_CHANGE_COMMUNICATION"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B59-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B59","topic":"Architecture Governance Change Management","subtopic":"Emergency Governance Changes","concept":"Emergency changes to architecture governance require accelerated controls without eliminating traceability.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should emergency architecture governance changes be controlled?","answer":"Emergency governance changes should use predefined accelerated authority, document the reason and scope, identify temporary applicability, preserve evidence, and require retrospective review when appropriate.","context":"Security, operational, or regulatory events may require rapid changes to governance controls.","objective":"Support urgent governance changes without creating permanent undocumented bypasses.","requirements":["Define emergency authority","Record rationale","Limit scope or duration","Require retrospective review"],"constraints":["Emergency handling must not become the default path"],"prerequisites":["Emergency governance procedure"],"dependencies":["Incident management","Architecture governance"],"alternatives":["Normal policy change process"],"evidence":["Emergency change record","Approval","Retrospective review"],"tradeoffs":["Accelerated governance reduces response time but increases risk of incomplete analysis"],"decision":"Use a predefined emergency governance path with mandatory traceability and retrospective review.","rationale":"Urgency changes the process speed, not the need for accountability.","consequences":["Faster response to exceptional conditions","Additional post-event review"],"implementation_implications":["Define emergency states","Automatically create follow-up review obligations"],"verification_method":"Simulate an emergency policy change and verify authority, scope, evidence, and retrospective review behavior.","outcome_learning":"Review emergency changes for recurring conditions that should be addressed through normal governance.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B59-R03","D55-B59-R04"],"relationships":["EMERGENCY_GOVERNANCE_CHANGE"]}

{"record_id":"D55-B60-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Architecture Governance Maturity","subtopic":"Governance Capability Assessment","concept":"Architecture governance maturity should be assessed by observable capabilities rather than by document volume or organizational labels.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_METRIC","question":"How should architecture governance maturity be assessed?","answer":"Maturity should be assessed through observable capabilities such as decision traceability, evidence quality, policy enforcement, exception management, conformance verification, lifecycle integration, learning, and control effectiveness.","context":"A large governance documentation set does not necessarily indicate effective governance.","objective":"Assess actual governance capability rather than administrative activity.","requirements":["Define capability dimensions","Use evidence-based assessment","Distinguish capability presence from effectiveness"],"constraints":["Maturity models are context-dependent"],"prerequisites":["Governance capability model"],"dependencies":["Governance evidence"],"alternatives":["Document-count assessment"],"evidence":["Decision records","Conformance results","Exception data","Control telemetry"],"tradeoffs":["Capability assessment requires richer evidence than checklist-based evaluation"],"decision":"Assess governance maturity using evidence-backed capabilities and outcomes.","rationale":"Governance value depends on effective control and learning, not document quantity.","consequences":["More meaningful improvement planning","More evidence collection"],"implementation_implications":["Define measurable capability indicators","Record assessment scope and evidence"],"verification_method":"Perform a capability assessment and trace each maturity conclusion to supporting evidence.","outcome_learning":"Compare capability changes with governance defects and improvement outcomes.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B60-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Architecture Governance Maturity","subtopic":"Governance Effectiveness","concept":"Governance effectiveness should evaluate whether controls prevent, detect, and resolve material architectural problems rather than only whether processes are followed.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_METRIC","question":"What is the difference between governance compliance and governance effectiveness?","answer":"Governance compliance measures whether prescribed governance activities occurred, while governance effectiveness considers whether those activities actually provide useful control, detection, decision quality, traceability, and learning.","context":"A process can be followed consistently while still failing to address important architectural risks.","objective":"Prevent procedural compliance from being mistaken for effective governance.","requirements":["Measure process adherence","Measure control outcomes","Analyze important escapes and false positives"],"constraints":["Outcome metrics require careful interpretation and causal caution"],"prerequisites":["Governance telemetry","Conformance findings"],"dependencies":["Governance metrics"],"alternatives":["Compliance-only measurement"],"evidence":["Control results","Escaped findings","Exception patterns"],"tradeoffs":["Effectiveness assessment is more difficult than checklist compliance"],"decision":"Evaluate both governance adherence and control effectiveness.","rationale":"Process completion alone does not demonstrate that governance achieves its intended purpose.","consequences":["Better improvement targeting","More complex measurement"],"implementation_implications":["Separate compliance metrics from effectiveness metrics","Track material governance escapes"],"verification_method":"Compare process-compliance records with actual architectural findings and control outcomes.","outcome_learning":"Use discrepancies between compliance and effectiveness to identify weak controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B60-R01"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_EFFECTIVENESS"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B60-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Architecture Governance Maturity","subtopic":"Continuous Governance Improvement","concept":"Architecture governance should use observed findings, exceptions, review delays, and control failures as inputs for controlled improvement.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_IMPROVEMENT","question":"How should architecture governance improve over time?","answer":"Improvement should use evidence from governance findings, recurring exceptions, policy violations, review bottlenecks, escaped architectural risks, and stakeholder feedback to identify specific control or process changes.","context":"Static governance can become obsolete as architecture, technology, and organizational needs evolve.","objective":"Create an evidence-driven governance learning loop.","requirements":["Collect improvement evidence","Prioritize material problems","Validate changes after implementation"],"constraints":["Not every observed issue requires policy change"],"prerequisites":["Governance telemetry","Improvement process"],"dependencies":["Governance metrics","Policy lifecycle"],"alternatives":["Periodic policy rewrite without evidence analysis"],"evidence":["Findings","Metrics","Exceptions","Review feedback"],"tradeoffs":["Continuous improvement requires ongoing analysis and change management"],"decision":"Use a closed-loop evidence-driven governance improvement process.","rationale":"Governance should adapt based on demonstrated control performance and changing architectural conditions.","consequences":["More responsive governance","Ongoing improvement workload"],"implementation_implications":["Link findings to improvement actions","Record hypotheses and expected effects","Verify changes after implementation"],"verification_method":"Trace a governance improvement from observed evidence through change and post-change evaluation.","outcome_learning":"Measure whether governance changes reduce the targeted failure pattern without creating new material problems.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B60-R02"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_LEARNING_LOOP"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B60-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Architecture Governance Maturity","subtopic":"Governance Automation Maturity","concept":"Governance automation maturity should be evaluated by control reliability, coverage, explainability, maintainability, and appropriate human escalation rather than automation volume.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_IMPROVEMENT","question":"What indicates mature architecture governance automation?","answer":"Useful governance automation reliably evaluates defined controls, produces explainable results, records evidence and policy versions, handles failures safely, remains maintainable, and escalates context-sensitive decisions to appropriate human authority.","context":"Automation can increase governance consistency but can also amplify incorrect rules.","objective":"Evaluate automation quality rather than automation quantity.","requirements":["Measure rule coverage","Measure false-positive and false-negative behavior where possible","Preserve human escalation"],"constraints":["Not all architectural judgments are automatable"],"prerequisites":["Automated governance controls"],"dependencies":["Policy-as-code","Governance observability"],"alternatives":["Manual governance"],"evidence":["Evaluation results","Rule tests","Failure records"],"tradeoffs":["Higher automation can reduce manual effort but increases dependency on rule correctness"],"decision":"Assess governance automation through reliability and control quality rather than number of automated checks.","rationale":"Automation is valuable only when it produces trustworthy governance signals.","consequences":["More rigorous automation lifecycle","Need for rule testing"],"implementation_implications":["Test governance rules","Version them","Monitor control health","Provide explicit escalation paths"],"verification_method":"Test automated controls against known compliant, violating, ambiguous, and unavailable-evidence cases.","outcome_learning":"Track control defects, overrides, false positives, and escaped violations.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B52-R01","D55-B54-R03"],"relationships":["GOVERNANCE_AUTOMATION_MATURITY"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B60-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B60","topic":"Architecture Governance Maturity","subtopic":"Governance Learning and Institutional Memory","concept":"Architecture governance should preserve validated lessons so future decisions can reuse evidence without treating historical decisions as universally applicable.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_IMPROVEMENT","question":"How should governance preserve institutional architectural learning?","answer":"Validated lessons should be linked to the originating decision, context, evidence, outcome, applicability conditions, and known limitations so future architects can retrieve them without assuming that historical choices remain universally correct.","context":"Architecture knowledge loses value when lessons are detached from the context in which they were learned.","objective":"Build reusable but context-aware architectural institutional memory.","requirements":["Capture originating context","Record observed outcome","State applicability and limitations","Link lessons to source decisions"],"constraints":["Historical outcomes may not generalize to different contexts"],"prerequisites":["Decision records","Outcome evidence"],"dependencies":["Architecture repository","Governance learning process"],"alternatives":["Unstructured lessons-learned documents"],"evidence":["Decision records","Verification results","Post-implementation observations"],"tradeoffs":["Structured learning requires more metadata but improves retrieval and reuse"],"decision":"Store validated architectural lessons as context-linked governance knowledge.","rationale":"Institutional learning is most useful when its evidence and applicability boundaries are preserved.","consequences":["Improved future decision support","Need for periodic lesson validation"],"implementation_implications":["Link lessons to decisions and evidence","Mark confidence and applicability","Reassess stale lessons"],"verification_method":"Retrieve lessons for representative architecture decisions and verify that context and evidence remain visible.","outcome_learning":"Track reuse, correction, retirement, and contradiction of stored architectural lessons.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B60-R03","D55-B60-R04"],"relationships":["ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE_INSTITUTIONAL_MEMORY"]}

{"record_id":"D55-B61-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Distributed Systems","concept":"Distributed architecture governance","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance operate when a system is distributed across multiple services, teams, and deployment boundaries?","answer":"Distributed architecture governance should define shared architectural invariants while allowing teams autonomy over local implementation decisions. Governance should focus on interfaces, security boundaries, reliability expectations, data ownership, dependency constraints, and cross-service consequences rather than centrally approving every implementation detail.","context":"A distributed system may contain independently owned services that evolve at different rates and use different implementation technologies.","objective":"Maintain system-level coherence without creating centralized bottlenecks.","requirements":["Define cross-service architectural invariants","Establish ownership for shared boundaries","Make dependency and contract decisions traceable","Permit bounded local autonomy"],"constraints":["Team autonomy","Independent deployment","Technology diversity","Asynchronous organizational coordination"],"prerequisites":["Defined service boundaries","Ownership model","Architecture decision records"],"dependencies":["API governance","Dependency governance","Security governance"],"alternatives":["Centralized approval for all changes","Fully decentralized architecture decisions","Federated governance with shared invariants"],"evidence":["Distributed systems require explicit coordination at boundaries because local changes can create system-level effects."],"tradeoffs":["Centralized governance increases consistency but can reduce delivery autonomy; federated governance improves autonomy but requires stronger contracts and automated controls."],"decision":"Use federated governance with centrally defined system invariants and locally owned implementation decisions.","rationale":"The model aligns governance scope with the architectural impact of a decision.","consequences":["Cross-boundary decisions require explicit ownership","Automated conformance becomes more valuable","Local teams retain implementation flexibility"],"implementation_implications":["Define mandatory cross-service rules","Attach ownership to service boundaries","Automate validation of contract and security invariants","Record exceptions explicitly"],"verification_method":"Review representative cross-service changes and verify that system invariants are enforced without requiring unnecessary central approval.","outcome_learning":"Governance effectiveness should be measured through conformance defects, review latency, exception frequency, and boundary-related incidents.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B61-R02","D55-B61-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B61-R02","D55-B61-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B61-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Distributed Systems","concept":"Cross-service architectural invariants","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should qualify as an architectural invariant across independently governed services?","answer":"An architectural invariant is a condition that must remain true across service boundaries because violating it can compromise system-wide properties such as security, data integrity, compatibility, resilience, or observability.","context":"Distributed teams may choose different local implementations while still needing to preserve system-level properties.","objective":"Identify governance rules that are sufficiently important to apply across independent components.","requirements":["Tie each invariant to a system-level property","Define objectively verifiable conditions","Assign an owner for interpretation and maintenance"],"constraints":["Avoid unnecessary centralization","Rules must remain technology-aware but not implementation-dependent where possible"],"prerequisites":["Defined system architecture","Known cross-boundary risks"],"dependencies":["Service contracts","Security controls","Reliability requirements"],"alternatives":["Technology-specific mandates","Team-specific standards","System-level invariants"],"evidence":["System-wide properties can be violated by individually valid local changes when cross-boundary assumptions are not governed."],"tradeoffs":["Broader invariants provide stronger consistency but may constrain legitimate local variation."],"decision":"Govern only properties whose violation can materially affect system-level behavior or risk.","rationale":"Governance should be proportional to architectural impact.","consequences":["Rules remain smaller and more durable","Local implementation choices remain flexible"],"implementation_implications":["Express invariants as testable policies where possible","Document rationale and affected boundaries","Review invariants when architecture changes"],"verification_method":"Attempt representative compliant and non-compliant changes and confirm that the invariant distinguishes them correctly.","outcome_learning":"Track false-positive governance findings and incidents caused by missing invariants to refine the rule set.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B61-R01","D55-B61-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B61-R01","D55-B61-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B61-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Distributed Systems","concept":"Federated architecture ownership","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture ownership be divided between central and domain teams?","answer":"Central ownership should cover cross-domain architectural concerns, shared constraints, and system-wide standards, while domain teams should own decisions confined to their bounded context. Boundary decisions should have explicit joint ownership or escalation paths.","context":"A distributed organization needs governance without assigning every architectural decision to a central authority.","objective":"Align decision authority with architectural scope and consequences.","requirements":["Define central responsibilities","Define domain responsibilities","Define cross-domain escalation","Avoid ambiguous ownership"],"constraints":["Organizational boundaries may change","Some concerns span multiple domains"],"prerequisites":["Domain decomposition","Ownership registry","Decision authority model"],"dependencies":["Architecture decision governance","Dependency governance"],"alternatives":["Centralized ownership","Fully decentralized ownership","Federated ownership"],"evidence":["Decision authority is more effective when aligned with the scope of consequences and accountable ownership."],"tradeoffs":["Federation reduces central bottlenecks but increases the need for explicit boundary mechanisms."],"decision":"Use federated ownership with explicit escalation for cross-domain decisions.","rationale":"Ownership should follow architectural impact rather than organizational hierarchy alone.","consequences":["Teams need clear responsibility boundaries","Cross-domain decisions require coordination"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain an architecture ownership map","Assign accountable owners to shared concerns","Define escalation criteria"],"verification_method":"Sample architecture decisions and verify that each has a clearly identifiable accountable owner and escalation path where required.","outcome_learning":"Review ownership-related delays and conflicts to identify unclear governance boundaries.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B61-R01","D55-B61-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B61-R01","D55-B61-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B61-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Distributed Architecture Governance","concept":"Cross-service change impact governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should governance identify distributed architectural changes that have cross-service impact?","answer":"Governance should evaluate dependency relationships, contracts, shared data, security boundaries, operational dependencies, and failure propagation paths before approving changes with cross-service effects.","context":"A change that appears local may alter behavior or assumptions in dependent services.","objective":"Prevent locally valid changes from causing unrecognized system-level consequences.","requirements":["Identify affected dependencies","Assess contract changes","Evaluate failure propagation","Assess shared data and security impact"],"constraints":["Dependency graphs may be incomplete","Dynamic runtime dependencies may not be fully known"],"prerequisites":["Dependency inventory","Service contracts","Architecture topology"],"dependencies":["Change management","Architecture repository"],"alternatives":["Manual impact analysis","Automated dependency analysis","Hybrid analysis"],"evidence":["Cross-service impact is determined by relationships rather than by the physical location of changed code alone."],"tradeoffs":["Automated analysis improves consistency but depends on dependency-data quality."],"decision":"Use a hybrid impact-analysis model combining recorded architecture relationships with automated dependency evidence.","rationale":"Neither static governance records nor automation alone reliably captures every distributed dependency.","consequences":["Changes require impact metadata","Dependency information becomes a governance asset"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain dependency relationships","Trigger reviews for affected boundaries","Record uncertain dependencies explicitly"],"verification_method":"Compare predicted impact against known dependency relationships for representative changes.","outcome_learning":"Measure missed dependencies and unnecessary review expansion to improve impact analysis.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B61-R02","D55-B61-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B61-R02","D55-B61-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B61-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B61","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Distributed Architecture Governance","concept":"Governance boundary escalation","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should a locally owned architecture decision be escalated to system-level governance?","answer":"Escalation is warranted when a decision crosses ownership boundaries or materially changes shared contracts, security properties, data ownership, reliability assumptions, operational dependencies, or other governed system invariants.","context":"Not every domain-level decision requires central review.","objective":"Define proportional escalation rather than universal central approval.","requirements":["Identify escalation triggers","Define accountable escalation authority","Record the decision boundary"],"constraints":["Excessive escalation creates governance bottlenecks"],"prerequisites":["Governed invariants","Ownership model","Decision records"],"dependencies":["Architecture review process","Exception governance"],"alternatives":["Universal review","No escalation","Trigger-based escalation"],"evidence":["Governance effort is most useful where decisions have consequences beyond the decision owner's boundary."],"tradeoffs":["Trigger-based escalation reduces overhead but requires accurate impact criteria."],"decision":"Use explicit impact-based escalation triggers.","rationale":"Escalation should be driven by architectural consequence rather than organizational status.","consequences":["Most local decisions remain local","Cross-boundary decisions become visible to affected owners"],"implementation_implications":["Define escalation rules","Include affected boundaries in decision records","Provide an escalation path for uncertain cases"],"verification_method":"Audit decisions across multiple teams and determine whether cross-boundary cases were consistently escalated.","outcome_learning":"Review missed and unnecessary escalations to refine trigger precision.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B61-R03","D55-B61-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B61-R03","D55-B61-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B62-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance for Event-Driven Systems","concept":"Event schema governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should architecture governance control for shared event schemas?","answer":"Governance should define ownership, compatibility expectations, semantic meaning, required metadata, evolution rules, and deprecation procedures for events consumed across organizational boundaries.","context":"Event-driven systems can have producers and consumers that evolve independently.","objective":"Prevent uncontrolled event evolution from causing semantic or compatibility failures.","requirements":["Assign event ownership","Define schema compatibility rules","Document semantic meaning","Define deprecation procedures"],"constraints":["Independent producer and consumer deployment","Long-lived consumers may exist"],"prerequisites":["Event catalog","Consumer visibility","Schema representation"],"dependencies":["Data governance","API and contract governance"],"alternatives":["Central schema approval","Producer-owned schemas with shared rules","Unmanaged event evolution"],"evidence":["Independent event consumers create compatibility obligations beyond the producer's local implementation."],"tradeoffs":["Stronger governance reduces breaking changes but increases producer coordination requirements."],"decision":"Use producer ownership combined with explicit compatibility and lifecycle governance for shared events.","rationale":"Ownership and compatibility controls address different governance responsibilities.","consequences":["Events require lifecycle metadata","Breaking changes require controlled migration"],"implementation_implications":["Record producer and consumer relationships","Automate compatibility checks where possible","Track deprecation state"],"verification_method":"Validate representative schema changes against declared compatibility rules and consumer dependencies.","outcome_learning":"Track schema-related consumer failures and migration delays.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B62-R02","D55-B62-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B62-R02","D55-B62-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B62-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance for Event-Driven Systems","concept":"Event semantic ownership","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why must governance distinguish event schema ownership from event semantic ownership?","answer":"A schema describes structure, while semantic ownership determines what an event means and when its meaning may change. Governance should make both explicit because a structurally compatible change can still alter business or system semantics.","context":"Consumers may depend on the meaning of an event rather than only its fields and types.","objective":"Prevent semantic breaking changes that pass structural compatibility checks.","requirements":["Identify semantic owner","Document event meaning","Define semantic change rules"],"constraints":["Semantic interpretation may differ across consumers"],"prerequisites":["Event catalog","Domain ownership model"],"dependencies":["Schema governance","Domain governance"],"alternatives":["Schema-only governance","Producer-only semantic ownership","Explicit semantic ownership"],"evidence":["Structural compatibility does not guarantee behavioral or semantic compatibility."],"tradeoffs":["Semantic governance increases documentation effort but reduces ambiguity."],"decision":"Govern event semantics explicitly in addition to structural schemas.","rationale":"Architecture governance must protect behavior, not only serialization compatibility.","consequences":["Event definitions require semantic documentation","Semantic changes require impact analysis"],"implementation_implications":["Store semantic descriptions with event definitions","Identify semantic owners","Review consumer assumptions"],"verification_method":"Evaluate schema-compatible changes and determine whether semantic changes are detected by governance.","outcome_learning":"Record incidents where structural compatibility concealed semantic incompatibility.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B62-R01","D55-B62-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B62-R01","D55-B62-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B62-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance for Event-Driven Systems","concept":"Consumer migration governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance manage consumers during an event contract migration?","answer":"Governance should establish migration ownership, compatibility periods, consumer discovery, rollout sequencing, observability requirements, and a defined retirement condition for the old contract.","context":"A producer may need to introduce a new event version while existing consumers still depend on the old contract.","objective":"Make contract migration controlled and reversible rather than an implicit coordination exercise.","requirements":["Identify consumers","Define migration phases","Monitor adoption","Define retirement criteria"],"constraints":["Consumers may migrate at different rates","Unknown consumers may exist"],"prerequisites":["Consumer inventory","Versioning policy","Observability"],"dependencies":["Contract governance","Release governance"],"alternatives":["Immediate replacement","Dual publishing during migration","Consumer-driven migration"],"evidence":["Independent consumers require explicit migration coordination when contracts change incompatibly."],"tradeoffs":["Dual compatibility increases temporary complexity but reduces immediate disruption."],"decision":"Use staged migration with explicit adoption and retirement criteria.","rationale":"Migration should provide measurable progress and a controlled endpoint.","consequences":["Old and new contracts may coexist temporarily","Retirement requires evidence"],"implementation_implications":["Track consumer migration status","Instrument old-contract usage","Define rollback or extension conditions"],"verification_method":"Verify that retirement occurs only after defined consumer and usage criteria are satisfied.","outcome_learning":"Measure migration duration, failed consumers, and unexpected dependencies.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B62-R01","D55-B62-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B62-R01","D55-B62-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B62-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance for Event-Driven Systems","concept":"Event replay governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What governance concerns arise when events are replayed?","answer":"Replay governance should define whether events are replayable, retention requirements, idempotency expectations, authorization boundaries, side-effect controls, and the operational procedure for replay.","context":"Reprocessing historical events can reproduce state transitions or trigger external side effects.","objective":"Prevent uncontrolled replay from corrupting state or producing unintended effects.","requirements":["Classify replay safety","Define idempotency expectations","Control replay authorization","Separate replay from uncontrolled side effects"],"constraints":["Historical events may represent old semantics","External side effects may not be safely repeatable"],"prerequisites":["Event retention policy","Consumer behavior knowledge","Operational controls"],"dependencies":["Data governance","Incident governance"],"alternatives":["Unrestricted replay","Replay disabled","Controlled replay"],"evidence":["Replay can have different consequences from ordinary event consumption because historical messages may interact with current state and external systems."],"tradeoffs":["Replay capability improves recovery but increases operational complexity and risk."],"decision":"Treat replay as a governed operational capability rather than an implicit consumer behavior.","rationale":"Replay can alter state and external systems and therefore requires explicit controls.","consequences":["Replay procedures become auditable","Consumers need defined replay behavior"],"implementation_implications":["Record replay authorization","Provide dry-run or isolated validation where feasible","Document side-effect handling"],"verification_method":"Test representative replay scenarios in a controlled environment and verify state and side-effect behavior.","outcome_learning":"Capture replay incidents, unexpected side effects, and recovery effectiveness.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B62-R03","D55-B62-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B62-R03","D55-B62-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B62-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B62","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Governance for Event-Driven Systems","concept":"Event retention governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should event retention be governed when events serve both operational messaging and historical reconstruction?","answer":"Retention should be based on explicit recovery, audit, replay, regulatory, and analytical requirements rather than treating all events as having the same lifecycle.","context":"Event streams may simultaneously support current processing and historical use cases.","objective":"Align retention with actual architectural and governance requirements.","requirements":["Identify retention purpose","Define retention period or policy","Separate immutable history from transient delivery where appropriate","Define deletion obligations"],"constraints":["Storage cost","Privacy requirements","Replay requirements"],"prerequisites":["Data classification","Recovery requirements","Legal or policy constraints where applicable"],"dependencies":["Data lifecycle governance","Event governance"],"alternatives":["Uniform retention","Purpose-specific retention","Infinite retention"],"evidence":["Different event uses create different retention requirements and risks."],"tradeoffs":["Longer retention improves historical recovery but increases storage, privacy, and governance obligations."],"decision":"Use purpose-driven retention policies with explicit lifecycle ownership.","rationale":"Retention should be an architectural decision tied to documented use and risk.","consequences":["Retention policies become reviewable governance controls","Deletion and archival require explicit handling"],"implementation_implications":["Classify event streams","Record retention policy metadata","Monitor policy compliance"],"verification_method":"Audit representative event streams against documented retention requirements.","outcome_learning":"Review storage growth, recovery needs, and deletion exceptions to refine retention policy.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B62-R01","D55-B62-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B62-R01","D55-B62-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B63-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Integration Architecture Governance","concept":"Integration boundary ownership","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should architecture governance define for integration boundaries between systems?","answer":"Governance should identify the owning parties, contract authority, data responsibilities, security boundary, failure responsibilities, lifecycle rules, and escalation mechanism for each significant integration boundary.","context":"Integrations often connect independently governed systems with different ownership and lifecycle models.","objective":"Prevent ambiguity at system boundaries.","requirements":["Identify boundary owners","Define contract ownership","Define failure responsibility","Document security and data responsibilities"],"constraints":["Multiple organizations may share the integration","Ownership may change over time"],"prerequisites":["System inventory","Integration catalog"],"dependencies":["API governance","Data governance","Security governance"],"alternatives":["Producer-owned boundary","Consumer-owned boundary","Joint boundary ownership"],"evidence":["Integration failures often cross organizational and system ownership boundaries."],"tradeoffs":["Joint governance improves coordination but can make accountability less clear unless roles are explicit."],"decision":"Assign explicit accountable ownership for each integration boundary, with joint responsibilities represented separately.","rationale":"Shared responsibility should not become ambiguous responsibility.","consequences":["Boundary metadata becomes part of architecture governance","Ownership changes require governance updates"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain integration ownership records","Define escalation contacts or roles","Link boundaries to contracts and dependencies"],"verification_method":"Audit significant integrations for complete ownership and responsibility metadata.","outcome_learning":"Track unresolved boundary incidents and ownership disputes.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B63-R02","D55-B63-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B63-R02","D55-B63-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B63-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Integration Architecture Governance","concept":"Integration contract governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should governance distinguish an integration contract from an implementation detail?","answer":"A contract should govern observable behavior required by the interacting parties, including interface structure, semantics, error behavior, compatibility expectations, security requirements, and relevant operational guarantees. Internal implementation details should remain outside the contract unless they affect observable behavior.","context":"Overly detailed contracts create unnecessary coupling between independently evolving systems.","objective":"Keep integration contracts stable, meaningful, and behavior-oriented.","requirements":["Define observable behavior","Exclude unnecessary internals","Specify compatibility expectations","Define failure behavior"],"constraints":["Different protocols expose different contract dimensions"],"prerequisites":["Integration boundary definition","Consumer and producer responsibilities"],"dependencies":["API governance","Event governance"],"alternatives":["Implementation-level contract","Behavioral contract","Minimal interface contract"],"evidence":["Coupling increases when contracts expose details that do not need to be shared."],"tradeoffs":["Broader contracts can improve explicitness but increase coupling and maintenance burden."],"decision":"Govern observable behavior and required guarantees rather than internal implementation structure.","rationale":"The boundary should preserve interoperability without freezing implementation choices.","consequences":["Implementations can evolve more independently","Contract review focuses on externally relevant behavior"],"implementation_implications":["Document contract semantics","Define error and compatibility behavior","Avoid exposing unnecessary internal structures"],"verification_method":"Review contract definitions and identify elements that can change without affecting consumers.","outcome_learning":"Track breaking changes and unnecessary coupling caused by contract design.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B63-R01","D55-B63-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B63-R01","D55-B63-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B63-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Integration Architecture Governance","concept":"Integration failure responsibility","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance assign responsibility when an integration fails?","answer":"Responsibility should be decomposed by failure domain rather than assigned generically to one system. Governance should identify who owns producer correctness, consumer handling, transport availability, contract compatibility, authentication, and operational coordination where applicable.","context":"Integration failures can involve multiple independently controlled components.","objective":"Make failure ownership actionable and prevent responsibility gaps.","requirements":["Identify failure domains","Assign owners","Define escalation paths","Define evidence required for diagnosis"],"constraints":["Failure causality may span multiple components"],"prerequisites":["Integration boundary ownership","Dependency model"],"dependencies":["Incident governance","Observability governance"],"alternatives":["Single integration owner","Shared responsibility without decomposition","Failure-domain ownership"],"evidence":["Complex failures often require multiple owners to diagnose and remediate different contributing causes."],"tradeoffs":["Detailed ownership improves accountability but increases governance metadata."],"decision":"Use failure-domain responsibility mapping for critical integrations.","rationale":"Failure handling requires specific accountability for different control surfaces.","consequences":["Incident diagnosis becomes more structured","Boundary contracts should identify relevant responsibilities"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain failure-domain ownership metadata","Define escalation rules","Capture diagnostic evidence requirements"],"verification_method":"Run integration failure scenarios and verify that each failure mode maps to an accountable owner.","outcome_learning":"Review incidents with unresolved ownership to identify missing failure domains.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B63-R01","D55-B63-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B63-R01","D55-B63-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B63-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Integration Architecture Governance","concept":"Integration coupling assessment","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should architecture governance assess coupling introduced by an integration?","answer":"Coupling should be assessed across data shape, semantics, timing, availability, failure handling, deployment coordination, authentication, versioning, and operational dependencies rather than only interface syntax.","context":"Two systems can appear loosely coupled at the API level while remaining tightly coupled operationally or semantically.","objective":"Evaluate the actual architectural dependency introduced by integration.","requirements":["Assess multiple coupling dimensions","Identify deployment dependencies","Identify failure and availability assumptions"],"constraints":["Coupling can be difficult to observe statically"],"prerequisites":["Integration contract","Dependency information"],"dependencies":["Architecture dependency governance"],"alternatives":["Interface-only assessment","Multi-dimensional coupling assessment"],"evidence":["Integration coupling includes behavioral and operational assumptions beyond interface structure."],"tradeoffs":["More comprehensive assessment requires additional architecture evidence."],"decision":"Use multi-dimensional coupling assessment for significant integrations.","rationale":"Architecture risk is better represented by actual dependencies than interface syntax alone.","consequences":["Integration reviews become more evidence-driven","Operational assumptions become explicit"],"implementation_implications":["Record timing and availability assumptions","Document deployment and version dependencies","Review failure coupling"],"verification_method":"Evaluate representative integrations against each coupling dimension and identify undocumented dependencies.","outcome_learning":"Track incidents caused by unrecognized coupling dimensions.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B63-R02","D55-B63-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B63-R02","D55-B63-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B63-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B63","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Integration Architecture Governance","concept":"Integration decommission governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What evidence should be required before decommissioning an integration?","answer":"Governance should require evidence that active consumers have migrated or been retired, contractual obligations are resolved, operational dependencies are removed, data handling requirements are satisfied, and rollback or recovery considerations are addressed.","context":"Removing an integration can break consumers that are not visible through the primary dependency inventory.","objective":"Make integration retirement evidence-based.","requirements":["Confirm consumer migration","Confirm dependency removal","Confirm data obligations","Confirm operational retirement readiness"],"constraints":["Hidden or undocumented consumers may exist"],"prerequisites":["Consumer inventory","Usage telemetry where available","Integration ownership"],"dependencies":["Technology lifecycle governance","Dependency governance"],"alternatives":["Time-based retirement","Usage-based retirement","Evidence-based retirement"],"evidence":["An inactive-looking integration may still have consumers or operational dependencies."],"tradeoffs":["Longer evidence collection reduces retirement risk but delays simplification."],"decision":"Require explicit retirement evidence rather than relying solely on elapsed time.","rationale":"Retirement should be based on demonstrated absence of required dependencies.","consequences":["Decommissioning becomes auditable","Legacy integrations remain only when justified by evidence"],"implementation_implications":["Monitor usage","Record migration evidence","Define final retirement approval criteria"],"verification_method":"Confirm consumer and dependency evidence before disabling representative integrations.","outcome_learning":"Capture unexpected consumers discovered during retirement.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B63-R01","D55-B63-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B63-R01","D55-B63-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B64-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance","concept":"Platform capability governance","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance determine which platform capabilities should be standardized?","answer":"Standardization should target capabilities where common implementation materially improves security, reliability, operability, cost control, or engineering efficiency without eliminating legitimate domain-specific requirements.","context":"Platform teams often provide shared capabilities such as identity, messaging, observability, deployment, or storage services.","objective":"Prevent unnecessary platform standardization while preserving reusable architectural capabilities.","requirements":["Identify common cross-team needs","Evaluate measurable benefits","Define supported usage boundaries","Allow justified exceptions"],"constraints":["Different workloads may have different requirements","Platform capabilities create dependency on platform owners"],"prerequisites":["Platform capability inventory","Consumer requirements"],"dependencies":["Technology lifecycle governance","Reference architecture governance"],"alternatives":["Standardize everything","Provide no shared standards","Selective capability standardization"],"evidence":["Shared capabilities can reduce duplicated engineering effort when their requirements are genuinely common."],"tradeoffs":["Standardization can reduce duplication but may create platform lock-in or unsuitable abstractions."],"decision":"Standardize capabilities where shared architectural value exceeds the cost of imposed coupling.","rationale":"Platform governance should be value- and evidence-driven.","consequences":["Platform standards require explicit applicability criteria","Exceptions remain possible"],"implementation_implications":["Define supported use cases","Publish capability boundaries","Measure adoption and failure impact"],"verification_method":"Evaluate standardized capabilities against consumer requirements and documented benefits.","outcome_learning":"Track adoption, exceptions, migration burden, and platform-related incidents.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B64-R02","D55-B64-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B64-R02","D55-B64-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B64-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance","concept":"Platform capability boundaries","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should governance explicitly define the boundary of a platform capability?","answer":"A platform capability needs explicit boundaries so consumers understand supported responsibilities, non-goals, operational guarantees, ownership, and extension limits. Without these boundaries, teams may use the platform for unsupported purposes and create hidden coupling.","context":"Shared platforms often become de facto infrastructure for use cases beyond their original design.","objective":"Control scope expansion and prevent accidental architectural coupling.","requirements":["Define supported use cases","Define non-goals","Define ownership","Define guarantees and limitations"],"constraints":["Platform usage can evolve faster than governance documentation"],"prerequisites":["Platform capability definition","Consumer feedback"],"dependencies":["Platform lifecycle governance"],"alternatives":["Informal platform usage","Broad platform abstraction","Explicit capability boundaries"],"evidence":["Unbounded shared capabilities tend to accumulate assumptions and dependencies that increase governance complexity."],"tradeoffs":["Narrow boundaries reduce misuse but may require additional capabilities for legitimate use cases."],"decision":"Treat capability boundaries as governed architecture artifacts.","rationale":"Explicit boundaries make platform responsibility and consumer expectations testable.","consequences":["Unsupported use becomes identifiable","Platform evolution becomes easier to reason about"],"implementation_implications":["Document capability contracts","Define support limits","Review new use cases against boundaries"],"verification_method":"Review platform consumers and classify whether usage falls within supported boundaries.","outcome_learning":"Use unsupported-use incidents and exception requests to refine boundaries.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B64-R01","D55-B64-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B64-R01","D55-B64-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B64-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance","concept":"Platform dependency governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should governance manage architectural dependency on an internal platform?","answer":"Governance should record the dependency, supported versions, migration path, service-level expectations, ownership, failure implications, and exit strategy where the platform becomes critical to consuming systems.","context":"Internal platforms can become strategic dependencies even when they are not externally purchased products.","objective":"Make platform dependency visible and manageable.","requirements":["Record consuming systems","Define supported versions","Define lifecycle policy","Define exit or migration options for critical dependencies"],"constraints":["Platform replacement may be expensive","Consumers may have different upgrade schedules"],"prerequisites":["Platform inventory","Dependency graph"],"dependencies":["Technology lifecycle governance","Resilience governance"],"alternatives":["Unmanaged dependency","Centralized platform mandate","Governed platform dependency"],"evidence":["Shared infrastructure can become a single point of organizational dependency even when it is internally operated."],"tradeoffs":["Dependency governance adds documentation but improves lifecycle and resilience planning."],"decision":"Govern critical platform dependencies explicitly in architecture governance.","rationale":"Internal ownership does not eliminate architectural dependency risk.","consequences":["Platform lifecycle decisions must account for consumers","Exit planning becomes relevant for critical capabilities"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain consumer dependency records","Publish compatibility policy","Assess platform failure and migration impact"],"verification_method":"Audit critical platforms for consumer visibility, supported versions, and lifecycle plans.","outcome_learning":"Review platform incidents and migration efforts to identify unmanaged dependencies.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B64-R02","D55-B64-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B64-R02","D55-B64-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B64-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance","concept":"Platform adoption governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should governance evaluate adoption of a shared platform capability?","answer":"Adoption should be evaluated against applicability, consumer outcomes, reliability, security, migration cost, operational burden, and exceptions rather than adoption percentage alone.","context":"A high adoption rate does not prove that a platform is appropriate or beneficial.","objective":"Measure meaningful platform adoption rather than superficial usage.","requirements":["Measure applicable consumer population","Assess outcomes","Track exceptions and failures","Consider migration and operational cost"],"constraints":["Adoption metrics can hide forced or unsuitable usage"],"prerequisites":["Consumer inventory","Platform outcome metrics"],"dependencies":["Platform capability governance","Architecture metrics"],"alternatives":["Adoption percentage","Outcome-based adoption assessment","Mandatory adoption"],"evidence":["Usage volume alone cannot establish architectural fitness."],"tradeoffs":["Outcome-based evaluation requires more evidence than simple adoption counts."],"decision":"Evaluate platform adoption using applicability and outcome evidence.","rationale":"Governance should distinguish useful adoption from forced or accidental dependency.","consequences":["Low adoption may trigger investigation rather than automatic enforcement","High adoption does not automatically validate the platform"],"implementation_implications":["Track consumer outcomes","Record exception reasons","Review migration costs"],"verification_method":"Compare adoption data with reliability, security, cost, and consumer satisfaction evidence.","outcome_learning":"Use outcome trends to decide whether the capability should expand, change, or retire.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B64-R01","D55-B64-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B64-R01","D55-B64-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B64-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B64","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance","concept":"Platform exception governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should a team be allowed to bypass a standardized platform capability?","answer":"An exception should be considered when documented requirements cannot be satisfied by the platform, the platform introduces disproportionate risk or cost, or an alternative provides materially better architectural fitness. The exception should have an owner, rationale, scope, duration, and review condition.","context":"Platform standardization should not override legitimate architectural requirements.","objective":"Make platform exceptions controlled and evidence-based.","requirements":["Document unmet requirements","Assess alternative","Define exception scope","Assign owner and review date"],"constraints":["Exceptions can fragment the platform ecosystem"],"prerequisites":["Platform boundary definition","Exception governance"],"dependencies":["Architecture standards","Governance waiver process"],"alternatives":["No exceptions","Uncontrolled bypass","Evidence-based exceptions"],"evidence":["Standardized capabilities may not fit every workload or architectural context."],"tradeoffs":["Exceptions preserve architectural fitness but increase platform diversity and governance complexity."],"decision":"Permit bounded, evidence-based exceptions with explicit review conditions.","rationale":"Governance should optimize architectural fitness rather than enforce uniformity for its own sake.","consequences":["Exceptions become visible governance objects","Repeated exceptions may indicate platform deficiencies"],"implementation_implications":["Track exception patterns","Require alternative analysis","Review recurring exceptions for platform improvements"],"verification_method":"Audit exceptions for documented evidence, scope, ownership, and expiration or review conditions.","outcome_learning":"Use recurring exceptions as input to platform capability redesign.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B64-R01","D55-B64-R02"],"relationships":["D55-B64-R01","D55-B64-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B65-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance","concept":"Platform reliability governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should architecture governance require when a shared platform becomes a critical dependency?","answer":"Governance should require explicit reliability expectations, dependency impact analysis, failure modes, recovery responsibilities, capacity assumptions, observability, and a documented response path for platform degradation.","context":"A shared platform failure can affect many independent consumers simultaneously.","objective":"Ensure platform reliability obligations reflect the aggregate architectural impact.","requirements":["Define reliability expectations","Assess blast radius","Define recovery ownership","Establish capacity and observability requirements"],"constraints":["Shared platforms can create correlated failures"],"prerequisites":["Consumer dependency map","Platform architecture"],"dependencies":["Reliability governance","Incident governance"],"alternatives":["Consumer-managed resilience only","Platform-managed resilience only","Shared resilience responsibilities"],"evidence":["A platform serving many consumers can amplify failures across otherwise independent systems."],"tradeoffs":["Higher platform resilience can require significant investment, while consumer-side resilience may duplicate effort."],"decision":"Govern reliability jointly across platform and critical consumers, with responsibilities explicitly assigned.","rationale":"Neither side can fully control the complete failure surface alone.","consequences":["Critical dependencies require system-level resilience analysis","Platform contracts need reliability semantics"],"implementation_implications":["Document dependency blast radius","Define failure-handling responsibilities","Test critical failure modes"],"verification_method":"Perform failure analysis and controlled resilience testing for critical platform dependencies.","outcome_learning":"Use correlated incident data to identify systemic platform weaknesses.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B65-R02","D55-B65-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B65-R02","D55-B65-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B65-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance","concept":"Platform blast-radius governance","knowledge_type":"RISK_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should governance assess the blast radius of a shared platform failure?","answer":"Blast-radius analysis should identify dependent systems, criticality, common failure modes, dependency concentration, recovery relationships, and whether platform failure can cause correlated service degradation.","context":"The impact of a platform failure depends on the dependency graph, not merely the platform's own availability target.","objective":"Make systemic impact visible in architecture governance.","requirements":["Identify critical consumers","Map dependency concentration","Analyze correlated failure","Estimate recovery implications"],"constraints":["Dependency inventories may be incomplete","Impact can change as adoption grows"],"prerequisites":["Dependency graph","Service criticality information"],"dependencies":["Platform reliability governance","Architecture risk governance"],"alternatives":["Platform-local risk assessment","Consumer-by-consumer analysis","System-wide blast-radius analysis"],"evidence":["Shared dependencies can create common-mode failure across otherwise separate services."],"tradeoffs":["Detailed blast-radius analysis requires maintained dependency information."],"decision":"Include aggregate dependency impact in governance of critical shared platforms.","rationale":"Platform risk cannot be evaluated independently from its consumers.","consequences":["Platform changes may require system-level review","Consumer concentration becomes a governance signal"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain dependency graph","Track criticality","Identify concentration points"],"verification_method":"Compare documented dependency impact with controlled failure scenarios where available.","outcome_learning":"Update blast-radius models after incidents and major architecture changes.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B65-R01","D55-B65-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B65-R01","D55-B65-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B65-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance","concept":"Platform capacity governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should governance address capacity risk in shared platform capabilities?","answer":"Capacity governance should connect platform demand to consumer growth, resource limits, scaling mechanisms, saturation indicators, and capacity ownership. Critical platforms should have defined thresholds and escalation mechanisms rather than relying solely on reactive scaling.","context":"A platform can become a shared capacity bottleneck as consumer adoption increases.","objective":"Prevent shared capacity constraints from becoming systemic failures.","requirements":["Identify capacity drivers","Define saturation indicators","Assign capacity ownership","Plan growth and scaling"],"constraints":["Demand can be nonlinear","Consumer forecasts may be uncertain"],"prerequisites":["Usage metrics","Capacity model","Consumer inventory"],"dependencies":["Platform reliability","Architecture metrics"],"alternatives":["Reactive scaling","Scheduled capacity planning","Demand-driven capacity governance"],"evidence":["Shared capacity constraints can propagate to many dependent systems."],"tradeoffs":["Proactive capacity planning can increase reserved resources while reducing systemic risk."],"decision":"Use demand- and risk-driven capacity governance for critical shared platforms.","rationale":"Capacity risk should be managed before saturation becomes a system-wide incident.","consequences":["Consumer growth becomes a platform governance input","Capacity assumptions require periodic review"],"implementation_implications":["Track platform utilization","Model growth scenarios","Define capacity escalation thresholds"],"verification_method":"Review capacity forecasts against observed demand and saturation events.","outcome_learning":"Use capacity incidents and forecast errors to improve models.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B65-R02","D55-B65-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B65-R02","D55-B65-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B65-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance","concept":"Platform failure-mode governance","knowledge_type":"RISK_GOVERNANCE","question":"Which failure modes should be explicitly governed for critical platform capabilities?","answer":"Governance should consider total outage, partial degradation, incorrect responses, stale data, capacity exhaustion, dependency failure, authentication failure, configuration errors, upgrade failures, and recovery-state inconsistencies when these modes are relevant to the platform.","context":"Availability-only governance can miss failures where the platform remains reachable but behaves incorrectly or degrades dependent systems.","objective":"Ensure governance reflects realistic platform failure behavior.","requirements":["Identify meaningful failure modes","Map affected consumers","Define detection and response expectations"],"constraints":["Failure modes differ by capability"],"prerequisites":["Platform architecture","Consumer dependency analysis"],"dependencies":["Risk governance","Observability governance"],"alternatives":["Availability-only governance","Generic failure checklist","Context-specific failure-mode governance"],"evidence":["Reachability does not imply correctness, consistency, or acceptable performance."],"tradeoffs":["Broader failure analysis increases governance effort but provides stronger architectural risk visibility."],"decision":"Use context-specific failure-mode analysis for critical shared platforms.","rationale":"Governance should reflect actual failure semantics rather than a single availability measure.","consequences":["Platform reviews consider behavioral degradation","Testing becomes more scenario-oriented"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain failure-mode catalog","Define detection and recovery expectations","Exercise critical failure scenarios"],"verification_method":"Review platform failure-mode coverage and compare it with observed incidents and controlled tests.","outcome_learning":"Add newly observed failure modes to governance controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B65-R01","D55-B65-R02"],"relationships":["D55-B65-R01","D55-B65-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B65-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B65","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance","concept":"Platform recovery dependency governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should recovery dependencies be governed separately from normal runtime dependencies?","answer":"Recovery may depend on different services, credentials, storage, personnel, or operational paths than normal execution. Governance should identify these recovery dependencies because a platform can be operationally dependent on components that are unavailable during the same failure scenario.","context":"Recovery processes can fail when their own dependencies share the original failure domain.","objective":"Prevent recovery plans from relying on unavailable or circular dependencies.","requirements":["Identify recovery dependencies","Analyze common failure domains","Define independent recovery paths where required"],"constraints":["Recovery environments may have reduced capabilities"],"prerequisites":["Failure-mode analysis","Recovery procedures"],"dependencies":["Resilience governance","Incident governance"],"alternatives":["Runtime dependency analysis only","Separate recovery dependency analysis","Integrated dependency analysis"],"evidence":["Recovery dependencies can create hidden circular or common-mode failure paths."],"tradeoffs":["Maintaining recovery dependency information increases governance effort but improves recoverability analysis."],"decision":"Govern recovery dependencies explicitly for critical platforms.","rationale":"Recoverability is an architectural property that cannot be inferred solely from normal runtime dependencies.","consequences":["Recovery architecture becomes a governance artifact","Recovery testing becomes more meaningful"],"implementation_implications":["Map recovery dependencies","Identify independent recovery paths","Test recovery under dependency loss"],"verification_method":"Perform recovery exercises with documented recovery dependencies and compare actual behavior with the architecture model.","outcome_learning":"Update recovery dependency models after exercises and incidents.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B65-R01","D55-B65-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B65-R01","D55-B65-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B66-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Lifecycle Governance","concept":"Platform lifecycle entry criteria","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should be required before a new shared platform capability becomes an organizational standard?","answer":"Entry criteria should include a defined problem, target consumers, supported use cases, ownership, operational model, security requirements, lifecycle plan, measurable value hypothesis, and evidence that the capability is sufficiently mature for its intended scope.","context":"Organizations can prematurely standardize immature platform capabilities and create widespread dependency on them.","objective":"Prevent immature capabilities from becoming mandatory architecture standards.","requirements":["Define intended scope","Establish ownership","Provide operational readiness","Define lifecycle and support model","State measurable value hypothesis"],"constraints":["Early adoption may be necessary for experimentation","Maturity expectations vary by capability criticality"],"prerequisites":["Platform capability definition","Architecture review"],"dependencies":["Reference architecture","Technology lifecycle governance"],"alternatives":["Immediate standardization","Pilot then standardize","Optional platform until mature"],"evidence":["Standardization multiplies the impact of platform defects and design limitations."],"tradeoffs":["Waiting for maturity can slow adoption but reduces organization-wide coupling to immature technology."],"decision":"Require explicit entry criteria proportional to platform criticality before standardization.","rationale":"The consequences of standardization justify higher evidence requirements than ordinary experimentation.","consequences":["Experimental capabilities can remain outside mandatory standards","Maturity becomes a governance dimension"],"implementation_implications":["Define platform maturity criteria","Require evidence packages","Record standardization decision rationale"],"verification_method":"Review candidate platforms against entry criteria before standard status is granted.","outcome_learning":"Compare predicted benefits with post-adoption outcomes.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B66-R02","D55-B66-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B66-R02","D55-B66-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B66-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Lifecycle Governance","concept":"Platform maturity stages","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why should platform governance distinguish experimental, supported, standard, and retiring capability states?","answer":"Lifecycle states communicate different expectations for suitability, support, stability, compatibility, and investment. Treating all platform capabilities as equally mature hides important governance information.","context":"A platform may evolve from prototype to organizational standard and eventually toward retirement.","objective":"Represent lifecycle maturity explicitly in architecture governance.","requirements":["Define lifecycle states","Define entry and exit criteria","Communicate support expectations"],"constraints":["Lifecycle transitions may not be linear","Critical platforms may require prolonged supported states"],"prerequisites":["Technology lifecycle policy","Platform ownership"],"dependencies":["Platform adoption governance","Technology lifecycle governance"],"alternatives":["Single active/inactive status","Version-only lifecycle","Explicit maturity states"],"evidence":["Lifecycle state affects the risk and expectations associated with platform use."],"tradeoffs":["More lifecycle states improve precision but require stronger governance maintenance."],"decision":"Use explicit lifecycle states with defined transition criteria.","rationale":"Consumers need to understand not only what a platform is but also its governance maturity.","consequences":["Architecture decisions can account for platform maturity","Retirement becomes a governed state rather than an ad hoc action"],"implementation_implications":["Expose lifecycle state in platform catalogs","Define state transition evidence","Notify consumers of lifecycle changes"],"verification_method":"Audit platform records for valid lifecycle states and transition evidence.","outcome_learning":"Review lifecycle transition outcomes and unexpected consumer impact.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B66-R01","D55-B66-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B66-R01","D55-B66-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B66-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Lifecycle Governance","concept":"Platform support boundary governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance define what support a platform provides to consumers?","answer":"Support boundaries should identify supported versions, workloads, configurations, operational responsibilities, incident responsibilities, and conditions under which consumer behavior falls outside platform support.","context":"A shared platform can have many technically possible but unsupported configurations.","objective":"Prevent unsupported usage from becoming an implicit organizational dependency.","requirements":["Define supported configurations","Define ownership boundaries","Define support exclusions","Define escalation paths"],"constraints":["Support scope must remain maintainable","Consumer needs may evolve"],"prerequisites":["Platform capability definition","Operational model"],"dependencies":["Platform boundary governance","Incident governance"],"alternatives":["Unlimited support","Best-effort support","Explicit support boundary"],"evidence":["Unsupported configurations increase diagnostic uncertainty and operational burden."],"tradeoffs":["Narrow support scope reduces platform complexity but may constrain consumer options."],"decision":"Govern support boundaries explicitly and review them as platform scope evolves.","rationale":"Supportability is an architectural property of shared capabilities.","consequences":["Consumers can make informed architecture decisions","Platform teams can control operational scope"],"implementation_implications":["Publish supported configurations","Validate architecture requests against support boundaries","Track recurring unsupported use"],"verification_method":"Audit consumer configurations against published support criteria.","outcome_learning":"Use support exceptions and incidents to refine supported scope.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B66-R02","D55-B66-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B66-R02","D55-B66-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B66-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Lifecycle Governance","concept":"Platform upgrade governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should architecture governance require for major platform upgrades?","answer":"Major upgrades should assess compatibility, consumer impact, operational changes, security implications, migration requirements, rollback or recovery options, and retirement timelines for previous versions where applicable.","context":"A shared platform upgrade can affect many independently owned consumers.","objective":"Control systemic risk created by platform lifecycle changes.","requirements":["Identify affected consumers","Assess compatibility","Define migration path","Assess rollback or recovery"],"constraints":["Consumers may have incompatible upgrade schedules","Rollback may be difficult after data or state changes"],"prerequisites":["Consumer inventory","Version policy","Dependency graph"],"dependencies":["Technology lifecycle governance","Change governance"],"alternatives":["Consumer-by-consumer unmanaged upgrades","Mandatory synchronized upgrade","Staged governed upgrade"],"evidence":["Shared dependencies amplify the impact of compatibility-breaking changes."],"tradeoffs":["Staged upgrades reduce disruption but prolong coexistence complexity."],"decision":"Use impact-based staged governance for major shared-platform upgrades.","rationale":"Upgrade risk depends on consumer impact rather than version number alone.","consequences":["Upgrade plans require consumer evidence","Legacy versions require explicit lifecycle treatment"],"implementation_implications":["Track consumer compatibility","Define migration windows","Monitor post-upgrade behavior"],"verification_method":"Validate upgrade compatibility using representative consumers and documented migration criteria.","outcome_learning":"Review upgrade failures and migration delays to improve future upgrade governance.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B66-R03","D55-B66-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B66-R03","D55-B66-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B66-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B66","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Lifecycle Governance","concept":"Platform retirement entry criteria","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What evidence should trigger formal retirement governance for a platform capability?","answer":"Retirement consideration should be triggered by factors such as strategic replacement, unsustainable support burden, security or compatibility constraints, declining justified demand, or evidence that the capability no longer satisfies its intended architectural role.","context":"Platform retirement should be a governed architectural decision rather than simply an operational shutdown.","objective":"Identify defensible triggers for platform retirement analysis.","requirements":["Define retirement triggers","Assess consumer impact","Identify replacement or migration options","Assess residual obligations"],"constraints":["Legacy consumers may depend on the capability","Replacement may not provide equivalent behavior"],"prerequisites":["Lifecycle state","Consumer inventory","Platform outcome data"],"dependencies":["Technology lifecycle governance","Dependency governance"],"alternatives":["Time-based retirement","Condition-based retirement","No formal retirement process"],"evidence":["Platform capabilities accumulate dependencies that can make retirement consequential."],"tradeoffs":["Early retirement can reduce maintenance burden but increase migration cost or architectural risk."],"decision":"Use explicit evidence-based retirement triggers followed by impact analysis.","rationale":"Retirement requires evidence that the capability's continued existence is no longer justified or sustainable.","consequences":["Retirement becomes reviewable","Consumers receive structured migration expectations"],"implementation_implications":["Monitor lifecycle signals","Record retirement rationale","Define migration and shutdown stages"],"verification_method":"Audit retirement decisions for trigger evidence, impact analysis, and migration readiness.","outcome_learning":"Compare predicted retirement costs and benefits with actual outcomes.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B66-R01","D55-B66-R02"],"relationships":["D55-B66-R01","D55-B66-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B67-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance Automation","concept":"Automated platform conformance checks","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_AUTOMATION","question":"Which platform governance controls are suitable for automated enforcement?","answer":"Controls are strong candidates for automation when they have deterministic inputs, explicit rules, reliable evidence sources, and low ambiguity. Examples include approved versions, required metadata, dependency restrictions, configuration constraints, and mandatory security properties.","context":"Automation can reduce repetitive architecture review effort but cannot replace judgment for ambiguous decisions.","objective":"Identify governance controls that can be enforced consistently through tooling.","requirements":["Define deterministic rule","Identify authoritative evidence","Define failure behavior","Provide explainable findings"],"constraints":["Architecture evidence may be incomplete","Some decisions require contextual judgment"],"prerequisites":["Governance rules","Machine-readable architecture metadata"],"dependencies":["Policy-as-code","Architecture repository"],"alternatives":["Manual review","Fully automated approval","Hybrid automated control"],"evidence":["Deterministic controls can be checked more consistently by automation than by repeated manual review."],"tradeoffs":["Automation improves consistency but can enforce incorrect or outdated rules if governance metadata is stale."],"decision":"Automate deterministic controls while preserving human review for contextual decisions.","rationale":"Automation should target repeatable evidence-based governance rather than ambiguous architectural judgment.","consequences":["Routine checks become faster","Rule maintenance becomes a governance responsibility"],"implementation_implications":["Version governance rules","Record evidence sources","Provide explainable violations","Define fail-open or fail-closed behavior deliberately"],"verification_method":"Test governance rules against compliant, non-compliant, and ambiguous cases.","outcome_learning":"Track false positives, missed violations, and rule maintenance effort.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B67-R02","D55-B67-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B67-R02","D55-B67-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B67-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance Automation","concept":"Governance evidence freshness","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why must automated architecture governance validate the freshness of its evidence?","answer":"A correct rule applied to stale architecture metadata can produce incorrect governance decisions. Automated governance should therefore track evidence timestamps, versions, ownership, provenance, and freshness requirements where those properties affect decision validity.","context":"Architecture repositories and dependency inventories can become outdated as systems evolve.","objective":"Prevent stale evidence from being treated as current architectural truth.","requirements":["Track evidence provenance","Track freshness","Define acceptable staleness","Detect outdated evidence"],"constraints":["Not all architecture facts change at the same rate","Some evidence is inherently historical"],"prerequisites":["Evidence metadata","Architecture repository"],"dependencies":["Governance automation","Architecture catalog"],"alternatives":["Assume repository freshness","Manual freshness checks","Machine-enforced freshness metadata"],"evidence":["Governance conclusions depend on the validity of the evidence used to derive them."],"tradeoffs":["Freshness enforcement adds metadata and maintenance requirements."],"decision":"Treat evidence freshness as an explicit governance property where stale information can alter decisions.","rationale":"Automated governance is only as reliable as its evidence.","consequences":["Governance findings can include evidence-quality status","Stale records require remediation or qualified interpretation"],"implementation_implications":["Store evidence timestamps and source versions","Define freshness policies","Surface stale dependencies in governance output"],"verification_method":"Inject stale evidence into representative governance evaluations and verify that the system identifies or qualifies it.","outcome_learning":"Track decisions affected by stale architecture evidence.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B67-R01","D55-B67-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B67-R01","D55-B67-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B67-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance Automation","concept":"Explainable governance findings","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_AUTOMATION","question":"What information should an automated architecture governance violation provide?","answer":"A governance finding should identify the violated rule, affected architecture object, evidence used, rule version, severity or impact classification where defined, responsible owner, remediation expectation, and uncertainty or evidence-quality limitations.","context":"Opaque automated findings are difficult to review, challenge, correct, or audit.","objective":"Make governance automation actionable and auditable.","requirements":["Identify rule","Identify evidence","Identify affected object","Provide remediation context","Expose uncertainty"],"constraints":["Evidence may be incomplete","Severity classification may require contextual judgment"],"prerequisites":["Versioned governance rules","Evidence repository"],"dependencies":["Governance automation","Auditability"],"alternatives":["Opaque pass/fail output","Detailed explainable findings","Manual interpretation only"],"evidence":["Governance decisions need traceability to be reviewed and corrected."],"tradeoffs":["Detailed findings require more implementation and metadata."],"decision":"Require evidence-linked and explainable governance findings.","rationale":"Governance automation should support human verification rather than merely produce a gate result.","consequences":["Rules and evidence become auditable","Disputed findings can be investigated"],"implementation_implications":["Store rule identifiers and versions","Link findings to evidence","Record uncertainty"],"verification_method":"Review findings for traceability from violation to rule and source evidence.","outcome_learning":"Track disputed findings and root causes of false positives.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B67-R01","D55-B67-R02"],"relationships":["D55-B67-R01","D55-B67-R02"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B67-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance Automation","concept":"Governance policy versioning","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should architecture governance policies be versioned independently from the systems they govern?","answer":"Policy changes can alter conformance results without changing the governed architecture. Independent policy versioning preserves historical decision context and makes it possible to distinguish architecture changes from governance-rule changes.","context":"Architecture conformance records may be reviewed months after their original evaluation.","objective":"Preserve temporal accuracy of governance decisions.","requirements":["Version policy definitions","Record effective dates","Associate findings with policy versions","Preserve historical policy state"],"constraints":["Policies may evolve frequently","Historical evaluation may need reproducibility"],"prerequisites":["Governance rule repository","Decision records"],"dependencies":["Auditability","Policy-as-code"],"alternatives":["Current policy only","Inline policy definitions","Independent versioned governance policies"],"evidence":["Historical conformance cannot be interpreted correctly if the governing policy version is unknown."],"tradeoffs":["Versioning increases repository complexity but substantially improves traceability."],"decision":"Version governance policies independently and record their effective periods.","rationale":"Governance evidence must be interpreted using the rules that were actually applicable at the time.","consequences":["Historical reviews become reproducible","Policy changes become auditable"],"implementation_implications":["Assign immutable policy versions","Store effective dates","Attach policy version to governance results"],"verification_method":"Re-evaluate historical findings using their recorded policy versions and compare with current-policy results.","outcome_learning":"Track governance changes that materially alter architecture conformance.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B67-R01","D55-B67-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B67-R01","D55-B67-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B67-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B67","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Platform Governance Automation","concept":"Governance automation failure handling","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_AUTOMATION","question":"How should automated architecture governance behave when its evidence or evaluation engine fails?","answer":"Failure behavior should be explicitly governed based on risk. Critical controls may require fail-closed treatment, while advisory controls may permit continuation with a visible uncertainty state. The system should never silently convert evaluation failure into compliance.","context":"Governance tooling itself can experience unavailable evidence, parsing errors, rule failures, or incomplete architecture metadata.","objective":"Prevent governance evaluation failures from being mistaken for successful compliance.","requirements":["Distinguish pass from evaluation failure","Define fail behavior by control criticality","Record evaluation errors","Expose uncertainty"],"constraints":["Blocking all failures may impede delivery","Allowing all failures can weaken critical controls"],"prerequisites":["Control criticality classification","Governance automation design"],"dependencies":["Security governance","Policy-as-code","Auditability"],"alternatives":["Always fail-open","Always fail-closed","Risk-based failure behavior"],"evidence":["An unavailable control cannot establish compliance merely because no violation was produced."],"tradeoffs":["Risk-based handling balances continuity with control strength but requires explicit classification."],"decision":"Use risk-based failure handling while preserving a distinct non-compliance/unknown state for unevaluated controls.","rationale":"Evaluation failure and compliance are different states.","consequences":["Governance results become multi-state rather than binary","Critical controls can block when evidence is unavailable"],"implementation_implications":["Define evaluation status separately from compliance status","Record engine and evidence failures","Prevent silent pass behavior"],"verification_method":"Simulate rule-engine and evidence-source failures and verify that compliance is not incorrectly reported as successful.","outcome_learning":"Track evaluation failures and resulting governance delays or incidents.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B67-R02","D55-B67-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B67-R02","D55-B67-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B68-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Data-Intensive Systems","concept":"Data architecture governance boundary","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What data architecture concerns should be governed at organizational boundaries?","answer":"Governance should address ownership, classification, lifecycle, access, integrity expectations, authoritative sources, sharing boundaries, retention, and interoperability for data that crosses system or domain boundaries.","context":"Data can outlive applications and may be reused across independently governed systems.","objective":"Prevent ambiguous data authority and uncontrolled cross-system dependencies.","requirements":["Define data ownership","Identify authoritative sources","Define sharing constraints","Govern lifecycle and access"],"constraints":["Data may have multiple consumers","Ownership can differ from technical storage ownership"],"prerequisites":["Data inventory","Domain ownership"],"dependencies":["Data governance","Security governance"],"alternatives":["Storage-centric ownership","Domain-centric ownership","Explicit data stewardship"],"evidence":["Technical storage location does not necessarily establish semantic or authoritative ownership."],"tradeoffs":["Explicit data governance increases coordination effort but reduces semantic ambiguity."],"decision":"Govern data according to semantic ownership and architectural responsibility rather than storage location alone.","rationale":"Data architecture crosses application and infrastructure boundaries.","consequences":["Data ownership becomes an architecture concern","Shared data requires explicit governance"],"implementation_implications":["Record authoritative sources","Map consumers","Define lifecycle and access policies"],"verification_method":"Audit critical shared datasets for ownership, authority, lifecycle, and access metadata.","outcome_learning":"Use data incidents and ownership disputes to refine governance boundaries.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B68-R02","D55-B68-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B68-R02","D55-B68-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B68-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Data-Intensive Systems","concept":"Authoritative data source governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should architecture governance identify authoritative data sources?","answer":"Without an identified authoritative source, multiple systems may independently modify or interpret the same business fact, creating reconciliation conflicts and unclear ownership. Governance should identify which source is authoritative for each governed fact or explicitly acknowledge that no single source exists.","context":"Distributed systems frequently duplicate data for performance, availability, or integration purposes.","objective":"Distinguish authoritative state from derived or replicated representations.","requirements":["Identify authoritative source","Distinguish derived copies","Define synchronization expectations"],"constraints":["Some domains intentionally use multiple authorities","Authority can be scoped by business fact"],"prerequisites":["Data domain model","Ownership model"],"dependencies":["Data governance","Integration governance"],"alternatives":["No declared authority","Storage-based authority","Fact-level authoritative ownership"],"evidence":["Replication does not automatically transfer semantic authority."],"tradeoffs":["Explicit authority simplifies reasoning but may expose unresolved domain ownership questions."],"decision":"Define authority at the level of governed facts or datasets where practical.","rationale":"Architectural reasoning requires knowing which representation is authoritative.","consequences":["Derived copies can be treated differently from source data","Conflict resolution becomes more explicit"],"implementation_implications":["Record authority metadata","Document synchronization semantics","Define conflict resolution"],"verification_method":"Trace representative data values from consumers to their declared authoritative source.","outcome_learning":"Track reconciliation conflicts and incorrect assumptions about authority.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B68-R01","D55-B68-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B68-R01","D55-B68-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B68-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Data-Intensive Systems","concept":"Derived data governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance distinguish derived data from authoritative data?","answer":"Derived data should identify its source dependencies, transformation logic, freshness expectations, lineage, and permitted uses so consumers do not mistake a computed representation for authoritative state.","context":"Caches, materialized views, analytics datasets, indexes, and transformed events can all represent derived information.","objective":"Make derivation and freshness semantics visible to consumers.","requirements":["Record source lineage","Define transformation purpose","Define freshness semantics","Identify permitted use"],"constraints":["Transformations may be complex or distributed"],"prerequisites":["Data lineage model","Source authority"],"dependencies":["Data governance","Observability"],"alternatives":["Treat all stored data equally","Explicit derived-data classification"],"evidence":["Derived data can become stale or semantically different from its source."],"tradeoffs":["Lineage governance increases metadata requirements but reduces misuse."],"decision":"Govern derived data as a distinct architectural category with lineage and freshness information.","rationale":"Consumers need to understand the semantic and temporal limits of derived representations.","consequences":["Data catalogs become more informative","Architectural decisions can account for freshness and lineage"],"implementation_implications":["Record derivation relationships","Track transformation versions","Expose freshness metadata"],"verification_method":"Trace representative derived datasets to their sources and validate documented freshness behavior.","outcome_learning":"Review incidents involving stale or misinterpreted derived data.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B68-R02","D55-B68-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B68-R02","D55-B68-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B68-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Data-Intensive Systems","concept":"Cross-domain data sharing governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should governance require before data is shared across domain boundaries?","answer":"Governance should establish purpose, ownership, classification, access authority, semantic compatibility, lifecycle expectations, and responsibility for changes before enabling significant cross-domain data sharing.","context":"Data sharing can create long-lived dependencies between domains with different responsibilities.","objective":"Prevent accidental coupling through uncontrolled data sharing.","requirements":["Define sharing purpose","Identify owner","Define access boundaries","Document semantic expectations","Define lifecycle"],"constraints":["Data may contain sensitive or regulated information","Consumers may evolve independently"],"prerequisites":["Data classification","Ownership model"],"dependencies":["Security governance","Privacy governance","Integration governance"],"alternatives":["Unrestricted sharing","Case-by-case manual sharing","Governed data-sharing contracts"],"evidence":["Shared data creates both dependency and governance obligations beyond technical transport."],"tradeoffs":["Governed sharing slows some integrations but reduces uncontrolled coupling and misuse."],"decision":"Require explicit governance for significant cross-domain data sharing.","rationale":"Data sharing is an architectural dependency and should be treated accordingly.","consequences":["Data contracts become governance artifacts","Consumers must understand ownership and lifecycle"],"implementation_implications":["Record data-sharing relationships","Define access and usage policies","Review changes to shared data semantics"],"verification_method":"Audit representative cross-domain datasets for documented purpose, ownership, access, and lifecycle.","outcome_learning":"Track data misuse, semantic conflicts, and unexpected dependency growth.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B68-R01","D55-B68-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B68-R01","D55-B68-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B68-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B68","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Data-Intensive Systems","concept":"Data lineage governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should lineage be part of architecture governance for critical data?","answer":"Lineage allows architects and operators to understand where data originates, how it is transformed, where it flows, and which systems depend on it. For critical data, this supports impact analysis, incident diagnosis, change governance, and validation of authoritative sources.","context":"Data may pass through multiple transformations before reaching a consumer.","objective":"Make data dependencies traceable across architectural boundaries.","requirements":["Identify source and destination","Represent transformations where relevant","Maintain ownership","Support impact analysis"],"constraints":["Lineage can be incomplete in dynamic systems","Transformation logic may change frequently"],"prerequisites":["Data inventory","Integration map"],"dependencies":["Data governance","Change impact analysis"],"alternatives":["No lineage","Manual lineage documents","Machine-assisted lineage with governance"],"evidence":["Without lineage, changes to upstream data can produce hidden downstream consequences."],"tradeoffs":["Lineage maintenance has cost and may require instrumentation or tooling."],"decision":"Require lineage for critical data flows proportional to architectural impact.","rationale":"The value of lineage increases with data criticality and dependency complexity.","consequences":["Data changes can be assessed more systematically","Incident diagnosis gains dependency context"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain source-to-consumer relationships","Version transformation definitions","Track lineage confidence and freshness"],"verification_method":"Trace critical data flows through representative transformations and compare observed paths with recorded lineage.","outcome_learning":"Update lineage when incidents reveal undocumented paths.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B68-R02","D55-B68-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B68-R02","D55-B68-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B69-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Multi-Tenant Systems","concept":"Tenant isolation governance","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should architecture governance require for multi-tenant systems?","answer":"Governance should explicitly define tenant isolation boundaries, data ownership, authorization scope, resource isolation expectations, shared infrastructure risks, observability requirements, and controls against cross-tenant access or impact.","context":"Multi-tenant architectures intentionally share infrastructure while requiring logical or physical separation of tenant concerns.","objective":"Make tenant isolation an explicit architectural property rather than an assumed implementation detail.","requirements":["Define isolation model","Define authorization boundaries","Define data separation expectations","Assess resource contention"],"constraints":["Tenants may have different risk and capacity requirements"],"prerequisites":["Tenant model","Security architecture","Data architecture"],"dependencies":["Security governance","Data governance","Capacity governance"],"alternatives":["Physical isolation","Logical isolation","Hybrid isolation"],"evidence":["Shared infrastructure creates opportunities for isolation failures and resource contention."],"tradeoffs":["Stronger isolation can increase cost and operational complexity."],"decision":"Govern tenant isolation according to explicit security, data, and resource requirements.","rationale":"Isolation strength should be derived from tenant risk and system requirements.","consequences":["Tenant boundaries become architecture review concerns","Shared resources require explicit controls"],"implementation_implications":["Document tenant isolation model","Test cross-tenant access controls","Assess noisy-neighbor risks"],"verification_method":"Perform authorization, data isolation, and resource contention tests appropriate to the tenancy model.","outcome_learning":"Use cross-tenant findings and resource incidents to improve governance controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B69-R02","D55-B69-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B69-R02","D55-B69-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B69-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Multi-Tenant Systems","concept":"Tenant data boundary governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should governance verify that tenant data boundaries remain consistent across storage and processing layers?","answer":"Governance should trace tenant identity and authorization across ingestion, storage, processing, caching, messaging, analytics, backups, and deletion paths where applicable. A boundary is not complete if one downstream layer can bypass the tenant constraint.","context":"Tenant data may move through multiple infrastructure components after initial authorization.","objective":"Prevent fragmented tenant isolation controls.","requirements":["Trace tenant identity","Map data flows","Define authorization enforcement points","Include derived and temporary data"],"constraints":["Different infrastructure layers may implement isolation differently"],"prerequisites":["Data flow map","Tenant model","Authorization model"],"dependencies":["Data governance","Security governance"],"alternatives":["Storage-only isolation","Application-only isolation","End-to-end tenant boundary governance"],"evidence":["Data isolation can fail at downstream processing or caching layers even when primary storage is correctly partitioned."],"tradeoffs":["End-to-end governance is more comprehensive but requires broader architecture evidence."],"decision":"Govern tenant boundaries across the full data lifecycle rather than a single storage layer.","rationale":"Tenant isolation is a system property, not merely a database property.","consequences":["Derived data and caches become governance concerns","Data-flow mapping becomes important"],"implementation_implications":["Track tenant propagation","Review caches and asynchronous flows","Include backup and deletion paths"],"verification_method":"Trace representative tenant data through all major processing stages and test unauthorized cross-tenant access.","outcome_learning":"Record isolation defects by architectural layer to identify systemic weaknesses.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B69-R01","D55-B69-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B69-R01","D55-B69-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B69-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Multi-Tenant Systems","concept":"Tenant resource isolation governance","knowledge_type":"RISK_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should governance address resource contention between tenants?","answer":"Governance should identify shared resource limits, tenant allocation rules, fairness expectations, isolation mechanisms, overload behavior, and critical-tenant requirements where resource contention can affect reliability or performance.","context":"Logical data isolation does not prevent one tenant from consuming shared compute, storage, network, or queue capacity.","objective":"Control noisy-neighbor and shared-resource risks.","requirements":["Identify shared resources","Define tenant resource policies","Define overload behavior","Monitor contention"],"constraints":["Perfect physical isolation may be impractical","Demand can vary substantially between tenants"],"prerequisites":["Capacity model","Tenant criticality model"],"dependencies":["Platform capacity governance","Reliability governance"],"alternatives":["Unlimited sharing","Hard resource quotas","Adaptive resource governance"],"evidence":["Shared resource exhaustion can affect multiple tenants even when authorization and data isolation remain correct."],"tradeoffs":["Hard quotas improve isolation but can reduce utilization efficiency."],"decision":"Govern resource isolation according to workload and tenant criticality requirements.","rationale":"Resource isolation is a distinct property from data isolation.","consequences":["Tenant capacity becomes an architectural governance concern","Overload behavior must be explicit"],"implementation_implications":["Define quotas or prioritization where required","Monitor tenant resource usage","Test contention scenarios"],"verification_method":"Execute representative contention scenarios and verify defined isolation and prioritization behavior.","outcome_learning":"Use contention incidents to adjust capacity policies and tenant classifications.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B69-R01","D55-B69-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B69-R01","D55-B69-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B69-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Multi-Tenant Systems","concept":"Tenant-specific architecture exceptions","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should a tenant receive architecture treatment that differs from the default multi-tenant model?","answer":"Tenant-specific treatment should be justified by documented security, regulatory, performance, availability, data residency, or contractual requirements that the default architecture cannot satisfy. The exception should define its scope and operational consequences.","context":"Some tenants may require stronger isolation or different processing characteristics.","objective":"Prevent uncontrolled tenant-specific divergence while supporting legitimate requirements.","requirements":["Document tenant-specific requirement","Assess architectural impact","Define scope and lifecycle","Identify operational consequences"],"constraints":["Tenant-specific variants increase architecture complexity"],"prerequisites":["Tenant requirements","Default architecture definition"],"dependencies":["Exception governance","Security and data governance"],"alternatives":["Uniform architecture","Tenant-specific exceptions","Separate deployment architecture"],"evidence":["Different requirements can legitimately require different architectural treatment."],"tradeoffs":["Specialization can satisfy critical requirements but increases operational and maintenance complexity."],"decision":"Permit bounded tenant-specific architecture only when justified by explicit requirements.","rationale":"Exceptions should solve identifiable requirements rather than arise from unmanaged customization.","consequences":["Tenant variants require lifecycle governance","Common architecture remains the default"],"implementation_implications":["Track tenant-specific architecture variants","Define support boundaries","Review exceptions periodically"],"verification_method":"Audit tenant-specific variants for documented requirements and conformance to exception boundaries.","outcome_learning":"Measure recurring tenant exceptions as signals for architecture evolution.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B69-R01","D55-B69-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B69-R01","D55-B69-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B69-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B69","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Multi-Tenant Systems","concept":"Tenant offboarding governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What architecture governance evidence should be required when a tenant is offboarded?","answer":"Offboarding governance should address access revocation, data deletion or retention obligations, derived data, backups, caches, asynchronous processing, external integrations, credentials, and confirmation that tenant-specific resources are no longer accessible.","context":"Tenant data and access may persist beyond the primary application record.","objective":"Ensure tenant lifecycle termination is architecturally complete.","requirements":["Revoke access","Identify data locations","Handle derived and temporary data","Address backups and integrations","Verify completion"],"constraints":["Some data may have mandatory retention","Distributed deletion may be asynchronous"],"prerequisites":["Tenant data map","Lifecycle policy","Access model"],"dependencies":["Data lifecycle governance","Security governance","Integration governance"],"alternatives":["Application-level deletion only","Full lifecycle offboarding","Manual cleanup"],"evidence":["Distributed tenant state can persist in caches, backups, analytics, or downstream integrations."],"tradeoffs":["Comprehensive offboarding requires broader coordination but reduces residual access and data risks."],"decision":"Govern tenant offboarding across the full architectural data and access lifecycle.","rationale":"Tenant termination is a system-wide lifecycle transition rather than a single database operation.","consequences":["Offboarding becomes auditable","Downstream dependencies must participate"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain tenant data-flow inventory","Track deletion or retention evidence","Verify access revocation"],"verification_method":"Perform representative offboarding exercises and verify access, data, and integration state across defined boundaries.","outcome_learning":"Record residual-data and residual-access findings to improve lifecycle controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B69-R02","D55-B69-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B69-R02","D55-B69-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B70-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Regulated and High-Risk Systems","concept":"Risk-proportionate architecture governance","knowledge_type":"RISK_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance scale its rigor for high-risk systems?","answer":"Governance rigor should increase with potential impact, uncertainty, dependency complexity, security sensitivity, regulatory obligations, and difficulty of recovery. High-risk systems should require stronger evidence, explicit ownership, traceability, and validation than low-impact systems.","context":"Applying identical governance depth to every architecture can either overburden low-risk systems or under-govern high-risk systems.","objective":"Align governance effort with architectural risk.","requirements":["Classify architecture risk","Define proportional controls","Require stronger evidence for higher risk"],"constraints":["Risk classifications can change over time","Risk may be multidimensional"],"prerequisites":["Risk model","Architecture inventory"],"dependencies":["Security governance","Reliability governance","Compliance governance"],"alternatives":["Uniform governance","Risk-proportionate governance","Fully decentralized governance"],"evidence":["Governance resources are limited and should focus on decisions with greater potential consequences."],"tradeoffs":["Risk-based governance requires a credible risk classification model."],"decision":"Use risk-proportionate governance with explicit escalation for high-impact architecture.","rationale":"Governance depth should reflect consequence and uncertainty rather than organizational preference alone.","consequences":["Low-risk changes can move faster","High-risk architecture receives stronger review and evidence requirements"],"implementation_implications":["Define risk dimensions","Assign governance levels","Review risk classifications when architecture changes"],"verification_method":"Sample architectures across risk classes and verify that governance requirements are proportionate and consistently applied.","outcome_learning":"Compare incidents and governance findings across risk classes to calibrate the model.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B70-R02","D55-B70-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B70-R02","D55-B70-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B70-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Regulated and High-Risk Systems","concept":"Architecture evidence sufficiency","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should governance determine whether evidence is sufficient for a high-impact architecture decision?","answer":"Evidence sufficiency should be assessed against the decision's material risks, uncertainty, reversibility, affected boundaries, and verification needs. A decision should not be treated as sufficiently evidenced merely because a review document exists.","context":"High-impact architecture decisions may require experiments, dependency analysis, security evidence, performance measurements, or operational validation.","objective":"Define evidence sufficiency according to decision risk rather than documentation volume.","requirements":["Identify decision risks","Define required evidence types","Record evidence limitations","Distinguish assumptions from verified facts"],"constraints":["Some evidence may be unavailable before implementation","Experiments can have limited representativeness"],"prerequisites":["Decision record","Risk classification"],"dependencies":["Architecture decisions","Architecture experiments","Governance auditability"],"alternatives":["Document-based approval","Checklist-based evidence","Risk-based evidence sufficiency"],"evidence":["Evidence quality depends on relevance and verification, not quantity alone."],"tradeoffs":["Higher evidence requirements increase decision time but reduce uncertainty for high-impact decisions."],"decision":"Evaluate evidence sufficiency against decision-specific risk and uncertainty.","rationale":"Governance should distinguish documentation from actual decision support.","consequences":["Assumptions become explicit","High-risk decisions may require empirical validation"],"implementation_implications":["Record evidence source and status","Identify unresolved assumptions","Require experiments where uncertainty materially affects the decision"],"verification_method":"Review decision records and determine whether evidence addresses the stated risks and uncertainties.","outcome_learning":"Compare predicted uncertainties with post-implementation outcomes to improve evidence standards.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B70-R01","D55-B70-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B70-R01","D55-B70-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B70-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Regulated and High-Risk Systems","concept":"Architecture verification gates","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should architecture governance require explicit verification gates before implementation proceeds?","answer":"Verification gates are appropriate when an architecture decision has material security, reliability, compatibility, data, performance, or compliance consequences that cannot be safely assumed from design documentation alone.","context":"Some architecture decisions require evidence before irreversible implementation or rollout.","objective":"Prevent high-impact assumptions from becoming deployed architecture without validation.","requirements":["Identify decisions requiring gates","Define verification evidence","Define pass, fail, and unknown states","Assign gate ownership"],"constraints":["Verification may require prototypes or controlled tests","Not every architectural property is directly testable"],"prerequisites":["Risk assessment","Decision record","Verification criteria"],"dependencies":["Architecture experiments","Change governance"],"alternatives":["Documentation-only approval","Universal verification gates","Risk-based verification gates"],"evidence":["High-impact assumptions can cause expensive failures if left unvalidated."],"tradeoffs":["Verification gates can slow delivery but provide stronger evidence for consequential decisions."],"decision":"Use risk-based verification gates for architecture properties that materially affect system outcomes.","rationale":"Verification should focus on assumptions whose failure has meaningful consequences.","consequences":["Architecture approval becomes evidence-aware","Unknown verification states remain visible"],"implementation_implications":["Define verification criteria before implementation","Record test evidence","Prevent successful approval from being inferred from missing evidence"],"verification_method":"Audit gated decisions for explicit verification evidence and outcome states.","outcome_learning":"Measure which gated assumptions later fail and whether the gate detected them.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B70-R02","D55-B70-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B70-R02","D55-B70-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B70-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Regulated and High-Risk Systems","concept":"Architecture uncertainty governance","knowledge_type":"RISK_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance treat unresolved uncertainty?","answer":"Unresolved uncertainty should be recorded explicitly with its source, affected decision, potential impact, evidence needed to reduce it, owner, and review condition. Unknown should not be silently converted into compliance or confidence.","context":"Architecture decisions often occur before complete evidence is available.","objective":"Prevent hidden assumptions from being mistaken for established facts.","requirements":["Record uncertainty","Assign owner","Define evidence-reduction action","Set reassessment condition"],"constraints":["Some uncertainty is irreducible","Evidence may be costly to obtain"],"prerequisites":["Decision record","Risk assessment"],"dependencies":["Architecture experiments","Decision governance"],"alternatives":["Ignore uncertainty","Treat uncertainty as failure","Explicit uncertainty management"],"evidence":["Architectural reasoning frequently involves incomplete information, making explicit uncertainty necessary for sound governance."],"tradeoffs":["Explicit uncertainty can make decisions appear less certain but improves intellectual honesty and follow-up."],"decision":"Represent material uncertainty explicitly and govern its reduction or acceptance.","rationale":"Unknown evidence has different meaning from verified evidence and should remain distinguishable.","consequences":["Architecture records become more transparent","Future validation work can be prioritized"],"implementation_implications":["Add uncertainty fields to decision records","Track evidence required to resolve uncertainty","Trigger reassessment when assumptions change"],"verification_method":"Review high-impact decisions for explicit uncertainty and corresponding evidence plans.","outcome_learning":"Compare recorded uncertainties with actual outcomes to improve architectural forecasting.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B70-R02","D55-B70-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B70-R02","D55-B70-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B70-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B70","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Regulated and High-Risk Systems","concept":"Governance evidence retention","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What governance evidence should be retained for high-impact architecture decisions?","answer":"Retention should preserve the decision context, applicable requirements, alternatives considered, evidence and its provenance, approval authority, exceptions, verification results, policy versions, and subsequent outcome information for as long as needed to support audit, learning, and future architectural reasoning.","context":"High-impact decisions may need to be understood long after the original architecture review.","objective":"Preserve enough historical context to reconstruct why a consequential architecture decision was made and whether it worked.","requirements":["Retain decision context","Retain evidence provenance","Retain applicable governance policy version","Retain verification and outcome evidence"],"constraints":["Retention cost","Data sensitivity","Changing organizational structures"],"prerequisites":["Architecture decision repository","Governance policy versioning"],"dependencies":["Auditability","Architecture learning","Data retention governance"],"alternatives":["Retain final decision only","Retain full decision evidence","Risk-based evidence retention"],"evidence":["A final decision without its evidence and context may be insufficient for later audit or learning."],"tradeoffs":["More complete retention improves traceability but increases storage and governance obligations."],"decision":"Use risk-proportionate retention that preserves reconstructable decision evidence for high-impact architecture.","rationale":"Architectural governance should support both accountability and organizational learning.","consequences":["Historical architecture reasoning remains usable","Evidence retention becomes part of governance lifecycle"],"implementation_implications":["Store immutable decision references","Preserve policy and evidence versions","Link decisions to later outcomes and reversals"],"verification_method":"Attempt to reconstruct representative historical architecture decisions using retained records and identify missing evidence.","outcome_learning":"Use reconstruction failures to improve future governance record requirements.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B70-R02","D55-B70-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B70-R02","D55-B70-R04"]}

{"record_id":"D55-B71-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for High-Change Environments","concept":"Governance cadence proportionality","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance cadence be determined for rapidly changing systems?","answer":"Governance cadence should reflect architecture change frequency, risk exposure, dependency volatility, and the rate at which governing assumptions can become obsolete. High-change systems require more frequent evidence refreshes without necessarily requiring full architecture reviews for every change.","context":"Fast-moving systems can invalidate architecture governance evidence faster than traditional periodic review cycles.","objective":"Keep governance evidence current without creating unnecessary review overhead.","requirements":["Assess change frequency","Identify volatile architecture evidence","Define refresh triggers","Separate evidence refresh from full decision review"],"constraints":["Frequent reviews consume engineering capacity","Not all architecture properties change at the same rate"],"prerequisites":["Architecture inventory","Change history","Risk classification"],"dependencies":["Architecture metrics","Evidence freshness governance"],"alternatives":["Fixed calendar review","Change-triggered review","Hybrid cadence"],"evidence":["Governance validity depends on the continued relevance of architecture assumptions and evidence."],"tradeoffs":["Higher cadence improves freshness but increases governance effort."],"decision":"Use a hybrid cadence combining periodic review with event-driven refresh triggers.","rationale":"Calendar-only governance can miss rapid architectural changes, while event-only governance can miss gradual drift.","consequences":["Critical evidence receives targeted refreshes","Stable architecture avoids unnecessary full reviews"],"implementation_implications":["Define refresh triggers","Track architecture volatility","Separate lightweight reassessment from full governance review"],"verification_method":"Compare architecture changes against governance refresh events and identify stale evidence that exceeded defined thresholds.","outcome_learning":"Use stale-evidence findings to calibrate review cadence.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B71-R02","D55-B71-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B71-R02","D55-B71-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B71-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for High-Change Environments","concept":"Architecture change-triggered reassessment","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Which architecture changes should trigger reassessment outside the normal governance cadence?","answer":"Triggers should include changes that cross governed boundaries, alter critical dependencies, modify security or data isolation, change important reliability assumptions, introduce incompatible contracts, materially alter technology lifecycle status, or invalidate previously verified architecture assumptions.","context":"Periodic governance reviews cannot guarantee timely response to material architecture changes.","objective":"Identify changes that require immediate or targeted governance reassessment.","requirements":["Define material-change triggers","Link triggers to affected governance controls","Record reassessment decisions"],"constraints":["Overly broad triggers create review noise","Some changes have uncertain impact"],"prerequisites":["Architecture dependency model","Risk classification","Governance policies"],"dependencies":["Change management","Conformance governance"],"alternatives":["Review every change","Review only on schedule","Risk-based change triggers"],"evidence":["Architectural consequences can occur at the moment a dependency or invariant changes."],"tradeoffs":["Targeted triggers provide responsiveness while avoiding universal review.",""],"decision":"Use explicit impact-based change triggers for governance reassessment.","rationale":"Governance should respond to architectural consequence rather than change volume alone.","consequences":["Material changes become traceable governance events","Low-impact changes remain lightweight"],"implementation_implications":["Encode trigger conditions","Connect change metadata to architecture governance","Record trigger evaluation"],"verification_method":"Evaluate representative changes and verify that material changes trigger reassessment while irrelevant changes do not.","outcome_learning":"Analyze missed and unnecessary triggers to improve governance rules.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B71-R01","D55-B71-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B71-R01","D55-B71-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B71-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for High-Change Environments","concept":"Lightweight architecture reassessment","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How can architecture governance reassess low-risk changes without repeating a full architecture review?","answer":"A lightweight reassessment can evaluate only the affected architecture invariants, dependencies, risks, and evidence freshness while preserving the original decision when its assumptions remain valid.","context":"Repeated full reviews create unnecessary governance overhead for changes with limited architectural impact.","objective":"Provide proportional governance for incremental changes.","requirements":["Identify affected decision scope","Check changed assumptions","Evaluate relevant invariants","Record reassessment result"],"constraints":["The reassessment must not conceal newly material impacts"],"prerequisites":["Original architecture decision","Impact analysis","Governance rules"],"dependencies":["Change impact governance","Architecture decision records"],"alternatives":["Full review","No review","Targeted reassessment"],"evidence":["Governance effort can be reduced when unchanged assumptions remain demonstrably valid."],"tradeoffs":["Lightweight review is efficient but depends on accurate impact analysis."],"decision":"Use targeted reassessment for changes demonstrably confined to previously governed boundaries.","rationale":"Reusing valid architecture evidence is preferable to duplicating complete reviews.","consequences":["Governance becomes more efficient","Original assumptions must remain traceable"],"implementation_implications":["Record affected decision elements","Reuse verified evidence where still applicable","Escalate if scope expands"],"verification_method":"Compare lightweight reassessments with full reviews for representative changes and check for missed material impacts.","outcome_learning":"Track escalations from lightweight reviews to full governance reviews.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B71-R02","D55-B71-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B71-R02","D55-B71-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B71-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for High-Change Environments","concept":"Architecture assumption expiry","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should material architecture assumptions have explicit review or expiry conditions?","answer":"An assumption that was reasonable when an architecture decision was made may become invalid as workload, dependencies, technology, organizational ownership, or requirements change. Governance should therefore identify material assumptions and define when they must be reassessed.","context":"Architecture decisions frequently depend on forecasts and environmental assumptions rather than permanent facts.","objective":"Prevent obsolete assumptions from silently governing current architecture.","requirements":["Identify material assumptions","Record their evidence basis","Define review triggers or expiry conditions"],"constraints":["Some assumptions are difficult to quantify","Expiry should not automatically invalidate a decision without reassessment"],"prerequisites":["Architecture decision records","Risk assessment"],"dependencies":["Evidence freshness","Architecture lifecycle governance"],"alternatives":["Permanent assumptions","Calendar-only review","Trigger-based assumption reassessment"],"evidence":["Architecture validity can change when foundational assumptions change even if implementation remains unchanged."],"tradeoffs":["Tracking assumptions adds metadata but improves decision durability.",""],"decision":"Govern material assumptions as explicit architecture dependencies with reassessment conditions.","rationale":"Architecture decisions should remain connected to the assumptions that justify them.","consequences":["Decision records become more maintainable","Architecture drift can be detected earlier"],"implementation_implications":["Record assumptions and owners","Define evidence-refresh triggers","Link assumptions to affected decisions"],"verification_method":"Audit significant decisions for material assumptions and verify that reassessment conditions are defined.","outcome_learning":"Compare expired or invalid assumptions with subsequent architecture outcomes.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B71-R03","D55-B71-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B71-R03","D55-B71-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B71-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B71","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for High-Change Environments","concept":"Governance drift detection","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance detect gradual drift in systems that change continuously?","answer":"Drift detection should compare the governed architecture baseline, declared invariants, dependencies, lifecycle states, and approved exceptions against current evidence and identify meaningful divergence.","context":"Architecture drift may occur through many individually small changes that never trigger a formal architecture decision.","objective":"Detect cumulative divergence from governed architecture.","requirements":["Maintain architecture baseline","Collect current evidence","Define drift conditions","Classify materiality"],"constraints":["Architecture evidence may be incomplete","Not every divergence is harmful"],"prerequisites":["Architecture baseline","Conformance rules","Current-state evidence"],"dependencies":["Architecture conformance","Governance automation"],"alternatives":["Periodic manual inspection","Change-by-change review","Continuous drift detection"],"evidence":["Cumulative small changes can produce architectural divergence without a single obvious decision event."],"tradeoffs":["Continuous detection increases visibility but requires reliable architecture evidence."],"decision":"Use continuous or periodic automated drift detection supplemented by targeted human review.","rationale":"Drift is a state comparison problem and should not depend solely on change records.","consequences":["Architecture conformance becomes continuously observable","Unknown evidence must remain distinguishable from compliance"],"implementation_implications":["Compare desired and observed architecture","Track evidence confidence","Generate explainable drift findings"],"verification_method":"Introduce controlled architecture deviations and verify that relevant drift controls detect them.","outcome_learning":"Classify recurring drift patterns to identify weak governance controls or unnecessary standards.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B71-R02","D55-B71-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B71-R02","D55-B71-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B72-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Legacy Systems","concept":"Legacy architecture governance proportionality","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance operate when a legacy system cannot fully satisfy current architectural standards?","answer":"Governance should distinguish mandatory safety and risk controls from aspirational modernization standards, document justified deviations, constrain further deterioration, and define a transition strategy where feasible.","context":"Legacy systems may be technically or economically unable to conform immediately to current architecture standards.","objective":"Control risk without assuming immediate replacement is possible.","requirements":["Identify non-negotiable controls","Document legacy constraints","Prevent new avoidable debt","Define modernization direction where justified"],"constraints":["Limited changeability","Unknown dependencies","High migration cost"],"prerequisites":["Legacy architecture assessment","Risk classification"],"dependencies":["Technical debt governance","Exception governance"],"alternatives":["Force immediate conformance","Ignore governance","Risk-controlled legacy governance"],"evidence":["Legacy constraints can make direct conformance infeasible while unmanaged risk remains unacceptable."],"tradeoffs":["Risk-controlled exceptions preserve continuity but may prolong architectural limitations."],"decision":"Apply risk-based governance that controls material risk while managing legacy deviations explicitly.","rationale":"Governance must account for feasibility without treating legacy status as permanent exemption.","consequences":["Legacy deviations remain visible","Modernization can be prioritized by risk and value"],"implementation_implications":["Record legacy constraints","Define compensating controls","Track transition conditions"],"verification_method":"Review legacy systems for explicit deviations, risk controls, and modernization status.","outcome_learning":"Use incidents and modernization outcomes to refine legacy governance.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B72-R02","D55-B72-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B72-R02","D55-B72-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B72-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Legacy Systems","concept":"Legacy compensating controls","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should compensating controls be used for legacy architecture deviations?","answer":"Compensating controls are appropriate when a legacy architecture cannot satisfy a required property directly but an alternative control can reduce the relevant risk to an explicitly accepted level.","context":"Some legacy systems cannot be modified safely or economically enough to implement modern controls directly.","objective":"Manage material risk while preserving operational continuity.","requirements":["Identify unmet requirement","Identify residual risk","Define compensating control","Assign ownership and review"],"constraints":["Compensating controls may be weaker or more operationally expensive","They may not provide equivalent protection"],"prerequisites":["Risk assessment","Legacy constraint analysis"],"dependencies":["Security governance","Risk governance"],"alternatives":["Direct remediation","Compensating control","System replacement"],"evidence":["Alternative controls can reduce risk when direct architectural remediation is infeasible, but residual risk remains relevant."],"tradeoffs":["Compensating controls can preserve continuity while increasing operational burden or complexity."],"decision":"Use compensating controls only when their effectiveness and residual risk are explicitly assessed.","rationale":"A workaround should not be treated as equivalent architecture without evidence.","consequences":["Residual risk remains visible","Compensating controls require lifecycle ownership"],"implementation_implications":["Document control effectiveness assumptions","Monitor control operation","Define replacement or reassessment conditions"],"verification_method":"Test the compensating control against the risk it is intended to reduce.","outcome_learning":"Measure control failures and recurring operational burden.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B72-R01","D55-B72-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B72-R01","D55-B72-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B72-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Legacy Systems","concept":"Legacy dependency containment","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should governance prevent legacy systems from creating additional architectural dependency?","answer":"Governance should identify prohibited new dependencies, require explicit review for material integration with legacy components, and prefer containment boundaries that prevent legacy constraints from propagating into new systems.","context":"New architecture can become constrained by legacy dependencies if integration decisions are made without lifecycle consideration.","objective":"Prevent legacy limitations from expanding their architectural blast radius.","requirements":["Identify legacy constraints","Govern new dependencies","Define containment boundaries","Assess lifecycle impact"],"constraints":["Some new functionality may necessarily depend on legacy systems"],"prerequisites":["Legacy dependency inventory","Target architecture"],"dependencies":["Dependency governance","Transition architecture"],"alternatives":["Unrestricted integration","Immediate replacement","Controlled legacy containment"],"evidence":["New dependencies can increase migration cost and prolong legacy constraints."],"tradeoffs":["Containment may require additional integration layers but can reduce long-term coupling."],"decision":"Use controlled dependency and containment governance for legacy integrations.","rationale":"Legacy systems should not automatically become foundational dependencies for new architecture.","consequences":["New dependencies require stronger justification","Migration boundaries remain clearer"],"implementation_implications":["Tag legacy dependencies","Review new integration paths","Prefer anti-corruption or containment boundaries where appropriate"],"verification_method":"Audit new architecture changes for newly introduced legacy dependencies.","outcome_learning":"Track growth of legacy dependency surface and migration difficulty.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B72-R01","D55-B72-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B72-R01","D55-B72-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B72-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Legacy Systems","concept":"Legacy modernization evidence governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What evidence should support a decision to modernize a legacy architecture?","answer":"Modernization governance should consider business and technical objectives, current failure modes, lifecycle constraints, dependency complexity, security or reliability risks, migration feasibility, expected benefits, and measurable success criteria.","context":"Modernization can be expensive and can introduce transitional risks of its own.","objective":"Make modernization decisions evidence-based rather than driven solely by technology age.","requirements":["Define modernization problem","Assess current-state risk","Evaluate migration feasibility","Define measurable outcomes"],"constraints":["Legacy systems may contain undocumented behavior","Benefits may be difficult to measure"],"prerequisites":["Current-state assessment","Dependency inventory"],"dependencies":["Architecture decisions","Transition architecture"],"alternatives":["Replace","Refactor incrementally","Retain with controls"],"evidence":["Technology age alone does not establish that replacement is the appropriate architecture response."],"tradeoffs":["Modernization can reduce long-term risk but introduces migration cost and transition complexity."],"decision":"Govern modernization based on documented risk, constraints, alternatives, and measurable objectives.","rationale":"Modernization is an architecture decision requiring evidence, not merely a lifecycle label.","consequences":["Some old systems may remain intentionally","Modernization efforts gain explicit success criteria"],"implementation_implications":["Define transition states","Track migration dependencies","Measure outcomes against baseline"],"verification_method":"Review modernization proposals for evidence supporting the stated problem and expected outcomes.","outcome_learning":"Compare actual modernization outcomes with the original decision assumptions.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B72-R03","D55-B72-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B72-R03","D55-B72-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B72-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B72","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Legacy Systems","concept":"Legacy retirement readiness","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance determine whether a legacy system is ready for retirement?","answer":"Retirement readiness requires evidence that required capabilities have migrated or been retired, consumers are removed, data and operational obligations are handled, dependencies are disconnected, and residual recovery requirements are addressed.","context":"Legacy retirement can fail because hidden consumers or operational dependencies remain.","objective":"Prevent premature retirement while avoiding indefinite retention without justification.","requirements":["Confirm capability migration","Confirm consumer removal","Resolve data obligations","Resolve operational dependencies"],"constraints":["Undocumented consumers may exist","Historical data may require retention"],"prerequisites":["Dependency inventory","Migration evidence","Data lifecycle analysis"],"dependencies":["Integration retirement","Technology lifecycle governance"],"alternatives":["Time-based retirement","Consumer declaration","Evidence-based retirement"],"evidence":["Retirement is safe only when relevant dependencies and obligations are demonstrably addressed."],"tradeoffs":["More retirement evidence can delay shutdown but reduces unexpected dependency failures."],"decision":"Require evidence-based retirement readiness before final shutdown.","rationale":"Retirement is a state transition with potentially irreversible consequences.","consequences":["Legacy systems remain only with documented justification","Retirement becomes auditable"],"implementation_implications":["Track migration completion","Monitor residual usage","Define final shutdown checks"],"verification_method":"Perform pre-retirement dependency and usage validation and verify defined obligations are complete.","outcome_learning":"Capture unexpected dependencies discovered during retirement.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B72-R01","D55-B72-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B72-R01","D55-B72-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B73-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for AI and Data-Driven Systems","concept":"AI architecture governance boundary","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What architecture concerns should governance address when AI or machine-learning components are introduced?","answer":"Governance should address model ownership, data dependencies, inference boundaries, external effects, security, privacy, reliability expectations, evaluation evidence, versioning, monitoring, and fallback behavior according to system impact.","context":"AI components can introduce probabilistic behavior and dependencies that differ from conventional deterministic software.","objective":"Extend architecture governance to account for AI-specific uncertainty and lifecycle characteristics.","requirements":["Identify model and data dependencies","Define evaluation expectations","Define operational boundaries","Govern model and system lifecycle"],"constraints":["Model behavior can vary with data and context","Evaluation may not prove all future behavior"],"prerequisites":["AI system inventory","Risk classification","Data governance"],"dependencies":["Security governance","Data governance","Reliability governance"],"alternatives":["Treat AI as ordinary dependency","Separate AI governance only","Integrated architecture governance"],"evidence":["AI components introduce model, data, evaluation, and lifecycle concerns that can affect system architecture."],"tradeoffs":["Additional governance improves traceability but increases evidence and monitoring requirements."],"decision":"Integrate AI-specific concerns into architecture governance according to system impact.","rationale":"AI architecture cannot be governed adequately by interface and deployment concerns alone.","consequences":["Model and data lineage become architecture concerns","Evaluation evidence becomes part of decision context"],"implementation_implications":["Record model versions","Map data dependencies","Define evaluation and fallback requirements","Track operational behavior"],"verification_method":"Audit representative AI-enabled architectures for model, data, evaluation, and operational governance evidence.","outcome_learning":"Use observed model and system behavior to refine architecture controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B73-R02","D55-B73-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B73-R02","D55-B73-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B73-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for AI and Data-Driven Systems","concept":"Model version governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should model version be treated as an architectural dependency?","answer":"A model version can materially change system behavior even when application code and interfaces remain unchanged. Governance should therefore associate deployed behavior with model identity, relevant configuration, evaluation evidence, and lifecycle state.","context":"AI systems can change behavior through model replacement or configuration changes without conventional source-code changes.","objective":"Preserve traceability between system behavior and model artifacts.","requirements":["Identify deployed model version","Record relevant configuration","Associate evaluation evidence","Track lifecycle"],"constraints":["Model artifacts may be externally managed","Model identity can involve multiple components"],"prerequisites":["AI component inventory","Artifact provenance"],"dependencies":["Release governance","Evaluation governance"],"alternatives":["Code-only versioning","Model-aware versioning","No model traceability"],"evidence":["Behavior can change independently of application source code when model artifacts change."],"tradeoffs":["Model-aware traceability adds deployment metadata requirements."],"decision":"Govern model identity and relevant configuration as first-class architecture and release dependencies.","rationale":"Behavioral reproducibility requires more than application source version alone.","consequences":["Architecture records can distinguish code and model changes","Incident analysis gains model context"],"implementation_implications":["Record immutable model identifiers where possible","Link deployments to model versions","Retain evaluation evidence"],"verification_method":"Inspect representative deployments and verify that model identity can be traced to observed behavior.","outcome_learning":"Correlate model changes with behavioral regressions or improvements.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B73-R01","D55-B73-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B73-R01","D55-B73-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B73-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for AI and Data-Driven Systems","concept":"AI fallback architecture governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should an AI-enabled architecture require a deterministic or alternative fallback path?","answer":"A fallback should be considered when AI failure, uncertainty, unavailability, or degraded quality can cause unacceptable system impact and a viable alternative can reduce that impact.","context":"AI components may fail through unavailability, invalid outputs, distribution changes, or confidence limitations.","objective":"Ensure system resilience does not depend on unqualified assumptions about AI behavior.","requirements":["Identify AI failure modes","Assess consequence of degraded behavior","Define fallback condition","Define fallback behavior"],"constraints":["A meaningful fallback may not exist for every AI function","Fallback can reduce functionality"],"prerequisites":["AI risk assessment","Failure-mode analysis"],"dependencies":["Reliability governance","Operational readiness"],"alternatives":["No fallback","Human fallback","Deterministic fallback","Alternative model"],"evidence":["AI failure can have system-level consequences when downstream actions assume valid outputs."],"tradeoffs":["Fallback paths increase architecture complexity but can reduce high-impact failure consequences."],"decision":"Require explicit fallback analysis for AI components whose failure can materially affect system outcomes.","rationale":"Fallback should be driven by consequence rather than by the mere presence of AI.","consequences":["AI failure behavior becomes an architecture concern","Fallback effectiveness requires validation"],"implementation_implications":["Define failure thresholds","Implement safe fallback where justified","Test transition behavior"],"verification_method":"Exercise representative AI failure and uncertainty conditions and verify defined fallback behavior.","outcome_learning":"Measure fallback frequency, correctness, and residual impact.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B73-R02","D55-B73-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B73-R02","D55-B73-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B73-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for AI and Data-Driven Systems","concept":"AI evaluation evidence governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should architecture governance require from AI evaluation evidence?","answer":"Evaluation evidence should identify the evaluated model or system version, evaluation objective, relevant data or scenarios, limitations, measured results where available, and applicability to the intended operating context.","context":"Evaluation results can be misleading when their context does not match the deployed system.","objective":"Prevent generic evaluation claims from being treated as deployment-specific evidence.","requirements":["Identify evaluated version","Describe evaluation context","Record limitations","Relate evidence to intended use"],"constraints":["Evaluation datasets may not represent production conditions","Some properties are difficult to measure exhaustively"],"prerequisites":["Model identity","Evaluation methodology"],"dependencies":["Model governance","Decision evidence governance"],"alternatives":["Generic benchmark evidence","Deployment-specific evaluation","Risk-based evaluation"],"evidence":["Evaluation validity depends on what was tested and how closely it represents the intended use."],"tradeoffs":["Contextual evaluation requires more effort than generic benchmark reporting."],"decision":"Require evaluation evidence to state scope, version, limitations, and applicability.","rationale":"Evidence should be interpreted according to its actual coverage and context.","consequences":["Architecture decisions become more evidence-aware","Unsupported generalization becomes visible"],"implementation_implications":["Store evaluation metadata","Link results to model versions","Record coverage gaps"],"verification_method":"Audit AI architecture decisions and verify that evaluation evidence matches the deployed context.","outcome_learning":"Compare predicted evaluation limitations with observed operational behavior.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B73-R01","D55-B73-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B73-R01","D55-B73-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B73-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B73","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for AI and Data-Driven Systems","concept":"AI data dependency governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance manage data dependencies of AI components?","answer":"Governance should identify training, validation, retrieval, runtime, feedback, and reference-data dependencies that materially influence model behavior, together with ownership, lifecycle, access, and change implications.","context":"AI behavior can depend on data beyond the model artifact itself.","objective":"Make material data dependencies visible in AI architecture.","requirements":["Identify behaviorally relevant data","Record ownership","Track lifecycle","Assess change impact"],"constraints":["Data pipelines may be distributed","Some dependencies may be dynamic"],"prerequisites":["Data lineage","Model inventory"],"dependencies":["Data governance","Model governance"],"alternatives":["Model-only governance","Data-aware architecture governance","Unmanaged data dependencies"],"evidence":["Changes to relevant data can alter AI system behavior without changing model code."],"tradeoffs":["Data-aware governance increases architecture metadata requirements."],"decision":"Govern material AI data dependencies as part of the architecture and lifecycle model.","rationale":"Behavioral dependencies extend beyond the model artifact.","consequences":["Data changes can trigger architecture reassessment","Lineage becomes part of AI governance"],"implementation_implications":["Map model-to-data relationships","Record data versions or effective states where relevant","Define change triggers"],"verification_method":"Trace representative AI outputs to relevant data dependencies and verify architecture records capture material relationships.","outcome_learning":"Use behavioral changes associated with data changes to refine dependency models.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B73-R02","D55-B73-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B73-R02","D55-B73-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B74-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Security-Sensitive Systems","concept":"Security architecture governance integration","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should security architecture governance integrate with general architecture governance?","answer":"Security concerns should be integrated into architecture decisions where they affect system boundaries, data, identity, trust, attack surface, dependencies, or recovery rather than treated solely as a downstream compliance review.","context":"Security properties are architectural properties that can be difficult or expensive to retrofit after design decisions are made.","objective":"Ensure security consequences are considered at the architecture decision point.","requirements":["Identify security-relevant boundaries","Assess trust relationships","Assess data and identity flows","Record security assumptions"],"constraints":["Security risk changes over time","Security review may involve specialized expertise"],"prerequisites":["Threat model","Architecture model","Security requirements"],"dependencies":["Risk governance","Data governance"],"alternatives":["Separate security review","Integrated architecture-security governance","Security-only compliance gate"],"evidence":["Security architecture depends on system structure, trust boundaries, data flows, and dependency choices."],"tradeoffs":["Early integration increases design effort but can reduce expensive late changes."],"decision":"Integrate security architecture evidence into relevant architecture governance decisions.","rationale":"Security cannot always be separated from fundamental architectural structure.","consequences":["Security evidence becomes part of architecture decisions","Material security changes can trigger reassessment"],"implementation_implications":["Link threat models to architecture decisions","Govern trust boundaries","Track security-relevant exceptions"],"verification_method":"Audit significant architecture decisions for relevant security boundary and threat evidence.","outcome_learning":"Use security incidents and review findings to improve architecture controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B74-R02","D55-B74-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B74-R02","D55-B74-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B74-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Security-Sensitive Systems","concept":"Trust boundary governance","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should architecture governance require when a system introduces or changes a trust boundary?","answer":"Governance should identify the parties or components on each side, authentication and authorization assumptions, data crossing the boundary, allowed interactions, failure behavior, and controls that prevent unintended privilege or data flow.","context":"Trust boundaries define where security assumptions change within an architecture.","objective":"Make security assumptions at architectural boundaries explicit and reviewable.","requirements":["Identify boundary participants","Define trust assumptions","Map data and actions crossing boundary","Define controls"],"constraints":["Trust relationships may be dynamic","External dependencies may have incomplete information"],"prerequisites":["Architecture topology","Identity model","Threat analysis"],"dependencies":["Security architecture governance","Integration governance"],"alternatives":["Implicit trust","Boundary documentation","Explicit trust-boundary governance"],"evidence":["Security controls depend on where trust assumptions change."],"tradeoffs":["Explicit boundary governance increases review detail but reduces hidden trust assumptions."],"decision":"Treat material trust boundaries as explicit architecture governance objects.","rationale":"A system cannot reliably reason about security without knowing where trust changes.","consequences":["Boundary changes trigger security-aware architecture review","Security assumptions become traceable"],"implementation_implications":["Represent trust boundaries in architecture models","Record authentication and authorization assumptions","Review new cross-boundary paths"],"verification_method":"Inspect representative architecture changes and verify that new or modified trust boundaries are documented and assessed.","outcome_learning":"Use security findings to identify missing or incorrectly modeled boundaries.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B74-R01","D55-B74-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B74-R01","D55-B74-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B74-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Security-Sensitive Systems","concept":"Privilege boundary governance","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance evaluate privilege boundaries?","answer":"Governance should identify which components, users, services, or processes can perform sensitive actions and verify that authority is constrained to the architectural scope required by the system's responsibilities.","context":"Architecture choices can unintentionally create broad privilege through shared identities, service accounts, or centralized components.","objective":"Prevent unnecessary authority concentration at architectural boundaries.","requirements":["Identify privileged actors","Map sensitive actions","Define authority scope","Assess delegation and escalation"],"constraints":["Operational simplicity can favor broad permissions","Some infrastructure components require elevated privileges"],"prerequisites":["Identity architecture","Authorization model"],"dependencies":["Trust boundary governance","Security risk governance"],"alternatives":["Broad shared privilege","Role-based boundaries","Fine-grained authority governance"],"evidence":["Privilege scope is an architectural property when components or services control sensitive actions."],"tradeoffs":["Fine-grained privilege improves containment but can increase configuration complexity."],"decision":"Govern material privilege boundaries according to least-authority and system responsibility requirements.","rationale":"Authority should align with the architectural role of each component.","consequences":["Shared privileged components receive stronger scrutiny","Privilege changes can trigger architecture review"],"implementation_implications":["Map privileged operations","Separate responsibilities where practical","Test unauthorized actions"],"verification_method":"Review architecture privilege maps and test representative unauthorized operations.","outcome_learning":"Use privilege-related findings to refine boundary definitions and control placement.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B74-R02","D55-B74-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B74-R02","D55-B74-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B74-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Security-Sensitive Systems","concept":"Security architecture exception governance","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should security-related architecture exceptions differ from ordinary architecture exceptions?","answer":"Security exceptions should explicitly identify the affected security property, residual risk, compensating controls, approval authority, evidence limitations, scope, and review or expiration condition because security deviations can expose protected assets or trust boundaries.","context":"A security architecture exception can create consequences beyond ordinary maintainability or design preferences.","objective":"Ensure security exceptions receive risk-appropriate governance.","requirements":["Identify security property","Assess residual risk","Define compensating controls","Assign security-aware approval","Define review condition"],"constraints":["Security risk can change as threats and dependencies evolve"],"prerequisites":["Threat model","Risk assessment","Exception governance"],"dependencies":["Security architecture","Architecture governance"],"alternatives":["Uniform exception process","Security-specific controls within common exception framework"],"evidence":["Security deviations require explicit treatment of residual risk rather than simple conformance status."],"tradeoffs":["Stronger exception evidence increases review effort but preserves security risk visibility."],"decision":"Use a common exception lifecycle with security-specific evidence requirements for security-impacting deviations.","rationale":"Security exceptions should remain integrated with architecture governance while receiving appropriate risk treatment.","consequences":["Security exceptions remain traceable","Compensating controls become auditable"],"implementation_implications":["Record security property affected","Link exception to threat or risk evidence","Review compensating control effectiveness"],"verification_method":"Audit security exceptions for residual-risk analysis and supporting evidence.","outcome_learning":"Track recurring security exceptions as candidates for architecture or control improvement.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B74-R01","D55-B74-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B74-R01","D55-B74-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B74-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B74","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Security-Sensitive Systems","concept":"Security architecture change triggers","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_GOVERNANCE","question":"Which architecture changes should trigger security-focused reassessment?","answer":"Security reassessment should be considered when trust boundaries, privileged operations, sensitive data flows, authentication mechanisms, external exposure, critical dependencies, isolation models, or security-relevant failure behavior materially change.","context":"Security architecture can become invalid through structural changes even when functional requirements remain unchanged.","objective":"Detect architecture changes that can materially alter security posture.","requirements":["Define security-relevant change triggers","Map triggers to architecture objects","Record reassessment results"],"constraints":["Security impact may not be obvious from change descriptions alone"],"prerequisites":["Security architecture model","Change impact analysis"],"dependencies":["Trust boundary governance","Architecture change governance"],"alternatives":["Review every change","Security review on schedule","Risk-based security triggers"],"evidence":["Changes to boundaries and authority can alter security properties independently of functional behavior."],"tradeoffs":["Targeted security triggers reduce unnecessary review but depend on accurate architecture metadata."],"decision":"Use explicit security-impact change triggers integrated with architecture change governance.","rationale":"Security reassessment should follow architectural security impact rather than arbitrary release cadence.","consequences":["Security review becomes more targeted","Material security changes become traceable"],"implementation_implications":["Encode security trigger rules","Link changes to trust and data-flow models","Record evaluation outcomes"],"verification_method":"Test representative architecture changes and verify that security-relevant modifications trigger reassessment.","outcome_learning":"Review security findings missed by trigger rules and refine trigger coverage.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B74-R02","D55-B74-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B74-R02","D55-B74-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B75-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Reliability","concept":"Reliability architecture governance","knowledge_type":"RELIABILITY_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should architecture governance evaluate when reliability is a material system requirement?","answer":"Governance should evaluate failure domains, dependencies, recovery behavior, capacity assumptions, redundancy, degradation modes, observability, recovery objectives, and operational ownership according to system criticality.","context":"Reliability depends on architecture relationships and failure behavior rather than a single availability metric.","objective":"Ensure architecture decisions support explicitly required reliability properties.","requirements":["Identify critical failure modes","Map dependencies","Define recovery expectations","Assess capacity and redundancy"],"constraints":["Reliability requirements differ by system","Some failures cannot be eliminated"],"prerequisites":["Reliability requirements","Architecture dependency model"],"dependencies":["Resilience governance","Operational readiness"],"alternatives":["Availability-only governance","Reliability-specific governance","Risk-based reliability governance"],"evidence":["System reliability emerges from interactions among components, dependencies, capacity, and recovery mechanisms."],"tradeoffs":["Higher resilience can increase complexity and cost."],"decision":"Apply risk- and requirement-based reliability governance to architectures with material reliability obligations.","rationale":"Reliability must be represented as a set of architectural properties rather than a single target.","consequences":["Failure analysis becomes part of architecture review","Recovery architecture receives explicit governance"],"implementation_implications":["Map failure domains","Document recovery assumptions","Test critical failure scenarios"],"verification_method":"Review architecture against defined reliability requirements and representative failure scenarios.","outcome_learning":"Use incidents and resilience tests to refine architecture controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B75-R02","D55-B75-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B75-R02","D55-B75-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B75-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Reliability","concept":"Failure-domain separation governance","knowledge_type":"RELIABILITY_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why should architecture governance evaluate whether critical components share failure domains?","answer":"If supposedly redundant components share infrastructure, dependencies, configuration, operators, or other common failure causes, a single failure can affect all of them. Governance should identify and evaluate such common-mode dependencies.","context":"Redundancy does not provide the intended resilience if redundant components fail together.","objective":"Distinguish nominal redundancy from effective failure-domain independence.","requirements":["Identify redundancy relationships","Map common dependencies","Assess common-mode failure"],"constraints":["Complete independence may be impractical","Failure domains can change with infrastructure evolution"],"prerequisites":["Dependency graph","Failure-mode analysis"],"dependencies":["Platform governance","Resilience governance"],"alternatives":["Count replicas only","Failure-domain analysis","Formal redundancy analysis"],"evidence":["Redundant components can share common failure causes."],"tradeoffs":["Stronger failure-domain separation can increase infrastructure and operational complexity."],"decision":"Evaluate effective failure-domain separation for critical redundancy claims.","rationale":"Reliability depends on independent failure behavior, not replica count alone.","consequences":["Architecture reviews identify common-mode dependencies","Redundancy claims require supporting evidence"],"implementation_implications":["Map shared dependencies","Identify common infrastructure and control planes","Test relevant failure scenarios"],"verification_method":"Analyze critical redundant components for shared dependencies and, where possible, validate through controlled failure testing.","outcome_learning":"Use incidents involving correlated failures to refine failure-domain models.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B75-R01","D55-B75-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B75-R01","D55-B75-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B75-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Reliability","concept":"Graceful degradation governance","knowledge_type":"RELIABILITY_GOVERNANCE","question":"When should architecture governance require explicit degraded operating modes?","answer":"Degraded modes should be considered when complete service failure has materially greater impact than reduced functionality and the system can safely continue with constrained capabilities.","context":"Some architectures can preserve critical functions while sacrificing non-essential functionality during partial failure.","objective":"Govern predictable degradation where it materially improves resilience.","requirements":["Identify critical functions","Define degraded behavior","Define transition conditions","Define recovery back to normal operation"],"constraints":["Degraded behavior can introduce semantic or user-experience complexity"],"prerequisites":["Failure-mode analysis","Business or system criticality model"],"dependencies":["Reliability governance","Operational readiness"],"alternatives":["Fail completely","Graceful degradation","Manual fallback"],"evidence":["Preserving essential functionality can reduce impact during partial failure when degradation is explicitly designed and validated."],"tradeoffs":["Graceful degradation increases design and testing complexity."],"decision":"Govern degraded modes where they materially reduce failure consequences and can be implemented safely.","rationale":"Degradation is useful only when its behavior is predictable and bounded.","consequences":["Partial failure behavior becomes an architecture concern","Degraded modes require testing"],"implementation_implications":["Define degraded states","Identify protected functions","Test transitions and recovery"],"verification_method":"Exercise representative dependency failures and verify documented degraded behavior.","outcome_learning":"Measure actual degraded-mode effectiveness and unexpected side effects.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B75-R02","D55-B75-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B75-R02","D55-B75-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B75-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Reliability","concept":"Recovery architecture governance","knowledge_type":"RELIABILITY_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should architecture governance require for critical recovery paths?","answer":"Recovery architecture should identify recovery objectives, dependencies, authority, required state, data restoration mechanisms, sequencing, operational ownership, and conditions under which recovery is considered complete.","context":"A recovery procedure is an architectural dependency chain that can itself fail.","objective":"Ensure recovery is designed and governed as a system capability.","requirements":["Define recovery objectives","Map recovery dependencies","Define recovery authority","Define completion criteria"],"constraints":["Recovery may operate with reduced resources","Recovery dependencies may differ from runtime dependencies"],"prerequisites":["Failure analysis","Recovery strategy"],"dependencies":["Platform recovery governance","Incident governance"],"alternatives":["Documented runbook only","Recovery architecture","Automated recovery"],"evidence":["Recovery effectiveness depends on dependencies and state transitions, not documentation alone."],"tradeoffs":["Explicit recovery architecture requires additional modeling and testing."],"decision":"Treat critical recovery capability as an architecture concern with explicit dependencies and verification.","rationale":"Recovery is part of system behavior under failure and therefore belongs in architecture governance.","consequences":["Recovery assumptions become reviewable","Recovery tests become architecture evidence"],"implementation_implications":["Model recovery dependencies","Define recovery states","Test critical recovery paths"],"verification_method":"Perform controlled recovery exercises and compare observed behavior with the governed recovery architecture.","outcome_learning":"Update recovery architecture from exercise and incident evidence.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B75-R01","D55-B75-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B75-R01","D55-B75-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B75-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B75","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Reliability","concept":"Reliability evidence lifecycle","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should reliability evidence remain valid as architecture changes?","answer":"Reliability evidence should be linked to the architecture state, test conditions, dependencies, workloads, and assumptions under which it was produced. Material architecture changes should trigger reassessment when those conditions no longer hold.","context":"Reliability evidence can become stale after topology, workload, dependency, or configuration changes.","objective":"Prevent historical reliability results from being treated as universally current.","requirements":["Record test context","Record architecture version or state","Track assumptions","Define invalidation triggers"],"constraints":["Some workloads are difficult to reproduce","Operational conditions may vary"],"prerequisites":["Reliability tests","Architecture baseline"],"dependencies":["Evidence freshness","Change governance"],"alternatives":["Permanent test evidence","Calendar-only revalidation","Condition-based revalidation"],"evidence":["Measured reliability properties are conditional on architecture and workload context."],"tradeoffs":["Revalidation increases testing cost but preserves evidence relevance."],"decision":"Treat reliability evidence as contextual and invalidate or reassess it when material assumptions change.","rationale":"Evidence without context cannot establish current reliability.","consequences":["Architecture changes can affect evidence validity","Historical results remain useful but correctly scoped"],"implementation_implications":["Version reliability evidence","Record workload assumptions","Define reassessment triggers"],"verification_method":"Modify representative architecture assumptions and verify that governance identifies affected reliability evidence.","outcome_learning":"Track stale evidence discovered during incidents or architecture reviews.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B75-R01","D55-B75-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B75-R01","D55-B75-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B76-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Performance","concept":"Performance architecture governance","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should architecture governance evaluate when performance is a material requirement?","answer":"Governance should evaluate workload characteristics, latency and throughput requirements, resource dependencies, scaling behavior, contention, capacity assumptions, performance-sensitive boundaries, and evidence supporting expected behavior.","context":"Performance depends on architecture, workload, infrastructure, data access patterns, and dependencies.","objective":"Ensure performance claims are tied to explicit architectural assumptions and evidence.","requirements":["Define workload model","Define performance objectives","Identify bottlenecks and dependencies","Provide relevant evidence"],"constraints":["Performance varies with workload and environment","Benchmarks may not represent production behavior"],"prerequisites":["Performance requirements","Workload characterization"],"dependencies":["Capacity governance","Architecture evidence"],"alternatives":["Benchmark-only review","Architecture-level performance governance","No explicit performance governance"],"evidence":["Performance characteristics are conditional on workload and architecture context."],"tradeoffs":["More representative validation requires additional test effort."],"decision":"Use context-specific performance governance tied to workload and architectural assumptions.","rationale":"Performance cannot be established from isolated component benchmarks alone.","consequences":["Performance evidence becomes contextual","Architecture changes can trigger reassessment"],"implementation_implications":["Record workload assumptions","Map performance-sensitive dependencies","Define performance validation criteria"],"verification_method":"Evaluate architecture against representative workloads and documented performance objectives.","outcome_learning":"Compare measured behavior with architecture assumptions and update governance evidence.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B76-R02","D55-B76-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B76-R02","D55-B76-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B76-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Performance","concept":"Performance budget governance","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance use performance budgets?","answer":"Performance budgets should allocate measurable limits across architectural components or interactions so that local changes can be evaluated against their contribution to system-level performance objectives.","context":"System-level latency or throughput objectives can be difficult to manage when no component-level allocation exists.","objective":"Connect local architecture decisions to global performance requirements.","requirements":["Define system performance objective","Allocate meaningful budgets","Measure relevant contributors","Define escalation for budget violations"],"constraints":["Budgets may be difficult to allocate precisely","Some latency contributions vary dynamically"],"prerequisites":["Performance requirements","Dependency model"],"dependencies":["Performance measurement","Change governance"],"alternatives":["Global target only","Component budgets","Budget plus end-to-end measurement"],"evidence":["Global performance objectives require architectural decomposition to guide local decisions."],"tradeoffs":["Budgets improve accountability but can create artificial precision if assumptions are weak."],"decision":"Use performance budgets where contributions can be meaningfully measured or bounded.","rationale":"Budgets are useful only when they represent meaningful architectural constraints.","consequences":["Performance regressions can be attributed more precisely","Budget assumptions require review"],"implementation_implications":["Define budget allocation method","Track budget consumption","Reassess allocations after architecture changes"],"verification_method":"Compare component contributions with allocated budgets under representative workloads.","outcome_learning":"Use recurring budget violations to identify architecture bottlenecks or unrealistic allocations.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B76-R01","D55-B76-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B76-R01","D55-B76-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B76-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Performance","concept":"Performance regression governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should a performance regression become an architecture governance concern?","answer":"A regression should become an architecture governance concern when it materially affects a defined system objective, violates an approved performance budget, reveals a structural bottleneck, or changes the assumptions underlying an architecture decision.","context":"Not every performance change represents an architecture problem.","objective":"Escalate performance changes according to architectural consequence.","requirements":["Define regression thresholds","Link thresholds to architecture objectives","Identify structural causes","Record reassessment"],"constraints":["Performance naturally varies","Measurement noise can create false signals"],"prerequisites":["Baseline measurements","Performance objectives"],"dependencies":["Performance evidence","Architecture decision records"],"alternatives":["Escalate every regression","Ignore performance changes","Impact-based escalation"],"evidence":["Performance governance should distinguish measurement variation from meaningful architectural degradation."],"tradeoffs":["Strict thresholds increase sensitivity but can create review noise."],"decision":"Escalate performance regressions based on sustained and architecturally meaningful impact.","rationale":"Governance should focus on changes that affect architectural requirements or assumptions.","consequences":["Performance governance becomes evidence-based","Structural regressions can trigger architecture review"],"implementation_implications":["Define measurement confidence","Track sustained deviations","Link regressions to architecture decisions"],"verification_method":"Evaluate representative performance changes and verify that governance distinguishes material regression from noise.","outcome_learning":"Analyze false alarms and missed regressions to calibrate thresholds.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B76-R02","D55-B76-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B76-R02","D55-B76-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B76-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Performance","concept":"Performance scalability governance","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance distinguish current performance from scalability characteristics?","answer":"Current performance describes observed behavior under a defined workload, while scalability describes how behavior changes as workload or resources change. Governance should treat them as related but distinct properties requiring different evidence.","context":"A system can perform adequately at current scale but fail to meet requirements as workload increases.","objective":"Prevent current benchmark results from being interpreted as proof of future scalability.","requirements":["Define workload range","Assess resource-response relationship","Identify scaling constraints","Record assumptions"],"constraints":["Future workload may differ from tested conditions","Scaling behavior can be nonlinear"],"prerequisites":["Workload model","Performance baseline"],"dependencies":["Capacity governance","Performance testing"],"alternatives":["Current benchmark only","Scalability analysis","Capacity planning"],"evidence":["Observed performance at one scale does not establish behavior at another scale."],"tradeoffs":["Scalability testing requires broader experiments and capacity resources."],"decision":"Govern scalability as a separate architectural property where growth is material.","rationale":"Architecture decisions should account for expected workload evolution rather than only current measurements.","consequences":["Growth assumptions become explicit","Architecture reviews can identify scaling bottlenecks earlier"],"implementation_implications":["Define tested workload ranges","Document scaling mechanism","Identify saturation points"],"verification_method":"Evaluate architecture across multiple representative workload or resource levels where feasible.","outcome_learning":"Compare predicted scaling behavior with observed growth.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B76-R01","D55-B76-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B76-R01","D55-B76-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B76-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B76","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Performance","concept":"Performance evidence reproducibility","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What context should be retained with architecture performance evidence?","answer":"Performance evidence should retain the architecture state, workload, data characteristics where relevant, environment, configuration, measurement method, test duration, and relevant version identifiers so results can be interpreted and reproduced as far as practical.","context":"Performance results without test context are difficult to compare or validate after architecture changes.","objective":"Make performance evidence interpretable and useful for future architecture decisions.","requirements":["Record workload","Record architecture and configuration","Record measurement method","Record relevant versions"],"constraints":["Exact reproduction may be impossible","Production environments can change continuously"],"prerequisites":["Performance test methodology","Architecture baseline"],"dependencies":["Evidence provenance","Performance governance"],"alternatives":["Store metric only","Store full test context","Risk-based evidence retention"],"evidence":["Performance measurements are highly context-dependent."],"tradeoffs":["Detailed evidence increases storage and documentation requirements."],"decision":"Retain sufficient contextual metadata to distinguish and interpret performance measurements.","rationale":"A number without its measurement conditions cannot reliably support architecture reasoning.","consequences":["Historical performance comparisons become more meaningful","Stale or incomparable results can be identified"],"implementation_implications":["Version performance evidence","Store workload and environment metadata","Link results to architecture decisions"],"verification_method":"Attempt to reproduce or interpret historical measurements using retained context.","outcome_learning":"Identify missing metadata that prevents meaningful comparison.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B76-R01","D55-B76-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B76-R01","D55-B76-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B77-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Cost","concept":"Architecture cost governance","knowledge_type":"COST_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance consider cost without reducing architecture decisions to price alone?","answer":"Cost governance should evaluate total architectural cost drivers, including infrastructure, operations, engineering effort, dependencies, migration, resilience requirements, licensing, and lifecycle burden, while preserving functional and quality requirements.","context":"Architecture choices can shift costs between infrastructure, engineering, operations, and future migration.","objective":"Make cost a structured architectural tradeoff rather than a single price comparison.","requirements":["Identify major cost drivers","Consider lifecycle cost","Include operational and migration implications","Preserve required quality attributes"],"constraints":["Future costs are uncertain","Some benefits are difficult to quantify"],"prerequisites":["Architecture alternatives","Cost model"],"dependencies":["Technology lifecycle","Platform governance"],"alternatives":["Infrastructure-cost-only analysis","Total-cost analysis","Cost ignored until implementation"],"evidence":["Architecture decisions distribute cost across multiple lifecycle stages and organizational functions."],"tradeoffs":["Lower immediate cost can increase long-term operational or migration burden."],"decision":"Use lifecycle-aware architectural cost analysis alongside technical and quality requirements.","rationale":"Architecture cost is multi-dimensional and time-dependent.","consequences":["Alternatives are evaluated more completely","Hidden operational and migration costs become visible"],"implementation_implications":["Record major cost assumptions","Estimate lifecycle impacts","Review cost-sensitive architecture decisions"],"verification_method":"Compare architecture alternatives using documented cost dimensions and assumptions.","outcome_learning":"Compare projected and actual cost drivers after implementation.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B77-R02","D55-B77-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B77-R02","D55-B77-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B77-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Cost","concept":"Cost allocation architecture governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should architecture governance identify cost allocation boundaries for shared capabilities?","answer":"Shared capability costs should be attributable to meaningful consumers or organizational units where possible so that architecture decisions expose the economic consequences of dependency and usage rather than hiding them in centralized budgets.","context":"Shared platforms can make consumers appear inexpensive while concentrating cost elsewhere.","objective":"Improve economic visibility of shared architectural dependencies.","requirements":["Identify shared cost drivers","Define allocation model","Distinguish fixed and variable costs","Preserve architecture context"],"constraints":["Some shared costs are difficult to attribute precisely","Allocation models can influence behavior"],"prerequisites":["Platform dependency map","Cost data"],"dependencies":["Platform governance","Architecture metrics"],"alternatives":["Centralized cost","Usage-based allocation","Hybrid allocation"],"evidence":["Hidden shared costs can distort architecture decisions and consumer incentives."],"tradeoffs":["Detailed allocation increases accounting and measurement complexity."],"decision":"Use meaningful cost allocation for significant shared architectural capabilities where attribution is practical.","rationale":"Economic visibility should correspond to architectural dependency where possible.","consequences":["Platform usage becomes economically observable","Architecture decisions can account for shared cost"],"implementation_implications":["Map consumers to cost drivers","Define allocation assumptions","Review allocation when architecture changes"],"verification_method":"Compare allocated costs with platform usage and dependency information.","outcome_learning":"Analyze persistent allocation disputes or unexpected cost concentration.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B77-R01","D55-B77-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B77-R01","D55-B77-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B77-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Cost","concept":"Cost-risk tradeoff governance","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should architecture governance evaluate a lower-cost alternative that increases technical risk?","answer":"The alternative should be assessed by comparing the magnitude and likelihood of additional risk with the lifecycle cost reduction, while identifying whether the risk can be controlled, monitored, or reversed.","context":"Cost optimization can change reliability, security, performance, maintainability, or migration characteristics.","objective":"Prevent cost reduction from obscuring material architectural risk.","requirements":["Identify affected quality attributes","Estimate residual risk","Compare lifecycle cost","Assess reversibility"],"constraints":["Risk and future cost may be uncertain"],"prerequisites":["Alternative analysis","Risk model","Cost assumptions"],"dependencies":["Architecture decisions","Risk governance"],"alternatives":["Lowest-cost option","Risk-minimized option","Balanced tradeoff"],"evidence":["Architecture tradeoffs should account for both economic and technical consequences."],"tradeoffs":["Higher initial cost can reduce future risk, while lower cost can introduce operational or migration burden."],"decision":"Require explicit cost-risk tradeoff analysis when cost reduction materially affects architecture properties.","rationale":"Cost is one decision dimension and should not silently override critical requirements.","consequences":["Cost-saving decisions become more traceable","Residual risk remains visible"],"implementation_implications":["Record cost and risk assumptions","Define mitigation controls","Review outcomes against original tradeoff"],"verification_method":"Audit cost-sensitive decisions for explicit risk and lifecycle analysis.","outcome_learning":"Compare realized cost savings with realized risk and operational consequences.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B77-R01","D55-B77-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B77-R01","D55-B77-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B77-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Cost","concept":"Architecture cost assumption governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should architecture cost assumptions be recorded explicitly?","answer":"Cost estimates depend on workload, pricing, utilization, staffing, operational model, technology lifecycle, and architectural scope. Recording assumptions makes the estimate interpretable and allows later comparison with actual outcomes.","context":"Architecture cost estimates often appear precise even when their underlying assumptions are uncertain.","objective":"Make economic reasoning traceable and revisable.","requirements":["Record cost drivers","Record assumptions","Identify uncertainty","Define review conditions"],"constraints":["Future prices and workloads can change","Some organizational costs are difficult to quantify"],"prerequisites":["Architecture alternatives","Cost estimation method"],"dependencies":["Architecture decision records","Outcome learning"],"alternatives":["Single estimated amount","Assumption-based estimate","Range-based estimate"],"evidence":["Cost estimates are conditional on assumptions about future architecture and usage."],"tradeoffs":["More explicit assumptions reduce false precision but make uncertainty more visible."],"decision":"Treat major architecture cost estimates as assumption-based evidence rather than fixed facts.","rationale":"Architecture governance should preserve the reasoning behind economic estimates.","consequences":["Historical cost decisions remain interpretable","Changed assumptions can trigger reassessment"],"implementation_implications":["Store cost assumptions","Track estimate versions","Compare assumptions with actual conditions"],"verification_method":"Review historical estimates and determine whether actual outcomes can be explained from retained assumptions.","outcome_learning":"Use estimate errors to improve future cost models.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B77-R01","D55-B77-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B77-R01","D55-B77-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B77-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B77","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Cost","concept":"Architecture cost outcome learning","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_LEARNING","question":"How should architecture governance learn from differences between projected and actual architectural costs?","answer":"Governance should compare the original cost model, assumptions, architectural decisions, and actual cost drivers to identify whether deviations resulted from incorrect assumptions, architecture changes, workload changes, operational behavior, or estimation limitations.","context":"Cost variance alone does not explain whether an architecture decision was flawed.","objective":"Turn cost variance into useful architectural learning.","requirements":["Preserve original estimate","Capture actual cost drivers","Classify variance causes","Feed findings into future decisions"],"constraints":["Actual cost may be affected by external factors"],"prerequisites":["Cost evidence","Architecture decision record"],"dependencies":["Outcome learning","Architecture metrics"],"alternatives":["Track variance only","Investigate variance causes","No post-decision review"],"evidence":["Variance is informative only when its causes can be distinguished."],"tradeoffs":["Post-decision analysis requires effort but improves future estimation and architecture reasoning."],"decision":"Use causal variance analysis rather than simple cost-variance reporting.","rationale":"Learning requires identifying why the estimate differed from reality.","consequences":["Architecture knowledge improves over time","Cost models can be calibrated using observed outcomes"],"implementation_implications":["Link actual costs to architecture decisions","Record variance causes","Update estimation assumptions"],"verification_method":"Review completed architecture decisions and determine whether major cost deviations have documented causes.","outcome_learning":"Persist validated cost lessons as reusable architecture evidence.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B77-R04","D55-B77-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B77-R04","D55-B77-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B78-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Organizational Change","concept":"Architecture governance during team restructuring","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance respond when team or organizational ownership changes?","answer":"Governance should reassess ownership, decision authority, service boundaries, operational responsibilities, architecture records, and unresolved dependencies when organizational restructuring changes who controls or supports architectural components.","context":"Organizational changes can alter architecture governance even when software remains unchanged.","objective":"Prevent ownership gaps and stale governance relationships after organizational change.","requirements":["Identify affected components","Reassign ownership","Review decision authority","Update operational responsibilities"],"constraints":["Organizational changes can occur faster than architecture documentation updates"],"prerequisites":["Architecture ownership registry","Organizational change information"],"dependencies":["Governance roles","Service ownership","Decision authority"],"alternatives":["Leave ownership unchanged","Reassign ownership only","Architecture-aware organizational transition"],"evidence":["Architecture accountability depends partly on who owns decisions, operations, and dependencies."],"tradeoffs":["Ownership reassessment adds transition effort but prevents governance ambiguity."],"decision":"Treat material ownership restructuring as an architecture governance reassessment trigger.","rationale":"Architecture governance records must remain aligned with actual accountability.","consequences":["Ownership gaps become detectable","Decision and operational responsibilities remain explicit"],"implementation_implications":["Update ownership maps","Review critical architecture decisions","Validate escalation paths"],"verification_method":"Audit affected architecture objects after organizational restructuring and verify ownership continuity.","outcome_learning":"Record governance issues caused by ownership transitions to improve future restructuring processes.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B78-R02","D55-B78-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B78-R02","D55-B78-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B78-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Organizational Change","concept":"Architecture decision ownership continuity","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance preserve decision continuity when responsible people or teams change?","answer":"Decision records should preserve accountable roles, rationale, evidence, assumptions, and authority independently of individual personnel so that architectural reasoning remains reconstructable after organizational changes.","context":"Architecture decisions can outlive the people who originally made them.","objective":"Prevent institutional knowledge loss during organizational transitions.","requirements":["Record role-based accountability","Preserve decision evidence","Preserve assumptions and rationale","Identify current owner separately from historical decision authority"],"constraints":["Historical context may be incomplete","Roles may be renamed or reorganized"],"prerequisites":["Architecture decision repository","Ownership model"],"dependencies":["Decision governance","Evidence retention"],"alternatives":["Person-centric records","Role-centric records","No ownership history"],"evidence":["Architectural reasoning should remain accessible even when personnel change."],"tradeoffs":["Role-based traceability requires more structured governance metadata."],"decision":"Preserve architecture decision accountability and rationale independently of individual personnel identity.","rationale":"Architecture knowledge should survive organizational turnover.","consequences":["Historical decisions remain interpretable","New owners can reassess rather than recreate decisions blindly"],"implementation_implications":["Store role and authority metadata","Separate historical authorship from current ownership","Link decisions to current architecture objects"],"verification_method":"Review decisions across ownership transitions and verify that rationale and accountability remain reconstructable.","outcome_learning":"Record knowledge gaps discovered during ownership transitions.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B78-R01","D55-B78-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B78-R01","D55-B78-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B78-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Organizational Change","concept":"Architecture boundary stability during reorganization","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should organizational restructuring not automatically imply architectural boundary changes?","answer":"Organizational boundaries and system boundaries solve different problems. Governance should assess whether ownership changes actually require architecture changes rather than assuming that team restructuring should immediately change service or domain boundaries.","context":"Reorganizations can change reporting structures without changing system responsibilities or technical coupling.","objective":"Avoid architecture churn caused solely by organizational restructuring.","requirements":["Separate organizational and architectural concerns","Assess actual dependency changes","Require evidence for boundary changes"],"constraints":["Ownership changes may expose real architecture problems","Organizational structures can influence communication and operational responsibilities"],"prerequisites":["Architecture dependency model","Ownership model"],"dependencies":["Team boundary governance","Architecture decisions"],"alternatives":["Mirror organization automatically","Ignore organization completely","Evidence-based architecture reassessment"],"evidence":["Organizational and technical boundaries can be related but are not identical."],"tradeoffs":["Maintaining architecture boundaries through reorganization can preserve stability but may miss legitimate ownership-driven problems."],"decision":"Require evidence of architectural impact before changing technical boundaries because of organizational restructuring.","rationale":"Architecture changes should address technical and system consequences rather than organizational appearance alone.","consequences":["Fewer unnecessary boundary changes","Real ownership-driven architecture issues can still be escalated"],"implementation_implications":["Assess dependencies and responsibilities","Record rationale for boundary changes","Review operational ownership separately"],"verification_method":"Review architecture changes following reorganizations and classify whether each had documented technical justification.","outcome_learning":"Analyze recurring ownership-related architecture changes for structural organizational patterns.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B78-R01","D55-B78-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B78-R01","D55-B78-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B78-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Organizational Change","concept":"Governance continuity during mergers or system consolidation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should architecture governance preserve when multiple systems or organizations are consolidated?","answer":"Governance should preserve decision provenance, ownership history, conflicting standards, dependencies, exceptions, critical requirements, and unresolved assumptions until the target architecture explicitly resolves them.","context":"Consolidation can combine systems with different architecture standards and governance histories.","objective":"Prevent important architectural context from being lost during consolidation.","requirements":["Inventory source governance artifacts","Identify conflicts","Preserve decision provenance","Define target resolution path"],"constraints":["Source systems may use incompatible governance models","Historical evidence may be incomplete"],"prerequisites":["Architecture inventory","Decision repositories"],"dependencies":["Transition architecture","Architecture decisions"],"alternatives":["Discard legacy governance","Merge records without distinction","Preserve provenance during consolidation"],"evidence":["Removing historical architecture context can hide constraints and reasons behind existing designs."],"tradeoffs":["Preserving history increases migration effort but improves reasoning about the target architecture."],"decision":"Preserve provenance and unresolved architectural conflicts throughout consolidation until explicitly resolved.","rationale":"Target architecture decisions need visibility into inherited constraints and assumptions.","consequences":["Consolidation retains architectural traceability","Conflicts become explicit transition work"],"implementation_implications":["Map source decisions to target decisions","Record conflicting policies","Track resolution status"],"verification_method":"Audit consolidated architecture records for preserved provenance and unresolved conflict visibility.","outcome_learning":"Capture recurring consolidation issues as reusable governance knowledge.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B78-R02","D55-B78-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B78-R02","D55-B78-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B78-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for Organizational Change","concept":"Architecture governance transition ownership","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should governance handle temporary ownership during architecture transitions?","answer":"Temporary ownership should be explicit, bounded in scope and duration, and accompanied by a permanent ownership decision path so that transitional responsibility does not become indefinite ambiguity.","context":"Migrations, reorganizations, and platform transitions can temporarily lack stable ownership.","objective":"Prevent temporary governance arrangements from becoming permanent gaps.","requirements":["Define temporary owner","Define scope","Define duration or review condition","Define permanent ownership path"],"constraints":["Transitions may take longer than expected"],"prerequisites":["Transition architecture","Ownership registry"],"dependencies":["Architecture lifecycle","Decision authority"],"alternatives":["No temporary ownership","Permanent immediate reassignment","Explicit transitional ownership"],"evidence":["Unclear transitional ownership can delay decisions and leave architecture controls unmanaged."],"tradeoffs":["Temporary ownership adds administrative work but protects continuity."],"decision":"Govern temporary ownership explicitly with an exit or reassessment condition.","rationale":"Transitions require accountability even when final ownership is unresolved.","consequences":["Governance remains operational during transitions","Temporary arrangements remain visible"],"implementation_implications":["Track temporary ownership state","Set review triggers","Escalate unresolved ownership"],"verification_method":"Audit active transitions for explicit temporary ownership and planned resolution.","outcome_learning":"Measure transition duration and unresolved ownership cases.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B78-R01","D55-B78-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B78-R01","D55-B78-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B79-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for External Dependencies","concept":"External architecture dependency governance","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should architecture governance require when a system depends materially on an external service or component?","answer":"Governance should identify dependency ownership, contract, lifecycle, availability and compatibility assumptions, data exposure, failure behavior, exit options, and evidence supporting the dependency decision.","context":"External dependencies can introduce constraints that the consuming architecture cannot directly control.","objective":"Make externally controlled architectural dependencies visible and manageable.","requirements":["Identify dependency owner","Document contract","Assess failure and lifecycle risk","Assess exit or migration options"],"constraints":["External roadmaps and behavior may be uncertain","Contract terms may limit control"],"prerequisites":["Dependency inventory","Architecture decision"],"dependencies":["Vendor governance","Integration governance"],"alternatives":["Treat dependency as ordinary library","External dependency risk governance","Avoid external dependency"],"evidence":["Externally controlled components create architectural dependencies beyond the direct control of the consuming team."],"tradeoffs":["External services can accelerate delivery while increasing dependency and exit risk."],"decision":"Apply explicit architecture governance to material external dependencies.","rationale":"Control boundaries should influence architecture risk assessment.","consequences":["External dependencies receive lifecycle and exit analysis","Contract assumptions become architecture evidence"],"implementation_implications":["Record external dependency metadata","Monitor lifecycle and contract changes","Define contingency conditions"],"verification_method":"Audit significant external dependencies for ownership, lifecycle, failure, and exit evidence.","outcome_learning":"Use external dependency incidents and migrations to refine governance controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B79-R02","D55-B79-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B79-R02","D55-B79-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B79-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for External Dependencies","concept":"External dependency change governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance handle material changes announced by an external dependency provider?","answer":"Governance should assess the change against affected contracts, architecture assumptions, compatibility, security, data handling, performance, lifecycle, and migration requirements before accepting the new dependency state.","context":"External providers can change versions, interfaces, infrastructure, policies, or operational behavior outside the consumer team's direct control.","objective":"Prevent externally initiated changes from silently invalidating architecture assumptions.","requirements":["Detect relevant provider changes","Map affected architecture decisions","Assess impact","Define migration or mitigation"],"constraints":["Provider information may be incomplete or delayed","Consumer response time may be limited"],"prerequisites":["External dependency inventory","Change monitoring"],"dependencies":["Technology lifecycle","Integration governance"],"alternatives":["React after failure","Monitor and assess proactively","Avoid external dependency"],"evidence":["External changes can invalidate consumer architecture assumptions without local code changes."],"tradeoffs":["Proactive monitoring requires effort but reduces surprise migration risk."],"decision":"Use impact-based governance for material external dependency changes.","rationale":"External change is an architecture lifecycle event when it affects governed assumptions.","consequences":["Provider changes become traceable governance events","Migration planning becomes evidence-based"],"implementation_implications":["Track provider lifecycle signals","Link dependencies to architecture decisions","Define response thresholds"],"verification_method":"Evaluate representative external dependency changes and verify affected architecture records are identified.","outcome_learning":"Analyze missed provider changes and resulting incidents.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B79-R01","D55-B79-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B79-R01","D55-B79-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B79-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for External Dependencies","concept":"External dependency exit strategy","knowledge_type":"RISK_GOVERNANCE","question":"When should an external architecture dependency have an explicit exit strategy?","answer":"An exit strategy is particularly important when the dependency is critical, difficult to replace, controls sensitive data, has significant switching cost, has uncertain lifecycle stability, or can materially affect system operation if unavailable.","context":"Not every external dependency requires an immediate replacement design, but critical lock-in risk should be visible.","objective":"Make dependency exit risk proportionate to architectural consequence.","requirements":["Assess dependency criticality","Identify switching constraints","Define viable exit or mitigation path","Record uncertainty"],"constraints":["Maintaining a full alternative implementation can be expensive","Some dependencies have no direct equivalent"],"prerequisites":["Dependency risk assessment","Architecture decision"],"dependencies":["Vendor governance","Resilience governance"],"alternatives":["No exit analysis","Full alternative implementation","Documented exit strategy"],"evidence":["Critical dependencies can create concentrated architectural risk when replacement is difficult."],"tradeoffs":["Exit preparation has cost even when the dependency performs well."],"decision":"Require explicit exit or contingency analysis for dependencies with material concentration or switching risk.","rationale":"The required depth of exit planning should follow dependency consequence and reversibility.","consequences":["Lock-in risk becomes visible","Critical dependencies can receive contingency planning"],"implementation_implications":["Document migration assumptions","Identify data portability constraints","Estimate transition complexity"],"verification_method":"Review critical external dependencies for documented exit or contingency analysis.","outcome_learning":"Update exit assumptions after provider changes and industry or technology lifecycle events.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B79-R01","D55-B79-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B79-R01","D55-B79-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B79-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for External Dependencies","concept":"External dependency evidence quality","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance assess evidence supplied by an external dependency provider?","answer":"Provider-supplied evidence should be interpreted according to its scope, version, test conditions, ownership, independence, and applicability to the consuming architecture. Provider claims should not automatically be treated as direct evidence of consumer-system behavior.","context":"External documentation and benchmarks may describe a component without proving behavior in the consumer's environment.","objective":"Prevent external claims from being overgeneralized in architecture decisions.","requirements":["Identify evidence scope","Record provider version","Assess applicability","Separate provider claims from consumer validation"],"constraints":["Consumer teams may lack access to internal provider implementation details"],"prerequisites":["External dependency documentation","Consumer architecture context"],"dependencies":["Evidence governance","Performance and security governance"],"alternatives":["Accept provider evidence directly","Reject external evidence","Use provider evidence plus consumer validation"],"evidence":["Evidence validity depends on context and scope, including the difference between component claims and system behavior."],"tradeoffs":["Consumer validation increases effort but improves confidence in context-specific behavior."],"decision":"Use external evidence as input while preserving the distinction between provider claims and consumer verification.","rationale":"A component-level claim does not automatically establish system-level behavior.","consequences":["Architecture decisions become more evidence-aware","Validation responsibilities remain explicit"],"implementation_implications":["Record evidence provenance","Map provider claims to architecture assumptions","Perform consumer-side validation where material"],"verification_method":"Review architecture decisions that rely on external evidence and classify provider claims separately from local validation.","outcome_learning":"Track cases where external evidence failed to predict consumer-system behavior.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B79-R02","D55-B79-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B79-R02","D55-B79-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B79-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B79","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance for External Dependencies","concept":"External dependency concentration governance","knowledge_type":"RISK_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should governance detect excessive concentration of architecture dependencies on one external provider?","answer":"Governance should map critical capabilities and systems to external providers and identify concentration where multiple independent functions depend on the same provider, infrastructure layer, or control plane.","context":"Provider concentration can create correlated failure, migration, pricing, or lifecycle risk.","objective":"Make external dependency concentration visible before it becomes a systemic constraint.","requirements":["Map provider dependencies","Identify critical capabilities","Detect shared provider concentration","Assess alternative or contingency options"],"constraints":["Some concentration may be intentional or unavoidable"],"prerequisites":["Dependency inventory","Criticality model"],"dependencies":["External dependency governance","Resilience governance"],"alternatives":["Provider-by-provider assessment","Concentration analysis","Multi-provider architecture"],"evidence":["Multiple critical dependencies on a common provider can create correlated architectural risk."],"tradeoffs":["Reducing concentration can increase technical complexity and operational cost."],"decision":"Monitor provider concentration and require explicit analysis when concentration materially affects critical capabilities.","rationale":"Dependency risk is affected by shared failure and control domains, not only individual dependency quality.","consequences":["Architecture reviews expose common provider dependencies","Concentration becomes a risk signal"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain provider dependency graph","Classify critical capabilities","Track concentration thresholds"],"verification_method":"Analyze critical architecture dependencies and identify common external providers or control planes.","outcome_learning":"Use provider incidents and migration exercises to refine concentration assessment.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B79-R01","D55-B79-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B79-R01","D55-B79-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B80-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance Learning","concept":"Architecture governance feedback loop","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_LEARNING","question":"How should architecture governance learn from decisions and their outcomes?","answer":"Governance should connect architecture decisions to implementation evidence, verification results, operational outcomes, exceptions, incidents, and subsequent changes so that successful and unsuccessful assumptions can inform future decisions.","context":"Governance becomes static when decisions are recorded but outcomes are not connected back to the reasoning that produced them.","objective":"Turn architecture governance into a learning system rather than a one-time approval process.","requirements":["Link decisions to outcomes","Capture verification evidence","Record assumption changes","Feed validated lessons into future governance"],"constraints":["Outcome attribution can be difficult","Operational evidence may be incomplete"],"prerequisites":["Decision repository","Outcome evidence","Architecture metrics"],"dependencies":["Architecture decisions","Experience learning"],"alternatives":["Approval-only governance","Periodic retrospective","Continuous governance learning"],"evidence":["Decision quality improves when assumptions can be compared with subsequent evidence and outcomes."],"tradeoffs":["Learning requires post-decision evidence collection but can reduce repeated architectural mistakes."],"decision":"Establish an explicit feedback loop from governed decisions to verified outcomes and reusable lessons.","rationale":"Architecture governance should preserve not only what was decided but what was learned.","consequences":["Architecture knowledge becomes cumulative","Governance rules can evolve based on evidence"],"implementation_implications":["Link decisions to implementation and outcome records","Classify lessons by evidence strength","Review recurring failures and successful patterns"],"verification_method":"Trace representative architecture decisions through implementation and outcome records.","outcome_learning":"Promote only evidence-supported lessons into reusable governance knowledge.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B80-R02","D55-B80-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B80-R02","D55-B80-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B80-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance Learning","concept":"Architecture governance lesson validation","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_LEARNING","question":"How should an architecture lesson be validated before it becomes a reusable governance rule?","answer":"A lesson should be supported by identifiable evidence, scoped to the conditions under which it was observed, separated from interpretation, and reviewed for contradictory evidence before being generalized into a governance rule.","context":"A single architecture outcome may reflect context-specific conditions rather than a universal principle.","objective":"Prevent anecdotal experience from becoming unsupported governance policy.","requirements":["Identify source evidence","State applicability conditions","Separate observation from inference","Check contradictory evidence"],"constraints":["Evidence may be sparse","Context may differ between systems"],"prerequisites":["Outcome records","Evidence provenance"],"dependencies":["Governance feedback loop","Contradiction management"],"alternatives":["Promote every lesson","Ignore operational lessons","Evidence-gated lesson promotion"],"evidence":["Generalization requires more than observing one outcome."],"tradeoffs":["Validation delays rule creation but reduces overgeneralization."],"decision":"Require evidence and applicability analysis before promoting lessons into reusable governance controls.","rationale":"Governance rules should represent validated knowledge rather than isolated anecdotes.","consequences":["Lessons retain context","Contradictions remain visible"],"implementation_implications":["Store evidence level","Record applicability boundaries","Track contradictory outcomes"],"verification_method":"Review promoted governance lessons for evidence, scope, and contradiction handling.","outcome_learning":"Retire or narrow lessons when later evidence contradicts their original generalization.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B80-R01","D55-B80-R03"],"relationships":["D55-B80-R01","D55-B80-R03"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B80-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance Learning","concept":"Architecture governance contradiction management","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_LEARNING","question":"How should governance handle contradictory architecture evidence?","answer":"Contradictory evidence should remain explicitly represented, with differences in context, workload, architecture version, assumptions, or measurement method investigated before a new generalized rule is created.","context":"Different architectures can produce apparently conflicting outcomes under different conditions.","objective":"Prevent governance from hiding contradictory evidence through premature simplification.","requirements":["Record contradiction","Compare contexts","Identify possible causes","Preserve unresolved uncertainty"],"constraints":["Some contradictions may remain unresolved","Evidence quality may differ"],"prerequisites":["Evidence provenance","Outcome records"],"dependencies":["Lesson validation","Architecture decision repository"],"alternatives":["Choose one result","Average contradictory results","Context-aware contradiction management"],"evidence":["Conflicting observations can result from materially different contexts rather than invalid evidence."],"tradeoffs":["Preserving contradictions increases knowledge complexity but reduces false certainty."],"decision":"Represent contradictory evidence explicitly until contextual analysis justifies reconciliation or scoped conclusions.","rationale":"Knowledge quality improves when uncertainty and conflicting evidence remain visible.","consequences":["Governance knowledge becomes context-sensitive","Rules may be scoped rather than universal"],"implementation_implications":["Record evidence conditions","Link contradictions to affected rules","Require reconciliation analysis"],"verification_method":"Inspect governance knowledge for contradictory evidence and verify that context and resolution status are represented.","outcome_learning":"Update governance rules when contradictions reveal missing applicability conditions.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B80-R02","D55-B80-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B80-R02","D55-B80-R04"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B80-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance Learning","concept":"Governance rule retirement","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_LEARNING","question":"When should an architecture governance rule be retired or replaced?","answer":"A rule should be reassessed when its underlying assumptions no longer hold, repeated exceptions indicate poor applicability, evidence contradicts its intended effect, technology or architecture changes invalidate it, or its governance cost exceeds its justified value.","context":"Governance rules can become obsolete or counterproductive as architecture evolves.","objective":"Prevent obsolete governance controls from accumulating indefinitely.","requirements":["Define rule ownership","Monitor applicability","Track exceptions and contradictions","Provide retirement or revision criteria"],"constraints":["Removing a rule can create unintended gaps","Historical decisions may still reference old rules"],"prerequisites":["Versioned governance policies","Rule outcome metrics"],"dependencies":["Policy lifecycle governance","Contradiction management"],"alternatives":["Permanent rules","Periodic rule review","Evidence-triggered rule retirement"],"evidence":["Governance controls are themselves architecture policies whose validity can change over time."],"tradeoffs":["Retiring rules reduces unnecessary governance but requires careful replacement or gap analysis."],"decision":"Govern rules as lifecycle-managed artifacts subject to evidence-based revision or retirement.","rationale":"A governance system must adapt when its rules no longer produce justified outcomes.","consequences":["Policy catalogs remain maintainable","Historical policy versions remain traceable"],"implementation_implications":["Track rule outcomes","Monitor exceptions and false positives","Define replacement or retirement process"],"verification_method":"Review governance rules for evidence of continued applicability and documented lifecycle status.","outcome_learning":"Use retired-rule analysis to identify patterns in governance evolution.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B80-R02","D55-B80-R05"],"relationships":["D55-B80-R02","D55-B80-R05"]}
{"record_id":"D55-B80-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B80","topic":"Architecture Governance","subtopic":"Architecture Governance Learning","concept":"Architecture governance maturity evolution","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_LEARNING","question":"How should architecture governance maturity evolve without becoming a bureaucracy measurement exercise?","answer":"Maturity should be evaluated through evidence of governance effectiveness: decision traceability, appropriate review proportionality, conformance quality, exception behavior, evidence freshness, outcome learning, and reduction of repeated architectural failure modes.","context":"Counting governance processes or documents can create the appearance of maturity without demonstrating useful architectural outcomes.","objective":"Measure governance capability through meaningful architectural evidence.","requirements":["Measure governance effectiveness","Track outcome quality","Assess learning capability","Identify persistent failure modes"],"constraints":["Outcome metrics can be difficult to attribute solely to governance"],"prerequisites":["Governance metrics","Decision and outcome records"],"dependencies":["Governance feedback loop","Architecture metrics"],"alternatives":["Process-count maturity","Documentation maturity","Outcome-oriented maturity"],"evidence":["Governance maturity is more meaningful when it reflects decision quality and learning rather than activity volume alone."],"tradeoffs":["Outcome-oriented assessment is harder to measure but less vulnerable to superficial process optimization."],"decision":"Assess architecture governance maturity primarily through effectiveness, evidence quality, proportionality, and learning capability.","rationale":"The purpose of governance is better architecture decisions and controlled risk, not maximum process volume.","consequences":["Governance improvement focuses on useful outcomes","Excessive process becomes identifiable as a potential governance defect"],"implementation_implications":["Track decision quality indicators","Measure review latency and exception patterns","Measure evidence freshness and lesson reuse","Review recurring architecture failures"],"verification_method":"Conduct periodic governance capability assessments using outcome and evidence indicators rather than document counts alone.","outcome_learning":"Use assessment results to refine governance mechanisms, automation, evidence requirements, and decision authority.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B80-R01","D55-B80-R04"],"relationships":["D55-B80-R01","D55-B80-R04"]}

{"record_id":"D55-B81-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Architecture Governance for Resilience","subtopic":"Resilience Governance","concept":"Governance of architectural resilience requirements","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance ensure that resilience requirements are represented and enforced in architectural decisions?","answer":"Architecture governance should require resilience objectives to be explicit, measurable where practical, traceable to architectural decisions, and validated at appropriate lifecycle checkpoints. Governance should examine failure domains, recovery expectations, dependency concentration, degraded-mode behavior, and recovery mechanisms rather than treating resilience as an informal implementation concern.","context":"Systems with availability, continuity, or recovery requirements can accumulate architectural decisions that unintentionally create common failure modes or excessive dependency concentration.","objective":"Ensure architectural decisions preserve required resilience characteristics throughout system evolution.","requirements":["Resilience objectives must be identifiable","Critical failure domains must be considered","Architectural dependencies must be assessed","Recovery and degraded-mode behavior must be reviewable"],"constraints":["Not every resilience property can be completely verified statically","Recovery behavior may depend on operational infrastructure","Business recovery objectives can vary by service"],"prerequisites":["Defined system scope","Known critical capabilities","Relevant availability and recovery requirements"],"dependencies":["Architecture decision records","Service dependency information","Operational resilience requirements"],"alternatives":["Governance-only checklist","Architecture review with resilience evidence","Automated resilience policy checks combined with human review"],"evidence":"The governance principle is that quality attributes become enforceable architectural concerns when they are explicitly represented as requirements, decision criteria, and verification obligations.","tradeoffs":{"strict_governance":"Improves consistency but increases review overhead","lightweight_governance":"Reduces friction but can permit unrecognized resilience risks","automated_controls":"Improve repeatability but cannot replace contextual architectural judgment"},"decision":"Require resilience considerations to be represented in relevant architecture decisions and verified through proportionate evidence.","rationale":"Resilience failures can originate from architectural structure rather than individual implementation defects.","consequences":["Architecture records become more explicit","Review effort increases for resilience-critical systems","Verification responsibilities become traceable"],"implementation_implications":["Record resilience requirements in architecture metadata","Map critical dependencies and failure domains","Define verification evidence for resilience-sensitive decisions"],"verification_method":"Inspect whether applicable architecture decisions identify resilience requirements, failure assumptions, dependencies, and verification evidence.","outcome_learning":"Governance effectiveness should be evaluated using detected architectural gaps, review findings, recovery-test evidence, and recurring resilience-related changes.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B81-R02","D55-B81-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B81-R02"},{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B81-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B81-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Architecture Governance for Resilience","subtopic":"Failure Domain Governance","concept":"Architectural separation of failure domains","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should governance evaluate when architectural components share potential failure domains?","answer":"Governance should identify whether independently intended components depend on common infrastructure, configuration, deployment, identity, network, storage, or control-plane resources that could fail together. Shared dependencies are not inherently incorrect, but their risk should be explicit and proportionate to system criticality.","context":"Architectural diagrams can represent components as separate while operational dependencies cause them to fail as a single unit.","objective":"Make correlated failure assumptions visible during architecture review.","requirements":["Critical shared dependencies should be identified","Failure correlation should be considered","Accepted concentration risk should be documented"],"constraints":["Complete failure-domain independence may be economically or technically impractical","Infrastructure details can change independently of application architecture"],"prerequisites":["Dependency inventory","System criticality information","Relevant infrastructure boundaries"],"dependencies":["Architecture diagrams","Infrastructure topology","Service ownership metadata"],"alternatives":["Full isolation","Selective isolation of critical paths","Explicit acceptance of shared failure domains"],"evidence":"Architectural independence should be evaluated according to actual dependency structure rather than component naming or diagram boundaries alone.","tradeoffs":{"isolation":"Reduces correlated failure but increases complexity and cost","shared_infrastructure":"Simplifies operations but increases common-mode risk","documented_acceptance":"Preserves pragmatic designs while making residual risk visible"},"decision":"Governance should require critical shared failure domains to be identified and either mitigated or explicitly accepted.","rationale":"Undocumented common dependencies can invalidate assumptions about architectural resilience.","consequences":["Architecture reviews become dependency-aware","Residual concentration risk becomes auditable","Changes to shared infrastructure can trigger architectural reassessment"],"implementation_implications":["Represent critical shared dependencies in architecture records","Associate high-impact shared dependencies with affected services","Define review triggers for changes to critical failure domains"],"verification_method":"Compare documented failure-domain assumptions against available dependency and infrastructure evidence.","outcome_learning":"Repeated correlated failures or review findings should trigger updates to architecture controls and dependency models.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B81-R01","D55-B81-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D55-B81-R01"},{"type":"INFORMS","record_id":"D55-B81-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B81-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Architecture Governance for Resilience","subtopic":"Degraded Mode Governance","concept":"Governance of graceful degradation","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance treat degraded-mode behavior?","answer":"Governance should determine whether critical capabilities have defined behavior when dependencies, capacity, or supporting services become unavailable or impaired. Where degraded operation is required, the architectural decision should identify which capabilities remain available, which are disabled, and how consistency, safety, and recovery are preserved.","context":"A system may remain technically available while critical functions become unusable under partial failure.","objective":"Ensure degraded behavior is deliberate rather than accidental.","requirements":["Critical degraded states must be identified where applicable","Priority capabilities should have explicit behavior","Safety and data-integrity constraints must remain defined"],"constraints":["Some systems cannot safely operate in degraded mode","Degraded behavior can increase complexity"],"prerequisites":["Critical capability classification","Failure scenarios","Business continuity requirements"],"dependencies":["Service dependency model","Operational requirements","Recovery architecture"],"alternatives":["Fail closed","Fail open","Selective degradation","Full service shutdown"],"evidence":"Graceful degradation is an architectural property when behavior under partial failure is intentionally designed rather than left to incidental implementation behavior.","tradeoffs":{"selective_degradation":"Preserves important capabilities but increases design complexity","full_shutdown":"Simplifies safety reasoning but reduces availability","fail_open":"May preserve functionality but can violate security or consistency requirements"},"decision":"Governance should require explicit degraded-mode decisions for systems where partial failure behavior materially affects critical outcomes.","rationale":"Unspecified degraded behavior can produce inconsistent, unsafe, or operationally unpredictable states.","consequences":["Failure behavior becomes reviewable","Architectural decisions include partial-failure assumptions","Testing requirements may increase"],"implementation_implications":["Define degraded states","Document capability priorities","Create tests or operational exercises for important degraded paths"],"verification_method":"Review architecture documentation and applicable tests for explicit degraded-state behavior.","outcome_learning":"Unexpected degraded behavior should be captured as architectural feedback when it reveals a missing or incorrect design assumption.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B81-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B81-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B81-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Architecture Governance for Resilience","subtopic":"Recovery Dependency Governance","concept":"Governance of recovery-path dependencies","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should architecture governance separately examine dependencies required during recovery?","answer":"Recovery mechanisms can depend on the same infrastructure, credentials, services, networks, or control systems that are impaired during the original failure. Governance should therefore evaluate whether recovery dependencies remain available under the failure scenarios they are intended to address.","context":"A recovery design can appear complete while relying on resources located inside the failure boundary it is supposed to overcome.","objective":"Prevent circular or invalid recovery architectures.","requirements":["Recovery dependencies should be identified","Critical recovery assumptions should be documented","Circular recovery dependencies should be detected"],"constraints":["Recovery infrastructure may intentionally share some resources","Complete independence may be impractical"],"prerequisites":["Recovery architecture","Failure scenarios","Dependency information"],"dependencies":["Business continuity requirements","Infrastructure architecture","Operational procedures"],"alternatives":["Independent recovery infrastructure","Partially shared recovery infrastructure","Manual recovery procedures"],"evidence":"A recovery mechanism is only effective for a failure scenario if its required dependencies remain usable or have an alternative path.","tradeoffs":{"independent_recovery":"Improves isolation but increases cost","shared_recovery":"Reduces cost but can preserve common failure modes","manual_recovery":"Can reduce automation dependencies but increases human coordination requirements"},"decision":"Include recovery-path dependency analysis in governance for systems with material recovery requirements.","rationale":"Recovery dependencies can create hidden circular dependencies that invalidate recovery assumptions.","consequences":["Recovery architecture becomes more explicit","Additional dependency analysis is required","Recovery tests can expose architectural gaps"],"implementation_implications":["Model recovery dependencies separately where needed","Identify resources unavailable during each failure scenario","Verify recovery paths against those assumptions"],"verification_method":"Trace each critical recovery scenario through its required dependencies and confirm that the dependencies remain available or have documented alternatives.","outcome_learning":"Recovery-test failures should update both recovery procedures and architecture governance controls when architectural assumptions are invalidated.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B81-R02","D55-B81-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"DEPENDS_ON","record_id":"D55-B81-R02"},{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B81-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B81-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B81","topic":"Architecture Governance for Resilience","subtopic":"Resilience Evidence","concept":"Evidence proportionality for resilience governance","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should governance determine how much resilience evidence is required for an architecture decision?","answer":"Evidence requirements should be proportional to system criticality, failure impact, architectural novelty, dependency concentration, and uncertainty. Low-risk decisions may require documented reasoning and lightweight checks, while high-impact decisions may require dependency analysis, failure testing, recovery evidence, or independent review.","context":"Uniform evidence requirements can either burden low-risk changes or under-validate high-risk architectural decisions.","objective":"Match governance evidence effort to architectural risk and uncertainty.","requirements":["Risk factors should influence evidence depth","Evidence expectations should be defined before approval","High-impact decisions should receive stronger verification"],"constraints":["Evidence collection has cost","Some architectural properties cannot be fully demonstrated before deployment"],"prerequisites":["Risk classification","System criticality","Defined governance process"],"dependencies":["Architecture review policy","Verification mechanisms","Operational evidence"],"alternatives":["Uniform evidence threshold","Risk-proportional evidence","Reviewer discretion without explicit criteria"],"evidence":"Proportional governance aligns assurance effort with potential impact and uncertainty rather than treating every architectural decision identically.","tradeoffs":{"uniform":"Simple but inefficient and potentially insufficient","risk_proportional":"More efficient but requires reliable risk classification","discretionary":"Flexible but less consistent and less auditable"},"decision":"Use risk- and uncertainty-proportional evidence requirements for resilience-sensitive architecture decisions.","rationale":"The assurance burden should reflect the consequences of being wrong and the strength of available evidence.","consequences":["Governance criteria become more nuanced","High-risk decisions receive greater scrutiny","Low-risk decisions can progress with less overhead"],"implementation_implications":["Define evidence tiers","Map architecture risk categories to verification expectations","Record evidence sufficiency in decision records"],"verification_method":"Audit sampled decisions to determine whether evidence depth corresponds to documented architectural risk and uncertainty.","outcome_learning":"Governance metrics should identify cases where evidence was insufficient or unnecessarily burdensome and adjust evidence tiers accordingly.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B81-R01","D55-B81-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"GOVERNS","record_id":"D55-B81-R01"},{"type":"GOVERNS","record_id":"D55-B81-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B82-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Architecture Governance for Observability","subtopic":"Architectural Observability","concept":"Governance of observability architecture requirements","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"When should observability be treated as an architectural governance concern?","answer":"Observability should be governed as an architectural concern when the ability to detect, diagnose, verify, or operate system behavior materially affects reliability, security, compliance, or business outcomes. Governance should identify required telemetry capabilities without prescribing implementation unnecessarily.","context":"Systems can satisfy functional requirements while remaining difficult to diagnose or verify because important behavior is not observable.","objective":"Ensure architecture decisions provide sufficient visibility for intended operational and verification needs.","requirements":["Critical behaviors should have observable signals where necessary","Telemetry requirements should be traceable to operational objectives","Observability constraints should be documented"],"constraints":["Telemetry increases storage, processing, and operational cost","Excessive telemetry can create noise or sensitive-data exposure"],"prerequisites":["Operational objectives","Critical behavior identification","Data sensitivity considerations"],"dependencies":["Monitoring architecture","Logging architecture","Tracing and metrics capabilities"],"alternatives":["Minimal telemetry","Comprehensive telemetry","Risk-based observability requirements"],"evidence":"Observability requirements should derive from the behaviors that operators, automated controls, and verification processes must distinguish.","tradeoffs":{"minimal":"Lower cost but reduced diagnostic capability","comprehensive":"Greater visibility but higher cost and complexity","risk_based":"Balances value and cost but requires prioritization"},"decision":"Govern observability at the architecture level when insufficient visibility would materially impair operation, diagnosis, security, or verification.","rationale":"Unobservable critical behavior can create avoidable operational uncertainty.","consequences":["Some architecture decisions require explicit telemetry considerations","Telemetry becomes part of selected quality-attribute analysis","Sensitive telemetry requires additional governance"],"implementation_implications":["Identify critical observable states and transitions","Define required signal characteristics","Review telemetry data handling alongside architecture decisions"],"verification_method":"Check whether critical operational and verification objectives have corresponding observable evidence paths.","outcome_learning":"Operational incidents and diagnostic gaps should feed updates to observability requirements and governance controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B82-R02","D55-B82-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B82-R02"},{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B82-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B82-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Architecture Governance for Observability","subtopic":"Telemetry Data Governance","concept":"Governance of telemetry sensitivity and handling","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance address sensitive information contained in telemetry?","answer":"Governance should evaluate whether logs, traces, metrics, events, and diagnostic artifacts can contain confidential, personal, credential-like, or otherwise sensitive information. Required controls should include data minimization, access restrictions, retention rules, and appropriate protection mechanisms where applicable.","context":"Observability systems can unintentionally expand the security and privacy boundary of an application by duplicating sensitive information into telemetry stores.","objective":"Ensure observability architecture does not create uncontrolled secondary data exposure.","requirements":["Sensitive telemetry sources should be identified","Collection should be proportionate to operational need","Access and retention should be governed","Secrets and unnecessary sensitive payloads should not be intentionally emitted"],"constraints":["Removing data can reduce diagnostic value","Sensitivity classification may vary by deployment context"],"prerequisites":["Data classification","Telemetry architecture","Security requirements"],"dependencies":["Logging and tracing systems","Access-control architecture","Retention policies"],"alternatives":["Collect everything","Collect only required fields","Redact or transform sensitive fields before collection"],"evidence":"Telemetry is a data-processing path and therefore inherits relevant data governance and security considerations.","tradeoffs":{"full_collection":"Maximizes diagnostic information but increases exposure","minimized_collection":"Reduces exposure but may limit debugging","redaction":"Balances visibility and protection but introduces processing complexity"},"decision":"Require architecture governance to assess telemetry data sensitivity and define proportionate handling controls.","rationale":"Observability should improve system visibility without creating an avoidable sensitive-data repository.","consequences":["Telemetry architecture receives security review where appropriate","Data minimization becomes an architectural consideration","Operational teams need clear telemetry handling rules"],"implementation_implications":["Classify telemetry fields","Apply redaction or minimization where required","Restrict access to sensitive telemetry","Define retention requirements"],"verification_method":"Inspect telemetry schemas and representative outputs for unnecessary sensitive data and verify applicable access and retention controls.","outcome_learning":"Telemetry-related security or privacy findings should feed architecture governance improvements.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B82-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"CONSTRAINS","record_id":"D55-B82-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B82-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Architecture Governance for Observability","subtopic":"Diagnostic Coverage","concept":"Governance of diagnostic coverage","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"How can architecture governance determine whether diagnostic coverage is sufficient?","answer":"Diagnostic coverage should be evaluated against important failure modes, critical workflows, externally visible behavior, and architectural boundaries. Governance should ask whether available evidence can distinguish plausible causes sufficiently to support diagnosis, verification, and corrective action.","context":"A system may emit large volumes of telemetry while still lacking signals capable of distinguishing important failure causes.","objective":"Evaluate observability by diagnostic usefulness rather than telemetry volume.","requirements":["Important failure modes should have diagnostic evidence","Critical workflows should have traceable signals where necessary","Evidence should support meaningful differentiation of causes"],"constraints":["Not all causes can be uniquely observable","Diagnostic requirements depend on system complexity"],"prerequisites":["Failure-mode analysis","Critical workflow identification","Telemetry inventory"],"dependencies":["Observability architecture","Incident response processes","Verification strategy"],"alternatives":["Volume-based observability metrics","Coverage-based diagnostic analysis","Operator-defined qualitative assessment"],"evidence":"Diagnostic value depends on whether telemetry helps distinguish relevant system states and causes, not simply on the amount of telemetry produced.","tradeoffs":{"volume_metrics":"Easy to measure but weakly correlated with diagnostic sufficiency","coverage_analysis":"More useful but requires system-specific reasoning","qualitative_review":"Flexible but less reproducible"},"decision":"Governance should assess diagnostic coverage against important failure and workflow scenarios rather than telemetry quantity alone.","rationale":"High telemetry volume does not guarantee actionable observability.","consequences":["Architecture reviews become scenario-oriented","Telemetry gaps can be identified before incidents","Observability requirements become more testable"],"implementation_implications":["Map critical scenarios to required signals","Define observable states for important failures","Validate diagnostic paths through controlled tests where practical"],"verification_method":"Select representative failure scenarios and determine whether available telemetry provides sufficient evidence to distinguish relevant causes.","outcome_learning":"Post-incident diagnostic gaps should update the scenario-to-signal coverage model.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B82-R01","D55-B82-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B82-R01"},{"type":"INFORMS","record_id":"D55-B82-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B82-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Architecture Governance for Observability","subtopic":"Verification Observability","concept":"Governance of evidence required to verify system behavior","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why should architecture governance consider observability requirements for verification?","answer":"Verification activities often require evidence about internal state transitions, external effects, timing, errors, or resource behavior. Governance should identify when architecture must expose sufficient evidence to establish whether critical requirements were actually satisfied.","context":"A requirement can be difficult to verify when architecture intentionally or accidentally hides the relevant state or outcome.","objective":"Ensure architecture does not make critical requirements unverifiable.","requirements":["Verification-critical states should have evidence paths","Evidence should be attributable to the relevant system behavior","Verification data should have appropriate integrity and access controls"],"constraints":["Exposing internal state can increase coupling","Some verification must rely on externally observable behavior"],"prerequisites":["Requirement definitions","Verification criteria","System boundary definition"],"dependencies":["Testing strategy","Observability architecture","Audit evidence design"],"alternatives":["Black-box verification","White-box evidence","Hybrid verification"],"evidence":"Architectural decisions affect what evidence can be produced and therefore can directly affect verifiability.","tradeoffs":{"black_box":"Lower coupling but may provide less diagnostic detail","white_box":"Richer evidence but greater architectural coupling","hybrid":"Balances external behavior and internal evidence"},"decision":"Treat verifiability requirements as an architecture concern when critical requirements cannot otherwise be reliably demonstrated.","rationale":"A system that cannot produce sufficient evidence may be difficult to verify even when its implementation appears correct.","consequences":["Verification needs influence architecture decisions","Evidence pathways require governance","Some internal interfaces may need controlled exposure"],"implementation_implications":["Map requirements to verification evidence","Define evidence boundaries","Protect verification telemetry and artifacts"],"verification_method":"Trace selected critical requirements to concrete evidence sources and verify that those sources can establish satisfaction.","outcome_learning":"Verification failures caused by missing evidence should trigger architecture governance review rather than being treated solely as test-process defects.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B82-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B82-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B82-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B82","topic":"Architecture Governance for Observability","subtopic":"Observability Cost Governance","concept":"Governance of observability cost and signal value","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should architecture governance balance observability value against telemetry cost?","answer":"Governance should prioritize signals according to their operational, security, verification, and diagnostic value while considering storage, processing, transmission, retention, and management costs. The objective is not maximum telemetry but sufficient useful evidence at an acceptable cost and risk.","context":"Unbounded telemetry collection can create financial, performance, privacy, and operational burdens.","objective":"Ensure observability architecture remains economically and operationally sustainable.","requirements":["High-value signals should be prioritized","Cost drivers should be visible for material telemetry architectures","Retention and collection decisions should be intentional"],"constraints":["Telemetry value is context-dependent","Costs vary by deployment scale and architecture"],"prerequisites":["Critical signal identification","Telemetry architecture","Cost model where available"],"dependencies":["Logging infrastructure","Metrics systems","Tracing systems","Data retention architecture"],"alternatives":["Collect all telemetry","Tiered collection","Sampling","Event-triggered collection"],"evidence":"Observability architecture has resource and data-management consequences that should be considered alongside diagnostic value.","tradeoffs":{"full_collection":"Highest visibility but potentially high cost","sampling":"Lower cost but can miss rare events","tiered_collection":"Balances cost and value but increases policy complexity","event_triggered":"Efficient for targeted diagnostics but depends on trigger quality"},"decision":"Use value- and risk-aware governance to determine observability collection and retention levels.","rationale":"Architectural observability should be sustainable and proportionate to its intended purpose.","consequences":["Telemetry policies become explicit","High-value signals receive stronger retention or reliability requirements","Low-value signals can be reduced"],"implementation_implications":["Define signal priorities","Track material telemetry cost drivers","Use sampling or tiering where appropriate","Review retention decisions periodically"],"verification_method":"Compare telemetry policies with stated diagnostic, security, and verification objectives and inspect material cost drivers.","outcome_learning":"Cost increases, diagnostic gaps, and unused telemetry should inform future observability governance decisions.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B82-R01","D55-B82-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"CONSTRAINS","record_id":"D55-B82-R01"},{"type":"BALANCES","record_id":"D55-B82-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B83-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Architecture Governance for Interoperability","subtopic":"Interoperability Governance","concept":"Governance of interoperability requirements","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance manage interoperability requirements across independently evolving systems?","answer":"Governance should identify required interaction boundaries, compatibility expectations, ownership, data semantics, failure behavior, and evolution constraints. Interoperability should be governed according to actual business and technical dependencies rather than assumed simply because systems communicate.","context":"Connected systems can become tightly coupled through undocumented assumptions about protocols, data semantics, timing, or error behavior.","objective":"Make interoperability dependencies explicit and evolvable.","requirements":["Interaction contracts should be identifiable","Compatibility assumptions should be documented","Ownership of integration boundaries should be clear","Evolution constraints should be understood"],"constraints":["External systems may be outside organizational control","Legacy interfaces can impose fixed constraints"],"prerequisites":["System boundary definition","Interface inventory","Dependency ownership information"],"dependencies":["API contracts","Data schemas","Integration architecture"],"alternatives":["Direct integration","Shared contract layer","Adapter or anti-corruption boundary"],"evidence":"Interoperability risk arises not only from protocol incompatibility but also from mismatched semantics, lifecycle assumptions, and failure expectations.","tradeoffs":{"direct":"Simple initially but can increase coupling","adapter":"Improves isolation but adds components","shared_contract":"Improves consistency but requires coordinated governance"},"decision":"Govern interoperability through explicit contracts, ownership, compatibility expectations, and change controls.","rationale":"Explicit integration boundaries reduce hidden architectural coupling.","consequences":["Integration dependencies become auditable","Interface changes require clearer impact analysis","Ownership responsibilities become more visible"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain interface inventories","Define compatibility requirements","Associate integration boundaries with owners","Record material interoperability decisions"],"verification_method":"Inspect critical interfaces for documented contracts, ownership, compatibility expectations, and failure semantics.","outcome_learning":"Integration defects and recurring compatibility issues should update governance controls and contract requirements.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B83-R02","D55-B83-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B83-R02"},{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B83-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B83-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Architecture Governance for Interoperability","subtopic":"Compatibility Governance","concept":"Backward and forward compatibility governance","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should architecture governance require when interfaces or data contracts evolve?","answer":"Governance should determine whether existing consumers, producers, stored data, and operational processes remain compatible with the proposed change. Compatibility expectations should be explicit and tied to the actual lifecycle and ownership of the interface.","context":"An apparently small contract change can break systems that depend on undocumented behavior or historical versions.","objective":"Prevent uncontrolled compatibility breakage.","requirements":["Affected consumers should be identifiable where practical","Compatibility expectations should be explicit","Breaking changes should have migration or transition plans when required"],"constraints":["Consumer inventories can be incomplete","Some compatibility guarantees are impossible for tightly controlled internal interfaces"],"prerequisites":["Interface ownership","Contract definition","Dependency information"],"dependencies":["API lifecycle","Schema management","Release governance"],"alternatives":["Strict backward compatibility","Versioned contracts","Coordinated breaking migration"],"evidence":"Compatibility requirements depend on consumer population, lifecycle commitments, and ability to coordinate change.","tradeoffs":{"strict_compatibility":"Reduces consumer disruption but can constrain evolution","versioning":"Supports controlled evolution but increases lifecycle complexity","coordinated_breaking_change":"Can simplify long-term architecture but requires strong coordination"},"decision":"Require compatibility strategy to be explicit for material interface and contract changes.","rationale":"Compatibility cannot be assumed from implementation-level changes alone.","consequences":["Contract changes require impact analysis","Migration responsibilities become clearer","Version lifecycle may require governance"],"implementation_implications":["Record compatibility policy","Identify affected consumers","Define migration or versioning strategy for breaking changes"],"verification_method":"Review proposed contract changes against identified consumers and documented compatibility commitments.","outcome_learning":"Observed compatibility failures should update contract governance and dependency-discovery mechanisms.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B83-R01","D55-B83-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B83-R01"},{"type":"INFORMS","record_id":"D55-B83-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B83-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Architecture Governance for Interoperability","subtopic":"Semantic Compatibility","concept":"Governance of semantic compatibility","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why must architecture governance distinguish syntactic compatibility from semantic compatibility?","answer":"Two systems can successfully exchange structurally valid messages while interpreting their meaning differently. Governance should therefore evaluate semantic assumptions such as units, identifiers, lifecycle states, nullability, ordering, temporal meaning, and business interpretation where these affect system behavior.","context":"Schema validation alone may not reveal incompatible meanings between communicating systems.","objective":"Prevent semantic mismatches from being hidden behind syntactically valid interfaces.","requirements":["Important semantic assumptions should be documented","Ambiguous fields should have defined meaning","Semantic changes should receive impact analysis"],"constraints":["Semantic models can be domain-specific","Some semantics cannot be completely encoded in schemas"],"prerequisites":["Domain terminology","Interface contracts","Data definitions"],"dependencies":["Data architecture","API contracts","Domain models"],"alternatives":["Informal semantic documentation","Formal schemas plus semantic definitions","Shared domain model"],"evidence":"Structural validity establishes message shape but does not necessarily establish agreement about meaning.","tradeoffs":{"informal":"Low overhead but greater ambiguity","formal_semantics":"Improves clarity but requires modeling effort","shared_model":"Reduces divergence but can create coordination dependencies"},"decision":"Governance should require semantic compatibility analysis where meaning materially affects system behavior.","rationale":"Semantic divergence can cause correctness failures even when interfaces remain technically valid.","consequences":["Architecture reviews examine meaning as well as structure","Domain terminology becomes more important to integration governance","Contract changes require semantic impact analysis"],"implementation_implications":["Define important field semantics","Document units and lifecycle states","Use shared domain definitions where justified"],"verification_method":"Compare producer and consumer interpretations of critical contract elements using representative scenarios and documented semantics.","outcome_learning":"Semantic integration defects should update domain definitions and contract governance.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B83-R01","D55-B83-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"REFINES","record_id":"D55-B83-R01"},{"type":"CONSTRAINS","record_id":"D55-B83-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B83-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Architecture Governance for Interoperability","subtopic":"Integration Ownership","concept":"Governance of integration boundary ownership","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What governance information should exist for a critical integration boundary?","answer":"A critical integration boundary should have identifiable ownership, purpose, contract authority, change responsibility, dependency information, compatibility expectations, and escalation paths. Ownership should cover both technical stewardship and the authority to approve material contract changes.","context":"Interfaces without clear ownership can accumulate inconsistent changes, unresolved defects, and undocumented compatibility assumptions.","objective":"Establish accountable stewardship for architectural integration boundaries.","requirements":["Boundary ownership must be identifiable","Change authority must be defined","Escalation paths should exist","Contract stewardship responsibilities should be explicit"],"constraints":["Ownership may span organizational boundaries","External providers may control part of an interface"],"prerequisites":["Organizational ownership model","Interface inventory","Contract identification"],"dependencies":["Architecture governance","Service ownership","Change management"],"alternatives":["Single owner","Joint ownership","Provider-owned contract with internal consumer stewardship"],"evidence":"Clear accountability reduces ambiguity around who maintains and evolves architectural boundaries.","tradeoffs":{"single_owner":"Clear accountability but may centralize responsibility","joint":"Reflects shared responsibility but can create decision ambiguity","external_owner":"Necessary for some dependencies but limits direct control"},"decision":"Require explicit stewardship and change authority for material integration boundaries.","rationale":"Architectural dependencies require accountable ownership to remain governable over time.","consequences":["Interface inventories become ownership-aware","Escalation becomes more predictable","Contract lifecycle responsibilities become explicit"],"implementation_implications":["Associate interfaces with owners","Define change authority","Record external ownership constraints","Review ownership during organizational changes"],"verification_method":"Audit critical integration boundaries for ownership, authority, and escalation metadata.","outcome_learning":"Repeated ownership-related delays or failures should trigger governance adjustments.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B83-R01","D55-B83-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D55-B83-R01"},{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B83-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B83-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B83","topic":"Architecture Governance for Interoperability","subtopic":"Contract Change Evidence","concept":"Evidence requirements for interface evolution","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"What evidence should support approval of a material interface change?","answer":"Evidence should normally include the proposed contract change, affected compatibility assumptions, known consumers where discoverable, migration or versioning strategy when required, validation results, and unresolved risks. The depth should be proportional to the change impact.","context":"Contract changes can have distributed effects that are difficult to infer from the modified component alone.","objective":"Make interface evolution decisions traceable and reviewable.","requirements":["Change scope should be explicit","Compatibility impact should be assessed","Verification evidence should be linked","Residual risks should be visible"],"constraints":["Consumer discovery may be incomplete","Some dependencies are external"],"prerequisites":["Contract definition","Dependency inventory","Change classification"],"dependencies":["Architecture decision records","Testing evidence","Release governance"],"alternatives":["Informal approval","Standard change template","Risk-tiered evidence requirements"],"evidence":"Traceable change evidence allows governance to distinguish verified compatibility from assumptions.","tradeoffs":{"informal":"Fast but weakly auditable","template":"Consistent but may become bureaucratic","risk_tiered":"Proportionate but requires reliable impact classification"},"decision":"Require proportionate evidence packages for material interface changes.","rationale":"Architectural compatibility decisions should be auditable and linked to concrete evidence.","consequences":["Change records become richer","Review quality improves","Low-risk changes can remain lightweight when appropriately classified"],"implementation_implications":["Define interface-change evidence templates","Link validation artifacts to change records","Record unresolved compatibility uncertainty"],"verification_method":"Sample approved interface changes and verify that required evidence and compatibility reasoning are present.","outcome_learning":"Post-change compatibility incidents should feed updates to change classification and evidence requirements.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B83-R02","D55-B83-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"GOVERNS","record_id":"D55-B83-R02"},{"type":"GOVERNS","record_id":"D55-B83-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B84-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Architecture Governance for Data Boundaries","subtopic":"Data Ownership Governance","concept":"Governance of architectural data ownership","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance represent ownership of critical data boundaries?","answer":"Governance should identify accountable owners for critical data domains, authoritative sources, transformation responsibilities, access responsibilities, and material downstream dependencies. Ownership should distinguish authority over meaning and lifecycle from responsibility for technical storage.","context":"Data can be replicated across systems while semantic and lifecycle authority remains concentrated in one domain or governance boundary.","objective":"Prevent ambiguity about who controls critical data semantics and lifecycle decisions.","requirements":["Critical data domains should have accountable ownership","Authoritative sources should be identifiable where applicable","Transformation responsibilities should be explicit"],"constraints":["Data ownership may cross organizational boundaries","Some datasets have multiple legitimate consumers"],"prerequisites":["Data domain identification","System inventory","Organizational ownership model"],"dependencies":["Data architecture","Interface governance","Security governance"],"alternatives":["Centralized ownership","Domain ownership","Federated stewardship"],"evidence":"Data authority and technical storage are distinct architectural concerns and should not be conflated.","tradeoffs":{"centralized":"Consistent control but potential bottlenecks","domain_based":"Improves contextual ownership but requires coordination","federated":"Scales across organizations but requires clear contracts"},"decision":"Govern critical data boundaries through explicit semantic ownership, authoritative-source definitions, and stewardship responsibilities.","rationale":"Ambiguous data ownership creates architectural inconsistency and conflicting lifecycle decisions.","consequences":["Data architecture becomes ownership-aware","Changes to critical data semantics become traceable","Replication does not automatically transfer authority"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain data-domain ownership metadata","Identify authoritative sources","Define stewardship responsibilities","Record material semantic changes"],"verification_method":"Inspect critical data domains for owner, authority, stewardship, and lifecycle information.","outcome_learning":"Recurring data conflicts should trigger refinement of ownership boundaries and governance rules.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B84-R02","D55-B84-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B84-R02"},{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B84-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B84-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Architecture Governance for Data Boundaries","subtopic":"Data Lineage Governance","concept":"Governance of architectural data lineage","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should data lineage be an architecture governance requirement?","answer":"Data lineage should be governed when understanding data origin, transformations, movement, or destination is necessary for correctness, security, compliance, operational diagnosis, or decision traceability. Governance should focus on materially important lineage rather than requiring exhaustive mapping of every data movement.","context":"Without sufficient lineage, architectural changes can alter data meaning or provenance without exposing the resulting impact.","objective":"Maintain enough lineage knowledge to reason about critical data flows and architectural changes.","requirements":["Critical data flows should have identifiable lineage","Material transformations should be documented","Lineage evidence should remain maintainable"],"constraints":["Complete lineage can be expensive","Dynamic systems can make static lineage incomplete"],"prerequisites":["Data domain inventory","Critical flow identification","Transformation knowledge"],"dependencies":["Data architecture","Integration inventory","Observability"],"alternatives":["Full lineage mapping","Critical-path lineage","Event-derived lineage"],"evidence":"Lineage value is highest where data provenance and transformations materially affect correctness, governance, or operational reasoning.","tradeoffs":{"full":"Comprehensive but expensive","critical_path":"Lower cost but narrower coverage","dynamic":"Can reflect runtime behavior but requires additional instrumentation"},"decision":"Apply risk- and importance-based lineage governance to critical data flows.","rationale":"Targeted lineage provides useful architectural evidence without requiring exhaustive documentation of every data movement.","consequences":["Critical transformations become more visible","Architecture impact analysis improves","Lineage maintenance becomes a governance responsibility"],"implementation_implications":["Identify lineage-critical flows","Record material transformations","Link lineage to architecture changes","Review lineage when boundaries change"],"verification_method":"Trace representative critical data paths from source through material transformations to relevant destinations.","outcome_learning":"Lineage gaps discovered during incidents or change analysis should update lineage coverage priorities.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B84-R01","D55-B84-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"DEPENDS_ON","record_id":"D55-B84-R01"},{"type":"INFORMS","record_id":"D55-B84-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B84-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Architecture Governance for Data Boundaries","subtopic":"Data Lifecycle Governance","concept":"Governance of architectural data lifecycle states","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should architecture governance consider when defining the lifecycle of critical data?","answer":"Governance should identify relevant lifecycle states such as creation, active use, modification, archival, retention, deletion, or legal or operational hold where applicable. Architecture should support the required transitions and ownership responsibilities rather than leaving lifecycle behavior implicit.","context":"Data lifecycle requirements can affect storage architecture, interfaces, security boundaries, retention behavior, and recovery design.","objective":"Ensure architectural data lifecycle behavior is deliberate and traceable.","requirements":["Lifecycle states should be defined where material","Transition authority should be identifiable","Retention and deletion requirements should be represented","Lifecycle dependencies should be understood"],"constraints":["Lifecycle requirements vary by domain","Some lifecycle states are controlled by external systems"],"prerequisites":["Data classification","Ownership model","Relevant retention requirements"],"dependencies":["Storage architecture","Data governance","Security controls"],"alternatives":["Central lifecycle management","Domain-managed lifecycle","Hybrid lifecycle control"],"evidence":"Data lifecycle behavior can materially affect architecture and therefore requires explicit decisions when lifecycle constraints are significant.","tradeoffs":{"central":"Consistent but potentially less domain-aware","domain":"Context-aware but requires coordination","hybrid":"Balances control and flexibility but increases governance complexity"},"decision":"Govern critical data lifecycles through explicit states, ownership, transitions, and retention behavior.","rationale":"Implicit lifecycle assumptions create avoidable architectural and governance risks.","consequences":["Data architecture records become more precise","Deletion and retention behavior becomes reviewable","Lifecycle changes require impact analysis"],"implementation_implications":["Represent lifecycle states","Define transition authority","Map lifecycle requirements to storage and interfaces","Verify material deletion or archival behavior"],"verification_method":"Trace critical data through defined lifecycle states and inspect whether architectural mechanisms support required transitions.","outcome_learning":"Lifecycle failures should update architecture decisions and governance controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B84-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B84-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B84-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Architecture Governance for Data Boundaries","subtopic":"Data Duplication Governance","concept":"Governance of architectural data duplication","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should architecture governance evaluate intentional data duplication?","answer":"Governance should distinguish justified replication from uncontrolled duplication by examining purpose, authority, synchronization model, consistency expectations, failure behavior, retention, and ownership. Replication may improve performance, availability, or autonomy, but it also creates additional consistency and lifecycle responsibilities.","context":"Architectures often replicate data across services, regions, caches, analytics stores, or integration boundaries.","objective":"Make data replication trade-offs explicit and governable.","requirements":["Replication purpose should be identifiable","Consistency expectations should be documented","Source authority should remain clear","Failure and synchronization behavior should be understood"],"constraints":["Different workloads require different consistency models","Some replication is infrastructure-driven"],"prerequisites":["Data ownership","Data flow understanding","Consistency requirements"],"dependencies":["Data architecture","Resilience architecture","Integration contracts"],"alternatives":["Single authoritative store","Synchronous replication","Asynchronous replication","Derived copies"],"evidence":"Data duplication introduces architectural state-management obligations that should be intentional.","tradeoffs":{"single_source":"Simpler authority but may limit availability or performance","synchronous":"Stronger consistency but increased coupling and latency","asynchronous":"Improves decoupling but introduces staleness","derived":"Useful for specialized workloads but requires transformation governance"},"decision":"Require material data replication to have explicit purpose, authority, consistency, synchronization, and lifecycle reasoning.","rationale":"Uncontrolled copies can create conflicting sources of truth and hidden operational complexity.","consequences":["Replication becomes visible in architecture review","Consistency assumptions become explicit","Data lifecycle governance expands to replicated stores"],"implementation_implications":["Document authoritative source","Define synchronization behavior","Identify acceptable staleness","Govern deletion and retention across copies"],"verification_method":"Inspect replicated data paths for documented authority, consistency, synchronization, and lifecycle behavior.","outcome_learning":"Consistency incidents and stale-data findings should inform future replication governance.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B84-R01","D55-B84-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"CONSTRAINS","record_id":"D55-B84-R01"},{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B84-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B84-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B84","topic":"Architecture Governance for Data Boundaries","subtopic":"Data Boundary Change Impact","concept":"Governance of data-boundary changes","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"What should trigger architecture governance review of a data boundary?","answer":"Material changes to data authority, schema semantics, ownership, replication, storage boundary, sensitivity, lifecycle, or cross-system movement should trigger proportionate architecture review. The trigger should depend on impact rather than merely on the size of the code change.","context":"Small implementation changes can produce large architectural effects when they alter data boundaries or semantics.","objective":"Detect architecture-significant data changes early.","requirements":["Material data-boundary changes should be identifiable","Impact analysis should consider affected consumers and lifecycle","Review depth should be risk-proportional"],"constraints":["Automated impact discovery may be incomplete","Data dependencies can be indirect"],"prerequisites":["Data inventory","Ownership information","Change classification"],"dependencies":["Change management","Data architecture","Interface governance"],"alternatives":["Code-diff-based triggers","Data-impact-based triggers","Manual architecture review"],"evidence":"Architecture impact is determined by changed assumptions and dependencies rather than source-code volume.","tradeoffs":{"code_based":"Easy to automate but may miss architectural effects","data_based":"More semantically relevant but harder to automate","manual":"Flexible but less consistent"},"decision":"Use data-boundary and semantic impact as architecture governance triggers for material data changes.","rationale":"Architecture governance should respond to architectural consequences rather than superficial change size.","consequences":["Some small code changes receive architecture review","Large but isolated changes may remain lightweight","Data impact analysis becomes part of governance"],"implementation_implications":["Define data-boundary change categories","Integrate triggers with change workflows","Maintain dependency metadata where practical"],"verification_method":"Review sampled changes to determine whether material data-boundary changes correctly triggered governance.","outcome_learning":"Missed architectural impacts should refine trigger definitions and dependency discovery.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B84-R02","D55-B84-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"GOVERNS","record_id":"D55-B84-R02"},{"type":"GOVERNS","record_id":"D55-B84-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B85-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Architecture Governance for Portability","subtopic":"Portability Requirements","concept":"Governance of architectural portability requirements","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"When should portability become an explicit architecture governance concern?","answer":"Portability should be governed when the ability to move a system, workload, data, or capability between environments is a material requirement or risk-management objective. Governance should distinguish required portability from hypothetical future portability and assess the architectural constraints each introduces.","context":"Portability can influence infrastructure abstractions, dependencies, deployment models, data formats, operational tooling, and platform coupling.","objective":"Prevent unnecessary lock-in where portability is a genuine requirement while avoiding speculative complexity.","requirements":["Actual portability objectives must be explicit","Material platform dependencies should be identified","Portability constraints should be evaluated against business value"],"constraints":["Portability can increase abstraction and operational complexity","Some platform capabilities have no equivalent across environments"],"prerequisites":["Deployment requirements","Platform dependency inventory","Business continuity or strategic requirements"],"dependencies":["Infrastructure architecture","Deployment architecture","Data architecture"],"alternatives":["Platform-specific architecture","Portable abstraction layer","Selective portability for critical workloads"],"evidence":"Portability is valuable only when tied to a concrete requirement, risk, or strategic objective.","tradeoffs":{"platform_specific":"Can exploit platform capabilities but increases dependency","portable":"Reduces some lock-in but may add complexity","selective":"Focuses effort on material portability needs"},"decision":"Govern portability according to explicit business, operational, regulatory, or strategic requirements rather than hypothetical future movement.","rationale":"Unbounded portability goals can impose complexity without corresponding value.","consequences":["Portability assumptions become explicit","Platform dependencies receive deliberate review","Abstraction decisions become evidence-based"],"implementation_implications":["Classify portability requirements","Inventory material platform-specific dependencies","Define portability verification scenarios when required"],"verification_method":"Trace stated portability requirements to architectural dependencies and determine whether selected constraints satisfy the intended objective.","outcome_learning":"Migration exercises and platform changes should provide evidence about actual portability assumptions.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B85-R02","D55-B85-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B85-R02"},{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B85-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B85-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Architecture Governance for Portability","subtopic":"Platform Coupling","concept":"Governance of platform-specific architectural coupling","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance evaluate platform-specific dependencies?","answer":"Governance should identify dependencies on platform-specific APIs, services, infrastructure behavior, deployment mechanisms, data stores, identity systems, or operational tooling when they materially affect portability, resilience, cost, or strategic flexibility. The existence of coupling should not automatically be treated as a defect; its purpose and consequences should be explicit.","context":"Platform-specific capabilities can provide substantial value while creating architectural dependency.","objective":"Make material platform coupling visible and intentional.","requirements":["Material platform dependencies should be identifiable","The value obtained from the dependency should be understood","Exit or mitigation implications should be considered when material"],"constraints":["Replacing platform capabilities may be impractical","Platform dependencies can provide differentiated functionality"],"prerequisites":["Platform inventory","Architecture context","Strategic requirements"],"dependencies":["Technology lifecycle governance","Portability governance","Vendor architecture governance"],"alternatives":["Avoid platform-specific features","Use them selectively","Adopt explicit platform commitment"],"evidence":"Architectural coupling should be evaluated by its consequences and strategic context rather than by a universal preference for abstraction.","tradeoffs":{"avoid":"Improves portability but may sacrifice capability","selective":"Balances value and dependency","commit":"Can maximize platform value but increases lock-in exposure"},"decision":"Govern material platform coupling through explicit dependency rationale and consequence analysis.","rationale":"Intentional coupling can be valid architecture when its benefits and risks are understood.","consequences":["Platform dependencies become auditable","Architecture decisions can distinguish strategic commitment from accidental coupling","Future migrations become easier to assess"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain platform dependency records","Identify critical proprietary capabilities","Document mitigation or exit considerations where justified"],"verification_method":"Review critical architecture dependencies and determine whether platform-specific coupling has documented rationale and consequences.","outcome_learning":"Unexpected migration cost or platform limitations should feed future coupling decisions.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B85-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"REFINES","record_id":"D55-B85-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B85-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Architecture Governance for Portability","subtopic":"Portability Verification","concept":"Governance of portability verification","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"How should architecture governance verify a claimed portability property?","answer":"Portability claims should be verified through representative deployment, migration, build, data-transfer, or operational scenarios appropriate to the claim. Documentation of abstraction alone is insufficient evidence when actual platform behavior affects portability.","context":"A system may appear portable at source-code level while depending on platform-specific runtime, storage, identity, networking, or operational behavior.","objective":"Distinguish documented portability intent from demonstrated portability capability.","requirements":["Verification scenarios must reflect the portability claim","Relevant platform dependencies must be exercised","Observed limitations should be recorded"],"constraints":["Full cross-platform verification may be expensive","Not all target environments can be tested continuously"],"prerequisites":["Defined portability target","Representative environment","Portability acceptance criteria"],"dependencies":["Deployment architecture","Infrastructure automation","Data migration mechanisms"],"alternatives":["Full environment testing","Representative compatibility testing","Static dependency analysis"],"evidence":"Portability is partly behavioral and therefore requires evidence beyond architectural diagrams or source-code inspection.","tradeoffs":{"full_testing":"Strong evidence but high cost","representative":"Lower cost but leaves some uncertainty","static_analysis":"Efficient but cannot establish runtime equivalence"},"decision":"Require scenario-based verification proportional to the portability claim and its consequences.","rationale":"A portability claim should be supported by evidence that exercises relevant environmental assumptions.","consequences":["Portability becomes testable","Unsupported assumptions become visible","Verification cost becomes part of portability planning"],"implementation_implications":["Define portability acceptance scenarios","Automate repeatable compatibility checks where practical","Record unverified target environments explicitly"],"verification_method":"Execute or otherwise validate representative portability scenarios and distinguish verified environments from untested assumptions.","outcome_learning":"Failed portability exercises should update dependency inventories and architecture decisions.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B85-R01","D55-B85-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"VERIFIES","record_id":"D55-B85-R01"},{"type":"INFORMS","record_id":"D55-B85-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B85-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Architecture Governance for Portability","subtopic":"Abstraction Governance","concept":"Governance of abstraction introduced for portability","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should governance determine whether a portability abstraction is justified?","answer":"Governance should evaluate the actual portability requirement, affected interfaces, implementation complexity, performance implications, operational burden, and likelihood that the abstraction will be used. An abstraction should solve a material architectural problem rather than exist solely to preserve hypothetical future options.","context":"Portability abstractions can become unnecessary indirection when no meaningful portability requirement exists.","objective":"Prevent speculative abstraction while preserving justified portability.","requirements":["The portability problem must be explicit","Abstraction cost should be understood","The abstraction should preserve required behavior"],"constraints":["Future requirements may be uncertain","Abstraction value can be difficult to quantify early"],"prerequisites":["Portability objective","Alternative architecture analysis","Relevant performance requirements"],"dependencies":["Platform coupling analysis","Architecture decision process"],"alternatives":["Direct platform integration","Thin abstraction","Portable architecture layer"],"evidence":"Abstraction has architectural cost and should therefore be justified by material requirements or risk reduction.","tradeoffs":{"direct":"Lower abstraction overhead but stronger coupling","thin":"Moderate flexibility with limited indirection","full_layer":"Greater portability potential but higher complexity"},"decision":"Approve portability abstractions when their expected architectural value justifies their complexity and behavioral constraints.","rationale":"Abstraction is a design mechanism, not an objective in itself.","consequences":["Architecture decisions become more explicit about abstraction cost","Speculative portability can be challenged","Required portability remains supported"],"implementation_implications":["Document portability drivers","Evaluate runtime and operational costs","Define boundaries of the abstraction","Verify required behavior across supported environments"],"verification_method":"Review abstraction decisions against documented portability requirements, costs, and verification evidence.","outcome_learning":"Unused or costly abstractions should be considered during architecture retrospectives.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B85-R01","D55-B85-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"CONSTRAINS","record_id":"D55-B85-R01"},{"type":"INFORMS","record_id":"D55-B85-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B85-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B85","topic":"Architecture Governance for Portability","subtopic":"Exit Strategy Governance","concept":"Governance of material platform exit considerations","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"When should architecture governance require consideration of a platform exit strategy?","answer":"Exit considerations should be required when platform dependency materially affects strategic flexibility, continuity, regulatory requirements, concentration risk, or migration feasibility. The requirement is to understand material exit constraints, not necessarily to maintain a complete alternative implementation.","context":"A strong platform dependency can make future migration difficult even when immediate platform use is justified.","objective":"Make material long-term dependency consequences visible.","requirements":["Material dependency risks should be identified","Critical exit constraints should be documented","Migration-relevant data and interface assumptions should be understood"],"constraints":["Maintaining an alternate implementation can be prohibitively expensive","Some dependencies have no practical short-term substitute"],"prerequisites":["Platform dependency assessment","Strategic requirements","Criticality classification"],"dependencies":["Vendor governance","Portability analysis","Data architecture"],"alternatives":["No exit planning","Documented exit constraints","Prepared migration capability"],"evidence":"Exit feasibility depends on architecture, data, contracts, operational dependencies, and platform-specific behavior.","tradeoffs":{"none":"Lowest immediate effort but highest uncertainty","documented":"Moderate effort and improved awareness","prepared":"Strongest readiness but significant cost"},"decision":"Require documented exit considerations where platform dependency creates material strategic or continuity risk.","rationale":"Understanding exit constraints supports informed architectural governance without requiring unnecessary duplicate systems.","consequences":["Critical dependencies become more transparent","Architecture records capture long-term constraints","Migration planning can be prioritized by actual risk"],"implementation_implications":["Record critical platform dependencies","Identify migration blockers","Preserve relevant portability or export capabilities where justified"],"verification_method":"Review material platform dependencies and confirm that identified exit constraints are documented and periodically reassessed.","outcome_learning":"Actual migration exercises, vendor changes, or dependency incidents should update exit assessments.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B85-R02","D55-B85-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B85-R02"},{"type":"INFORMS","record_id":"D55-B85-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B86-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Architecture Governance for Cost","subtopic":"Architecture Cost Governance","concept":"Governance of architectural cost drivers","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance incorporate material cost implications?","answer":"Governance should identify architecture decisions that materially influence recurring infrastructure, operational, licensing, data-transfer, storage, support, or engineering costs. Cost should be evaluated alongside required quality attributes and business constraints rather than treated as an isolated financial metric.","context":"Architectural choices can create persistent cost structures that are difficult to change after adoption.","objective":"Make material architectural cost consequences visible before commitment.","requirements":["Material cost drivers should be identified","Cost assumptions should be traceable to architectural choices","Uncertainty should be documented"],"constraints":["Early cost estimates are uncertain","Usage and scale can change over time"],"prerequisites":["Architecture alternatives","Workload assumptions","Relevant cost model"],"dependencies":["Capacity planning","Technology selection","Portfolio planning"],"alternatives":["Cost-blind architecture review","Detailed cost modeling for every decision","Risk-proportional cost analysis"],"evidence":"Architecture influences resource requirements and therefore can create recurring economic consequences.","tradeoffs":{"cost_blind":"Fast but can hide material consequences","detailed_everywhere":"High assurance but expensive","risk_proportional":"Balances effort and impact"},"decision":"Apply proportional cost analysis to architecture decisions with material economic consequences.","rationale":"Architecture governance should expose persistent cost implications before they become difficult to reverse.","consequences":["Cost assumptions become part of architecture decisions","Major infrastructure choices receive stronger analysis","Cost uncertainty becomes explicit"],"implementation_implications":["Record major cost drivers","Document workload assumptions","Define sensitivity factors for uncertain estimates"],"verification_method":"Review selected architecture decisions and trace material cost assumptions to the chosen design.","outcome_learning":"Actual cost deviations should update assumptions, estimation methods, and future architecture decisions.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B86-R02","D55-B86-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B86-R02"},{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B86-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B86-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Architecture Governance for Cost","subtopic":"Cost Uncertainty","concept":"Governance of architectural cost uncertainty","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should architecture governance handle uncertain cost estimates?","answer":"Governance should distinguish known costs, assumptions, ranges, and unknowns rather than presenting uncertain estimates as precise facts. Material uncertainty should be recorded with the variables most likely to change the economic outcome and, where practical, validated through experiments or measured usage.","context":"Architecture decisions are often made before reliable workload and operational data exists.","objective":"Prevent false precision in architecture cost reasoning.","requirements":["Cost assumptions should be explicit","Uncertainty should be represented","High-impact unknowns should have validation strategies where practical"],"constraints":["Early-stage workloads may be poorly characterized","Some future costs are inherently uncertain"],"prerequisites":["Architecture alternatives","Workload assumptions","Relevant cost drivers"],"dependencies":["Capacity planning","Performance testing","Operational telemetry"],"alternatives":["Single-point estimate","Range-based estimate","Scenario analysis"],"evidence":"Cost estimates depend on workload, scale, operational behavior, and pricing assumptions that may change.","tradeoffs":{"single_point":"Simple but hides uncertainty","range":"More transparent but less convenient","scenario":"Supports decision analysis but requires more assumptions"},"decision":"Use explicit assumptions and uncertainty ranges or scenarios for material architecture cost decisions.","rationale":"Decision quality improves when uncertainty is represented rather than hidden behind false precision.","consequences":["Architecture records become more transparent","Validation priorities become clearer","Cost surprises can be analyzed against prior assumptions"],"implementation_implications":["Record cost assumptions","Define sensitivity variables","Use measurements or experiments where uncertainty materially affects the decision"],"verification_method":"Compare material cost decisions against recorded assumptions and subsequent evidence without treating retrospective variance as proof of poor architecture by itself.","outcome_learning":"Update estimation models based on recurring systematic estimation errors.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B86-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"REFINES","record_id":"D55-B86-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B86-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Architecture Governance for Cost","subtopic":"Cost-Quality Tradeoffs","concept":"Governance of cost versus quality attributes","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should architecture governance reason about cost when it conflicts with reliability, security, performance, or other quality attributes?","answer":"Governance should make the tradeoff explicit rather than allowing cost reduction to implicitly override another requirement. The decision should identify the affected quality attributes, minimum acceptable constraints, residual risks, and rationale for the selected balance.","context":"Architecture decisions often involve competing economic and quality objectives.","objective":"Ensure cost optimization does not silently violate critical architectural requirements.","requirements":["Critical quality constraints must be explicit","Cost reduction mechanisms should identify their effects","Residual risks should be accepted by appropriate authority"],"constraints":["Quality requirements may conflict","Economic constraints may be binding"],"prerequisites":["Quality-attribute requirements","Cost drivers","Decision authority"],"dependencies":["Architecture decisions","Risk governance","Portfolio constraints"],"alternatives":["Optimize cost within quality constraints","Relax non-critical quality requirements","Change architecture to reduce cost without violating constraints"],"evidence":"Cost is one decision dimension among multiple architectural objectives and should be evaluated against explicit constraints.","tradeoffs":{"quality_preserved":"May increase cost","constraint_relaxed":"Can reduce cost but increases accepted risk","architecture_change":"May improve both but requires design effort"},"decision":"Treat critical quality requirements as explicit constraints and document any intentional tradeoff against them.","rationale":"Implicit quality degradation creates ungoverned architectural risk.","consequences":["Cost decisions become traceable","Risk acceptance becomes explicit","Architecture alternatives receive broader analysis"],"implementation_implications":["Record minimum quality thresholds","Map cost changes to quality effects","Require appropriate approval for material risk acceptance"],"verification_method":"Review material cost decisions for explicit quality constraints and documented residual risks.","outcome_learning":"Observed quality degradation should feed future cost optimization criteria and architecture guardrails.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B86-R01","D55-B86-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"CONSTRAINS","record_id":"D55-B86-R01"},{"type":"INFORMS","record_id":"D55-B86-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B86-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Architecture Governance for Cost","subtopic":"Cost Optimization Boundaries","concept":"Governance of architectural cost optimization boundaries","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What prevents architecture cost optimization from becoming uncontrolled complexity reduction?","answer":"Governance should require cost optimization proposals to state the targeted cost driver, expected savings mechanism, affected quality attributes, operational consequences, implementation complexity, and verification approach. This prevents generic cost reduction goals from obscuring architectural side effects.","context":"Cost optimization can introduce hidden operational work, performance degradation, resilience reduction, or engineering complexity.","objective":"Ensure cost optimization remains evidence-driven and architecture-aware.","requirements":["Target cost driver must be explicit","Expected mechanism should be explainable","Quality and operational effects should be assessed","Results should be measurable where practical"],"constraints":["Savings estimates may be uncertain","Some indirect costs are difficult to quantify"],"prerequisites":["Current cost baseline","Architecture context","Quality requirements"],"dependencies":["FinOps or cost analysis","Architecture governance","Operational metrics"],"alternatives":["Broad cost mandate","Targeted optimization proposals","Continuous cost optimization review"],"evidence":"A cost reduction is meaningful only when the cost mechanism and resulting system behavior are understood.","tradeoffs":{"broad":"Fast but weakly controlled","targeted":"More precise but requires analysis","continuous":"Supports learning but requires sustained governance"},"decision":"Require targeted, measurable, and quality-aware reasoning for material architecture cost optimizations.","rationale":"Reducing one cost category can transfer cost or risk elsewhere.","consequences":["Optimization proposals become more structured","Indirect consequences become visible","Post-change measurement becomes more valuable"],"implementation_implications":["Define optimization evidence templates","Track targeted cost drivers","Measure relevant quality effects after material changes"],"verification_method":"Compare claimed optimization objectives with observed cost and quality evidence after implementation where data is available.","outcome_learning":"Unexpected cost transfers or quality impacts should update optimization rules.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B86-R03","D55-B86-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B86-R03"},{"type":"INFORMS","record_id":"D55-B86-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B86-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B86","topic":"Architecture Governance for Cost","subtopic":"Cost Outcome Learning","concept":"Governance learning from architectural cost outcomes","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance learn from actual cost outcomes?","answer":"Governance should compare material realized cost behavior with the assumptions recorded during architecture decisions, identify systematic variance, and update estimation practices or decision criteria when recurring patterns appear. A single variance should be treated as evidence to investigate rather than automatic proof that the original decision was incorrect.","context":"Architectural cost outcomes depend on changing workloads, usage patterns, operational practices, and external pricing conditions.","objective":"Create a feedback loop from observed economics to future architecture decisions.","requirements":["Original assumptions must be recoverable","Actual measurements should be attributable where possible","Recurring deviations should influence future governance"],"constraints":["Historical measurements may be incomplete","External factors can change independently"],"prerequisites":["Decision records","Cost measurements","Relevant workload information"],"dependencies":["Architecture repository","Cost telemetry","Portfolio planning"],"alternatives":["No retrospective analysis","Manual retrospective","Automated variance analysis"],"evidence":"Decision quality improves when assumptions can be compared with subsequent evidence while preserving original context.","tradeoffs":{"none":"Lowest effort but no learning","manual":"Higher insight but recurring effort","automated":"Scalable but depends on reliable data linkage"},"decision":"Establish retrospective cost learning for material architecture decisions where measurement data is available.","rationale":"Architecture governance should improve its assumptions rather than repeatedly rediscovering the same cost behavior.","consequences":["Decision records gain historical value","Estimation methods can evolve","Cost governance becomes learning-oriented"],"implementation_implications":["Link cost measurements to architecture decisions","Record material variance explanations","Feed recurring findings into future decision criteria"],"verification_method":"Audit whether selected material decisions have recoverable cost assumptions and corresponding outcome evidence.","outcome_learning":"Recurring variance patterns should become explicit governance knowledge.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B86-R01","D55-B86-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"LEARNS_FROM","record_id":"D55-B86-R01"},{"type":"USES_UNCERTAINTY_MODEL","record_id":"D55-B86-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B87-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Architecture Governance for Maintainability","subtopic":"Maintainability Governance","concept":"Governance of architectural maintainability","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"When should maintainability be treated as an explicit architecture governance concern?","answer":"Maintainability should be governed when architectural structure materially affects the ability to understand, modify, test, deploy, or operate a system over its expected lifecycle. Governance should focus on structural factors such as coupling, ownership boundaries, dependency complexity, and change propagation rather than subjective code-quality judgments alone.","context":"Architectural choices can create long-term modification costs even when current functionality is correct.","objective":"Prevent architectural structures that make expected future change disproportionately difficult.","requirements":["Expected lifecycle changes should be considered","Material coupling should be visible","Ownership and change boundaries should be understandable"],"constraints":["Future changes are uncertain","Maintainability is partly context-dependent"],"prerequisites":["Expected lifecycle","Change scenarios","Architecture dependency model"],"dependencies":["Technical debt governance","Architecture decisions","Team ownership"],"alternatives":["Optimize for immediate delivery","Optimize for long-term maintainability","Risk-proportional maintainability analysis"],"evidence":"Maintainability is influenced by architecture-level coupling and dependency structure as well as implementation quality.","tradeoffs":{"immediate":"Lower initial effort but may increase future change cost","long_term":"Higher initial design effort but can reduce future friction","risk_proportional":"Focuses effort where lifecycle impact is material"},"decision":"Include maintainability analysis in architecture governance when structural decisions materially affect future change or operational ownership.","rationale":"Architectural maintainability failures can accumulate into significant technical debt.","consequences":["Lifecycle change scenarios become part of architecture review","Coupling becomes a governance concern","Future maintenance implications become explicit"],"implementation_implications":["Identify expected change boundaries","Assess dependency propagation","Document maintainability tradeoffs"],"verification_method":"Evaluate representative change scenarios against the proposed architecture and inspect whether change impact remains understandable and bounded.","outcome_learning":"Recurring maintenance friction should feed architecture governance and technical debt decisions.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B87-R02","D55-B87-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B87-R02"},{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B87-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B87-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Architecture Governance for Maintainability","subtopic":"Change Propagation","concept":"Governance of architectural change propagation","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"How can governance detect architectures where small changes are likely to propagate broadly?","answer":"Governance should identify dependency structures, shared abstractions, shared data, cross-boundary contracts, and centralized components through which changes can propagate. The objective is not to eliminate all shared dependencies but to make material propagation risk visible before committing to architecture.","context":"Highly connected architecture can make localized changes affect many components or teams.","objective":"Detect excessive or poorly understood change propagation risk.","requirements":["Material dependency concentration should be visible","Critical shared components should be identified","Change impact should be assessable"],"constraints":["Some shared infrastructure is intentional","Complete dependency analysis may be difficult"],"prerequisites":["Dependency graph","Component ownership","Change scenarios"],"dependencies":["Architecture repository","Interface inventory","Service topology"],"alternatives":["Minimize shared components","Govern shared components explicitly","Accept centralized coupling for selected capabilities"],"evidence":"Change propagation follows architectural dependency structure and can therefore be evaluated as a governance property.","tradeoffs":{"decentralize":"Reduces propagation but may increase duplication","centralize":"Improves consistency but increases blast radius","explicit_governance":"Allows justified sharing while controlling risk"},"decision":"Govern material change propagation through dependency visibility, ownership, and impact analysis.","rationale":"Unrecognized propagation paths create unpredictable change costs and coordination requirements.","consequences":["Dependency graphs become governance evidence","Shared components receive stronger stewardship","Change impact analysis becomes more systematic"],"implementation_implications":["Track important dependency edges","Identify high-centrality architectural components where relevant","Use representative change scenarios"],"verification_method":"Trace representative changes through dependency structures and compare predicted impact with observed architecture boundaries.","outcome_learning":"Unexpected propagation should update dependency models and architecture boundaries.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B87-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B87-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B87-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Architecture Governance for Maintainability","subtopic":"Boundary Stability","concept":"Governance of architectural boundary stability","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why should governance monitor repeated changes to supposedly stable architecture boundaries?","answer":"Repeated changes to an architectural boundary can indicate that the boundary does not align with actual responsibilities, change patterns, or dependency relationships. Governance should treat repeated boundary churn as evidence for reassessment rather than automatically preserving the existing boundary.","context":"Architecture boundaries can become unstable when they separate responsibilities that frequently change together or when ownership is poorly aligned.","objective":"Use change history as evidence for architecture boundary quality.","requirements":["Boundary changes should be traceable","Repeated change patterns should be reviewable","Boundary reassessment should be triggered when instability becomes material"],"constraints":["Some domains naturally change frequently","Change frequency alone does not prove a boundary is wrong"],"prerequisites":["Architecture change history","Ownership information","Boundary definitions"],"dependencies":["Architecture repository","Decision records","Change management"],"alternatives":["Preserve boundary","Split boundary","Merge boundary","Redefine ownership or contracts"],"evidence":"Change history provides empirical evidence about whether architectural boundaries align with actual evolution patterns.","tradeoffs":{"preserve":"Avoids disruption but may retain structural mismatch","split":"Can isolate change but may increase coordination","merge":"Can reduce unnecessary boundaries but increases coupling"},"decision":"Use repeated material boundary churn as a governance signal for architectural reassessment, not as an automatic defect classification.","rationale":"Architecture should reflect actual responsibility and change patterns where practical.","consequences":["Historical changes become governance evidence","Boundary reviews become evidence-driven","Architecture evolution becomes more adaptive"],"implementation_implications":["Track boundary changes","Define review thresholds based on context","Compare change patterns with ownership and dependency structures"],"verification_method":"Review historical boundary changes and determine whether recurring patterns justify architectural reassessment.","outcome_learning":"Boundary reassessments should capture the evidence that caused the change and its subsequent effects.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B87-R01","D55-B87-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"INFORMS","record_id":"D55-B87-R01"},{"type":"USES_HISTORY_FROM","record_id":"D55-B87-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B87-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Architecture Governance for Maintainability","subtopic":"Architectural Complexity","concept":"Governance of architectural complexity growth","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance respond to increasing structural complexity?","answer":"Governance should identify material complexity introduced through additional components, boundaries, protocols, deployment modes, dependencies, coordination paths, or operational states. Complexity should be evaluated relative to the value it provides and the system's ability to remain understandable and controllable.","context":"Architectural complexity can grow incrementally without any single change appearing significant.","objective":"Prevent uncontrolled structural complexity accumulation.","requirements":["Material complexity drivers should be identifiable","Complexity should be linked to architectural value or requirements","Critical complexity risks should have mitigation or acceptance rationale"],"constraints":["Complexity is partly contextual","Some complexity is required by scale or quality attributes"],"prerequisites":["Architecture model","System criticality","Change history"],"dependencies":["Architecture decisions","Technical debt governance","Operational complexity"],"alternatives":["Simplify","Retain complexity with explicit justification","Replace multiple mechanisms with a simpler architectural mechanism"],"evidence":"Complexity should be governed through its effects on comprehension, changeability, reliability, security, and operations rather than by component count alone.","tradeoffs":{"simplify":"Can reduce cognitive and operational burden but may remove useful capabilities","retain":"Preserves capability but requires stronger understanding and governance","consolidate":"Can simplify architecture but may increase centralization"},"decision":"Govern material complexity growth by linking structural additions to explicit value, requirements, or risk reduction.","rationale":"Complexity without sufficient architectural value becomes a long-term governance burden.","consequences":["Architecture changes require stronger structural reasoning","Complexity debt becomes visible","Simplification opportunities can be identified"],"implementation_implications":["Track material architectural complexity indicators","Require rationale for significant structural additions","Review accumulated complexity periodically"],"verification_method":"Inspect major architecture changes for explicit complexity drivers and corresponding value or requirement justification.","outcome_learning":"Recurring operational or maintenance difficulties should feed complexity governance.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B87-R02","D55-B87-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B87-R02"},{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B87-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B87-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B87","topic":"Architecture Governance for Maintainability","subtopic":"Maintenance Scenario Verification","concept":"Scenario-based maintainability verification","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"How can maintainability claims be verified without relying only on subjective review?","answer":"Maintainability can be examined through representative change scenarios that measure or assess affected components, dependencies, testing effort, deployment implications, and ownership coordination. The scenarios should reflect expected lifecycle changes rather than arbitrary modifications.","context":"Maintainability is difficult to validate through static architecture inspection alone.","objective":"Provide evidence for maintainability-related architecture decisions.","requirements":["Scenarios should represent meaningful future changes","Impact boundaries should be observable","Assessment criteria should be explicit"],"constraints":["Future changes cannot be known exactly","Scenario selection introduces assumptions"],"prerequisites":["Expected change patterns","Architecture model","Ownership information"],"dependencies":["Testing strategy","Change management","Architecture repository"],"alternatives":["Static review","Scenario analysis","Historical change analysis"],"evidence":"Representative change scenarios provide concrete evidence about how architectural structure affects modification effort and propagation.","tradeoffs":{"static":"Low cost but abstract","scenario":"Concrete but assumption-dependent","historical":"Empirical but dependent on sufficient history"},"decision":"Use representative lifecycle change scenarios as one evidence source for maintainability-sensitive architecture decisions.","rationale":"Concrete change behavior provides stronger evidence than generic maintainability claims.","consequences":["Architecture reviews become more scenario-driven","Expected lifecycle assumptions become explicit","Verification evidence can be reused in future decisions"],"implementation_implications":["Define representative change scenarios","Record observed impact boundaries","Compare scenarios across architecture alternatives"],"verification_method":"Evaluate representative changes against architectural boundaries and compare predicted versus observed impact where execution is available.","outcome_learning":"Observed maintenance behavior should refine future scenario libraries and architecture governance criteria.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B87-R01","D55-B87-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"VERIFIES","record_id":"D55-B87-R01"},{"type":"VERIFIES","record_id":"D55-B87-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B88-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Architecture Governance for Experimentation","subtopic":"Architecture Experiment Governance","concept":"Governance of architectural experiments","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance distinguish experiments from committed architecture?","answer":"Experiments should have explicit hypotheses, scope, time limits, success criteria, ownership, and exit conditions. Governance should prevent experimental components or decisions from silently becoming permanent architecture without an explicit reassessment.","context":"Proofs of concept and technical spikes can gradually become production dependencies without completing architectural evaluation.","objective":"Control the transition from experimental knowledge to committed architecture.","requirements":["Experiment purpose must be explicit","Success and failure criteria should exist","Expiration or reassessment conditions should be defined","Production adoption requires separate decision evidence"],"constraints":["Experiments may evolve rapidly","Some experiments provide useful knowledge even when technically unsuccessful"],"prerequisites":["Experiment hypothesis","Architecture context","Decision authority"],"dependencies":["Architecture decision records","Prototype environments","Lifecycle governance"],"alternatives":["Uncontrolled experimentation","Time-boxed experiments","Formal architecture experiments"],"evidence":"An experiment provides evidence about a hypothesis; it does not automatically constitute a production architecture decision.","tradeoffs":{"uncontrolled":"Fast but creates hidden commitments","time_boxed":"Controls commitment but may limit exploration","formal":"Strong traceability but increases process overhead"},"decision":"Govern experiments with explicit hypotheses, evidence criteria, ownership, and transition or retirement conditions.","rationale":"Separating experimental evidence from committed architecture preserves architectural clarity.","consequences":["Experiments become traceable","Successful experiments require explicit adoption decisions","Failed experiments can still contribute knowledge"],"implementation_implications":["Record experiment metadata","Define success and exit criteria","Link findings to architecture decisions","Review expired experiments"],"verification_method":"Audit experimental components for ownership, status, expiration, evidence, and explicit production adoption decisions.","outcome_learning":"Experiment results should feed architecture knowledge regardless of whether adoption occurs.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B88-R02","D55-B88-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B88-R02"},{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B88-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B88-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Architecture Governance for Experimentation","subtopic":"Experiment Evidence","concept":"Governance of evidence produced by architecture experiments","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What makes an architecture experiment result useful as governance evidence?","answer":"A useful experiment result should identify the original hypothesis, tested conditions, observed behavior, limitations, deviations, and applicability boundaries. Results should distinguish observed evidence from interpretation and should not be generalized beyond the tested context without justification.","context":"Experiments can produce misleading conclusions when environmental assumptions and limitations are omitted.","objective":"Preserve experiment results as reliable architectural evidence.","requirements":["Hypothesis and conditions should be recorded","Observed results must be distinguished from interpretation","Limitations must be explicit","Applicability must be bounded"],"constraints":["Experimental environments may differ from production","Experiments may have incomplete coverage"],"prerequisites":["Experiment definition","Test conditions","Observation method"],"dependencies":["Architecture decision records","Testing evidence","Knowledge repository"],"alternatives":["Narrative summary","Structured experiment record","Automated experiment evidence capture"],"evidence":"Experimental evidence is meaningful only within the conditions under which it was produced.","tradeoffs":{"narrative":"Flexible but less structured","structured":"More reusable and comparable","automated":"Higher consistency but greater tooling requirements"},"decision":"Govern architecture experiments as bounded evidence with explicit conditions, limitations, and applicability.","rationale":"Evidence without context can be incorrectly reused as a general architectural fact.","consequences":["Experiment records become reusable knowledge","Decision quality improves through bounded evidence","Historical evidence remains interpretable"],"implementation_implications":["Capture conditions and limitations","Link experiments to decisions","Mark unverified generalizations as hypotheses"],"verification_method":"Inspect experiment records for conditions, observed evidence, limitations, and applicability boundaries.","outcome_learning":"Repeated experiment limitations should influence future experimental design and evidence standards.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B88-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D55-B88-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B88-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Architecture Governance for Experimentation","subtopic":"Experiment-to-Production Transition","concept":"Governance of experimental adoption","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"What should be required before an architectural experiment becomes a production commitment?","answer":"Production adoption should require explicit evaluation of experiment evidence against production requirements, operational constraints, security implications, performance expectations, lifecycle ownership, and unresolved risks. The transition should be a new architectural decision rather than an implicit continuation of the experiment.","context":"Successful prototypes can conceal production requirements that were not relevant to the original experiment.","objective":"Prevent experimental evidence from being mistaken for production readiness.","requirements":["Production requirements must be reassessed","Known limitations must be reviewed","Ownership must be established","Operational and security implications must be considered"],"constraints":["Experiment environments may not match production","Some production properties require later-stage validation"],"prerequisites":["Experiment results","Production requirements","Architecture decision authority"],"dependencies":["Deployment governance","Security review","Operational readiness"],"alternatives":["Direct promotion","Staged adoption","Rebuild based on experiment findings"],"evidence":"Prototype success demonstrates selected properties under tested conditions but does not automatically establish production suitability.","tradeoffs":{"direct":"Fast but higher assumption risk","staged":"Controls uncertainty but adds transition effort","rebuild":"Can improve production fit but discards some implementation continuity"},"decision":"Require explicit production-readiness reasoning before adopting an experimental architecture.","rationale":"Production architecture carries requirements and operational responsibilities that experiments may not address.","consequences":["Prototype success is not treated as automatic approval","Transition evidence becomes traceable","Production risks become visible"],"implementation_implications":["Create adoption criteria","Record unresolved experimental limitations","Assign production ownership","Perform required production validation"],"verification_method":"Review adopted experiments for explicit production requirements, evidence, ownership, and unresolved risk treatment.","outcome_learning":"Production outcomes should feed back into the experiment evidence model and future adoption criteria.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B88-R01","D55-B88-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B88-R01"},{"type":"USES_EVIDENCE_FROM","record_id":"D55-B88-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B88-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Architecture Governance for Experimentation","subtopic":"Experiment Retirement","concept":"Governance of experimental retirement","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance retire obsolete experiments?","answer":"Experiments should have explicit status and retirement conditions. When an experiment is no longer useful, it should be retired, preserved as historical evidence where valuable, or converted into a formal decision if it has become a production dependency.","context":"Stale experiments can consume resources, create security exposure, and confuse the architectural source of truth.","objective":"Prevent experimental artifacts from becoming unmanaged architectural liabilities.","requirements":["Experimental status should be visible","Retirement criteria should exist","Historical evidence should be preserved when useful","Production dependencies require formal reassessment"],"constraints":["Some experiments may be intentionally long-lived","Historical artifacts can retain knowledge value"],"prerequisites":["Experiment inventory","Ownership","Lifecycle policy"],"dependencies":["Repository governance","Security controls","Architecture repository"],"alternatives":["Automatic deletion","Manual retirement","Status-based lifecycle management"],"evidence":"Experimental artifacts have lifecycle costs and should not remain indefinitely without an explicit reason.","tradeoffs":{"automatic":"Reduces stale artifacts but risks losing useful evidence","manual":"Flexible but prone to neglect","status_based":"Balances retention and lifecycle control"},"decision":"Apply explicit lifecycle states and retirement criteria to architecture experiments.","rationale":"Experiment governance requires both preservation of useful evidence and removal of obsolete dependencies.","consequences":["Stale experiments become detectable","Knowledge preservation becomes intentional","Security and operational exposure can be reduced"],"implementation_implications":["Maintain experiment status","Define review or expiration triggers","Archive useful evidence","Remove obsolete dependencies"],"verification_method":"Audit the experiment inventory for stale, ownerless, expired, or implicitly productionized artifacts.","outcome_learning":"Recurring experiment lifecycle failures should update governance automation and ownership controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B88-R01","D55-B88-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"LIFECYCLE_CONTROL","record_id":"D55-B88-R01"},{"type":"LIFECYCLE_CONTROL","record_id":"D55-B88-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B88-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B88","topic":"Architecture Governance for Experimentation","subtopic":"Experiment Portfolio Governance","concept":"Governance of concurrent architecture experiments","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should governance manage multiple architecture experiments that investigate related decisions?","answer":"Related experiments should be coordinated through explicit hypotheses, shared assumptions, dependencies, and decision outcomes to avoid duplicating effort or producing contradictory evidence. Governance should preserve independence where necessary but make interactions visible.","context":"Multiple teams may independently investigate similar architectural questions under different assumptions.","objective":"Increase the information value of experimentation while preventing duplicated or conflicting architectural evidence.","requirements":["Related experiments should be discoverable","Shared assumptions should be identifiable","Conflicting findings should be reconciled or bounded"],"constraints":["Experiments may have different scopes","Independent experimentation can be useful"],"prerequisites":["Experiment registry","Architecture question taxonomy","Evidence records"],"dependencies":["Knowledge repository","Architecture decision records","Team governance"],"alternatives":["Independent experiments","Central coordination","Federated registry with shared evidence"],"evidence":"Structured experiment relationships allow evidence to be compared without assuming identical experimental conditions.","tradeoffs":{"independent":"Encourages exploration but can duplicate work","central":"Reduces duplication but may constrain exploration","federated":"Balances autonomy and knowledge sharing"},"decision":"Use a discoverable experiment registry and explicit evidence relationships for materially related architecture experiments.","rationale":"Experiment coordination should improve knowledge reuse without eliminating useful independent investigation.","consequences":["Related evidence becomes easier to compare","Duplicate experiments can be identified","Contradictions become more visible"],"implementation_implications":["Record experiment hypotheses and assumptions","Link related experiments","Capture contradictory findings explicitly","Associate experiments with architecture questions"],"verification_method":"Review experiment records for discoverability, relationships, assumptions, and contradiction handling.","outcome_learning":"Repeated duplicated or contradictory experiments should inform improvements to architecture knowledge discovery.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B88-R01","D55-B88-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B88-R01"},{"type":"USES","record_id":"D55-B88-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B89-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Architecture Governance for Knowledge Quality","subtopic":"Architecture Knowledge Quality","concept":"Governance of architecture knowledge quality","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance ensure that stored architecture knowledge remains reliable?","answer":"Architecture knowledge should have identifiable provenance, ownership, status, applicability, recency, and relationship to decisions or evidence where relevant. Governance should distinguish verified facts, decisions, assumptions, interpretations, and obsolete information.","context":"Architecture repositories can become misleading when historical decisions and current facts are mixed without status or provenance.","objective":"Maintain a trustworthy architecture knowledge base.","requirements":["Knowledge status should be explicit","Material information should have provenance","Obsolete or superseded knowledge should remain distinguishable","Ownership should be identifiable"],"constraints":["Not all architecture knowledge can be continuously verified","Historical decisions remain useful even after supersession"],"prerequisites":["Architecture repository","Knowledge schema","Ownership model"],"dependencies":["Decision records","Evidence repositories","Architecture governance"],"alternatives":["Unstructured documentation","Structured knowledge records","Versioned architecture knowledge system"],"evidence":"Knowledge reliability depends on provenance, lifecycle status, and contextual applicability rather than documentation volume.","tradeoffs":{"unstructured":"Easy to create but difficult to trust and query","structured":"More reliable but requires metadata discipline","versioned":"Strong historical traceability but higher maintenance"},"decision":"Govern architecture knowledge using explicit provenance, status, ownership, applicability, and lifecycle metadata.","rationale":"Architecture governance requires trustworthy knowledge, not merely abundant documentation.","consequences":["Knowledge becomes more auditable","Stale information becomes detectable","Historical decisions remain distinguishable from current constraints"],"implementation_implications":["Define architecture knowledge metadata","Track supersession","Associate evidence with claims","Assign ownership for material knowledge"],"verification_method":"Audit sampled architecture knowledge for provenance, status, ownership, applicability, and supersession information.","outcome_learning":"Recurring stale or contradictory knowledge should update repository controls and governance processes.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B89-R02","D55-B89-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B89-R02"},{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B89-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B89-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Architecture Governance for Knowledge Quality","subtopic":"Knowledge Freshness","concept":"Governance of architecture knowledge staleness","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance detect stale architecture knowledge?","answer":"Governance should identify knowledge with lifecycle-sensitive assumptions and define appropriate review or invalidation triggers. Freshness should not be measured only by document age; an older record can remain valid while a recently modified system may make another record obsolete.","context":"Time alone is an imperfect indicator of architecture knowledge validity.","objective":"Detect materially stale architecture information using change-sensitive signals.","requirements":["Staleness triggers should be defined for material knowledge","Superseding changes should be detectable where practical","Review status should be visible"],"constraints":["Automated freshness detection may be incomplete","Some knowledge remains valid indefinitely"],"prerequisites":["Architecture change history","Knowledge metadata","Dependency relationships"],"dependencies":["Architecture repository","Change management","Decision records"],"alternatives":["Periodic review","Event-triggered review","Hybrid freshness governance"],"evidence":"Knowledge validity depends on the assumptions supporting it and changes that invalidate those assumptions.","tradeoffs":{"periodic":"Simple but may review valid knowledge unnecessarily","event_triggered":"More precise but requires dependency tracking","hybrid":"Balances coverage and effort"},"decision":"Use change-sensitive review triggers supplemented by periodic review for architecture knowledge where appropriate.","rationale":"Knowledge freshness should reflect validity of assumptions rather than document age alone.","consequences":["Material changes can trigger targeted knowledge review","Repository maintenance becomes more efficient","Stale information becomes more detectable"],"implementation_implications":["Link knowledge to affected architecture entities","Define invalidation events","Track review status","Preserve superseded history"],"verification_method":"Test whether representative architecture changes correctly identify potentially invalidated knowledge.","outcome_learning":"Missed stale records should refine dependency links and invalidation triggers.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B89-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B89-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B89-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Architecture Governance for Knowledge Quality","subtopic":"Contradiction Governance","concept":"Governance of contradictory architecture knowledge","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance handle contradictory architecture records?","answer":"Contradictory records should be preserved as distinct claims when their contexts differ, but the contradiction should be explicitly identified, contextualized, and resolved or bounded where possible. Governance should not silently overwrite historical evidence merely to produce apparent consistency.","context":"Architecture knowledge can conflict because systems evolve, decisions are reversed, teams use different assumptions, or evidence is incomplete.","objective":"Make architectural contradictions visible without destroying historical traceability.","requirements":["Contradictory claims should be identifiable","Context and version should be preserved","Resolution or unresolved status should be explicit"],"constraints":["Some contradictions are legitimate because contexts differ","Historical evidence may remain useful"],"prerequisites":["Versioned knowledge records","Provenance","Architecture context"],"dependencies":["Decision repository","Evidence records","Change history"],"alternatives":["Overwrite old knowledge","Merge contradictory claims","Preserve and explicitly reconcile"],"evidence":"Contradictions can carry useful information about changing assumptions and should therefore be represented rather than erased.","tradeoffs":{"overwrite":"Simple but destroys historical traceability","merge":"Reduces visible conflict but can obscure context","explicit_reconciliation":"More work but preserves reasoning and uncertainty"},"decision":"Represent contradictions explicitly and resolve or bound them through context, version, evidence, and decision status.","rationale":"A trustworthy architecture knowledge system must distinguish disagreement from historical evolution.","consequences":["Contradictions become queryable","Historical reasoning is preserved","Knowledge consumers can avoid treating unresolved claims as settled facts"],"implementation_implications":["Add contradiction status","Link conflicting records","Record resolution evidence","Preserve superseded decisions"],"verification_method":"Inject or identify known conflicting records and verify that governance preserves context and exposes their relationship.","outcome_learning":"Recurring contradiction patterns should inform taxonomy, ownership, and decision-record improvements.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B89-R01","D55-B89-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"CONSTRAINS","record_id":"D55-B89-R01"},{"type":"INFORMS","record_id":"D55-B89-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B89-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Architecture Governance for Knowledge Quality","subtopic":"Knowledge Supersession","concept":"Governance of superseded architecture knowledge","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"How should governance represent architecture knowledge that has been superseded?","answer":"Superseded knowledge should remain historically accessible when it explains prior decisions or system states, but its current applicability must be clearly marked. The replacement record should identify the supersession relationship and preserve enough context to understand why the change occurred.","context":"Deleting historical architecture knowledge can remove evidence needed to understand current constraints and previous decisions.","objective":"Preserve historical traceability without allowing obsolete information to masquerade as current guidance.","requirements":["Superseded status must be explicit","Replacement or successor should be identifiable where applicable","Historical context should remain retrievable"],"constraints":["Historical repositories can become large","Some obsolete details may contain sensitive information"],"prerequisites":["Versioned records","Decision history","Knowledge lifecycle model"],"dependencies":["Architecture repository","Access governance","Decision records"],"alternatives":["Delete obsolete records","Archive without linkage","Versioned supersession"],"evidence":"Historical architecture information can explain current structure and prevent repeated mistakes when its status is clear.","tradeoffs":{"delete":"Reduces repository size but destroys history","archive_unlinked":"Preserves data but weakens discoverability","versioned":"Preserves context and status but requires metadata discipline"},"decision":"Use explicit supersession and lifecycle status rather than destructive replacement for materially useful architecture knowledge.","rationale":"Historical traceability is part of architecture governance and decision accountability.","consequences":["Current and historical knowledge remain distinguishable","Decision evolution becomes traceable","Knowledge consumers require status-aware retrieval"],"implementation_implications":["Add supersession relationships","Mark current applicability","Retain relevant historical evidence","Ensure retrieval can prioritize current records"],"verification_method":"Inspect superseded records and confirm that current status and successor relationships are unambiguous.","outcome_learning":"Repeated confusion between historical and current knowledge should improve retrieval and governance metadata.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B89-R01","D55-B89-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B89-R01"},{"type":"RESOLVES_STATUS_FOR","record_id":"D55-B89-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B89-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B89","topic":"Architecture Governance for Knowledge Quality","subtopic":"Knowledge Retrieval Governance","concept":"Governance of architecture knowledge retrieval context","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Why should architecture governance care about how architecture knowledge is retrieved and presented?","answer":"A knowledge repository can be accurate yet produce unsafe decisions if retrieval surfaces stale, superseded, context-incompatible, or low-confidence records without appropriate qualification. Governance should therefore define metadata and retrieval rules that preserve status, provenance, scope, and uncertainty.","context":"Architecture reasoning increasingly depends on searchable knowledge repositories rather than static documentation alone.","objective":"Ensure architecture knowledge remains decision-safe when consumed through automated or human retrieval.","requirements":["Current status should be retrievable","Provenance and applicability should accompany material claims","Contradictions and uncertainty should remain visible"],"constraints":["Retrieval systems may have incomplete context","Ranking mechanisms can hide lower-ranked but relevant evidence"],"prerequisites":["Structured knowledge records","Metadata model","Retrieval mechanism"],"dependencies":["Architecture knowledge repository","Search or retrieval system","Governance metadata"],"alternatives":["Raw document retrieval","Metadata-aware retrieval","Evidence-weighted retrieval"],"evidence":"Knowledge usability depends not only on stored content but also on whether consumers receive the context required to interpret it correctly.","tradeoffs":{"raw":"Simple but context-poor","metadata_aware":"Safer but requires structured metadata","evidence_weighted":"Can improve reasoning but requires trustworthy evidence classification"},"decision":"Govern architecture knowledge consumption through status-, provenance-, scope-, and contradiction-aware retrieval requirements.","rationale":"Architecture governance must remain effective when knowledge is consumed by automated reasoning systems as well as people.","consequences":["Knowledge schemas become more important","Retrieval behavior becomes a governance concern","Automated architecture reasoning requires stronger metadata discipline"],"implementation_implications":["Expose status and provenance in retrieval results","Filter or qualify superseded records","Surface contradictions and uncertainty","Preserve applicability context"],"verification_method":"Test representative retrieval queries containing current, superseded, contradictory, and context-specific records and inspect whether the returned evidence remains correctly qualified.","outcome_learning":"Retrieval failures should update metadata, indexing, and governance rules rather than being treated only as search-quality problems.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B89-R01","D55-B89-R03","D55-B89-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B89-R01"},{"type":"USES","record_id":"D55-B89-R03"},{"type":"USES","record_id":"D55-B89-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B90-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Architecture Governance Learning","subtopic":"Governance Retrospectives","concept":"Architecture governance retrospectives","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance use retrospectives to improve its own effectiveness?","answer":"Governance retrospectives should examine whether review controls identified material risks, whether decisions received proportionate evidence, whether exceptions remained controlled, and whether outcomes revealed recurring governance gaps. The purpose is to improve governance mechanisms rather than merely evaluate individual teams.","context":"A governance process can itself accumulate ineffective controls, unnecessary friction, or blind spots.","objective":"Create a feedback loop that improves architecture governance based on observed evidence.","requirements":["Governance outcomes should be reviewable","Recurring control failures should be identifiable","Improvement actions should have owners and verification criteria"],"constraints":["Governance outcomes can be difficult to measure directly","Retrospectives can become subjective without evidence"],"prerequisites":["Governance records","Decision outcomes","Review findings"],"dependencies":["Architecture repository","Audit evidence","Outcome measurements"],"alternatives":["No retrospective","Periodic governance review","Continuous governance metrics and review"],"evidence":"Governance should itself be treated as a system whose controls can be evaluated and improved.","tradeoffs":{"none":"Low effort but no learning","periodic":"Structured learning with moderate effort","continuous":"Faster feedback but higher measurement and process complexity"},"decision":"Use evidence-based retrospectives to evaluate and improve architecture governance controls.","rationale":"A governance system that cannot learn from its own outcomes can preserve ineffective controls indefinitely.","consequences":["Governance changes become evidence-driven","Control failures can generate explicit improvement work","Process quality becomes part of architecture governance"],"implementation_implications":["Define governance review metrics","Capture control failures and false positives","Assign improvement actions","Verify changes after implementation"],"verification_method":"Review governance retrospectives for evidence, identified control gaps, assigned actions, and follow-up verification.","outcome_learning":"Repeated findings should update governance policy, automation, evidence requirements, or decision authority models.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B90-R02","D55-B90-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B90-R02"},{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B90-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B90-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Architecture Governance Learning","subtopic":"Governance Control Effectiveness","concept":"Measurement of governance control effectiveness","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How can architecture governance distinguish useful controls from controls that create activity without sufficient assurance value?","answer":"Governance controls should be evaluated through evidence such as defects prevented or detected, review findings, exception patterns, decision quality, cycle time, recurrence of known failures, and unnecessary rejection or escalation where measurable. No single metric should be treated as a complete measure of governance effectiveness.","context":"A governance process can become bureaucratic while providing limited improvement in architectural outcomes.","objective":"Evaluate whether governance controls provide meaningful assurance relative to their cost.","requirements":["Control objectives should be explicit","Relevant outcome evidence should be collected","Control overhead should be considered","Metrics should be interpreted in context"],"constraints":["Causal attribution can be difficult","Some governance value is preventive and not directly observable"],"prerequisites":["Defined governance controls","Outcome data","Architecture decision history"],"dependencies":["Governance metrics","Audit records","Retrospectives"],"alternatives":["Activity metrics","Outcome metrics","Mixed control-effectiveness assessment"],"evidence":"Control activity volume alone does not demonstrate control effectiveness; outcomes and evidence provide stronger signals when interpreted carefully.","tradeoffs":{"activity_only":"Easy to measure but weakly connected to outcomes","outcome_only":"More meaningful but difficult to attribute","mixed":"Balances operational visibility and outcome evidence"},"decision":"Evaluate governance controls using a combination of control objectives, outcome evidence, recurrence patterns, and proportional overhead.","rationale":"Governance should optimize for useful architectural assurance rather than process volume.","consequences":["Metrics become more outcome-oriented","Ineffective controls can be redesigned","Governance overhead becomes an explicit consideration"],"implementation_implications":["Define control objectives","Collect outcome and exception data","Review recurring false positives and misses","Assess control cost"],"verification_method":"For selected controls, compare intended objective with available outcome evidence and observed governance burden.","outcome_learning":"Controls with persistent low assurance value or excessive friction should be candidates for redesign or removal.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B90-R01","D55-B90-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"INFORMS","record_id":"D55-B90-R01"},{"type":"INFORMS","record_id":"D55-B90-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B90-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Architecture Governance Learning","subtopic":"Governance Failure Analysis","concept":"Analysis of architecture governance misses","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance analyze cases where material architectural problems were not detected by existing controls?","answer":"Governance misses should be analyzed by reconstructing the decision context, available evidence, control expectations, detection opportunity, assumptions, and reason the control did not identify the issue. The analysis should distinguish missing controls from inadequate execution, incomplete evidence, incorrect assumptions, or genuinely unforeseeable conditions.","context":"Not every architecture problem indicates that a governance control was defective.","objective":"Improve governance based on causally useful analysis of misses.","requirements":["The missed condition should be reconstructed","Control behavior should be examined","Root cause categories should distinguish control failure from unforeseeable events","Improvement actions should target the identified cause"],"constraints":["Historical evidence may be incomplete","Causal attribution can be uncertain"],"prerequisites":["Governance records","Architecture evidence","Incident or outcome information"],"dependencies":["Retrospectives","Decision records","Audit evidence"],"alternatives":["Add another review gate","Modify existing control","Improve evidence collection","Accept residual uncertainty"],"evidence":"Governance improvement requires understanding why a control failed or was absent rather than simply adding more process.","tradeoffs":{"more_gates":"May improve detection but increases friction","better_controls":"Targets specific weaknesses but requires analysis","better_evidence":"Improves reasoning but may require tooling","acceptance":"Avoids unnecessary process when uncertainty is unavoidable"},"decision":"Analyze governance misses by cause before introducing new controls or process steps.","rationale":"Unstructured process expansion can increase complexity without addressing the actual failure mechanism.","consequences":["Governance improvements become more targeted","Repeated failure causes become identifiable","Unnecessary control proliferation can be avoided"],"implementation_implications":["Define miss-analysis taxonomy","Capture evidence and uncertainty","Link corrective actions to identified causes","Track recurrence"],"verification_method":"Review governance misses and verify that corrective actions correspond to documented failure mechanisms.","outcome_learning":"Recurring misses should trigger architecture governance redesign or stronger evidence mechanisms where justified.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B90-R01","D55-B90-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B90-R01"},{"type":"USES","record_id":"D55-B90-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B90-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Architecture Governance Learning","subtopic":"Governance Adaptation","concept":"Adaptive architecture governance","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance evolve when system scale, architecture, or organizational structure changes?","answer":"Governance should adapt when the risk profile, decision volume, system criticality, organizational boundaries, technology landscape, or evidence capabilities materially change. Adaptation should preserve governance objectives while adjusting control depth, authority, automation, and review mechanisms to current conditions.","context":"A governance model designed for a small system or centralized organization may become inefficient or insufficient as architecture evolves.","objective":"Maintain governance effectiveness as architectural and organizational conditions change.","requirements":["Governance assumptions should be reviewable","Material context changes should trigger reassessment","Control changes should preserve explicit governance objectives"],"constraints":["Change itself can introduce temporary uncertainty","Governance cannot optimize for every possible future state"],"prerequisites":["Governance model","Architecture context","Outcome evidence"],"dependencies":["Governance metrics","Organizational changes","Architecture evolution"],"alternatives":["Fixed governance model","Periodic governance redesign","Trigger-based adaptive governance"],"evidence":"Governance effectiveness depends on alignment between control mechanisms and the current architectural risk environment.","tradeoffs":{"fixed":"Stable but may become mismatched","periodic":"Predictable but can lag rapid change","trigger_based":"Responsive but requires reliable change detection"},"decision":"Use trigger-based and periodic reassessment to adapt architecture governance to material changes in system and organizational context.","rationale":"Governance should evolve with the architecture it controls rather than becoming a static process layer.","consequences":["Governance itself becomes lifecycle-managed","Control depth can change with risk","Architecture and organizational changes can trigger governance review"],"implementation_implications":["Define governance reassessment triggers","Track architectural scale and risk changes","Review decision authority and automation levels","Preserve governance history"],"verification_method":"Evaluate whether material architectural or organizational changes trigger appropriate governance reassessment.","outcome_learning":"Governance adaptation outcomes should be recorded as evidence for future governance design.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B90-R01","D55-B90-R02","D55-B90-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B90-R01"},{"type":"USES_METRICS_FROM","record_id":"D55-B90-R02"},{"type":"USES_FAILURE_ANALYSIS_FROM","record_id":"D55-B90-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B90-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B90","topic":"Architecture Governance Learning","subtopic":"Governance Closure","concept":"Closure criteria for architecture governance improvement actions","knowledge_type":"CONFORMANCE_RULE","question":"When can an architecture governance improvement action be considered closed?","answer":"An improvement action should be considered closed only when the intended governance change has been implemented, the responsible evidence or control exists, and the defined verification criteria have been satisfied. Recording an action as completed because a task was performed is insufficient when the governance outcome itself remains unverified.","context":"Governance improvement programs can confuse implementation of a change with verification that the change achieved its intended control objective.","objective":"Separate governance-action execution from demonstrated governance improvement.","requirements":["Improvement intent must be defined","Implementation status must be separate from verification","Verification criteria must be explicit","Residual uncertainty must be recorded"],"constraints":["Some governance effects require time to observe","Outcome attribution may remain uncertain"],"prerequisites":["Improvement action","Control objective","Verification criteria"],"dependencies":["Governance retrospectives","Control-effectiveness metrics","Architecture evidence"],"alternatives":["Task-completion closure","Implementation-plus-verification closure","Outcome-based closure"],"evidence":"A governance mechanism being implemented does not establish that its intended objective has been satisfied.","tradeoffs":{"task_only":"Fast but weak assurance","implementation_plus_verification":"Stronger assurance with manageable effort","outcome_based":"Strongest evidence but may require extended observation"},"decision":"Close governance improvements using distinct implementation and verification states, with residual uncertainty preserved.","rationale":"Governance learning requires evidence that corrective actions actually address the identified control objective.","consequences":["Governance records become more truthful","Unverified improvements remain visible","Follow-up measurement becomes explicit"],"implementation_implications":["Track implementation and verification separately","Define closure evidence","Retain unresolved outcome uncertainty","Schedule follow-up when delayed evidence is required"],"verification_method":"Audit closed governance improvements and verify that closure evidence satisfies the defined verification criteria rather than merely confirming task completion.","outcome_learning":"Improvements that fail verification should reopen the governance learning cycle and update the corrective action.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B90-R01","D55-B90-R03","D55-B90-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"CLOSES_LOOP_WITH","record_id":"D55-B90-R01"},{"type":"USES","record_id":"D55-B90-R03"},{"type":"VERIFIES","record_id":"D55-B90-R04"}]}

{"record_id":"D55-B91-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Architecture Governance Closure","subtopic":"Governance Exit Criteria","concept":"Architecture governance activities should have explicit closure criteria so that reviews, exceptions, remediation actions, and decisions do not remain indefinitely open.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What constitutes a valid closure condition for an architecture governance item?","answer":"A governance item should close only when its required decision or review has been completed, applicable conditions have been satisfied or formally accepted, required evidence has been recorded, ownership is clear, unresolved risks have an explicit disposition, and any resulting implementation or follow-up obligations have been assigned. Closure should be evidence-based rather than inferred from inactivity or elapsed time.","context":"Architecture governance frequently produces review findings, exceptions, action items, and conditional approvals that can become stale if closure is not explicitly governed.","objective":"Define deterministic closure criteria for architecture governance work items.","requirements":["Decision or review outcome must be recorded.","Required evidence must be available.","Open risks must have an explicit disposition.","Residual actions must have accountable owners.","Closure state must be distinguishable from inactive or abandoned state."],"constraints":["Closure criteria vary by governance process.","Some obligations remain open after architectural approval.","Emergency decisions may require deferred evidence completion."],"prerequisites":["Defined governance states","Named ownership","Evidence retention mechanism"],"dependencies":["Architecture review process","Exception management","Risk governance"],"alternatives":["Automatic closure after a fixed period","Manual closure without evidence","Explicit evidence-based closure"],"evidence":"The governance principle is that a control state should represent a demonstrated condition rather than inactivity. Explicit closure reduces ambiguity between completed, deferred, rejected, and abandoned governance work.","tradeoffs":["Evidence-based closure requires more process discipline.","Automatic closure reduces administrative effort but can conceal unresolved obligations."],"decision":"Use explicit evidence-based closure states with required closure evidence and unresolved-obligation handling.","rationale":"Governance cannot reliably demonstrate control effectiveness when completed and abandoned items are indistinguishable.","consequences":["Governance records become auditable.","Stale items become detectable.","Closure requires deliberate evidence capture."],"implementation_implications":["Define lifecycle states such as OPEN, IN_REVIEW, CONDITIONALLY_APPROVED, REMEDIATION_REQUIRED, CLOSED, and REJECTED.","Require closure metadata and evidence references.","Detect overdue unresolved items separately from closed items."],"verification_method":"Inspect representative governance items and verify that every CLOSED item satisfies all mandatory closure predicates.","outcome_learning":"Measure reopened items, overdue closures, closure-without-evidence events, and post-closure findings to assess whether closure criteria are effective.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B91-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Architecture Governance Closure","subtopic":"Governance Reopening","concept":"A closed governance decision may require reopening when materially changed evidence, requirements, risks, assumptions, or architecture invalidate the conditions under which it was closed.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should an architecture governance item be reopened after closure?","answer":"Reopening should be triggered when a material change invalidates a decision assumption, introduces a previously unknown risk, changes a mandatory requirement, alters a critical dependency, violates an accepted condition, or produces evidence that the governed architecture no longer conforms to the approved state. Reopening should preserve the original record while creating an explicit revision or reassessment path.","context":"Governance closure is not equivalent to permanent validity because architecture and its operating conditions evolve.","objective":"Prevent stale governance decisions from being treated as permanently valid.","requirements":["Material change criteria must be defined.","Original governance history must remain immutable.","Reassessment must identify the triggering change.","Reopened items must retain linkage to the original decision."],"constraints":["Not every architectural change requires governance reopening.","Overly sensitive triggers can create governance overload."],"prerequisites":["Decision history","Change detection","Materiality criteria"],"dependencies":["Architecture change management","Risk management","Decision records"],"alternatives":["Never reopen closed decisions","Periodic review only","Event-driven reopening with periodic review"],"evidence":"Governance validity depends on assumptions and conditions remaining materially applicable; therefore changes that invalidate those conditions require reassessment.","tradeoffs":["Event-driven reopening improves responsiveness but requires reliable change signals.","Periodic review is simpler but may leave invalid decisions undiscovered between review cycles."],"decision":"Use explicit event-driven reopening triggers supplemented by risk-based periodic review.","rationale":"This preserves governance continuity while preventing historical approval from being mistaken for perpetual authorization.","consequences":["Governance history remains traceable.","Material architectural changes can trigger reassessment.","Review volume depends on trigger quality."],"implementation_implications":["Define material-change predicates.","Link reopened records to their predecessor decisions.","Record trigger evidence and reassessment outcome.","Prevent silent mutation of the original closure record."],"verification_method":"Inject representative material and non-material changes and verify that only defined material changes reopen governance state.","outcome_learning":"Track reopening frequency, trigger accuracy, and cases where post-closure evidence revealed an unrecognized material change.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B91-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B91-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B91-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Architecture Governance Closure","subtopic":"Governance State Integrity","concept":"Governance lifecycle states must be mutually understandable and transition-controlled so that an item cannot appear approved, closed, rejected, and remediating simultaneously without an explicitly modeled composite state.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should governance state integrity be maintained?","answer":"Governance state integrity should be maintained through an explicit state model, permitted transitions, transition prerequisites, ownership rules, and immutable transition history. States should distinguish decision status from implementation status and verification status when those dimensions can evolve independently.","context":"A single status field can conceal important differences between architectural approval, implementation readiness, remediation, and verified conformance.","objective":"Prevent ambiguous or contradictory governance states.","requirements":["Allowed states must be defined.","Transitions must have prerequisites.","Transition history must be retained.","Decision, implementation, and verification status should not be conflated when they have independent lifecycles."],"constraints":["Simple governance processes may not need a complex state machine.","Excessive state granularity can reduce usability."],"prerequisites":["Governance lifecycle definition","State ownership","Transition rules"],"dependencies":["Architecture review","Implementation governance","Conformance verification"],"alternatives":["Single status field","Free-form status values","Explicit controlled state machine"],"evidence":"State integrity is improved when governance transitions are explicit and constrained by predicates rather than relying on informal status descriptions.","tradeoffs":["Controlled states improve consistency but require lifecycle design.","Separating status dimensions improves precision but increases data-model complexity."],"decision":"Use controlled governance states and separate materially independent lifecycle dimensions.","rationale":"Architecture approval does not inherently prove implementation or conformance.","consequences":["Ambiguous states decrease.","Automation becomes more reliable.","Governance data requires stronger schema discipline."],"implementation_implications":["Define enumerated states and valid transitions.","Store transition actor, timestamp, reason, and evidence.","Validate state transitions before persistence."],"verification_method":"Attempt invalid state transitions and verify rejection; inspect valid transitions for required evidence and history.","outcome_learning":"Monitor invalid transition attempts, contradictory status combinations, and manual overrides.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B91-R01","D55-B91-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D55-B91-R01"},{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D55-B91-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B91-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Architecture Governance Closure","subtopic":"Deferred Obligations","concept":"Governance approval may create obligations that remain unresolved after the decision and therefore require explicit tracking rather than being hidden by the approval state.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should governance handle obligations deferred beyond an architectural approval?","answer":"Deferred obligations should be recorded as explicit commitments with an owner, due condition or date, acceptance authority, risk context, evidence requirement, and closure criteria. Approval should indicate the scope and conditions of the decision rather than implying that every obligation has already been completed.","context":"Conditional approvals and phased implementation can require remediation or evidence after an architecture decision.","objective":"Ensure deferred governance obligations remain visible and accountable.","requirements":["Each obligation requires ownership.","The deferral rationale must be recorded.","Risk acceptance must be explicit when applicable.","Closure evidence must be defined."],"constraints":["Some obligations depend on external milestones.","Dates alone may be insufficient when closure depends on an architectural event."],"prerequisites":["Conditional approval model","Risk ownership","Action tracking"],"dependencies":["Exception governance","Architecture decisions","Verification"],"alternatives":["Approve without tracking","Track generic action items","Track explicit governance obligations"],"evidence":"An approval decision and completion of all conditions are separate states; explicit obligations preserve that distinction.","tradeoffs":["Explicit obligations improve accountability but increase governance administration.","Event-based due conditions may be more accurate than fixed dates but are harder to automate."],"decision":"Model deferred obligations as first-class governance objects linked to the originating decision.","rationale":"Untracked conditions are effectively unenforceable governance controls.","consequences":["Conditional approvals become auditable.","Overdue or unmet conditions can be escalated.","Governance workload becomes measurable."],"implementation_implications":["Store obligation status independently from decision status.","Support date-based and event-based completion conditions.","Require evidence before closure."],"verification_method":"Create conditional approvals with deferred obligations and verify that the obligations remain visible and cannot be silently satisfied by approval alone.","outcome_learning":"Measure overdue obligations, repeated deferrals, and obligations that become architectural risks.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B91-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"DEPENDS_ON","record_id":"D55-B91-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B91-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B91","topic":"Architecture Governance Closure","subtopic":"Closure Quality Assurance","concept":"Governance closure quality should be evaluated independently from throughput so that rapid closure is not mistaken for effective governance.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How can architecture governance closure be measured without incentivizing superficial completion?","answer":"Closure quality should be evaluated using multiple measures such as evidence completeness, reopened decisions, overdue obligations, post-closure findings, unresolved-risk aging, and sampling-based audit accuracy. Closure volume or speed should not be treated as a standalone effectiveness measure.","context":"Metrics can distort governance behavior when teams optimize for throughput rather than control effectiveness.","objective":"Design closure metrics that measure governance quality rather than administrative completion.","requirements":["Quality indicators must accompany throughput indicators.","Post-closure outcomes must be considered.","Metrics must distinguish workload from effectiveness."],"constraints":["Some outcomes have long feedback cycles.","Small governance populations can make raw rates unstable."],"prerequisites":["Closure records","Outcome tracking","Audit sampling"],"dependencies":["Governance metrics","Architecture audit","Learning loop"],"alternatives":["Measure closure count only","Measure closure time only","Use balanced quality and outcome measures"],"evidence":"A process metric such as closure speed measures activity, not whether the underlying governance objective was satisfied.","tradeoffs":["Outcome metrics provide stronger signals but require longer observation.","Balanced metrics are more complex than single throughput measures."],"decision":"Use a balanced closure-quality measurement model combining process, evidence, and post-closure outcome indicators.","rationale":"Governance effectiveness requires evidence that controls achieved their intended purpose.","consequences":["Fast but weak closure becomes visible.","Long-lived but justified governance work is not automatically treated as failure.","Measurement requires longitudinal data."],"implementation_implications":["Define closure-quality indicators.","Use periodic sampling and retrospective review.","Track reopened and post-closure findings."],"verification_method":"Compare governance metrics against sampled closure records and determine whether the metrics identify known quality deficiencies.","outcome_learning":"Use metric trends and sampled findings to refine closure criteria and governance workflows.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B91-R01","D55-B91-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"MEASURES","record_id":"D55-B91-R01"},{"type":"MEASURES","record_id":"D55-B91-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B92-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Architecture Governance Evidence","subtopic":"Evidence Completeness","concept":"Governance evidence should be evaluated for completeness relative to the decision or control requirement rather than merely checking whether an attachment exists.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What makes architecture governance evidence sufficient?","answer":"Evidence is sufficient when it directly supports the claim or condition being governed, covers the required scope and time period, has identifiable provenance, is understandable to an independent reviewer, and permits the relevant decision or conformance conclusion to be reproduced or challenged.","context":"A governance record can contain documents without containing evidence that actually supports the governed assertion.","objective":"Distinguish evidence presence from evidence sufficiency.","requirements":["Evidence must map to a specific requirement or assertion.","Scope and applicability must be identifiable.","Provenance must be recorded.","Reviewers must be able to evaluate the evidence independently."],"constraints":["Evidence requirements differ by governance control.","Highly dynamic systems may require generated evidence rather than static documents."],"prerequisites":["Control requirements","Evidence taxonomy","Provenance model"],"dependencies":["Auditability","Conformance governance","Decision traceability"],"alternatives":["Attachment-presence validation","Manual evidence review","Requirement-to-evidence mapping"],"evidence":"Evidence has governance value only when its content and provenance support the assertion for which it is cited.","tradeoffs":["Detailed evidence mapping improves assurance but increases preparation effort.","Automated evidence is scalable but requires trustworthy collection mechanisms."],"decision":"Require requirement-to-evidence mapping for material governance claims.","rationale":"An arbitrary document attachment cannot demonstrate that a specific governance condition was satisfied.","consequences":["Evidence gaps become identifiable.","Governance review becomes more reproducible.","Evidence metadata becomes part of the governance record."],"implementation_implications":["Assign evidence identifiers.","Map each material requirement to supporting evidence.","Record evidence scope, source, timestamp, and verification state."],"verification_method":"Sample governance records and determine whether each material conclusion has traceable supporting evidence.","outcome_learning":"Track unsupported assertions, rejected evidence, and recurring evidence gaps.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B92-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Architecture Governance Evidence","subtopic":"Evidence Freshness","concept":"Governance evidence has a validity period or freshness requirement when the underlying architecture or operational condition can change.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"When should architecture governance evidence have freshness requirements?","answer":"Freshness requirements should apply when the governed condition can materially change over time, when evidence is used to demonstrate current conformance, or when external dependencies invalidate old evidence. The required freshness interval should be risk- and volatility-dependent rather than universally fixed.","context":"Static evidence may remain technically correct while no longer representing the current architecture.","objective":"Prevent stale evidence from being treated as current proof.","requirements":["Freshness criteria must reflect change frequency and risk.","Evidence timestamps must be retained.","Expired evidence must be distinguishable from current evidence."],"constraints":["Some evidence is durable and does not need frequent renewal.","Frequent evidence collection can create unnecessary cost."],"prerequisites":["Evidence timestamps","Change-rate understanding","Risk classification"],"dependencies":["Architecture conformance","Continuous governance","Audit"],"alternatives":["No freshness policy","Universal fixed expiration","Risk- and volatility-based freshness"],"evidence":"Evidence validity depends on the stability of the condition it represents; rapidly changing conditions require more current evidence.","tradeoffs":["Frequent renewal improves current-state assurance but increases collection overhead.","Risk-based freshness is more efficient but requires classification."],"decision":"Use risk- and volatility-based evidence freshness policies.","rationale":"Evidence should remain proportionate to the rate and impact of change.","consequences":["Stale evidence becomes detectable.","Evidence collection can be prioritized.","Governance policies become more context-sensitive."],"implementation_implications":["Assign freshness metadata to evidence types.","Define expiration or review thresholds.","Trigger revalidation after material architecture changes."],"verification_method":"Use synthetic evidence records with different ages and volatility classifications and verify correct freshness evaluation.","outcome_learning":"Measure stale-evidence findings and whether freshness thresholds correlate with actual architecture change rates.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B92-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B92-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B92-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Architecture Governance Evidence","subtopic":"Evidence Provenance","concept":"Architecture governance evidence should retain provenance sufficient to establish where it originated, when it was produced, under which scope it applies, and whether it was independently verified.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What provenance should architecture governance evidence retain?","answer":"Material evidence should identify its source, generation or observation time, applicable system or scope, relevant version or configuration where applicable, collection method, responsible authority, and verification state. Provenance should be immutable or versioned so later changes do not silently rewrite historical evidence.","context":"Governance conclusions can become unreliable when evidence origin and applicability cannot be reconstructed.","objective":"Make governance evidence traceable and auditable.","requirements":["Source identity must be recorded.","Applicable scope must be explicit.","Version or configuration context must be retained when relevant.","Verification state must be distinguishable from generation state."],"constraints":["Some evidence sources are externally controlled.","Highly sensitive evidence may require restricted access."],"prerequisites":["Evidence identity","Access controls","Version metadata"],"dependencies":["Auditability","Security governance","Evidence repository"],"alternatives":["Store evidence without metadata","Minimal timestamp-only metadata","Structured provenance"],"evidence":"Evidence without provenance cannot reliably establish applicability or historical state.","tradeoffs":["Rich provenance improves auditability but increases metadata requirements.","Immutable records improve trust but require versioned correction mechanisms."],"decision":"Use structured provenance for material governance evidence and preserve historical versions.","rationale":"Traceability is necessary to evaluate whether evidence actually supported a past governance conclusion.","consequences":["Historical reviews become reproducible.","Storage and metadata requirements increase.","Sensitive evidence requires access governance."],"implementation_implications":["Define provenance fields.","Prevent silent mutation of historical evidence metadata.","Link evidence to governed requirements and decisions."],"verification_method":"Modify or supersede evidence and verify that historical provenance remains recoverable and linked to the original governance decision.","outcome_learning":"Track provenance gaps discovered during audits and determine recurring metadata deficiencies.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B92-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D55-B92-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B92-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Architecture Governance Evidence","subtopic":"Evidence Contradictions","concept":"Contradictory evidence should be represented explicitly rather than silently selecting the evidence that supports a preferred governance conclusion.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance handle contradictory evidence?","answer":"Contradictory evidence should be recorded as a conflict, with each evidence item retaining provenance and scope. Governance should determine whether the conflict results from timing, scope differences, measurement limitations, configuration drift, or genuine uncertainty. A decision should explicitly state how unresolved contradictions affect confidence and authorization.","context":"Different architecture inventories, assessments, and implementation observations may disagree.","objective":"Prevent contradictory evidence from being hidden during governance decisions.","requirements":["Conflicting evidence must remain traceable.","Scope and timestamp differences must be evaluated.","Unresolved contradictions must affect confidence or decision conditions when material."],"constraints":["Some contradictions are caused by legitimate time or scope differences.","Not every discrepancy requires escalation."],"prerequisites":["Evidence provenance","Conflict classification","Decision confidence model"],"dependencies":["Evidence quality","Decision governance","Architecture conformance"],"alternatives":["Select one source silently","Average conflicting evidence","Explicit contradiction analysis"],"evidence":"Evidence quality requires accounting for disagreement and uncertainty instead of presenting a false single-state representation.","tradeoffs":["Explicit contradiction handling increases review effort but preserves epistemic honesty.","Automatic conflict resolution may be efficient but can conceal important uncertainty."],"decision":"Represent material evidence contradictions explicitly and require disposition or confidence impact.","rationale":"Governance decisions should expose uncertainty rather than convert disagreement into unsupported certainty.","consequences":["Audits can identify unresolved evidence conflicts.","Decision confidence becomes more meaningful.","Some governance decisions may remain conditional."],"implementation_implications":["Assign contradiction identifiers.","Classify conflict causes.","Link contradictions to affected decisions and controls.","Require explicit disposition for material conflicts."],"verification_method":"Provide conflicting evidence with differing scopes and timestamps and verify that the system records the contradiction without silently deleting either source.","outcome_learning":"Measure recurring contradiction types and use them to improve evidence collection and architecture inventory quality.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B92-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"DEPENDS_ON","record_id":"D55-B92-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B92-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B92","topic":"Architecture Governance Evidence","subtopic":"Evidence Access Governance","concept":"Architecture governance evidence must be accessible to authorized reviewers while protecting sensitive information according to its classification.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should access to architecture governance evidence be governed?","answer":"Evidence access should follow classification, least privilege, need-to-know, role-based authorization, and auditability requirements. Governance records should expose sufficient metadata and conclusions to authorized reviewers without unnecessarily distributing sensitive underlying artifacts.","context":"Architecture evidence may contain security details, credentials-adjacent information, proprietary designs, or other sensitive material.","objective":"Balance auditability with information protection.","requirements":["Evidence must have an access classification.","Access decisions must be attributable.","Sensitive artifacts must not be broadly exposed merely for governance convenience.","Authorized reviewers must still receive sufficient evidence to evaluate the decision."],"constraints":["Some governance participants may need conclusions without raw artifacts.","Access controls can complicate cross-team review."],"prerequisites":["Information classification","RBAC or equivalent authorization","Audit logging"],"dependencies":["Security governance","Evidence repository","Compliance controls"],"alternatives":["Open evidence access","Strictly centralized access","Classified evidence with controlled reviewer access"],"evidence":"Governance evidence can itself contain sensitive architectural information and therefore requires controlled access without undermining reviewability.","tradeoffs":["Restricting access reduces exposure but can reduce independent review if over-applied.","Broad access improves collaboration but increases information exposure."],"decision":"Use classified, least-privilege evidence access with auditable reviewer authorization.","rationale":"Governance assurance and information protection are simultaneous requirements.","consequences":["Evidence access becomes auditable.","Review workflows require appropriate authorization.","Evidence repositories require stronger security controls."],"implementation_implications":["Classify evidence.","Enforce authorization before retrieval.","Log access to material evidence.","Provide controlled summaries where raw artifacts cannot be broadly shared."],"verification_method":"Test authorized and unauthorized evidence access across representative classifications and verify both access control and audit logging.","outcome_learning":"Monitor denied access, inappropriate access findings, and review delays caused by authorization boundaries.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B92-R03","D55-B92-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"DEPENDS_ON","record_id":"D55-B92-R03"},{"type":"PROTECTS","record_id":"D55-B92-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B93-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Architecture Governance Effectiveness","subtopic":"Control Effectiveness","concept":"Architecture governance effectiveness should be assessed by whether governance controls influence architectural outcomes and risk, not merely whether governance activities occurred.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance effectiveness be evaluated?","answer":"Effectiveness should be evaluated through evidence that governance controls identify material risks, improve decision quality, prevent or detect nonconformance, manage exceptions, and produce traceable outcomes. Activity counts such as number of reviews or approvals are useful workload indicators but are insufficient as effectiveness measures.","context":"A governance program can be highly active while failing to identify important architectural problems.","objective":"Separate governance activity from governance effectiveness.","requirements":["Effectiveness metrics must link to governance objectives.","Outcome and control-performance evidence must be considered.","Activity metrics must not be treated as proof of effectiveness."],"constraints":["Some architecture outcomes are difficult to attribute solely to governance.","Long feedback periods may delay effectiveness measurement."],"prerequisites":["Governance objectives","Control definitions","Outcome measurement"],"dependencies":["Governance metrics","Risk management","Audit"],"alternatives":["Activity-based measurement","Stakeholder satisfaction only","Outcome- and control-based measurement"],"evidence":"Controls are effective when they achieve their intended control objectives, not merely when they execute their procedures.","tradeoffs":["Outcome measurement is more informative but harder to attribute.","Activity metrics are easier to collect but can encourage volume optimization."],"decision":"Evaluate governance using control effectiveness, risk outcomes, conformance outcomes, and decision quality alongside activity metrics.","rationale":"Architecture governance exists to influence architecture and risk, not simply to generate records.","consequences":["Governance programs become outcome-oriented.","Measurement requires stronger evidence collection.","Some governance effects remain probabilistic or difficult to attribute."],"implementation_implications":["Define control objectives and measurable indicators.","Link governance findings to subsequent outcomes where feasible.","Use periodic independent sampling."],"verification_method":"Map governance controls to intended objectives and test whether collected metrics provide evidence for each objective.","outcome_learning":"Review effectiveness indicators periodically and retire metrics that do not distinguish meaningful governance performance.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B93-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Architecture Governance Effectiveness","subtopic":"False Assurance","concept":"Architecture governance can create false assurance when documented approval is mistaken for actual implementation, conformance, resilience, security, or operational success.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should governance prevent approval from being interpreted as proof of operational correctness?","answer":"Governance records should explicitly separate architectural approval from implementation completion, implementation verification, runtime conformance, and observed outcomes. Each state should have its own evidence requirements so that approval communicates the scope of the decision rather than an unsupported claim that the architecture has succeeded operationally.","context":"A design may be approved but implemented differently, incompletely, or under materially changed conditions.","objective":"Prevent governance records from overstating what approval proves.","requirements":["Approval status must have a precise meaning.","Implementation status must be separate.","Verification status must be separate.","Operational outcome evidence must not be inferred from approval."],"constraints":["Governance processes differ in how much implementation verification they own.","Some outcomes are outside architecture governance control."],"prerequisites":["Lifecycle state model","Implementation verification","Outcome tracking"],"dependencies":["D55 governance state integrity","Architecture conformance","Operational readiness"],"alternatives":["Treat approval as completion","Combine all statuses","Separate approval, implementation, verification, and outcome states"],"evidence":"A governance decision establishes a decision state; it does not automatically establish facts about later implementation or operation.","tradeoffs":["Separated states improve truthfulness but require additional lifecycle tracking.","Combined statuses are simpler but increase ambiguity."],"decision":"Maintain explicit separation between decision, implementation, verification, and outcome states.","rationale":"The architecture governance record must not claim evidence that belongs to a later lifecycle stage.","consequences":["False assurance decreases.","Post-approval verification becomes visible.","Governance records become more precise."],"implementation_implications":["Define independent status dimensions.","Prevent automatic transition from APPROVED to VERIFIED.","Require evidence for each subsequent state."],"verification_method":"Create approved but unimplemented and implemented-but-unverified cases and verify that the governance system preserves the distinction.","outcome_learning":"Track cases where approval was followed by implementation divergence or failed verification.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B91-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B91-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B93-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Architecture Governance Effectiveness","subtopic":"Governance Drift","concept":"Governance processes themselves can drift from their intended control objectives and require periodic assessment.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What is governance drift and how should it be detected?","answer":"Governance drift occurs when a governance process continues to operate while its rules, evidence requirements, decision boundaries, or control behavior no longer match its intended objectives or the architecture environment. Detection should combine policy review, sampled records, outcome analysis, stakeholder feedback, and comparison between defined controls and actual practice.","context":"Governance mechanisms can become obsolete even when they remain formally active.","objective":"Detect deterioration or obsolescence in the governance system itself.","requirements":["Governance controls require periodic review.","Actual practice must be compared with defined policy.","Material environmental changes must trigger reassessment."],"constraints":["Governance drift can be gradual.","Stakeholder feedback can be subjective."],"prerequisites":["Governance objectives","Control definitions","Historical governance data"],"dependencies":["Continuous improvement","Metrics","Audit"],"alternatives":["No governance meta-review","Annual document review only","Evidence-based governance effectiveness review"],"evidence":"A control can remain procedurally active while becoming ineffective when assumptions and architecture conditions change.","tradeoffs":["Frequent meta-review increases governance overhead.","Infrequent review increases risk of obsolete controls."],"decision":"Use periodic and event-triggered governance effectiveness reviews.","rationale":"Governance must adapt to changes in architecture, organization, technology, and risk.","consequences":["Obsolete controls become detectable.","Governance itself becomes subject to lifecycle management.","Review workload increases."],"implementation_implications":["Define governance-control review intervals.","Trigger reassessment after major organizational or architectural changes.","Compare intended versus observed control behavior."],"verification_method":"Compare sampled governance execution against documented control requirements and identify deviations or obsolete controls.","outcome_learning":"Use findings to revise governance rules, eliminate redundant controls, and improve control objectives.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B93-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D55-B93-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B93-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Architecture Governance Effectiveness","subtopic":"Governance Cost Effectiveness","concept":"Architecture governance should account for the cost of controls relative to the architectural risk and decision value they address.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should governance cost be evaluated without reducing governance to financial optimization?","answer":"Governance cost should be assessed alongside risk reduction, decision quality, prevented rework, compliance obligations, architectural consistency, and operational consequences. Cost should inform proportionality but should not override mandatory controls or create incentives to suppress necessary governance.","context":"Excessive governance can delay delivery while insufficient governance can increase downstream risk and rework.","objective":"Establish proportional governance without treating cost as the sole optimization target.","requirements":["Material risk and mandatory obligations must remain protected.","Control cost should be observable.","Governance intensity should be proportionate to decision impact and uncertainty."],"constraints":["Risk reduction is often difficult to quantify precisely.","Mandatory controls may not be optional regardless of cost."],"prerequisites":["Risk classification","Governance cost data","Control objectives"],"dependencies":["Risk governance","Governance metrics","Architecture review"],"alternatives":["Uniform governance intensity","Cost-only optimization","Risk-proportional governance"],"evidence":"Governance controls should be proportionate to the significance, uncertainty, and potential consequences of architectural decisions.","tradeoffs":["More governance can reduce architectural risk but increase delivery friction.","Less governance reduces immediate overhead but may increase downstream remediation."],"decision":"Use risk-proportional governance while preserving mandatory controls and minimum assurance requirements.","rationale":"Governance should optimize assurance proportionality rather than maximize or minimize process volume.","consequences":["Low-impact changes can move faster.","High-impact decisions receive stronger scrutiny.","Governance policy requires meaningful risk classification."],"implementation_implications":["Define governance tiers.","Map tiers to evidence and review requirements.","Measure control cost and downstream outcomes."],"verification_method":"Compare governance effort against risk tiers and inspect whether higher-impact decisions receive stronger controls.","outcome_learning":"Analyze whether governance tiers correlate with review findings, exceptions, rework, and post-implementation issues.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B93-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"CONSTRAINS","record_id":"D55-B93-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B93-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B93","topic":"Architecture Governance Effectiveness","subtopic":"Governance Signal Quality","concept":"Governance metrics and alerts should distinguish meaningful architectural signals from administrative noise.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance reduce low-value governance signals?","answer":"Governance signals should be evaluated for relevance, actionability, severity, novelty, and evidence quality. Repeated signals that do not change decisions or reveal meaningful risk should be consolidated, reprioritized, or retired while preserving material historical information.","context":"Large governance programs can generate many alerts and findings that obscure important architectural risks.","objective":"Improve governance signal-to-noise ratio.","requirements":["Signals need defined purpose.","Severity and actionability must be distinguishable.","Repeated equivalent findings should be correlated.","Signal retirement must preserve relevant history."],"constraints":["Aggressive suppression can hide recurring problems.","Signal quality depends on underlying evidence quality."],"prerequisites":["Governance signal taxonomy","Correlation mechanism","Severity model"],"dependencies":["Governance metrics","Evidence quality","Risk governance"],"alternatives":["Emit every signal independently","Manual filtering only","Correlated and prioritized governance signals"],"evidence":"Governance attention is limited; therefore signals should be prioritized according to materiality and actionability rather than raw volume.","tradeoffs":["Correlation reduces noise but can incorrectly merge distinct issues.","High thresholds reduce workload but can delay detection."],"decision":"Use evidence-backed correlation and risk-aware prioritization for governance signals.","rationale":"Decision-makers need material governance information rather than unfiltered administrative volume.","consequences":["Review workload can decrease.","Signal quality becomes measurable.","Correlation quality becomes a governance concern."],"implementation_implications":["Define signal identity and correlation rules.","Track suppressed or merged signals.","Allow materiality overrides with auditability."],"verification_method":"Feed duplicate, related, and materially distinct governance findings and verify correct correlation and preservation of distinct risks.","outcome_learning":"Measure signal precision, repeated findings, ignored alerts, and material findings discovered outside normal governance signals.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B93-R01","D55-B93-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D55-B93-R01"},{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D55-B93-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B94-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Architecture Governance Learning","subtopic":"Post-Implementation Review","concept":"Architecture governance should include structured post-implementation review to compare assumptions, decisions, implementation, and observed outcomes.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What should a post-implementation architecture review examine?","answer":"A post-implementation review should compare the original context, requirements, assumptions, alternatives, decision rationale, implementation state, verification evidence, operational observations, deviations, incidents or defects where relevant, and lessons learned. The objective is learning and governance improvement rather than retrospective blame.","context":"Architecture decisions are often made under uncertainty and should be evaluated against subsequent evidence.","objective":"Close the architecture governance learning loop.","requirements":["Original decision context must remain available.","Actual implementation must be observable.","Relevant outcomes and deviations must be captured.","Lessons must be linked to future governance improvement."],"constraints":["Some outcomes take significant time to observe.","Attribution between architecture and outcomes may be uncertain."],"prerequisites":["Architecture decision records","Implementation evidence","Outcome observations"],"dependencies":["D54 architecture decisions","Governance closure","Continuous improvement"],"alternatives":["No post-implementation review","Stakeholder retrospective only","Evidence-based post-implementation review"],"evidence":"Comparing predicted consequences with observed results provides a mechanism for improving future decision quality.","tradeoffs":["Reviews require time after implementation.","Delayed outcomes may require multiple review points."],"decision":"Use risk-proportional post-implementation architecture reviews for material decisions.","rationale":"Architecture governance should learn from actual evidence rather than ending at approval.","consequences":["Decision assumptions can be evaluated.","Repeated reasoning failures become detectable.","Governance knowledge improves over time."],"implementation_implications":["Define review triggers and timing.","Compare predicted versus observed consequences.","Record deviations and lessons without rewriting the original decision."],"verification_method":"Select completed material decisions and verify that post-implementation review compares original claims with observed evidence.","outcome_learning":"Feed validated lessons into architecture standards, decision guidance, review criteria, and future decision records.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B94-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Architecture Governance Learning","subtopic":"Prediction Versus Outcome","concept":"Architecture governance can improve decision quality by explicitly comparing expected consequences with verified or observed outcomes.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should expected architecture consequences be compared with later outcomes?","answer":"The comparison reveals which assumptions and trade-offs were supported by evidence, which were incorrect, and which remained uncertain. It should preserve the distinction between predicted outcomes, measured outcomes, and causal attribution rather than automatically treating correlation as proof that the architecture caused an observed result.","context":"Architecture decisions commonly include expected performance, reliability, maintainability, cost, security, or organizational consequences.","objective":"Create evidence-based learning from architecture decisions.","requirements":["Predictions must be recorded before or at decision time.","Observed outcomes must have provenance.","Attribution confidence must be explicit."],"constraints":["Not every outcome is directly caused by the architecture.","Operational environments change between decision and observation."],"prerequisites":["Decision consequence records","Outcome measurement","Evidence provenance"],"dependencies":["Post-implementation review","Decision governance","Learning system"],"alternatives":["Qualitative retrospective only","Compare prediction and outcome","Compare with explicit attribution uncertainty"],"evidence":"Prediction-outcome comparison provides stronger learning when both sides are explicitly represented and uncertainty is preserved.","tradeoffs":["Measurement improves learning but increases data requirements.","Causal attribution can remain uncertain despite good measurements."],"decision":"Record prediction, observation, attribution confidence, and explanation as separate fields.","rationale":"Separating these dimensions prevents retrospective narratives from being mistaken for verified causal knowledge.","consequences":["Governance learning becomes more evidence-driven.","Incorrect assumptions become visible.","Some lessons remain explicitly uncertain."],"implementation_implications":["Capture expected consequences in decision records.","Link later observations to those predictions.","Record causal confidence separately from outcome measurement."],"verification_method":"Review completed decisions and verify that predictions and outcomes cannot be silently overwritten or conflated.","outcome_learning":"Use validated comparisons to update decision heuristics and governance controls while preserving uncertainty where causality is unresolved.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B94-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D55-B94-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B94-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Architecture Governance Learning","subtopic":"Lessons Validation","concept":"Architecture lessons should not become governance rules merely because they were recorded; lessons require validation, applicability boundaries, and evidence strength.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture lessons be validated before becoming reusable governance knowledge?","answer":"A lesson should identify its source decision or event, observed evidence, context, applicability boundaries, confidence, contradictions, and whether it has been independently corroborated. Only lessons with sufficient evidence should influence broad governance standards; context-specific lessons should retain their scope limitations.","context":"Unvalidated retrospective conclusions can turn isolated experiences into inappropriate universal rules.","objective":"Prevent weak lessons from contaminating governance knowledge.","requirements":["Lesson provenance must be retained.","Applicability boundaries must be explicit.","Evidence strength and confidence must be recorded.","Contradictory evidence must remain visible."],"constraints":["Some lessons are inherently context-specific.","Independent corroboration may be expensive."],"prerequisites":["Post-implementation evidence","Lesson taxonomy","Confidence model"],"dependencies":["Knowledge management","Governance standards","Decision learning"],"alternatives":["Promote every lesson immediately","Human-only informal lessons","Evidence-qualified lessons"],"evidence":"Reusable engineering knowledge should preserve context and evidence strength to avoid overgeneralization.","tradeoffs":["Validation delays reuse but improves reliability.","Rapid reuse can accelerate improvement but increases propagation of weak conclusions."],"decision":"Qualify lessons by evidence, scope, confidence, and corroboration before broad governance reuse.","rationale":"Governance knowledge should be evidence-backed rather than anecdotal.","consequences":["Weak lessons remain appropriately scoped.","Strong lessons can inform future governance.","Knowledge maintenance becomes explicit."],"implementation_implications":["Define lesson states such as PROPOSED, CORROBORATED, CONTEXTUAL, CONTRADICTED, and RETIRED.","Link lessons to source evidence.","Prevent unqualified lessons from automatically becoming mandatory standards."],"verification_method":"Submit lessons with differing evidence quality and verify that only qualified lessons can influence broad governance rules.","outcome_learning":"Track lesson adoption, later contradiction, retirement, and corroboration rates.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B94-R01","D55-B94-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"REFINES","record_id":"D55-B94-R01"},{"type":"REFINES","record_id":"D55-B94-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B94-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Architecture Governance Learning","subtopic":"Governance Rule Retirement","concept":"Architecture governance rules should have retirement mechanisms because obsolete rules can create unnecessary constraints or conflict with newer validated knowledge.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"When should an architecture governance rule be retired?","answer":"A rule should be considered for retirement when its underlying requirement disappears, its evidence becomes obsolete, a replacement rule provides stronger control, repeated evidence demonstrates that the rule is ineffective, or the rule creates unnecessary conflict or burden without corresponding value. Retirement should preserve historical traceability.","context":"Governance standards can accumulate over time and become obsolete or contradictory.","objective":"Prevent governance accumulation from producing unnecessary complexity.","requirements":["Rules must have identifiable ownership.","Retirement rationale must be recorded.","Historical decisions affected by the rule must remain traceable.","Replacement controls must be evaluated when applicable."],"constraints":["Some rules implement mandatory obligations and cannot be retired solely for convenience.","Retirement may require transition planning."],"prerequisites":["Rule inventory","Evidence history","Ownership"],"dependencies":["Governance standards","Technology lifecycle","Lessons learned"],"alternatives":["Never retire rules","Periodic manual cleanup","Evidence-driven retirement"],"evidence":"Governance knowledge is subject to change and should evolve when its assumptions or objectives no longer hold.","tradeoffs":["Retirement reduces complexity but can remove controls prematurely if evidence is weak.","Keeping every rule preserves history but increases conflicting constraints."],"decision":"Use controlled, evidence-based rule retirement with historical traceability and replacement analysis.","rationale":"Governance quality depends on both adding useful controls and removing obsolete ones.","consequences":["Rule inventories remain maintainable.","Historical traceability is preserved.","Retirement requires explicit review."],"implementation_implications":["Track rule lifecycle state.","Require retirement evidence and authority.","Check dependencies and downstream references before retirement."],"verification_method":"Retire a representative non-mandatory rule and verify dependency analysis, historical preservation, and prevention of unintended active enforcement.","outcome_learning":"Measure retired rules later reinstated, rules with no observable value, and recurring sources of governance obsolescence.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B94-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"DEPENDS_ON","record_id":"D55-B94-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B94-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B94","topic":"Architecture Governance Learning","subtopic":"Governance Knowledge Feedback","concept":"Validated governance learning should feed back into decision guidance, standards, controls, and review criteria through controlled change rather than silent policy mutation.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should validated architecture lessons influence future governance?","answer":"Validated lessons should be transformed into explicit proposals for changes to standards, decision guidance, review checklists, control thresholds, reference architectures, or evidence requirements. Each change should retain the originating lesson and approval history so that governance evolution remains traceable.","context":"Learning becomes operationally useful only when validated knowledge changes future behavior in a controlled manner.","objective":"Connect governance learning to future governance behavior.","requirements":["Only qualified lessons may propose mandatory changes.","Proposed changes require review and ownership.","Changes must retain links to their evidence and originating lessons.","Previous versions must remain recoverable."],"constraints":["Not every lesson warrants a policy change.","Policy changes can affect many teams and systems."],"prerequisites":["Validated lessons","Governance change process","Versioned standards"],"dependencies":["Lessons validation","Rule retirement","Governance standards"],"alternatives":["Informal knowledge sharing","Direct automatic policy mutation","Controlled learning-to-governance pipeline"],"evidence":"Governance improvement requires a traceable mechanism from observed evidence to changed control behavior.","tradeoffs":["Controlled change reduces accidental policy mutation but slows propagation.","Automatic mutation is faster but unsafe for mandatory governance behavior."],"decision":"Use a controlled proposal-and-approval pipeline from validated lessons to governance changes.","rationale":"Governance must learn without allowing unverified observations to silently become organizational rules.","consequences":["Learning becomes actionable.","Governance evolution remains auditable.","Change management becomes part of the learning loop."],"implementation_implications":["Create lesson-to-change proposals.","Require impact analysis and approval.","Version changed governance artifacts.","Retain links between evidence, lesson, proposal, and resulting rule."],"verification_method":"Trace a validated lesson through proposal, review, approval, publication, and subsequent governance use.","outcome_learning":"Measure time from validated lesson to adopted change and evaluate whether adopted changes improve the intended governance outcome.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B94-R03","D55-B94-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"DEPENDS_ON","record_id":"D55-B94-R03"},{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B94-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B95-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Architecture Governance Resilience","subtopic":"Governance Under Failure","concept":"Architecture governance must remain usable during organizational, tooling, or infrastructure failures without bypassing critical controls.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance operate when normal governance infrastructure is unavailable?","answer":"Governance should define degraded-mode procedures for critical decisions, including minimum evidence requirements, authorized decision roles, emergency documentation, temporary controls, and mandatory retrospective reconciliation. Degraded mode should reduce nonessential process overhead without silently disabling critical authorization or audit requirements.","context":"Governance systems and collaboration tools may themselves become unavailable during incidents or major disruptions.","objective":"Preserve essential governance integrity during degraded operating conditions.","requirements":["Critical authorization boundaries must remain enforceable.","Emergency decisions must be recorded.","Deferred evidence must have explicit reconciliation requirements.","Normal governance must be restored after the event."],"constraints":["Emergency conditions reduce available information and personnel.","Some governance tooling may be unavailable."],"prerequisites":["Emergency governance procedure","Decision authority","Fallback evidence mechanism"],"dependencies":["Incident management","Business continuity","Architecture change governance"],"alternatives":["Bypass governance entirely","Require full normal governance","Risk-proportional degraded governance"],"evidence":"Critical controls should remain meaningful under failure while allowing emergency decisions to proceed when delay itself creates unacceptable risk.","tradeoffs":["Strict normal governance can delay necessary response.","Excessive emergency bypass can create uncontrolled architectural change."],"decision":"Define controlled degraded governance with explicit emergency authority and retrospective reconciliation.","rationale":"Governance resilience requires continuity of critical controls rather than either total bypass or impossible normal processing.","consequences":["Emergency decisions remain traceable.","Some evidence may be temporarily incomplete.","Post-event reconciliation becomes mandatory."],"implementation_implications":["Define emergency states and authorities.","Provide minimal fallback record capability.","Create reconciliation queues after recovery.","Audit emergency governance usage."],"verification_method":"Simulate loss of normal governance tooling and verify that authorized emergency decisions can be recorded without bypassing mandatory controls.","outcome_learning":"Review emergency governance records for repeated failure modes and improve both normal and degraded procedures.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B95-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Architecture Governance Resilience","subtopic":"Governance Continuity","concept":"Critical architecture governance records and authorities should remain recoverable when governance infrastructure is disrupted.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What governance information should be protected for continuity?","answer":"Continuity planning should identify critical decisions, current exceptions, active obligations, ownership information, applicable standards, emergency authorities, and evidence needed to safely continue governance. Recovery should preserve record integrity, version history, and authorization boundaries.","context":"Loss of governance records can create uncertainty about which architecture decisions and exceptions remain valid.","objective":"Maintain governance continuity through infrastructure failure.","requirements":["Critical records must be recoverable.","Recovery must preserve integrity and version information.","Active obligations and exceptions must remain discoverable.","Authorization data must have a recovery strategy."],"constraints":["Not every governance artifact requires identical recovery objectives.","Sensitive records require protected backups."],"prerequisites":["Criticality classification","Backup/recovery mechanism","Governance record inventory"],"dependencies":["Business continuity","Evidence governance","Access control"],"alternatives":["Recover all records equally","No dedicated governance continuity","Risk-based governance continuity"],"evidence":"Governance continuity depends on preserving the records and authorities required to determine the current approved and constrained architectural state.","tradeoffs":["Higher resilience requires additional storage and recovery testing.","Over-classifying every record as critical increases cost."],"decision":"Use risk-based continuity objectives for governance records and authority data.","rationale":"Recovery priorities should reflect the consequences of losing governance state.","consequences":["Critical governance state remains recoverable.","Recovery testing becomes part of governance assurance.","Record classification becomes necessary."],"implementation_implications":["Define recovery priorities.","Protect backups according to information classification.","Test restoration and record integrity.","Verify recovered records preserve historical versions."],"verification_method":"Restore governance records from a controlled recovery scenario and verify completeness, integrity, authorization, and historical traceability.","outcome_learning":"Measure recovery gaps and use failed restoration tests to improve governance continuity controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B95-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D55-B95-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B95-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Architecture Governance Resilience","subtopic":"Governance Recovery Verification","concept":"Recovery of governance records must be tested rather than assumed from the existence of backups.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should recovery of architecture governance data be verified?","answer":"Recovery verification should restore representative governance records into a controlled environment and validate completeness, integrity, relationships, version history, access controls, evidence references, and lifecycle state. A successful backup operation alone is not proof of successful governance recovery.","context":"Governance continuity depends on usable recovered records, not merely backup artifacts.","objective":"Verify that governance recovery actually restores usable control state.","requirements":["Recovery tests must exercise representative records.","Relationships and historical state must remain intact.","Access controls must be revalidated after restoration.","Recovery results must be recorded."],"constraints":["Full production recovery tests may be disruptive.","Sensitive evidence may require sanitized test environments."],"prerequisites":["Backup system","Recovery procedure","Representative test dataset"],"dependencies":["Governance continuity","Evidence provenance","Access governance"],"alternatives":["Backup existence check","Restore testing","Full recovery exercise"],"evidence":"Recovery claims require evidence that restored governance information remains usable and trustworthy.","tradeoffs":["Frequent recovery testing consumes resources.","Infrequent testing increases uncertainty about recovery capability."],"decision":"Require periodic evidence-based recovery verification for material governance records.","rationale":"Unverified recovery capability is an assumption, not demonstrated resilience.","consequences":["Recovery confidence becomes evidence-backed.","Recovery defects become actionable findings.","Governance continuity gains measurable assurance."],"implementation_implications":["Define recovery test cases.","Validate records and relationships after restoration.","Record recovery duration and defects.","Protect test evidence according to classification."],"verification_method":"Perform controlled restoration and compare restored records with known-good source records, including state and relationship integrity.","outcome_learning":"Track recovery failures and use them to update backup architecture, schemas, and continuity procedures.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B95-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"VERIFIES","record_id":"D55-B95-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B95-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Architecture Governance Resilience","subtopic":"Governance Dependency Failure","concept":"Governance processes should identify dependencies whose failure can prevent architecture review, authorization, evidence collection, or conformance verification.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Which governance dependencies should be considered for resilience?","answer":"Dependencies include identity and authorization systems, architecture repositories, evidence sources, service inventories, review workflows, policy engines, communication channels, build or deployment evidence, and human decision authorities. Critical dependencies should have fallback or degraded-mode procedures proportional to their impact.","context":"Governance is itself a system of people, information, tools, and controls.","objective":"Identify governance dependencies that can become single points of failure.","requirements":["Critical dependencies must be identified.","Dependency failure impact must be assessed.","Fallback or degraded procedures must be defined where necessary."],"constraints":["Some dependencies are external and cannot be fully controlled.","Human authority may be inherently difficult to substitute."],"prerequisites":["Governance process map","Dependency inventory","Criticality classification"],"dependencies":["Governance continuity","Access control","Evidence systems"],"alternatives":["Ignore governance dependencies","Document every dependency equally","Risk-based dependency resilience"],"evidence":"A governance control cannot operate reliably when a critical prerequisite is unavailable and no fallback exists.","tradeoffs":["Redundancy improves resilience but adds cost.","Fallback processes may be slower or less automated."],"decision":"Maintain a risk-based dependency resilience model for material governance controls.","rationale":"Governance resilience depends on the availability and integrity of its prerequisites.","consequences":["Single points of governance failure become visible.","Fallback planning becomes targeted.","Governance architecture gains explicit dependencies."],"implementation_implications":["Map critical governance dependencies.","Assign failure modes and fallback procedures.","Test high-impact dependency failures.","Record residual limitations."],"verification_method":"Simulate failure of selected governance dependencies and verify expected degraded behavior and escalation.","outcome_learning":"Use failure observations to refine governance dependency classifications and fallback designs.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B95-R01","D55-B95-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D55-B95-R01"},{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B95-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B95-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B95","topic":"Architecture Governance Resilience","subtopic":"Emergency Governance Reconciliation","concept":"Emergency architecture changes require explicit reconciliation after normal governance capability is restored.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should happen after an emergency architecture change bypasses normal review steps?","answer":"The emergency change should enter a reconciliation process that reconstructs the decision context, evidence, authorization, implementation state, residual risk, and deviations from normal governance. The outcome may confirm, modify, replace, or reject the temporary architecture decision, with all conclusions linked to the emergency record.","context":"Emergency changes may be necessary before full governance evidence is available.","objective":"Prevent emergency governance from becoming permanent unreviewed architecture.","requirements":["Emergency decisions must be identifiable.","Normal review must be scheduled or triggered after recovery.","Temporary assumptions and risks must be reassessed.","Final disposition must preserve emergency history."],"constraints":["Some emergency changes become permanent in practice unless actively reviewed.","Post-event information may differ from information available during the emergency."],"prerequisites":["Emergency governance record","Normal governance restoration","Post-event evidence"],"dependencies":["D55-B95-R01","Change management","Architecture decisions"],"alternatives":["Leave emergency approval permanent","Re-run normal governance without emergency context","Explicit emergency reconciliation"],"evidence":"Emergency authorization addresses immediate circumstances and does not inherently establish long-term architectural suitability.","tradeoffs":["Reconciliation adds post-event workload but prevents emergency exceptions from becoming invisible permanent architecture.","Rejecting emergency decisions retrospectively may itself require migration planning."],"decision":"Require explicit post-emergency reconciliation for material architecture changes.","rationale":"Temporary emergency authority should have a controlled path back to normal architecture governance.","consequences":["Emergency changes receive eventual review.","Temporary risk acceptance becomes visible.","Permanent adoption requires explicit disposition."],"implementation_implications":["Create emergency-to-normal reconciliation states.","Link final decision to the emergency record.","Track unresolved remediation and migration obligations."],"verification_method":"Execute a simulated emergency governance path and verify that recovery automatically produces a reconciliation requirement with preserved evidence.","outcome_learning":"Analyze emergency changes to identify recurring architectural weaknesses and opportunities for stronger normal governance.","code_applicability":"CODE_LIMITED","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B95-R01","D55-B95-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B95-R01"},{"type":"DEPENDS_ON","record_id":"D55-B95-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B96-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Architecture Governance Integration","subtopic":"Portfolio Alignment","concept":"Architecture governance should connect material architecture decisions to portfolio, investment, and capability planning without allowing financial prioritization to erase mandatory technical constraints.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance interact with portfolio planning?","answer":"Material architecture decisions should expose dependencies, capability implications, risk, investment needs, and sequencing constraints to portfolio planning. Portfolio decisions may prioritize among feasible alternatives, but they should not silently override mandatory security, compliance, safety, or architectural control requirements.","context":"Architecture decisions often create investment and sequencing consequences beyond a single technical team.","objective":"Connect architecture governance with strategic planning while preserving technical control integrity.","requirements":["Material dependencies must be visible.","Investment implications must be traceable to architecture decisions.","Mandatory constraints must remain explicit.","Architecture uncertainty must be represented."],"constraints":["Portfolio prioritization may occur under incomplete information.","Not all architectural effects can be financially quantified."],"prerequisites":["Architecture decision records","Capability model","Portfolio planning process"],"dependencies":["Architecture decisions","Investment governance","Risk management"],"alternatives":["Separate architecture and portfolio governance","Portfolio controls architecture directly","Linked governance with explicit decision boundaries"],"evidence":"Architecture decisions can create long-lived dependencies and costs that require portfolio-level visibility.","tradeoffs":["Integration improves strategic coherence but adds coordination complexity.","Strict separation simplifies governance boundaries but can hide architectural investment implications."],"decision":"Link material architecture decisions to portfolio planning through traceable dependencies and constraints.","rationale":"Architecture and investment decisions affect each other but should retain distinct authorities.","consequences":["Investment plans gain architectural context.","Architecture teams gain visibility into sequencing constraints.","Decision boundaries remain explicit."],"implementation_implications":["Expose architecture dependencies and investment implications.","Link roadmap items to architecture records.","Record portfolio decisions that materially alter architectural sequencing."],"verification_method":"Trace a material architecture decision into portfolio planning and verify that dependencies and constraints remain visible.","outcome_learning":"Review whether portfolio decisions repeatedly create architectural exceptions or deferred obligations.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B96-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Architecture Governance Integration","subtopic":"Product Lifecycle Integration","concept":"Architecture governance should align with product lifecycle checkpoints so architecture decisions occur when they can still influence requirements, design, implementation, and operational readiness.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"Where should architecture governance integrate with a product lifecycle?","answer":"Governance should integrate at decision points where architectural constraints and alternatives can still materially affect the product, including discovery, requirement shaping, significant design changes, implementation readiness, operational readiness, and material post-release changes. The exact checkpoints should be risk-proportional rather than uniformly applied to every change.","context":"Late architecture governance can identify issues after expensive implementation commitments have already been made.","objective":"Place governance at lifecycle points where it has meaningful decision influence.","requirements":["Material architectural changes must trigger appropriate review.","Governance must occur early enough to influence decisions.","Operational and post-release evidence must feed back into governance."],"constraints":["Early requirements may be uncertain.","Too many checkpoints can create process overhead."],"prerequisites":["Product lifecycle","Change classification","Architecture review criteria"],"dependencies":["Architecture change governance","Operational readiness","Post-implementation learning"],"alternatives":["Single architecture review","Uniform lifecycle gates","Risk-based lifecycle governance"],"evidence":"Governance timing affects whether identified architectural risks can still be addressed economically and technically.","tradeoffs":["Earlier review improves influence but may operate with incomplete information.","Later review has more evidence but fewer low-cost correction options."],"decision":"Use risk-based architecture governance checkpoints throughout the product lifecycle.","rationale":"Architecture governance should be continuous enough to catch material changes while remaining proportionate.","consequences":["Material changes receive timely governance.","Teams retain flexibility for low-impact changes.","Governance becomes lifecycle-integrated rather than a single approval event."],"implementation_implications":["Define change classes and corresponding checkpoints.","Link architecture decisions to lifecycle milestones.","Require reassessment when material assumptions change."],"verification_method":"Map representative product changes to lifecycle checkpoints and verify correct governance routing.","outcome_learning":"Measure findings discovered at each lifecycle stage and whether earlier governance reduces late remediation.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B96-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B96-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B96-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Architecture Governance Integration","subtopic":"Engineering Workflow Integration","concept":"Architecture governance should integrate with engineering workflows through machine-readable controls and evidence rather than relying exclusively on manual review meetings.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How can architecture governance integrate with engineering workflows without becoming a separate administrative system?","answer":"Governance controls can be integrated into engineering workflows through versioned architecture metadata, automated conformance checks, change classification, policy evaluation, evidence generation, and review triggers. Automation should support governance decisions rather than fabricate approval or verification.","context":"Manual governance detached from engineering workflows can become stale and disconnected from actual implementation.","objective":"Make architecture governance operationally connected to engineering activity.","requirements":["Engineering changes must produce identifiable governance signals where relevant.","Automated controls must have explicit scope.","Automation must not claim unperformed human or runtime verification.","Governance evidence must remain traceable to source changes."],"constraints":["Not every architectural concern is automatable.","Automated checks can produce false positives and false negatives."],"prerequisites":["Version control or equivalent change source","Architecture metadata","Policy evaluation capability"],"dependencies":["Governance automation","Conformance","CI/CD"],"alternatives":["Manual governance only","Fully automated governance","Human-governed automation-assisted workflow"],"evidence":"Automation improves consistency when it evaluates explicit rules and produces traceable evidence, but it cannot eliminate the need for human judgment in all architectural decisions.","tradeoffs":["Automation reduces repetitive work but requires maintenance.","Manual review handles ambiguity but scales poorly."],"decision":"Use automation for deterministic governance checks and human review for decisions requiring contextual judgment.","rationale":"Architecture governance benefits from automation without pretending that every architectural decision is mechanically decidable.","consequences":["Routine conformance can scale.","Human attention can focus on ambiguous material decisions.","Policy maintenance becomes important."],"implementation_implications":["Define machine-checkable governance predicates.","Attach generated evidence to source changes.","Route uncertain or material cases to human review.","Record automation execution status separately from verification outcome."],"verification_method":"Submit compliant, noncompliant, and ambiguous changes and verify correct automated classification and escalation.","outcome_learning":"Track false positives, false negatives, manual overrides, and controls that repeatedly require human intervention.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B96-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B96-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B96-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Architecture Governance Integration","subtopic":"Architecture and Delivery Metrics","concept":"Architecture governance metrics should be integrated with delivery and operational metrics without allowing local metrics to replace architecture-specific control objectives.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should architecture governance metrics relate to engineering delivery metrics?","answer":"Architecture governance metrics should complement delivery and operational metrics by explaining architectural risk, conformance, debt, exceptions, decision latency, and post-implementation outcomes. They should not be reduced to delivery throughput measures because architecture quality often has delayed and indirect effects.","context":"Organizations may already have extensive delivery metrics that can overshadow architecture-specific concerns.","objective":"Preserve architecture governance visibility within broader engineering measurement systems.","requirements":["Architecture-specific objectives must remain explicit.","Metrics must not reward bypassing governance.","Cross-domain metrics should preserve source meaning."],"constraints":["Metrics can become overloaded or contradictory.","Some architecture outcomes emerge over long periods."],"prerequisites":["Architecture metrics","Delivery metrics","Outcome model"],"dependencies":["Governance effectiveness","Portfolio governance","Operational metrics"],"alternatives":["Separate metrics completely","Replace architecture metrics with delivery metrics","Integrated but semantically distinct metrics"],"evidence":"Different engineering dimensions measure different properties and should not be collapsed into a single throughput proxy.","tradeoffs":["Integrated dashboards improve visibility but can encourage oversimplification.","Separate metrics preserve meaning but can reduce organizational visibility."],"decision":"Integrate architecture metrics with broader engineering measurement while preserving their distinct semantics and objectives.","rationale":"Architecture governance needs organizational visibility without losing technical signal fidelity.","consequences":["Leadership gains broader context.","Architecture risks remain separately measurable.","Metric interpretation requires clear definitions."],"implementation_implications":["Define metric ownership and semantics.","Link architecture indicators to delivery and operational context.","Prevent composite metrics from hiding material architecture failures."],"verification_method":"Inspect integrated metric views and verify that architecture-specific risk and conformance signals remain independently recoverable.","outcome_learning":"Evaluate whether integrated reporting improves decision quality without reducing architecture signals to throughput proxies.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B96-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B96-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B96-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B96","topic":"Architecture Governance Integration","subtopic":"Governance Boundary Management","concept":"Integration between governance domains requires explicit authority boundaries so that linked processes do not create conflicting approvals or duplicated control ownership.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should overlapping governance domains avoid conflicting authority?","answer":"Each governance control should have an identified owner, decision authority, scope, escalation path, and relationship to adjacent controls. Integration should share evidence and dependencies while preserving which authority is responsible for the final decision within each domain.","context":"Architecture, security, data, compliance, product, operations, and financial governance can overlap on the same change.","objective":"Prevent governance fragmentation and conflicting control authority.","requirements":["Control ownership must be explicit.","Overlapping controls must have defined interaction rules.","Conflicts require escalation rather than silent precedence.","Shared evidence must retain source ownership."],"constraints":["Organizations may have federated authorities.","Some controls are mandatory regardless of domain preference."],"prerequisites":["Governance ownership model","Control inventory","Escalation process"],"dependencies":["Security governance","Data governance","Portfolio governance"],"alternatives":["Single universal governance authority","Independent governance silos","Federated controls with explicit boundaries"],"evidence":"Overlapping controls are manageable when their authority and interaction boundaries are explicit.","tradeoffs":["Centralization simplifies authority but may reduce domain expertise.","Federation preserves expertise but requires stronger conflict resolution."],"decision":"Use explicit governance boundaries with defined interaction and escalation rules.","rationale":"Shared architectural decisions require coordination without creating ambiguous authority.","consequences":["Conflicting controls become resolvable.","Evidence can be reused without transferring ownership.","Governance integration becomes more predictable."],"implementation_implications":["Maintain control ownership metadata.","Define conflict-resolution precedence where legitimate.","Link related governance decisions and evidence."],"verification_method":"Present a change affected by multiple governance domains and verify that each control has a clear owner and conflict path.","outcome_learning":"Track recurring authority conflicts and use them to refine governance boundaries.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B96-R03","D55-B96-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"CONSTRAINS","record_id":"D55-B96-R03"},{"type":"CONSTRAINS","record_id":"D55-B96-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B97-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Architecture Governance Automation","subtopic":"Policy Evaluation Integrity","concept":"Automated architecture governance must distinguish policy evaluation from authoritative approval and from successful implementation.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What must an automated architecture policy evaluator not claim?","answer":"A policy evaluator should report what it actually evaluated and its result. It should not claim human approval, runtime verification, successful deployment, operational correctness, or requirement satisfaction unless those activities were independently performed and evidenced.","context":"Automation can create false assurance when a policy check is interpreted as complete governance validation.","objective":"Preserve execution and verification truth in automated governance.","requirements":["Evaluation scope must be explicit.","Execution status must be recorded.","Verification must be separate from policy evaluation.","Human approval must not be inferred from machine output."],"constraints":["Some controls can be fully automated while others require human judgment.","Policy evaluation may operate on incomplete snapshots."],"prerequisites":["Policy definitions","Evaluation engine","Evidence model"],"dependencies":["Governance automation","Evidence provenance","Verification"],"alternatives":["Treat policy pass as approval","Separate evaluation and approval","Use layered governance states"],"evidence":"An automated check demonstrates only the predicate that it actually evaluated against the input available at evaluation time.","tradeoffs":["Separating states improves truthfulness but increases workflow complexity.","Combining states simplifies workflow but risks false assurance."],"decision":"Keep policy evaluation, authorization, implementation, and verification as distinct governance states.","rationale":"Governance automation must remain evidence-bounded.","consequences":["Automation output becomes trustworthy as a bounded assertion.","Additional workflow state may be required.","False completion claims decrease."],"implementation_implications":["Record evaluated policy version and input scope.","Store evaluation result separately from approval.","Prevent automatic promotion to VERIFIED without required evidence."],"verification_method":"Run policy checks on compliant and noncompliant examples and verify that output contains only supported claims.","outcome_learning":"Track cases where automated governance output was later contradicted by implementation or verification evidence.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B97-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Architecture Governance Automation","subtopic":"Policy Version Governance","concept":"Automated governance decisions must identify the exact policy and configuration version used because policy evolution can change evaluation outcomes.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why must automated architecture governance retain policy version information?","answer":"Policy version information establishes which rule set produced an evaluation and allows the result to be reproduced or interpreted historically. Without it, the same architecture state may appear to have contradictory governance outcomes because different policy versions were applied.","context":"Governance rules evolve as architecture standards, security requirements, and organizational constraints change.","objective":"Make automated governance evaluations reproducible and historically interpretable.","requirements":["Policy identity and version must be retained.","Evaluation configuration must be captured when material.","Historical policies must remain addressable."],"constraints":["Policies can depend on external configuration.","Long-term policy retention requires lifecycle management."],"prerequisites":["Versioned policy repository","Evaluation metadata","Evidence storage"],"dependencies":["Policy-as-code","Governance evidence","Auditability"],"alternatives":["Store only pass/fail","Store policy name only","Store policy identity, version, and evaluation context"],"evidence":"A governance result is meaningful only relative to the rules and configuration used to produce it.","tradeoffs":["Version retention improves reproducibility but increases storage and maintenance.","Immutable policy history requires stronger lifecycle controls."],"decision":"Record immutable policy identity, version, and material evaluation configuration with each automated result.","rationale":"Historical governance outcomes require reproducible rule context.","consequences":["Past decisions can be reconstructed.","Policy changes become traceable.","Automation metadata becomes richer."],"implementation_implications":["Version policies.","Persist policy references with evaluation results.","Prevent silent replacement of historical policy definitions."],"verification_method":"Evaluate the same input under two policy versions and verify that both results retain their respective policy context.","outcome_learning":"Analyze policy-version changes that cause significant evaluation differences and feed findings into governance change review.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B97-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D55-B97-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B97-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Architecture Governance Automation","subtopic":"Automation Override Governance","concept":"Human overrides of automated architecture governance results require explicit authority, rationale, evidence, and traceability.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should human overrides of automated governance findings be controlled?","answer":"An override should identify the affected automated result, authorized decision-maker, reason, supporting evidence, scope, expiration or review condition where applicable, and resulting risk disposition. The original automated result must remain unchanged so that the override is visible as a separate governance event.","context":"Automation cannot perfectly model every architectural context and may require authorized human judgment.","objective":"Allow justified overrides without destroying automated governance evidence.","requirements":["Override authority must be explicit.","Original result must remain immutable.","Rationale and evidence must be recorded.","Temporary overrides require expiration or reassessment conditions."],"constraints":["Some organizations may prohibit overrides for mandatory controls.","Excessive override volume can indicate poor policy quality."],"prerequisites":["Policy evaluation","Authority model","Exception governance"],"dependencies":["Governance exceptions","Policy-as-code","Auditability"],"alternatives":["No overrides","Silent manual correction","Explicit governed override"],"evidence":"Human judgment can legitimately supplement automated checks, but undocumented overrides destroy traceability and can conceal systemic control failures.","tradeoffs":["Overrides preserve flexibility but can weaken control if poorly governed.","No overrides maximize determinism but may block legitimate contextual decisions."],"decision":"Permit only explicitly authorized and auditable overrides, with stronger controls for mandatory policies.","rationale":"Governance automation should support judgment without creating an untracked bypass mechanism.","consequences":["Exceptional decisions become visible.","Recurring overrides can reveal policy deficiencies.","Auditability improves."],"implementation_implications":["Store override as a separate event.","Require authority and rationale.","Monitor override frequency by policy and team.","Require expiration where the override is temporary."],"verification_method":"Attempt authorized and unauthorized overrides and verify enforcement of authority, evidence, and immutable history.","outcome_learning":"Analyze recurring overrides and determine whether policies, architecture guidance, or exception criteria should change.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B97-R01","D55-B97-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B97-R01"},{"type":"DEPENDS_ON","record_id":"D55-B97-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B97-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Architecture Governance Automation","subtopic":"Governance Automation Failure","concept":"Automated governance must fail safely when required inputs, policies, dependencies, or evidence are unavailable or invalid.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should happen when an automated architecture governance check cannot evaluate reliably?","answer":"The system should report an explicit evaluation failure or unknown state rather than silently treating missing or invalid inputs as compliant. The response should identify the missing prerequisite, prevent unsupported approval where required, and route the case according to its materiality and emergency policy.","context":"Automation can produce false compliance when unavailable data is interpreted as absence of a violation.","objective":"Prevent fail-open governance behavior caused by missing evidence.","requirements":["Missing required inputs must be detectable.","Unknown must be distinct from PASS.","Material unknown states must trigger appropriate escalation.","Evaluation failure must be auditable."],"constraints":["Some low-risk checks may permit deferred evaluation.","Emergency operations may require controlled temporary handling."],"prerequisites":["Input validation","Policy evaluation state model","Escalation rules"],"dependencies":["Evidence governance","Exception management","Emergency governance"],"alternatives":["Treat missing data as pass","Treat every failure as hard block","Explicit UNKNOWN/FAILED evaluation with risk-based routing"],"evidence":"A governance control cannot establish compliance when required evidence is absent or invalid.","tradeoffs":["Fail-closed behavior can interrupt workflows when dependencies fail.","Fail-open behavior can create unrecognized governance gaps."],"decision":"Use explicit UNKNOWN or FAILED states and risk-based escalation; never infer compliance from missing evidence.","rationale":"Governance must not convert uncertainty into unsupported compliance.","consequences":["Dependency failures become visible.","Some workflows may pause or require emergency handling.","Automation reliability becomes measurable."],"implementation_implications":["Validate all required inputs.","Separate PASS, FAIL, UNKNOWN, and ERROR states.","Record dependency and input failures.","Define materiality-based routing."],"verification_method":"Remove required evidence and induce evaluation errors, then verify that the evaluator produces UNKNOWN/ERROR rather than PASS.","outcome_learning":"Track failure causes, false unknowns, and repeated dependency failures to improve governance automation resilience.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B97-R01","D55-B95-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"CONSTRAINS","record_id":"D55-B97-R01"},{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B95-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B97-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B97","topic":"Architecture Governance Automation","subtopic":"Automation Control Testing","concept":"Automated governance rules require regression testing so policy changes do not silently alter established architectural controls.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance automation be regression-tested?","answer":"Governance policies should have representative compliant, noncompliant, boundary, exception, missing-input, and conflict cases. Policy changes should be evaluated against expected outcomes, with material differences reviewed before publication. Tests should identify the policy version and remain traceable to the control requirement they represent.","context":"A governance policy change can unintentionally weaken, strengthen, or otherwise alter architectural controls.","objective":"Prevent regression in machine-enforced governance.","requirements":["Policies need representative test cases.","Expected results must be versioned.","Boundary and failure states must be tested.","Material behavior changes require review."],"constraints":["Complete architectural state space is usually infeasible to enumerate.","Tests can become stale as architecture evolves."],"prerequisites":["Versioned policies","Governance test corpus","Change review"],"dependencies":["Policy version governance","Governance automation","Evidence management"],"alternatives":["Manual policy review only","Syntax-only policy testing","Behavioral regression testing"],"evidence":"A syntactically valid policy can still implement incorrect governance behavior; behavioral regression tests provide stronger assurance.","tradeoffs":["More tests improve coverage but increase maintenance.","Minimal tests reduce maintenance but leave more behavior unverified."],"decision":"Require behavior-focused regression tests for material automated governance controls.","rationale":"Governance policy is executable control logic and should be tested accordingly.","consequences":["Policy regressions become detectable before publication.","Governance controls gain measurable test coverage.","Policy maintenance requires test maintenance."],"implementation_implications":["Map tests to policy requirements.","Include negative and boundary cases.","Compare old and new policy outcomes for material changes.","Retain test execution evidence."],"verification_method":"Change a representative policy and verify that regression tests detect intentionally introduced behavioral differences.","outcome_learning":"Analyze recurring policy regressions and update policy design and test coverage accordingly.","code_applicability":"CODE_RELEVANT","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B97-R02","D55-B97-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"VERIFIES","record_id":"D55-B97-R02"},{"type":"VERIFIES","record_id":"D55-B97-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B98-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Architecture Governance Maturity","subtopic":"Capability Maturity","concept":"Architecture governance maturity should describe demonstrated capabilities rather than organizational labels or process volume.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"How should architecture governance maturity be assessed?","answer":"Maturity should be assessed against observable capabilities such as decision traceability, evidence quality, risk-proportional review, automated conformance, exception management, lifecycle integration, outcome measurement, resilience, and learning. A maturity level should require evidence that the capability operates as intended rather than merely that documentation exists.","context":"Maturity models can become checklist exercises that reward documentation without demonstrating operational capability.","objective":"Create evidence-based governance capability assessment.","requirements":["Maturity criteria must be observable.","Documentation alone must not establish capability.","Capability gaps must be distinguishable from missing evidence."],"constraints":["Maturity frameworks vary by organization.","Some capabilities are contextually unnecessary."],"prerequisites":["Governance capability model","Evidence criteria","Assessment method"],"dependencies":["Governance effectiveness","Evidence governance","Continuous improvement"],"alternatives":["Process-count maturity","Documentation maturity","Capability-and-evidence maturity"],"evidence":"Capability maturity is meaningful when higher levels represent demonstrated improvements in control quality and outcomes.","tradeoffs":["Evidence-based maturity is more rigorous but more expensive to assess.","Simple checklists are easier but can create false assurance."],"decision":"Assess governance maturity using capability evidence and outcome indicators rather than process volume.","rationale":"Maturity should represent actual governance capability, not administrative activity.","consequences":["Maturity assessments become more evidence-driven.","Organizations can identify specific capability gaps.","Assessment effort increases."],"implementation_implications":["Define capability dimensions.","Specify evidence for each capability level.","Allow NOT_APPLICABLE where a capability is legitimately outside scope.","Separate missing evidence from demonstrated absence."],"verification_method":"Assess representative governance capabilities and verify that maturity conclusions are supported by observable evidence.","outcome_learning":"Compare maturity assessments with later governance outcomes and refine capability criteria when they fail to predict meaningful differences.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B98-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Architecture Governance Maturity","subtopic":"Capability Gap Prioritization","concept":"Governance capability gaps should be prioritized according to architectural risk, control impact, evidence confidence, and remediation feasibility.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance capability gaps be prioritized?","answer":"Prioritization should consider the consequence of the missing capability, affected architectural scope, likelihood or exposure where evidence supports it, mandatory obligations, uncertainty, dependency effects, and feasibility of remediation. Priority should not be determined solely by the number of missing controls.","context":"Governance improvement programs commonly identify more gaps than can be addressed simultaneously.","objective":"Direct improvement effort toward material capability deficiencies.","requirements":["Gap severity must be evidence-based.","Mandatory obligations must receive appropriate treatment.","Uncertainty must be visible.","Prioritization rationale must be recorded."],"constraints":["Risk estimates may be uncertain.","Some foundational capabilities enable multiple downstream controls."],"prerequisites":["Capability assessment","Risk model","Dependency analysis"],"dependencies":["Maturity assessment","Governance roadmap","Risk governance"],"alternatives":["Fix gaps alphabetically","Fix highest count first","Risk- and dependency-aware prioritization"],"evidence":"Governance resources should focus on deficiencies with the greatest material impact and enabling value.","tradeoffs":["Risk-based prioritization improves focus but depends on quality of risk evidence.","Foundational controls may have delayed visible benefits."],"decision":"Prioritize governance capability gaps using materiality, mandatory requirements, dependencies, uncertainty, and feasibility.","rationale":"Not all governance gaps have equivalent consequences or remediation value.","consequences":["Improvement becomes more targeted.","Foundational gaps can be identified as enabling dependencies.","Prioritization decisions become auditable."],"implementation_implications":["Record prioritization factors.","Model dependencies among capability gaps.","Reassess priority as evidence changes."],"verification_method":"Provide multiple governance gaps with different materiality and dependencies and verify that prioritization reflects the defined criteria.","outcome_learning":"Measure whether completed high-priority improvements produce expected control improvements and adjust prioritization rules accordingly.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B98-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"DEPENDS_ON","record_id":"D55-B98-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B98-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Architecture Governance Maturity","subtopic":"Maturity Regression","concept":"Governance capability can regress when ownership, tooling, architecture complexity, organizational structure, or evidence quality changes.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance detect capability regression?","answer":"Regression detection should compare current capability evidence with prior validated baselines and investigate material deterioration in decision traceability, conformance, evidence quality, exception aging, review coverage, automation reliability, or learning effectiveness. Regression should be distinguished from intentional capability redesign.","context":"Governance maturity is not permanently guaranteed after a capability is established.","objective":"Detect deterioration of previously demonstrated governance capabilities.","requirements":["Baselines must be versioned.","Material degradation indicators must be defined.","Intentional changes must be distinguishable from accidental regression."],"constraints":["Metric changes can reflect measurement changes rather than true regression.","Organizational changes can legitimately alter governance mechanisms."],"prerequisites":["Capability baseline","Historical metrics","Change history"],"dependencies":["Maturity assessment","Governance metrics","Organizational change"],"alternatives":["Periodic maturity snapshot only","Continuous comparison with baseline","Event-triggered and periodic regression review"],"evidence":"Established capabilities can weaken as dependencies and conditions change; therefore capability evidence needs longitudinal comparison.","tradeoffs":["Continuous monitoring increases visibility but requires stable metrics.","Periodic assessment is simpler but can miss rapid deterioration."],"decision":"Use versioned baselines with periodic and event-triggered regression assessment.","rationale":"Governance capability should be treated as an evolving operational property.","consequences":["Regression becomes visible.","Governance changes require contextual interpretation.","Historical baselines gain importance."],"implementation_implications":["Store capability assessment versions.","Define regression thresholds.","Trigger reassessment after major organizational or architecture changes."],"verification_method":"Introduce controlled degradation in a governance capability and verify that comparison against the baseline identifies the regression.","outcome_learning":"Analyze regression causes and incorporate recurring causes into governance resilience planning.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B98-R01","D55-B98-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B98-R01"},{"type":"DEPENDS_ON","record_id":"D55-B98-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B98-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Architecture Governance Maturity","subtopic":"Maturity Evidence Boundaries","concept":"Governance maturity assessments should distinguish demonstrated capability, partial evidence, unknown state, and non-applicability.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"Why should maturity assessments distinguish missing evidence from absent capability?","answer":"Missing evidence means the assessment cannot establish the capability state, while absent capability means there is evidence that the capability is not implemented or effective. Conflating these states produces misleading maturity conclusions and can cause inappropriate remediation.","context":"Organizations may have weak documentation even when some governance behavior exists, or extensive documentation without actual capability.","objective":"Preserve epistemic accuracy in maturity assessment.","requirements":["Evidence state must be explicit.","UNKNOWN must not automatically equal ABSENT.","NOT_APPLICABLE must be distinguishable.","Assessment conclusions must reflect evidence confidence."],"constraints":["Obtaining sufficient evidence can be expensive.","Capability boundaries can change over time."],"prerequisites":["Evidence model","Capability definitions","Assessment criteria"],"dependencies":["Evidence quality","Maturity assessment","Auditability"],"alternatives":["Binary present/absent assessment","Documentation-based assessment","Multi-state evidence-aware assessment"],"evidence":"A valid assessment requires distinguishing what is known from what is not known.","tradeoffs":["Multi-state assessment is more accurate but more complex.","Binary models are easier to report but conceal uncertainty."],"decision":"Use evidence-aware capability states including DEMONSTRATED, PARTIAL, ABSENT, UNKNOWN, and NOT_APPLICABLE where appropriate.","rationale":"Governance assessment should not convert evidence gaps into unsupported conclusions.","consequences":["Assessment uncertainty becomes visible.","Improvement work can target evidence gaps separately from capability gaps.","Reporting becomes more nuanced."],"implementation_implications":["Define state semantics.","Require evidence for DEMONSTRATED and ABSENT where material.","Track evidence acquisition separately."],"verification_method":"Provide assessment cases with complete, partial, and missing evidence and verify correct state classification.","outcome_learning":"Track how often UNKNOWN assessments become demonstrated or absent after evidence collection.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B98-R01","D55-B98-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"CONSTRAINS","record_id":"D55-B98-R01"},{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D55-B98-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B98-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B98","topic":"Architecture Governance Maturity","subtopic":"Maturity Review Cadence","concept":"Governance capability review cadence should depend on change rate, architectural criticality, control volatility, and evidence stability.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should architecture governance maturity review frequency be determined?","answer":"Review frequency should increase with architectural criticality, governance volatility, organizational change, control failure history, and rapid technology or regulatory change. Stable low-risk environments can use less frequent assessment. Cadence should be revisited when material conditions change.","context":"Uniform annual maturity reviews can be too slow for volatile environments and unnecessarily costly for stable ones.","objective":"Set evidence-based governance maturity review cadence.","requirements":["Cadence criteria must be defined.","Material events can trigger out-of-cycle review.","Review scope should be proportionate to risk and change."],"constraints":["Exact intervals depend on organizational context.","Some mandatory reviews may have externally defined cadence."],"prerequisites":["Criticality model","Change indicators","Historical governance outcomes"],"dependencies":["Maturity assessment","Governance resilience","Metrics"],"alternatives":["Fixed calendar cadence","Continuous full assessment","Risk- and volatility-based cadence"],"evidence":"Review frequency should reflect how quickly the assumptions underlying governance capability can change.","tradeoffs":["Frequent reviews improve detection but increase cost.","Infrequent reviews reduce overhead but increase stale-assessment risk."],"decision":"Use risk- and volatility-based maturity review cadence with event-triggered reassessment.","rationale":"Assessment timing should be proportional to the rate at which governance capability can materially change.","consequences":["Assessment resources become targeted.","High-change areas receive greater scrutiny.","Cadence itself requires governance."],"implementation_implications":["Define cadence factors.","Record rationale for assessment frequency.","Trigger review after material governance or organizational changes."],"verification_method":"Assign different volatility and criticality profiles and verify that review cadence follows the defined policy.","outcome_learning":"Compare cadence decisions with detected regressions to determine whether thresholds require adjustment.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"MEDIUM","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B98-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D55-B98-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B99-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Architecture Governance Audit","subtopic":"End-to-End Traceability","concept":"Material architecture governance should support traceability from requirement and context through decision, implementation, verification, outcome, and learning.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should end-to-end architecture governance traceability connect?","answer":"A material governance chain should connect the originating context and requirements to constraints, alternatives, evidence, decision, authorization, implementation change, conformance or verification evidence, observed outcomes, exceptions, and validated lessons. Each link should preserve identity and version information where relevant.","context":"Fragmented governance records make it difficult to determine why an architecture exists and whether its intended controls remain valid.","objective":"Provide end-to-end governance traceability.","requirements":["Material records need stable identifiers.","Relationships must be explicit.","Historical versions must remain recoverable.","Evidence and outcomes must retain provenance."],"constraints":["Not every low-impact change requires complete traceability.","Cross-system identifiers may require integration."],"prerequisites":["Decision records","Evidence model","Change records","Outcome model"],"dependencies":["D54 Architecture Decisions","Governance evidence","Post-implementation review"],"alternatives":["Document-only traceability","Partial linkage","Explicit lifecycle graph"],"evidence":"End-to-end traceability improves the ability to audit decision rationale and determine whether later implementation and outcomes remain aligned with the original governance intent.","tradeoffs":["Full traceability increases data and integration requirements.","Partial traceability is cheaper but creates investigation gaps."],"decision":"Require end-to-end traceability for material architecture decisions and changes, with proportional treatment for low-impact work.","rationale":"Governance value depends on connecting decisions to what was actually implemented and observed.","consequences":["Audits become more efficient.","Historical reasoning can be reconstructed.","Integration requirements increase."],"implementation_implications":["Define stable identifiers and relationship types.","Preserve versioned links across governance systems.","Detect broken or missing material relationships."],"verification_method":"Trace representative material decisions through implementation and outcome records and verify every mandatory relationship exists.","outcome_learning":"Measure broken traceability links and use recurring gaps to improve governance integration.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":[],"relationships":[]}
{"record_id":"D55-B99-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Architecture Governance Audit","subtopic":"Audit Reconstruction","concept":"A governance audit should be able to reconstruct what was known, decided, authorized, implemented, and verified at the relevant historical point in time.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What does historical reconstruction require in an architecture governance audit?","answer":"Historical reconstruction requires time-aware decision records, evidence provenance, policy versions, architecture or configuration versions where material, authorization history, implementation changes, and verification results. Current state alone is insufficient to explain a historical governance decision.","context":"Architecture changes continuously and later information can differ from what was available when a decision was made.","objective":"Enable accurate historical reconstruction without hindsight contamination.","requirements":["Historical records must be immutable or versioned.","Evidence timestamps must be preserved.","Policy and architecture versions must be identifiable.","Later knowledge must not overwrite earlier decision context."],"constraints":["Historical data may be incomplete.","Some external evidence may not be retained indefinitely."],"prerequisites":["Versioned records","Evidence provenance","Change history"],"dependencies":["D55-B99-R01","Policy version governance","Evidence governance"],"alternatives":["Current-state audit","Document archive only","Time-aware reconstruction"],"evidence":"Historical decisions must be evaluated using the information and controls applicable at the time rather than silently applying later state.","tradeoffs":["Historical fidelity requires stronger retention.","Long retention increases storage and privacy or security considerations."],"decision":"Use time-aware, versioned governance records for material architectural decisions.","rationale":"Auditability requires preserving historical context rather than reconstructing decisions from current state alone.","consequences":["Past governance decisions become explainable.","Retention requirements increase.","Historical policy versions become important."],"implementation_implications":["Timestamp material governance events.","Retain applicable policy versions.","Link implementation and verification evidence to historical states."],"verification_method":"Reconstruct a prior architecture decision using only records valid at that historical point and compare the result with the original governance record.","outcome_learning":"Identify historical reconstruction gaps and improve retention and provenance policies.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B99-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B99-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B99-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Architecture Governance Audit","subtopic":"Governance Contradiction Audit","concept":"Architecture governance audits should identify contradictions between approved architecture, documented standards, implementation evidence, and observed system state.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What contradictions should an architecture governance audit search for?","answer":"Audits should examine contradictions such as approved architecture differing from implementation, policy records conflicting with current standards, exception records inconsistent with active constraints, architecture inventories differing from deployment evidence, and verification claims lacking supporting evidence. Each contradiction should retain provenance and disposition.","context":"Governance assurance can fail when different records describe incompatible versions of architectural reality.","objective":"Detect contradictions across governance information sources.","requirements":["Contradictions must be represented explicitly.","Source provenance must be preserved.","Material contradictions require disposition.","Current and historical contradictions must be distinguishable."],"constraints":["Some differences are caused by legitimate timing or scope.","Automated comparison may not understand semantic equivalence."],"prerequisites":["Traceability graph","Versioned records","Evidence model"],"dependencies":["D55-B99-R01","D55-B99-R02","Evidence contradiction handling"],"alternatives":["Manual document comparison","Single-source audit","Cross-source contradiction analysis"],"evidence":"Governance integrity depends on consistency among decisions, standards, evidence, and actual implementation state.","tradeoffs":["Cross-source analysis improves detection but requires integration.","Automated comparison may generate false positives."],"decision":"Include cross-source contradiction analysis in material architecture governance audits.","rationale":"Contradictions can reveal drift, stale evidence, undocumented exceptions, or governance failure.","consequences":["Hidden inconsistencies become auditable findings.","Governance repositories need compatible identifiers.","Some findings require contextual human analysis."],"implementation_implications":["Define contradiction categories.","Compare records using scope and version context.","Require disposition for material contradictions.","Do not delete conflicting historical records."],"verification_method":"Introduce controlled inconsistencies across architecture, policy, and implementation records and verify that material contradictions are detected and preserved.","outcome_learning":"Analyze recurring contradiction classes to improve synchronization, evidence collection, and governance controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B99-R01","D55-B99-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"VERIFIES","record_id":"D55-B99-R01"},{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B99-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B99-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Architecture Governance Audit","subtopic":"Audit Sampling","concept":"Architecture governance audits can use risk-based sampling while retaining full traceability for material decisions and exceptions.","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF_ANALYSIS","question":"How should architecture governance audits balance complete coverage and sampling?","answer":"Material high-impact decisions, mandatory controls, significant exceptions, and known-risk areas should receive stronger coverage, while lower-risk populations can be sampled according to defined criteria. Sampling methodology, population boundaries, selection logic, and limitations must be recorded so conclusions are not overstated.","context":"Large governance populations may make exhaustive manual review impractical.","objective":"Use efficient audit coverage without creating unsupported assurance claims.","requirements":["Sampling population must be defined.","Selection criteria must be explicit.","Material categories require appropriate coverage.","Audit conclusions must state sampling limitations."],"constraints":["Sampling can miss rare failures.","Risk classification itself may contain uncertainty."],"prerequisites":["Risk classification","Governance population inventory","Sampling method"],"dependencies":["Governance effectiveness","Audit evidence","Risk governance"],"alternatives":["Exhaustive review","Unstructured sampling","Risk-based structured sampling"],"evidence":"Sampling can provide useful assurance when population, method, and limitations are explicit; it cannot establish complete absence of defects in unsampled items.","tradeoffs":["Exhaustive review increases confidence but costs more.","Sampling is efficient but introduces residual uncertainty."],"decision":"Use risk-based structured sampling with explicit limitations and enhanced coverage for material governance items.","rationale":"Audit effort should be proportional to materiality while preserving truthful assurance boundaries.","consequences":["Audit scalability improves.","Residual uncertainty remains explicit.","Sampling methodology becomes part of audit evidence."],"implementation_implications":["Define sampling strata.","Record population and selection criteria.","Track unsampled residual risk.","Escalate material exceptions found outside expected strata."],"verification_method":"Apply the sampling method to a known population containing seeded defects and evaluate whether the audit correctly reports coverage and limitations.","outcome_learning":"Compare sampled findings with later discovered issues to assess whether sampling strategy requires adjustment.","code_applicability":"CODE_NOT_APPLICABLE","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B99-R03"],"relationships":[{"type":"COMPLEMENTS","record_id":"D55-B99-R03"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B99-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B99","topic":"Architecture Governance Audit","subtopic":"Audit Finding Lifecycle","concept":"Architecture governance audit findings require explicit lifecycle management from identification through validation, remediation, verification, closure, or accepted residual risk.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"How should architecture governance audit findings be managed?","answer":"Each finding should capture the observed condition, evidence, affected scope, materiality, owner, required action, risk disposition, target condition, verification method, and final status. Closure should require evidence that the required condition was achieved or that an authorized residual-risk decision replaced the remediation requirement.","context":"Audit findings can lose value when they become unowned action lists without verification.","objective":"Ensure governance findings produce verified remediation or explicit risk disposition.","requirements":["Findings require stable identifiers.","Ownership and disposition must be explicit.","Remediation completion must be verified.","Accepted residual risk must be authorized."],"constraints":["Some findings require long-term remediation.","A finding may have multiple dependent actions."],"prerequisites":["Audit process","Finding taxonomy","Verification capability"],"dependencies":["Governance closure","Risk governance","Evidence governance"],"alternatives":["Track findings as informal tasks","Close on owner assertion","Evidence-based finding lifecycle"],"evidence":"A remediation claim is not equivalent to verified remediation; governance findings require evidence-based closure.","tradeoffs":["Verification adds effort but prevents false closure.","Informal closure is faster but weakens assurance."],"decision":"Use evidence-based finding lifecycle management with explicit residual-risk disposition.","rationale":"Audit findings are governance controls only when their outcomes can be demonstrated.","consequences":["False remediation closure decreases.","Residual risk remains visible.","Finding management becomes auditable."],"implementation_implications":["Define finding states.","Link findings to evidence and corrective actions.","Require independent or defined verification for material findings.","Preserve historical closure evidence."],"verification_method":"Create findings with successful, unsuccessful, and residual-risk outcomes and verify correct lifecycle transitions.","outcome_learning":"Analyze recurring finding categories, reopened findings, and remediation effectiveness to improve governance controls.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B99-R03","D55-B99-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B99-R03"},{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B99-R04"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B100-R01","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Architecture Governance Finalization","subtopic":"Governance Operating Model","concept":"A complete architecture governance operating model should connect authority, decisions, standards, evidence, exceptions, conformance, implementation, audit, and learning into one traceable lifecycle.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What constitutes a complete architecture governance operating model?","answer":"A complete model defines governance scope, decision authority, lifecycle states, standards, review mechanisms, exceptions, evidence requirements, conformance controls, implementation integration, auditability, resilience, metrics, post-implementation review, and learning. Each capability should have ownership, explicit boundaries, evidence expectations, and lifecycle management.","context":"Architecture governance becomes fragmented when individual controls exist without a coherent operating model.","objective":"Define the integrated end-state for architecture governance.","requirements":["Governance responsibilities must be explicit.","Lifecycle and state transitions must be defined.","Evidence and auditability must span material governance activities.","Learning must feed controlled governance improvement."],"constraints":["The operating model must remain proportional to organizational complexity.","Some governance capabilities may be outside scope for low-risk environments."],"prerequisites":["Architecture decisions","Governance standards","Evidence model","Audit model","Learning model"],"dependencies":["All material D55 governance capabilities"],"alternatives":["Independent governance controls","Central governance bureaucracy","Integrated lifecycle governance model"],"evidence":"Governance effectiveness depends on interaction among decision, control, evidence, implementation, verification, and learning mechanisms rather than isolated process components.","tradeoffs":["Integrated governance improves coherence but requires stronger information relationships.","Highly centralized governance can simplify consistency but may reduce local adaptability."],"decision":"Use an integrated, evidence-driven architecture governance lifecycle with explicit decision boundaries and feedback loops.","rationale":"Architecture governance must control and learn across the architecture lifecycle rather than ending at review approval.","consequences":["Governance becomes a connected lifecycle capability.","Cross-system traceability becomes important.","Governance improvements can be evidence-driven."],"implementation_implications":["Maintain a canonical governance ontology.","Link decisions, controls, evidence, changes, findings, and lessons using stable identifiers.","Separate generated, evaluated, executed, tested, and verified states.","Provide audit and learning views without mutating source history."],"verification_method":"Perform an end-to-end trace from an architecture requirement through decision, approval, implementation, verification, audit finding, closure, and validated lesson.","outcome_learning":"Use end-to-end governance evidence to identify systemic control gaps and update governance capabilities through controlled change.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B99-R01","D55-B99-R05"],"relationships":[{"type":"SYNTHESIZES","record_id":"D55-B99-R01"},{"type":"SYNTHESIZES","record_id":"D55-B99-R05"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B100-R02","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Architecture Governance Finalization","subtopic":"Governance Quality Gate","concept":"The final governance quality gate should determine whether the governance system itself is sufficiently complete, evidence-backed, traceable, and operationally bounded for its declared scope.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What should a final architecture governance quality gate verify?","answer":"The final quality gate should verify scope coverage, ownership, lifecycle integrity, evidence sufficiency, exception handling, conformance mechanisms, automation truthfulness, auditability, resilience, metric quality, post-implementation learning, and absence of unresolved critical governance gaps. A quality gate should report unknown or partially evidenced areas explicitly rather than converting them into PASS.","context":"A governance framework can appear complete while containing untested or unsupported capabilities.","objective":"Establish a truthful final quality gate for architecture governance.","requirements":["All declared governance capabilities require coverage assessment.","Critical gaps must be explicit.","Unknown and partially evidenced states must remain distinguishable.","Evidence must support the quality conclusion."],"constraints":["Not every governance capability is applicable to every environment.","A final gate cannot prove future operational outcomes."],"prerequisites":["Governance capability inventory","Evidence coverage model","Audit findings"],"dependencies":["D55-B100-R01","D55 maturity and audit records"],"alternatives":["Checklist completion","Documentation review","Evidence-backed governance quality gate"],"evidence":"A quality gate is meaningful only when each conclusion is supported by evidence within a defined scope and verification boundary.","tradeoffs":["Strong gates prevent unsupported completion claims but may expose unresolved work.","Weak gates improve apparent completion but reduce assurance."],"decision":"Use an evidence-backed quality gate with explicit COVERED, PARTIALLY_COVERED, MISSING, NOT_APPLICABLE, and UNKNOWN states.","rationale":"Governance completeness must be represented truthfully rather than inferred from documentation volume.","consequences":["Final status becomes auditable.","Known gaps remain visible.","Future validation remains distinguishable from current evidence."],"implementation_implications":["Create a governance coverage matrix.","Map each required capability to evidence and verification.","Block final closure for material unresolved gaps unless explicitly authorized as residual risk."],"verification_method":"Run the quality gate against complete, partial, missing, and unknown capability cases and verify accurate classification.","outcome_learning":"Review post-gate findings to identify capabilities that require stronger evidence or better quality criteria.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B100-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"VERIFIES","record_id":"D55-B100-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B100-R03","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Architecture Governance Finalization","subtopic":"Governance Knowledge Integrity","concept":"The final architecture governance knowledge base should preserve provenance, versioning, contradictions, relationships, and validation state so that governance knowledge remains trustworthy for future reasoning.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What integrity properties should the final architecture governance knowledge base preserve?","answer":"Each material governance knowledge item should retain stable identity, source provenance, version context, applicability, evidence level, confidence, contradiction status where relevant, relationships, validation state, and lifecycle status. Historical records should not be silently overwritten because future governance reasoning may depend on what was known previously.","context":"Governance knowledge becomes a reusable engineering asset only when its epistemic and historical boundaries are preserved.","objective":"Protect the integrity of governance knowledge for future retrieval and reasoning.","requirements":["Stable identifiers must be preserved.","Historical versions must remain recoverable.","Evidence and inference must be distinguishable.","Contradictions must remain visible.","Validation state must not be fabricated."],"constraints":["Knowledge evolves and may require supersession.","Historical retention has storage and access implications."],"prerequisites":["Knowledge schema","Provenance model","Versioning","Deduplication"],"dependencies":["Knowledge management","Governance learning","Architecture decision records"],"alternatives":["Mutable knowledge notes","Versioned evidence-backed knowledge","Immutable historical records with supersession"],"evidence":"Reusable engineering knowledge requires explicit provenance and validation boundaries so downstream systems can reason about reliability and applicability.","tradeoffs":["Rich metadata increases storage and schema complexity.","Strong historical preservation improves auditability and learning."],"decision":"Maintain versioned, provenance-backed, contradiction-aware governance knowledge with immutable historical states and explicit supersession.","rationale":"Governance knowledge should remain suitable for retrieval, reasoning, audit, and future learning.","consequences":["Knowledge can be reused safely.","Superseded guidance remains traceable.","Deduplication and relationship maintenance become ongoing requirements."],"implementation_implications":["Use stable record IDs.","Store validation and execution status separately.","Represent relationships explicitly.","Mark superseded knowledge instead of deleting historical evidence."],"verification_method":"Create, supersede, contradict, and validate representative governance knowledge items and verify preservation of history and metadata.","outcome_learning":"Audit knowledge quality periodically and use retrieval errors, contradictions, and stale guidance to improve the governance knowledge fabric.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B100-R01","D55-B100-R02"],"relationships":[{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D55-B100-R01"},{"type":"SUPPORTS","record_id":"D55-B100-R02"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B100-R04","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Architecture Governance Finalization","subtopic":"Governance Lifecycle Completion","concept":"Architecture governance is complete as a lifecycle only when learning from verified outcomes can influence future governance decisions through controlled change.","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_GOVERNANCE","question":"What closes the architecture governance lifecycle?","answer":"The lifecycle closes when a governed decision has been implemented or otherwise dispositioned, relevant verification and outcome evidence has been collected, residual risks and obligations have been handled, lessons have been validated, and applicable lessons can enter a controlled improvement process. Closure therefore includes learning, not merely administrative completion.","context":"A governance system that ends after approval or implementation cannot systematically improve its decision quality.","objective":"Define lifecycle completion as verified closure plus validated learning.","requirements":["Decision and implementation status must be resolved or explicitly deferred.","Relevant verification evidence must be recorded.","Residual risk must have a disposition.","Lessons must be validated before reuse.","Governance changes must follow controlled change management."],"constraints":["Some outcomes require long observation periods.","Not every decision produces a reusable lesson."],"prerequisites":["End-to-end traceability","Post-implementation review","Lesson validation","Governance change process"],"dependencies":["D55-B94-R03","D55-B94-R05","D55-B99-R01"],"alternatives":["Close after approval","Close after implementation","Close after verified outcome and learning disposition"],"evidence":"Governance becomes a learning system only when verified experience can influence future controls without bypassing evidence qualification.","tradeoffs":["Full lifecycle closure requires more time and evidence.","Early administrative closure is faster but loses learning opportunities."],"decision":"Define governance lifecycle completion as verified disposition plus explicit learning disposition and controlled feedback.","rationale":"Architecture governance should continuously improve rather than repeatedly execute the same static process.","consequences":["Governance becomes adaptive.","Historical outcomes inform future decisions.","Lifecycle completion may remain open until sufficient evidence exists."],"implementation_implications":["Track lifecycle completion separately from implementation completion.","Require learning disposition for material decisions.","Link validated lessons to governance-change proposals."],"verification_method":"Trace a material decision through implementation, verification, outcome review, lesson validation, and governance improvement proposal.","outcome_learning":"Measure whether validated governance lessons produce controlled improvements and whether those improvements change subsequent decision outcomes.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B94-R03","D55-B94-R05","D55-B99-R01"],"relationships":[{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B94-R05"},{"type":"EXTENDS","record_id":"D55-B99-R01"}]}
{"record_id":"D55-B100-R05","dataset_id":"D55","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B100","topic":"Architecture Governance Finalization","subtopic":"Dataset and Governance Scope Closure","concept":"The final architecture governance knowledge set should be locked only after coverage, deduplication, provenance, contradiction handling, implementation relevance, and validation-state checks are complete.","knowledge_type":"GOVERNANCE_CONTROL","question":"What conditions should be satisfied before locking the architecture governance knowledge set?","answer":"Locking should require scope coverage review, duplicate and near-duplicate analysis, stable record identifiers, relationship integrity, provenance completeness, evidence-level labeling, contradiction representation, implementation-applicability classification, validation and execution status accuracy, and explicit identification of remaining unknowns or non-applicable areas. Locking freezes the validated version; future changes should create a new version rather than silently altering the locked set.","context":"A knowledge dataset used for retrieval and autonomous engineering reasoning requires stable semantics and reproducible version boundaries.","objective":"Define a truthful final lock procedure for architecture governance knowledge.","requirements":["All records must satisfy the defined schema.","Duplicate and near-duplicate records must be resolved.","No fabricated execution or verification claims may remain.","Known gaps must be explicit.","Stable IDs and relationships must be preserved.","Locked content must be versioned."],"constraints":["Some knowledge may remain inferential rather than externally verified.","Future architecture governance changes will require versioned updates."],"prerequisites":["All D55 batches","Deduplication review","Coverage audit","Validation audit"],"dependencies":["D55-B100-R02","D55-B100-R03","D55-B100-R04"],"alternatives":["Immediate lock by record count","Manual final review only","Evidence-backed quality gate followed by version lock"],"evidence":"A dataset is ready for reliable downstream use when its scope and evidence boundaries are explicit and its records are internally consistent and deduplicated.","tradeoffs":["Thorough locking requires additional validation effort.","Premature locking creates downstream retrieval and reasoning errors."],"decision":"Lock D55 only after evidence-backed coverage, deduplication, provenance, contradiction, relationship, and validation audits pass, with unresolved limitations explicitly recorded.","rationale":"Stable high-quality knowledge is more valuable for autonomous engineering than a larger but inconsistent corpus.","consequences":["D55 obtains a reproducible version boundary.","Future corrections require versioned updates.","Downstream systems can depend on stable identifiers and semantics."],"implementation_implications":["Generate a final D55 lock report.","Run exact, near-semantic, and implementation-level deduplication.","Validate every record's status and provenance fields.","Run relationship-integrity and coverage audits.","Freeze the locked version and create a new dataset version for future changes."],"verification_method":"Execute the complete final quality-gate checklist and confirm that every required criterion has an explicit PASS, PARTIALLY_COVERED, NOT_APPLICABLE, or UNKNOWN state with supporting evidence.","outcome_learning":"After downstream use, collect validated retrieval, reasoning, and governance-quality feedback as a separate versioned learning stream rather than mutating the locked dataset.","code_applicability":"CODE_SUPPORTING","validation_status":"REQUIREMENT_SATISFIED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","evidence_level":"ENGINEERING_INFERENCE","confidence":"HIGH","provenance":{"source_type":"ENGINEERING_METHODOLOGY","source":"D55 Architecture Governance scope and quality specification","status":"INFERENTIAL","verification":"NOT_EXECUTED"},"related_records":["D55-B100-R01","D55-B100-R02","D55-B100-R03","D55-B100-R04"],"relationships":[{"type":"FINALIZES","record_id":"D55-B100-R01"},{"type":"FINALIZES","record_id":"D55-B100-R02"},{"type":"FINALIZES","record_id":"D55-B100-R03"},{"type":"FINALIZES","record_id":"D55-B100-R04"}]}

